The first three equal steps, with successive directions differing by $120^\circ$, trace an equilateral triangle $ABC$ and return to A. The fourth retraces $AB$, ending at B. Thus the set of traced points is the boundary of a regular triangle, but the four-step parametrized walk is not closed: its endpoint differs from its start.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; size(220);
pair A=(0,0),B=(0,2),C=(sqrt(3),1); draw(A--B--C--cycle,Arrow); draw(A+(0.08,0)--B+(0.08,0),dashed,Arrow);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,NW);label("$C$",C,E);label("$100$",(0,1),W);
\end{asy}

A $120^\circ$ arc of radius $r$ has length $\dfrac{2\pi r}{3}$. The radii sum to $\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{2}}{1-\dfrac{1}{2}}=1$. Hence the length is $\boxed{\dfrac{2\pi}{3}}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; size(220);
pair O=(0,0);draw(arc(O,1,0,120));draw(O--dir(0),dashed);draw(O--dir(120),dashed);label("$O$",O,SW);label("$r$",(0.5,0),S);label("$120^\circ$",(0.25,0.25));label("$A$",dir(0),E);label("$B$",dir(120),NW);
\end{asy}

Pythagoras gives $b^2+p^2=(p+1)^2$, so $b^2=2p+1$. Thus $b$ is odd; write $b=2k+1$. Then $p=2k(k+1)$ and the hypotenuse is $2k(k+1)+1$, for $k\ge 1$. Conversely these lengths satisfy Pythagoras and $b\le p$, so they are all solutions. The legs $1,2$ instead give hypotenuse $\sqrt 5$, not $3$, because $1^2+2^2=5\ne 9$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; size(220);
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(0,3);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw((0.2,0)--(0.2,0.2)--(0,0.2));label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NW);label("$p$",(2,0),S);label("$b$",(0,1.5),W);label("$p+1$",(2,1.5),NE);
\end{asy}

Write $AB=BC=a$, $AC=CD=b$, and $\angle DAB=\theta$. Coordinates $A=(0,0)$, $D=(a+b,0)$, $B=(a\cos\theta,a\sin\theta)$, $C=(a+a\cos\theta,a\sin\theta)$ give $b^2=2a^2(1+\cos\theta)$ from $AC$, and $a^2=2ab\cos\theta$ from $CD=b$. With $t=\dfrac{b}{a}>0$, these imply $t^3-2t-1=0=(t+1)(t^2-t-1)$, hence $t=\dfrac{1+\sqrt 5}{2}$ and $\cos\theta=\dfrac{\sqrt 5-1}{4}=\cos 72^\circ$. Thus $\angle A=72^\circ$, $\angle B=108^\circ$. Triangle $ABC$ is isosceles, so $\angle BAC=36^\circ$; consequently $\angle CAD=36^\circ$. Since $AC=CD$, $\angle D=36^\circ$, and $\angle C=144^\circ$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; size(220);
real a=1,b=(1+sqrt(5))/2;pair A=(0,0),D=(a+b,0),B=dir(72),C=B+(1,0);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--C,dashed);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,NW);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,SE);
\end{asy}

The dog's arc overlaps the fixed hand arc exactly when its starting point lies in an arc of length $0.2$. Thus every independent throw has probability $\dfrac{1}{5}$ of first contact, conditional on no earlier contact. The waiting time is geometric with expectation $\boxed 5$. Saliva on other arcs does not affect first contact with a fixed hand position.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; size(220);
draw(circle((0,0),1));draw(arc((0,0),1,0,36),red+linewidth(2));draw(arc((0,0),1,25,61),blue+linewidth(2));label("hand arc",dir(10),E);label("bite arc",dir(60),N);dot((0,0));label("$O$",(0,0),S);
\end{asy}

Place the rectangle at $A=(0,0)$, $B=(w,0)$, $C=(w,h)$, $D=(0,h)$, with $w=8.5,h=11$. The fold is $wx+hy=\dfrac{w^2+h^2}{2}$, meeting $AD$ at $P$ and $BC$ at $Q$. Set $t=BQ=DP=\dfrac{h^2-w^2}{2h}$. Reflection sends $A$ to $C$ and $B$ to $B\prime$, outside the right edge. The resulting boundary is $PDCB\prime Q$: its two horizontal/reflected-width edges have length $w$, two short edges length $t$, and fold edge $PQ$ has length $\sqrt{w^2+(\dfrac{w^2}{h})^2}=\dfrac{w\sqrt{w^2+h^2}}{h}$. Hence\[P=2w+\dfrac{h^2-w^2}{h}+\dfrac{w\sqrt{w^2+h^2}}{h}\approx 32.174,\]which rounds to $\boxed{32.2\text{ inches}}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; size(220);
real w=8.5,h=11,S=w*w+h*h,t=(h*h-w*w)/(2*h);pair A=(0,0),B=(w,0),C=(w,h),D=(0,h),P=(0,h-t),Q=(w,t),Bp=B+((h*h-w*w)/S)*(w,h);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,gray+dashed);draw(P--D--C--Bp--Q--cycle);draw(P--Q,dashed);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$P$",P,W);label("$Q$",Q,S);label("$B\prime$",Bp,E);
\end{asy}

Draw diagonal $AC$. Since $AD=DC$, $\angle DAC=\angle ACD=35^\circ$. Thus $\angle ACB=95^\circ$. By the sine rule in $ADC$, $AC=2\sin 55^\circ$ after scaling $AD=DC=CB=1$. In $ABC$, use the sine rule with $\angle ABC=85^\circ-\angle BAC$. It gives $2\sin 55^\circ\sin\angle BAC=\sin(85^\circ-\angle BAC)$. Expanding and solving, $\tan\angle BAC=\dfrac{\sin 85^\circ}{2\sin 55^\circ+\cos 85^\circ}=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt 3}$, using $2\sin 55^\circ+\cos 85^\circ=\sqrt 3\sin 85^\circ$. Since $0<\angle BAC<85^\circ$, it equals $30^\circ$, and $\boxed{\angle ABC=55^\circ}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; size(220);
pair D=(0,0),C=(1,0),A=dir(110),B=C+dir(50); draw(A--B--C--D--cycle); draw(A--C,dashed); label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,NE);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,SW); label("$110^\circ$",D+(0.13,0.18));label("$130^\circ$",C+(-0.05,0.15));
\end{asy}

The base has length $2$ on the $x$-axis. Every point on the circle has $1\le y\le3$, so its altitude to that line is at most $3$. Equality is attained at $(3,3)$. Hence the maximum area is $\dfrac{1}{2}(2)(3)=\boxed3$.
\begin{asy}
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(2,0),O=(3,2),C=(3,3),H=(3,0);draw(circle(O,1));draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(C--H,dashed);dot(O);label("$A(0,0)$",A,SW);label("$B(2,0)$",B,S);label("$O(3,2)$",O,E);label("$C(3,3)$",C,N);label("$H$",H,S);draw(O--C,dashed);label("$1$",(O+C)/2,W);
\end{asy}

For side length $n\ge2$, the four counts are $(n-2)^3$, $6(n-2)^2$, $12(n-2)$, and $8$. The first three functions are strictly increasing for integer $n\ge2$, so Cerena's, Faith's, and Edna's side lengths can each be recovered uniquely. The last count is always eight corners, so Veronica's report does not determine her side length. Even excluding the three other side lengths leaves infinitely many possibilities. The recoverable cubes are therefore those of $\boxed{\text{Cerena, Faith and Edna}}$.
\begin{asy}
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(3,0),C=(3,3),D=(0,3),v=(1.2,.8);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(B--(B+v)--(C+v)--C);draw(D--(D+v)--(C+v));draw(A--(A+v)--(B+v),dashed);draw((A+v)--(D+v),dashed);
for(int i=1;i<3;++i){draw((i,0)--(i,3),gray);draw((0,i)--(3,i),gray);draw((3,i)--((3,i)+v),gray);draw((i,3)--((i,3)+v),gray);}
label("$n$",(1.5,0),S);label("corner: 3 faces",C+v,NE);label("edge: 2 faces",B+.5v,SE);label("face interior: 1 face",(1.5,1.5));
\end{asy}

The small cubes have $0,1,2,3$ painted faces in counts $1,6,12,8$. A one-painted-face cube works if that face remains visible, with probability $\dfrac{5}{6}$. A two-painted-face cube works if a painted face is hidden, with probability $\dfrac{2}{6}$. Zero- and three-painted-face cubes cannot work. Thus the probability is $\dfrac{6}{27}\dfrac{5}{6}+\dfrac{12}{27}\dfrac{2}{6}=\boxed{\dfrac{1}{3}}$.
\begin{asy}
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(3,0),C=(3,3),D=(0,3),v=(1.2,.8);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(B--(B+v)--(C+v)--C);draw(D--(D+v)--(C+v));draw(A--(A+v)--(B+v),dashed);draw((A+v)--(D+v),dashed);
for(int i=1;i<3;++i){draw((i,0)--(i,3),gray);draw((0,i)--(3,i),gray);draw((3,i)--((3,i)+v),gray);draw((i,3)--((i,3)+v),gray);}
label("$n$",(1.5,0),S);label("corner: 3 faces",C+v,NE);label("edge: 2 faces",B+.5v,SE);label("face interior: 1 face",(1.5,1.5));
\end{asy}

Choose coordinates $A=0$ and $F=73$ along the line. Then $C=26$, $D=73-33=40$, $B=40-22=18$, and $E=26+31=57$, giving $BE=39$ and $CD=14$. Write $G=(x,h)$ with the line as the horizontal axis. Subtracting $(x-40)^2+h^2=900$ from $(x-26)^2+h^2=1600$ gives $28x-924=700$, so $x=58$. Therefore $h^2=1600-32^2=576$ and $|h|=24$. Reflection across the line does not affect area. Thus $[BGE]=\dfrac{1}{2}(39)(24)=\boxed{468}$. The sketch shows $h>0$; the reflected configuration is congruent.
\begin{asy}
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(18,0),C=(26,0),D=(40,0),E=(57,0),F=(73,0),G=(58,24),H=(58,0);draw(A--F);draw(B--G--E);draw(C--G--D,dashed);draw(G--H,dashed);pair[] P={A,B,C,D,E,F};string[] N={"A","B","C","D","E","F"};for(int i=0;i<6;++i){dot(P[i]);label("$"+N[i]+"$",P[i],S);}label("$G$",G,N);label("$H$",H,SE);label("$40$",(C+G)/2,NW);label("$30$",(D+G)/2,E);
\end{asy}

Drop perpendiculars $AE$ and $BF$ to $CD$. Since the two lower angles are acute, $D,E,F,C$ occur in that order. Let $h=AE=8\sin65^\circ$. Then $DE=8\cos65^\circ$, $FC=h\cot72^\circ$ and $BC=\dfrac{h}{\sin72^\circ}$. Thus $CD=11+8\cos65^\circ+h\cot72^\circ$. Consequently the perimeter is $\boxed{30+8\cos65^\circ+8\sin65^\circ\cot72^\circ+\dfrac{8\sin65^\circ}{\sin72^\circ}}$, and the area is $\boxed{4\sin65^\circ(22+8\cos65^\circ+8\sin65^\circ\cot72^\circ)}$. These formulas use degrees throughout.
\begin{asy}
size(7cm);
real h=8*sin(65*pi/180),u=8*cos(65*pi/180),v=h/tan(72*pi/180);pair D=(0,0),A=(u,h),B=A+(11,0),C=(u+11+v,0),E=(u,0),F=(u+11,0);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--E,dashed);draw(B--F,dashed);label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,NE);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,SW);label("$E$",E,S);label("$F$",F,S);label("$8$",(A+D)/2,NW);label("$11$",(A+B)/2,N);label("$65^\circ$",D+(.8,.4),NE);label("$72^\circ$",C+(-.9,.35),NW);label("$h$",(A+E)/2,E);
\end{asy}

Triangles $AXZ$ and $AYZ$ have $AX=AY$ by construction, $XZ=YZ$ because the arc radii are equal, and common side $AZ$. They are congruent by SSS. Thus $\angle XAZ=\angle ZAY$, which proves that $AZ$ is the angle bisector.
\begin{asy}
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),X=3*dir(15),Y=3*dir(75),Z=X+Y;draw(A--4.8*dir(15));draw(A--4.8*dir(75));draw(arc(A,3,15,75),gray);draw(X--Z--Y,dashed);draw(A--Z);dot(A);dot(X);dot(Y);dot(Z);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$X$",X,S);label("$Y$",Y,NW);label("$Z$",Z,NE);
\end{asy}

Since $CI$ bisects angle $C$ and $IX\parallel BC$, the angles $XCI$ and $CIX$ are equal, so $XI=XC$. Similarly, $YI=YB$. Therefore $XY=XI+IY=XC+YB$, and the perimeter is $AX+AY+XY=(AX+XC)+(AY+YB)=AC+AB=\boxed{30}$.
\begin{asy}
size(7cm);
pair B=(0,0),C=(24,0),A=(8.25,sqrt(144-8.25^2));pair I=(24*A+18*B+12*C)/54;real t=(A.y-I.y)/A.y;pair X=A+t*(C-A),Y=A+t*(B-A);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(X--Y);draw(B--I--C,dashed);dot(I);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$X$",X,NE);label("$Y$",Y,NW);label("$I$",I,S);label("$12$",(A+B)/2,NW);label("$18$",(A+C)/2,NE);label("$24$",(B+C)/2,S);
\end{asy}

Each element may be included or excluded, so $a=2^9=512$. No three octagon vertices are collinear, so $b=\binom83=56$. The quadratic factors as $(x-2)(x-3)=0$, giving $c=2^2+3^2=13$. Therefore $(a+b+c)^2=581^2=\boxed{337561}$. The dashed triangle is one of the vertex choices.
\begin{asy}
size(7cm);
pair[] V;for(int i=0;i<8;++i){V.push(dir(45*i));label("$"+string(i+1)+"$",V[i],dir(45*i));}draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--V[6]--V[7]--cycle);draw(V[0]--V[2]--V[5]--cycle,dashed);
\end{asy}

The areas are $A_1=n\binom nm$ and $A_2=n^2\binom{2n}{m}$. Hence $\boxed{\dfrac{A_1}{A_2}=\dfrac{n!(2n-m)!}{n(n-m)!(2n)!}}$. This follows by substituting the factorial expressions and cancelling $m!$. It also applies when $m=0$, giving $\dfrac{1}{n}$. The schematic rectangles below show the side labels; they are not drawn to scale.
\begin{asy}
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(3,0),C=(3,1.5),D=(0,1.5);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle); label("$n$",(1.5,0),S); label("$\binom{n}{m}$",(0,.75),W);
pair E=(5,0),F=(9,0),G=(9,2.5),H=(5,2.5);
draw(E--F--G--H--cycle); label("$n^2$",(7,0),S); label("$\binom{2n}{m}$",(9,1.25),E);
\end{asy}

For fixed $x$, the admissible $(y,z)$ occupy a right triangle with legs $x$, so the conditional probability is $\dfrac{x^2}{2}$. Integrating over $x$ gives $\int_0^1\dfrac{x^2}{2}\,dx=\boxed{\dfrac{1}{6}}$.
\begin{asy}
size(6cm); pair O=(0,0),A=(3,0),B=(0,3); filldraw(O--A--B--cycle,lightgray,black); draw((-0.3,0)--(3.6,0),Arrow);draw((0,-0.3)--(0,3.6),Arrow);label("$y$",(3.6,0),E);label("$z$",(0,3.6),N);label("$0$",O,SW);label("$x$",A,S);label("$x$",B,W);label("$y+z=x$",(1.5,1.5),NE);
\end{asy}

Put $D=(0,0)$ and $C=(10,0)$. Symmetry makes the horizontal offset of each leg $3$, and the height is $\sqrt{5^2-3^2}=4$. Thus $A=(3,4)$ and $B=(7,4)$. Since $B$ is the midpoint of $DE$, $E=(14,8)$. The right angle at $F$ and the horizontal line $DF$ give $F=(14,0)$. Hence $CF=14-10=\boxed4$, choice (D).
\begin{asy}
import geometry;
size(240);
pair D=(0,0),C=(10,0),F=(14,0),E=(14,8),A=(3,4),B=(7,4);draw(D--E--F--D);draw(D--A--B--C);label("$D$",D,SW);label("$C$",C,S);label("$F$",F,SE);label("$E$",E,N);label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,NW);
\end{asy}

Write $r=3k,h=4k$. The slant height is $5k$ by Pythagoras, so the curved area is $\pi r\ell=15\pi k^2=375\pi$. Thus $k=5$, $r=15$, and $h=20$. The volume is $\dfrac{1}{3}\pi r^2h=\boxed{1500\pi\text{ cm}^3}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry;
size(240);
pair A=(0,4),O=(0,0),R=(3,0);draw(ellipse(O,3,.6));draw((-3,0)--A--R);draw(A--O--R,dashed);label("$h=4k$",(0,2),W);label("$r=3k$",(1.5,0),S);label("$5k$",(1.5,2),E);label("$O$",O,SW);
\end{asy}

If the included angle is $\theta$, the area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot9\cdot12\sin\theta=54\sin\theta$. Thus $\sin\theta=1$. Since $0<\theta<180^\circ$, it follows that $\theta=90^\circ$. The third side is therefore $\sqrt{9^2+12^2}=\boxed{15}$, and the right triangle indeed has area $54$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry;
