Choose coordinates with circle $x^2+y^2=R^2$, $A=(u,v)$, $B=(u,-v)$, $u>0$, and $u^2+v^2=R^2$. Write $D=(x,y)$. The tangent lines are $uX+vY=R^2$ and $uX-vY=R^2$. The product of the distances from $D$ to these lines is\[DG\cdot DF=\dfrac{(R^2-ux-vy)(R^2-ux+vy)}{R^2}=\dfrac{(R^2-ux)^2-v^2y^2}{R^2}.\]Using $y^2=R^2-x^2$ and $v^2=R^2-u^2$, the numerator simplifies to $R^2(x-u)^2$. Since $AB$ is $X=u$, this says $DG\cdot DF=DE^2$. Hence $DE=\sqrt{9\cdot4}=\boxed6$.
\begin{asy}
