The hypotenuse is $BC=2$. Set $B=(0,0)$, $C=(2,0)$, and $A=(\dfrac{1}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2})$. The circle has centre $(1,0)$ and radius $1$; the central angles of $A,B,C$ are $120^\circ,180^\circ,0^\circ$. Therefore $M=(\dfrac{3}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2})$ and $N=(1-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2},\dfrac{1}{2})$. The horizontal base $AM$ has length $1$ and the altitude from $N$ is $\dfrac{\sqrt3-1}{2}$. The area is $\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt3-1}{4}}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry;
size(240);
pair B=(0,0),C=(2,0),O=(1,0),A=O+dir(120),M=O+dir(60),N=O+dir(150);draw(arc(O,1,0,180));draw(B--C--A--B);draw(A--N--M--A,blue);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$M$",M,NE);label("$N$",N,W);
\end{asy}

Let $AD=b$, $BD=d$, and $AE:EB=b:d$ by the angle-bisector theorem in triangle $ADB$. Put $t=\dfrac{AE}{AB}=\dfrac{b}{b+d}$. Use vectors with $A=0,B=\mathbf u,D=\mathbf v$, so $C=\mathbf u+\mathbf v$ and $E=t\mathbf u$. Solving the line intersections gives $F=\mathbf u-\dfrac{1-t}{t}\mathbf v$ and $G=-\dfrac{t}{1-t}\mathbf v$. Thus $BF=\dfrac{1-t}{t}b=d$, while $AG=\dfrac{t}{1-t}b=\dfrac{b^2}{d}$. Their product is $b^2=BC^2$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry;
size(240);
