Scale so $BM=AC=1$ and put $M=(0,0)$, $A=(a,0)$ with $a>0$, $B=(u,t)$ and $t>0$. Then $BH=t=CM$, so $u^2+t^2=1$ and $C=(-tu,-t^2)$. Because $AC=1$ and $\angle MAC=30^\circ$, the vertical separation of $A,C$ is $\dfrac{1}{2}$. Thus $t^2=\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $u=\pm\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}$. The ray $CA$ has direction $30^\circ$ from the positive horizontal axis. The ray $CB$ has direction $45^\circ$ if $u>0$ and $135^\circ$ if $u<0$. Hence $\angle ACB$ is $\boxed{15^\circ\text{ or }105^\circ}$. Both occur: take $t=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}$, the two signs of $u$, and $a=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}-tu>0$.
\begin{asy}
