(a) Let $A=(0,0)$, $B=(s,0)$, $C=(\dfrac{s}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt3s}{2})$. A circle through the origin with center $O=(u,v)$ has equation $x^2+y^2-2ux-2vy=0$. Substituting $D=(10,0)$ and $E=(\dfrac{11}{2},\dfrac{11\sqrt3}{2})$ gives $u=5,v=2\sqrt3$, so its radius is $\sqrt{37}$. The line $BC$ has equation $\sqrt3x+y-\sqrt3s=0$. Tangency implies $\dfrac{\sqrt3|7-s|}{2}=\sqrt{37}$. Since $s\ge11$, $\boxed{s=7+\dfrac{2\sqrt{111}}{3}}$.
(b) The opposite right angles make $PQRS$ cyclic with diameter $PR$. Its angle at $P$ is $120^\circ$, so the cosine law in $PQS$ gives $QS^2=3^2+4^2-2(3)(4)\cos120^\circ=37$. The extended sine law in $QRS$ gives $QS=PR\sin60^\circ$, hence $\boxed{PR=\dfrac{2\sqrt{111}}{3}}$. The first answer is exactly $7$ more than the second. Both computations involve the same length $\sqrt{37}$.
\begin{asy}
size(7cm);
real s=7+2*sqrt(111)/3;pair A=(0,0),B=(s,0),C=(s/2,sqrt(3)*s/2),D=(10,0),E=(5.5,5.5sqrt(3)),O=(5,2sqrt(3));draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(circle(O,sqrt(37)));dot(O);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,S);label("$E$",E,NW);label("$O$",O,W);
\end{asy}

