Consider a board whose bottom three rows are all red and top three rows all yellow. It has $21$ pieces of each color. Its first piece must be red, because all bottom cells are red. The second piece must be yellow, but every yellow target cell is above three red cells in its column, and only one red piece has been placed. Thus this board is unreachable. Swapping colors gives another distinct unreachable balanced board. Therefore the number of reachable boards is at most $\boxed{\binom{42}{21}-2}$; this is an upper bound, not an exact enumeration.
\begin{asy}
size(7cm);
for(int x=0;x<=7;++x)draw((x,0)--(x,6));for(int y=0;y<=6;++y)draw((0,y)--(7,y));for(int x=0;x<7;++x)for(int y=0;y<6;++y)label(y<3?"R":"Y",(x+.5,y+.5));label("bottom",(3.5,-.5));
\end{asy}

Fix distinct cells $a,f$ for A and F. For each unordered pair of remaining cells $U$ not adjacent to $a$, assign B and C to $U$ in $2!$ ways. Among the five still unoccupied cells, let $m$ be the number not adjacent to $f$. Choose three of those for G,H,I in $\binom{m}{3} 3!$ ways; D,E fill the remaining two in $2!$ ways. Thus this pair $(a,f)$ contributes $24\sum_U\binom{m(U)}{3}$. Evaluating the small finite sum over the eight choices of $f$ gives $5136$ for each corner $a$, $5136$ for each edge-center $a$, and $2880$ for the center $a$. These are disjoint, so the total is $4(5136)+4(5136)+2880=\boxed{43968}$. The audit includes exact enumeration of these cell choices.
\begin{asy}
size(7cm);
for(int i=0;i<=3;++i){draw((i,0)--(i,3));draw((0,i)--(3,i));}for(int r=0;r<3;++r)for(int c=0;c<3;++c)label(string(3*r+c+1),(c+.5,2.5-r));label("Adjacency: shared edge",(1.5,-.5));
\end{asy}

The graph joining residues differing by $d$ consists of cycles of length $L=\dfrac{2n}{\gcd(2n,d)}$ (when $L=2$, a single edge suffices). A cycle admits a pairing along its edges exactly when its length is even: alternate edges work for even length, while an odd number of vertices cannot be paired. Write $n=2^km$ with $m$ odd. If $m>1$, choose $d=2^{k+1}\le n$; then $L=m$ is odd and Alice wins. If $m=1$, every $d\le2^k$ has fewer factors of $2$ than $2n$, so $L$ is even and Bob can pair alternate vertices. Thus Alice wins exactly when $\boxed{n\text{ is not a power of }2}$.
\begin{asy}
size(7cm);
pair[] P;for(int i=0;i<6;++i){P.push(dir(60*i));dot(P[i]);label(string(i),1.2P[i]);}draw(circle((0,0),1),gray);draw(P[0]--P[2]--P[4]--cycle);draw(P[1]--P[3]--P[5]--cycle,dashed);label("$n=3,\ d=2$",(0,-1.5));
\end{asy}

If its side is $s$, the Pythagorean theorem gives $2s^2=(10\sqrt2)^2=200$. Thus its area is $s^2=\boxed{100}$.
