Relabel so a is the longest side BC, and let $AB=c$, $AC=b$. The altitude AH meets BC internally because the angles at B and C are acute. Set $BH=x$, $AH=h$. Pythagoras gives $c^2=h^2+x^2$ and $b^2=h^2+(a-x)^2$, hence $2 ax=a^2+c^2-b^2$. Since $\Delta=\dfrac{ah}{2}$,\[16\Delta^2=4 a^2 c^2-(a^2+c^2-b^2)^2=((a+c)^2-b^2)(b^2-(a-c)^2).\]Factoring yields $(a+b+c)(a+c-b)(a+b-c)(b+c-a)=16 s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)$. Taking positive square roots proves the formula.
