int n=6;real r=1/(2*sin(pi/n)),a=r*cos(pi/n);pair[] P;for(int i=0;i<n;++i)P.push((0,a)+r*dir(-90-180/n+360*i/n));path g=P[0];for(int i=1;i<n;++i)g=g--P[i];draw(g--cycle);draw((-2,0)--(2,0),dashed);dot((0,a));draw((0,0)--(0,a),dashed);label("$O$",(0,a),N);label("$h$",(0,a/2),E);label("rotation axis",(1.5,0),S);label("$1$",(0,0),S);
\end{asy}

For a planar region of area A wholly on one side of its rotation axis, rotating about that axis gives volume $2\pi A h$, where h is the distance of its centroid from the axis. Indeed a thin vertical strip with lower and upper heights u,v gives washer volume $\pi(v^2-u^2)\,dx=2\pi\int_u^v y\,dy\,dx$, and integrating gives the formula. The regular polygon's centroid is its center. Its area is $A=2(1+\sqrt 2)$ and its apothem is $h=\dfrac{1+\sqrt 2}{2}$. Therefore $V=2\pi Ah=\boxed{(6+4\sqrt 2)\pi}$.
