\end{asy}

Let the legs be a,b and hypotenuse c. Then $ab=40$ and $a+b=40-c$. Pythagoras gives $c^2=(a+b)^2-2 ab=(40-c)^2-80$, hence $80 c=1520$ and $\boxed{c=19}$ centimeters. Such a triangle exists: its legs sum 21 and multiply 40, so they are $\dfrac{21\pm\sqrt{281}}{2}$, both positive.
