The powers that pass the test are $\boxed{1,2,4,8,128}$, at exponents $0,1,2,3,7$. A reproducible exhaustive method starts with $v=1$ and, for $k=0,1,\ldots,39$, repeatedly takes the remainder of v on division by 10 to inspect each digit, rejecting if a digit is not 1,2,4,8, then doubles v for the next exponent. Equivalently the complete test is:\begin{verbatim}
v = 1
for k in range(40):
    if all(c in "1248" for c in str(v)):
        print(k, v)
    v *= 2
\end{verbatim}
This examines every allowed exponent exactly once. It makes no assertion about larger powers.

Let the half-base be $x$ and each equal side be $s$. The perimeter gives $s+x=18$. Pythagoras gives $s^2-x^2=12^2$, so $(s-x)(s+x)=144$ and $s-x=8$. Hence $x=5$ and the full base is $10$. The area is $\dfrac{1}{2}(10)(12)=\boxed{60\text{ cm}^2}$.
