\end{asy}

A point off the three cutting planes has its coordinates in exactly one of the six strict orders, such as $x<y<z$. Each ordering defines a nonempty convex region, so it is connected and forms one piece. Different orderings cannot be connected without crossing an equality plane. Thus there are $\boxed{3!=6}$ pieces. Their closures are congruent tetrahedra related by coordinate permutations; the sketch highlights $0\le x\le y\le z\le1$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
