Put $C=(0,0)$, $A=(a,0)$ and $B=(0,b)$ with $a,b>0$. Since $AB$ has slope $-\dfrac{b}{a}$, its perpendicular $CD$ has slope $\dfrac{a}{b}$. Therefore $\dfrac{D_y}{D_x}=\dfrac{a}{b}$. The two distances are respectively $D_y$ and $D_x$, since $D$ lies inside the first quadrant. Their ratio is $\dfrac{a}{b}=\dfrac{CA}{CB}$, as required.
