A corner-circle center lies on the $60^\circ$ angle bisector at distance $\dfrac{r}{\sin30^\circ}=2r$ from the vertex. The centroid is $\dfrac{s}{\sqrt3}$ from the vertex on the same bisector. The two equal circles are externally tangent, so their centers are $2r$ apart. Thus $\dfrac{s}{\sqrt3}=2r+2r=4r$, giving $\boxed{\dfrac{r}{s}=\dfrac{1}{4\sqrt3}=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{12}}$.
