Let H be the perpendicular projection of D onto the base plane. If F is the perpendicular foot on any base-side line, then $DF^2=DH^2+HF^2$, so H is at equal distance $r=\sqrt{49-25}=2\sqrt 6$ from all three base-side lines. Since the side faces are acute, each F lies inside its corresponding base edge. The point H must therefore be the incenter, not an excenter (an excenter has two perpendicular feet on side extensions). The base area is $\Delta=\dfrac{3\cdot 60}{5}=36$, giving semiperimeter $s=\dfrac{\Delta}{r}=3\sqrt 6$. But every triangle satisfies $s\ge 3\sqrt 3 r$. To prove this bound, write its tangent lengths as positive u,v,w: Heron's formula gives $r^2=\dfrac{uvw}{u+v+w}$, and AM--GM gives $(u+v+w)^3\ge 27 uvw$, hence $s^2\ge 27 r^2$. Here $s^2=54$ while $27 r^2=648$, a contradiction. Thus $\boxed{\text{no such tetrahedron exists}}$.
