Scale the triangle so that its inradius is $1$. Set $u=\sqrt3$, $v=\sqrt2$, and $L=u+v+1$. Coordinates realizing the angles are $A=(0,0)$, $B=(L,0)$, $C=\left(\dfrac{L}{u+1},\dfrac{uL}{u+1}\right)$. The bisector of $\angle A$ has slope $\dfrac{1}{u}$; together with distance $1$ from $AB$ this gives $I=(u,1)$. The bisector at $B$ is consistent since $L-u=v+1=\cot22.5^\circ$. Since $BC$ has slope $-1$, the altitude from $A$ is $y=x$; the altitude from $C$ is vertical. Thus $H=\left(\dfrac{L}{u+1},\dfrac{L}{u+1}\right)$. Equating distances to $A,B,C$ gives $O=\left(\dfrac{L}{2},L\left(1-\dfrac{u}{2}\right)\right)$. Let $U=O-I$ and $V=H-I$. Using $u^2=3,v^2=2$ gives $U=\dfrac{1}{2}(1+v-u,u+2v-uv-3)$ and $V=\dfrac{u-1}{2}(v-2,v)$. Direct multiplication gives $U\cdot V=(u-1)(1-v)<0$ and $\det(U,V)=(u-1)(2-u)(v-1)>0$. Therefore $\theta=\angle OIH$ is obtuse and $\tan(180^\circ-\theta)=\dfrac{\det(U,V)}{-U\cdot V}=2-\sqrt3=\tan15^\circ$. Hence $\boxed{\theta=165^\circ}$.
