The centre square has no knight moves. The other eight squares form an eight-cycle in the knight-attack graph: in row-column coordinates the successive vertices are $(1,1),(2,3),(3,1),(1,2),(3,3),(2,1),(1,3),(3,2)$, then back to $(1,1)$. These are vertices $1$ through $8$ in the sketch, and each has precisely its two cycle neighbors as knight moves. Without the centre, an independent set of four must alternate around this cycle, giving two placements. With the centre, choose three independent vertices of the eight-cycle. Of $\binom83=56$ triples, each of eight cycle edges belongs to six triples, subtracting $48$. Triples containing two edges are exactly the eight consecutive three-vertex paths, so add eight back. This gives $56-48+8=16$ placements with the centre. Thus there are $18$ nonattacking sets out of $\binom94=126$ equally likely placements, and the probability is $\boxed{\dfrac{1}{7}}$. 
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
import geometry;
size(260);
pair[] P;for(int i=0;i<8;++i){P.push(2*dir(90-45*i));dot(P[i]);label(string(i+1),1.18*P[i]);}for(int i=0;i<8;++i)draw(P[i]--P[(i+1)%8]);dot((4,0));label("centre",(4,0),S);label("Knight-attack graph",(1,-3));
\end{asy}

Choose two of the nine vertical dot columns and two of the nine horizontal dot rows, giving $\binom92^2=1296$ rectangles. An integer width-height pair with product $12$ and both dimensions at most eight must be $(2,6),(3,4),(4,3)$, or $(6,2)$. For dimensions $(w,h)$ there are $(9-w)(9-h)$ positions. The favorable count is $2(7\cdot3+6\cdot5)=102$. Therefore the probability is $\dfrac{102}{1296}=\boxed{\dfrac{17}{216}}$. 
