Each side segment of the quadrilateral lies on a line that meets the boundary of the convex hexagon at most twice, so at most $4\cdot2=8$ distinct intersections are possible. To attain eight, draw a regular hexagon of circumradius $1$ and a square centered at its center with sides $x=\pm0.8$, $y=\pm0.8$. Every square vertex is outside the hexagon, but the midpoint of each square side is inside; each side therefore crosses the hexagon twice. The maximum is $\boxed8$.
