Let the sphere radius be $r$. Their centres all lie at height $r$ and at a common horizontal distance $\rho$ from the cone axis. In an axial cross-section, the side of the cone has equation $hx+z-h=0$, where $h=2\sqrt2$. Its distance from $(\rho,r)$ is $\dfrac{h-h\rho-r}{\sqrt{h^2+1}}=\dfrac{h-h\rho-r}{3}=r$. Thus $\rho=1-\sqrt2r$. The four centres lie on a horizontal circle of radius $\rho$. Tangencies join centres at distance $2r$; a tangency cannot join nonconsecutive centres around that circle. Indeed, each of the two arcs between nonconsecutive centres contains another centre; choose a shorter arc, of angular length at most $180^\circ$. Its interior centre is closer to either endpoint than the endpoints are to each other, contradicting non-overlap. The degree-two contact graph is a four-cycle, so the centres form an inscribed equilateral quadrilateral, hence a square. Its side is $2r$, giving $\rho=\sqrt2r$. Combining the relations gives $2\sqrt2r=1$, or $\boxed{r=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{4}}$.
