pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(0,3),M=(B+C)/2;draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--M,dashed);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$M$",M,NE);
\end{asy}

The triangle is right at $B$. The angle-bisector theorem gives $BD:DC=3:5$, so $BD=\dfrac{3}{2}$. In right triangle $ABD$, $AD=\sqrt{9+\dfrac{9}{4}}=\dfrac{3\sqrt5}{2}$. Therefore $\cos\angle ADB=\dfrac{BD}{AD}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt5}{5}}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; usepackage("amsmath"); size(240);
pair A=(0,3),B=(0,0),C=(4,0),D=(1.5,0);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--D,dashed);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);
\end{asy}

Scale to $A=(0,0),B=(1,0),C=(1,1),D=(0,1)$. Then $E=(1,\dfrac{1}{3})$ and $F=(0,\dfrac{2}{3})$. The lines are $y=\dfrac{x}{3}$ and $y=\dfrac{2}{3}(1-x)$, giving $G=(\dfrac{2}{3},\dfrac{2}{9})$. Triangle $ABG$ has base 1 and height $\dfrac{2}{9}$, so the ratio is $\boxed{\dfrac{1}{9}}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; usepackage("amsmath"); size(240);
pair A=(0,0),B=(1,0),C=(1,1),D=(0,1),Epoint=(1,1.0/3),F=(0,2.0/3),G=(2.0/3,2.0/9);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--Epoint^^B--F);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$E$",Epoint,E);label("$F$",F,W);label("$G$",G,N);
\end{asy}

Each small radius is $r=45$. Adjacent centres are $2r$ apart; in a regular hexagon, side length equals circumradius. Thus the centres lie $2r$ from the common centre, and the large radius is $3r$. The unoccupied area is $\pi(3r)^2-6\pi r^2=3\pi r^2=\boxed{6075\pi}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; usepackage("amsmath"); size(240);
draw(circle((0,0),3));for(int k=0;k<6;++k){pair C=2*dir(60*k);draw(circle(C,1),blue);dot(C);}dot((0,0));label("$O$",(0,0),S);draw((0,0)--(3,0),dashed);label("$2r$",(1,0),N);label("$r$",(2.5,0),N);
\end{asy}

The original surface area is $6\cdot2^2=24$. The unit cube adds five exposed faces but covers one unit of the old surface, for a net increase of 4. Thus the percentage increase is $\dfrac{4}{24}\cdot100\%=\boxed{16\dfrac{2}{3}\%}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; usepackage("amsmath"); size(240);
pair v=(.6,.4);draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,2)--(0,2)--cycle);draw((0,2)--(0,2)+v--(2,2)+v--(2,2)^^(2,2)+v--(2,0)+v--(2,0));draw((.5,2)--(1.5,2)--(1.5,3)--(.5,3)--cycle);draw((.5,3)--(.5,3)+v/2--(1.5,3)+v/2--(1.5,3)^^(1.5,3)+v/2--(1.5,2)+v/2--(1.5,2));label("$2$",(1,0),S);label("$1$",(1,3),N);
\end{asy}

The minute hand is $40\cdot6=240^\circ$ clockwise from 12. The hour hand is $5\cdot30+40\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}=170^\circ$ clockwise from 12. The smaller angle is 70 degrees, so the larger is $360-70=\boxed{290^\circ}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; usepackage("amsmath"); size(240);
draw(circle((0,0),1));pair H=.65*dir(90-170),M=.9*dir(90-240);draw((0,0)--H,blue+linewidth(1));draw((0,0)--M,red);label("12",(0,1),N);label("minute",M,W);label("hour",H,S);dot((0,0));
\end{asy}

Let $BH=HC=x$ and $AH=h$. Coordinates $H=(0,0),A=(0,h),C=(x,0),D=(-5,0)$ give $\overrightarrow{AD}\cdot\overrightarrow{AC}=-5x+h^2=0$, so $h^2=5x$. Pythagoras in $AHC$ gives $x^2+5x=169$, hence $x=\dfrac{-5+\sqrt{701}}{2}$. This exceeds 5, so $D$ lies on $BH$ as required. Therefore $BD=x-5=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt{701}-15}{2}}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; usepackage("amsmath"); size(240);
real x=(-5+sqrt(701))/2,h=sqrt(5*x);pair A=(0,h),B=(-x,0),C=(x,0),H=(0,0),D=(-5,0);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--D^^A--H,dashed);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$H$",H,S);label("$5$",(-2.5,0),N);
\end{asy}

In an axial section put the base at $z=0$ and apex at $z=12$. If $\rho$ is radial distance from the axis, distance to the sloping side is $\dfrac{60-5z-12\rho}{13}$, because the half-section is a 5--12--13 triangle. The point must satisfy $z\ge1$ and $5z+12\rho\le47$. These inequalities form a smaller cone with base plane $z=1$, base radius $\dfrac{42}{12}=3.5$, and apex $z=\dfrac{47}{5}=9.4$. Its height is 8.4, so all lengths are 0.7 times the original. The volume ratio is $0.7^3=\boxed{0.343}$. The perpendicular foot on the side generator lies on that segment for points in the original cone, so the line-distance condition is valid; the base-disk distance is $z$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; usepackage("amsmath"); size(240);
draw((-5,0)--(0,12)--(5,0)--cycle);filldraw((-3.5,1)--(0,9.4)--(3.5,1)--cycle,lightblue,blue);draw((0,0)--(0,12),dashed);label("$12$",(0,10.5),E);label("$5$",(2.5,0),S);label("$z=1$",(3.5,1),E);label("inset cone",(0,5));
\end{asy}

If the total length is $L$, the part over water is $\dfrac{L}{2}$. Its component perpendicular to the banks is $\dfrac{L}{2}\sin45^\circ=200$. Hence $L=\boxed{400\sqrt2}$ feet.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; usepackage("amsmath"); size(240);
draw((-2,0)--(5,0));draw((-2,2)--(5,2));draw((-1,-1)--(3,3),blue);draw((0,0)--(0,2),dashed);label("$200$",(0,1),W);label("$45^\circ$",(.7,.2));label("bridge",(3,3),NE);
\end{asy}

Let the legs be $a\le b$ and hypotenuse $c$, with $a+b+c=56$. A right triangle has $c<28$, and $a+b\le\sqrt2c$ gives $c\ge\dfrac{56}{1+\sqrt2}>23$. Thus $c=24,25,26,27$. Since $ab=\dfrac{(56-c)^2-c^2}{2}=1568-56c$, the discriminant for the legs is $(a-b)^2=(56-c)^2-4(1568-56c)=c^2+112c-3136$. For the four candidates these values are $128,289,452,617$. Only 289 is a square, yielding $c=25$ and legs 7,24. Thus the largest piece is $\boxed{25}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; usepackage("amsmath"); size(240);
pair A=(0,7),B=(0,0),C=(24,0);draw(A--B--C--cycle);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);
\end{asy}

There are 8 corner cubes with three painted faces, $12(8)=96$ edge cubes with two, $6(8^2)=384$ face-interior cubes with one, and $8^3=512$ unpainted cubes. Therefore the probability is $\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^8\left(\dfrac{2}{3}\right)^{96}\left(\dfrac{5}{6}\right)^{384}=2^{-296}3^{-480}5^{384}$. Thus $a+b+c=\boxed{-392}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; usepackage("amsmath"); size(240);
pair A=(0,0),B=(3,0),C=(3,3),D=(0,3),v=(1,1);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(D--D+v--C+v--C^^C+v--B+v--B);for(int i=1;i<10;++i){real t=3*i/10.0;draw((t,0)--(t,3),gray);draw((0,t)--(3,t),gray);}label("$10$",(.9,-.2),S);label("corner",C,SE);label("edge",(3,1.5),E);label("face",(1.5,1.5));
\end{asy}

Put $B=(-3,0)$, $C=(3,0)$, $A=(0,4)$. For $P=(x,y)$, the sum is $3x^2+3y^2-8y+34=3[x^2+(y-\dfrac{4}{3})^2]+\dfrac{86}{3}$. The desired region is the disk centred at $G=(0,\dfrac{4}{3})$ of radius $\dfrac{2}{3}$. Its distances to the base and the two other sides are $\dfrac{4}{3},\dfrac{8}{5},\dfrac{8}{5}$, so the disk lies entirely inside the triangle. The triangle has area 12, giving probability $\dfrac{\pi(\dfrac{2}{3})^2}{12}=\boxed{\dfrac{\pi}{27}}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; usepackage("amsmath"); size(240);
pair A=(0,4),B=(-3,0),C=(3,0),G=(0,4.0/3);draw(A--B--C--cycle);filldraw(circle(G,2.0/3),lightblue,blue);dot(G);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$G$",G,N);
\end{asy}

From an island $v$ there are three neighbours. Each has at most two other neighbours, so at most $1+3+3\cdot2=10$ islands are within two steps. Thus 10 is an upper bound. It is attained by the Petersen graph: take all ten 2-element subsets of $\{1,2,3,4,5\}$ and join two exactly when they are disjoint. Each vertex has $\binom32=3$ neighbours. If two vertices overlap, their union has three elements; its 2-element complement is disjoint from both and provides a two-edge path. If they are disjoint they are already adjacent. Thus the maximum is $\boxed{10}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; usepackage("amsmath"); size(240);
pair[] U,V;for(int i=0;i<5;++i){U.push(2*dir(90+72*i));V.push(dir(90+72*i));}for(int i=0;i<5;++i){draw(U[i]--U[(i+1)%5]);draw(U[i]--V[i]);draw(V[i]--V[(i+2)%5]);dot(U[i]);dot(V[i]);label("$u_"+string(i)+"$",U[i],unit(U[i]));label("$v_"+string(i)+"$",V[i],unit(V[i]));}
\end{asy}

Let the cone radius be $R$, its height $6R$, and the cylinder radius be $r$, height $3r$. Similar triangles in an axial section give $\dfrac{r}{R}=1-\dfrac{3r}{6R}$, so $r=\dfrac{2R}{3}$ and the cylinder height is $2R$. The volume ratio is $\dfrac{\pi(\dfrac{2R}{3})^2(2R)}{(\dfrac{1}{3})\pi R^2(6R)}=\boxed{\dfrac{4}{9}}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; usepackage("amsmath"); size(240);
pair A=(0,6),B=(-1,0),C=(1,0);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw((-2.0/3,0)--(-2.0/3,2)--(2.0/3,2)--(2.0/3,0),blue);draw((0,0)--A,dashed);label("$6R$",(0,4),E);label("$R$",(.5,0),S);label("$r$",(1.0/3,2),N);label("$3r$",(-2.0/3,1),W);
\end{asy}

Scale the outer circumradius to 1. Then $OA=OB=AB=1$ and the angle between vectors $OA,OB$ is $60^\circ$. Since $A_1=\dfrac{3}{5}A+\dfrac{2}{5}B$, we have $OA_1^2=\dfrac{9}{25}+\dfrac{4}{25}+2\dfrac{3}{5}\dfrac{2}{5}\cos60^\circ=\dfrac{19}{25}$. Regular hexagons are similar and their area ratio equals the square of the circumradius ratio, so the answer is $\boxed{\dfrac{19}{25}}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; usepackage("amsmath"); size(240);
pair O=(0,0),A=dir(0),B=dir(60),P=.6*A+.4*B;for(int k=0;k<6;++k){draw(dir(60*k)--dir(60*(k+1)));draw(rotate(60*k)*P--rotate(60*(k+1))*P,blue);}draw(O--A^^O--B^^O--P,dashed);label("$O$",O,SW);label("$A$",A,E);label("$B$",B,N);label("$A_1$",P,NE);
\end{asy}

Put $C=(0,0)$, $B=(b,0)$, $F=(f,0)$ and $A=(u,v)$, where $0<f<b$ and $v\ne0$. Since $E$ is the midpoint of $AF$, $E=(\dfrac{u+f}{2},\dfrac{v}{2})$. Write $D=\lambda E$. As $D$ lies on $AB$, comparison of its coordinates gives $\lambda=\dfrac{2b}{b+f}$. Therefore $CE=\dfrac{CD}{\lambda}=\dfrac{b+f}{2}$, because $CD=CB=b$. It follows that $DE=b-\dfrac{b+f}{2}=\dfrac{b-f}{2}=\dfrac{BF}{2}$. Thus $BF=\boxed{8}$, choice C.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; usepackage("amsmath"); size(240);
pair C=(0,0),B=(10,0),F=(2,0),A=(5,sqrt(95)),Epoint=(A+F)/2,D=(10/6.0)*Epoint;draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--F^^C--D);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,SW);label("$D$",D,NE);label("$E$",Epoint,W);label("$F$",F,S);
\end{asy}

The triangle inequality gives $6<x<10$. The angle at $B$, opposite the side 8, is obtuse exactly when $8^2>x^2+2^2$, or $x<\sqrt{60}=2\sqrt{15}$. Combining gives $\boxed{6<x<2\sqrt{15}}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry; usepackage("amsmath"); size(240);
pair A=(-2,sqrt(48)),B=(0,0),C=(2,0);draw(A--B--C--cycle);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);
\end{asy}

A chord at distance $d$ from the center of a radius-$R$ circle has squared length $4(R^2-d^2)$, by the perpendicular bisector and Pythagorean theorem. Hence $AB^2=4(R^2-r^2)$, $AC^2=4(R^2-s^2)$, and $DE^2=4(r^2-s^2)=AC^2-AB^2=29^2-21^2=400$. Therefore $\boxed{DE=20}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
real R=16,r=sqrt(256-110.25),s=sqrt(256-210.25);pair O=(0,0),A=(16,0),B=16dir(2acos(r/R)*180/pi),C=16dir(-2acos(s/R)*180/pi),D=(s,10),Ep=(s,-10);draw(circle(O,R));draw(circle(O,r),gray);draw(circle(O,s),blue);draw(A--B^^A--C^^D--Ep);draw(O--(A+B)/2^^O--(A+C)/2,dashed);dot("$O$",O,W);label("$A$",A,E);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,S);label("$D$",D,N);label("$E$",Ep,S);
\end{asy}

After step $k$, the coordinate sum is $x+y=k$. There are $44$ steps, so the sum of all coordinate sums is $0+1+\cdots+44=990$. Therefore the $y$-coordinate sum is $990-271=\boxed{719}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(20,0)--(20,24),gray);draw((0,0)--(0,20)--(1,20)--(1,21)--(20,21)--(20,24),blue);dot("$(0,0)$",(0,0),SW);dot("$(20,24)$",(20,24),NE);label("one possible path",(10,-3));
\end{asy}

Let the square side be $s$, the cut length $d$, and its endpoint heights above the bottom be $a,b$. Perimeters are $s+a+b+d$ and $3s-a-b+d$, so their sum gives $2s+d=70$ and their difference gives $|a+b-s|=6$. The area difference is $s|a+b-s|=135$, hence $s=\dfrac{45}{2}$. Therefore $d=70-2s=\boxed{25\text{ miles}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
real s=22.5,t=sqrt(25^2-s^2),a=(s+6-t)/2,b=(s+6+t)/2;draw((0,0)--(s,0)--(s,s)--(0,s)--cycle);draw((0,a)--(s,b),blue);label("$a$",(0,a/2),W);label("$b$",(s,b/2),E);label("$s$",(s/2,0),S);label("$d$",(s/2,(a+b)/2),N);
\end{asy}

The nine ants force every row and column sum to be exactly $3$. Classify the first row. A permutation of $(3,0,0)$ has $3$ choices; the second row in the two available columns can be $(0,3),(1,2),(2,1),(3,0)$, giving $12$. A permutation of $(2,1,0)$ has $6$ choices; the second-row entries are bounded by $(1,2,3)$ and sum to $3$. Its first two entries have $2\cdot3=6$ choices, each giving a valid third entry, for $36$. Finally first row $(1,1,1)$ leaves second-row entries at most $2$ summing to $3$: $\binom52-3=7$ possibilities. The third row is always forced. Thus $12+36+7=\boxed{55}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int i=0;i<=3;++i){draw((i,0)--(i,3));draw((0,i)--(3,i));}int[][] V={{2,1,0},{0,2,1},{1,0,2}};for(int i=0;i<3;++i)for(int j=0;j<3;++j)label(string(V[i][j]),(j+.5,2.5-i));label("one valid configuration",(1.5,-.5));
\end{asy}

Unfolding the eight folds divides each original square into a $16\times16$ array of congruent cells. The cut becomes one diagonal in each cell, with adjacent diagonals reflected across their shared grid edge. Give the original grid integer coordinates from $0$ to $16$. For sheet $A$, all cut endpoints have even coordinate sum; for sheet $B$, all have odd coordinate sum. Each cut graph has $256$ edges and is connected to the boundary. Including the outer boundary, Euler's formula gives the number of regions as $1+E-V_{\rm int}$: boundary edges and vertices cancel. Among interior grid points $1\le x,y\le15$, the even-parity count is $8^2+7^2=113$, and the odd-parity count is $2\cdot8\cdot7=112$. Therefore sheet $A$ yields $1+256-113=144$ pieces and sheet $B$ yields $1+256-112=145$. The total is $\boxed{289}$. The sketches show the fully unfolded cuts.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int p=0;p<2;++p){real t=20*p;draw((t,0)--(t+16,0)--(t+16,16)--(t,16)--cycle);for(int i=0;i<16;++i)for(int j=0;j<16;++j){if((i+j)%2==p)draw((t+i,j)--(t+i+1,j+1),blue);else draw((t+i+1,j)--(t+i,j+1),blue);}label(p==0?"sheet $A$: $144$":"sheet $B$: $145$",(t+8,-2));}
\end{asy}

The consecutive edge vectors are $u,v,w,-u,-v,-w$, all of length $3$. Rotate coordinates so their first three directions are $0,\alpha,\beta$ with $0<\alpha<\beta<\pi$. The shoelace formula gives area $K=u\times v+u\times w+v\times w=9[\sin\alpha+\sin\beta+\sin(\beta-\alpha)]$. The sum-to-product identity makes this $K=36\sin\dfrac{\beta}{2}\cos\dfrac{\alpha}{2}\cos\dfrac{\beta-\alpha}{2}$. Meanwhile $TU=|u+v|=6\cos\dfrac{\alpha}{2}$, $RM=|v+w|=6\cos\dfrac{\beta-\alpha}{2}$, and $UA=|w-u|=6\sin\dfrac{\beta}{2}$. Their opposite counterparts $MN,AT,NR$ have the same respective lengths. Therefore the requested product is $(TU\cdot RM\cdot UA)^2=(6K)^2=36\cdot25=\boxed{900}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
real lo=0,hi=pi/2;for(int k=0;k<60;++k){real b=(lo+hi)/2;if(36*sin(b/2)*cos(b/4)^2<5)lo=b;else hi=b;}real b=(lo+hi)/2;pair u=(3,0),v=3dir(b/2*180/pi),w=3dir(b*180/pi),T=(0,0),R=u,U=u+v,M=u+v+w,A=v+w,Np=w;draw(T--R--U--M--A--Np--cycle);draw(T--U^^R--M^^U--A^^M--Np^^A--T^^Np--R,dashed+blue);label("$T$",T,SW);label("$R$",R,S);label("$U$",U,S);label("$M$",M,NE);label("$A$",A,N);label("$N$",Np,N);
\end{asy}

Let tangent lengths from the three vertices be $u,v,w$, with $u$ opposite $a$. Then $a=v+w,b=u+w,c=u+v$, so $s=u+v+w$ and $u=s-a$. Joining the incenter to the vertices partitions the triangle into triangles with bases $a,b,c$ and altitude $r$. Thus $K=\dfrac{r(a+b+c)}{2}=rs$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(1,4),B=(0,0),C=(5,0);real a=abs(B-C),b=abs(A-C),c=abs(A-B);pair I=(a*A+b*B+c*C)/(a+b+c);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--I--B^^C--I,dashed);draw(circle(I,I.y),blue);draw(I--(I.x,0));label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$a$",(B+C)/2,S);label("$b$",(A+C)/2,E);label("$c$",(A+B)/2,W);label("$I$",I,W);label("$r$",(I+(I.x,0))/2,E);
\end{asy}

The sine rule gives ratio $\sin15^\circ:\sin75^\circ:1$. The angle formulas give $\sin15^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt6-\sqrt2}{4}$ and $\sin75^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt6+\sqrt2}{4}$. Thus the ratio is $\boxed{(\sqrt6-\sqrt2):(\sqrt6+\sqrt2):4}$. Absolute lengths require a scale measurement.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(0,4tan(15*pi/180));draw(A--B--C--cycle);label("$90^\circ$",A,SW);label("$15^\circ$",B,SE);label("$75^\circ$",C,N);label("hypotenuse",(B+C)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

The two differently colored lines cannot intersect. A diagonal intersects every other line, so the two lines must be parallel rows or parallel columns. Count the row case. If exactly two rows are uniform, one of each symbol, choose the nonuniform row in $3$ ways, assign symbols to the other rows in $2$ ways, and fill the nonuniform row in $2^3-2=6$ ways: $36$ configurations. If all three rows are uniform, $2^3-2=6$ assignments use both symbols. Thus there are $42$ row configurations and $42$ column configurations. They are disjoint because opposite uniform rows prevent any uniform column. Total $\boxed{84}$, choice (D).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int i=0;i<=3;++i){draw((i,0)--(i,3));draw((0,i)--(3,i));}for(int j=0;j<3;++j){label("$\triangle$",(j+.5,2.5));label("$\bigcirc$",(j+.5,.5));}label("$\triangle$",(.5,1.5));label("$\bigcirc$",(1.5,1.5));label("$\triangle$",(2.5,1.5));
\end{asy}

Tangent segments from the same vertex are equal. Their lengths $u,v,w$ satisfy $u+v=8$, $u+w=13$, $v+w=17$. Adding the first two equations and subtracting the third gives $2u=4$, so $u=2,v=6$. Therefore $\boxed{r:s=1:3}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(8,0),C=(-3.5,sqrt(627)/2),I=(17*A+13*B+8*C)/38,T=(2,0);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(circle(I,I.y));draw(I--T,dashed);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$T$",T,S);label("$2$",(1,0),S);label("$6$",(5,0),S);label("$13$",(A+C)/2,W);label("$17$",(B+C)/2,E);
\end{asy}

The semiperimeter is $26$. Heron's formula gives area $K=\sqrt{26\cdot13\cdot8\cdot5}=52\sqrt5$. (a) $R=\dfrac{abc}{4K}=\dfrac{13\cdot18\cdot21}{208\sqrt5}=\boxed{\dfrac{189\sqrt5}{40}}$. (b) $r=\dfrac{K}{s}=\boxed{2\sqrt5}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(21,0),C=(143.0/21,104sqrt(5)/21),I=(18*A+13*B+21*C)/52,O=(10.5,(169-21*C.x)/(2*C.y));draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(circle(I,2sqrt(5)),blue);draw(circle(O,189sqrt(5)/40),gray);dot("$I$",I,W);dot("$O$",O,E);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$13$",(A+C)/2,W);label("$18$",(B+C)/2,E);label("$21$",(A+B)/2,S);
\end{asy}

(a) Three examples are $\begin{pmatrix}8&1&6\\3&5&7\\4&9&2\end{pmatrix}$, $\begin{pmatrix}4&3&8\\9&5&1\\2&7&6\end{pmatrix}$, $\begin{pmatrix}2&9&4\\7&5&3\\6&1&8\end{pmatrix}$. Every row and column sums to $15$, necessarily $\dfrac{1+\cdots+9}{3}$. (b) Examples are $\begin{pmatrix}16&2&3&13\\5&11&10&8\\9&7&6&12\\4&14&15&1\end{pmatrix}$, $\begin{pmatrix}4&9&5&16\\14&7&11&2\\15&6&10&3\\1&12&8&13\end{pmatrix}$, $\begin{pmatrix}1&15&14&4\\12&6&7&9\\8&10&11&5\\13&3&2&16\end{pmatrix}$. Their common sum is $34=\dfrac{1+\cdots+16}{4}$. Within each group the latter two grids are rotations of the first, which preserve the required sums.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
int[][] A={{8,1,6},{3,5,7},{4,9,2}},B={{16,2,3,13},{5,11,10,8},{9,7,6,12},{4,14,15,1}};for(int i=0;i<=3;++i){draw((i,0)--(i,3));draw((0,i)--(3,i));}for(int i=0;i<3;++i)for(int j=0;j<3;++j)label(string(A[i][j]),(j+.5,2.5-i));for(int i=0;i<=4;++i){draw((5+i,0)--(5+i,4));draw((5,i)--(9,i));}for(int i=0;i<4;++i)for(int j=0;j<4;++j)label(string(B[i][j]),(5+j+.5,3.5-i));label("row sum $15$",(1.5,-.5));label("row sum $34$",(7,-.5));
\end{asy}

Initial volume is $16w\cdot5=80w$. The new base area is $10w$, so depth becomes $\dfrac{80w}{10w}=\boxed{8\text{ cm}}$.
