usepackage("amsmath");
size(260);
pair A=(0,0),B=(2,0),C=(1,sqrt(3)); draw(A--B--C--cycle); label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N); label("$s$",(A+B)/2,S);
\end{asy}

There are $\binom{12}{6}=924$ unrestricted paths. Write the forbidden points as $A,B,C$ in the stated order. Paths through $A$ number $\binom21\binom{10}{5}=504$; through $B$ or $C$ they number $\binom72\binom51=105$ each. Paths through both $A,B$ number $\binom21\binom51\binom51=50$, and the same holds for $A,C$. No increasing path visits both $B,C$, since this would require a coordinate to decrease. Inclusion--exclusion gives $924-504-105-105+50+50=\boxed{310}$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry;
usepackage("amsmath");
size(260);
for(int i=4;i<=10;++i){draw((i,4)--(i,10),lightgray);draw((4,i)--(10,i),lightgray);} pair ptS=(4,4),T=(10,10),A=(5,5),B=(6,9),C=(9,6); dot(ptS);dot(T);dot(A,red);dot(B,red);dot(C,red);label("$S(4,4)$",ptS,SW);label("$T(10,10)$",T,NE);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,E);
\end{asy}

A dilation of scale factor $s>0$ maps any circle of radius $r$ onto a circle of radius $sr$. Every inscribed polygon has its perimeter multiplied by $s$. Taking the limit of regular inscribed polygon perimeters therefore shows that circumference is also multiplied by $s$. The diameter is multiplied by $s$ as well, so $\dfrac{C^{\prime}}{d^{\prime}}=\dfrac{sC}{sd}=\dfrac{C}{d}$. Any two circles are related by a dilation and a translation, so the ratio is universal. This common ratio is denoted by $\pi$.
\begin{asy}
import geometry;
usepackage("amsmath");
size(260);
pair O=(0,0),P=(5,0);draw(circle(O,1));draw(circle(P,2));draw(O--(1,0));draw(P--(7,0));label("$O$",O,SW);label("$O^{\prime}$",P,SW);label("$r$",(.5,0),N);label("$2r$",(6,0),N);label("scale factor $2$",(2.5,2.2));
\end{asy}

Only the vertex $A^{\prime}$ opposite $A$ is farther than one edge length away; its distance is $\sqrt2$. By symmetry, fix the first move to an equatorial vertex $B$. The second move has three possibilities: $A^{\prime}$ and the two equatorial neighbours of $B$. If it goes to $A^{\prime}$ on move two, it cannot end there on move three. If it goes to either equatorial neighbour, exactly one of its three allowed next edges goes to $A^{\prime}$. Therefore the probability is $\dfrac{2}{3}\cdot\dfrac{1}{3}=\boxed{\dfrac{2}{9}}$. 
\begin{asy}
import geometry;
usepackage("amsmath");
size(260);
pair A=(0,2),Ap=(0,-2),B=(-2,0),C=(0,.8),D=(2,0),ptE=(0,-.8);draw(B--C--D--ptE--cycle);draw(A--B^^A--C^^A--D^^A--ptE);draw(Ap--B^^Ap--D^^Ap--ptE);draw(Ap--C,dashed);label("$A$",A,N);label("$A^{\prime}$",Ap,S);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,NW);label("$D$",D,E);label("$E$",ptE,SE);
\end{asy}

There are $\binom{25}{3}=2300$ three-point subsets. Subtract collinear triples. Horizontal and vertical lines contribute $2\cdot5\binom53=100$. In either diagonal direction of slope $1$ or $-1$, line lengths are $1,2,3,4,5,4,3,2,1$, contributing $2\binom33+2\binom43+\binom53=20$; both directions contribute $40$. The remaining possible slopes of a line containing at least three grid points are $2,-2,\dfrac{1}{2},-\dfrac{1}{2}$. Each has exactly three three-point lines, contributing $12$. No other primitive step can be repeated twice within a $5\times5$ grid. Thus the answer is $2300-100-40-12=\boxed{2148}$. 
\begin{asy}
import geometry;
usepackage("amsmath");
size(260);
for(int i=0;i<5;++i)for(int j=0;j<5;++j)dot((i,j));draw((0,0)--(2,4),dashed);label("$(0,0)$",(0,0),SW);label("$(1,2)$",(1,2),NW);label("$(2,4)$",(2,4),N);label("$(4,4)$",(4,4),NE);
\end{asy}

Use $A=(0,0),B=(8,0),D=(d,0),C=(d,6)$. The midpoints are $M=(\dfrac{d}{2},0)$ and $N=(\dfrac{8+d}{2},3)$. Their coordinate difference is $(4,3)$, regardless of $d$, so $MN=\sqrt{4^2+3^2}=\boxed5$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(8,0),D=(3,0),C=(3,6),M=(A+D)/2,Q=(B+C)/2;draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(C--D,dashed);draw(M--Q,blue);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,S);label("$M$",M,S);label("$N$",Q,E);
\end{asy}

In one tank choose a bottom corner $O$ and the three corners $X,Y,Z$ joined to it by edges. Tilt the tank so $X,Y,Z$ lie at the same height, above $O$, and drain until the water surface passes through these three corners. The retained water is a tetrahedron with mutually perpendicular edges of lengths $L,W,H$, so its volume is $\dfrac{1}{6}LWH$, exactly $10$ gallons. This orientation is possible by making the three edge rises equal; the other tank corners are higher. Pour this measured amount into the other, initially empty tank, and repeat five times. The receiving tank then contains $5\cdot10=\boxed{50}$ gallons. No length measurement is needed: only alignment with existing corners.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair O=(0,0),X=(4,0),Y=(1.5,1),Z=(0,3);draw(O--X--X+Y--Y--cycle);draw(Z--Z+X--Z+X+Y--Z+Y--cycle);draw(O--Z^^X--X+Z^^Y--Y+Z^^X+Y--X+Y+Z);fill(X--Y--Z--cycle,lightblue+opacity(.5));draw(X--Y--Z--cycle,blue);label("$O$",O,SW);label("$X$",X,SE);label("$Y$",Y,N);label("$Z$",Z,NW);label("water surface",(2,1.8),E);
\end{asy}

Write $\beta=\angle DBA$, $t=\angle DBC$, and $u=\angle BDC$. Triangle $BDC$ gives $t+u=\beta$, so $u=\beta-t>0$. Triangle $ABD$ gives $\dfrac{AB}{BD}=\cos\beta$, and the sine rule in $BCD$ gives $\dfrac{BC}{BD}=\dfrac{\sin u}{\sin\beta}$. In triangle $ABC$, the angles at $A,B$ are $2u,\beta+t$, and the sine rule gives $\dfrac{BC}{AB}=\dfrac{\sin2u}{\sin(3\beta-t)}$. Combining these and canceling $\sin u>0$ yields $\sin(3\beta-t)=\sin2\beta\cos(\beta-t)=\dfrac{1}{2}[\sin(3\beta-t)+\sin(\beta+t)]$. Thus $\sin(3\beta-t)=\sin(\beta+t)$. Both arguments lie strictly between $0^\circ$ and $180^\circ$. Equality of the arguments would give $u=0$, impossible. Therefore they sum to $180^\circ$, giving $4\beta=180^\circ$ and $\boxed{\beta=45^\circ}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair B=(0,0),D=(1,0),A=(.5,.5),C=sin(25*pi/180)/sin(pi/4)*dir(-20);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--C^^B--D,dashed);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,S);label("$D$",D,E);
\end{asy}

A full rotation measures $\boxed{360^\circ=2\pi\text{ radians}}$. This is an angle measure, not a count of angles contained in a circle.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw(circle((0,0),1));draw((0,0)--(1,0));draw(arc((0,0),.7,10,350),Arrow);label("$2\pi$",(0,0),S);label("$O$",(0,0),NW);
\end{asy}

The semiperimeter is $21$, so Heron's formula gives area $\sqrt{21\cdot8\cdot7\cdot6}=84$. If $h$ is the requested altitude, $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot14h=84$, giving $\boxed{h=12}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(5,12),B=(0,0),C=(14,0),H=(5,0);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--H,dashed);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$H$",H,S);label("$13$",(A+B)/2,W);label("$15$",(A+C)/2,E);label("$14$",(7,0),S);
\end{asy}

If their centers coincide, distinctness means different radii, so no point is on both. Otherwise subtract their equations $(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2$ and $(x-c)^2+(y-d)^2=s^2$. The quadratic terms cancel, giving a nontrivial line equation because $(a,b)\ne(c,d)$. Every common point lies on this line. A line meets a circle in at most two points: parameterizing the line and substituting into the circle equation gives a quadratic with positive leading coefficient. Hence there are at most two common points. Identical circles must be excluded from the claim.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair O=(0,0),P=(1.2,0);draw(circle(O,1));draw(circle(P,1));draw((.6,-1.2)--(.6,1.2),dashed);label("$O$",O,W);label("$P$",P,E);label("common chord line",(.6,1.3));pair Q=(4,0);draw(circle(Q,1));draw(circle(Q,.6));dot(Q);label("same center",Q+(0,-1.3));
\end{asy}

On the circle, write $(x,y)=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$. The other equation gives $1=4\sin^22\theta$, so $|\sin2\theta|=\dfrac{1}{2}$. The vertex angles are $15^\circ,75^\circ,105^\circ,165^\circ,195^\circ,255^\circ,285^\circ,345^\circ$, with alternating gaps $60^\circ,30^\circ$. Splitting into eight central triangles gives area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot4(\sin60^\circ+\sin30^\circ)=\sqrt3+1$. Thus $a=1,b=3$, and $\boxed{2a^2+b^2=11}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
path p;int[] angles={15,75,105,165,195,255,285,345};for(int k=0;k<8;++k){pair V=dir(angles[k]);p=p--V;draw((0,0)--V,dashed);label(string(k+1),1.13*V);}draw(circle((0,0),1),gray);draw(p--cycle);label("$O$",(0,0),S);
\end{asy}

Let $AB=AC$ and let $M$ be the midpoint of $BC$. Triangles $ABM$ and $ACM$ have three equal corresponding sides, so are congruent by SSS. Their angles at $B,C$ are therefore equal. In the right-triangle case these acute angles sum to $90^\circ$, so each is $\boxed{45^\circ}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,2),B=(-2,0),C=(2,0),M=(0,0);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--M,dashed);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$M$",M,S);
\end{asy}

By symmetry, the plain region has vertical extent $2\sqrt{1-(1-x)^2}-1$ for $1-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\le x\le\dfrac{1}{2}$, with a reflected copy on the other side. Thus its area is $P=4\int_{\dfrac{1}{2}}^{\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}}\sqrt{1-u^2}\,du-(\sqrt3-1)$. Using the antiderivative $\dfrac{1}{2}(u\sqrt{1-u^2}+\arcsin u)$, the integral is $\dfrac{\pi}{12}$, so $P=1+\dfrac{\pi}{3}-\sqrt3$. The colored area is $1-P=\sqrt3-\dfrac{\pi}{3}$. Hence $k=\dfrac{1+\dfrac{\pi}{3}-\sqrt3}{\sqrt3-\dfrac{\pi}{3}}\approx0.4601668$, giving $\boxed{1:0.460}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(1,0),C=(1,1),D=(0,1);path p=arc(D,1,300,330)--arc(A,1,30,60)--arc(B,1,120,150)--arc(C,1,210,240)--cycle;fill(A--B--C--D--cycle,lightgray);fill(p,white);draw(p,blue);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw((.5,0)--(.5,1),dashed);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("plain",(.5,.5));
\end{asy}

A complete test examines every three-digit-or-shorter integer $m$ from $77$ through $769$, forms $n=13m$, keeps $1000\le n\le9999$, and checks whether deleting one of the four digits of $n$ yields $m$. These bounds cover every possible quotient. The successful values of $n$ are $1300,1625,1950,2600,2925,3250,3900,6500,9750$. Their sum is $\boxed{33800}$. For a reproducible exhaustive check:\begin{verbatim}
ans = []
for m in range(77, 770):
    n = 13*m
    if not 1000 <= n <= 9999:
        continue
    s = str(n)
    if any(int(s[:i]+s[i+1:]) == m for i in range(4)):
        ans.append(n)
print(ans, sum(ans))
\end{verbatim}

If its radial distance is $r$, its distances to the circumferences are $|r-1|,|r-5|,|r-7|$. The radius-$5$ circle is closest exactly when $3<r<6$, bounded by radial midpoints. This annulus has area $\pi(6^2-3^2)=27\pi$, while the board has area $49\pi$. The probability is $\boxed{\dfrac{27}{49}}$; boundary ties have area zero.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
fill(circle((0,0),6),lightgray);fill(circle((0,0),3),white);int[] radii={1,5,7};for(int r:radii)draw(circle((0,0),r));draw(circle((0,0),3),dashed);draw(circle((0,0),6),dashed);draw((0,0)--(7,0),dashed);dot((0,0));label("$O$",(0,0),W);label("$1$",(1,0),S);label("$3$",(3,0),S);label("$5$",(5,0),S);label("$6$",(6,0),N);label("$7$",(7,0),S);
\end{asy}

Right triangle $AEB$ gives $EB=12$. Write $EC=x>0$, $ED=y>0$; integer diagonal lengths make $x,y$ integers. The area condition gives $(16+x)(12+y)=500$. The only factor pairs with first entry greater than $16$ and second greater than $12$ are $(20,25)$ and $(25,20)$. Thus $(x,y)=(4,13)$ or $(9,8)$, and $CD^2=x^2+y^2$ is $185$ or $145$. Both yield convex quadrilaterals. The sum is $\boxed{330}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
