We have $\angle ABC=84^\circ$, $\angle BCD=70^\circ$, $\angle BAC=74^\circ$, and $\angle BDC=64^\circ$. Applying the sine rule in $ABC$ and $BCD$ gives $\dfrac{AB}{BD}=\dfrac{\sin22^\circ\sin64^\circ}{\sin74^\circ\sin70^\circ}$. Put $\theta=\angle ADB$. The sine rule in $ABD$ gives $\dfrac{\sin\theta}{\sin(142^\circ-\theta)}=\dfrac{\sin22^\circ\sin64^\circ}{\sin74^\circ\sin70^\circ}=\dfrac{\sin18^\circ}{\sin56^\circ}$. To verify the last identity, expand both triple products with product-to-sum formulas; their difference, multiplied by four, is $\dfrac{1}{2}+\cos8^\circ-\sin38^\circ-\sin22^\circ-\sin54^\circ+\sin18^\circ=0$, since $\sin38^\circ+\sin22^\circ=\cos8^\circ$ and $\sin54^\circ-\sin18^\circ=\dfrac{1}{2}$. On $0<\theta<142^\circ$, the first ratio is strictly increasing: its derivative has the positive sign of $\sin142^\circ$. Therefore $\boxed{\theta=18^\circ}$.
