Use $\phi(n)=n\prod_{p\mid n}(1-\dfrac{1}{p})$, with the product over distinct prime factors. Checking integers $1000$ through $1434$ gives the qualifying list $1240,1252,1269,1434$, with totients $480,624,828,476$ respectively. The right sides are $12\cdot40,12\cdot52,12\cdot69,14\cdot34$, matching these. Thus the fourth value is $\boxed{1434}$. The following exact algorithm verifies the list and its completeness:\begin{verbatim}
answers=[]
for n in range(1000,1435):
    m=n; value=n; p=2
    while p*p<=m:
        if m%p==0:
            value=value//p*(p-1)
            while m%p==0: m//=p
        p+=1
    if m>1: value=value//m*(m-1)
    if value==(n//100)*(n%100): answers.append(n)
assert answers==[1240,1252,1269,1434]
\end{verbatim}

