Let $r=DB=DC$. Since $D$ is the arc midpoint, $\angle BDC=180^\circ-A$, so $r=\dfrac{12}{\cos(\dfrac{A}{2})}$. The cosine rule gives $\cos A=\dfrac{19^2+23^2-24^2}{2\cdot19\cdot23}=\dfrac{157}{437}$, hence $\cos^2(\dfrac{A}{2})=\dfrac{297}{437}$ and $r^2=\dfrac{16\cdot437}{33}$. Ptolemy in cyclic quadrilateral $ABDC$ gives $AD\cdot24=(19+23)r$, so $AD=\dfrac{7}{4}r$. The power of $A$ relative to the circle centered at $D$ is $AD^2-r^2=\dfrac{33}{16}r^2=437$. Along ray $AC$, this gives $AE\cdot AC=437$, so $\boxed{AE=19}$. Because $19<23$, the other intersection indeed lies on segment $AC$.
