pair A=(0,0),B=(0,sqrt(26)),C=(5sqrt(26),0),M=(B+C)/2,D=B+dot(A-B,C-B)/dot(C-B,C-B)*(C-B);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--D,blue);draw(A--M,dashed);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,N);label("$M$",M,NE);label("$5$",(A+D)/2,W);label("$13$",(A+M)/2,S);
\end{asy}

Unrolling the cone gives a sector of radius $6$ whose angle is $\dfrac{2\pi\cdot2}{6}=\dfrac{2\pi}{3}$. Opposite azimuths are separated by half that angle, $\dfrac{\pi}{3}$. Other unrollings differ by full-sector angles, so this is the least absolute angular separation. If the current slant distance is $r$, the next is $\dfrac{r}{2}$. The shortest unfolded path is a straight chord; the cosine rule gives length $\sqrt{r^2+\dfrac{r^2}{4}-r^2\cos(\dfrac{\pi}{3})}=\dfrac{\sqrt3r}{2}$. Since the slant distances are $6,3,\dfrac{3}{2},\ldots$, their sum is $12$. The total is $\boxed{6\sqrt3}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair X=(0,0),P=(6,0),Q=3dir(60);draw(X--arc(X,6,0,120)--cycle);draw(P--Q,blue+linewidth(1.2));draw(X--Q,dashed);label("$X$",X,SW);label("$P$",P,SE);label("$Q$",Q,N);label("$6$",(3,0),S);label("$3$",Q/2,NW);label("$60^\circ$",.8dir(30));label("unrolled cone",(2.5,5.8));
\end{asy}

Except on measure-zero boundaries, the disk crosses the nearest horizontal and vertical grid lines and no others. Let $u,v\in[0,1]$ be its center's distances from those lines. They are independent uniform variables. The disk reaches the fourth square precisely when its distance to the nearest grid vertex is less than $1$, or $u^2+v^2<1$. Thus exactly three squares occur outside a quarter unit disk in the unit square. The probability is $\boxed{1-\dfrac{\pi}{4}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int k=-1;k<=2;++k){draw((2*k,-2)--(2*k,4),gray);draw((-2,2*k)--(4,2*k),gray);}pair O=(.8,.8);draw(circle(O,1),blue);dot("center",O,NE);draw(O--(0,0),dashed);label("$u$",(.4,0),S);label("$v$",(0,.4),W);label("nearest vertex",(0,0),SW);
\end{asy}

Let the legs be $a,b$ and hypotenuse $c$. Arrange four copies with their right angles at the corners of a square of side $a+b$, leaving a central quadrilateral whose four sides are hypotenuses. Adjacent acute angles of the triangles sum to $90^\circ$, so each central angle is $90^\circ$ and the quadrilateral is a square of side $c$. Comparing areas gives $(a+b)^2=4\cdot\dfrac{ab}{2}+c^2$, which simplifies to $\boxed{a^2+b^2=c^2}$.
\begin{asy}
