\end{asy}

There are only six faces, so six is an upper bound. It is attainable. Label vertices by binary coordinates $(x,y,z)\in\{0,1\}^3$; assign $1,4,6,7,8,5,3,2$ to coordinates $000,100,010,110,001,101,011,111$, respectively. Every face sums to $18$: the opposite face pairs have sums $(1+4+6+7,8+5+3+2)$, $(1+6+8+3,4+7+5+2)$, and $(1+4+8+5,6+7+3+2)$. Thus $\boxed6$ face sums can be equal.
