Let face areas be positive integers $A,B,C$ and volume $V$, so $ABC=V^2$ and the edge lengths are $\dfrac{V}{A},\dfrac{V}{B},\dfrac{V}{C}$. Each must be nonintegral, so each of $A,B,C$ has a prime exponent larger than its exponent in $V$. One prime cannot witness this for two face areas: their exponent sum would exceed its exponent in $V^2$. Thus $V$ has at least three distinct prime factors, giving $V\ge2\cdot3\cdot5=30$. Equality is attained by edges $\dfrac{15}{2},\dfrac{10}{3},\dfrac{6}{5}$, whose face areas are $25,9,4$ and volume $30$. Hence $\boxed{30}$.
