The base circumradius is $\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3}$, so the altitude is $h=\sqrt{4-\dfrac{4}{3}}=\dfrac{2\sqrt6}{3}$. Put the base plane at height zero and the apex at height $h$. For an axis point at height $z\le h$, its distance to each side plane is $\dfrac{h-z}{3}$. To see the factor, the base inradius is $\rho=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}$; in the perpendicular section through an edge midpoint the side plane is represented by a line joining $(0,h)$ to $(\rho,0)$, giving distance $\dfrac{\rho(h-z)}{\sqrt{h^2+\rho^2}}=\dfrac{h-z}{3}$.
Tangency to the base requires radius $|z|$, so $|z|=\dfrac{h-z}{3}$. For $z\ge0$ this gives $z=\dfrac{h}{4}$; for $z<0$ it gives $z=-\dfrac{h}{2}$. Thus radii are $\dfrac{\sqrt6}{6}$ and $\dfrac{\sqrt6}{3}$. Their volumes sum to
\[\dfrac{4\pi}{3}\left[\left(\dfrac{\sqrt6}{6}\right)^3+\left(\dfrac{\sqrt6}{3}\right)^3\right]=\boxed{\dfrac{\pi\sqrt6}{3}}.\]
