Write their counts as $2a,3b,5c,7d$, so we count nonnegative solutions of $2a+3b+5c+7d=2023$. A one-dimensional dynamic program counts these exactly. Initially only total zero is possible. When processing denomination $c$, updating totals in increasing order permits any nonnegative number of copies of $c$; all previously processed denominations remain allowed. Thus after four passes the count at $2023$ is precisely the required number.
\begin{verbatim}
ways = [0]*2024
ways[0] = 1
for c in [2,3,5,7]:
    for total in range(c,2024):
        ways[total] += ways[total-c]
print(ways[2023])
# 6653921
\end{verbatim}
The answer is $\boxed{6{,}653{,}921}$.

For $n$ attempts, the complementary probability is $4^{-n}\sum_{j=0}^3\binom nj3^{n-j}$. Exact evaluation at $n=14$ gives a value above $\dfrac{1}{2}$; at $n=15$ it is below $\dfrac{1}{2}$. These checks can be reproduced by:
