A finite exhaustive proof is short because there are only $16$ squares. Index $(r,c)$ by $4r+c$, where $0\le r,c<4$, and form a bitmask of the squares attacked from each position. Check subsets in increasing size:
\begin{verbatim}
from itertools import combinations
attack = []
for k in range(16):
    r,c = divmod(k,4)
    attack.append(sum(1 << (4*a+b)
        for a in range(4) for b in range(4)
        if sorted((abs(a-r),abs(b-c))) == [1,2]))
for size in range(17):
    witness = None
    for group in combinations(range(16),size):
        covered = 0
        for k in group:
            covered |= attack[k]
        if covered == (1 << 16)-1:
            witness = group
            break
    if witness is not None:
        print(size,witness)
        break
# 6 (0, 1, 5, 6, 10, 11)
\end{verbatim}
The first successful size proves minimality, and the displayed placement proves existence. Occupied squares are not automatically covered. Thus $\boxed6$.
