A pair of rectangles has at most eight crossings, because each side crosses the other convex rectangle at most twice. Thus $n$ rectangles yield at most $2+8(1+\cdots+(n-1))=4n(n-1)+2$ regions. Equality is attainable by squares with distinct small rotations and generic tiny translations: all pairs have eight transverse crossings, with no triple crossings. For $n=68$ the bound is $18{,}226$; for $n=69$ it is $18{,}770$. The answer is $\boxed{69}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath"); size(220);
path t=(-1,-1)--(1,-1)--(1,1)--(-1,1)--cycle; draw(t,blue); draw(rotate(15)*t,red); label("$R_1$",(1,-1),SE); label("$R_2$",rotate(15)*(1,1),NE);
\end{asy}

Use the sieve of Eratosthenes: for every prime $p\le\sqrt{2{,}003{,}999}$, remove its multiples starting at $p^2$. Every composite has a prime factor no greater than its square root, so precisely the primes remain. The following exact computation sums them:
\begin{verbatim}
from math import isqrt
N = 2004000
prime = bytearray([1]) * N
prime[0:2] = bytes(2)
for p in range(2, isqrt(N-1)+1):
    if prime[p]:
        for k in range(p*p, N, p):
            prime[k] = 0
print(sum(k for k in range(N) if prime[k]))
# 143460374601
\end{verbatim}
The requested digits are $\boxed{1434}$.

