There are $\sum_{k=1}^7k^2=140$ squares, so at least $70$ must contain a blue vertex. A point $(x,y)$, $0\le x,y\le7$, belongs to
\[\min(x,y)+\min(x,7-y)+\min(7-x,y)+\min(7-x,7-y)\]
squares, by considering the four possible directions from that vertex. Only the four central points $(3,3),(3,4),(4,3),(4,4)$ have incidence $13$; all others have incidence at most $11$.
Suppose six points are blue, of which $c$ are central. Their incidence sum is at most $66+2c$. For $c\le1$ this is below $70$. For $c=2$, the central unit square is counted twice, reducing coverage to at most $69$. For $c=3$, it is counted three times (overcount two), and the two adjacent central pairs each lie on an additional outward unit square (two more overcounts), giving at most $72-4=68$. For $c=4$, the central unit square has overcount three, and the four adjacent central pairs give four more, leaving at most $74-7=67$. Thus six points cannot suffice.
Seven points do suffice: colour $(4,4),(3,3),(5,2),(2,5),(6,5),(3,4),(2,2)$ blue. The numbers of squares hit of side lengths $1,\ldots,7$ are $22,24,14,6,4,0,0$, totalling $70$. These counts may be verified by testing the lower-left corner and side length of each square:
\begin{verbatim}
blue={(4,4),(3,3),(5,2),(2,5),(6,5),(3,4),(2,2)}
hits=[]
for d in range(1,8):
    hits.append(sum(bool(blue & {(x,y),(x+d,y),
        (x,y+d),(x+d,y+d)})
        for x in range(8-d) for y in range(8-d)))
print(hits) # [22,24,14,6,4,0,0]
\end{verbatim}
The answer is $\boxed7$, choice A.
