Let $\theta$ be the degree measure of $\angle HAD$. Since $H$ lies on side $CD$ of the unit square, $0\le\theta\le45$ and the right triangle $ADH$ gives $AH=\sec\theta$. Thus $\theta\cos\theta=15\sqrt3$, with cosine evaluated in degrees. The function $u\cos(\dfrac{\pi u}{180})$ is strictly increasing on $[0,45]$: its derivative is $\cos t-t\sin t>0$ for $0\le t\le\dfrac{\pi}{4}$, since $t\tan t\le\dfrac{\pi}{4}<1$. At $u=30$, the value is $30\cos30^\circ=15\sqrt3$. Therefore $\theta=30^\circ$ uniquely, and $\boxed{\angle DHA=60^\circ}$, choice C.
The configuration is feasible: take $A=(0,0),D=(0,1),C=(1,1)$, $E=(\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2},\dfrac{1}{2})$, and $F=E+(-\dfrac{1}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2})$. Then $H=(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3},1)$ lies on both required segments.
