Each smaller triangle has a $45^\circ$ angle at the original right-angle vertex. Its other angle at an original acute vertex must be $45^\circ$ or $67.5^\circ$ to be isosceles: the alternative $90^\circ$ is impossible there. The two original acute angles sum to $90^\circ$, forcing both to be $45^\circ$. Thus the original triangle is isosceles right, with legs $\sqrt2$ and area $\boxed1$.
