Count nonnegative integer solutions to $2a+3b+5c+7d=37$. For each $d,c$, put $R=37-7d-5c$. Then $0\le b\le\lfloor\dfrac{R}{3}\rfloor$ and $b\equiv R\pmod2$, determining $a=\dfrac{R-3b}{2}$. Summing these counts gives $\boxed{74}$. This complete finite implementation verifies the count:\begin{verbatim}
count=0
for d in range(6):
    for c in range((37-7*d)//5+1):
        R=37-7*d-5*c
        count+=sum((R-3*b)%2==0
                   for b in range(R//3+1))
assert count==74
\end{verbatim}

Such an $n$ divides $97^{1500}-2$. Its smallest divisor greater than one is prime, so only primes need testing. The following exact modular search checks all candidates below $89$ and confirms none works. For $89$, Fermat gives $97^{1500}\equiv8^{1500}\equiv8^4=4096\equiv2\pmod{89}$. Thus $\boxed{n=89}$.\begin{verbatim}
assert all(pow(97,1500,n)!=2 for n in range(2,89))
assert pow(97,1500,89)==2
\end{verbatim}

