For $n=\prod p_i^{e_i}$, the divisor count is $\prod(e_i+1)$, because each exponent in a divisor can range independently from zero to $e_i$. Applying this criterion gives $\boxed{360,420,480,504,540,600,630,660,672,756,780,792,864,924,936,990,1020}$. The following independent divisor-pair enumeration covers the entire stated domain:\begin{verbatim}
