Scale the edge length to 1. The straight distance between opposite vertices is $\sqrt2$. On the surface, unfold two adjacent faces on opposite sides of an equatorial edge; they form two equilateral triangles sharing a base. The line between the opposite vertices then has length $2\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}=\sqrt3$ and folds to a valid path through the edge midpoint. No shorter route exists: any surface path must meet the equatorial square, whose nearest points to either opposite vertex are edge midpoints, at Euclidean distance $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$. Each part of the path is at least that distance. Thus the speeds are in ratio $\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt2}{\sqrt3}=\dfrac{\sqrt6}{3}}$.
