Its centre $(h,k)$ lies on the perpendicular bisector $h+k=6$. Its squared radius is $r^2=(h-1)^2+(4-h)^2=2h^2-10h+17$. First-quadrant containment requires $r\le h$ and $r\le6-h$. These give $h\ge5-2\sqrt2$ and $h\le-1+2\sqrt5$ (the other roots lie outside $0\le h\le6$). The convex quadratic attains its maximum over this interval at an endpoint. At the left endpoint $r=5-2\sqrt2$; at the right $r=7-2\sqrt5$, which is larger. Thus $\boxed{r=7-2\sqrt5}$.
