(a) The sum is nonnegative and vanishes only when all coordinates vanish. Absolute homogeneity follows from $|\lambda x_i|=|\lambda||x_i|$. The real triangle inequality in each coordinate gives $\|x+y\|\le\|x\|+\|y\|$. Thus it is a norm.
For completeness, any Cauchy sequence in this norm is Cauchy in every coordinate because $|x_i-y_i|\le\|x-y\|$ (all weights are at least one). Each coordinate converges in $\mathbb R$. The weighted sum of the four coordinate errors then tends to zero, proving convergence in the norm. Thus the space is complete.
(b) $\|A-v\|=4+3+1+12=20$ and $\|B-v\|=6+1+2+6=15$, so $\boxed B$ is closer. This conclusion depends on the chosen target, features, and weights; the norm measures those choices, not an independent notion of quality.
