Put $A=(-a,0),B=(a,0)$ with $a>0$. For a fixed angle $\alpha$, define $h=a\cot\alpha$ and $R=\dfrac{a}{\sin\alpha}$. The locus is the arc strictly above $AB$ of the circle centred at $(0,h)$ with radius $R$, together with its reflection below $AB$, omitting $A,B$. For $\alpha<90^\circ$ these are major arcs; for $\alpha>90^\circ$ they are minor arcs; for $\alpha=90^\circ$ they form the circle with diameter $AB$, minus its endpoints.
Indeed, any admissible $P$ has circumradius $\dfrac{AB}{2\sin\alpha}=R$ by the extended sine rule. Its circumcentre lies on the perpendicular bisector, at the indicated signed height on the side determined by the angle. Conversely, the inscribed-angle theorem gives angle $\alpha$ at every point of each specified arc. The opposite arc of either circle gives the supplementary angle and is therefore not included, except at $90^\circ$.
