size(7cm);
path pie=(0,0)--(0,1)--arc((0,0),1,90,360)--cycle;filldraw(pie,lightgray);real a=1/sqrt(2);filldraw((-a,0)--(a,0)--(a,-a)--(-a,-a)--cycle,lightblue);dot((0,0));label("$O$",(0,0),NW);label("$\sqrt2R$",(0,-a),S);label("$\dfrac{R}{\sqrt2}$",(a,-a/2),E);label("Removed quarter",(0.7,0.65));
\end{asy}

Let the height be $h$ and slant height $\ell=\sqrt{h^2+9}$. The area is $\pi(6+3)\ell+9\pi$, so $K=9\ell+9$. Its volume is
\[V=\dfrac{\pi h}{3}(36+18+9)=21\pi h.\]
The rounding condition $95.5\le V<96.5$ gives $1.4<h<1.5$. Hence $38<K<40$, so the only integer possibility is $K=39$. Therefore $\ell=\dfrac{10}{3}$ and
\[h=\sqrt{\dfrac{100}{9}-9}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt{19}}{3}}.\]
Its actual volume is $7\pi\sqrt{19}\approx95.86$, which does round to $96$, verifying existence.
