Let the circle radius be $R$, chord length $L$, and the smaller central angle be $\theta\in(0,\pi)$ in radians. The minor segment has area $\dfrac{R^2}{2}(\theta-\sin\theta)=\dfrac{\pi R^2}{3}$, so $\theta$ is the unique solution of
\[\theta-\sin\theta=\dfrac{2\pi}{3},\quad0<\theta<\pi,\]
because the left side increases strictly from zero to $\pi$. The chord satisfies $L=2R\sin(\dfrac{\theta}{2})$.
The square's cut-off right-isosceles triangle has hypotenuse $L$ and area $\dfrac{L^2}{4}$. Such a corner triangle has at most half the square's area, so it is the smaller one-third region, making square area $\dfrac{3L^2}{4}$. Consequently the desired ratio is
\[\boxed{\dfrac{\pi}{3\sin^2(\dfrac{\theta}{2})}}\approx1.126248,\]
where $\theta\approx2.605326$.
