There are $\binom{100}{3}=161700$ total triples. The grid has $180$ adjacent cell pairs; each lies in $98$ triples. If a triple contains two adjacency edges, they share a middle cell. The number of such edge pairs is $4\binom22+32\binom32+64\binom42=484$, using corner, boundary and interior degrees. No three cells form a cycle of three adjacency edges, because the grid is bipartite. Inclusion--exclusion therefore gives $161700-180\cdot98+484=\boxed{144544}$.
\begin{asy}
