Put the sphere centre at height zero. The fraction of volume below height $z\in[-1,1]$ is $F(z)=\dfrac{\int_{-1}^z\pi(1-t^2)\,dt}{\dfrac{4\pi}{3}}=\dfrac{2+3z-z^3}{4}$. The second and third cuts have fractions $\dfrac{1}{4},\dfrac{3}{8}$, so $3z_2-z_2^3=-1$ and $3z_3-z_3^3=-\dfrac{1}{2}$. For $z=2\sin\theta$ with $|\theta|\le\dfrac{\pi}{6}$, $3z-z^3=2\sin3\theta$. Thus $z_2=-2\sin\dfrac{\pi}{18}$ and $z_3=-2\sin(\dfrac{1}{3}\arcsin\dfrac{1}{4})$. Their separation is $\boxed{2\sin\dfrac{\pi}{18}-2\sin(\dfrac{1}{3}\arcsin\dfrac{1}{4})}$. Uniqueness follows because $F$ strictly increases inside the sphere. For radius $R$, multiply this result by $R$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw(circle((0,0),1));real z2=-2*sin(pi/18),z3=-2*sin(asin(.25)/3);draw((-sqrt(1-z2*z2),z2)--(sqrt(1-z2*z2),z2),blue);draw((-sqrt(1-z3*z3),z3)--(sqrt(1-z3*z3),z3),red);label("second cut",(-1.1,z2),W);label("third cut",(1.1,z3),E);draw((0,-1)--(0,1),dashed);label("unit sphere: axial section",(0,-1.4));
\end{asy}

At vertex $C$, the equilateral triangle gives $\angle BCD=60^\circ$. Diagonal $AC$ of a rhombus bisects the angles at its endpoints (the two triangles across it are congruent by three equal sides). Hence $\angle ACD=\boxed{30^\circ}$.
