The shortest route consists of two tangent segments and the shorter connecting circle arc, on either side by symmetry. Any exterior portion can be shortened to a straight segment, and optimal transition points must be tangent; the shortest boundary connection is the shorter arc. Each tangent length is $\sqrt{6^2-3^2}=3\sqrt3$. The radius-to-tangent-point angle from the ray toward the corresponding endpoint is $60^\circ$, leaving a connecting central angle $180-60-60=60^\circ$. Its arc length is $3\cdot\dfrac{\pi}{3}=\pi$. The total is $\boxed{6\sqrt3+\pi}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(-6,0),B=(6,0),O=(0,0),T=3dir(120),U=3dir(60);draw(circle(O,3),gray);draw(A--T,blue);draw(arc(O,3,60,120),blue+linewidth(1.2));draw(U--B,blue);draw(A--(T.x,-T.y),dashed);draw(arc(O,3,240,300),dashed);draw((U.x,-U.y)--B,dashed);draw(O--T^^O--U,dashed);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$O$",O,S);label("$T$",T,NW);label("$U$",U,NE);label("$3$",O--U,E);
\end{asy}

Let there be $v$ stations. A connected union of nine lines contains a spanning tree with $v-1$ edges. Choosing a line containing each tree edge shows that at most $v-1$ lines connect all stations. Since eight never suffice, $v\ge10$. A cycle of ten stations, with each line consisting of one cycle edge, attains $v=10$. Now in any minimal configuration, suppose a line had at least two edges. Choose nine lines containing it; their union is connected. Extend the edges of that path to a spanning tree (a forest always extends in a connected graph). The tree has nine edges, at least two supplied by the same line, so at most eight distinct lines supply all its edges, contradicting the hypothesis. A line with no edge would also be impossible: a connected union of nine containing it would already be a connected union of eight. Thus each line has one edge and two stations. The average is $\boxed2$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair[] v;for(int k=0;k<10;++k)v.push(2dir(90+36*k));for(int k=0;k<10;++k){draw(v[k]--v[(k+1)%10]);dot(v[k]);label(string(k+1),1.15v[k]);}label("ten single-edge lines",(0,-2.8));
\end{asy}

Let the circumradius be $R$ and take $A_1=(R,0)$, $A_2=(\dfrac{\sqrt3R}{2},\dfrac{R}{2})$. The line through opposite vertices $A_5,A_{11}$ is $y=-\sqrt3x$. Parametrize $X=A_1+t(A_2-A_1)$; substitution gives $t=-(3+2\sqrt3)$. Hence $XA_2=(4+2\sqrt3)s$, where $s=A_1A_2$. Also $s^2=R^2(2-\sqrt3)$, so the given product is $(4+2\sqrt3)(2-\sqrt3)R^2=2R^2=10$. Thus $R^2=5$. The dodecagon splits into twelve triangles of area $\dfrac{1}{2}R^2\sin30^\circ$, giving area $3R^2=\boxed{15}$.
