Every finite term is defined, since no integer is an odd multiple of $\dfrac{\pi}{2}$. Interchanging $m,n$ in the finite square sum does not change it. Adding the two numerators gives $n(\sec^2m-\tan^2m)+m(\sec^2n-\tan^2n)=n+m$. Therefore $2S_N=\sum_{n=1}^N\sum_{m=1}^N3^{-(m+n)}=(\sum_{j=1}^N3^{-j})^2$. The inner geometric sum tends to $\dfrac{1}{2}$, so $\boxed{\lim S_N=\dfrac{1}{8}}$. Finite symmetric sums justify the cancellation without any unproved interchange of infinite series.
