Reflect $P$ through $N$ to construct $Q=2N-P$. Since $M,N$ are midpoints, $AB=2BM$ and $BC=2BN$. Also $C=2N-B$, so $CD=2CP=2BQ$. Therefore $B$ is equidistant from $M,N,Q$: it is their circumcentre, constructed as the intersection of two perpendicular bisectors. These three points are noncollinear because $M,N,P$ are. Reflect $B$ through $M$ to get $A$, reflect $B$ through $N$ to get $C$, and reflect $C$ through $P$ to get $D$. These operations recover the original midpoint relations and equal side lengths, proving correctness and uniqueness.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(2,0),C=(3,sqrt(3)),D=(1,sqrt(3)),M=(A+B)/2,Np=(B+C)/2,P=(C+D)/2,Q=2Np-P;draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(M--Np--Q--cycle,dashed);draw(B--M^^B--Np^^B--Q,blue);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$M$",M,S);label("$N$",Np,E);label("$P$",P,N);label("$Q$",Q,S);
\end{asy}

The outer triangle contributes one. The first subdivision contributes its four half-side triangles: three corner triangles and the central triangle. The second subdivision contributes four quarter-side triangles inside that central triangle. This totals $1+4+4=\boxed9$. No other size is possible: every drawn edge has one of the three original side directions, and the only supporting lines added in the second stage terminate inside the central triangle, so any triangle using one of them is one of its four subdivisions.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(2,2sqrt(3)),M=(A+B)/2,Np=(B+C)/2,P=(C+A)/2;draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(M--Np--P--cycle);draw((M+Np)/2--(Np+P)/2--(P+M)/2--cycle,blue);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);
\end{asy}

The three circle pairs have at most $2\binom32=6$ intersections. The nine line-circle pairs have at most $18$, and the three line pairs at most $3$. Thus the bound is $\boxed{27}$. It is attainable: choose three nearly coincident circles with pairwise intersecting boundaries and a common interior region, then three general-position secant lines crossing that region, avoiding all prior intersections and tangencies. Small perturbations avoid triple intersections while preserving every pair count.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw(circle((0,0),2));draw(circle((.4,.1),2));draw(circle((.15,.45),2));draw((-3,-.4)--(3,.2),blue);draw((-3,.7)--(3,-.8),red);draw((-.7,-3)--(.5,3),green);label("generic secants and overlapping circles",(0,-3.5));
\end{asy}

A diagonal of length $d$ contributes $d(d-1)$ ordered source-destination pairs. In either diagonal direction, the lengths are $1,2,\ldots,7,8,7,\ldots,1$. Thus the total over both directions is $2[2\sum_{d=1}^7d(d-1)+8\cdot7]=2(224+56)=560$. No distinct square pair shares both directions. Divide by $64$ starting squares: $s=\dfrac{560}{64}=\dfrac{35}{4}$, so $\boxed{4s=35}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int k=0;k<=8;++k){draw((k,0)--(k,8),gray);draw((0,k)--(8,k),gray);}dot((3.5,3.5));draw((.5,.5)--(7.5,7.5)^^(.5,6.5)--(6.5,.5),blue);label("bishop",(3.5,3.5),E);
\end{asy}

If $Q$ has $n$ sides, the angle difference is $\dfrac{360}{n}-\dfrac{360}{3n}=\dfrac{240}{n}=16$, so $n=15$ and $P$ has $45$ sides. The diagonal count is $\dfrac{k(k-3)}{2}$ for a $k$-gon. The difference is $\dfrac{45\cdot42-15\cdot12}{2}=\boxed{855}$.
