Since $1296=6^4$, the strings for $0$ through $1295$ padded to four base-six digits use each digit $0,\ldots,5$ exactly $6^3=216$ times in each position. Their digit sum is $4\cdot216\cdot15=12960$; $1296=10000_6$ adds one, while removing zero changes nothing. The decimal digit sum is $16740$, obtained by the following exact enumeration. The combined sum is $\boxed{12961+16740=29701}$.
\begin{verbatim}
print(sum(sum(map(int,str(n))) for n in range(1,1297)))
# 16740
\end{verbatim}

Choose coordinates $O=(0,0)$, $A=(-24,7)$, $B=(24,7)$, since the half-length of $AB$ is $\sqrt{25^2-7^2}=24$. Write $X=(u,7)$. The inequality $AX>BX$ gives $u>0$. Line $CD$ has angle $60^\circ$ or $-60^\circ$ to the positive horizontal axis; its distance from $O$ is respectively $\dfrac{|\sqrt3u-7|}{2}$ or $\dfrac{|\sqrt3u+7|}{2}$. Setting these equal to $15$ and retaining $u>0$ yields $u=\dfrac{37}{\sqrt3}$ or $\dfrac{23}{\sqrt3}$. Both are less than $24$, so both intersections lie inside the chord. Thus $\boxed{BX=24-\dfrac{37}{\sqrt3}\quad\text{or}\quad24-\dfrac{23}{\sqrt3}}$. In the requested original $x+y+z$ form these give $64$ and $50$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int k=0;k<2;++k){pair off=(65*k,0),O=off,A=off+(-24,7),B=off+(24,7);real u=(k==0?37:23)/sqrt(3);pair X=off+(u,7),v=dir(k==0?60:-60);real w=dot(X-O,v),disc=sqrt(w*w+625-dot(X-O,X-O));pair C=X+(-w+disc)*v,D=X+(-w-disc)*v;draw(circle(O,25));draw(A--B^^C--D,blue);dot(X);label("$O$",O,S);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$X$",X,S);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,S);label(k==0?"$u=\dfrac{37}{\sqrt3}$":"$u=\dfrac{23}{\sqrt3}$",off+(0,-32));}
\end{asy}

The common mean must equal the whole-set mean, $\dfrac{11}{2}$. A valid block $S$ therefore satisfies $2\sum_{x\in S}x=11|S|$. To count without ordering the blocks, always take the block containing the least remaining element, and recurse on its complement. This uniquely specifies the first block of every partition, so there is neither omission nor overcounting. The exact computation yields $\boxed{64}$.
\begin{verbatim}
from functools import lru_cache
@lru_cache(None)
def count(mask):
 if not mask: return 1
 first=mask&-mask
 total=0
 sub=mask
 while sub:
  if sub&first:
   values=[i+1 for i in range(10) if (sub>>i)&1]
   if 2*sum(values)==11*len(values):
    total+=count(mask^sub)
  sub=(sub-1)&mask
 return total
print(count((1<<10)-1))
# 64
\end{verbatim}

Since $14^2+48^2=2500=50^2$, the triangle is right. Its area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot14\cdot48=\boxed{336}$.
