Put $CA=a,CB=b$. Then $a+b=5$. The cosine rule gives $9=a^2+b^2-ab=25-3ab$, so $ab=\dfrac{16}{3}$. Thus the area is $\dfrac{1}{2}ab\sin60^\circ=\boxed{\dfrac{4\sqrt3}{3}}$. Such a triangle exists: $a,b$ are the positive roots of $t^2-5t+\dfrac{16}{3}=0$, namely $\dfrac{15\pm\sqrt{33}}{6}$, whose difference is less than 3.
