Reversing the current list when a pass starts on the right makes index 0 the first encountered position. Delete indices divisible by the pass number, then reverse back. The following exact simulation always removes at least one label and never removes all labels while at least two remain, so it terminates.\begin{verbatim}
a=list(range(1,1501)); k=2; left=True
while len(a)>1:
    if not left: a.reverse()
    a=[v for i,v in enumerate(a) if i%k!=0]
    if not left: a.reverse()
    left=not left
    k+=1
print(a[0]) # 764
\end{verbatim}
The survivor is $\boxed{764}$.

Maintain the already computed terms, appending the sum of the latest three. The recurrence proves each appended term correct by induction.\begin{verbatim}
a=[0,1,2]
for n in range(3,101):
    a.append(sum(a[-3:]))
print(a[100]) # 151404293106684183601223222
\end{verbatim}
Thus $\boxed{L_{100}=151404293106684183601223222}$.

A quarter-turn changes radius by $e^{\dfrac{b\pi}{2}}=\phi$, so $\boxed{b=\dfrac{2\ln\phi}{\pi}}$. Sample $\theta_j=\dfrac{4\pi j}{1000}$ and convert polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates.\begin{verbatim}
from math import sqrt,log,pi,cos,sin,exp
phi=(1+sqrt(5))/2
b=2*log(phi)/pi
points=[]
for j in range(1001):
    t=4*pi*j/1000
    r=exp(b*t)
    points.append((r*cos(t),r*sin(t)))
\end{verbatim}
Joining successive samples draws a polygonal approximation; the analytic curve, unlike the source's joined quarter-circles, is exactly logarithmic.
