The minimizing number uses consecutive primes, with nonincreasing positive exponents: moving an exponent to a smaller unused prime, or swapping an inverted pair, reduces the number without changing its divisor count. Thus enumerate multiplicative partitions of the desired divisor count, using factors one larger than the successive exponents. The following complete recurrence gives $f(200)=498960$ and $f(50)=6480$, hence the quotient is $\boxed{77}$.
\begin{verbatim}
from functools import lru_cache
primes=[2,3,5,7,11,13,17,19]
@lru_cache(None)
def f(d,i=0,cap=200):
    if d==1: return 1
    return min((primes[i]**(v-1)*f(d//v,i+1,v-1)
                for v in range(2,min(d,cap+1)+1)
                if d%v==0),default=float('inf'))
assert f(200)==498960 and f(50)==6480
assert f(200)//f(50)==77
\end{verbatim}

Each region consists of its fixed circular segment and a triangle with the corresponding square side as base. Opposite triangles have equal bases and heights summing to the distance between the opposite sides, so the two opposite regions sum to half the disk. The other fractions are consequently $\dfrac{1}{2}-\dfrac{1}{5}=\dfrac{3}{10}$ and $\dfrac{1}{2}-\dfrac{1}{3}=\dfrac{1}{6}$. In descending order these are $\dfrac{1}{3},\dfrac{3}{10},\dfrac{1}{5},\dfrac{1}{6}$, so the answer is $\boxed{\dfrac{3}{10}}$.
