Unfold the reflections into the standard triangular tiling, with basis vectors $u=(1,0)$ and $v=(\dfrac{1}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2})$ from the starting vertex. The first vertex reached by a straight ray has coordinates $mu+nv$ with positive coprime integers $m,n$; non-coprime coordinates would meet a vertex earlier. The three families of side lines give respectively $m-1,n-1,m+n-1$ interior crossings, for $2(m+n)-3$ reflections. Thus $m+n=1250$. The vertex labels in the reflected tiling are given by $m-n$ modulo three, as is checked on one triangle and preserved by reflecting across each edge. Return to the original vertex therefore requires $m-n\equiv0\pmod3$. We must count $1\le m\le1249$ with $\gcd(m,1250)=1$ and $m\equiv1\pmod3$. There are $417$ integers in the latter progression; exclude $208$ even values and $83$ multiples of five, then restore the $42$ multiples of ten. The answer is $417-208-83+42=\boxed{168}$.
\begin{verbatim}
from math import gcd
assert sum(gcd(m,1250)==1 and (2*m-1250)%3==0
           for m in range(1,1250))==168
\end{verbatim}
