pair A=(0,0),B=(5,0),C=(2,4),H=(2,0),J=B+dot(A-B,C-B)/dot(C-B,C-B)*(C-B);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(C--H^^A--J,blue);label("altitudes",(2.5,-.6));
\end{asy}

Label the vertices $0,\ldots,7$ on one ring and $8,\ldots,15$ on the other. Each has its two ring neighbours and its corresponding vertex on the other ring. Let $F(U,v)$ count completions from current vertex $v$ after exactly the visited set $U$, starting at vertex zero. For each unvisited neighbour $w$, add $F(U\cup\{w\},w)$. When all vertices are visited, return one if $v$ is adjacent to zero and zero otherwise. This branches once for each legal next edge and therefore counts every oriented route exactly once. Exact evaluation gives $\boxed{20}$:
\begin{verbatim}
from functools import lru_cache
adj=[[(i//8)*8+(i+1)%8,(i//8)*8+(i-1)%8,i^8]
     for i in range(16)]
@lru_cache(None)
def f(mask,v):
    if mask==65535: return int(0 in adj[v])
    return sum(f(mask|(1<<w),w) for w in adj[v]
               if not (mask>>w)&1)
assert f(1,0)==20
\end{verbatim}
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair v=(1,1.5);pair[] p;for(int i=0;i<8;++i)p.push(2dir(45i));for(int i=0;i<8;++i){draw(p[i]--p[(i+1)%8]);draw((p[i]+v)--(p[(i+1)%8]+v));draw(p[i]--(p[i]+v));}dot("$A$",p[5],SW);
\end{asy}

Write $A=(0,0),L=(s,0),E=(s,s),U=(u,v)$ and $z=s^2$. Perpendicularity gives $u^2-su+v^2=0$. From $UL^2=3136$ obtain $su=z-3136$; subtracting $UL^2$ from $UE^2=4225$ gives $sv=\dfrac{z-1089}{2}$. Substitution in $UL^2$ yields $3136^2+\dfrac{(z-1089)^2}{4}=3136z$, or $(z-3665)(z-11057)=0$. For both positive roots, $u=\dfrac{z-3136}{\sqrt z}$ and $v=\dfrac{z-1089}{2\sqrt z}$ lie strictly between zero and $s$, so both are valid. Their sum is $\boxed{14722}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
real z=3665,s=sqrt(z);pair A=(0,0),L=(s,0),Ee=(s,s),X=(0,s),U=((z-3136)/s,(z-1089)/(2s));draw(A--L--Ee--X--cycle);draw(A--U--L^^U--Ee,blue);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$L$",L,SE);label("$E$",Ee,NE);label("$X$",X,NW);label("$U$",U,W);label("one valid configuration",(s/2,-s/7));
\end{asy}

The answer is $\boxed6$. Here is an upper bound valid also by a slight generic perturbation when vertices or sides coincide with grid lines. If $v,h$ vertical and horizontal grid lines cross the square's interior and $g$ grid vertices lie strictly inside it, the number of cells met is $1+v+h+g$: add the parallel lines first, then each transverse line creates one more region plus one for each interior intersection. The square's horizontal and vertical spans are at most $\sqrt2<2$, so $v,h\le2$. It cannot contain three grid vertices strictly inside: among any three distinct integer points some pair has distance at least $\sqrt2$, whereas two interior points of a unit square have distance strictly less than $\sqrt2$. Thus $g\le2$. If $g\le1$, the count is at most 6. If $g=2$, the two interior grid vertices must be adjacent. Rotate or reflect the grid so they are $(0,0),(1,0)$. Let the square axes be $(c,s),(-s,c)$ with $c=\cos\theta,s=\sin\theta\ge0$, and write its centre's coordinates in those axes as $\alpha,\beta$. Containment of the two vertices gives $\alpha<\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\beta<\dfrac{1}{2}-s$. Thus its greatest vertical coordinate is less than $\alpha s+\beta c+\dfrac{s+c}{2}<s+c-sc\le1$, the last inequality following from $(1-s)(1-c)\ge0$. Reflecting vertically gives its least coordinate greater than-1. Hence only the horizontal grid line 0 crosses its interior, so $h=1$, and again $1+v+h+g\le6$. Attainment: use a square centred at $(\dfrac{1}{3},\dfrac{1}{3})$ rotated 30 degrees. Its spans cross both grid lines 0 and 1 in each direction, and it contains the origin strictly inside. Therefore $1+2+2+1=6$ cells are met.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair O=(1/3,1/3),u=.5*dir(30),v=.5*dir(120);for(int k=-1;k<=2;++k){draw((k,-1)--(k,2),lightgray);draw((-1,k)--(2,k),lightgray);}filldraw(O+u+v--O-u+v--O-u-v--O+u-v--cycle,lightblue+opacity(.4),blue);dot((0,0));dot(O);label("$(0,0)$",(0,0),SW);label("$C$",O,E);
\end{asy}

The hypotenuse has length 2, so $P=(-1,0),R=(1,0)$ and the right-angle vertex is $Q=(0,1)$. Inside the square, the triangle consists of points with $0\le y\le1-|x|$. Its area there is a unit square minus two triangles of legs $\dfrac{1}{2},\dfrac{1}{2}$, namely $1-2\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}=\boxed{\dfrac{3}{4}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair P=(-1,0),R=(1,0),Q=(0,1),A=(-.5,0),B=(.5,0),C=(.5,1),D=(-.5,1);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(P--Q--R--cycle,blue);label("$P$",P,SW);label("$R$",R,SE);label("$Q$",Q,N);label("$A$",A,S);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);
\end{asy}

Their integer square roots range from 10 to 31. Exhaustive digit testing is:\begin{verbatim}
ans=[k*k for k in range(10,32)
     if (k*k//10)%10==k//10 and (k*k)%10!=k%10]
print(ans,sum(ans)) # [529,729] 1258
\end{verbatim}
Thus the requested sum is $\boxed{1258}$.

Put $A=(0,0),B=(s,0),C=(s,s),D=(0,s)$, so $E=(s,3),F=(4,s)$. The tangent-of-angle-difference formula gives $\dfrac{\dfrac{s}{4}-\dfrac{3}{s}}{1+\dfrac{3}{4}}=1$, hence $s^2-7s-12=0$. Therefore $EF^2=(s-4)^2+(s-3)^2=2s^2-14s+25=49$, and $\boxed{EF=7}$. The positive root $s=\dfrac{7+\sqrt{97}}{2}>4$ validates the specified side points.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
real s=(7+sqrt(97))/2;pair A=(0,0),B=(s,0),C=(s,s),D=(0,s),Ep=(s,3),F=(4,s);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--Ep--F--A,blue);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$E$",Ep,E);label("$F$",F,N);label("$3$",(B+Ep)/2,E);label("$4$",(D+F)/2,N);
\end{asy}

The ten ratio units total $180^\circ$, so one is $18^\circ$. The largest angle is $5(18^\circ)=\boxed{90^\circ}$.
\begin{asy}
