\end{asy}

The triangle is right because $9^2+40^2=41^2$, so its area is $180$. An altitude to side $s$ has length $\dfrac{360}{s}$, minimized for the longest side $41$. Thus the shortest altitude is $\boxed{\dfrac{360}{41}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(40,0),C=(0,9),H=B+dot(A-B,C-B)/dot(C-B,C-B)*(C-B);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--H,blue);label("$40$",(20,0),S);label("$9$",(0,4.5),W);label("$41$",(20,4.5),N);
\end{asy}

Any one of the three points can be the common vertex of two adjacent sides. Vector addition gives possible fourth vertices $(7,12),(1,-2),(-1,2)$. Their $x$-coordinates sum to $\boxed7$.
\begin{asy}
