The cube's body diagonal equals the outer diameter $4$. If its edge is $s$, then $s\sqrt3=4$. The inner sphere has diameter $s$, so its radius is $\boxed{\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3}=\dfrac{2\sqrt3}{3}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(-1,-1),B=(1,-1),C=(1,1),D=(-1,1),v=(.7,.7);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw((A+v)--(B+v)--(C+v)--(D+v)--cycle);draw(A--(A+v)^^B--(B+v)^^C--(C+v)^^D--(D+v));draw(circle((.35,.35),1),blue);draw(circle((.35,.35),2),gray);label("schematic projection",(.35,-2.1));
\end{asy}

Place $A=(0,0),C=(16,0),E=(12,0)$ and $D=(12,d)$ with $d>0$. Perpendicularity $AD\perp DC$ gives $(12,d)\cdot(4,-d)=0$, hence $d=4\sqrt3$ and $AD=8\sqrt3$. Because $G$ lies on segment $AD$ and $AG=\dfrac{1}{4}AD$, $G=(3,\sqrt3)$. Therefore $EG=\sqrt{(12-3)^2+(0-\sqrt3)^2}=\boxed{2\sqrt{21}}$. These conditions are consistent, for instance with $B=(4,12\sqrt3)$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,12sqrt(3)),C=(16,0),D=(12,4sqrt(3)),Ep=(12,0),F=(3/7,9sqrt(3)/7),G=(3,sqrt(3));draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--D^^D--Ep^^Ep--F,blue);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,E);label("$E$",Ep,S);label("$F$",F,W);label("$G$",G,N);
\end{asy}

The two directions from the house are perpendicular. Hence the school-to-tournament distance is $\sqrt{5^2+12^2}=13$ miles, giving $\dfrac{13}{0.5}=\boxed{26}$ hours.
\begin{asy}
