Each occupies five of the rectangle's six cells, so delete one cell. Deleting a corner produces two chiral types: the four corners split into two orbits under a half-turn. Deleting either of the two noncorner cells gives one further type, and all three remain connected. No quarter-turn identifies the two corner types, because the bounding rectangle would change orientation. Hence the answer is $\boxed3$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int k=0;k<3;++k){int omit=k==0?0:k==1?2:1;for(int y=0;y<2;++y)for(int x=0;x<3;++x)if(3y+x!=omit)filldraw(shift(4k+x,y)*unitsquare,lightgray,black);}
\end{asy}

The sum is $720^\circ$, also equal to $(6-2)180^\circ$. Dividing by six gives $\boxed{120^\circ}$.
\begin{asy}
