Use the unit square $A=(0,0),B=(1,0),C=(1,1),D=(0,1)$. Equal lengths give $E=(1,t),F=(t,1)$ with $t=2-\sqrt3$. Then $M=(\dfrac{1+t}{2},\dfrac{1+t}{2})$. Substitution of $P=\lambda(1,t)$ in $PM^2=FM^2$ gives roots $\lambda=1,\dfrac{1}{2}$, so $P=(\dfrac{1}{2},\dfrac{t}{2})$. Direction $MP$ has slope $\dfrac{1}{t}=2+\sqrt3=\tan75^\circ$, and $Q=(1-t,1)$. Ray $QC$ points right while ray $QP$ points down-left; their smaller angle is $180^\circ-75^\circ=\boxed{105^\circ}$.
\begin{asy}
