In polar coordinates, the original triangle has radius $r(\alpha)=\dfrac{6\sqrt3}{\sqrt3\cos\alpha+\sin\alpha}$ for $0\le\alpha\le90^\circ$. On any angle interval its maximum occurs at an endpoint, because the positive denominator is concave. For a direction $0\le\theta\le90^\circ$ the swept radius is $\max(6,r(\theta))$; $r(\theta)=6$ at $0^\circ,60^\circ$. For $90^\circ\le\theta\le180^\circ$ the longest leg sweeps radius $6\sqrt3$. Thus the union splits into a radius-six sector of $60^\circ$, the original triangular wedge between $60^\circ$ and $90^\circ$, and a radius-$6\sqrt3$ sector of $90^\circ$. The wedge vertices are $(0,0),(3,3\sqrt3),(0,6\sqrt3)$, giving area $9\sqrt3$. Total area is $6\pi+9\sqrt3+27\pi=\boxed{33\pi+9\sqrt3}$.
