For an equilateral triangle, the circumradius is twice the inradius. Since the next circumradius equals the current inradius, successive side lengths halve and areas scale by $\dfrac{1}{4}$. The first area is $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}$, so the sum is $\dfrac{\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}}{1-\dfrac{1}{4}}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt3}{3}}$.
