The denominator is $(a+3)(a-2)$, so positivity requires $a\ge3$. Also it is at most $1260$, forcing $a\le35$ because it strictly increases and its value at $36$ is $1326$. Testing this finite interval gives $a=3,4,6,9,11$, with denominators $6,14,36,84,126$. These divide $1260$; every other candidate fails. The following exact check certifies completeness.\begin{verbatim}
assert [a for a in range(3,36)
        if 1260%(a*a+a-6)==0]==[3,4,6,9,11]
\end{verbatim}
The count is $\boxed5$.

For legs $a,b$ and hypotenuse $c$, the equation is $\dfrac{ab}{2}=a+b+c$. Since area equals $rs$ and perimeter is $2s$, the inradius is $r=2$. The right-triangle identity $r=\dfrac{a+b-c}{2}$ gives $c=a+b-4$. Substituting into $c^2=a^2+b^2$ gives $(a-4)(b-4)=8$. Both factors are positive because $a+b-c<\min(a,b)$. The unordered positive factor pairs $(1,8),(2,4)$ yield triangles $(5,12,13)$ and $(6,8,10)$. The sum is $\boxed{23}$.
