Write $z=x+iy$ and let $r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$. If $z=0$, take $r=0$ and any real angle $\theta$. Otherwise the point $(\dfrac{x}{r},\dfrac{y}{r})$ lies on the unit circle. By the parametrization of that circle there is an angle $\theta$ with $\cos\theta=\dfrac{x}{r}$ and $\sin\theta=\dfrac{y}{r}$. Hence $z=r(\cos\theta+i\sin\theta)$, the required polar representation. For $z\ne0$, $r$ is unique and $\theta$ is unique modulo $2\pi$.
\begin{asy}
