Put $C=(0,0),A=(1,0),B=(0,1)$. Since $CDE$ is right isosceles, $D=(0,t),E=(t,0)$ with $0<t<1$. For $AEF$ to be right isosceles, either its right angle is at $E$, giving $F=(t,1-t)$, or at $F$, giving $F=(\dfrac{1+t}{2},\dfrac{1-t}{2})$; a right angle at $A$ cannot put $F$ on $AB$. Similarly $BFD$ gives $F=(1-t,t)$ or $F=(\dfrac{1-t}{2},\dfrac{1+t}{2})$. Equating these possibilities gives $t=\dfrac{1}{2}$ or $t=\dfrac{1}{3}$; the fourth pairing gives the forbidden $t=0$. In both allowed cases direct dot products verify $DEF$ is right isosceles. Thus the distinct lengths are $\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt2}{3}$, summing to $\boxed{5\dfrac{\sqrt2}{6}}$.
