\end{asy}

All reachable values are rational, so exact breadth-first search using fractions certifies the minimum.\begin{verbatim}
from fractions import Fraction
level={Fraction(2)}
for moves in range(12):
 if Fraction(2016) in level:
  print(moves);break
 level={y for x in level for y in (x+2,x-2,2*x,x/2)}
# 11
\end{verbatim}
Induction shows level contains precisely the values attainable in that many moves. An explicit 11-move route is $2,4,8,16,32,64,128,126,252,504,1008,2016$. Hence $\boxed{11}$.

Label the opposite vertex pairs $(0,1),(2,3),(4,5)$. Edges join vertices from distinct pairs; faces select one vertex from each pair. This yields the following complete enumeration.\begin{verbatim}
from itertools import combinations,product
edges=[(a,b) for a,b in combinations(range(6),2) if a//2!=b//2]
index={e:i for i,e in enumerate(edges)}
faces=[[index[tuple(sorted(e))] for e in combinations(f,2)]
       for f in product([0,1],[2,3],[4,5])]
ans=sum(all(len({col[i] for i in f})==3 for f in faces)
        for col in product(range(3),repeat=12))
print(ans) # 24
\end{verbatim}
Every labelled edge assignment is checked exactly once, so the answer is $\boxed{24}$.
