The triangle altitude is $3+4+5=12$ by Viviani's area decomposition. Label the sides so their distances are 3 to $AB$,4 to $BC$,5 to $CA$, and use $A=(0,0),B=(8\sqrt3,0),C=(4\sqrt3,12)$, with starting point $P=(\dfrac{13}{\sqrt3},3)$. For any order of visiting the sides, reflect the start successively across the first two side-lines. The length is at least the perpendicular distance from that reflected point to the third line. Direct reflection gives bound 15 for either order starting with $AB$,16 for orders starting with $BC$, and 17 for orders starting with $CA$ (equivalently altitude plus the distance to the first side). The lower bound 15 is attained: go to $X=(\dfrac{13}{\sqrt3},0)$, then $Y=(\dfrac{13}{\sqrt3},11)$ on $BC$, then $Z=(\dfrac{23}{2\sqrt3},\dfrac{23}{2})$ on $CA$. Their successive distances are 3,11,1, totaling $\boxed{15}$.
