\end{asy}

Take $B=(0,0),C=(9,0),A=(\dfrac{9}{2},9\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}),D=(4,0)$. Write $E=A+u(C-A),F=A+v(B-A)$, with $0\le u,v\le1$. Because $DE$ and $DF$ have equal length and are perpendicular, $\overrightarrow{DF}$ is the 90-degree counterclockwise rotation of $\overrightarrow{DE}$; the other rotation would put $F$ below $BC$. Equating coordinates gives $1-9v=-9\sqrt3(1-u)$ and $9\sqrt3(1-v)=1+9u$. Solving yields $u=\dfrac{14-4\sqrt3}{9},v=\dfrac{13-5\sqrt3}{9}$, both in $[0,1]$. Thus $EA+FA=9(u+v)=\boxed{27-9\sqrt3}$.
