Both rectangles have diagonal $BD$, hence share its midpoint $O$ and circumcircle. Their other diagonals are $AC$ and $EF$. Since $ACEF$ forms a square, these two diameters are perpendicular. Congruence means the acute angles which $BD$ makes with $AC$ and $EF$ are equal, so both are $45^\circ$. If the common diagonal length is $d$, either rectangle has area $\dfrac{1}{2}d^2\sin45^\circ=\dfrac{d^2}{2\sqrt2}=1$. The square has diagonals $d,d$, giving area $\dfrac{d^2}{2}=\boxed{\sqrt2}$.
