Reflect the pentagon across its fifth side. The union is a simple octagon with all eight boundary sides 2 and twice the original area. The polygonal isoperimetric inequality states that a simple n-gon of perimeter $P$ has area at most $\dfrac{P^2}{4n\tan(\dfrac{\pi}{n})}$, attained by the regular n-gon. For $n=8,P=16$, this bounds the doubled area by $8\cot(\dfrac{\pi}{8})=8(1+\sqrt2)$. Equality is attained by taking half a regular octagon cut along a diameter through opposite vertices. Thus the greatest pentagon area is $\boxed{4+4\sqrt2}$.
