Four distinct rectangle shapes would have area at least $1+2+3+4=10>9$, so at most three rectangles fit. To count three-piece tilings, every tiling of a rectangle by three rectangles has a full horizontal or vertical separating cut: otherwise the boundary between the first two rectangles would require at least four rectangles around its first junction. Such a tiling consists either of three parallel strips or of one full strip with the remaining rectangle split perpendicularly. Three parallel strips here are congruent and forbidden. In the second case the full strip has width $1$ or $2$ and length $3$. If its width is $1$, the remaining $2\times3$ region must split into $2\times1$ and $2\times2$ rectangles: two choices for the location of that cut. If its width is $2$, the remaining $1\times3$ region splits into $1\times1$ and $1\times2$: again two choices. There are four choices of boundary side for the full strip. Thus $m=3$, $n=4\cdot2\cdot2=16$, and $\boxed{mn=48}$.
