Two triangles suffice: use $(0,0),(2,0),(0,2)$ and $(2,2),(2,0),(0,2)$. Their boundaries contain the eight perimeter points and the center $(1,1)$. To prove one does not suffice, the following exhaustive check examines all $\binom93=84$ vertex triples, skips collinear triples, and checks boundary membership by an exact cross product and bounding-box test. It prints $6$, the largest number of the nine points on one such boundary.
\begin{verbatim}
from itertools import product,combinations
P=list(product(range(3),repeat=2))
def cross(a,b,p):
    return (b[0]-a[0])*(p[1]-a[1])-(b[1]-a[1])*(p[0]-a[0])
def on(a,b,p):
    return (cross(a,b,p)==0
            and min(a[0],b[0])<=p[0]<=max(a[0],b[0])
            and min(a[1],b[1])<=p[1]<=max(a[1],b[1]))
best=0
for T in combinations(P,3):
    if cross(*T)==0: continue
    edges=list(zip(T,T[1:]+T[:1]))
    best=max(best,sum(any(on(a,b,p) for a,b in edges) for p in P))
print(best)
\end{verbatim}
