Use coordinates $O_1=(0,0)$ and $O_2=(11,0)$. Subtracting the circle equations shows that their common chord $CD$ lies on $x=16$; hence choose $D=(16,12)$ and $C=(16,-12)$. Write $E=(x,y)$. Since $E$ lies on the smaller circle, $x^2+y^2=22x+48$. Power of a point in the large circle gives $ED\cdot EB=400-x^2-y^2=22(16-x)$, so $ED=\sqrt2(16-x)$. Squaring this distance yields $2(16-x)^2=448-10x-24y$, or $y=\dfrac{-x^2+27x-32}{12}$. Substitution into the smaller-circle equation simplifies to $(x-16)^2(x-23)(x+1)=0$. The intersection $E$ is strictly inside the large circle, so $x<16$; only $x=-1$ remains. Thus $O_1E^2=22(-1)+48=\boxed{26}$.
