Count walks avoiding the starting vertex $0$ after every positive step. Number the other vertices $1$ through $5$ consecutively. Let $v_k(j)$ count such walks of length $k$ ending at $j$; any move to $0$ is discarded. From $v_1=(1,0,0,0,1)$, each new vector is obtained by adding neighbouring entries on this path of five vertices. The successive vectors are $(0,1,0,1,0)$, $(1,0,2,0,1)$, $(0,3,0,3,0)$, $(3,0,6,0,3)$, $(0,9,0,9,0)$, $(9,0,18,0,9)$, and $(0,27,0,27,0)$. Thus $54$ of the $256$ equally likely walks avoid return. The desired probability is $1-\dfrac{54}{256}=\dfrac{101}{128}$, and $a+b=\boxed{229}$.
