Write the parabola as $y=a(x-h)^2+k$, with $a\ne0$. Its focus is $(h,k+\dfrac{1}{4a})$, while the secant line is $x+4y-20=0$. The two given points imply $h=2+\dfrac{1}{8a}$ and $k=\dfrac{9}{2}-4a-\dfrac{1}{64a}$. The focus is below the line at the specified distance exactly when $h+4k+\dfrac{1}{a}-20=-16$. Substitution gives $256a^2-256a-17=0$. Both real nonzero roots define valid parabolas and satisfy the signed distance condition. Their sum is $1$ and product is $-\dfrac{17}{256}$, so the sum of their reciprocal values is $-\dfrac{256}{17}$. Therefore the two vertex heights sum to $9-4+\dfrac{4}{17}=\dfrac{89}{17}$, giving $p+q=\boxed{106}$.
