Write $AB=b$, $AC=c$, $AD=d$, and $\cos\angle BAC=t$. Let $L=AM$. Since $D,E$ are on the perpendicular bisector, $MD=AD=d$ and $ME=AE=7$. The median vector is half the sum of the side vectors; expanding these two distance equalities gives $d(b+ct)=L^2$ and $7(bt+c)=L^2$. Power of $A$ in the circle $BDEC$ gives $db=7c$. Substituting $d=\dfrac{7c}{b}$ into the two equalities yields $(c^2-b^2)t=0$. If $t=0$, triangle $ABC$ is right at $A$, so its median to the hypotenuse satisfies $AM=MC=ME=7$. Then $7c=L^2=49$, so $c=7=AE$, forcing $E=C$, contrary to distinctness. Thus $b=c$. Now $d=7$, and $MC=ME=7$ gives $BC=14$. By the right triangle $AMC$, $L^2=b^2-49$. Since $MB=MC=ME=7$, $M$ is the centre of the circle through the three noncollinear points $B,C,E$. Power of $A$ therefore gives $7b=L^2-49=b^2-98$. Hence $(b-14)(b+7)=0$, so $b=14$ and $\boxed{AM^2=147}$.
