Let $v,e$ be the vertex and edge counts. Then $3v=2e$, so $v$ is even. A connected component has at least 4 vertices. With 4 vertices the graph would be $K_4$, impossible for unit distances: a unit equilateral triangle has no point at unit distance from all three vertices. With 6 vertices the complement is a 2-regular simple graph, hence either two triangles or a 6-cycle. The original graph is respectively $K_{3,3}$ (nonplanar) or the triangular prism. The prism cannot have a planar unit-edge drawing. If its outer face is a triangle, that unit equilateral triangle cannot contain the other disjoint unit triangle of equal area. Otherwise the outer face is a quadrilateral, hence a rhombus. Its three quadrilateral faces have corresponding opposite edges parallel, so the two triangular faces are translates by a vector $w$. For the three oriented sides $u_1,u_2,u_3$ of one triangle, $u_1+u_2+u_3=0$. The three rhombus areas are $|u_i\times w|$; each is at most the sum of the other two. But the outer rhombus must contain the two other rhombi and two positive-area triangles, a contradiction. Thus every possible component has at least 8 vertices, so $e\ge12$. For attainment, form a diamond from the two equilateral triangles on the edge joining $(-\dfrac{1}{2},0)$ to $(\dfrac{1}{2},0)$; its other vertices are $(0,\pm\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2})$. Translate this five-edge diamond by $(\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2},\dfrac{1}{2})$, and join each pair of corresponding degree-two vertices. The two added segments have length 1, and the displayed coordinates give disjoint diamonds and noncrossing joining edges. All eight vertices have degree 3. Hence $\boxed{12}$.
