Rotate axes to make the square axis-parallel, and let its side be $s$. The origin is outside it. If its nearest point lies on an edge, let that edge be at distance $d\ge2$. The opposite edge is distance $d+s$ in that coordinate, and at least one of its vertices has other-coordinate magnitude at least $\dfrac{s}{2}$. Hence $25\ge(d+s)^2+\dfrac{s^2}{4}\ge(2+s)^2+\dfrac{s^2}{4}$, giving $s\le\dfrac{14}{5}$. If the closest point is a vertex $(a,b)$ with $a,b\ge0$ and $a^2+b^2\ge4$, its opposite vertex has squared distance $a^2+b^2+2s(a+b)+2s^2\ge4+4s+2s^2$. This implies $s\le\dfrac{-2+\sqrt{46}}{2}<\dfrac{14}{5}$. The bound is attained by the square $[2,\dfrac{24}{5}]\times[-\dfrac{7}{5},\dfrac{7}{5}]$, whose nearest side is tangent to the inner circle and far vertices lie on the outer circle. Thus $\boxed{\dfrac{14}{5}}$.
