The chord $AB$ subtends a 60-degree minor arc. Its arc avoiding $C,D$ must be 60 rather than 300: in the latter case an angle at $C$ would exceed 150 degrees, whereas every angle of this isosceles trapezoid is 70 or 110. Let equal side arcs $BC,DA$ have measure $u$, and the arc $CD$ avoiding $A,B$ have measure $v$. The angle at $C$ is $\dfrac{u+60}{2}$. If it were 110, then $u=160$, leaving $v<0$; hence it is 70 and $u=80$. Thus $v=360-60-160=140$, already minor, giving $\boxed{140^\circ}$.
