Scale the edge to 1. A shortest path need not revisit a face: the portion between two visits can be replaced by the straight chord inside that convex face, with no greater length. Consequently it suffices to consider simple paths in the face-adjacency graph, which is a cube. Unfold each such face chain into the plane. The path length is at least the distance between its unfolded endpoint centres. The 18 simple cube paths between opposite vertices have the following squared endpoint distances: six three-edge paths give $\dfrac{7}{3}$, six five-edge paths give $\dfrac{13}{3}$, and six seven-edge paths give $\dfrac{25}{3}$. The exact enumeration below verifies all cases. Its coordinates $(u,v)$ represent $(u,\sqrt3v)$, so the squared norm is $u^2+3v^2$; moving across an edge reflects the opposite vertex. Paths passing through vertices follow by a limit of edge-crossing paths, so the same lower bound holds. For attainability, unfold the four triangles with vertices successively $(0,0),(1,0),(\dfrac{1}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2})$; replace the first vertex by $(\dfrac{3}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2})$, then the second by $(1,\sqrt3)$, then the third by $(2,\sqrt3)$. The segment from $(\dfrac{1}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt3}{6})$ to $(\dfrac{3}{2},\dfrac{5\sqrt3}{6})$ crosses their shared edges in order and has squared length $\dfrac{7}{3}$. Rescaling gives $\boxed{\dfrac{10\sqrt{21}}{3}}$.
\begin{verbatim}
from fractions import Fraction as F
P=[(F(0),F(0)),(F(1),F(0)),(F(1,2),F(1,2))]
def add(a,b):return(a[0]+b[0],a[1]+b[1])
def sub(a,b):return(a[0]-b[0],a[1]-b[1])
def mul(a,c):return(a[0]*c,a[1]*c)
def dot(a,b):return a[0]*b[0]+3*a[1]*b[1]
counts={}
def walk(v,p,path):
 if v==7:
  c=mul(add(add(p[0],p[1]),p[2]),F(1,3))
  u=sub(c,(F(1,2),F(1,6)))
  key=(len(path)-1,dot(u,u))
  counts[key]=counts.get(key,0)+1
  return
 for i in range(3):
  w=v^(1<<i)
  if w in path:continue
  j,k=[t for t in range(3) if t!=i]
  a,b,q=p[j],p[k],p[i];u=sub(b,a)
  h=add(a,mul(u,dot(sub(q,a),u)/dot(u,u)))
  pn=p.copy();pn[i]=sub(mul(h,2),q)
  walk(w,pn,path+[w])
walk(0,P,[0])
assert counts=={(3,F(7,3)):6,(5,F(13,3)):6,(7,F(25,3)):6}
\end{verbatim}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(1,0),C=(.5,sqrt(3)/2),D=(1.5,sqrt(3)/2),Ee=(1,sqrt(3)),F=(2,sqrt(3)),U=(.5,sqrt(3)/6),V=(1.5,5*sqrt(3)/6);draw(A--B--C--cycle^^B--D--C^^C--Ee--D^^D--F--Ee);draw(U--V,red+1bp);dot(U);dot(V);label("start",U,SW);label("finish",V,NE);label("edge 1",(A+B)/2,S);label("four-face unfolding",(1,-.4));
\end{asy}

Classify line directions modulo 180 degrees. Directions connected by an angle 60 degrees come in disjoint triples $\theta,\theta+60^\circ,\theta+120^\circ$. If a triple contains $a,b,c$ lines, it contributes $ab+bc+ca\le\dfrac{(a+b+c)^2}{3}$ pairs. Summing over triples, and using at most 12 lines, gives at most $\dfrac{12^2}{3}=48$. Four equilateral triangles with parallel corresponding sides and generic translations have twelve distinct lines, four in each of three directions. Every pair in different directions meets at 60 degrees, giving $3\cdot4\cdot4=\boxed{48}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int k=0;k<4;++k){pair A=(.32*k,.17*k),B=A+(2,0),C=A+(1,sqrt(3));draw(A--B--C--cycle);label("$T_{"+string(k+1)+"}$",A,SW);}label("four translated equilateral triangles",(1,-.5));
\end{asy}

The visibility kernel of a simple polygon is the intersection of the inward half-planes of its directed edges: violating an edge half-plane blocks points immediately beyond that edge, and satisfying all such half-planes makes every radial segment stay inside. Put $A=(0,0),B=(1,0),C=(1,1),D=(0,1)$, with $E=(\dfrac{1}{2},-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2})$ and the other apexes obtained by quarter-turns. Intersecting the eight inward half-planes gives the convex octagon with vertices $A,(\dfrac{1}{2},-\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt3}),B,(1+\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt3},\dfrac{1}{2}),C,(\dfrac{1}{2},1+\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt3}),D,(-\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt3},\dfrac{1}{2})$. For example its lower two edges lie on $y=-\dfrac{x}{\sqrt3}$ and $y=\dfrac{x-1}{\sqrt3}$, the extended edges adjacent to the left and right external triangles; symmetry gives the rest, and substitution checks all eight original inequalities. It consists of the unit square and four triangles of base 1 and height $\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt3}$. Its area is $1+4\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt3}=\boxed{1+\dfrac{\sqrt3}{3}}$.
