The sphere centres project into a disk of radius 1 and their heights range over an interval of length at most $h-2$. Among any four points of that disk, some pair has distance at most $\sqrt2$: if no point is the centre, order their polar angles and choose a gap at most 90 degrees; their squared separation is $r^2+s^2-2rs\cos\theta\le2$. A centre point makes the claim immediate. The corresponding sphere centres, at distance at least 2, must therefore differ in height by at least $\sqrt{4-2}=\sqrt2$. Hence $h\ge2+\sqrt2$. Equality is attained by centres $(1,0,1),(0,1,1+\sqrt2),(-1,0,1),(0,-1,1+\sqrt2)$. Adjacent projected points are $\sqrt2$ apart and opposite points 2 apart, so all spheres are disjoint or tangent. Thus $\boxed{h=2+\sqrt2}$.
