An exact enumeration over all $9!=362880$ grids gives $\boxed{432}$. Each permutation specifies the grid in row-major order, so every allowed arrangement occurs once. The test below uses sets exactly as stated. No geometric identifications are taken: rotations and reflections remain distinct grids. The enumeration is small enough to check directly and certifies the count.
\begin{verbatim}
from itertools import permutations
count=0
for a in permutations(range(1,10)):
 rows={a[3*i]*a[3*i+1]*a[3*i+2] for i in range(3)}
 cols={a[i]*a[i+3]*a[i+6] for i in range(3)}
 if rows==cols:count+=1
assert count==432
\end{verbatim}

There are $\boxed7$. For an exhaustive construction, use axial coordinates $(x,y)$ for hexagon centres, with neighbours displaced by $(1,0),(0,1),(-1,1),(-1,0),(0,-1),(1,-1)$. Starting from one cell, add an adjacent cell three times. Every connected four-cell shape has a spanning tree, so a leaf-removal order shows that this process finds it. Canonicalize by translations, six rotations $(x,y)\mapsto(-y,x+y)$, and reflection $(x,y)\mapsto(y,x)$. The following exact finite enumeration leaves precisely the seven shapes illustrated.
\begin{verbatim}
def rot(p):x,y=p;return(-y,x+y)
def refl(p):x,y=p;return(y,x)
def canon(P):
 forms=[]
 for flip in [False,True]:
  Q=[refl(p) if flip else p for p in P]
  for _ in range(6):
   m=min(x for x,y in Q);n=min(y for x,y in Q)
   forms.append(tuple(sorted((x-m,y-n) for x,y in Q)))
   Q=[rot(p) for p in Q]
 return min(forms)
S={((0,0),)}
D=[(1,0),(0,1),(-1,1),(-1,0),(0,-1),(1,-1)]
for n in range(3):
 S={canon(set(P)|{(x+dx,y+dy)}) for P in S
    for x,y in P for dx,dy in D if (x+dx,y+dy) not in P}
assert len(S)==7
\end{verbatim}

