Every candidate is encoded by 11 letters D (double) and M (subtract 1). A sequence ending at 120 never reaches a nonpositive value, since neither operation can restore positivity. On positive values the two operations give different results, so encodings are unique. Exhaustively checking the $2^{11}$ encodings gives exactly four: DDDDDDMMMMD, DDDDDMDMDMM, DDMMDDDMDDD, DMDDDDDMMDD. The following certificate performs each operation and checks the final value, proving $\boxed4$.
