The second point $D$ is the excenter opposite $A$: it lies on $AI$, and $\angle BDC=90^\circ-\dfrac{A}{2}$, supplementary to $\angle BIC=90^\circ+\dfrac{A}{2}$, so it lies on the same circle. Since $CD$ is an external angle bisector at $C$, its angle with $CB$ is $90^\circ-\dfrac{C}{2}$. The tangent-chord theorem makes this equal to $A$. Hence $2A+C=180^\circ$, and comparison with $A+B+C=180^\circ$ gives $A=B$. Thus $BC=AC=25$. Put $A=(0,0),B=(14,0),C=(7,24)$. The triangle area is 168 and semiperimeter 32. The $A$-exradius is $\dfrac{168}{32-25}=24$, while the $A$-angle bisector has slope $\dfrac{3}{4}$, so $D=(32,24)$. Therefore $CD=25$ and its distance from $B$ is 24. Hence $[BCD]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot25\cdot24=\boxed{300}$.
