Put $X=(0,0,0)$ and the cube at $[-1,1]^3$. For a vertex $v=(\pm1,\pm1,\pm1)$, the inequality $|p|^2<|p-v|^2$ is $p\cdot v<\dfrac{3}{2}$. Requiring it for all signs is $|x|+|y|+|z|<\dfrac{3}{2}$. The full octahedron defined by this inequality has volume $8\cdot\dfrac{1}{6}(\dfrac{3}{2})^3=\dfrac{9}{2}$. Six disjoint caps lie beyond the cube faces. For example the cap $x>1$ has cross-sectional area $2(\dfrac{3}{2}-x)^2$, and therefore volume $\dfrac{2}{3}(\dfrac{1}{2})^3=\dfrac{1}{12}$, equivalently a pyramid of base area $\dfrac{1}{2}$ and height $\dfrac{1}{2}$. Removing six such caps gives $\dfrac{9}{2}-6\cdot\dfrac{1}{12}=\boxed4$. Strict or weak distance inequalities give the same volume.
