If the centre triangle side is $d$, non-overlap requires $d\ge2$. A containing square must have side 2 larger than a square containing the three centres, because every centre is at least 1 from each side. For an equilateral triangle of side $d$, symmetry permits its base angle to the horizontal to be $0\le\theta\le30^\circ$. Its horizontal span is $d\cos\theta$, and its vertical span is $d\sin(60^\circ+\theta)$. The first decreases and the second increases in this interval. Their maximum is minimized when they coincide, at $\theta=15^\circ$, giving $d\cos15^\circ$. Choosing the least allowed $d=2$ yields square side $2+2\cos15^\circ=\boxed{2+\dfrac{\sqrt6+\sqrt2}{2}}$.
