Let $R$ be the circumradius and $r=OM$. Since $OM\perp AB$, $R^2-r^2=12^2=144$. The circle centred at $O$ of radius $r$ meets line $BC$ at two points whose distances from $B$ multiply to 144 and sum to 25. They are 9 and 16, so $BK=9,CK=16$. On $CA$, the distances from $C$ multiply to 144 and sum to 26, giving 8 and 18; thus $CL=8,AL=18$. Removing the three corner triangles from $ABC$ yields the area fraction $1-\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\dfrac{9}{25}-\dfrac{16}{25}\cdot\dfrac{8}{26}-\dfrac{18}{26}\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}=\dfrac{18}{65}$. Heron gives $[ABC]=\sqrt{\dfrac{75}{2}\dfrac{27}{2}\dfrac{25}{2}\dfrac{23}{2}}=\dfrac{225\sqrt{23}}{4}$. Hence $[MLK]=\dfrac{405\sqrt{23}}{26}$ and $\boxed{a+b+c=454}$.
