Track the publicly possible pairs $(c,d)$, initially all nonnegative pairs with $c+d\in\{18,20\}$. On Carol's turn she knows exactly when all surviving pairs with her value of $c$ have the same total; analogously for David and $d$. A public pass deletes all pairs in which that speaker would know. This finite deduction is implemented below.
\begin{verbatim}
possible = {(c,total-c) for total in (18,20)
             for c in range(total+1)}
actual = (12,8)
for day in range(1,21):
    for speaker in (0,1):
        knows = {s for s in possible
                 if len({sum(t) for t in possible
                         if t[speaker] == s[speaker]}) == 1}
        if actual in knows:
            assert (day,speaker) == (5,0)
            print('Carol, day 5, total 20')
            break
        possible -= knows
    else:
        continue
    break
\end{verbatim}
The state update follows exactly from common knowledge and truthful passing, so this exhaustive finite reasoning proves that $\boxed{\text{Carol announces 20 on day 5}}$.

