A prime $p$ divides the product for every $n$ exactly when $S$ represents every residue class modulo $p$: one factor is then 0 for every choice of $n$, and conversely a missing residue supplies $n$ for which no factor vanishes. Since $|S|\le10$, primes greater than 10 cannot have all residues represented. It therefore suffices to check $p=2,3,5,7$ for each of the 1024 subsets.
\begin{verbatim}
count=0
for mask in range(1<<10):
    S=[a for a in range(10) if mask>>a&1]
    if all(len({a%p for a in S})<p for p in (2,3,5,7)):
        count+=1
assert count==45
\end{verbatim}
The answer, including the empty set, is $\boxed{45}$; if nonempty subsets alone were intended, it would be 44.

There are three rows, three columns, and two diagonals, giving $\boxed8$.
