The angles $BOC,COD$ are supplementary, so $\angle BCD+\angle BAD=180^\circ$ and the quadrilateral is cyclic. By the intersecting-chords angle formula, $2\angle BOC=\widehat{BC}+\widehat{AD}$, while $2\angle BCD=\widehat{BA}+\widehat{AD}$. Therefore arcs $BC,BA$ are equal, giving $BC=BA$. Consequently $\angle BAO=\angle BAC=\angle BDC=\angle BDA$. Also $\angle ABO=\angle ABD$, so triangles $BAO$ and $BDA$ are similar, with $\dfrac{BO}{BA}=\dfrac{BA}{BD}$. Hence $BO=\dfrac{AB^2}{BD}=\boxed{\dfrac{16}{5}}$.
