\end{asy}

Place $A=(0,0)$, $B=(\sqrt3,0)$, and $D=(-\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2},\dfrac{3}{2})$. The perpendicular to $AB$ at $B$ is $x=\sqrt3$. The perpendicular to $AD$ at $D$ is $y-\dfrac{3}{2}=\sqrt3(x+\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2})$, so $C=(\sqrt3,9)$. Thus $BC=9$ and $DC=5\sqrt3$. The two right triangles along $AC$ give area $\dfrac{1}{2}(AB\cdot BC+AD\cdot DC)=12\sqrt3$. Hence $\boxed{p+q=15}$.
