Let $a=14,b=9,c=12,s=\dfrac{35}{2}$, and let $r$ be the inradius. The half-angle formula and Heron's formula give $\sin^2\dfrac{A}{2}=\dfrac{(s-b)(s-c)}{bc}$ and $r^2=\dfrac{(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}{s}$. Since $AI=\dfrac{r}{\sin(\dfrac{A}{2})}$, it follows that $AI^2=\dfrac{bc(s-a)}{s}=\dfrac{108}{5}$. Thus $\boxed{AI=\dfrac{6\sqrt{15}}{5}}$.
