Each original apex angle is $30^\circ$. Joining along the unequal bases produces a rhombus whose other diagonal gives congruent triangles. Joining equal legs with opposite endpoint types gives a parallelogram, again producing congruent triangles. Therefore the noncongruent case joins equal legs with apex matched to apex, forming a kite. Place the common apex at $A=(0,0)$, common leg endpoint at $S=(9,0)$, and outer vertices at $B=(\dfrac{9\sqrt3}{2},\dfrac{9}{2})$ and $C=(\dfrac{9\sqrt3}{2},-\dfrac{9}{2})$. The new diagonal $BC$ has length $9$. Its distances to $A,S$ are $\dfrac{9\sqrt3}{2}$ and $9-\dfrac{9\sqrt3}{2}$. The larger area is therefore $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot9\cdot\dfrac{9\sqrt3}{2}=\boxed{\dfrac{81\sqrt3}{4}}$.
