A palindrome is uniquely determined by its first nine bits, so there are only $2^9=512$ candidates. The following exhaustive certificate tests each candidate and each of its seventeen possible splits. Each accepted string is counted once, regardless of how many splits work. It gives $\boxed{36}$.\begin{verbatim}
from itertools import product
count=0
for h in product("01",repeat=9):
    s="".join(h)+"".join(h[::-1])
    if any(s[:k]==s[:k][::-1] and
           s[k:]==s[k:][::-1] for k in range(1,18)):
        count+=1
assert count==36
\end{verbatim}

The tile apothem is $7\sqrt3$. The center must remain at least $\sqrt3$ from every side, leaving a concentric regular hexagon of apothem $6\sqrt3$ and side $12$. By similarity, the probability is $\left(\dfrac{12}{14}\right)^2=\dfrac{36}{49}$. Thus $p+q=\boxed{85}$.
