The swaps implement a permutation, and every permutation can be implemented by adjacent swaps. A permutation with cycle lengths $c_1,\ldots,c_k$ returns after $\operatorname{lcm}(c_1,\ldots,c_k)$ days. Thus we maximize this lcm over partitions of $17$. The following complete recursion lists each nondecreasing partition once and checks its lcm. It gives $\boxed{210}$, attained by cycles of lengths $2,3,5,7$.
\begin{verbatim}
from math import lcm
def partitions(n,lo=1):
    if n==0:
        yield ()
    for a in range(lo,n+1):
        for tail in partitions(n-a,a):
            yield (a,)+tail
best=max(lcm(*p) for p in partitions(17))
assert best==210
assert lcm(2,3,5,7)==210
\end{verbatim}

Reflect $B$ to $B^{\prime}=(b,-v)$. For every bank point $P$, $PB=PB^{\prime}$, so $AP+PB=AP+PB^{\prime}\ge AB^{\prime}=\sqrt{(a-b)^2+(u+v)^2}$. Equality holds when $P$ is the intersection of segment $AB^{\prime}$ with the bank, which lies on that segment because the endpoint heights have opposite signs. Thus the shortest route has length $\boxed{\sqrt{(a-b)^2+(u+v)^2}}$. The intersection is $P=(\dfrac{va+ub}{u+v},0)$.
