The three gaps between consecutive chosen vertices contain positive integer numbers of hexagon edges summing to six. The triangle angles are half the corresponding $60^\circ$ arcs, so their values are $30^\circ$ times those gaps. The smallest is $30^\circ$ exactly when at least one gap is one. Of $\binom63=20$ triangles, the only ones with all gaps at least two are the two alternating equilateral triangles, with gaps $(2,2,2)$. Thus the answer is $\boxed{18}$.
