An affine change of coordinates preserves all midpoint and intersection relations and scales all areas equally, so use the model $A=(-\dfrac{3}{2},3)$, $B=(\dfrac{3}{2},3)$, $C=(2,-4)$, $D=(-2,-4)$, whose diagonals meet at the origin and whose area is $\dfrac{49}{2}$. The actual area is $\dfrac{10}{7}$ times the model area. In the model, the two trapezoids overlap for $-2\le y\le\dfrac{3}{2}$. Their horizontal widths there are $w_1=\dfrac{12}{5}+\dfrac{y}{5}$ and $w_2=\dfrac{24}{11}-\dfrac{5y}{11}$. They are equal at $y=-\dfrac{1}{3}$, with width $\dfrac{7}{3}$. The common region therefore consists of two trapezoids of areas $\dfrac{1}{2}(2+\dfrac{7}{3})\cdot\dfrac{5}{3}=\dfrac{65}{18}$ and $\dfrac{1}{2}(\dfrac{7}{3}+\dfrac{3}{2})\cdot\dfrac{11}{6}=\dfrac{253}{72}$. Their sum is $\dfrac{57}{8}$ in the model, so the requested area is $\dfrac{10}{7}\cdot\dfrac{57}{8}=\boxed{\dfrac{285}{28}}$.
