Put $\angle BAC=4t$, so ray $AI$ makes angle $2t$ with $AB$, and ray $AO$ makes angle $3t$ with $AB$ and $t$ with $AC$. If the circumradius is $R$, the isosceles triangles formed with the center give $AB=2R\cos3t$ and $AC=2R\cos t$. Also $BC=2R\sin4t$. Therefore $\dfrac{7}{21}=\dfrac{\sin4t}{\cos3t+\cos t}=2\sin t$, yielding $\sin t=\dfrac{1}{6}$ and $\cos2t=\dfrac{17}{18}$. Write $b=AC,c=AB$. The cosine rule gives $7^2=21^2-4bc\cos^2(2t)$, so $bc\cos^2(2t)=98$. With semiperimeter $14$, the inradius is $r=\dfrac{bc\sin4t}{28}$ and $AI=\dfrac{r}{\sin2t}=\dfrac{bc\cos2t}{14}=\dfrac{7}{\cos2t}$. Consequently $\boxed{AI=\dfrac{126}{17}}$.
