Use $A=(0,0)$, $B=(5,0)$, $C=(5,5)$, $D=(0,5)$, $P=(2,1)$, and $T=(x,y)$. Shoelace gives absolute areas $\dfrac{5}{2}|1+y-x|$ and $\dfrac{5}{2}|3-x-y|$. Their sum is at most $10$ exactly when $|4-2x|\le4$ and $|2y-2|\le4$, using $|u|+|v|=\max(|u+v|,|u-v|)$. Within the square this restricts $T$ to $0\le x\le4$, $0\le y\le3$. To enforce simplicity, check the four possible crossings: $AP$ with $CT$, $PC$ with $TA$, $BP$ with $DT$, and $PD$ with $TB$. The segment-crossing test says two segments cross properly exactly when each pair of endpoints has opposite signed cross products relative to the other segment. Applying it partitions the allowed rectangle into four disjoint triangles (apart from boundaries):\[\begin{gathered}
P,(0,0),(0,\dfrac{5}{3});\qquad P,(1,3),(\dfrac{7}{2},3);\\
P,(\dfrac{5}{4},0),(\dfrac{5}{2},0);\qquad P,(4,\dfrac{1}{3}),(4,2).
\end{gathered}\]For direct verification, their bounding lines through $P$ are $y=\dfrac{x}{2}$, $y=\dfrac{5-x}{3}$, $y=\dfrac{4x-5}{3}$, and $y=5-2x$; the accepted sectors are respectively left, above, below, and right of $P$ as shown. Their areas are $\dfrac{5}{3},\dfrac{5}{2},\dfrac{5}{8},\dfrac{5}{3}$, summing to $\dfrac{155}{24}$. Divide by square area $25$ to obtain $\boxed{\dfrac{31}{120}}$.
