The external point $T$ lies beyond $B$. Put $DC=x$, so $BC=x+2$ and $TB=8-x$, with $0<x<8$. The angle-bisector theorem gives $AB:AC=2:x$. By the tangent--chord theorem and sine rule in $TAB,TAC$, $\dfrac{TB}{TC}=\left(\dfrac{AB}{AC}\right)^2$, so $\dfrac{8-x}{10}=\dfrac{4}{x^2}$. Thus $x^3-8x^2+40=(x+2)(x^2-10x+20)=0$. The valid roots are $x=5\pm\sqrt5$. Power of $T$ yields $AT^2=TB\cdot TC=10(8-x)=(10-x)^2$, using $x^2=10x-20$. Therefore $\boxed{AT=5-\sqrt5\text{ or }5+\sqrt5}$. Both are realizable: choose $AB=3$, $AC=\dfrac{3x}{2}$, and $BC=x+2$ for either root; these satisfy strict triangle inequalities and the required tangent ratio.
