During the pivot about the right-angle corner at the circle center, the center does not move. At each of the two arc endpoints, the body pivots through $90^\circ$, so the center traces a quarter-circle of radius one, contributing $\dfrac{\pi}{2}$ twice. While the circular arc rolls on the line, the center stays one unit above it and moves horizontally through the arc length $\dfrac{\pi}{2}$. These phases total a full $360^\circ$ turn. The center's total path length is therefore $\boxed{\dfrac{3\pi}{2}}$. Its net horizontal displacement is instead the quarter-disk perimeter $2+\dfrac{\pi}{2}$, distinguishing displacement from distance traveled.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((-.3,0)--(5,0),gray);draw(arc((1,0),1,90,180),blue);draw((1,1)--(1+pi/2,1),blue);draw(arc((1+pi/2,0),1,0,90),blue);label("stationary start",(0,0),S);label("center path",(1.8,1),N);label("finish",(2+pi/2,0),S);
\end{asy}

Testing each integer by its digit sum gives the qualifying list $100,102,108,110,111,112,114,117,120,126,132,133,135,140,144,150,152,153,156,162,171,180,190,192,195,198,200$. There are $\boxed{27}$. The complete finite test below certifies that none are omitted.\begin{verbatim}
