\end{asy}

The expression equals $1+\cos(x-y)+\cos(y-z)+\cos(z-x)=4\cos\dfrac{x-y}{2}\cos\dfrac{y-z}{2}\cos\dfrac{z-x}{2}$. Order the three angles around the circle. Examining the signs of these half-angle cosines shows the product is positive exactly when one of the three circular gaps exceeds $\pi$, equivalently when the three points lie in an open semicircle. For each chosen point, the probability that both others lie within the following clockwise semicircle is $\dfrac{1}{4}$. These three events are disjoint except for zero-probability boundary cases, since a gap exceeding $\pi$ is unique. Their total probability is $3\cdot\dfrac{1}{4}=\boxed{\dfrac{3}{4}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw(circle((0,0),1));pair[]v={dir(20),dir(70),dir(150)};for(int j=0;j<3;++j){draw((0,0)--v[j],dashed);dot(v[j]);label("$"+string(j+1)+"$",v[j],v[j]);}draw(arc((0,0),1.1,20,200),blue);label("one containing semicircle",(0,-1.4));
\end{asy}

Write $a=BC$, $b=CA$, $c=AB$, $d=a+c$, and $\gamma=\angle C$. First the collinearity and cyclicity imply $b=d(1+2\cos\gamma)$. Here is a coordinate verification: take $C=(0,0)$, $B=(a,0)$, $A=(b\cos\gamma,b\sin\gamma)$, $D=(d,0)$, and $E=(-a\cos\gamma,-a\sin\gamma)$. The circumcircle has equation $X^2+Y^2-aX+\dfrac{a\cos\gamma-b}{\sin\gamma}Y=0$. Its second intersection with $BE$, written $B+t(E-B)$, has $t=\dfrac{a+2a\cos\gamma-b}{2a(1+\cos\gamma)}$. Substituting this into the line equation for $AD$ and using $c^2=a^2+b^2-2ab\cos\gamma$ simplifies to the claimed relation. Put $u=\dfrac{b}{d}$. The triangle inequality gives $0<u<1$, and $2\cos\gamma=u-1$. The cosine rule in $ACD$ gives $AD^2=b^2+d^2-2bd\cos\gamma=d(d+b)$. By the exterior angle-bisector theorem, $K$ lies beyond $A$ and $AK=\dfrac{b\,AD}{d-b}$. The condition $AK=d+b$ therefore implies $(1-u)^2(1+u)=u^2$, or $u^3-2u^2-u+1=0$. This polynomial decreases strictly on $(0,1)$ and has its unique root there at $u=1+2\cos\dfrac{4\pi}{7}$. To verify the trigonometric value, set $z=e^{\dfrac{4\pi i}{7}}$ and divide $1+z+\cdots+z^6=0$ by $z^3$; with $v=z+z^{-1}$ one obtains $v^3+v^2-2v-1=0$, exactly the stated equation after $u=1+v$. Hence $\gamma=\dfrac{4\pi}{7}$. The cosine rule for $ABC$ also yields $\dfrac{a}{c}=1-u=-2\cos\dfrac{4\pi}{7}=2\sin\dfrac{\pi}{14}$. By the sine rule, $\sin\angle A=2\sin\dfrac{\pi}{14}\sin\dfrac{4\pi}{7}=\sin\dfrac{\pi}{7}$. Since $0<\angle A<\pi-\gamma=\dfrac{3\pi}{7}$, the only possible angle is $\boxed{\dfrac{180^\circ}{7}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
real g=4*pi/7,u=1+2*cos(g),a=(1-u)/(2-u),b=u;pair C=(0,0),Bp=(a,0),A=b*dir(g*180/pi),D=(1,0),Ep=-a*dir(g*180/pi),K=A+b/(1-b)*(A-D);real t=(a+2*a*cos(g)-b)/(2*a*(1+cos(g)));pair L=Bp+t*(Ep-Bp);draw(A--Bp--C--cycle);draw(K--D,blue);draw(Ep--L,red);draw(C--K,dashed);pair[]v={A,Bp,C,D,Ep,K,L};string[]names={"A","B","C","D","E","K","L"};for(int j=0;j<v.length;++j)label("$"+names[j]+"$",v[j],dir(90+50*j));
\end{asy}

The cubic has one root $c\in(-1,0)$, one root $b\in(0,1)$, and $a\in(2,3)$ by its signs at $-1,0,1,2,3$. Since $b+c=3-a>0$, we have $b>|c|$. Thus for odd exponent $2019$, $0<b^{2019}+c^{2019}<1$. Let $s_n=a^n+b^n+c^n$. Vieta gives $s_0=3,s_1=3,s_2=9$, and the root equation gives $s_n=3s_{n-1}-s_{n-3}$, so every $s_n$ is an integer. It follows that $\lfloor a^{2019}\rfloor=s_{2019}-1$. The recurrence modulo $17$ gives $s_{2019}\equiv7$, hence the requested remainder is $\boxed6$.\begin{verbatim}
s=[3,3,9]
for n in range(3,2020):
    s.append((3*s[-1]-s[-3])%17)
assert s[2019]==7
\end{verbatim}

Grow polyominoes one square at a time along an exposed edge. Canonicalize every shape by translating each of its eight rotations and reflections to have minimum coordinates zero and choosing the lexicographically least coordinate tuple. This identifies exactly the allowed congruences. Every connected shape has a cell whose deletion leaves it connected (take a leaf of a spanning tree of its adjacency graph), so growth generates all shapes. The exact enumeration below yields $P(1),\ldots,P(6)=1,1,2,5,12,35$, hence $\boxed{P(5)+P(6)=47}$.\begin{verbatim}
def canonical(shape):
    forms=[]
    for swap in [False,True]:
        for sx in [-1,1]:
            for sy in [-1,1]:
                pts=[(sx*(y if swap else x),sy*(x if swap else y))
                     for x,y in shape]
                mx=min(x for x,y in pts);my=min(y for x,y in pts)
                forms.append(tuple(sorted((x-mx,y-my) for x,y in pts)))
    return min(forms)
shapes={((0,0),)}
counts=[1]
for n in range(2,7):
    new=set()
    for shape in shapes:
        cells=set(shape)
        for x,y in shape:
            for dx,dy in [(1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1)]:
                cell=(x+dx,y+dy)
                if cell not in cells:new.add(canonical(cells|{cell}))
    shapes=new
    counts.append(len(shapes))
assert counts==[1,1,2,5,12,35]
\end{verbatim}
\begin{asy}
