For squared contribution at most $20$, each coordinate contributes the generating polynomial $1+2t+2t^4+2t^9+2t^{16}$; each positive square has two signs. Thus the desired counts are the coefficients of $t^{19},t^{20}$ in its tenth power. Truncated coefficient convolution gives $r_{10}(19)=1563840$ and $r_{10}(20)=2050344$, so the sum is $\boxed{3614184}$. The following integer computation includes every coordinate and sign choice.\begin{verbatim}
dp=[1]+[0]*20
for coordinate in range(10):
    dp=[sum(dp[n-k]*(1 if k==0 else 2)
            for k in [0,1,4,9,16] if n>=k)
        for n in range(21)]
assert (dp[19],dp[20])==(1563840,2050344)
assert dp[19]+dp[20]==3614184
\end{verbatim}

Choose $B=(0,0)$, $C=(0,20)$, $A=(-5,0)$, and $D=(-15,20)$. The circle has center $(0,10)$ and radius $10$. Line $AD$ is $2x+y+10=0$, whose distance from the center is $\dfrac{20}{\sqrt5}=4\sqrt5$. Thus its chord length is $2\sqrt{10^2-(4\sqrt5)^2}=\boxed{4\sqrt5}$. Substitution shows the intersection heights are $2,10$, both within side $AD$, so the full chord is the requested segment.
