For a prime $p$, put $r=\lfloor\sqrt p\rfloor$. Its distance to the nearest square is $\min(p-r^2,(r+1)^2-p)$; no integer ties because consecutive squares differ by an odd number. A sieve through $20000$ supplies more than $2021$ primes, with the 2021st equal to $17579$. Exact summation by the following code gives $\boxed{84849}$.\begin{verbatim}
from math import isqrt
limit=20000
prime=[True]*(limit+1)
prime[0]=prime[1]=False
for p in range(2,isqrt(limit)+1):
    if prime[p]:
        for n in range(p*p,limit+1,p):prime[n]=False
ps=[n for n in range(2,limit+1) if prime[n]][:2021]
assert len(ps)==2021 and ps[-1]==17579
answer=sum(min(p-isqrt(p)**2,(isqrt(p)+1)**2-p) for p in ps)
assert answer==84849
\end{verbatim}

