\end{asy}

If each original leg is $x$, the new legs are $x+1,x+2$. Thus $\dfrac{(x+1)(x+2)-x^2}{2}=10$, or $3x+2=20$. Hence $x=6$, and the original area is $\dfrac{6^2}{2}=\boxed{18}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair Bp=(0,0),A=(6,0),C=(0,6),Ap=(7,0),Cp=(0,8);draw(Bp--Ap--Cp--cycle);draw(A--C,blue);label("$B$",Bp,SW);label("$A$",A,S);label("$C$",C,W);label("$A^{\prime}$",Ap,S);label("$C^{\prime}$",Cp,N);
\end{asy}

If prime $p$ divides $n$, then $p-1$ divides $24$, so $p$ is among $2,3,5,7,13$. The factor $p^{e-1}$ in the totient also divides $24$, bounding their respective exponents by $4,2,1,1,1$. Testing these finite exponent choices in $\varphi(n)=\prod_{p^e\parallel n}p^{e-1}(p-1)$ yields exactly $35,39,45,52,56,70,72,78,84,90$. Their sum is $\boxed{621}$. The enumeration below certifies completeness within the proved bounds.\begin{verbatim}
from itertools import product
primes=[2,3,5,7,13]
answers=[]
for exps in product(range(5),range(3),range(2),range(2),range(2)):
    n=phi=1
    for p,e in zip(primes,exps):
        n*=p**e
        if e: phi*=p**(e-1)*(p-1)
    if phi==24: answers.append(n)
assert sorted(answers)==[35,39,45,52,56,70,72,78,84,90]
\end{verbatim}

Set $A=(0,0)$, $B=(1,0)$, $C=(1,1)$, $D=(0,1)$. The condition is $4(x^2+y^2)<(x-1)^2+y^2$, or $(x+\dfrac{1}{3})^2+y^2<\dfrac{4}{9}$. This circle is centered at $(-\dfrac{1}{3},0)$ with radius $\dfrac{2}{3}$. Its upper cap inside the square is a $60^\circ$ sector minus the right triangle with legs $\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{3}$. Thus the area is $\dfrac{1}{2}(\dfrac{2}{3})^2\dfrac{\pi}{3}-\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\dfrac{1}{3}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{3}=\boxed{\dfrac{4\pi-3\sqrt3}{54}}$. Strict boundaries have zero area.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle);draw(arc((-1/3,0),2/3,0,60),blue);draw((-1/3,0)--(0,sqrt(3)/3),dashed);draw((-1/3,0)--(1/3,0));label("$A$",(0,0),S);label("$B$",(1,0),S);label("$C$",(1,1),N);label("$D$",(0,1),N);label("$O$",(-1/3,0),W);
\end{asy}

All $6^3=216$ concatenations are equally likely. Check all seven possible starting positions for the substring LMT in each concatenation; there are $126$ successful strings. A string with several occurrences is counted just once by the membership test in the following exhaustive computation. Hence the probability is $\dfrac{126}{216}=\boxed{\dfrac{7}{12}}$.\begin{verbatim}
from itertools import permutations,product
blocks=[''.join(p) for p in permutations('LMT')]
favorable=sum('LMT' in ''.join(parts)
              for parts in product(blocks,repeat=3))
assert favorable==126
\end{verbatim}

The semiperimeter is $47$, so equal tangent lengths give $BD=47-32=15$ and $DC=47-27=20$. Triangles $ACD$ and $ABD$ share their altitude from $A$ to line $BC$, so their area ratio is $\dfrac{20}{15}$. Computing the same ratio using the common side $AD$ gives $\dfrac{32\sin\angle CAD}{27\sin\angle BAD}=\dfrac{20}{15}$. Thus the requested ratio is $\boxed{\dfrac{9}{8}}$.
