\end{asy}

Center the hexagon at the origin with a vertex on the positive horizontal axis. For a square of fixed orientation, centering it loses no feasibility: for an inner square, average it with its reflection through the origin and use convexity of the hexagon; for an outer square, each projected hexagon interval is symmetric and has fixed width, so centering gives the smallest required side. By rotations and reflections reduce the square orientation to $0\le\theta\le15^\circ$. For an inner square of side $s$, the support in a unit direction making angle $\phi$ with a square axis is $\dfrac{s}{2}(|\cos\phi|+|\sin\phi|)$. The hexagon has support-line distance $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$ in its three normal directions. Among these, the greatest square factor is $\cos(30^\circ+\theta)+\sin(30^\circ+\theta)$, minimized at $\theta=0$. Consequently $s\le\dfrac{2\sqrt3}{1+\sqrt3}=3-\sqrt3$, attained at that orientation, and $A=12-6\sqrt3$. For the outer square the two required widths are $2\cos\theta$ and $2\cos(30^\circ-\theta)$. Their maximum on this interval is $2\cos\theta$, minimized at $15^\circ$. Thus $B=4\cos^2(15^\circ)=2+\sqrt3$. Subtraction gives $\boxed{B-A=7\sqrt3-10}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
path h=dir(0);for(int k=1;k<6;++k)h=h--dir(60*k);draw(h--cycle);real a=(3-sqrt(3))/2,b=cos(15*pi/180);draw((-a,-a)--(a,-a)--(a,a)--(-a,a)--cycle,blue);path q=(-b,-b)--(b,-b)--(b,b)--(-b,b)--cycle;draw(rotate(15)*q,red);label("inner optimum",(0,-1.45),blue);label("outer optimum",(0,1.5),red);
\end{asy}

Put the corner at $(0,0)$ and the central circle center at $(\dfrac{1}{2},\dfrac{1}{2})$. If the corner circle has radius $r$, its center is $(r,r)$. External tangency gives $\sqrt2(\dfrac{1}{2}-r)=\dfrac{1}{2}+r$. Solving and rationalizing yields $\boxed{r=\dfrac{3-2\sqrt2}{2}}$. This radius is positive and below $\dfrac{1}{2}$, so the stated configuration exists.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
real r=(3-2*sqrt(2))/2;draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle);draw(circle((.5,.5),.5));draw(circle((r,r),r),blue);draw((r,r)--(.5,.5),dashed);label("$r$",(r,r),NE);label("$\dfrac{1}{2}$",(.5,.5),E);
\end{asy}

Let the rhombus side be $s$, and choose coordinates $A=(0,0)$, $E=(s,0)$, $C=(s+3,0)$, $B=(s\cos\theta,s\sin\theta)$, and $D=(s+s\cos\theta,s\sin\theta)$. The circle through $A,C$ has equation $x^2+y^2-(s+3)x+vy=0$. Subtracting its equations at $B,D$ gives $2s^2\cos\theta-3s=0$, hence $2s\cos\theta=3$. Then $CD^2=s^2-6s\cos\theta+9=s^2$, so $s=4$. Finally $AD^2=2s^2(1+\cos\theta)=32(1+\dfrac{3}{8})=\boxed{44}$.
\begin{asy}
