\end{asy}

All states reachable from $1,\ldots,2023$ lie in $0,\ldots,2025$: rounding to the nearest square in this range never exceeds $2025$, and digit subtraction decreases a positive state. Consecutive squares have odd difference, so no integer is tied between them. Form the directed graph of the two permitted moves. Breadth-first search from $0$ along reversed edges gives exactly the shortest distance from every state to $0$. This complete finite computation returns $\boxed{232847}$. Digit subtraction alone eventually reaches zero, so all distances exist.\begin{verbatim}
from math import isqrt
from collections import deque
M=2025
reverse=[[] for _ in range(M+1)]
for n in range(1,M+1):
    r=isqrt(n)
    q=r*r if n-r*r<(r+1)**2-n else (r+1)**2
    for v in {q,n-max(map(int,str(n)))}:
        reverse[v].append(n)
distance=[None]*(M+1)
distance[0]=0
queue=deque([0])
while queue:
    v=queue.popleft()
    for w in reverse[v]:
        if distance[w] is None:
            distance[w]=distance[v]+1
            queue.append(w)
assert sum(distance[1:2024])==232847
\end{verbatim}

The polygon area is $5000n\sin\dfrac{2\pi}{n}$, so the deficit is $D_n=10000\pi-5000n\sin\dfrac{2\pi}{n}$. For $0<t<\pi$, the function $\dfrac{\sin t}{t}$ is strictly decreasing: its derivative has numerator $t\cos t-\sin t<0$, since $\sin t-t\cos t$ starts at zero and has derivative $t\sin t>0$. Thus $D_n$ decreases with $n\ge3$. Direct evaluation gives $1.002864<D_{454}<1.002866$ and $0.998461<D_{455}<0.998463$. These bounds can also be certified by the alternating sine series through its seventh-degree term, whose next term at $t<0.014$ changes the deficit by less than $10^{-15}$. Hence the least integer is $\boxed{455}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw(circle((0,0),1));path p=dir(0);for(int j=1;j<12;++j)p=p--dir(30*j);draw(p--cycle,blue);draw((0,0)--dir(0));draw((0,0)--dir(30));label("$100$",(.5,0),S);label("$\dfrac{2\pi}{n}$",(.3,.12),N);label("schematic polygon",(0,-1.4));
\end{asy}

The intercepts are $(\pm1,0)$ and $(0,\pm1)$. Even exponents give symmetry across both axes, and exchanging $x,y$ preserves the equation. In the first quadrant, $y=(1-x^{10})^{\dfrac{1}{10}}$ for $0\le x\le1$. Thus the enclosed area is $\boxed{4\int_0^1(1-x^{10})^{\dfrac{1}{10}}\,dx}$. This curve is a superellipse, lying inside the square $[-1,1]^2$ and touching it at the four intercepts.
\begin{asy}
