usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(24,0)--(24,12)--(0,12)--cycle);draw((2,2)--(22,2)--(22,10)--(2,10)--cycle);label("$24$",(12,0),S);label("$12$",(24,6),E);label("$2$",(1,6));
\end{asy}

By Burnside's lemma, average the number of valid colorings fixed by each of the $24$ cube rotations. The identity fixes every onto coloring, numbering $4^6-4\cdot3^6+6\cdot2^6-4=1560$ by inclusion--exclusion. Each of the three half-turns about opposite face centers has four face orbits (two singletons and two pairs); using all four colors forces one color per orbit, giving $4!=24$ fixed colorings. Each remaining rotation has at most three face orbits: the six quarter-turns have three, the eight vertex-axis third-turns have two, and the six edge-axis half-turns have three. None can use all four colors. The answer is $\dfrac{1560+3\cdot24}{24}=\boxed{68}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),Bp=(2,0),C=(2,2),D=(0,2),v=(.8,.7);filldraw(A--Bp--C--D--cycle,lightblue);filldraw(D--C--C+v--D+v--cycle,lightgray);filldraw(Bp--Bp+v--C+v--C--cycle,lightgreen);draw(A--A+v--Bp+v^^A+v--D+v,dashed);label("six faces, four labeled colors",(1.4,-.4));
\end{asy}

Each unordered pair of circles contributes at most two intersections. There are $\binom52=10$ pairs, so at most $20$ points. This bound is attainable: take five distinct centers very close to one another with equal radii much larger than all center separations, ensuring every pair crosses twice. Perturb the centers slightly to avoid triple intersections, while preserving all pairwise crossings. Thus the maximum is $\boxed{20}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
