\end{asy}

By rotational symmetry fix $A$ anywhere. For fixed $A,B$, let $L=AB$ and let $d$ be the distance from the common center to line $AB$. Player $H$ can choose $C$ on the radius-three circle at the greatest distance $3+d$ from that line, so the largest possible area is $\dfrac{L(3+d)}{2}$. The reverse triangle inequality gives $L\ge2-1=1$, and $d\ge0$, so this is at least $\dfrac{3}{2}$. Player $D$ attains that lower bound by choosing $B$ on the same radial ray as $A$, giving $L=1,d=0$. Hence optimal play yields $\boxed{\dfrac{3}{2}}$.
\begin{asy}
