The orbital radius is $2501$, so its circumference is $5002\pi$ kilometers. Required speed is at least $\dfrac{5002\pi}{3\pi}=\dfrac{5002}{3}$. The smallest integer is $\boxed{1668}$ kilometers per hour.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw(circle((0,0),1));draw(circle((0,0),1.15),blue);draw((0,0)--(1.15,0));label("planet",(0,-.3));label("orbit radius $2501$ km",(.6,.2),N);label("schematic",(0,-1.5));
\end{asy}

Each triangle has area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}$ and inradius $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{6}$, so its panel has area $\dfrac{\pi}{12}$. The unused total area is $576(\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}-\dfrac{\pi}{12})=\boxed{144\sqrt3-48\pi}$ square meters.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),Bp=(1,0),C=(.5,sqrt(3)/2),O=(.5,sqrt(3)/6);draw(A--Bp--C--cycle);draw(circle(O,sqrt(3)/6),blue);label("$1$",(.5,0),S);label("one panel",(.5,-.3));
\end{asy}

A thin annular band has area $2\pi r\,dr$, so multiplying by density proportional to $\dfrac{1}{r}$ makes radial probability proportional to $dr$. Thus each radius is uniform on an interval of length $10000$. Normalize this interval to $[0,1]$; the condition is $|x-y|<\dfrac{2}{5}$. In the unit square, the excluded region is two right triangles with legs $\dfrac{3}{5}$, of total area $\dfrac{9}{25}$. The desired probability is $\boxed{\dfrac{16}{25}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
filldraw((0,0)--(.4,0)--(1,.6)--(1,1)--(.6,1)--(0,.4)--cycle,lightgray);draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle);label("$x$",(1,0),SE);label("$y$",(0,1),NW);label("$|x-y|<\dfrac{2}{5}$",(.5,.5));
\end{asy}

The asteroid distance at time $t$ is $180-20t-5t^2$. Before reaching the ship, it meets the satellite only at the initial-facing point of the orbit, so a positive-time interception has $t=\dfrac{kR}{5}$ for an integer number $k\ge1$ of full revolutions. Put $m=kR$. Then $R=180-4m-\dfrac{m^2}{5}>0$. Since $R,m$ are integers, $5\mid m^2$, hence $m=5j$ for a positive integer $j$. Positivity gives $j\le4$, and $R=180-20j-5j^2$ has values $155,120,75,20$ for $j=1,2,3,4$. The further condition $m=kR$ requires $R\mid5j$; only $R=20$, $j=4$ works. It intercepts at $t=4$ seconds after one revolution. An immediate interception at $R=180$ is larger. Thus the minimum is $\boxed{20}$ kilometers.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw(circle((0,0),1));dot((0,0));dot((1,0));draw((3,0)--(1,0),red,Arrow);label("ship",(0,0),S);label("satellite",(1,0),S);label("asteroid",(3,0),N);label("$R$",(.5,0),N);
\end{asy}

The condition means the product of the six numbers is $6^k$ for an integer $k$. Each number can therefore have no prime factor other than $2,3$, leaving $1,2,3,4,6,8,9,12,16,18,24$. Among the $\binom{11}{6}=462$ six-element subsets, exactly $18$ have equal total exponents of $2$ and $3$, as the complete enumeration below verifies. Each is equally likely, so the answer is $\boxed{\dfrac{1}{18}}$.\begin{verbatim}
from itertools import combinations
from math import prod
allowed=[1,2,3,4,6,8,9,12,16,18,24]
count=0
for ticket in combinations(allowed,6):
    product=prod(ticket)
    while product%6==0:
        product//=6
    count+=(product==1)
assert count==18
\end{verbatim}

The radius at height $0.7$ is $0.6+0.2(0.7)=0.74$ meter by similarity. The collected volume is $\dfrac{\pi(0.7)}{3}(0.6^2+0.6\cdot0.74+0.74^2)=\dfrac{23653\pi}{75000}$ cubic meters, or $\dfrac{23653\pi}{75}$ liters. At ten liters per minute the filling time is $\dfrac{23653\pi}{750}$ minutes. Three cups per minute have been sold, so the integer count is $\left\lfloor\dfrac{23653\pi}{250}\right\rfloor=\boxed{297}$, since the expression is approximately $297.23$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((-.6,0)--(.6,0)--(.8,1)--(-.8,1)--cycle);draw((-.74,.7)--(.74,.7),blue);draw((0,0)--(0,1),dashed);label("$0.7$",(.76,.35),E);label("$1$",(-.85,.5),W);label("$0.6$",(.3,0),S);label("$0.74$",(.37,.7),N);label("axial cross-section",(0,-.3));
\end{asy}

For radius $r$, the two route lengths are $\dfrac{\pi r}{2}$ and $2r$. Hence $\dfrac{(4-\pi)r}{2}=\dfrac{4-\pi}{4\pi}$, and since $4-\pi>0$, cancellation gives $\boxed{r=\dfrac{1}{2\pi}}$.
