\end{asy}

For adjacent sides $b,c$, opposite side $a$, and internal angle bisector length $\ell$, the angle-bisector theorem together with Stewart's theorem gives $\ell^2=bc\left(1-\dfrac{a^2}{(b+c)^2}\right)$. Substituting gives $64=162\left(1-\dfrac{a^2}{729}\right)$, hence $a^2=441$. Therefore $\boxed{BC=21}$. This also satisfies the source condition $BC>AC$.
\begin{asy}
