\end{asy}

The semiperimeter is $s=105$. Brahmagupta's formula, valid for a cyclic quadrilateral, gives $K=\sqrt{60\cdot63\cdot45\cdot42}=1890\sqrt2$. For any tangential quadrilateral, decomposing into four triangles from the incenter gives $K=rs$. Hence $r=\dfrac{1890\sqrt2}{105}=\boxed{18\sqrt2}$, and $K=\boxed{1890\sqrt2}$. The square-root-of-product formula also uses cyclicity: Pitot's relation makes $s-a=c$, $s-b=d$, and so on. Tangency alone does not imply cyclicity; for example a rhombus with side $1$ and angle $60^\circ$ is tangential but has area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$ rather than $1$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),Bp=(1,0),C=(1.5,sqrt(3)/2),D=(.5,sqrt(3)/2),O=(.75,sqrt(3)/4);draw(A--Bp--C--D--cycle);draw(circle(O,sqrt(3)/4));label("unit-side rhombus counterexample",(.75,-.3));
\end{asy}

\begin{asy}
