For $n=10a+b$, its digit sum is $a+b$, where $1\le a\le9$ and $0\le b\le9$. Thus testing these $90$ possibilities is exhaustive. The exact loop below gives $\boxed{23}$.
\begin{verbatim}
values=[10*a+b for a in range(1,10) for b in range(10)
        if (10*a+b)%(a+b)==0]
assert len(values)==23
\end{verbatim}

A new plane meets the previous $k-1$ planes in at most $k-1$ lines. These divide its circular cross-section into at most $1+(k-1)+\binom{k-1}{2}$ regions; each adds at most one solid piece. Starting with one piece, six cuts give $1+\sum_{k=1}^6[1+(k-1)+\binom{k-1}{2}]=1+6+15+20=\boxed{42}$. General-position planes chosen so all required intersections occur inside the sphere attain the bound.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
