By symmetry take the unit square and $X,Y$ on its bottom side. A third point on the bottom side gives a degenerate triangle; a point on either adjacent side cannot enclose the center except in boundary cases of probability zero. Thus $Z$ must be on the opposite side, which has probability $\dfrac{1}{4}$. Write $Z=(z,1)$. At height $\dfrac{1}{2}$, the triangle spans between horizontal coordinates $\dfrac{x+z}{2}$ and $\dfrac{y+z}{2}$. It contains the center exactly when $1-z$ is between the independent uniform $x,y$. Conditional probability is $2(1-z)z$. Integrating over $0\le z\le1$ gives $\dfrac{1}{3}$, hence total probability $\dfrac{1}{12}$. Thus $a+b=\boxed{13}$.
