The radius-$10$ and radius-$100$ disks have centers $110$ apart; their two farthest points are $220$ apart. Any containing disk therefore has radius at least $110$. Put their centers at $(110,0)$ and $(0,0)$. The radius-$110$ disk centered at $(10,0)$ contains both. If the small-circle center is $(x,y)$, tangency gives $x^2+y^2=101^2$ and $(x-110)^2+y^2=11^2$, hence $x=\dfrac{1109}{11}$. Its squared distance from $(10,0)$ is $101^2-20x+100=\dfrac{91131}{11}<109^2$. Thus its radius-$1$ disk is also contained. The lower bound is attained, so the answer is $\boxed{110}$.
