Five are possible: two equilateral triangles sharing a side form a rhombus with four unit sides and one unit diagonal. Six are impossible. If all six pairwise distances of four points were equal, three points would form an equilateral triangle. A fourth point at the same distance from the first two must be the third point or its reflection across their line. The reflected point is at $\sqrt3$ times that distance from the third, not the same distance. Hence the maximum is $\boxed5$.
