At least two rectangle vertices lie on the same triangle side. They must be adjacent vertices: if opposite vertices lay on that side, the other two would lie on opposite sides of its line, contradicting containment. Use this side as a horizontal base. If rectangle height is $h$, similar triangles give its width $1-\dfrac{2h}{\sqrt3}$, where $0<h<\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$. Its perimeter is $2+\left(2-\dfrac{4}{\sqrt3}\right)h$, a decreasing function of $h$. It approaches $\sqrt3$ as $h$ approaches the triangle altitude, and is always larger for nondegenerate rectangles. Therefore the sharp universal lower bound is $\boxed{P=\sqrt3}$, though no nondegenerate rectangle attains it.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
