draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(.5,sqrt(3)/2)--cycle);real h=.4,x=h/sqrt(3);draw((x,0)--(1-x,0)--(1-x,h)--(x,h)--cycle,blue);label("$h$",(x,h/2),W);label("$1$",(.5,0),S);
\end{asy}

The triangle altitude is $H=\dfrac{s\sqrt3}{2}$, and the inradius is $\dfrac{H}{3}$. Each cut lies at height $\dfrac{2H}{3}$ above its parallel side, leaving a corner triangle of altitude $\dfrac{H}{3}$ and side $\dfrac{s}{3}$. Subtracting the three corner areas from the original gives central area $\dfrac{\sqrt3s^2}{4}-3\dfrac{\sqrt3s^2}{36}=\dfrac{\sqrt3s^2}{6}$. Equating this to $\dfrac{9\sqrt3}{2}$ gives $s^2=27$, hence $\boxed{s=3\sqrt3}$.
