Take $O=(0,0)$, $A=(10,0)$, and choose the orientation with $B=(-\dfrac{14}{5},\dfrac{48}{5})$; these coordinates follow from $OB=10$ and $AB=16$. Since $PO=PA$, every possible $P$ lies on $x=5$. The distance from $B$ to this line is $5+\dfrac{14}{5}=\dfrac{39}{5}$, so $PB$ is at least this value. The perpendicular foot $P=(5,\dfrac{48}{5})$ is attainable: the line $P\cdot C=50$ intersects the radius-$10$ circle at $A$ and a distinct second point $C$, since $|P|>5$. The two intersections are not antipodal because the line does not pass through $O$. Thus $AOC$ is nondegenerate and the minimum is $\boxed{\dfrac{39}{5}}$.
