The triangle has diameter 1, so it cannot meet two opposite square sides, whose separation is 20. Thus it can meet at most two sides of the square, necessarily adjacent. Each side line intersects a noncoincident triangle boundary at most twice, so the finite maximum is at most 4. To attain 4, use the square's first-quadrant corner. Put two triangle vertices at $(-\varepsilon,\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}-\varepsilon)$ and $(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}-\varepsilon,-\varepsilon)$, for a small positive $\varepsilon $, and place the third vertex on the northeast side of this unit segment. The triangle cuts both positive coordinate axes twice and remains far from the other square sides. Hence the maximum is $\boxed4 $.
