size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(8,0),C=(4,4sqrt(3)),P=(3,0);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(C--P,blue);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$P$",P,S);
\end{asy}

The endpoint polar radii are 10 and $ r=3\sqrt2 $, with angular separation $\dfrac{\pi}{4}$. If a route uses circular roads and the smallest radius of any used circle is the integer $ k $, its total angular travel costs at least $ k\cdot\dfrac{\pi}{4}$, and radial travel costs at least $|10-k|+|r-k|$. This bound is attained by travelling radially to radius $ k $, turning through $\dfrac{\pi}{4}$ there, then travelling radially to the destination. A route through the origin is the case $ k=0 $. For integers $ k\le r $, the expression $ 10+r-2k+\dfrac{\pi k}{4}$ decreases with $ k $; for $ r\le k\le10 $, it increases with $ k $; beyond 10 it also increases. Only $ k=4,5 $ need comparison. Their lengths are $ 2+3\sqrt2+\pi $ and $ 10-3\sqrt2+\dfrac{5\pi}{4}$; the first is smaller because $ 8-6\sqrt2+\dfrac{\pi}{4}>0 $. Therefore the minimum is $\boxed{2+3\sqrt2+\pi}$.
