size(7cm);
for(int j=0;j<24;++j)draw((0,0)--10.5dir(15j),lightgray);for(int k=1;k<=10;++k)draw(circle((0,0),k),lightgray);pair A=(10,0),B=(3,3),P=(4,0),Q=4dir(45);draw(A--P,blue+linewidth(1.5));draw(arc((0,0),4,0,45),blue+linewidth(1.5));draw(Q--B,blue+linewidth(1.5));label("$(10,0)$",A,E);label("$(3,3)$",B,NE);label("radius $4$",Q,NW);
\end{asy}

There are exactly $ 9\cdot10^3 $ digit assignments. Testing divisibility on their explicit place-value expression gives the unique assignment $(A,B,C,D)=(4,9,5,2)$:
\begin{verbatim}
sol=[]
for a in range(1,10):
 for b in range(10):
  for c in range(10):
   for d in range(10):
    n=int(f'{a}58{b}3602{c}91{d}')
    if n%10101==0:sol.append((a,b,c,d))
assert sol==[(4,9,5,2)]
\end{verbatim}
This enumeration covers every permissible leading and interior digit and checks the exact remainder. The answer is $\boxed{4952}$.

A line meets a circle in at most two points, so the three sides give at most 6. An equilateral triangle whose incircle radius is 1 and circumradius is 2 is crossed twice on each side by its concentric circle of radius $\dfrac{3}{2}$. Thus six intersections are attainable, and the maximum is $\boxed6 $.
