For disjoint equal unit circles, centre distance is the gap plus 2, so the centres form a $ 3 $-$ 4 $-$ 5 $ right triangle. Any enclosing circle of radius $ R $ must have its centre within $ R-1 $ of all three centres. The smallest circle enclosing the centres has radius $\dfrac{5}{2}$: the hypotenuse endpoints force at least that radius, and their midpoint covers the right triangle. Adding 1 gives $\boxed{R=\dfrac{7}{2}}$.
