pair A=(0,0),B=(3,0),C=(0,4),O=(B+C)/2;draw(circle(A,1));draw(circle(B,1));draw(circle(C,1));draw(circle(O,3.5),blue);draw(A--B--C--cycle,dashed);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NW);label("$O$",O,E);
\end{asy}

Because both feet lie on the sides, the triangle is acute and $ F $ lies between each vertex and its altitude foot. Set $ D=(0,0),C=(3,0),F=(0,h)$ with $ h>0 $. Then $ B=(0,9h)$ and $ E=F+2(F-C)=(-6,3h)$. Perpendicularity $ BE\perp CE $ gives $(B-E)\cdot(C-E)=(6,6h)\cdot(9,-3h)=54-18h^2=0 $. Thus $ h=\sqrt3 $, and $[CDF]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot3h=\boxed{\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}}$. The configuration is attained with $ A=(-9,0)$, so all side-foot conditions hold.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(-9,0),C=(3,0),D=(0,0),B=(0,9sqrt(3)),F=(0,sqrt(3)),PtE=(-6,3sqrt(3));draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(B--D^^C--PtE,dashed);filldraw(C--D--F--cycle,lightgray);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$E$",PtE,W);label("$F$",F,E);
\end{asy}

First work above $ AB $. A point $ I $ is an incenter of some triangle $ ABX $ exactly when $\angle AIB>90^\circ $: the converse constructs $ X $ by doubling the angles of $ AI $ and $ BI $ with the base, and their sum is then less than $ 180^\circ $. Thus the first image is the open upper semicircle with diameter $ AB $. If $ Y $ lies there, $\angle AYB>90^\circ $, so its incenter $ J $ satisfies $\angle AJB=90^\circ+\dfrac{1}{2}\angle AYB>135^\circ $. The same doubled-angle construction proves every such $ J $ occurs. With $ A=(0,0),B=(1,0)$, this upper locus is the circular segment above $ AB $ from the circle centered at $(\dfrac{1}{2},-\dfrac{1}{2})$ with radius $\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}$. Its central angle is $ 90^\circ $, and its area is $\dfrac{\pi}{8}-\dfrac{1}{4}$. Reflecting below $ AB $ supplies a disjoint equal region. Therefore the total area is $\boxed{\dfrac{\pi-2}{4}}$; excluded boundaries have area zero.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(1,0);path upper=arc((.5,-.5),1/sqrt(2),45,135)--cycle;path lower=arc((.5,.5),1/sqrt(2),225,315)--cycle;filldraw(upper,lightblue);filldraw(lower,lightblue);draw(A--B);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("upper image",(0.5,0.3),N);label("lower image",(0.5,-0.3),S);
\end{asy}

This is a finite reachability game. For a state $ s $ and budget $ k $, let $ W(s,k)$ mean Zandar can guarantee reaching sum 2 within $ k $ moves. A state of sum 2 is winning; otherwise $ W(s,0)$ is false. For each of Leptina's four swaps, Zandar must have at least one legal response leading to a winning state with budget $ k-1 $. Responses subtracting zero are included as legal moves. Responses dropping below 2 cannot later succeed because sums never increase. The exact minimax recurrence below gives false at budget 5 and true at budget 6, certifying both a lower bound against every strategy and a successful strategy.
\begin{verbatim}
from functools import lru_cache
@lru_cache(None)
def win(s,k):
 if sum(s)==2:return True
 if k==0:return False
 for i in range(4):
  t=list(s);j=(i+1)%4;t[i],t[j]=t[j],t[i];ok=False
  for u in range(4):
   v=(u+1)%4;a,b=t[u],t[v]
   if a<b:u,v=v,u;a,b=b,a
   r=t.copy();r[u]=a-b
   if sum(r)>=2 and win(tuple(r),k-1):ok=True;break
  if not ok:return False
 return True
assert not win((1,2,3,4),5)
assert win((1,2,3,4),6)
\end{verbatim}
Thus the answer is $\boxed6 $.

The Pythagorean theorem gives $(2x+9)^2-(2x+1)^2=144 $. Factoring yields $ 8(4x+10)=144 $, so $ x=\boxed2 $. The resulting sides 5, 12, 13 are positive and form a right triangle.
