size(7cm);
draw(ellipse((0,0),1,0.3));draw(ellipse((0,2),1,0.3));draw((-1,0)--(-1,2)^^(1,0)--(1,2));label("height $1$",(1,1),E);label("circumference $1$",(0,2.4),N);
\end{asy}

Dough amount is proportional to area. The available area is $ 8\pi6^2+12\pi4^2=480\pi $, while each new pizza requires $ 25\pi $. Thus the count is $\lfloor\dfrac{480}{25}\rfloor=\boxed{19}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw(circle((0,0),6));draw(circle((14,0),4));draw(circle((27,0),5));label("diameter $12$",(0,0));label("diameter $8$",(14,0));label("diameter $10$",(27,0));
\end{asy}

Set $ A=(0,0,0),B=(1,1,1)$. A point on a face not incident to $ A $ has one coordinate 1, so Euclidean distance at least 1, with equality only at a neighbouring vertex. Thus every permissible $ X $ lies on a face incident to $ A $. By symmetry write $ X=(x,y,0)$ with $ x,y\ge0 $ and $ x^2+y^2=1 $; the straight face segment from $ A $ gives this exact condition. Unfolding the bottom face with either adjacent side gives valid paths from $ B $ of lengths $\sqrt{(2-x)^2+(1-y)^2}$ and $\sqrt{(1-x)^2+(2-y)^2}$. Assume $ x\ge y $; the first squared length is $ 6-4x-2y $. Since $(2x+y)^2=4-3y^2+4xy\ge4 $, we have $ 2x+y\ge2 $, so that path has length at most $\sqrt2 $. The straight unfolded segments cross their shared edges within the unit edge because their horizontal or vertical coordinates interpolate between 0 and 1. Equality is attained at $ X=(1,0,0)$: it and $ B $ lie on a common face at Euclidean distance $\sqrt2 $, which no surface path can improve. Hence the maximum is $\boxed{\sqrt2}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,1)--(0,1)--cycle);draw((1,0)--(1,1),dashed);pair A=(0,0),X=(0.8,0.6),B=(2,1);draw(A--X,blue);draw(X--B,red);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$X$",X,S);label("unfolded $B$",B,NE);label("bottom face",(0.5,1),N);label("side face",(1.5,1),N);
\end{asy}

Doubling side lengths multiplies area by 4, so the original area is 150. Its perimeter is therefore 75. Doubling lengths makes the new perimeter $\boxed{150}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(3,0),C=(0.8,2);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw((5,0)--(11,0)--(6.6,4)--cycle);label("original",(1.5,-0.4));label("scale factor $2$",(8,-0.4));
\end{asy}

In a regular pentagon, $\angle CDE=108^\circ $. Since $\angle CDF=60^\circ $, $\angle FDE=48^\circ $. Also $ DE=DC=DF $, so isosceles triangle $ DEF $ has base angle $\angle DEF=66^\circ $. The diagonal $ EB $ makes $\angle BED=72^\circ $; for example, the intercepted arc $ BD $ through $ C $ is $ 144^\circ $, giving half that inscribed angle. Therefore $\angle BEF=72^\circ-66^\circ=\boxed{6^\circ}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair C=(-0.5,0),D=(0.5,0),PtE=D+dir(72),A=PtE+dir(144),B=A+dir(216),F=(0,sqrt(3)/2);draw(A--B--C--D--PtE--cycle);draw(C--F--D^^PtE--B^^PtE--F,blue);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,SW);label("$D$",D,SE);label("$E$",PtE,E);label("$F$",F,S);
\end{asy}

The minimum is $\boxed{12}$, meaning the largest union has size 18. One construction uses $\{1,7,11,13,16,17,19,23,25,27,29\}$, whose members are pairwise coprime, and $\{2,8,10,14,18,22,26\}$, whose pairwise gcds are 2. To certify the upper bound, fix the common gcd $ d $. All selected numbers are multiples of $ d $, and allowed pairs are exactly those with gcd $ d $. Every valid subset is contained in a maximal clique of this graph. The following exhaustive maximal-clique enumeration for every $ 1\le d\le30 $, followed by every pair of resulting subsets, establishes that no union exceeds 18.
\begin{verbatim}
import math
families=set()
for d in range(1,31):
 V=set(range(d,31,d))
 N={x:{y for y in V if x!=y and math.gcd(x,y)==d} for x in V}
 def visit(R,P,X):
  if not P and not X:
   families.add(sum(1<<(x-1) for x in R));return
  u=max(P|X,key=lambda v:len(P&N[v]))
  for v in list(P-N[u]):
   visit(R|{v},P&N[v],X&N[v]);P.remove(v);X.add(v)
 visit(set(),V,set())
assert max((a|b).bit_count() for a in families for b in families)==18
\end{verbatim}
Singleton selections are also included and cannot improve the result.

All side-altitude products equal twice the area, so the side ratio is $\dfrac{1}{5}:\dfrac{1}{6}:\dfrac{1}{7}=42:35:30 $. These integers have gcd 1, so any integer triple in this ratio must be a positive integer multiple of $(42,35,30)$: a rational scale whose products are all integral has denominator dividing their gcd. The triple satisfies the triangle inequality, so the minimum perimeter is $ 42+35+30=\boxed{107}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(42,0);real x=(30^2+42^2-35^2)/(2*42);pair C=(x,sqrt(30^2-x*x));draw(A--B--C--cycle);label("$42$",(A+B)/2,S);label("$30$",(A+C)/2,W);label("$35$",(B+C)/2,E);
\end{asy}

The annular area is $\pi(6^2-5^2)=11\pi $, compared with total area $ 36\pi $. The probability is $\boxed{\dfrac{11}{36}}$.
