The diagonals' division ratio equals the ratio of parallel bases, so the other base is either $\dfrac{L}{2}$ or $ 2L $. With legs 1, the first case has height $\sqrt{1-(\dfrac{L}{4})^2}$ and exists exactly for $ 0<L<4 $. The second has height $\sqrt{1-(\dfrac{L}{2})^2}$ and exists exactly for $ 0<L<2 $. Each determines one congruence class. Thus exactly one exists for $ 2\le L<4 $, whose least value is $\boxed2 $.
\begin{asy}
