The exponent interval for $ n $ digits has length $\log_2(10)$, strictly between 3 and 4. Thus each $ f(n)$ is 3 or 4, including $ f(1)=4 $. The total number of powers below $ 10^{2013}$ is $ T=\lfloor2013\log_2(10)\rfloor+1=6688 $. If $ a $ intervals contain 3 powers, then $ 3a+4(2013-a)=6688 $, giving $\boxed{a=1364}$. The integer count can also be verified without logarithmic rounding:
\begin{verbatim}
x=1;T=0;bound=10**2013
while x<bound:
 T+=1;x*=2
assert T==6688
\end{verbatim}

No. Take both triangles with base $ A=(0,0),B=(2,0)$, and third vertices $ C=(0,1)$ and $ D=(1,1)$. Both have base two and altitude one, hence area one. But triangle $ ABC $ has a side of length one, while triangle $ ABD $ has side lengths $ 2,\sqrt2,\sqrt2 $. Thus they are not congruent. 
