Different multisets can yield the same value, so count totals rather than selections. Let $ S_0=\{0\}$ and $ S_{k+1}=\{s+c:s\in S_k,\ c\in D\}$ for the denomination set $ D $. Induction shows $ S_k $ is exactly the set of totals from $ k $ coins. The following exact computation gives $\boxed{419}$:
\begin{verbatim}
D=[1,2,5,10,20,50,100,200]
S={0}
for k in range(5):S={s+c for s in S for c in D}
assert len(S)==419
\end{verbatim}

The place-value equation is $(101A+10B)B=1100C+11D $. Every possibility is covered by the finite digit test:
\begin{verbatim}
sol=[(a,b,c,d) for a in range(1,10) for b in range(1,10)
     for c in range(1,10) for d in range(10)
     if len({a,b,c,d})==4 and (101*a+10*b)*b==1100*c+11*d]
assert sol==[(7,3,2,1)]
\end{verbatim}
Indeed $ 737\cdot3=2211 $. Hence the sole digit sum, and requested total, is $\boxed{13}$.

Take $ A $ as vector origin, $ B=u,D=v $, so $ C=u+v $, $ E=\dfrac{2}{3}u $, $ F=\dfrac{2}{3}v $. A point on $ BF $ is $(1-t)u+\dfrac{2t}{3}v $ and one on $ DE $ is $\dfrac{2s}{3}u+(1-s)v $. Equality gives $ 1-t=\dfrac{2s}{3}$ and $\dfrac{2t}{3}=1-s $, whose solution is $ t=s=\dfrac{3}{5}$. Thus $ K=\dfrac{2}{5}(u+v)=\dfrac{2}{5}C $. Consequently $ CK=\dfrac{3}{5}AC=\boxed6 $.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0),D=(2,4),C=B+D,Ep=2B/3,F=2D/3,K=2C/5;draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(B--F^^D--Ep,blue);draw(A--C,dashed);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$E$",Ep,S);label("$F$",F,W);dot("$K$",K,N);
\end{asy}

The angle sum gives $ 30\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}=180(n-2)$, hence $(n-3)(n-8)=0 $. A convex polygon has all interior angles below 180 degrees, so the possibility $ n=8 $ is excluded. A $ 30 $-$ 60 $-$ 90 $ triangle exists, proving $\boxed{n=3}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(sqrt(3),0),C=(0,1);draw(A--B--C--cycle);label("$90^\circ$",A+(.22,.15));label("$30^\circ$",B+(-.35,.1));label("$60^\circ$",C+(.15,-.25));label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);
\end{asy}

Parallel sides make triangles $ ABC,ADE $ similar, with ratio $\dfrac{BC}{DE}=\dfrac{1}{3}$. Thus $ AD=3AB=12 $, and $ BD=AD-AB=\boxed8 $.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,12),D=(0,0),Ep=(9,0),B=A+(D-A)/3,C=A+(Ep-A)/3;draw(A--D--Ep--cycle);draw(B--C,blue);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,SW);label("$E$",Ep,SE);label("$3$",(B+C)/2,N);label("$9$",(D+Ep)/2,S);label("$4$",(A+B)/2,NW);
\end{asy}

After scaling and reflecting, place $ A=(0,0)$, $ B=(1,0)$, $ C=(\dfrac{1}{2},v)$, $ D=(-\dfrac{1}{2},v)$ with $ v>0 $; the horizontal coordinate of $ C $ follows from $ AC=BC $. Write $ P=(t,0)$ with $ t>1 $ and $ K=v^2+\dfrac{1}{4}$. The first circumcircle is $ x^2+y^2-\dfrac{K}{v} y=0 $. Substituting $ P+s(D-P)$ gives the two intersections $ s=1 $ and $ s=\dfrac{t^2}{(t+\dfrac{1}{2})^2+v^2}$. Thus
\[Q=\left(\dfrac{t(\dfrac{t}{2}+K)}{(t+\dfrac{1}{2})^2+v^2},\dfrac{t^2v}{(t+\dfrac{1}{2})^2+v^2}\right).\]
Consequently line $ AQ $ meets $ y=v $, the line $ CD $, at $ X=(\dfrac{1}{2}+\dfrac{K}{t},v)$. The circle through $ A,P,Q $ has equation $ x^2+y^2-tx+\dfrac{t}{2v}y=0 $, as direct substitution verifies. Put $ R=P+w(C-P)$. Substitution and removal of the root $ w=0 $ yield
\[w=\dfrac{t(t-1)}{(t-\dfrac{1}{2})^2+v^2}.\]
Both second intersections lie on the stated segments because their parameters are strictly between zero and one. The line through $ B $ and $ R $ meets $ y=v $ at horizontal coordinate
\[1+\dfrac{R_x-1}{w}=1+\dfrac{t-1}{w}+\dfrac{1}{2}-t=\dfrac{1}{2}+\dfrac{K}{t}.\]
It therefore passes through the same point $ X $, proving concurrency.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
real v=1,t=2,K=v*v+.25,den=(t+.5)^2+v*v,s=t*t/den,w=t*(t-1)/((t-.5)^2+v*v);pair A=(0,0),B=(1,0),C=(.5,v),D=(-.5,v),P=(t,0),Q=P+s*(D-P),R=P+w*(C-P),X=(.5+K/t,v);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(P--D^^P--C,dashed);draw(A--X^^B--X^^D--X,blue);draw(circle((0,K/(2v)),K/(2v)),gray);pair O=(t/2,-t/(4v));draw(circle(O,abs(O)),gray);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$P$",P,E);label("$Q$",Q,W);label("$R$",R,SE);label("$X$",X,NE);
\end{asy}

For each permitted $ x $, the left side is a positive integer $ N $. It is a square exactly when $\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor^2=N $, testable by integer square root. Exhausting all 21 cases yields:
\begin{verbatim}
from math import isqrt
solutions=[]
for x in range(21):
    N=1+2**x+2**(2*x+1)
    q=isqrt(N)
    if q*q==N:solutions.extend([(x,-q),(x,q)])
assert solutions==[(0,-2),(0,2),(4,-23),(4,23)]
\end{verbatim}
Thus $\boxed{(x,y)=(0,\pm2),(4,\pm23)}$. Substitution verifies all four pairs.

Put $ A=(0,0),B=(w,0),C=(w,h),D=(0,h)$, so $ E=(w,\dfrac{h}{2})$ and $ F=(0,f)$ with $ 0\le f\le h $. Equality of squared distances gives $(\dfrac{h}{2})^2=(f-\dfrac{h}{2})^2 $, hence $ f=0 $ or $ h $. For a nondegenerate triangle choose $ F=D $. Its base $ AD=h $ and perpendicular height $ w $ give area $\dfrac{wh}{2}$, so the ratio is $\boxed{\dfrac{1}{2}}$. The diameter merely says $ w^2+h^2=100 $ and does not affect the ratio.
