The centre is outside the triangle exactly when the three points lie in some open semicircle, apart from zero-probability boundary cases. Such a semicircle has a unique first selected point in clockwise order. For each of three choices of that first point, the other two must fall in the following half-circle, with probability $\dfrac{1}{4}$. These three events are disjoint except on null boundaries. Thus the desired probability is $ 1-3\cdot\dfrac{1}{4}=\boxed{\dfrac{1}{4}}$. 
