A distribution is a partition of ten into at most six positive parts. Conjugating its Ferrers diagram identifies these with partitions whose parts are at most six. Let $ p_j(n)$ count partitions of $ n $ using parts at most $ j $. Separating those with a part $ j $ gives $ p_j(n)=p_{j-1}(n)+p_j(n-j)$, with $ p_j(0)=1 $ and negative arguments zero. This yields $\boxed{35}$:
\begin{verbatim}
p=[1]+[0]*10
for j in range(1,7):
    for n in range(j,11):p[n]+=p[n-j]
assert p[10]==35
\end{verbatim}

A partition disregards the order of its positive summands. Starting with the empty partition of zero, successively allow each possible part. The update $ p(n)\gets p(n)+p(n-j)$ adds exactly partitions containing a part $ j $ after removing one such part. Ascending $ n $ permits repeated parts. The exact recurrence gives $\boxed{56,77,101,135,176}$:
\begin{verbatim}
p=[1]+[0]*15
for j in range(1,16):
    for n in range(j,16):p[n]+=p[n-j]
assert p[11:16]==[56,77,101,135,176]
\end{verbatim}

Assume $ a\le b $ to avoid swapping legs. Test every $ 1\le a\le b<c<100 $ against the Pythagorean equation. This checks all allowed triangles and finds
