For circumradius $ R $, diagonal lengths are $ 2R\sin(\dfrac{k\pi}{12})$ for $ k=2,3,4,5,6 $. The smallest is $ R $ and the largest $ 2R $; every other diagonal length lies strictly between them. A ratio of 2 can therefore occur only between the smallest and largest, forcing $ R=1 $. The twelve central triangles each have area $\dfrac{1}{2}R^2\sin30^\circ=\dfrac{1}{4}$, so the area is $\boxed3 $. 
