Set $ P=(0,0),Q=(w,0),R=(w,h),S=(0,h)$, with $ w<h $. The bisector is $ y=x $, so $ U=(w,w)$ and $ T=(\dfrac{3w}{4},\dfrac{3w}{4})$. Triangle $ PTS $ has base $ PS=h $ and perpendicular height $\dfrac{3w}{4}$, giving area $\dfrac{3wh}{8}$. Dividing by $ wh $ yields $\boxed{\dfrac{3}{8}}$. For consistency, the diagonal equation also forces $ h=3w $, which is allowed.
