assert sum(f(n) in (19,28) for n in range(1,2013))==101
\end{verbatim}
The answer is $\boxed{101}$.

Choose $ A=(0,0),B=(14,0)$ and the upper external tangent. The circle intersections satisfy $ x^2+y^2=169 $ and $(x-14)^2+y^2=225 $, yielding $(5,\pm12)$; the nearer one is $ P=(5,12)$. A common upward unit normal $ n=(n_x,n_y)$ at the two contacts satisfies $ n\cdot(B-A)=13-15=-2 $, so $ n=(-\dfrac{1}{7},\dfrac{4\sqrt3}{7})$. Hence $ C=13n $ and $ D=B+15n $, making $ M=\dfrac{C+D}{2}=(5,8\sqrt3)$. Therefore $\boxed{PM=8\sqrt3-12}$. The lower tangent gives the reflected configuration and the same length.
