\end{asy}

Let the greatest prime factor be $ p $. For two-digit numbers the digit sum is at most 18, so $ p\in\{2,3,5,7,11,13,17\}$. The following complete finite check identifies all eligible values:
\begin{verbatim}
from math import isqrt
def prime(p):return p>=2 and all(p%d for d in range(2,isqrt(p)+1))
good=[n for n in range(2,100) if sum(map(int,str(n)))==
      max(p for p in range(2,n+1) if n%p==0 and prime(p))]
assert good==[2,3,5,7,12,50,70]
\end{verbatim}
The greatest is $\boxed{70}$.

Every move preserves checkerboard colour, so at most half the 320 squares, or 160, are reachable. Conversely the same-colour squares are connected by diagonal unit steps: from any square move diagonally toward the bottom edge, then move along that edge in two-column zigzags through the adjacent row until reaching the starting corner. The bottom-edge column has the correct parity; every such zigzag stays on the board. Hence all same-colour squares are reachable, giving $\boxed{160}$. 
