for(int x=0;x<20;++x)for(int y=0;y<16;++y)filldraw((x,y)--(x+1,y)--(x+1,y+1)--(x,y+1)--cycle,(x+y)%2==0?lightgray:white,gray);dot("start",(.5,.5),SW);draw((.5,.5)--(1.5,1.5)--(2.5,.5)--(3.5,1.5)--(4.5,.5),blue);label("20 columns",(10,-1));label("16 rows",(21,8));
\end{asy}

A complete factor-count search increases candidates until five qualify. Trial division records each prime only once regardless of exponent, so the test is exact:
\begin{verbatim}
def omega(n):
    k=0;p=2
    while p*p<=n:
        if n%p==0:
            k+=1
            while n%p==0:n//=p
        p+=1
    return k+(n>1)
good=[n for n in range(1,4291) if omega(n)>=5]
assert good==[2310,2730,3570,3990,4290]
\end{verbatim}
Thus the answer is $\boxed{4290}$.

There are at most $\binom63=20 $ triples. Use the five vertices of a regular pentagon and its centre. Every triple of pentagon vertices is isosceles: its three cyclic gap sizes are either 1, 1, 3 or 1, 2, 2, giving two equal chords. Every triangle containing the centre has two equal radii. No such triple is collinear because the pentagon has no opposite pair of vertices. Thus all 20 triples work, attaining $\boxed{20}$. 
