Bases above 217 give the single digit 217, which fails. For $ 15\le b\le217 $, the representation has at most two digits; it can be 10 or 11 only, giving $ b=217 $ or 216. Check the remaining bases by repeated division:
\begin{verbatim}
def digits(n,b):
    out=[]
    while n:out.append(n%b);n//=b
    return out
assert [b for b in range(2,15) if max(digits(217,b))<=1]==[2,6]
\end{verbatim}
Indeed $ 217=(11011001)_2=(1001)_6=(11)_{216}=(10)_{217}$. Thus $\boxed{b\in\{2,6,216,217\}}$.

A small disk centre $(x,y)$ must be at distance at most $\sqrt2-r $ from each large centre. Thus $ x^2+y^2+2|x|\le(\sqrt2-r)^2-1=:h^2 $. In particular every possible centre lies within radius $ h $ of the origin, so the two centres have separation at most $ 2h $. Disjoint open disks require separation at least $ 2r $, hence $ r\le h $. Squaring yields $ r^2\le1-2\sqrt2r+r^2 $, or $ r\le\dfrac{\sqrt2}{4}$. Equality is achieved with centres $(0,\pm r)$: they are $ 2r $ apart and each has distance $\sqrt{1+r^2}=\sqrt2-r $ from each large centre. Thus $\boxed{r=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{4}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
