\end{asy}

For a tuple of available numbers, recursively split it into two nonempty subtuples. Every binary expression has such a final split, so combining every attainable pair with each permitted operation generates every expression value. Conversely every generated value comes from a valid expression. Exact rational arithmetic avoids rounding:
\begin{verbatim}
from functools import lru_cache
from fractions import Fraction as F
@lru_cache(None)
def values(v):
    if len(v)==1:return {F(v[0])}
    out=set()
    for mask in range(1,2**len(v)-1):
        a=tuple(v[i] for i in range(len(v)) if mask>>i&1)
        b=tuple(v[i] for i in range(len(v)) if not mask>>i&1)
        for x in values(a):
            for y in values(b):
                out.update((x+y,x-y,x*y))
                if y:out.add(x/y)
    return out
V=values((1,2,3,12))
assert all(F(n) in V for n in range(1,44)) and F(44) not in V
\end{verbatim}
Thus $\boxed{n=43}$.

The second premise has contrapositive $ x\in C\Rightarrow x\in B $, so $ C\subseteq B\subseteq A $. Thus $\boxed{\text{(iii) and (iv)}}$ necessarily hold. The strict nested example $ C=\{1\}$, $ B=\{1,2\}$, $ A=\{1,2,3\}$ disproves (i) and (ii). 
