Measure clockwise angle from the bottom. On the first turn $ r_1<2\pi $ and $ r_1-\dfrac{\pi}{4}>0 $, so survival is certain. During turn two, the only possible crossing is clockwise through angle $ 2\pi $; hence survival is equivalent to $ r_1+r_2<\dfrac{9\pi}{4}$. The subsequent reverse rotation cannot reach 0, since $ r_1+r_2-\dfrac{\pi}{2}>0 $. In the square of side $\dfrac{2\pi}{3}$ representing the independent choices, failure is a corner triangle whose legs have length $\dfrac{8\pi}{3}-\dfrac{9\pi}{4}=\dfrac{5\pi}{12}$. Its probability is $\dfrac{1}{2}(\dfrac{5}{8})^2=\dfrac{25}{128}$. Thus survival has probability $\boxed{\dfrac{103}{128}}$.
