Modular exponentiation computes each remainder exactly without forming huge powers. Checking every residue class once gives $ f(2016)=833,f(2015)=455,f(2014)=190,f(2013)=117 $:
\begin{verbatim}
def f(n):return sum(pow(x,2017,n)==x%n for x in range(1,n+1))
assert [f(n) for n in [2016,2015,2014,2013]]==[833,455,190,117]
\end{verbatim}
The requested combination is $ 833-455+190-117=\boxed{451}$.

Initialize $\phi(n)=n $. For each prime divisor $ p $ of $ n $, multiply by $ 1-\dfrac{1}{p}$, as supplied by inclusion--exclusion over multiples of its distinct prime factors. A sieve applies each such factor once:
\begin{verbatim}
phi=list(range(2018))
for p in range(2,2018):
    if phi[p]==p:
        for n in range(p,2018,p):phi[n]-=phi[n]//p
assert sum(phi[1:])==1237456
\end{verbatim}
This includes $\phi(1)=1 $, and yields $\boxed{1237456}$.

