Olivia occupies one of eight winning lines. Her three moves have $3!$ orders. Xavier occupies three of the remaining six squares, in $3!$ orders, but those squares must not form a winning line or he would have won first. For each of the six row or column choices for Olivia, exactly two of the $\binom63=20$ Xavier subsets are winning lines (the two parallel lines). For either diagonal choice, none is a winning line disjoint from Olivia. Thus the count is $3!3![6(20-2)+2(20)]=\boxed{5328}$. Olivia cannot have won before her third move.
