A state is the sorted tuple of remaining chain lengths, all at least 2. For a cut splitting a length $n$ into $i,n-i$, the immediate reward is the number of length-one pieces; the other pieces remain. Let $F$ be the largest possible difference between the current player's future total and the opponent's. Then $F(\text{state})=\max(\text{reward}-F(\text{next state}))$, with $F(\varnothing)=0$. This is complete because every move is one of these cuts, and every cut reduces the number of links. The exact recursion gives $F((6))=2$.
\begin{verbatim}
from functools import lru_cache
@lru_cache(None)
def F(state):
    if not state: return 0
    best=-100
    for k,n in enumerate(state):
        for i in range(1,n):
            pieces=(i,n-i)
            reward=pieces.count(1)
            nxt=tuple(sorted(state[:k]+state[k+1:]+
                             tuple(x for x in pieces if x>1)))
            best=max(best,reward-F(nxt))
    return best
assert F((6,))==2
\end{verbatim}
All 6 sausages are eventually eaten, so the first player gets $\dfrac{6+2}{2}=\boxed4$ and the second gets $\boxed2$.

Let the rectangle dimensions be $w,h$. For a segment, its length is at most the sum of its absolute horizontal and vertical displacements, with strict inequality if both are nonzero. For the three sides of a triangle, total absolute horizontal displacement is twice its horizontal span, at most $2w$; likewise the vertical total is at most $2h$. A nondegenerate triangle cannot have all three sides horizontal or vertical, so at least one segment inequality is strict. Thus its perimeter is strictly less than $2w+2h$.
