Colour a cell $(x,y)$, with zero-based coordinates, by $x+y$ modulo 3. Every straight tromino covers one cell of each colour. The board has colour counts 21,22,21, so the hole must have $x+y\equiv1\pmod3$. Colouring by $x-y$ instead gives counts 22,21,21, forcing $x-y\equiv0\pmod3$. Hence $x\equiv y\equiv2\pmod3$: the hole is in row 3 or 6 and column 3 or 6. The diagram constructs a tiling with hole $(3,3)$ in one-based coordinates. Reflect it horizontally or vertically to obtain the other three possibilities. The following exact list and verification encode the construction; each triple is a zero-based lower-left cell and an orientation flag,1 for horizontal,0 for vertical.
\begin{verbatim}
T=[(0, 5, 0), (5, 7, 1), (0, 2, 0), (0, 1, 1), (1, 2, 0), (0, 0, 1), (1, 5, 0), (2, 7, 1), (3, 0, 1), (7, 0, 0), (6, 0, 0), (3, 1, 1), (5, 3, 1), (3, 2, 1), (2, 3, 1), (2, 4, 1), (2, 5, 1), (2, 6, 1), (5, 4, 1), (5, 5, 1), (5, 6, 1)]
covered=set()
for x,y,horizontal in T:
    block={(x+i if horizontal else x,
            y if horizontal else y+i) for i in range(3)}
    assert not covered & block
    covered |= block
assert covered=={(x,y) for x in range(8) for y in range(8)}-{(2,2)}
\end{verbatim}
Thus precisely $\boxed{(3,3),(3,6),(6,3),(6,6)}$ are possible.
