Label the vertices $A,B,C,D,E,F$ cyclically. If only the three opposite-vertex diagonals are selected, each crosses at most two others. Thus a short diagonal, say $AC$, must be selected. Its only possible crossing diagonals are $BD,BE,BF$, so all three are required. In turn $BD$ requires $CE,CF$, and $BF$ requires $AD,AE$. Then $AE$ requires $DF$. All nine diagonals are now selected, but $AD$ crosses $BE,BF,CE,CF$, four others. This contradiction proves $\boxed{\text{no}}$.
