Unfold each reflection by reflecting the wedge instead of the ball’s path. The path becomes a straight half-line, and successive wall images are rays at 45-degree increments from the common vertex. As a point moves along a straight half-line not through the vertex, its polar angle changes monotonically by strictly less than 180 degrees. It can therefore cross at most four such rays. Four are achievable: start at polar angle 22.5 degrees and travel in direction 190 degrees. The polar angle increases toward 190 degrees, crossing 45,90,135,180. Folding back gives four collisions. Thus $\boxed4$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int k=0;k<=4;++k)draw((0,0)--4*dir(45*k),gray);pair P=dir(22.5);draw(P--P+5*dir(190),blue,Arrow);dot(P);label("start",P,N);label("unfolded walls",(0,-1));
\end{asy}

The longest side is 1. A nondegenerate obtuse triangle requires $x+y>1$ and $x^2+y^2<1$. In the unit square, this is the part of the quarter unit disk above the diagonal $x+y=1$. Its area is $\dfrac{\pi}{4}-\dfrac{1}{2}\approx0.2854$. The nearest choice is $\boxed{0.29}$.
