At least two square vertices lie on the same triangle side, and they must be adjacent; otherwise the other two lie on opposite sides of that supporting line. For a base side of length $b$ and corresponding altitude $h$, similarity gives square side $s=\dfrac{bh}{b+h}$. Using either leg gives $b=h=4$, hence $s=2$, area 4. Using the hypotenuse gives $b=4\sqrt2,h=2\sqrt2$, hence $s=\dfrac{4\sqrt2}{3}$, area $\dfrac{32}{9}$. These exhaust the possibilities, so the sum is $\boxed{\dfrac{68}{9}}$.
