from itertools import combinations
d=[n for n in range(1,37) if 36%n==0]
sets=list(combinations(d,6))
good=sum(all(s[j-1]%s[i-1]==0 for i in range(1,7)
 for j in range(i+1,7) if j%i==0) for s in sets)
assert len(sets)==84 and good==40
\end{verbatim}

Unique prime factorization makes the condition equivalent to being squarefree with between one and three prime factors. Sieve all integers through 100000: for each prime increment the distinct-factor count on its multiples and mark multiples of its square as nonsquarefree. The following exact computation counts precisely the requested numbers, giving $\boxed{52824}$.
\begin{verbatim}
N=100000
omega=[0]*(N+1)
squarefree=[True]*(N+1)
for p in range(2,N+1):
 if omega[p]==0:
  for k in range(p,N+1,p):omega[k]+=1
  for k in range(p*p,N+1,p*p):squarefree[k]=False
answer=sum(squarefree[k] and 1<=omega[k]<=3 for k in range(2,N+1))
assert answer==52824
\end{verbatim}

