The number must lie between 1000 and 2499 because its product remains four-digit. An exact search over this complete range gives the sole answer $\boxed{2178}$, with $ 4\cdot2178=8712 $:
\begin{verbatim}
sol=[n for n in range(1000,2500) if str(4*n)==str(n)[::-1]]
assert sol==[2178]
\end{verbatim}

Because adjacent angles $ A,B $ are supplementary, $ AD\parallel BC $. A cyclic trapezoid is isosceles; let the equal legs be $ l $, and the bases be $ x=AD<y=BC $. The base angles give height $ h=\dfrac{l}{2}$. If $ s=x+y $, then perimeter gives $ s+2l=32 $, and area gives $\dfrac{sl}{4}=32 $. Thus $ l(32-2l)=128 $, or $(l-8)^2=0 $, giving $ l=8,s=16,h=4 $. The horizontal projection of diagonal $ AC $ is $\dfrac{s}{2}=8 $, so $ AC^2=8^2+4^2=80 $. Since $\angle ABC=30^\circ $, the extended sine rule gives circumradius $ R=AC $. Triangle $ ACO $ therefore has all three sides $ R $ and is equilateral, giving area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}R^2=\boxed{20\sqrt3}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
real x=8-4sqrt(3),y=8+4sqrt(3);pair A=(-x/2,4),D=(x/2,4),B=(-y/2,0),C=(y/2,0);real oy=(16+(x*x-y*y)/4)/8;pair O=(0,oy);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(circle(O,4sqrt(5)));draw(A--O--C--A,blue);label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,NE);label("$O$",O,S);
\end{asy}

The right triangle $ ABC $ gives $ AC=13 $. Let $\theta=\angle BCA=\angle ACD $. Since $\sin\theta=\dfrac{12}{13}$, the perpendicular distance from $ D $ to line $ AC $ is $ CD\sin\theta=12 $. Therefore $[ADE]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot AE\cdot12=\boxed{18}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair B=(0,0),C=(5,0),A=(0,12),u=(A-C)/13;pair v=(-1,0),w=2dot(v,u)*u-v,D=C+13w,PtE=A+3*(C-A)/13;draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--C);filldraw(A--D--PtE--cycle,lightblue);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,E);label("$E$",PtE,W);
\end{asy}

For $ x\ge4 $ every term is nonnegative and increasing. At $ x=28 $ the sum already exceeds 2018, so only 1 through 27 require testing; smaller positive integers are included directly. Exact rational arithmetic avoids any integrality rounding:
\begin{verbatim}
from fractions import Fraction as F
sol=[]
for x in range(1,28):
 v=sum(F(x-j,j)**j for j in range(1,5))
 if v.denominator==1 and v<2018:sol.append(x)
assert sum(F(28-j,j)**j for j in range(1,5))>2018
assert sol==[12,24]
\end{verbatim}
Thus the requested sum is $\boxed{36}$.

Let $\theta=\angle DAB $ and $\phi=\angle PAB $. Since $ P $ is interior and $ H\in AB $, $ 0<\phi<\theta<180^\circ $ and $\phi<90^\circ $. Put $ AD=AP=r $. The chord formula in isosceles triangle $ ADP $ gives $ DP=2r\sin(\dfrac{\theta-\phi}{2})$, while $ HP=r\sin\phi $. Equality $ DP=2HP $ forces $\theta=3\phi $, because both sine arguments lie between 0 and $ 90^\circ $. The direction of $ PD $ makes angle $ 90^\circ+\dfrac{\theta+\phi}{2}=90^\circ+2\phi $ with $ AB $, while $ PH $ points downward. Hence $\angle DPH=180^\circ-2\phi $. Equating this to $\theta=3\phi $ gives $\phi=36^\circ $, $\theta=108^\circ $. Adjacent parallelogram angles sum to 180 degrees, so $\boxed{\angle ABC=72^\circ}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(1.5,0),D=dir(108),C=B+D,P=dir(36),H=(P.x,0);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--P--D^^P--H,blue);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$P$",P,E);label("$H$",H,S);
\end{asy}

The hypotenuse $ AC=10 $. The altitude from $ B $ to $ AC $ is $\dfrac{8\cdot6}{10}=\dfrac{24}{5}$. Since $ D $ is the midpoint of $ BC $, its distance from line $ AC $ is half this, $\dfrac{12}{5}$. Every point $ E $ on the parallel line has the same distance, so $[AEC]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot10\cdot\dfrac{12}{5}=12 $. As the angle at $ E $ is right, $ AE\cdot EC=24 $. Thus the requested product is $\boxed{240}$. The two possible intersections of the parallel line with the diameter circle give the same area.
