Put $\alpha=\angle BAE $, so $ 0<\alpha<60^\circ $, and write $ r=BE $. By the sine rule, $ r=\dfrac{39\sin\alpha}{\sin3\alpha}=\dfrac{39}{4\cos^2\alpha-1}>13 $. Also $ AE=2r\cos\alpha $. Using $ A=(0,0),B=(39,0)$, the horizontal coordinate of $ E $ is $ 2r\cos^2\alpha=\dfrac{r+39}{2}$. Hence $ DE^2=AE^2-40E_x+400=r^2+19r-380 $. The perimeter is $ 19+r+\sqrt{r^2+19r-380}$, strictly increasing for $ r>13 $, since both terms increase there. As $\alpha\to0^+$, $ r\to13 $ and $ DE\to6 $, so the infimum is $\boxed{38}$ with limiting $\boxed{BE=13}$. It is not attained because $ r=13 $ would make $ ABE $ degenerate.
\begin{asy}
