Each of the triangle's three side lines meets a convex pentagon's boundary at most twice, giving an upper bound 6. This is attainable by placing a triangle around a smaller pentagon so that all three triangle vertices lie outside and each triangle side passes through the pentagon's interior. The illustrated regular pentagon and centered equilateral triangle give six distinct crossings. Thus the maximum is $\boxed6 $.
