Both sides are even, so it suffices to count positive solutions and double. Necessarily $0<x\le7$ and the cosine must be positive. On $(0,\dfrac{1}{2})$ the difference $7\cos(\pi x)-x$ decreases from 7 to a negative value, so has one root. On each interval $(2k-\dfrac{1}{2},2k+\dfrac{1}{2})$ for $k=1,2,3$, the difference is negative at the endpoints and positive at the centre. Its second derivative is strictly negative throughout each interval; its derivative strictly decreases, so it has exactly two roots there. There are seven positive roots and no zero root, hence $\boxed{14}$ real roots.
