The answer is $\boxed7 $. Number rows and columns from 0 to 4. Strike $(0,0),(0,2),(1,4),(2,1),(3,3),(4,0),(4,3)$; every cell is then covered. For a finite exhaustive lower-bound certificate, represent the cells broken by each strike as a 25-bit mask. The code checks every six-element subset of possible strikes. None covers the board. Any solution with fewer than six distinct strikes could be extended to six, so none with at most six works.
\begin{verbatim}
from itertools import combinations
from functools import reduce
from operator import or_
M=[]
for r in range(5):
 for c in range(5):
  M.append(sum(1<<(5*x+y) for x,y in
   [(r,c),(r-1,c),(r+1,c),(r,c-1),(r,c+1)]
   if 0<=x<5 and 0<=y<5))
full=(1<<25)-1
assert not any(reduce(or_,(M[i] for i in C),0)==full
 for C in combinations(range(25),6))
assert reduce(or_,(M[i] for i in [0,2,9,11,18,20,23]),0)==full
\end{verbatim}

