For the first walker, two of four first steps survive. At step 2 each surviving position has three interior neighbors; at step 3 every reachable interior position has two interior neighbors except the centre, which has four. Direct counting gives 16 surviving three-step sequences out of 64, so survival probability $\dfrac{1}{4}$. For the centre-starting walker, the four first steps survive, each has three interior second steps, and counting the third step gives 32 surviving sequences, probability $\dfrac{1}{2}$. Independence gives exactly one stop probability $\dfrac{3}{4}\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}+\dfrac{1}{4}\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}=\dfrac{1}{2}$, hence $\boxed{12}$.
