\end{asy}

The row and column contribute 15 squares for every placement. For one diagonal direction, the diagonal lengths are 1,2,...,7,8,7,...,2,1. A diagonal of length $l$ contributes $l(l-1)$ ordered pairs of distinct squares. Thus the expected additional attacked squares from both diagonal directions are $\dfrac{2}{64}\left(2\sum_{l=1}^7l(l-1)+8\cdot7\right)=\dfrac{2}{64}(224+56)=\dfrac{35}{4}$. Total $15+\dfrac{35}{4}=\boxed{\dfrac{95}{4}}$.
