\end{asy}

Place the base endpoints at $(0,0),(14,0)$ and the third vertex at $(t,12)$. If its other integer sides are $ m,n $, then $ t=\dfrac{m^2-n^2+196}{28}$ is rational and $ t^2=m^2-144 $ is an integer; a rational number with integer square is integral. Factoring $(m-t)(m+t)=144 $ gives possible absolute horizontal distances $|t|\in\{0,5,9,16,35\}$, paired with hypotenuses 12,13,15,20,37. Both $ t $ and $ 14-t $ must belong to $\{0,\pm5,\pm9,\pm16,\pm35\}$. The only possibilities are $ t=5,9 $, giving sides 13,15. The perimeter is $\boxed{42}$.
