Expanding and completing squares gives $(n+4)^2+(k+3)^2=157 $. Positivity gives $ n+4\ge2 $ and $ k+3\ge5 $. Checking squares at most 157, the only representation as two positive squares is $ 157=6^2+11^2 $. Hence $(n+4,k+3)$ is $(6,11)$ or $(11,6)$, giving $\boxed{(n,k)=(2,8),(7,3)}$.
