\end{asy}

There are $\binom94=126$ four-point sets. Exactly eight lines contain three grid points: the three rows, three columns, and two long diagonals. For any such triple, the fourth point has six choices. A four-point set cannot contain two different collinear triples, because two distinct lines share at most one point, so their two triples would require at least five points. Thus the answer is $126-8\cdot6=\boxed{78}$.
