The $k$ th line meets previous lines at at most $k-1$ points, so it adds at most $k$ regions. Thus $n$ cuts give at most $1+\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}$ pieces. For 62 cuts the bound is 1954, while for 63 it is 2017. Choose 63 lines with no parallels or triple concurrence and all their intersections inside the disk; this is possible by choosing slightly displaced lines through a sufficiently small central neighborhood. Every new segment is cut at all previous intersections, attaining the bound. Therefore $\boxed{63}$ cuts are necessary and sufficient.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw(circle((0,0),2));draw((-2,0)--(2,0));draw((0,-2)--(0,2));draw((-1.4,-1.4)--(1.4,1.4));draw((-1.5,1.2)--(1.6,-1.1));label("schematic line cuts",(0,-2.5));
\end{asy}

(a) Yes. The incircle disk is contained in the triangular region, itself contained in the circumdisk, so its radius is at most the circumradius; equality would force identical disks, impossible since a triangle cannot contain its circumdisk. (b) No. Four nearby points on a short arc of the unit circle fit in a disk of radius less than 1; for example points at angles $\pm10^\circ,\pm20^\circ$ fit in the disk centred at $(\cos20^\circ,0)$ with radius $\sin20^\circ$. (c) No: in an obtuse triangle two altitudes meet outside. (d) No; the exhaustive integer certificate below checks all allowed pairs against all 100 cubes. (e) No: trisecting 60 degrees would construct 20 degrees. But $t=2\cos20^\circ$ satisfies $t^3-3t-1=0$, irreducible over the rationals by the rational-root test. Its degree 3 is not a power of 2, whereas straightedge-and-compass constructible numbers have degree a power of 2. (f) Yes: these are six points on the nine-point circle. To verify the circle claim, take the circumcenter as vector origin and orthocenter $h=a+b+c$. Its centre is $\dfrac{h}{2}$ and radius $\dfrac{R}{2}$. A midpoint $\dfrac{b+c}{2}$ is distance $\dfrac{|a|}{2}=\dfrac{R}{2}$ from this centre. An altitude foot $d$ has reflection $2d-h$ on the circumcircle, so $|d-\dfrac{h}{2}|=\dfrac{R}{2}$ as well. Thus the answers are $\boxed{Y,N,N,N,N,Y}$.
\begin{verbatim}
cubes = {c**3 for c in range(1,101)}
assert not any(a**3+b**3 in cubes
               for a in range(1,101) for b in range(1,101))
\end{verbatim}

\begin{asy}
