\end{asy}

Diagonal $AC$ is less than $AB+BC=22$, and diagonal $BD$ is less than $BC+CD=26$. Thus every diagonal is below 26. A triangle with sides 10,20,$d$ exists for $10<d<30$, and one with sides 12,14,$d$ exists for $2<d<26$. Glue these on opposite sides of their common length-$d$ base, with $d$ tending to 26 from below. Near 26 the 12–14 triangle is arbitrarily flat, and the other triangle has its altitude foot within the base, so the resulting quadrilateral is convex. Its diagonal tends to 26. Therefore the supremum is $\boxed{26}$, but no nondegenerate quadrilateral attains it.
