(a) True: in an acute triangle the perpendicular bisectors meet inside. (b) True: scaling any acute non-equilateral triangle with perimeter $p$ and area $K$ by factor $\lambda=\dfrac{p}{10000K}$ gives the ratio 10000. (c) True under the stated acute restriction: write the side sum as $2R(\sin A+\sin B+\sin C)$. On $[0,90^\circ]$, $\sin t\ge\dfrac{2t}{\pi}$ in radians, so the sine sum is at least 2; hence the perimeter is at least $4R>3R$. (d) True: taking $O$ as vector origin gives orthocenter vector $h=a+b+c$ because $(h-a)\cdot(b-c)=(b+c)\cdot(b-c)=|b|^2-|c|^2=0$. Thus $h=3g$. (e) True: $\angle OAB=90^\circ-C$ and $\angle HAB=90^\circ-B$ sum to $A$, so the lines $AO,AH$ are reflections in the $A$ bisector; similarly at the other vertices. (f) False: each excenter is also fixed, since its joining lines are internal or external angle bisectors, each invariant under reflection in the internal bisector. The answers are $\boxed{T,T,T,T,T,F}$.
