The remainders identify an integer modulo the least common multiple $ L $ of the chosen moduli. They distinguish all integers 1 through 10000 exactly when $ L\ge10000 $. One modulus cannot suffice; two have lcm at most $ 100\cdot99=9900 $, so at least three are needed. The triple 21,22,23 has lcm 10626 and sum 66. The arithmetic-geometric mean inequality rules out sums at most 64, since the product would be at most $(\dfrac{64}{3})^3<10000 $. The following finite check includes all triples with sum at most 65 and verifies that none has sufficient lcm. Therefore the minima are $\boxed{3\text{ moduli, with sum }66}$.
\begin{verbatim}
from math import lcm
assert lcm(21,22,23)==10626
assert all(lcm(a,b,c)<10000
 for a in range(1,66) for b in range(a,66)
 for c in range(b,66) if a+b+c<=65)
\end{verbatim}

