Let $\gamma=\angle ACB$. The altitude is $2\sqrt6$, so $\sin\gamma=\dfrac{2\sqrt6}{7}$, $\cos\gamma=\dfrac{5}{7}$, and the inradius is $r=\dfrac{10\sqrt6}{12}=\dfrac{5\sqrt6}{6}$. Write $PC=PQ=x$. The cosine rule gives $CQ=2x\cos\gamma=\dfrac{10x}{7}$. The original incircle is the $C$-excircle of $PQC$; its radius equals area divided by semiperimeter minus $PQ$. This is $\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{2} x(CQ)\sin\gamma}{\dfrac{CQ}{2}}=x\sin\gamma$. Therefore $x=\dfrac{35}{12}$. The triangles share the angle at $C$, giving ratio $\dfrac{PC\cdot CQ}{AC\cdot BC}=\dfrac{x^2}{49}=\boxed{\dfrac{25}{144}}$.
