\end{asy}

Let $D_j(s)$ count ways to form $s$ using the first $j$ denominations. The recurrence adds either no bill of the newest denomination $d$ or one such bill to a smaller total: $D_j(s)=D_{j-1}(s)+D_j(s-d)$, with empty total counted once and negative totals zero. The exact computation gives $\boxed{4562}$.
\begin{verbatim}
ways=[1]+[0]*100
for d in [1,2,5,10,20,50]:
 for s in range(d,101):ways[s]+=ways[s-d]
assert ways[100]==4562
\end{verbatim}

Let $t$ be minutes since 12:00, with $0\le t<720$, and let $u$ be the time represented after interchange. The angular conditions are $u\equiv12t\pmod{720}$ and $12u\equiv t\pmod{720}$. Combining gives $143t\equiv0\pmod{720}$. Thus $t=\dfrac{720k}{143}$ for $k=0,\ldots,142$. Conversely each such $t$ has a unique $u\in[0,720)$ satisfying both congruences. There are $\boxed{143}$ instants.
