Compute the complementary probability. The points lie on the same side with probability $\dfrac{1}{4}$. Conditional on this, two independent uniform positions in $[0,1]$ differ by less than $\dfrac{1}{2}$ with probability $\dfrac{3}{4}$. They lie on adjacent sides with probability $\dfrac{1}{2}$; measuring distances from their common corner, the favourable region is a quarter disk of radius $\dfrac{1}{2}$ inside a unit square, of area $\dfrac{\pi}{16}$. Opposite sides cannot give distance below $\dfrac{1}{2}$. Thus the complement is $\dfrac{3}{16}+\dfrac{\pi}{32}$, and the desired probability is $\dfrac{26-\pi}{32}$. Therefore $\boxed{a+b+c=59}$.
