The six quadruples are $(A,E,H,F)$, $(B,D,H,F)$, $(C,D,H,E)$, $(A,B,D,E)$, $(B,C,E,F)$, and $(C,A,D,F)$. Their circles have diameters $AH,BH,CH,AB,BC,CA$, respectively. For example, $AE\perp EH$ and $AF\perp FH$, so $E,F$ lie on the circle with diameter $AH$. Also $AD\perp DB$ and $AE\perp EB$, so $D,E$ lie on the circle with diameter $AB$. The other four follow by cyclic relabeling. This proves all six assertions by Thales' theorem.
