Oppositely marked winning lines cannot intersect. The only disjoint winning lines are two distinct rows or two distinct columns. Count row configurations first. Choose a triangle row and a different circle row in $3\cdot2=6$ ways, then fill the remaining row in $8$ ways, initially giving $48$. Configurations with three monochromatic rows of both types are counted twice: there are $2^3-2=6$. Hence there are $42$ row configurations. Similarly there are $42$ column configurations. A row configuration cannot also be a column configuration, since a monochromatic row forbids an oppositely marked column. Thus the answer is $\boxed{84}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
