\end{asy}

Parameterize a line from $P$ by signed distance $t$ along a unit vector $u$. Points on the circle satisfy $|P+tu-O|^2=r^2$, or $t^2+2u\cdot(P-O)t+PO^2-r^2=0$. Vieta makes the product of its two signed intersection distances $PO^2-r^2$. For an exterior secant directed toward the circle both roots are positive, giving the stated unsigned product. At tangency the radius is perpendicular to $PT$, so the Pythagorean theorem gives $PT^2=PO^2-r^2$. Hence in the example $PT=\sqrt{4\cdot9}=\boxed6$.
