Construct any segment $UV$ and rays at its endpoints making angles $\alpha,\beta$ on the same side. Their intersection $W$ gives the required shape. Transfer the three side lengths consecutively to a ray to construct its perimeter $p$. By the intercept theorem construct the scale factor $\dfrac{s}{p}$: from a common origin mark lengths $p,s$ on one ray and $UV$ on another; the parallel through the point marked $s$ to the joining segment constructs a length $\dfrac{s}{p}UV$. Repeat the angle construction on this new base. The resulting triangle is similar to $UVW$, so its angles are unchanged and its perimeter is $\dfrac{s}{p}p=s$. Similarity also proves uniqueness up to congruence.
