\end{asy}

Let the original vertices be $V_0,\ldots,V_5$ in order, with centre $O$. Start with the three congruent quadrilateral sectors $OV_0V_1V_2$, $OV_2V_3V_4$, and $OV_4V_5V_0$. Replace each radial boundary $OV_{2j}$ by a slightly bent two-segment path, choosing the three bends as rotations of one another by $120^\circ$. For sufficiently small nonzero bends the paths meet only at $O$ and remain inside the hexagon. Each sector now has six corners: $O$, the first bend, two consecutive outer vertices, the third outer vertex, and the second bend. Rotations by $120^\circ$ carry each region to the next, proving congruence. The explicit construction below makes all six corners noncollinear with their neighbours.
