Put $t=2\cos1^\circ$ and define $P_0(X)=2$, $P_1(X)=X$, and $P_{k+1}(X)=XP_k(X)-P_{k-1}(X)$. The cosine addition identity gives $P_k(t)=2\cos k^\circ$ by induction. For every $k\ge1$, $P_k$ is a monic polynomial with integer coefficients. Therefore $t$ is a root of the monic integer polynomial $P_{60}(X)-1$. A rational root of a monic integer polynomial is an integer: writing it as $\dfrac{a}{b}$ in lowest terms and clearing denominators forces $b\mid a^{60}$, hence $b=1$. But $1<2\cos1^\circ<2$, because cosine decreases strictly between $0^\circ$ and $60^\circ$. Thus $t$, and consequently $\cos1^\circ$, is irrational.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair O=(0,0),A=(1,0),B=dir(1);draw(unitcircle);draw((-1.2,0)--(1.2,0),Arrow);draw((0,-1.2)--(0,1.2),Arrow);draw(O--A,red);draw(O--B,blue);dot(B);label("$O$",O,SW);label("$(1,0)$",A,SE);label("$(\cos1^\circ,\sin1^\circ)$",B,NE);label("$1^\circ$",(.55,.3));draw((.55,.24)--.6dir(.5),dashed);
\end{asy}

Let the side length be $s$, altitude $h$, and perpendicular distances $d_1,d_2,d_3$. Joining the point to the vertices partitions the triangle into three triangles of total area $\dfrac{s(d_1+d_2+d_3)}{2}$. This also equals $\dfrac{sh}{2}$, so $h=50$. Since $h=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s$, the side length is $\boxed{\dfrac{100\sqrt3}{3}\text{ miles}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0),C=(3,3sqrt(3)),P=(3,2);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--P--B);draw(C--P);pair F=(P.x,0),G=B+dot(P-B,C-B)/abs(C-B)^2*(C-B),H=A+dot(P-A,C-A)/abs(C-A)^2*(C-A);draw(P--F,dashed);draw(P--G,dashed);draw(P--H,dashed);label("$d_1$",P/2+(1.5,0),E);label("$d_2$",(P+G)/2,NE);label("$d_3$",(P+H)/2,NW);
\end{asy}

Project each set onto the horizontal axis. A continuous image of a connected set is connected, so each projection is a nonempty interval, possibly unbounded or missing endpoints. The intervals intersect pairwise because the original sets do. Let $L$ be the largest lower endpoint and $U$ the smallest upper endpoint, allowing infinite endpoints. Pairwise intersection implies $L\le U$. If $L<U$, choose a finite real number strictly between them; it lies in every interval. If $L=U=c$, this common endpoint is finite. Choose an interval $I$ with lower endpoint $c$ and an interval $J$ with upper endpoint $c$. Their intersection forces both to contain $c$. Every other interval $K$ meets both $I$ and $J$, so it contains points on both sides of $c$ or $c$ itself; its interval property then forces $c\in K$. Hence all projections have a common real number $c$. The vertical line $x=c$ meets every original set. The argument works for any fixed direction of projection.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw(circle((-.5,0),1),blue);draw(circle((.5,0),1),red);draw(circle((0,.6),1),green);draw((0,-1.5)--(0,1.9),black+linewidth(1.2));label("$x=c$",(0,1.9),N);label("$S_1$",(-1.2,0),W);label("$S_2$",(1.2,0),E);label("$S_3$",(.3,1.4),E);label("illustrative connected sets",(0,-1.8));
\end{asy}

(a) Use the vertices of a regular pentagon. Among its vertices, every distance is either a side length or a diagonal length. Any triangle therefore has three sides of only two possible lengths, so two sides are equal. No three pentagon vertices are collinear. (b) Add the centre of the pentagon. A triple containing the centre has two equal radii, while triples of vertices are covered by part (a). No line through two pentagon vertices contains the centre, because a regular pentagon has no antipodal pair. Hence no three of the six points are collinear.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair[] V;for(int i=0;i<5;++i){V.push(dir(90+72*i));dot(V[i]);}draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--cycle);dot((0,0));label("$O$",(0,0),S);draw((0,0)--V[0],dashed);draw((0,0)--V[2],dashed);
\end{asy}

Discard edges until a spanning tree remains, without increasing length. Traverse each tree edge once in each direction, obtaining a closed walk of length twice the tree length. List houses in their order of first appearance, and join successive listed houses, including the last to the first. The triangle inequality shows this 100-vertex cycle has total length no greater than the closed walk. Its alternating edges form two perfect matchings. Their lengths sum to the cycle length, so at least one is no greater than half the cycle length, hence no greater than the tree length and therefore the original wire length. Crossings of the pairing segments are allowed.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair[] V={(0,0),(2,0),(4,1),(4,3),(2,4),(0,3)};draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,gray);for(int i=0;i<6;++i)dot(V[i]);draw(V[0]--V[1],blue+linewidth(1.4));draw(V[2]--V[3],blue+linewidth(1.4));draw(V[4]--V[5],blue+linewidth(1.4));label("alternating matching (six-house illustration)",(2,-1));
\end{asy}

Triangle $ABC$ is right at $B$ and has equal legs $AB=BC$. Its other two angles are equal and sum to $90^\circ$, so each is $\boxed{45^\circ}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(0,3)--cycle);draw((0,0)--(3,3),blue);label("$A$",(0,0),SW);label("$B$",(3,0),SE);label("$C$",(3,3),NE);label("$D$",(0,3),NW);
\end{asy}

A convex polygon is the intersection of the closed half-planes bounded by its edge lines and containing it. Since $P$ is outside, it violates at least one of these half-plane inequalities strictly. Choose the corresponding edge. Every point $Q$ of that edge is on its bounding line. Every point of the open segment $PQ$ remains strictly in the opposite half-plane, so it is outside the polygon. Hence every point of that edge is visible.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair P=(-2,1),A=(0,0),B=(3,0),C=(3,2),D=(0,2);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(P--A,blue);draw(P--D,blue);draw(A--D,red+linewidth(1.2));dot(P);label("$P$",P,W);
\end{asy}

Classify each point by the parities of its two coordinates: even-even, even-odd, odd-even, or odd-odd. There are four classes and five points, so two points share a class. Both coordinate sums of that pair are even, making both midpoint coordinates integers.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int i=0;i<5;++i)for(int j=0;j<4;++j)dot((i,j),gray);draw((0,0)--(4,2),blue);dot((0,0));dot((4,2));dot((2,1),red);label("integer midpoint",(2,1),N);
\end{asy}

The three small triangles have the same altitude, the inradius, to their respective sides. Their area ratios are therefore the side ratios. Thus $BC=\dfrac{48\cdot7}{24}=14$ and $AC=\dfrac{48\cdot25}{24}=50$. Since $14^2+48^2=50^2$, the triangle is right at $B$. Its area is $\boxed{\dfrac{14\cdot48}{2}=336}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair B=(0,0),A=(48,0),C=(0,14),I=(6,6);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--I--B);draw(C--I);label("$A$",A,SE);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,N);label("$I$",I,NE);
\end{asy}

A nonvertical tangent to the first parabola has equation $y=mx-4m^2$, touching at $(8m,4m^2)$. A nonhorizontal tangent to the second can be written $y=\dfrac{x}{n}+4n$. Neither a vertical nor a horizontal line can be tangent to both. Equality of slopes and intercepts gives $n=\dfrac{1}{m}$ and $-4m^2=\dfrac{4}{m}$, hence $m=-1$. Thus $A=(-8,4),B=(4,-8)$. The nonzero intersection is $C=(16,16)$, found by substituting one parabola into the other. The determinant area is $\dfrac{1}{2}|12\cdot12-(-12)\cdot24|=\boxed{216}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
import graph;
draw(graph(new real(real x){return x*x/16;},-10,18));draw(rotate(90)*reflect((0,0),(1,0))*graph(new real(real x){return x*x/16;},-10,18));pair A=(-8,4),B=(4,-8),C=(16,16);draw(A--B--C--cycle,blue);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,NE);
\end{asy}

Write $\theta=\angle BAX$, so $0<\theta<60^\circ$. Put $A=(0,0),X=(1,0),B=(2\cos\theta,2\sin\theta),C=(3\cos2\theta,-3\sin2\theta)$. The collinearity determinant is $-6\sin3\theta+3\sin2\theta+2\sin\theta=0$. Dividing by $2\sin\theta>0$ and using the triple-angle identity yields $12c^2-3c-4=0$, where $c=\cos\theta$. Since $c>\dfrac{1}{2}$, only $c=\dfrac{3+\sqrt{201}}{24}$ qualifies; this is less than 1 and greater than \dfrac{1}{2}. The opposite signs of the $y$ coordinates ensure $X$ lies between $B,C$. Now $CX^2=10-6\cos2\theta=16-12c^2=12-3c=\boxed{\dfrac{93-\sqrt{201}}{8}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
real c=(3+sqrt(201))/24,s=sqrt(1-c*c);pair A=(0,0),X=(1,0),B=(2*c,2*s),C=(3*(2*c*c-1),-6*s*c);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--X,blue);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,S);label("$X$",X,E);
\end{asy}

The finite search below checks every legal assignment of the seven letters in the equation. For each assignment, the unused digits must be assigned to $H,I,J$ increasingly to minimize the full number. Lexicographic minimization gives $(A,B,C,D,E,F,G,H,I,J)=(1,4,8,9,3,2,0,5,6,7)$. Indeed $2401-1418=983$. Therefore the requested sum is $1489+320+56+7=\boxed{1872}$.
\begin{verbatim}
from itertools import permutations
sol=[]
for A,B,C,D in permutations(range(1,10),4):
 if not A<B<C<D:continue
 for E,F,G in permutations([x for x in range(10) if x not in (A,B,C,D)],3):
  if F and 1000*F+100*B+10*G+A-(1000*A+100*B+10*A+C)==100*D+10*C+E:
   H,I,J=sorted(set(range(10))-{A,B,C,D,E,F,G})
   sol.append((A,B,C,D,E,F,G,H,I,J))
assert min(sol)==(1,4,8,9,3,2,0,5,6,7)
\end{verbatim}

Exhaustively checking distinct digits with nonzero leading digits gives the unique assignment $(A,B,C,D,E,F)=(4,3,2,8,6,5)$. It satisfies $432\cdot2=864$, $432\cdot8=3456$, and $864+34560=35424$. Thus $ABC+DE+F=432+86+5=\boxed{523}$. The exhaustive certificate establishes uniqueness, not merely existence.
\begin{verbatim}
from itertools import permutations
sol=[]
for A,B,C,D,E,F in permutations(range(10),6):
 N=100*A+10*B+C
 if A*B*D and N*C==100*D+10*E+A and N*D==1000*B+100*A+10*F+E and N*C+10*N*D==10000*B+1000*F+100*A+10*C+A:
  sol.append((A,B,C,D,E,F))
assert sol==[(4,3,2,8,6,5)]
\end{verbatim}

The horizontal line succeeds only if the three seats to the right all sing, with probability $\dfrac{1}{27}$. For each of the other three lines, let $L,R$ be the consecutive singing run lengths on the two sides, truncated once they reach 3. Failure means $L+R\le2$. Writing $p=\dfrac{1}{3},q=\dfrac{2}{3}$, its probability is $q^2\sum_{s=0}^2(s+1)p^s=\dfrac{8}{9}$: for total $s$ there are $s+1$ splits and both runs end in a nonsinger. The four line events depend on disjoint sets of seats other than the certain centre, so they are independent. Hence the desired probability is $1-\dfrac{26}{27}(\dfrac{8}{9})^3=\boxed{\dfrac{6371}{19683}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int x=-3;x<=3;++x)for(int y=-3;y<=3;++y)dot((x,y),gray);draw((-3,0)--(3,0));draw((0,-3)--(0,3));draw((-3,-3)--(3,3));draw((-3,3)--(3,-3));dot((0,0),blue+3);dot((-1,0),red+3);label("certain singer",(0,0),SE);label("nonsinger",(-1,0),NW);
\end{asy}

Let $CD$ lie on the horizontal axis with coordinates $D=d,M=m,C=c$. Since $AD=AM$, the horizontal coordinate of $A$ is $\dfrac{d+m}{2}$. Similarly the coordinate of $B$ is $\dfrac{m+c}{2}$. The equal height of $A,B$ gives $AB=\dfrac{c-d}{2}=\dfrac{CD}{2}$. Hence $CD=\boxed{10}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
real h=sqrt(231)/10,u=1.3,v=3.7;pair D=(-u,0),M=(u,0),A=(0,h),B=(u+v,h),C=(u+2v,0);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--M--B);label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,NE);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,SW);label("$M$",M,S);
\end{asy}

The first area ratio gives $BD:DC=2:1$, so $[ACD]=\dfrac{1}{3}[ABC]=3\sqrt3$. The second gives $AE:ED=2:1$. Triangles $ACE,ACD$ have bases $AE,AD$ on the same line and equal altitude from $C$, so $k=\dfrac{2}{3}[ACD]=2\sqrt3$. Thus $\boxed{k^2=12}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(3,3sqrt(3)),B=(0,0),C=(6,0),D=(4,0),Ee=A+2(D-A)/3;draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--D);draw(B--Ee--C);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$E$",Ee,E);
\end{asy}

The two half-square pieces each have area $\dfrac{1}{2}$. Their union after folding has area at least $\dfrac{1}{2}$, with equality when they coincide. Folding along a midline parallel to a side achieves this. Thus $A=\dfrac{1}{2}$ and the requested integer is $\boxed{50}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle);draw((.5,0)--(.5,1),dashed);filldraw((.5,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(.5,1)--cycle,lightgray);label("fold",(.5,1),N);
\end{asy}

Place $A=(-5,0,0),B=(5,0,0)$. Equal distances to $A,B$ force $C,D$ into the perpendicular bisector plane $x=0$, at distance $\sqrt{36-25}=\sqrt{11}$ from the origin $O$. In triangle $OCD$, $OC=OD=\sqrt{11}$ and $CD=6$, so its altitude to $CD$ is $\sqrt{11-9}=\sqrt2$ and its area is $3\sqrt2$. The tetrahedron consists of pyramids $AOCD,BOCD$ with heights $5$ each; its volume is $\dfrac{1}{3}(3\sqrt2)(10)=10\sqrt2$. Thus $\boxed{V^2=200}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(-3,0),B=(3,0),C=(0,2),D=(1,-1),O=(0,0);draw(A--B--C--A--D--B);draw(C--D);draw(O--C,dashed);draw(O--D,dashed);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,S);label("$O$",O,SW);
\end{asy}

Put $A=(0,0),B=(2,0),D=(0,2)$, and $F=(0,f)$, where $0\le f\le2$. Then $E=(1,\dfrac{f}{2})$. The equation $DE=2$ gives $1+(\dfrac{f}{2}-2)^2=4$, so $\dfrac{f}{2}=2-\sqrt3$ (the other sign puts $F$ outside the side). The area $[ABE]$ equals the height of $E$ above $AB$, namely $2-\sqrt3$. Thus $a+b=\boxed5$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(2,0),C=(2,2),D=(0,2),F=(0,4-2sqrt(3)),Ee=(B+F)/2;draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(B--F);draw(D--Ee);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$F$",F,W);label("$E$",Ee,S);
\end{asy}

Every walk has $10$ steps. If $k$ are diagonal, the remaining numbers of horizontal and vertical steps both equal $\dfrac{10-k}{2}$, so $k$ is even. Placing $k$ nonadjacent diagonal steps among $10$ positions is possible in $\binom{11-k}{k}$ ways; choose half of the other positions to be horizontal in $\binom{10-k}{\dfrac{10-k}{2}}$ ways. Only $k=0,2,4$ fit. The total is $\binom{11}{0}\binom{10}{5}+\binom92\binom84+\binom74\binom63=252+2520+700=\boxed{3472}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int i=0;i<=10;++i){draw((i,0)--(i,10),lightgray);draw((0,i)--(10,i),lightgray);}draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,2)--(3,3)--(5,3)--(5,5)--(6,6)--(8,6)--(8,8)--(10,8)--(10,10),blue+1);label("$(0,0)$",(0,0),SW);label("$(10,10)$",(10,10),NE);
\end{asy}

An area-bisecting line passes through the square's center: for each fixed direction the area on one side changes strictly as the line translates through the square, and the central line bisects by symmetry. Reflecting the full square across the fold gives another centered square. Let $I$ be the overlap area of the two full squares. The overlap of the two folded halves is $\dfrac{I}{2}$ by central symmetry, so the folded union has area $1-\dfrac{I}{2}$. By symmetry their relative angle may be taken as $0\le\phi\le45^\circ$. For $0<\phi\le45^\circ$, put $c=\cos\phi,s=\sin\phi$. Each of the four corners cut off from the first square is a right triangle with legs $\dfrac{c+s-1}{2c}$ and $\dfrac{c+s-1}{2s}$. Hence $I=1-\dfrac{(c+s-1)^2}{2cs}=\dfrac{2}{1+c+s}$. The same formula holds at $\phi=0$ by continuity. Thus the folded area is $1-\dfrac{1}{1+c+s}$, maximized when $c+s=\sqrt2$, giving $B=2-\sqrt2\approx0.585786$. The required nearest integer is $\boxed{59}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair[] p={(-.5,-.5),(.5,-.5),(.5,.5),(-.5,.5)};draw(p[0]--p[1]--p[2]--p[3]--cycle);draw(rotate(45)*(p[0]--p[1]--p[2]--p[3]--cycle),dashed);draw(-.8dir(22.5)--.8dir(22.5),blue);label("fold",.8dir(22.5),E);label("two full squares",(0,-.9));
\end{asy}

Choose coordinates $D=(0,0),A=(0,a),H=(0,h),B=(b,0),C=(c,0)$, with $a\ne0$. The altitude from $B$ is perpendicular to $AC$, so $bc=-ah$. Write $O=(u,v)$. Equal distances to $B,C,A$ give $u=\dfrac{b+c}{2}$ and $v=\dfrac{a^2+bc}{2a}=\dfrac{a-h}{2}$. The right-angle condition gives $0=(A-O)\cdot(H-O)=u^2+\dfrac{(a+h)(3h-a)}{4}$. Therefore $BC^2=(b-c)^2=4u^2-4bc=(a-h)^2+4h(a-h)$. Since $(a-h)^2=64$ and $h^2=9$, the possible values are $64\pm96$. A squared positive length cannot be $-32$, leaving $\boxed{160}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
real u=sqrt(7),v=sqrt(40);pair A=(0,11),H=(0,3),D=(0,0),B=(u-v,0),C=(u+v,0),O=(u,4);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--D);draw(A--O--H,dashed);dot(H);dot(O);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$H$",H,W);label("$O$",O,E);
\end{asy}

Count strings that avoid HHT by states $0,1,2$, according as their longest suffix that is a prefix of HHT is empty, H, or HH. Appending T sends states $0,1$ to $0$ and terminates state $2$; appending H sends $0$ to $1$ and $1,2$ to $2$. Starting with counts $(1,0,0)$, iterate this transition for $11$ flips. The number in state $2$ is $143$, and exactly these prefixes terminate on the next T. Thus the probability is $\dfrac{143}{2^{12}}=\dfrac{143}{4096}$, already reduced, so $m+n=\boxed{4239}$. The following exact computation verifies the count.
\begin{verbatim}
a,b,c=1,0,0
for _ in range(11):
    a,b,c=a+b,a,b+c
assert c==143
\end{verbatim}

By Thales' theorem, $ABD$ is right at $A$, with $AD=15$. Place $A=(0,0),B=(8,0),D=(0,15)$. Its incenter is $I=(3,3)$ and inradius $r=\dfrac{8+15-17}{2}=3$. The circumcenter is $(4,\dfrac{15}{2})$, radius $\dfrac{17}{2}$. The point on the opposite arc from $A$ equidistant from $B,D$ is $C=(\dfrac{23}{2},\dfrac{23}{2})$. Thus $AI=3\sqrt2$ and $AC=\dfrac{23}{\sqrt2}$ lie on one ray. If the line through $A$ makes angle $\theta$ with this ray and $t=|\sin\theta|$, its distance from $I$ is $3\sqrt2t$, its chord length is $2\sqrt{9-18t^2}$, and its distance from $C$ is $\dfrac{23}{\sqrt2}t$. Therefore $[CPQ]=\dfrac{23}{\sqrt2}t\sqrt{9-18t^2}$ for $0\le t\le\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}$. Squaring, the variable factor is $9t^2-18t^4$, maximized at $t^2=\dfrac{1}{4}$. The maximum area is $\dfrac{69}{4}$, so $m+n=\boxed{73}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(8,0),D=(0,15),C=(11.5,11.5),I=(3,3);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(B--D);draw(circle((4,7.5),8.5),dashed);draw(circle(I,3));pair u=dir(15);real k=dot(I,u),h=sqrt(9-dot(I,I)+k*k);pair P=(k-h)*u,Q=(k+h)*u;draw(A--Q);draw(C--P--Q--C);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$I$",I,N);label("$P$",P,S);label("$Q$",Q,S);
\end{asy}

The polygonal path from $A$ through $D,E$ to $C$ has length at least $AC=6$ by the triangle inequality. Equality is attained by $D=A,E=C$, which are allowed endpoints. Hence the minimum is $\boxed6$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0),C=(3,3sqrt(3)),D=(2,0),Ee=(5,sqrt(3));draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--D--Ee--C,blue);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,S);label("$E$",Ee,NE);
\end{asy}

The diagonal of $A$ and side of $B$ both equal the diameter 20. If the side of $A$ is $a$, then $a\sqrt2=20$. The ratio is $\dfrac{10\sqrt2}{20}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((-1,-1)--(1,-1)--(1,1)--(-1,1)--cycle);draw(unitcircle,blue);draw((1,0)--(0,1)--(-1,0)--(0,-1)--cycle,red);label("$B$",(1,1),NE);label("$A$",(0.5,0.5),SW);
\end{asy}

An inscribed triangle is right exactly when one side is a diameter. There are six antipodal vertex pairs and ten choices for the third vertex, giving 60 right triangles. Each is counted once, since a triangle has only one right angle. Out of $\binom{12}{3}=220$ triangles, the probability is $\boxed{\dfrac{3}{11}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair[] V;for(int i=0;i<12;++i)V.push(dir(30*i));draw(unitcircle);for(int i=0;i<12;++i)dot(V[i]);draw(V[0]--V[6]--V[3]--cycle,blue);
\end{asy}

Let $D(n)$ be the minimum number of allowed chapters needed for $n$ pages. Then $D(0)=0$ and $D(n)=1+\min_{c\le n}D(n-c)$, where $c$ ranges over 3 and powers of 2. This recurrence considers every possible final chapter and so is exact by induction. The following finite computation shows that every positive total below 509 needs at most seven chapters, but 509 needs eight. Thus the answer is $\boxed{509}$.
\begin{verbatim}
coins=[1,2,3,4,8,16,32,64,128,256]
D=[0]
for n in range(1,510):
 D.append(1+min(D[n-c] for c in coins if c<=n))
assert max(D[1:509])<=7 and D[509]==8
\end{verbatim}

The value lies between 1.075 and 1.085, as certified by exact integer inequalities after raising these positive endpoints to the 123rd power. Therefore it rounds to $\boxed{1.08}$. This avoids relying on a rounded logarithm near a rounding boundary.
\begin{verbatim}
assert 1075**123 < 12345*1000**123 < 1085**123
\end{verbatim}

Scale the circle to radius 1 and write the three ordered angular gaps as $\alpha,\beta,\gamma>0$, whose sum is $2\pi$. If the unit position vectors are $a,b,c$, then $OG\le1$ in the original scale is equivalent to $|a+b+c|\le1$. The identity $|a+b+c|^2=3+2(\cos\alpha+\cos\beta+\cos\gamma)=1-8\cos(\dfrac{\alpha}{2})\cos(\dfrac{\beta}{2})\cos(\dfrac{\gamma}{2})$ shows this occurs precisely when all three gaps are at most $\pi$. (At most one gap can exceed $\pi$.) Apart from probability-zero endpoint cases, the complement means all three points lie in a semicircle. There is then a unique point starting the clockwise semicircle that contains the other two. For each of three starting points, the other two lie in its semicircle with probability $\dfrac{1}{4}$, and these events are disjoint. Hence the required probability is $1-\dfrac{3}{4}=\boxed{\dfrac{1}{4}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=3dir(10),B=3dir(145),C=3dir(260),G=(A+B+C)/3;draw(circle((0,0),3));draw(circle((0,0),1),dashed);draw(A--B--C--cycle);dot(G,red);label("$G$",G,E);label("$O$",(0,0),W);
\end{asy}

For each $i$, repeatedly square and reduce modulo $i$ to compute the remainder without retaining the full power. Summing the 100 exact remainders gives $\boxed{1576}$. The following complete finite calculation is also a reproducible certificate.
\begin{verbatim}
def remainder_power(n):
 result=1%n
 base=2%n
 exponent=n
 while exponent:
  if exponent%2: result=result*base%n
  base=base*base%n
  exponent//=2
 return result
assert sum(remainder_power(i) for i in range(1,101))==1576
\end{verbatim}

Take $A=(0,0),B=(2,0),C=(2,2),D=(0,2)$, so $M=(1,0),N=(0,1)$. The lines are $y=1-\dfrac{x}{2}$ and $y=2x-2$, giving $E=(\dfrac{6}{5},\dfrac{2}{5})$. The shoelace formula on $N,E,C,D$ gives area $\dfrac{1}{2}|(0+\dfrac{12}{5}+4+0)-(\dfrac{6}{5}+\dfrac{4}{5}+0+0)|=\boxed{\dfrac{11}{5}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(2,0),C=(2,2),D=(0,2),M=(1,0),Nn=(0,1),Ee=(1.2,0.4);fill(Nn--Ee--C--D--cycle,lightblue);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(B--Nn);draw(C--M);label("$N$",Nn,W);label("$E$",Ee,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$M$",M,S);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);
\end{asy}

Place $A=(0,0),D=(L,0)$ and the polygon above $AD$. Then $B=(4,4\sqrt3)$ and $C=(L-5,5\sqrt3)$. Consequently $BC^2=(L-9)^2+3=9$, so $L=9\pm\sqrt6$. Convexity requires the turn at $B$ to have the same negative cross-product sign as the turn at $A$. At $B$ this cross product is $4\sqrt3(10-L)$, so $L>10$. Only $\boxed{9+\sqrt6}$ qualifies. For this value the turn at $C$ is also negative, since its cross product is $-5\sqrt3(L-8)$; the other two prescribed angles and all lengths are satisfied.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
real L=9+sqrt(6);pair A=(0,0),B=(4,4sqrt(3)),C=(L-5,5sqrt(3)),D=(L,0);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,NW);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,SE);
\end{asy}

Put $B=(-\dfrac{x}{2},0),C=(\dfrac{x}{2},0),A=(0,\dfrac{\sqrt3x}{2})$. The third centre $P$ is equidistant from $B,C$, so lies on their perpendicular bisector. Since $AP=1$ and $P$ is below $A$, it is $(0,\dfrac{\sqrt3x}{2}-1)$. External tangency gives $PB=2$, hence $\dfrac{x^2}{4}+(\dfrac{\sqrt3x}{2}-1)^2=4$, or $x^2-\sqrt3x-3=0$. Its sole positive root is $\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt3+\sqrt{15}}{2}}$. This root gives positive height for $P$ and satisfies both tangencies, so it is valid.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
real x=(sqrt(3)+sqrt(15))/2;pair B=(-x/2,0),C=(x/2,0),A=(0,sqrt(3)*x/2),P=A-(0,1);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(circle(B,1));draw(circle(C,1));draw(circle(P,1),blue);dot(P);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,S);label("$P$",P,E);
\end{asy}

By Vieta, positivity of the root sum and product requires $n>1$ and $(n-6)^2<24$, so $2\le n\le10$. The exact check below lists all positive integer root pairs in this range: $(2,4)$ for $n=2$ and $(1,23)$ for $n=5$. Each has exactly one prime root and a power of 2 (including $1=2^0$). Hence the sum is $\boxed7$.
\begin{verbatim}
v=[]
for n in range(2,11):
 S=6*n-6
 for a in range(1,S):
  c=S-a
  if a<c and a*c==-n*n+12*n-12:v.append((n,a,c))
assert v==[(2,2,4),(5,1,23)]
\end{verbatim}

The orthocentre distance identities give $AH=2R|\cos A|$ and $BH=2R|\cos B|$, so $|\cos A|=\dfrac{3}{5}$, $|\cos B|=\dfrac{7}{25}$, with $\sin A=\dfrac{4}{5}$, $\sin B=\dfrac{24}{25}$. If both $A,B$ are acute, then $\cos C=\sin A\sin B-\cos A\cos B=\dfrac{3}{5}$, giving $CH=30$. If $B$ is obtuse and $A$ acute, then $\cos C=\dfrac{117}{125}$, giving $CH=\dfrac{234}{5}$; this is valid since the acute angle with cosine $\dfrac{3}{5}$ is smaller than the acute angle with cosine $\dfrac{7}{25}$. If $A$ is obtuse and $B$ acute, their sum exceeds $180^\circ$ by the same comparison, so no triangle results. Both cannot be obtuse. Thus $\boxed{30,\ \dfrac{234}{5}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int j=0;j<2;++j){real ca=0.6,sa=0.8,cb=(j==0?0.28:-0.28),sb=0.96,cc=sa*sb-ca*cb;pair u=(65*j,0),A=u,B=u+(50sqrt(1-cc^2),0),C=u+48*(ca,sa);real hx=C.x-u.x,hy=-(C.x-B.x)*hx/C.y;pair H=u+(hx,hy);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--H--B,dashed);draw(C--H,dashed);dot(H,red);label("$H$",H,E);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);}
\end{asy}

Removing $A_1B_1$ splits the tree into two components. The remaining path $B_1A_2B_2$ puts $B_2$ in the component opposite $A_1$, so the new edge joins the two components and creates no cycle. It also joins an $A$-vertex to a $B$-vertex, preserving bipartiteness. Let the potential be the sum of all edge lengths. Every allowed move strictly decreases this potential, so no tree can recur. There are only finitely many subsets of the finite set of possible edges, and hence finitely many spanning trees. Therefore infinitely many moves are impossible; every sequence terminates.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A1=(0,0),B1=(4,0),A2=(4,3),B2=(1,2);draw(A1--B1--A2--B2);draw(A1--B2,dashed+blue);label("$A_1$",A1,SW);label("$B_1$",B1,SE);label("$A_2$",A2,NE);label("$B_2$",B2,NW);
\end{asy}

The altitude is $\sqrt{2^2-1^2}=\sqrt3$, so $E=(0,\sqrt3)$. The medians meet at the centroid, the average of the three vertex coordinates. Hence $\boxed{G=(0,\dfrac{\sqrt3}{3})}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(-1,0),C=(1,0),E0=(0,sqrt(3)),B=(0,0),D=(C+E0)/2,F=(A+E0)/2,G=(0,sqrt(3)/3);draw(A--C--E0--cycle);draw(A--D);draw(C--F);draw(B--E0);label("A",A,SW);label("B",B,S);label("C",C,SE);label("D",D,E);label("E",E0,N);label("F",F,W);label("G",G,E);
\end{asy}

(a) The Pythagorean theorem gives distance $\sqrt{15^2+10^2}=\boxed{5\sqrt{13}\text{ feet}}$. (b) No. The old probability constrains only the old setup. A model assigning success probability $\dfrac{1}{2}$ to the new setup and another assigning $\dfrac{3}{4}$ both agree with the given old percentage. Without a specified model relating distance and target geometry to success, the new probability is undetermined.
