draw((0,0)--(15,0)--(15,10)--cycle);label("15 ft",(7.5,0),S);label("10 ft",(15,5),E);label("target",(15,10),N);label("shooter floor point",(0,0),W);
\end{asy}

The original point satisfies $|y|\le\dfrac{x}{2}$ and $0\le x\le6$. The translated sloping-boundary conditions are automatically satisfied, since $|y|\le\dfrac{x}{2}<\dfrac{x+3}{2}$. Its remaining condition is $x+3\le6$, or $x\le3$. This is a similar triangle with scale factor $\dfrac{1}{2}$, so its area ratio is $\boxed{\dfrac{1}{4}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(6,3)--(6,-3)--cycle);filldraw((0,0)--(3,1.5)--(3,-1.5)--cycle,lightgray);label("$x=3$",(3,-1.5),S);label("$x=6$",(6,-3),S);
\end{asy}

If the radii are $r,s,t$, then their pairwise sums are $17,25,28$. Adding gives $r+s+t=35$, so the radii are $35-17=18$, $35-25=10$, and $35-28=7$. Their total area is $\pi(18^2+10^2+7^2)=\boxed{473\pi}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(17,0);real x=(25^2+17^2-28^2)/34;pair C=(x,sqrt(25^2-x*x));draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(circle(A,7));draw(circle(B,10));draw(circle(C,18));label("$7$",A,S);label("$10$",B,S);label("$18$",C,N);
\end{asy}

Place $B=(0,0),A=(0,4),C=(2,0)$. The internal bisector is the ray $y=x\ge0$. The given perpendicular distance forces $E=(8,8)$. (These coordinates also satisfy the stated equal-angle condition.) The area is $\dfrac{1}{2}|\det((2,-4),(8,4))|=\dfrac{1}{2}(8+32)=\boxed{20}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair B=(0,0),A=(0,4),C=(2,0),Ee=(8,8),F=(8,0);draw(A--B--C--A);draw(B--Ee--C);draw(Ee--A);draw(Ee--F,dashed);draw(C--F,dashed);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,S);label("$E$",Ee,N);label("$F$",F,S);
\end{asy}

Label cells $0,\ldots,15$ row by row from southwest. An arrangement is a bijection $f$ from cells to adjacent cells, with $f(0)=1$ and no pair $f(i)=j,f(j)=i$. The following exhaustive backtracking chooses each token destination once, rejecting an occupied destination or a two-cycle as soon as it occurs. Every valid arrangement appears exactly once. It yields $\boxed{44}$.
\begin{verbatim}
adj=[[j for j in range(16)
      if abs(i//4-j//4)+abs(i%4-j%4)==1] for i in range(16)]
f=[-1]*16
f[0]=1
count=0
def dfs(i,used):
    global count
    if i==16:
        count+=1
        return
    for j in adj[i]:
        if not (used>>j)&1 and f[j]!=i:
            f[i]=j
            dfs(i+1,used|(1<<j))
            f[i]=-1
dfs(1,2)
assert count==44
\end{verbatim}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int i=0;i<=4;++i){draw((i,0)--(i,4));draw((0,i)--(4,i));}for(int j=0;j<4;++j)for(int i=0;i<4;++i)label(string(4j+i),(i+.5,j+.5));draw((.5,.5)--(1.5,.5),blue,Arrow);label("forced first move",(2,-.5));
\end{asy}

Every primitive Pythagorean triple is generated uniquely by coprime integers $m>n>0$ of opposite parity, with legs $m^2-n^2,2mn$ and hypotenuse $m^2+n^2$. Thus it suffices to count those parameter pairs with $m^2+n^2<100000$. We have $m\le316$, so the following finite enumeration is exhaustive and gives $\boxed{15919}$.
\begin{verbatim}
from math import gcd
count=sum(1 for m in range(2,317) for n in range(1,m)
          if m*m+n*n<100000 and gcd(m,n)==1 and (m-n)%2)
assert count==15919
\end{verbatim}

Form the set of all 100 possible cubes $c^3$ and test all 10000 ordered pairs $(a,b)$. The following exact integer computation exhausts the stated finite domain; its empty result proves the assertion.
\begin{verbatim}
cubes={c**3 for c in range(1,101)}
solutions=[(a,b) for a in range(1,101) for b in range(1,101)
           if a**3+b**3 in cubes]
assert solutions==[]
\end{verbatim}

If the outer radius is $R$ and inner radius is $r$, the inner diameter lies at distance $r$ from the outer diameter. Its endpoint is horizontally $r$ and vertically $r$ from the outer center, so $R^2=2r^2$. Areas therefore form a geometric series with first term $\dfrac{\pi}{2}$ and ratio $\dfrac{1}{2}$. Their sum is $\dfrac{\dfrac{\pi}{2}}{1-\dfrac{1}{2}}=\boxed\pi$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
real r=1/sqrt(2);draw(arc((0,0),1,0,180));draw((-1,0)--(1,0));draw(arc((0,r),r,180,360));draw((-r,r)--(r,r));draw((0,0)--(r,r),dashed);label("$R$",(.35,.4),SE);label("$r$",(0,r),N);
\end{asy}

The crease is the perpendicular bisector of $AP$, so the marked point is its midpoint. The map $P\mapsto\dfrac{A+P}{2}$ is a homothety with center $A$ and factor $\dfrac{1}{2}$. The locus is that circle with the point $A$ omitted, because $P=A$ was excluded. Its closure is the full circle with one-half the original radius, whose enclosed area is $\dfrac{1}{4}\cdot80=\boxed{20}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(2,0),P=2dir(120),M=(A+P)/2;draw(circle((0,0),2));draw(circle((1,0),1));draw(A--P);dot(M);label("$A$",A,E);label("$P$",P,NW);label("$M$",M,N);
\end{asy}

The rectangle successively pivots through four right angles around its four corners. The chosen corner is at distances $0,6,10,8$ from those pivot corners, where $10$ is the diagonal. Its path therefore has total length $\dfrac{\pi}{2}(0+6+10+8)=\boxed{12\pi}$ feet.
\begin{asy}
