size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(8,0)--(8,6)--(0,6)--cycle);draw((0,0)--(8,6),dashed);label("$6$",(0,3),W);label("$8$",(4,0),S);label("$10$",(4,3),NW);dot((0,0));label("fixed corner",(0,0),SW);
\end{asy}

The minute hand gains $360^\circ$ on the hour hand every $\dfrac{12}{11}$ hours, so it makes $22$ relative revolutions in $24$ hours. Each relative revolution contains two right-angle positions, giving $\boxed{44}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw(unitcircle);draw((0,0)--(.75,0));draw((0,0)--(0,.55));label("$90^\circ$",(.2,.2));
\end{asy}

Pythagoras gives the other leg length $\sqrt B$, so the condition is $A=\lceil\sqrt B\rceil$. For $A=1$, $C=a_1=2$ and $B=2$, but $\lceil\sqrt2\rceil=2\ne1$. For $A=2$, $C=a_2=5$ and $B=5$, but $\lceil\sqrt5\rceil=3\ne2$. For $A=3$, $C=a_3=3\cdot5-2=13$, so $B=1+3=4$, but $\lceil\sqrt4\rceil=2\ne3$. For $A=4$, $C=a_4=3\cdot13-5=34$ and $B=7$, giving $\lceil\sqrt7\rceil=3\ne4$. For $A=5$, $C=a_5=3\cdot34-13=89$, so $B=17$ and $\lceil\sqrt{17}\rceil=5$. Thus the smallest solution is $\boxed{(5,17,89)}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair C=(0,0),A=(sqrt(17),0),D=(0,sqrt(17));draw(A--C--D--cycle);label("$A_0$",A,E);label("$C_0$",C,SW);label("$D_0$",D,N);label("$\sqrt B$",A/2,S);label("$\sqrt B$",D/2,W);label("$\sqrt{2B}$",(A+D)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

Fix the first point by rotational symmetry. A chord of length at most the radius subtends a smaller central angle at most 60 degrees, since its length is $8\sin(\dfrac{\theta}{2})$. Thus the second point must lie in the 120-degree arc centred at the first. The probability is $\dfrac{120}{360}=\boxed{\dfrac{1}{3}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair O=(0,0),A=(4,0),B=4*dir(60),C=4*dir(-60);draw(circle(O,4));draw(arc(O,4,-60,60),blue+2);draw(O--A--B--O);label("O",O,W);label("A",A,E);label("B",B,NE);label("4",(2,0),S);
\end{asy}

The exterior consists of the curved side and one circular base. Its area is $2\pi rh+\pi r^2=2\pi(3)(10)+\pi(3)^2=\boxed{69\pi}$ square centimeters.
