\end{asy}

View one drilled cube as 27 unit cubes with the seven cubes on the three central axes removed. The eight corner cubes each expose three unit faces, and the twelve edge-middle cubes each expose four, giving $8\cdot3+12\cdot4=72$ square inches per drilled cube. In the assembly there are $3\cdot(2\cdot3\cdot3)=54$ interfaces between small cubes. Each interface has area $3^2-1^2=8$ of wood on each side, which is no longer exposed. Thus the total is $27\cdot72-2\cdot54\cdot8=\boxed{1080\text{ square inches}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(0,3)--cycle);draw((0,3)--(1,4)--(4,4)--(3,3));draw((3,0)--(4,1)--(4,4));draw((1,1)--(2,1)--(2,2)--(1,2)--cycle,blue);draw((1.333,3.333)--(2.333,3.333)--(2.667,3.667)--(1.667,3.667)--cycle,blue);draw((3.333,1.333)--(3.667,1.667)--(3.667,2.667)--(3.333,2.333)--cycle,blue);label("one drilled cube; side 3",(2,-.6));label("front opening: side 1",(1.5,1.5));
\end{asy}

The slope between the first and third points is $\dfrac{2b}{2a}=\dfrac{b}{a}$. Collinearity with $(b,0)$ gives the determinant equation $3b(3a-b)=5b(a-b)$, hence $4a+2b=0$. Thus $b=-2a$, and the slope is $\boxed{-2}$. This value gives distinct points, so no exceptional vertical line occurs.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((-2,0)--(3,-10));dot((-2,0));dot((1,-6));dot((3,-10));label("$(b,0)$",(-2,0),NW);label("$(a,3b)$",(1,-6),NE);label("$(3a,5b)$",(3,-10),SE);
\end{asy}

Put $d^2=h^2+k^2>1$. A tangent at a point $T=(u,v)$ of the unit circle has equation $ux+vy=1$, since the radius is perpendicular to the tangent. Requiring passage through $P$ gives $hu+kv=1$. Decompose $T$ in the perpendicular directions $(h,k)$ and $(-k,h)$. The solutions are $u=\dfrac{h\mp k\sqrt{d^2-1}}{d^2}$ and $v=\dfrac{k\pm h\sqrt{d^2-1}}{d^2}$. Substitution into $ux+vy=1$ gives the two requested tangent lines.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair P=(2,0),A=(0.5,sqrt(3)/2),B=(0.5,-sqrt(3)/2);draw(unitcircle);draw(P--A);draw(P--B);draw((0,0)--A--P,blue);label("$P$",P,E);label("$T_+$",A,N);label("$T_-$",B,S);label("$O$",(0,0),W);
\end{asy}

Scale the square to side $2$. For opposite sides, every joining segment meets the disk: a line through $(-1,y_1),(1,y_2)$ has distance $\dfrac{|y_1+y_2|}{\sqrt{4+(y_2-y_1)^2}}\le1$ from its centre, and its intersection with the disk lies inside the square chord. For adjacent sides, use distances $u,v\in[0,2]$ from their common corner. The centre is $(1,1)$ relative to that corner, and the joining line is $vx+uy=uv$. A direct distance calculation shows that it misses the disk exactly when $(2-u)(2-v)>2$. The area of this region is $\int_1^2(2-\dfrac{2}{a})\,da=2-2\log2$ in a parameter square of area $4$. Thus the conditional miss probability is $\dfrac{1-\log2}{2}$. Four of the six side pairs are adjacent, so the overall intersection probability is $1-\dfrac{4}{6}\cdot\dfrac{1-\log2}{2}=\boxed{\dfrac{2+\log2}{3}}$, where log is natural.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,2)--(0,2)--cycle);draw(circle((1,1),1),blue);draw((0,0.5)--(0.7,0),red);draw((0,1.7)--(1.8,0),green);label("$O$",(1,1),N);label("$u$",(0.7,0),S);label("$v$",(0,0.5),W);
\end{asy}

Arrange four congruent copies of the triangle inside a square of side $a+b$, leaving a central square whose four sides are hypotenuses of length $c$. The two acute angles of each right triangle sum to $90^\circ$, so each central angle is $90^\circ$. Comparing areas gives $(a+b)^2=4\cdot\dfrac{ab}{2}+c^2$, which simplifies to $a^2+b^2=c^2$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
