No. Modulo $2$, both sum and difference are exclusive-or: the upper parity is the sum of the two lower parities modulo $2$. Consequently the six bottom parities determine the entire array. Since the numbers $1,\ldots,21$ include exactly eleven odd numbers, the parity array would need eleven ones. The following exhaustive check of all $2^6=64$ bottom parity rows proves this never occurs; hence no original arrangement can exist.
\begin{verbatim}
from itertools import product
for row in product((0,1),repeat=6):
    odd=sum(row)
    while len(row)>1:
        row=tuple(a^b for a,b in zip(row,row[1:]))
        odd+=sum(row)
    assert odd!=11
\end{verbatim}

