Use an exact dynamic program. After considering the first $i$ integers, retain a seven-bit mask of whether $i,i-1,\ldots,i-6$ were chosen; bit zero refers to $i$. The next integer may be omitted, or included only if bits $3$ and $6$ are zero, excluding differences $4$ and $7$. Keep the largest count for each mask. These are the only relevant earlier elements, so induction on $i$ proves every feasible subset is represented and every retained count is attainable. The exhaustive recurrence below returns $\boxed{905}$.
\begin{verbatim}
D={0:0}
for i in range(1989):
    E={}
    for m,v in D.items():
        n=(m<<1)&127
        E[n]=max(E.get(n,-1),v)
        if not (m&8 or m&64):
            E[n|1]=max(E.get(n|1,-1),v+1)
    D=E
assert max(D.values())==905
\end{verbatim}

Each side of the inscribed regular hexagon equals the radius, so its perimeter is $6$. Each half-side of the circumscribed hexagon has length $\tan30^\circ=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}$, so its perimeter is $4\sqrt3$. The circle circumference lies strictly between these polygonal perimeters, giving $6<2\pi<4\sqrt3$, hence the claimed bounds.
