\end{asy}

The interior angle $ACB$ is $74^\circ$, so $ABC=74^\circ$ and $BAC=32^\circ$. Scale $AC$ to $1$ and put $A=(0,0),C=(1,0),D=(2,0),B=(\cos32^\circ,\sin32^\circ)$. At $D$, the horizontal and vertical components toward $B$ give $\tan\angle BDC=\dfrac{\sin32^\circ}{2-\cos32^\circ}$. Both components are positive in magnitude and the angle is acute, so the exact answer is $\boxed{\arctan\left(\dfrac{\sin32^\circ}{2-\cos32^\circ}\right)}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),C=(1,0),D=(2,0),B=dir(32);draw(A--B--C--A);draw(C--D--B);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,S);label("$D$",D,SE);
\end{asy}

Let $a=BC,b=CA,c=AB$, and let $l_a,l_b$ be the internal bisector lengths from $A,B$. The angle-bisector theorem and the area formula give $l_a=\dfrac{2bc\cos(\dfrac{A}{2})}{b+c}$ and $l_b=\dfrac{2ac\cos(\dfrac{B}{2})}{a+c}$. For example $l_a$ follows by writing the total area as $\dfrac{1}{2}l_a(b+c)\sin(\dfrac{A}{2})=\dfrac{1}{2}bc\sin A$. If $a>b$, then $A>B$, so $\cos(\dfrac{A}{2})<\cos(\dfrac{B}{2})$ and $\dfrac{b}{b+c}<\dfrac{a}{a+c}$. Hence $l_a<l_b$, a contradiction. Interchanging $a,b$ excludes $a<b$. Thus $a=b$, and the triangle is isosceles.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,3), B=(-2,0), C=(2,0), D=(0,0), T=(2*sqrt(13)/(4+sqrt(13)),12/(4+sqrt(13))); draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--D,blue);draw(B--T,red);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$T$",T,NE);
\end{asy}

Traverse the grid in a snake path, going across the first row, back across the second, and so on. Consecutive desks on this path share a side. Pair the first $120$ desks consecutively, leaving the last desk alone. Each pair holds at most one person, giving an upper bound $60+1=61$. Occupy all desks of the larger colour class in a checkerboard colouring; this occupies $61$ desks without side adjacency. Thus the maximum is $\boxed{61}$.
