Write $[ABC]=s $. Since $ T $ is a quarter of the way from $ D $ toward $ A $, $[TBC]=\dfrac{s}{4}$. Similarly $ T $ is a fifth of the way from $ E $ toward $ B $, so $[ATC]=\dfrac{s}{5}$. Therefore $[ATB]=s-\dfrac{s}{4}-\dfrac{s}{5}=\dfrac{11s}{20}$. The ratio of areas $ ATC $ and $ ATB $ equals that of $ ADC $ and $ ADB $, since the common factor is $\dfrac{AT}{AD}$; these latter areas have base ratio $\dfrac{CD}{BD}$. Hence $\boxed{CD:BD=4:11}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,5),B=(0,0),C=(15,0),D=(11,0),T=(8.25,1.25),Ee=(10.3125,1.5625);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--D^^B--Ee);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$E$",Ee,NE);label("$T$",T,S);
\end{asy}

The two legs are $ 8 $, so the triangle area is $\dfrac{8\cdot8}{2}=32 $. Its inradius is $\dfrac{8+8-8\sqrt2}{2}=8-4\sqrt2 $. Subtracting the disk area gives $\boxed{32-\pi(8-4\sqrt2)^2}$.
\begin{asy}
