\end{asy}

Fix a boundary edge. If its triangle has a specified third vertex, the remaining choices are independent triangulations of the polygons on its two sides. Let $ C_m $ count triangulations of a polygon with $ m+2 $ vertices, with $ C_0=1 $ for an empty side. Then $ C_m=\sum_{i=0}^{m-1}C_iC_{m-1-i}$. Starting with $ 1 $, this recurrence gives $ C_1=1,C_2=2,C_3=5,C_4=14,C_5=42,C_6=132 $. Thus the octagon has $\boxed{132}$ triangulations.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair[] P;for(int k=0;k<8;++k)P.push(dir(45*k));path poly=P[0];for(int k=1;k<8;++k)poly=poly--P[k];draw(poly--cycle);for(int k=2;k<7;++k)draw(P[0]--P[k],dashed);for(int k=0;k<8;++k)label(string(k+1),P[k],P[k]);
\end{asy}

Let the total area be $ K $. Similarity makes the top $ j $ bands have combined area $\dfrac{j^2K}{100}$. Hence band $ j $ has area $\dfrac{(2j-1)K}{100}$. Each adjacent blue-red pair differs by $\dfrac{2K}{100}$, and there are five pairs. Thus $\dfrac{K}{10}=20 $, so $\boxed{K=200}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,sqrt(3)),B=(-1,0),C=(1,0);for(int j=1;j<=10;++j){real u=(j-1)/10.0,v=j/10.0;pair L=A+u*(B-A),R=A+u*(C-A),LL=A+v*(B-A),RR=A+v*(C-A);filldraw(L--R--RR--LL--cycle,(j%2==1)?lightred:lightblue,black);}label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);
\end{asy}

Let $ k $ edges be positive and let $ f $ faces be negative. The total is $(2k-12)+(6-2f)=2k-2f-6 $. A negative face must contain an odd number, hence at least one, of positive edges, because it has four edges. Each positive edge borders two faces, so $ f\le2k $, and also $ f\le6 $. If $ k\le3 $, then $ f-k\le k\le3 $; if $ k\ge3 $, then $ f-k\le6-k\le3 $. Thus the total is at least $-12 $. Equality is attained by making just three edges positive, one parallel to each coordinate axis, with their incident face pairs all distinct: use the $ x $ edge at $ y=z=0 $, the $ y $ edge at $ x=0,z=1 $, and the $ z $ edge at $ x=y=1 $. Every face then has exactly one positive edge, so all face products are negative. The minimum is $\boxed{-12}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair[] P={(0,0),(3,0),(4,1),(1,1),(0,3),(3,3),(4,4),(1,4)};int[] I={0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,0,1,2,3},J={1,2,3,0,5,6,7,4,4,5,6,7};for(int k=0;k<12;++k){bool pos=(k==0||k==7||k==10);draw(P[I[k]]--P[J[k]],pos?red+linewidth(1.5):black);label(pos?"$+1$":"$-1$",(P[I[k]]+P[J[k]])/2,dir(90));}
\end{asy}

Construct a regular pentagon to obtain its central angle $ 72^\circ $ and bisect it to obtain $ 36^\circ $. An equilateral triangle gives $ 60^\circ $, whose bisector gives $ 30^\circ $. Transfer the two angles to the same initial ray: their difference is $ 6^\circ $. Bisect again to obtain $ 3^\circ $. Repeated angle copying constructs all $ 61 $ multiples from $ 0^\circ $ through $ 180^\circ $. For completeness, the regular-pentagon angle is constructible since $\cos72^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{4}$: construct $\sqrt5 $ as the hypotenuse of legs $ 1,2 $, perform the indicated subtraction and divisions, and intersect the vertical line at that coordinate with the unit circle. The cosine value follows from the real part of $ 1+\zeta+\cdots+\zeta^4=0 $: if $ u=\cos72^\circ $, then $ 1+2u+2(2u^2-1)=0 $, whose positive root is the stated value.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(4,0));draw((0,0)--4*dir(30));draw((0,0)--4*dir(36));draw((0,0)--4*dir(3),red);label("$O$",(0,0),SW);label("$30^\circ$",4*dir(30),SE);label("$36^\circ$",4*dir(36),N);label("$3^\circ$",4*dir(3),N);
\end{asy}

Fix a vertex $ v $. Its neighbour set $ A $ has size $ a $, and the remaining set $ B $ has size $ n-a-1 $. Count edges between these sets. Each vertex in $ A $ has one neighbour $ v $, exactly $ b $ neighbours in $ A $, and hence $ a-1-b $ in $ B $. Each vertex in $ B $ has exactly $ c $ neighbours in $ A $. Counting the same edges from both sides gives $ a(a-1-b)=c(n-a-1)$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair[] P;for(int k=0;k<5;++k)P.push(dir(90+72*k));for(int k=0;k<5;++k){draw(P[k]--P[(k+1)%5]);dot(P[k]);}label("$v$",P[0],N);label("$A_1$",P[1],W);label("$B_1$",P[2],S);label("$B_2$",P[3],S);label("$A_2$",P[4],E);label("Example: five-cycle",(0,-1.5));
\end{asy}

Write $ AB=c,AC=b,BC=a $ and $ AU=AV=t $. Equal tangents give $ BG=BU=c-t $, $ CH=CV=b-t $ and $ XG=XH $. Since $ BG=BX+XG $ and $ CH=CX+XH $, subtraction yields $ BX-CX=c-b $. In a triangle with base length $ a $ and other sides $ u,v $ incident respectively to the first and second base vertices, the first tangent segment on the base has length $\dfrac{a+u-v}{2}$, by adding the three equal-tangent equations. For $ ABC $ it is $\dfrac{a+c-b}{2}$, and for $ BCX $ it is $\dfrac{a+BX-CX}{2}$, the same value. Thus the contact points coincide.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0),C=(0,8),X=(42/23,40/23),G=(18/13,25/13),H=(49/25,32/25),T=(18/5,16/5);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(B--G^^C--H);draw(circle((1,1),1));draw(circle((2,2),2),blue);draw(circle((30/11,28/11),12/11),red);dot(T);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$X$",X,W);label("$G$",G,W);label("$H$",H,E);label("$T$",T,NE);label("$U$",(1,0),S);label("$V$",(0,1),W);
\end{asy}

Pair $ j $ with $ 7-j $ to obtain $ 7\left(\cos\dfrac{2\pi}{7}+\cos\dfrac{4\pi}{7}+\cos\dfrac{6\pi}{7}\right)$. The sum of the seventh roots of unity is zero. Taking real parts gives $ 1+2\left(\cos\dfrac{2\pi}{7}+\cos\dfrac{4\pi}{7}+\cos\dfrac{6\pi}{7}\right)=0 $. The answer is $\boxed{-\dfrac{7}{2}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
