draw(unitcircle);for(int k=0;k<7;++k){pair P=dir(360*k/7.0);draw((0,0)--P,dashed);dot(P);label("$\\zeta^{"+string(k)+"}$",P,1.2*P);}label("$O$",(0,0),SW);
\end{asy}

We have $\angle A=60^\circ $, $\angle ACM=10^\circ $ and $\angle ABN=20^\circ $. By the sine rule in $ ABN $, $ ACM $ and $ ABC $, \[\dfrac{AN}{AM}=\dfrac{\sin20^\circ\sin50^\circ\sin110^\circ}{\sin100^\circ\sin70^\circ\sin10^\circ}=\dfrac{\sin80^\circ}{\sin40^\circ}.\]For the last identity cancel $\sin110^\circ=\sin70^\circ $ and use $\sin20^\circ=2\sin10^\circ\cos10^\circ $, $\sin50^\circ=\cos40^\circ $ and $ 2\sin40^\circ\cos40^\circ=\sin80^\circ=\cos10^\circ $. A triangle with included angle $ 60^\circ $ and this ratio of its adjacent sides is similar, by SAS, to a triangle with other angles $ 80^\circ,40^\circ $. Thus $\angle AMN=80^\circ $. Also $\angle AMC=110^\circ $, and the two rays lie inside that angle, so $\angle NMC=110^\circ-80^\circ=\boxed{30^\circ}$.
\begin{asy}
