Failure in all $ n $ games has probability $\left(\dfrac{323}{324}\right)^n $. Thus $ n\ge\dfrac{\log(\dfrac{1}{4})}{\log(\dfrac{323}{324})}\approx448.4659 $, giving $\boxed{449}$. The following exact integer comparisons certify the rounding.
\begin{verbatim}
assert 4*323**448 > 324**448
assert 4*323**449 <= 324**449
\end{verbatim}

A composition $(a,b,c)$ with $ a+b+c=6 $ has probability $\dfrac{\binom5a\binom5b\binom8c}{\binom{18}{6}}$. Exhausting its small domain shows the maximum numerator is $ 2800 $, attained at exactly $\boxed{(2,2,2),(2,1,3),(1,2,3)}$. The exact check below supplies all candidates and compares their weights.
\begin{verbatim}
from math import comb
w={(a,b,6-a-b):comb(5,a)*comb(5,b)*comb(8,6-a-b)
   for a in range(6) for b in range(6) if 0<=6-a-b<=8}
m=max(w.values())
assert m==2800
assert {k for k,v in w.items() if v==m}=={(2,2,2),(2,1,3),(1,2,3)}
\end{verbatim}

The shared side is internal, so the exposed edge count is $ 5+6-2=9 $. At each end of the shared side the interior angle is $ 108^\circ+120^\circ=228^\circ $, not $ 180^\circ $, so no exposed edges merge into one straight side. The answer is $\boxed9 $.
