For $n\ge1$, $\dfrac{1}{(n+4)^2}<\dfrac{1}{n^2+4n+16}<\dfrac{1}{(n+2)^2}$, proving convergence by comparison. If $S_N$ is the first $N$ terms, integration of these decreasing bounds gives $\dfrac{1}{N+5}<S-S_N<\dfrac{1}{N+2}$. At $N=1000$, both bounds lie between $0.2715$ and $0.2725$, as the rational certificate verifies. Thus the rounded answer is $\boxed{0.272}$.\begin{verbatim}
from fractions import Fraction as F
s=sum((F(1,n*n+4*n+16) for n in range(1,1001)),F(0))
lo=s+F(1,1005);hi=s+F(1,1002)
assert F(2715,10000)<lo<hi<F(2725,10000)
\end{verbatim}

The signed area is half the determinant of the edge vectors, $\dfrac{1}{2}\det\begin{pmatrix}4&1\\1&5\end{pmatrix}=\dfrac{1}{2}(20-1)$. Thus the area is $\boxed{\dfrac{19}{2}}$. The shoelace computation is the same determinant in cyclic form: $\dfrac{1}{2}|0\cdot1+4\cdot5+1\cdot0-(0\cdot4+1\cdot1+5\cdot0)|$.
