Use the unique green disk to fix one vertex, eliminating rotations. The two red vertices can be chosen among five in $\binom52=10$ ways. The remaining reflection fixes the opposite vertex and swaps two vertex pairs. A two-red subset is invariant exactly when it is one of those two pairs, giving 2 fixed choices. Burnside’s lemma gives $\dfrac{10+2}{2}=\boxed6$.
