Let $O$ be the midpoint of $BD$. In each right triangle with hypotenuse $BD$, this midpoint is equidistant from all three vertices; this follows immediately by placing the legs on coordinate axes. Hence $OA=OB=OC=OD$. To derive the angle rule, join the circle centre to a chord’s endpoints and to a vertex on the circle. The resulting triangles with two radii are isosceles. Each base angle is half of 180 degrees minus its central angle; adding or subtracting the base angles shows that the vertex angle is half the opposite arc’s angular measure. Thus vertices on the same side of a chord see equal angles. Vertices $A,D$ are on the same side of chord $BC$, proving the first equality. Vertices $C,D$ are on the same side of chord $AB$, proving the second.
\begin{asy}
