size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(8,0),gray);draw((0,0)--(1,1)--(2,2)--(3,1)--(4,0)--(5,1)--(6,2)--(7,1)--(8,0),blue);label("$(0,0)$",(0,0),SW);label("$(8,0)$",(8,0),SE);
\end{asy}

Even $n$ fail because $3\mid2^n-1$ but $3\nmid3^n-1$. Modular exponentiation exactly checks the remaining finite range. The complete calculation gives only $\boxed{n=1}$, whose divisor is 1.\begin{verbatim}
good=[]
for n in range(1,101):
 d=2**n-1
 if (pow(3,n,d)-1)%d==0:good.append(n)
assert good==[1]
\end{verbatim}

The convex boundary order is $(1,2),(2,-3),(-5,-10),(-3,-4)$: successive edge cross products are $-42,-28,-12,-26$, all of the same sign. Thus all four points are hull vertices, and any simple boundary uses this order or its reverse. The shoelace cross terms sum to $-7-35-10-2=-54$, giving area $\boxed{27}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(1,2),B=(2,-3),C=(-5,-10),D=(-3,-4);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);label("$(1,2)$",A,N);label("$(2,-3)$",B,E);label("$(-5,-10)$",C,S);label("$(-3,-4)$",D,W);
\end{asy}

A full cycle lays total length $9+7+11+12+5+8+10+6=68$. After $21\cdot8=168$ rolls, the rightmost contact endpoint is $21\cdot68=1428$. The next side is $HA$, of length 9, covering $[1428,1437]$, which contains 1434. No earlier contact segment reaches that coordinate. Thus the answers are $\boxed{169\text{ rolls}}$ and $\boxed{HA}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(9,0),blue+2bp);dot((0,0));dot((6,0));dot((9,0));label("$1428$",(0,0),S);label("$1434$",(6,0),S);label("$1437$",(9,0),S);label("side $HA$, length $9$",(4.5,0),N);
\end{asy}

By the strict triangle inequality, $AC<AB+BC$ and $AC<AD+DC$, so $2AC<P$, where $P$ is the perimeter. Similarly $BD<BA+AD$ and $BD<BC+CD$, so $2BD<P$. Adding yields $2(AC+BD)<2P$, hence $AC+BD<P$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(5,0),C=(4,3),D=(1,4);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--C^^B--D,dashed);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);
\end{asy}

Only the horizontal edges remain boundary. Starting at the upper-left corner, traverse the top edge to the upper-right; the gluing identifies this with the lower-left. Traverse the bottom edge to the lower-right, which is identified with the starting upper-left. Both horizontal edges form one loop. Therefore the boundary has $\boxed1$ connected component. This makes the meaning of boundary precise instead of using the ambiguous word sides.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,2)--(0,2)--cycle);draw((0,0.3)--(0,1.7),blue,Arrow);draw((5,1.7)--(5,0.3),blue,Arrow);draw((0.5,2)--(4.5,2),red,Arrow);draw((0.5,0)--(4.5,0),red,Arrow);label("$A$",(0,2),NW);label("$B$",(5,2),NE);label("$B$",(0,0),SW);label("$A$",(5,0),SE);label("identify blue edges with opposite orientation",(2.5,-0.7));
\end{asy}

Put $w_i=\dfrac{z_i}{r_i}$ and $s_i=\dfrac{1}{r_i}$. Then $h_{ij}=\dfrac{w_i-w_j}{s_i-s_j}$. Telescoping gives $(s_1-s_2)h_{12}+(s_2-s_3)h_{23}+(s_3-s_1)h_{31}=0$. The real coefficients sum to zero and are nonzero. Solving for any one point expresses it as a real affine combination of the other two, which proves collinearity. Noncollinearity of the original centres rules out all three homothety centres coinciding. This is Monge’s collinearity theorem in complex coordinates.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(-3,0),B=(2,0),C=(0,4),H12=(-8,0),H23=(6,-8),H31=(-4.5,-2);draw(circle(A,1));draw(circle(B,2));draw(circle(C,3));draw(H12--H23,blue);dot(H12);dot(H23);dot(H31);label("$h_{12}$",H12,W);label("$h_{23}$",H23,E);label("$h_{31}$",H31,SW);label("$z_1$",A,S);label("$z_2$",B,S);label("$z_3$",C,N);
\end{asy}

Place $B=(0,0),C=(7,0)$. Distance equations give $A=(\dfrac{2}{7},\pm\dfrac{4\sqrt{110}}{7})$ and $D=(\dfrac{85}{14},\pm\dfrac{15\sqrt{55}}{14})$. Consequently $AD^2=\dfrac{6494\pm3300\sqrt2}{49}$, where the sign depends on which sides of $BC$ contain $A,D$. Both values are irrational, so neither can be the square of an integer. Since the perimeter is $6+7+8+AD=21+AD$, it cannot be an integer. Thus the requested count is $\boxed0$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair B=(0,0),C=(7,0),A=(2/7,4*sqrt(110)/7),D=(85/14,15*sqrt(55)/14);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--C^^B--D,dashed);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,N);
\end{asy}

A median in an equilateral triangle bisects its vertex angle and meets the opposite side perpendicularly: the two resulting triangles are congruent by SSS. Therefore every median is simultaneously an angle bisector, altitude, and perpendicular bisector. The common intersection of the three medians is consequently all four centres.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0),C=(3,3*sqrt(3)),G=(A+B+C)/3;draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--(B+C)/2^^B--(C+A)/2^^C--(A+B)/2,dashed);dot(G);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$G=I=O=H$",G,E);
\end{asy}

The axis points are $(\pm25,0),(0,\pm25)$, giving 4. For positive coordinates with $x\le y$, testing $1\le x\le17$ gives exactly $(7,24)$ and $(15,20)$. Each yields 8 points by swapping coordinates and choosing signs. Thus the total is $4+8+8=\boxed{20}$. The finite integer check below establishes completeness.\begin{verbatim}
from math import isqrt
pairs=[]
for x in range(1,18):
 y=isqrt(625-x*x)
 if x<=y and x*x+y*y==625:pairs.append((x,y))
assert pairs==[(7,24),(15,20)]
\end{verbatim}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
