draw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,1)--(0,1)--cycle);draw((0,1)--(0.5,1.5)--(5.5,1.5)--(5,1));draw((5,0)--(5.5,0.5)--(5.5,1.5));label("$1011$",(2.5,0),S);label("$2$",(0,0.5),W);label("$1$",(5.4,1.3),E);label("not to scale",(2.5,2));
\end{asy}

The grid is connected and bipartite, with checkerboard classes of sizes 8 and 7. Neighbours must have opposite parity; thus one entire class has even digit 2, while each cell of the other class may independently be 1 or 3. Choosing which class is 2 gives $2^8+2^7=\boxed{384}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int x=0;x<5;++x)for(int y=0;y<3;++y){path p=(x,y)--(x+1,y)--(x+1,y+1)--(x,y+1)--cycle;if((x+y)%2==0)fill(p,lightblue);draw(p);label((x+y)%2==0?"1 or 3":"2",(x+0.5,y+0.5));}
\end{asy}

In triangle $ABE$, the angles at $A,E$ are equal, so $BE=AB=54$ and $BD=54-18=36$. Let their common angle be $\theta$. Then $\angle ABD=180^\circ-2\theta$, and the angle-bisector condition gives $\angle DBC=180^\circ-2\theta$. Since $\angle BCD=\theta$, triangle $BCD$ also has $\angle BDC=\theta$, so $BC=BD=36$. The triangles $BCD$ and $BAE$ are similar with scale $\dfrac{36}{54}=\dfrac{2}{3}$. Therefore $CD=\dfrac{2}{3}\cdot AE=30$. The perimeter is $54+36+30+18+45=\boxed{183}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair B=(0,0),Ept=(54,0),D=(36,0),A=(35.25,sqrt(54^2-35.25^2)),C=(2*A.x/3,-2*A.y/3);draw(A--B--C--D--Ept--cycle);draw(B--Ept,dashed);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,S);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$E$",Ept,E);
\end{asy}

Use coordinates $A=(2,0),U=(1,\sqrt3),S=(-1,\sqrt3),T=(-2,0),I=(-1,-\sqrt3),N=(1,-\sqrt3)$. Then $M=(-\dfrac{3}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2})$. Applying the shoelace formula to the ordered vertices $M,I,N,U,S$ gives signed cross-product sum $9\sqrt3$. Hence the area is $\boxed{\dfrac{9\sqrt3}{2}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(2,0),U=(1,sqrt(3)),Spt=(-1,sqrt(3)),T=(-2,0),I=(-1,-sqrt(3)),Npt=(1,-sqrt(3)),M=(Spt+T)/2;draw(A--U--Spt--T--I--Npt--cycle);filldraw(M--I--Npt--U--Spt--cycle,lightblue,black);label("$A$",A,E);label("$U$",U,NE);label("$S$",Spt,NW);label("$T$",T,W);label("$I$",I,SW);label("$N$",Npt,SE);label("$M$",M,W);
\end{asy}

An axial section is an isosceles triangle with sides 5,5,6 and altitude 4. Its incircle radius is area divided by semiperimeter, $r_0=\dfrac{12}{8}=\dfrac{3}{2}$. The cone half-angle $\alpha$ satisfies $\sin\alpha=\dfrac{3}{5}$. A tangent sphere of radius $r$ has centre distance $\dfrac{r}{\sin\alpha}$ from the vertex. For adjacent radii $r>r'$, tangency gives $\dfrac{r-r'}{\sin\alpha}=r+r'$, so $r'=\dfrac{1}{4}r$. Volumes therefore have ratio $\dfrac{1}{64}$. Their sum is $\dfrac{\dfrac{4}{3}\pi(\dfrac{3}{2})^3}{1-\dfrac{1}{64}}=\boxed{\dfrac{32\pi}{7}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((-3,0)--(3,0)--(0,4)--cycle);for(int k=0;k<4;++k){real r=1.5/(4^k);draw(circle((0,4-5*r/3),r),blue);}draw((0,0)--(0,4),dashed);label("radius $3$",(1.5,0),S);label("height $4$",(3,2),E);label("$r_0=\dfrac{3}{2}$",(0,1.5),W);
\end{asy}

Let the inradius be $r$. The tangent lengths from the right-angle vertices $B,D$ are $r$, so those from $A,E$ are $4-r$, and those from $C$ are $9-r$. In particular $0<r<4$ and $AE=8-2r$. Equal tangent lengths at $A,E$ imply their half-angles have equal cotangents, hence their angles are equal; write this angle as $\theta$. The pentagon angle sum gives $2\theta+\angle C=360^\circ$. Set $u=\cot(\dfrac{\theta}{2})=\dfrac{4-r}{r}$. Then $\cot(\dfrac{\angle C}{2})=-\cot\theta=\dfrac{1-u^2}{2u}=\dfrac{9-r}{r}$. Clearing denominators yields $r^2-17r+44=0$. The root below 4 is $r=\dfrac{17-\sqrt{113}}{2}$. Consequently $\boxed{AE=\sqrt{113}-9}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
real r=(17-sqrt(113))/2;pair B=(0,0),C=(9,0),A=(0,4),I=(r,r),v=unit(C-I),D=I+2*dot(B-I,v)*v-(B-I),Ept=I+2*dot(A-I,v)*v-(A-I);draw(A--B--C--D--Ept--cycle);draw(circle(I,r),blue);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,NE);label("$E$",Ept,NW);label("$I$",I,N);
\end{asy}

Place the shared vertex at $ A=(0,0)$, one endpoint at $ B=(7,0)$ and the other at $ C=(5\cos60^\circ,5\sin60^\circ)=(\dfrac{5}{2},\dfrac{5\sqrt3}{2})$. Pythagoras then gives $ BC^2=(7-\dfrac{5}{2})^2+(\dfrac{5\sqrt3}{2})^2=39 $, so $\boxed{BC=\sqrt{39}}$. This is the cosine rule $ 5^2+7^2-2(5)(7)\cos60^\circ $ for a non-right triangle.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(7,0),C=(2.5,5*sqrt(3)/2),H=(2.5,0);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(C--H,dashed);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$H$",H,S);label("$5$",C/2,W);label("$7$",(3.5,0),S);
\end{asy}

A path is determined by its current cell and set of visited cells. Recursively try every unvisited neighbour, counting 1 when all nine cells have been visited. Each valid path has a unique sequence of these choices, so the recurrence counts it once and rejects only forbidden revisits. The exact certificate gives $\boxed8$ paths.
\begin{verbatim}
from functools import lru_cache
@lru_cache(None)
def count(v,mask):
    if mask==511:return 1
    x,y=divmod(v,3)
    total=0
    for dx,dy in ((1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1)):
        a,b=x+dx,y+dy
        if 0<=a<3 and 0<=b<3:
            w=3*a+b
            if not mask>>w&1:
                total+=count(w,mask|1<<w)
    return total
assert count(0,1)==8
\end{verbatim}
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int i=0;i<3;++i)for(int j=0;j<3;++j)draw((i,j)--(i+1,j)--(i+1,j+1)--(i,j+1)--cycle,gray);draw((0.5,2.5)--(1.5,2.5)--(2.5,2.5)--(2.5,1.5)--(1.5,1.5)--(0.5,1.5)--(0.5,0.5)--(1.5,0.5)--(2.5,0.5),blue,Arrow);label("start",(0.5,2.5),N);
\end{asy}

Its exterior angle is $180^\circ-179.6^\circ=0.4^\circ$. Exterior angles total $360^\circ$, so the number of sides is $\dfrac{360}{0.4}=\boxed{900}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(-2,0),B=(0,0),C=2dir(0.4);draw(A--B--C);draw(B--(2,0),dashed);label("$A$",A,S);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,N);label("exterior turn $0.4^\circ$",(0,0.7));
\end{asy}

The altitude bisects the base, so its square is $x^2-(\dfrac{x}{2})^2=\dfrac{3x^2}{4}$. The altitude is $\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}x}$, strictly less than $x$. Rotation preserves lengths: it changes direction but cannot turn the side length $x$ into the shorter altitude. Aligning an axis does not guarantee coincident endpoints.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(-1,0),B=(1,0),C=(0,sqrt(3));draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(C--(0,0),blue);label("$x$",(0,0),S);label("$x$",(0.5,sqrt(3)/2),NE);label("$h$",(0,sqrt(3)/2),W);
\end{asy}

Use coordinates 0 through 7, so the rook starts $(0,0)$, the pawn is $(7,6)$, and its forward square is $(7,5)$. Maintain exact rational probabilities at every uncaptured rook square. For each square, enumerate ray destinations, stopping at the pawn when encountered, then divide its probability equally among them. Captures accumulate as immediate success. After three rounds, a noncapture is successful precisely if its rank is 5 or its file is 7 with rank below 6. This condition includes occupying $h6$ and excludes the blocked attack from $h8$. The following complete finite computation gives $\boxed{\dfrac{390343}{1854944}}$.
\begin{verbatim}
from fractions import Fraction
from collections import defaultdict
pawn=(7,6)
D={(0,0):Fraction(1)}
win=Fraction(0)
for step in range(3):
    E=defaultdict(Fraction)
    for (x,y),p in D.items():
        moves=[]
        for dx,dy in ((1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1)):
            a,b=x+dx,y+dy
            while 0<=a<8 and 0<=b<8:
                moves.append((a,b))
                if (a,b)==pawn:break
                a+=dx
                b+=dy
        for t in moves:
            if t==pawn:win+=p/len(moves)
            else:E[t]+=p/len(moves)
    D=E
win+=sum(p for (x,y),p in D.items() if y==5 or (x==7 and y<6))
assert win==Fraction(390343,1854944)
\end{verbatim}

Put $ t=x^2\ge0 $. Substitution gives $ 4t^2+t-4=0 $, whose roots are $\dfrac{-1\pm\sqrt{65}}{8}$. Exactly one is nonnegative, namely $ t=\dfrac{\sqrt{65}-1}{8}>0 $. This gives two opposite nonzero values of $ x $ and one value $ y=2t $. Thus there are $\boxed2 $ intersections.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
import graph;draw(circle((0,0),2));real f(real x){return 2*x*x;}draw(graph(f,-1.2,1.2),blue);real t=(sqrt(65)-1)/8;dot((sqrt(t),2*t));dot((-sqrt(t),2*t));label("$y=2x^2$",(1.1,2.6),E);label("$x^2+y^2=4$",(0,-2),S);
\end{asy}

The eight roots are $\pm3,\pm4,\pm5,\pm i $. The positive roots $ 3,4,5 $ satisfy $ 3^2+4^2=5^2 $ and the triangle inequality, so they are the sides of a right triangle. Its area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot3\cdot4=\boxed6 $.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(4,0)--(0,3)--cycle);label("$4$",(2,0),S);label("$3$",(0,1.5),W);label("$5$",(2,1.5),NE);draw((0.2,0)--(0.2,0.2)--(0,0.2));
\end{asy}

Place one vertex at the origin and let its adjacent vertex vectors be $ u,v $. The fourth vertex is $ u+v $. The diagonal from $ 0 $ to $ u+v $ has midpoint $\dfrac{u+v}{2}$, which is also the midpoint of the diagonal from $ u $ to $ v $. Thus the diagonals bisect each other.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),D=(1,2),C=B+D,O=C/2;draw(A--B--C--D--cycle^^A--C^^B--D);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$O$",O,N);
\end{asy}

Use vertices $(0,0),(b,0),(u+a,h),(u,h)$ with $ a,b,h>0 $. The leg midpoints are $(\dfrac{u}{2},\dfrac{h}{2})$ and $(\dfrac{b+u+a}{2},\dfrac{h}{2})$. They have equal heights and horizontal separation $\dfrac{a+b}{2}$, establishing both claims.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0),C=(4,3),D=(1,3),M=(A+D)/2,Nn=(B+C)/2;draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(M--Nn,blue);label("$b$",(A+B)/2,S);label("$a$",(C+D)/2,N);label("$M$",M,W);label("$N$",Nn,E);
\end{asy}

Place adjacent side vectors $ u,v $ at a common vertex, so $|u|=|v|$. The diagonal direction vectors are $ u+v $ and $ u-v $. Their dot product is $(u+v)\cdot(u-v)=|u|^2-|v|^2=0 $. Since the rhombus is nondegenerate, both diagonals are nonzero and therefore perpendicular.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(-3,0),B=(0,2),C=(3,0),D=(0,-2);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--C^^B--D);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,S);
\end{asy}

Each inscribed angle equals half the measure of its intercepted arc. The arcs intercepted by a pair of opposite angles together form the entire circle, of measure $ 360^\circ $. Thus the two angles sum to $\dfrac{1}{2}(360^\circ)=180^\circ $.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=dir(20),B=dir(100),C=dir(200),D=dir(280);draw(unitcircle);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);label("$A$",A,E);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,SW);label("$D$",D,S);
\end{asy}

Let $ S,T $ be the correct counts. They are independent binomial variables. The event is $ S+2T\ge40 $. Consequently its exact probability is \[\sum_{t=5}^{8}\sum_{s=40-2t}^{30}\binom8t\left(\dfrac{3}{5}\right)^t\left(\dfrac{2}{5}\right)^{8-t}\binom{30}s\left(\dfrac{4}{5}\right)^s\left(\dfrac{1}{5}\right)^{30-s}.\]This is $\boxed{\dfrac{15511498392858393223102464}{363797880709171295166015625}}\approx0.04263768 $. The following exact computation evaluates the finite sum.
\begin{verbatim}
from fractions import Fraction as F
from math import comb
p=sum((F(comb(8,t)*3**t*2**(8-t),5**8)*
       F(comb(30,s)*4**s,5**30)
       for t in range(5,9) for s in range(40-2*t,31)),F(0))
assert p==F(15511498392858393223102464,363797880709171295166015625)
