Unique prime factorisation says that precisely the numbers $ p^aq^br^c\le1000 $ qualify, where $ p<q<r $ are primes and $ a,b,c\ge1 $. No fourth prime may divide the number. To count them without duplicates, sieve the number of distinct prime divisors of each integer. A prime adds one to the count of each of its multiples. The complete calculation below gives $\boxed{275}$.
\begin{verbatim}
omega=[0]*1001
for p in range(2,1001):
 if omega[p]==0: # no smaller prime divides p, so p is prime
  for n in range(p,1001,p):omega[n]+=1
answers=[n for n in range(1,1001) if omega[n]==3]
assert len(answers)==275
# answers is the complete increasing list
\end{verbatim}

Write $ u=10a+b $. The equation is $(10u+c)u=1000d+100c+10b+a $. Since the right side is at most $ 9999 $ and the left side is at least $ 10u^2 $, necessarily $ 10\le u\le31 $. Thus only 22 choices of $ u $, ten of $ c $, and nine of $ d $ remain. Exhausting this exact finite domain gives no solutions, as certified below. Hence the answer is $\boxed{\text{none}}$.
\begin{verbatim}
solutions=[]
for u in range(10,32):
 a,b=divmod(u,10)
 for c in range(10):
  for d in range(1,10):
   if (10*u+c)*u==1000*d+100*c+10*b+a:
    solutions.append(1000*a+100*b+10*c+d)
assert solutions==[]
\end{verbatim}

