Let triangle base and altitude be $ s,h $. At height $ y $, the horizontal cross-section has width $ s(1-\dfrac{y}{h})$ by similarity. Setting it equal to $\dfrac{s}{4}$ gives $ y=\dfrac{3h}{4}$. The rectangle area is $\dfrac{s}{4}\cdot\dfrac{3h}{4}=\dfrac{3sh}{16}=\dfrac{3}{8}\cdot40=\boxed{15\text{ cm}^2}$. Its height $\dfrac{3\sqrt3s}{8}$ exceeds its width $\dfrac{s}{4}$, so the horizontal side is indeed shorter.
