If both centres are a point $O$, its perpendicular distances to the three sides equal the inradius. The sides are chords of the same circumcircle at equal distances from its centre; a chord at distance $d$ in radius $R$ has length $2\sqrt{R^2-d^2}$, so all three sides are equal. Conversely the symmetry of an equilateral triangle makes both centres the intersection of its medians. For a nonequilateral triangle with angles $60^\circ,50^\circ,70^\circ$, the incenter still satisfies $\angle BIC=90^\circ+\dfrac{60^\circ}{2}=120^\circ$, but the centres cannot coincide by the proved criterion.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair B=(0,0),C=(1,0),A=sin(70*pi/180)/sin(60*pi/180)*dir(50);real aa=abs(B-C),bb=abs(A-C),cc=abs(A-B);pair I=(aa*A+bb*B+cc*C)/(aa+bb+cc),O=(0.5,(dot(A,A)-A.x)/(2*A.y));draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(B--I--C,blue);dot(I);dot(O);label("$A:60^\circ$",A,N);label("$B:50^\circ$",B,SW);label("$C:70^\circ$",C,SE);label("$I$",I,W);label("$O$",O,E);
\end{asy}

The second equation gives $x=4(y-\dfrac{7}{8})^2+\dfrac{95}{16}>2$. The first then implies $y-x=x^2+x-6=(x+3)(x-2)>0$, so $y>x$. But the second also gives $x-y=4(y-1)^2+5>0$, a contradiction. Thus there are $\boxed0$ intersections.
