Put $u=(a,b)$ and $v=(c,d)$, so $\det(u,v)=1$. The expression is $|u+\dfrac{v}{2}|^2+\dfrac{3}{4}|v|^2$. By AM-GM and the determinant bound $|\det(w,v)|\le|w||v|$, it is at least $\sqrt3|u+\dfrac{v}{2}||v|\ge\sqrt3|\det(u+\dfrac{v}{2},v)|=\sqrt3$. Equality is attainable: choose $v=(0,t)$ and $u=(\dfrac{1}{t},-\dfrac{t}{2})$, where $t^2=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3}$. Then the determinant is $1$, the two relevant vectors are perpendicular, and their lengths have the AM-GM equality ratio. Hence the minimum is $\boxed{\sqrt3}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair O=(0,0),V=(0,2),Wv=(sqrt(3),0),U=Wv-V/2;draw(O--V,blue,Arrow);draw(O--U,red,Arrow);draw(O--Wv,Arrow);draw(U--Wv,dashed);label("$v$",V,N);label("$u$",U,SE);label("$u+\dfrac{v}{2}$",Wv,E);label("equality configuration, scaled",(1,-1.6));
\end{asy}

Let $O$ be the square's center. The circle with diameter $AB$ contains $P$ by Thales' theorem. It also contains $O$: if $AB=s$, then $AO=BO=\dfrac{s}{\sqrt2}$, so $AO^2+BO^2=AB^2$ and $\angle AOB=90^\circ$. On this circle, $O$ is the midpoint of the arc $AB$ lying toward the square, since $AO=BO$. Thus $\angle APO=\angle OPB$ as inscribed angles subtending equal arcs. So the angle-bisector line passes through $O$. A half-turn about the center of a square preserves the square and exchanges the two half-planes of any line through its center, so that line cuts its area into equal halves.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(4,4),D=(0,4),O=(2,2),P=(2+2cos(240*pi/180),2sin(240*pi/180));draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(circle((2,0),2),dashed);draw(A--P--B);draw(P--(O+2*(O-P)),blue);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$O$",O,E);label("$P$",P,S);
\end{asy}

Write the angles as $x,x+d,x+2d$. Their sum is $180^\circ$, so $x+d=60^\circ$. The largest is $x+2(60-x)=\boxed{120-x}$ degrees. The possible range is $0<x\le60$.

