A finite exhaustive check of $a=2,3,\ldots,3335$ gives the first success $a=3335$, with $9997a=33339995$, whose digits are all odd. The digit sum of $a$ is $3+3+3+5=\boxed{14}$. The following exact certificate proves minimality by testing every smaller admissible positive integer.
\begin{verbatim}
def odd_digits(n):return all(int(c)%2==1 for c in str(n))
assert all(not odd_digits(9997*a) for a in range(2,3335))
assert 9997*3335==33339995 and odd_digits(9997*3335)
assert sum(map(int,str(3335)))==14
\end{verbatim}

First, for two reals $u,v$, $(u+v)^2=u^2+2uv+v^2\le u^2+2|u||v|+v^2=(|u|+|v|)^2$. Both absolute-value sides are nonnegative, so $|u+v|\le|u|+|v|$. The general statement follows by induction: apply this two-term inequality to $u=x_1+\cdots+x_{n-1}$ and $v=x_n$, then apply the induction hypothesis to $|u|$. The case $n=1$ is equality.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((-.5,0)--(4,0),Arrow);draw((0,.3)--(3,.3),blue,Arrow);draw((3,.7)--(2,.7),red,Arrow);draw((0,-.4)--(2,-.4),Arrow);label("$u=3$",(1.5,.3),N);label("$v=-1$",(2.5,.7),N);label("$u+v=2$",(1,-.4),S);label("$0$",(0,0),S);label("illustration",(3.5,-.8));
\end{asy}

Let $e_i\in\{0,1\}$ be the parity of the number of uses of the edge joining coin $i$ to coin $i+1$, with indices modulo $8$. The final tail indicator at coin $i$ is $e_{i-1}+e_i$ modulo $2$. For each of the two alternating target patterns, choosing $e_0$ determines all other parities recursively; the two choices both close consistently around the circle. Altogether there are four edge-parity patterns, each with exactly four odd edges (for example $00110011$ and its complement for one target). To realize one such pattern in six moves, first assign one move to each odd edge, leaving two extra moves. Either one of the four odd edges is used three times, giving $4\cdot\dfrac{6!}{3!}=480$ sequences, or one of the four even edges is used twice, giving $4\cdot\dfrac{6!}{2!}=1440$. Thus each pattern gives $1920$ sequences, and the total is $\boxed{7680}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair[] P;for(int i=0;i<8;++i)P.push(2dir(90-45i));for(int i=0;i<8;++i){draw(P[i]--P[(i+1)%8]);filldraw(circle(P[i],.25),(i%2==0)?white:lightgray);label((i%2==0)?"H":"T",P[i]);}label("one alternating target",(0,-2.8));
\end{asy}

The graph is connected: a component with $k$ vertices would require $k-1\ge\dfrac{n}{2}$, so two components would together contain more than $n$ vertices. Choose a longest simple path $v_1,\ldots,v_k$. Every neighbor of either endpoint lies on the path, or the path could be extended. Among indices $i=1,\ldots,k-1$, at least one has both edges $v_1v_{i+1}$ and $v_iv_k$: otherwise the two sets of such indices would be disjoint, implying $\deg(v_1)+\deg(v_k)\le k-1$, contradicting their sum at least $n\ge k$. These edges close a cycle through all path vertices, traversed as $v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_i,v_k,v_{k-1},\ldots,v_{i+1},v_1$. If $k<n$, connectedness supplies an edge from a vertex outside this cycle to a vertex on it. Starting at that outside vertex and going around the cycle produces a path with $k+1$ vertices, contradicting maximality. Thus $k=n$ and the cycle is Hamiltonian.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair[] V;for(int i=0;i<6;++i)V.push(dir(90-60i));for(int i=0;i<6;++i){draw(V[i]--V[(i+1)%6],blue);dot(V[i]);label("$v_{"+string(i+1)+"}$",V[i],unit(V[i]));}draw(V[0]--V[3],dashed);draw(V[1]--V[4],dashed);draw(V[2]--V[5],dashed);label("example with degree 3",(0,-1.5));
\end{asy}

Put $r=\dfrac{AX}{BX}>0$. Dividing the condition by $BX^2$ gives $r^2=r+1$. The positive root is $\boxed{\dfrac{1+\sqrt5}{2}}$.
