size(7cm);
real r=(1+sqrt(5))/2;pair A=(0,0),X=(r,0),B=(r+1,0);draw(A--B);dot(A);dot(X);dot(B);label("$A$",A,S);label("$X$",X,S);label("$B$",B,S);label("$r$",(r/2,0),N);label("$1$",((2r+1)/2,0),N);
\end{asy}

Suppose no triple works. For each consecutive triple, let $M$ be its largest entry. Failure of the strict triangle inequality means the sum of that triple is at most $2M$. Summing over all $15$ triples, every entry is counted three times, so the left side is $3(4+5+\cdots+18)=3\cdot165=495$. Each entry can be the largest in at most the three triples containing it. Hence the sum of the fifteen maxima is at most three times the sum of the five largest entries: $3(14+15+16+17+18)=240$. The assumed inequalities would give $495\le2\cdot240=480$, a contradiction. Therefore a valid triple exists.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0),C=(3.75,sqrt(25-3.75^2));draw(A--B--C--cycle);label("$a_k$",(A+B)/2,S);label("$a_{k+1}$",(B+C)/2,NE);label("$a_{k+2}$",(C+A)/2,NW);label("a successful consecutive triple",(3,-1));
\end{asy}

The bounds give a finite domain of $98\cdot99$ pairs. For each pair, first require divisibility of the numerator by the positive denominator, then test whether the quotient is a prime square. The following exact computation checks every pair and finds none. Thus $\boxed{\text{there is no such prime under the stated bounds}}$.
\begin{verbatim}
from math import isqrt
sol=[]
for x in range(3,101):
    for y in range(2,101):
        a,b=x**3-8,y**3-1
        if a%b:continue
        q=a//b
        p=isqrt(q)
        if p*p==q and p>1 and all(p%d for d in range(2,isqrt(p)+1)):
            sol.append((p,x,y))
assert sol==[]
\end{verbatim}

Let its legs be $a,b$ and hypotenuse $c$. The area gives $ab=40$, and the perimeter gives $a+b=32-c$. By Pythagoras, $(a+b)^2=c^2+2ab=c^2+80$. Thus $(32-c)^2=c^2+80$, so $64c=944$ and $\boxed{c=\dfrac{59}{4}}$. This is feasible: $a+b=\dfrac{69}{4}$ and $ab=40$ give positive roots $\dfrac{69\pm\sqrt{2201}}{8}$, whose squared sum is $c^2$.
