\end{asy}

For each row, its three distinct entries have all $3!=6$ relative orders equally likely. The row-order events are independent: after choosing which numbers belong to each row, the three within-row permutations are independent and uniform. Thus the number of increasing rows is binomial with success probability $\dfrac{1}{6}$. The required probability is $\binom32(\dfrac{1}{6})^2\dfrac{5}{6}+(\dfrac{1}{6})^3=\boxed{\dfrac{2}{27}}$.
\begin{asy}
