Place the original centroid at the origin and let its vertex vectors be $a,b,c$, with $a+b+c=0$. The median from vertex $a$ has vector $-\dfrac{3}{2}a$ and length $\dfrac{3}{2}|a|$, and similarly for the others. The vectors $a,b,c$ close to form a nondegenerate triangle with vertices $0,a,a+b$. Its area is $\dfrac{1}{2}|a\times b|$. The original triangle has area $\dfrac{1}{2}|(b-a)\times(c-a)|=\dfrac{3}{2}|a\times b|$, so the auxiliary triangle has one third of the original area. Scaling it by $\dfrac{3}{2}$ makes its side lengths equal to the three medians, multiplying area by $\dfrac{9}{4}$. The final ratio is therefore $\dfrac{1}{3}\cdot\dfrac{9}{4}=\boxed{\dfrac{3}{4}}$.
