(a) The pyramid apices are $(\pm2,0,0),(0,\pm2,0),(0,0,\pm2)$. Every edge of the resulting rhombic dodecahedron joins such an apex to a cube corner at distance $\sqrt{1+1+1}=\sqrt3$. Adjacent triangular pyramid faces sharing a cube edge are coplanar and form one rhombic face. The volume is $8+6\left(\dfrac{1}{3}\cdot4\cdot1\right)=\boxed{16}$. (b) Scaling the edge from $\sqrt3$ to $n$ gives volume $16\left(\dfrac{n}{\sqrt3}\right)^3=\dfrac{16n^3}{3\sqrt3}$. The determinant of the three displayed vectors has absolute value $\dfrac{4}{3\sqrt3}$, which is the volume of one parallelepiped. Dividing volumes shows that any such tiling contains $\boxed{4n^3}$ tiles. This is a necessary tile count under the stated tiling assumption, not a proof of tiling existence.
