\end{asy}

If $O$ is the centre and $AB$ a chord, then $OA=OB$ because both are radii. The locus of points equidistant from $A,B$ is their perpendicular bisector, so $O$ lies on it. The same holds for a second chord $CD$. Since the chords are nonparallel, their perpendicular bisectors are also nonparallel and have exactly one intersection. Both contain $O$, so that intersection is $O$.
