Use coordinates $A=(0,0)$, $B=(1,0)$, $C=(1,1)$, $D=(0,1)$, $X=\left(\dfrac{1}{2},-h\right)$ and $Y=\left(\dfrac{1}{2},1+h\right)$, where $h=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$. At height $y$ in $[0,1]$, the left boundary of the intersection is $\dfrac{\max(y,1-y)}{2(1+h)}$; its right boundary is the reflection in $x=\dfrac{1}{2}$. Thus its width at heights 0 and 1 is $1-\dfrac{1}{1+h}$ and its width at height $\dfrac{1}{2}$ is $1-\dfrac{1}{2(1+h)}$. The two halves are trapezoids of height $\dfrac{1}{2}$, so their total area is $1-\dfrac{3}{4(1+h)}=\boxed{\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}-2}$.
