(a) Three vertices of an equilateral triangle work. Any four points either form a convex quadrilateral, which has an interior angle at least $90^\circ$, or have one point inside the triangle of the other three. In the latter case the three angles around the interior point sum to $360^\circ$, so one is at least $120^\circ$; because the point is strictly inside the triangle, each consecutive angle is less than $180^\circ$ and is a triangle angle. Either case produces a triangle that is not acute. Thus the maximum is $\boxed{3}$. (b) Yes. Choose $(i,i^2)$ for $i=1,\ldots,n$. No three lie on a line because a line meets a parabola in at most two points. For $i<j<k$, the dot product of vectors from the middle point to the outer points is $(i-j)(k-j)\bigl[1+(i+j)(k+j)\bigr]<0$. Thus the angle at $(j,j^2)$ is obtuse. This works for every $n$.
