Let $P=(t,t^2)$, $A=(0,1)$ and $B=(14,46)$. The expression is $PA+PB$, which by the triangle inequality is at least $AB=\sqrt{14^2+45^2}=\sqrt{2221}$. Equality holds if $P$ lies on segment $AB$. Its line has equation $y=1+\dfrac{45}{14}x$. Intersecting with $y=x^2$ gives $14t^2-45t-14=0$, whose roots are $\dfrac{7}{2}$ and $-\dfrac{2}{7}$. The first lies between 0 and 14 and hence on the segment. Therefore the minimum is $\boxed{\sqrt{2221}}$, attained at $t=\dfrac{7}{2}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
import graph; pair A=(0,1),B=(14,46),P=(3.5,12.25); real f(real x){return x*x;}draw(graph(f,-.5,7),blue);draw(A--B);dot(A);dot(B);dot(P);label("$A=(0,1)$",A,W);label("$B=(14,46)$",B,E);label("$P$",P,E);label("$y=x^2$",(6,36),W);
\end{asy}

Apply Burnside's lemma to the four rotations. The identity fixes $5^4$ colourings. A half-turn pairs opposite cells and fixes $5^2$. Each quarter-turn requires all four cells to match and fixes 5. Thus the number of orbits is $\boxed{\dfrac{5^4+5^2+5+5}{4}=165}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,2)--(0,2)--cycle);draw((1,0)--(1,2));draw((0,1)--(2,1));label("$a$",(.5,1.5));label("$b$",(1.5,1.5));label("$d$",(.5,.5));label("$c$",(1.5,.5));label("Quarter-turn cycle: a,b,c,d",(1,-.5));
\end{asy}

All right triangles sharing an acute angle are similar. Their ratios of corresponding sides are therefore equal, but a dilation can multiply every side length by any positive factor. For example, one $30^\circ$ right triangle has opposite leg 1 and hypotenuse 2; scaling it by 5 gives opposite leg 5 and hypotenuse 10. Both have $\sin30^\circ=\dfrac{1}{2}$, despite different lengths.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(sqrt(3),0),C=(sqrt(3),1);draw(A--B--C--cycle);label("$30^\circ$",A+(0.5,.12));label("$1$",(B+C)/2,E);label("$2$",(A+C)/2,NW);pair D=(3,0),Ept=(3+2*sqrt(3),0),F=(3+2*sqrt(3),2);draw(D--Ept--F--cycle);label("$30^\circ$",D+(.6,.15));label("$5$",(Ept+F)/2,E);label("$10$",(D+F)/2,NW);label("Not drawn to a common scale",(3,-.6));
\end{asy}

Use coordinates in which $(u,v)$ represents the ordinary point $(u,\sqrt3v)$. Then $A=(1,0)$, $C=\left(-\dfrac{1}{2},\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$, $E=\left(-\dfrac{1}{2},-\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$, $X=\left(\dfrac{3}{4},\dfrac{1}{4}\right)$, $Y=\left(-\dfrac{3}{4},\dfrac{1}{4}\right)$ and $Z=\left(0,-\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$. Solving the relevant pairs of side-line equations gives the intersection hexagon in cyclic order as $\left(\dfrac{5}{8},\dfrac{1}{8}\right)$, $\left(\dfrac{1}{4},\dfrac{1}{4}\right)$, $\left(-\dfrac{1}{2},\dfrac{1}{4}\right)$, $\left(-\dfrac{1}{2},0\right)$, $\left(-\dfrac{1}{8},-\dfrac{3}{8}\right)$, $\left(\dfrac{1}{4},-\dfrac{1}{4}\right)$. The shoelace formula gives area $\dfrac{15}{32}$ in these coordinates. The vertical coordinate was compressed by a factor $\sqrt3$, so the actual area is $\boxed{\dfrac{15\sqrt3}{32}}$.
