Scale coordinates to $B=(0,0),A=(0,3),C=(4,0),M=(2,0)$. Write $t=\dfrac{BN}{NA}>0$, so $N=(0,\dfrac{3t}{1+t})$. Intersecting $AM$ and $CN$ gives $P=(\dfrac{4}{t+2},\dfrac{3t}{t+2})$, from which $\dfrac{AP}{PM}=\dfrac{2}{t}$. Therefore the stated product is $(\dfrac{1}{2})t(\dfrac{2}{t})=1$ for every permitted $N$: it supplies no additional constraint. The line $AC$ has equation $\dfrac{x}{4}+\dfrac{y}{3}=1$. Its left side at $P$ is $\dfrac{1+t}{t+2}$, so $\dfrac{BP}{BQ}=\dfrac{1+t}{2+t}=1-\dfrac{1}{2+t}$. As $t$ ranges over positive reals, this ranges over $\boxed{(\dfrac{1}{2},1)}$, with every value attained. It is not uniquely determined.
