There are $\binom{10}{3}=120 $ triples. In each of the three lattice directions, one line contains 4 points and another contains 3, yielding $\binom43+\binom33=5 $ collinear triples. There are no further collinear triples: in lattice coordinates $(i,j)$ with $ i,j\ge0,i+j\le3 $, three collinear integer points would have endpoints differing by twice a primitive integer direction. The only such directions fitting this triangle are $(1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)$. Thus $ 15 $ triples are degenerate and the answer is $\boxed{105}$.
