In a unit square, a horizontal segment joining the left and right sides has length 1, while a diagonal joining opposite vertices has length $\sqrt2 $. Even avoiding vertices, the segment from $(0,\dfrac{1}{4})$ to $(1,\dfrac{3}{4})$ has length $\dfrac{\sqrt5}{2}>1 $. Thus the general claim is false. Segments perpendicular to the same pair of opposite sides all have length equal to the side length, because that is the distance between the parallel side lines.
