The triples $(V,E,F)$ are tetrahedron $(4,6,4)$, cube $(8,12,6)$, octahedron $(6,12,8)$, dodecahedron $(20,30,12)$, and icosahedron $(12,30,20)$. Each gives $ 2 $. For the general proof, project the boundary graph of a convex polyhedron onto the plane after choosing one face as the outside face. While a cycle remains, remove one cycle edge: it merges two faces and decreases both $ E $ and $ F $ by one, preserving $ V-E+F $ and connectivity. The final graph is a tree, with $ E=V-1 $ and one face, so its value is $ V-(V-1)+1=2 $. Therefore the original value is also 2.
