The final health is $H=1000-n+\lfloor\dfrac{(n-m)^3}{420}\rfloor$. All 10000 ordered pairs are equally likely. Since $1000-n$ is an integer, $H>0$ is equivalent to $(n-m)^3\ge420(n-999)$. The exact enumeration below counts 2681 successful pairs, giving $\boxed{\dfrac{2681}{10000}}$. The first hit alone never eliminates the character, because $1000-n\ge900$.
\begin{verbatim}
count=sum((n-m)**3>=420*(n-999)
          for n in range(1,101) for m in range(101,201))
assert count==2681
\end{verbatim}

The prism’s four vertical faces have area $4(6)(3)=72$. The pyramid’s slant height is $\sqrt{4^2+3^2}=5$, so its four triangular faces total $\dfrac{4(6)(5)}{2}=60$. The shared interface has area 36 and is counted once as specified. Total frosted area is $\boxed{72+60+36=168}$ square units.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0),C=(8,2),D=(2,2),u=(0,3),T=(4,8);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--A+u^^B--B+u^^C--C+u^^D--D+u);draw(A+u--B+u--C+u--D+u--cycle);draw(T--A+u^^T--B+u^^T--C+u^^T--D+u);label("$6$",(3,0),S);label("$3$",(0,1.5),W);label("pyramid height 4",(4,7),E);
\end{asy}

Put $OP=p$. The ratio equation is $\dfrac{p-a}{d-p}=\dfrac{p-b}{c-p}$. Cross multiplication and cancellation give $p(b+d-a-c)=bd-ac$, hence $\boxed{p=\dfrac{bd-ac}{b+d-a-c}}$. The denominator is $(b-a)+(d-c)>0$. Moreover $p-b=\dfrac{(b-a)(c-b)}{b+d-a-c}>0$ and $c-p=\dfrac{(d-c)(c-b)}{b+d-a-c}>0$, verifying the required location and nonzero denominators.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(8,0));real[] x={0,1,3,19/5,5,8};string[] s={"O","A","B","P","C","D"};for(int i=0;i<6;++i){dot((x[i],0));label("$"+s[i]+"$",(x[i],0),S);}
\end{asy}

No. A nondegenerate triangle requires the sum of any two sides to exceed the third, but $3+4=7$. These lengths describe only a degenerate collinear configuration.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(7,0));dot((3,0));label("$3$",(1.5,0),N);label("$4$",(5,0),N);label("total length 7",(3.5,0),S);
\end{asy}

The linear scale factor is $\dfrac{3}{4}$, so the regular box’s surface area is $\dfrac{9}{16}$ of the larger box’s. Relative to the regular box, the increase is $(\dfrac{16}{9}-1)100\%= \boxed{\dfrac{700}{9}\%}$, or $77\dfrac{7}{9}$ percent.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,3)--(0,3)--cycle);draw((4,0)--(5.5,0)--(5.5,2.25)--(4,2.25)--cycle);label("linear scale 1",(1,-0.5));label("linear scale $\dfrac{3}{4}$",(4.75,-0.5));
\end{asy}

The twice-applied permutation has cycles $(A\,E)(B\,C)(D\,F\,G)$. The two 2-cycles can be obtained by squaring either of the two 4-cycles interleaving their letters; the 3-cycle has the unique square root $(D\,G\,F)$ on its letters. A square root commutes with its square, so it preserves the union of the two 2-cycles and the unique 3-cycle; thus these exhaust the possibilities. The resulting strings are $\boxed{\text{BEAGCDF},\ \text{CAEGBDF}}$. The following direct check over all permutations also proves completeness.
\begin{verbatim}
from itertools import permutations
names="ABCDEFG";sol=[]
for p in permutations(range(7)):
 if "".join(names[p[p[i]]] for i in range(7))=="ECBFAGD":
  sol.append("".join(names[x] for x in p))
assert sol==["BEAGCDF","CAEGBDF"]
\end{verbatim}

There are $n+1$ horizontal grid lines, each containing $n$ unit edges, giving $n(n+1)$ horizontal edges. There are equally many vertical edges. Thus the total is $\boxed{2n(n+1)}$. Shared edges of adjacent squares are counted only once.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int i=0;i<=4;++i){draw((0,i)--(4,i));draw((i,0)--(i,4));}label("$n=4$ illustration",(2,-0.6));
\end{asy}

The midpoint condition gives $GW=2GK=\dfrac{4y}{5}$. Hence $4y=135-50y$, so $y=\dfrac{5}{2}$. Therefore $GW=\dfrac{4(\dfrac{5}{2})}{5}=\boxed2$. The value $\dfrac{5}{2}$ is $y$, not the requested length.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((0,0)--(4,0));dot((2,0));label("$G$",(0,0),S);label("$K$",(2,0),S);label("$W$",(4,0),S);
\end{asy}

(a) Each layer holds $24\cdot8=192$ cans, and three layers fit, giving $\boxed{576}$. (b) Use nine staggered rows per layer, alternately containing 24 and 23 cans. Adjacent rows have centre separation $\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}$ vertically and offset $\dfrac{3}{2}$ horizontally, so nearest centres are 3 inches apart. Total width is $3+8(\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2})=3+12\sqrt3<24$, while each long row spans 72 inches. Each layer holds $5(24)+4(23)=212$ cans; three layers hold $\boxed{636}$. This disproves 576 as an unrestricted maximum.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
for(int j=0;j<9;++j)for(int i=0;i<(j%2==0?24:23);++i)draw(circle((1.5+3*i+(j%2)*1.5,1.5+j*1.5*sqrt(3)),1.5));draw((0,0)--(72,0)--(72,24)--(0,24)--cycle);
label("$72$ inches",(36,0),S);label("$24$ inches",(72,12),E);
\end{asy}

Joining the centre to the five vertices divides it into five triangles of base 1 and altitude equal to the apothem $a$. Bisecting one gives $a=\dfrac{1}{2}\cot36^\circ$. Thus area is $\dfrac{5}{4}\cot36^\circ$. The regular-pentagon diagonal ratio is $\phi=\dfrac{1+\sqrt5}{2}$ (applying Ptolemy’s theorem to four consecutive vertices gives $\phi^2=1+\phi$, since their four boundary lengths are $1,1,1,\phi$ and both diagonals have length $\phi$), and the isosceles triangle of two sides and a diagonal gives $\cos36^\circ=\dfrac{\phi}{2}$. Therefore $\cot^2 36^\circ=\dfrac{5+2\sqrt5}{5}$, yielding $\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt{25+10\sqrt5}}{4}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair[] V;real R=1/(2*sin(pi/5));for(int i=0;i<5;++i)V.push(R*dir(90+72*i));for(int i=0;i<5;++i){draw(V[i]--V[(i+1)%5]);draw((0,0)--V[i],gray);}pair M=(V[0]+V[1])/2;draw((0,0)--M,blue);label("$a$",M/2,NW);label("$1$",M,NW);
\end{asy}

Checking the finite range gives $\boxed{(1,1,1)}$ and the three permutations of $\boxed{(-5,4,4)}$. Directly, $1+1+1=3$ and $-125+64+64=3$. To prove completeness in the stated range, store all allowed cubes and, for each ordered pair $x,y$, look up the required third cube $3-x^3-y^3$:
\begin{verbatim}
cubes={z**3:z for z in range(-100,101)}
sol=[]
for x in range(-100,101):
 for y in range(-100,101):
  z=cubes.get(3-x**3-y**3)
  if z is not None:sol.append((x,y,z))
assert sorted(sol)==[(-5,4,4),(1,1,1),(4,-5,4),(4,4,-5)]
\end{verbatim}

The common height is $ h=2r $. The sphere contains $\dfrac{4}{3}\pi r^3 $, and the cone contains $\dfrac{1}{3}\pi r^2(2r)=\dfrac{2}{3}\pi r^3 $. Their sum is $ 2\pi r^3 $, exactly the cylinder volume $\pi r^2(2r)$. Thus the cylinder is $\boxed{100\%}$ full.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw(circle((0,1),1));draw((-1,1)--(1,1),dashed);label("sphere",(0,-.4));label("$r$",(.5,1),N);draw((3,0)--(5,0)--(4,2)--cycle);draw((4,0)--(4,2),dashed);label("cone",(4,-.4));label("$2r$",(4,1),E);draw((7,0)--(9,0)--(9,2)--(7,2)--cycle);draw((8,0)--(9,0),blue);label("cylinder",(8,-.4));label("$r$",(8.5,0),S);label("$2r$",(9,1),E);label("axial cross-sections",(4,2.6));
\end{asy}

Expanding gives $\sum_i(x_{i+1}+x_i)(y_{i+1}-y_i)=\sum_i(x_{i+1}y_{i+1}-x_iy_i)+\sum_i(x_iy_{i+1}-x_{i+1}y_i)$. The first sum is zero by cyclic reindexing. Thus the two signed sums are equal. The unsigned area is half the absolute value of either sum. Without absolute value, the shoelace sum gives positive area for counterclockwise order and negative area for clockwise order. Therefore an absolute value may be omitted only with the appropriate orientation assumption.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair[] V={(0,0),(3,0),(2,2),(0,1)};draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--cycle);for(int i=0;i<4;++i)label(string(i+1),V[i],dir(90*i+225));draw((0.7,0)--(1.5,0),Arrow);label("counterclockwise order",(1.5,-0.6));
\end{asy}

(a) The divisor involution $d\mapsto \dfrac{n^2}{d}$ pairs divisors below $n$ with those above it, leaving only $n$ fixed. There are $(28+1)(34+1)(6+1)(4+1)=35525$ divisors, so the answer is $\boxed{17762}$. (b) Similarly $n^4$ has $(56+1)(68+1)(12+1)(8+1)=460161$ divisors, with sole fixed divisor $n^2$, giving $\boxed{230080}$. (c) The cutoff $n^2$ is not the square root of $n^3$, so the same halving argument does not apply. Every divisor is uniquely $2^a3^b5^c7^d$ with $0\le a\le42,0\le b\le51,0\le c\le9,0\le d\le6$. Exact enumeration of this finite domain gives $\boxed{126480}$ below $n^2$.
\begin{verbatim}
n=2**14*3**17*5**3*7**2
count=sum(2**a*3**b*5**c*7**d<n*n
          for a in range(43) for b in range(52)
          for c in range(10) for d in range(7))
assert count==126480
\end{verbatim}

Take a cube of side 6 and divide it into eight cubes of side 3. Leave five of these intact. Subdivide each of the other three into 27 unit cubes. In two of those three blocks, keep one corner $2\times2\times2$ block as a single side 2 cube rather than eight unit cubes. Each of these two side 3 blocks now contains $27-8+1=20$ cubes; the third contains 27. The total is $5+20+20+27=\boxed{72}$. Equivalently, the final pieces are five side 3 cubes, two side 2 cubes, and 65 unit cubes; their volumes total $5(27)+2(8)+65=216=6^3$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair pr(real x,real y,real z){return (x+0.45*y,0.35*y+z);}for(int j=0;j<=2;++j){real a=3*j;draw(pr(a,0,0)--pr(a,6,0)--pr(a,6,6)--pr(a,0,6)--cycle,gray);draw(pr(0,a,0)--pr(6,a,0)--pr(6,a,6)--pr(0,a,6)--cycle,gray);draw(pr(0,0,a)--pr(6,0,a)--pr(6,6,a)--pr(0,6,a)--cycle,gray);}label("eight initial side-3 blocks",(4,-1));
\end{asy}

If the edge length is $s>0$, then $6s^2=216$. Hence $s^2=36$, so $s=6$. Its volume is $s^3=\boxed{216\text{ cubic units}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(5.2,1.2),D=(1.2,1.2),H=(0,3); pair F=B+H,G=C+H,J=D+H; draw(A--B--C--G--J--H--cycle); draw(B--F--G); draw(H--F); draw(A--D--C,dashed); draw(D--J,dashed); label("$6$",(A+B)/2,S); label("$6$",(B+C)/2,SE); label("$6$",H/2,W);
\end{asy}

The three distinct face areas are $2\cdot5=10$, $5\cdot3=15$, and $2\cdot3=6$. Each occurs twice, so the surface area is $2(10+15+6)=\boxed{62\text{ square units}}$. The volume is $2\cdot5\cdot3=\boxed{30\text{ cubic units}}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(5.2,1.2),D=(1.2,1.2),H=(0,3); pair F=B+H,G=C+H,J=D+H; draw(A--B--C--G--J--H--cycle); draw(B--F--G); draw(H--F); draw(A--D--C,dashed); draw(D--J,dashed); label("$2$",(A+B)/2,S); label("$5$",(B+C)/2,SE); label("$3$",H/2,W);
\end{asy}

Equating the powers gives\[x^2+y^2-9=(x-4)^2+y^2-4=x^2+y^2-8x+12.\] Thus $8x=21$. The radical axis is $\boxed{x=\dfrac{21}{8}}$, a vertical line. The centers $(0,0)$ and $(4,0)$ lie on a horizontal line, so the radical axis is perpendicular to their line of centers.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair Oa=(0,0),Ob=(4,0); draw(circle(Oa,3)); draw(circle(Ob,2)); draw((-3.5,0)--(6.5,0),dashed); draw((21.0/8,-3.5)--(21.0/8,3.5),blue); dot(Oa); dot(Ob); label("$O_1=(0,0)$",Oa,SW); label("$O_2=(4,0)$",Ob,SE); label("$x=\dfrac{21}{8}$",(21.0/8,3.5),N);
