If the inner radius is $r$, then $\pi(100-r^2)=64\pi$, giving $r=6$. The perpendicular distance from the common centre to the chord is 6. The radius 10 and half-chord form a right triangle, so the half-chord is $\sqrt{100-36}=8$. The chord length is $\boxed{16}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw(circle((0,0),10));draw(circle((0,0),6));draw((-8,6)--(8,6),blue);draw((0,0)--(0,6)--(8,6)--cycle,dashed);label("$6$",(0,3),W);label("$10$",(4,3),SE);
\end{asy}

Number the positions clockwise modulo $N$, with Fabian at $0$. Marco is at $6$, and Julio is at $10\pmod N$. Julio is also at $-8\pmod N$, so $N$ divides $18$. Since an eighth other child is counted, $N\ge9$, leaving $N=9$ or $18$. For $N=9$, Julio is at $1$; clockwise he reaches Marco after $5$ steps and Fabian after $8$, contrary to the condition. For $N=18$, Julio is at $10$ and reaches Fabian after $8$ steps, then Marco after $14$. Thus $\boxed{18}$ children satisfy all conditions.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw(circle((0,0),3)); for(int i=0;i<18;++i) dot(3*dir(90-20*i)); label("Fabian",3*dir(90),N); label("Marco",3*dir(-30),SE); label("Julio",3*dir(-110),SW); draw(reverse(arc((0,0),3.5,40,80)),Arrow); label("clockwise",(3.8,2));
\end{asy}

Writing the radius as $r$, $PO=r+2$ and $OT=r$. The right triangle $PTO$ gives $r^2+16=(r+2)^2$, so $\boxed{r=3}$. The tangent–secant theorem instead uses the whole secant to the far intersection $C$, which has length $PC=2+2r$, not $r+2=PO$. Its correct equation is $16=2(2+2r)$, giving the same radius.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair O=(0,0),P=(5,0),B=(3,0),C=(-3,0),T=(1.8,2.4);draw(circle(O,3));draw(C--P--T--O);label("$O$",O,S);label("$P$",P,E);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,W);label("$T$",T,N);label("$4$",(P+T)/2,NE);label("$2$",(P+B)/2,S);
\end{asy}

The lines have parallel directions exactly when their normal vectors $(A_1,B_1)$ and $(A_2,B_2)$ are proportional. In two dimensions this is equivalent to $\boxed{A_1B_2-A_2B_1=0}$. This also handles vertical lines. In the example, $3(-4)-6(-2)=0$, so the directions are parallel. Doubling the first equation gives $6x-4y=14$, which differs from the second, so the lines are distinct and parallel.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
draw((-1,-5)--(3,1));draw((-1,-1.75)--(3,4.25));label("$3x-2y=7$",(3,1),E);label("$6x-4y=1$",(3,4.25),E);
\end{asy}

We have $\overrightarrow{QP}=(-2,-3)$ and $\overrightarrow{QR}=(-10,-15)=5\overrightarrow{QP}$. Thus $QP$ is one fifth of $QR$, and $PR$ is the remaining four fifths. The ratios are $\boxed{QP:PR=1:4}$ and $\boxed{QP:QR=1:5}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
pair Q=(1,7),P=(-1,4),R=(-9,-8);draw(Q--R);dot(P);label("$Q$",Q,NE);label("$P$",P,E);label("$R$",R,SW);
\end{asy}

question:
Four congruent shaded rectangles are placed around a white square, pinwheel fashion, so that together they form a larger square, as shown. The white square has area $36$, and each shaded rectangle has area $216$. What is the perimeter of one shaded rectangle?
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill((0,0)--(18,0)--(18,12)--(0,12)--cycle,gray(0.8));
fill((18,0)--(30,0)--(30,18)--(18,18)--cycle,gray(0.8));
fill((12,18)--(30,18)--(30,30)--(12,30)--cycle,gray(0.8));
fill((0,12)--(12,12)--(12,30)--(0,30)--cycle,gray(0.8));
draw((0,0)--(18,0)--(18,12)--(0,12)--cycle);
draw((18,0)--(30,0)--(30,18)--(18,18)--cycle);
draw((12,18)--(30,18)--(30,30)--(12,30)--cycle);
draw((0,12)--(12,12)--(12,30)--(0,30)--cycle);
draw((0,0)--(30,0)--(30,30)--(0,30)--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((12,12)--(18,12)--(18,18)--(12,18)--cycle);
\end{asy}

solution:
Let each rectangle have length $L$ and width $W$ with $L>W$. In the pinwheel, one side of the outer square consists of a length and a width, and a side of the white square is the difference $L-W$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill((0,0)--(18,0)--(18,12)--(0,12)--cycle,gray(0.8));
fill((18,0)--(30,0)--(30,18)--(18,18)--cycle,gray(0.8));
fill((12,18)--(30,18)--(30,30)--(12,30)--cycle,gray(0.8));
fill((0,12)--(12,12)--(12,30)--(0,30)--cycle,gray(0.8));
draw((0,0)--(18,0)--(18,12)--(0,12)--cycle);
draw((18,0)--(30,0)--(30,18)--(18,18)--cycle);
draw((12,18)--(30,18)--(30,30)--(12,30)--cycle);
draw((0,12)--(12,12)--(12,30)--(0,30)--cycle);
draw((0,0)--(30,0)--(30,30)--(0,30)--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((12,12)--(18,12)--(18,18)--(12,18)--cycle);

label("$L$",(9,0),S);label("$W$",(24,0),S);label("$L-W$",(15,12),S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The white square has side $6$, so $L-W=6$. Also $LW=216$. Hence $L(L-6)=216$, so $L^2-6L-216=(L-18)(L+12)=0$ and $L=18$, $W=12$. As a check, the outer square has side $30$ and area $900=36+4\cdot216$.

The perimeter of a rectangle is $2(18+12)=\boxed{60}$.

A row contains $5$ points. With only two colours, some colour appears at least $3$ times in that row, and those three points are collinear. So at least $3$ colours are needed.

Three colours suffice. Label the colours $A,B,C$ and colour the rows (top to bottom) as
\[\begin{array}{ccccc}B&B&C&A&A\\A&A&B&B&C\\A&A&B&C&C\end{array}\]
Place the bottom-left point at $(0,0)$ and the top-right at $(4,2)$. Each row uses every colour at most twice, and each column (three points) is not monochromatic: the columns read $BAA,BAA,CBB,ABC,ACC$ from top to bottom. Every other line through three array points contains exactly three points; there are eight of them: the lines of slope $1$ starting at $(0,0),(1,0),(2,0)$; of slope $-1$ starting at $(0,2),(1,2),(2,2)$; and slope $\pm\dfrac12$ through $(0,0),(2,1),(4,2)$ and $(0,2),(2,1),(4,0)$. Their colours are
\[AAC,\ ABA,\ BBA;\quad BAB,\ BBC,\ CBC;\quad ABA,\ BBC,\]
none monochromatic.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
string[] row2={"B","B","C","A","A"},row1={"A","A","B","B","C"},row0={"A","A","B","C","C"};
for(int x=0;x<5;++x){label(row2[x],(x,2));label(row1[x],(x,1));label(row0[x],(x,0));}
draw((0,0)--(2,2),dotted);draw((0,0)--(4,2),dotted);draw((0,2)--(4,0),dotted);
\end{asy}


Hence the minimum is $\boxed{3}$.

question:
Trapezoid $ABCD$ has $AB\parallel DC$, $AB=12$, $CD=8$ and area $50$. Points $P_1,P_2,P_3$ lie on $AB$ (in order from $A$) and points $Q_1,Q_2$ lie on $DC$ (in order from $D$). Segments $DP_1$, $P_1Q_1$, $Q_1P_2$, $P_2Q_2$, $Q_2P_3$, $P_3C$ divide the trapezoid into seven triangles: the four light triangles $AP_1D$, $P_1P_2Q_1$, $P_2P_3Q_2$, $P_3BC$ and the three dark triangles $DQ_1P_1$, $Q_1Q_2P_2$, $Q_2CP_3$. Find the total light area minus the total dark area.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(12,0),C=(10,5),D=(2,5),P1=(3,0),P2=(6,0),P3=(9,0),Q1=(4.5,5),Q2=(7,5);
fill(A--P1--D--cycle,gray(0.85));fill(P1--P2--Q1--cycle,gray(0.85));fill(P2--P3--Q2--cycle,gray(0.85));fill(P3--B--C--cycle,gray(0.85));
fill(D--Q1--P1--cycle,gray(0.45));fill(Q1--Q2--P2--cycle,gray(0.45));fill(Q2--C--P3--cycle,gray(0.45));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(D--P1--Q1--P2--Q2--P3--C);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$P_1$",P1,S);label("$P_2$",P2,S);label("$P_3$",P3,S);label("$Q_1$",Q1,N);label("$Q_2$",Q2,N);
\end{asy}

solution:
Let $h$ be the height of the trapezoid. Then $\dfrac{(12+8)h}{2}=50$, so $h=5$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(12,0),C=(10,5),D=(2,5),P1=(3,0),P2=(6,0),P3=(9,0),Q1=(4.5,5),Q2=(7,5);
fill(A--P1--D--cycle,gray(0.85));fill(P1--P2--Q1--cycle,gray(0.85));fill(P2--P3--Q2--cycle,gray(0.85));fill(P3--B--C--cycle,gray(0.85));
fill(D--Q1--P1--cycle,gray(0.45));fill(Q1--Q2--P2--cycle,gray(0.45));fill(Q2--C--P3--cycle,gray(0.45));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(D--P1--Q1--P2--Q2--P3--C);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$P_1$",P1,S);label("$P_2$",P2,S);label("$P_3$",P3,S);label("$Q_1$",Q1,N);label("$Q_2$",Q2,N);
draw((12.8,0)--(12.8,5),Arrows);label("$h$",(12.8,2.5),E);
\end{asy}

Each light triangle has its base on $AB$ and its third vertex on line $DC$, so its height is $h$. The light bases $AP_1,P_1P_2,P_2P_3,P_3B$ together make up $AB$, so the light area is $\dfrac{12h}{2}=30$.
Each dark triangle has its base on $DC$ and apex on $AB$, and the dark bases $DQ_1,Q_1Q_2,Q_2C$ make up $DC$, so the dark area is $\dfrac{8h}{2}=20$.

The difference is $30-20=\boxed{10}$, independent of where the points are placed.

Place $A=(0,0)$, $B=(16,0)$, $C=(8,6)$ (the apex is $6$ above the midpoint since $10^2-8^2=36$). Line $BC$ is $y=-\dfrac34(x-16)$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(16,0),C=(8,6),Y=(9.6,4.8),X=(12,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--Y--X,dashed);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$Y$",Y,NE);label("$X$",X,S);
draw(Y+0.8*dir(Y--A)--Y+0.8*dir(Y--A)+0.8*dir(Y--X)--Y+0.8*dir(Y--X));
\end{asy}

Let $\theta=\angle YAX$; in right triangle $AXY$, $\tan\theta=\dfrac{XY}{AY}$.

\emph{Case $AY=2XY$}: $\tan\theta=\dfrac12$, so $Y$ lies on $y=\dfrac x2$. Intersecting with $BC$: $\dfrac x2=12-\dfrac{3x}4$, so $Y=(9.6,4.8)$ and $AY=\sqrt{9.6^2+4.8^2}=4.8\sqrt5$. Then $AX=\dfrac{AY}{\cos\theta}=4.8\sqrt5\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt5}{2}=12$, and $X=(12,0)$ is on $AB$.

\emph{Case $XY=2AY$}: $\tan\theta=2$, so $Y$ is on $y=2x$; then $2x=12-\dfrac{3x}{4}$ gives $x=\dfrac{48}{11}$ and $AX=\dfrac{AY}{\cos\theta}=\sqrt5\,AY=5x=\dfrac{240}{11}>16$, so $X$ is not on side $AB$.

Hence the hypotenuse is $\boxed{12}$.

The top and bottom are each joined to all four middle vertices; each middle vertex is joined to top, bottom and two middle vertices.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair T=(0,2.2),Bt=(0,-2.2),P=(-1.8,0.1),Q=(-0.4,-0.5),R=(1.8,-0.1),U=(0.5,0.5);
draw(T--P);draw(T--Q);draw(T--R);draw(T--U,dashed);draw(Bt--P);draw(Bt--Q);draw(Bt--R);draw(Bt--U,dashed);
draw(P--Q--R);draw(R--U--P,dashed);
dot(T);dot(Bt);label("top",T,N);label("bottom",Bt,S);
\end{asy}

Let $t_n,m_n,b_n$ be the numbers of $n$-edge walks from the top ending at the top, at a particular middle vertex (summed over all four: $M_n$), and at the bottom. From the adjacencies,
\[t_{n+1}=M_n,\quad b_{n+1}=M_n,\quad M_{n+1}=4t_n+4b_n+2M_n.\]
Starting from $(t_0,M_0,b_0)=(1,0,0)$:
\[\begin{array}{c|cccccccc}n&0&1&2&3&4&5&6&7\\\hline t_n&1&0&4&8&48&160&704&2688\\ M_n&0&4&8&48&160&704&2688&\\ b_n&0&0&4&8&48&160&704&2688\end{array}\]
Hence there are $\boxed{2688}$ such walks.

Adjacent hour marks are $\dfrac{360^\circ}{12}=30^\circ$ apart. At $8\!:\!00$ the minute hand points at $12$ and the hour hand at $8$, which is $4$ hour marks away going the short way ($8\to9\to10\to11\to12$).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),1));
for(int i=0;i<12;++i){pair p=dir(90-30*i);draw(0.9*p--p);}
label("$12$",0.75*dir(90));label("$8$",0.75*dir(90-240));label("$4$",0.75*dir(90-120));
draw((0,0)--0.85*dir(90),linewidth(1.2));draw((0,0)--0.55*dir(90-240),linewidth(2));
draw(arc((0,0),0.25,90,210),Arrow);label("$120^\circ$",0.35*dir(150),NW,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The smaller angle is $4\cdot30^\circ=\boxed{120^\circ}$.

question:
A circle is inscribed in a square. In one corner of the square a $3$ cm by $6$ cm rectangle is drawn with two of its sides along the two sides of the square at that corner, and with its opposite vertex on the circle; apart from that vertex the rectangle lies outside the circle. What is the radius of the circle?
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=15;
draw((0,0)--(30,0)--(30,30)--(0,30)--cycle);draw(circle((15,15),15));
filldraw((0,0)--(6,0)--(6,3)--(0,3)--cycle,gray(0.8));
label("$6$",(3,0),S);label("$3$",(0,1.5),W);
\end{asy}

solution:
Put the corner at the origin, so the circle has centre $(r,r)$ and radius $r$. The far vertex of the rectangle is $V=(6,3)$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=15;
draw((0,0)--(30,0)--(30,30)--(0,30)--cycle);draw(circle((15,15),15));
filldraw((0,0)--(6,0)--(6,3)--(0,3)--cycle,gray(0.8));
label("$6$",(3,0),S);label("$3$",(0,1.5),W);

pair O=(15,15),V=(6,3);draw(O--V,dashed);dot(O);dot(V);label("$O$",O,NE);label("$V$",V,NW);draw(O--(15,3),dotted);draw((6,3)--(15,3),dotted);label("$r-6$",(12,3),N);label("$r-3$",(15,9),E);
\end{asy}

Then
\[(r-6)^2+(r-3)^2=r^2\iff r^2-18r+45=0\iff (r-3)(r-15)=0.\]
If $r=3$ the square has side $6$ and the rectangle covers half of it, containing points inside the circle, which is not allowed. Hence $r=\boxed{15}$ cm.

Let $BH=x$. From $AB^2-x^2=AC^2-(8-x)^2$: $49-x^2=81-64+16x-x^2$, so $x=2$ and $AH=\sqrt{49-4}=3\sqrt5$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(8,0),A=(2,3*sqrt(5)),H=(2,0),M=(A+C)/2,P=(2,3*sqrt(5)*2/5/1);
P=B+(2/5)*(M-B);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--H,dashed);draw(B--M);
draw(H+(0.35,0)--H+(0.35,0.35)--H+(0,0.35));
dot(P);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$H$",H,S);label("$M$",M,NE);label("$P$",P,W);
label("$7$",(A+B)/2,W);label("$9$",A+0.3*(C-A),NE);label("$2$",(1,0),S);label("$6$",(5,0),S);
\end{asy}

Use coordinates $B=(0,0)$, $C=(8,0)$, $A=(2,3\sqrt5)$, so $M=(5,\dfrac{3\sqrt5}{2})$. Points of $BM$ are $tM=(5t,\dfrac{3\sqrt5}{2}t)$; this meets the line $x=2$ at $t=\dfrac25$. Hence $BP=\dfrac25BM$ and $PM=\dfrac35BM$, so
\[BP:PM=\boxed{2:3}.\]

question:
Square $ABCD$ has side length $4$. On each side, a semicircle is drawn inside the square with that side as diameter (so its centre is the midpoint of the side). The shaded region consists of all points lying inside at least two of the semicircles (four ``petals''). Find the area of the shaded region in terms of $\pi$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(4,4),D=(0,4);
path s1=arc((2,0),2,0,180),s2=arc((4,2),2,90,270),s3=arc((2,4),2,180,360),s4=arc((0,2),2,-90,90);
fill(arc((2,0),2,90,180)--arc((0,2),2,0,-90,CW)--cycle,gray(0.7));
fill(arc((2,0),2,0,90)--arc((4,2),2,180,270)--cycle,gray(0.7));
fill(arc((2,4),2,180,270)--arc((0,2),2,0,90,CCW)--cycle,gray(0.7));
fill(arc((2,4),2,270,360)--arc((4,2),2,90,180)--cycle,gray(0.7));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(s1);draw(s2);draw(s3);draw(s4);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);
\end{asy}

solution:
Each semicircle has radius $2$ and passes through the centre $O$ of the square. Every point of the square lies in at least one semicircle, and no point lies in three of them except $O$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(4,4),D=(0,4);
path s1=arc((2,0),2,0,180),s2=arc((4,2),2,90,270),s3=arc((2,4),2,180,360),s4=arc((0,2),2,-90,90);
fill(arc((2,0),2,90,180)--arc((0,2),2,0,-90,CW)--cycle,gray(0.7));
fill(arc((2,0),2,0,90)--arc((4,2),2,180,270)--cycle,gray(0.7));
fill(arc((2,4),2,180,270)--arc((0,2),2,0,90,CCW)--cycle,gray(0.7));
fill(arc((2,4),2,270,360)--arc((4,2),2,90,180)--cycle,gray(0.7));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(s1);draw(s2);draw(s3);draw(s4);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);
pair O=(2,2);dot(O);label("$O$",O,SE,Fill(white));draw(A--C,dotted);draw(B--D,dotted);
\end{asy}

Hence adding the four semicircle areas counts the square once and each petal twice:
\[4\cdot\dfrac{\pi\cdot2^2}{2}=16+\text{(petal area)}.\]
So the shaded area is $8\pi-16=\boxed{8\pi-16}$.

(Check directly: a petal is two circular segments, each a quarter circle of radius $2$ minus a right triangle: $2\left(\pi-2\right)$; four petals give $8\pi-16$.)

In right triangle $ABD$ (right angle at $D$), $\sin B=\dfrac{AD}{AB}=\dfrac25$. In right triangle $ADC$, $\angle C=90^\circ-\angle B$, so $\dfrac{AD}{AC}=\sin C=\cos B=\sqrt{1-\dfrac4{25}}=\dfrac{\sqrt{21}}5$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real ab=5, ad=2; real bd=sqrt(ab^2-ad^2);
pair D=(0,0),A=(0,ad),B=(-bd,0),C=(ad^2/bd,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--D,dashed);
draw(D+(0.2,0)--D+(0.2,0.2)--D+(0,0.2));draw(A+0.25*dir(A--B)--A+0.25*dir(A--B)+0.25*dir(A--C)--A+0.25*dir(A--C));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);
\end{asy}

Therefore $\dfrac{AC}{AD}=\dfrac5{\sqrt{21}}=\boxed{\dfrac{5\sqrt{21}}{21}}$.

(Equivalently $\dfrac1{AD^2}=\dfrac1{AB^2}+\dfrac1{AC^2}$.)

Extend the legs $DA$ and $CB$ to meet at $P$. Triangles $PAB$, $PEF$, $PDC$ are similar (all their bases are parallel), so their areas are $k\cdot14^2$, $k\cdot EF^2$, $k\cdot30^2$ for some constant $k$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(14,0),C=(22,-12),D=(-8,-12);
real t=(sqrt(548)-14)/16; pair PE=A+t*(D-A),PF=B+t*(C-B);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(PE--PF,dashed);
label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,NE);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,SW);label("$E$",PE,W);label("$F$",PF,E);
label("$14$",(7,0),N);label("$30$",(7,-12),S);
pair P=extension(A,D,B,C);draw(A--P--B,dotted);label("$P$",P,N);
\end{asy}

The two parts of the trapezoid have areas $k(EF^2-196)$ and $k(900-EF^2)$. Setting them equal gives $EF^2=\dfrac{196+900}{2}=548$, so
\[EF=\sqrt{548}=\boxed{2\sqrt{137}}.\]

question:
Two circles each have radius $1$, and each passes through the centre of the other. Find the area of the region common to both circles.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O1=(0,0),O2=(1,0),P=(0.5,sqrt(3)/2),Q=(0.5,-sqrt(3)/2);
fill(arc(O1,1,-60,60)--arc(O2,1,120,240)--cycle,gray(0.8));
draw(circle(O1,1));draw(circle(O2,1));dot(O1);dot(O2);
\end{asy}

solution:
Let the centres be $O_1,O_2$ and the intersection points $P,Q$. Triangles $O_1O_2P$ and $O_1O_2Q$ are equilateral with side $1$, so $\angle PO_1Q=120^\circ$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O1=(0,0),O2=(1,0),P=(0.5,sqrt(3)/2),Q=(0.5,-sqrt(3)/2);
fill(arc(O1,1,-60,60)--arc(O2,1,120,240)--cycle,gray(0.8));
draw(circle(O1,1));draw(circle(O2,1));dot(O1);dot(O2);
draw(O1--P--O2--Q--cycle,dashed);draw(P--Q,dashed);label("$O_1$",O1,W);label("$O_2$",O2,E);label("$P$",P,N);label("$Q$",Q,S);
\end{asy}

The common region is two congruent circular segments cut off by chord $PQ$. One segment is a $120^\circ$ sector minus triangle $O_1PQ$:
\[\dfrac{\pi}{3}-\dfrac12\cdot1\cdot1\cdot\sin120^\circ=\dfrac\pi3-\dfrac{\sqrt3}4.\]
The common area is $\boxed{\dfrac{2\pi}3-\dfrac{\sqrt3}2}$.

The three vertices split the $12$ equal arcs of the circumcircle into three arcs of $a,b,c\ge1$ steps with $a+b+c=12$. Two such triangles are congruent exactly when they have the same multiset $\{a,b,c\}$ (the arc counts determine the angles $15a^\circ,15b^\circ,15c^\circ$ and the circumradius is fixed; conversely equal multisets give triangles related by a rotation or reflection).

So we count partitions of $12$ into three positive parts:
\[\begin{gathered}(10,1,1),(9,2,1),(8,3,1),(8,2,2),(7,4,1),(7,3,2),\\(6,5,1),(6,4,2),(6,3,3),(5,5,2),(5,4,3),(4,4,4),\end{gathered}\]
giving $\boxed{12}$ triangles. For example, arcs $(6,4,2)$:
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<12;++i) dot(dir(90-30*i));draw(circle((0,0),1),gray);
draw(dir(90)--dir(90-180)--dir(90-300)--cycle);label("$6$",1.15*dir(0),E);label("$4$",1.15*dir(-150),SW);label("$2$",1.15*dir(120),NW);
\end{asy}

Let $AB=a$, $BC=b$, so $a+b=2013$ and $ab=2013$. Place $A=(0,0)$, $B=(a,0)$, $C=(a,b)$, $D=(0,b)$. Then
\[P=\left(\dfrac a2,-\dfrac{\sqrt3}2a\right),\ Q=\left(a+\dfrac{\sqrt3}2b,\dfrac b2\right),\ R=\left(\dfrac a2,b+\dfrac{\sqrt3}2a\right),\ S=\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt3}2b,\dfrac b2\right).\]
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=2.6,bb=1.3;
pair A=(0,0),B=(a,0),C=(a,bb),D=(0,bb);
pair P=(a/2,-a*sqrt(3)/2),Q=(a+bb*sqrt(3)/2,bb/2),R=(a/2,bb+a*sqrt(3)/2),S=(-bb*sqrt(3)/2,bb/2);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--P--B);draw(B--Q--C);draw(C--R--D);draw(D--S--A);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,W);
label("$P$",P,S);label("$Q$",Q,E);label("$R$",R,N);label("$S$",S,W);
draw(P--Q--R--S--cycle,dashed);draw(P--R,dotted);draw(S--Q,dotted);
\end{asy}

The diagonals $PR$ (vertical, length $b+\sqrt3a$) and $SQ$ (horizontal, length $a+\sqrt3b$) are perpendicular, so
\[[PQRS]=\dfrac{(b+\sqrt3a)(a+\sqrt3b)}2=\dfrac{4ab+\sqrt3(a^2+b^2)}{2}.\]
Now $a^2+b^2=(a+b)^2-2ab=2013^2-2\cdot2013=2013\cdot2011$. Dividing by $ab=2013$:
\[\dfrac{[PQRS]}{[ABCD]}=\dfrac{4+2011\sqrt3}{2}=\boxed{2+\dfrac{2011\sqrt3}{2}}.\]

(Sketch not to scale.) Triangles $BCE$ and $BEF$ share the altitude from $B$ to line $CF$, so $\dfrac{CE}{EF}=10$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0),C=(6,4),D=(0,4),F=(4,0);
pair G=extension(C,F,A,D),E=extension(C,F,B,D);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(B--D);draw(C--G);draw(A--G,dashed);
dot(E);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$F$",F,S);label("$G$",G,SW);label("$E$",E,NE);
\end{asy}

Since $FB\parallel DC$, triangles $EFB$ and $ECD$ are similar with ratio $\dfrac{EF}{EC}$, so $\dfrac{FB}{DC}=\dfrac1{10}$. With $AB=DC$, $AF=\dfrac9{10}AB=9\,FB$.

Since $AG\parallel BC$, triangles $FAG$ and $FBC$ are similar with ratio $\dfrac{AF}{FB}=9$, so $[AFG]=81[BFC]$ and $[BFC]=\dfrac{2013}{81}$.

Finally $[BEF]=\dfrac{EF}{FC}[BFC]=\dfrac1{11}\cdot\dfrac{2013}{81}=\dfrac{183}{81}=\boxed{\dfrac{61}{27}}$.

From $AB^2-BD^2=AC^2-CD^2$: $169-BD^2=400-(21-BD)^2$, giving $42BD=210$, $BD=5$ and $AD=12$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(21,0),A=(5,12),D=(5,0),X=(5,20/3);
pair Ef=A+dot(B-A,C-A)/dot(C-A,C-A)*(C-A);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--D);draw(B--Ef);dot(X);
draw(D+(0.6,0)--D+(0.6,0.6)--D+(0,0.6));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$E$",Ef,NE);label("$X$",X,W);
\end{asy}

Take $B=(0,0)$, $C=(21,0)$, $A=(5,12)$, so $X=(5,y)$ lies on $AD$. Since $BX\perp AC$ and $\overrightarrow{AC}=(16,-12)$, we need $5\cdot16-12y=0$, so $y=\dfrac{20}3$. Then $XD=\dfrac{20}3$ and $AX=12-\dfrac{20}3=\dfrac{16}3$, so
\[AX:XD=\boxed{4:5}.\]

Ratios along a line are unchanged by stretching the rectangle, so take $D=(0,0)$, $C=(3,0)$, $B=(3,1)$, $A=(0,1)$, $F=(1,0)$, $E=(2,0)$. Points of $BD$ are $(3t,t)$, with $t$ measuring the fraction of $DB$ from $D$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair D=(0,0),C=(6,0),B=(6,3),A=(0,3),F=(2,0),Ep=(4,0);
pair X=extension(A,F,B,D),Y=extension(A,Ep,B,D);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(B--D);draw(A--F);draw(A--Ep);dot(X);dot(Y);
label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,NE);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,SW);label("$F$",F,S);label("$E$",Ep,S);label("$X$",X,NW);label("$Y$",Y,N);
\end{asy}

Line $AF$: $x+y=1$, giving $4t=1$, $t=\dfrac14$. Line $AE$: $\dfrac x2+y=1$, giving $\dfrac{5t}{2}=1$, $t=\dfrac25$. Hence
\[\dfrac{XY}{BD}=\dfrac25-\dfrac14=\boxed{\dfrac{3}{20}}.\]

(Alternatively, $\triangle XDF\sim\triangle XBA$ gives $\dfrac{DX}{XB}=\dfrac13$, and $\triangle YDE\sim\triangle YBA$ gives $\dfrac{DY}{YB}=\dfrac23$.)

question:
A derangement of $n$ objects is an arrangement in which no object is in its original position; let $D_n$ be the number of derangements. A student conjectures that $D_n=\dfrac{(n-1)\cdot(n-1)!}{2}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Show that the conjecture agrees with the true values for $n=3$ and $n=4$.
\item Find $D_5$ and show the conjecture is false.
\item Using the recurrence $D_n=(n-1)(D_{n-1}+D_{n-2})$, find $D_6$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
We have $B+C=2A$ and $A+B+C=180^\circ$, so $3A=180^\circ$ and $A=60^\circ$. \textbf{(B)}
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(1.3,2.6);draw(A--B--C--cycle);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);
\end{asy}

Every such polygon has the same centre as the $24$-gon, and consecutive vertices are separated by the same number $k$ of steps around the $24$-gon; returning to the start after $N$ steps with no repetition requires $Nk=24$. So $N$ is a divisor of $24$ with $N\ge3$, and conversely every such divisor works (take every $\dfrac{24}N$th vertex).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<24;++i) draw(dir(15*i)--dir(15*(i+1)),gray);
draw(dir(0)--dir(120)--dir(240)--cycle);draw(dir(45)--dir(135)--dir(225)--dir(315)--cycle,dashed);
\end{asy}

The divisors are $3,4,6,8,12,24$: six values. \textbf{(A)}

question:
Six circles of radius $1$ are arranged in two rows of three inside a rectangle; each circle is tangent to its neighbours and to the adjacent sides of the rectangle. The circles are cut out. What is the area of the remaining (shaded) region?
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill((0,0)--(6,0)--(6,4)--(0,4)--cycle,gray(0.8));
for(int i=0;i<3;++i) for(int j=0;j<2;++j) filldraw(circle((1+2i,1+2j),1),white);
draw((0,0)--(6,0)--(6,4)--(0,4)--cycle);
\end{asy}


$\textbf{(A)}\ $$20-6\pi$ \qquad $\textbf{(B)}\ $$24-6\pi$ \qquad $\textbf{(C)}\ $$28-6\pi$ \qquad $\textbf{(D)}\ $$30-6\pi$ \qquad $\textbf{(E)}\ $$32-6\pi$

solution:
The rectangle is $3$ diameters by $2$ diameters, i.e. $6$ by $4$, with area $24$. The circles have total area $6\pi$. The remaining area is $24-6\pi$. \textbf{(B)}
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill((0,0)--(6,0)--(6,4)--(0,4)--cycle,gray(0.8));
for(int i=0;i<3;++i) for(int j=0;j<2;++j) filldraw(circle((1+2i,1+2j),1),white);
draw((0,0)--(6,0)--(6,4)--(0,4)--cycle);label("$6$",(3,0),S);label("$4$",(6,2),E);draw((1,1)--(2,1));label("$1$",(1.5,1),N);
\end{asy}

Let the square be $PQRU$ with $P$ on the table and $PQ$ at $30^\circ$ to the table. Go from $P$ to $Q$ and then from $Q$ to $R$: $PQ$ rises $2\sin30^\circ=1$ and $QR$, perpendicular to $PQ$, makes $60^\circ$ with the table and rises $2\sin60^\circ=\sqrt3$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(0,0),Q=2*dir(30),R=Q+2*dir(120),U=2*dir(120);
draw((-2,0)--(3,0));draw(P--Q--R--U--cycle);
draw(R--(R.x,0),dashed);draw(Q--(Q.x,0),dotted);
label("$30^\circ$",P+0.75*dir(12),E,fontsize(8pt));label("$2$",0.7*Q,SE);label("$2$",(Q+R)/2,NE);
label("$P$",P,S);label("$Q$",Q,E);label("$R$",R,N);
\end{asy}

The top vertex $R$ is $1+\sqrt3$ above the table. \textbf{(B)}

Let $O$ be the centre and $M$ the midpoint of $AC$. The centre lies on the bisector $BM$ of angle $B$, and $OA\perp AB$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,12),A=(-5,0),C=(5,0);real r=65/12;pair O=(0,-25/12);
draw(A--B--C);draw(A--C,dashed);draw(circle(O,r));draw(O--A,dashed);draw(O--C,dashed);draw(B--O,dotted);
dot(O);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,E);label("$O$",O,S);label("$M$",(0,0),NE);
\end{asy}

In right triangle $BMA$: $AM=5$, $AB=13$, $BM=12$. Triangles $BAO$ and $BMA$ are similar (right angles at $A$ and $M$, common angle at $B$), so $\dfrac{OA}{AB}=\dfrac{AM}{BM}$, giving $OA=\dfrac{13\cdot5}{12}=\dfrac{65}{12}$. \textbf{(D)}

Let $A=(0,0)$, $B=(a,0)$, $C=(a,b)$, $D=(0,b)$, and let $M=\left(\dfrac a2,b\right)$ be the midpoint of $CD$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=sqrt(2),bb=1;pair A=(0,0),B=(a,0),C=(a,bb),D=(0,bb);pair Fp=(a/2,bb);pair H=A+dot(B-A,C-A)/dot(C-A,C-A)*(C-A);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--C);draw(B--Fp,dashed);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$M$",Fp,N);label("$H$",H,W);
draw(H+0.08*dir(H--B)--H+0.08*dir(H--B)+0.08*dir(H--C)--H+0.08*dir(H--C));
\end{asy}

$\overrightarrow{BM}=\left(-\dfrac a2,b\right)$ must be perpendicular to $\overrightarrow{AC}=(a,b)$: $-\dfrac{a^2}{2}+b^2=0$, so $\dfrac ab=\sqrt2\approx1.414$. The closest choice is $\dfrac75=1.4$. \textbf{(C)}

Tiling a rectangle exactly with congruent squares of side $s$ (with no cutting) requires $s$ to divide both side lengths, so $s$ divides $\gcd(12,16,8)=4$. To use as few squares as possible take $s=4$, each of area $16$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair o=(0,0),x=(16,0),y=(5,3.5),z=(0,8);
draw(o--x--x+z--z--cycle);draw(z--z+y--x+z+y--x+z);draw(x--x+y--x+y+z);
draw(o--y,dashed);draw(y--y+x,dashed);draw(y--y+z,dashed);
label("$16$",x/2,S);label("$8$",z/2,W);label("$12$",x+y/2,SE);
\end{asy}

The faces have areas $192$ (two), $128$ (two) and $96$ (two). Leave uncovered one of the largest faces, $16\times12$. The covered area is $192+2\cdot128+2\cdot96=640$, so the number of squares is $\dfrac{640}{16}=\boxed{40}$.

The hypotenuse is $\sqrt{7^2+24^2}=25$. Let the right angle be at $C$, with $CA=7$, $CB=24$, and let $H$ be the foot of the altitude.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),A=(0,7),B=(24,0);pair H=A+dot(C-A,B-A)/dot(B-A,B-A)*(B-A);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(C--H,dashed);
draw(C+(0.8,0)--C+(0.8,0.8)--C+(0,0.8));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,SW);label("$H$",H,NE);label("$7$",(0,3.5),W);label("$24$",(12,0),S);
\end{asy}

Triangles $AHC$ and $ACB$ are similar (they share $\angle A$ and each has a right angle), so $\dfrac{AH}{AC}=\dfrac{AC}{AB}$, giving
\[AH=\dfrac{AC^2}{AB}=\boxed{\dfrac{49}{25}}.\]

question:
Segment $BC$ has length $10$. Points $E$ and $D$ lie on the same side of line $BC$ with $EB\perp BC$, $DC\perp BC$, $EB=2$ and $DC=3$. Segments $BD$ and $CE$ meet at $A$. Find the area of triangle $ABC$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(10,0),Ep=(0,2),D=(10,3);pair A=extension(B,D,C,Ep);
fill(A--B--C--cycle,gray(0.85));draw(B--C--D);draw(B--Ep);draw(B--D);draw(C--Ep);
label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$E$",Ep,W);label("$D$",D,E);label("$A$",A,N);
label("$2$",(0,1),W);label("$3$",(10,1.5),E);label("$10$",(5,0),S);
\end{asy}

solution:
Place $B=(0,0)$, $C=(10,0)$, $E=(0,2)$, $D=(10,3)$. Let $h$ be the height of $A$ above $BC$, and let $A$ be at horizontal distance $p$ from $B$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(10,0),Ep=(0,2),D=(10,3);pair A=extension(B,D,C,Ep);
fill(A--B--C--cycle,gray(0.85));draw(B--C--D);draw(B--Ep);draw(B--D);draw(C--Ep);
label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$E$",Ep,W);label("$D$",D,E);label("$A$",A,N);
label("$2$",(0,1),W);label("$3$",(10,1.5),E);label("$10$",(5,0),S);
draw(A--(A.x,0),dashed);label("$h$",(A.x,A.y/2),E);
\end{asy}

On $BD$, height grows by $3$ over $10$ units, so $h=\dfrac{3p}{10}$. On $CE$, $h=\dfrac{2(10-p)}{10}$. Then $3p=20-2p$, $p=4$, $h=\dfrac65$. (In general $h=\dfrac{2\cdot3}{2+3}$.)

The area is $\dfrac12\cdot10\cdot\dfrac65=\boxed{6}$.

Triangles with the same apex and bases on the same line have areas proportional to their bases.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-3,0),C=(2,0),B=(0.5,-4),D=(-0.8,1.6);pair O=extension(A,C,B,D);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--C);draw(B--D);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,N);label("$O$",O,NE);
\end{asy}

Triangles $ABO$ and $CBO$ share apex $B$ with bases $AO,OC$ on $AC$, so $\dfrac{AO}{OC}=\dfrac{30}{40}$. Triangles $ADO$ and $CDO$ share apex $D$, so $\dfrac{[DAO]}{[CDO]}=\dfrac{AO}{OC}=\dfrac34$. Hence $[DAO]=\dfrac34\cdot20=\boxed{15}$.

(Equivalently $[ABO]\cdot[CDO]=[BCO]\cdot[DAO]$.)

The apex angle is $180^\circ-150^\circ=30^\circ$. Put the apex at $A$ with legs $AB=AC=12$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(12,0),C=12*dir(30);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,N);
label("$30^\circ$",A,6*dir(15));label("$12$",(6,0),S);label("$12$",C/2,NW);
draw(C--(C.x,0),dashed);label("$6$",(C.x,3),W);
\end{asy}

The altitude from $C$ to line $AB$ is $12\sin30^\circ=6$, so the area is $\dfrac12\cdot12\cdot6=\boxed{36}$.

Both equations are symmetric under $x\to-x$ and $y\to-y$, so first solve with $x,y\ge0$: $y=10-2x$ and $x^2+(10-2x)^2=20$, i.e. $5x^2-40x+80=0$, $(x-4)^2=0$. So $x=4$, $y=2$: the segment is tangent to the circle.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-6,0)--(6,0),gray);draw((0,-11)--(0,11),gray);
draw((5,0)--(0,10)--(-5,0)--(0,-10)--cycle);draw(circle((0,0),sqrt(20)));
dot((4,2));dot((4,-2));dot((-4,2));dot((-4,-2));label("$(4,2)$",(4,2),NE);
\end{asy}

The solutions are $(\pm4,\pm2)$: $\boxed{4}$ pairs.

Since $DA\perp AB$ and $AB$ is a diameter, $DA$ is tangent to $\omega$ at $A$. By power of a point, $DP\cdot DQ=DA^2=144$, so $DP=8$ and $PQ=10$. Thus the diameter of $\omega$ is $10$ (so $AB=10<12=BC$), and indeed $DM=\sqrt{12^2+5^2}=13=8+5$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=5; pair A=(0,0),B=(10,0),C=(10,12),D=(0,12),M=(5,0);
pair u=unit(M-D);pair P=M-5*u,Q=M+5*u;
real R=8;
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(circle(M,5));draw(D--Q);
pair X=intersectionpoints(circle(P,R),circle(M,5))[0];
draw(arc(P,R,200,340),dotted);draw(P--X,dashed);draw(X--Q,dashed);
dot(P);dot(Q);dot(X);dot(M);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$M$",M,S);label("$P$",P,NE);label("$Q$",Q,SE);label("$X$",X,S);
\end{asy}

Now $PX=PD=8$, and $PQ$ is a diameter of $\omega$ with $X$ on $\omega$, so $\angle PXQ=90^\circ$. Hence $QX=\sqrt{10^2-8^2}=\boxed{6}$. (This holds for either intersection point $X$.)

The possible points form a $6\times6$ grid $\{1,\dots,6\}^2$. A non-vertical line through $6$ grid points has $6$ different $x$-coordinates, so it passes through $(k,y_1+(k-1)s)$ for $k=1,\dots,6$, where $s=y_2-y_1$ is an integer and $|5s|\le5$. Thus $s\in\{-1,0,1\}$, and the lines containing $6$ grid points are the $6$ rows, the $6$ columns and the $2$ main diagonals.

Every row must miss at least one point, and the rows are disjoint, so at least $6$ points are missing and at most $30$ are plotted.

Thirty is possible: omit
\[(1,2),(2,4),(3,6),(4,3),(5,5),(6,1).\]
These use each $x$-value and each $y$-value once, so every row and column misses a point; $(5,5)$ lies on the diagonal $y=x$ and $(4,3)$ on the diagonal $x+y=7$. The remaining $30$ points have no $6$ on a line.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int a=1;a<=6;++a) for(int b=1;b<=6;++b) dot((a,b));
pair[] om={(1,2),(2,4),(3,6),(4,3),(5,5),(6,1)};for(pair p:om){filldraw(circle(p,0.18),white);}
draw((1,1)--(6,6),dotted);draw((1,6)--(6,1),dotted);
\end{asy}


The answer is $\boxed{30}$.

Let $AB=11$ units, so $EB=2$, $EA=9$, $DA=DB=5.5$ and $DE=9-5.5=3.5$. Let $h$ be the height of $C$ above $AB$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(11,0),C=(3.5,6);
pair Dm=(A+B)/2,Ep=(9,0),F=B+(2/7)*(C-B);
fill(Dm--Ep--F--cycle,gray(0.8));
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(Dm--F--Ep);draw(A--B);draw(Dm--C,dashed);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",Dm,S);label("$E$",Ep,S);label("$F$",F,NE);
\end{asy}

Since $BF=\dfrac27BC$, the height of $F$ above $AB$ is $\dfrac27h$. Thus
\[[DEF]=\dfrac12\cdot3.5\cdot\dfrac27h=\dfrac h2=\dfrac1{11}\cdot\dfrac{11h}{2}=\dfrac{[ABC]}{11}.\]
So $[ABC]=132$, and since $D$ is the midpoint of $AB$, $[ADC]=\dfrac{132}2=\boxed{66}$.

Let $H$ be the foot of the altitude from $A$, so $BH=\sqrt{13^2-12^2}=5$. Let $BC=AC=x$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real x=16.9; pair B=(0,0),C=(x,0);
real bh=5; pair H=(bh,0); pair A=(bh,12);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--H,dashed);draw(H+(0.6,0)--H+(0.6,0.6)--H+(0,0.6));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$H$",H,S);label("$13$",(A+B)/2,W);label("$12$",(bh,6),E);
\end{asy}

If $H$ lies between $B$ and $C$, then $CH=x-5$ and $x^2=12^2+(x-5)^2$, so $10x=169$, $x=16.9$. If $B$ lies between $H$ and $C$, then $x^2=144+(x+5)^2$, which is impossible. If $C$ lies between $B$ and $H$, then $x=BC<BH=5$, contradicting $x=AC\ge AH=12$.

So $BC=16.9$ and the area is $\dfrac12\cdot16.9\cdot12=\boxed{\dfrac{507}{5}}$.

From $AB^2-BN^2=AC^2-(12-BN)^2$: $121-BN^2=169-144+24BN-BN^2$, so $BN=4$ and $AN=\sqrt{105}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(12,0),A=(4,sqrt(105)),Nn=(4,0);pair M=(A+C)/2,K=(4,sqrt(105)/4);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--Nn,dashed);draw(B--M);dot(K);
draw(Nn+(0.5,0)--Nn+(0.5,0.5)--Nn+(0,0.5));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$N$",Nn,S);label("$M$",M,NE);label("$K$",K,NW);
\end{asy}

With $B=(0,0)$, $C=(12,0)$, $A=(4,\sqrt{105})$ we get $M=(8,\dfrac{\sqrt{105}}2)$. Line $BM$ reaches $x=4$ halfway, at $K=(4,\dfrac{\sqrt{105}}4)$. So $KN=\dfrac{\sqrt{105}}4$, $AK=\dfrac{3\sqrt{105}}4$, and $AK:KN=\boxed{3:1}$.

Let the triple be $n-1,n,n+1$ (integers may be negative or zero).
\begin{itemize}
\item $(n-1)+n=n+1$: $n=2$, triple $1,2,3$.
\item $(n-1)+(n+1)=n$: $n=0$, triple $-1,0,1$.
\item $n+(n+1)=n-1$: $n=-2$, triple $-3,-2,-1$.
\end{itemize}
There are $3$ triples. \textbf{(D)}

There are $\binom{16}{2}=120$ pairs of segments. Fix two segments of $T$; their four endpoints are four points on the circle, and conditioned on this set of four points, the two segments form each of the three pairings of those four points with equal probability. Exactly one of the three pairings crosses.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<8;++i) dot(dir(45*i));draw(circle((0,0),1),gray);
draw(dir(0)--dir(135));draw(dir(90)--dir(225));draw(dir(45)--dir(180),dashed);draw(dir(270)--dir(315),dashed);
\end{asy}

So each pair crosses with probability $\dfrac13$, and by linearity of expectation the expected number is $120\cdot\dfrac13=40$. \textbf{(B)}

The unordered pairs $\{a_{2k-1},a_{2k}\}$ form a partition of $\{1,\dots,2022\}$ into $1011$ pairs with differences $1$ or $2$. Conversely, given such a partition, we can assign the pairs to the $1011$ position-pairs in $1011!$ ways and order each pair in $2$ ways. So $N=M\cdot1011!\cdot2^{1011}$, where $M$ is the number of such partitions of $\{1,\dots,2m\}$ with $m=1011$.

In such a partition, the element $1$ pairs with $2$ or with $3$. If $1$ pairs with $3$, then $2$ must pair with $4$ (its only remaining option). So the partition begins with the block $\{1,2\}$ or with the block $\{1,3\},\{2,4\}$, giving $M_m=M_{m-1}+M_{m-2}$ with $M_1=1$, $M_2=2$: $M_m=F_{m+1}$ (Fibonacci). Since $F_j$ is even exactly when $3\mid j$ and $1012\equiv1\pmod3$, $M$ is odd.

By Legendre's formula, $v_2(1011!)=1011-s_2(1011)$, where $s_2$ is the binary digit sum; $1011=1111110011_2$ has $8$ ones, so $v_2(1011!)=1003$. Therefore $n=0+1003+1011=2014$. \textbf{(D)}

Use $A=(0,0)$, $B=(2,0)$, $C=(0,6)$. Rotate the plane by $90^\circ$ clockwise about $C$; let $P\mapsto P'$ and $A\mapsto A'=(-6,6)$. Then $CP'=CP$ and $\angle PCP'=90^\circ$, so $PP'=\sqrt2\,CP$; and $P'A'=PA$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(2,0),C=(0,6),Ap=(-6,6),P=(0.72,0.96),Pp=(-5.04,5.28);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(C--Ap--Pp--C,dashed);draw(B--Ap,dotted);draw(P--A);draw(P--B);draw(P--C);draw(P--Pp,dashed);
dot(P);dot(Pp);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$A'$",Ap,NW);label("$P$",P,E);label("$P'$",Pp,S);
\end{asy}

Hence
\[AP+BP+\sqrt2\,CP=BP+PP'+P'A'\ge BA'=\sqrt{8^2+6^2}=10.\]
Equality needs $B,P,P',A'$ collinear in that order. Take $P=\left(\dfrac{18}{25},\dfrac{24}{25}\right)$, which is inside the triangle. Its image is $P'=\left(-\dfrac{126}{25},\dfrac{132}{25}\right)$, and both points lie on segment $BA'$, i.e. $\left(2-8t,\ 6t\right)$ with $t=\dfrac4{25}$ and $t=\dfrac{22}{25}$. So the minimum $10$ is attained. \textbf{(D)}

Let each square have side $2$ and centre $O$. The intersection is a regular octagon whose sides are at distance $1$ from $O$ (its inradius is $1$).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=1;
path sq1=(-1,-1)--(1,-1)--(1,1)--(-1,1)--cycle;
path sq2=rotate(45)*sq1;
fill(sq1,gray(0.9));fill(sq2,gray(0.9));
real t=tan(pi/8);
path oct=(1,t)--(t,1)--(-t,1)--(-1,t)--(-1,-t)--(-t,-1)--(t,-1)--(1,-t)--cycle;
fill(oct,gray(0.6));draw(sq1);draw(sq2);
dot((0,0));label("$O$",(0,0),S);draw((0,0)--(1,0),dashed);label("$1$",(0.5,0),S);
\end{asy}

A regular octagon with inradius $1$ consists of $8$ isosceles triangles of height $1$ and base $2\tan22.5^\circ=2(\sqrt2-1)$, so
\[B=8\cdot\dfrac12\cdot2(\sqrt2-1)\cdot1=8(\sqrt2-1).\]
The union has area $A=4+4-B=16-8\sqrt2=8\sqrt2(\sqrt2-1)$. Therefore $\dfrac AB=\boxed{\sqrt2}$.

Take $A=(0,0)$, $B=(7,0)$, $C=(7,4)$, $D=(0,4)$, $E=(2,0)$, $F=(5,0)$. The figure is symmetric about $x=3.5$, so $G$ has $x=3.5$; on $DF$, $y=4-\dfrac45x=1.2$. So $G=(3.5,1.2)$.
