question:
Mark marks $0$ on a number line and places his pencil there. He then performs the following four steps in some order, each time marking the number where he stops:
\begin{itemize}
\item move $2$ units right; \item move $3$ units right; \item move $4$ units right;
\item move to the point halfway between the last two numbers he has marked (this step needs at least two marked numbers, and $0$ counts as marked).
\end{itemize}
At the end he has marked four nonzero numbers. What is the greatest possible product of these four numbers?

solution:
Take the vertical cross-section through both centres. The centres are $9$ and $4$ above the table and $9+4=13$ apart; their heights differ by $5$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O1=(0,9),O2=(12,4);
draw((-10,0)--(18,0));draw(circle(O1,9));draw(circle(O2,4));
draw(O1--O2);draw(O1--(0,0),dashed);draw(O2--(12,0),dashed);draw((12,4)--(0,4),dotted);
dot(O1);dot(O2);dot((0,0));dot((12,0));
label("$9$",(0,2),W);label("$4$",(12,2),E);label("$13$",(O1+O2)/2,NE);label("$5$",(0,6.5),W);label("$d$",(6,0),S);
\end{asy}

The horizontal distance is $d=\sqrt{13^2-5^2}=\boxed{12}$ (in general $2\sqrt{Rr}$).

Place $B=(0,0)$, $A=(0,3)$, with $D$ and $C=(5,0)$ on the positive $x$-axis.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),A=(0,3),D=(9,0),C=(5,0),Ep=(1.8,2.4);
draw(A--B--D--cycle);draw(B--Ep);draw(Ep--C);
draw(B+(0.4,0)--B+(0.4,0.4)--B+(0,0.4));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$D$",D,SE);label("$C$",C,S);label("$E$",Ep,NE);
label("$3$",(0,1.5),W);label("$5$",(2.5,0),S);
\end{asy}

Since $\angle BEC=90^\circ$, $E$ lies on the circle with diameter $BC$: $x^2-5x+y^2=0$. Also $BE=3$: $x^2+y^2=9$. Subtracting, $5x=9$, so $E=\left(\dfrac95,\dfrac{12}5\right)$ (taking $y>0$, as $E$ is on $AD$).

Line $AE$ has slope $\dfrac{\dfrac{12}5-3}{\dfrac95}=-\dfrac13$, so it meets the $x$-axis at $D=(9,0)$ (and $C$ lies between $B$ and $D$). Therefore $AD=\sqrt{9^2+3^2}=\boxed{3\sqrt{10}}$.

question:
A $4\times4$ array of dots has coordinates $(x,y)$ with $x,y\in\{0,1,2,3\}$. Nested $L$-shapes divide it into four sets: $S_k$ consists of the dots with $\max(x,y)=k$, for $k=0,1,2,3$, containing $1,3,5,7$ dots. In how many ways can one dot be chosen from each set so that no three chosen dots are collinear?
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int x=0;x<4;++x) for(int y=0;y<4;++y) dot((x,y),linewidth(4));
draw((-0.3,0.3)--(0.3,0.3)--(0.3,-0.3)--(-0.3,-0.3)--cycle);
draw((-0.3,1.3)--(1.3,1.3)--(1.3,-0.3)--(0.7,-0.3)--(0.7,0.7)--(-0.3,0.7)--cycle);
draw((-0.3,2.3)--(2.3,2.3)--(2.3,-0.3)--(1.7,-0.3)--(1.7,1.7)--(-0.3,1.7)--cycle);
draw((-0.3,3.3)--(3.3,3.3)--(3.3,-0.3)--(2.7,-0.3)--(2.7,2.7)--(-0.3,2.7)--cycle);
\end{asy}

solution:
There are $1\cdot3\cdot5\cdot7=105$ choices. Count the bad ones. The dot of $S_0$ is the origin $O$.

Collinear triples with one dot from each of three sets:
\begin{itemize}
\item $S_0,S_1,S_2$: lines through $O$ and a dot of $S_1$ are the $x$-axis, $y$-axis and $y=x$, each meeting $S_2$ once: $3$ triples, each with $7$ choices from $S_3$: $21$ selections.
\item $S_0,S_1,S_3$: likewise $3$ triples, times $5$: $15$.
\item $S_0,S_2,S_3$: only the same three lines work (the lines through $(2,1)$ and $(1,2)$ meet no dot of $S_3$): $3$ triples, times $3$: $9$.
\item $S_1,S_2,S_3$: on rows $y=0$ ($1$ triple) and $y=1$ ($2$ triples, since $(0,1),(1,1)\in S_1$ both precede $(2,1),(3,1)$), and symmetrically columns $x=0,1$ ($3$ triples); the diagonal $y=x$ ($1$); and the lines through $(0,1),(1,2),(2,3)$ and $(1,0),(2,1),(3,2)$ ($2$). Total $9$ triples, $9$ selections.
\end{itemize}
This gives $21+15+9+9=54$, but a selection with two collinear triples has all four dots collinear (two triples share two dots); these are the $3$ selections along the axes and the diagonal, each counted $4$ times. So the number of bad selections is $54-3\cdot3=45$, and the answer is $105-45=\boxed{60}$. A bad selection (collinear on $y=1$) is shown:
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int x=0;x<4;++x) for(int y=0;y<4;++y) dot((x,y),linewidth(4));
draw((-0.3,0.3)--(0.3,0.3)--(0.3,-0.3)--(-0.3,-0.3)--cycle);
draw((-0.3,1.3)--(1.3,1.3)--(1.3,-0.3)--(0.7,-0.3)--(0.7,0.7)--(-0.3,0.7)--cycle);
draw((-0.3,2.3)--(2.3,2.3)--(2.3,-0.3)--(1.7,-0.3)--(1.7,1.7)--(-0.3,1.7)--cycle);
draw((-0.3,3.3)--(3.3,3.3)--(3.3,-0.3)--(2.7,-0.3)--(2.7,2.7)--(-0.3,2.7)--cycle);
dot((0,0),red+linewidth(7));dot((1,1),red+linewidth(7));dot((2,1),red+linewidth(7));dot((3,1),red+linewidth(7));draw((1,1)--(3,1),red);
\end{asy}

$\angle ADE=\angle ADC+\angle CDE=90^\circ+60^\circ=150^\circ$ and $DA=DC=DE$, so triangle $ADE$ is isosceles with $\angle DAE=\angle DEA=15^\circ$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(1,0),C=(1,1),D=(0,1),Ep=(0.5,1+sqrt(3)/2);pair F=extension(A,Ep,C,D);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(C--Ep--D);draw(A--Ep);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,W);label("$E$",Ep,N);label("$F$",F,SE);
\end{asy}

In triangle $ADF$, $\angle ADF=90^\circ$ and $\angle DAF=15^\circ$, so $\angle AFD=75^\circ$. Angles $AFD$ and $CFE$ are vertical angles, so $\angle CFE=\boxed{75^\circ}$.

Add triangle $ABX$ to each: $[APX]+[ABX]=[ABP]$ and $[BQX]+[ABX]=[ABQ]$. Hence the difference is $[ABP]-[ABQ]$.
\begin{asy}
