Triangle $ABP$ has base $AP=7$ on line $AC$ and height $BC=20$: area $70$. Triangle $ABQ$ has base $BQ=7$ on line $BC$ and height $AC=15$: area $\dfrac{105}{2}$. The difference is $70-\dfrac{105}2=\boxed{\dfrac{35}{2}}$.

Let the side be $s$. The circle through $A$ tangent to $BC$ at $M$ has diameter $AM$ (its centre lies on the perpendicular to $BC$ at $M$, which passes through $A$).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=sqrt(3); pair A=(0,h),B=(-1,0),C=(1,0),M=(0,0);
pair O=(0,h/2); real r=h/2;
pair X=B+0.25*(A-B), Y=C+0.25*(A-C);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(circle(O,r));dot(X);dot(M);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$M$",M,S);label("$X$",X,W);label("$Y$",Y,E);
\end{asy}

By the power of the point $B$: $BX\cdot BA=BM^2$, so $BX=\dfrac{\left(\dfrac s2\right)^2}{s}=\dfrac s4$ and $XA=\dfrac{3s}4$. Hence $BX:XA=\boxed{1:3}$.

question:
The eight vertices and eight side-midpoints of a regular octagon are marked. An \emph{inner polygon} is formed by choosing some of the marked points so that every side of the octagon (including its two endpoints) contains exactly one chosen point, and joining the chosen points in order around the octagon. How many inner polygons are there?
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<8;++i){pair P=dir(22.5+45*i),Q=dir(22.5+45*(i+1)); draw(P--Q); dot(P); dot((P+Q)/2,linewidth(3));}
\end{asy}

solution:
Decide first which vertices are chosen. Two adjacent vertices cannot both be chosen (their common side would contain two chosen points). Conversely, given a set of pairwise non-adjacent vertices: a side with a chosen endpoint must not use its midpoint, and a side with no chosen endpoint must use its midpoint. So each such vertex set gives exactly one inner polygon, and every polygon has at least $4$ chosen points.

For example, choosing vertices $V_0,V_2,V_5$ (numbered around the octagon) forces the midpoints of sides $V_3V_4$ and $V_6V_7$, giving the pentagon below.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<8;++i){pair P=dir(22.5+45*i),Q=dir(22.5+45*(i+1)); draw(P--Q); dot(P); dot((P+Q)/2,linewidth(3));}
pair V0=dir(22.5),V2=dir(22.5+90),V5=dir(22.5+225);
pair m3=(dir(22.5+135)+dir(22.5+180))/2,m6=(dir(22.5+270)+dir(22.5+315))/2;
draw(V0--V2--m3--V5--m6--cycle,dashed+linewidth(1));dot(V0,linewidth(6));dot(V2,linewidth(6));dot(V5,linewidth(6));dot(m3,linewidth(6));dot(m6,linewidth(6));
label("$V_0$",V0,NE);label("$V_2$",V2,NW);label("$V_5$",V5,S);
\end{asy}

The number of ways to choose non-adjacent vertices of an $n$-cycle is the Lucas number $L_n$, which satisfies $L_n=L_{n-1}+L_{n-2}$ with $L_1=1$, $L_2=3$ (a path argument: condition on whether a fixed vertex is chosen). Thus $L_3=4$, $L_4=7$, $L_5=11$, $L_6=18$, $L_7=29$, $L_8=47$.

There are $\boxed{47}$ inner polygons.

A point $(a,b)$ on the circle has tangent line $ax+by=5$. It passes through $(4,3)$ iff $4a+3b=5$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
draw(circle((0,0),sqrt(5)));
pair P=(4,3),T1=(-0.4,2.2),T2=(2,-1);
draw(P--T1);draw(P--T2);draw((0,0)--T1,dashed);draw((0,0)--T2,dashed);
dot(P);dot(T1);dot(T2);dot((0,0));
label("$(4,3)$",P,NE);label("$\left(-\dfrac25,\dfrac{11}5\right)$",T1,NW);label("$(2,-1)$",T2,SE);label("$O$",(0,0),SW);
draw((-3,0)--(5,0),gray);draw((0,-3)--(0,4),gray);
\end{asy}

With $b=\dfrac{5-4a}3$: $9a^2+(5-4a)^2=45$, so $25a^2-40a-20=0$, $5a^2-8a-4=0$, $a=2$ or $a=-\dfrac25$. The points are $(2,-1)$ and $\left(-\dfrac25,\dfrac{11}5\right)$.

The tangent at $(a,b)$ has slope $-\dfrac ab$: at $(2,-1)$ the slope is $2$; at $\left(-\dfrac25,\dfrac{11}5\right)$ it is $\dfrac{2}{11}$. The smaller slope occurs at $(a,b)=\boxed{\left(-\dfrac25,\dfrac{11}5\right)}$.

question:
Let $n,N,k$ be positive integers.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Use inclusion-exclusion to find a formula for the number of solutions of $a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_n=N$ in integers with $0\le a_i\le k-1$ for every $i$.
\item Evaluate it for $n=5$, $N=12$, $k=5$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Without the upper bound there are $\binom{N+n-1}{n-1}$ solutions (stars and bars). For a set $I$ of $m$ indices, the solutions with $a_i\ge k$ for all $i\in I$ correspond (by subtracting $k$ from those variables) to non-negative solutions of a sum equal to $N-mk$: there are $\binom{N-mk+n-1}{n-1}$ of them when $N-mk\ge0$ and none otherwise. There are $\binom nm$ such sets $I$. By inclusion-exclusion the count is
\[\boxed{\sum_{m=0}^{\left\lfloor \dfrac{N}{k}\right\rfloor}(-1)^m\binom nm\binom{N-mk+n-1}{n-1}}.\]
(The coefficient is $\binom nm$, the number of ways to choose which $m$ variables exceed the bound.)
\item $\binom{16}4-\binom51\binom{11}4+\binom52\binom64=1820-1650+150=\boxed{320}$.
\end{enumerate}

After three folds the rope lies in $8$ layers, each of length $s=\dfrac L8$, with both free ends at the same end of the bundle. Let the cut be at distance $x$ from that end ($0<x<s$).

Unfolded, the cut points are at distances $x,\ 2s-x,\ 2s+x,\ 4s-x,\ 4s+x,\ 6s-x,\ 6s+x,\ 8s-x$ from one end of the rope. So the pieces have lengths $x$ (the two end pieces), $2s-2x$ and $2x$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=6.5;
for(int i=0;i<8;++i){real y=-0.35*i; draw((0,y)--(s,y));}
for(int i=0;i<7;++i){real y1=-0.35*i,y2=-0.35*(i+1); if(i%2==0) draw(arc((s,(y1+y2)/2),0.175,90,-90,CW)); else draw(arc((0,(y1+y2)/2),0.175,90,270));}
draw((2,0.3)--(2,-2.8),dashed);label("cut",(2,0.3),N);label("$x$",(1,0),N);label("$s-x$",(4.25,0),N);
label("free ends",(0,0),W);
\end{asy}

The lengths $9$ and $4$ are two of the three values $x,\ 2x,\ 2s-2x$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\{x,2x\}$: impossible ($9\ne2\cdot4$ and $4\ne2\cdot9$).
\item $x=4$, $2s-2x=9$: $s=8.5$, $L=68$. \quad $x=9$, $2s-2x=4$: $s=11$, $L=88$.
\item $2x=4$, $2s-2x=9$: $x=2$, $s=6.5$, $L=52$. \quad $2x=9$, $2s-2x=4$: $x=4.5$, $s=6.5$, $L=52$.
\end{itemize}
In each case $0<x<s$. The possible lengths are $\boxed{52,\ 68,\text{ or }88}$ metres.

Some two points $A$ and $B$ have the same colour, say red; let $AB$ point in the positive direction and put $A=a$, $B=b$. Consider
\[C=2a-b,\qquad M=\dfrac{a+b}2,\qquad E=2b-a.\]
If $C$ is red, then $C,A,B$ are red with $A$ the midpoint of $CB$. If $M$ is red, then $A,M,B$ work. If $E$ is red, then $A,B,E$ work with $B$ the midpoint of $AE$. Otherwise $C,M,E$ are all blue, and $M$ is the midpoint of $CE$ since $\dfrac{(2a-b)+(2b-a)}2=\dfrac{a+b}2$. In every case the required triple exists. $\blacksquare$
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-1,0)--(9,0));pair C=(0,0),A=(2,0),M=(3,0),B=(4,0),E=(6,0);dot(C);dot(A,red+linewidth(5));dot(M);dot(B,red+linewidth(5));dot(E);
label("$2a-b$",C,S);label("$a$",A,S);label("$\dfrac{a+b}{2}$",M,N);label("$b$",B,S);label("$2b-a$",E,S);
\end{asy}

Each such triangle has angles $80^\circ,50^\circ,50^\circ$.

Look at a corner $A$ of the square. Any piece containing points near $A$ must have $A$ as a vertex: $A$ is a corner of the square, so it cannot lie inside a piece or inside a side of a piece (the piece would then stick out of the square). Hence the angles of the pieces at $A$ fill the $90^\circ$ angle exactly, and $90$ would be a sum of $50$s and $80$s.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(2.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(0,3)--cycle);draw((0,0)--(1.2,0.9));draw((0,0)--(0.6,1.3));draw(arc((0,0),0.45,0,90));label("$90^\circ$",0.45*dir(10),E,fontsize(8pt));label("$A$",(0,0),SW);
\end{asy}

But such sums are $50,80,100,130,\dots$ (any sum with at least two terms is at least $100$), never $90$. So \textbf{no}, it is impossible.

question:
Twenty children are split into $10$ pairs, each a boy and a girl, and in every pair the boy is taller than the girl. Later they are paired differently (again each pair a boy and a girl). Is it possible that
\begin{enumerate}
\item in all $10$ new pairs the girl is taller than the boy?
\item in exactly $9$ of the $10$ new pairs the girl is taller than the boy?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{No.} The tallest of the $20$ children is taller than his or her original partner, so is a boy (every girl was shorter than her partner). In the new pairing this boy is taller than his partner.
\item \textbf{Yes.} Let the heights be $b_i=2i$ and $g_i=2i-1$ for pair $i=1,\dots,10$, so each boy is taller than his original partner. New pairing: boy $i$ with girl $i+1$ for $i=1,\dots,9$ (heights $2i<2i+1$, girl taller), and boy $10$ with girl $1$ (boy taller). Exactly $9$ pairs have the girl taller.
\end{enumerate}

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item In how many ways can you choose $3$ fish from $5$ types of fish if repetition is allowed and order does not matter?
\item In how many ways can $5$ identical donuts be given to $3$ distinct fish (some fish may get none)?
\item Explain why the answers differ, and which ``stars and bars'' count each one is.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\begin{enumerate}
\item A choice is a solution of $a_1+\cdots+a_5=3$ in non-negative integers ($a_i$ = number of fish of type $i$): $\binom{3+5-1}{5-1}=\binom74=\boxed{35}$.
\item A distribution is a solution of $b_1+b_2+b_3=5$ in non-negative integers: $\binom{5+3-1}{3-1}=\binom72=\boxed{21}$.
\item The first problem has $5$ bins (types) and $3$ stars (choices); the second has $3$ bins (fish) and $5$ stars (donuts). ``Choose $r$ from $n$ types with repetition'' is ``distribute $r$ identical objects into $n$ bins'', $\binom{r+n-1}{r}$. The donut problem is the same as choosing $5$ fish from $3$ types with repetition, not $3$ from $5$; the roles of $n$ and $r$ are swapped, so the counts differ.
\end{enumerate}

Let the side be $s$. In right triangle $XPB$, $\angle B=60^\circ$, so $BP=BX\cos60^\circ=\dfrac{BX}2$; similarly $CQ=\dfrac{CY}2$.
