Let $x=\dfrac{a(a+1)}2$, $y=\dfrac{b(b+1)}2$ with $a>b\ge1$. Then $x-y=\dfrac{(a-b)(a+b+1)}2=14$, so $(a-b)(a+b+1)=28$. The two factors have opposite parity, and $a+b+1>a-b$. The options are $1\cdot28$ and $4\cdot7$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $a-b=1$, $a+b=27$: $a=14$, $b=13$, $x=105$, $y=91$, $z=392$.
\item $a-b=4$, $a+b=6$: $a=5$, $b=1$, $x=15$, $y=1$, $z=32$.
\end{itemize}
So $z=\boxed{32\text{ or }392}$.

Place the cube as $[0,1]^3$ and take the plane $x+y+z=\dfrac32$, perpendicular to the space diagonal through the centre. It crosses six edges at their midpoints:
\[\left(1,\dfrac12,0\right),\left(1,0,\dfrac12\right),\left(\dfrac12,0,1\right),\left(0,\dfrac12,1\right),\left(0,1,\dfrac12\right),\left(\dfrac12,1,0\right).\]
