Let $AC=2$, so $AD=1$ and $AF=\dfrac1{\cos10^\circ}$. In triangle $AGC$, $\angle GAC=20^\circ$ and $\angle GCA=30^\circ$, so $\angle AGC=130^\circ$ and, by the law of sines, $AG=\dfrac{2\sin30^\circ}{\sin130^\circ}=\dfrac1{\sin50^\circ}=\dfrac1{\cos40^\circ}$. Hence
\[\dfrac{AF}{AG}=\dfrac{\cos40^\circ}{\cos10^\circ}.\]
In triangle $AFG$ the angle at $A$ is $\angle GAF=10^\circ$. Write $x=\angle AGF$; then $\angle AFG=170^\circ-x$, and the law of sines gives
\[\dfrac{AF}{AG}=\dfrac{\sin x}{\sin(170^\circ-x)}=\dfrac{\sin x}{\sin(10^\circ+x)}.\]
For $x=30^\circ$ this is $\dfrac{\sin30^\circ}{\sin40^\circ}=\dfrac{1}{2\sin40^\circ}$, and $\dfrac{\cos40^\circ}{\cos10^\circ}=\dfrac{\cos40^\circ}{\sin80^\circ}=\dfrac{\cos40^\circ}{2\sin40^\circ\cos40^\circ}=\dfrac1{2\sin40^\circ}$. They agree. Since $\dfrac{\sin x}{\sin(10^\circ+x)}=\dfrac{1}{\cos10^\circ+\cot x\sin10^\circ}$ is strictly increasing on $0<x<170^\circ$, the value is unique: $\angle AGF=\boxed{30^\circ}$.

By Ptolemy's theorem for the cyclic (isosceles) trapezoid, $AC\cdot BD=AD\cdot BC+AB\cdot CD$, i.e. $AC^2=9+40=49$, so $AC=7$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=3*sqrt(3)/2;pair D=(0,0),C=(8,0),A=(1.5,h),B=(6.5,h);pair P=A+(3/11)*(C-A);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--C);draw(D--P,dashed);dot(P);
