By the triple-angle formula $\sin3y=3\sin y-4\sin^3y$, the inequality is equivalent to $4\sin^3y\ge0$, i.e. $\sin y\ge0$. So it holds exactly for $\boxed{360^\circ k\le y\le360^\circ k+180^\circ}$, $k\in\mathbb Z$.

The inequality is not an identity. At $y=90^\circ$, $\sin y=1\ge0$ and it holds ($-1\le3$). At $y=-30^\circ$, $\sin y<0$ and it fails ($-1>-\dfrac32$). Although $\sin270^\circ=\sin(-90^\circ)$, the values $3\sin90^\circ$ and $3\sin(-30^\circ)$ differ: replacing $3y$ by another angle with the same sine does not keep $y$'s sine.
