The interior lattice points are those with $|x|,|y|\le2$ except the four points $(\pm2,\pm2)$: $21$ points, giving $\binom{21}3=1330$ triples. Count collinear triples by lines:
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),sqrt(8)),gray);for(int x=-2;x<=2;++x) for(int y=-2;y<=2;++y) if(x*x+y*y<8) dot((x,y));
draw((-2,-1)--(1,2),dashed);draw((-1,-2)--(1,2),dotted);
\end{asy}

\begin{itemize}
\item Horizontal: rows $y=0,\pm1$ have $5$ points ($\binom53=10$ each), rows $y=\pm2$ have $3$ ($1$ each): $32$. Vertical: likewise $32$.
\item Slope $1$: the lines $y=x$ ($3$ points), $y=x\pm1$ ($4$ points each), $y=x\pm2$ ($3$ each): $1+4+4+1+1=11$. Slope $-1$: $11$.
\item Slopes $\pm2,\pm\dfrac12$: only the lines through the origin such as $(-1,-2),(0,0),(1,2)$ contain $3$ points: $4$.
\end{itemize}
Total $32+32+22+4=90$. The probability is $\dfrac{1330-90}{1330}=\boxed{\dfrac{124}{133}}$.

Let $a=\cos^2\theta$ and $b=\sin^2\theta$, so $a,b\in[0,1]$ and $a+b=1$.

\emph{Upper bound.} $a^n\le a$ and $b^n\le b$ since $0\le a,b\le1$, so $a^n+b^n\le a+b=1$.

\emph{Lower bound.} The function $t\mapsto t^n$ is convex on $[0,\infty)$, so $\dfrac{a^n+b^n}2\ge\left(\dfrac{a+b}2\right)^n=\dfrac1{2^n}$ (power mean inequality), i.e. $a^n+b^n\ge\dfrac1{2^{n-1}}$. Equality holds at $\theta=45^\circ$ (lower) and $\theta=0^\circ$ (upper). $\blacksquare$
