Call $t$ bad if some vertex is black. The vertex $t+90^\circ k$ is black exactly when $t$ lies in the black set rotated by $-90^\circ k$ and reduced into $[0^\circ,90^\circ)$. Cutting the circle into four quarter-arcs and rotating each onto $[0^\circ,90^\circ)$ shows that the set of bad $t$ has total measure at most the total black length, which is less than $\dfrac15\cdot360^\circ=72^\circ<90^\circ$. Hence some $t\in[0^\circ,90^\circ)$ is not bad, and that square works. $\blacksquare$

