question:
Let $a,b$ be positive integers.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Prove that if $x^2+ax+b$ factors into linear factors with integer coefficients, then so does $x^2-ax+b$.
\item Prove or disprove: if $x^2+ax+b$ factors this way, then $x^2+ax-b$ does not.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $x^2+ax+b=(x+r)(x+s)$ with integers $r,s$, then replacing $x$ by $-x$: $x^2-ax+b=(x-r)(x-s)$. $\blacksquare$
\item False. Counterexample: $x^2+5x+6=(x+2)(x+3)$ and $x^2+5x-6=(x+6)(x-1)$ both factor. (Likewise $x^2-5x-6=(x-6)(x+1)$.)
\end{enumerate}

Any $4$ vertices form a convex quadrilateral whose sides join cyclically consecutive chosen vertices; a quadrilateral side is an $n$-gon side iff two chosen vertices are adjacent. So we subtract from $\binom n4$ the number of ways to choose $4$ pairwise non-adjacent vertices of an $n$-cycle.

Fix vertex $1$. If it is not chosen, choose $4$ non-adjacent vertices from the path $2,\dots,n$ of $n-1$ vertices: $\binom{(n-1)-4+1}{4}=\binom{n-4}4$ (the usual gap argument). If it is chosen, its two neighbours are excluded and we choose $3$ non-adjacent vertices from the path $3,\dots,n-1$ of $n-3$ vertices: $\binom{n-5}3$. Hence the answer is
\[\boxed{\binom n4-\binom{n-4}4-\binom{n-5}3}\quad\left(=\binom n4-\dfrac n{n-4}\binom{n-4}4\right).\]
Check: for $n=8$ this gives $70-1-1=68$; the two excluded choices are the two sets of alternate vertices.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<8;++i){draw(dir(45i)--dir(45(i+1)));dot(dir(45i));}draw(dir(0)--dir(45)--dir(180)--dir(270)--cycle,linewidth(1));label("shared side",dir(22.5),NE,fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

Let $\theta=\dfrac{2k\pi}7$ with $k\in\{1,2,3\}$ and $c=\cos\theta$. Since $7\theta$ is a multiple of $2\pi$, $4\theta=2k\pi-3\theta$, so $\cos4\theta=\cos3\theta$. Using $\cos4\theta=8c^4-8c^2+1$ and $\cos3\theta=4c^3-3c$:
\[8c^4-4c^3-8c^2+3c+1=0\iff(c-1)(8c^3+4c^2-4c-1)=0.\]
For $k=1,2,3$, $c\ne1$, and the three values are distinct (cosine is decreasing on $[0,\pi]$). So they are the roots of $\boxed{8x^3+4x^2-4x-1}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),1));draw((-1.3,0)--(1.3,0));for(int k=1;k<=3;++k){pair P=dir(360k/7);draw((0,0)--P);draw(P--(P.x,0),dashed);dot(P);dot((P.x,0));}label("$\cos\dfrac{2\pi}7$",(Cos(360/7),0),S,fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

Perimeters $2(a+b)$ are even, so the difference is even. It cannot be $0$: equal area $ab=cd$ and equal $a+b=c+d$ force $\{a,b\}=\{c,d\}$ (both pairs are the roots of the same quadratic), i.e. congruent rectangles. Difference $2$ occurs: $2\times6$ and $3\times4$ have area $12$ and perimeters $16$ and $14$. \textbf{(C)}
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(6,0)--(6,2)--(0,2)--cycle);draw((8,0)--(12,0)--(12,3)--(8,3)--cycle);label("$6$",(3,0),S);label("$2$",(0,1),W);label("$4$",(10,0),S);label("$3$",(8,1.5),W);
\end{asy}

With signs $s_1,s_2,s_3\in\{\pm1\}$ the coefficient matrix has determinant $1+s_1s_2s_3$. If an even number of signs are minus ($4$ of $8$ cases), the determinant is $2$ and there is a unique solution.

Otherwise the system is singular; check consistency:
\begin{itemize}
\item $(+,+,-)$: $(y+z)-(x+y)=z-x$ gives $89-55=34$, consistent (infinitely many solutions).
\item $(+,-,+)$: $(x+y)-(y-z)=x+z$ gives $55-89=34$, false.
\item $(-,+,+)$: $(z+x)-(y+z)=x-y$ gives $34-89=55$, false.
\item $(-,-,-)$: adding gives $0=55+89+34$, false.
\end{itemize}
So $5$ of the $8$ cases work: $\dfrac58$. \textbf{(B)}

$[ABD]=2[ABC]=120$. In triangle $ABD$, $AC$ and $BM$ are medians ($C$ is the midpoint of $BD$), so $N$ is the centroid.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(4,0),D=(8,0),A=(1.5,4);pair M=(A+D)/2;pair N=extension(A,C,B,M);
fill(A--M--N--cycle,gray(0.8));draw(A--B--D--cycle);draw(A--C);draw(B--M);dot(N);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,S);label("$D$",D,SE);label("$M$",M,NE);label("$N$",N,W);
\end{asy}

The three medians cut a triangle into six triangles of equal area, and $AMN$ is one of them (between vertex $A$, the midpoint $M$ of $AD$, and the centroid). So $[AMN]=\dfrac{120}6=20$. \textbf{(D)}

As a quadratic in $s=\sin\theta$: $4s^2-(2+2\sqrt3)s+\sqrt3=(2s-1)(2s-\sqrt3)=0$. So $\sin\theta=\dfrac12$ or $\dfrac{\sqrt3}2$:
\[\theta=30^\circ,\ 60^\circ,\ 120^\circ,\ 150^\circ\ (+360^\circ k).\]
