\[6=\dfrac12\cdot3\cdot1+\dfrac12\cdot4\cdot\dfrac12+\dfrac12\cdot5\cdot d=\dfrac32+1+\dfrac52d,\]
so $d=\boxed{\dfrac75}$.

$\angle PMN=\angle PMA+\angle AMN$. The inscribed angle $\angle PMA$ subtends arc $PA$, equal to $\angle PCA=\dfrac C2$; similarly $\angle AMN=\angle ABN=\dfrac B2$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=dir(110),B=dir(210),C=dir(330);draw(circle((0,0),1));draw(A--B--C--cycle);
pair M=dir(270),N=dir(40),P=dir(160);draw(A--M,dashed);draw(B--N,dashed);draw(C--P,dashed);draw(P--M--N,linewidth(1));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$M$",M,S);label("$N$",N,NE);label("$P$",P,NW);
\end{asy}

So $70^\circ=\dfrac{B+C}2=\dfrac{180^\circ-A}2$, giving $A=\boxed{40^\circ}$.

The base angles are $70^\circ$, so $\angle ABU=35^\circ$ and $U$ is the midpoint of arc $AC$ not containing $B$; the arcs are $\overset{\frown}{AU}=\overset{\frown}{UC}=70^\circ$ (half of the $140^\circ$ arc $AC$ subtended by $\angle B$).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),A=dir(90),B=dir(90+140),C=dir(90-140);
pair U=dir(20);pair V=extension(B,U,A,O);
draw(circle(O,1));draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(B--U,dashed);draw(A--(A.x,-1),dotted);draw(O--U);draw(U--V);dot(O);dot(V);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$O$",O,E);label("$U$",U,NW);label("$V$",V,E);
\end{asy}

The arcs not containing the third vertex are: arc $BC=2\angle A=80^\circ$, arc $CA=2\angle B=140^\circ$, arc $AB=140^\circ$. $U$ is the midpoint of arc $CA$, so arc $UC=70^\circ$, and the arc from $U$ through $C$ to $B$ is $70^\circ+80^\circ=150^\circ$ (it does not contain $A$). Hence the central angle $\angle UOB=150^\circ$, and in isosceles triangle $OUB$ ($OU=OB$), $\angle OUB=\dfrac{180^\circ-150^\circ}2=15^\circ$. Since $V$ lies on segment $UB$, $\angle OUV=\boxed{15^\circ}$.

The right angle is at $B$. Put $B=(0,0)$, $A=(4,0)$, $C=(0,3)$, $P=(3,0)$. The circumcircle has diameter $AC$: $(x-2)^2+\left(y-\dfrac32\right)^2=\dfrac{25}4$, i.e. $x^2+y^2-4x-3y=0$. The small circle: $(x-3.5)^2+y^2=\dfrac14$, i.e. $x^2+y^2-7x+12=0$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),A=(4,0),C=(0,3),P=(3,0);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(circle((2,1.5),2.5),dashed);draw(circle((3.5,0),0.5));
pair K=(3.5,-0.5);dot(K);draw(K--P);
label("$A$",A,E);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,N);label("$P$",P,S);label("$K$",K,S);
\end{asy}

Subtracting: $3x-3y-12=0$, so $y=x-4$ (the radical axis). Substituting into the small circle: $x^2+(x-4)^2-7x+12=0$, $2x^2-15x+28=0$, $x=4$ (point $A$) or $x=\dfrac72$, giving $K=\left(\dfrac72,-\dfrac12\right)$. Then $KP=\sqrt{\dfrac14+\dfrac14}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt2}2}$.

Let the radius be $r$. The pentagon splits into $5$ triangles with two sides $r$ and included angle $72^\circ$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),1));pair[] V;for(int i=0;i<5;++i) V.push(dir(90+72i));draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--cycle);
draw((0,0)--V[0],dashed);draw((0,0)--V[1],dashed);label("$72^\circ$",(0,0),3*dir(126),fontsize(8pt));label("$r$",V[0]/2,E);dot((0,0));
\end{asy}

Its area is $5\cdot\dfrac12r^2\sin72^\circ$, so the ratio is $\dfrac{5\sin72^\circ}{2\pi}$. Since $\sin72^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt{10+2\sqrt5}}4$, this is $\boxed{\dfrac{5\sqrt{10+2\sqrt5}}{8\pi}}\approx0.757$.

Join the centre $O$ to all vertices, forming $n$ congruent isosceles triangles with apex angle $\dfrac{360^\circ}n$ and base $s$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int n=6;pair O=(0,0);draw(polygon(n));pair A=dir(0),B=dir(60),M=(A+B)/2;draw(O--A);draw(O--B);draw(O--M,dashed);dot(O);
label("$O$",O,W);label("$s$",(A+B)/2,NE);label("$h$",M/2,SE);
\end{asy}

The altitude $h$ from $O$ bisects the apex angle and the base, so in the right triangle with legs $h$ and $\dfrac s2$, $\tan\dfrac{180^\circ}n=\dfrac{s}{2h}$, giving $h=\dfrac s{2\tan\dfrac{180^\circ}n}$. Each triangle has area $\dfrac12sh=\dfrac{s^2}{4\tan\dfrac{180^\circ}n}$, and multiplying by $n$ gives the formula. $\blacksquare$

Triangles $PAC$ and $PDA$ share $\angle P$, and $\angle PAC=\angle PDA$ (tangent-chord angle), so they are similar: $\dfrac{AC}{DA}=\dfrac{PA}{PD}$. Likewise $\dfrac{BC}{DB}=\dfrac{PB}{PD}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);real R=1;pair P=(2.6,0);real t=degrees(acos(R/2.6));pair A=dir(t),B=dir(-t);
pair D=dir(165);path sec=P--D;pair C=intersectionpoints(P--D,circle(O,R))[0];
draw(circle(O,R));draw(P--A);draw(P--B);draw(P--D);draw(A--C--B);draw(A--D--B);
dot(P);label("$P$",P,E);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,W);
\end{asy}

Since $PA=PB$, $\dfrac{AC}{DA}=\dfrac{BC}{DB}$, so $BC=\dfrac{AC\cdot DB}{DA}=\dfrac{4.5\cdot5}{6}=\boxed{3.75}$ cm.

Choose $E$ on the same side of $AB$ as $C$ (inside the angle $DAB$) so that $\angle BAE=\angle CAD$ and $\angle ABE=\angle ACD$. Then $\triangle ABE\sim\triangle ACD$, giving $\dfrac{AB}{AC}=\dfrac{BE}{CD}=\dfrac{AE}{AD}$, so $AB\cdot CD=AC\cdot BE$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,-0.5),C=(4.6,2.4),D=(0.8,3.2);pair Ee=(2.5,0.9);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--C);draw(B--D);draw(A--Ee,dashed);draw(B--Ee,dashed);draw(Ee--D,dashed);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$E$",Ee,S);
\end{asy}

Also $\dfrac{AB}{AC}=\dfrac{AE}{AD}$ and $\angle BAC=\angle EAD$ (add $\angle EAC$ to the equal angles), so $\triangle ABC\sim\triangle AED$, giving $\dfrac{BC}{ED}=\dfrac{AC}{AD}$, i.e. $AD\cdot BC=AC\cdot ED$. Adding,
\[AB\cdot CD+AD\cdot BC=AC(BE+ED)\ge AC\cdot BD\]
by the triangle inequality, with equality iff $E$ lies on segment $BD$. The hypothesis gives equality, so $E\in BD$ and $\angle ABD=\angle ABE=\angle ACD$. Equal angles subtended by $AD$ at $B$ and $C$ (on the same side) mean $A,B,C,D$ are concyclic. $\blacksquare$

Opposite angles $B$ and $D$ are supplementary, so $\cos D=-\cos B$. By the law of cosines in triangles $ABC$ and $ADC$:
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=dir(200),B=dir(130),C=dir(40),D=dir(-60);draw(circle((0,0),1));draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--C,dashed);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,NW);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$6$",(A+B)/2,W);label("$7$",(B+C)/2,N);label("$8$",(C+D)/2,E);label("$9$",(D+A)/2,SW);
\end{asy}

\[AC^2=36+49-84\cos B=64+81+144\cos B.\]
So $228\cos B=-60$, $\cos B=-\dfrac5{19}$, and $AC^2=85+84\cdot\dfrac5{19}=\boxed{\dfrac{2035}{19}}$.

Let $s=\sqrt{21}$ and put $A=(0,0)$, $B=(s,0)$, $D=(0,s)$. The cut line $PQ$ is $x+y=\dfrac s2$. The inscribed circle of the square does not fit: its centre is at distance $\dfrac{s}{2\sqrt2}<\dfrac s2$ from $PQ$.

By symmetry about diagonal $AC$, the best circle has centre $(t,t)$ and touches $BC$, $CD$ and $PQ$:
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(4,4),D=(0,4),P=(0,2),Q=(2,0);
real r=3*4*(2-sqrt(2))/4; pair O=(4-r,4-r);
fill(P--Q--B--C--D--cycle,lightgray);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,dotted);draw(P--Q--B--C--D--cycle);
draw(circle(O,r));dot(O);draw(O--(4,4-r));draw(O--O-r*dir(45),dashed);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$P$",P,W);label("$Q$",Q,S);label("$r$",(4-r/2,4-r),N);
\end{asy}

\[r=s-t=\dfrac{2t-\dfrac s2}{\sqrt2}.\]
Solving, $r=\dfrac{3s}{2(2+\sqrt2)}=\dfrac{3(2-\sqrt2)}4\,s\approx0.44s$. This circle also stays away from sides $AB$ and $AD$, since $t-r>0$. So it fits, and it is the largest: any larger circle would have to cross one of $BC$, $CD$, $PQ$. Its area is
\[\pi r^2=\pi\cdot\dfrac{9(6-4\sqrt2)}{16}\cdot21=\boxed{\dfrac{189(3-2\sqrt2)}{8}\,\pi}\approx12.73.\]

By the Law of Sines, $a=2R\sin A$, $b=2R\sin B$ and $c=2R\sin C$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(5,0),C=(1.6,2.8);draw(A--B--C--cycle);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$c$",(A+B)/2,S);label("$b$",(A+C)/2,NW);label("$a$",(B+C)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

Using sum-to-product formulas,
\[\dfrac{a-b}{a+b}=\dfrac{\sin A-\sin B}{\sin A+\sin B}=\dfrac{2\cos\dfrac{A+B}2\sin\dfrac{A-B}2}{2\sin\dfrac{A+B}2\cos\dfrac{A-B}2}=\dfrac{\tan\dfrac{A-B}2}{\tan\dfrac{A+B}2}.\]
For Mollweide, $\sin C=2\sin\dfrac C2\cos\dfrac C2$ and $\cos\dfrac{A+B}2=\sin\dfrac C2$ because $\dfrac{A+B}2=90^\circ-\dfrac C2$. So
\[\dfrac{a-b}{c}=\dfrac{2\cos\dfrac{A+B}2\sin\dfrac{A-B}2}{2\sin\dfrac C2\cos\dfrac C2}=\dfrac{\sin\dfrac{A-B}2}{\cos\dfrac C2}.\ \blacksquare\]
So both are indeed consequences of the Law of Sines and standard identities.

Extend $CA$ beyond $A$ to $D$ with $AD=AB=c$, so $CD=b+c$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(5,0);real b=28;pair A=intersectionpoint(B--B+8*dir(b),C--C+8*dir(180-(180-3*b)));
pair D=A+(A-C)/length(A-C)*length(A-B);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--D--B,dashed);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$c$",(A+D)/2,NE);label("$c$",(A+B)/2,SE);label("$b$",(A+C)/2,NE);label("$a$",(B+C)/2,S);
\end{asy}

Triangle $ABD$ is isosceles, and its exterior angle at $A$ is $\angle BAC=2\angle B$, so $\angle ADB=\angle ABD=\angle B$. Triangles $CBD$ and $CAB$ share angle $C$ and have $\angle CDB=\angle CBA$, so they are similar. Hence
\[\dfrac{CB}{CA}=\dfrac{CD}{CB}\implies a^2=b(b+c)=b^2+bc.\ \blacksquare\]

Let the centres be $O_1,O_2$, the radii $r_1,r_2$ and $d=O_1O_2>r_1+r_2$. For any $P$ on the first circle and $Q$ on the second, the triangle inequality along $O_1PQO_2$ gives
\[d\le O_1P+PQ+QO_2=r_1+PQ+r_2\implies PQ\ge d-r_1-r_2.\]
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O1=(0,0),O2=(6,0);real r1=1.5,r2=2;draw(circle(O1,r1));draw(circle(O2,r2));
pair P=O1+r1*dir(0),Q=O2-r2*dir(0);draw(O1--O2,dashed);draw(P--Q,linewidth(1));
pair P2=O1+r1*dir(60),Q2=O2+r2*dir(150);draw(O1--P2--Q2--O2,dotted);
dot(O1);dot(O2);dot(P);dot(Q);label("$O_1$",O1,S);label("$O_2$",O2,S);label("$P_0$",P,SE);label("$Q_0$",Q,SW);label("$P$",P2,N);label("$Q$",Q2,N);
\end{asy}

Equality requires $O_1,P,Q,O_2$ to be collinear in this order. The points $P_0,Q_0$ where segment $O_1O_2$ meets the circles achieve $P_0Q_0=d-r_1-r_2$. Therefore the minimum is attained exactly by $P_0Q_0$, which lies on the line of centres. $\blacksquare$

We need $3$ vertices with no two adjacent around the cycle. Fix whether vertex $1$ is used.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int n=10;pair[] V;for(int k=0;k<n;++k){V.push(dir(90+36*k));}
for(int k=0;k<n;++k){draw(V[k]--V[(k+1)%n]);dot(V[k]);}
filldraw(V[0]--V[3]--V[6]--cycle,lightgray);
\end{asy}

If vertex $1$ is not used, delete it: we choose $3$ pairwise non-adjacent vertices from a path of $n-1$ vertices, which can be done in $\dbinom{n-3}{3}$ ways. If vertex $1$ is used, its neighbours are excluded, and we choose $2$ non-adjacent vertices from a path of $n-3$ vertices, in $\dbinom{n-4}{2}$ ways. Hence the count is
\[\dbinom{n-3}3+\dbinom{n-4}2=\dfrac{n(n-4)(n-5)}6.\]
For $n=10$ this gives $\dbinom73+\dbinom62=35+15=\boxed{50}$.

\textbf{No.} We show that in any dissection of a convex polygon into parallelograms, every side has another side parallel to it.

Take a side $s$. A parallelogram $Q_1$ of the dissection has a side along part of $s$; its opposite side is parallel to $s$ and strictly farther from $s$. If that opposite side is not on the boundary, the region just beyond part of it belongs to another parallelogram $Q_2$, whose side contains that piece and hence is parallel to $s$. Its opposite side is farther still. Since the distance from $s$ strictly increases and there are finitely many parallelograms, the chain stops at a side of the polygon parallel to $s$ and different from $s$. (In the hexagon below, dissected into three parallelograms, side $s$ is paired with the opposite side $s'$.)
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);pair[] V;for(int k=0;k<6;++k){V.push(2*dir(30+60*k));}
fill(O--V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--cycle,lightgray);
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle);draw(O--V[0]);draw(O--V[2]);draw(O--V[4]);
label("$s$",(V[4]+V[5])/2,SE);label("$s'$",(V[1]+V[2])/2,NW);
\end{asy}

In a convex polygon, at most two sides are parallel to a given direction. So the sides split into disjoint parallel pairs, and the number of sides is even. Since $13$ is odd, the dissection is $\boxed{\text{impossible}}$.

Let each side of length $3$ subtend a central angle $2\alpha$ and each side of length $5$ a central angle $2\beta$. Then $6\alpha+6\beta=360^\circ$, so $\beta=60^\circ-\alpha$. With radius $R$,
\[3=2R\sin\alpha,\qquad 5=2R\sin(60^\circ-\alpha).\]
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=7/sqrt(3);real a=2*aTan(3*sqrt(3)/13),bb=120-a;
pair[] P;real t=90+1.5*a;for(int k=0;k<6;++k){P.push(R*dir(t));t-=(k<3?a:bb);}
draw(circle((0,0),R),gray);draw(P[0]--P[1]--P[2]--P[3]--P[4]--P[5]--cycle);draw(P[0]--P[3],linewidth(1));
label("$3$",(P[0]+P[1])/2,NE);label("$3$",(P[1]+P[2])/2,E);label("$3$",(P[2]+P[3])/2,NE);label("$5$",(P[3]+P[4])/2,S);label("$5$",(P[4]+P[5])/2,W);label("$5$",(P[5]+P[0])/2,NW);dot((0,0));
\end{asy}

Therefore $3\left(\dfrac{\sqrt3}2\cos\alpha-\dfrac12\sin\alpha\right)=5\sin\alpha$, so $\tan\alpha=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{13}$. This gives $\sin\alpha=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{14}$ and $R=\dfrac{3}{2\sin\alpha}=\dfrac7{\sqrt3}$.

The chord spans the three short sides, so its length is
\[2R\sin3\alpha=2R(3\sin\alpha-4\sin^3\alpha)=\dfrac{14}{\sqrt3}\cdot\dfrac{180\sqrt3}{343}=\dfrac{360}{49}.\]
So $m+n=\boxed{409}$.

\textbf{No.} Suppose such a square lies inside the disc. Its diagonal joins two points of the disc, so its length is at most the diameter. But the diagonal is $\sqrt2\approx1.414>1.4$, a contradiction.
