(Equivalently, the smallest disc containing a unit square is its circumscribed disc, of diameter $\sqrt2$.) $\boxed{\text{Impossible}}$.

The circle $\omega$ with centre $B$ and radius $a$ passes through $C$. Since $AC\perp BC$, line $AC$ is tangent to $\omega$ at $C$. Line $AB$ meets $\omega$ at $D$ (between $A$ and $B$) and $E$ (beyond $B$), with $AD=c-a$ and $AE=c+a$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),B=(3,0),A=(0,4);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(circle(B,3));
pair D=B+3*unit(A-B),E=B-3*unit(A-B);draw(A--E,dashed);
dot(D);dot(E);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,SW);label("$D$",D,NE);label("$E$",E,SE);
label("$a$",(B+C)/2,S);label("$b$",(A+C)/2,W);
\end{asy}

The power of $A$ with respect to $\omega$ gives
\[AC^2=AD\cdot AE\implies b^2=(c-a)(c+a)=c^2-a^2,\]
so $a^2+b^2=c^2$. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{Claim:} the number of regions is $1+\dbinom n2+\dbinom n4$.

Start with the disc (one region) and draw the chords one at a time. When a chord is drawn that crosses $m$ earlier chords at interior points, it is cut into $m+1$ pieces, and each piece splits one region into two. So it adds $m+1$ regions. Summing over all chords, the total is $1+(\text{number of chords})+(\text{number of interior crossings})$.

Every crossing is determined by $4$ of the points (the two chords are the diagonals of the convex quadrilateral they form), and every $4$ points give exactly one crossing. So the number of crossings is $\dbinom n4$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V;real[] ang={90,160,215,300,20};for(int k=0;k<5;++k){V.push(dir(ang[k]));}
draw(circle((0,0),1));for(int i=0;i<5;++i){dot(V[i]);for(int j=i+1;j<5;++j){draw(V[i]--V[j]);}}
\end{asy}

Hence the region counts for $n=1,\dots,6$ are $\boxed{1,2,4,8,16,31}$. The doubling pattern for $n\le5$ breaks at $n=6$.

In the numerator, $3\cos^2A+3\sin^2A=3$ and $\cos^4A+\sin^2A\cos^2A=\cos^2A$, so it equals $\sin A\,(3+\cos^2A)$.

In the denominator, $\sec A-\sin A\tan A=\dfrac{1-\sin^2A}{\cos A}=\cos A$, so it equals $\tan A\cos A=\sin A$.

Hence $f(A)=3+\cos^2A$, defined exactly when $\sin A\ne0$ and $\cos A\ne0$, i.e. $0<\cos^2A<1$.
