Equating, $\dfrac43x=-\dfrac34x+75$, so $\dfrac{25}{12}x=75$ and $x=36$, $y=48$. The target is at $\boxed{(36,48)}$, at distance $\sqrt{36^2+48^2}=60$ from the origin. (The two directions are perpendicular, since $(3,4)\cdot(-4,3)=0$.)

\textbf{True.} Let $ABCD$ have $AB\parallel CD$, and place $A=(0,0)$, $B=(b,0)$, $D=(p,h)$, $C=(q,h)$ with $b,h>0$. Because $ABCD$ is a simple quadrilateral traversed in order, $\overrightarrow{DC}$ points in the direction of $\overrightarrow{AB}$, so $q>p$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0),C=(4.5,2.5),D=(1.5,2.5);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--C,dashed);draw(B--D,dashed);
draw(D--(1.5,0),dotted);draw(C--(4.5,0),dotted);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$h$",(1.5,1.25),W);
\end{asy}

$AC=BD$ gives $q^2+h^2=(p-b)^2+h^2$, so $q=b-p$ or $q=p-b$. The second option gives $q-p=-b<0$, which is impossible. Hence $q=b-p$ and
\[AD^2=p^2+h^2=(b-q)^2+h^2=BC^2.\]
So the legs are equal. (If the trapezoid happens to be a parallelogram, equal diagonals make it a rectangle, whose legs are also equal.) $\blacksquare$

Let $c=AB=DE$, $b=AC=DF$, $\gamma=\angle C=\angle F$, and let $x=BC$, $y=EF$. By the Law of Cosines, both $x$ and $y$ are positive roots of
\[t^2-2b\cos\gamma\,t+(b^2-c^2)=0.\]
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),A=(2.2,1.6);draw(C--(5,0));draw(C--A);draw(circle(A,2.6),dotted);
pair B=(A.x+sqrt(2.6^2-A.y^2),0);draw(A--B);label("$C$",C,SW);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,S);label("$b$",(A+C)/2,NW);label("$c$",(A+B)/2,NE);label("$\gamma$",C,(3,0.8));
\end{asy}

The product of the roots is $b^2-c^2\le0$. If $c>b$, the roots have opposite signs, so there is exactly one positive root. If $c=b$, the roots are $0$ and $2b\cos\gamma$, so again exactly one positive root. Hence $x=y$, and the triangles are congruent by SSS. $\blacksquare$ (The circle centred at $A$ with radius $c\ge b$ meets the ray from $C$ only once.)

