Here $r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, $x=r\cos\theta$ and $y=r\sin\theta$. Since the point is not on the non-positive $x$-axis, $\cos\theta\ne-1$, so $x+r>0$. By the half-angle formula,
\[\tan\dfrac\theta2=\dfrac{\sin\theta}{1+\cos\theta}=\dfrac{\dfrac yr}{1+\dfrac xr}=\dfrac{y}{x+r}.\]
