draw((P.x,0)--P,dotted);label("$O$",O,S);label("$K(-r,0)$",K,SW);label("$P(x,y)$",P,NE);draw(arc(O,0.4,0,60));draw(arc(K,0.8,0,30));label("$\theta$",O+0.55*dir(30));label("$\theta/2$",K+(1.25,0.3));
\end{asy}

(Geometrically, with $K=(-r,0)$, the inscribed angle $\angle OKP$ is $\dfrac\theta2$ and $\tan\angle OKP=\dfrac{y}{x+r}$.) Since $\dfrac\theta2\in\left(-\dfrac\pi2,\dfrac\pi2\right)$, the range of $\arctan$, we get $\dfrac\theta2=\arctan\dfrac{y}{x+r}$. $\blacksquare$

A regular tetrahedron of edge $\sqrt6$ has height $\sqrt6\cdot\sqrt{\dfrac23}=2$. So with $z$ the height above $ABC$, $P$ is at $z=2$ on the axis and $Q$ at $z=1$. At height $z$, the cross-section of $ABCP$ is a copy of $ABC$ scaled by $1-\dfrac z2$, and the cross-section of $A'B'C'Q$ is a copy with the same orientation scaled by $\dfrac{z-1}2$, both centred on the axis.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(6,0)--(6,3)--(0,3)--cycle);pair P=(3,2),Q=(3,1);
draw((0,0)--P--(6,0),dashed);draw((0,3)--Q--(6,3),dashed);
fill((2.25,1.5)--P--(3.75,1.5)--Q--cycle,lightgray);draw((2.25,1.5)--P--(3.75,1.5)--Q--cycle);
dot(P);dot(Q);label("$P$",P,N);label("$Q$",Q,S);label("$3$",(6,1.5),E);label("$z=\dfrac32$",(3.75,1.5),E);
\end{asy}

The intersection at height $z$ is the smaller triangle. The two scales are equal at $z=\dfrac32$, both $\dfrac14$. So the intersection is a double pyramid with apexes $Q$ and $P$ over a triangle of area $\dfrac1{16}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}4\cdot6=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{32}$, total height $1$:
\[V=\dfrac13\cdot\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{32}\cdot1=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{32}.\]
So $m+n=3+32=\boxed{35}$.

Let $R$ be the circumradius and $C$ the angle at $C$. Both excentres lie on the external bisector at $C$, and standard formulas give
\[I_AI_B=4R\cos\dfrac C2,\qquad r_a+r_b=4R\cos\dfrac C2\left(\sin\dfrac A2\cos\dfrac B2+\cos\dfrac A2\sin\dfrac B2\right)=4R\cos^2\dfrac C2.\]
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(5,0),C=(2.5,2.625);pair IA=(6.125,2.625),IB=(-1.125,2.625);real r=2.625;
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(circle(IA,r));draw(circle(IB,r));draw((A-(4,0))--(B+(4,0)),gray);draw(A--(A+2.3*(C-A)),gray);draw(B--(B+2.3*(C-B)),gray);
draw(IB--IA,dashed);dot(IA);dot(IB);label("$A$",A,S);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,N);label("$I_A$",IA,E);label("$I_B$",IB,W);
\end{asy}

The circles are disjoint, so the shortest distance is $I_AI_B-r_a-r_b=4R\cos\dfrac C2\left(1-\cos\dfrac C2\right)=2$. Also $AB=2R\sin C=4R\sin\dfrac C2\cos\dfrac C2=5$. Dividing, $\dfrac{1-\cos\dfrac C2}{\sin\dfrac C2}=\tan\dfrac C4=\dfrac25$, so $\tan\dfrac C2=\dfrac{2\cdot\dfrac25}{1-\dfrac4{25}}=\dfrac{20}{21}$. Then
\[2\pi(r_a+r_b)=2\pi\cdot4R\cos\dfrac C2\cdot\cos\dfrac C2=2\pi\cdot\dfrac5{\sin\dfrac C2}\cos\dfrac C2=10\pi\cdot\dfrac{21}{20}=\dfrac{21}2\pi.\]
So $m+n=\boxed{23}$.

Here $AX=11$ and $AY=27$. Let $\omega$ be the circle $BXY$ and let it meet line $AC$ again at $Y'$. By power of the point $A$, $AY\cdot AY'=AX\cdot AB=297$, so $AY'=11$. Hence $\omega$ meets the two sides at distances $11$ and $27$ from $A$ on each, so $\omega$ is symmetric about the bisector of angle $A$ and $O$ lies on it. The foot of the perpendicular from $O$ to $AB$ is the midpoint of $XB$, at distance $19$ from $A$.
