The power of $C$ is $CY\cdot CY'=7\cdot23=161=CO^2-R^2$. So $CO^2=\dfrac{828}7+161=\dfrac{1955}7$ and $m+n=\boxed{1962}$.

Keep a list of pairs consistent with all statements so far. Each ``I do not know'' removes the pairs that the speaker would have identified. Starting from all $55$ pairs, the pairs that would be identified at each turn are:
\begin{itemize}
\item Sam, turn $0$: $(1,1),(1,2),(9,10),(10,10)$ (unique sums);
\item Pat, turn $1$: $25$ pairs with product now unique, e.g. $(1,3),(1,5),(1,7),(3,5),\dots$;
\item Sam, turn $2$: $(2,2)$ (sum $4$, since $(1,3)$ is gone) and $(4,10)$;
\item Pat, turn $3$: $(1,4)$ (product $4$, since $(2,2)$ is gone) and $(5,8)$;
\item Sam, turn $4$: $(2,3)$ (sum $5$, since $(1,4)$ is gone);
\item Pat, turn $5$: $(1,6)$ (product $6$, since $(2,3)$ is gone);
\item Sam, turn $6$: no pair can be identified.
\end{itemize}
After turn $6$ nothing is ever eliminated again, so the remaining $20$ pairs lead to an endless conversation. The longest finite conversation is for $(A,B)=(1,6)$: five statements of ``I do not know'', then Pat knows. So $k=\boxed{5}$.

Let $\vec v=(c-a,\,d-b)$ and let $\vec w=(-(d-b),\,c-a)$ be $\vec v$ rotated by $90^\circ$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(0,0),Q=(3,1),w=(-1,3);draw(P--Q--Q+w--P+w--cycle);draw(P--Q--Q-w--P-w--cycle,dashed);
pair M=(P+Q)/2;draw(P--(M+w/2)--Q--(M-w/2)--cycle,dotted);
dot(P);dot(Q);label("$P(a,b)$",P,W);label("$Q(c,d)$",Q,E);
\end{asy}

\emph{$PQ$ is a side.} There are two squares, one on each side of $PQ$. The other vertices are
\[Q+\vec w,\ P+\vec w,\qquad\text{or}\qquad Q-\vec w,\ P-\vec w.\]
For example, the first square has vertices $(c-d+b,\ d+c-a)$ and $(a-d+b,\ b+c-a)$.

\emph{$PQ$ is a diagonal.} The centre is $M=\left(\dfrac{a+c}2,\dfrac{b+d}2\right)$, and the other diagonal has the same length and is perpendicular to $PQ$. So the other vertices are
\[M\pm\dfrac12\vec w=\left(\dfrac{a+c+b-d}2,\ \dfrac{b+d+c-a}2\right),\ \left(\dfrac{a+c-b+d}2,\ \dfrac{b+d-c+a}2\right).\]
Altogether there are $\boxed{3}$ squares.

