\emph{$12$ is impossible.} With $N$ points in $6$ rows, row $i$ having $k_i$ points, the distances from the leftmost point of each row to its other points give $\sum(k_i-1)=N-6$ positive differences, each in $\{1,\dots,5\}$, and distinct within a row. If $N\ge12$, there are at least $6>5$ of them, so two rows share a difference $d$. The two horizontal segments of length $d$ in different rows form a parallelogram. The answer is $\boxed{11}$.

