There are $2^6=64$ markings. Use Burnside over the $24$ rotations, noting that a rotation moves a face's mark to a definite direction on the image face.
\begin{itemize}
\item Identity: $64$.
\item Face rotations by $\pm90^\circ$ ($6$): the mark on the fixed top face turns by $90^\circ$, so nothing is fixed: $0$.
\item Face rotations by $180^\circ$ ($3$): top and bottom marks are free ($2\cdot2$), and the side faces are swapped in pairs with directions preserved ($2\cdot2$): $16$ each.
\item Vertex rotations ($8$): two $3$-cycles of faces, each determined by one face: $4$ each.
\item Edge rotations ($6$): three $2$-cycles of faces, each determined by one face: $8$ each.
\end{itemize}
\[\dfrac{64+0+3\cdot16+8\cdot4+6\cdot8}{24}=\dfrac{192}{24}=\boxed{8}.\]
(Treating the marks as labels A or B on faces ignores how rotations turn the marks, which is why $\dfrac{2^4}4\cdot3$ fails.)
