So the linear ratio is $\varphi^2$ and the area ratio is $\varphi^4=(\varphi+1)^2=3\varphi+2=\dfrac{7+3\sqrt5}2$. Then $a+b+c+d=7+3+5+2=\boxed{17}$.

Let the hypotenuse be $m+k$ with $k\in\{1,2\}$. Then $n^2=(m+k)^2-m^2=k(2m+k)$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(0,3);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw((0.25,0)--(0.25,0.25)--(0,0.25));label("$n$",(0,1.5),W);label("$m$",(2,0),S);label("$m+k$",(2,1.5),NE);
\end{asy}

If $n$ is odd, $k$ must be $1$ (since $k=2$ makes $n^2$ even), and $m=\dfrac{n^2-1}2$. If $n$ is even, $k=1$ would make $n^2$ odd, so $k=2$ and $m=\dfrac{n^2}4-1$. Hence
\[m=\boxed{\begin{cases}\dfrac{n^2-1}2,&n\text{ odd},\\[2mm]\dfrac{n^2}4-1,&n\text{ even}.\end{cases}}\]

Let the triangles be $ABC$ and $ADC$ with right angles at $B$ and $D$ and common hypotenuse $AC$, and let $M$ be the midpoint of $AC$. In a right triangle, the midpoint of the hypotenuse is equidistant from all three vertices, so $MB=MA=MC$ and $MD=MA=MC$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),A=dir(180),C=dir(0),B=dir(115),D=dir(-60);draw(circle(O,1),gray);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--C,dashed);dot(O);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,S);label("$M$",O,S);
\end{asy}

Hence $A,B,C,D$ all lie on the circle with centre $M$ and radius $\dfrac{AC}2$. $\blacksquare$ (Equivalently, $\angle B+\angle D=180^\circ$.)

The centres are $2$ apart, so each centre together with an intersection point forms an equilateral triangle, and each circle's arc inside the other subtends $120^\circ$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),P=(2,0);filldraw(circle(O,2),opacity(0.15)+gray);filldraw(circle(P,2),opacity(0.15)+gray);pair X=(1,sqrt(3)),Y=(1,-sqrt(3));draw(O--X--P--Y--cycle,dashed);draw(O--P);dot(O);dot(P);label("$O_x$",O,W);label("$O_y$",P,E);label("$2$",(1,0),S);
\end{asy}

The lens is twice a circular segment: $2\left(\dfrac{120}{360}\cdot4\pi-\dfrac{\sqrt3}4\cdot4\right)=\dfrac{8\pi}3-2\sqrt3$. The union has area $8\pi-\dfrac{8\pi}3+2\sqrt3=\dfrac{16\pi+6\sqrt3}3$, so $a+b=\boxed{22}$.

Since $B$ and $C$ lie on the circle, $A'B$ and $A'C$ are the tangents at $B$ and $C$. So $A'B=A'C$, and by the reflection $AB=AC$. The angle between the tangents is $\angle BA'C=180^\circ-\angle BOC=180^\circ-2\angle A$, while the reflection gives $\angle BA'C=\angle A$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(-3,0),C=(3,0),A=(0,3*sqrt(3)),Ap=(0,-3*sqrt(3));pair O=(0,sqrt(3));draw(circle(O,2*sqrt(3)));draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(B--Ap--C,dashed);dot(O);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,E);label("$A'$",Ap,S);
\end{asy}

Thus $\angle A=60^\circ$ and the isosceles triangle is equilateral with side $6$. Its area is $9\sqrt3$, and the square is $\boxed{243}$.

The circumcircle of such a triangle is the polygon's circumcircle, so the right angle's opposite side must be a diameter. For odd $n$ there are no diameters, and $T_n=0$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(2.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] P;for(int i=0;i<8;++i)P[i]=dir(22.5+45*i);draw(P[0]--P[1]--P[2]--P[3]--P[4]--P[5]--P[6]--P[7]--cycle);draw(circle((0,0),1),gray);draw(P[1]--P[5],dashed);draw(P[1]--P[3]--P[5]);
\end{asy}

For $n=2x$ there are $x$ diameters, each completed by any of the other $2x-2$ vertices, so $T_{2x}=2x(x-1)=4\dbinom x2$. By the hockey-stick identity,
\[\sum_{x=2}^{23}4\dbinom x2=4\dbinom{24}3=\boxed{8096}.\]

The two perimeters together count the original perimeter plus the cut twice: $32+48=60+2c$, so the cut has length $c=10$. It is parallel to a side, so one side is $10$ and the other is $30-10=20$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(20,0)--(20,10)--(0,10)--cycle);draw((7,0)--(7,10),dashed);label("$10$",(0,5),W);label("$20$",(10,0),S);
\end{asy}

The area is $\boxed{200}$.

The other side is $\dfrac{36}2-12=6$, so the area is $12\cdot6=\boxed{72}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(12,0)--(12,6)--(0,6)--cycle);label("$12$",(6,0),S);label("$6$",(12,3),E);
\end{asy}

The radius is $7.5$, so the area is $56.25\pi\approx176.6$, i.e. about $\boxed{177}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),1));draw((0,0)--(1,0));dot((0,0));label("$7.5$",(0.5,0),N);
\end{asy}

Triangles $ABE$ and $ECD$ are congruent (legs $a,b$), so $\angle AEB+\angle DEC=\angle AEB+\angle BAE=90^\circ$ and thus $\angle AED=90^\circ$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),E=(0,2),C=(0,5),A=(3,0),D=(2,5);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--E--D);label("$A$",A,SE);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,NW);label("$D$",D,NE);label("$E$",E,W);label("$a$",(1.5,0),S);label("$b$",(0,1),W);label("$a$",(0,3.5),W);label("$b$",(1,5),N);label("$c$",(1.5,1),NE);label("$c$",(1,3.5),SE);
\end{asy}

The trapezoid has height $a+b$ and parallel sides $a,b$, so its area is $\dfrac{(a+b)^2}2$. It also equals the sum of the three right triangles, $\dfrac{ab}2+\dfrac{ab}2+\dfrac{c^2}2$. Hence $a^2+2ab+b^2=2ab+c^2$, i.e. $a^2+b^2=c^2$. $\blacksquare$ (This proof is due to President James A. Garfield.)

Substituting $x^2=y+a$: $y^2+y+a-a^2=0$, i.e. $(y+a)(y-a+1)=0$. The root $y=-a$ gives $x=0$, a single point. The root $y=a-1$ gives $x^2=2a-1$, which yields two more points exactly when $2a-1>0$. For example, $a=1$:
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;draw(circle((0,0),1));draw(graph(new real(real x){return x^2-1;},-1.6,1.6));dot((0,-1));dot((1,0));dot((-1,0));draw((-1.8,0)--(1.8,0),gray,Arrows(4));draw((0,-1.4)--(0,1.8),gray,Arrows(4));
\end{asy}
 So there are exactly three points iff $\boxed{a>\dfrac12}$.

The flaw: requiring distinct $y$-roots is not enough, since each $y$-root yields $0$, $1$ or $2$ points depending on the sign of $x^2=y+a$. One must count points, not roots in $y$.

The long part $OB$ sweeps a $270^\circ$ sector of radius $16$, of area $\dfrac34\cdot256\pi=192\pi$. The short part $OA$ points the opposite way and sweeps a $270^\circ$ sector of radius $4$ starting $180^\circ$ further round.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(arc((0,0),4,0,270),gray);fill((0,0)--arc((0,0),4,0,270)--cycle,lightgray);fill((0,0)--arc((0,0),1,270,360)--cycle,gray);draw((0,0)--(4,0));draw((0,0)--(-1,0));dot((0,0));label("$O$",(0,0),NE);label("$16$",(2,0),S);label("$4$",(-0.5,0),S);
\end{asy}

If $OB$ sweeps angles $0^\circ$ to $270^\circ$, then $OA$ sweeps $180^\circ$ to $450^\circ$. Only the part from $270^\circ$ to $360^\circ$ lies outside the large sector: a quarter disc of area $4\pi$. The total is $196\pi$, so $A=\boxed{196}$.

$\sqrt{30^2+30^2}=30\sqrt2\approx\boxed{42.43}$ ft (exactly $30\sqrt2$).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(3,0)--(0,3)--cycle);draw((0.3,0)--(0.3,0.3)--(0,0.3));label("$30$",(1.5,0),S);label("$30$",(0,1.5),W);
\end{asy}

Consecutive vertices are $15^\circ$ apart on the circumcircle. With $A,\dots,X$ numbered $0,\dots,23$: $K=10$, $R=17$ and $U=20$. The arc $UK$ not containing $R$ runs $20\to23\to0\to10$, which is $14$ steps, or $210^\circ$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V;string L="ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWX";for(int i=0;i<24;++i)V[i]=dir(90-15*i);draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--V[6]--V[7]--V[8]--V[9]--V[10]--V[11]--V[12]--V[13]--V[14]--V[15]--V[16]--V[17]--V[18]--V[19]--V[20]--V[21]--V[22]--V[23]--cycle);
draw(V[10]--V[17]--V[20]);draw(V[10]--(0,0)--V[20],dashed);dot((0,0));for(int i:new int[]{0,10,17,20})label("$"+substr(L,i,1)+"$",V[i],V[i]);
\end{asy}

The inscribed angle is half the arc: $\angle KRU=\boxed{105^\circ}$.

Equal base angles give $OR=OZ$: $\dfrac{39x+25}5=2x+5$, so $39x+25=10x+25$ and $x=\boxed{0}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(2.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,4),R=(-3,0),Z=(3,0);draw(O--R--Z--cycle);draw(O--(0,0),dashed);draw((0,0.3)--(0.3,0.3)--(0.3,0));label("$O$",O,N);label("$R$",R,W);label("$Z$",Z,E);label("$6$",(0,0),S);label("$h$",(0,2),W);
\end{asy}

Check: $OR=OZ=5$, half the base is $3$, and the altitude is $\sqrt{25-9}=4=6\cdot0+4$.

The roots are $3$ and $6$, so the edge is $6$. The sphere's diameter is the cube's space diagonal $6\sqrt3$, so the radius is $3\sqrt3$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=1.4;pair d=(0.5,0.35);pair[] F={(0,0),(s,0),(s,s),(0,s)};draw(F[0]--F[1]--F[2]--F[3]--cycle);draw(F[1]--F[1]+d--F[2]+d--F[3]+d--F[3]);draw(F[2]--F[2]+d);draw(F[0]--F[0]+d--F[1]+d,dashed);draw(F[0]+d--F[3]+d,dashed);
draw(F[0]--F[2]+d,red);pair M=(F[0]+F[2]+d)/2;draw(circle(M,abs(F[2]+d-F[0])/2));dot(M);label("$6\sqrt3$",M,SE,red);label("$6$",(s/2,0),S);
\end{asy}

The volume is $\dfrac43\pi(3\sqrt3)^3=\dfrac43\pi\cdot81\sqrt3=\boxed{108\sqrt3\,\pi}$.

We count lattice points $(x,y)$ with $x^2+y^2\le100$. For each $x$ from $-10$ to $10$, the number of $y$ values is $2\lfloor\sqrt{100-x^2}\rfloor+1$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=-11;i<=11;++i){draw((i,-11)--(i,11),gray(0.85));draw((-11,i)--(11,i),gray(0.85));}draw(circle((0,0),10));dot((0,0));draw((0,0)--(6,8));label("$10$",(3,4),NW);
\end{asy}

For $x=0,\pm1,\dots,\pm10$ the counts of $\lfloor\sqrt{100-x^2}\rfloor$ are $10,9,9,9,9,8,8,7,6,4,0$. So the total is $21+2(19+19+19+19+17+17+15+13+9+1)=21+296=317$. Excluding himself, he reaches $\boxed{316}$ people.

question:
Find the error in this ``proof'' that $9+10=21$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $27-27+30-30=63-63$;
\item $9(3-3)+10(3-3)=21(3-3)$;
\item therefore $9+10=21$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the sides be $a<b$. The bisector from a corner cuts off an isosceles right triangle with legs $a$, of area $\dfrac{a^2}2=6$, so $a=2\sqrt3$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(3*sqrt(3),0)--(3*sqrt(3),2*sqrt(3))--(0,2*sqrt(3))--cycle);draw((0,0)--(2*sqrt(3),2*sqrt(3)));fill((0,2*sqrt(3))--(0,0)--(2*sqrt(3),2*sqrt(3))--cycle,lightgray);label("$6$",(0.8,2.4));label("$12$",(3.8,1.3));label("$a$",(0,sqrt(3)),W);label("$b$",(1.5*sqrt(3),0),S);
\end{asy}

The total area $ab=18$ gives $b=3\sqrt3$. Then $P=2(5\sqrt3)=10\sqrt3$ and $P^2=\boxed{300}$.

The small hexagon's apothem is $\sqrt3$. The tangent side of the large hexagon lies at distance $1$ beyond the midpoint of each small side, so the large apothem is $\sqrt3+1$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real ap=sqrt(3);pair[] P,Q;for(int i=0;i<6;++i){P[i]=2*dir(60*i);Q[i]=(2+2*sqrt(3)/3)*dir(60*i);}
draw(P[0]--P[1]--P[2]--P[3]--P[4]--P[5]--cycle);draw(Q[0]--Q[1]--Q[2]--Q[3]--Q[4]--Q[5]--cycle);
for(int i=0;i<6;++i){pair M=(P[i]+P[(i+1)%6])/2;real a=degrees(M);draw(arc(M,1,a-90,a+90));}
pair M0=(P[0]+P[1])/2;draw((0,0)--M0,dashed);draw(M0--M0+dir(degrees(M0)),red);dot((0,0));label("$\sqrt3$",M0/2,SE);label("$1$",M0+0.5*dir(degrees(M0)),SE,red);
\end{asy}

A regular hexagon with apothem $h$ has side $\dfrac{2h}{\sqrt3}$. So the side is $\dfrac{2(\sqrt3+1)}{\sqrt3}=\boxed{2+\dfrac{2\sqrt3}3}$.

The original surface area is $2\cdot4+4\cdot16=72$. Each cut creates two new $2\times2$ faces, adding $8$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=2,c=2;pair d=(1.2,0.8);path f=(0,0)--(8,0)--(8,2)--(0,2)--cycle;draw(f);draw((8,0)--(8,0)+d--(8,2)+d--(0,2)+d--(0,2));draw((8,2)--(8,2)+d);for(int i=1;i<8;++i)draw((i,0)--(i,2)--(i,2)+d,dashed);label("$8$",(4,0),S);label("$2$",(0,1),W);
\end{asy}

We need $8k=72$, so $k=\boxed{9}$ cuts.

The perpendicular from $O$ to a chord bisects it, so $r^2=15^2+14^2=421$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);real R=sqrt(421);draw(circle(O,R));pair M=(0,-14);pair A=(-15,-14),B=(15,-14);draw(A--B);draw(O--M,dashed);draw(O--B);draw(M+(0.8,0)--M+(0.8,0.8)--M+(0,0.8));dot(O);label("$O$",O,N);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$M_1$",M,S);label("$14$",(0,-7),W);label("$15$",(7.5,-14),S);
\end{asy}

The area is $421\pi\approx\boxed{1321.94}$.

$|x-y|+|x+y|=2\max(|x|,|y|)$. In the region $|y|\le x$ the equation is $x^2+y^2=6x$, i.e. $(x-3)^2+y^2=9$. Only the arc with $|y|\le x$ counts: it runs from $(3,-3)$ through $(6,0)$ to $(3,3)$, a semicircle of radius $3$ on the chord from $(3,-3)$ to $(3,3)$. By symmetry the same happens in all four regions.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int k=0;k<4;++k){pair c=3*dir(90*k);pair p=rotate(90*k)*(3,-3),q=rotate(90*k)*(3,3);filldraw((0,0)--p--arc(c,3,90*k-90,90*k+90)--q--cycle,lightgray,black);}
draw((-3,-3)--(3,3),dashed);draw((-3,3)--(3,-3),dashed);draw((-7,0)--(7,0),gray,Arrows(4));draw((0,-7)--(0,7),gray,Arrows(4));dot((3,0));label("$(3,0)$",(3,0),S);
\end{asy}

Each quarter of the region is the triangle $(0,0),(3,3),(3,-3)$ of area $9$ plus a half-disc of area $\dfrac{9\pi}2$. The total is $36+18\pi$, so $m+n=\boxed{54}$.

The flaws: there are four circles, not three (the student missed $x^2+(y+3)^2=9$). Also, each circle contributes only its arc lying in its own quarter-plane, so the region is not the union of whole circles and no overlap subtraction is needed.

Joining $O$ to the vertices splits $ABC$ into three triangles with bases $a,b,c$ and heights $2$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(7,0),C=(2.2,4.6);real a=abs(B-C),bb=abs(C-A),cc=abs(A-B);pair O=(a*A+bb*B+cc*C)/(a+bb+cc);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(O--A,dashed);draw(O--B,dashed);draw(O--C,dashed);
pair F1=(O.x,0);pair F2=A+dot(O-A,C-A)/dot(C-A,C-A)*(C-A);pair F3=B+dot(O-B,C-B)/dot(C-B,C-B)*(C-B);draw(O--F1);draw(O--F2);draw(O--F3);dot(O);label("$O$",O,E);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$2$",(O+F1)/2,E);
\end{asy}

The area is $\dfrac12\cdot2\,(a+b+c)=\boxed{20}$ cm$^2$.

$\angle RZO=74^\circ$, so $\angle ROZ=180^\circ-37^\circ-74^\circ=\boxed{69^\circ}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair R=(0,0),Z=(4,0);pair O=extension(R,R+dir(37),Z,Z+dir(180-74));draw(O--R--Z--cycle);label("$O$",O,N);label("$R$",R,SW);label("$Z$",Z,SE);label("$37^\circ$",R,5*dir(18));label("$74^\circ$",Z,5*dir(143));
\end{asy}

With the $60$ m side along the wall, the fence is $36+60+36$: $6$ posts along the $60$ m side and $3$ more on each $36$ m side, $12$ in all. With the $36$ m side along the wall: $6+6$ posts on the $60$ m sides and $2$ more on the far side, $14$. The fewest is $\boxed{\textbf{(B)}\ 12}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(0,36)--(60,36)--(60,0));draw((-10,0)--(70,0),linewidth(2)+gray);for(int i=0;i<=5;++i)dot((12*i,36));for(int j=0;j<3;++j){dot((0,12*j));dot((60,12*j));}label("$60$",(30,36),N);label("$36$",(0,18),W);label("wall",(65,0),S);
\end{asy}

Let $I$ be the incentre and $D,E,F$ the points where the incircle touches $BC,CA,AB$. Cut along $ID$, $IE$ and $IF$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0),C=(1.5,4);real a=abs(B-C),bb=abs(C-A),cc=abs(A-B);pair I=(a*A+bb*B+cc*C)/(a+bb+cc);
pair F=(I.x,0);pair E=A+dot(I-A,C-A)/dot(C-A,C-A)*(C-A);pair D=B+dot(I-B,C-B)/dot(C-B,C-B)*(C-B);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(I--D);draw(I--E);draw(I--F);draw(A--I--B,dashed);draw(C--I,dashed);dot(I);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$I$",I,NE);label("$D$",D,NE);label("$E$",E,NW);label("$F$",F,S);
\end{asy}

Each piece, e.g. $AFIE$, is a kite: $AF=AE$ (equal tangents) and $IF=IE$ (radii). So it is symmetric about $AI$, and turning it over gives the same shape. Flip each kite in place, fur side up; each still fits its own corner of the hole, so together they fill it. This works for every triangle, including obtuse ones.

Let $P,Q$ be inside. The distance to $Q$ from a fixed point is a convex function, so over the triangle it is maximised at a vertex; applying this twice, $PQ$ is at most the longest distance between two vertices, i.e. the longest side, say $a$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0),C=(2,3.5);pair P=(1.5,1),Q=(4,0.8);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(P--Q,dashed);dot(P);dot(Q);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$P$",P,W);label("$Q$",Q,N);
\end{asy}

By the triangle inequality $a<b+c$, so $2a<a+b+c$ and $PQ\le a<\dfrac{a+b+c}2$. $\blacksquare$

Let $AB=BC=1$ and $AC=x$. By the angle bisector theorem, $\dfrac{BD}{DC}=\dfrac{AB}{AC}=\dfrac1x$, so $BD=\dfrac1{1+x}$. Setting $BD=x$ gives $x^2+x-1=0$ and $x=\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}2$. Then $\sin\dfrac B2=\dfrac x2=\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}4=\sin18^\circ$, so $\angle B=\boxed{36^\circ}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0);pair A=dir(-72),C=dir(-108);A=4*A;C=4*C;pair D=B+(C-B)*abs(A-C)/abs(C-B);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--D);label("$B$",B,N);label("$A$",A,SE);label("$C$",C,SW);label("$D$",D,W);label("$36^\circ$",B,9*dir(-90));
\end{asy}

Check: in the $36^\circ$--$72^\circ$--$72^\circ$ triangle, $\angle DAC=36^\circ$ and $\angle ADC=72^\circ$, so $AC=AD$. Also $\angle DAB=36^\circ=\angle B$, so $AD=BD$. Hence $AC=BD$.

Let $\angle A=\theta$. From $AE=ED$, $\angle EDA=\theta$, so the exterior angle $\angle DEB=2\theta$. From $ED=DB$, $\angle DBE=2\theta$. Then $\angle BDC=\angle A+\angle ABD=3\theta$. From $DB=BC$, $\angle BCD=3\theta$, and $\angle ABC=\angle ACB=3\theta$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(2.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real th=180/7;pair B=(-1,0),C=(1,0),A=(0,tan(radians(3*th)));pair D=intersectionpoints(circle(B,2),A--C)[0];if(abs(D-C)<1e-6)D=intersectionpoints(circle(B,2),A--C)[1];
pair E=intersectionpoints(circle(D,2),A--B)[0];draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(B--D);draw(D--E);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,2*E);label("$E$",E,W);
\end{asy}

So $7\theta=180^\circ$ and $\angle ABC=\dfrac{540}7$, giving $m+n=\boxed{547}$.

For a right triangle the inradius is $r=\dfrac{a+b-c}2=\dfrac{3+4-5}2=1$. (Check: area $6=rs=1\cdot6$.)
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(0,3);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(circle((1,1),1));dot((1,1));draw((1,1)--(1,0));label("$1$",(1,0.5),E);label("$4$",(2,0),S);label("$3$",(0,1.5),W);label("$5$",(2,1.5),NE);
\end{asy}

So the area is $\pi\approx\boxed{3.1}$.

Equal chords of a circle are equidistant from its centre, so $P$ is equidistant from the three sides. Since the chords lie on the sides themselves, $P$ is the incentre. Then $PX$ and $PY$ bisect $\angle X$ and $\angle Y$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair X=(0,0),Y=(5,0);pair Z=extension(X,X+dir(60),Y,Y+dir(135));real a=abs(Y-Z),bb=abs(Z-X),cc=abs(X-Y);pair P=(a*X+bb*Y+cc*Z)/(a+bb+cc);
draw(X--Y--Z--cycle);draw(X--P--Y,dashed);draw(circle(P,1.3*P.y),gray);dot(P);
label("$X$",X,SW);label("$Y$",Y,SE);label("$Z$",Z,N);label("$P$",P,N);
\end{asy}

So $\angle XPY=180^\circ-30^\circ-22.5^\circ=\boxed{127.5^\circ}$.

Join the incentre $I$ to the vertices. This splits the triangle into triangles $IBC$, $ICA$, $IAB$ with bases $a,b,c$ and common height $r$ (the incircle touches each side perpendicularly).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0),C=(1.5,4);real a=abs(B-C),bb=abs(C-A),cc=abs(A-B);pair I=(a*A+bb*B+cc*C)/(a+bb+cc);real r=I.y;draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(circle(I,r));draw(A--I--B);draw(C--I);draw(I--(I.x,0));dot(I);label("$r$",(I.x,r/2),E);label("$I$",I,N);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);
\end{asy}

So the area is $\dfrac12r(a+b+c)=rs$. $\blacksquare$

Let $BB'$ be a diameter of the circumcircle. Triangle $BCB'$ has a right angle at $C$ (angle in a semicircle). Also $\angle BB'C=\angle BAC$ (inscribed angles on arc $BC$), or its supplement if $A$ and $B'$ lie on opposite sides of $BC$; the sine is the same either way. Hence $a=BC=BB'\sin\angle BB'C=2R\sin A$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);pair A=dir(200),B=dir(-20),C=dir(100);pair A2=-B;draw(circle(O,1));draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(B--A2,dashed);draw(A2--C,dashed);dot(O);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,N);label("$B'$",A2,W);label("$O$",O,S);
\end{asy}

Using the formula area $=\dfrac12bc\sin A$ (next item), the area is $\dfrac12bc\cdot\dfrac a{2R}=\dfrac{abc}{4R}$. $\blacksquare$

Take side $a$ as base. The altitude from the opposite vertex has length $b\sin\theta$: it is a leg opposite $\theta$ in a right triangle with hypotenuse $b$. (If $\theta$ is obtuse, the foot lies on the extension, and $\sin(180^\circ-\theta)=\sin\theta$.)
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),B=(5,0),A=3*dir(55);pair H=(A.x,0);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--H,dashed);draw(H+(0.25,0)--H+(0.25,0.25)--H+(0,0.25));label("$C$",C,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$A$",A,N);label("$a$",(2.5,0),S);label("$b$",A/2,NW);label("$\theta$",C,4*dir(27));label("$b\sin\theta$",(A+H)/2,E);
\end{asy}

So the area is $\dfrac12\cdot a\cdot b\sin\theta$. $\blacksquare$

A circle through $A$ and $B$ has its centre equidistant from them, i.e. on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$; likewise on the perpendicular bisector of $BC$. These two lines are not parallel, since $AB$ and $BC$ are not parallel (the points are noncollinear). So they meet in exactly one point $O$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0.5),C=(1.2,3);pair u=(-(B-A).y,(B-A).x),v=(-(C-B).y,(C-B).x);pair M1=(A+B)/2,M2=(B+C)/2;pair O=extension(M1,M1+u,M2,M2+v);draw(circle(O,abs(O-A)),gray);
draw(A--B--C);draw(M1-0.3*u--O,dashed);draw(M2-0.3*v--O,dashed);dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(O);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,N);label("$O$",O,W);
\end{asy}

Then $OA=OB=OC$, so the circle with centre $O$ and radius $OA$ passes through all three points. Any such circle must have centre $O$ and radius $OA$, so it is unique. $\blacksquare$

A chord is at most a diameter, so $2r\ge6$ and $r\ge3$, with equality when $AB$ is a diameter.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-3,0),B=(3,0);draw(circle((0,0),3));draw(A--B);draw(circle((0,2.5),sqrt(9+6.25)),gray+dashed);dot(A);dot(B);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$6$",(0,0),S);
\end{asy}

The least area is $\boxed{9\pi}$.

Classify $1,\dots,49$ by the power of $7$: $42$ numbers have none, $6$ have exactly one ($7,14,\dots,42$), and $1$ has two ($49$). We need the total exponent to be at least $3$; count the complement (total at most $2$):

\begin{itemize}
\item total $0$: $42^3=74088$;
\item total $1$: $3\cdot6\cdot42^2=31752$;
\item total $2$: $3\cdot6^2\cdot42+3\cdot42^2=4536+5292=9828$.
\end{itemize}
The answer is $49^3-115668=117649-115668=\boxed{1981}$.

Complete the frustum to a cone of height $H$. By similar triangles, $\dfrac{H-h}H=\dfrac rR$, so $H=\dfrac{hR}{R-r}$ and $H-h=\dfrac{hr}{R-r}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-3,0),B=(3,0),C=(1.5,2),D=(-1.5,2),V=(0,4);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(D--V--C,dashed);draw((0,0)--(0,4),dotted);label("$R$",(1.5,0),S);label("$r$",(0.75,2),N);label("$h$",(0,1),W);
\end{asy}

Then
\[V=\dfrac\pi3\left(R^2H-r^2(H-h)\right)=\dfrac{\pi h}3\cdot\dfrac{R^3-r^3}{R-r}=\boxed{\dfrac{\pi h}3\left(R^2+Rr+r^2\right)}.\]

In an equilateral triangle, the median to a side is also the altitude, since the triangle is symmetric about it. It forms a right triangle with hypotenuse $a$ and leg $\dfrac a2$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(2.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(2,0),C=(1,sqrt(3)),M=(1,0);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(C--M,dashed);draw(M+(0.1,0)--M+(0.1,0.1)--M+(0,0.1));label("$a$",(0.5,sqrt(3)/2),NW);label("$\dfrac a2$",(0.5,0),S);label("$M$",M,SE);
\end{asy}

So its length is $\sqrt{a^2-\dfrac{a^2}4}=\dfrac{\sqrt3}2a$. $\blacksquare$

The perimeter is $4b+4a-2a=4b+2a=86$, so $a=43-2b$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(19,0)--(19,19)--(0,19)--cycle);draw((19,0)--(24,0)--(24,5)--(19,5));label("$b$",(9.5,0),S);label("$a$",(21.5,0),S);label("$a$",(24,2.5),E);
\end{asy}

Then $(43-2b)^2+b^2=386$ gives $5b^2-172b+1463=0$, so $b=19$ (the other root $15.4$ is not an integer), and $a=5$. Check: $25+361=386$. So $a+b=\boxed{24}$.

The right angles at $B,D,E,F$ all subtend $AC$, so all four points lie on the circle with diameter $AC=\sqrt5$. The reflection across $AC$ swaps the rectangles, sending $B\mapsto E$ and $D\mapsto F$. So $BD$ and $EF$ are both diameters, and $BEDF$ is a rectangle whose diagonals have length $\sqrt5$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(2,0),C=(2,1),D=(0,1),E=(1.2,1.6),F=(0.8,-0.6);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--E--C--F--cycle);draw(A--C,dashed);fill(B--E--D--F--cycle,opacity(0.2)+gray);draw(B--E--D--F--cycle,linewidth(1));
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,W);label("$E$",E,N);label("$F$",F,S);
\end{asy}

If $\theta$ is the angle between $BD$ and $AC$, then $EF$ makes angle $2\theta$ with $BD$. The diagonals of $ABCD$ satisfy $\cos\theta=\dfrac{2^2-1^2}{5}=\dfrac35$ and $\sin\theta=\dfrac45$, so $\sin2\theta=\dfrac{24}{25}$. The area is $\dfrac12\cdot5\cdot\dfrac{24}{25}=\boxed{\dfrac{12}5}$.

The longer leg is adjacent to the $36^\circ$ angle. So the hypotenuse is $\dfrac1{\cos36^\circ}=\dfrac4{1+\sqrt5}=\sqrt5-1$, and the short leg is $\sqrt{(\sqrt5-1)^2-1}=\sqrt{5-2\sqrt5}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(1,0),C=(0,tan(radians(36)));draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw((0.07,0)--(0.07,0.07)--(0,0.07));label("$1$",(0.5,0),S);label("$\sqrt{5-2\sqrt5}$",(0,0.36),W);label("$\sqrt5-1$",(0.5,0.36),NE);label("$36^\circ$",B,8*dir(170));
\end{asy}

So the legs are $1$ and $\boxed{\sqrt{5-2\sqrt5}}$ and the hypotenuse is $\boxed{\sqrt5-1}$.

Squaring: $(\sin x+\cos x)^2=\sin^2x+\cos^2x+2\sin x\cos x=1+\sin2x$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(2.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(0.8,0),C=(0.8,0.6);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw((0.75,0)--(0.75,0.05)--(0.8,0.05));label("$1$",(0.4,0.3),NW);label("$\cos x$",(0.4,0),S);label("$\sin x$",(0.8,0.3),E);label("$x$",A,6*dir(18));
\end{asy}

So $1+\sin2x=\dfrac{36}{25}$ and $\sin2x=\boxed{\dfrac{11}{25}}$.

The region is a right triangle of area $50$. The favourable part is a quarter disc of radius $2$ (which fits inside), with area $\pi$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill((0,0)--arc((0,0),2,0,90)--cycle,gray);draw((0,0)--(10,0)--(0,10)--cycle);draw(arc((0,0),2,0,90));label("$10$",(5,0),S);label("$10$",(0,5),W);label("$2$",(1,0),S);
\end{asy}

The probability is $\boxed{\dfrac\pi{50}}$.

The right angle is at $B$, so $H=B$ and $O$ is the midpoint of $AC$. Place $B=(0,0)$, $A=(0,3)$, $C=(4,0)$. Then $O=(2,\dfrac32)$, and with inradius $\dfrac{3+4-5}2=1$, $I=(1,1)$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),A=(0,3),C=(4,0);pair O=(2,1.5),I=(1,1),H=B;draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(circle(I,1),gray);fill(O--I--H--cycle,lightgray);draw(O--I--H--cycle);dot(O);dot(I);dot(H);label("$A$",A,N);label("$C$",C,E);label("$B=H$",B,SW);label("$O$",O,NE);label("$I$",I,W);
\end{asy}

The area is $\dfrac12\left|2\cdot1-1\cdot\dfrac32\right|=\boxed{\dfrac14}$.

The homothety with centre the centroid $G$ and ratio $-\dfrac12$ maps $A,B,C$ to $D,E,F$ respectively. (For example, $B\mapsto E$ because $G$ divides the median $BE$ in the ratio $2:1$.) So it maps $\angle ABC$ onto $\angle DEF$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1,4),B=(0,0),C=(6,0);pair D=(B+C)/2,E=(A+C)/2,F=(A+B)/2;real a=abs(B-C),bb=abs(C-A),cc=abs(A-B);pair I=(a*A+bb*B+cc*C)/(a+bb+cc);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(D--E--F--cycle,gray);draw(B--I,red);pair u=unit(unit(D-E)+unit(F-E));draw(E--E+1.6*u,red+dashed);dot(I);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$E$",E,NE);label("$F$",F,W);label("$I$",I,N);
\end{asy}

A homothety maps angle bisectors to angle bisectors and each line to a parallel line. So the bisector of $\angle DEF$ is parallel to the bisector of $\angle ABC$, which is $BI$. $\blacksquare$

$\dfrac{6-3}{4-2}=\boxed{\dfrac32}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;draw((-0.5,0)--(5,0),gray,Arrows(4));draw((0,-0.5)--(0,7),gray,Arrows(4));draw((0,0)--(4.6,6.9));dot((2,3));dot((4,6));label("$(2,3)$",(2,3),NW);label("$(4,6)$",(4,6),SE);draw((2,3)--(4,3)--(4,6),dashed);label("$2$",(3,3),S);label("$3$",(4,4.5),E);
\end{asy}

Put $O=(0,0)$, $J=(1,0)$, $H=(0,1)$, $N=(1,1)$. The radius is $ON=\sqrt2$, so $R=(\sqrt2,0)$ and $Z=(0,\sqrt2)$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),J=(1,0),H=(0,1),N=(1,1),R=(sqrt(2),0),Z=(0,sqrt(2));draw(O--J--N--H--cycle);draw(arc(O,sqrt(2),-5,95),gray);draw(J--R);draw(H--Z);fill(N--R--Z--cycle,opacity(0.3)+gray);draw(N--R--Z--cycle);
label("$O$",O,SW);label("$J$",J,S);label("$H$",H,W);label("$N$",N,NE);label("$R$",R,S);label("$Z$",Z,W);
\end{asy}

The area is $\dfrac12\left|(\sqrt2-1)(\sqrt2-1)-(-1)(-1)\right|=\dfrac12\left|2-2\sqrt2\right|=\boxed{\sqrt2-1}$.

$OR=OZ$ and $\angle ROZ=60^\circ$, so triangle $ROZ$ is equilateral and the radius is $512$. Let $OJ=p$ and $OH=q$. Then $JR=p-512$ and $ZH=|512-q|$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);pair H=300*dir(60),J=(700,0);pair N=J+H;pair R=(512,0),Z=512*dir(60);draw(O--J--N--H--cycle);draw(arc(O,512,-5,70),gray);draw(H--Z);draw(R--Z,dashed);
label("$O$",O,SW);label("$J$",J,SE);label("$H$",H,NW);label("$N$",N,NE);label("$R$",R,S);label("$Z$",Z,NW);label("$60^\circ$",O,8*dir(30));
\end{asy}

If $q>512$, then $p-512=q-512$ gives $p=q$, which contradicts $p>q$. So $q<512$ and $p-512=512-q$, i.e. $p+q=1024$. The perimeter is $\boxed{2048}$.

Let $a=PC$ and $b=QC$. By the triangle inequality, $PQ\ge|a-b|$. So $\left|\dfrac1a-\dfrac1b\right|=\dfrac{|a-b|}{ab}=PQ\ge|a-b|$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);draw(circle(O,1),gray);pair P=0.6*dir(40),Q=1.8*dir(-20);draw(O--P--Q--cycle,dashed);dot(O);dot(P);dot(Q);label("$C$",O,W);label("$P$",P,N);label("$Q$",Q,E);label("$1$",dir(150),NW);
\end{asy}

If $a=b$, then $PQ=0$, contradicting $P\ne Q$. So $|a-b|>0$, and dividing gives $ab\le1$. Hence $\min(a,b)\le1$, and one point lies on or inside the unit sphere about $C$. $\blacksquare$

Near the vertex $(0,0,0)$ we need nonnegative $(x,y,z)$ with $x^2+y^2+z^2=169$. The only ways to write $169$ as a sum of three squares are $13^2+0+0$, $12^2+5^2+0$ and $12^2+4^2+3^2$. Their permutations give $3+6+6=15$ points per vertex, all inside the cube since every coordinate is at most $13$.

No point is counted twice. Being $13$ from two vertices that differ in, say, the $x$-coordinate would need $x\in\{0,3,4,5,12,13\}$ and $25-x\in\{0,3,4,5,12,13\}$, so $x\in\{12,13\}$. With $x=13$, the first vertex forces $y=z=0$, while the second needs a coordinate pair from $\{(5,0),(4,3),\dots\}$ with the $12$ in $x$, which fails; $x=12$ is symmetric. The total is $8\cdot15=\boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ 120}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=2.5;pair d=(1.1,0.8);pair[] F={(0,0),(s,0),(s,s),(0,s)};draw(F[0]--F[1]--F[2]--F[3]--cycle);draw(F[1]--F[1]+d--F[2]+d--F[3]+d--F[3]);draw(F[2]--F[2]+d);draw(F[0]--F[0]+d--F[1]+d,dashed);draw(F[0]+d--F[3]+d,dashed);draw(arc(F[0],1.3,0,90),red);dot(F[0]);label("$(0,0,0)$",F[0],SW);label("$25$",(s/2,0),S);label("$13$",1.3*dir(45),NE,red);
\end{asy}

For $x$: the square's far vertex $(x,x)$ lies on the hypotenuse $\dfrac X4+\dfrac Y3=1$, so $\dfrac x4+\dfrac x3=1$ and $x=\dfrac{12}7$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),A=(0,3),B=(4,0);real x=12/7;draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw((x,0)--(x,x)--(0,x));label("$x$",(x/2,0),S);label("$3$",(0,1.5),W);label("$4$",(2,0),S);
\end{asy}

For $y$: with the hypotenuse $c=5$ as base and altitude $h=\dfrac{12}5$, the part of the triangle above the square is similar to the whole: $\dfrac{h-y}h=\dfrac yc$, so $y=\dfrac{ch}{c+h}=\dfrac{12}{\dfrac{37}5}=\dfrac{60}{37}$. Then $\dfrac xy=\dfrac{12}7\cdot\dfrac{37}{60}=\boxed{\dfrac{37}{35}}$.

The stronghold lies on the line $y=\dfrac74x$ and on the line through $(50,0)$ with slope $3$, $y=3(x-50)$. Solving $\dfrac74x=3x-150$ gives $x=120$ and $y=210$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;draw((-10,0)--(140,0),gray,Arrows(4));draw((0,-10)--(0,230),gray,Arrows(4));draw((0,0)--(120,210));draw((50,0)--(120,210));dot((0,0));dot((50,0));dot((120,210));dot((4,7));dot((53,9));label("$(120,210)$",(120,210),E);label("$(50,0)$",(50,0),SE);label("$O$",(0,0),W);
\end{asy}

The stronghold is at $\boxed{(120,210)}$.

Let the legs be $a,b$ and the hypotenuse $c$. We show $12\mid ab$, so the area $\dfrac{ab}2$ is a multiple of $6$.

\emph{Factor $3$:} squares are $0$ or $1\pmod3$. If $3\nmid a$ and $3\nmid b$, then $c^2\equiv2$, which is impossible. So $3\mid ab$.

\emph{Factor $4$:} if $a,b$ are both odd, $c^2\equiv2\pmod4$, which is impossible. If both are even, $4\mid ab$. Otherwise say $a$ is even and $b,c$ are odd. Odd squares are $\equiv1\pmod8$, so $a^2=c^2-b^2\equiv0\pmod8$, which forces $4\mid a$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(2.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),A=(0,3),B=(4,0);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw((0.25,0)--(0.25,0.25)--(0,0.25));label("$a$",(0,1.5),W);label("$b$",(2,0),S);label("$c$",(2,1.5),NE);
\end{asy}

So $12\mid ab$, and the area is an integer divisible by $6$. $\blacksquare$

Suppose the point of tangency moves through an angle $\theta$ around the clock. The arc of the clock face rolled over has length $20\theta$, so the disk turns through $\dfrac{20\theta}{10}=2\theta$ relative to the line joining the two centres. That line itself has turned through $\theta$, so the disk has turned through a total of $2\theta+\theta=3\theta$ in the plane.

The arrow next points up when $3\theta=360^\circ$, i.e. $\theta=120^\circ$. Moving $120^\circ$ clockwise from $12$ o'clock reaches $4$ o'clock.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
draw(circle((0,0),2));dot((0,0));label("$O$",(0,0),SW);
pair P0=(0,3),P1=3*dir(-30);
draw(circle(P0,1),gray);draw(P0--P0+(0,0.8),gray,Arrow);
draw(circle(P1,1));draw(P1--P1+(0,0.8),Arrow);
draw(arc((0,0),4.3,80,-12,CW),dashed,Arrow);
draw((0,0)--P1,dotted);draw((0,0)--P0,dotted);
label("$12$",(0,1.75));label("$4$",1.75*dir(-30));
label("$120^\circ$",(0,0),5*dir(30));
