question:
Each time a player completes a certain dungeon, a rare item drops with probability $0.09\%$, independently of all other runs.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the probability that the item drops on the $23$rd run?
\item[(b)] The player stops as soon as the item drops for the first time. What is the probability that the player stops after exactly $23$ runs?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
$\dfrac5{108}=\dfrac{10}{216}$, so $n$ is a sum obtained in exactly $10$ of the $216$ ordered outcomes of three dice. The numbers of ways to get $3,4,\dots,10$ are $1,3,6,10,15,21,25,27$, and the counts are symmetric about $10.5$. So $n=6$ or $n=15$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $n=6$: two dice give sum $6$ in $5$ ways, so the probability is $\dfrac5{36}$.
\item $n=15$: two dice cannot sum to $15$, so the probability is $0$.
\end{itemize}
The possible values are $\boxed{\dfrac5{36}\text{ or }0}$.

