question:
Let $a,b$ be positive integers.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $\dfrac{a+b}{ab}$ is in lowest terms if and only if $\gcd(a,b)=1$.
\item[(b)] Deduce what happens when $a$ is prime, and when $a$ and $b$ are both prime.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The base and frosting cost $15$, $17$, $20$, $22$, $22$ or $24$ dollars. The toppings cost an even amount, so the remainder $30-(\text{base}+\text{frosting})$ must be even:
\begin{itemize}
\item \$20 ($15+5$): $5$ toppings, $\binom{10}5=252$ ways;
\item \$22 ($15+7$ or $17+5$): $4$ toppings, $2\binom{10}4=420$ ways;
\item \$24 ($17+7$): $3$ toppings, $\binom{10}3=120$ ways.
\end{itemize}
