question:
A student claims that the infinite tower $t=e^{\dfrac\pi2e^{\dfrac\pi2e^{\cdots}}}$ satisfies $t=e^{\dfrac{\pi t}2}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the equation $t=e^{\dfrac{\pi t}2}$ has no real solution.
\item[(b)] Show that $t=i$ is a complex solution.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Equal chords are equidistant from $O$. Put $O$ at the origin with $AB$ on $y=t$ and $CD$ on $x=t$ for some $t>0$. Then $E=(t,t)$ lies on the line $y=x$ through $O$, at distance $OE=t\sqrt2$.

Each square has its sides along the chords, so its diagonal from $E$ lies along the line $y=x$, i.e. along the diameter through $E$. The larger square's diagonal runs from $E$ through $O$ to the circle, with length $6+OE$. The smaller one runs from $E$ away from $O$, with length $6-OE$. Together they form a diameter.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real t=(12-6*sqrt(3))/sqrt(2),R=6;real w=sqrt(R^2-t^2);
pair O=(0,0),E=(t,t);
draw(circle(O,R));
pair A=(-w,t),B=(w,t),C=(t,w),D=(t,-w);draw(A--B);draw(C--D);
real s1=(6+sqrt(2)*t)/sqrt(2),s2=(6-sqrt(2)*t)/sqrt(2);
filldraw(E--E-(s1,0)--E-(s1,s1)--E-(0,s1)--cycle,opacity(0.15)+blue,blue);
filldraw(E--E+(s2,0)--E+(s2,s2)--E+(0,s2)--cycle,opacity(0.15)+red,red);
draw(E-(s1,s1)--E+(s2,s2),dashed);
dot(O);label("$O$",O,SE);dot(E);label("$E$",E,SE);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,S);
\end{asy}

Areas are proportional to squared diagonals, so $(6+OE)^2=3(6-OE)^2$, i.e. $6+OE=\sqrt3(6-OE)$. Hence
\[OE=\dfrac{6(\sqrt3-1)}{\sqrt3+1}=6(2-\sqrt3)=12-6\sqrt3,\qquad t^2=\dfrac{OE^2}{2}=\dfrac{252-144\sqrt3}{2}=126-72\sqrt3.\]
The chord $AB$ is at distance $t$ from $O$, so
\[AB=2\sqrt{36-t^2}=2\sqrt{72\sqrt3-90}\approx2\sqrt{34.708}\approx\boxed{11.78}\text{ cm}.\]

question:
