Let $n$ be a positive integer. Consider
\begin{itemize}
\item the number of lattice paths from $(0,0)$ to $(n,n)$ using unit steps right ($R$) and up ($U$) that never go above the line $y=x$, and
\item the number of ways to arrange $n$ opening and $n$ closing parentheses so that they match correctly (every prefix has at least as many opening as closing parentheses).
\end{itemize}
Prove that these two numbers are equal, and that both equal $\dfrac1{n+1}\binom{2n}{n}$.

solution:
In the hexagonal tiling, two hexagons that share a vertex also share an edge. Colour the hexagons with three colours so that neighbouring hexagons get different colours: in axial coordinates $(q,s)$ (the six neighbours differ by $(\pm1,0)$, $(0,\pm1)$, $\pm(1,-1)$), give the hexagon $(q,s)$ the colour $(q-s)\bmod3$. The difference $q-s$ changes by $\pm1$ or $\pm2$ between neighbours, never by a multiple of $3$, so neighbours get different colours.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=0.55;pen[] col={rgb(0.85,0.3,0.3),rgb(0.3,0.6,0.85),rgb(0.95,0.8,0.3)};
for(int q=-3;q<=3;++q)for(int s=-2;s<=2;++s){pair c=(sqrt(3)*r*(q+s/2),1.5*r*s);
if(abs(c.x)>2.2) continue;
path h=c+r*dir(30)--c+r*dir(90)--c+r*dir(150)--c+r*dir(210)--c+r*dir(270)--c+r*dir(330)--cycle;
int k=((q-s)%3+3)%3;filldraw(h,col[k],black);label(string(k+1),c);}
\end{asy}

The $1998$ marked hexagons fall into $3$ colour classes, so by the pigeonhole principle some class contains at least $\dfrac{1998}{3}=666$ marked hexagons. Choose $666$ of them. They all have the same colour, so no two of them are neighbours, and no two share a vertex. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{Step 1 (an identity).} Fix $m\ge1$ and let $\theta_k=\dfrac{k\pi}{2m+1}$ for $k=1,\dots,m$. By de Moivre,
\[\sin((2m+1)\theta)=\operatorname{Im}(\cos\theta+i\sin\theta)^{2m+1}=\sin^{2m+1}\theta\sum_{j=0}^{m}(-1)^j\binom{2m+1}{2j+1}\cot^{2m-2j}\theta.\]
At $\theta=\theta_k$ the left side is $0$ and $\sin\theta_k\ne0$. So each $x_k=\cot^2\theta_k$ is a root of
\[p(x)=\binom{2m+1}{1}x^m-\binom{2m+1}{3}x^{m-1}+\cdots.\]
The $x_k$ are distinct (cotangent is decreasing and positive on $\left(0,\dfrac\pi2\right)$), so they are all $m$ roots of $p$. By Vieta,
\[\sum_{k=1}^m\cot^2\theta_k=\dfrac{\binom{2m+1}{3}}{\binom{2m+1}{1}}=\dfrac{m(2m-1)}{3}.\]

\textbf{Step 2 (an inequality).} For $0<\theta<\dfrac\pi2$, comparing triangle $OAP$, sector $OAP$ and triangle $OAT$ in the unit circle gives $\sin\theta<\theta<\tan\theta$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real t=40;pair O=(0,0),A=(1,0),P=dir(t),Q=(1,tan(radians(t)));
draw(arc(O,1,-5,70));draw(O--A--Q--cycle);draw(O--P);draw(P--(P.x,0),dashed);
draw(arc(O,0.25,0,t));label("$\theta$",0.25*dir(t/2),E);
label("$O$",O,SW);label("$A$",A,SE);label("$P$",P,NW);label("$T$",Q,NE);
\end{asy}

Hence $\cot^2\theta<\dfrac1{\theta^2}<\dfrac{1}{\sin^2\theta}=1+\cot^2\theta$.

\textbf{Step 3 (squeeze).} Summing over $\theta=\theta_k$, and using $\dfrac1{\theta_k^2}=\dfrac{(2m+1)^2}{\pi^2k^2}$,
\[\dfrac{m(2m-1)}{3}<\dfrac{(2m+1)^2}{\pi^2}\sum_{k=1}^m\dfrac1{k^2}<m+\dfrac{m(2m-1)}3=\dfrac{2m(m+1)}{3}.\]
Multiply by $\dfrac{\pi^2}{(2m+1)^2}$ and let $m\to\infty$. Both $\dfrac{\pi^2m(2m-1)}{3(2m+1)^2}$ and $\dfrac{2\pi^2m(m+1)}{3(2m+1)^2}$ tend to $\dfrac{\pi^2}{6}$, so $\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^\infty\dfrac1{k^2}=\dfrac{\pi^2}6$. $\blacksquare$

Let $x=AF$, $y=BD$, $z=CE$ and $u=BF$, $v=CD$, $w=AE$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0),C=(2,4),P=(2.6,1.5);
pair ft(pair X,pair U,pair V){return U+dot(X-U,V-U)/dot(V-U,V-U)*(V-U);}
pair D=ft(P,B,C),E=ft(P,A,C),F=ft(P,A,B);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(P--D,dashed);draw(P--E,dashed);draw(P--F,dashed);
draw(P--A,dotted);draw(P--B,dotted);draw(P--C,dotted);
dot(P);label("$P$",P,N);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);
label("$D$",D,NE);label("$E$",E,NW);label("$F$",F,S);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Carnot's relation.} By Pythagoras in the right triangles at $F$: $AF^2+PF^2=PA^2$ and $BF^2+PF^2=PB^2$, so $AF^2-BF^2=PA^2-PB^2$. Similarly $BD^2-CD^2=PB^2-PC^2$ and $CE^2-AE^2=PC^2-PA^2$. Adding,
\[x^2+y^2+z^2=u^2+v^2+w^2.\]

\textbf{Symmetric functions.} The hypotheses say $xyz=uvw$ and $\dfrac{xy+yz+zx}{xyz}=\dfrac{uv+vw+wu}{uvw}$, so $xy+yz+zx=uv+vw+wu$. Then
\[(x+y+z)^2=x^2+y^2+z^2+2(xy+yz+zx)=(u+v+w)^2,\]
and since all lengths are positive, $x+y+z=u+v+w$.

So $x,y,z$ and $u,v,w$ have the same elementary symmetric functions. They are therefore the roots of the same cubic $t^3-(x+y+z)t^2+(xy+yz+zx)t-xyz$, and the multisets $\{x,y,z\}$ and $\{u,v,w\}$ are equal. $\blacksquare$

We need $x\ne1$ and $-1<\dfrac{x+1}{x-1}<1$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $\dfrac{x+1}{x-1}<1\iff\dfrac{2}{x-1}<0\iff x<1$.
\item $\dfrac{x+1}{x-1}>-1\iff\dfrac{2x}{x-1}>0\iff x<0\text{ or }x>1$.
\end{itemize}
Both hold exactly when $x<0$, so the domain is $\boxed{(-\infty,0)}$.

question:
A nonvertical line through the origin meets the parabola $y=1001x^2-9$ at exactly $n$ points.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the sum of all possible values of $n$.
\item[(b)] Answer the same question for the parabola $y=1001x^2+9$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
A nonvertical line through the origin is $y=mx$. Its intersections with $y=1001x^2+k$ satisfy $1001x^2-mx+k=0$, with discriminant $m^2-4004k$.

(a) $k=-9$: the discriminant $m^2+36036>0$ for every $m$, so there are always exactly $2$ points. The only value is $n=2$, and the sum is $\boxed{2}$.

(b) $k=9$: the discriminant $m^2-36036$ can be negative, zero or positive (for example $m=0$, $m=\sqrt{36036}$, $m=200$), giving $n=0,1,2$. The sum is $\boxed{3}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
draw(graph(new real(real x){return 0.3*x^2-1;},-2.8,2.8),blue);
draw((-3,0)--(3,0),Arrow);draw((0,-1.6)--(0,1.8),Arrow);
draw((-1.6,-0.96)--(1.6,0.96),red);draw((-2.4,-0.24)--(2.4,0.24),red+dashed);
dot((0,0));label("$O$",(0,0),SE);label("$y=1001x^2-9$",(-2.8,1.35),N,blue);label("$y=mx$",(-1.6,-0.96),W,red);
\end{asy}

(The sketch shows case (a): every line through $O$ crosses the downward-shifted parabola twice, because $O$ lies inside it.)

Write $X\sim Y$ if $X$ can be cut into finitely many polygonal pieces that reassemble into $Y$. Clearly $\sim$ is symmetric. It is also transitive: if $X\sim Y$ and $Y\sim Z$, overlay on $Y$ the two systems of cuts. Their common refinement cuts $Y$ into pieces, each of which lies inside a piece of both systems; moving these small pieces back gives $X\sim Z$.

\textbf{Step 1: a triangle is equivalent to a rectangle.} Take the longest side $AB$ as base, so the foot of the altitude from $C$ lies on $AB$; let the height be $h$. Cut along the midline $MN$ ($M,N$ midpoints of $CA$, $CB$) and along the perpendicular $CK$ from $C$ to $MN$. Rotating triangle $CMK$ by $180^\circ$ about $M$ and triangle $CKN$ by $180^\circ$ about $N$ fills the two corners, giving a rectangle with base $AB$ and height $\dfrac h2$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(5,0),C=(1.8,3);pair M=(A+C)/2,N=(B+C)/2,K=(C.x,M.y),H=(C.x,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(M--N);draw(C--K,dashed);
fill(A--(0,M.y)--M--cycle,opacity(0.3)+red);fill(B--(5,M.y)--N--cycle,opacity(0.3)+blue);
fill(C--M--K--cycle,opacity(0.3)+red);fill(C--K--N--cycle,opacity(0.3)+blue);
draw(A--(0,M.y)--(5,M.y)--B,dotted);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$M$",M,W);label("$N$",N,NE);label("$K$",K,S);
label("$\dfrac h2$",(5,M.y/2),E);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 2: parallelograms on a common base.} Two parallelograms with the same base and the same height are equivalent. If one is obtained from the other by sliding the top side a distance at most the base length, cut off a triangle from one end and translate it to the other end. A larger slide is a chain of such small slides, so transitivity finishes it.

\textbf{Step 3: any rectangle is equivalent to a rectangle of width $1$.} Let the rectangle be $W\times H$. If $H>1$, cut it in half and place the halves side by side to get $2W\times\dfrac H2$; repeat until $H\le1$. Now tilt: by Step 2 the rectangle is equivalent to a parallelogram with base $W$, height $H$ and a slanted side of length $1$ (possible since $1\ge H$). Viewing that parallelogram as standing on a side of length $1$, Step 2 again makes it equivalent to a rectangle with a side $1$ and the other side $WH$.

\textbf{Step 4.} Triangulate $\mathcal{P}$ into triangles $T_1,\dots,T_m$. Each $T_i$ is equivalent to a $1\times[T_i]$ rectangle, and stacking these gives $\mathcal{P}\sim$ a $1\times[\mathcal{P}]$ rectangle. Likewise $\mathcal{Q}\sim$ a $1\times[\mathcal{Q}]$ rectangle, which is the same rectangle since $[\mathcal{P}]=[\mathcal{Q}]$. By transitivity, $\mathcal{P}\sim\mathcal{Q}$. $\blacksquare$

The right angles of the rectangle subtend its diagonals, so each diagonal is a diameter, of length $2$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=degrees(atan2(1,1.6));pair O=(0,0);pair P=dir(a),Q=dir(180-a),R=dir(180+a),S2=dir(-a);
draw(circle(O,1));draw(P--Q--R--S2--cycle);draw(P--R,dashed);dot(O);label("$O$",O,S);
label("$a$",(P+Q)/2,N);label("$b$",(P+S2)/2,E);label("$2$",(0.35,0.3),NW);
\end{asy}

With sides $a,b$: $a^2+b^2=4$. By the QM-AM inequality (or $(a-b)^2\ge0$), $(a+b)^2\le2(a^2+b^2)=8$, so $a+b\le2\sqrt2$, with equality when $a=b=\sqrt2$ (a square). The maximum perimeter is $2(a+b)=\boxed{4\sqrt2}$.

Since $75^2+100^2=125^2$, the angle at $B$ is right. Place $B=(0,0)$, $C=(100,0)$ and $A=(0,75)$.

In a right triangle, $CD=\dfrac{BC^2}{CA}=\dfrac{10000}{125}=80$, so $D$ lies $\dfrac{80}{125}=\dfrac{16}{25}$ of the way from $C$ to $A$:
\[D=(100,0)+\dfrac{16}{25}(-100,75)=(36,48).\]
Then $E=(36,0)$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $D$ to $BC$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(5,0),A=(0,3.75),D=(1.8,2.4),E=(1.8,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(B--D);draw(D--E,dashed);
draw((0,0.2)--(0.2,0.2)--(0.2,0));draw((1.8,0.2)--(1.6,0.2)--(1.6,0));
pair u=unit(A-C),w=unit(B-D);draw(D+0.2*u--D+0.2*u+0.2*w--D+0.2*w);
label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,NE);label("$E$",E,S);
label("$75$",(0,1.9),W);label("$BC=100$",(3.4,0),S);label("$36$",(0.9,0),S);label("$48$",(1.8,1.2),E);
\end{asy}

So $[BDE]=\dfrac12\cdot36\cdot48=\boxed{864}$.

question:
A rook stands on an otherwise empty $8\times8$ chessboard. On each move it chooses uniformly at random one of its $14$ possible moves (any number of squares horizontally or vertically).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If the rook starts on a square that is neither in the top row nor in the left column, find the expected number of moves until it first reaches the top-left square.
\item[(b)] If instead the starting square is chosen uniformly at random among all $64$ squares (starting on the target counts as $0$ moves), find the expected number of moves.
\end{enumerate}

Two of four ponies (pink, purple, rainbow and blue) are chosen uniformly at random for a ride.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the probability that the pink and purple ponies are both chosen?
\item[(b)] A student says ``each pony has a $\dfrac14$ chance of being chosen''. Find the correct probability that a given pony is chosen, and explain the error.
\end{enumerate}

A rare pet drops with probability $\dfrac1{30000000}$ each time a monster is defeated, independently.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the probability that at least one pet drops in $28315$ defeats?
\item[(b)] What is the expected number of defeats needed to get one pet?
\end{enumerate}

Effective health is computed as $H\left(1+\dfrac{D}{100}\right)$, where $H$ is health and $D$ is defence.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the effective health when $H=1570$ and $D=661$.
\item[(b)] A bonus increases both $H$ and $D$ by $5\%$. Find the new effective health, to the nearest integer.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $AB=AC=1$ with $\angle A=36^\circ$, and let $BC=x$. Draw the bisector $CD$ of $\angle C$, with $D$ on $AB$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,cos(radians(18))),B=(-sin(radians(18)),0),C=(sin(radians(18)),0);
pair D=A+(2*sin(radians(18)))*unit(B-A);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(C--D,dashed);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,W);
label("$36^\circ$",A-(0,0.33));label("$1$",(A+C)/2,E);label("$x$",(B+C)/2,S);
\end{asy}

Then $\angle DCA=36^\circ=\angle A$, so $AD=DC$; and $\angle CDB=72^\circ=\angle B$, so $DC=BC=x$. Hence $AD=x$ and $DB=1-x$. Triangles $CBD$ and $ABC$ are similar (both have angles $36^\circ,72^\circ,72^\circ$), so
\[\dfrac{BD}{BC}=\dfrac{BC}{AB}\implies\dfrac{1-x}{x}=\dfrac x1\implies x^2+x-1=0.\]
So $x=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{2}}$.

Let the edges be $a,b,c$ with $ab=60$, $bc=30$, $ca=18$. Multiplying, $(abc)^2=60\cdot30\cdot18=32400$, so $abc=180$. Then $c=\dfrac{180}{60}=3$, $a=\dfrac{180}{30}=6$ and $b=\dfrac{180}{18}=10$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair d=(1.5,1.0);real L=6,H=3;
draw((0,0)--(L,0)--(L,H)--(0,H)--cycle);draw((L,0)--(L,0)+d--(L,H)+d--(L,H));draw((0,H)--(0,H)+d--(L,H)+d);
draw((0,0)--d--d+(L,0),dashed);draw(d--d+(0,H),dashed);
label("$a=6$",(L/2,0),S);label("$c=3$",(0,H/2),W);label("$b=10$",(L,0)+d/2,SE);
\end{asy}

The volume is $180$ and the surface area is $2(60+30+18)=216$, so the answer is $180-216=\boxed{-36}$.

The area is a continuous function of the three points on the circle, which range over a closed bounded set, so a maximum is attained.

\textbf{Claim.} In a triangle of maximum area, $AB=AC$. Keep $B,C$ fixed. The area is $\dfrac12BC\cdot h$, where $h$ is the distance from $A$ to line $BC$. Among points of the circle, $h$ is largest at the midpoint $A'$ of the arc $BC$ that is farther from line $BC$, i.e. the point where the perpendicular bisector of $BC$ meets the circle on the far side. This point is unique, and it satisfies $A'B=A'C$. If $AB\ne AC$, then $A\ne A'$ and replacing $A$ by $A'$ strictly increases the area.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);pair A=dir(145),B=dir(210),C=dir(330),P=dir(90);
draw(circle(O,1));draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(P--B,dashed);draw(P--C,dashed);draw(P--(0,B.y),dotted);
dot(O);label("$O$",O,E);label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$A'$",P,N);
\end{asy}

Applying the claim at every vertex, a maximal triangle has $AB=AC$ and $BA=BC$, so it is equilateral. All equilateral triangles inscribed in the circle are congruent, so the maximum is attained exactly by the equilateral triangles. $\blacksquare$

Each angle is $120^\circ$. Extend the sides $a_1$, $a_3$, $a_5$ until they meet. Each cut-off corner triangle has two angles of $180^\circ-120^\circ=60^\circ$, so it is equilateral, with side equal to the hexagon side it sits on ($a_2$, $a_4$ or $a_6$). The three extended lines form a large equilateral triangle.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real[] s={4,1,3,3,2,2};pair P=(0,0);pair[] V;
for(int i=0;i<6;++i){V.push(P);P=P+s[i]*dir(60*i);}
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(1));
pair Ta=extension(V[0],V[1],V[2],V[3]),Tb=extension(V[2],V[3],V[4],V[5]),Tc=extension(V[4],V[5],V[0],V[1]);
draw(V[1]--Ta--V[2],dashed);draw(V[3]--Tb--V[4],dashed);draw(V[5]--Tc--V[0],dashed);
for(int i=0;i<6;++i) label("$a_{"+string(i+1)+"}$",(V[i]+V[(i+1)%6])/2,-dir(60*i+90));
\end{asy}

Its sides are $a_6+a_1+a_2$, $a_2+a_3+a_4$ and $a_4+a_5+a_6$, and they are equal:
\[a_6+a_1+a_2=a_2+a_3+a_4=a_4+a_5+a_6.\]
The first equality gives $a_1-a_4=a_3-a_6$, and the second gives $a_3-a_6=a_5-a_2$. Taking absolute values, the positive differences of opposite sides are equal. $\blacksquare$

The totals over $10$ minutes for each start are:
\begin{itemize}
\item start $6$: $6,9,6,9,\dots$, total $5\cdot15=75$;
\item start $9$: likewise $75$;
\item start $4$: $4+9\cdot2=22$;
\item start $2$: $10\cdot2=20$;
\item start $0$: $0$.
\end{itemize}
The expected total is $\dfrac{75+75+22+20+0}{5}=\boxed{\dfrac{192}5}$.

$X$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$, so $XA=XB$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(14,0),A=(12,5);pair M=(A+B)/2;
pair X=extension(M,M+rotate(90)*(A-B),B,C);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--X,dashed);draw(M--X,dotted);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$X$",X,S);label("$M$",M,NW);label("$AB=13$",0.75*A,NW);label("$BC=14$",(10.5,0),S);
\end{asy}

Then $N=AX+XC+CA=BX+XC+CA=BC+CA$, while $M=AB+BC+CA$. So $M-N=AB=\boxed{13}$.

By Heron's formula with $s=75$, the area is $\sqrt{75\cdot26\cdot25\cdot24}=\sqrt{1170000}=300\sqrt{13}$. The longest altitude is the one to the shortest side, $49$:
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(49,0);real xa=(50^2-51^2+49^2)/(2*49);pair A=(xa,sqrt(50^2-xa^2));
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--(xa,0),dashed);draw((xa,0)--(xa,2)--(xa+2*(xa<0?-1:1),2)--(xa+2*(xa<0?-1:1),0));
label("$49$",(24.5,0),S);label("$50$",(A+B)/2,NW);label("$51$",(A+C)/2,NE);label("$h$",(xa,A.y/2),W);
\end{asy}

\[h=\dfrac{2\cdot300\sqrt{13}}{49}=\dfrac{600\sqrt{13}}{49}.\]
So $x+y+z=600+13+49=\boxed{662}$.

Let the legs be $a,b$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),A=(4,0),B=(0,2.4);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw((0,0.2)--(0.2,0.2)--(0.2,0));
label("$a$",(0,1.2),W);label("$b$",(2,0),S);label("$c$",(2,1.2),NE);
\end{asy}

Then $\dfrac12ab=c$ and $a^2+b^2=c^2$, so $(a+b)^2=c^2+4c$ and $a+b=\sqrt{c^2+4c}$. The perimeter is $\boxed{c+\sqrt{c^2+4c}}$.

Real legs with this sum and product exist iff $(a-b)^2=c^2-4c\ge0$, i.e. $c\ge4$.

Let $AB=a$, $BC=c$ and the side be $s$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),A=(0,4),C=(6,0);real s=4*6/10;draw(A--B--C--cycle);
filldraw((0,0)--(0,s)--(s,s)--(s,0)--cycle,opacity(0.15)+blue,blue);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$X$",(0,s),W);label("$Y$",(s,0),S);label("$P$",(s,s),NE);
label("$s$",(s/2,s),N);label("$a$",(0,1.2),W);label("$c$",(4.3,0),S);
\end{asy}

Triangle $AXP$ is similar to $ABC$ (since $XP\parallel BC$), so $\dfrac{XP}{BC}=\dfrac{AX}{AB}$, i.e. $\dfrac sc=\dfrac{a-s}{a}$. Hence $as=ac-cs$ and
\[s=\dfrac{ac}{a+c}=\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac{2ac}{a+c},\]
which is half the harmonic mean of $a$ and $c$. $\blacksquare$

Let $M$ be the midpoint of $BC$. Triangle $OBC$ has two $45^\circ$ angles, so it is a right isosceles triangle with $OM\perp BC$ and $OM=\dfrac{BC}2=8$. Also $AM\perp BC$ and $AM=\sqrt{17^2-8^2}=15$. So $BC$ is perpendicular to the plane $OAM$, and the distance from $O$ to plane $ABC$ equals the distance from $O$ to line $AM$ (the foot $F$ lies on $AM$).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Bp=(0,0),Cp=(6,0),Ap=(4.2,2.6),Mp=(3,0);pair Op=(2.4,3.6);pair Fp=Mp+0.285*(Ap-Mp);
draw(Bp--Cp--Ap--cycle);draw(Op--Bp,dashed);draw(Op--Cp,dashed);draw(Op--Mp,dashed);draw(Mp--Ap,dotted);draw(Op--Fp,red);
dot(Fp,red);label("$B$",Bp,SW);label("$C$",Cp,SE);label("$A$",Ap,E);label("$M$",Mp,S);label("$O$",Op,N);label("$F$",Fp,SE,red);
\end{asy}

In triangle $OAM$, $OM=8$, $AM=15$, $OA=\sqrt{161}$, so
\[\cos\angle OMA=\dfrac{64+225-161}{2\cdot8\cdot15}=\dfrac{8}{15},\qquad\sin\angle OMA=\dfrac{\sqrt{161}}{15}.\]
The distance is $OM\sin\angle OMA=\dfrac{8\sqrt{161}}{15}$. (The foot $F$ is $8\cdot\dfrac8{15}=\dfrac{64}{15}<15$ from $M$ toward $A$, so it lies inside the triangle.) With $161=7\cdot23$ squarefree, $a+b+c=8+161+15=\boxed{184}$.

Triangles $ABP$ and $ACP$ have right angles at $P$, so their circumcentres are the midpoints of their hypotenuses: $O_1$ is the midpoint of $AB$ and $O_2$ the midpoint of $AC$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(17,0),A=(6,22),P=(6,0);pair O1=(A+B)/2,O2=(A+C)/2;
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--P,dashed);filldraw(O1--O2--P--cycle,opacity(0.2)+blue,blue);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$P$",P,S);label("$O_1$",O1,W);label("$O_2$",O2,E);
\end{asy}

So $O_1O_2\parallel BC$ with $O_1O_2=\dfrac{BC}2=17$, and it lies on the midline at height $\dfrac{AP}2=22$ above $BC$. Hence $[O_1O_2P]=\dfrac12\cdot17\cdot22=\boxed{187}$.

Let $AB=c$ and $BC=a$, so $AC^2=a^2+c^2$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),A=(0,4),C=(6,0);draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(A--(3,0),dashed);draw(C--(0,2),dashed);draw(B--(3,2),dashed);draw((0,0.25)--(0.25,0.25)--(0.25,0));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$x$",(1.5,2),E);label("$z$",(3.5,1),N);label("$y$",(1.5,1),SE);
\end{asy}

The median from $A$ goes to the midpoint of $BC$: $x^2=c^2+\dfrac{a^2}4$. Similarly $z^2=a^2+\dfrac{c^2}4$. The median from the right angle is half the hypotenuse, so $y^2=\dfrac{a^2+c^2}4$. Then
\[x^2+z^2=\dfrac54(a^2+c^2)=5y^2,\]
so $y=\sqrt{\dfrac{x^2+z^2}5}$. $\blacksquare$

Triangles $IBD$ and $ICD$ share the height from $I$ to $BC$, so their area ratio is $\dfrac{BD}{DC}=\dfrac{AB}{AC}$ by the angle bisector theorem. Similarly $\dfrac{[IAF]}{[IBF]}=\dfrac{AF}{FB}=\dfrac{CA}{CB}$ and $\dfrac{[ICE]}{[IAE]}=\dfrac{CE}{EA}=\dfrac{BC}{BA}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.5,4),B=(0,0),C=(5,0);real a=abs(B-C),bb=abs(C-A),c=abs(A-B);pair I=(a*A+bb*B+c*C)/(a+bb+c);
pair D=extension(A,I,B,C),E=extension(B,I,A,C),F=extension(C,I,A,B);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--D,dashed);draw(B--E,dashed);draw(C--F,dashed);dot(I);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$E$",E,NE);label("$F$",F,W);label("$I$",I,SE);
\end{asy}

With $a=BC$, $b=CA$, $c=AB$: $\dfrac cb=\dfrac{18}{19}$ and $\dfrac ba=\dfrac{20}{21}$. Then
\[\dfrac ac=\dfrac{a}{b}\cdot\dfrac bc=\dfrac{21}{20}\cdot\dfrac{19}{18}=\dfrac{399}{360}=\dfrac{133}{120},\]
so $m+n=\boxed{253}$.

Let $a=BC$, $b=CA$, $c=AB$, so $2a=b+c$. By the angle bisector theorem $BD=\dfrac{ac}{b+c}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(5,0);pair A=(1.1,3.84);real a=abs(B-C),bb=abs(C-A),c=abs(A-B);pair I=(a*A+bb*B+c*C)/(a+bb+c);pair D=extension(A,I,B,C);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--D,dashed);draw(B--I,dotted);dot(I);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$I$",I,E);
\end{asy}

In triangle $ABD$, $BI$ bisects $\angle B$, so again by the angle bisector theorem
\[\dfrac{AI}{ID}=\dfrac{AB}{BD}=\dfrac{c(b+c)}{ac}=\dfrac{b+c}{a}=2.\ \blacksquare\]

Let $D,E,F$ be the feet of the altitudes from $A,B,C$ (on the lines $BC,CA,AB$).

The circles with diameters $AB$ and $AC$ both pass through $A$. They also both pass through $D$, because $\angle ADB=\angle ADC=90^\circ$ (Thales). If $D\ne A$ these are two distinct common points, so their common chord lies on line $AD$, the altitude from $A$. Similarly the other two common chords lie on the altitudes from $B$ and from $C$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.3,3.2),B=(0,0),C=(4,0);pair D=(A.x,0);
pair foot(pair P,pair U,pair V){return U+dot(P-U,V-U)/dot(V-U,V-U)*(V-U);}
pair E=foot(B,A,C),F=foot(C,A,B);pair H=extension(A,D,B,E);
draw(circle((B+C)/2,abs(B-C)/2),gray);draw(circle((A+C)/2,abs(A-C)/2),gray);draw(circle((A+B)/2,abs(A-B)/2),gray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));draw(A--D,dashed);draw(B--E,dashed);draw(C--F,dashed);dot(H);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,S);label("$E$",E,NE);label("$F$",F,NW);label("$H$",H,E);
\end{asy}

The three altitudes of a triangle are concurrent (at the orthocentre $H$), so the three common chords lie on concurrent lines. $\blacksquare$

(Equivalently: the common chords are the radical axes of the three circles taken in pairs, which always concur at the radical centre.)

\textbf{Power of a point.} Let line $PQ$ meet line $T_1T_2$ at $M'$ (it does, since the common chord is not parallel to a common tangent unless the circles are congruent, a case handled by symmetry below). The power of $M'$ with respect to the first circle is $M'T_1^2=M'P\cdot M'Q$, and with respect to the second circle it is $M'T_2^2=M'P\cdot M'Q$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O1=(0,0),O2=(3,-0.3);real r1=1.5,r2=2.2;
path c1=circle(O1,r1),c2=circle(O2,r2);draw(c1);draw(c2);
pair[] X=intersectionpoints(c1,c2);pair P=X[0],Q=X[1];if(P.y<Q.y){pair t=P;P=Q;Q=t;}
pair d=O2-O1;real D=abs(d);real phi=degrees(acos((r2-r1)/D));
pair n1=rotate(phi)*unit(d),n2=rotate(-phi)*unit(d);pair n=((O1-r1*n1).y>(O1-r1*n2).y)?n1:n2;
pair T1=O1-r1*n,T2=O2-r2*n;pair M=(T1+T2)/2;
draw(interp(T1,T2,-0.2)--interp(T1,T2,1.2),linewidth(1));draw(interp(Q,M,-0.3)--interp(Q,M,1.25),dashed);
dot(T1);dot(T2);dot(M);dot(P);dot(Q);
label("$T_1$",T1,NW);label("$T_2$",T2,NE);label("$M$",M,N);label("$P$",P,E);label("$Q$",Q,W);
\end{asy}

So $M'T_1=M'T_2$, and $M'$ is the midpoint $M$ of $T_1T_2$. Hence $M$ lies on $PQ$. (If the circles are congruent, the configuration is symmetric in the perpendicular bisector of the centres, which is line $PQ$, and it passes through the midpoint of $T_1T_2$.) $\blacksquare$

With radius $r$, the metal area is the base plus the side: $\pi r^2+2\pi r(1.21)=19.11$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1.54,h=1.21;
draw(ellipse((0,0),r,0.35*r));draw(ellipse((0,h*1.6),r,0.35*r),dashed);
draw((-r,0)--(-r,h*1.6));draw((r,0)--(r,h*1.6));
draw((0,0)--(r,0));label("$r$",(r/2,0),S);label("$1.21$",(r,0.8*h),E);label("open top",(0,h*1.6+0.5),N);
\end{asy}

So $r^2+2.42r-\dfrac{19.11}\pi=0$. With $\dfrac{19.11}{\pi}\approx6.0829$,
\[r=\dfrac{-2.42+\sqrt{2.42^2+4\cdot6.0829}}2=\dfrac{-2.42+\sqrt{30.188}}2\approx1.5372.\]
The volume is $\pi r^2h\approx\pi\cdot2.3630\cdot1.21\approx\boxed{8.98}$ cubic metres.

Let $K=(1,10)$. For any $W$, the distance to the disc ranges between $WK-2.4$ and $WK+2.4$, so the minimum of $WZ$ is $\min WK-2.4$ and the maximum is $\max WK+2.4$ (the disc does not meet the rectangle).

The point of the rectangle nearest to $K$ is $(1,5)$, with $WK=5$; the farthest is a far corner, $(-5,0)$, with $WK=\sqrt{36+100}=2\sqrt{34}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
fill((-5,0)--(5,0)--(5,5)--(-5,5)--cycle,opacity(0.15)+blue);draw((-5,0)--(5,0)--(5,5)--(-5,5)--cycle,blue);
filldraw(circle((1,10),2.4),opacity(0.15)+red,red);dot((1,10));label("$(1,10)$",(1,10),SE);
draw((1,5)--(1,7.6),linewidth(1));draw((-5,0)--(1,10)+2.4*unit((1,10)-(-5,0)),dashed);
dot((1,5));dot((-5,0));label("$(1,5)$",(1,5),S);label("$(-5,0)$",(-5,0),SW);
label("min",(1,6.3),E);label("max",(-2.5,4.2),W);
\end{asy}

So the minimum is $2.6$ and the maximum is $2\sqrt{34}+2.4$, with product $2.6(2.4+2\sqrt{34})=6.24+5.2\sqrt{34}$. Thus $s=6.24$, $r=5.2$, $q=34$, and $rqs=5.2\cdot34\cdot6.24=\boxed{1103.232}$.

question:
Each day, independently, Alan has a $\dfrac23$ chance of being praised in a group chat.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the probability that he is not praised on any day of a $365$-day year.
\item[(b)] Find the expected number of days he is praised, to the nearest day.
\item[(c)] Estimate the probability that he is praised on at least $182$ days.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Track the gaps (numbers of edges) between consecutive frogs around the cycle; they always sum to $12$ and are even (each gap changes by $-2,0,2$ per minute and starts at $4$). Up to symmetry the non-terminal states are $(4,4,4)$, $(2,4,6)$ and $(2,2,8)$, and the process stops when some gap becomes $0$.

Enumerating the $8$ equally likely joint moves from a representative of each state:
\begin{itemize}
\item from $(4,4,4)$: $2$ moves keep $(4,4,4)$ and $6$ give $(2,4,6)$;
\item from $(2,4,6)$: $2$ moves stop, $1$ gives $(4,4,4)$, $4$ keep $(2,4,6)$ and $1$ gives $(2,2,8)$;
\item from $(2,2,8)$: $4$ moves stop, $2$ give $(2,4,6)$ and $2$ keep $(2,2,8)$.
\end{itemize}
With $E_1,E_2,E_3$ the expected remaining times from these states,
\[E_1=1+\dfrac14E_1+\dfrac34E_2,\qquad E_2=1+\dfrac18E_1+\dfrac12E_2+\dfrac18E_3,\qquad E_3=1+\dfrac14E_2+\dfrac14E_3.\]
The first gives $E_1=\dfrac43+E_2$ and the third $E_3=\dfrac{4+E_2}{3}$. Substituting into the second,
\[\dfrac12E_2=1+\dfrac16+\dfrac{E_2}8+\dfrac16+\dfrac{E_2}{24}\implies\dfrac13E_2=\dfrac43\implies E_2=4.\]
So $E_1=\dfrac{16}3$, and $m+n=\boxed{19}$.

Through each vertex of $\triangle ABC$ draw the line parallel to the opposite side. These three lines form a triangle $A'B'C'$ (with $A'$ opposite $A$, and so on).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.2,3),B=(0,0),C=(4,0);
pair A2=B+C-A,B2=A+C-B,C2=A+B-C;
draw(A2--B2--C2--cycle,dashed);draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
pair foot(pair P,pair U,pair V){return U+dot(P-U,V-U)/dot(V-U,V-U)*(V-U);}
pair D=foot(A,B,C),E=foot(B,A,C),F=foot(C,A,B);pair H=extension(A,D,B,E);
draw(A--D,dotted);draw(B--E,dotted);draw(C--F,dotted);dot(H);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,E);label("$A'$",A2,S);label("$B'$",B2,NE);label("$C'$",C2,NW);label("$H$",H,E);
\end{asy}

Quadrilaterals $ABCB'$ and $ACBC'$ are parallelograms (opposite sides parallel), so $AB'=BC=C'A$. Hence $A$ is the midpoint of $B'C'$, and similarly $B$ and $C$ are midpoints of $C'A'$ and $A'B'$.

The altitude from $A$ is perpendicular to $BC$, hence to $B'C'\parallel BC$, and passes through the midpoint $A$ of $B'C'$: it is the perpendicular bisector of $B'C'$. Likewise the other two altitudes are the perpendicular bisectors of the other sides of $A'B'C'$. The perpendicular bisectors of a triangle meet at its circumcentre (a point equidistant from two vertices lies on their bisector, so the intersection of two bisectors lies on the third). Hence the altitudes concur. $\blacksquare$

question:
A vehicle travels $1821$ km. Its wheels have radius $0.16$ m.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many revolutions does each wheel make (to the nearest thousand)?
\item[(b)] At an average speed of $60$ km per hour, how long does the journey take?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) Each revolution covers one circumference, $2\pi(0.16)\approx1.00531$ m.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),1));dot((0,0));draw((0,0)--dir(30));label("$0.16$",0.5*dir(30),NW);draw((-1.6,-1)--(1.6,-1));
\end{asy}

The number of revolutions is $\dfrac{1821000}{2\pi\cdot0.16}\approx1811382$, i.e. about $\boxed{1811000}$.

(b) $\dfrac{1821}{60}=30.35$ hours, i.e. $\boxed{30\text{ hours }21\text{ minutes}}$.

Put the quarter circle's centre $O$ at the origin, with bounding radii along the positive axes. A circle tangent to both axes (inside the quarter) has centre $C=(r,r)$, so $OC=r\sqrt2$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=4;real r=R/(1+sqrt(2));pair O=(0,0),C=(r,r);
draw(O--(R,0));draw(O--(0,R));draw(arc(O,R,0,90));draw(circle(C,r));
pair T=R*dir(45);draw(O--T,dashed);dot(O);dot(C);
draw(C--(r,0),dotted);draw(C--(0,r),dotted);
label("$O$",O,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$r$",(r,r/2),E);label("$R$",(R/2,0),S);label("$T$",T,NE);
\end{asy}

Internal tangency with the arc means the centres and the tangency point $T$ are collinear, with $OT=OC+CT$, i.e. $R=r\sqrt2+r$. Hence $r=\dfrac{R}{1+\sqrt2}=(\sqrt2-1)R$. $\blacksquare$

Since $AB$ is a diameter, $\angle ACB=90^\circ$, so the bisector $CD$ makes $\angle BCD=45^\circ$. Also $\angle CBD=90^\circ$. So triangle $CBD$ is an isosceles right triangle with hypotenuse $CD$ and $BC=BD$. The median $BM$ to the hypotenuse of an isosceles triangle is also an altitude, so $\angle BMD=90^\circ$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-3,0),B=(3,0),C=3*dir(115);
draw(circle((0,0),3),gray);draw(A--B--C--cycle);
pair u=unit(unit(A-C)+unit(B-C));pair Bp=B+rotate(90)*(B-C);
pair D=extension(C,C+u,B,Bp);pair E=A+dot(B-A,unit(D-A))*unit(D-A);pair M=(C+D)/2;
draw(C--D);draw(B--D);draw(A--D,dashed);draw(B--E,dashed);draw(B--M,dotted);
draw(circle((B+D)/2,abs(B-D)/2),blue);
dot(E);dot(M);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,S);label("$E$",E,SW);label("$M$",M,W);
\end{asy}

By construction $\angle BED=90^\circ$. So $M$ and $E$ both see the segment $BD$ at a right angle, and by the converse of Thales' theorem they lie on the circle with diameter $BD$. Hence $B,D,E,M$ are concyclic. $\blacksquare$

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $2^a+3^b+7^c=2016$ has no solutions in nonnegative integers $a,b,c$.
\item[(b)] Find nonnegative integers $a,b,c$ with $2^a\cdot3^b\cdot7^c=2016$, and compute $a+b+c$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $\angle P=x$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real x=180/7;pair P=(0,0);pair B=dir(-x/2),J=dir(x/2);B=4*B;J=4*J;
real s=abs(B-J);pair L=P+s*unit(B-P);pair I=P+(2*s*Cos(x))*unit(J-P);
draw(P--B--J--cycle);draw(L--I--B);
label("$P$",P,W);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$J$",J,NE);label("$L$",L,S);label("$I$",I,N);
\end{asy}

\begin{itemize}
\item $PL=LI$: $\angle LIP=x$, so the exterior angle $\angle ILB=2x$.
\item $LI=IB$: $\angle IBL=2x$, so $\angle LIB=180^\circ-4x$.
\item On line $PJ$: $\angle BIJ=180^\circ-x-(180^\circ-4x)=3x$.
\item $IB=BJ$: $\angle BJI=3x$.
\end{itemize}
Since $PB=PJ$, $\angle PBJ=\angle PJB=3x$, and the angle sum gives $7x=180^\circ$. So $\angle ILP=180^\circ-2x=180^\circ-\dfrac{360^\circ}7=\boxed{\dfrac{900}{7}^\circ}\approx128.6^\circ$.

Rotate $\triangle APC$ by $60^\circ$ about $A$ so that $C$ goes to $B$; let $P$ go to $P'$. Then $AP'=AP=1$ and $\angle PAP'=60^\circ$, so $\triangle APP'$ is equilateral with $PP'=1$; and $BP'=CP=2$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=sqrt(7);pair A=(0,0),B=(s,0),C=s*dir(60);
real ax=(1-3+7)/(2*s);pair P=(ax,sqrt(1-ax^2));
pair P2=A+rotate(-60)*(P-A);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(P--A);draw(P--B);draw(P--C);draw(P--P2--A,dashed);draw(P2--B,dashed);
dot(P);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$P$",P,N);label("$P'$",P2,S);
\end{asy}

In $\triangle PP'B$: $PP'^2+PB^2=1+3=4=P'B^2$, so $\angle P'PB=90^\circ$. Therefore $\angle APB=60^\circ+90^\circ=150^\circ$, and by the law of cosines
\[s^2=AB^2=1+3-2\sqrt3\cos150^\circ=4+3=7,\qquad s=\boxed{\sqrt7}.\]

The midpoint of the hypotenuse is the circumcentre, so $DA=DB=DC$. Triangle $ABD$ is isosceles with $\angle DBA=\angle DAB=\alpha$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),A=(0,3),C=(4,0),D=(A+C)/2;draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(B--D);draw((0,0.25)--(0.25,0.25)--(0.25,0));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,NE);label("$\alpha$",A,5*dir(-70));label("$\alpha$",B,6*dir(55));
\end{asy}

$\angle BDC$ is an exterior angle of $\triangle ABD$, so $\angle BDC=\angle DAB+\angle DBA=\boxed{2\alpha}$.

The graph is the circle $x^2+y^2=25$, and $x=3$ gives the points $(3,4)$ and $(3,-4)$. A tangent to a circle is perpendicular to the radius. The radius to $(3,4)$ has slope $\dfrac43$ and the one to $(3,-4)$ has slope $-\dfrac43$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;draw(circle((0,0),5));draw((-6.5,0)--(6.5,0),Arrow);draw((0,-6.5)--(0,6.5),Arrow);
pair P=(3,4),Q=(3,-4);dot(P);dot(Q);
draw(P+2.5*unit((4,-3))--P-2.5*unit((4,-3)),red);draw(Q+2.5*unit((4,3))--Q-2.5*unit((4,3)),red);
draw((0,0)--P,dashed);draw((0,0)--Q,dashed);
label("$(3,4)$",P,NE);label("$(3,-4)$",Q,SE);label("$x^2+y^2=25$",(-3.5,3.5),NW);
\end{asy}

So the tangent slopes are $\boxed{-\dfrac34}$ at $(3,4)$ and $\boxed{\dfrac34}$ at $(3,-4)$.

The question is whether \emph{all three} want beer.
\begin{itemize}
\item If the first did not want beer, he would know the answer is ``no''. Since he says ``I don't know'', he wants beer (but cannot speak for the others).
\item The second hears this and reasons the same way: saying ``I don't know'' means he also wants beer.
\item The third now knows the first two want beer. He wants beer too, so he can answer $\boxed{\text{yes}}$. (Had he not wanted beer, he would have said ``no''.)
\end{itemize}

question:
A friend's birthday is one of these $10$ dates:
\begin{center}May 15, May 16, May 19, June 17, June 18, July 14, July 16, August 14, August 15, August 17.\end{center}
She tells Albert only the month and Bernard only the day. Then:
\begin{itemize}
\item Albert: ``I don't know the birthday, but I know that Bernard doesn't know either.''
\item Bernard: ``At first I didn't know, but now I do.''
\item Albert: ``Then I also know.''
\end{itemize}
When is her birthday?

solution:
$s^2+r^2=181$ with positive integers gives $\{s,r\}=\{9,10\}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real k=1;pair S=k*(9,10),R=k*(27,sqrt(1000));
draw((0,0)--(34,0),Arrow);draw((0,0)--(0,36),Arrow);draw((0,0)--S,dashed);draw((0,0)--R,dashed);dot(S);dot(R);
label("$S(9,10)$",S,E);label("$R(27,10\sqrt{10})$",R,E);label("$\sqrt{181}$",S/2,SE);
\end{asy}
 Then $R=\left(s^{\dfrac32},r^{\dfrac32}\right)$, whose squared distance from the origin is $s^3+r^3=729+1000=\boxed{1729}$.

\textbf{$n=4$.} The angles sum to $360^\circ$, so four obtuse angles would sum to more than $360^\circ$. Three are possible, as in the quadrilateral below.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(4.6,2.0),D=(2.2,3.2);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,N);
label("$55.5^\circ$",A,3*dir(28));label("$106.7^\circ$",B,3*dir(125));label("$99.9^\circ$",C,3*dir(215));label("$97.9^\circ$",D,3*dir(260));
\end{asy}

\textbf{$n\ge5$.} Each interior angle of a regular $n$-gon is $180^\circ-\dfrac{360^\circ}n\ge108^\circ$, so all $n$ angles can be obtuse.

So the maximum is $\boxed{3}$ for $n=4$ and $\boxed{n}$ for $n\ge5$.

The vertices lie on a circle, with consecutive vertices $6^\circ$ apart. The chords $A_1A_{20}$ and $A_{10}A_{59}$ cross inside the circle (the endpoints alternate around the circle: $A_{59},A_1,A_{10},A_{20}$).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P(int k){return dir(90+6*(k-1));}
draw(circle((0,0),1),gray);
draw(P(1)--P(20),linewidth(0.9));draw(P(10)--P(59),linewidth(0.9));
pair X=extension(P(1),P(20),P(10),P(59));dot(X);
dot(P(1));dot(P(20));dot(P(10));dot(P(59));
label("$A_1$",P(1),N);label("$A_{20}$",P(20),W);label("$A_{10}$",P(10),NW);label("$A_{59}$",P(59),NE);
\end{asy}

The angle between two chords crossing inside a circle is half the sum of the intercepted arcs. The arc $A_1A_{10}$ is $9\cdot6^\circ=54^\circ$ and the arc $A_{20}A_{59}$ (the other side) is $39\cdot6^\circ=234^\circ$. So one angle is $\dfrac{54^\circ+234^\circ}2=144^\circ$, and the acute angle is $180^\circ-144^\circ=\boxed{36^\circ}$.

The circle has centre $K(-3,1)$ and radius $2$. A chord of length $2\sqrt3$ has half-length $\sqrt3$, so its distance from the centre is $\sqrt{4-3}=1$.

The vertical line $x=4$ is $7$ from $K$, so it fails. Otherwise the line is $y=m(x-4)$, i.e. $mx-y-4m=0$, and
\[\dfrac{|-3m-1-4m|}{\sqrt{m^2+1}}=1\iff(7m+1)^2=m^2+1\iff48m^2+14m=0.\]
So $m=0$ or $m=-\dfrac7{24}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;draw(circle((-3,1),2));dot((-3,1));label("$K(-3,1)$",(-3,1),NW);
dot((4,0));label("$A(4,0)$",(4,0),SE);
draw((-6,0)--(5,0),blue);real m=-7/24;draw((-6,m*(-10))--(5,m*1),red);
draw((-3,1)--(-3,0),dashed);label("$1$",(-3,0.5),E);
\end{asy}

The lines are $\boxed{y=0}$ and $\boxed{7x+24y-28=0}$.

Write the sides as $a-d$, $a$, $a+d$ with $d\ge0$; the hypotenuse is the largest, $a+d$. Pythagoras gives
\[(a-d)^2+a^2=(a+d)^2\iff a^2=4ad\iff a=4d\]
(as $a>0$). So the sides are $3d,4d,5d$, and $d>0$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),A=(4,0),B=(0,3);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw((0,0.25)--(0.25,0.25)--(0.25,0));
label("$4d$",(2,0),S);label("$3d$",(0,1.5),W);label("$5d$",(2,1.5),NE);label("$\alpha$",A,5*dir(160));label("$\beta$",B,5*dir(-70));
\end{asy}

The sines of the acute angles are $\dfrac{3d}{5d}$ and $\dfrac{4d}{5d}$, with sum $\boxed{\dfrac75}$.

Consider $A,C,D$. If one of them lay on the shorter arc between the other two, one of the three distances would be the sum of the other two; but $40\ne25+35$, $25\ne40+35$ and $35\ne40+25$. So the three shorter arcs $AC$, $CD$, $DA$ go once around the circle, and the circumference is $40+25+35=100$ miles.

Then $AB=50$ is half the circumference, so $B$ is diametrically opposite $A$. Going around from $A$: $C$ at $40$, $B$ at $50$, $D$ at $65$ (or the mirror image).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=1.6;draw(circle((0,0),R));
pair P(real s){return R*dir(90-3.6*s);}
dot(P(0));label("$A$",P(0),N);dot(P(40));label("$C$",P(40),E);dot(P(50));label("$B$",P(50),SE);dot(P(65));label("$D$",P(65),S);
label("$40$",1.25*dir(90-72),SW);label("$25$",1.25*dir(90-3.6*52.5),NW);label("$35$",1.25*dir(90-3.6*82.5),E);label("$10$",1.9*dir(90-3.6*45),E);
\end{asy}

So $BC=\boxed{10}$ miles.

question:
The tiles spelling BANANAGRAMS are given.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many distinct arrangements of the letters are there?
\item[(b)] The tiles are shuffled randomly. What is the probability that they spell GRAMBANANAS?
\item[(c)] One argument for (b) gives $\dfrac{4!\cdot2!}{\dfrac{11!}{4!\,2!}}$, using the small example TOO (``$2$ ways to make TOO out of $3$ arrangements, so $\dfrac23$''). Explain the error.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
$\dfrac43\pi r^3=\dfrac13\pi r^2h$ gives $h=4r$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),1));draw(ellipse((0,0),1,0.3),dashed);
draw((2,-1)--(4,-1)--(3,3)--cycle);draw(ellipse((3,-1),1,0.3));
draw((3,-1)--(4,-1));label("$r$",(3.5,-1),S);draw((0,0)--(1,0));label("$r$",(0.5,0),N);
draw((4.4,-1)--(4.4,3),Arrows);label("$h=4r$",(4.4,1),E);
\end{asy}

So $a=\boxed{4}$.

Let the trapezoid be $ABCD$ with $AB=a$, $DC=b$, $AB\parallel DC$, and let $M,N$ be the midpoints of $AC$ and $BD$. Let $P,Q$ be the midpoints of the legs $AD$ and $BC$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(7,0),C=(5,2.5),D=(1.5,2.5);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--C,dashed);draw(B--D,dashed);
pair M=(A+C)/2,N=(B+D)/2,P=(A+D)/2,Q=(B+C)/2;draw(P--Q,dotted);dot(M);dot(N);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$M$",M,S);label("$N$",N,S);label("$P$",P,W);label("$Q$",Q,E);
\end{asy}

By the midline theorem in triangle $ADC$, $PM\parallel DC$ with $PM=\dfrac b2$; in triangle $ABD$, $PN\parallel AB$ with $PN=\dfrac a2$. Both segments lie on the line through $P$ parallel to the bases (the midline $PQ$), on the same side of $P$. Hence $d=MN=PN-PM=\dfrac{a-b}2$, i.e. $b=a-2d$. $\blacksquare$

