$y=\dfrac{x^2-x+18}{10}$ must be an integer, so $x^2-x+18\equiv0\pmod{10}$; checking residues, this means $x\equiv2$ or $4\pmod5$ (and $x^2-x$ is always even).
\begin{itemize}
\item $x\ge0$: $y\le x$ means $x^2-x+18\le10x$, i.e. $x^2-11x+18=(x-2)(x-9)\le0$, so $2\le x\le9$. With the congruence: $x=2,4,7,9$.
\item $x<0$: $y\le-x$ means $x^2-x+18\le-10x$, i.e. $x^2+9x+18=(x+3)(x+6)\le0$, so $-6\le x\le-3$: $x=-6,-3$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;real u=0.35;
draw((-8*u,0)--(11*u,0),Arrow);draw((0,-0.3)--(0,11*u),Arrow);
draw((-8*u,8*u)--(0,0)--(10.5*u,10.5*u),blue);
draw(graph(new real(real x){return u*((x/u)^2-(x/u)+18)/10;},-8*u,10.2*u),red);
pair[] pts={(2,2),(4,3),(7,6),(9,9),(-6,6),(-3,3)};for(pair p:pts) dot(u*p);
label("$y=|x|$",(10.5*u,10.5*u),E,blue);label("$y=\dfrac{x^2-x+18}{10}$",(-8*u,9.5*u),N,red);
\end{asy}

The points are $\boxed{(2,2),(4,3),(7,6),(9,9),(-6,6),(-3,3)}$.

Put the centre at the origin, $P=e^{i\theta}$ and $P_k=e^{i\theta_k}$ with $\theta_k=\dfrac{2\pi k}n$. Then $PP_k^2=2-2\cos(\theta-\theta_k)$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int n=6;draw(circle((0,0),1),gray);pair[] V;for(int k=0;k<n;++k){V.push(dir(90+360*k/n));}
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle);pair P=dir(-15);dot(P);label("$P$",P,E);
for(int k=0;k<n;++k){draw(P--V[k],dashed);label("$P_{"+string(k+1)+"}$",V[k],V[k]);}dot((0,0));label("$O$",(0,0),SW);
\end{asy}

For $n\ge3$ the sums $\sum_k\cos(\theta-\theta_k)$ and $\sum_k\cos2(\theta-\theta_k)$ vanish (they are real parts of sums of all $n$th roots of unity, or of all of them twice, rotated). Hence
\[\sum PP_k^2=2n,\qquad \sum PP_k^4=\sum\bigl(4-8\cos\alpha_k+4\cos^2\alpha_k\bigr)=4n+2n=6n,\]
using $\cos^2\alpha=\dfrac{1+\cos2\alpha}2$. By Cauchy-Schwarz,
\[\sum PP_k^3=\sum PP_k\cdot PP_k^2\le\sqrt{\sum PP_k^2}\sqrt{\sum PP_k^4}=\sqrt{2n\cdot6n}=2\sqrt3\,n.\ \blacksquare\]

$\cos\theta$ is the $x$-coordinate of the point reached by turning $\theta$ counterclockwise from $(1,0)$ on the unit circle. Since $720^\circ=2\cdot360^\circ$ is two full turns, the point returns to $(1,0)$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),1));draw((-1.3,0)--(1.3,0),Arrow);draw((0,-1.3)--(0,1.3),Arrow);
dot((1,0));label("$(\cos720^\circ,\sin720^\circ)=(1,0)$",(1,0),NE);
path s=arc((0,0),0.25,0,360);draw(s,Arrow);path t=arc((0,0),0.4,0,360);draw(t,Arrow);label("$2$ full turns",(0,-0.45),SW);
\end{asy}

So $\cos720^\circ=\cos0^\circ=\boxed{1}$ (cosine has period $360^\circ$).

$(x-399)^2+(y-21)^2=(x+70)^2+(y-303)^2$ simplifies to $-938x+564y+62933=0$, i.e. $938x-564y-62933=0$. (It is the perpendicular bisector of $FG$, through the midpoint $(164.5,162)$.)
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;real k=1/100;pair F=k*(399,21),G=k*(-70,303),M=(F+G)/2;
draw((-1.2,0)--(4.3,0),Arrow);draw((0,-0.3)--(0,3.5),Arrow);
draw(F--G,dashed);dot(F);dot(G);dot(M);label("$F(399,21)$",F,SE);label("$G(-70,303)$",G,N);label("$M$",M,NE);
pair d=unit(rotate(90)*(G-F));draw(M-1.6*d--M+1.6*d,blue);label("$\ell$",M+1.6*d,E,blue);
\end{asy}

Since $938=2\cdot7\cdot67$ and $62933$ is odd, the coefficients have no common factor. So $A=938$, $B=-564$, and $A+B=\boxed{374}$.

Let the incircle touch $BC$, $CA$ at $X$, $Y$. Tangent segments from a point are equal: $AZ=AY=x$, $BZ=BX=y$, $CX=CY=z$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0),C=(1.5,4);real a=abs(B-C),bb=abs(C-A),c=abs(A-B);pair I=(a*A+bb*B+c*C)/(a+bb+c);
pair Z=(I.x,0);pair Y=A+(abs(Z-A))*unit(C-A);pair X=B+(abs(Z-B))*unit(C-B);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(circle(I,I.y));dot(Z);dot(Y);dot(X);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$Z$",Z,S);label("$Y$",Y,W);label("$X$",X,E);
\end{asy}

Then $x+y=c$, $y+z=a$, $z+x=b$, so $AZ=x=\boxed{\dfrac{b+c-a}2}$.

The proposed formula fails, e.g. for an equilateral triangle of side $s$: the true value is $\dfrac s2$, but the formula gives $\dfrac{60}{90}\cdot\dfrac s2=\dfrac s3$.

All angles are $120^\circ$, so the side directions turn by $60^\circ$ each time. Let $u_k$ be the unit vector at angle $60k^\circ$. Going around, $10u_0+16u_1+19u_2+DE\,u_3+EF\,u_4+15u_5=0$. Since $u_3=-u_0$, $u_4=-u_1$, $u_5=-u_2$, comparing components (with $u_2=u_1-u_0$) gives $10+16=DE+EF$ and $16+19=EF+15$. So $EF=20$ and $DE=6$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real[] s={10,16,19,6,20,15};pair P=(0,0);pair[] V;
for(int i=0;i<6;++i){V.push(P);P=P+s[i]*dir(60*i);}
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle);draw(V[0]--V[3],dashed);draw(V[1]--V[4],dashed);draw(V[2]--V[5],dashed);
string[] L={"A","B","C","D","E","F"};pair c=(V[0]+V[3])/2;for(int i=0;i<6;++i) label("$"+L[i]+"$",V[i],unit(V[i]-c));
for(int i=0;i<6;++i) label("$"+string(s[i])+"$",(V[i]+V[(i+1)%6])/2,-dir(60*i+90));
\end{asy}

Using $u_0\cdot u_1=u_1\cdot u_2=\dfrac12$ and $u_0\cdot u_2=-\dfrac12$, and similarly for the other triples:
\[AD^2=|10u_0+16u_1+19u_2|^2=100+256+361+(160-190+304)=991,\]
\[BE^2=|16u_1+19u_2+6u_3|^2=256+361+36+(304-96+114)=975,\]
\[CF^2=|19u_2+6u_3+20u_4|^2=361+36+400+(114-380+120)=651.\]
The sum is $991+975+651=\boxed{2617}$.

Triangles $ADH$ and $CDB$ are similar: both have a right angle at $D$, and $\angle DAH=90^\circ-\angle B=\angle DCB$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(7,0),C=(2.2,5);pair D=(C.x,0);
pair foot(pair P,pair U,pair V){return U+dot(P-U,V-U)/dot(V-U,V-U)*(V-U);}
pair E=foot(B,A,C);pair H=extension(C,D,B,E);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(C--D,dashed);draw(B--E,dashed);draw(A--foot(A,B,C),dashed);dot(H);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,S);label("$H$",H,E);
\end{asy}

So $\dfrac{AD}{DH}=\dfrac{CD}{DB}$, i.e. $AD\cdot DB=DH\cdot DC=15\cdot(15+18)=\boxed{495}$.

The centroid $G$ lies on the Euler line with $OG:GH=1:2$ (schematic sketch below), so $G=\dfrac{2O+H}3=\left(\dfrac{17}3,\dfrac{14}3\right)$ and $A+B+C=3G=(17,14)$.

The fourth vertex is $A+B-C$, $B+C-A$ or $C+A-B$; these three points sum to $A+B+C=(17,14)$.
