The solid is symmetric about its centre $O$, so opposite faces are parallel, and the required height is twice the distance from $O$ to a face.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import three;
pair p(triple v){return (v.x+0.35*v.y,v.z+0.25*v.y);}
triple[] H;for(int k=0;k<6;++k)H.push((cos(pi*k/3),sin(pi*k/3),0));
triple T=(0,0,1),Bt=(0,0,-1);
for(int k=0;k<6;++k){draw(p(H[k])--p(H[(k+1)%6]));draw(p(T)--p(H[k]));draw(p(Bt)--p(H[k]),gray);}
label("$1$",p(T)/2+(0.1,0),E);dot(p(T));dot(p(Bt));
\end{asy}

Take a face through the apex at height $1$ and a hexagon edge whose midpoint is at horizontal distance $\dfrac{\sqrt3}2$ (the apothem) from $O$. In the vertical cross-section through $O$, the apex and that midpoint, the face appears as the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs $1$ and $\dfrac{\sqrt3}2$. The distance from $O$ to it is
\[\dfrac{1\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}2}{\sqrt{1+\dfrac34}}=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{\sqrt7}.\]
So the height is $\dfrac{2\sqrt3}{\sqrt7}=\boxed{\dfrac{2\sqrt{21}}7}$ cm.

\begin{itemize}
\item If $x\le2$: $y\le x$, so $x^2+y^2\le2x^2\le8$.
\item If $2<x\le3$: $0<y\le4-x$, so $x^2+y^2\le x^2+(4-x)^2=2(x-2)^2+8\le2+8=10$.
\end{itemize}
So $x^2+y^2\le10$, with equality at $(x,y)=(3,1)$. $\blacksquare$

Classify each right triangle by the direction of its legs at the right-angle vertex $P$.

\textbf{Legs parallel to the axes.} For each of the $16$ points $P$, choose another point in its row ($3$ ways) and in its column ($3$ ways): $16\cdot9=144$ triangles.

\textbf{Legs along the diagonals (slopes $1$ and $-1$).} For each $P$, multiply the numbers of other array points on its two diagonals and add over $P$: this gives $40$.

\textbf{Legs with slopes $2$ and $-\dfrac12$, or $\dfrac12$ and $-2$.} Counting in the same way gives $16$. No other perpendicular direction pairs fit in a $4\times4$ array.


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<4;++i)for(int j=0;j<4;++j)dot((i,j));
draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(0,3)--cycle,blue);draw((1,1)--(3,3)--(2,0)--cycle,red);draw((0,1)--(1,3)--(3,2)--cycle,deepgreen);
\end{asy}

(The sketch shows one triangle of each type: axis-parallel legs in blue, diagonal legs in red, and slopes $2$ and $-\dfrac12$ in green.) The total is $144+40+16=\boxed{200}$. The student counted only some shapes (two triangles, with their copies) instead of every choice of right-angle vertex and legs.

By the law of sines, $\sin B=\dfrac{AC\sin A}{BC}=\dfrac{10\cdot\dfrac12}{6}=\dfrac56$. So $B=\arcsin\dfrac56\approx56.4^\circ$ or $B=180^\circ-56.4^\circ\approx123.6^\circ$. Both are valid because $A+B<180^\circ$ in each case.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0);real t=30;pair C=10*dir(t);
pair B1=(10*Cos(t)-sqrt(36-100*Sin(t)^2),0),B2=(10*Cos(t)+sqrt(36-100*Sin(t)^2),0);
draw(A--(B2.x+1,0));draw(A--C);draw(C--B1);draw(C--B2,dashed);draw(arc(C,6,240,330),dotted);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$C$",C,N);label("$B_1$",B1,S);label("$B_2$",B2,S);label("$30^\circ$",A,6*dir(15));label("$10$",C/2,NW);label("$6$",(C+B1)/2,W);label("$6$",(C+B2)/2,E);
\end{asy}

Geometrically, the circle of radius $6$ about $C$ meets the ray from $A$ at two points $B_1,B_2$ (since $AC\sin30^\circ=5<6<10=AC$). So $\angle ABC\approx\boxed{56.4^\circ\text{ or }123.6^\circ}$.

