pair P=(0,2.2),A=(6,0),Q=(6,2.2),G=(0,0);
draw(P--Q--A--cycle);draw(G--A,dashed);draw(P--G,dashed);
dot(P);dot(A);dot(Q);label("plane",P,W);label("atoll",A,SE);label("$Q$",Q,NE);
label("$895$ m",(6,1.1),E);label("$d$",(3,2.2),N);
draw(arc(P,0.9,-20.1,0));label("$14^\circ$",P+(1.05,-0.2),E);
\end{asy}

So $\tan14^\circ=\dfrac{895}d$, giving $d=\dfrac{895}{\tan14^\circ}\approx3590$ m $\approx\boxed{3.59\text{ km}}$.

The map $(x,y)\mapsto\left(\dfrac abx,\,y\right)$ sends the circle $x^2+y^2=b^2$ onto the ellipse, since $\dfrac{(ax/b)^2}{a^2}+\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{b^2}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;draw(scale(2,1)*unitcircle);draw(unitcircle,dashed);
draw((-2.4,0)--(2.4,0),Arrow);draw((0,-1.4)--(0,1.4),Arrow);
label("$a$",(1.5,0),S);label("$b$",(0,0.5),W);label("$x$-scale by $a/b$",(1.3,0.85),NE);
draw((0.6,0.8)--(1.2,0.8),Arrow);
\end{asy}

Stretching in one direction by the factor $\dfrac ab$ multiplies every area by $\dfrac ab$: every horizontal strip of the region keeps its height and has its length multiplied by $\dfrac ab$ (Cavalieri's principle). So the area of the ellipse is $\dfrac ab\cdot\pi b^2=\boxed{\pi ab}$. $\blacksquare$

A regular hexagon inscribed in a circle of radius $r$ has side $r$, and its inradius is the height of an equilateral triangle of side $r$, namely $\dfrac{\sqrt3}2r$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
