With $\theta=\angle POQ\le60^\circ$ we have $\cos\theta\ge\dfrac12$, so by the law of cosines, writing $m=\min(OP,OQ)$ and $M=\max(OP,OQ)$,
\[PQ^2=OP^2+OQ^2-2\,OP\cdot OQ\cos\theta\le m^2+M^2-mM\le M^2,\]
because $m^2\le mM$. Hence $PQ\le M<1$. $\blacksquare$

Since $BD$ bisects $\angle B$, the areas give $\dfrac{AD}{DC}=\dfrac{15}{30}$, and by the angle bisector theorem $\dfrac{AB}{BC}=\dfrac{AD}{DC}=\dfrac12$. Put $AB=c$, $BC=2c$.
