\[1=\dfrac52\cdot\dfrac12\left(\dfrac52-s\right)\left(\dfrac52-t\right)=\dfrac54\left(\dfrac{25}4-\dfrac52(s+t)+st\right)=\dfrac54\left(st-\dfrac54\right).\]
So $st=\dfrac45+\dfrac54=\boxed{\dfrac{41}{20}}$.

Put $O$ at the origin with $P$ at height $1$. A point a fraction $f$ of the way up an edge from a base vertex has height $f$ and lies at horizontal distance $(1-f)\sqrt2$ from the axis. So $B'$ has height $\dfrac14$ at distance $\dfrac34\sqrt2$, and $D'$ has height $\dfrac15$ at distance $\dfrac45\sqrt2$, on opposite sides of the axis (in the vertical plane $BPD$).

The plane meets the axis $PO$ at the point $O'$ of segment $B'D'$ above $O$. It divides $B'D'$ in the ratio of the horizontal distances, $\dfrac34:\dfrac45=15:16$ (not $3:4$, which is the error). Hence
\[OO'=\dfrac{16\cdot\dfrac14+15\cdot\dfrac15}{31}=\dfrac7{31}.\]
Now work in the vertical plane $APC$: $A=(-\sqrt2,0)$, $O'=(0,\dfrac7{31})$, and $C'=(\sqrt2s,1-s)$ on $PC$.
