$Y$: the line through $A$ perpendicular to $BC$ (direction $(-b,c)$) is $\{(ct,bt)\}$. Substituting into $DE$: $bct(b^2+c^2)=-b^2c^2$, so $Y=\left(-\dfrac{bc^2}k,-\dfrac{b^2c}k\right)$.

$X$: the line through $A$ perpendicular to $DE$ is $\{(b^3u,c^3u)\}$. On $BC$ ($\dfrac xb+\dfrac yc=1$): $(b^2+c^2)u=1$, so $X=\left(\dfrac{b^3}k,\dfrac{c^3}k\right)$.

Then $\overrightarrow{YX}=\left(\dfrac{b(b^2+c^2)}k,\dfrac{c(c^2+b^2)}k\right)=(b,c)$, so $XY=\sqrt{b^2+c^2}=BC$. $\blacksquare$

