Let the arcs (not containing the other vertices) be $AB=\alpha$, $BC=\beta$, $CD=\gamma$, $DA=\delta$, so $\alpha+\beta+\gamma+\delta=360^\circ$, and $\angle ADB=\dfrac\alpha2$.

$AC\perp BD$ gives $\dfrac{\alpha+\gamma}2=90^\circ$, so $\alpha+\gamma=180^\circ$. $\angle DAB=50^\circ$ gives $\beta+\gamma=100^\circ$.

Since $M$ bisects arc $DA$, $\angle MBD=\dfrac\delta4=\angle MCA$, i.e. $\angle YBX=\angle YCX$, so $B,C,X,Y$ are concyclic. Hence the exterior angle $\angle XYM$ of this cyclic quadrilateral at $Y$ equals $\angle XCB=\angle MCB=\dfrac12\left(\alpha+\dfrac\delta2\right)$.
