Use coordinates with $H=(0,0)$, $B=(p,0)$, $C=(0,q)$ ($p,q>0$), so that line $AB$ is the $x$-axis and $\angle BHC=90^\circ$. Put $k=p^2+q^2$.

$A=(a,0)$ with $AC=AB$: $a^2+q^2=(p-a)^2$ gives $a=\dfrac{p^2-q^2}{2p}$ and $AC=\dfrac k{2p}$. Then $D=C+\dfrac{q}{AC}(A-C)$ is
\[D=\left(\dfrac{q(p^2-q^2)}k,\ \dfrac{q(p-q)^2}k\right),\qquad H'=\left(\dfrac{pq^2}k,\ \dfrac{p^2q}k\right),\]
the latter being the foot from the origin to $\dfrac xp+\dfrac yq=1$.

$HT$ is the line $y=x$, and $CM$ (with $M=\left(\dfrac p2,0\right)$) is $\dfrac{2x}p+\dfrac yq=1$; they meet at $X=(t,t)$ with $t=\dfrac{pq}{p+2q}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real p=2,q=3,k=p^2+q^2;pair H=(0,0),B=(p,0),C=(0,q),A=((p^2-q^2)/(2*p),0);
pair D=(q*(p^2-q^2)/k,q*(p-q)^2/k),Hp=(p*q^2/k,p^2*q/k),M=(p/2,0),X=(p*q/(p+2*q),p*q/(p+2*q));
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(C--H);draw(H--Hp,dotted);draw(D--Hp);draw(C--M);draw(H--(2*X-H),dashed);dot(X);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$H$",H,S);label("$D$",D,W);label("$H'$",Hp,NE);label("$M$",M,S);label("$X$",X,E);
\end{asy}

It remains to show that $D$, $H'$, $X$ are collinear. Using $k=p^2+q^2$:
\[k(H'-D)=\big(pq^2-q(p^2-q^2),\ p^2q-q(p-q)^2\big)=\big(q(q^2+pq-p^2),\ q^2(2p-q)\big),\]
\[\dfrac{k(p+2q)}q(X-D)=\big(pk-(p^2-q^2)(p+2q),\ pk-(p-q)^2(p+2q)\big)=\big(2q(q^2+pq-p^2),\ 2q^2(2p-q)\big),\]
after expanding ($pk=p^3+pq^2$, $(p^2-q^2)(p+2q)=p^3+2p^2q-pq^2-2q^3$, $(p-q)^2(p+2q)=p^3-3pq^2+2q^3$). The second vector is twice the first, so $X$ lies on line $DH'$. Hence the three lines pass through $X$. $\blacksquare$

The verticals at the two cities meet at the Earth's centre at an angle of $7.2^\circ$, which is $\dfrac1{50}$ of a full turn, so the circumference is $50\cdot800=40000$ km.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),1));pair S=dir(70),A=dir(110);draw((0,0)--S);draw((0,0)--A);draw(S--S*1.3);draw(A--A*1.3);draw(arc((0,0),0.25,70,110));label("Syene",S*1.3,NE);label("Alexandria",A*1.3,NW);label("$7.2^\circ$",(0,0.25),N);label("$800$ km",dir(90)*1.3,N);label("(not to scale)",(0,-1.2),S);
\end{asy}

The radius is $r=\dfrac{40000}{2\pi}=\dfrac{20000}\pi$ km, and the volume is $\dfrac43\pi r^3=\dfrac{4\cdot8\cdot10^{12}}{3\pi^2}\approx\boxed{1.08\times10^{12}\text{ km}^3}$.

A cube has $8$ vertices, each on $3$ edges, and each edge touches $2$ vertices.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair o=(0,0),x=(2,0),y=(0.8,0.6),z=(0,2);draw(o--x--x+z--z--cycle);draw(z--z+y--x+y+z--x+z);draw(x--x+y--x+y+z);draw(o--y,dashed);draw(y--y+z,dashed);draw(y--x+y,dashed);
\end{asy}

Adding the vertex sums counts every label twice: $8S=2(1+2+\cdots+12)=156$, so $S=19.5$, not an integer. So $\boxed{\text{no}}$.

By the law of cosines, $7=1+9-6\cos A$, so $\cos A=\dfrac12$, $A=\dfrac\pi3$ and $n=3$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(3,0),C=(0.5,0.866);draw(A--B--C--cycle);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$3$",(1.5,0),S);label("$1$",(A+C)/2,W);label("$\sqrt7$",(B+C)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

Now $3^{2007}\bmod1000$: modulo $8$, $3^2\equiv1$ so $3^{2007}\equiv3$. Modulo $125$, $3^{100}\equiv1$ (Euler), so $3^{2007}\equiv3^7=2187\equiv62$. The number $\equiv3\pmod8$ and $\equiv62\pmod{125}$ is $187$. The remainder is $\boxed{187}$.

With radii $r_1,r_2$ and heights $2h,3h$:
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(ellipse((0,0),2,0.4));draw((-2,0)--(0,3)--(2,0));draw((0,0)--(0,3),dashed);label("$r_1$",(1,0),S);label("$2h$",(0,1.5),W);
draw(ellipse((6,0),1,0.25));draw(ellipse((6,4.5),1,0.25));draw((5,0)--(5,4.5));draw((7,0)--(7,4.5));label("$r_2$",(6.5,0),S);label("$3h$",(7,2.25),E);
\end{asy}

\[\dfrac{\dfrac13\pi r_1^2\cdot2h}{\pi r_2^2\cdot3h}=\dfrac{2r_1^2}{9r_2^2}=\dfrac89,\]
so $r_1^2=4r_2^2$ and $r_1:r_2=\boxed{2:1}$.

In an isosceles right triangle with legs $1$ the hypotenuse is $\sqrt2$:
\begin{asy}
