size(2.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(0,1)--cycle);label("$1$",(0.5,0),S);label("$1$",(0,0.5),W);label("$\sqrt2$",(0.5,0.5),NE);label("$45^\circ$",(1,0),8*dir(165));
\end{asy}

so $\sin45^\circ=\dfrac1{\sqrt2}=\dfrac{\sqrt2}2$, and $x=\boxed{2}$.

A triangle inside a circle of radius $20$ has perimeter at most that of the inscribed equilateral triangle, $3\cdot20\sqrt3\approx103.9$ (for a triangle inside the circle, moving its vertices out to the circle along rays from a suitable point only increases the perimeter, and among inscribed triangles the equilateral one has the largest perimeter). So the perimeter is at most $103$.

The triangle $31,35,37$ has perimeter $103$. It is acute ($31^2+35^2>37^2$), and its circumradius is $R=\dfrac{abc}{4K}$ with $16K^2=4041411$, so $K\approx502.58$ and $R\approx19.97<20$. Hence it fits inside the circle (it is inscribed in a slightly smaller concentric circle), and the answer is $\boxed{103}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),20));real R=19.969;real A=2*asin(31/(2*R)),B=2*asin(35/(2*R));pair P=R*dir(90),Q=R*dir(90+degrees(A)),S=R*dir(90-degrees(B));draw(P--Q--S--cycle);label("$31$",(P+Q)/2,NW);label("$35$",(P+S)/2,NE);label("$37$",0.8*(Q+S)/2,N);dot((0,0));
\end{asy}

The whole board is the larger square, so its area is $16\cdot\dfrac94=36$ and its side is $\boxed{6}$ inches.
