usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(shift(-1,-1)*scale(2)*unitsquare);draw(shift(-1.5,-1.5)*scale(3)*unitsquare);label("$4$",(0,-1),N);label("$6$",(0,-1.5),S);
\end{asy}

The error in the attempt was taking $36$ as the side-length-squared of $9$: if the area is $36$, the side is $\sqrt{36}=6$, not $9$.

The rotation sends $O$ to $O'$ with $PO'=PO=2$ and $\angle OPO'=90^\circ$, so $OO'=2\sqrt2$. The common chord $MN$ is perpendicular to $OO'$ at its midpoint, which is $\sqrt2$ from $O$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),P=(2,0),Op=P+rotate(90)*(O-P);draw(circle(O,4));draw(circle(Op,4),dashed);dot(O);dot(P);dot(Op);draw(O--P--Op,dotted);
pair m=(O+Op)/2,u=unit(rotate(90)*(Op-O));pair M=m+sqrt(14)*u,N=m-sqrt(14)*u;draw(M--N);dot(M);dot(N);
label("$O$",O,SW);label("$P$",P,SE);label("$O'$",Op,NE);label("$M$",M,N);label("$N$",N,S);
\end{asy}

So $\dfrac{MN}2=\sqrt{16-2}=\sqrt{14}$ and $MN=\boxed{2\sqrt{14}}$.

By symmetry every chord of the path subtends the same central angle $\theta$, so the $j$th point hit is at angle $j\theta$ from the gap. Returning first after $323$ chords means $323\theta$ is a multiple of $360^\circ$ but no smaller multiple of $\theta$ is: $\theta=\dfrac{360^\circ k}{323}$ with $1\le k\le322$ and $\gcd(k,323)=1$. (The path is a regular star polygon; the picture shows a smaller case.)
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(unitcircle);int n=17,k=3;for(int i=0;i<n;++i) draw(dir(90+360*k*i/n)--dir(90+360*k*(i+1)/n),gray);dot(dir(90));label("gap",dir(90),N);
\end{asy}

Each such $k$ corresponds to a different initial direction ($k$ and $323-k$ are mirror images, which are different directions). Since $323=17\cdot19$, the count is $\varphi(323)=16\cdot18=\boxed{288}$.

By the difference formula,
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(unitcircle);pair P=dir(25);draw((0,0)--P);draw((0,0)--(1,0));draw(P--(P.x,0),dashed);label("$A$",(0,0),5*dir(12));label("$\cos A$",(P.x/2,0),S);label("$\sin A$",(P.x,P.y/2),E);
\end{asy}

\[\sqrt2\cos(45^\circ-A)=\sqrt2\left(\cos45^\circ\cos A+\sin45^\circ\sin A\right)=\sqrt2\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt2}2(\cos A+\sin A)=\sin A+\cos A.\ \blacksquare\]

Each tip angle is an inscribed angle subtending the arc between the two neighbouring points that are joined to it, avoiding the tip. The points split the circle into $n$ arcs of $\dfrac{360^\circ}n$; the arc opposite a tip consists of the arcs not between the tip and its two neighbours, i.e. $n-2k$ small arcs.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int n=7,k=3;pair[] P;for(int i=0;i<n;++i) P.push(dir(90+360*i/n));for(int i=0;i<n;++i) draw(P[i]--P[(i+k)%n]);for(int i=0;i<n;++i) dot(P[i]);label("tip",P[0],N);
\end{asy}

So each tip angle is $\dfrac12(n-2k)\dfrac{360^\circ}n$, and the $n$ tips sum to $180^\circ(n-2k)$. For the usual five-pointed star, $S(5,2)$, this is $180^\circ$.

For seven points: $S(7,2)$ gives $\boxed{540^\circ}$ and $S(7,3)$ gives $\boxed{180^\circ}$.

For the pentagon to be concave, $E$ lies inside the square $ABCD$, and the pentagon is the square with triangle $DEA$ removed.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(3,3),B=(3,-3),C=(-3,-3),D=(-3,3),E=(0.5,-5/3);fill(D--E--A--cycle,lightgray);draw(A--B--C--D--E--cycle);draw(D--A,dashed);
label("$A$",A,NE);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,SW);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$E(t,u)$",E,S);
