$AC=\sqrt{8^2-6^2}=\boxed{2\sqrt7}$.

If $K$ is the area and $a,b,c$ the sides, then $a=\dfrac{2K}{h_a}$, etc., and $K=rs=\dfrac r2(a+b+c)=rK\left(\dfrac1{h_a}+\dfrac1{h_b}+\dfrac1{h_c}\right)$. So
\[\dfrac1r=\dfrac1{h_a}+\dfrac1{h_b}+\dfrac1{h_c}=\dfrac1{24}+\dfrac1{24}+\dfrac1{20}=\dfrac{16}{120},\]
and $r=\boxed{\dfrac{15}2}$. (Such a triangle exists: it is isosceles with sides in ratio $5:5:6$, e.g. sides $25,25,30$, with altitudes $24,24,20$.)
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(30,0),C=(15,20);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(C--(15,0),dashed);label("$25$",(A+C)/2,NW);label("$25$",(B+C)/2,NE);label("$30$",(15,0),S);label("$20$",(15,10),E);
\end{asy}

Split the $15$ positions into $6$ groups in which every two balls touch: the top ball alone; a pair in rows $2$-$3$; and four triangles of three mutually touching balls, as shown (thick lines join balls in the same group).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=1;pair P(int r,int i){return (i-r/2.0,-r*sqrt(3)/2);}for(int r=0;r<5;++r) for(int i=0;i<=r;++i) draw(circle(P(r,i),0.5));
draw(P(1,0)--P(2,0),linewidth(2));draw(P(1,1)--P(2,1)--P(2,2)--cycle,linewidth(2));draw(P(3,0)--P(4,0)--P(4,1)--cycle,linewidth(2));draw(P(3,1)--P(3,2)--P(4,2)--cycle,linewidth(2));draw(P(3,3)--P(4,3)--P(4,4)--cycle,linewidth(2));dot(P(0,0));
\end{asy}

Seven striped balls in six groups: by the pigeonhole principle two are in the same group, and those two touch. $\blacksquare$

Let $PQ$ be a longest side and project all vertices orthogonally onto line $PQ$. Going around the polygon from $Q$ back to $P$ through the other vertices $V_1,\dots,V_k$ ($k\ge1$), suppose no $V_i$ projects onto the segment $PQ$. The projections start at $Q$ and end at $P$, so some consecutive pair of vertices on this path (possibly including $Q$ or $P$ itself) projects to points on opposite sides of segment $PQ$, at least one of them strictly outside it. The side joining them then has projection longer than $PQ$, and a segment is at least as long as its projection, contradicting the choice of $PQ$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V={(0,0),(5,0),(6.5,2),(4,4),(1,3.5),(-0.8,1.8)};draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle);draw(V[0]--V[1],linewidth(1.5));pair A=V[4];pair H=(A.x,0);draw(A--H,dashed);dot(H);label("$P$",V[0],SW);label("$Q$",V[1],SE);label("$A$",A,N);
\end{asy}

So some vertex $A=V_i$ projects onto segment $PQ$. $\blacksquare$

The path must go over the rim. Unroll the outer surface and, reflected across the rim, the inner surface. Arnav must cover half the circumference horizontally, $\dfrac12\cdot2\pi\cdot2=2\pi$, and a vertical distance of $4$ up to the rim plus $4$ down inside, $8$ in total.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(12.57,0)--(12.57,8)--(0,8)--cycle);draw((0,8)--(12.57,8));draw((0,4)--(6.28,8)--(12.57,4),dashed);
draw((0,8)--(12.57,8),linewidth(1));label("rim",(6.28,8),N);dot((0,4));dot((12.57,4));label("outside",(3,2));label("inside (reflected)",(9.5,2));draw((6.28,0)--(6.28,8),dotted);
\end{asy}

In the unrolled picture the shortest path is a straight segment, of length $\sqrt{8^2+(2\pi)^2}=\boxed{2\sqrt{16+\pi^2}}\approx9.3$ inches. (In the textbook problem "diametrically opposite" does mean the point at the same height on the opposite side.)

$(x+3)y=(x-3)(x+3)$ means $(x+3)(y-x+3)=0$: the graph is the union of the line $y=x-3$ and the vertical line $x=-3$. (Dividing by $x+3$ loses the second line.)
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-5,0)--(5,0),Arrow);draw((0,-7)--(0,3),Arrow);draw((-4,-7)--(5,2));draw((-3,-7)--(-3,3));dot((0,-3));dot((3,0));dot((-3,0));label("$(0,-3)$",(0,-3),E);label("$(3,0)$",(3,0),SE);label("$(-3,0)$",(-3,0),NW);
\end{asy}


$(0,a)$ is on $y=x-3$: $a=-3$. The points on the $x$-axis are $(3,0)$ and $(-3,0)$. So $a+b+c=-3+3-3=\boxed{-3}$.

Here $\angle B=\angle C=80^\circ$ and $\angle A=20^\circ$. Take $BC=1$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(1,0),A=(0.5,0.5*tan(80*pi/180));pair E=extension(B,B+dir(70),A,C),D=extension(C,C+dir(120),A,B);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(B--E);draw(C--D);draw(D--E,dashed);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,W);label("$E$",E,E);
\end{asy}

In $\triangle BCE$: $\angle BEC=30^\circ$, so $BE=\dfrac{\sin80^\circ}{\sin30^\circ}=2\sin80^\circ$. In $\triangle BCD$: $\angle BDC=40^\circ$, so $BD=\dfrac{\sin60^\circ}{\sin40^\circ}$.

In $\triangle BDE$, $\angle DBE=10^\circ$. Let $\angle DEB=x$, so $\angle BDE=170^\circ-x$, and by the law of sines
\[\dfrac{\sin(170^\circ-x)}{\sin x}=\dfrac{BE}{BD}=\dfrac{2\sin80^\circ\sin40^\circ}{\sin60^\circ}.\]
From $\sin\theta\sin(60^\circ-\theta)\sin(60^\circ+\theta)=\dfrac14\sin3\theta$ with $\theta=20^\circ$, $\sin20^\circ\sin40^\circ\sin80^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt3}8$, so the right side equals $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4\sin20^\circ}\cdot\dfrac2{\sqrt3}=\dfrac1{2\sin20^\circ}=\dfrac{\sin150^\circ}{\sin20^\circ}$. Thus $x=20^\circ$ works, and it is the only solution because $\dfrac{\sin(170^\circ-x)}{\sin x}=\sin170^\circ\cot x-\cos170^\circ$ is strictly decreasing on $(0^\circ,170^\circ)$. So $\angle DEB=\boxed{20^\circ}$.

The diagonal is a side of both pieces. The hexagon uses $5$ sides of the octagon, leaving $3$ octagon sides for the other piece, which with the diagonal has $\boxed{4}$ sides.
