Their distance is $\sqrt{4^2+4^2}=\boxed{4\sqrt2}$.

For a vertex $v$ and a target vertex $w$, exactly one rotation and one reflection send $v$ to $w$ if $w\ne v$, and only one reflection (the axis through $v$) fixes $v$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V;for(int k=0;k<6;++k) V.push(dir(60k));draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle);for(int k=0;k<3;++k){draw(1.2*dir(60k)--1.2*dir(60k+180),dashed);draw(1.2*dir(60k+30)--1.2*dir(60k+210),dashed);}dot(V[0]);label("$P$",V[0],E);
\end{asy}

Let $p_1,\dots,p_6$ be the positions of $P$ after each step; they must be the six vertices in some order. Any of the $11$ transformations may come first ($p_1$ may even be $P$ itself). After $j$ vertices have been visited, the next step must go from $p_j$ to one of the $6-j$ unvisited vertices, and each such move can be made by exactly $2$ transformations. So
\[k=11\cdot(2\cdot5)\cdot(2\cdot4)\cdot(2\cdot3)\cdot(2\cdot2)\cdot(2\cdot1)=\boxed{42240}.\]

Square $\sin^2x+\cos^2x=1$ (the unit-circle relation):
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(unitcircle);pair P=dir(35);draw((0,0)--P);draw((-1.2,0)--(1.2,0));draw((0,-1.2)--(0,1.2));draw(P--(P.x,0),dashed);label("$\cos x$",(P.x/2,0),S);label("$\sin x$",(P.x,P.y/2),E);label("$x$",0.45*dir(17));
\end{asy}

$\sin^4x+\cos^4x=(\sin^2x+\cos^2x)^2-2\sin^2x\cos^2x=1-\dfrac12(2\sin x\cos x)^2=1-\dfrac12\sin^22x$. This is never $0$ (it is at least $\dfrac12$), so taking reciprocals gives the identity. $\blacksquare$

Any point of the chord is $P=(1-t)A+tB$ for some $t\in[0,1]$ (using vectors from $O$).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(unitcircle);pair A=dir(150),B=dir(20),O=(0,0);draw(A--B);draw(O--A,dashed);draw(O--B,dashed);pair P=0.4*A+0.6*B;draw(O--P,dotted);dot(P);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$O$",O,S);label("$P$",P,N);
\end{asy}

By the triangle inequality, $|OP|=|(1-t)\vec{OA}+t\vec{OB}|\le(1-t)|OA|+t|OB|=(1-t)r+tr=r$. So $P$ is in the closed disc. (Equality holds only at $A$ and $B$, since $\vec{OA}$ and $\vec{OB}$ are not in the same direction.) $\blacksquare$

Lower bound: each row must contain a shot (else a horizontal ship fits in it), and so must each column. With only $4$ shots there would be exactly one per row and per column. A row whose only shot is in column $1$ or $4$ still has room for a ship (columns $2$-$4$ or $1$-$3$), so each row's shot is in column $2$ or $3$; likewise each column's shot is in row $2$ or $3$. But the shots in rows $1$ and $4$ are then the only shots in their columns and lie outside rows $2,3$, a contradiction. So at least $5$ shots are needed.

$5$ shots suffice, e.g. the shaded squares:
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<=4;++i){draw((i,0)--(i,4));draw((0,i)--(4,i));}pair[] S={(1,0),(0,1),(3,1),(2,2),(1,3)};for(pair s:S) fill(shift(s)*unitsquare,gray);
\end{asy}

every line of three consecutive squares, in any row or column, contains a shaded square (checked directly). The answer is $\boxed{5}$.

The point at angle $x$ on the unit circle is $P=(\cos x,\sin x)$. Reflecting in the $y$-axis sends the ray at angle $x$ to the ray at angle $\pi-x$ (the angle from the negative $x$-axis is again $x$), so the point at angle $\pi-x$ is $Q=(-\cos x,\sin x)$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(unitcircle);pair P=dir(40),Q=dir(140);draw((-1.2,0)--(1.2,0));draw((0,-1.2)--(0,1.2));draw((0,0)--P);draw((0,0)--Q);draw(P--Q,dashed);label("$x$",(0.25,0.08),E);label("$\pi-x$",(-0.3,0.12),W);label("$(\cos x,\sin x)$",P,NE);label("$(-\cos x,\sin x)$",Q,NW);
\end{asy}

The $y$-coordinates agree, so $\sin(\pi-x)=\sin x$ (and also $\cos(\pi-x)=-\cos x$). $\blacksquare$

With legs $a,b$ and hypotenuse $c$: $a^2+b^2+c^2=2c^2=98$, so $c=7$ and $a+b=9$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(6.83,0),C=(0,1.17);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw((0.3,0)--(0.3,0.3)--(0,0.3));label("$a$",(3.4,0),S);label("$b$",(0,0.6),W);label("$c=7$",(3.4,0.6),NE);
\end{asy}

Then $2ab=(a+b)^2-(a^2+b^2)=81-49=32$, and the area is $\dfrac{ab}2=\boxed{8}$.

$\boxed{\text{Yes}}$. Positions are $1,\dots,2011$; the mirror of $i$ is $2012-i$, and the middle position $1006$ is its own mirror.

For the first $1006$ moves she does nothing. When letter $k>1006$ arrives, its mirror $m=2012-k$ is already filled; the positions $k+1,\dots,2011$ are still empty, and we keep the invariant that every filled position other than $1,\dots,m$ and the middle already matches its mirror. If $s_m=s_k$ she does nothing. Otherwise:
\begin{itemize}
\item if some position $p\in\{1,\dots,m-1\}\cup\{1006\}$ holds the letter $s_k$, she swaps $s_p$ and $s_m$, so now $s_m=s_k$;
\item if not, all those positions hold the other letter $s_m$; in particular $s_{1006}=s_m$, and she swaps $s_k$ with $s_{1006}$, so now $s_k=s_m$ (the middle may hold anything).
\end{itemize}
Positions $1,\dots,m-1$ have unfilled mirrors, so changing them does no harm, and the invariant is kept. After move $2011$ every pair matches, so the row is a palindrome. $\blacksquare$

Let $n=\overline{MBMT}=1010M+100B+T$. Then $0.\overline{MBMT}=\dfrac n{9999}$ with $9999=3^2\cdot11\cdot101$, so $q=\dfrac{9999}{\gcd(n,9999)}$. The divisors of $9999$ below $303$ are $1,3,9,11,33,99,101$, and getting $q\le101$ needs $101\mid n$ or $99\mid n$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $n\equiv T-B\pmod{101}$ (as $1010\equiv0$ and $100\equiv-1$), so $101\mid n$ forces $B=T$.
\item $99\mid n$ needs $11\mid 2M-B-T$ and $9\mid 2M+B+T$. Checking $2M-B-T\in\{-11,0,11\}$ (other multiples of $11$ are out of range) gives no solution with distinct digits.
\end{itemize}
So $q\ge303$, which needs $\gcd(n,9999)=33$: for example $n=1419=3\cdot11\cdot43$ gives $\dfrac{1419}{9999}=\dfrac{43}{303}$. The minimum is $q=\boxed{303}$.

A white square is a one-square component exactly when all its neighbours (including diagonal ones) are black. By linearity of expectation, add up the probabilities:
\begin{itemize}
\item $98^2=9604$ inner squares, $8$ neighbours: probability $2^{-9}$ each;
\item $4\cdot98=392$ edge squares, $5$ neighbours: probability $2^{-6}$;
\item $4$ corner squares, $3$ neighbours: probability $2^{-4}$.
\end{itemize}
The expected number is $\dfrac{9604}{512}+\dfrac{392}{64}+\dfrac4{16}\approx18.76+6.13+0.25=25.13$, so the answer is $\boxed{25}$.

Use complex numbers with $A=0$, $B=1$. If $\ell$ has direction $u$ ($|u|=1$), then $X=\pm u$ and $Y=1\pm iu$, so the midpoint is
\[M=\dfrac12+\dfrac{(\pm1\pm i)u}2.\]
Since $\left|\dfrac{\pm1\pm i}2\right|=\dfrac{\sqrt2}2$ and $u$ runs over the whole unit circle, every choice of signs traces the same circle with centre $\dfrac12$ (the midpoint of $AB$) and radius $\dfrac{\sqrt2}2$:
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(1,0);real t=55;pair u=dir(t),X=u,Y=B+dir(t+90),M=(X+Y)/2;draw(circle((0.5,0),sqrt(2)/2),dashed);
draw(A-0.4*u--A+1.4*u);draw(B-0.4*dir(t+90)--B+1.4*dir(t+90));draw(X--Y,gray(0.5));dot(A);dot(B);dot(X);dot(Y);dot(M);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$X$",X,NE);label("$Y$",Y,W);label("$M$",M,N);label("$\ell$",A+1.4*u,N);label("$\ell'$",B+1.4*dir(t+90),N);
\end{asy}

Its length is $2\pi\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt2}2=\boxed{\pi\sqrt2}$.

Let $b_i$ be the $n$ products, each $\pm1$. The sum is $0$, so $n$ is even and exactly $\dfrac n2$ of the $b_i$ are $-1$. Each $a_j$ appears in exactly four products, so $\prod b_i=\prod a_j^4=1$ and the number of $-1$s, $\dfrac n2$, is even: $4\mid n$. Also $n=4$ fails, since then all four products equal $a_1a_2a_3a_4$.

Every $n=4m$ with $m\ge2$ works. Start with all $a_j=1$ (all products $+1$). A single $-1$ makes the $4$ products containing it negative; two adjacent $-1$s make exactly $2$ products negative (the $3$ windows containing both stay positive). Blocks separated by at least three $1$s do not interact. We need $2m$ negative products:
\begin{itemize}
\item $m$ even: $\dfrac m2$ single $-1$s, each followed by three $1$s ($2m\le n$ places);
\item $m$ odd: $\dfrac{m-1}2$ such singles and one adjacent pair followed by three $1$s ($2m+3\le n$ places).
\end{itemize}
So the possible $n$ are $8,12,\dots,100$: $\boxed{24}$ values.

If the square has side $s$, then $[PAB]+[PCD]=\dfrac s2(d_1+d_2)=\dfrac{s^2}2$, where $d_1,d_2$ are the distances from $P$ to $AB$ and $CD$; likewise $[PBC]+[PDA]=\dfrac{s^2}2$. So opposite triangles have equal sums.

If the two $25$s are opposite, the fourth area $x$ satisfies $15+x=50$, so $x=35$ (side $10$). If a $25$ is opposite the $15$, then $25+x=25+15$, so $x=15$ (side $\sqrt{80}$). Both cases occur with $P$ inside (for example $P$ at distances $5$ from $AB$ and $3$ from $AD$ in a square of side $10$):
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(10,0),C=(10,10),Dd=(0,10),P=(3,5);draw(A--B--C--Dd--cycle);draw(A--P--B);draw(C--P--Dd);dot(P);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",Dd,NW);label("$P$",P,E);label("$25$",(A+B+P)/3);label("$35$",(B+C+P)/3);label("$25$",(C+Dd+P)/3);label("$15$",(Dd+A+P)/3);
\end{asy}

The possible areas are $\boxed{15\text{ and }35}$.

Put the centre $O$ at the origin with $\ell$ the $x$-axis, and let $m$ touch the semicircle at $T=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$, so $m$ is $x\cos\theta+y\sin\theta=1$; by symmetry take $0\le\theta\le90^\circ$. The largest circle lies on the far side of the semicircle from where $m$ meets $\ell$; let it have centre $(-h,r)$ with $h>0$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real th=50;real s=(Cos(th)+2*sqrt(1+Sin(th)))/(1+Sin(th));real r=(s*s-1)/2;pair O=(0,0),T=dir(th),X=(1/Cos(th),0),d=(-Sin(th),Cos(th));
draw((-4,0)--(2.5,0));draw(arc(O,1,0,180));draw((-1,0)--(1,0));draw(X-0.8*d--X+6*d);draw(circle((-s,r),r));dot(T);dot(O);
label("$\ell$",(2.5,0),E);label("$m$",X+6*d,N);label("$T$",T,NE);label("$O$",O,S);label("$r$",(-s,r)+(0,r/2),E);draw((-s,r)--(-s,2*r),dashed);
\end{asy}

Tangency to the semicircle gives $h^2+r^2=(1+r)^2$, so $h=\sqrt{1+2r}$. Tangency to $m$ (on the semicircle's side) gives $1+h\cos\theta-r\sin\theta=r$. Writing $h=u$ and $r=\dfrac{u^2-1}2$, this becomes
\[(1+\sin\theta)u^2-2u\cos\theta-(3+\sin\theta)=0,\qquad u=\dfrac{\cos\theta+2\sqrt{1+\sin\theta}}{1+\sin\theta}\]
(the discriminant is $16(1+\sin\theta)$). As $\theta$ goes from $0$ to $90^\circ$, both $\dfrac{\cos\theta}{1+\sin\theta}$ and $\dfrac2{\sqrt{1+\sin\theta}}$ decrease, so $u$ decreases from $3$ to $\sqrt2$ and $r$ decreases from $4$ to $\dfrac12$. Hence $[a,b]=\left[\dfrac12,4\right]$ and $b-a=\boxed{\dfrac72}$.

Describe the solid by the heights $h(i,j)$ of its $25$ columns over a $5\times5$ base. The top view forces every $h(i,j)\ge1$; the front and side views force the tallest column in each row and in each column of the base to have height $5$.

The largest volume is $125$ (the full cube). For the smallest, the height-$5$ columns must meet every row and column of the base, so there are at least $5$ of them, and the other columns have height at least $1$: volume at least $5\cdot5+20=45$. This is achieved with the tall columns on a diagonal (heights shown from above):
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<5;++i) for(int j=0;j<5;++j){draw(shift(i,j)*unitsquare);label((i==j)?"$5$":"$1$",(i+0.5,j+0.5));}
\end{asy}

The difference is $125-45=\boxed{80}$.

The scaling (homothety) centred at the cone's apex that maps the bottom ball onto the middle ball (both are balls inscribed in the cone) has ratio $\dfrac23$. It maps the cone to itself and the middle ball to a ball inscribed in the cone and tangent to the image of the bottom ball, i.e. tangent to the middle ball on the apex side: that is the top ball.
