pair V=(0,9);real sa=0.2,ca=sqrt(1-sa*sa);draw(V--V+(9/ca)*(-sa,-ca));draw(V--V+(9/ca)*(sa,-ca));draw((-3,0)--(3,0),gray(0.6));
real[] r={1.5,1,2/3};for(int i=0;i<3;++i){real d=r[i]/sa;draw(circle(V-(0,d),r[i]));}
label("$3$",V-(0,7.5),E);label("$2$",V-(0,5),E);label("$?$",V-(0,10/3),E);dot(V);
\end{asy}

So the diameters form a geometric sequence $3,2,\dfrac43$, and the top ball has diameter $\boxed{\dfrac43\text{ ft}}$.

$\boxed{\text{Yes}}$; in fact the whole plane can be lit. Choose axes so that no two poles have the same $y$-coordinate, and a horizontal line with two poles above it ($U_1$ left of $U_2$) and two below ($L_1$ left of $L_2$).

Aim $L_1$ up-right (the quadrant $x\ge x_{L_1}$, $y\ge y_{L_1}$) and $L_2$ up-left ($x\le x_{L_2}$, $y\ge y_{L_2}$). Since $x_{L_1}\le x_{L_2}$, every point with $y\ge\max(y_{L_1},y_{L_2})$ is lit by one of them. Symmetrically aim $U_1$ down-right and $U_2$ down-left; they light every point with $y\le\min(y_{U_1},y_{U_2})$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair U1=(-1,2),U2=(2,1.6),L1=(-2,-1),L2=(1.5,-1.5);draw((-4,0.3)--(4,0.3),dashed);
fill(L1--(4,-1)--(4,3)--(L1.x,3)--cycle,opacity(0.15)+blue);fill(L2--(-4,-1.5)--(-4,3)--(L2.x,3)--cycle,opacity(0.15)+blue);
fill(U1--(4,2)--(4,-3)--(U1.x,-3)--cycle,opacity(0.15)+red);fill(U2--(-4,1.6)--(-4,-3)--(U2.x,-3)--cycle,opacity(0.15)+red);
dot(U1);dot(U2);dot(L1);dot(L2);label("$U_1$",U1,N);label("$U_2$",U2,N);label("$L_1$",L1,S);label("$L_2$",L2,S);
\end{asy}

Since the upper poles are above the dividing line and the lower ones below, these two half-planes together cover the plane. $\blacksquare$

Let $AC=b$ and $BC=a$. By Pythagoras in triangles $ACN$ and $BCM$:
