Rotating $k$ times by $48^\circ$ gives a symmetry by $48k^\circ$, and angles are taken mod $360^\circ$.

(a) $\boxed{\text{Yes}}$: $48\cdot9=432=360+72$, so rotating by $48^\circ$ nine times is a rotation by $72^\circ$.

(b) $\boxed{\text{No}}$: the rotations $48k^\circ$ mod $360^\circ$ are exactly the multiples of $\gcd(48,360)=24^\circ$, and $90$ is not one. For example, a regular $15$-gon centred at $P$ is unchanged by $48^\circ$ (a multiple of $24^\circ$) but not by $90^\circ$.
