Write $c(i,j)$ for the colour of row $i$, column $j$.

Step 1. In two adjacent rows, column $j+2$ shows the same two colours as column $j$ (both are the complement of column $j+1$'s colours), either in the same order or swapped.

Step 2. If some row is not $2$-periodic, i.e. $c(i,j)\ne c(i,j+2)$, then columns $j$ and $j+2$ are swapped in rows $i,i+1$: $c(i+1,j+2)=c(i,j)$ and $c(i,j+2)=c(i+1,j)$; so $c(i+1,j)\ne c(i+1,j+2)$ too, and likewise for row $i-1$. By induction every row is swapped at $j$, which gives $c(k+2,j)=c(k+1,j+2)=c(k,j)$: column $j$ is $2$-periodic. Its neighbours then alternate between the two complementary colours, so they are $2$-periodic too, and so on: every column is $2$-periodic.

So either every row or every column is $2$-periodic; say every row (the other case is symmetric). Then $c(1,8)=c(1,2)$ and $c(8,8)=c(8,2)$. Consecutive rows use complementary pairs of colours in columns $1,2$, so rows $1$ and $8$ (an odd distance apart) use complementary pairs. Hence $\{c(1,1),c(1,8)\}$ and $\{c(8,1),c(8,8)\}$ are complementary pairs of two colours each, and all four corners differ. $\blacksquare$
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<4;++i) for(int j=0;j<4;++j){ int c=2*(i%2)+(j%2); pen p=(c==0)?white:(c==1)?gray(0.75):(c==2)?gray(0.5):gray(0.25); fill(shift(j,i)*unitsquare,p); draw(shift(j,i)*unitsquare);}label("every $2\times2$ block has all four colours",(2,-0.3),S);
\end{asy}

$P$ is convex and is the intersection of the $3$ half-planes bounding the triangle and the $4$ bounding the trapezoid. Each side of $P$ lies on one of these $7$ lines, and a convex polygon has at most one side on each line, so $P$ has at most $7$ sides; and a polygon has at least $3$. All of $\boxed{3,4,5,6,7}$ occur. With the trapezoid $(-4,0),(4,0),(2,3),(-2,3)$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $3$ sides: triangle $(-1,1),(1,1),(0,2)$ inside the trapezoid;
\item $4$ sides: a large triangle such as $(-10,-1),(10,-1),(0,20)$ containing the trapezoid;
