Use vectors with origin $B$. Then $P=2A-D$ and $Q=-E$, where $E=A+s(C-A)$ for some $s$.

We claim $O=2A-3C$. First, $D$ lies on $BC$, so $P=2A-D$ and $2A-3C$ differ by $D-3C$, a vector along $BC$; hence $2A-3C$ lies on $\ell$. Second, $(2A-3C)-Q=2A-3C+A+s(C-A)=(s-3)(C-A)$, which is parallel to $AC$; hence $2A-3C$ lies on $m$.
