\end{asy}

This is an integer exactly when $n$ divides $360=2^3\cdot3^2\cdot5$, which has $4\cdot3\cdot2=24$ divisors. Excluding $n=1$ and $n=2$ (no polygon), there are $\boxed{22}$ values.

Going from February $22$ of a year $Y$ ending in $22$ to February $22$ of $Y+100$ passes $100\cdot365+L$ days, where $L$ counts the leap days in between: the years $Y+2,Y+6,\dots,Y+98$, that is $25$ years divisible by $4$, one of which is a century year. That century year is a leap year only if it is divisible by $400$. Since $365\equiv1\pmod7$, the weekday moves forward by $100+24\equiv5$ days, or by $100+25\equiv6$ days when the century year is divisible by $400$.

Starting from Tuesday in $2022$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $2122$: $+5$ (2100 is not leap) gives Sunday;
\item $2222$: $+5$ (2200 is not leap) gives Friday;
\item $2322$: $+5$ (2300 is not leap) gives Wednesday;
\item $2422$: $+6$ (2400 is leap) gives Tuesday.
\end{itemize}
The total shift over $400$ years is $5+5+5+6=21\equiv0\pmod7$, so the pattern repeats every $400$ years, and Tuesday occurs once in each cycle. The answer is $\boxed{400}$ years (the next one is in $2422$).

question:
Let $f(x)=-2\cdot5^{-x}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Is $f$ increasing or decreasing?
\item[(b)] What happens to $f(x)$ as $x\to\infty$ and as $x\to-\infty$?
\item[(c)] A teacher says the graph is ``growing'' and a student says it is ``decaying''. Writing $f(x)=a\cdot b^x$, explain in what sense each statement is correct, and which is the standard classification.
\end{enumerate}

You and your $k$ friends ($k\ge1$) share a box of $n$ chocolates ($n\ge0$). The chocolates are split equally among your $k$ friends, each getting as many as possible, and you keep the remainder. Call the pair $(n,k)$ \emph{generous} if you end up with no more chocolates than each of your friends.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Describe all generous pairs.
\item[(b)] Show that for each $k$ there are exactly $\dfrac{k(k-1)}2$ values of $n$ for which $(n,k)$ is not generous. Deduce that, for fixed $k$, the proportion of $n\in\{0,1,\dots,N-1\}$ that are generous tends to $1$ as $N\to\infty$.
\end{enumerate}

An isosceles triangle has two legs of length $L$. Find the altitude to the base
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] if the vertex angle (between the legs) is $\theta$;
\item[(b)] if each base angle is $\alpha$.
\item[(c)] Evaluate both answers to two decimal places when $L=10$ and the known angle is $40^\circ$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the triangle be $ABC$ with $AB=AC=L$, and let $AD$ be the altitude to $BC$. In an isosceles triangle the altitude from the apex is also the median and the angle bisector, so triangle $ABD$ has a right angle at $D$, $\angle BAD=\dfrac\theta2$ and $\angle ABD=\alpha$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,3),B=(-2,0),C=(2,0),D=(0,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--D,dashed);draw((0,0)--(0.18,0)--(0.18,0.18)--(0,0.18));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);
label("$L$",(A+B)/2,NW);label("$L$",(A+C)/2,NE);label("$h$",(0,1.5),E);
label("$\dfrac{\theta}{2}$",A+(-0.25,-0.75),fontsize(8pt));label("$\alpha$",B+(0.55,0.17));
\end{asy}

(a) $h=AD=L\cos\dfrac\theta2$.

(b) $h=AD=L\sin\alpha$. (The two agree, since $\alpha=90^\circ-\dfrac\theta2$.)

(c) If the vertex angle is $40^\circ$, then $h=10\cos20^\circ\approx\boxed{9.40}$. If a base angle is $40^\circ$, then $h=10\sin40^\circ\approx\boxed{6.43}$. So it matters which angle is known.

Since $\angle D+\angle C=150^\circ<180^\circ$, the rays $DA$ and $CB$ meet at a point $P$ beyond $A$ and $B$, with $\angle P=30^\circ$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair D=(0,0),C=(7,0);pair A=5*dir(70);pair B=C+6*dir(100);pair P=14*Sin(80)*dir(70);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(A--P--B,dashed);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,SW);label("$P$",P,N);
label("$5$",(A+D)/2,W);label("$6$",(B+C)/2,E);label("$7$",(C+D)/2,S);
label("$70^\circ$",D+(1.2,0.4),fontsize(7pt));label("$80^\circ$",C+(-1.1,0.4),fontsize(7pt));draw(arc(P,1.3,degrees(A-P),degrees(B-P)));label("$30^\circ$",P+(1.25,-1.1),fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

By the law of sines in triangle $PDC$,
\[PD=\dfrac{7\sin80^\circ}{\sin30^\circ}=14\sin80^\circ\approx13.787,\qquad PC=14\sin70^\circ\approx13.156,\]
so $PA=PD-5>0$ and $PB=PC-6>0$. Using area $=\dfrac12xy\sin30^\circ=\dfrac{xy}4$ for both triangles with the angle at $P$,
\[[ABCD]=[PDC]-[PAB]=\dfrac{PD\cdot PC-(PD-5)(PC-6)}4=\dfrac{6PD+5PC-30}4=21\sin80^\circ+17.5\sin70^\circ-7.5.\]
Numerically this is $20.68097+16.44462-7.5\approx\boxed{29.626}$.

Let the side be $1$, the centre $O$, and the vertices $V_1,\dots,V_6$; each circle has radius $1$. Circles centred at adjacent vertices $V_1,V_2$ (distance $1$ apart) meet at $O$ and at $O'$, the reflection of $O$ in $V_1V_2$. Circles at $V_1,V_3$ (distance $\sqrt3$) meet at $V_2$ and $O$, and circles at opposite vertices are tangent at $O$. So the six new points are the reflections of $O$ in the six sides.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);pair[] V,Wp;for(int k=0;k<6;++k){V.push(dir(60*k));Wp.push(sqrt(3)*dir(60*k+30));}
for(int k=0;k<6;++k) draw(circle(V[k],1),gray(0.7));
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(Wp[0]--Wp[1]--Wp[2]--Wp[3]--Wp[4]--Wp[5]--cycle,dashed);
draw(O--Wp[0],dotted);
for(int k=0;k<6;++k){dot(V[k]);dot(Wp[k],red);}
dot(O);label("$O$",O,W);label("$V_1$",V[0],E);label("$V_2$",V[1],NE);label("$O'$",Wp[0],NE);
\end{asy}

The distance from $O$ to a side is $\dfrac{\sqrt3}2$, so each new point is at distance $\sqrt3$ from $O$. The new points form a regular hexagon (rotated by $30^\circ$) with circumradius $\sqrt3$, compared with $1$ for the original. The area ratio is $(\sqrt3)^2=3$, answer $\boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ 3}$.

She bakes at most one pie each year. After a year with a pie she bakes one the next year with probability $\dfrac12$; after a year without, with probability $\dfrac56$. We find the probability of at most one pie in the next $5$ years (this year she baked).

No pies: $\dfrac12\left(\dfrac16\right)^4=\dfrac1{2592}$.

Exactly one pie, in year $j$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $j=1$: $\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac12\cdot\left(\dfrac16\right)^3=\dfrac3{2592}$;
\item $j=2,3,4$: one transition into the pie year ($\dfrac56$), one out of it ($\dfrac12$), the first year without a pie ($\dfrac12$) and two further no-pie years ($\dfrac16$ each), giving $\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac56\cdot\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac1{36}=\dfrac{15}{2592}$ each;
\item $j=5$: $\dfrac12\left(\dfrac16\right)^3\dfrac56=\dfrac5{2592}$.
\end{itemize}
In total $\dfrac{1+3+45+5}{2592}=\dfrac{54}{2592}=\dfrac1{48}$, so the desired probability is $\dfrac{47}{48}$ and $p+q=\boxed{95}$.

Place $A=(0,0)$, $B=(3,0)$, $C=(3,3)$, $D=(0,3)$, so $E=(0,2)$ and $F=(f,0)$. The right angle is at $E$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(3,0),C=(3,3),D=(0,3),E=(0,2),F=(2/3,0);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(C--E--F--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
pair u=unit(C-E),v=unit(F-E);draw(E+0.25u--E+0.25u+0.25v--E+0.25v);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$E$",E,W);label("$F$",F,S);
label("$2$",(0,1),W);label("$1$",(0,2.5),W);label("$3$",(1.83,0),S);
\end{asy}

So $\overrightarrow{EC}\cdot\overrightarrow{EF}=(3,1)\cdot(f,-2)=3f-2=0$ and $f=\dfrac23$. Then
\[CF^2=\left(3-\dfrac23\right)^2+3^2=\dfrac{49}9+9=\dfrac{130}9,\]
and $m+n=\boxed{139}$.

Put $B=(0,0)$ with the tangent line $PB$ as the $x$-axis, $P=(3,0)$ and $O=(0,r)$. Since $PA=AB$, $A$ lies on the perpendicular bisector $x=\dfrac32$ of $PB$, say $A=\left(\dfrac32,y\right)$ with $y>0$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=36/7;real y=sqrt(81/28);real r=s/(2*y);
pair B=(0,0),P=(3,0),O=(0,r),A=(1.5,y),M=(A+B)/2;
draw(circle(O,r));draw((-0.8,0)--(3.6,0));draw(P--A--B);draw(O--M,dashed);draw(O--B,dotted);
draw((1.5,0)--(1.5,y),dotted);
dot(O);dot(M);dot(A);dot(B);dot(P);
label("$O$",O,W);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$P$",P,S);label("$A$",A,NE);label("$M$",M,SE);label("$3$",(1.5,0),S);label("$1$",(O+M)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

$A$ on the circle gives $\dfrac94+(y-r)^2=r^2$, i.e. $AB^2=\dfrac94+y^2=2ry$. Let $s=AB^2$; then $r=\dfrac s{2y}$ and $y^2=s-\dfrac94$. Since $M$ is the midpoint of the chord $AB$, $OM\perp AB$ and
\[1=OM^2=r^2-\dfrac s4=\dfrac{s^2}{4y^2}-\dfrac s4=\dfrac{s^2}{4s-9}-\dfrac s4.\]
Multiplying by $4(4s-9)$: $4s^2-s(4s-9)=4(4s-9)$, so $9s=16s-36$ and $s=\dfrac{36}7$. (Then $y^2=\dfrac{81}{28}>0$, and $P$ is indeed outside the circle.) Hence $m+n=36+7=\boxed{43}$.

Two segments joining boundary points of a convex region cross exactly when their endpoints alternate around the boundary.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(4,4),D=(0,4);draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);
pair p1=(1.2,0),q1=(4,3.2),p2=(2.8,0),q2=(0,2.4);
draw(p1--q1,blue+linewidth(0.9));draw(p2--q2,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(p1,blue);dot(q1,blue);dot(p2,red);dot(q2,red);
label("$P_1$",p1,S);label("$Q_1$",q1,E);label("$P_2$",p2,S);label("$Q_2$",q2,W);
\end{asy}

Each person's pair of sides is uniform among the $6$ pairs, independently, and the order of points on a common side is equally likely either way. Compare Charles's pair with James's.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Same pair} (probability $\dfrac16$): on each of the two sides the order is random, and the endpoints alternate for exactly one of the two relative orders on the second side. Probability $\dfrac12$.
\item \textbf{Exactly one common side} (probability $\dfrac46$): on the common side the two points are in random order, and exactly one order makes them alternate. Probability $\dfrac12$.
\item \textbf{Disjoint pairs} (probability $\dfrac16$): if James's sides are opposite, Charles's are the other two opposite sides and the endpoints always alternate; if James's sides are adjacent, Charles's are the other two adjacent sides and they never alternate. James's sides are opposite with probability $\dfrac26=\dfrac13$.
\end{itemize}
In total
\[\dfrac16\cdot\dfrac12+\dfrac46\cdot\dfrac12+\dfrac16\cdot\dfrac13=\dfrac{3+12+2}{36}=\dfrac{17}{36},\]
so $m+n=\boxed{53}$.

\textbf{Step 1: $DE=AC$.} By inscribed angles, $\angle XDE=\angle CDE=\angle CAE=\angle CAX$ and $\angle XED=\angle AED=\angle ACD=\angle ACX$, so triangles $XDE$ and $XAC$ are similar. Equal areas force the ratio to be $1$, so $DE=AC$, and the arc $DE$ through $B$ measures $120^\circ$.

\textbf{Step 2: $\angle AXC=120^\circ$.} The angle between two chords meeting inside a circle is half the sum of the intercepted arcs: $\angle AXC=\dfrac{120^\circ+120^\circ}2=120^\circ$.

\textbf{Step 3: a similarity.} In triangle $AXC$, $\angle XAC+\angle XCA=60^\circ=\angle BAX+\angle XAC$, so $\angle YAX=\angle BAX=\angle XCA=\angle YCA$. Triangles $YAX$ and $YCA$ share the angle at $Y$, so they are similar and $\dfrac{YX}{YC}=\dfrac{YA^2}{YC^2}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),A=dir(90),B=dir(210),C=dir(330);pair Y=A+(B-A)/4;
pair[] I=intersectionpoints(C--(C+3*(Y-C)),unitcircle);pair D=(abs(I[0]-C)>0.01)?I[0]:I[1];
pair E=rotate(120)*D;pair X=extension(C,D,A,E);
fill(A--X--Y--cycle,rgb(1,0.85,0.85));fill(B--X--C--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.9,1));
draw(unitcircle,gray);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(C--D);draw(A--E);draw(D--E,dashed);draw(B--X,dotted);
dot(X);dot(Y);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,W);label("$E$",E,S);label("$X$",X,SE);label("$Y$",Y,W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 4: computing.} Let the side be $s$ and $AY=ts$. Then $[AYC]=52t$, and by the law of cosines with $\angle A=60^\circ$, $YC^2=s^2(t^2-t+1)$. Hence
\[1=[AXY]=[AYC]\cdot\dfrac{YX}{YC}=52t\cdot\dfrac{t^2}{t^2-t+1},\]
so $52t^3-t^2+t-1=0$, which factors as $(4t-1)(13t^2+3t+1)=0$. The quadratic has no real roots, so $t=\dfrac14$.

Then $\dfrac{YX}{YC}=\dfrac{t^2}{t^2-t+1}=\dfrac1{13}$. Also $[BYC]=(1-t)\cdot52=39$ and $[BYX]=\dfrac1{13}[BYC]=3$. Therefore $[BXC]=39-3=\boxed{36}$.

\textbf{Upper bound.} A line meets the annulus in at most two segments, and two lines cross at most once. Draw the lines one at a time; cutting a region along a segment that runs from boundary to boundary splits it into at most two pieces.

The first line adds at most $1$ piece. If it misses the hole it is a single segment. If it passes through the hole it gives two segments, each running from the outer circle to the inner one; the first of them does not disconnect the annulus (it only opens up the ring), and the second adds at most $1$.

The second line consists of at most two segments, cut by the first line into at most $3$ pieces, each adding at most $1$. So there are at most $1+1+3=5$ pieces.

\textbf{Construction.} Take outer radius $3$ and inner radius $1$, and two lines that both pass through the hole and cross at a point of the ring, such as $(0,2)$. The figure shows the $5$ pieces.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill(circle((0,0),3),rgb(0.93,0.88,0.75));fill(circle((0,0),1),white);
draw(circle((0,0),3));draw(circle((0,0),1));
pair X=(0,2);draw((0.6*1.55,-2*0.55)--(-0.6*0.55,2*1.55));draw((-0.6*1.55,-2*0.55)--(0.6*0.55,2*1.55));
label("$1$",(0,2.55),fontsize(9pt));label("$2$",(0,1.45),fontsize(9pt));label("$3$",(-2,0),fontsize(9pt));label("$4$",(2,0),fontsize(9pt));label("$5$",(0,-2),fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

The greatest number is $\boxed{5}$.

Put the start at $S=(0,0)$ with east and north positive. The path visits $(0,-20)$, $(40,-20)$, $(40,-40)$, $(20,-40)$ and returns to $S$. The last leg, from $(20,-40)$ to $(0,0)$, lies on the line $y=-2x$, which meets the second leg ($y=-20$, $0\le x\le40$) at $X=(10,-20)$. So the path crosses itself at $X$ and encloses two pieces (in the figure $1$ unit is $10$ m).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair S0=(0,0),P1=(0,-2),P2=(4,-2),P3=(4,-4),P4=(2,-4),X=(1,-2);
fill(S0--P1--X--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.93,0.85));fill(X--P2--P3--P4--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.93,0.85));
draw(S0--P1,MidArrow(4));draw(P1--P2,MidArrow(4));draw(P2--P3,MidArrow(4));draw(P3--P4,MidArrow(4));draw(P4--X,MidArrow(4));draw(X--S0);
dot(X);label("$S$",S0,N);label("$X$",X,NE);
label("$20$",(0,-1),W);label("$40$",(2.5,-2),N);label("$20$",(4,-3),E);label("$20$",(3,-4),S);
label("$100$",(0.27,-1.72),fontsize(7pt));label("$500$",(2.8,-3),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\begin{itemize}
\item The triangle $S,(0,-20),X$ has legs $20$ and $10$, area $100$.
\item The trapezoid $X,(40,-20),(40,-40),(20,-40)$ has parallel sides $30$ and $20$ and height $20$, area $\dfrac{30+20}2\cdot20=500$.
\end{itemize}
The park has area $\boxed{600}$ square meters.

question:
Jasmine writes down an integer $n>1$ exactly when all of the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $n<1000$;
\item[(b)] ignoring the factor $1$, $n$ has an even number of positive factors;
\item[(c)] ignoring the factor $1$, every positive factor of $n$ is even.
\end{enumerate}
How many numbers does she write down?

solution:
$OP=OQ=5$ (radii) and $PQ=5$, so triangle $OPQ$ is equilateral.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),P=5*dir(200),Q=5*dir(260);draw(circle(O,5));draw(O--P--Q--cycle);
dot(O);label("$O$",O,NE);label("$P$",P,W);label("$Q$",Q,S);label("$5$",(O+P)/2,N);label("$5$",(O+Q)/2,E);label("$5$",(P+Q)/2,NE);
