Hence $\angle PQO=\boxed{60^\circ}$.

question:
Sage has a circular hoop of radius $1$ metre. She bends a piece of wire into an equilateral triangle (a rigid flat frame) that she wants to pass completely through the hoop. What is the greatest possible height of the triangle
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] if the triangle must stay parallel to the plane of the hoop as it passes through;
\item[(b)] if the triangle may be tilted and turned in any way while it passes through?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) Moving parallel to the hoop, the whole triangle must fit inside the circle. The largest equilateral triangle in a circle of radius $1$ is inscribed in it, with circumradius $1$ and height $\dfrac32$. Answer: $\boxed{\dfrac32}$ m.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(unitcircle);pair A=dir(90),B=dir(210),C=dir(330);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(A--(0,-0.5),dashed);dot((0,0));
label("$1$",(0,0)--C,N,fontsize(8pt));draw((0,0)--C,dotted);label("$\dfrac32$",(0,0.25),E,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

(b) \textbf{Height $2$ is possible.} Hold the triangle perpendicular to the plane of the hoop, with its altitude $AD$ along a diameter of the hoop and the base $BC$ at one end of that diameter, and slide it through in the direction of $BC$. At each moment the part of the frame in the plane of the hoop lies on that diameter, between the base and the side, so its length is at most $AD=h$. If $h\le2$ it always fits.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(4/sqrt(3),0),A=(2/sqrt(3),2);draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
for(real x=0.2;x<1.1;x+=0.25){draw((x,0)--(x,x*sqrt(3)),gray+dashed);}
draw((A.x,0)--A,linewidth(1.2));label("$h$",(A.x,0.7),E);
draw((-0.4,0)--(-0.4,2),Arrows(4));label("$2$",(-0.4,1),W);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Height more than $2$ is impossible.} Let $\Pi$ be the plane of the hoop. While the triangle passes through, every point of the frame that lies in $\Pi$ must lie inside the hoop, so any two such points are at most $2$ apart. Look at the signed distances of the three vertices from $\Pi$: at the start all three are negative and at the end all are positive. The middle one of the three values changes continuously, so at some moment it is $0$. At that moment a vertex $V$ lies in $\Pi$, one other vertex $U$ is on or behind $\Pi$ and the third $W$ is on or beyond it.

If $U$ or $W$ also lies in $\Pi$, then a whole side, of length greater than $h$, lies in $\Pi$. Otherwise the side $UW$ crosses $\Pi$ at a point $Z$. Then $VZ$ is at least the distance from $V$ to the line $UW$, which is $h$. Either way two points of the frame in $\Pi$ are at distance at least $h$, so $h\le2$. (If the triangle lies in $\Pi$ at some moment, it must be inside the hoop, so $h\le\dfrac32$.)

Answer: $\boxed{2}$ m.

question:
$AM_0P_0$ is a right triangle with $AM_0=1$, $AP_0=2$ and the right angle at $A$. Let $M_1$ be the midpoint of $AP_0$, let $M_2$ be the point such that $A$ is the midpoint of $M_0M_2$, and let $M_3$ be the point such that $A$ is the midpoint of $M_1M_3$. Indices of $M$ are taken mod $4$. For every positive integer $n$, $P_n$ is the point on line $AM_{n+1}$ such that $P_{n-1}P_n\perp M_{n-1}P_{n-1}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the number of digits of the number of digits of $AP_{100}$.
\item[(b)] Find $\angle P_{99}P_{100}P_{101}$ to the nearest whole degree.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Put $A$ at the origin, $M_0=(1,0)$ and $P_0=(0,2)$. Then $M_1=(0,1)$, $M_2=(-1,0)$ and $M_3=(0,-1)$: the four points $M_k$ are the unit points on the axes, and $M_{k+2}=-M_k$.

$P_{n-1}$ lies on line $AM_n$, which is perpendicular to line $AM_{n-1}$, and $P_n$ lies on line $AM_{n+1}$, which is the same line as $AM_{n-1}$. So in triangle $M_{n-1}P_{n-1}P_n$ the angle at $P_{n-1}$ is right and $A$ is the foot of the altitude from $P_{n-1}$. Hence
\[AP_{n-1}^2=AM_{n-1}\cdot AP_n=AP_n.\]
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),M0=(1,0),M1=(0,1),M2=(-1,0),M3=(0,-1),P0=(0,2),P1=(-4,0);
draw((-4.6,0)--(1.6,0));draw((0,-1.4)--(0,2.6));
draw(M0--P0--P1,linewidth(0.9));
pair u=unit(M0-P0),v=unit(P1-P0);draw(P0+0.25u--P0+0.25u+0.25v--P0+0.25v);
dot(A);dot(M0);dot(M1);dot(M2);dot(M3);dot(P0);dot(P1);
label("$A$",A,SE);label("$M_0$",M0,S);label("$M_1$",M1,W);label("$M_2$",M2,S);label("$M_3$",M3,W);label("$P_0$",P0,N);label("$P_1$",P1,S);
label("$2$",(0,1.55),W,fontsize(8pt));label("$4$",(-2,0),S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

(For $n=1$: $P_1=(-4,0)$ and $AP_1=4=2^2$.) With $AP_0=2$ this gives $AP_n=2^{2^n}$; indeed $P_2=(0,-16)$ and $P_3=(256,0)$. Also $P_n$ lies on the ray $AM_{n+1}$.

(a) $AP_{100}=2^{2^{100}}$ has $D=\lfloor2^{100}\log_{10}2\rfloor+1$ digits. Since $2^{100}\approx1.2677\times10^{30}$ and $\log_{10}2\approx0.30103$, $2^{100}\log_{10}2\approx3.816\times10^{29}$. So $10^{29}<D<10^{30}$, and $D$ has $\boxed{30}$ digits.

(b) $P_{99}$ and $P_{101}$ lie on the rays $AM_0$ and $AM_2$, on opposite sides of $A$ along one line, and $P_{100}$ lies on the perpendicular ray $AM_1$. With $x=AP_{99}$ we have $AP_{100}=x^2$ and $AP_{101}=x^4$, so
\[\angle P_{99}P_{100}P_{101}=\arctan\dfrac{x}{x^2}+\arctan\dfrac{x^4}{x^2}=\arctan\dfrac1x+\left(90^\circ-\arctan\dfrac1{x^2}\right).\]
Since $x=2^{2^{99}}$ is astronomically large, the angle exceeds $90^\circ$ by a negligible amount: it is $\boxed{90^\circ}$ to the nearest degree.

All twelve vertices lie on a circle with centre $O$. A quadrilateral inscribed in a circle is a rectangle exactly when both of its diagonals are diameters. If it is a rectangle, each right angle is an inscribed angle standing on a diameter (Thales). Conversely, two diameters bisect each other and have equal length, so their endpoints form a rectangle.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V;for(int k=0;k<12;++k) V.push(dir(30*k));
draw(unitcircle,gray(0.7));
for(int k=0;k<12;++k) draw(V[k]--V[(k+1)%12]);
for(int k=0;k<6;++k) draw(V[k]--V[k+6],dotted);
draw(V[1]--V[4]--V[7]--V[10]--cycle,linewidth(1));
dot((0,0));label("$O$",(0,0),SE);
for(int k=0;k<12;++k) dot(V[k]);
\end{asy}

The twelve vertices form $6$ diametrically opposite pairs, so the number of rectangles is $\dbinom62=\boxed{15}$ (including the $3$ squares).

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that if the quadratic $ax^2+bx+c$ ($a\ne0$) has roots $r$ and $s$, then $r+s=-\dfrac ba$ and $rs=\dfrac ca$.
\item[(b)] State and prove the analogous formulas for the roots $r,s,t$ of $ax^3+bx^2+cx+d$ ($a\ne0$).
\item[(c)] Without solving, find $r^2+s^2+t^2$ for the roots of $x^3-2x^2-5x+6$, and check your answer by finding the roots.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Write the ellipse as $u(x-h)^2+v(y-k)^2=1$ with $u,v>0$.

\textbf{Centre.} The points $(-2,-12)$ and $\left(-2,-\dfrac{15}2\right)$ lie on the vertical line $x=-2$, so they are symmetric about the horizontal axis $y=k$: $k=\dfrac{-12-7.5}2=-\dfrac{39}4$. Then both $(-2,-12)$ and $\left(0,-\dfrac{15}2\right)$ have $|y-k|=\dfrac94$, so $(-2-h)^2=(0-h)^2$, giving $h=-1$.

\textbf{Axes.} Now $(-2,-12)$ gives $u+\dfrac{81}{16}v=1$, and $\left(-\dfrac23,-6\right)$ gives $\dfrac19u+\dfrac{225}{16}v=1$. Subtracting $\dfrac19$ of the first equation from the second: $\dfrac{216}{16}v=\dfrac89$, so $v=\dfrac{16}{243}$ and $u=1-\dfrac13=\dfrac23$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real h=-1,k=-39/4,a=sqrt(3/2),bb=sqrt(243/16);
draw(shift(h,k)*scale(a,bb)*unitcircle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((-3.2,0)--(1.5,0),Arrow(4));draw((0,-14.5)--(0,1),Arrow(4));label("$x$",(1.5,0),E);label("$y$",(0,1),N);
pair[] P={(-2,-12),(0,-7.5),(-2/3,-6),(-2,-7.5)};
for(pair p:P) dot(p);
label("$(-2,-12)$",P[0],W,fontsize(7pt));label("$\left(0,-\dfrac{15}{2}\right)$",P[1],E,fontsize(7pt));label("$\left(-\dfrac23,-6\right)$",P[2],NE,fontsize(7pt));label("$\left(-2,-\dfrac{15}{2}\right)$",P[3],W,fontsize(7pt));
dot((h,k));label("centre $\left(-1,-\dfrac{39}{4}\right)$",(h,k)+(0.7,-1.1),E,fontsize(7pt));
draw((-2,-12)--(-2,-7.5),dashed);draw((h,k-0.3)--(h,k+0.3));
\end{asy}

The ellipse is
\[\boxed{\dfrac{(x+1)^2}{\dfrac32}+\dfrac{\left(y+\dfrac{39}4\right)^2}{\dfrac{243}{16}}=1},\]
centred at $\left(-1,-\dfrac{39}4\right)$ with semi-axes $\sqrt{\dfrac32}$ (horizontal) and $\dfrac{9\sqrt3}4$ (vertical). (Without the condition that the axes are parallel to the coordinate axes, four points do not determine an ellipse.)

question:
A group of $10$ balloons is made using red, orange, yellow, blue and purple balloons (balloons of the same colour are identical). How many different groups are possible if
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] there is at least one balloon of each colour;
\item[(b)] there is at least one red, one orange and one yellow balloon;
\item[(c)] there are at least $1$ red, $2$ orange and $3$ yellow balloons;
\item[(d)] there is at least one balloon of each colour except purple, and no purple balloons?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
$(a,b)$ lies on $ax+by=169$ exactly when $a\cdot a+b\cdot b=169$. So the lucky points are exactly the points of the circle $x^2+y^2=169$ of radius $13$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),13),linewidth(0.9));draw((-15,0)--(15,0),gray);draw((0,-15)--(0,15),gray);
pair P=(12,5),Q=(-12,-5);dot(P);dot(Q);draw(P--Q,dashed);
draw((12,5)+8*unit((-5,12))--(12,5)+5*unit((5,-12)),dotted);
label("$(12,5)$",P,E,fontsize(8pt));label("$(-12,-5)$",Q,W,fontsize(8pt));label("$x^2+y^2=169$",13*dir(225),SW,fontsize(8pt));label("$12x+5y=169$",(12,5)+5*unit((5,-12)),SE,fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

The greatest distance between two points of a circle is its diameter, $26$, attained for example by $(12,5)$ and $(-12,-5)$. The answer is $26^2=\boxed{676}$. (The line $ax+by=169$ is the tangent to the circle at $(a,b)$.)

If $x^2=1$, the first equation gives $2x=0$, a contradiction; so $x\ne\pm1$, and similarly $y,z\ne\pm1$. Hence
\[y=\dfrac{2x}{1-x^2},\qquad z=\dfrac{2y}{1-y^2},\qquad x=\dfrac{2z}{1-z^2}.\]
Write $x=\tan a$ with $-\dfrac\pi2<a<\dfrac\pi2$ (possible for every real $x$). By the double-angle formula $\tan2\theta=\dfrac{2\tan\theta}{1-\tan^2\theta}$, we get $y=\tan2a$, $z=\tan4a$ and $x=\tan8a$. So $\tan8a=\tan a$, which means $8a=a+k\pi$, i.e. $a=\dfrac{k\pi}7$ with $k\in\{-3,\dots,3\}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(unitcircle,gray);draw((-1.3,0)--(1.3,0));draw((0,-1.3)--(0,1.3));draw((1,-1.3)--(1,2.4),dashed);
real a=180/7;
pair[] Q={dir(a),dir(2*a),dir(4*a)};
string[] L={"$a$","$2a$","$4a$"};
for(int i=0;i<3;++i){draw((0,0)--Q[i]);dot(Q[i]);label(L[i],0.72*Q[i]+0.12*dir(degrees(Q[i])+90),fontsize(8pt));}
draw((0,0)--(1,Tan(a)),dotted);dot((1,Tan(a)));label("$x=\tan a$",(1,Tan(a)),E,fontsize(8pt));
draw((0,0)--(1,Tan(2*a)),dotted);dot((1,Tan(2*a)));label("$y=\tan 2a$",(1,Tan(2*a)),E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$a=\dfrac{\pi}{7}$",(-1.2,1.2),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Conversely, for $a=\dfrac{k\pi}7$ none of $a,2a,4a$ is an odd multiple of $\dfrac\pi2$ (since $7$ is odd), so all three tangents exist, and the equations hold. The solutions are the seven triples
\[\boxed{(x,y,z)=\left(\tan\dfrac{k\pi}7,\ \tan\dfrac{2k\pi}7,\ \tan\dfrac{4k\pi}7\right),\quad k=0,1,\dots,6}\]
(where $k$ and $k-7$ give the same triple).

Let $x=\dfrac\theta{180^\circ}\in\left(0,\dfrac12\right)$, written in binary as $x=0.b_1b_2b_3\ldots$. Since $\tan$ has period $180^\circ$, $\tan(2^n\theta)$ depends on the fractional part of $2^nx$, which is $0.b_{n+1}b_{n+2}\ldots$; it is positive when this lies in $\left(0,\dfrac12\right)$, i.e. $b_{n+1}=0$, and negative when it lies in $\left(\dfrac12,1\right)$, i.e. $b_{n+1}=1$. (The values $0$ and $\dfrac12$ are excluded, since the tangent is never zero or undefined here.)

So $b_{n+1}=0$ exactly when $3\mid n$: the binary digits are $0,1,1,0,1,1,\dots$, and
\[x=0.\overline{011}_2=\dfrac{3}{7},\qquad\theta=\dfrac37\cdot180^\circ=\dfrac{540}7^\circ.\]
Check: $\theta\approx77.1^\circ$ (tan $>0$), $2\theta\approx154.3^\circ$ ($<0$), $4\theta\equiv128.6^\circ$ ($<0$), and $8\theta\equiv\theta\pmod{180^\circ}$, so the pattern repeats.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(unitcircle,gray);draw((-1.3,0)--(1.3,0));draw((0,-1.3)--(0,1.3));
fill((0,0)--arc((0,0),1,0,90)--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.95,0.85));fill((0,0)--arc((0,0),1,180,270)--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.95,0.85));
real t=540/7;
pair[] Q={dir(t),dir(2*t),dir(4*t)};
string[] L={"$\theta$","$2\theta$","$4\theta$"};
for(int i=0;i<3;++i){draw((0,0)--Q[i],linewidth(0.9));dot(Q[i]);label(L[i],1.18*Q[i]);}
label("$\tan>0$",(0.55,0.4),fontsize(8pt));label("$\tan>0$",(-0.55,-0.4),fontsize(8pt));label("$8\theta\equiv\theta$",(-1.05,1.1),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Thus $p+q=540+7=\boxed{547}$.

All $\boxed{20}$ amounts can be measured.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Multiples of $3$:} pour full $3$-cup measures into the $20$-cup container: $3,6,\dots,18$; and $9$ directly.
\item \textbf{Amounts $\equiv2\pmod3$:} fill the $20$-cup container and remove $3$ cups at a time by pouring into the empty $3$-cup container: $20,17,14,11,8,5,2$.
\item \textbf{Amounts $\equiv1\pmod3$:} measure $2$ cups (as above) and pour them into the empty $3$-cup container. Refill the $20$-cup container and pour into the $3$-cup container until it is full; this removes exactly $1$ cup, leaving $19$. Removing $3$ cups at a time then gives $16,13,10,7,4,1$.
\end{itemize}

question:
On day $1$ Bob eats $1$ pizza, on day $2$ he eats $\dfrac12$ of a pizza, on day $3$ he eats $\dfrac13$ of a pizza, and in general on day $n$ he eats $\dfrac1n$ of a pizza.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] After how many days has he eaten a total of at least $2$ pizzas? At least $3$? $4$? $5$?
\item[(b)] Using $H_n=1+\dfrac12+\dots+\dfrac1n\approx\ln n+\gamma$, where $\gamma\approx0.5772$, estimate how many days he needs to eat $100$ pizzas.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Since $11^2+13^2=290<400=20^2$, the angle $B$ is obtuse, and $\cos B=\dfrac{121+169-400}{2\cdot11\cdot13}=-\dfrac5{13}$.

\textbf{Position of $D$ and $E$.} A point $X$ lies inside the circle with diameter $AC$ exactly when $\angle AXC>90^\circ$; so $B$ is inside the circle. Each line through $B$ therefore meets the circle at two points on opposite sides of $B$. One of them is $A$ (respectively $C$), so $D$ lies on ray $AB$ beyond $B$, and $E$ lies on ray $CB$ beyond $B$.

\textbf{Computing $DE$.} By Thales, $\angle ADC=\angle AEC=90^\circ$, so $D$ and $E$ are the feet of the perpendiculars from $C$ to line $AB$ and from $A$ to line $BC$. In the right triangles $BDC$ and $BEA$, the angle at $B$ is $180^\circ-B$, so $BD=13|\cos B|=5$ and $BE=11|\cos B|=\dfrac{55}{13}$. Thus $\dfrac{BD}{BC}=\dfrac{BE}{BA}=|\cos B|$, and triangles $BDE$ and $BCA$ are similar (the angles $\angle DBE$ and $\angle CBA$ are vertical angles). Hence
\[DE=AC\cdot|\cos B|=20\cdot\dfrac5{13}=\boxed{\dfrac{100}{13}}.\]
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),C=(20,0);real c=(20^2+11^2-13^2)/(2*20);pair B=(c,sqrt(11^2-c^2));pair O=(10,0);
pair D=A+(dot(C-A,B-A)/dot(B-A,B-A))*(B-A);pair Ee=C+(dot(A-C,B-C)/dot(B-C,B-C))*(B-C);
draw(circle(O,10),gray);draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(B--D,dashed);draw(B--Ee,dashed);draw(C--D,dotted);draw(A--Ee,dotted);draw(D--Ee,linewidth(1));
dot(O);label("$A$",A,W);label("$C$",C,E);label("$B$",B,S);label("$D$",D,N);label("$E$",Ee,N);label("$O$",O,N);
label("$11$",(A+B)/2,NW,fontsize(8pt));label("$13$",(B+C)/2,NE,fontsize(8pt));label("$20$",(5,0),S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Yes.} Take the vertices of a regular pentagon. Each of the $5$ sides has the same length $s$, and each of the $5$ diagonals has the same length $d=\dfrac{1+\sqrt5}2\,s$, so only two distances occur.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
