Each hexagon has area $\dfrac{3\sqrt3}2\cdot2^2=6\sqrt3$ and apothem (inradius) $\sqrt3$. Their intersection is a regular dodecagon. Its sides lie on the twelve side-lines of the two hexagons, all at distance $\sqrt3$ from the centre, so the dodecagon has inradius $\sqrt3$. A regular $12$-gon with inradius $r$ has area $12r^2\tan15^\circ=12\cdot3(2-\sqrt3)=72-36\sqrt3$.
\begin{asy}
