By Legendre's formula $v_2(72!)=36+18+9+4+2+1=70$. The condition says $v_2\!\left(\dfrac{72!}n\right)=70-v_2(n)=n$, i.e. $n+v_2(n)=70$. If $v_2(n)=k$ then $n=70-k$ must have $v_2(70-k)=k$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $k=0$: $n=70$, but $v_2(70)=1$;
\item $k=2$: $n=68$, and $v_2(68)=2$ works;
\item $k=6$: $n=64$, and $v_2(64)=6$ works;
\item every other $k\le6$ fails, and $k\ge7$ would need $n\ge128$.
\end{itemize}
Both $68$ and $64$ divide $72!$. The sum is $68+64=132$. Answer $\boxed{\textbf{(E)}\ 132}$.

Since $PA=PC$, $P$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AC$, which passes through $B$ and the midpoint $M$ of $AC$. Let $h=BM=AM=MC$; then $X=h^2$.

\textbf{Case 1: $P$ on ray $BM$.} Then $PM=|h-2|$ and $h^2+(h-2)^2=9$, so $2h^2-4h-5=0$, $h=1+\dfrac{\sqrt{14}}2$ and $X=h^2=2h+\dfrac52=\dfrac92+\sqrt{14}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=1+sqrt(14)/2;real s=h*sqrt(2);pair B=(0,0),A=(0,s),C=(s,0),M=(A+C)/2,P=2*dir(45);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(B--M,dotted);draw(A--P--C,dashed);draw(B--P,linewidth(0.9));dot(P);
draw((0,0)--(0.2,0)--(0.2,0.2)--(0,0.2));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,E);label("$P$",P,E);label("$M$",M,NE);label("$2$",B--P,NW,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Case 2: $P$ on the opposite ray.} Then $PM=h+2$ and $h^2+(h+2)^2=9$, so $2h^2+4h-5=0$, $h=-1+\dfrac{\sqrt{14}}2>0$ and $X=\dfrac52-2h=\dfrac92-\sqrt{14}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=-1+sqrt(14)/2;real s=h*sqrt(2);pair B=(0,0),A=(0,s),C=(s,0),M=(A+C)/2,P=-2*dir(45);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(M--P,dotted);draw(A--P--C,dashed);draw(B--P,linewidth(0.9));dot(P);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,dir(-60),fontsize(8pt));label("$C$",C,E);label("$P$",P,SW);label("$2$",B--P,NW,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The sum is $9$. Answer $\boxed{\textbf{(E)}\ 9}$.

Adding all five equations gives $15(v+w+x+y+z)=15$, so $v+w+x+y+z=1$.

Subtracting each equation from the one before it:
\begin{itemize}
\item first minus second: $v+w+x+y-4z=1$, i.e. $(v+w+x+y+z)-5z=1$, so $z=0$;
\item second minus third: $v+w+x-4y+z=1$, so $y=0$;
\item third minus fourth: $v+w-4x+y+z=1$, so $x=0$;
\item fourth minus fifth: $v-4w+x+y+z=1$, so $w=0$.
\end{itemize}
Then $v=1$. The unique solution is $\boxed{(v,w,x,y,z)=(1,0,0,0,0)}$, which indeed satisfies all five equations.

