Sort the numbers by their remainder mod $42$. Since $547=13\cdot42+1$, each remainder class has $13$ members except remainder $1$, which has $14$ (the extra number is $547$).

Two numbers have a sum divisible by $42$ exactly when their remainders $r,s$ satisfy $r+s\equiv0\pmod{42}$. Hence:
\begin{itemize}
\item at most one number with remainder $0$, and at most one with remainder $21$;
\item for each pair $\{r,42-r\}$ with $1\le r\le20$, numbers may be taken from only one of the two classes, so at most $\max(|r|,|42-r|)$ from the pair.
\end{itemize}
This gives at most $1+1+14+19\cdot13=263$. The bound is attained by taking all numbers with remainders $1,2,\dots,20$ together with $21$ and $42$: no two remainders among $1,\dots,20$ add to $42$, and $21$ and $42$ add to $0$ or $21$ with nothing chosen. The answer is $\boxed{263}$.

By the angle bisector theorem, $\dfrac{CE}{EB}=\dfrac{AC}{AB}$ and $\dfrac{BD}{DA}=\dfrac{BC}{AC}$. Hence
\[\dfrac{CE}{EB}\cdot\dfrac{BD}{DA}=\dfrac{BC}{AB}=\dfrac{16}{14}=\boxed{\dfrac87}.\]
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(14,0);real a=16,c=14,bb=15;real cx=(bb^2+c^2-a^2)/(2*c);pair C=(cx,sqrt(bb^2-cx^2));
pair D=(a*A+bb*B)/(a+bb),Ept=(c*C+bb*B)/(c+bb),F=(c*C+a*A)/(c+a);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);draw(C--D,dashed);draw(A--Ept,dashed);draw(B--F,dashed);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,S);label("$E$",Ept,NE);label("$F$",F,NW);
label("$14$",A+0.25*(B-A),S,fontsize(8pt));label("$16$",B+0.3*(C-B),E,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

(The length $CF=8$ is not needed. It is consistent: $\dfrac{AF}{FC}=\dfrac{AB}{BC}=\dfrac78$ gives $AF=7$ and $AC=15$, and Ceva's theorem gives the same product, $\dfrac{FC}{AF}=\dfrac87$.)

