question:
A sphere of radius $r$ rests on a flat floor and is lit by a point light source $L$ outside the sphere, at height $h$ above the floor. Someone claims that, apart from degenerate cases, the boundary of the sphere's shadow on the floor is always a parabola. Prove or disprove. Specifically, show that the boundary is
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] an ellipse if $h>2r$ (a circle if $L$ is directly above the centre);
\item[(b)] a parabola if $h=2r$;
\item[(c)] (part of) one branch of a hyperbola if $h<2r$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The claim is false: the typical shadow, cast by a lamp higher than the sphere, is an ellipse.

\textbf{The boundary is a conic.} The rays from $L$ that just touch the sphere form a right circular cone $K$ with apex $L$ and axis $LO$, where $O$ is the centre. The shadow boundary is the intersection of $K$ with the floor, so it is a conic section. Its type depends on the horizontal plane $\Pi$ through $L$, which is parallel to the floor. The section is an ellipse if $\Pi$ meets $K$ only at $L$, a parabola if $\Pi$ contains exactly one generator (is tangent to $K$), and a hyperbola if $\Pi$ contains two generators. A generator lies in $\Pi$ exactly when it is a horizontal tangent line from $L$, touching the sphere at a point of height $h$.

(a) If $h>2r$, no point of the sphere has height $h$, so $\Pi$ meets $K$ only at $L$: every tangent ray goes downwards and hits the floor, and the boundary is a closed curve, an ellipse. If $L$ is directly above $O$, the configuration is symmetric about the vertical axis, and the shadow is a disc bounded by a circle.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1;pair O=(0,1),L=(-1.6,3.6);
real d=abs(L-O);real phi=degrees(L-O);real be=aCos(r/d);
pair T1=O+r*dir(phi+be),T2=O+r*dir(phi-be);
pair G1=L+(L.y/(L.y-T1.y))*(T1-L),G2=L+(L.y/(L.y-T2.y))*(T2-L);
draw((-3,0)--(4,0));draw(circle(O,r));draw(L--G1,dashed);draw(L--G2,dashed);draw(G1--G2,linewidth(2));
dot(L);dot(O);label("$L$",L,N);label("$O$",O,S);draw((L.x,0)--L,dotted);label("$h>2r$",(L.x,L.y/2),W,fontsize(8pt));label("floor",(4,0),E,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

(b) If $h=2r$, $\Pi$ is the tangent plane at the top point $T$ of the sphere. The line $LT$ is the only tangent line from $L$ lying in $\Pi$, so $\Pi$ is tangent to $K$ along $LT$, and the boundary is a parabola. The shadow is unbounded in the direction of $LT$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1;pair O=(0,1),L=(-2.4,2),T=(0,2);
real d=abs(L-O);real phi=degrees(L-O);real be=aCos(r/d);
pair T2=O+r*dir(phi+be);pair G2=L+(L.y/(L.y-T2.y))*(T2-L);
draw((-3,0)--(4.2,0));draw(circle(O,r));draw(L--(4,2),dashed);draw(L--G2,dashed);draw(G2--(4.2,0),linewidth(2),Arrow(5));
dot(L);dot(T);label("$L$",L,N);label("$T$",T,N);draw((L.x,0)--L,dotted);label("$h=2r$",(L.x,1),W,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

(c) If $h<2r$, $\Pi$ cuts the sphere in a circle with $L$ outside it (as $L$ is outside the sphere), so there are two tangent lines from $L$ within $\Pi$. They are two generators of $K$ in $\Pi$, and the floor cuts $K$ in a hyperbola. The shadow boundary is the part of one branch formed by the downward rays.

So a parabola occurs only in the special case $h=2r$. $\blacksquare$

A $36^\circ$ arc is $\dfrac{36}{360}=\dfrac1{10}$ of the circle, so the circumference is $50$ and $r=\dfrac{50}{2\pi}=\dfrac{25}\pi$.
