\end{asy}

In triangle $ABP$, the exterior angle at $B$ is $\angle PBF=2\theta=\angle PAB+\angle APB=\theta+\angle APB$, so $\angle APB=\theta=\angle PAB$ and $AB=BP$.

In triangle $BPC$, $\angle PBC=2\theta$ and $\angle PCB=180^\circ-3\theta$, so $\angle BPC=\theta$. By the law of sines,
\[\dfrac{BC}{\sin\theta}=\dfrac{BP}{\sin(180^\circ-3\theta)}=\dfrac{BP}{\sin3\theta}.\]
Hence, using $\sin3\theta=3\sin\theta-4\sin^3\theta$,
\[\dfrac{AB}{BC}=\dfrac{BP}{BC}=\dfrac{\sin3\theta}{\sin\theta}=3-4\sin^2\theta.\]
Since $0<\theta<30^\circ$, $\sin^2\theta<\dfrac14$, so $\dfrac{AB}{BC}>3-1=2$, i.e. $AB>2BC$. $\blacksquare$

On the line, $y=-\dfrac x2$. Substituting, the $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$ are the roots $x_1,x_2$ of
\[x^2+\dfrac x2-a=0,\]
so $x_1+x_2=-\dfrac12$ and $x_1x_2=-a$. Then $(x_1-x_2)^2=\dfrac14+4a$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
