label("$A$",A,SW,fontsize(8pt));label("$B$",B,S,fontsize(8pt));label("$y=x^2-a$",(0.75,f(0.75)),E,fontsize(7pt));label("$x+2y=0$",(0.8,-0.4),E,fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

Since both points are on a line of slope $-\dfrac12$,
\[AB^2=(x_1-x_2)^2\left(1+\dfrac14\right)=\dfrac54\left(\dfrac14+4a\right)=1.\]
So $\dfrac14+4a=\dfrac45$ and $a=\boxed{\dfrac{11}{80}}$.

Let the rectangle be $A(0,0)$, $B(4,0)$, $C(4,3)$, $D(0,3)$, with the circle through $A$ and $C$. Its centre can be any point of the perpendicular bisector $\ell$ of $AC$, the line through $O\left(2,\dfrac32\right)$ perpendicular to $AC$:
\[\ell:\ 4x+3y=\dfrac{25}2.\]
The distance from the centre to $A$ or $C$ is the radius, which is at least $\dfrac{AC}2=\dfrac52$. The distance to $B$ is at least the distance from $B$ to $\ell$:
\[\dfrac{\left|16-\dfrac{25}2\right|}{5}=\dfrac{7}{10},\]
attained when the centre is the foot $X$ of the perpendicular from $B$ to $\ell$. By symmetry the same holds for $D$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
