pair u=(-3,4)/5; pair Fb=O+dot(B-O,u)*u;
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(A--C,gray);
draw((O-2.7*u)--(O+2.1*u),dashed);
draw(B--Fb);dot(Fb);dot(O);
draw(circle(Fb,abs(A-Fb)),gray+dotted);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$O$",O,N,fontsize(8pt));label("$X$",Fb,W);label("$\ell$",O+2.1*u,N,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

So the least possible distance is $\boxed{\dfrac{7}{10}}$ inch.

We use coordinates (no trigonometry). Put $B=(0,0)$ and $C=(17,0)$, and let $A=(p,q)$ with $q>0$. From $p^2+q^2=400$ and $(p-17)^2+q^2=144$ we get $34p=545$, so
\[p=\dfrac{545}{34},\qquad q=\dfrac{105\sqrt{15}}{34},\qquad [ABC]=\dfrac12\cdot17\cdot q=\dfrac{105\sqrt{15}}{4}\approx101.67.\]
The circumcircle passes through $B$ and $C$, so it has an equation $x^2+y^2-17x+Ey=0$. Substituting $A$ gives $400-17p+Eq=0$, so
\[E=\dfrac{17p-400}{q}=-\dfrac{289}{7\sqrt{15}}.\]
Since $\angle A<90^\circ$ (because $12^2+20^2>17^2$), the minor arc $BC$ is the arc on the other side of $BC$ from $A$, below the $x$-axis. Triangle $BKP$ is a right isosceles triangle, so $P=(t,-t)$ with $t=BK>0$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(17,0),A=(545/34,105*sqrt(15)/34);
pair O=(8.5,289/(14*sqrt(15)));real R=abs(O-B);
real t=(17-289/(7*sqrt(15)))/2;pair P=(t,-t),K=(t,0);
draw(circle(O,R),gray);draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(B--P--K,linewidth(0.8));draw(P--C,dashed);
draw(K+(-0.6,0)--K+(-0.6,-0.6)--K+(0,-0.6));
dot(P);dot(K);
label("$A$",A,NE);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,E);label("$P$",P,SW);label("$K$",K,N);
label("$45^\circ$",B+(1.3,-0.45),fontsize(6pt));
\end{asy}

Substituting into the circle,
\[2t^2-17t-Et=0,\qquad t=\dfrac{17+E}{2}=\dfrac12\left(17-\dfrac{289}{7\sqrt{15}}\right)\approx3.170.\]
So $[BPK]=\dfrac{t^2}{2}\approx5.024$, and the ratio is
\[\dfrac{[BPK]}{[ABC]}\approx\dfrac{5.024}{101.67}\approx\boxed{0.049}.\]

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] One integer is chosen at random from the integers greater than $12$ and less than $21$. What is the probability that it is a multiple of $4$ or $5$?
\item[(b)] If instead two different integers are chosen at random from the same set, what is the probability that at least one of them is a multiple of $4$ or $5$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Put $A=(-5,0)$, $C=(5,0)$, $B=(0,12)$, with midpoint $M=(0,0)$ of $AC$. A clockwise quarter-turn sends a vector $(x,y)$ to $(y,-x)$, so it sends $A-M$, $C-M$, $B-M$ to $(0,5)$, $(0,-5)$, $(12,0)$. With dilation factor $k$ and new midpoint $(x_0,y_0)$,
\[A'=(x_0,\,y_0+5k),\qquad C'=(x_0,\,y_0-5k),\qquad B'=(x_0+12k,\,y_0).\]
$C'$ on $AC$ gives $y_0=5k$, so $A'=(x_0,10k)$ and $B'=(x_0+12k,5k)$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $A'$ on line $AB$ (equation $y=\dfrac{12}{5}(x+5)$): $x_0=\dfrac{25k}{6}-5$.
\item $B'$ on line $BC$ (equation $x=\dfrac{5}{12}(12-y)$): $x_0+12k=5-\dfrac{25k}{12}$.
\end{itemize}
Hence $\dfrac{25k}{6}-5+12k=5-\dfrac{25k}{12}$, so $219k=120$ and $k=\dfrac{40}{73}$. All three points then lie on the sides themselves (not just the lines), and

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real k=40/73,x0=25k/6-5;
pair A=(-5,0),B=(0,12),C=(5,0);
