The four corner triangles are congruent right triangles with legs $13$ and $14$, so the four segments have length $\sqrt{365}$. At each mark, the two acute angles of the adjacent corner triangles add up to $90^\circ$, so the angle of the inner quadrilateral there is $180^\circ-90^\circ=90^\circ$. Hence the inner figure is a square, and its area is
\[27^2-4\cdot\dfrac12\cdot13\cdot14=729-364=365\text{ cm}^2.\]

question:
Interpret each infinite expression as the limit of its finite truncations (replace the innermost ``$\ldots$'' by a starting value and let the depth grow). Decide whether it has a real value, and find the value if it does.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $2+\sqrt{3-2\left(2+\sqrt{3-2\left(2+\sqrt{3-2(\ldots)}\right)}\right)}$
\item[(b)] $2-\sqrt{3-2\left(2-\sqrt{3-2\left(2-\sqrt{3-2(\ldots)}\right)}\right)}$
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The truncations satisfy $x_{k+1}=2+\sqrt{3-2x_k}$. Any value $x_{k+1}$ that is defined is at least $2$, and then $3-2x_{k+1}\le-1<0$, so $x_{k+2}$ is not a real number. (For example, starting from $0$: $x_1=2+\sqrt3$, and $3-2x_1<0$.) Likewise a limit $x$ would have to satisfy $x=2+\sqrt{3-2x}$, which needs both $x\ge2$ and $x\le\dfrac32$. So (a) has \textbf{no real value}.

\textbf{(b)} Now $y_{k+1}=2-\sqrt{3-2y_k}$. A limit $y$ must satisfy $y=2-\sqrt{3-2y}$, so $(2-y)^2=3-2y$ with $y\le2$, i.e. $(y-1)^2=0$ and $y=1$; indeed $2-\sqrt{1}=1$.

The truncations do converge to $1$ for every starting value $y_0<1$:
\begin{itemize}
\item if $y_k<1$, then $3-2y_k>1$, so $y_{k+1}<1$;
\item $y_{k+1}>y_k$, because $2-y_k>\sqrt{3-2y_k}$ is equivalent to $(y_k-1)^2>0$.
\end{itemize}
So the sequence increases and is bounded by $1$; its limit is a solution of $y=2-\sqrt{3-2y}$, i.e. $1$. The value of (b) is $\boxed{1}$. (Convergence is slow, since the fixed point is neutral; a starting value above $1$ moves away from $1$ until the square root is undefined.)

