question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why $\sqrt[b]a=a^{\dfrac1b}$ for $a>0$ and a positive integer $b$.
\item[(b)] Is $\sqrt[1]a$ the same as $\sqrt a$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
We need $x+y\ge0$ and $|x-y|-2|x|=\pm(x+y)$, i.e.
\[\text{(i) } |x-y|=2|x|+x+y\qquad\text{or}\qquad\text{(ii) } |x-y|=2|x|-x-y.\]
\emph{Case $x\ge0$.}
\begin{itemize}
\item (ii) reads $|x-y|=x-y$, i.e. $y\le x$. With $x+y\ge0$ this gives the whole region $-x\le y\le x$.
\item (i) reads $|x-y|=3x+y$: for $y\le x$ it gives $y=-x$ (already in the region); for $y>x$ it gives $x=0$, the ray $x=0,\ y>0$.
\end{itemize}
\emph{Case $x<0$.}
\begin{itemize}
\item (i) reads $|x-y|=y-x$, i.e. $y\ge x$. With $x+y\ge0$ this gives the whole region $y\ge-x$.
\item (ii) reads $|x-y|=-3x-y$, which gives only points with $y=-x$ (already included).
\end{itemize}
So the graph is the union of two closed right-angled wedges with vertex at the origin:
\[\{(x,y):x\ge0,\ -x\le y\le x\}\ \cup\ \{(x,y):x\le0,\ y\ge-x\}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real L=3;
fill((0,0)--(L,-L)--(L,L)--cycle,lightgray);
fill((0,0)--(0,L)--(-L,L)--cycle,lightgray);
draw((0,0)--(L,-L),linewidth(1));draw((0,0)--(L,L),linewidth(1));
draw((0,0)--(0,L),linewidth(1));draw((0,0)--(-L,L),linewidth(1));
draw((-L,0)--(L+0.3,0),gray,Arrow(4));draw((0,-L)--(0,L+0.3),gray,Arrow(4));
label("$x$",(L+0.3,0),E);label("$y$",(0,L+0.3),N);
label("$y=x$",(L,L),E,fontsize(8pt));label("$y=-x$",(L,-L),E,fontsize(8pt));label("$y=-x$",(-L,L),W,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The first wedge lies between the rays $y=-x$ and $y=x$ in the right half-plane; the second lies between the positive $y$-axis and the ray $y=-x$ in the second quadrant.

MISSISSIPPI has one M, four I's, four S's and two P's. A choice is determined by how many of each letter are taken, i.e. by the coefficient of $t^4$ in
\[(1+t)(1+t+t^2+t^3+t^4)^2(1+t+t^2).\]
Equivalently, count multisets by the number of different letters used:
\begin{itemize}
\item one kind: IIII or SSSS: $2$;
\item two kinds, split $3+1$: I or S triple with one of the other three letters: $2\cdot3=6$; split $2+2$: two kinds from $\{\text{I},\text{S},\text{P}\}$: $3$;
\item three kinds, split $2+1+1$: the doubled letter is I, S or P and the other two are chosen from the remaining three letters: $3\cdot3=9$;
\item four kinds: MISP: $1$.
\end{itemize}
The total is $2+6+3+9+1=\boxed{21}$. (If the $11$ letters were treated as distinguishable the answer would be $\binom{11}{4}=330$; ordered $4$-letter strings number $176$.)

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Describe the graph of $\dfrac{|x+y|+|x-y|}{2}=s$, where $s>0$.
\item[(b)] Give a single equation whose graph is the regular $n$-gon with centre at the origin, a vertex direction of your choice, and inradius (apothem) $r$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} For real $u,v$, $\dfrac{|u+v|+|u-v|}{2}=\max(|u|,|v|)$ (check the cases $|u|\ge|v|$ and $|u|<|v|$). So the equation says $\max(|x|,|y|)=s$: the graph is the square with vertices $(\pm s,\pm s)$, whose side length is $2s$, not $s$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=1;
draw((-1.6,0)--(1.6,0),gray,Arrow(4));draw((0,-1.6)--(0,1.6),gray,Arrow(4));
draw((s,s)--(-s,s)--(-s,-s)--(s,-s)--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((s,s)--(-s,-s),dashed+gray);draw((-s,s)--(s,-s),dashed+gray);
label("$(s,s)$",(s,s),NE,fontsize(8pt));label("$(s,-s)$",(s,-s),SE,fontsize(8pt));
label("$y=x$",(-1.25,-1.25),SW,fontsize(7pt));label("$y=-x$",(-1.25,1.25),NW,fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} Let $\theta_k=\dfrac{2\pi k}{n}$ for $k=0,1,\dots,n-1$ be the directions of the perpendiculars from the centre to the sides. The point $(x,y)$ lies on the side with outward normal $(\cos\theta_k,\sin\theta_k)$ exactly when $x\cos\theta_k+y\sin\theta_k=r$, and inside the polygon all these expressions are at most $r$. Hence the polygon is
\[\max_{0\le k\le n-1}\left(x\cos\theta_k+y\sin\theta_k\right)=r.\]
For $n=4$ and $\theta_k=\dfrac{\pi k}{2}$ this is $\max(|x|,|y|)=r$, as in (a).

Place $A=(0,0)$, $B=(20,0)$, $C=(0,15)$, so $D=(18,0)$. Points of ray $CA$ have the form $E=(0,e)$ with $e\le15$, and points of ray $CB$ the form $F=(20u,15-15u)$ with $u\ge0$. ``$CD$ is a median'' means $D$ is the midpoint of $EF$:
\[\dfrac{20u}{2}=18,\qquad\dfrac{e+15-15u}{2}=0.\]
So $u=\dfrac95$, $F=(36,-12)$, and $e=12$, $E=(0,12)$.

