\begin{itemize}
\item If $x+1=p$ is prime, Wilson's theorem gives $(p-1)!\equiv-1\pmod p$, so $p\mid x!+1$.
\item If $x+1$ is composite and $x+1>4$, then $x+1\mid x!$ (write $x+1=ab$ with $1<a\le b<x+1$; if $a<b$ both are factors of $x!$, and if $a=b$ then $a$ and $2a$ are distinct factors of $x!$ because $2a<a^2=x+1$). So $x!+1\equiv1\pmod{x+1}$.
\item $x+1=4$: $3!+1=7$ is not divisible by $4$.
\end{itemize}
So $x+1$ must be a prime $p\le50$, and the sum is $\sum(p-1)$ over the $15$ primes $p\le47$. These primes add up to $328$, so the answer is $328-15=\boxed{313}$.

