question:
In triangle $ABC$, $AB=AC$, $\angle BAC=90^\circ$ and $BC=14$; $AD\perp BC$ at $D$. Point $E$ moves on side $AC$. On segment $DE$ as hypotenuse, an isosceles right triangle $DEF$ is constructed on the side of $DE$ that contains $A$, and $AF$ is drawn.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $AF=EF$ for every position of $E$ (whether $F$ lies on $AD$ or not).
\item[(b)] Show that $AF\ge\dfrac72$. In particular, $AF=\sqrt2$ never happens.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Since $\angle DFE=90^\circ$ and $FD=FE$, the circle with centre $F$ through $D$ and $E$ sees $DE$ under a central angle of $90^\circ$. So every point of the arc on the same side of $DE$ as $F$ sees $DE$ under the inscribed angle $45^\circ$.

Now $AD$ bisects the right angle at $A$, so $\angle DAE=\angle DAC=45^\circ$, and $A$ is on the same side of $DE$ as $F$. Since the points on that side seeing $DE$ under $45^\circ$ form exactly that arc, $A$ lies on the circle, and $FA=FD=FE$.

