\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} Triangles $ABC$ and $A'B'C'$ are congruent by SAS ($AB=A'B'$, $\angle B=\angle B'$, $BC=B'C'$), so $AC=A'C'$ and $\angle ACB=\angle A'C'B'$. Since the quadrilaterals are convex, $\angle ACD=\angle C-\angle ACB=\angle C'-\angle A'C'B'=\angle A'C'D'$. Then triangles $ACD$ and $A'C'D'$ are congruent by SAS ($AC=A'C'$, $\angle ACD=\angle A'C'D'$, $CD=C'D'$). Placing the two pairs of congruent triangles together shows the quadrilaterals are congruent. $\blacksquare$

