Each of the $\dbinom{16}{2}=120$ pairs of points lies on one line. Lines containing more points:
\begin{itemize}
\item $4$ points: $4$ rows, $4$ columns and $2$ main diagonals, $10$ lines, each containing $\dbinom42=6$ pairs;
\item $3$ points: the $4$ diagonals parallel to the main diagonals next to them, each containing $\dbinom32=3$ pairs.
\end{itemize}
These account for $60+12=72$ pairs. Every other pair lies on a line with exactly $2$ points (in a $4\times4$ grid, a line through three points must have slope $0$, $\infty$ or $\pm1$), so there are $120-72=\boxed{48}$ such lines.

Yes. Let $\omega=30^\circ$. Then $\angle PAB=A-\omega$, $\angle PBC=B-\omega$ and $\angle PCA=C-\omega$, all positive. By the trigonometric form of Ceva's theorem for the cevians through $P$,
\[\dfrac{\sin\angle PAB}{\sin\angle PAC}\cdot\dfrac{\sin\angle PBC}{\sin\angle PBA}\cdot\dfrac{\sin\angle PCA}{\sin\angle PCB}=1,\quad\text{so}\quad\sin(A-\omega)\sin(B-\omega)\sin(C-\omega)=\sin^3\omega.\]
The three angles $A-\omega$, $B-\omega$, $C-\omega$ are positive with sum $180^\circ-3\omega=90^\circ$. Since $\ln\sin$ is strictly concave on $(0^\circ,180^\circ)$, Jensen's inequality gives
\[\sin(A-\omega)\sin(B-\omega)\sin(C-\omega)\le\sin^3 30^\circ=\sin^3\omega,\]
with equality only when $A-\omega=B-\omega=C-\omega=30^\circ$. Equality does hold, so $A=B=C=60^\circ$: the triangle is equilateral (and $P$ is its centre).

