Since $PI=PE'$ and $EI=EE'$, the quadrilateral is a kite symmetric about $PE$, which is therefore a diameter. Take the circle to be the unit circle with $P=(-1,0)$, $E=(1,0)$, $I=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$ and $E'=(\cos\theta,-\sin\theta)$; the condition $PI<IE$ means $90^\circ<\theta<180^\circ$. Then
\[[IEE']=\dfrac12\cdot2\sin\theta\cdot(1-\cos\theta)=\sin\theta\,(1-\cos\theta).\]
Its derivative is $\cos\theta-\cos^2\theta+\sin^2\theta=-(2\cos\theta+1)(\cos\theta-1)$, which vanishes on $(90^\circ,180^\circ)$ only at $\cos\theta=-\dfrac12$: the area increases before and decreases after. So $\theta=120^\circ$, and $IEE'$ is equilateral.

