\emph{At most $4$.} Since $K\ne0$, the second equation gives $y=\dfrac{L-Hx(Ix+J)}{K}$, a quadratic in $x$ with nonzero leading coefficient. Substituting into the first equation gives a polynomial equation in $x$ of degree exactly $4$ (its $x^4$ coefficient is $DE\left(\dfrac{HI}{K}\right)^2\ne0$). It has at most $4$ real roots, and each root $x$ gives exactly one $y$. So $k\le4$.

\emph{All of $1,2,3,4$ occur.} Take the second equation $x(-x+2)+y=3$, i.e. the parabola $y=(x-1)^2+2$ ($H=1$, $I=-1$, $J=2$, $K=1$, $L=3$), and as the first equation one of the following circles ($A=B=D=E=1$, $C=-2$):
\begin{itemize}
\item $x(x-2)+y(y-8)=-15$, the circle with centre $(1,4)$ and radius $\sqrt2$: $4$ solutions;
\item $x(x-2)+y(y-6)=-9$, centre $(1,3)$, radius $1$: $3$ solutions;
\item $x(x-2)+y(y-4)=-4$, centre $(1,2)$, radius $1$: $2$ solutions;
\item $x(x-2)+y(y-2)=-1$, centre $(1,1)$, radius $1$, tangent to the parabola at its vertex: $1$ solution.
\end{itemize}
(These are the circles $u^2+(w-b)^2=\rho^2$ with $(b,\rho^2)=(2,2),(1,1),(0,1),(-1,1)$ and the parabola $w=u^2$, shifted by $u=x-1$, $w=y-2$; for example the first gives $u^4-3u^2+2=0$, so $u^2\in\{1,2\}$.) All coefficients are nonzero. Hence $\mathcal S=\{1,2,3,4\}$, and the sum is $\boxed{10}$.

