Splitting any one square into four raises the count by $3$, so from $4,6,8$ we get every $n\ge6$ (and $7=4+3$).

\emph{$n=2,3,5$ are impossible.} Each corner of the big square $S$ (side $s$) lies in a small square, and two corners cannot lie in the same small square unless it is all of $S$. So at least $4$ squares are needed, ruling out $2$ and $3$.

Suppose $S$ is cut into $5$ squares: corner squares $Q_1,Q_2,Q_3,Q_4$ (bottom-left, bottom-right, top-right, top-left, with sides $a,b,c,d$) and one more square $Q_5$. A side of $S$ not completely covered by its two corner squares must contain part of a side of $Q_5$. $Q_5$ cannot touch two adjacent sides (it would contain a corner) or two opposite sides (it would be all of $S$), so at least three sides of $S$ are exactly covered by their corner squares. Say the left, top and right sides are, with $d+a=s$, $c+d=s$, $b+c=s$. Then $a=c$ and $b=d$, so the bottom side is covered too ($a+b=s$). If $a>b$, the squares $Q_1=[0,a]^2$ and $Q_3=[s-a,s]^2=[b,s]^2$ overlap. Likewise $b>a$ is impossible. So $a=b=\dfrac s2$, the four corner squares fill $S$, and there is no room for $Q_5$.

Hence the largest impossible $n$ is $\boxed{5}$.

