The four vertices of a square form a full set: every triangle on them is a right isosceles triangle.

No full set has $5$ points. No three points can be collinear (a collinear triple has a $180^\circ$ angle, so it is not a non-obtuse triangle). Consider the convex hull of five points.
\begin{itemize}
\item If it is a pentagon, its angles add up to $540^\circ$, so some angle is at least $108^\circ$, and that vertex with its two neighbours forms an obtuse triangle.
\item Otherwise some point $P$ lies inside the hull. Triangulate the hull from one of its vertices; $P$ lies inside one of these triangles, say $XYZ$ (not on a side, as no three points are collinear). The angles $\angle XPY$, $\angle YPZ$, $\angle ZPX$ add up to $360^\circ$, so one of them is at least $120^\circ$, giving an obtuse triangle.
\end{itemize}

