Let arc $A_iA_{i+1}$ (not containing the other vertices) measure $2\alpha_i$, so $\alpha_1+\cdots+\alpha_6=\pi$ and $A_iA_{i+1}=2r\sin\alpha_i$.

By the midline theorem, $M_jM_{j-1}\parallel A_{j+1}A_{j-1}$ and $M_jM_{j+1}\parallel A_jA_{j+2}$. The chords $A_{j-1}A_{j+1}$ and $A_jA_{j+2}$ cross at an angle equal to half the sum of the intercepted arcs, $\alpha_{j-1}+\alpha_{j+1}$. So the angle of the midpoint hexagon at $M_j$ is
\[\angle M_j=\pi-(\alpha_{j-1}+\alpha_{j+1}).\]
In a cyclic hexagon, alternate angles add up to $2\pi$. Applying this to $M_2,M_4,M_6$:
\[3\pi-2(\alpha_1+\alpha_3+\alpha_5)=2\pi,\qquad\text{so}\qquad\alpha_1+\alpha_3+\alpha_5=\dfrac\pi2.\]
Here $\sin\alpha_1=\sin\alpha_3=\dfrac{5}{2r}$, so $\alpha_1=\alpha_3$, and $\sin\alpha_5=\dfrac{23}{2r}$. Since $\alpha_5=\dfrac\pi2-2\alpha_1$,
\[\dfrac{23}{2r}=\cos2\alpha_1=1-2\cdot\dfrac{25}{4r^2},\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad2r^2-23r-25=(2r-25)(r+1)=0.\]
So $r=\dfrac{25}{2}$ and $r^2=\dfrac{625}{4}$, giving $m+n-1=\boxed{628}$.

