The point of the unit circle at angle $x$ is $P=(\cos x,\sin x)$. Reflecting the plane in the line $y=x$ swaps coordinates and sends the ray at angle $x$ to the ray at angle $90^\circ-x$. So the point at angle $90^\circ-x$ is $(\sin x,\cos x)$, and its first coordinate gives $\cos\left(90^\circ-x\right)=\sin x$. $\blacksquare$ (For acute $x$ this is the familiar fact that the two acute angles of a right triangle are complementary, and the side opposite one is adjacent to the other.)

