\emph{Step 1: the cosine formula.} The chord between the unit-circle points at angles $0$ and $\alpha+\beta$ has the same length as the chord between the points at angles $-\beta$ and $\alpha$, since both subtend the angle $\alpha+\beta$ at the centre. Squaring the distances:
\[(\cos(\alpha+\beta)-1)^2+\sin^2(\alpha+\beta)=(\cos\alpha-\cos\beta)^2+(\sin\alpha+\sin\beta)^2,\]
which simplifies to $2-2\cos(\alpha+\beta)=2-2\left(\cos\alpha\cos\beta-\sin\alpha\sin\beta\right)$. So $\cos(\alpha+\beta)=\cos\alpha\cos\beta-\sin\alpha\sin\beta$. Replacing $\beta$ by $-\beta$ gives $\cos(\alpha-\beta)=\cos\alpha\cos\beta+\sin\alpha\sin\beta$.

\emph{Step 2.} Using $\sin\theta=\cos\left(90^\circ-\theta\right)$ twice,
\[\sin(\alpha+\beta)=\cos\left(\left(90^\circ-\alpha\right)-\beta\right)=\cos\left(90^\circ-\alpha\right)\cos\beta+\sin\left(90^\circ-\alpha\right)\sin\beta=\sin\alpha\cos\beta+\cos\alpha\sin\beta.\ \blacksquare\]

