Since $ABCD$ is cyclic, $\angle B+\angle D=180^\circ$, so $\cos D=-\cos B$. The law of cosines in triangles $ABC$ and $ADC$ gives
\[AC^2=a^2+b^2-2ab\cos B=c^2+d^2+2cd\cos B.\]
Hence $\cos B=\dfrac{a^2+b^2-c^2-d^2}{2(ab+cd)}$, and
\[AC^2=a^2+b^2-\dfrac{ab\left(a^2+b^2-c^2-d^2\right)}{ab+cd}=\dfrac{cd\left(a^2+b^2\right)+ab\left(c^2+d^2\right)}{ab+cd}=\dfrac{(ac+bd)(ad+bc)}{ab+cd}.\]
The formula for $BD^2$ follows in the same way from triangles $ABD$ and $CBD$, with the roles of the side pairs exchanged. $\blacksquare$

