A person can say it on a truthful day followed by a lying day, or on a lying day followed by a truthful day.
\begin{itemize}
\item Stacy: Monday (truthful, then a lying Tuesday) or Thursday (lying, then a truthful Friday).
\item Anthony: Friday (truthful, then a lying Saturday) or Monday (lying, then a truthful Tuesday).
\end{itemize}
The common day is $\boxed{\text{Monday}}$.

Each point is on a uniformly random side. With probability $\dfrac13$ both are on the same side, and their distance $|s-t|$ (with $s,t$ uniform on $[0,1]$) exceeds $\dfrac12$ with probability $\dfrac14$.

With probability $\dfrac23$ they are on different sides, which meet at a $60^\circ$ angle. If their distances from the common vertex are $x$ and $y$, the distance squared is $x^2-xy+y^2$. The region $x^2-xy+y^2\le\dfrac14$ in the first quadrant lies inside the unit square and has area
\[\dfrac12\int_0^{\dfrac\pi2}\dfrac{\dfrac14}{1-\sin\theta\cos\theta}\,d\theta=\dfrac{\pi}{6\sqrt3}=\dfrac{\pi\sqrt3}{18}\]
(polar coordinates; the integral uses $\displaystyle\int_0^{\pi}\dfrac{du}{1-\dfrac12\sin u}=\dfrac{8\pi}{3\sqrt3}$).

