If $r$ occupies positions $p_r$ and $p_r+r$, then summing all $2n$ positions,
\[\sum_{r=1}^{n}(2p_r+r)=1+2+\cdots+2n=n(2n+1),\qquad\text{so}\qquad2\sum p_r=n(2n+1)-\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}=\dfrac{n(3n+1)}{2}.\]
Hence $\dfrac{n(3n+1)}{2}$ is even, i.e. $4\mid n(3n+1)$, which holds exactly for $n\equiv0,1\pmod4$ (for $n\equiv2,3$ we get $n(3n+1)\equiv2\pmod4$). $\blacksquare$

Examples:
\begin{itemize}
\item $n=1$: $1,1$;
\item $n=4$: $4,2,3,2,4,3,1,1$;
\item $n=5$: $5,2,4,2,3,5,4,3,1,1$;
\item $n=8$: $8,5,7,2,6,2,5,4,8,7,6,4,3,1,1,3$;
\item $n=9$: $9,7,8,4,2,6,2,4,7,9,8,6,5,3,1,1,3,5$.
\end{itemize}
(Th.~Skolem proved that such sequences exist for every $n\equiv0,1\pmod4$.)

Let $Q=\sqrt{\dfrac{a^2+b^2}{2}}$ and $A=\dfrac{a+b}{2}$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $Q^2-A^2=\dfrac{(a-b)^2}{4}\ge0$, so $Q\ge A$. Then $\dfrac{a^2+b^2}{a+b}=\dfrac{Q^2}{A}\ge Q$.
\item Logarithmic mean versus $A$ and $\sqrt{ab}$: by symmetry let $b>a$ and $t=\dfrac ba>1$; the three means are $a\cdot\dfrac{1+t}{2}$, $a\cdot\dfrac{t-1}{\ln t}$ and $a\sqrt t$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $\dfrac{1+t}{2}\ge\dfrac{t-1}{\ln t}$ means $h(t)=\ln t-\dfrac{2(t-1)}{t+1}\ge0$. Here $h(1)=0$ and $h'(t)=\dfrac1t-\dfrac{4}{(t+1)^2}=\dfrac{(t-1)^2}{t(t+1)^2}\ge0$.
\item With $x=\sqrt t>1$, $\dfrac{t-1}{\ln t}\ge\sqrt t$ means $g(x)=\dfrac12\left(x-\dfrac1x\right)-\ln x\ge0$. Here $g(1)=0$ and $g'(x)=\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{2x^2}\ge0$.
\end{itemize}
\item $\sqrt{ab}\ge\dfrac{2ab}{a+b}$ is equivalent to $a+b\ge2\sqrt{ab}$, i.e. AM--GM.
\end{itemize}
$\blacksquare$ (All inequalities are strict for $a\ne b$; for $a=b$ all the means equal $a$, the logarithmic mean being defined as $a$.)

Let $\angle BAC=2\theta$, so $\angle BCA=90^\circ-2\theta$. Let the bisector of $\angle A$ meet $BC$ at $D$; in right triangle $ABD$, $\angle ADB=90^\circ-\theta$. Since $CP=CB$, triangle $BCP$ is isosceles with $\angle CBP=\angle CPB$. There are two positions for $P$ (called $P_1$ and $P_2$ in the sketch).

\emph{Case 1: $P$ and $B$ are on opposite sides of $AC$.} Going around $C$, the rays $CB$, $CA$, $CP$ come in this order, so $\angle BCP=(90^\circ-2\theta)+90^\circ=180^\circ-2\theta<180^\circ$, and $P$ lies on the same side of $BC$ as $A$. Hence $\angle CBP=\dfrac{180^\circ-(180^\circ-2\theta)}{2}=\theta$. The rays $BP$ and $DA$ leave line $BC$ into the same half-plane with $\angle PBD=\theta$ and $\angle ADB=90^\circ-\theta$, so they meet at a point $Q$ with $\angle BQD=180^\circ-\theta-(90^\circ-\theta)=90^\circ$. Thus $BP\perp AD$.

\emph{Case 2: $P$ and $B$ are on the same side of $AC$.} Now $\angle ACB<90^\circ=\angle ACP$, so ray $CB$ lies inside $\angle ACP$, $\angle BCP=90^\circ-(90^\circ-2\theta)=2\theta$, and $P$, $A$ are on opposite sides of line $BC$. Hence $\angle CBP=\dfrac{180^\circ-2\theta}{2}=90^\circ-\theta=\angle ADB$. These are alternate angles for the lines $BP$ and $DA$ cut by the transversal $BD$, so $BP\parallel AD$.

In both cases line $BP$ is perpendicular or parallel to the bisector of $\angle A$. $\blacksquare$

