Use metres, with the field $[0,1000]\times[0,1000]$ and the walk from $(500,0)$ to $(500,1000)$. Kevin is seen if he lies within $200$ of a point of the first half of the path or within $100$ of a point of the second half (shaded).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill((3,0)--(7,0)--(7,5)--arc((5,5),2,0,60)--(6,10)--(4,10)--arc((5,5),2,120,180)--cycle,lightgray);
draw((0,0)--(10,0)--(10,10)--(0,10)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw((5,0)--(5,10),dashed);dot((5,0));dot((5,5));dot((5,10));
draw((5,5)--(5,5)+2*dir(150));label("$200$",(5,5)+dir(150),N,fontsize(8pt));
draw((5,7.5)--(6,7.5));label("$100$",(5.5,7.5),N,fontsize(8pt));
draw((6,5)--(6,5+sqrt(3)),dotted);draw((4,5)--(4,5+sqrt(3)),dotted);draw((3,5)--(7,5),dotted);
label("start",(5,0),S,fontsize(8pt));label("halfway",(5,5),SE,fontsize(8pt));label("$1000$",(10,5),E,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\begin{itemize}
\item First half: the points within $200$ of the segment from $(500,0)$ to $(500,500)$ that lie in the field form the rectangle $[300,700]\times[0,500]$ (area $200000$) together with the half-disc of radius $200$ centred at $(500,500)$ above $y=500$ (area $20000\pi$).
\item Second half: the rectangle $[400,600]\times[500,1000]$ (area $100000$); its end caps lie inside the first region or outside the field.
\end{itemize}
The half-disc and the second rectangle overlap in the part of the half-disc with $|x-500|\le100$. The lines $x=400$ and $x=600$ meet the circle at height $100\sqrt3$ above the centre, so the overlap consists of two right triangles with legs $100$ and $100\sqrt3$ (total area $10000\sqrt3$) and a $60^\circ$ sector of radius $200$ (area $\dfrac{20000\pi}{3}$). The seen region therefore has area
\[200000+20000\pi+100000-10000\sqrt3-\dfrac{20000\pi}{3}=300000-10000\sqrt3+\dfrac{40000\pi}{3}.\]
Dividing by $10^6$, the probability is $\dfrac{90-3\sqrt3+4\pi}{300}$, so $(a,b,c,d,e)=(90,3,3,4,300)$ and
\[\dfrac12(90+3+3+4+300)-13=200-13=\boxed{\textbf{(C) }187}.\]

The three regions are the two outer pieces, one beyond each line, and the band between the lines; each has area $\dfrac\pi3$. A line through the disc cuts it into two pieces, and the piece containing the centre $O$ (either piece, if the line passes through $O$) has area at least $\dfrac\pi2$. So if $O$ lay in an outer piece or on one of the lines, that outer piece would have area at least $\dfrac\pi2>\dfrac\pi3$. Hence $O$ lies strictly inside the band, the two lines are on opposite sides of $O$, and each line cuts off a cap, smaller than a half-disc, of area $\dfrac\pi3$.

A cap whose chord subtends an angle $\theta$ at $O$ has area (sector minus triangle)
\[\dfrac12\theta-\dfrac12\sin\theta,\]
and its chord is at distance $\cos\dfrac\theta2$ from $O$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=0.264932;real w=sqrt(1-h^2);
fill(arc((0,0),1,degrees(asin(h)),180-degrees(asin(h)))--cycle,lightgray);
fill(arc((0,0),1,180+degrees(asin(h)),360-degrees(asin(h)))--cycle,lightgray);
draw(unitcircle,linewidth(0.8));draw((-1.25,h)--(1.25,h));draw((-1.25,-h)--(1.25,-h));
pair O=(0,0);dot(O);draw(O--(w,h),dashed);draw(O--(-w,h),dashed);
draw(arc(O,0.18,degrees(asin(h)),180-degrees(asin(h))));label("$\theta$",(0,0.18),N,fontsize(8pt));
draw((1.15,-h)--(1.15,h),Arrows(4));label("$d$",(1.15,0),E);label("$O$",O,S);
\end{asy}

The function $g(\theta)=\theta-\sin\theta$ is strictly increasing on $(0,\pi)$ (as $g'(\theta)=1-\cos\theta>0$) with $g(0)=0$ and $g(\pi)=\pi$, so $g(\theta)=\dfrac{2\pi}{3}$ has exactly one solution there. Both caps therefore have the same angle $\theta$, the lines are at distance $\cos\dfrac\theta2$ on opposite sides of $O$, and
\[d=2\cos\dfrac\theta2.\]
This equation has no elementary closed-form solution, so solve it numerically, e.g. by Newton's method $\theta\mapsto\theta-\dfrac{\theta-\sin\theta-\dfrac{2\pi}{3}}{1-\cos\theta}$: starting from $\theta=2.6$ gives $2.60533$, which is already stable. Then
\[d=2\cos(1.302666)\approx\boxed{0.530}.\]

question:
A student answers an $11$-question true--false quiz by guessing, each answer being correct with probability $\dfrac12$ independently.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Using the fact that the numbers in each row of Pascal's triangle add up to a power of $2$, find the probability, as a percentage, that the student gets at least $6$ answers correct.
\item[(b)] Another student proposes the product $\dbinom{11}{6}\dfrac{1}{11^6}\cdot\dbinom{11}{7}\dfrac{1}{11^7}\cdots\dbinom{11}{11}\dfrac{1}{11^{11}}$. Explain what is wrong with it.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
A dilation with centre $O$ and scale factor $3$ moves every point $P$ along the ray $OP$ to the point $P'$ that is $3$ times as far from $O$; the shape stays the same, but every length is multiplied by $3$. With centre at the origin this simply multiplies both coordinates by $3$: $(x,y)\mapsto(3x,3y)$. Hence
\[\boxed{G'(-3,-6),\quad H'(-3,9),\quad J'(12,9),\quad K'(12,-6)}.\]
All side lengths triple: $GHJK$ is in fact a square of side $5$, and its image is a square of side $15$.

