question:
A set $S$ (the ``Mathian numbers'') has an operation $\oplus$ with the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $x\oplus y=y\oplus x$ for all $x,y\in S$;
\item $(x\oplus y)\oplus z=x\oplus(y\oplus z)$ for all $x,y,z\in S$;
\item for any $x,y\in S$ there is some $z\in S$ with $x\oplus z=y$.
\end{enumerate}
Prove that if $x\oplus z=y\oplus z$, then $x=y$.

solution:
Let the planet have radius $R$ metres. The tight rope is a circle of radius $R$ and the floating rope is a concentric circle of radius $R+10$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);draw(circle(O,2),linewidth(0.8));draw(circle(O,2.6),dashed);dot(O);
draw(O--2*dir(35));label("$R$",dir(35),NW,fontsize(9pt));
draw(2*dir(-30)--2.6*dir(-30),Arrows(3));label("$10$",2.6*dir(-30),SE,fontsize(9pt));
label("planet",(0,-1.2),fontsize(8pt));label("rope",2.6*dir(120),NW,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The added length is
\[2\pi(R+10)-2\pi R=20\pi\approx62.8\text{ metres},\]
which does not involve $R$. So the extra rope is always $\boxed{20\pi\text{ m}}$, whatever the planet. $\blacksquare$

question:
The seven tetrominoes $I,O,T,S,Z,L,J$ (the Tetris pieces, each made of four unit squares) are shown below.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
void piece(pair[] c,pair off){for(int i=0;i<c.length;++i) filldraw(shift(off+c[i])*unitsquare,lightgray,black);}
pair[] pI={(0,0),(1,0),(2,0),(3,0)};pair[] pO={(0,0),(1,0),(0,1),(1,1)};pair[] pT={(0,1),(1,1),(2,1),(1,0)};
pair[] pS={(0,0),(1,0),(1,1),(2,1)};pair[] pZ={(0,1),(1,1),(1,0),(2,0)};pair[] pL={(0,0),(0,1),(0,2),(1,0)};pair[] pJ={(1,0),(1,1),(1,2),(0,0)};
piece(pI,(0,4));piece(pO,(5.5,4));piece(pT,(9,4));piece(pS,(13.5,4));
piece(pZ,(0,0));piece(pL,(4.5,0));piece(pJ,(7.5,0));
label("$I$",(2,4),S);label("$O$",(6.5,4),S);label("$T$",(10.5,4),S);label("$S$",(15,4),S);
label("$Z$",(1.5,0),S);label("$L$",(5,0),S);label("$J$",(8.5,0),S);
\end{asy}

Prove that it is impossible to place one copy of each of the seven pieces on a unit grid, without overlaps (rotations and reflections allowed), so that together they form a rectangle.

solution:
Suppose the seven pieces formed a rectangle. Its area is $7\cdot4=28$, so its sides are $1\times28$, $2\times14$ or $4\times7$; in each case one side is even. Colour the grid like a chessboard. Every row of even length (or every column of even length) has equally many cells of each colour, so the rectangle has exactly $14$ dark and $14$ light cells.

Now count the colours covered by each piece, in any position:
\begin{itemize}
\item $I$, $O$, $S$, $Z$, $L$ and $J$ can each be cut into two dominoes, and a domino always covers one dark and one light cell. So each of these six pieces covers $2$ dark and $2$ light cells.
\item $T$ consists of a centre cell and three cells adjacent to it, which all have the colour opposite to the centre. So $T$ covers $3$ cells of one colour and $1$ of the other.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
void sq(pair p,bool blk){filldraw(shift(p)*unitsquare,blk?gray(0.35):white,black);}
sq((0,1),false);sq((1,1),true);sq((2,1),false);sq((1,0),false);
sq((7,0),true);sq((7,1),false);sq((7,2),true);sq((8,0),false);
draw((7,1)--(8,1),linewidth(2));
label("$T$: $1$ dark, $3$ light",(1.5,-0.2),S,fontsize(8pt));label("$L$: two dominoes",(8,-0.2),S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Hence the seven pieces cover $12+3=15$ or $12+1=13$ dark cells, never $14$. This contradiction shows that no such rectangle exists. $\blacksquare$

\emph{Right triangles.} A right triangle with legs $p$ and $q$ is half of a $p\times q$ rectangle: a diagonal cuts the rectangle into two triangles that are congruent (SSS), so each has area $\dfrac12pq$.

\emph{General triangle.} Let the side be $BC=b$, and let $H$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $A$ to line $BC$, so $AH=h$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(3,0),A=(1.1,2),H=(1.1,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(A--H,dashed);draw(H+(0.18,0)--H+(0.18,0.18)--H+(0,0.18));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$H$",H,S);label("$h$",(A+H)/2,E);
label("Case 1",(1.5,-0.7),fontsize(8pt));
pair B2=(4.2,0),C2=(5.7,0),A2=(7,2),H2=(7,0);
draw(A2--B2--C2--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(C2--H2,dashed);draw(A2--H2,dashed);draw(H2+(-0.18,0)--H2+(-0.18,0.18)--H2+(0,0.18));
label("$A$",A2,N);label("$B$",B2,SW);label("$C$",C2,S);label("$H$",H2,SE);label("$h$",(A2+H2)/2,E);
label("Case 2",(5.6,-0.7),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\emph{Case 1: $H$ lies on segment $BC$.} Then $AH$ cuts the triangle into right triangles $ABH$ and $AHC$ (one of them is absent if $H$ is $B$ or $C$), so
\[[ABC]=\dfrac12\,BH\cdot h+\dfrac12\,HC\cdot h=\dfrac12(BH+HC)h=\dfrac12bh.\]

\emph{Case 2: $H$ lies outside segment $BC$}, say with $C$ between $B$ and $H$. Then triangle $ABH$ is made of the non-overlapping triangles $ABC$ and $ACH$, so
\[[ABC]=[ABH]-[ACH]=\dfrac12\,BH\cdot h-\dfrac12\,CH\cdot h=\dfrac12(BH-CH)h=\dfrac12bh.\ \blacksquare\]

Call polygons $F$ and $G$ \emph{equidecomposable}, $F\approx G$, if $F$ can be cut into finitely many polygons that can be rigidly moved to form $G$. Clearly $F\approx F$ and $F\approx G$ implies $G\approx F$.

\emph{Step 0: transitivity.} If $F\approx G$ and $G\approx H$, draw on $G$ both the cuts coming from $F$ and the cuts used to form $H$. The pieces of this common refinement are polygons, each lying in one piece of each dissection; moving them back gives $F$ and moving them forward gives $H$. So $F\approx H$. Also, if two polygons are each cut into parts with corresponding parts equidecomposable, the polygons are equidecomposable.

\emph{Step 1: parallelograms with a common side and equal heights.} Let $ABCD$ and $ABEF$ be parallelograms with $C,D,E,F$ on one line parallel to $AB$. In the strip between line $AB$ and line $CD$, the translates of $ABCD$ by integer multiples of $\overrightarrow{AB}$ tile the strip, and so do the translates of $ABEF$. Cut $ABEF$ into the finitely many nonempty pieces $ABEF\cap\left(ABCD+k\overrightarrow{AB}\right)$. Translating each piece by $-k\overrightarrow{AB}$ moves it to $\left(ABEF-k\overrightarrow{AB}\right)\cap ABCD$; since the translates of $ABEF$ tile the strip, these pieces fill $ABCD$ exactly. So $ABEF\approx ABCD$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real bb=1.5,off=3.4;
draw((-0.3,0)--(6.6,0),gray);draw((-0.3,1)--(6.6,1),gray);
for(int k=1;k<=4;++k) draw((k*bb,0)--(k*bb,1),dotted);
fill((0,0)--(bb,0)--(bb,1)--(0,1)--cycle,gray(0.85));
draw((0,0)--(bb,0)--(off+bb,1)--(off,1)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0)--(bb,0)--(bb,1)--(0,1)--cycle,linewidth(0.6));
label("$A$",(0,0),SW);label("$B$",(bb,0),S);label("$D$",(0,1),NW);label("$C$",(bb,1),N);label("$F$",(off,1),N);label("$E$",(off+bb,1),NE);
\end{asy}

\emph{Step 2: triangle to rectangle.} In triangle $ABC$ take $BC$ to be a longest side, so the foot of the altitude from $A$ lies on $BC$. Let $M,N$ be the midpoints of $AB,AC$ and $H$ the foot of the perpendicular from $A$ to $MN$. Rotating triangle $AMH$ by $180^\circ$ about $M$ and triangle $ANH$ by $180^\circ$ about $N$ turns the triangle into a rectangle with base $BC$ and height half the altitude.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(4,0),A=(1.2,2.4),M=(A+B)/2,N2=(A+C)/2,H=(1.2,1.2);
fill(A--M--H--cycle,gray(0.75));fill(A--N2--H--cycle,gray(0.88));
fill(B--M--(0,1.2)--cycle,gray(0.75));fill(C--N2--(4,1.2)--cycle,gray(0.88));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(M--N2,dashed);draw(A--H,dashed);
draw((0,0)--(0,1.2)--(4,1.2)--(4,0),dotted);
dot(M);dot(N2);dot(H);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$M$",M,NW);label("$N$",N2,NE);label("$H$",H,NE,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\emph{Step 3: rectangle to rectangle.} (a) Let a rectangle $ABCD$ have base $AB=x$ and height $y$, and let $z\ge y$. Since the distance from $A$ to line $DC$ is $y\le z$, there is a point $F$ on line $DC$ with $AF=z$; let $E=F+\overrightarrow{AB}$. By Step 1, $ABCD\approx ABEF$. Now regard $ABEF$ as a parallelogram on the side $AF$: its height over $AF$ is $\dfrac{xy}{z}$, so by Step 1 again it is equidecomposable with the rectangle on $AF$ of height $\dfrac{xy}{z}$. Hence an $x\times y$ rectangle is equidecomposable with a $z\times\dfrac{xy}{z}$ rectangle whenever $z\ge y$.

(b) Now let $R_1$ ($a\times b$) and $R_2$ ($c\times d$) have equal areas, and let $c$ be the largest of $a,b,c,d$ (otherwise swap the roles of $R_1$, $R_2$ or of $c$, $d$). Taking the shorter side of $R_1$ as its height, (a) gives $R_1\approx$ a $c\times\dfrac{ab}{c}=c\times d$ rectangle, i.e. $R_1\approx R_2$.

\emph{Step 4: the polygon.} Cut the polygon into triangles $T_1,\ldots,T_n$ (a triangulation). By Steps 2 and 3 each $T_i$ is equidecomposable with a $1\times[T_i]$ rectangle. Stacking these rectangles gives a $1\times A$ rectangle, where $A$ is the area of the polygon, and by Step 3 this is equidecomposable with the $\sqrt A\times\sqrt A$ square. By Step 0 the polygon is equidecomposable with the square. $\blacksquare$

(This is the Wallace--Bolyai--Gerwien theorem.)

Take a circle with centre $O$ and radius $r$, and mark six points on it at $60^\circ$ intervals; they form a regular hexagon. Each side subtends $60^\circ$ at $O$, so each triangle formed by $O$ and a side is isosceles with apex angle $60^\circ$, hence equilateral, and every side of the hexagon has length $r$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(unitcircle,linewidth(0.8));pair O=(0,0);dot(O);
pair[] P;for(int i=0;i<6;++i)P[i]=dir(60*i);
draw(P[0]--P[1]--P[2]--P[3]--P[4]--P[5]--cycle);
draw(O--P[0],dashed);draw(O--P[1],dashed);
label("$O$",O,SW);label("$r$",(O+P[0])/2,S);label("$r$",(O+P[1])/2,W);label("$r$",(P[0]+P[1])/2,SW,fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc(O,0.2,0,60));label("$60^\circ$",0.2*dir(30),NE,fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

Each side of the hexagon is a straight segment joining the ends of one of the six arcs into which the circle is divided, so it is shorter than that arc. Adding over the six arcs,
\[6r<2\pi r,\qquad\text{so}\qquad\pi>3.\ \blacksquare\]

Take $42$ metres as the unit of length, so the lifebuoys are discs of radius $1$.

\emph{A packing.} Let $s=2.005$ and consider the triangular lattice $\Lambda$ of points $iu+jv$ ($i,j$ integers), where $u$ and $v$ have length $s$ and make an angle of $60^\circ$. Distinct lattice points are at distance at least $s>2$, so the closed unit discs centred at the lattice points are pairwise disjoint. A fundamental rhombus $Q$ of $\Lambda$ (with sides $u$, $v$) has area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s^2$, and the discs cover the fraction
\[\rho=\dfrac{\pi}{\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s^2}=\dfrac{2\pi}{\sqrt3\cdot4.020025}>0.902\]
of the plane, in the sense that for any point $x$, the set of translation vectors $t\in Q$ for which $x$ lies in some open disc centred at a point of $\Lambda+t$ has area exactly $\pi$ (the discs reduce modulo $\Lambda$ to a single disc, with no overlap).

\emph{Averaging.} For $t\in Q$ let $N(t)$ be the number of people covered by the discs centred at the points of $\Lambda+t$. Integrating over $Q$ and summing over the ten people,
\[\int_QN(t)\,dt=10\pi=10\rho\,[Q]>9.02\,[Q].\]
If $N(t)\le9$ for every $t$, the integral would be at most $9[Q]$. Hence some translation $t$ has $N(t)=10$: every person lies inside one of the discs of $\Lambda+t$.

At most ten of these discs contain people; place the lifebuoys exactly on them (and any spare lifebuoys far away). They are pairwise disjoint and save everyone. $\blacksquare$

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
