\emph{The shadow.} The three faces through the top vertex have outward unit normals $\mathbf u,\mathbf v,\mathbf w$; call them the upper faces. A vertical line through an interior point of the shadow meets the convex cube in a segment, whose top end lies on an upper face (just above that point is outside the cube). (It cannot be an interior point of one of the three lower faces, whose outward normals $-\mathbf u,-\mathbf v,-\mathbf w$ point downward.) Conversely, at an interior point of an upper face the outward normal points upward, so just above it is outside and just below it is inside: it is the top end of its vertical segment. Hence the projections of the three upper faces cover the shadow and overlap only along edges, and the shadow area is the sum of the areas of these three projections.

\emph{Projected area.} Projecting a plane region of area $S$ with unit normal $\mathbf N$ vertically onto the table multiplies areas by $|\cos\varphi|=|\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf N|$, where $\varphi$ is the angle between the two planes. Each face has area $1$, so
\[\text{shadow area}=\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf u+\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf v+\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf w=h.\ \blacksquare\]

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find an inverse of $j(x)=\sin x$ by restricting its domain suitably.
\item[(b)] Explain why the domain must be restricted.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(b)} A function has an inverse only if it is one-to-one. The sine function is not: for example $\sin0=\sin\pi=0$, and every value in $[-1,1]$ is taken infinitely often. So ``$\sin^{-1}(0)$'' would not know whether to be $0$, $\pi$, $2\pi$, \ldots

\textbf{(a)} On $\left[-\dfrac\pi2,\dfrac\pi2\right]$ the sine function is increasing and takes every value in $[-1,1]$ exactly once. Its inverse is
\[\boxed{\arcsin:[-1,1]\to\left[-\dfrac\pi2,\dfrac\pi2\right]},\]
where $\arcsin x$ is the unique angle in $\left[-\dfrac\pi2,\dfrac\pi2\right]$ whose sine is $x$. Its graph is the reflection of the restricted sine graph in the line $y=x$.

