question:
Let $f(x)=x^2-6x+9$ for $x\in[3,\infty)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the inverse of $f$, with its domain.
\item[(b)] Why would the answer be different without the restriction $x\ge3$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{No.} Two examples:
\begin{itemize}
\item $a=d=0$, $b=c=1$ (so $ad-bc=-1$) and $w=z=0$, $x=1$, $y=-3$ (so $wz-xy=3$). Then $adwz-bcxy=0-1\cdot(-3)=3$, and $9(adwz-bcxy)=27$.
\item $a=d=0$, $b=c=1$ and $w=z=\sqrt3$, $x=y=0$. Then $adwz-bcxy=0-0=0$, and the value is $0$.
\end{itemize}
Both satisfy the conditions but give different values. In general, with $P=ad$, $Q=bc$, $R=wz$, $S=xy$ we have $P=Q-1$ and $R=S+3$, so $PR-QS=3Q-S-3$, which varies freely.

\emph{The ratio $BF:FC$.} Apply Menelaus' theorem to triangle $DBC$ with the line through $A$, $E$, $F$ ($E$ on $DB$, $F$ on $BC$, $A$ on line $CD$ extended):
\[\dfrac{DE}{EB}\cdot\dfrac{BF}{FC}\cdot\dfrac{CA}{AD}=1\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad1\cdot\dfrac{BF}{FC}\cdot\dfrac32=1,\]
so $BF:FC=2:3$ and $\dfrac{BF}{BC}=\dfrac25$. (With mass points: put mass $1$ at $A$ and $2$ at $C$, so $D$ has mass $3$; as $E$ is the midpoint of $BD$, $B$ has mass $3$; then $F$ on $BC$ satisfies $BF:FC=2:3$.)

\emph{Areas.} Since $DC=\dfrac13AC$, $[DBC]=\dfrac13\cdot360=120$. Triangles $EBF$ and $DBC$ share the angle at $B$, so
\[[EBF]=\dfrac{BE}{BD}\cdot\dfrac{BF}{BC}\cdot[DBC]=\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac25\cdot120=\boxed{24}.\]

