question:
A group of $n$ people sit in a row of $n$ chairs. After a break they sit down again in the same $n$ chairs in such a way that no two people who sat next to each other before the break sit next to each other after the break. Let $a_n$ be the number of possible seating orders after the break.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Use inclusion--exclusion to prove that
\[a_n=n!+\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}(-1)^k(n-k)!\sum_{j=1}^{k}2^j\binom{k-1}{j-1}\binom{n-k}{j}.\]
\item[(b)] Find $a_6$, the number of possible seating orders when there are $6$ people.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Number the people $1,\ldots,n$ in their original order, so the forbidden neighbours are the $n-1$ pairs $\{i,i+1\}$. For a set $S$ of these pairs let $N(S)$ be the number of seatings in which every pair in $S$ sits together; $N(\varnothing)=n!$. By inclusion--exclusion,
\[a_n=\sum_S(-1)^{|S|}N(S),\]
the sum running over all sets $S$ of forbidden pairs.

Let $|S|=k\ge1$. The pairs in $S$ join the people into maximal runs of consecutive integers, say $j$ runs. If a run $m,m+1,\ldots,m+\ell$ ($\ell\ge1$ pairs) must have all its pairs adjacent, then each inner person sits between his two run neighbours, so the run occupies a block of consecutive chairs in increasing or in decreasing order ($2$ ways). Gluing each run into a single block leaves $n-k$ objects (each pair in $S$ reduces the count by one), which can be arranged in $(n-k)!$ ways. So $N(S)=2^j(n-k)!$.

It remains to count the sets of $k$ pairs that form exactly $j$ runs. Think of the $n-1$ pairs as positions in a line, $k$ of them chosen. The chosen positions form $j$ blocks of consecutive positions: their lengths are a composition of $k$ into $j$ positive parts, in $\dbinom{k-1}{j-1}$ ways, and the blocks go into $j$ different gaps among the $n-k$ gaps around the $n-1-k$ unchosen positions, in $\dbinom{n-k}{j}$ ways. Hence there are $\dbinom{k-1}{j-1}\dbinom{n-k}{j}$ such sets, each contributing $(-1)^k2^j(n-k)!$, which gives the formula. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(b)} For $n=6$ the inner sums are:
\begin{itemize}
\item $k=1$: $2\cdot1\cdot5=10$;
\item $k=2$: $2\cdot1\cdot4+4\cdot1\cdot6=32$;
\item $k=3$: $2\cdot1\cdot3+4\cdot2\cdot3+8\cdot1\cdot1=38$;
\item $k=4$: $2\cdot1\cdot2+4\cdot3\cdot1=16$ (the term $j=3$ vanishes, as $\dbinom23=0$);
\item $k=5$: $2\cdot1\cdot1=2$.
\end{itemize}
Therefore
\[a_6=720-120\cdot10+24\cdot32-6\cdot38+2\cdot16-1\cdot2=720-1200+768-228+32-2=\boxed{90}.\]
(The same formula gives $a_4=2$, $a_5=14$ and $a_7=646$; evaluated by computer for $26$ people, it gives $a_{26}=54406969991009281966468810$.)

question:
Several figures can be made by attaching two equilateral triangles to the regular pentagon $ABCDE$ in two of the five positions shown (each triangle has a side of the pentagon as one of its sides and lies outside the pentagon).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] P;for(int i=0;i<5;++i)P[i]=dir(90+72*i);
draw(P[0]--P[1]--P[2]--P[3]--P[4]--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
for(int i=0;i<5;++i){pair m=(P[i]+P[(i+1)%5])/2;pair q=m+(sqrt(3)/2)*abs(P[(i+1)%5]-P[i])*unit(m);draw(P[i]--q--P[(i+1)%5],dashed);}
label("$A$",P[0],-P[0]);label("$B$",P[1],-P[1]);label("$C$",P[2],-P[2]);label("$D$",P[3],-P[3]);label("$E$",P[4],-P[4]);
\end{asy}

How many non-congruent figures can be constructed in this way?

$\textbf{(A) }1\qquad\textbf{(B) }2\qquad\textbf{(C) }3\qquad\textbf{(D) }4\qquad\textbf{(E) }5$

solution:
There are $\dbinom52=10$ ways to choose the two sides. Any two sides of a pentagon are either \emph{adjacent} (sharing a vertex; $5$ such pairs) or have \emph{one side between them} on one side of the pentagon and two sides between them on the other (the remaining $5$ pairs).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] P;for(int i=0;i<5;++i)P[i]=dir(90+72*i);
void tri(pair sh,int i){pair m=(P[i]+P[(i+1)%5])/2;pair q=m+(sqrt(3)/2)*abs(P[(i+1)%5]-P[i])*unit(m);filldraw(shift(sh)*(P[i]--q--P[(i+1)%5]--cycle),lightgray,black);}
draw(P[0]--P[1]--P[2]--P[3]--P[4]--cycle,linewidth(0.8));tri((0,0),0);tri((0,0),1);
draw(shift((3.3,0))*(P[0]--P[1]--P[2]--P[3]--P[4]--cycle),linewidth(0.8));tri((3.3,0),0);tri((3.3,0),2);
label("adjacent sides",(0,-2.1),fontsize(8pt));label("one side between",(3.3,-2.1),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The rotations of the regular pentagon about its centre (by multiples of $72^\circ$) carry any adjacent pair of sides to any other adjacent pair, so all $5$ figures of the first type are congruent; likewise all $5$ figures of the second type are congruent. The two types are not congruent: in the first, the two triangles meet at a common vertex of the pentagon, and in the second they have no common point. So there are exactly $\boxed{\textbf{(B) }2}$ figures.

(In short: once the first triangle is placed, the second goes either next to it or with one side in between.)

Let $n$ be the number of $45$-seat buses in the first plan. Then the number of students is $45n+15=60(n-1)$, so $15n=75$, $n=5$, and there are $240$ students.
\begin{itemize}
\item $45$-seat buses: $\dfrac{240}{45}=5\dfrac13$, so $6$ buses are needed, costing $6\cdot250=1500$ dollars.
\item $60$-seat buses: $\dfrac{240}{60}=4$ buses, costing $4\cdot300=1200$ dollars.
\end{itemize}
So renting $\boxed{4\text{ large (60-seat) buses}}$ is cheaper, saving $\boxed{\$300}$.

question:
Let $\phi=\dfrac{1+\sqrt5}{2}$ (the golden ratio).
