\emph{When do the curves meet?} They meet exactly when some real $x$ satisfies $Ax^2+B=Cx^2+D$, that is,
\[(A-C)x^2=D-B.\]
Since $A\ne C$ and $B\ne D$, this means $x^2=\dfrac{D-B}{A-C}$, and a real $x$ exists exactly when this quotient is positive: $A-C$ and $D-B$ have the same sign.

In picture form: both curves are upward parabolas with vertices on the $y$-axis. The one with the larger leading coefficient is narrower. If the narrower one starts lower (smaller constant term), it rises faster and must cross the wider one; if it starts higher, it stays above the wider one forever.

