solution:
In triangle $ABE$, the segment $AD$ bisects $\angle BAE$ (because $\angle BAD=\angle DAE$) and $D$ lies on $\overline{BE}$. By the angle bisector theorem,
\[\dfrac{BD}{DE}=\dfrac{AB}{AE}.\]
In triangle $ADC$, the segment $AE$ bisects $\angle DAC$ and $E$ lies on $\overline{DC}$, so
\[\dfrac{DE}{EC}=\dfrac{AD}{AC}.\]
The first ratio says $BD:DE=AB\cdot AD:AE\cdot AD$, and the second says $DE:EC=AD\cdot AE:AC\cdot AE$. Both use the same middle term $AD\cdot AE$, so
\[BD:DE:EC=AB\cdot AD:AD\cdot AE:AE\cdot AC.\ \blacksquare\]
Unlike the angle bisector theorem, the ratios involve the trisectors $AD$ and $AE$ themselves, not only the sides.

