question:
Let $\ell$ be the graph of a linear function $y=mx+b$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that the reflection in a line $L$ maps $\ell$ onto itself if and only if $L=\ell$ or $L\perp\ell$. (So a line has infinitely many axes of symmetry.)
\item[(b)] For which $m$ does $\ell$ have a vertical axis of symmetry $x=h$ (as a parabola does), and which vertical lines are axes of symmetry then?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} If $L=\ell$, the reflection fixes every point of $\ell$. If $L\perp\ell$, let $X$ be any point of $\ell$. Its image $X'$ lies on the line through $X$ perpendicular to $L$, and that line is $\ell$ itself; so $X'\in\ell$, and the reflection maps $\ell$ onto $\ell$.

Conversely, suppose the reflection maps $\ell$ onto itself and $L\ne\ell$. Choose $X\in\ell$ not on $L$. Its image $X'$ is on $\ell$ and $X'\ne X$, so $\ell$ is the line $XX'$. But $XX'\perp L$ by the definition of reflection. Hence $\ell\perp L$. $\blacksquare$

