Let $M$ and $N$ be the midpoints of the legs $\overline{AB}$ and $\overline{CD}$.

Since $\overline{BC}\parallel\overline{AD}$, $\angle A+\angle B=180^\circ$, so in triangle $AXB$ the angles at $A$ and $B$ add up to $90^\circ$ and $\angle AXB=90^\circ$. The midpoint of the hypotenuse is equidistant from the vertices, so $MX=MA=MB=2$. Then $\angle MXA=\angle MAX=\angle XAD$, so $\overline{MX}\parallel\overline{AD}$: $X$ lies on the midline $\overline{MN}$, at distance $2$ from $M$.

In the same way $\angle CYD=90^\circ$, $NY=\dfrac{CD}2=4$, and $Y$ lies on $\overline{MN}$ at distance $4$ from $N$. The midline has length $MN=\dfrac{6+12}2=9$, so
\[XY=9-2-4=3\qquad\text{and}\qquad XY^2=\boxed{\textbf{(D) }9}.\]
(Such a trapezoid exists: with $A=(0,0)$ and $D=(12,0)$ one gets $B=\left(-1,\sqrt{15}\right)$ and $C=\left(5,\sqrt{15}\right)$, and then $X=\left(\dfrac32,\dfrac{\sqrt{15}}2\right)$, $Y=\left(\dfrac92,\dfrac{\sqrt{15}}2\right)$.)

