Let $K$ be the midpoint of $\overline{BC}$ and $O$ the centre of $\omega$. Since $\angle A=90^\circ$, $K$ is the circumcentre of $ABC$, so $KA=KC$ and $\angle KAC=\angle KCA=30^\circ$.

\emph{The centre $O$ lies on $AC$.} The centre lies on the line through $K$ perpendicular to $BC$ (the radius to the point of tangency). Let this line meet line $AC$ at $P$; then $CP=\dfrac{CK}{\cos30^\circ}=\dfrac{BC}{\sqrt3}<\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}BC=AC$, so $P$ lies between $A$ and $C$. In right triangle $PKC$, $\angle KPC=60^\circ$, so $\angle APK=120^\circ$, and in triangle $APK$ we get $\angle AKP=180^\circ-120^\circ-30^\circ=30^\circ=\angle PAK$. Hence $PA=PK$, so $P$ is equidistant from $A$ and $K$ and lies on the perpendicular to $BC$ at $K$: $P=O$. Therefore $\overline{AN}$ is a diameter of $\omega$.

\emph{Conclusion.} The common chord $\overline{AM}$ of $\omega$ and the circumcircle is perpendicular to the line of centres $OK$, and $OK\perp BC$, so $AM\parallel BC$. Since $\overline{AN}$ is a diameter of $\omega$, $\angle AMN=90^\circ$ (inscribed angle in a semicircle), i.e. $MN\perp AM$. Hence $MN\perp BC$. $\blacksquare$

