Let $R=\dfrac{10^n-1}{9}$, the $n$-digit repunit. Then $A_n=aR$, $B_n=bR$, and since $\dfrac{10^{2n}-1}9=R\left(10^n+1\right)=R(9R+2)$, $C_n=cR(9R+2)$. The equation $C_n-B_n=A_n^2$ becomes, after dividing by $R$,
\[9cR+2c-b=a^2R,\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad\left(9c-a^2\right)R=b-2c.\]
If this holds for two values of $n$, it holds for two different values of $R$, which forces $9c-a^2=0$ and $b=2c$. So $a=3\sqrt c$ with $c\in\{1,4,9\}$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $c=1$: $a=3$, $b=2$, sum $6$;
\item $c=4$: $a=6$, $b=8$, sum $18$;
\item $c=9$: $b=18$ is not a digit.
\end{itemize}
In the valid cases the equation then holds for every $n$. The greatest value is $\boxed{18}$.

\emph{Constructible numbers.} Fix a unit length and coordinates. Every point we can construct is an intersection of lines and circles drawn through points already constructed. If all coordinates so far lie in a field $F$ (a set of reals closed under $+,-,\times,\div$), then lines and circles through these points have equations with coefficients in $F$. Intersecting two lines means solving linear equations, so the new coordinates are in $F$. Intersecting a line and a circle (or two circles, whose equations can be subtracted to give a line) leads to a quadratic equation over $F$, so the new coordinates lie in $F\left(\sqrt k\right)=\left\{p+q\sqrt k:p,q\in F\right\}$ for some $k\in F$, $k>0$. Hence every constructible length lies in a field at the end of a chain
\[\mathbb Q=F_0\subset F_1\subset\cdots\subset F_m,\qquad F_{i+1}=F_i\left(\sqrt{k_i}\right).\]

\emph{Lemma.} Let $t^3+at^2+bt+c$ have coefficients in $F$, and suppose it has a root in $F\left(\sqrt k\right)$, where $\sqrt k\notin F$. Then it has a root in $F$. Indeed, write the root as $r=p+q\sqrt k$. If $q=0$ we are done. Otherwise, the conjugation $p+q\sqrt k\mapsto p-q\sqrt k$ is well defined (as $\sqrt k\notin F$) and respects sums and products, and it fixes the coefficients, so $r'=p-q\sqrt k\ne r$ is also a root. The third root is $-a-r-r'=-a-2p\in F$.

\emph{The cubic.} If $60^\circ$ could be trisected, we could construct a $20^\circ$ angle and then, by dropping a perpendicular, the length $c=\cos20^\circ$. The triple-angle formula $\cos3\theta=4\cos^3\theta-3\cos\theta$ gives $4c^3-3c=\dfrac12$, so $y=2c$ satisfies
\[y^3-3y-1=0.\]
A rational root of this monic integer cubic would be an integer dividing $1$, but $1-3-1\ne0$ and $-1+3-1\ne0$. So it has no rational root. By the lemma applied down the chain $F_m,F_{m-1},\dots,F_0$, a root in $F_m$ would give a root in $\mathbb Q$, which is a contradiction. Hence $\cos20^\circ$, and therefore the $20^\circ$ angle, is not constructible. $\blacksquare$

