All interior angles are $135^\circ$, so the sides of length $3$ lie along the sides of a square and the sides of length $1$ cut off its corners as isosceles right triangles with legs $\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}$. The square has side $3+\sqrt2$.

\emph{Area.} The four corners have total area $4\cdot\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac12=1$, so the area is $\left(3+\sqrt2\right)^2-1=10+6\sqrt2$.

\emph{Largest circle.} The centre is at distance $\dfrac{3+\sqrt2}{2}\approx2.21$ from the long sides and $\dfrac{3\sqrt2+1}{2}\approx2.62$ from the short sides, so no circle touches all eight sides. Any circle inside the octagon lies inside the square, so its radius is at most $\dfrac{3+\sqrt2}{2}$; the circle of that radius about the centre stays inside the octagon because the short sides are farther away. So $r=\dfrac{3+\sqrt2}{2}$.

The sum is $10+6\sqrt2+\dfrac{3+\sqrt2}{2}=\boxed{\dfrac{23+13\sqrt2}{2}}$.

