Each face of the cube has area $\dfrac{648}{6}=108$ square feet, so the edge is $s=\sqrt{108}=6\sqrt3$ feet.

\emph{Cube.} The bottom is not painted and the top is covered by the roof, so only the four side faces are painted, minus the windows: $4\cdot108-4\cdot2^2=416$ square feet.

\emph{Roof.} The cross-section has base $6\sqrt3$ and height $9$, so its equal sides are $\sqrt{\left(3\sqrt3\right)^2+9^2}=\sqrt{108}=6\sqrt3$: it is equilateral. The two triangular ends have total area $2\cdot\dfrac12\cdot6\sqrt3\cdot9=54\sqrt3$, and the two sloping faces are $6\sqrt3\times6\sqrt3$ squares with total area $216$.

The painted area is $416+216+54\sqrt3=632+54\sqrt3\approx725.5$ square feet. The paint is $\dfrac14$ inch $=\dfrac1{48}$ foot thick, so its volume is $\dfrac{632+54\sqrt3}{48}\approx15.1$ cubic feet (as $93.5<54\sqrt3<93.6$, it lies between $15.1$ and $15.2$). Seven cans hold $14$ cubic feet and eight hold $16$, so $\boxed{8}$ cans are needed.

