\emph{Errors.}
\begin{itemize}
\item The first step is unjustified: formally $\left(i^i\right)^2=i^{2i}$, not $i^{-1}$ (the exponent $2i$ is not $-1$). With principal values, $\left(i^i\right)^2=e^{-\pi}$, while $i^{-1}=-i$.
\item The third step is wrong: $\left(\dfrac1i\right)^2=\dfrac1{i^2}=-1$, not $1$ (and $-1^2$ is $-1$ anyway).
\item The steps do not connect: squaring $\dfrac1i$ computes neither $i^i$ nor a power of it. Even if some power $\left(i^i\right)^k$ were $1$, we could not conclude $i^i=1$, because $1$ has $k$ different complex $k$-th roots.
\end{itemize}
\emph{Correct value.} $i=e^{\dfrac{i\pi}{2}}$, so $i^i=e^{i\cdot\dfrac{i\pi}{2}}=\boxed{e^{-\dfrac{\pi}{2}}\approx0.2079}$, a real number different from $1$. (Other branches of the logarithm give $e^{-\dfrac\pi2-2\pi k}$, none of which equals $1$.)

A rookable set is the graph of a permutation $\sigma$ of $\{0,1,2,3\}$ (square $(i,\sigma(i))$ in row $i$), so there are $24$ sets. Count orbits under the $8$ symmetries of the square with Burnside's lemma, by counting the sets each symmetry fixes:
\begin{itemize}
\item identity: $24$;
\item rotation by $180^\circ$, $(i,j)\mapsto(3-i,3-j)$: we need $\sigma(3-i)=3-\sigma(i)$, i.e. $\sigma$ maps the pairs $\{0,3\},\{1,2\}$ to pairs; $2\cdot2\cdot2=8$;
\item rotations by $\pm90^\circ$, $(i,j)\mapsto(j,3-i)$: we need $\sigma(\sigma(i))=3-i$, which forces the $4$-cycles $(0\,1\,3\,2)$ and $(0\,2\,3\,1)$; $2$ each;
\item reflections in the two mid-lines, e.g. $(i,j)\mapsto(3-i,j)$: we would need $\sigma(3-i)=\sigma(i)$, impossible; $0$ each;
\item reflections in the diagonals: $\sigma=\sigma^{-1}$ (or its analogue for the other diagonal), the $10$ involutions; $10$ each.
\end{itemize}
The number of orbits is $\dfrac{24+8+2+2+0+0+10+10}{8}=\boxed{7}$.

