For a regular $n$-gon, the outward unit normals of its sides point in the directions $\theta,\theta+\dfrac{2\pi}{n},\dots,\theta+\dfrac{2\pi(n-1)}{n}$ for some $\theta$. Call the class of $\theta$ modulo $\dfrac{2\pi}{n}$ the \emph{type} of the polygon.

Suppose polygons $P$ and $Q$ share a boundary segment. It lies on a side of each, and since $P$ and $Q$ do not overlap they lie on opposite sides of this line, so the two outward normals there are $u$ and $-u$. Hence
\[\operatorname{type}(Q)=\operatorname{type}(P)+\pi\pmod{\dfrac{2\pi}{n}}.\]
Since $n$ is odd, $\pi$ is not a multiple of $\dfrac{2\pi}{n}$ (that would need $n=2m$), so neighbouring polygons have different types. Applying the shift twice adds $2\pi\equiv0$. So along the bug's route the type alternates between two different values $\tau$ and $\tau+\pi$, and after $k$ moves it is $\tau$ exactly when $k$ is even. Returning to the starting polygon forces $k$ to be even. $\blacksquare$

