Let $O$ be the centre of the circle where the hemisphere meets the cylinder, and $r=\dfrac12$.

\emph{Volume.} The hemisphere holds $\dfrac23\pi r^3=\dfrac{\pi}{12}$. Suppose the surface plane meets the axis at the point $X$ with $OX=L$, and cuts only the side wall of the cylinder. The part of the cylinder between $O$ and the plane then has volume $\pi r^2L$: the pieces of the cylinder above and below the plane, on either side of the cross-section through $X$, are congruent (a half-turn about a line through $X$ swaps them). So
\[\dfrac{\pi}{12}+\dfrac{\pi}{4}L=3\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad L=\dfrac{12}{\pi}-\dfrac13\approx3.486.\]
The plane meets the wall where the axial distance from $X$ is at most $r\cot30^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\approx0.87$, i.e. between about $2.62$ and $4.35$ cm from $O$, so it indeed cuts only the wall of the $8$ cm cylinder, and the hemisphere is full.

\emph{Depth.} $X$ is $L\sin30^\circ=\dfrac L2$ above $O$. The lowest point of the tube is directly below $O$ on the hemisphere, $r=\dfrac12$ below $O$. So the depth is
\[d=\dfrac L2+\dfrac12=\dfrac6\pi-\dfrac16+\dfrac12=\boxed{\dfrac6\pi+\dfrac13\approx2.24\text{ cm}}.\]

