\emph{The area.} The lines $x=\dfrac y2+7$ and $y=\dfrac x2+7$ meet at $(14,14)$, and the region is nonempty only for $s>14$. Then it is the square $[14,s]^2$ minus two right triangles with legs $u=s-14$ and $\dfrac u2$, so
\[f(s)=u^2-2\cdot\dfrac{u^2}{4}=\dfrac{(s-14)^2}{2}.\]

\emph{Rational $s$.} Write $s-14=\dfrac pq$ in lowest terms with $p,q>0$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $p$ is odd, $f=\dfrac{p^2}{2q^2}$ is in lowest terms, so $p^2+2q^2=187$.
\item If $p=2p'$ is even, then $q$ is odd and $f=\dfrac{2p'^2}{q^2}$ is in lowest terms, so $q^2+2p'^2=187$.
\end{itemize}
The solutions of $X^2+2Y^2=187$ in positive integers with $X$ odd are found by trying $Y=1,\dots,9$: only $(X,Y)=(13,3)$ and $(5,9)$, both coprime. So
\[s-14\in\left\{\dfrac{13}3,\ \dfrac59,\ \dfrac{6}{13},\ \dfrac{18}{5}\right\},\qquad s\in\left\{\dfrac{55}{3},\ \dfrac{131}{9},\ \dfrac{188}{13},\ \dfrac{88}{5}\right\},\]
with $f=\dfrac{169}{18},\dfrac{25}{162},\dfrac{18}{169},\dfrac{162}{25}$ respectively. The requested sum is
\[(55+3)+(131+9)+(188+13)+(88+5)=\boxed{492}.\]

