The second point lies on the same side as the first with probability $\dfrac13$ and on one of the other two sides with probability $\dfrac23$.

\emph{Same side.} The positions $x,y$ are uniform on $[0,2]$. The region $|x-y|>1$ consists of two triangles of area $\dfrac12$ in the square of area $4$, so $P(|x-y|\le1)=\dfrac34$.

\emph{Different sides.} The two sides meet at a vertex $V$ at $60^\circ$; let $u,v\in[0,2]$ be the distances from $V$ (uniform and independent). By the law of cosines the distance $d$ satisfies $d^2=u^2+v^2-uv$, so we need the area of $\left\{u,v\ge0:\ u^2-uv+v^2\le1\right\}$. (This region lies inside $[0,2]^2$, since $u^2-uv+v^2\ge\dfrac34u^2$ gives $u\le\dfrac2{\sqrt3}$.) The linear map $(u,v)\mapsto u\mathbf e_1-v\mathbf e_2$, where $\mathbf e_1,\mathbf e_2$ are unit vectors at $60^\circ$, satisfies $\left|u\mathbf e_1-v\mathbf e_2\right|^2=u^2+v^2-uv$, multiplies areas by $\sin60^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$, and maps the quadrant onto a $120^\circ$ angle. So the region maps onto a $120^\circ$ sector of the unit disc, of area $\dfrac\pi3$, and its own area is $\dfrac{\pi}{3}\cdot\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3}=\dfrac{2\pi\sqrt3}{9}$. The probability is $\dfrac14\cdot\dfrac{2\pi\sqrt3}{9}=\dfrac{\pi\sqrt3}{18}$.

\emph{Total.}
\[\dfrac13\cdot\dfrac34+\dfrac23\cdot\dfrac{\pi\sqrt3}{18}=\boxed{\dfrac14+\dfrac{\pi\sqrt3}{27}\approx0.4515}.\]

