question:
An ice-cream shop offers three flavours (strawberry, chocolate, vanilla) and three decorations (chocolate sprinkles, strawberry sprinkles, rainbow sprinkles). An order is one flavour with one decoration, and costs the price of the flavour plus the price of the decoration. These orders are known to cost:
\begin{itemize}
\item chocolate with rainbow sprinkles: $\$10$; vanilla with chocolate sprinkles: $\$6$;
\item strawberry with rainbow sprinkles: $\$8$; strawberry with strawberry sprinkles: $\$4$;
\item chocolate with strawberry sprinkles: $\$6$; chocolate with chocolate sprinkles: $\$7$.
\end{itemize}
Also, the chocolate flavour and the chocolate sprinkles cost the same amount. (a) How much does strawberry with chocolate sprinkles cost? (b) How many different orders are possible?

solution:
Put the centre of the rhombus at the origin: $A=(-4,0)$, $C=(4,0)$, $B=(0,3)$, $D=(0,-3)$. Line $BC$ is $3x+4y=12$.

A circle tangent to lines $BC$ and $CD$ has its centre on a bisector of the angle between them: the line $AC$ or the line $x=4$. A centre $(4,k)$ would have radius $\dfrac{4|k|}{5}$, less than its distance $\sqrt{64+k^2}$ to $A$; impossible. So the centre is $I=(h,0)$, with radius $|h+4|$ (the distance to $A$) equal to the distance $\dfrac{|3h-12|}{5}$ to line $BC$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $-4<h<4$: $5(h+4)=12-3h$, so $h=-1$ and $r=3$. The tangency point on line $BC$ is $(0.8,2.4)$, which lies on segment $BC$ (and symmetrically on $CD$).
\item $h\ge4$: $5(h+4)=3h-12$ gives $h=-16$, a contradiction.
\item $h<-4$: $-5(h+4)=12-3h$ gives $h=-16$, $r=12$, but then the circle touches line $BC$ at $(-8.8,9.6)$, outside the segment.
\end{itemize}
So the radius is $\boxed{3}$.

