(The sketch shows $h=k=0$, $p=1$: $PF=PQ$ for every point $P$ of the curve.)

Let the tetrahedron be $ABCD$ with base $ABC$, and let $G$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $D$ to the plane $ABC$. The right triangles $DGA$, $DGB$, $DGC$ share the leg $DG$ and have equal hypotenuses $DA=DB=DC=s$, so $GA=GB=GC$: $G$ is the circumcentre of the equilateral triangle $ABC$, which is also its centroid. Hence $GA$ is $\dfrac23$ of a median:
\[GA=\dfrac23\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s=\dfrac{s}{\sqrt3}.\]
By the Pythagorean theorem in triangle $DGA$,
\[h^2=s^2-\dfrac{s^2}{3}=\dfrac{2s^2}{3},\qquad h=s\sqrt{\dfrac23}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt6}{3}s}.\]

\begin{asy}
