Let each face be a regular $p$-gon and let $q$ faces meet at each vertex, with $p\ge3$ and $q\ge3$. Each face angle is $\dfrac{180^\circ(p-2)}{p}$. At a vertex of a convex polyhedron the face angles add up to less than $360^\circ$ (if they added up to $360^\circ$ or more, the faces around the vertex could not fold up into a corner). Hence
\[q\cdot\dfrac{180(p-2)}{p}<360\iff q(p-2)<2p\iff(p-2)(q-2)<4.\]
With $p-2\ge1$ and $q-2\ge1$, the only possibilities are
\[(p,q)=(3,3),\ (3,4),\ (3,5),\ (4,3),\ (5,3).\]
For $p\ge6$ even three faces already give at least $3\cdot120^\circ=360^\circ$; for squares and pentagons only $q=3$ works ($4\cdot90^\circ=360^\circ$, $4\cdot108^\circ>360^\circ$), and for triangles $q\le5$ ($6\cdot60^\circ=360^\circ$).

The five pairs are realised by the tetrahedron $(3,3)$, the octahedron $(3,4)$, the icosahedron $(3,5)$, the cube $(4,3)$ and the dodecahedron $(5,3)$. So there are $\boxed{5}$ Platonic solids. $\blacksquare$

