From each vertex $V$ there is a segment to each of the other $n-1$ vertices. Two of these segments are sides (to the two neighbours of $V$), so $V$ is an endpoint of $n-3$ diagonals. Adding over the $n$ vertices gives $n(n-3)$, but each diagonal is counted twice, once from each endpoint. Hence there are $\dfrac{n(n-3)}{2}$ diagonals. $\blacksquare$

