Count the ordered selections of $k$ different elements in two ways.
\begin{itemize}
\item Directly: there are $n$ choices for the first element, $n-1$ for the second, and so on, giving $n(n-1)\cdots(n-k+1)=\dfrac{n!}{(n-k)!}$.
\item Via subsets: choose the $k$-element subset ($\dbinom nk$ ways) and then order it ($k!$ ways).
\end{itemize}
Hence $\dbinom nk\cdot k!=\dfrac{n!}{(n-k)!}$, which gives the formula. (For $k=0$ both sides equal $1$.) $\blacksquare$

The second point is on the same side as the first with probability $\dfrac14$, on an adjacent side with probability $\dfrac12$, and on the opposite side with probability $\dfrac14$.
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Same side.} The positions $x,y$ are uniform on $[0,3]$. The region $|x-y|>2$ consists of two right triangles with legs $1$, of total area $1$, so the probability is $1-\dfrac19=\dfrac89$.
\item \emph{Adjacent sides.} Let $x$, $y$ be the distances from the common corner, uniform on $[0,3]$. The distance is $\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\le2$ on a quarter disc of radius $2$, which lies inside the $3\times3$ square of outcomes. The probability is $\dfrac{\pi}{9}$.
\item \emph{Opposite sides.} The distance is at least $3$, so the probability is $0$.
\end{itemize}
In total,
\[\dfrac14\cdot\dfrac89+\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac\pi9=\boxed{\dfrac{4+\pi}{18}}\approx0.397.\]

