The answer is \textbf{no}. Fix any strategy of the hunter, that is, a rule choosing each move $B_n$ from the points $P_1,\dots,P_n$ reported so far. We describe moves of the rabbit and reports of the device (depending on this rule) for which the distance $d_n=A_nB_n$ exceeds $100$ after round $10^9$.

\emph{Lemma.} Suppose that after round $n$ the distance is $d=d_n\le100$. Then the rabbit and the device can act in the next $r=1000$ rounds so that $d_{n+r}^2>d^2+\dfrac12$.

\emph{Proof.} Take coordinates with $B_n=(0,0)$ and $A_n=(d,0)$, and let $\ell$ be the $x$-axis. Let $s=\sqrt{r^2-1}$ and $Y_{\pm}=(d+s,\pm1)$; both points are at distance $\sqrt{s^2+1}=r$ from $A_n$. The rabbit walks to one of them along a straight segment in $r$ steps of length $1$, so after $k$ steps it is at $\left(d+\dfrac{ks}{r},\pm\dfrac kr\right)$. In each of these rounds the device reports the point $\left(d+\dfrac{ks}{r},0\right)$ of $\ell$, which is within $\dfrac kr\le1$ of the rabbit. The reports, and the history before round $n$, are the same whichever of $Y_+$ and $Y_-$ the rabbit heads for, so the hunter's rule makes the same moves in both cases; let $H=(x,y)$ be her position after these $r$ rounds. The rabbit takes the target on the other side of $\ell$ from $H$: $Y_+$ if $y\le0$ and $Y_-$ if $y>0$. Then the vertical distance between $H$ and the rabbit is at least $1$.

