The hunter is within $r$ of the origin, so $x\le r$, and the horizontal distance from $H$ to the rabbit satisfies $d+s-x\ge d-\varepsilon$, where
\[\varepsilon=r-s=\dfrac{1}{r+s}<\dfrac1r=\dfrac{1}{1000}.\]
If $d\ge\varepsilon$, then
\[d_{n+r}^2\ge(d-\varepsilon)^2+1=d^2+1-2d\varepsilon+\varepsilon^2>d^2+1-\dfrac{2\cdot100}{1000}>d^2+\dfrac12.\]
If $d<\varepsilon$, then $d_{n+r}^2\ge1>\varepsilon^2+\dfrac12>d^2+\dfrac12$. $\square$

\emph{Getting far away.} We have $d_0=0$. Apply the lemma in consecutive blocks of $1000$ rounds for as long as the distance at the start of a block is at most $100$. Each block raises $d^2$ by more than $\dfrac12$, and $\dfrac{20001}{2}>100^2$, so this can continue for at most $20001$ blocks. Hence at some block boundary $N=1000m$ with $m\le20001$, so $N\le20001000<10^9$, we have $d_N>100$.

\emph{Staying far away.} From round $N$ on, in every round the rabbit moves $1$ unit directly away from the hunter (along the ray from $B_n$ through $A_n$), and the device reports the rabbit's true position. Then $A_{n+1}B_n=d_n+1$, and since the hunter moves only $1$ unit,
\[d_{n+1}=A_{n+1}B_{n+1}\ge A_{n+1}B_n-B_nB_{n+1}=d_n.\]
So the distance never decreases, and $d_{10^9}\ge d_N>100$.

Hence the hunter cannot guarantee a distance of at most $100$ after $10^9$ rounds: the answer is $\boxed{\text{No}}$. $\blacksquare$

\emph{At most one.} If a hyperbola with foci $F_1$, $F_2$ passes through $P$, its constant must be $2a=\left|PF_1-PF_2\right|$, so it is determined.

\emph{At least one.} Let $2a=\left|PF_1-PF_2\right|$. We need $0<2a<F_1F_2$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $2a=0$ means $PF_1=PF_2$, i.e. $P$ is on the perpendicular bisector, which is excluded.
\item By the triangle inequality in triangle $PF_1F_2$, $\left|PF_1-PF_2\right|<F_1F_2$ when $P$ is not on line $F_1F_2$. If $P$ is on the line strictly between $F_1$ and $F_2$, then $\left|PF_1-PF_2\right|<PF_1+PF_2=F_1F_2$ as well. (Equality $\left|PF_1-PF_2\right|=F_1F_2$ happens exactly for the excluded points of the line outside the open segment.)
\end{itemize}
So $\left\{X:\left|XF_1-XF_2\right|=2a\right\}$ is a hyperbola with foci $F_1$, $F_2$, and it contains $P$. Hence there is $\boxed{\text{exactly one}}$ such hyperbola. $\blacksquare$

