Let $\beta_1=\angle ABD$ and $\beta_2=\angle DBC$, and write $\angle A$, $\angle C$ for the angles of triangle $ABC$.

By the tangent--chord angle theorem in circle $BDC$, the angle between the tangent $DC_1$ and the chord $DB$ equals the inscribed angle in the alternate segment: $\angle BDC_1=\angle BCD=\angle C$. Since $\angle BDA=180^\circ-\angle BDC=\angle C+\beta_2>\angle C$, the ray $DC_1$ lies inside angle $BDA$, so $C_1$ is on segment $AB$. Similarly, in circle $ADB$, $\angle BDA_1=\angle BAD=\angle A$, and $A_1$ is on segment $BC$.

By the law of sines in triangles $BDC_1$ and $BDA_1$,
\[BC_1=\dfrac{BD\sin\angle C}{\sin(\beta_1+\angle C)},\qquad BA_1=\dfrac{BD\sin\angle A}{\sin(\beta_2+\angle A)}.\]
Since $(\beta_1+\angle C)+(\beta_2+\angle A)=180^\circ$, the two denominators are equal. Hence, using the law of sines in triangle $ABC$,
\[\dfrac{BC_1}{BA_1}=\dfrac{\sin\angle C}{\sin\angle A}=\dfrac{BA}{BC},\qquad\text{so}\qquad\dfrac{BC_1}{BA}=\dfrac{BA_1}{BC}.\]
Thus triangle $BC_1A_1$ is a scaled copy of triangle $BAC$ with centre $B$, and $A_1C_1\parallel AC$. $\blacksquare$

