solution:
A one-sixth arc of radius $2$ subtends $60^\circ$ at its centre, so its chord has length $2$: $TR=SR=2$. Also $TS=2$ (triangle $UTS$ is isosceles with a $60^\circ$ apex, hence equilateral). So $R$ is the reflection of $U$ in $TS$, and $UTRS$ is a rhombus made of two equilateral triangles of side $2$, with area $2\cdot\sqrt3=2\sqrt3$.

Each arc bends into the rhombus, cutting off a circular segment between the arc and its chord. A segment of a $60^\circ$ arc of radius $2$ has area
\[\dfrac16\pi\cdot2^2-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot2^2=\dfrac{2\pi}{3}-\sqrt3.\]
The region is the rhombus minus two such segments:
\[2\sqrt3-2\left(\dfrac{2\pi}3-\sqrt3\right)=\boxed{4\sqrt3-\dfrac{4\pi}{3}}.\]

