Name the elements of $V_3$: $0=\varnothing$, $1=\{0\}$, $c=\{1\}$, $2=\{0,1\}$. Then $V_4$ is the set of all $16$ subsets of $\{0,1,c,2\}$; it contains $0,1,c,2$ themselves (they are subsets of $\{0,1\}$), and $12$ other sets. An element $x\in V_5$ is any subset of $V_4$.

Let $T=x\cap\{0,1,c,2\}$. Every element $y$ of $x$ is a set of elements of $V_3$, so $y\subseteq x$ exactly when $y\subseteq T$. Hence $x$ is transitive exactly when
\begin{itemize}
\item every $t\in T$ satisfies $t\subseteq T$ ($T$ is ``closed''), and
\item every other element $y\in x$ (one of the $12$) satisfies $y\subseteq T$.
\end{itemize}
For a closed $T$, the second part of $x$ can be any set of subsets of $T$ that are not among $0,1,c,2$. Closedness means: $1\in T\Rightarrow0\in T$; $c\in T\Rightarrow1\in T$; $2\in T\Rightarrow0,1\in T$. The closed sets and their counts are:
\begin{itemize}
\item $T=\varnothing$, $\{0\}$, $\{0,1\}$: every subset of $T$ is one of $0,1,c,2$ (namely $\varnothing,\{0\},\{1\},\{0,1\}$), so $1$ set $x$ each;
\item $T=\{0,1,2\}$ and $T=\{0,1,c\}$: $T$ has $8$ subsets, $4$ of them special, so $2^4=16$ each;
\item $T=\{0,1,c,2\}$: $16-4=12$ free subsets, so $2^{12}=4096$.
\end{itemize}
The total is $1+1+1+16+16+4096=\boxed{4131}$.

Measure positions by the angle $\theta=\angle SOX$ from the south pole. Going $d$ units south lowers $\theta$ by $\dfrac dR=10^{-19}$, going north raises it by the same amount, and going east keeps $\theta$ and moves along a circle of latitude of circumference $2\pi R\sin\theta$.

Let the ant reach $B$ after the first leg and $C$ after the second. The third leg runs up the meridian of $C$ to the latitude of $A$. It ends at $A$ only if $A$ is the north pole (where all meridians meet) or $C$ is on the meridian of $A$, i.e. $C=B$. The north pole gives $\angle SOA=\pi$, which is huge after multiplying by $10^{26}$. Otherwise the eastward leg must be a whole number $k\ge1$ of laps:
\[2\pi R\sin\theta_B=\dfrac dk,\qquad\sin\theta_B=\dfrac{10^{-19}}{2\pi k}.\]
Since $\theta_A=\theta_B+10^{-19}\le\pi$, the angle $\theta_B$ is the small solution $\arcsin\dfrac{10^{-19}}{2\pi k}$. Hence
\[10^{26}\angle SOA=10^{7}+10^{26}\arcsin\dfrac{10^{-19}}{2\pi k}.\]
The second term is positive, and since $\arcsin t\le\dfrac\pi2t$ for $0\le t\le1$, it is at most $\dfrac{10^7}{4k}<1$ once $k>2.5\cdot10^6$. So the floor is at least $10^7$ for every allowed $A$, and equals $10^7$ for all large $k$. The smallest possible value is $\boxed{10000000}$.

