\emph{Lemma.} Every simple polygon with $n\ge4$ vertices has a diagonal.

Let $v$ be a lowest vertex (if several, the rightmost of them). The interior angle at $v$ is less than $180^\circ$. Let $u$ and $w$ be the neighbours of $v$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If no other vertex lies in the closed triangle $uvw$, then $uw$ is a diagonal. Indeed, a side of the polygon meeting the inside of the triangle would have to cross its boundary twice; it cannot cross the sides $vu$, $vw$, and a segment cannot cross the line $uw$ twice. (And $uw$ is not a side because $n\ge4$.)
\item Otherwise, among the vertices in triangle $uvw$ other than $u,v,w$, let $z$ be one farthest from line $uw$. Let $\ell$ be the line through $z$ parallel to $uw$ (grey in the sketch). The part of the triangle between $v$ and $\ell$ contains no vertex in its interior. A side crossing segment $vz$ would enter this part and would have to leave it through $\ell$ again, or end inside it; both are impossible. So $vz$ is a diagonal.
\end{itemize}

