\emph{Induction.} For $n=3$ the polygon is a triangle. For $n\ge4$, a diagonal splits the polygon into two simple polygons with $k+1$ and $n-k+1$ vertices ($2\le k\le n-2$), both fewer than $n$, which can be cut into $(k+1)-2$ and $(n-k+1)-2$ triangles by the induction hypothesis. Together this cuts the whole polygon into $n-2$ triangles.

\emph{Counting.} In any cutting by diagonals, the vertices of the triangles are vertices of the polygon, so the angles of the triangles exactly fill the interior angles of the polygon. Applied to the cutting just constructed, this shows that the interior angles of the polygon add up to $180^\circ(n-2)$. So for any such cutting into $t$ triangles, $180^\circ\cdot t=180^\circ(n-2)$ and $t=n-2$. $\blacksquare$

(Fermat's method.) For a positive integer $N$ let $r=\left(\dfrac ba\right)^{\dfrac1N}>1$, and divide $[a,b]$ at the points $a,ar,ar^2,\dots,ar^N=b$, which form a geometric sequence. Since $x^p$ is non-decreasing, on $\left[ar^k,ar^{k+1}\right]$ it lies between $\left(ar^k\right)^p$ and $\left(ar^{k+1}\right)^p$. So the area $A$ lies between the lower and upper rectangle sums
\[L_N=\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\left(ar^{k+1}-ar^k\right)\left(ar^k\right)^p=a^{p+1}(r-1)\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}r^{k(p+1)}=a^{p+1}(r-1)\cdot\dfrac{r^{N(p+1)}-1}{r^{p+1}-1}\]
and $U_N=r^pL_N$. Using $a^{p+1}r^{N(p+1)}=b^{p+1}$,
\[L_N=\left(b^{p+1}-a^{p+1}\right)\cdot\dfrac{r-1}{r^{p+1}-1}.\]
As $N\to\infty$ we have $r\to1$, so $\dfrac{r-1}{r^{p+1}-1}\to\dfrac1{p+1}$ (the given limit with $q=p+1$) and $r^p\to1$. Both $L_N$ and $U_N$ tend to $\dfrac{b^{p+1}-a^{p+1}}{p+1}$, and $L_N\le A\le U_N$ for every $N$. Hence
\[A=\dfrac{b^{p+1}-a^{p+1}}{p+1}.\ \blacksquare\]
(For an integer $p$ no limit theorem is needed: $\dfrac{r^{p+1}-1}{r-1}=1+r+\cdots+r^p\to p+1$.)

