question:
Points $P,A,B,C,D$ in the plane satisfy $\triangle PAB\sim\triangle PCD$ (with $P\leftrightarrow P$, $A\leftrightarrow C$, $B\leftrightarrow D$).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that if the two triangles have the same orientation, then $\triangle PAC\sim\triangle PBD$.
\item[(b)] Show by an example that the conclusion can fail if the triangles have opposite orientations.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Use complex numbers with $P=0$.

\textbf{(a)} A similarity preserving orientation and fixing $P=0$ is $z\mapsto\lambda z$ for a nonzero complex number $\lambda$ (a rotation about $P$ followed by a scaling). So $C=\lambda A$ and $D=\lambda B$. Let $\mu=\dfrac BA$, so $B=\mu A$. Then
\[D=\lambda B=\lambda\mu A=\mu(\lambda A)=\mu C.\]
So the map $z\mapsto\mu z$, also a rotation and scaling about $P$, sends $P,A,C$ to $P,B,D$. Hence $\triangle PAC\sim\triangle PBD$. $\blacksquare$

