\textbf{(b)} Take $P=0$, $A=1$, $B=1+2i$, and reflect in the line $y=x$, i.e. apply $z\mapsto i\,\overline z$: $C=i$ and $D=2+i$. Triangle $PCD$ is the mirror image of triangle $PAB$, so the two are similar (even congruent) with opposite orientations. But triangle $PAC$ has sides $PA=PC=1$, $AC=\sqrt2$, while triangle $PBD$ has sides $PB=PD=\sqrt5$, $BD=\sqrt2$. Both are isosceles with apex $P$, and $\dfrac{AC}{PA}=\sqrt2\ne\dfrac{BD}{PB}=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{\sqrt5}$, so they are not similar. $\blacksquare$

