\emph{One-to-one.} If $t(X)=t(Y)$, then $X+\vec v=Y+\vec v$, and subtracting $\vec v$ gives $X=Y$.

\emph{Onto.} Given any point $Y$, the point $X=Y-\vec v$ satisfies $t(X)=Y$.

So $t$ is a bijection of the plane, and its inverse is the translation by $-\vec v$. $\blacksquare$

