Choose coordinates. Line $AB$ is the zero set of a non-constant linear function $L(x,y)=\alpha x+\beta y+\gamma$, and replacing $L$ by $-L$ if needed, $\mathcal H=\{L>0\}$. For points $X,Y$ and $0\le t\le1$,
\[L\bigl((1-t)X+tY\bigr)=(1-t)L(X)+tL(Y),\]
because $L$ is linear plus a constant.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $L(X)>0$ and $L(Y)>0$, the right side is positive for all $t$, so $\mathcal H$ is convex.
\item If $L(X)\ge0$ and $L(Y)\ge0$, it is nonnegative, so $\mathcal H\cup\overleftrightarrow{AB}=\{L\ge0\}$ is convex.
\item For $\mathcal H\cup\overline{AB}$: if $X,Y\in\mathcal H$, use the first case. If $X,Y\in\overline{AB}$, then $\overline{XY}\subseteq\overline{AB}$. If $X\in\mathcal H$ and $Y\in\overline{AB}$, then $L(Y)=0$ and $L\bigl((1-t)X+tY\bigr)=(1-t)L(X)>0$ for $t<1$; so every point of $\overline{XY}$ other than $Y$ is in $\mathcal H$, and $Y\in\overline{AB}$.
\end{itemize}
Hence all three sets are convex. $\blacksquare$

