question:
A student writes $\sin\theta=\dfrac{\text{opposite leg}}{\text{adjacent leg}}$ for an acute angle $\theta$ of a right triangle.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Correct the definitions of $\sin\theta$, $\cos\theta$ and $\tan\theta$, and say which side is which.
\item[(b)] A right triangle has legs $3$ and $4$, and $\theta$ is the angle opposite the leg of length $3$. Find $\sin\theta$, $\cos\theta$ and $\tan\theta$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Relative to the acute angle $\theta$: the \emph{hypotenuse} is the side opposite the right angle; the \emph{opposite} leg is the leg not touching $\theta$; the \emph{adjacent} leg is the leg that forms $\theta$ together with the hypotenuse. Then
\[\sin\theta=\dfrac{\text{opposite}}{\text{hypotenuse}},\qquad\cos\theta=\dfrac{\text{adjacent}}{\text{hypotenuse}},\qquad\tan\theta=\dfrac{\text{opposite}}{\text{adjacent}}.\]
The student's ratio is $\tan\theta$, not $\sin\theta$.

\textbf{(b)} The hypotenuse is $\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5$, so
\[\sin\theta=\boxed{\dfrac35},\qquad\cos\theta=\boxed{\dfrac45},\qquad\tan\theta=\boxed{\dfrac34}.\]

