There are infinitely many perfect squares greater than $4$ and infinitely many greater than $9$, yet it seems that $4$ has ``more'' squares above it than $9$ does.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find a one-to-one correspondence between the set of perfect squares greater than $4$ and the set of perfect squares greater than $9$.
\item[(b)] Explain in what sense the two sets have the same size, and in what sense the first is bigger.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Extend four alternate sides of the octagon to form a square. Each corner of the square is cut off along an isosceles right triangle whose hypotenuse is a side of the octagon, so its legs are $\dfrac{x}{\sqrt2}$. The square has side $x+2\cdot\dfrac{x}{\sqrt2}=x\left(1+\sqrt2\right)$. Hence
\[[\text{octagon}]=x^2\left(1+\sqrt2\right)^2-4\cdot\dfrac12\cdot\left(\dfrac{x}{\sqrt2}\right)^2=x^2\left(3+2\sqrt2\right)-x^2=\boxed{2\left(1+\sqrt2\right)x^2}.\]

