A circle tangent to both rays from $A$ has its centre on the bisector of angle $A$, which is the altitude $AM$ to $BC$ ($M$ the midpoint of $BC$). The two circles are the incircle (radius $r$, centre $I$) and the excircle opposite $A$ (radius $r_a$, centre $I_a$), both touching $BC$ at $M$.

For a circle of radius $\rho$ tangent to both rays, the distance from $A$ to its centre is $\dfrac{\rho}{\sin30^\circ}=2\rho$.
\begin{itemize}
\item Incircle: $AM=AI+IM=2r+r=3r$.
\item Excircle: $AM=AI_a-I_aM=2r_a-r_a=r_a$.
\end{itemize}
So $r_a=3r$, and the ratio is $\boxed{3}$.

