question:
Here is a method for factoring $ax^2+bx+c$, where $a>0$, $b$, $c$ are integers: factor $x^2+bx+ac=(x-r)(x-s)$ with integers $r,s$; then replace $x-r$ by $\dfrac agx-\dfrac rg$, where $g=\gcd(a,r)$, and $x-s$ by $\dfrac ahx-\dfrac sh$, where $h=\gcd(a,s)$; the product of the two new factors is the answer.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Use the method to factor $3x^2-17x+24$.
\item[(b)] Prove that the method always gives a correct factorisation when $\gcd(a,b,c)=1$.
\item[(c)] Show that the method fails for $2x^2+4x+2$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Take the common perimeter to be $12$. The square has side $3$ and area $9$. The hexagon has side $2$ and consists of six equilateral triangles of side $2$, so its area is $6\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot2^2=6\sqrt3$. The ratio is
\[\dfrac{9}{6\sqrt3}=\dfrac{3}{2\sqrt3}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(0,3)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));label("$3$",(1.5,0),S);
pair c=(6,1.5);pair[] v;for(int i=0;i<6;++i)v[i]=c+2*dir(60*i);
draw(v[0]--v[1]--v[2]--v[3]--v[4]--v[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
for(int i=0;i<6;++i)draw(c--v[i],dashed);label("$2$",(v[4]+v[5])/2,S);
\end{asy}

question:
A circle has centre at the origin $O$ and radius $r$. A point $P$ starts at $(r,0)$ and moves counterclockwise along the circle through a central angle $\theta$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why $P=(r\cos\theta,\ r\sin\theta)$, whether $\theta$ is measured in degrees or in radians.
\item[(b)] Find the exact coordinates of $P$ when $r=6$ and $\theta=150^\circ$, and when $r=4$ and $\theta=\dfrac{5\pi}{4}$ radians.
\item[(c)] For $r=6$ and $\theta=140^\circ$, express the coordinates of $P$ using the reference angle $40^\circ$, and give them to two decimal places.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} If $0^\circ<\theta<90^\circ$, drop the perpendicular $PQ$ to the $x$-axis. The right triangle $OQP$ has hypotenuse $OP=r$ and angle $\theta$ at $O$, so $OQ=r\cos\theta$ and $QP=r\sin\theta$, and $P=(r\cos\theta,r\sin\theta)$. For a general angle, $\cos\theta$ and $\sin\theta$ are \emph{defined} as the coordinates of the point of the unit circle reached from $(1,0)$ by turning counterclockwise through $\theta$. The dilation with centre $O$ and factor $r$ takes the unit circle to our circle and that point to $P$, so again $P=(r\cos\theta,r\sin\theta)$. Degrees and radians are just two units for the same angle ($180^\circ=\pi$); the formula holds in either unit, as long as $\cos$ and $\sin$ are evaluated in that unit.

In practice one uses the reference angle $\alpha$ between $OP$ and the $x$-axis: then $|x|=r\cos\alpha$ and $|y|=r\sin\alpha$ from the right triangle $OQP$, and the signs come from the quadrant of $P$.

\textbf{(b)} For $\theta=150^\circ$ the reference angle is $30^\circ$ and $P$ is in the second quadrant:
\[P=\left(-6\cos30^\circ,\ 6\sin30^\circ\right)=\boxed{\left(-3\sqrt3,\ 3\right)}.\]
For $\theta=\dfrac{5\pi}{4}=225^\circ$ the reference angle is $45^\circ$ and $P$ is in the third quadrant:
\[P=\left(-4\cos45^\circ,\ -4\sin45^\circ\right)=\boxed{\left(-2\sqrt2,\ -2\sqrt2\right)}.\]

\textbf{(c)} For $\theta=140^\circ$ the reference angle is $180^\circ-140^\circ=40^\circ$ and $P$ is in the second quadrant (see the sketch):
\[P=\left(-6\cos40^\circ,\ 6\sin40^\circ\right)\approx\boxed{(-4.60,\ 3.86)}.\]
This agrees with $\cos140^\circ=-\cos40^\circ$ and $\sin140^\circ=\sin40^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=2.4;pair O=(0,0);pair P=r*dir(140),Q=(P.x,0),U=(r,0);
draw((-3.1,0)--(3.2,0),gray(0.6),Arrow(4));draw((0,-2.8)--(0,3.0),gray(0.6),Arrow(4));
draw(circle(O,r),linewidth(0.8));
draw(O--P,linewidth(0.8));draw(P--Q,dashed);
draw(Q+(0.18,0)--Q+(0.18,0.18)--Q+(0,0.18));
draw(arc(O,0.42,0,140),blue,Arrow(4));label("$\theta$",0.62*dir(55),blue+fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc(O,0.95,140,180));label("$40^\circ$",1.3*dir(163),fontsize(7pt));
dot(O);dot(P);dot(Q);dot(U);
label("$O$",O,SE,fontsize(8pt));label("$P$",P,NW);label("$Q$",Q,S,fontsize(8pt));label("$(r,0)$",U,SE,fontsize(8pt));label("$r$",P/2,NE,fontsize(8pt));
label("$x$",(3.2,0),E,fontsize(8pt));label("$y$",(0,3.0),N,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Let $O$ be the circumcentre and let $X'$ be the point diametrically opposite $C$ on the circumcircle.

Since $CX'$ is a diameter, $\angle CAX'=90^\circ$, so $X'A\perp AC$. Also $BH\perp AC$ (altitude from $B$). Hence $X'A\parallel BH$. In the same way $\angle CBX'=90^\circ$ gives $X'B\perp BC$, and $AH\perp BC$, so $X'B\parallel AH$. Therefore $AHBX'$ is a parallelogram (it is not degenerate: $H$ is inside the triangle, while $X'$ lies on the arc $AB$ not containing $C$, and $X'\ne A,B$ because no angle of the triangle is right).

The diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other, so the midpoint $N$ of $AB$ is also the midpoint of $HX'$. Thus $X'$ lies on the ray from $H$ through $N$, beyond $N$, with $NX'=HN$. Since the triangle is acute, $H$ is inside the circumcircle, so this ray meets the circle at exactly one point. Hence $X=X'$ and $NX=HN$. $\blacksquare$

\begin{asy}
