draw(arc((0,0),0.75,0,20),red+linewidth(0.8));label("$\le22.5^\circ$",0.8*dir(10),E,red+fontsize(8pt));
for(int k=1;k<8;++k)draw(arc((0,0),0.45,th[k-1],th[k]),gray);
draw(arc((0,0),0.45,160,180),gray);
\end{asy}

Represent the pair $(a,b)$ as a point of the square $[0,99]^2$, which has area $99^2$. The pairs with $|a-b|>15$ form two right isosceles triangles in the corners, each with legs $99-15=84$, of total area $84^2$. So the probability is
\[1-\dfrac{84^2}{99^2}=1-\dfrac{7056}{9801}=\dfrac{2745}{9801}=\boxed{\dfrac{305}{1089}}\approx0.280.\]
(If instead both numbers are integers from $0$ to $99$, chosen uniformly and independently, the number of pairs with $|a-b|\le15$ is $100+2(99+98+\cdots+85)=2860$, and the probability is $\dfrac{2860}{10000}=\dfrac{143}{500}$.)

