\emph{A triangle.} Since $a^2+ab+b^2=a^2+b^2-2ab\cos120^\circ$, take a point $P$ and three rays from $P$ at $120^\circ$ to each other, and mark $X$, $Y$, $Z$ on them with $PX=a$, $PY=b$, $PZ=c$. By the law of cosines, $XY=5$, $YZ=7$ and $ZX=8$. The three angles at $P$ are $120^\circ$, so $P$ lies inside triangle $XYZ$ and
\[[XYZ]=[PXY]+[PYZ]+[PZX]=\dfrac12\sin120^\circ\,(ab+bc+ca)=\dfrac{\sqrt3}4(ab+bc+ca).\]
By Heron's formula with $s=10$, $[XYZ]=\sqrt{10\cdot5\cdot3\cdot2}=10\sqrt3$. Hence $ab+bc+ca=40$.

\emph{Finish.} Adding the three equations, $2\left(a^2+b^2+c^2\right)+(ab+bc+ca)=138$, so $a^2+b^2+c^2=49$. Therefore
\[(a+b+c)^2=a^2+b^2+c^2+2(ab+bc+ca)=49+80=\boxed{129}.\]

