Let $x,y,z\ge1$ be the numbers of $2\times3$, $4\times5$ and $6\times7$ pieces. Comparing areas, $6x+20y+42z=184$, i.e. $3x+10y+21z=92$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $z=1$: $3x+10y=71$, so $y\equiv2\pmod3$: $(x,y)=(17,2)$ or $(7,5)$, totals $20$ and $13$.
\item $z=2$: $3x+10y=50$: $(x,y)=(10,2)$, total $14$ ($y=5$ would give $x=0$).
\item $z=3$: $3x+10y=29$: $(x,y)=(3,2)$, total $8$.
\item $z\ge4$ leaves $3x+10y\le8$, impossible.
\end{itemize}
\emph{Total $8$ is impossible.} Every piece has both sides at least $2$. Take a $6\times7$ piece $P$. A piece covering a point directly above or below $P$ (strictly inside $P$'s width) cannot overlap $P$, so it lies entirely in the gap between $P$ and the top or the bottom side of the big rectangle. If $P$ were $7$ tall, these two gaps would have total height $1$, too small for any piece. So $P$ lies $7$ wide and $6$ tall, and its two gaps have total height $2$. A gap of height strictly between $0$ and $2$ cannot be filled, so $P$ touches the top or the bottom side, and the $7\times2$ strip on its other side is covered by pieces of height exactly $2$, that is, by $2\times3$ pieces lying $3$ wide. Two $6\times7$ pieces cannot overlap horizontally ($6+6>8$), so the three strips have total width $21$, while each $2\times3$ piece covers a width of at most $3$ of them. So at least $7$ such pieces are needed, but $x=3$. Contradiction.

\emph{Total $13$ is possible}, with $7$, $5$ and $1$ pieces, as shown. So the minimum is $\boxed{13}$.

