Factor $168=2^3\cdot3\cdot7$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $p=2$: $p^6-1=63$ is odd. $p=3$: $p^6-1\equiv-1\pmod3$. $p=7$: $p^6-1\equiv-1\pmod7$. So these three primes fail.
\item Any other prime $p$ is odd, so $p^2\equiv1\pmod8$ and $8\mid p^2-1\mid p^6-1$. Also $3\nmid p$, so $p^2\equiv1\pmod3$ and $3\mid p^6-1$. Finally $7\nmid p$, so $p^6\equiv1\pmod7$ by Fermat's little theorem.
\end{itemize}
Since $8$, $3$ and $7$ are pairwise coprime, $168\mid p^6-1$ exactly for $\boxed{\text{all primes }p\ne2,3,7}$.

