Write $a=BC$, $b=CA$, $c=AB$. The perimeters are $PA+PB+c$, $PB+PC+a$ and $PC+PA+b$, and they are equal exactly when
\[PA-a=PB-b=PC-c.\qquad(*)\]

Take $AB=AC=3$ and $BC=4$, and suppose an interior point $P$ satisfies $(*)$: $PA-4=PB-3=PC-3$, so $PB+PC=2PA-2$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $P$ is not on line $BC$, so the triangle inequality in $PBC$ gives $PB+PC>BC=4$, hence $PA>3$.
\item Extend $AP$ beyond $P$ to meet $BC$ at $Q$. Then $PA<QA$, and along the segment $BC$ the distance to $A$ is largest at an endpoint, so $QA\le\max(AB,AC)=3$. Hence $PA<3$.
\end{itemize}
This contradiction shows that no such interior point exists for this triangle. $\blacksquare$

(Such a point does exist for some triangles, e.g. the centre of an equilateral triangle. For the triangle above, with $B=(-2,0)$, $C=(2,0)$, $A=(0,\sqrt5)$, the only point satisfying $(*)$ is $P=(0,-1)$, outside the triangle: $PA=1+\sqrt5$, $PB=PC=\sqrt5$, and all three perimeters equal $4+2\sqrt5$.)
