A route goes: top face $\to$ some faces of the top ring $\to$ some faces of the bottom ring $\to$ bottom face. It never returns to the top face, and it cannot go back up from the bottom ring.
\begin{itemize}
\item Enter the top ring at any of its $5$ faces. Since faces cannot be revisited, the route then stays put or walks $1,2,3$ or $4$ steps around the ring in one of the two directions: $1+2\cdot4=9$ possibilities.
\item From the last top-ring face, go down to one of its $2$ neighbours in the bottom ring.
\item In the bottom ring there are again $9$ possibilities (stay, or walk $1$ to $4$ steps in either direction), and then the move to the bottom face.
\end{itemize}
Hence the number of routes is $5\cdot9\cdot2\cdot9=\boxed{810}$.

Let the liquid depths be $h_1$ (narrow cone) and $h_2$ (wide cone). The liquid in each cone is itself a cone, and the volumes are equal:
\[\dfrac13\pi\cdot3^2h_1=\dfrac13\pi\cdot6^2h_2,\qquad\text{so}\qquad h_1=4h_2.\]
Call the common volume $V$. In a given cone, liquid of depth $h$ is similar to the original liquid cone, so its volume is $V\left(\dfrac{h}{h_i}\right)^3$. After the marble (volume $\dfrac43\pi$) is added, the new depth $h_i'$ satisfies
\[V\left(\dfrac{h_i'}{h_i}\right)^3=V+\dfrac43\pi,\qquad\text{so}\qquad h_i'=\lambda h_i\quad\text{with}\quad\lambda=\sqrt[3]{1+\dfrac{4\pi}{3V}},\]
the \emph{same} factor $\lambda$ for both cones. The rises are $(\lambda-1)h_1$ and $(\lambda-1)h_2$, so their ratio is $\dfrac{h_1}{h_2}=\boxed{4:1}$.
