By Vieta's formulas the six roots (with multiplicity) have sum $10$ and product $16$. The product $16=2^4$ forces every root to be a power of $2$. Six powers of $2$ with product $2^4$ and sum $10$: the options are $\{1,1,2,2,2,2\}$ (sum $10$), $\{1,1,1,2,2,4\}$ (sum $11$), $\{1,1,1,1,4,4\}$ (sum $12$), $\{1,1,1,1,2,8\}$ (sum $14$) and $\{1,1,1,1,1,16\}$ (sum $21$). So the roots are $1,1,2,2,2,2$.

$B=-e_3$, where $e_3$ is the sum of all products of three roots:
\begin{itemize}
\item two $1$s and one $2$: $4$ products equal to $2$;
\item one $1$ and two $2$s: $2\cdot6=12$ products equal to $4$;
\item three $2$s: $4$ products equal to $8$.
\end{itemize}
So $e_3=8+48+32=88$ and $B=\boxed{-88}$.

Since $CB=CD$, triangle $BCD$ is isosceles and the median $CP$ is perpendicular to $BD$. Also $AD\perp BD$, so $AD\parallel CP$.

First, $O$ lies between $D$ and $P$: if it were between $P$ and $B$, then from $AB\parallel CD$ we would get $\dfrac{AO}{OC}=\dfrac{BO}{OD}<1$, while from $AD\parallel CP$ we would get $\dfrac{AO}{OC}=\dfrac{DO}{OP}>1$. So let $DO=x$; then $DP=PB=x+11$ and $BO=x+22$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $AB\parallel CD$: triangles $OAB$ and $OCD$ are similar, so $\dfrac{AO}{OC}=\dfrac{BO}{DO}=\dfrac{x+22}{x}$.
\item $AD\parallel CP$: triangles $OAD$ and $OCP$ are similar, so $\dfrac{AO}{OC}=\dfrac{DO}{PO}=\dfrac{x}{11}$.
\end{itemize}
Hence $x^2=11x+242$, i.e. $(x-22)(x+11)=0$, so $x=22$ and $DP=33$. In right triangle $CPD$, $CP=\sqrt{43^2-33^2}=\sqrt{760}=2\sqrt{190}$. Finally $\dfrac{AD}{CP}=\dfrac{DO}{OP}=2$, so $AD=4\sqrt{190}$ and $m+n=\boxed{194}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=sqrt(190);
pair D=(0,0),B=(66,0),P=(33,0),O=(22,0),C=(33,2*s),A=(0,-4*s);
real t=-degrees(atan2(2*s,33));
transform T=rotate(t);
pair d=T*D,b=T*B,p=T*P,o=T*O,c=T*C,a=T*A;
draw(a--b--c--d--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(a--c);draw(b--d);draw(c--p,dashed);
pair u=unit(b-d),v=unit(a-d);draw(d+3*u--d+3*u+3*v--d+3*v);
pair w=unit(c-p);draw(p+3*u--p+3*u+3*w--p+3*w);
dot(o);dot(p);
label("$A$",a,SW);label("$B$",b,SE);label("$C$",c,NE);label("$D$",d,NW);
label("$O$",o,S);label("$P$",p,N);
label("$43$",(b+c)/2,E);label("$43$",(c+d)/2,N);
\end{asy}

ALOHOMORA has $9$ letters: A twice, O three times, and L, H, M, R once each. The vowels are A, A, O, O, O.
\begin{itemize}
\item All arrangements: $\dfrac{9!}{2!\,3!}=30240$.
\item Vowels together: treat the block of vowels as one unit with the $4$ consonants, giving $5!=120$ orders, and arrange the block internally in $\dfrac{5!}{2!\,3!}=10$ ways: $1200$ arrangements.
\end{itemize}
The probability is $\dfrac{1200}{30240}=\boxed{\dfrac{5}{126}}$.

For each $k$ he hides $\left\lfloor\dfrac{100}{k}\right\rfloor$ carrots, so the total is $\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{50}\left\lfloor\dfrac{100}{k}\right\rfloor$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $k=1,\dots,10$: $100+50+33+25+20+16+14+12+11+10=291$;
\item $k=11,\dots,20$: $9+8+7+7+6+6+5+5+5+5=63$;
\item $k=21,\dots,25$: $5\cdot4=20$; $k=26,\dots,33$: $8\cdot3=24$; $k=34,\dots,50$: $17\cdot2=34$.
\end{itemize}
The total is $291+63+20+24+34=\boxed{432}$.

(Check: hole $n$ receives one carrot for each divisor of $n$ that is at most $50$. Summing the divisor counts of $1,\dots,100$ gives $\sum_{k=1}^{100}\left\lfloor\dfrac{100}k\right\rfloor=482$, and the $50$ holes $51,\dots,100$ each miss exactly one divisor, themselves: $482-50=432$.)

question:
A student claims that the real numbers in the interval $[0,1)$ can be matched one-to-one with the positive integers, contrary to Cantor's theorem. The argument has two parts.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] ``Cantor's diagonal argument assumes that a real number has countably many decimal digits, but a real number has uncountably many digits.''
\item[(ii)] ``Write $x=0.d_1d_2d_3\dots$ in decimal and cut the string of digits at every digit $9$. Each piece contains no $9$, so it can be read as a base-$9$ numeral; record its value and also its number of leading zeros (so that pieces such as $01$ and $1$ are not confused). This turns $x$ into a list $a_1,a_2,\dots,a_m$ of nonnegative integers. By the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic the list can be recovered from the positive integer $2^{a_1+1}3^{a_2+1}5^{a_3+1}\cdots p_m^{a_m+1}$, where $p_m$ is the $m$th prime. Different numbers $x$ give different lists, hence different positive integers.''
\end{enumerate}
Explain what is wrong with each part.

solution:
The points may lie in the order $B,D,E,C$ or $B,E,D,C$ (both shown below; the figures are drawn with $AB=AC$, as the result says).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(-3,0),C=(3,0),A=(0,4),D=(-1,0),Ep=(1,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));draw(A--D);draw(A--Ep);
draw(arc(A,0.75,degrees(B-A),degrees(D-A)));draw(arc(A,0.75,degrees(Ep-A),degrees(C-A)));
dot(D);dot(Ep);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$E$",Ep,S);
label("$x$",(B+D)/2,S);label("$x$",(Ep+C)/2,S);
pair s=(8,0);
pair B2=B+s,C2=C+s,A2=A+s,D2=(1,0)+s,E2=(-1,0)+s;
draw(A2--B2--C2--cycle,linewidth(0.9));draw(A2--D2);draw(A2--E2);
draw(arc(A2,0.65,degrees(B2-A2),degrees(D2-A2)));draw(arc(A2,0.95,degrees(E2-A2),degrees(C2-A2)));
dot(D2);dot(E2);
label("$A$",A2,N);label("$B$",B2,SW);label("$C$",C2,SE);label("$D$",D2,S);label("$E$",E2,S);
draw((B2+(0,-0.75))--(D2+(0,-0.75)),Arrows(TeXHead));label("$x$",(B2+D2)/2+(0,-0.75),S);
draw((E2+(0,-1.55))--(C2+(0,-1.55)),Arrows(TeXHead));label("$x$",(E2+C2)/2+(0,-1.55),S);
\end{asy}

\emph{Step 1.} $BE=CD$ and $\angle BAE=\angle CAD$. In the order $B,D,E,C$: $BE=BD+DE=CE+DE=CD$, and $\angle BAE=\angle BAD+\angle DAE=\angle CAE+\angle EAD=\angle CAD$. In the order $B,E,D,C$ the same holds with differences: $BE=BD-ED=CE-ED=CD$ and $\angle BAE=\angle BAD-\angle EAD=\angle CAE-\angle DAE=\angle CAD$.

\emph{Step 2.} Triangles with equal bases on line $BC$ and common apex $A$ have equal areas. Using $[XYZ]=\dfrac12\,XY\cdot XZ\sin\angle YXZ$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $[ABD]=[ACE]$ (as $BD=CE$): $\dfrac12AB\cdot AD\sin\angle BAD=\dfrac12AC\cdot AE\sin\angle CAE$, so $AB\cdot AD=AC\cdot AE$.
\item $[ABE]=[ACD]$ (as $BE=CD$): $\dfrac12AB\cdot AE\sin\angle BAE=\dfrac12AC\cdot AD\sin\angle CAD$, so $AB\cdot AE=AC\cdot AD$.
\end{itemize}
Multiplying the two equations, $AB^2\cdot AD\cdot AE=AC^2\cdot AD\cdot AE$, so $AB^2=AC^2$ and $AB=AC$. $\blacksquare$

question:
A unit hexagram is composed of a regular hexagon of side length $1$ and its $6$ equilateral triangular extensions, one on each side, as shown in the diagram. What is the ratio of the area of the extensions to the area of the original hexagon?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V;for(int k=0;k<6;++k)V[k]=dir(60k);
for(int k=0;k<6;++k) filldraw(V[k]--(V[k]+V[(k+1)%6])--V[(k+1)%6]--cycle,lightgray,linewidth(0.8));
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
label("$1$",(V[0]+V[1])/2,SW);
\end{asy}

$\textbf{(A) }1:1\qquad\textbf{(B) }6:5\qquad\textbf{(C) }3:2\qquad\textbf{(D) }2:1\qquad\textbf{(E) }3:1$

solution:
Join the centre $O$ of the hexagon to its six vertices. This splits the hexagon into $6$ equilateral triangles of side $1$ (each has two sides equal to the circumradius $1$ and a $60^\circ$ angle at $O$). Each extension is also an equilateral triangle of side $1$. So the extensions and the hexagon both consist of $6$ congruent triangles, and the ratio is $\boxed{\textbf{(A) }1:1}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V;for(int k=0;k<6;++k)V[k]=dir(60k);
for(int k=0;k<6;++k) filldraw(V[k]--(V[k]+V[(k+1)%6])--V[(k+1)%6]--cycle,lightgray,linewidth(0.8));
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
for(int k=0;k<6;++k) draw((0,0)--V[k],dashed);
dot((0,0));label("$O$",(0,0),S);
label("$1$",(V[0]+V[1])/2,SW);label("$1$",V[0]/2,S);
\end{asy}

question:
Let $x=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{20202020}}$, written as a decimal.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the first four digits after the decimal point.
\item[(b)] Find the first four nonzero digits.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
$n=1$ is a root, and dividing gives
\[n^3-8n^2+20n-13=(n-1)(n^2-7n+13).\]
The second factor is $\left(n-\dfrac72\right)^2+\dfrac34>0$. For $n=1$ the value is $0$. For $n\ge2$ both factors are positive integers, and a prime needs one of them to equal $1$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $n-1=1$: $n=2$, value $1\cdot3=3$, prime;
\item $n^2-7n+13=1$: $(n-3)(n-4)=0$, so $n=3$ (value $2\cdot1=2$) or $n=4$ (value $3\cdot1=3$), both prime.
\end{itemize}
So there are $\boxed{3}$ such $n$.

question:
Replace each $\square$ by one of $+,-,\times,\div$, inserting brackets where needed (exponents, logarithms, factorials and joining digits are not allowed), so that each equation is true.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $6\square6\square6\square6\square6\square6=38$
\item[(b)] $5\square5\square5\square5\square5\square5=-6$
\end{enumerate}

solution:
$2006=2\cdot17\cdot59$. The factor $d=x+2\ge3$ is a divisor of $2006$, and then $x+y-5=\dfrac{2006}d$, so
\[x=d-2,\qquad y=\dfrac{2006}{d}+7-d.\]
The divisors $d\ge3$ are $17,34,59,118,1003,2006$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $d=17$: $x=15$, $y=118+7-17=108$;
\item $d=34$: $x=32$, $y=59+7-34=32$;
\item $d=59$: $y=34+7-59=-18<0$, and larger $d$ make $y$ even smaller.
\end{itemize}
So $\boxed{(x,y)=(15,108)\text{ or }(32,32)}$. Check: $17\cdot118=2006$ and $34\cdot59=2006$.

question:
In the figure, a diameter of each of the two smaller circles is a radius of the larger circle, and these two radii together form a diameter of the larger circle. The two smaller circles have a combined area of $1$ square unit. What is the area of the shaded region, which lies inside the larger circle and outside both smaller circles?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill(circle((0,0),2),lightgray);
filldraw(circle((-1,0),1),white,linewidth(0.8));filldraw(circle((1,0),1),white,linewidth(0.8));
draw(circle((0,0),2),linewidth(0.9));
\end{asy}

$\textbf{(A) }\dfrac14\qquad\textbf{(B) }\dfrac13\qquad\textbf{(C) }\dfrac12\qquad\textbf{(D) }1\qquad\textbf{(E) }\dfrac\pi2$

solution:
Let the larger circle have radius $R$. Each smaller circle has diameter $R$, i.e. radius $\dfrac R2$, so the two together have area $2\pi\left(\dfrac R2\right)^2=\dfrac{\pi R^2}{2}=1$. Hence the larger circle has area $\pi R^2=2$. The smaller circles lie inside it and overlap only at the centre, so the shaded area is $2-1=\boxed{\textbf{(D) }1}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill(circle((0,0),2),lightgray);
filldraw(circle((-1,0),1),white,linewidth(0.8));filldraw(circle((1,0),1),white,linewidth(0.8));
draw(circle((0,0),2),linewidth(0.9));
draw((-2,0)--(2,0),dashed);dot((0,0));dot((-1,0));dot((1,0));
label("$O$",(0,0),S);
draw((0,0)--2*dir(70),dashed);label("$R$",1.55*dir(70),E);
label("$\dfrac R2$",(1.5,0),S);label("$\dfrac R2$",(-1.5,0),S);
\end{asy}

question:
Thirteen circles of radius $1$ lie inside a larger circle, as in the figure. One small circle is at the centre; six surround it, each tangent to it and to its two neighbours; and six more each lie in the gap between two neighbouring circles of that ring, tangent to both of them and internally tangent to the large circle. All the intersections occur at points of tangency. What is the area of the shaded region, inside the larger circle but outside all the circles of radius $1$?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=1+2*sqrt(3);
fill(circle((0,0),R),lightgray);
pair[] c;c.push((0,0));
for(int i=0;i<6;++i){c.push(2*dir(60*i));c.push(2*sqrt(3)*dir(30+60*i));}
for(int i=0;i<c.length;++i) filldraw(circle(c[i],1),white,linewidth(0.7));
draw(circle((0,0),R),linewidth(0.9));
\end{asy}

$\textbf{(A) }4\pi\sqrt3\qquad\textbf{(B) }7\pi\qquad\textbf{(C) }\pi\left(3\sqrt3+2\right)\qquad\textbf{(D) }10\pi\left(\sqrt3-1\right)\qquad\textbf{(E) }\pi\left(\sqrt3+6\right)$

solution:
Let $O$ be the centre. The centres $P_1,P_2$ of two neighbouring circles of the ring are at distance $2$ from $O$ and from each other, so $OP_1P_2$ is equilateral with side $2$. An outer circle tangent to both has its centre $Q$ at distance $2$ from $P_1$ and $P_2$, so $P_1P_2Q$ is also equilateral, on the far side of $P_1P_2$. With $M$ the midpoint of $P_1P_2$, $O$, $M$, $Q$ are collinear and
\[OQ=OM+MQ=\sqrt3+\sqrt3=2\sqrt3.\]
The large circle is internally tangent to the outer circles, so its radius is $2\sqrt3+1$, and the shaded area is
\[\pi\left(1+2\sqrt3\right)^2-13\pi=\pi\left(13+4\sqrt3\right)-13\pi=\boxed{\textbf{(A) }4\pi\sqrt3}.\]
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=1+2*sqrt(3);
draw(circle((0,0),R),linewidth(0.9));
pair[] c;c.push((0,0));
for(int i=0;i<6;++i){c.push(2*dir(60*i));c.push(2*sqrt(3)*dir(30+60*i));}
for(int i=0;i<c.length;++i) draw(circle(c[i],1),linewidth(0.6));
pair O=(0,0),P1=2*dir(0),P2=2*dir(60),Q=2*sqrt(3)*dir(30),M=(P1+P2)/2;
draw(P1--P2--Q--cycle,dashed);draw(O--Q,dashed);draw(O--P1,dashed);draw(O--P2,dashed);draw(Q--R*dir(30),dotted);
dot(O);dot(P1);dot(P2);dot(Q);dot(M);
label("$O$",O,W);label("$P_1$",P1,S);label("$P_2$",P2,NW);label("$Q$",Q,N);label("$M$",M,dir(-15));
label("$1$",(Q+R*dir(30))/2,SE);
\end{asy}

question:
Line segment $\overline{AD}$ is trisected by points $B$ and $C$ so that $AB=BC=CD=2$. Three semicircles of radius $1$, the arcs $AEB$, $BFC$ and $CGD$, have their diameters on $\overline{AD}$, lie on the same side of it, and are tangent to line $EG$ at $E$, $F$ and $G$, respectively. A circle of radius $2$ has its centre at $F$. The area of the region inside this circle but outside the three semicircles (shaded in the figure) can be expressed in the form
\[\dfrac ab\cdot\pi-\sqrt c+d,\]
where $a,b,c$ and $d$ are positive integers and $a$ and $b$ are relatively prime. What is $a+b+c+d$?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(2,0),C=(4,0),D=(6,0),F=(3,1);
fill(circle(F,2),lightgray);
for(int k=0;k<3;++k) filldraw(arc((1+2k,0),1,0,180)--cycle,white,linewidth(0.8));
draw(circle(F,2),linewidth(0.9));draw(A--D,linewidth(0.8));
draw((-0.5,1)--(6.5,1),dashed);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot((1,1));dot(F);dot((5,1));
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,S);label("$D$",D,SE);
label("$E$",(1,1),NW);label("$F$",F,N);label("$G$",(5,1),NE);
\end{asy}

$\textbf{(A) }13\qquad\textbf{(B) }14\qquad\textbf{(C) }15\qquad\textbf{(D) }16\qquad\textbf{(E) }17$

solution:
Place $A=(0,0)$ and $D=(6,0)$, with the semicircles above $AD$; then $E=(1,1)$, $F=(3,1)$, $G=(5,1)$. Let $\Gamma$ be the circle of radius $2$ about $F$; its area is $4\pi$, and the shaded area is $4\pi$ minus the parts of the three half-discs inside $\Gamma$.
\begin{itemize}
\item The middle half-disc lies inside $\Gamma$: its points farthest from $F$ are $B$ and $C$, at distance $\sqrt2<2$. It contributes $\dfrac\pi2$.
\item Left half-disc (centre $M=(1,0)$). The leftmost point of $\Gamma$ is $E$, so only the quarter disc $x\ge1$ (area $\dfrac\pi4$) matters. Its arc from $E$ to $B$ lies inside $\Gamma$ (a point $(1+\cos t,\sin t)$ is at squared distance $6-4\cos t-2\sin t\le4$ from $F$ for $0\le t\le\dfrac\pi2$), and $\Gamma$ meets $AD$ at $P=(3-\sqrt3,0)$. So the quarter disc minus $\Gamma$ is the small region $R$ bounded by $EM$, $MP$ and the arc $PE$ of $\Gamma$ (shaded dark in the second sketch).
\end{itemize}
To find $R$, let $H=(3,0)$. The rectangle $MHFE$ has area $2$, and the part of $\Gamma$ inside it consists of the right triangle $FHP$ (legs $1$ and $\sqrt3$, area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}2$) and the $30^\circ$ sector $PFE$ of radius $2$ (area $\dfrac\pi3$). So
\[[R]=2-\dfrac{\sqrt3}2-\dfrac\pi3,\]
and the left (and, by symmetry, the right) half-disc covers $\dfrac\pi4-[R]$ of $\Gamma$. The shaded area is
\[4\pi-\dfrac\pi2-2\left(\dfrac\pi4-[R]\right)=3\pi+4-\sqrt3-\dfrac{2\pi}3=\dfrac73\pi-\sqrt3+4.\]
So $a+b+c+d=7+3+3+4=\boxed{\textbf{(E) }17}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(2,0),C=(4,0),D=(6,0),F=(3,1),Ep=(1,1),M=(1,0),P=(3-sqrt(3),0),H=(3,0);
draw(circle(F,2),linewidth(0.9));draw(A--D,linewidth(0.8));
for(int k=0;k<3;++k) draw(arc((1+2k,0),1,0,180),linewidth(0.8));
draw(M--H--F--Ep--cycle,dashed);
dot(Ep);dot(F);dot((5,1));dot(P);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,S);label("$D$",D,SE);label("$E$",Ep,NW);label("$F$",F,N);label("$G$",(5,1),NE);
label("$P$",P,S);label("$\Gamma$",F+2*dir(60),NE);
\end{asy}

Detail of the rectangle $MHFE$: the region $R$ (dark) and the part of $\Gamma$ inside the rectangle (light), made of triangle $FHP$ and the $30^\circ$ sector $PFE$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(2,0),F=(3,1),Ep=(1,1),M=(1,0),P=(3-sqrt(3),0),H=(3,0);
fill(Ep--M--P--arc(F,2,210,180,CW)--cycle,mediumgray);
fill(F--H--P--arc(F,2,210,180,CW)--cycle,palegray);
draw(M--H--F--Ep--cycle,dashed);
draw(arc(F,2,210,180,CW),linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc((1,0),1,0,90),linewidth(0.8));draw(arc((3,0),1,90,180),linewidth(0.8));
draw(F--P,dashed);
draw(arc(F,0.35,180,210));label("$30^\circ$",F+0.35*dir(195),W,fontsize(8pt));
dot(Ep);dot(F);dot(M);dot(P);dot(H);dot(B);
label("$E$",Ep,NW);label("$F$",F,NE);label("$M$",M,SW);label("$P$",P,S);label("$H$",H,SE);label("$B$",B,N);
draw((P.x,-0.18)--(3,-0.18),Arrows(TeXHead));label("$\sqrt3$",((P.x+3)/2,-0.18),S);
label("$1$",(F+H)/2,E);label("$R$",(1.1,0.35));
\end{asy}

Use coordinates with the axis of the cylinder as the $z$-axis and its centre $O$ at the origin, so the block is $x^2+y^2\le36$, $-4\le z\le4$. Take $A=(3,3\sqrt3,4)$ and $B=(3,-3\sqrt3,4)$ on the top rim; they subtend $120^\circ$ at the centre of the top face. The plane through $A$, $B$ and $O$ contains the line $x=3$, $z=4$ and the origin, so it is $z=\dfrac43x$.

The unpainted face is the set of points $\left(x,y,\dfrac43x\right)$ with $x^2+y^2\le36$ and $-4\le\dfrac43x\le4$, i.e. $|x|\le3$. Its orthogonal projection onto the $xy$-plane is the disc of radius $6$ with the two caps $|x|>3$ removed. Each cap is a circular segment with central angle $120^\circ$, of area $\dfrac12\cdot36\left(\dfrac{2\pi}3-\dfrac{\sqrt3}2\right)=12\pi-9\sqrt3$, so the projection has area
\[36\pi-2\left(12\pi-9\sqrt3\right)=12\pi+18\sqrt3.\]
The plane $z=\dfrac43x$ makes an angle $\theta$ with the horizontal where $\tan\theta=\dfrac43$, so $\cos\theta=\dfrac35$, and projecting multiplies areas by $\cos\theta$. Hence the face has area
\[\dfrac53\left(12\pi+18\sqrt3\right)=20\pi+30\sqrt3,\]
and $a+b+c=20+30+3=\boxed{53}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real k=0.35;
pair Pj(real x,real y,real z){return (y,z-k*x);}
path top=Pj(6,0,4);
for(int i=1;i<=360;++i){real t=radians(i);top=top--Pj(6cos(t),6sin(t),4);}
draw(top,linewidth(0.8));
path front;
for(int i=-90;i<=90;++i){real t=radians(i);front=(i==-90)?Pj(6cos(t),6sin(t),-4):(front--Pj(6cos(t),6sin(t),-4));}
draw(front,linewidth(0.8));
path bk;
for(int i=90;i<=270;++i){real t=radians(i);bk=(i==90)?Pj(6cos(t),6sin(t),-4):(bk--Pj(6cos(t),6sin(t),-4));}
draw(bk,dashed);
draw(Pj(0,6,4)--Pj(0,6,-4),linewidth(0.8));draw(Pj(0,-6,4)--Pj(0,-6,-4),linewidth(0.8));
path cut;
for(int i=60;i<=120;++i){real t=radians(i);cut=(i==60)?Pj(6cos(t),6sin(t),8cos(t)):(cut--Pj(6cos(t),6sin(t),8cos(t)));}
path cut2;
for(int i=240;i<=300;++i){real t=radians(i);cut2=(i==240)?Pj(6cos(t),6sin(t),8cos(t)):(cut2--Pj(6cos(t),6sin(t),8cos(t)));}
pair A=Pj(3,3*sqrt(3),4),B=Pj(3,-3*sqrt(3),4),A2=Pj(-3,3*sqrt(3),-4),B2=Pj(-3,-3*sqrt(3),-4);
