Let the arcs $VW,WX,XY,YZ,ZV$ (each not containing the other points) measure $\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta,\varepsilon$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $VY\parallel WX$: parallel chords cut off equal arcs, so $\alpha=\gamma$.
\item $VW\parallel ZY$: likewise $\beta+\gamma=\varepsilon$.
\item $\angle WXY=\dfrac{\delta+\varepsilon+\alpha}{2}$ and $\angle ZVW=\dfrac{\beta+\gamma+\delta}{2}$, so their difference is $\dfrac{\alpha+\varepsilon-\beta-\gamma}{2}=\dfrac\alpha2=30^\circ$. Hence $\alpha=60^\circ$, and $\angle VYW=\dfrac\alpha2=30^\circ$.
\end{itemize}
\emph{The altitude condition.} Let $D$ be the foot of the altitude from $W$, and put $D=(0,0)$, $V=(-p,0)$, $Y=(q,0)$, $W=(0,w)$. The altitude from $Y$ is the line $p(x-q)+wy=0$, which meets $WD$ at $H=\left(0,\dfrac{pq}{w}\right)$. $H$ is the midpoint of $WD$ when $\dfrac{pq}{w}=\dfrac w2$, i.e. $w^2=2pq$, i.e.
\[\tan\angle WVY\cdot\tan\angle VYW=\dfrac wp\cdot\dfrac wq=2.\]
So $\tan\angle WVY=\dfrac{2}{\tan30^\circ}=2\sqrt3$, giving $\cos\angle WVY=\dfrac1{\sqrt{13}}$ and $\sin\angle WVY=\dfrac{2\sqrt3}{\sqrt{13}}$. Write $\theta=\angle WVY$.

\emph{Lengths.} By the extended law of sines, $VW=2R\sin30^\circ$, so $R=26$. The legs are $VW=XY=26$ (equal arcs). Base $WX$ subtends arc $\beta=2\theta-\gamma=2\theta-60^\circ$, and base $VY$ subtends $\alpha+\beta+\gamma=2\theta+60^\circ$. So
\[WX=52\sin\left(\theta-30^\circ\right)=52\cdot\dfrac{3-\dfrac12}{\sqrt{13}}=10\sqrt{13},\qquad VY=52\sin\left(\theta+30^\circ\right)=52\cdot\dfrac{3+\dfrac12}{\sqrt{13}}=14\sqrt{13}.\]
The height is $\sqrt{26^2-\left(2\sqrt{13}\right)^2}=\sqrt{624}=4\sqrt{39}$, so the area is
\[\dfrac{10\sqrt{13}+14\sqrt{13}}{2}\cdot4\sqrt{39}=48\sqrt{507}=624\sqrt3,\]
and $a+b=\boxed{627}$. (The remaining arc is $\delta=300^\circ-4\theta\approx4.4^\circ>0$, so the configuration exists, and the triangle $VWY$ is acute, so $H$ lies on the altitude.)

