$s(1)=0$ and $s(2)=1$. For $x\ge3$ the numbers coprime to $x$ pair up as $k$ and $x-k$, so $s(x)=\dfrac{x\,\varphi(x)}{2}$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $x\ge3$ is even, $\varphi(x)$ is even, so $s(x)=x\cdot\dfrac{\varphi(x)}2$ is even.
\item If $x$ is odd, $s(x)$ is odd exactly when $\dfrac{\varphi(x)}{2}$ is odd, i.e. $\varphi(x)\equiv2\pmod4$. Each odd prime power $p^e$ dividing $x$ contributes the even factor $p^{e-1}(p-1)$ to $\varphi(x)$, so $x$ must be a prime power $p^e$ with $p\equiv3\pmod4$.
\end{itemize}
Below $30$ these are $3,7,9,11,19,23,27$, together with $x=2$. The sum is $2+3+7+9+11+19+23+27=\boxed{101}$.

Let $f(k)=\left\lfloor\sqrt k\right\rfloor$. For $j<k$ the slope is $\dfrac{(k-j)+f(k)-f(j)}{k-j}=1+\dfrac{f(k)-f(j)}{k-j}$, so $1<s<2$ exactly when $0<f(k)-f(j)<k-j$.

The values of $f$ form blocks: $f=1,2,3,4,5$ on $\{1,2,3\}$, $\{4,\dots,8\}$, $\{9,\dots,15\}$, $\{16,\dots,24\}$, $\{25\}$, of sizes $3,5,7,9,1$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $f(k)=f(j)$ (slope $1$) for $\dbinom32+\dbinom52+\dbinom72+\dbinom92=3+10+21+36=70$ pairs.
\item Since $f$ rises by at most $1$ per step, $f(k)-f(j)\le k-j$, with equality only if every step from $j$ to $k$ is a jump. As every block except the last has at least $3$ elements, this happens only for $(j,k)=(3,4),(8,9),(15,16),(24,25)$ (slope $2$).
\end{itemize}
So $\dbinom{25}{2}-70-4=226$ of the $300$ pairs work. The probability is $\dfrac{226}{300}=\dfrac{113}{150}$, and $m+n=\boxed{263}$.

