By the extended law of sines, $\Omega$ has radius $R=\dfrac{BC}{2\sin A}=\dfrac{216\sqrt2}{\sqrt2}=216$.

Describe points of $\Omega$ by their angles $a,b,c,\dots$ about the centre $O$. Since $AD\parallel BC$, $D$ is the reflection of $A$ in the perpendicular bisector of $BC$, which is the line through $O$ at angle $\dfrac{b+c}{2}$. Reflection in that line sends angle $\theta$ to $b+c-\theta$, so $D$ has angle $b+c-a$. Similarly $E$ has angle $a+c-b$ and $F$ has angle $a+b-c$. (The condition $\angle A<\angle B<\angle C$ makes the triangle scalene, so $D,E,F$ differ from $A,B,C$.)

So the central angle between $E$ and $F$ is $2(c-b)$, and $EF=2R\left|\sin(c-b)\right|$. The central angle $c-b$ of chord $BC$ is $\pm2A$ modulo $360^\circ$, hence $\left|\sin(c-b)\right|=\sin2A=\sin90^\circ=1$. Therefore $EF=2R=\boxed{432}$; in fact $EF$ is a diameter.

