By AA, triangles $ABC$ and $ADE$ are similar, so $\dfrac{AB}{AD}=\dfrac{AC}{AE}$, i.e. $\dfrac{AB}{AC}=\dfrac{AD}{AE}$.

Since the triangles have the same orientation, the rotation about $A$ taking ray $AB$ to ray $AC$ also takes ray $AD$ to ray $AE$ (both are rotations by the directed angle $\angle BAC=\angle DAE$). Hence the rotation taking ray $AB$ to ray $AD$ also takes ray $AC$ to ray $AE$, so $\angle BAD=\angle CAE$.

So triangles $ABD$ and $ACE$ have $\dfrac{AB}{AC}=\dfrac{AD}{AE}$ and equal included angles at $A$; by SAS they are similar, with $B\leftrightarrow C$ and $D\leftrightarrow E$. Therefore $\angle ADB=\angle AEC$. $\blacksquare$

