question:
A post on a website has a score that starts at $1$. Each voter either upvotes it (adding $1$) or downvotes it (subtracting $1$). The post now has a score of $55201$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that it is impossible for exactly $89\%$ of the voters to have upvoted.
\item[(b)] Suppose instead that the percentage of upvoters, rounded to the nearest whole percent, is $89\%$. What is the smallest possible number of voters?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the integers be $n,\dots,n+5$, with median $n+\dfrac52$. The new list is $2n,\ n+3,\ 2n+4,\ n+5,\ 2n+8,\ n+7$, and its median must be $m=n+\dfrac{13}{2}$. As $m$ is not an integer, exactly three entries are below $m$. Of $n+3$, $n+5$, $n+7$, exactly two are below $m$, so exactly one of $2n<2n+4<2n+8$ is: $2n<n+\dfrac{13}2<2n+4$, so $n\in\{3,4,5,6\}$. The median is then $\dfrac12\left(\max(n+5,2n)+\min(n+7,2n+4)\right)$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $n=3$: $\dfrac{8+10}{2}=9\ne9.5$; \quad $n=4$: $\dfrac{9+11}{2}=10\ne10.5$; \quad $n=5$: $\dfrac{10+12}{2}=11\ne11.5$;
\item $n=6$: $\dfrac{12+13}{2}=12.5=n+\dfrac{13}{2}$. \checkmark
\end{itemize}
So the lists are $6,\dots,11$ (median $8.5$) and $12,9,16,11,20,13$ (median $12.5$), and the sum of the medians is $\boxed{21}$.

Put $A=\left(-\dfrac s2,0\right)$, $B=\left(\dfrac s2,0\right)$. Then $E,F=\left(0,\pm\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s\right)$, so $EF=\sqrt3\,s$ lies on the $y$-axis. Let $\theta=\angle DAB$ and $c=\cos\theta$; then $D=A+s(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$ and $C=B+s(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$, at distances $s\left|c-\dfrac12\right|$ and $s\left|c+\dfrac12\right|$ from line $EF$. So
\[[DEF]=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s^2\left|c-\dfrac12\right|=1,\qquad[CEF]=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s^2\left|c+\dfrac12\right|=4,\]
and $\left|c+\dfrac12\right|=4\left|c-\dfrac12\right|$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $c>\dfrac12$: $c+\dfrac12=4c-2$ gives $c=\dfrac56$. Then $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s^2\cdot\dfrac13=1$, so $s^2=2\sqrt3$, and $\sin\theta=\dfrac{\sqrt{11}}{6}$. The area is $s^2\sin\theta=\dfrac{\sqrt{33}}{3}$. (Here $C$ and $D$ are on the same side of $EF$; first sketch.)
\item $-\dfrac12\le c<\dfrac12$: $c+\dfrac12=2-4c$ gives $c=\dfrac3{10}$. Then $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s^2\cdot\dfrac15=1$, so $s^2=\dfrac{10}{\sqrt3}$, and $\sin\theta=\dfrac{\sqrt{91}}{10}$. The area is $\dfrac{\sqrt{91}}{\sqrt3}=\dfrac{\sqrt{273}}{3}$. (Here line $EF$ separates $C$ and $D$; second sketch.)
\item $c<-\dfrac12$: $-c-\dfrac12=2-4c$ gives $c=\dfrac56$, a contradiction.
\end{itemize}
So the area of $ABCD$ is $\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt{33}}{3}\text{ or }\dfrac{\sqrt{273}}{3}}$.

