\emph{Trapezoid $ABME$} has sides $AB$, $BM$, $ME=13$, $EA=3$. If its three equal sides were $3$, the fourth side $13$ would exceed the sum $9$ of the others, which is impossible. So $AB=BM=ME=13$. The equal legs of an isosceles trapezoid are opposite sides; since $BM\ne EA$, the legs are $AB=ME=13$ and the bases are $BM\parallel EA$, of lengths $13$ and $3$. The height is $\sqrt{13^2-5^2}=12$, the area is $\dfrac{13+3}{2}\cdot12=96$, and the base angles at $B$ and $M$ satisfy $\cos\angle ABM=\cos\angle BME=\dfrac5{13}$. Call this angle $\theta$; then $\angle BMD=180^\circ-\theta$ is obtuse.

\emph{Trapezoid $CDMB$} has $DM=MB=13$, so exactly one of $CD$, $BC$ equals $13$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $BC=13\ne CD$, the legs are $DM=BC$ and the bases $CD\parallel MB$, and then $\angle MBC=\angle BMD=180^\circ-\theta$. So $\angle ABC=\theta+\left(180^\circ-\theta\right)=180^\circ$, which is not allowed.
\item So $CD=13\ne BC$: the legs are $CD=MB$ and the bases are $DM\parallel BC$. The angle at $M$ is obtuse, so $DM=13$ is the shorter base, and $BC=13+2\cdot13\cos\theta=23$. The height is $12$ and the area is $\dfrac{13+23}{2}\cdot12=216$. Now $\angle ABC=2\theta<180^\circ$, and all other angles are $\theta$ or $180^\circ-\theta$.
\end{itemize}
The area of $ABCDE$ is $96+216=\boxed{312}$.

