Let $u=\cos x+\cos y$. Then
\[u^2+1=(\cos x+\cos y)^2+(\sin x+\sin y)^2=2+2(\cos x\cos y+\sin x\sin y)=2+2\cos(x-y)\le4,\]
so $u^2\le3$ and $-\sqrt3\le u\le\sqrt3$. Equality needs $\cos(x-y)=1$, e.g. $x=y$ with $\sin x=\dfrac12$: $x=y=30^\circ$ gives $u=\sqrt3$ and $x=y=150^\circ$ gives $u=-\sqrt3$.

So the maximum is $\boxed{\sqrt3}$ and the minimum is $\boxed{-\sqrt3}$. Geometrically, $P+Q=(u,1)$, where $P=(\cos x,\sin x)$ and $Q=(\cos y,\sin y)$ are unit vectors, so $P+Q$ lies in the disc of radius $2$ and on the line $y=1$, between $\left(-\sqrt3,1\right)$ and $\left(\sqrt3,1\right)$. (The sketch shows $x=0$, $y=90^\circ$, with $u=1$.)

