Let the right angle be at $C$, with $BC=a$ and $AC=b$, and let the square be $PQRS$ with $P,Q$ on $AB$ (in the order $A,P,Q,B$), $S$ on $AC$ and $R$ on $BC$. Triangles $APS$ and $BQR$ are right-angled at $P$ and $Q$, with angles $A$ and $B$, and $\tan A=\dfrac ab$, $\tan B=\dfrac ba$. So
\[AP=\dfrac{s}{\tan A}=\dfrac{bs}{a},\qquad QB=\dfrac{s}{\tan B}=\dfrac{as}{b}.\]
Then
\[c=AP+PQ+QB=s\left(\dfrac ba+1+\dfrac ab\right)=s\cdot\dfrac{a^2+b^2+ab}{ab}=s\cdot\dfrac{c^2+ab}{ab},\]
so $\boxed{s=\dfrac{abc}{c^2+ab}}$. For example, for the $3$-$4$-$5$ triangle, $s=\dfrac{60}{37}$ (sketch). (A square in the corner at $C$ instead has side $\dfrac{ab}{a+b}$, which is always larger.)

