$AD=4\sqrt2$ and $DF=2\sqrt2$. Put $A=(0,0)$ and the line on the $x$-axis: $D=\left(4\sqrt2,0\right)$, $F=\left(6\sqrt2,0\right)$, $B=\left(2\sqrt2,2\sqrt2\right)$, $E=\left(5\sqrt2,\sqrt2\right)$.

\emph{Homothety.} $AB\parallel DE$ and $BD\parallel EF$ (all at $45^\circ$ to $AC$), so triangle $DEF$ is the image of triangle $ABD$ under a homothety with ratio $\dfrac12$. Its centre is where $BE$ meets $AD$, namely $C$. So $CD=\dfrac12CA$, i.e. $D$ is the midpoint of $AC$, and $AC=2AD=\boxed{8\sqrt2}$. (Check with coordinates: line $BE$ has slope $-\dfrac13$ and meets the $x$-axis at $5\sqrt2+3\sqrt2=8\sqrt2$.)

\emph{The infinite method.} The same homothety maps $DEF$ to a third triangle $FGH$ with apex $G$ on $BC$ and $FH=\sqrt2$, and so on. The images of $A$ under its powers approach the centre $C$, so the bases fill $AC$ and $AC=4\sqrt2\left(1+\dfrac12+\dfrac14+\cdots\right)=8\sqrt2$. This is correct, but the homothety argument gives the answer directly.

