\emph{At most $3$.} The exterior angles of a convex polygon are positive and add up to $360^\circ$. An acute interior angle has an exterior angle greater than $90^\circ$, so four acute angles would make the exterior angles add up to more than $360^\circ$. Hence there are at most $3$ acute angles.

\emph{Exactly $3$ is possible for every $n>3$.} Start with an equilateral triangle $ABC$ and replace side $BC$ by a slightly bulging convex chain $B,P_1,\dots,P_{n-3},C$, with the points $P_i$ on a very flat circular arc through $B$ and $C$ on the far side of $BC$ from $A$. The polygon $ABP_1\cdots P_{n-3}C$ has $n$ vertices and is convex. Its angle at $A$ is $60^\circ$, and its angles at $B$ and $C$ exceed $60^\circ$ only by the small angle between $BC$ and the arc, so they stay acute if the arc is flat enough. The angles at the $P_i$ are close to $180^\circ$, hence obtuse. So exactly $3$ angles are acute. (The figure shows $n=6$.)

