Place the square at $[0,2]\times[0,2]$; the midpoints of the sides are $(1,0)$, $(2,1)$, $(1,2)$, $(0,1)$. Consider the four discs of radius $1$ about them.

\emph{Opposite midpoints} are $2$ apart, so their discs meet only at the centre $(1,1)$.

\emph{Adjacent midpoints}, e.g. $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$, are $\sqrt2$ apart, and their discs meet in a lens with tips $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$. Seen from either centre, the common chord subtends a right angle, so the lens consists of two circular segments with central angle $90^\circ$:
\[\text{lens}=2\left(\dfrac{\pi}{4}-\dfrac12\right)=\dfrac{\pi}{2}-1.\]
A point within $1$ of $(1,0)$ has $0\le x\le2$, and a point within $1$ of $(0,1)$ has $0\le y\le2$, so the lens lies in the square.

Two different lenses would share only points within $1$ of two opposite midpoints, i.e. only the point $(1,1)$. So the favourable region is made of four lenses (shaded) of total area $4\left(\dfrac\pi2-1\right)=2\pi-4$, and the probability is
\[\dfrac{2\pi-4}{4}=\boxed{\dfrac{\pi-2}{2}}\approx0.571.\]

