We need $\cos kx\ne0$ for $k=1,2,\dots,101$.

\emph{Telescoping.} Since $\sin\left((k+1)x-kx\right)=\sin x$,
\[\tan(k+1)x-\tan kx=\dfrac{\sin(k+1)x\cos kx-\cos(k+1)x\sin kx}{\cos kx\cos(k+1)x}=\dfrac{\sin x}{\cos kx\cos(k+1)x}.\]

\emph{Case $\sin x=0$}, $x=m\pi$. Each term equals $\dfrac{1}{(-1)^{km}(-1)^{(k+1)m}}=(-1)^m$, so the sum is $\pm100\ne0$.

\emph{Case $\sin x\ne0$.} Summing the identity for $k=1,\dots,100$ gives
\[\sum_{k=1}^{100}\dfrac{1}{\cos kx\cos(k+1)x}=\dfrac{\tan101x-\tan x}{\sin x},\]
which vanishes exactly when $\tan101x=\tan x$, i.e. $100x=m\pi$ for an integer $m$ ($100\nmid m$, as $\sin x\ne0$).

\emph{Which $x=\dfrac{m\pi}{100}$ keep all terms defined?} We have $\cos\dfrac{km\pi}{100}=0$ exactly when $km$ is an odd multiple of $50$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $m$ odd: take $k=2$ if $25\mid m$, $k=10$ if $5\mid m$ but $25\nmid m$, and $k=50$ if $5\nmid m$; in each case $km$ is an odd multiple of $50$. Excluded.
\item $m\equiv2\pmod4$: $k=25$ gives $25m=50\cdot\dfrac m2$ with $\dfrac m2$ odd. Excluded.
\item $4\mid m$: then $4\mid km$, while an odd multiple of $50$ is $2\pmod4$. All terms are defined.
\end{itemize}
So $m=4j$ with $25\nmid j$, i.e.
\[\boxed{x=\dfrac{j\pi}{25},\quad j\in\mathbb Z,\ 25\nmid j.}\]
In terms of angles, these are the $48$ marked points of the circle below, repeated with period $2\pi$; the points $0$ and $\pi$ are excluded.

