Every solution has $\left|\dfrac{x}{100}\right|\le1$, so $|x|\le100$. Both sides are odd functions, so $x=0$ is a solution and the solutions with $x<0$ are the negatives of those with $x>0$.

Let $g(x)=\sin x-\dfrac{x}{100}$ for $0<x\le100$. On each interval $(2k\pi+\pi,\,2k\pi+2\pi)$ we have $\sin x<0<\dfrac{x}{100}$, so all positive solutions lie in the intervals $[2k\pi,2k\pi+\pi]$, $k=0,1,\dots,15$ (note $31\pi\approx97.4<100<32\pi$). On each such interval $g''(x)=-\sin x\le0$, so $g$ is concave there.
\begin{itemize}
\item $k=0$: $g(0)=0$, $g'(0)=1-\dfrac1{100}>0$ and $g(\pi)<0$. A concave function that starts at $0$ increasing has exactly one zero in $(0,\pi]$.
\item $1\le k\le15$: $g(2k\pi)<0$, $g\left(2k\pi+\dfrac\pi2\right)=1-\dfrac{(4k+1)\pi}{200}>0$ (as $61\pi<200$), and $g(2k\pi+\pi)<0$. A concave function with this sign pattern has exactly two zeros.
\end{itemize}
So there are $1+2\cdot15=31$ positive solutions, $31$ negative ones and $x=0$: $\boxed{63}$ solutions in total. (The sketch is compressed horizontally.)

