The diagonals of a rhombus are perpendicular bisectors of each other, so take $X$ as the origin with $A=(-p,0)$, $C=(p,0)$, $B=(0,q)$, $D=(0,-q)$, where $p>4$, $q>3$, and let $s=\sqrt{p^2+q^2}$ be the side. Then $Y=(-4,0)$ and $Z=(0,3)$, so line $EF$ is
\[3x-4y=-12.\]
\emph{Point $G$.} $G$ lies beyond $A$ on line $BA$ with $GA=8$, so $G=A-\dfrac8s(B-A)=\left(-p-\dfrac{8p}{s},\,-\dfrac{8q}{s}\right)$. Substituting into the line and multiplying by $s$:
\[3ps+24p-32q=12s.\qquad(1)\]
\emph{Point $H$.} $H$ lies beyond $C$ on line $DC$ with $HC=16$, so $H=C+\dfrac{16}{s}(C-D)=\left(p+\dfrac{16p}{s},\,\dfrac{16q}{s}\right)$, and
\[3ps+48p-64q=-12s.\qquad(2)\]
\emph{Solving.} $2\cdot(1)-(2)$ gives $3ps=36s$, so $p=12$. Then (1) becomes $24s=32q-288$, i.e. $s=\dfrac{4q-36}{3}$. With $s^2=144+q^2$:
\[(4q-36)^2=9\left(144+q^2\right)\iff7q^2=288q\iff q=\dfrac{288}{7},\]
and $s=\dfrac13\left(\dfrac{1152}{7}-36\right)=\boxed{\dfrac{300}{7}}$.

