The largest side is $ar^2$, so the only triangle inequality that can fail is
\[a+ar>ar^2\iff r^2-r-1<0\iff r<\dfrac{1+\sqrt5}{2}.\]
(The other two, $a+ar^2>ar$ and $ar+ar^2>a$, hold automatically for $r\ge1$.) So the possible ratios are
\[\boxed{1\le r<\dfrac{1+\sqrt5}{2}},\]
and the triangle need not be equilateral: for example $r=\dfrac32$ gives the triangle with sides $4,6,9$, since $4+6>9$.

