\emph{Constructions.} $k=1$ is trivial and $k=4$ uses the four quarter squares. A $3\times3$ grid with one $2\times2$ block merged gives $1+5=6$ squares, and a $4\times4$ grid with one $3\times3$ block merged gives $1+7=8$ squares. Cutting any square of a dissection into four quarters turns $k$ squares into $k+3$. Starting from $1$, $6$, $8$ (one in each residue class mod $3$) we obtain $1,4,7,\dots$, $6,9,12,\dots$ and $8,11,14,\dots$, i.e. every positive integer except $2$, $3$, $5$.

