\emph{Corners.} Suppose $k\ge2$. A corner of the big square cannot lie in the interior or in the middle of an edge of a small square (the small squares there would have to fill a $90^\circ$ angle with a $180^\circ$ or $360^\circ$ one), so it is a corner of some small square whose sides run along the sides of the big square. No small square contains two corners of the big square, since then it would be the whole square. Hence the four corners lie in four different small squares, and $k\ge4$. This rules out $k=2,3$.

\emph{$k=5$.} Let the big square be $[0,1]^2$, let the corner squares have sides $a$ (bottom left), $b$ (bottom right), $c$ (top right), $d$ (top left), and let $R$ be the fifth square. If the two corner squares on a side do not cover that side, the gap is covered by $R$, which then has an edge on that side. $R$ cannot have edges on two opposite sides (it would be the whole square) or on two adjacent sides (it would contain a corner already covered). So on at least three sides the corner squares fill the side exactly; by symmetry say the bottom, right and top sides:
\[a+b=1,\qquad b+c=1,\qquad c+d=1,\qquad\text{so}\qquad c=a,\ d=b.\]
If $a>\dfrac12$, the squares $[0,a]^2$ and $[1-a,1]^2$ overlap in a square of positive area. If $a<\dfrac12$ then $b>\dfrac12$, and the squares $[1-b,1]\times[0,b]$ and $[0,b]\times[1-b,1]$ overlap. So $a=b=c=d=\dfrac12$, the four corner squares already fill the big square, and there is no room for $R$. Contradiction.

So a dissection into $k$ squares exists exactly for $\boxed{k\notin\{2,3,5\}}$. $\blacksquare$

Since $BD$ is a diameter, $\angle BED=\angle BFD=90^\circ$, so $E$ and $F$ are the feet of the perpendiculars from $D$ to $AB$ and $BC$. Let $s$ be the side and $\alpha=\angle DAB$, which is acute because $\angle ABC$ is obtuse. Then $AE=s\cos\alpha=1$.

Put $A=(0,0)$, $B=(s,0)$ and $D=(s\cos\alpha,s\sin\alpha)=(1,h)$ with $h=\sqrt{s^2-1}$. The rhombus is symmetric in $BD$, so $F$ is the reflection of $E$ and $BF=BE=s-1$; along $BC$, whose direction is $\left(\dfrac1s,\dfrac hs\right)$,
\[F=\left(s+\dfrac{s-1}{s},\ \dfrac{(s-1)h}{s}\right).\]
Hence
\[AF^2=s^2+2(s-1)+\dfrac{(s-1)^2}{s^2}+\dfrac{(s-1)^2\left(s^2-1\right)}{s^2}=s^2+2(s-1)+(s-1)^2=2s^2-1.\]
From $AF=2$ we get $s^2=\dfrac52$, so $h=\sqrt{\dfrac32}$ and
\[[ABCD]=AB\cdot h=\sqrt{\dfrac52}\cdot\sqrt{\dfrac32}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt{15}}{2}}.\]
(Consistency: $\cos\alpha=\dfrac1s\approx0.63$, so $\alpha$ is acute; $AE=1<s$ and $BF=s-1<s$, so $E$ and $F$ lie on the sides.)

