Let $\alpha=\angle DAB$, acute since $\angle ABC$ is obtuse, and write $c=\cos\alpha$, $s=\sin\alpha$.

\emph{Key observation.} The diagonals of a rhombus are perpendicular, so $\angle AXB=90^\circ$ and $X$ lies on $\omega$. Also $FX\perp CD$ and $CD\parallel AB$, so the chord $FX$ of $\omega$ is perpendicular to the line $AB$ through the centre of $\omega$; hence $AB$ bisects it, and $F$ is the reflection of $X$ in $AB$. Finally $\angle AEB=90^\circ$, so $E$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $B$ to $AD$.

\emph{Coordinates.} Put $A=(0,0)$, $B=(1,0)$, $D=(c,s)$, $C=(1+c,s)$. Then
\[X=\left(\dfrac{1+c}{2},\dfrac s2\right),\qquad F=\left(\dfrac{1+c}{2},-\dfrac s2\right),\qquad E=\left(c^2,cs\right).\]
The centre $O$ of circle $EDC$ lies on the perpendicular bisector $x=c+\dfrac12$ of $DC$; write $O=\left(c+\dfrac12,k\right)$. The condition $OD=OF$ reads
\[\dfrac14+(s-k)^2=\dfrac{c^2}{4}+\left(k+\dfrac s2\right)^2\iff s^2=3sk\iff k=\dfrac s3,\]
using $1-c^2=s^2$. The condition $OE=OD$ then reads
\[\left(c^2-c-\dfrac12\right)^2+s^2\left(c-\dfrac13\right)^2=\dfrac14+\dfrac{4s^2}{9},\]
which, after substituting $s^2=1-c^2$ and simplifying, becomes
\[\dfrac{(1-c)\left(4c^2-1\right)}{3}=0.\]
As $0<c<1$, we get $c=\dfrac12$, i.e. $\alpha=60^\circ$.

\emph{Area.} Now $D=\left(\dfrac12,\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$, $E=\left(\dfrac14,\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\right)$ (the midpoint of $AD$) and $F=\left(\dfrac34,-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\right)$. With $E-D=\left(-\dfrac14,-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\right)$ and $F-D=\left(\dfrac14,-\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{4}\right)$,
\[[DEF]=\dfrac12\left|\left(-\dfrac14\right)\left(-\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{4}\right)-\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\right)\dfrac14\right|=\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt3}{8}}.\]

