Since $OM\perp TM$, $TM=\sqrt{OT^2-OM^2}=\sqrt{27-9}=3\sqrt2$. Then $MN=3\sqrt2$ is a chord of circle $B$ with $OM^2+ON^2=9+9=18=MN^2$, so $\angle MON=90^\circ$. There are two such points $N$, on either side of line $OM$. For the one on the same side as $T$, $\angle TMN=45^\circ$; for the other ($N'$ in the figure), $\angle TMN'=135^\circ$. So $N$ is on the side of $T$.

Now $ON\perp OM$ and $TM\perp OM$, so $ON\parallel TM$ and $TMON$ is a right trapezoid with parallel sides $TM=3\sqrt2$, $ON=3$ and height $OM=3$:
\[[TMON]=\dfrac{3\sqrt2+3}{2}\cdot3=\dfrac{9\left(1+\sqrt2\right)}{2}.\]
So $a=9$, $b=1$, $c=2$, $d=2$ (forced, since $c$ squarefree and $\gcd(a,d)=1$), and
\[(ac-b)(a+2b)=(18-1)(9+2)=\boxed{187}.\]

