solution:
The hypotenuse $BC$ is the longest side, so $C$ lies outside $\omega$, $X$ is on segment $BC$, and $B$ lies between $Y$ and $C$. Hence
\[CX=BC-AB,\qquad CY=BC+AB.\]
\emph{Similarity.} $XY$ is a diameter, so $\angle XAY=90^\circ$. Ray $AX$ lies inside $\angle BAC=90^\circ$ and ray $AB$ inside $\angle XAY=90^\circ$, so
\[\angle CAX=90^\circ-\angle BAX=\angle BAY.\]
Triangle $BAY$ is isosceles ($BA=BY$), so $\angle BAY=\angle BYA=\angle CYA$. Thus $\angle CAX=\angle CYA$, and the triangles $CAX$ and $CYA$ also share the angle at $C$, so they are similar (AA):
\[\dfrac{CA}{CY}=\dfrac{CX}{CA},\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad CA^2=CX\cdot CY.\]
(This is the power of the point $C$: $CA$ is tangent to $\omega$, being perpendicular to the radius $BA$.)

\emph{Conclusion.} $AC^2=(BC-AB)(BC+AB)=BC^2-AB^2$, so $BC^2=AB^2+AC^2$. $\blacksquare$ (The argument uses only similar triangles, so it does not assume the Pythagorean theorem.)

