Put $A$ at the origin with $AB$ along the positive $x$-axis, let $s$ be the side of the rhombus and $\alpha=\angle BAC$. Then $D=(s,0)$, $C=(s\cos\alpha,s\sin\alpha)$ and $E=D+C=(s+s\cos\alpha,\ s\sin\alpha)$. Since $\angle EBA=90^\circ$, $B$ is the foot of $E$ on line $AB$: $B=\left(s(1+\cos\alpha),0\right)$.

Triangle $ECA$ is half of the rhombus, and triangle $EBA$ has base $AB$ and height $s\sin\alpha$:
\[\dfrac12s^2\sin\alpha=2,\qquad\dfrac12s(1+\cos\alpha)\cdot s\sin\alpha=3.\]
Dividing, $1+\cos\alpha=\dfrac32$, so $\alpha=60^\circ$ and $s^2=\dfrac{4}{\sin60^\circ}=\dfrac{8}{\sqrt3}$. Now $AB=\dfrac32s$ and $AC=s$, and by the law of cosines
\[BC^2=\dfrac94s^2+s^2-2\cdot\dfrac32s\cdot s\cdot\dfrac12=\dfrac74s^2=\dfrac{14}{\sqrt3}=\dfrac{14\sqrt3}{3},\qquad BC=\boxed{\sqrt{\dfrac{14\sqrt3}{3}}}\approx2.84.\]
(Check: $AB^2=\dfrac94s^2<AC^2+BC^2=\dfrac{11}{4}s^2$, so the triangle is acute; $AB>AC$; $D$ lies on $AB$; and $E$, level with $C$ and directly above $B$, is outside the triangle.)

