Let $\beta=\angle ABC$. The ray $BA'$ is perpendicular to $BC$, on one side or the other, so the rotation angle $\theta=\angle ABA'$ is $90^\circ-\beta$ or $90^\circ+\beta$. Since $BA'=BA$, the law of cosines gives
\[AA'^2=2AB^2(1-\cos\theta),\]
which is smaller for the smaller angle $\theta=90^\circ-\beta$, where $\cos\theta=\sin\beta=\dfrac{AC}{BC}$:
\[AA'^2=2AB^2\left(1-\dfrac{AC}{BC}\right).\]
\emph{Which sides are given?} The hypotenuse $BC$ is the longest side, so it cannot be $3<2\sqrt3$. Either the two given lengths are the legs, and $BC=\sqrt{12+9}=\sqrt{21}$; or $BC=2\sqrt3$ and one leg is $3$, so the other leg is $\sqrt{12-9}=\sqrt3$ (a $30^\circ$-$60^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle). In each case either leg can be $AB$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $AB=3$, $AC=2\sqrt3$, $BC=\sqrt{21}$: $AA'^2=18\left(1-\dfrac{2}{\sqrt7}\right)\approx4.39$.
\item $AB=2\sqrt3$, $AC=3$, $BC=\sqrt{21}$: $AA'^2=24\left(1-\dfrac{3}{\sqrt{21}}\right)\approx8.29$.
\item $AB=3$, $AC=\sqrt3$, $BC=2\sqrt3$: $\beta=30^\circ$ and $\theta=60^\circ$, so triangle $ABA'$ is equilateral and $AA'^2=9$.
\item $AB=\sqrt3$, $AC=3$, $BC=2\sqrt3$: $\beta=60^\circ$ and $\theta=30^\circ$, so $AA'^2=6\left(1-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)=6-3\sqrt3\approx0.80$.
\end{itemize}
The least value comes from the last case (shown in the figure, where $A'$ is the rotation through $30^\circ$ and $A''$ the one through $150^\circ$):
\[AA'=\sqrt{6-3\sqrt3}=\boxed{\dfrac{3\sqrt2-\sqrt6}{2}}\approx0.90,\]
since $\left(\dfrac{3\sqrt2-\sqrt6}{2}\right)^2=\dfrac{24-12\sqrt3}{4}=6-3\sqrt3$.

