question:
Square $ABCD$ and equilateral triangle $ABE$ share the side $AB$, with $E$ inside the square. A circle lies inside the square and outside the triangle, and is tangent to sides $AD$ and $CD$ of the square and to side $AE$ of the triangle, as shown. If the radius of the circle is $9-3\sqrt3$, what is the side length of the square?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=18,r=9-3*sqrt(3);
pair A=(0,0),B=(s,0),C=(s,s),D=(0,s),Ep=(s/2,s*sqrt(3)/2);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(A--Ep--B);
draw(circle((r,s-r),r));
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$E$",Ep,N);
\end{asy}

solution:
Put $A=(0,0)$, $B=(s,0)$, $D=(0,s)$, where $s$ is the side. Line $AE$ makes $60^\circ$ with $AB$, so it is $\sqrt3x-y=0$. The circle is tangent to $x=0$ and $y=s$ and lies in the square, so its centre is $O=(r,s-r)$. It lies on the side of line $AE$ away from the triangle (where $\sqrt3x-y<0$), and its distance to the line equals $r$:
\[\dfrac{(s-r)-\sqrt3\,r}{2}=r\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad s=\left(3+\sqrt3\right)r.\]
With $r=9-3\sqrt3$:
\[s=\left(3+\sqrt3\right)\left(9-3\sqrt3\right)=27-9\sqrt3+9\sqrt3-9=\boxed{18}.\]
(The point of tangency on line $AE$ is at distance $\dfrac{r+\sqrt3(s-r)}{2}=s-r\approx14.2<18$ from $A$, so it lies on side $AE$, as required.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=18,r=9-3*sqrt(3);
pair A=(0,0),B=(s,0),C=(s,s),D=(0,s),Ep=(s/2,s*sqrt(3)/2),O=(r,s-r);
pair T=((O.x+sqrt(3)*O.y)/4)*(1,sqrt(3));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(A--Ep--B);
draw(circle(O,r));
draw(O--(0,O.y),dashed);draw(O--(O.x,s),dashed);draw(O--T,dashed);
dot(O);dot(T);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$E$",Ep,N);label("$O$",O,S);
label("$r$",(O+T)/2,SE,fontsize(8pt));label("$60^\circ$",A,5*dir(30),fontsize(8pt));label("$18$",(A+B)/2,S);
\end{asy}

question:
Find the error in the following ``proof'' that $a^n=1$ for every real number $a$ and every integer $n\ge0$.

\emph{We use strong induction on $n$. Base case: $a^0=1$. Inductive step: assume $a^k=1$ for all $k$ with $0\le k\le n$. Then}
\[a^{n+1}=\dfrac{a^n\cdot a^n}{a^{n-1}}=\dfrac{1\cdot1}{1}=1.\]

solution:
Let the side be $s$. In triangle $BCD$, $BC=CD=s$ and $\angle BCD=120^\circ$. The perpendicular from $C$ to $BD$ bisects $BD$ and the angle, giving two $30^\circ$-$60^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangles with hypotenuse $s$, so $BD=2\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s=s\sqrt3$. Then $s\sqrt3=3\sqrt3$, $s=3$, and the perimeter is $6s=\boxed{18}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] P;for(int i=0;i<6;++i)P[i]=3*dir(120+60*i);
pair A=P[0],B=P[1],C=P[2],D=P[3],Ee=P[4],F=P[5];
draw(A--B--C--D--Ee--F--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(B--D,dashed);
pair H=(B+D)/2;draw(C--H,dotted);
label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,SW);label("$D$",D,SE);label("$E$",Ee,E);label("$F$",F,NE);
label("$s$",(B+C)/2,W);label("$s$",(C+D)/2,S);draw(arc(C,0.35,0,120));label("$120^\circ$",C+0.35*dir(20),dir(20),fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

The condition says $\sin(A+B)=1$. Since $0^\circ<A+B<360^\circ$, this forces $A+B=90^\circ$.

Because $AB>CD$ and $A+B<180^\circ$, the legs $AD$ and $BC$ extended beyond $D$ and $C$ meet at a point $P$, and in triangle $PAB$
\[\angle APB=180^\circ-(A+B)=90^\circ.\]
Since $DC\parallel AB$, triangle $PDC$ is the image of triangle $PAB$ under the dilation centred at $P$ with ratio $\dfrac{CD}{AB}$. This dilation maps the midpoint $M$ of $AB$ to the midpoint $N$ of $DC$, so $P$, $N$, $M$ are collinear with $N$ between $P$ and $M$.

The midpoint of the hypotenuse of a right triangle is equidistant from the three vertices, so in the right triangles $PAB$ and $PDC$,
\[PM=\dfrac{AB}{2},\qquad PN=\dfrac{CD}{2}.\]
Therefore $MN=PM-PN=\dfrac{AB-CD}{2}$. $\blacksquare$

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(10,0),P=6.4279*dir(50);
pair D=P+0.4*(A-P),C=P+0.4*(B-P),M=(A+B)/2,Nm=(D+C)/2;
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(D--P--C,dashed);draw(P--M,dashed);
pair u=unit(A-P),v=unit(B-P);draw(P+0.4u--P+0.4u+0.4v--P+0.4v);
dot(M);dot(Nm);dot(P);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$P$",P,N);label("$M$",M,S);label("$N$",Nm,dir(-35));
\end{asy}

Since $\angle ABC=90^\circ$, the hypotenuse $AC$ is a diameter of the circumcircle, so the circumcentre $O$ is the midpoint of $\overline{AC}$. By construction $O$ is also the midpoint of $\overline{BB'}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),A=(0,3),C=(4,0),O=(2,1.5),Bp=(4,3);
draw(circle(O,2.5),gray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(A--Bp--C,linewidth(0.8));
draw(B--Bp,dashed);draw(A--C,dashed);
draw((0,0.3)--(0.3,0.3)--(0.3,0));
dot(O);label("$O$",O,N);
label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$B'$",Bp,NE);
\end{asy}

So the diagonals $AC$ and $BB'$ of quadrilateral $ABCB'$ bisect each other, and $ABCB'$ is a parallelogram. A parallelogram with a right angle is a rectangle (consecutive angles of a parallelogram are supplementary, and opposite angles are equal, so all four angles are $90^\circ$). Since $\angle ABC=90^\circ$, $ABCB'$ is a rectangle. (Also $BB'=2R=AC$: the diagonals are equal, as they must be in a rectangle.) $\blacksquare$

