For $1\le n\le100$, $s(n)\le18$. The chain $s(n),s(s(n)),\dots$ contains a multiple of $7$ exactly when $s(n)\in\{7,14\}$, or $s(n)=16$ (since $s(16)=7$); the other values $10,11,12,13,15,17,18$ lead to $1,2,3,4,6,8,9$. Counting:
\begin{itemize}
\item $s(n)=7$: $7,16,25,34,43,52,61,70$ ($8$ numbers);
\item $s(n)=14$: $59,68,77,86,95$ ($5$ numbers);
\item $s(n)=16$: $79,88,97$ ($3$ numbers).
\end{itemize}
So there are $\boxed{16}$ interesting numbers.

The proposition is false exactly when some card has a vowel on one side and an odd number on the other, so we must check every card that could be such a card.
\begin{itemize}
\item E: a vowel; if its other side is odd, the proposition fails. \emph{Turn it.}
\item K: a consonant; the proposition says nothing about consonants. Do not turn it.
\item $4$: an even number; whatever letter is on the back, the proposition holds for this card (it does not claim that even numbers have vowels: that would be the converse). Do not turn it.
\item $7$: an odd number; if its other side is a vowel, the proposition fails. \emph{Turn it.} (This is the contrapositive: ``odd number $\Rightarrow$ consonant''.)
\end{itemize}
So you must turn over $\boxed{\text{E and }7}$.

question:
Johnny wants to fly to the moon, which we assume is $40{,}000$ miles away. He can hold his breath for $15$ minutes, and there is a station on the moon where he can breathe if he gets there. His flight is a straight path from Earth towards the moon, and his distance from Earth after flying for $t$ hours is $f(t)=-16t^2+1600t$ miles.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Does his path reach the moon? If so, after how many hours?
\item[(b)] Can he reach the moon on a single breath?
\end{enumerate}

John plays a game on an $n\times n$ board. Before the game he chooses some tiles to be water tiles; all other tiles are air tiles. The starting position is stage $1$. To pass from stage $s$ to stage $s+1$, every air tile that shares a side with at least two water tiles becomes a water tile (all such changes happen at once). The game ends when a stage is the same as the previous one.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find, with proof, the least number of water tiles John must place so that eventually the whole board is water.
\item[(b)] Show that this number of tiles placed along a diagonal works, and find the stage at which the board first becomes entirely water.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the least possible positive difference between two positive integers whose squares differ by $400$?
\item[(b)] A student argues: consecutive squares differ by consecutive odd numbers, so if $x,y,z$ are consecutive integers with $y^2-x^2=199$ and $z^2-y^2=201$, then $z^2-x^2=400$ and $z-x=2$. Show that for every multiple $N$ of $4$ with $N>4$, the least positive difference between two positive integers whose squares differ by $N$ is $2$. What happens when $N=4$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
A dilation with centre $P$ and factor $k$ multiplies every distance by $k$. It maps the centre $O$ of the smaller circle to the centre $O'$ of the image, and a point at distance $r$ from $O$ to a point at distance $kr$ from $O'$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(0,0);pair O1=(2,0),O2=2.2*O1;real r=0.55;
draw(circle(O1,r));draw(circle(O2,2.2*r),linewidth(0.8));
pair Q1=O1+r*dir(-90),Q2=P+2.2*(Q1-P);
draw(P--O2,dashed);draw(P--Q2,dashed);
draw(O1--Q1);draw(O2--Q2,linewidth(0.8));
dot(P);dot(O1);dot(O2);
label("$P$",P,W);label("$r$",(O1+Q1)/2,E,fontsize(8pt));label("$R$",(O2+Q2)/2,E);
label("$O$",O1,2N,fontsize(8pt));label("$O'$",O2,2N);
\end{asy}

So $R=kr$. Then $D=2R=k(2r)=kd$ and $C=2\pi R=k(2\pi r)=kc$. Hence
\[\dfrac Cc=\dfrac Rr=\dfrac Dd=\boxed{k}.\]

question:
A student has $7$ days to work on the $7$ problems of a problem set. A schedule says which problems he works on each day, and it must satisfy:
\begin{enumerate}
\item he works on exactly $3$ different problems every day;
\item no two days have the same set of $3$ problems;
\item every one of the $7$ problems is worked on at least once.
\end{enumerate}
How many different schedules are there?

solution:
Number the vertices $0,1,\dots,7$ around the octagon, vertex $t$ at angle $45^\circ t$ on its circumcircle. The chord joining the points at angles $\alpha$ and $\beta$ is perpendicular to the direction $\dfrac{\alpha+\beta}{2}$, so its direction depends only on $\alpha+\beta$ modulo $360^\circ$. Hence the chords $\{i,j\}$ and $\{k,l\}$ are parallel exactly when $i+j\equiv k+l\pmod8$.

The sides $\{i,i+1\}$ have odd sums. For each odd residue $s$ there are $4$ chords with $i+j\equiv s$, namely $2$ sides and $2$ diagonals; for $s=1$ these are the sides $\{0,1\},\{4,5\}$ and the diagonals $\{2,7\},\{3,6\}$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V;for(int i=0;i<8;++i)V[i]=dir(-90-22.5+45*i);
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--V[6]--V[7]--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(V[0]--V[1],linewidth(2));draw(V[4]--V[5],linewidth(2));
draw(V[2]--V[7],dashed);draw(V[3]--V[6],dashed);
for(int i=0;i<8;++i)label("$"+string(i)+"$",V[i],V[i]);
\end{asy}

So $4\cdot2=8$ diagonals are parallel to a side. Diagonals with even sums are parallel to no side. The octagon has $\dfrac{8\cdot5}{2}=20$ diagonals, so $20-8=\boxed{12}$ are not parallel to any side.

question:
Let $ABCD$ be a square, and let $M$ and $N$ be the midpoints of $\overline{AB}$ and $\overline{BC}$, respectively.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $\overline{CM}\perp\overline{DN}$.
\item[(b)] A student's proof begins: ``Triangle $DCN$ is congruent to triangle $MCN$ by LL, since $MN=CN$ and $DC=CB$.'' What is wrong with this step, and which congruence should be used instead?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let $P$ be the intersection of $CM$ and $DN$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,1),B=(1,1),C=(1,0),D=(0,0);pair M=(0.5,1),Nn=(1,0.5);
pair P=extension(C,M,D,Nn);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(C--M);draw(D--Nn);
dot(M);dot(Nn);dot(P);
label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,NE);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,SW);
label("$M$",M,N);label("$N$",Nn,E);label("$P$",P,dir(160));
pair u=unit(M-P),v=unit(Nn-P);draw(P+0.07*u--P+0.07*u+0.07*v--P+0.07*v);
\end{asy}

Triangles $CBM$ and $DCN$ have $CB=DC$ (sides of the square), $BM=CN$ (half-sides) and right angles at $B$ and $C$, so they are congruent (SAS). Hence $\angle BCM=\angle CDN$. In right triangle $DCN$, $\angle CDN+\angle CND=90^\circ$. Therefore in triangle $PCN$,
