Track $A$'s lead $\ell$ (votes for $A$ minus votes for $B$), which must stay in $\{-2,\dots,2\}$. After an even number of votes, $\ell\in\{-2,0,2\}$. Look at the next two votes:
\begin{itemize}
\item from $\ell=0$: $AA$ gives $2$, $BB$ gives $-2$, and $AB$, $BA$ give $0$;
\item from $\ell=2$: the next vote must be $B$, and then $A$ gives $2$ while $B$ gives $0$ (similarly from $\ell=-2$).
\end{itemize}
Let $a_t$ be the number of ways to have $\ell=0$ after $2t$ votes and $b_t$ the number with $\ell=2$ (equal to the number with $\ell=-2$). Then $a_0=1$, $b_0=0$, and
\[a_{t+1}=2a_t+2b_t,\qquad b_{t+1}=a_t+b_t.\]
This gives $(a_t,b_t)=(2,1),(6,3),(18,9),(54,27),(162,81),(486,243)$ for $t=1,\dots,6$. The count ends tied after $12$ votes, so the answer is $a_6=\boxed{486}$.

$g(m)=2$ exactly when the largest proper divisor of $m$ is $1$, i.e. when $m$ is prime. So we need $g(n)$ to be prime. The largest proper divisor of $n$ is $\dfrac np$, where $p$ is the smallest prime factor of $n$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $n$ is prime, $g(n)=2$, which is prime: all $25$ primes up to $100$ count.
\item If $n$ is composite, $d=\dfrac np\ge2$ and we need $d+1$ prime, so $d$ is even (an odd $d\ge3$ makes $d+1$ even and greater than $2$). Then $2\mid n$, so $p=2$ and $n=2d\le100$. The even $d\le50$ with $d+1$ prime are $2,4,6,10,12,16,18,22,28,30,36,40,42,46$: $14$ values.
\end{itemize}
The total is $25+14=\boxed{39}$.

