question:
(The lifting-the-exponent lemma.) For a prime $p$ and a nonzero integer $m$, let $v_p(m)$ be the exponent of $p$ in the prime factorisation of $m$. Let $p$ be an odd prime and let $x\ne y$ be integers with $p\mid x-y$ and $p\nmid xy$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that for every positive integer $n$,
\[v_p\left(x^n-y^n\right)=v_p(x-y)+v_p(n).\]
\item[(b)] Use (a) to find the largest power of $3$ that divides $10^{27}-1$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $R$ be the radius of the circle and $d_1,d_2,d_3$ the distances from $P$ to the lines $XY$, $YZ$, $ZX$. The foot of the perpendicular from $P$ to a chord is the chord's midpoint, so
\[AB=2\sqrt{R^2-d_1^2},\qquad CD=2\sqrt{R^2-d_2^2},\qquad EF=2\sqrt{R^2-d_3^2}.\]
Equal chords give $d_1=d_2=d_3$. Also, since $A$ and $B$ lie on side $XY$, the midpoint of $AB$ does too. So the foot of the perpendicular from $P$ to line $XY$ lies on the side $XY$ itself, and similarly for the other two sides.

A point equidistant from lines $XY$ and $XZ$ lies on one of the two bisectors (internal or external) of the angle between them, and similarly at $Y$ and $Z$. So the points equidistant from all three side lines are the incentre $I$ and the three excentres.

$P$ is not an excentre. The excentre $J_X$ opposite $X$ is the centre of the excircle that touches line $XY$ at the foot of the perpendicular from $J_X$. The tangent length from $X$ to this circle is the semiperimeter $s>XY$, so this foot lies beyond $Y$, outside side $XY$. In the same way, the excentre opposite $Y$ has its foot on line $XY$ beyond $X$, and the excentre opposite $Z$ has its feet on lines $ZX$ and $ZY$ beyond $X$ and $Y$. Each excentre therefore has a foot outside a side, which we showed is impossible for $P$.

Hence $P=I$, the incentre. $\blacksquare$ (The given angles are not needed.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
