pair I=(a*X+b*Y+c*Z)/(a+b+c);
real r=I.y; real s=(a+b+c)/2;
real h=0.6*min(s-a,min(s-b,s-c)); real R=sqrt(r*r+h*h);
pair ft(pair P,pair U,pair V){return U+dot(P-U,unit(V-U))*unit(V-U);}
pair M1=ft(I,X,Y),M2=ft(I,Y,Z),M3=ft(I,Z,X);
pair A=M1-h*unit(Y-X),B=M1+h*unit(Y-X),C=M2-h*unit(Z-Y),D=M2+h*unit(Z-Y),Ep=M3-h*unit(X-Z),F=M3+h*unit(X-Z);
draw(X--Y--Z--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(circle(I,R));draw(circle(I,r),dotted);
draw(I--M1,dashed);draw(I--M2,dashed);draw(I--M3,dashed);
dot(I);dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(Ep);dot(F);
label("$X$",X,SW);label("$Y$",Y,SE);label("$Z$",Z,N);label("$P$",I,E,fontsize(9pt));
label("$A$",A,S);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NE);label("$E$",Ep,NW);label("$F$",F,NW);
\end{asy}

In the sketch, the dashed segments are the perpendiculars from $P$ to the chords, and the dotted circle is the incircle.

Raising $\dfrac{\sqrt x\,\sqrt[3]y}{z}=\dfrac{15^{\dfrac32}}{2^4}$ to the sixth power gives
\[2^{24}\,x^3y^2=3^9\,5^9\,z^6.\]
For a prime $q$ let $\alpha,\beta,\gamma$ be its exponents in $x,y,z$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $q=2$: $3\alpha+2\beta+24=6\gamma$, so $\gamma\ge4$ and $16\mid z$.
\item $q=3$ or $5$: $3\alpha+2\beta=9+6\gamma$. Then $\alpha$ is odd. Either $\alpha\ge3$, or $\alpha=1$ and $2\beta=6+6\gamma$, so $\beta\ge3$. Thus $q^3\mid x$, or else $q\mid x$ and $q^3\mid y$.
\end{itemize}
This leaves four cases for the primes $3$ and $5$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $3^3\cdot5^3\mid x$: then $x\ge3375$;
\item $3^3\cdot5\mid x$ and $5^3\mid y$: then $x+y+z\ge135+125+16=276$;
\item $3\cdot5^3\mid x$ and $3^3\mid y$: then $x+y+z\ge375+27+16=418$;
\item $3^3\cdot5^3\mid y$: then $y\ge3375$.
\end{itemize}
So $x+y+z\ge276$. Equality holds for $x=135$, $y=125$, $z=16$:
\[\dfrac{\sqrt{135}\,\sqrt[3]{125}}{16}=\dfrac{3\sqrt{15}\cdot5}{16}=\dfrac{15\sqrt{15}}{16}.\]
The minimum is $\boxed{276}$.

