question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Use inclusion--exclusion to count the integers from $1$ to $30$ that are relatively prime to $30$, and show that the count equals $30\left(1-\dfrac12\right)\left(1-\dfrac13\right)\left(1-\dfrac15\right)=8$.
\item[(b)] Explain why this is not a coincidence: if $p_1,\dots,p_k$ are the distinct prime factors of $n$, then the number of integers from $1$ to $n$ relatively prime to $n$ is $n\left(1-\dfrac{1}{p_1}\right)\cdots\left(1-\dfrac{1}{p_k}\right)$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} A number shares a factor with $30=2\cdot3\cdot5$ exactly when it is divisible by $2$, $3$ or $5$. Among $1,\dots,30$:
\begin{itemize}
\item there are $15$, $10$ and $6$ multiples of $2$, $3$ and $5$;
\item there are $5$, $3$ and $2$ multiples of $6$, $10$ and $15$;
\item there is $1$ multiple of $30$.
\end{itemize}
By inclusion--exclusion the count of numbers relatively prime to $30$ is
\[30-(15+10+6)+(5+3+2)-1=8,\]
namely $1,7,11,13,17,19,23,29$. Expanding the product gives exactly the same terms:
\[30\left(1-\dfrac12-\dfrac13-\dfrac15+\dfrac16+\dfrac1{10}+\dfrac1{15}-\dfrac1{30}\right)=30-15-10-6+5+3+2-1=8.\]

\textbf{(b)} For a set $T$ of the primes $p_i$, let $P_T$ be their product (with $P_\emptyset=1$). Then $P_T\mid n$, and exactly $\dfrac{n}{P_T}$ of the numbers $1,\dots,n$ are divisible by every prime in $T$. By inclusion--exclusion, the count of numbers divisible by none of the $p_i$ is
\[\sum_{T}(-1)^{|T|}\dfrac{n}{P_T}=n\left(1-\dfrac{1}{p_1}\right)\left(1-\dfrac{1}{p_2}\right)\cdots\left(1-\dfrac{1}{p_k}\right),\]
because expanding the product produces one term $(-1)^{|T|}\dfrac{1}{P_T}$ for each set $T$. $\blacksquare$

Diagonal $BD$ splits the quadrilateral into triangles $ABD$ and $BCD$. By the triangle inequality:
\begin{itemize}
\item in triangle $ABD$, $BD<AB+DA=5+9=14$;
\item in triangle $BCD$, $BD+CD>BC$, i.e. $BD>17-5=12$.
\end{itemize}
So $12<BD<14$, and the integer is $BD=\boxed{13}$. This value is possible: triangles with sides $5,9,13$ and $5,13,17$ exist, and placing them on opposite sides of a common side $BD=13$ gives the quadrilateral.

