question:
Write the equation of the line $2x-3y+5=0$ in
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] slope-intercept form $y=mx+c$;
\item[(b)] intercept form $\dfrac xa+\dfrac yb=1$;
\item[(c)] normal form $x\cos\omega+y\sin\omega=p$ with $p>0$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} $3y=2x+5$, so $y=\dfrac23x+\dfrac53$: slope $\dfrac23$ and $y$-intercept $\dfrac53$.

\textbf{(b)} $2x-3y=-5$. Dividing by $-5$ gives
\[\dfrac{x}{-\dfrac52}+\dfrac{y}{\dfrac53}=1,\]
so the intercepts are $-\dfrac52$ on the $x$-axis and $\dfrac53$ on the $y$-axis.

\textbf{(c)} Write the equation with a positive constant, $-2x+3y=5$, and divide by $\sqrt{(-2)^2+3^2}=\sqrt{13}$:
\[x\left(-\dfrac{2}{\sqrt{13}}\right)+y\cdot\dfrac{3}{\sqrt{13}}=\dfrac{5}{\sqrt{13}}.\]
So $\cos\omega=-\dfrac{2}{\sqrt{13}}$, $\sin\omega=\dfrac{3}{\sqrt{13}}$ (so $\omega\approx123.7^\circ$), and $p=\dfrac{5}{\sqrt{13}}=\dfrac{5\sqrt{13}}{13}$. Here $p$ is the distance from the origin to the line, and $\omega$ is the direction of the perpendicular $ON$ from the origin to the line, with foot $N=p(\cos\omega,\sin\omega)=\left(-\dfrac{10}{13},\dfrac{15}{13}\right)$.

\begin{asy}
