question:
In quadrilateral $ABCD$, angles $A$ and $B$ are supplementary, i.e. $\angle A+\angle B=180^\circ$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that angles $C$ and $D$ are also supplementary.
\item[(b)] Prove that $\overline{AD}\parallel\overline{BC}$.
\item[(c)] If, in addition, angles $A$ and $D$ are supplementary, what kind of quadrilateral is $ABCD$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The angles of a quadrilateral add up to $360^\circ$, so $\angle C+\angle D=360^\circ-180^\circ=180^\circ$.

In particular all four angles are less than $180^\circ$, so $ABCD$ is convex, and $C$ and $D$ lie on the same side of line $AB$.

\textbf{(b)} Lines $AD$ and $BC$ are cut by the transversal $AB$, and $\angle DAB$ and $\angle ABC$ are co-interior angles (on the same side of $AB$) with sum $180^\circ$. Hence the lines are parallel.

In detail: suppose the lines met at a point $X$. If $X$ is on the same side of $AB$ as $C$ and $D$, triangle $ABX$ would have angles $\angle A$, $\angle B$ and $\angle X>0$ summing to more than $180^\circ$. If $X$ is on the other side, its angles at $A$ and $B$ would be $180^\circ-\angle A$ and $180^\circ-\angle B$, which again sum to $180^\circ$. Both are impossible.

\textbf{(c)} By the same argument with transversal $AD$, $\angle A+\angle D=180^\circ$ gives $AB\parallel DC$. With both pairs of opposite sides parallel, $ABCD$ is a $\boxed{\text{parallelogram}}$. Conversely, every parallelogram has both properties. $\blacksquare$

