Nobody stays at home, so the people at a house are exactly the friends who chose it. Every friend meets someone exactly when each house that receives visitors receives at least $2$. The visitor counts then form a partition of $5$ into parts of size at least $2$: either $5$, or $3+2$.
\begin{itemize}
\item All five at one house is impossible, because its owner cannot choose their own house.
\item Three at house $h$ and two at house $k$: choose the ordered pair $(h,k)$ in $5\cdot4=20$ ways. The owner of $h$ cannot be at $h$, so is at $k$; likewise the owner of $k$ is at $h$. Of the other three friends, choose the one who joins the owner of $h$ at house $k$: $3$ ways. The remaining two go to $h$ with the owner of $k$.
\end{itemize}
This gives $20\cdot3=60$ favourable outcomes out of $4^5=1024$, so the probability is $\dfrac{60}{1024}=\dfrac{15}{256}$, and $m+n=\boxed{271}$.

\emph{Upper bound.} Two segments that do not overlap meet in at most one point. Each of the $4$ sides of one quadrilateral therefore meets each of the $4$ sides of the other at most once, giving at most $4\cdot4=16$ points.

\emph{Construction.} Take the arrowhead-shaped quadrilaterals
\[ABCD:\ A=(-9,0),\ B=(9,4),\ C=(-4,0),\ D=(9,-6);\qquad PQRS:\ P=(0,10),\ Q=(-4,-8),\ R=(0,4),\ S=(5,-8).\]
Both are non-self-intersecting, since $C$ lies inside triangle $ABD$ and $R$ inside triangle $PQS$. For $-4\le x\le9$, the four sides of $ABCD$ are the graphs of
\[y=\dfrac{2(x+9)}{9},\qquad y=\dfrac{4(x+4)}{13},\qquad y=-\dfrac{6(x+4)}{13},\qquad y=-\dfrac{x+9}{3}.\]
Each side of $PQRS$ lies in this strip and joins $Q$ or $S$ to $P$ or $R$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $Q$ and $S$ lie below all four graphs: at $x=-4$ the graphs are at least $-\dfrac53$, and at $x=5$ at least $-\dfrac{14}{3}$, while $Q$ and $S$ are at height $-8$.
\item $P$ and $R$ lie above them: at $x=0$ the graphs are at most $2$, while $P$ and $R$ are at heights $10$ and $4$.
\end{itemize}
So each side of $PQRS$ crosses each of the four graphs, giving $16$ crossings. They are distinct points: the sides of $ABCD$ meet each other only at its vertices, and the sides of $PQRS$ only at its vertices, and none of these vertices lies on the other quadrilateral. So the maximum is $\boxed{16}$.

