Start from $g(x)=x(x^2-1)(x^2-4)=x^5-5x^3+4x$, which has five roots $0,\pm1,\pm2$ and therefore wiggles four times. Shift it up so that only the leftmost branch crosses the axis:
\[f(x)=x^5-5x^3+4x+10.\]

\textbf{Four turning points.} $f'(x)=5x^4-15x^2+4$ is a quadratic in $x^2$ with roots
\[x^2=\dfrac{15\pm\sqrt{145}}{10},\]
and both are positive (as $\sqrt{145}<15$). So $f'$ has four distinct real roots $\pm t_1,\pm t_2$, each simple, and $f'$ changes sign at each of them. Hence $f$ has exactly four turning points. Since $\sqrt{145}<13$, all of them satisfy $x^2<\dfrac{28}{10}<4$, i.e. $|x|<2$.

\textbf{One $x$-intercept.} We show $g(x)>-10$ for $x\ge-2$:
\begin{itemize}
\item On $[-2,-1]$ and on $[0,1]$, the three factors $x$, $x^2-1$, $x^2-4$ give $g(x)\ge0$.
\item On $[-1,0]$: $|g(x)|=|x|\left(1-x^2\right)\left(4-x^2\right)\le1\cdot1\cdot4=4$.
\item On $[1,2]$: $g(x)=-x\left(x^2-1\right)\left(4-x^2\right)$, and by AM-GM $\left(x^2-1\right)\left(4-x^2\right)\le\left(\dfrac{3}{2}\right)^2$, so $g(x)\ge-2\cdot\dfrac94=-4.5$.
\item For $x\ge2$ all factors are nonnegative, so $g(x)\ge0$.
\end{itemize}
Hence $f(x)=g(x)+10\ge5.5>0$ for all $x\ge-2$. For $x\le-2$ we have $f'(x)=5x^2\left(x^2-3\right)+4\ge5\cdot4\cdot1+4>0$, so $f$ is strictly increasing there. Since $f(-3)=-110<0<10=f(-2)$, $f$ has exactly one root $r$, which lies in $(-3,-2)$.

So
\[\boxed{f(x)=x^5-5x^3+4x+10}\]
works. In the sketch below the vertical scale is compressed by a factor of $5$; the four dots are the turning points and $r$ is the only $x$-intercept.

