question:
Eight zeros are written on a board. In one turn we choose four of the numbers $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$ on the board and replace them with $a+3$, $b+3$, $c+2$, $d+1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the minimum number of turns after which the board can show eight consecutive integers?
\item[(b)] Can the board show eight numbers all equal to $2015$ after some turns?
\item[(c)] Can the product of the numbers on the board be $2145$ after some turns?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $x=[AXB_1]$ and $y=[BXA_1]$, so $[ABC]=p+q+r+x+y$.

\emph{Step 1: $xy=pq$.} Triangles $AXB_1$ and $A_1XB_1$ have the same height from $B_1$ to line $AA_1$, so $\dfrac xq=\dfrac{AX}{XA_1}$. Likewise triangles $ABX$ and $A_1BX$ have the same height from $B$, so $\dfrac py=\dfrac{AX}{XA_1}$. Hence $\dfrac xq=\dfrac py$, i.e. $xy=pq$.

\emph{Step 2.} Triangles $AB_1A_1$ and $CB_1A_1$ have the same height from $A_1$ to line $AC$, and so do triangles $ABB_1$ and $CBB_1$ from $B$. Both ratios equal $\dfrac{AB_1}{B_1C}$:
\[\dfrac{x+q}{r}=\dfrac{p+x}{r+q+y}.\]
Cross-multiplying, $xr+xq+xy+qr+q^2+qy=rp+rx$. Using $xy=pq$,
\[q(x+y)=rp-pq-q^2-qr.\]
Therefore
\[[ABC]=p+q+r+x+y=\dfrac{q(p+q+r)+rp-pq-q^2-qr}{q}=\boxed{\dfrac{pr}{q}}.\]
(Check: for the medians, $p=\dfrac{[ABC]}{3}$, $q=\dfrac{[ABC]}{12}$ and $r=\dfrac{[ABC]}{4}$, and indeed $\dfrac{pr}{q}=[ABC]$.)

