Two distinct circles meet in at most $2$ points, and there are $\dbinom42=6$ pairs of circles, so there are at most $6\cdot2=12$ intersection points.

Twelve are possible. Take four circles of radius $4$ centred at the vertices of a square of side $5$. Any two centres are at distance $5$ or $5\sqrt2\approx7.07$, both less than $8$, so every pair of circles meets in two points. No point lies on three of the circles: such a point would be at distance $4$ from three vertices of the square, so it would be the centre of the square, whose distance to the vertices is $\dfrac{5\sqrt2}{2}\approx3.54\ne4$. Hence the $12$ points are distinct. The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(D) } 12}$.

