question:
John wants to sell his new product to $20000$ Instagram users and has two options.
\begin{itemize}
\item Advertise on Instagram himself, at $3$ dollars per targeted user. Out of every $100$ users who learn about the product, $8$ buy it.
\item Hire a marketing company that charges $14$ dollars per targeted user and guarantees that at least $10\%$ of the targeted users will buy. Its best campaign ever reached $15\%$.
\end{itemize}
Each product sold brings $800$ dollars of profit before advertising costs.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find John's net profit with each option, assuming the company achieves exactly its guaranteed $10\%$. Which option should he choose?
\item[(b)] Find the smallest sales rate the company must achieve for its option to be better, and explain why the $15\%$ figure is not needed to decide.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $S=1^6+2^6+\cdots+2021^6$.
\begin{itemize}
\item Mod $2$: $n^6$ has the parity of $n$, and there are $1011$ odd numbers from $1$ to $2021$, so $S\equiv1011\equiv1\pmod2$.
\item Mod $3$: if $3\mid n$ then $n^6\equiv0$; otherwise $n^2\equiv1$, so $n^6\equiv1$. There are $673$ multiples of $3$ up to $2021$, so $S\equiv2021-673=1348\equiv1\pmod3$.
\end{itemize}
Hence $S\equiv1\pmod6$, and $S^S\equiv1^S=1\pmod6$. The remainder is $\boxed{1}$.

