question:
Let $c$ be a positive real number.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that if $c\ne1$, then $c^x=c^y$ for real numbers $x,y$ implies $x=y$.
\item[(b)] Show by an example that the conclusion fails for $c=1$.
\end{enumerate}
(So from $(AB)^x=(AB)^y$ with $AB>0$ one may conclude $x=y$ exactly when $AB\ne1$.)

solution:
The roots are
\[x=\dfrac{51\pm\sqrt{51^2-4\cdot279}}{2}=\dfrac{51\pm\sqrt{1485}}{2}=\dfrac{51\pm3\sqrt{165}}{2}.\]
Let $M$ be the midpoint of $AC$. Since $\angle B=90^\circ$, $B$ lies on the circle with diameter $AC$, so $BM=9$, and the altitude $BH$ is at most $BM=9$. The larger root exceeds $25$, so it is impossible. The smaller root works: $\sqrt{165}>11$ gives $x<\dfrac{51-33}{2}=9$, and $x>0$ because $9\cdot165<51^2$. (A point $B$ on the circle at height $x\le9$ above $AC$ exists.)

So $x=\dfrac{51-3\sqrt{165}}{2}\approx6.23$ is the only possibility, and the area is
\[\dfrac12\cdot18\cdot x=9x=\dfrac{459-27\sqrt{165}}{2}.\]
Here $a=459$, $b=-27$, $c=165=3\cdot5\cdot11$ (squarefree), $d=2$, and $\gcd(459,27,2)=1$. Hence
\[a+b+c+7d=459-27+165+14=\boxed{611}.\]

