\textbf{Opposite sides.} For every point $P$, the triangle inequality gives $AP+PB\ge AB$, with equality exactly when $P$ lies on the segment $AB$. Since $A$ and $B$ are on opposite sides of $\ell$, the segment $AB$ crosses $\ell$ at one point. So $P$ is the intersection of segment $AB$ with $\ell$, and the minimum is $AB$.

\textbf{Same side.} Let $B'$ be the reflection of $B$ in $\ell$. For every $P$ on $\ell$, $\ell$ is the perpendicular bisector of $BB'$, so $PB=PB'$ and
\[AP+PB=AP+PB'\ge AB',\]
with equality exactly when $P$ lies on the segment $AB'$. Now $A$ and $B'$ are on opposite sides of $\ell$, so segment $AB'$ meets $\ell$ at one point. That point is $P$, and the minimum is $AB'$.

$\boxed{P=AB\cap\ell\text{ (opposite sides)},\qquad P=AB'\cap\ell\text{ with }B'\text{ the reflection of }B\text{ (same side)}}$

