Left: same side. Right: opposite sides. In both, $P$ is where the perpendicular bisector $m$ meets $\ell$.

\textbf{Same side.} By the triangle inequality in triangle $APB$ (or on a line), $|AP-PB|\le AB$, with equality exactly when $A$, $B$, $P$ are collinear and $P$ is not strictly between $A$ and $B$. Since $A$ and $B$ are at different distances from $\ell$, line $AB$ is not parallel to $\ell$ and meets it at a point $P$. Because $A$ and $B$ are on the same side of $\ell$, this $P$ is not between them, so $|AP-PB|=AB$ there. So $P=AB\cap\ell$ and the maximum is $AB$.

\textbf{Opposite sides.} Let $B'$ be the reflection of $B$ in $\ell$; it is on the same side as $A$, at the same distance from $\ell$ as $B$. For $P$ on $\ell$, $PB=PB'$, so by the first case $|AP-PB|=|AP-PB'|\le AB'$, with equality at the point where line $AB'$ meets $\ell$ (it does, since $A$ and $B'$ are at different distances from $\ell$).

$\boxed{P=AB\cap\ell\text{ (same side, maximum }AB),\qquad P=AB'\cap\ell\text{ (opposite sides, maximum }AB')}$

