Through $D$ draw the line parallel to $BE$; it meets $AC$ at a point $G$ between $E$ and $C$.

In triangle $CBE$, $DG\parallel BE$, so by similar triangles $\dfrac{EG}{EC}=\dfrac{BD}{BC}=\mu$. Since $EC=(1-\lambda)AC$, this gives $EG=\mu(1-\lambda)AC$.

In triangle $ADG$, $FE\parallel DG$ with $F$ on $AD$ and $E$ on $AG$, so
\[\dfrac{AF}{FD}=\dfrac{AE}{EG}=\dfrac{\lambda\,AC}{\mu(1-\lambda)AC}=\dfrac{\lambda}{\mu(1-\lambda)}.\]
So $\boxed{AF:FD=\lambda:\mu(1-\lambda)}$. (For example, for two medians $\lambda=\mu=\dfrac12$ and $AF:FD=2:1$.)

