Let $a_1=\sqrt{132}$ and $a_{k+1}=\sqrt{132+a_k}$; the expression is the limit of $a_k$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $a_k<12$ for all $k$: true for $k=1$, and if $a_k<12$ then $a_{k+1}<\sqrt{144}=12$.
\item The sequence increases: $a_{k+1}^2-a_k^2=132+a_k-a_k^2=(12-a_k)(11+a_k)>0$.
\end{itemize}
An increasing bounded sequence has a limit $L>0$, and letting $k\to\infty$ in $a_{k+1}^2=132+a_k$ gives $L^2=132+L$, i.e. $(L-12)(L+11)=0$. So the value is $\boxed{12}$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Let $m$ and $n$ be integers with $n\ne0$. Prove that $\gcd(m,n)=\gcd(m-n,n)$.
\item[(b)] Use (a) to show that $\gcd(5k+3,\ 3k+2)=1$ for every positive integer $k$.
\end{enumerate}

Six dots are arranged in two rows of three, with unit spacing, as shown.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(2.2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<3;++i) for(int j=0;j<2;++j) dot((i,j));
\end{asy}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many distinct triangles can be drawn using three of the dots as vertices? (Triangles are distinct when their sets of vertices are different.)

$\textbf{(A)}\ 9\qquad\textbf{(B)}\ 12\qquad\textbf{(C)}\ 18\qquad\textbf{(D)}\ 20\qquad\textbf{(E)}\ 24$
\item[(b)] How many pairwise non-congruent triangles are among them?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} There are $\dbinom63=20$ sets of three dots. Three dots are collinear only when they form a whole row, which happens for $2$ sets. So there are $20-2=18$ triangles, answer $\boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ 18}$.

\textbf{(b)} Every triangle has two vertices in one row and one in the other. Use coordinates with the rows $y=0$ and $y=1$; by reflecting, the two same-row vertices can be taken in the bottom row.
\begin{itemize}
\item Base of length $1$, say $(0,0),(1,0)$: the third vertex $(0,1)$ or $(1,1)$ gives sides $1,1,\sqrt2$; the third vertex $(2,1)$ gives $1,\sqrt2,\sqrt5$.
\item Base of length $2$, $(0,0),(2,0)$: the third vertex $(1,1)$ gives $\sqrt2,\sqrt2,2$; the third vertex $(0,1)$ or $(2,1)$ gives $1,2,\sqrt5$.
\end{itemize}
The four side-length triples are different, so there are $\boxed{4}$ non-congruent triangles.

