A $3\times3\times3$ Rubik's cube has $8$ corner pieces, $12$ edge pieces and $6$ centre pieces. The centres never move relative to each other, so hold the cube with the centres fixed, and name the faces U, D, F, B, L, R (up, down, front, back, left, right). A \emph{position} records which piece is in each corner or edge slot and how it is turned there.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that taking the cube apart and reassembling it (centres fixed) gives $8!\cdot3^8\cdot12!\cdot2^{12}$ positions.
\item[(b)] Prove that at most $\dfrac1{12}$ of these positions can be reached from the solved cube by turning faces, and compute this upper bound.
\end{enumerate}
(It is a known theorem that the bound is attained, so it is the exact number of positions; you do not need to prove this.)

The positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$ satisfy
\[a+b+c=20=ab+bc-ca-b^2.\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find all possible values of the product $abc$.
\item[(b)] A solution begins: ``Without loss of generality, $a\ge b\ge c$.'' The right-hand side is not symmetric in $a$, $b$, $c$. Is this assumption justified? Explain.
