Let the sides be $x$, $x$, $y$. The longest side is unique only if it is the base and $y>x$; if $y<x$ the two legs tie for longest, and $y=x$ is equilateral. The triangle inequality requires $y<2x$. With $y=144-2x$:
\[144-2x>x\iff x<48,\qquad 144-2x<2x\iff x>36.\]
So $x=37,38,\dots,47$, which gives $\boxed{11}$ triangles.

