\textbf{Construction.} Take the ray from $C=(0,0)$ along the positive $x$-axis and the point $B=(16,12)$, so $BC=20$. The circle with centre $B$ and radius $13$ meets the $x$-axis where $(x-16)^2+144=169$, i.e. $x=11$ or $x=21$. Let $A=(11,0)$ and $X=(21,0)$, and let $Y=B$, $Z=C$.

So triangle $ABC$ has sides $BC=20$, $AB=13$, $CA=11$, and triangle $XYZ$ has $YZ=20$, $XY=13$, $ZX=21$.

\textbf{Check.} $\dfrac{AB}{XY}=\dfrac{13}{13}=1=\dfrac{20}{20}=\dfrac{BC}{YZ}$. By the law of cosines,
\[\cos\angle BCA=\dfrac{20^2+11^2-13^2}{2\cdot20\cdot11}=\dfrac{352}{440}=\dfrac45,\qquad\cos\angle YZX=\dfrac{20^2+21^2-13^2}{2\cdot20\cdot21}=\dfrac{672}{840}=\dfrac45,\]
so the two angles are equal. (In the construction this is clear: both are the angle between ray $CB$ and the $x$-axis.)

\textbf{Not similar.} Similar triangles have their sides in the same ratios when both are listed in increasing order. Here the sides are $11,13,20$ and $13,20,21$, and $\dfrac{11}{13}\ne\dfrac{13}{20}$. So the triangles are not similar (in any vertex correspondence). $\blacksquare$

