It is enough to show: \emph{every odd integer $c\ge3$ is a leg of a right triangle with integer sides whose hypotenuse is again odd and at least $3$.}

For odd $c$, the numbers $\dfrac{c^2-1}{2}$ and $\dfrac{c^2+1}{2}$ are integers, and
\[c^2+\left(\dfrac{c^2-1}{2}\right)^2=\dfrac{4c^2+c^4-2c^2+1}{4}=\left(\dfrac{c^2+1}{2}\right)^2.\]
So $\left(c,\dfrac{c^2-1}{2},\dfrac{c^2+1}{2}\right)$ is a right triangle with leg $c$. Its hypotenuse is odd: an odd square is $1\pmod8$, so $c^2+1\equiv2\pmod8$ and $\dfrac{c^2+1}{2}\equiv1\pmod4$. It is also larger than $c$.

The first hypotenuse is $5$, which is odd, so by induction every hypotenuse in the chain is odd and the next triangle always exists:
\[(3,4,5),\ (5,12,13),\ (13,84,85),\ (85,3612,3613),\ \dots\ \blacksquare\]
(Even hypotenuses would also be fine: an even $c\ge4$ is a leg of $\left(c,\dfrac{c^2}{4}-1,\dfrac{c^2}{4}+1\right)$.)

