\textbf{Necessity.} Every square is $0$ or $1\pmod4$: $(2k)^2=4k^2$ and $(2k+1)^2=4k^2+4k+1$. So $x^2-y^2\equiv0-0,\ 0-1,\ 1-0$ or $1-1$, that is $0$, $3$, $1$ or $0\pmod4$. It is never $2\pmod4$.

\textbf{Sufficiency.}
\begin{itemize}
\item If $n$ is odd, $n=2k+1=(k+1)^2-k^2$.
\item If $4\mid n$, $n=4k=(k+1)^2-(k-1)^2$.
\end{itemize}
These cover all $n\not\equiv2\pmod4$. So the integers that are not differences of two squares are exactly those $\boxed{n\equiv2\pmod4}$. $\blacksquare$

Let the side be $s$ and use coordinates $A=(0,0)$, $B=(s,0)$, $D=\left(\dfrac s2,\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s\right)$, $C=B+D=\left(\dfrac{3s}2,\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s\right)$, and $P=(x,y)$.

From $PA^2=9$ and $PB^2=1$: $x^2+y^2=9$ and $(x-s)^2+y^2=1$. Subtracting, $2sx-s^2=8$, so $x=\dfrac{s^2+8}{2s}$.

From $PC^2=12$: $x^2+y^2-3sx-\sqrt3sy+3s^2=12$. Using $x^2+y^2=9$ and $3sx=\dfrac{3\left(s^2+8\right)}{2}$,
\[\sqrt3sy=3s^2-\dfrac{3\left(s^2+8\right)}{2}-3=\dfrac{3\left(s^2-10\right)}{2},\qquad y=\dfrac{\sqrt3\left(s^2-10\right)}{2s}.\]
Substituting into $x^2+y^2=9$ and multiplying by $4s^2$:
\[\left(s^2+8\right)^2+3\left(s^2-10\right)^2=36s^2.\]
With $u=s^2$ this is $4u^2-80u+364=0$, i.e. $u^2-20u+91=0$, so $u=7$ or $u=13$.

$P$ is inside the rhombus, so $y>0$, which needs $s^2>10$. Hence $s^2=13$. (Then $P=\left(\dfrac{21}{2\sqrt{13}},\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2\sqrt{13}}\right)\approx(2.91,0.72)$, which is indeed inside. The value $s^2=7$ puts $P$ below line $AB$.) The area is
\[s^2\sin60^\circ=\boxed{\dfrac{13\sqrt3}{2}}.\]

