question:
Prove or disprove each statement: for any three circles in the plane there is a circle that is
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] externally tangent to all three;
\item[(b)] internally tangent to all three, containing them.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Both statements are \textbf{false}. Take three circles of radius $1$ with centres $O_1=(-3,0)$, $O_2=(0,0)$, $O_3=(3,0)$.

\textbf{(a)} A circle with centre $O$ and radius $R$ is externally tangent to a circle with centre $O_i$ and radius $1$ exactly when $OO_i=R+1$. So $O$ would be at the same distance from $O_1$, $O_2$ and $O_3$. The points equidistant from $O_1$ and $O_2$ form the perpendicular bisector $x=-\dfrac32$, and those equidistant from $O_2$ and $O_3$ form the line $x=\dfrac32$. These lines do not meet, so no such circle exists.

\textbf{(b)} Internal tangency with the small circle inside means $OO_i=R-1$ for each $i$, which again makes $O$ equidistant from $O_1$, $O_2$, $O_3$: impossible for the same reason. (A circle inside all three disks cannot exist either, since the disks are disjoint.)

So neither kind of tangent circle exists in general; this disproves both statements. $\blacksquare$

