Find the error in this ``proof'' that $2=1$. Let $a=b$, with $a\ne0$. Then
\begin{enumerate}
\item $a^2=ab$;
\item $a^2+a^2=a^2+ab$, so $2a^2=a^2+ab$;
\item $2a^2-2ab=a^2-ab$;
\item $2\left(a^2-ab\right)=1\cdot\left(a^2-ab\right)$;
\item therefore $2=1$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the circle have centre $O$ and radius $r$, and let $AB$ be any chord. By the triangle inequality (or trivially, if $O$ lies on line $AB$),
\[AB\le AO+OB=r+r=2r,\]
with equality exactly when $O$ lies on the segment $AB$, that is, when $AB$ is a diameter. So every chord has length at most $2r$, and the chords of length $2r$ are exactly the diameters. $\blacksquare$

