The lines $x=\dfrac y2+7$ and $y=\dfrac x2+7$ meet where $x=\dfrac{x}{4}+\dfrac72+7$, i.e. at $(14,14)$. The first two inequalities describe the wedge between these lines that opens towards larger $x$ and $y$. The line $x=\dfrac y2+7$ meets $y=\dfrac{55}3$ at $x=\dfrac{55}6+7=\dfrac{97}6<\dfrac{55}{3}$, and similarly $y=\dfrac x2+7$ meets $x=\dfrac{55}3$ at $y=\dfrac{97}6$. So the region is the quadrilateral with vertices
\[(14,14),\quad\left(\dfrac{55}3,\dfrac{97}6\right),\quad\left(\dfrac{55}3,\dfrac{55}3\right),\quad\left(\dfrac{97}6,\dfrac{55}3\right).\]
It is a kite, symmetric in the line $y=x$, so its diagonals are perpendicular. Their lengths are
\[\left(\dfrac{55}3-14\right)\sqrt2=\dfrac{13\sqrt2}3\qquad\text{and}\qquad\left(\dfrac{55}3-\dfrac{97}6\right)\sqrt2=\dfrac{13\sqrt2}6.\]
The area is half their product:
\[\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac{13\sqrt2}3\cdot\dfrac{13\sqrt2}6=\dfrac{169}{18}.\]
So $m+n=169+18=\boxed{187}$.

