\textbf{(b)} A diagonal cuts the square into two $45^\circ$--$45^\circ$--$90^\circ$ triangles with legs equal to the side $s$ and hypotenuse $10$. So $s\sqrt2=10$ and $s=\dfrac{10}{\sqrt2}=5\sqrt2$. The area is $s^2=\boxed{50}$, and the side is $\boxed{5\sqrt2}$.

question:
Fix the points $A=(0,0)$ and $B=(1,0)$. For $R>1$, let $C$ be a point chosen uniformly at random from the disc $D_R$ of radius $R$ centred at the midpoint $M=\left(\dfrac12,0\right)$ of $AB$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Let $p(R)$ be the probability that triangle $ABC$ is acute. Find $\displaystyle\lim_{R\to\infty}p(R)$.
\item[(b)] Let $q(R)$ be the probability that all three angles of triangle $ABC$ are less than $120^\circ$. Find $\displaystyle\lim_{R\to\infty}q(R)$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The points of line $AB$ have probability $0$, so we may assume that $C=(x,y)$ is not on this line. Write $[X]$ for the area of a region $X$; so $[D_R]=\pi R^2$.

\textbf{(a)} The angle at $A$ is acute exactly when $(C-A)\cdot(B-A)=x>0$, and the angle at $B$ is acute exactly when $(C-B)\cdot(A-B)=1-x>0$. So an acute triangle needs $0<x<1$. (The angle at $C$ is acute exactly when $C$ is outside the circle with diameter $AB$, but this is not even needed.) The points of $D_R$ with $0<x<1$ lie in the rectangle $0<x<1$, $-R\le y\le R$, of area $2R$. Hence
\[0\le p(R)\le\dfrac{2R}{\pi R^2}=\dfrac{2}{\pi R},\]
which tends to $0$. So $\displaystyle\lim_{R\to\infty}p(R)=\boxed{0}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
