\textbf{(b)} The angle at $A$ is at least $120^\circ$ exactly when the ray $AC$ makes an angle of at least $120^\circ$ with ray $AB$, the positive $x$-direction, that is, when $C$ lies in the wedge $W_A$ with apex $A$ and opening angle $120^\circ$, symmetric about the negative $x$-axis. Similarly, the angle at $B$ is at least $120^\circ$ exactly when $C$ lies in the wedge $W_B$ with apex $B$ and opening angle $120^\circ$, symmetric about the positive $x$-axis. Let $V$ be the set of points $C$ with $\angle ACB\ge120^\circ$. A triangle has at most one angle of at least $120^\circ$, so these three sets do not overlap, and
\[q(R)=1-\dfrac{[W_A\cap D_R]+[W_B\cap D_R]+[V\cap D_R]}{\pi R^2}.\]

\emph{The wedges.} Since $AM=\dfrac12$, the disc of radius $R-\dfrac12$ about $A$ lies inside $D_R$, and $D_R$ lies inside the disc of radius $R+\dfrac12$ about $A$. A wedge with apex $A$ and angle $120^\circ$ contains exactly one third of each of these discs, so
\[\dfrac{\pi}{3}\left(R-\dfrac12\right)^2\le[W_A\cap D_R]\le\dfrac{\pi}{3}\left(R+\dfrac12\right)^2.\]
After dividing by $\pi R^2$, both bounds tend to $\dfrac13$ as $R\to\infty$. The same holds for $W_B$.

\emph{The set $V$.} If $\angle ACB\ge120^\circ$, it is the largest angle of the triangle, so $AB=1$ is the longest side and $CA<1$. So $V$ lies in the disc of radius $1$ about $A$, and $[V\cap D_R]\le\pi$, which divided by $\pi R^2$ tends to $0$.

Therefore
\[\lim_{R\to\infty}q(R)=1-\dfrac13-\dfrac13-0=\boxed{\dfrac13}.\]

