Do any two circles have a centre of homothety? Let $\omega_1$ and $\omega_2$ be circles with centres $O_1\ne O_2$ and radii $r_1$, $r_2$. The homothety with centre $P$ and ratio $k\ne0$ sends each point $X$ to $P+k(X-P)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that this homothety maps $\omega_1$ onto $\omega_2$ exactly when $|k|=\dfrac{r_2}{r_1}$ and $O_2=P+k(O_1-P)$.
\item[(b)] Deduce that if $r_1\ne r_2$ there are exactly two such centres $P$, and if $r_1=r_2$ exactly one, and locate them on line $O_1O_2$.
\item[(c)] What happens if $O_1=O_2$ and $r_1\ne r_2$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The homothety multiplies all distances by $|k|$, so it maps the circle with centre $O_1$ and radius $r_1$ onto the circle with centre $P+k(O_1-P)$ and radius $|k|r_1$. Two circles coincide exactly when they have the same centre and radius, which gives the two conditions.

\textbf{(b)} From $O_2-P=k(O_1-P)$ we get $(1-k)P=O_2-kO_1$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $k=-\dfrac{r_2}{r_1}$: then $1-k\ne0$ and
\[P_{\text{int}}=\dfrac{r_1O_2+r_2O_1}{r_1+r_2},\]
the point of segment $O_1O_2$ with $O_1P:PO_2=r_1:r_2$ (the internal centre). It always exists.
\item $k=\dfrac{r_2}{r_1}$: if $r_1\ne r_2$, then $k\ne1$ and
\[P_{\text{ext}}=\dfrac{r_1O_2-r_2O_1}{r_1-r_2},\]
the point of line $O_1O_2$ outside the segment with $O_1P:O_2P=r_1:r_2$ (the external centre). If $r_1=r_2$, then $k=1$ and the condition $O_2=O_1$ fails, so there is no such centre (the circles are related by a translation instead).
\end{itemize}
So there are $\boxed{\text{two centres if }r_1\ne r_2\text{, one (the midpoint of }O_1O_2\text{) if }r_1=r_2}$. In particular every two circles with different centres have a centre of homothety.

\textbf{(c)} If $O_1=O_2$, the condition becomes $(1-k)(O_1-P)=0$ with $k=\pm\dfrac{r_2}{r_1}\ne1$, so $P=O_1$: the common centre is the only centre of homothety, with ratio $\dfrac{r_2}{r_1}$ or $-\dfrac{r_2}{r_1}$.

