We prove directly: \emph{for all integers $p$ and $q$ with $q\ge1$, $p^2\ne3q^2$.}

If $p=0$, then $p^2=0<3q^2$. If $p\ne0$, write the prime factorisations of $|p|$ and $q$; by the uniqueness of prime factorisation, the exponent of $3$ in $p^2$ is twice the exponent of $3$ in $p$, which is even, while the exponent of $3$ in $3q^2$ is one more than twice the exponent of $3$ in $q$, which is odd. Numbers whose factorisations contain $3$ to different powers are different, so $p^2\ne3q^2$.

Hence for every fraction $\dfrac pq$ we have $\left(\dfrac pq\right)^2\ne3$, so no fraction equals $\sqrt3$. That is exactly the statement that $\sqrt3$ is irrational. $\blacksquare$

question:
Explain this ``mind-reading'' trick, in which $B$, $S$, $G$ are the numbers of your brothers, sisters and living grandparents (each at most $9$), $F$ is your favourite number (a nonnegative integer) and $n$ is any integer with $0\le n<100$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Take $B$, multiply by $2$, add $3$, multiply by $5$, add $S$, multiply by $10$, add $G$, and add $125$. Show that the result is $100B+10S+G+275$, and explain how to read off $B$, $S$, $G$.
\item[(b)] Take $F$, multiply by $2$, add $5$, multiply by $50$, add $n$, multiply by $4$ and subtract $1000$. Show that the result is $400F+4n$, and explain how to recover $F$.
