Let $f$ be a function.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the graph of $y=f(4-x)$ is the reflection of the graph of $y=f(x)$ in the vertical line $x=2$.
\item[(b)] A student says: ``to get $y=f(4-x)$, reflect $y=f(x)$ in the $y$-axis and then shift it $4$ units to the left.'' Is this right? Give the two correct ways of combining a reflection in the $y$-axis with a horizontal shift, and explain why the order matters.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} A point $(a,b)$ lies on $y=f(x)$ exactly when $b=f(a)$, and then $(4-a,b)$ lies on $y=f(4-x)$, because $f(4-(4-a))=f(a)=b$; the converse is the same computation. The points $(a,b)$ and $(4-a,b)$ are mirror images in the line $x=2$, since their midpoint is $(2,b)$.

\textbf{(b)} Replacing $x$ by $-x$ reflects a graph in the $y$-axis, and replacing $x$ by $x-h$ shifts it $h$ units to the right.
\begin{itemize}
\item Reflect first: $y=f(-x)$; then shift $4$ units to the \emph{right}: $y=f(-(x-4))=f(4-x)$.
\item Shift first, $4$ units to the \emph{left}: $y=f(x+4)$; then reflect: $y=f(-x+4)=f(4-x)$.
\end{itemize}
The student's order (reflect, then shift left) gives $y=f(-(x+4))=f(-x-4)$, which is wrong in general. The order matters because the reflection also reverses the direction of any later shift.

