question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] For which real numbers $x$ is $\sin(\arcsin x)$ defined, and what is its value? Answer the same question for $\cos(\arccos x)$.
\item[(b)] For which real $x$ is $\arcsin(\sin x)=x$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Over the real numbers, $\arcsin x$ is defined only for $-1\le x\le1$: it is the angle in $\left[-\dfrac\pi2,\dfrac\pi2\right]$ whose sine is $x$. So $\sin(\arcsin x)$ is defined exactly for $-1\le x\le1$, and then it equals $x$; for $|x|>1$ it is undefined. In the same way $\arccos x$ is the angle in $[0,\pi]$ whose cosine is $x$, so $\cos(\arccos x)=x$ for $-1\le x\le1$ and is undefined otherwise. In short, both are $\boxed{x\text{ for }|x|\le1,\text{ undefined for }|x|>1}$.

\textbf{(b)} $\arcsin(\sin x)$ is defined for every real $x$, but its value always lies in $\left[-\dfrac\pi2,\dfrac\pi2\right]$. If $x$ is in this interval, it is the angle there with sine $\sin x$, so $\arcsin(\sin x)=x$. Otherwise the value differs from $x$. So $\arcsin(\sin x)=x$ exactly for $\boxed{-\dfrac\pi2\le x\le\dfrac\pi2}$.

