By the law of sines,
\[\sin B=\dfrac{CA\sin A}{BC}=\dfrac{8\cdot\dfrac12}{5}=\dfrac45.\]
So $B=\arcsin\dfrac45\approx53.13^\circ$ or $B=180^\circ-\arcsin\dfrac45\approx126.87^\circ$. Both are possible, because in each case $A+B<180^\circ$ (this is the ambiguous SSA case: $\angle A$ is acute and $CA\sin A<BC<CA$, i.e. $4<5<8$).

To find $c=AB$, use the law of cosines at $A$:
\[5^2=8^2+c^2-2\cdot8\cdot c\cos30^\circ\iff c^2-8\sqrt3c+39=0\iff c=4\sqrt3\pm3.\]
Both roots are positive. The larger side $c=4\sqrt3+3\approx9.93$ is opposite the larger angle $C=180^\circ-30^\circ-53.13^\circ=96.87^\circ$, so it goes with the acute $B$. So the two triangles are
\[\boxed{AB=4\sqrt3+3,\ \angle B=\arcsin\dfrac45\approx53.13^\circ}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\boxed{AB=4\sqrt3-3,\ \angle B\approx126.87^\circ}.\]
(Check with the law of sines: $\dfrac{c}{\sin C}=\dfrac{5}{\sin30^\circ}=10$, and $10\sin96.87^\circ\approx9.93$, $10\sin23.13^\circ\approx3.93$.)

