question:
Consider the following three statements.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Exactly one of the statements below this one is false.
\item This statement is true.
\item All of the statements above this one are true.
\end{enumerate}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that it is impossible for exactly one of the three statements to be true.
\item[(b)] Find all consistent assignments of ``true'' and ``false'' to the three statements.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Multiply out, using $i^2=-1$:
\[wz=rs\bigl[(\cos\theta\cos\varphi-\sin\theta\sin\varphi)+i(\sin\theta\cos\varphi+\cos\theta\sin\varphi)\bigr]=rs\bigl(\cos(\theta+\varphi)+i\sin(\theta+\varphi)\bigr)\]
by the angle-addition formulas.

\textbf{(b)} By (a), the point $z$ at distance $s$ from $0$ and angle $\varphi$ goes to the point at distance $rs$ and angle $\varphi+\theta$. Rotating $z$ about $0$ through $\theta$ gives the point at distance $s$ and angle $\varphi+\theta$; dilating by factor $r$ from $0$ then gives distance $rs$ at the same angle. So $z\mapsto wz$ is exactly this rotation followed by this dilation. (Both maps send $0$ to $0$.) In short: lengths multiply and angles add. Addition, by contrast, is a translation.

\textbf{(c)} $w=1+i=\sqrt2\left(\cos45^\circ+i\sin45^\circ\right)$, so multiplying by $w$ rotates by $45^\circ$ and scales by $\sqrt2$. Indeed
\[(1+i)(2+i)=2+i+2i+i^2=\boxed{1+3i},\]
and $|1+3i|=\sqrt{10}=\sqrt2\cdot\sqrt5=|w|\,|z|$. $\blacksquare$

