Put the centre of the hexagon at the origin, with vertices $V_1=A,\dots,V_6=F$ in counterclockwise order, and write $P_i=V_i+t_i\left(V_{i+1}-V_i\right)$ with $t_1,\dots,t_5$ independent and uniform on $[0,1]$.

\textbf{Shoelace formula.} The points $P_1,\dots,P_5$ lie on the boundary of a convex hexagon in counterclockwise order, so $P_1P_2P_3P_4P_5$ is a convex (possibly degenerate) polygon traversed counterclockwise, and its area is
\[[P_1\cdots P_5]=\dfrac12\sum_{i=1}^{5}\left(x_iy_{i+1}-x_{i+1}y_i\right)\qquad(P_6=P_1),\]
with no absolute value needed. Each term $x_iy_{i+1}$ is a product of a function of $t_i$ and a function of $t_{i+1}$, which are independent, so its expectation is $E[x_i]\,E[y_{i+1}]$. Since $P_i$ depends linearly on $t_i$, $E[P_i]=M_i$ is the midpoint of the $i$th side. By linearity of expectation, the expected area equals the area of the midpoint pentagon $M_1M_2M_3M_4M_5$.

\textbf{Area of the midpoint pentagon.} The hexagon has area $6\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}$. The pentagon is obtained by cutting off:
\begin{itemize}
\item the four corner triangles at $B,C,D,E$, each with two sides $\dfrac12$ enclosing $120^\circ$: area $\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac12\sin120^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{16}$ each;
\item the quadrilateral $M_5FAM_1$, made of triangle $M_5FA$ (sides $\dfrac12$ and $1$ enclosing $120^\circ$, area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{8}$) and triangle $M_5AM_1$. With $A=(1,0)$, $M_5=\left(0,-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$ and $M_1=\left(\dfrac34,\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\right)$, the shoelace formula gives the latter area $\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{16}$. So the quadrilateral has area $\dfrac{5\sqrt3}{16}$.
\end{itemize}
Hence
\[E\bigl[[P_1P_2P_3P_4P_5]\bigr]=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}-4\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{16}-\dfrac{5\sqrt3}{16}=\boxed{\dfrac{15\sqrt3}{16}}.\]

