question:
In triangle $ABC$ we are given the angle $\gamma=\angle C$, the side $b=CA$ adjacent to it, and the side $c=AB$ opposite it (``side--side--angle'').
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If $c=\sqrt{229}$, $b=12$ and $\gamma=60^\circ$, show that $BC$ is determined, and find it.
\item[(b)] Prove that, for given $\gamma$ (with $0^\circ<\gamma<180^\circ$), $b>0$ and $c>0$, there is exactly one triangle with these data (up to congruence) if and only if
\[c>b,\qquad\text{or}\qquad c=b\ \text{and}\ \gamma<90^\circ,\qquad\text{or}\qquad c=b\sin\gamma\ \text{and}\ \gamma<90^\circ.\]
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $a=BC$. By the law of cosines, a triangle with the given data exists with $BC=a$ exactly when $a>0$ and
\[a^2-(2b\cos\gamma)\,a+\left(b^2-c^2\right)=0.\tag{$*$}\]
Triangles with the same $a$, $b$, $\gamma$ are congruent (SAS), so the number of triangles is the number of positive roots of $(*)$. Its roots have sum $2b\cos\gamma$, product $b^2-c^2$ and discriminant $4\left(c^2-b^2\sin^2\gamma\right)$.

\textbf{(b)}
\begin{itemize}
\item $c>b$: the product is negative, so there is exactly one positive root. One triangle.
\item $c=b$: the roots are $0$ and $2b\cos\gamma$, so there is one triangle if $\gamma<90^\circ$ and none otherwise.
\item $c<b$: the product is positive, so the roots have the same sign (or are not real). If $\gamma\ge90^\circ$ the sum is $\le0$, so there is no positive root. If $\gamma<90^\circ$ the sum is positive, and:
\begin{itemize}
\item for $c<b\sin\gamma$ there are no real roots (no triangle);
\item for $c=b\sin\gamma$ there is a double root (one triangle, right-angled at $B$);
\item for $b\sin\gamma<c<b$ there are two positive roots (two triangles, the ``ambiguous case'').
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
Collecting the cases with exactly one triangle gives the stated condition. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(a)} Here $c=\sqrt{229}>12=b$, so by (b) the triangle is unique. Indeed, $(*)$ reads $a^2-12a+144-229=0$, i.e. $(a-17)(a+5)=0$, and the only positive root is $BC=\boxed{17}$.

