Let the triangle be $ABC$ with $BC=a$, $CA=b$, $AB=c$ and $\gamma=\angle C$. Let $H$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $B$ to line $CA$, and put $h=BH$, $x=CH$. By the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle $BHC$, $a^2=x^2+h^2$.

\textbf{If $\gamma<90^\circ$}, $H$ lies on ray $CA$ and $H\ne C$, so $x>0$ and $AH=|b-x|$. In the right triangle $BHA$,
\[c^2=h^2+(b-x)^2=\left(h^2+x^2\right)+b^2-2bx=a^2+b^2-2bx<a^2+b^2.\]
\textbf{If $\gamma>90^\circ$}, $H$ lies on the extension of $AC$ beyond $C$, with $x>0$ and $AH=b+x$, so
\[c^2=h^2+(b+x)^2=a^2+b^2+2bx>a^2+b^2.\]
So if $a^2+b^2=c^2$, the angle $\gamma$ is neither acute nor obtuse, hence $\gamma=90^\circ$. $\blacksquare$

(This is the law of cosines $c^2=a^2+b^2-2ab\cos\gamma$ in disguise, with $x=a|\cos\gamma|$.)

