question:
Eight different people are seated at a square table, two on each side, in eight fixed seats. Two seatings are considered the same if one can be obtained from the other by a rotation or a reflection of the table.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many different seatings are there?
\item[(b)] A student first divides $8!$ by $4$ for the rotations, and then divides by $4$ again because there are four reflections (in the two diagonals and the two midlines). Explain why this is wrong.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the angles of triangle $ABC$ be $\alpha=\angle A$, $\beta=\angle B$, $\gamma=\angle C$.

\textbf{Each angle of $DEF$ equals the corresponding angle of $ABC$ or its supplement.} The ray $DE$ is parallel to line $AB$, so it points either in the same direction as ray $AB$ or in the opposite direction; the same holds for ray $DF$ and ray $AC$. If both rays keep their directions, or both are reversed, then $\angle EDF=\alpha$. If exactly one is reversed, then $\angle EDF=180^\circ-\alpha$. In the same way $\angle DEF\in\{\beta,180^\circ-\beta\}$ and $\angle DFE\in\{\gamma,180^\circ-\gamma\}$.

\textbf{They are all equal.} Let $X$ be the set of vertices where the angle of $DEF$ is the supplement. Then
\[180^\circ=\angle D+\angle E+\angle F=(\alpha+\beta+\gamma)+\sum_{X}\left(180^\circ-2\theta\right)=180^\circ+2\sum_{X}\left(90^\circ-\theta\right),\]
where $\theta$ runs over the angles of $ABC$ at the vertices in $X$. So $\sum_X(90^\circ-\theta)=0$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $X$ has one vertex, its angle is $90^\circ$, and then the supplement equals the angle anyway.
\item If $X$ has two vertices, their angles sum to $180^\circ$, which is impossible in a triangle.
\item If $X$ has all three, the sum is $270^\circ-180^\circ=90^\circ\ne0$.
\end{itemize}
Hence $\angle D=\alpha$, $\angle E=\beta$, $\angle F=\gamma$, and the triangles are similar by AA. $\blacksquare$

