question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that there is no triangle $ABC$ with $AB=10$, $BC=15$ and $\angle C=60^\circ$.
\item[(b)] Triangle $ABC$ has $AB=15$, $BC=10$ and $\angle C=60^\circ$. Find $\angle A$, rounded to the nearest minute. (A calculator may be used.)
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The distance from $B$ to the line $CA$ is $BC\sin60^\circ=\dfrac{15\sqrt3}{2}\approx12.99$. Since $A$ lies on that line, $AB\ge12.99>10$. Equivalently, the law of sines would give $\sin A=\dfrac{BC\sin C}{AB}=\dfrac{15\sqrt3}{20}\approx1.30>1$. So no such triangle exists.

\textbf{(b)} By the law of sines,
\[\sin A=\dfrac{BC\sin C}{AB}=\dfrac{10\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}}{15}=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{3}\approx0.57735.\]
Since $BC<AB$, the angle $A$ is smaller than $C=60^\circ$, so it is acute and
\[\angle A=\arcsin\dfrac{\sqrt3}{3}\approx35.264^\circ\approx\boxed{35^\circ16'}.\]
(The third side is $CA=5+5\sqrt6\approx17.25$, from $15^2=10^2+CA^2-10\,CA$, so the triangle is scalene.)

