Let $P=(x,y)$. On the ellipse, $y^2=b^2-\dfrac{b^2x^2}{a^2}$, and $b^2=a^2-c^2$, so
\[d_1^2=(x+c)^2+y^2=x^2+2cx+c^2+a^2-c^2-\dfrac{\left(a^2-c^2\right)x^2}{a^2}=\dfrac{c^2x^2}{a^2}+2cx+a^2=\left(a+\dfrac{cx}{a}\right)^2.\]
Since $|x|\le a$ and $0<c<a$, we have $\left|\dfrac{cx}{a}\right|\le c<a$, so $a+\dfrac{cx}{a}>0$ and $d_1=a+\dfrac{cx}{a}$. Replacing $c$ by $-c$ in the same computation gives $d_2=a-\dfrac{cx}{a}$. Therefore
\[d_1+d_2=2a.\qquad\blacksquare\]

