The segment's endpoints lie on the boundary of the square. The diagonals split the square into equal halves, so they are excluded.

\textbf{Endpoints on opposite sides} (this includes a segment from a vertex to a point of a side not containing that vertex). The segment joins two parallel lines at distance $1$, so $L\ge1$.

\textbf{Endpoints on two adjacent sides}, away from their common corner. The line cuts off a right triangle with legs $u,v\le1$ and area $\dfrac{uv}{2}\le\dfrac12$. So the triangle is the smaller region, with area $\dfrac15$ of the square, and $uv=\dfrac25$. Then
\[L^2=u^2+v^2\ge2uv=\dfrac45,\]
with equality for $u=v=\sqrt{\dfrac25}<1$, which is allowed.

Since $\dfrac45<1$, the smallest possible value is $\boxed{L^2=\dfrac45}$.

