solution:
\textbf{Every such convex kite has an incircle.} The diagonal $AC$ is an axis of symmetry and bisects the angles at $A$ and $C$. The bisector of angle $B$ meets $AC$ at a point $I$, and by symmetry $I$ also lies on the bisector of angle $D$. So $I$ is equidistant from all four sides, and the circle about $I$ touching them is the incircle.

\textbf{Radius.} Joining $I$ to the vertices splits the kite into four triangles with heights $r$, so
\[[ABCD]=\dfrac r2(3+7+7+3)=10r.\]
On the other hand, $[ABCD]=2[ABC]=2\cdot\dfrac12\cdot3\cdot7\sin\angle ABC=21\sin\angle ABC\le21$. Hence $r\le\dfrac{21}{10}$, with equality when $\angle ABC=90^\circ$ (then $\angle ADC=90^\circ$ too, and the kite is convex). The largest possible radius is $\boxed{\dfrac{21}{10}}$.

