We claim
\[a_n=2\cos\dfrac{\pi}{3\cdot2^{n-1}}=2\cos\dfrac{60^\circ}{2^{n-1}}.\]
\textbf{Key identity.} For $0\le\theta\le180^\circ$, $2+2\cos\theta=4\cos^2\dfrac\theta2$ and $\cos\dfrac\theta2\ge0$, so
\[\sqrt{2+2\cos\theta}=2\cos\dfrac\theta2.\]
\textbf{Induction.} For $n=1$: $2\cos60^\circ=1=a_1$. If $a_n=2\cos\theta_n$ with $\theta_n=\dfrac{60^\circ}{2^{n-1}}$, then
\[a_{n+1}=\sqrt{2+2\cos\theta_n}=2\cos\dfrac{\theta_n}{2}=2\cos\theta_{n+1}.\]
So $\boxed{a_n=2\cos\dfrac{\pi}{3\cdot2^{n-1}}}$ for all $n\ge1$. For example $a_2=\sqrt3=2\cos30^\circ$. In particular $a_n\to2$.

