question:
Right triangle $ABC$ has $AC=8$ and $AB=15$. Its three medians meet at $G$. Find $CG$
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] if the right angle is at $C$;
\item[(b)] if the right angle is at $A$.
\end{enumerate}
Why can the right angle not be at $B$?

solution:
Let $M$ be the midpoint of $AB$. The median from $C$ is $CM$, and the centroid satisfies $CG=\dfrac23CM$.

\textbf{(a)} $AB=15$ is the hypotenuse. The midpoint of the hypotenuse is equidistant from all three vertices (it is the centre of the circumcircle), so $CM=\dfrac{15}{2}$ and
\[CG=\dfrac23\cdot\dfrac{15}{2}=\boxed{5}.\]

\textbf{(b)} The legs are $AC=8$ and $AB=15$, and $AM=\dfrac{15}{2}$. In the right triangle $CAM$,
\[CM=\sqrt{8^2+\left(\dfrac{15}{2}\right)^2}=\sqrt{\dfrac{481}{4}}=\dfrac{\sqrt{481}}{2},\qquad CG=\dfrac23\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt{481}}{2}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt{481}}{3}}\approx7.31.\]

A right angle at $B$ would make $AC$ the hypotenuse, but $AC=8<15=AB$. $\blacksquare$

