question:
Let $n>1$ be an integer and let $\alpha=\sqrt[n]{n}$ be the positive real $n$th root of $n$. We want a nonzero polynomial with rational coefficients, of the smallest possible degree, that has $\alpha$ as a root.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Let $d$ be the smallest positive integer for which $\alpha^d$ is rational. Prove that $x^d-\alpha^d$ is such a polynomial of smallest degree.
\item[(b)] Write $n=p_1^{e_1}p_2^{e_2}\cdots p_k^{e_k}$ (distinct primes $p_i$, exponents $e_i\ge1$) and let $g=\gcd\left(n,e_1,e_2,\dots,e_k\right)$. Prove that $d=\dfrac ng$ and that $\alpha^d=\sqrt[g]{n}$ is an integer.
\item[(c)] Write down the answer for $n=4$, $8$, $16$, $27$ and $36$. In particular, is $x^n-n$ always of smallest degree?
\end{enumerate}

When solving a trigonometric equation, one has to decide whether to add $2k\pi$ or $k\pi$ (with $k$ an integer) to a solution. Find all real solutions of each equation, and explain in each case why the general solution has that form.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $\sin x=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$
\item[(b)] $\tan x=\sqrt3$
\item[(c)] $\cos x=0$
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The rule: $\sin$ and $\cos$ repeat every $2\pi$, and $\tan$ repeats every $\pi$. So find all solutions in one interval of length $2\pi$, say $[0,2\pi)$, and add $2k\pi$ to each. If these solutions come in pairs exactly $\pi$ apart, the two families combine into a single family with $+k\pi$.

On the unit circle, the angle $x$ corresponds to the point $(\cos x,\sin x)$.

\textbf{(a)} The points with height $\dfrac{\sqrt3}2$ are at $x=\dfrac\pi3$ and $x=\dfrac{2\pi}3$ (the dashed line in the figure). They are $\dfrac\pi3$ apart, not $\pi$, so both families are needed:
\[\boxed{x=\dfrac\pi3+2k\pi\quad\text{or}\quad x=\dfrac{2\pi}3+2k\pi,\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.}\]

\textbf{(b)} $\tan x=\sqrt3$ means that the point lies on the line through $O$ with slope $\sqrt3$ (the dotted line). It meets the circle at $x=\dfrac\pi3$ and $x=\dfrac{4\pi}3$, which are $\pi$ apart. So the two families $\dfrac\pi3+2k\pi$ and $\dfrac{4\pi}3+2k\pi$ combine into
\[\boxed{x=\dfrac\pi3+k\pi,\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.}\]

\textbf{(c)} $\cos x=0$ at the top and bottom of the circle, $x=\dfrac\pi2$ and $x=\dfrac{3\pi}2$, which are $\pi$ apart. So
\[\boxed{x=\dfrac\pi2+k\pi,\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.}\]

