question:
A quadratic function $f(x)=ax^2+bx+c$ has integer coefficients $a\ne0$, $b$ and $c$ (the constant $c$ counts as a coefficient). The sum of the roots of $f$ is $-\dfrac32$, and the graph of $f$ passes through the point $(2,3)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find all such functions $f$, and show that each of them has real roots.
\item[(b)] Consider the functions $f$ for which $|a+b+c|\le100$. Find the sum of $a+b+c$ over all of them.
\item[(c)] Do the same with the bound $|a+b+c|\le1000$.
\end{enumerate}

A right circular cone has apex $V$, and the angle between its axis and its slant edge is $\theta$ (so the apex angle of the cross-section through the axis is $2\theta$). Two planes perpendicular to the axis cut the cone in circles, at distances $h$ and $h+d$ from $V$, where $h>0$ and $d>0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the ratio of the area of the larger circle to the area of the smaller circle.
\item[(b)] Show that $\theta$ and $d$ alone do not determine this ratio, and say what extra information is enough.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} In the cross-section through the axis, the radius of a circle at distance $s$ from $V$ is $s\tan\theta$ (right triangle with angle $\theta$ at $V$). So
\[r_1=h\tan\theta,\qquad r_2=(h+d)\tan\theta,\]
and the ratio of the areas is
\[\dfrac{\pi r_2^2}{\pi r_1^2}=\boxed{\left(\dfrac{h+d}{h}\right)^2}.\]
It does not depend on $\theta$: the two circles are similar figures with scale factor $\dfrac{h+d}h$.

\textbf{(b)} Keep $\theta$ and $d$ fixed. For $h=d$ the ratio is $4$, but for $h=2d$ it is $\dfrac94$. So $\theta$ and $d$ do not determine the ratio. It is determined once we also know the distance $h$ from the apex to the nearer section, or equivalently one of the radii, since $h=\dfrac{r_1}{\tan\theta}$.

