Use coordinates with $B=(0,0)$, $C=(a,0)$ and $A=(p,h)$. Since the angles at $B$ and $C$ are acute, $H=(p,0)$ lies strictly between $B$ and $C$, so $0<p<a$. The square is $A=(p,h)$, $H=(p,0)$, $G=(p-h,0)$, $F=(p-h,h)$.

\emph{The point $E$.} Points of line $CF$ are $(a+t(p-h-a),\ th)$, and points of line $AB$ are $(up,\ uh)$. Equal heights give $t=u$, and then $a+t(p-h-a)=tp$ gives $t=\dfrac{a}{a+h}$. So
\[E=\left(\dfrac{ap}{a+h},\ \dfrac{ah}{a+h}\right),\]
and since $0<\dfrac a{a+h}<1$, the point $E$ lies on side $AB$. Let $s=\dfrac{ah}{a+h}$ be its height.

\emph{The other vertices.} Side $AC$ goes from $(p,h)$ to $(a,0)$; its point at height $s$ is
\[X=\left(a-(a-p)\dfrac sh,\ s\right)=\left(\dfrac{a(h+p)}{a+h},\ s\right),\]
which lies on side $AC$ because $0<s<h$. Also $D=\left(\dfrac{ap}{a+h},0\right)$ and $Y=\left(\dfrac{a(h+p)}{a+h},0\right)$. Both lie on side $BC$, since $0<p$ and $p+h<a+h$.

\emph{It is a square.} $ED$ and $XY$ are vertical of length $s$, $DY$ is horizontal, and
\[EX=DY=\dfrac{a(h+p)-ap}{a+h}=\dfrac{ah}{a+h}=s.\]
So $EXYD$ is a rectangle with all sides equal to $s$, i.e. a square, with $E$ on $AB$, $X$ on $AC$ and $D,Y$ on $BC$. Its side length is $\boxed{\dfrac{ah}{a+h}}$. $\blacksquare$

\emph{Why the construction works.} The enlargement with centre $C$ and ratio $\dfrac{a}{a+h}$ maps $F$ to $E$. It maps the square $AHGF$ to a square whose vertex $A$ moves along line $CA$ and whose side $GH$ stays on line $CB$. That image is exactly $EXYD$.

