question:
Show that it is possible to write a number from the set $\{1,2,3,4,5\}$ in each square of a $5\times120$ board ($5$ columns and $120$ rows) so that the following conditions hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] all numbers in the same row are distinct;
\item[(ii)] all rows are distinct;
\item[(iii)] the board can be cut into $24$ boards of size $5\times5$ which can be reassembled, without rotating or turning over, into a board with $5$ rows and $120$ columns whose $120$ columns are distinct.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\emph{Analysis.} Suppose $C$ works.
\begin{itemize}
\item $BM\perp AC$ and $M$ lies on $AC$, so $\angle AMB=90^\circ$. Hence $M$ lies on the circle $\omega$ with diameter $AB$ (Thales), and also on $l$.
\item $N$ is the midpoint of $BC$ and lies on $l$. So $C$ is the image of $N$ under the enlargement with centre $B$ and ratio $2$. Therefore $C$ lies on the image $l'$ of $l$ under this enlargement. This is the line parallel to $l$ through the reflection $B^*$ of $B$ in $l$.
\end{itemize}

\emph{Construction.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Draw the circle $\omega$ with diameter $AB$, and let $M$ be a common point of $\omega$ and $l$.
\item Reflect $B$ in $l$ to get $B^*$, and draw the line $l'$ through $B^*$ parallel to $l$.
\item Let $C$ be the intersection of line $AM$ with $l'$.
\end{enumerate}
Line $AM$ is not parallel to $l$ (it meets $l$ only at $M$, since $A\notin l$), so it is not parallel to $l'$, and $C$ exists.

\emph{Proof.} The line $l'$ lies on the other side of $l$ from $B$, hence from $A$. So segment $AC$ crosses $l$, and it does so at $M$. Since $M$ is on $\omega$ and $M\ne A,B$, we have $\angle AMB=90^\circ$, i.e. $BM\perp AC$, so $BM$ is an altitude. The enlargement with centre $B$ and ratio $\dfrac12$ maps $C\in l'$ to the midpoint of $BC$ and maps $l'$ to $l$. So the midpoint of $BC$ lies on $l$; it is $N$, and $AN$ is a median. The triangle is not degenerate: if $C$ were on line $AB$, then $M$ would be on line $AB$ and on $\omega$, so $M=A$ or $M=B$, which are not on $l$.

\emph{Existence.} The construction gives one solution for each common point of $\omega$ and $l$, and the analysis shows that every solution arises this way. So there are \boxed{\text{two, one or no solutions}}, according as the distance from the midpoint of $AB$ to $l$ is less than, equal to, or greater than $\dfrac{AB}2$. In the sketch, $M'$ is the second common point and gives a second solution.

