Let the triangle have angles $A$, $B$, $C$ and circumradius $R=10$. By the law of sines its sides are $2R\sin A$, $2R\sin B$, $2R\sin C$, so the perimeter is
\[2R\left(\sin A+\sin B+\sin C\right).\]

\emph{Lemma.} For $x,y\in[0,\pi]$,
\[\sin x+\sin y=2\sin\dfrac{x+y}2\cos\dfrac{x-y}2\le2\sin\dfrac{x+y}2,\]
because $\sin\dfrac{x+y}2\ge0$ and $\cos\dfrac{x-y}2\le1$. Equality holds when $x=y$.

Apply the lemma three times, adding the angle $\dfrac\pi3$:
\[\sin A+\sin B+\sin C+\sin\dfrac\pi3\le2\sin\dfrac{A+B}2+2\sin\dfrac{C+\dfrac\pi3}{2}\le4\sin\dfrac{A+B+C+\dfrac\pi3}{4}=4\sin\dfrac\pi3.\]
All the angles used lie in $[0,\pi]$. Hence $\sin A+\sin B+\sin C\le3\sin\dfrac\pi3=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}2$, and the perimeter is at most
\[2\cdot10\cdot\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}=30\sqrt3.\]
Equality holds when $A=B=C=\dfrac\pi3$, i.e. for the equilateral triangle with side $10\sqrt3$ (triangle $ABC$ in the sketch; $PQT$ is a non-equilateral inscribed triangle with a smaller perimeter).

The maximum perimeter is $\boxed{30\sqrt3}\approx51.96$.

