Put the centre of $\omega$ at $O=(0,0)$. The centre of $\gamma$ is at distance $6-2=4$ from $O$, say $P=(0,-4)$, and the circles touch at $T=(0,-6)$. The horizontal tangent lines of $\gamma$ are $y=-6$ (tangent to $\omega$ as well, so not a chord) and $y=-2$. So the chord lies on $y=-2$.

Let one congruent circle have radius $r$ and centre $Q=(x,k)$ with $x>0$. It is tangent to the chord, so $k=-2\pm r$.

If $k=-2+r$ (above the chord), external tangency to $\gamma$ gives $x^2+(2+r)^2=(2+r)^2$, so $x=0$, which is impossible. Hence $Q=(x,-2-r)$, on the same side of the chord as $\gamma$. Then:
\begin{itemize}
\item internal tangency to $\omega$: $OQ=6-r$, so $x^2+(2+r)^2=(6-r)^2$, that is $x^2=32-16r$;
\item external tangency to $\gamma$: $PQ=2+r$, so $x^2+(2-r)^2=(2+r)^2$, that is $x^2=8r$.
\end{itemize}
Hence $8r=32-16r$, so $r=\boxed{\dfrac43}$, with $x^2=\dfrac{32}3$.

Check: $OQ^2=\dfrac{32}3+\left(\dfrac{10}3\right)^2=\dfrac{196}9$, so $OQ=\dfrac{14}3=6-\dfrac43$.

