question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Let $x$ be a real number. Prove that if real numbers $a$ and $b$ satisfy $a+b=x$, then $ab\le\left(\dfrac x2\right)^2$, with equality exactly when $a=b$.
\item[(b)] Now let $x$ be a positive integer, and let $a$, $b$ be nonnegative integers with $a+b=x$. Prove that the largest possible value of $ab$ is $\dfrac{x^2}4$ if $x$ is even and $\dfrac{x^2-1}4$ if $x$ is odd.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\emph{Lemma: a tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact.} Let $t$ be the tangent at $B$, so $t$ meets the circle only at $B$. Suppose $OB$ is not perpendicular to $t$, and let $F\ne B$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $O$ to $t$. Let $B'$ be the reflection of $B$ in $F$; it lies on $t$ and $B'\ne B$. Triangles $OFB$ and $OFB'$ are congruent (SAS: $OF$ is common, both angles at $F$ are right angles, and $FB=FB'$). So $OB'=OB$ is a radius, and $B'$ is a second common point of $t$ and the circle, a contradiction. Hence $OB\perp AB$, and likewise $OC\perp AC$.

\emph{Main proof.} Right triangles $ABO$ and $ACO$ have the common hypotenuse $AO$ and legs $OB=OC$, so they are congruent (RHS). Thus $\angle BAO=\angle CAO$, and since $B\ne C$, the points $B$ and $C$ lie on opposite sides of line $AO$. So
\[\angle BAC=\angle BAO+\angle OAC,\qquad\angle BOC=\angle BOA+\angle AOC.\]
In right triangle $ABO$, $\angle BAO+\angle BOA=90^\circ$, and in $ACO$, $\angle CAO+\angle COA=90^\circ$. Adding gives $\angle BAC+\angle BOC=180^\circ$.

The minor arc $BC$ facing $A$ has measure equal to the central angle $\angle BOC$, so $\angle BAC+\overset{\frown}{BC}=180^\circ$. $\blacksquare$

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);real d=2.6;
