question:
Let $\tau(i)$ denote the number of positive divisors of $i$, and let $T(n)=\tau(1)+\tau(2)+\cdots+\tau(n)$. (No simple closed form for $T(n)$ is known, but it can be computed quickly.)
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $T(n)=\displaystyle\sum_{d=1}^{n}\left\lfloor\dfrac nd\right\rfloor$.
\item[(b)] Let $m=\left\lfloor\sqrt n\right\rfloor$. Prove that $T(n)=2\displaystyle\sum_{d=1}^{m}\left\lfloor\dfrac nd\right\rfloor-m^2$.
\item[(c)] Compute $T(100)$.
\end{enumerate}

A function $f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ is \emph{strictly increasing} if $x<y$ implies $f(x)<f(y)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that every strictly increasing function $f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ is injective (one-to-one).
\item[(b)] Is every strictly increasing function $f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ a bijection? Prove it or give a counterexample.
\item[(c)] What happens to (a) if ``increasing'' only means that $x<y$ implies $f(x)\le f(y)$?
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Solve $\sqrt{2x+3}=x$ over the real numbers. Show that squaring both sides produces a value of $x$ that does not satisfy the original equation, and explain why.
\item[(b)] Solve $\dfrac{x^2}{x-2}=\dfrac{4}{x-2}$, and explain why one of the values found after clearing denominators must be rejected.
\end{enumerate}

A point $P$ lies outside a given rectangle.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show how to construct a line through $P$ that divides the rectangle into two parts of equal area, and prove that it works.
\item[(b)] Prove that this is the only such line through $P$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let $O$ be the centre of the rectangle, the intersection of its diagonals (constructed with a ruler). The required line is $PO$.

The half-turn about $O$ (rotation by $180^\circ$) maps the rectangle onto itself. It also maps line $PO$ onto itself and swaps the two sides of this line. So it maps the part of the rectangle on one side of $PO$ onto the part on the other side. The two parts are congruent and have equal areas. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(b)} Let $\ell$ be a line that does not pass through $O$, and let $\ell'$ be the line through $O$ parallel to $\ell$. By (a), $\ell'$ halves the area. The part of the rectangle beyond $\ell$ (on the side away from $O$) lies inside one half cut off by $\ell'$, and it misses the strip between $\ell'$ and $\ell$. That strip contains a small half-disc around $O$ inside the rectangle, since $O$ is an interior point. So this part has less than half the area, and $\ell$ does not halve the area.

Hence a halving line must pass through $O$. Since $P\ne O$, there is exactly one line through $P$ and $O$. $\blacksquare$

