Adding the steps,
\[x_n=\dfrac{1+2+\cdots+(n-1)}{23}=\dfrac{n(n-1)}{46}.\]
Since $n(n-1)$ is always even, $x_n$ is an integer exactly when $23\mid n(n-1)$. As $23$ is prime, this means $23\mid n$ or $23\mid n-1$.

For $1\le n\le1000$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $23\mid n$: $n=23,46,\dots,989$, which is $43$ values;
\item $n\equiv1\pmod{23}$: $n=1,24,\dots,990$, which is $44$ values.
\end{itemize}
In total $43+44=\boxed{87}$ terms are integers.

Place the half-disc as $\{(x,y):\ y\ge0,\ x^2+y^2\le25\}$. Every point of it has $0\le y\le5$.

\emph{Upper bound.} Let an equilateral triangle of side $s$ lie in the half-disc. Its three sides point in three directions that are $60^\circ$ apart, so they split the $180^\circ$ of possible directions into three arcs of $60^\circ$. Hence some side makes an angle of at most $30^\circ$ with the vertical, and so an angle of at least $60^\circ$ with the horizontal. The two endpoints of that side differ in height by at least $s\sin60^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt3}2s$. Both endpoints lie in the half-disc, so this difference is at most $5$. Hence $s\le\dfrac{10}{\sqrt3}$.

\emph{Construction.} The triangle $PQT$ with $P=\left(-\dfrac5{\sqrt3},0\right)$, $Q=\left(\dfrac5{\sqrt3},0\right)$, $T=(0,5)$ has side $\dfrac{10}{\sqrt3}$ and height $5$, and lies in the half-disc: $T$ is on the arc and $P$, $Q$ are on the diameter.

So the largest area is
\[\dfrac{\sqrt3}4\cdot\left(\dfrac{10}{\sqrt3}\right)^2=\boxed{\dfrac{25\sqrt3}{3}}\approx14.43.\]
(The dashed triangle in the sketch is a tilted triangle; any such triangle is no larger.)

