question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Write $4.9$ and $3.\overline{56}=3.565656\ldots$ as quotients of integers.
\item[(b)] Prove that every decimal that terminates, or eventually repeats, represents a rational number.
\item[(c)] Deduce that the decimal expansion of an irrational number, such as $\pi$ or $\sqrt2$, neither terminates nor eventually repeats.
\end{enumerate}

Let $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$ be integers with $b\ne0$ and $d\ne0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that
\[\dfrac ab+\dfrac cd=\dfrac{ad+bc}{bd}.\]
\item[(b)] Show by an example that the fraction $\dfrac{ad+bc}{bd}$ need not be in lowest terms, and compute $\dfrac16+\dfrac1{10}$ in lowest terms.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
There are $\dbinom92=36$ equally likely pairs. Let the chosen marbles be $i<j$. Going clockwise around the circumscribed circle, the three vertices cut it into arcs of
\[g_1=i-1,\qquad g_2=j-i,\qquad g_3=11-j\]
steps of $36^\circ$, where $g_1+g_2+g_3=10$ and each $g_k\ge1$. Each angle of the triangle is an inscribed angle subtending one of these arcs, so it equals $18^\circ\cdot g_k$.

The centre lies inside or on the triangle exactly when no angle is obtuse, i.e. every $g_k\le5$. (If an angle is obtuse, the centre lies beyond the opposite side; if it is right, the centre is the midpoint of the hypotenuse.)

The pairs $(i,j)$ correspond one-to-one to the triples $(g_1,g_2,g_3)$ of positive integers with sum $10$. A triple with some $g_k\ge6$ has its other two parts summing to at most $4$. For a given position $k$, put $g_k=5+e$ with $e\ge1$; then $e$ and the other two parts are positive with sum $5$, which gives $\dbinom42=6$ triples. Only one part can be $\ge6$, so $3\cdot6=18$ triples are bad, and $36-18=18$ are good.

The probability is $\dfrac{18}{36}=\boxed{\dfrac12}$. In the sketch, triangle $1,4,8$ (arcs $3,4,3$, shaded) contains the centre, while triangle $1,3,5$ (arcs $2,2,6$) does not.

