question:
A ten-sided die has the shape of a pentagonal trapezohedron: it has $10$ congruent faces, each a kite (a quadrilateral that is not a square). Five faces meet at a top vertex and five at a bottom vertex, and every other vertex lies on three faces.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Use Euler's formula $V-E+F=2$ to find the numbers of vertices $V$ and edges $E$.
\item[(b)] A Platonic solid is a convex polyhedron whose faces are congruent regular polygons, with the same number of faces meeting at every vertex. Give two reasons why this die is not a Platonic solid.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Each of the $F=10$ faces has $4$ edges, and each edge lies on exactly $2$ faces, so $E=\dfrac{10\cdot4}2=20$. By Euler's formula, $V=2-F+E=2-10+20=12$. So
\[\boxed{V=12,\qquad E=20}.\]
Check by counting edge ends at the vertices: the top and bottom vertices have $5$ edges each and the other $10$ vertices have $3$ each, giving $5+5+30=40=2E$.

\textbf{(b)}
\begin{itemize}
\item The faces are kites that are not squares, so they are not regular polygons. (A regular quadrilateral is a square.)
\item The vertices are not all alike: $5$ faces meet at the top and bottom vertices, but only $3$ at the other ten.
\end{itemize}
(Also, the only Platonic solid with quadrilateral faces is the cube, which has $6$ faces, not $10$.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real c=0.12, t=c*(1+Cos(36))/(1-Cos(36));
triple[] P={(0,0,t),(0,0,-t)};
for(int k=0;k<5;++k) P.push((Cos(72k),Sin(72k),c));
for(int k=0;k<5;++k) P.push((Cos(72k+36),Sin(72k+36),-c));
real el=15, az=-100;
triple v=(Cos(el)*Cos(az),Cos(el)*Sin(az),Sin(el));
triple e1=(-Sin(az),Cos(az),0);
triple e2=cross(v,e1);
pair pr(triple Q){return (dot(Q,e1),dot(Q,e2));}
int[][] F;
for(int k=0;k<5;++k){F.push(new int[]{0,2+k,7+k,2+(k+1)%5}); F.push(new int[]{1,7+k,2+(k+1)%5,7+(k+1)%5});}
bool front(int[] f){triple n=cross(P[f[1]]-P[f[0]],P[f[3]]-P[f[0]]); triple g=(P[f[0]]+P[f[1]]+P[f[2]]+P[f[3]])/4; if(dot(n,g)<0) n=-n; return dot(n,v)>0;}
path pf(int[] f){return pr(P[f[0]])--pr(P[f[1]])--pr(P[f[2]])--pr(P[f[3]])--cycle;}
fill(pf(F[6]),gray(0.88));
bool[][] seen=new bool[12][12], vis=new bool[12][12];
for(int i=0;i<12;++i) for(int j=0;j<12;++j){seen[i][j]=false; vis[i][j]=false;}
for(int[] f:F){bool fr=front(f); for(int j=0;j<4;++j){int a=min(f[j],f[(j+1)%4]), b=max(f[j],f[(j+1)%4]); seen[a][b]=true; if(fr) vis[a][b]=true;}}
for(int a=0;a<12;++a) for(int b=a+1;b<12;++b) if(seen[a][b] && !vis[a][b]) draw(pr(P[a])--pr(P[b]),dashed+gray(0.4));
for(int a=0;a<12;++a) for(int b=a+1;b<12;++b) if(vis[a][b]) draw(pr(P[a])--pr(P[b]),linewidth(0.8));
dot(pr(P[0]));dot(pr(P[1]));
label("$5$ faces",pr(P[0]),N,fontsize(9pt));label("$5$ faces",pr(P[1]),S,fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

$2001=3\cdot23\cdot29$. Since the three factors are distinct, at most one of them is $1$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If one factor is $1$, the other two are complementary divisors greater than $1$: $\{3,667\}$, $\{23,87\}$ or $\{29,69\}$, with sums $1+3+667=671$, $111$ and $99$.
\item If no factor is $1$, each factor is a product of at least one of the three primes, so the factors are $3,23,29$, with sum $55$.
\end{itemize}
The largest possible sum is $\boxed{671}$.

No genuine (nondegenerate) triangle exists.

Suppose $AB=AC=6$ and $BC=12$. Then $A$ lies on the circle of radius $6$ about $B$ and on the circle of radius $6$ about $C$. Since $BC=6+6$, these circles touch at exactly one point, the midpoint $M$ of $BC$. Indeed, if a common point $P$ were not on line $BC$, the triangle inequality in triangle $PBC$ would give $BC<PB+PC=12$, a contradiction; and the only point of line $BC$ at distance $6$ from both $B$ and $C$ is $M$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(12,0),M=(6,0);
draw(circle(B,6),gray);draw(circle(C,6),gray);
draw(B--C,linewidth(0.9));dot(B);dot(C);dot(M);
draw(B--(6*dir(60)),dashed);label("$6$",3*dir(60),NW);
draw(C--(C+6*dir(120)),dashed);label("$6$",C+3*dir(120),NE);
label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,E);label("$M$",M,S);label("$12$",(9,0),S);
\end{asy}

So the only possible position of $A$ is $M$: the three ``vertices'' are collinear and the figure has area $0$. This is a degenerate triangle, not a real one. In general, positive lengths $a\le b\le c$ form a nondegenerate triangle exactly when $a+b>c$; here $6+6=12$ is not greater than $12$. So the answer is $\boxed{\text{no}}$.

question:
Let $k$ be a positive integer and let $n_0,n_1,\dots,n_k$ be the sequence of consecutive whole numbers $0,1,2,\dots,k$, so that $n_i=i$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $n_i+n_{k-i}=k$ for every $i=0,1,\dots,k$, and that for each $i$ the index $j=k-i$ is the only index with $n_i+n_j=k$.
\item[(b)] Use (a) to prove that $0+1+2+\cdots+k=\dfrac{k(k+1)}{2}$.
\end{enumerate}

Six points in space are given, no three of them on a line. Each of the $15$ segments joining two of the points is coloured green or purple.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that there is a triangle whose three vertices are among the six given points and whose three sides all have the same colour.
\item[(b)] Show that the statement of (a) is false for five points.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Pick one of the points, $P$. It is joined to the other five points by five segments, so by the pigeonhole principle at least three of them have the same colour, say green: $PA$, $PB$, $PC$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If one of $AB$, $BC$, $CA$ is green, say $AB$, then $PAB$ is a triangle with three green sides.
\item Otherwise $AB$, $BC$, $CA$ are all purple, and $ABC$ is a triangle with three purple sides.
\end{itemize}
Either way there is a one-coloured triangle. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(b)} Take five points $1,2,3,4,5$ as the vertices of a regular pentagon (no three collinear). Colour the five sides green (solid) and the five diagonals purple (dashed).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] P;for(int i=0;i<5;++i)P.push(dir(90+72*i));
for(int i=0;i<5;++i)draw(P[i]--P[(i+1)%5],linewidth(1.2));
for(int i=0;i<5;++i)draw(P[i]--P[(i+2)%5],dashed);
for(int i=0;i<5;++i){dot(P[i]);label("$"+string(i+1)+"$",P[i],1.3*dir(90+72*i));}
\end{asy}

The green segments form the closed path $1,2,3,4,5,1$ and the purple segments form the closed path $1,3,5,2,4,1$. Three of the five points form a green triangle only if each two of them are consecutive on the green path, which is impossible for a path of length $5$; the same holds for purple. So no triangle has all sides of one colour.

