Let $O$ be the origin and let $P=(x,y)$ be a point of the coordinate plane at distance $d=OP$ from $O$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Without quoting the Pythagorean theorem, use similar triangles to prove that $x^2+y^2=d^2$.
\item[(b)] Deduce that the circle of radius $1$ centred at $O$ (the set of points at distance $1$ from $O$) is exactly the set of points $(x,y)$ with $x^2+y^2=1$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} If $x=0$, then $P$ lies on the $y$-axis and $d=|y|$, so $x^2+y^2=d^2$; similarly if $y=0$. Otherwise let $Q=(x,0)$. Triangle $OQP$ has a right angle at $Q$, legs $OQ=|x|$ and $QP=|y|$, and hypotenuse $OP=d$. Let $F$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $Q$ to $OP$. The angles of triangle $OQP$ at $O$ and at $P$ are acute (they add up to $90^\circ$), so $F$ lies between $O$ and $P$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),P=(0.574,0.819),Q=(P.x,0);pair F=dot(Q,P)*P;
draw(arc(O,1,-8,98),gray+dashed);
draw((-0.15,0)--(1.2,0),Arrow(4));draw((0,-0.15)--(0,1.15),Arrow(4));
draw(O--P--Q--cycle,linewidth(0.9));draw(Q--F,dashed);
pair u=unit(P),v=unit(Q-F);real r=0.05;
draw(Q+(-r,0)--Q+(-r,r)--Q+(0,r));draw(F+r*u--F+r*u+r*v--F+r*v);
dot(O);dot(P);dot(Q);dot(F);
label("$O$",O,SW);label("$Q(x,0)$",Q,S);label("$P(x,y)$",P,NE);label("$F$",F,NW);
label("$d$",0.75*P,NW);label("$x$",Q/2,S);label("$y$",(P+Q)/2,E);
label("$x$",(1.2,0),E);label("$y$",(0,1.15),N);
\end{asy}

\begin{itemize}
\item Triangles $OFQ$ and $OQP$ have right angles at $F$ and at $Q$ and share the angle at $O$, so they are similar. Hence $\dfrac{OF}{OQ}=\dfrac{OQ}{OP}$, i.e. $OF=\dfrac{x^2}d$.
\item Triangles $PFQ$ and $PQO$ have right angles at $F$ and at $Q$ and share the angle at $P$, so they are similar. Hence $\dfrac{PF}{PQ}=\dfrac{PQ}{PO}$, i.e. $PF=\dfrac{y^2}d$.
\end{itemize}
Since $F$ lies between $O$ and $P$,
\[d=OF+FP=\dfrac{x^2}d+\dfrac{y^2}d,\qquad\text{so}\qquad x^2+y^2=d^2.\]

\textbf{(b)} A point $P=(x,y)$ lies on the circle exactly when $d=1$. Since $d\ge0$, this holds exactly when $d^2=1$, i.e., by (a), when $x^2+y^2=1$. $\blacksquare$

(The argument in (a) is a proof of the Pythagorean theorem for the right triangle with legs $|x|$ and $|y|$. Some proof of that theorem is unavoidable here, because the equation $x^2+y^2=d^2$ \emph{is} the theorem for this triangle; what can be avoided is quoting it.)

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $\sin2\theta=2\sin\theta\cos\theta$ for every angle $\theta$ with $0^\circ<\theta<90^\circ$.
\item[(b)] Using the addition formula $\sin(\alpha+\beta)=\sin\alpha\cos\beta+\cos\alpha\sin\beta$, which holds for all angles, find all real numbers $c$ such that
\[\sin(c\theta)=c\sin\theta\cos\theta\qquad\text{for every angle }\theta.\]
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Draw an isosceles triangle $OAB$ with $OA=OB=1$ and $\angle AOB=2\theta$, and let $M$ be the midpoint of $AB$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real th=35;pair O=(0,0),A=3*dir(-90-th),B=3*dir(-90+th),M=(A+B)/2;
draw(A--O--B--cycle,linewidth(0.9));draw(O--M,dashed);
draw(arc(O,0.6,-90-th,-90));draw(arc(O,0.7,-90,-90+th));
label("$\theta$",O+0.95*dir(-90-th/2),fontsize(9pt));label("$\theta$",O+1.05*dir(-90+th/2),fontsize(9pt));
draw(M+0.25*dir(0)--M+0.25*dir(0)+0.25*dir(90)--M+0.25*dir(90));
label("$O$",O,N);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$M$",M,S);
label("$1$",(O+A)/2,W);label("$1$",(O+B)/2,E);label("$\cos\theta$",O+0.72*(M-O),E,fontsize(9pt));
label("$\sin\theta$",(A+M)/2,S,fontsize(9pt));label("$\sin\theta$",(M+B)/2,S,fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

Since the triangle is isosceles, $OM\perp AB$ and $OM$ bisects $\angle AOB$, so $\angle AOM=\theta$. In the right triangle $OMA$ with hypotenuse $1$, $AM=\sin\theta$ and $OM=\cos\theta$, so $AB=2\sin\theta$. Computing the area of $OAB$ in two ways,
\[\dfrac12\cdot OA\cdot OB\cdot\sin\angle AOB=\dfrac12\sin2\theta\qquad\text{and}\qquad \dfrac12\cdot AB\cdot OM=\sin\theta\cos\theta.\]
Hence $\sin2\theta=2\sin\theta\cos\theta$. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(b)} \emph{These values work.} For $c=2$, put $\alpha=\beta=\theta$ in the addition formula. For $c=-2$, use $\sin(-x)=-\sin x$: $\sin(-2\theta)=-2\sin\theta\cos\theta$. For $c=0$ both sides are $0$.

\emph{No others work.} Take $\theta=90^\circ$: then $\sin\left(c\cdot90^\circ\right)=0$, so $c\cdot90^\circ$ is a multiple of $180^\circ$ and $c=2m$ for an integer $m$. Take $\theta=45^\circ$: then $\sin\left(m\cdot90^\circ\right)=2m\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}=m$. Since $|\sin x|\le1$, $|m|\le1$.

So the identity holds for every $\theta$ exactly when $\boxed{c\in\{-2,0,2\}}$. For instance $c=3$ fails at $\theta=30^\circ$: $\sin90^\circ=1$ but $3\sin30^\circ\cos30^\circ=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{4}$.

