Call a direction (a unit vector $u$) \emph{good} if it is neither parallel nor perpendicular to any line through two of the given points. Only finitely many directions are bad, so there are infinitely many good directions, and good directions that are not parallel or perpendicular to each other give different pairs of lines.

Fix a good $u$ and let $v$ be perpendicular to $u$. Give each point $P$ the coordinates $s=P\cdot u$ and $t=P\cdot v$. Because $u$ is good, the $2n$ points have distinct $s$-values and distinct $t$-values.

\textbf{First line.} Let $\ell_1$ be the line $t=t_0$, where $t_0$ lies strictly between the $n$th and $(n+1)$th smallest $t$-values. It is parallel to $u$, misses all the points, and has exactly $n$ points below it and $n$ above it.

\textbf{Second line.} For a number $\sigma$ different from all $s$-values, let $\ell_2$ be the line $s=\sigma$, perpendicular to $\ell_1$. Let $a(\sigma)$ be the number of points below $\ell_1$ with $s<\sigma$ (the lower-left quadrant) and $b(\sigma)$ the number above $\ell_1$ with $s>\sigma$ (the opposite, upper-right quadrant), and put $D(\sigma)=a(\sigma)-b(\sigma)$.

For $\sigma$ smaller than every $s$-value, $D=0-n=-n$; for $\sigma$ larger than every $s$-value, $D=n-0=n$. As $\sigma$ increases past the $s$-value of one point (one at a time, since the $s$-values are distinct), either that point is below $\ell_1$ and $a$ increases by $1$, or it is above $\ell_1$ and $b$ decreases by $1$. Either way $D$ increases by exactly $1$. So $D$ goes from $-n$ to $n$ in steps of $1$ and equals $0$ for some $\sigma$ between two consecutive $s$-values. For that $\sigma$, $\ell_2$ misses all the points and the two opposite quadrants contain the same number of points.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] P={(0.5,3.2),(1.6,0.6),(2.4,2.1),(3.6,3.6),(4.1,1.0),(5.0,2.6)};
pair u=dir(20),v=dir(110);pair Oc=3.584*u+0.9428*v;
fill(Oc--(Oc-3.4*u)--(Oc-3.4*u-2.6*v)--(Oc-2.6*v)--cycle,lightgray);
fill(Oc--(Oc+3.4*u)--(Oc+3.4*u+2.6*v)--(Oc+2.6*v)--cycle,lightgray);
draw((Oc-3.4*u)--(Oc+3.4*u),linewidth(0.9));draw((Oc-2.6*v)--(Oc+2.6*v),linewidth(0.9));
for(pair p:P)dot(p);
label("$\ell_1$",Oc+3.4*u,E);label("$\ell_2$",Oc+2.6*v,N);
\end{asy}

(In the sketch, $n=3$; the shaded opposite quadrants contain one point each.)

Doing this for each of the infinitely many good directions gives infinitely many such pairs of lines. $\blacksquare$

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that if the quadratic equation $ax^2+bx+c=0$ with rational coefficients ($a\ne0$) has a rational root, then its other root is rational too.
\item[(b)] Show that the statement ``if one root of a quadratic is rational, then so is the other'' is false if the coefficients are allowed to be irrational.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Find the exponent of each prime in $M$ by adding its exponents in the six factorials:
\begin{itemize}
\item $2$: $4+7+7+8+8+10=44$;
\item $3$: $2+2+4+4+4+5=21$;
\item $5$: $1+1+1+2+2+2=9$;
\item $7$: $1\cdot6=6$;
\item $11$: $0+0+0+0+1+1=2$.
\end{itemize}
So $M=2^{44}3^{21}5^9 7^6 11^2$. A square factor has even exponents: $0,2,\dots,44$ for $2$ ($23$ choices), $0,\dots,20$ for $3$ ($11$), $0,\dots,8$ for $5$ ($5$), $0,\dots,6$ for $7$ ($4$), and $0,2$ for $11$ ($2$). The number of square factors is
\[23\cdot11\cdot5\cdot4\cdot2=\boxed{10120}.\]

question:
\textbf{Problem.} For each $i=1,2,3$, at least one of $a_i$ and $b_i$ has property $P$. Prove that at least two of $a_1,a_2,a_3$ have property $P$, or at least two of $b_1,b_2,b_3$ have property $P$.

A student writes: ``Suppose instead that no more than one of $a_1,a_2,a_3$, or no more than one of $b_1,b_2,b_3$, has property $P$. We know that at least $3$ numbers have property $P$, but under our hypothesis at most $1$ number can have property $P$. Contradiction.''
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain what is wrong with this proof.
\item[(b)] Give a correct proof.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Since $a<b<c$, we have $ab<ac<bc$, so in the second triangle the hypotenuse would be $bc$. We need
\[(ab)^2+(ac)^2=(bc)^2\iff a^2\left(b^2+c^2\right)=b^2c^2.\]
Substitute $c^2=a^2+b^2$:
\[a^2\left(a^2+2b^2\right)=b^2\left(a^2+b^2\right)\iff a^4+a^2b^2-b^4=0.\]
Dividing by $a^4$ and putting $r=\dfrac{b^2}{a^2}>0$ gives $r^2-r-1=0$, so $r=\varphi=\dfrac{1+\sqrt5}2$. Then $b=a\sqrt\varphi$ and $c^2=a^2(1+\varphi)=a^2\varphi^2$ (as $\varphi^2=\varphi+1$), so $c=a\varphi$. Each step can be reversed, so these triangles work, and $a<b<c$ holds since $1<\sqrt\varphi<\varphi$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real phi=(1+sqrt(5))/2;real a=2,b=2*sqrt(phi);
pair C=(0,0),B=(b,0),A=(0,a);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));draw((0.2,0)--(0.2,0.2)--(0,0.2));
label("$a$",(A+C)/2,W);label("$b$",(B+C)/2,S);label("$c$",(A+B)/2,NE);
pair d=(4.2,0);real s=0.5;
pair C2=d,B2=d+(s*a*b,0),A2=d+(0,s*a*phi*a);
draw(A2--B2--C2--cycle,linewidth(0.9));draw(C2+(0.2,0)--C2+(0.2,0.2)--C2+(0,0.2));
label("$ac$",(A2+C2)/2,W);label("$ab$",(B2+C2)/2,S);label("$bc$",(A2+B2)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

So the triangles are exactly those similar to
\[\boxed{a:b:c=1:\sqrt\varphi:\varphi,\qquad \varphi=\dfrac{1+\sqrt5}2}.\]
The sides form a geometric progression with ratio $\sqrt\varphi$ (the Kepler triangle), and $ab:ac:bc=\sqrt\varphi:\varphi:\varphi\sqrt\varphi$ is the same shape again. (The second triangle in the sketch is drawn at half scale.)

question:
The Sieve of Eratosthenes lists the primes up to $100$ by crossing out every multiple of $2$, $3$, $5$ and $7$ other than these primes themselves (and the number $1$).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why it is enough to cross out the multiples of these four primes.
\item[(b)] How many primes are less than or equal to $100$?
\end{enumerate}

A bank pays simple interest at an annual rate of $6\%$ on a deposit of \$1000, but pays it in two instalments each year: every six months it pays $3\%$ of the original deposit, and this interest is not reinvested.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How much money (deposit plus interest) is there after $3$ years? Explain why, for simple interest, paying the interest $m$ times a year at the rate $\dfrac rm$ per period gives the same total as paying it once a year.
\item[(b)] Compare with a rate of $6\%$ per year compounded twice a year, i.e. $3\%$ of the current balance every six months, for $3$ years.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{Step 1: $X$, $Y$, $Z$ are feet of perpendiculars.} Since $\angle AA_1O=\angle AA_2O=90^\circ$, the points $A_1$ and $A_2$ lie on the circle with diameter $AO$. They differ from $A$: if $A$ were on $b$, then $b$ would be the line $CA$ and $O$ would lie on side $CA$, which happens only for a right angle at $B$ (similarly for $c$). And $A_1\ne A_2$: a common point would lie on both $b$ and $c$, so it would be $O$, and $AO$ would be perpendicular to both of the non-parallel lines $b$ and $c$. So the circle through $A$, $A_1$, $A_2$ is the circle with diameter $AO$, which passes through $O$. The foot $F$ of the perpendicular from $A$ to $a$ also lies on this circle (as $\angle AFO=90^\circ$, or $F=A$). Moreover $F\ne O$: otherwise $AO\perp BC$, and since $O$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $BC$, line $AO$ would be that bisector and $AB=AC$. A line meets a circle in at most two points, so the intersection of $a$ with the circle other than $O$ is $X=F$. Similarly $Y$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $B$ to $b$, and $Z$ is the foot from $C$ to $c$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),A=3*dir(90),B=3*dir(250),C=3*dir(330);
pair pr(pair P,pair d){d=unit(d);return dot(P,d)*d;}
pair X=pr(A,C-B),Y=pr(B,A-C),Z=pr(C,B-A),A1=pr(A,A-C),A2=pr(A,B-A);
draw(circle(O,3),gray(0.6));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((-3.6*unit(C-B))--(3.6*unit(C-B)),gray);draw((-3.6*unit(A-C))--(3.6*unit(A-C)),gray);draw((-3.6*unit(B-A))--(3.6*unit(B-A)),gray);
label("$a$",3.6*unit(C-B),E,gray);label("$b$",-3.6*unit(A-C),SW,gray);label("$c$",-3.6*unit(B-A),N,gray);
draw(circle(A/2,1.5),dashed);
draw(A--A1,dotted);draw(A--A2,dotted);draw(A--X,dotted);
draw(X--Y--Z--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(O);dot(A1);dot(A2);dot(X,blue);dot(Y,blue);dot(Z,blue);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$O$",O,SW);
label("$A_1$",A1,W);label("$A_2$",A2,E);label("$X$",X,SE,blue);label("$Y$",Y,SE,blue);label("$Z$",Z,W,blue);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 2: one vector for all three.} Use vectors with origin $O$, so $|A|=|B|=|C|=R$, the circumradius. Let $u$ be a unit vector along $BC$. The foot of the perpendicular from $A$ to the line $a$ (through $O$ in direction $u$) is $X=(A\cdot u)\,u$. Put $H=A+B+C$. Since
\[(B+C)\cdot(C-B)=|C|^2-|B|^2=0,\]
we have $H\cdot u=A\cdot u$, so $X=(H\cdot u)\,u$. Likewise $Y=(H\cdot v)\,v$ and $Z=(H\cdot w)\,w$, with unit vectors $v$ along $CA$ and $w$ along $AB$. So $X,Y,Z$ are the projections of the single point $H$ onto the lines $a,b,c$. (In fact $H$ is the orthocentre, and $H\ne O$ because a triangle whose orthocentre is its circumcentre is equilateral.)

\textbf{Step 3: a lemma.} If two lines through $O$ make an angle $\phi$, the projections of $H$ onto them are $|H|\,|\sin\phi|$ apart. Choose axes with $H=(h,0)$, $u=(\cos\alpha,\sin\alpha)$, $v=(\cos\beta,\sin\beta)$. Then $X=h\cos\alpha\,u$ and $Y=h\cos\beta\,v$, and
\[\dfrac{|X-Y|^2}{h^2}=\cos^2\alpha+\cos^2\beta-2\cos\alpha\cos\beta\cos(\alpha-\beta).\]
Expanding $\cos(\alpha-\beta)=\cos\alpha\cos\beta+\sin\alpha\sin\beta$ and $\sin^2(\alpha-\beta)=(\sin\alpha\cos\beta-\cos\alpha\sin\beta)^2$, the difference between this expression and $\sin^2(\alpha-\beta)$ is
\[\cos^2\alpha\left(1-\cos^2\beta-\sin^2\beta\right)+\cos^2\beta\left(1-\cos^2\alpha-\sin^2\alpha\right)=0.\]
So $|X-Y|=h\,|\sin(\alpha-\beta)|$, as claimed.

\textbf{Step 4: compare the sides.} Lines $a$ and $b$ are parallel to $BC$ and $CA$, so the angle between them is $\angle C$ or $180^\circ-\angle C$, and $|\sin\phi|=\sin C$. Hence $XY=OH\sin C$, and similarly $YZ=OH\sin A$ and $ZX=OH\sin B$. By the extended law of sines, $AB=2R\sin C$, $BC=2R\sin A$, $CA=2R\sin B$. Therefore
\[\dfrac{XY}{AB}=\dfrac{YZ}{BC}=\dfrac{ZX}{CA}=\dfrac{OH}{2R}>0,\]
and by SSS similarity triangle $XYZ$ is similar to triangle $ABC$, with $X,Y,Z$ corresponding to $A,B,C$. $\blacksquare$

question:
Johnny picks one of the integers $0,1,\dots,5$ and Jane picks one of the integers $0,1,\dots,8$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Is it always possible to find a whole number that leaves Johnny's number as its remainder when divided by $6$ and Jane's number as its remainder when divided by $9$?
\item[(b)] For how many of the $54$ possible pairs of choices is it possible?
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove by contradiction that $\sqrt{15}$ is irrational.
\item[(b)] If the same argument is applied to $\sqrt{25}$, where exactly does it break down?
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why the sum of the coefficients of a polynomial $P(x)$ equals $P(1)$.
\item[(b)] Find the sum of the coefficients of the expansion of $\left(2x^2-x+1\right)^5$.
\item[(c)] Find the sum of the coefficients of the even powers of $x$ (including the constant term) in the same expansion.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the pentagon be $ABCDE$ with side $s=20$ and diagonal $d$. Each interior angle is $108^\circ$, and each diagonal makes $36^\circ$ with the two sides at its endpoint (for example, triangle $ABC$ is isosceles with apex angle $108^\circ$, so $\angle BAC=\angle BCA=36^\circ$).

Let diagonal $AC$ meet $BE$ at $P$ and $BD$ at $Q$; then $PQ$ is a side of the small pentagon.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V;for(int i=0;i<5;++i)V.push(dir(90+72*i));
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
for(int i=0;i<5;++i)draw(V[i]--V[(i+2)%5]);
pair Q=extension(V[0],V[2],V[1],V[3]),P=extension(V[0],V[2],V[1],V[4]);
pair[] I;for(int i=0;i<5;++i)I.push(extension(V[i],V[(i+2)%5],V[(i+1)%5],V[(i+3)%5]));
draw(I[0]--I[1]--I[2]--I[3]--I[4]--cycle,blue+linewidth(1.1));
label("$A$",V[0],N);label("$B$",V[1],W);label("$C$",V[2],SW);label("$D$",V[3],SE);label("$E$",V[4],E);
dot(P);dot(Q);label("$P$",P,NE,fontsize(8pt));label("$Q$",Q,W,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\begin{itemize}
\item Triangle $BQC$ has $\angle QBC=\angle QCB=36^\circ$, so it is similar to triangle $ABC$ (angles $36^\circ,108^\circ,36^\circ$). Hence $\dfrac{QC}{BC}=\dfrac{AB}{AC}$, i.e. $QC=\dfrac{s^2}d$. By symmetry $AP=\dfrac{s^2}d$ as well.
\item Triangle $ABQ$ has $\angle BAQ=36^\circ$ and $\angle ABQ=108^\circ-36^\circ=72^\circ$, so $\angle AQB=72^\circ$ and $AQ=AB=s$.
\end{itemize}
From $AC=AQ+QC$: $d=s+\dfrac{s^2}d$, so $\left(\dfrac ds\right)^2=\dfrac ds+1$ and $\dfrac ds=\varphi=\dfrac{1+\sqrt5}2$. Then
\[PQ=AC-AP-QC=d-\dfrac{2s^2}d=s\left(\varphi-\dfrac2\varphi\right)=s(2-\varphi)=s\cdot\dfrac{3-\sqrt5}2,\]
using $\dfrac1\varphi=\varphi-1$. With $s=20$, $PQ=30-10\sqrt5$, and the perimeter is
\[5\left(30-10\sqrt5\right)=\boxed{150-50\sqrt5}\approx38.2.\]

The point at angle $-\theta$ is obtained by turning through $\theta$ clockwise instead of anticlockwise. Reflection in the $x$-axis maps the unit circle to itself, fixes the positive $x$-axis and reverses the direction of turning, so it maps the point $P$ at angle $\theta$ to the point $P'$ at angle $-\theta$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;real t=35;pair P=dir(t),Q=dir(-t);
draw((-1.3,0)--(1.4,0),Arrow(4));draw((0,-1.3)--(0,1.3),Arrow(4));draw(unitcircle);
draw((0,0)--P);draw((0,0)--Q);draw(P--Q,dashed);
draw(arc((0,0),0.3,0,t),Arrow(3));draw(arc((0,0),0.4,0,-t),Arrow(3));
dot(P);dot(Q);label("$P(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$",P,NE);label("$P'(\cos\theta,-\sin\theta)$",Q,SE);
label("$\theta$",0.45*dir(t/2),fontsize(8pt));label("$-\theta$",0.58*dir(-t/2),fontsize(8pt));label("$x$",(1.4,0),E);label("$y$",(0,1.3),N);
\end{asy}

Reflection in the $x$-axis sends $(x,y)$ to $(x,-y)$. So
\[\left(\cos(-\theta),\sin(-\theta)\right)=P'=(\cos\theta,-\sin\theta),\]
that is, $\cos(-\theta)=\cos\theta$ and $\sin(-\theta)=-\sin\theta$. $\blacksquare$

In words: cosine depends only on the horizontal coordinate, which reflection does not change, while sine is the vertical coordinate, which changes sign. (For example $\sin(-30^\circ)=-\dfrac12$ but $\cos(-60^\circ)=\cos60^\circ=\dfrac12$.)

question:
A student claims that the number of factors of $y$ in $x!$, that is, the largest $k$ with $y^k\mid x!$, equals $\left\lfloor\dfrac xy\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\dfrac x{y^2}\right\rfloor$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the largest $k$ with $2^k\mid32!$, and show that the claim fails here. State and prove the correct formula when $y=p$ is a prime.
\item[(b)] Find the largest $k$ with $6^k\mid10!$, and show that the claim fails here too.
\end{enumerate}

Sue and Barry each try to find the area of triangle $ABC$. Sue mistakenly computes $\dfrac12\cdot AB$ times the length of the altitude from $A$ (instead of the altitude from $C$). Barry mistakenly computes $\dfrac12\cdot BC$ times the length of the altitude from $C$ (instead of the altitude from $A$).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Sue gets $12$ and Barry gets $27$. What is the area of triangle $ABC$?
\item[(b)] Suppose instead that Sue gets $120$ and Barry gets $150$. What is the area now?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $K$ be the area, $h_A$ the altitude from $A$ (to $BC$) and $h_C$ the altitude from $C$ (to $AB$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(7,0),A=(2,4.5);
pair F=B+dot(A-B,unit(C-B))*unit(C-B);pair G=B+dot(C-B,unit(A-B))*unit(A-B);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));draw(A--F,dashed);draw(C--G,dashed);
draw(F+0.25*dir(0)--F+0.25*dir(0)+0.25*dir(90)--F+0.25*dir(90));
pair ua=unit(A-B),uc=unit(C-G);draw(G+0.25*uc--G+0.25*uc+0.25*ua--G+0.25*ua);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$h_A$",(A+3*F)/4,E);label("$h_C$",(C+G)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

Then $K=\dfrac12\,AB\cdot h_C=\dfrac12\,BC\cdot h_A$. Sue's number is $s=\dfrac12\,AB\cdot h_A$ and Barry's is $t=\dfrac12\,BC\cdot h_C$, so
\[st=\dfrac14\,AB\cdot h_A\cdot BC\cdot h_C=\left(\dfrac12\,AB\cdot h_C\right)\left(\dfrac12\,BC\cdot h_A\right)=K^2,\qquad K=\sqrt{st}.\]

\textbf{(a)} $K=\sqrt{12\cdot27}=\sqrt{324}=\boxed{18}$.

\textbf{(b)} $K=\sqrt{120\cdot150}=\sqrt{18000}=\boxed{60\sqrt5}$.

(Such triangles exist: since $h_A=\dfrac{2K}{BC}$, Sue's number is $s=K\cdot\dfrac{AB}{BC}$, so any triangle with area $K$ and $\dfrac{AB}{BC}=\dfrac sK$ works, for instance a right triangle with legs $AB$ and $BC$.)

question:
Let $f$ be a function and $k>0$ a constant.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that the graph of $y=f(x+k)$ is the graph of $y=f(x)$ translated $k$ units to the left.
\item[(b)] Prove that the graph of $y=f(kx)$ is obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by moving each point $(x,y)$ to $\left(\dfrac xk,y\right)$, a horizontal compression by the factor $k$ towards the $y$-axis (a stretch if $k<1$).
\item[(c)] For $f(x)=x^2-4x+3$, find the vertices of the parabolas $y=f(x)$, $y=f(x+3)$ and $y=f(2x)$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} A point $(p,q)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x+k)$ exactly when $q=f(p+k)$, i.e. exactly when $(p+k,q)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$. So the new graph consists of the points $(u-k,q)$ for $(u,q)$ on the old graph: every point moves $k$ units left. (The shift is ``opposite'' to the sign of $k$ because the new function reaches the old value $f(u)$ at the smaller input $u-k$.)

\textbf{(b)} Similarly, $(p,q)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(kx)$ exactly when $(kp,q)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$. So the new graph consists of the points $\left(\dfrac uk,q\right)$ for $(u,q)$ on the old graph.

\textbf{(c)} $f(x)=(x-2)^2-1$ has vertex $(2,-1)$. For $y=f(x+3)$ the vertex is where $x+3=2$, at $(-1,-1)$. For $y=f(2x)$ it is where $2x=2$, at $(1,-1)$. The vertices are
\[\boxed{(2,-1),\quad(-1,-1),\quad(1,-1)}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return x^2-4*x+3;}
real g(real x){return f(x+3);}
real h(real x){return f(2*x);}
real r3=sqrt(3);
draw((-3.3,0)--(4.4,0),Arrow(4));draw((0,-1.6)--(0,2.9),Arrow(4));
draw(graph(f,2-r3,2+r3),linewidth(0.9));draw(graph(g,-1-r3,-1+r3),blue+linewidth(0.9));draw(graph(h,(2-r3)/2,(2+r3)/2),red+dashed+linewidth(0.9));
dot((2,-1));dot((-1,-1),blue);dot((1,-1),red);
draw((2,-1)--(-1,-1),gray,Arrow(4));label("$(1,-1)$",(1,-1),S,red+fontsize(8pt));
label("$y=f(x)$",(2+r3,2),E);label("$y=f(x+3)$",(-1-r3,2),W,blue);label("$y=f(2x)$",((2+r3)/2,2),N,red);
label("$(2,-1)$",(2,-1),SE,fontsize(8pt));label("$(-1,-1)$",(-1,-1),SW,blue+fontsize(8pt));label("$x$",(4.4,0),E);label("$y$",(0,2.9),N);
\end{asy}

Let the sides be positive integers $x$ and $y$ with $xy=36$. Since $x\ge1$ and $y\ge1$,
\[(x-1)(y-1)\ge0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad xy-x-y+1\ge0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad x+y\le xy+1=37.\]
So the perimeter $2(x+y)$ is at most $74$, with equality exactly when $x=1$ or $y=1$, i.e. for the $1\times36$ rectangle.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(36,0)--(36,1)--(0,1)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));label("$36$",(18,1),N);label("$1$",(36,0.5),E);
draw((0,-9)--(6,-9)--(6,-3)--(0,-3)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));label("$6$",(3,-9),S);label("$6$",(6,-6),E);
label("perimeter $74$",(18,-1.2),S,fontsize(9pt));label("perimeter $24$",(8,-6),E,fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

The greatest perimeter is $\boxed{74\text{ cm}}$. (The same argument shows that, for a fixed area, the long thin rectangle has the largest perimeter; the $6\times6$ square has the smallest, $24$ cm.)

question:
The Venn diagram shows sets $A$, $B$, $C$ inside a universal set (the rectangle), dividing it into the eight regions $R_1,\dots,R_8$. Write each region as an expression in $A$, $B$ and $C$ using union, intersection and complement (the complement of $X$ is written $X'$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair a=(-0.6,0.35),bb=(0.6,0.35),c=(0,-0.6);
draw((-2,-1.85)--(2,-1.85)--(2,1.6)--(-2,1.6)--cycle);
draw(circle(a,1));draw(circle(bb,1));draw(circle(c,1));
label("$A$",a+1.05*dir(135),NW);label("$B$",bb+1.05*dir(45),NE);label("$C$",c+1.05*dir(-45),SE);
label("$R_1$",(0,0.03),fontsize(8pt));label("$R_2$",(0,0.85),fontsize(8pt));label("$R_3$",(-0.7,-0.35),fontsize(8pt));
label("$R_4$",(-1.15,0.6),fontsize(8pt));label("$R_5$",(0.7,-0.35),fontsize(8pt));label("$R_6$",(1.15,0.6),fontsize(8pt));
label("$R_7$",(0,-1.2),fontsize(8pt));label("$R_8$",(1.65,-1.5),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

solution:
If $n=p_1^{e_1}\cdots p_r^{e_r}$ then $n$ has $(e_1+1)\cdots(e_r+1)$ divisors. Since $5\mid n$ and $7\mid n$, $r\ge2$, and $27$ must be a product of at least two factors greater than $1$: $27=9\cdot3$ or $27=3\cdot3\cdot3$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $n=p^8q^2$: the primes must be $5$ and $7$, but then $n\ge5^8\cdot7^2>9999$.
\item $n=p^2q^2r^2=(5\cdot7\cdot r)^2=1225r^2$ with a prime $r\ne5,7$: $r=2$ gives $4900$, while $r\ge3$ gives $n\ge11025$, too large.
\end{itemize}
So the only such number is $\boxed{4900}$ (indeed $4900=2^2\cdot5^2\cdot7^2$ has $3\cdot3\cdot3=27$ divisors).

The slope is $\dfrac{4-7}{2-(-1)}=\dfrac{-3}{3}=-1$. Using the point $(2,4)$: $y-4=-(x-2)$, so
\[\boxed{y=-x+6}\qquad(\text{equivalently } x+y=6).\]
Check: $-(-1)+6=7$ and $-2+6=4$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
draw((-2.5,0)--(7.5,0),Arrow(4));draw((0,-1)--(0,8.5),Arrow(4));
draw((-2,8)--(7,-1),linewidth(0.9));
dot((-1,7));dot((2,4));dot((6,0));dot((0,6));
label("$(-1,7)$",(-1,7),W);label("$(2,4)$",(2,4),NE);label("$(0,6)$",(0,6),NE);label("$(6,0)$",(6,0),SW);
label("$y=-x+6$",(5,1),NE);label("$x$",(7.5,0),E);label("$y$",(0,8.5),N);
\end{asy}

question:
Every day, three penguins sit in a circle, so that each penguin's two neighbours are the other two penguins, and each penguin is either happy or sad for that day. Over many days the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item on the days when both neighbours of a penguin are happy, that penguin is happy $\dfrac23$ of the time;
\item on the days when exactly one neighbour of a penguin is happy, that penguin is happy $\dfrac12$ of the time;
\item on the days when neither neighbour of a penguin is happy, that penguin is happy $\dfrac13$ of the time.
\end{enumerate}
On what fraction of the days are all three penguins happy?

solution:
For each $a$ there are $21$ products: the multiples of $3$ from $90$ to $150$, of $4$ from $120$ to $200$, and of $5$ from $150$ to $250$, $63$ in total. Remove the repeats:
\begin{itemize}
\item in both of the first two lists: multiples of $12$ in $[120,150]$: $120,132,144$ ($3$);
\item in the first and third: multiples of $15$ in $[150,150]$: $150$ ($1$);
\item in the second and third: multiples of $20$ in $[150,200]$: $160,180,200$ ($3$);
\item in all three: multiples of $60$ in $[150,150]$: none.
\end{itemize}
By inclusion--exclusion there are $63-3-1-3+0=\boxed{56}$ distinct products.

The parabola $y=(x+p)(x+q)$ has its vertex midway between the roots $-p$ and $-q$:
\[x_v=-\dfrac{p+q}2,\qquad y_v=\left(\dfrac{q-p}2\right)\left(\dfrac{p-q}2\right)=-\dfrac{(p-q)^2}4.\]
For $p=9$ and $p=7$ (with the same $q=b$), the vertices differ by $1$ horizontally and by
\[\dfrac{(9-b)^2-(7-b)^2}4=\dfrac{2(16-2b)}4=8-b\]
vertically. By the distance formula, $1+(8-b)^2=122$, so $8-b=\pm11$ and $b=-3$ or $b=19$.

The roots of $(x+9)(x+b)$ are $-9$ and $-b$, with sum $-9-b$:
\[\boxed{-6\ \text{ or }\ -28}.\]
For example, with $b=-3$ the vertices are $V=(-3,-36)$ and $V'=(-2,-25)$, at distance $\sqrt{1+121}=\sqrt{122}$.

