Let $N=m+n>n$. The condition is
\[\dfrac{n(n-1)(n-2)}{N(N-1)(N-2)}=\dfrac1{28}\iff N(N-1)(N-2)=28\,n(n-1)(n-2),\]
and $n\ge3$. The product of three consecutive integers increases with $N$, so check each $n$ in turn:
\begin{itemize}
\item $n=3$: $168$ lies strictly between $6\cdot5\cdot4=120$ and $7\cdot6\cdot5=210$;
\item $n=4$: $672$ lies strictly between $9\cdot8\cdot7=504$ and $10\cdot9\cdot8=720$;
\item $n=5$: $1680$ lies strictly between $12\cdot11\cdot10=1320$ and $13\cdot12\cdot11=1716$;
\item $n=6$: $3360=16\cdot15\cdot14$, so $N=16$ and $m=10$.
\end{itemize}
The least possible value is $n=\boxed{6}$ (with $10$ red marbles: $\dfrac{\binom63}{\binom{16}3}=\dfrac{20}{560}=\dfrac1{28}$).

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove or disprove: in every convex hexagon whose opposite sides are parallel, the three main diagonals (the segments joining opposite vertices) pass through one point.
\item[(b)] Prove that the three main diagonals do pass through one point if, in addition, opposite sides have equal lengths.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Label the hexagon $V_1V_2\dots V_6$; the main diagonals are $V_1V_4$, $V_2V_5$, $V_3V_6$.

\textbf{(a)} The statement is \textbf{false}. Take
\[V_1=(2,0),\ V_2=(9,0),\ V_3=(9,3),\ V_4=(6,6),\ V_5=(0,6),\ V_6=(0,2),\]
a convex hexagon (a right isosceles triangle with its three corners cut off). Opposite sides are parallel: $V_1V_2$ and $V_4V_5$ are horizontal, $V_2V_3$ and $V_5V_6$ are vertical, and $V_3V_4$ and $V_6V_1$ both have slope $-1$. Lines $V_1V_4$ ($y=\dfrac32(x-2)$) and $V_2V_5$ ($y=-\dfrac23(x-9)$) meet at $\left(\dfrac{54}{13},\dfrac{42}{13}\right)$. But line $V_3V_6$ is $y=2+\dfrac x9$, which at $x=\dfrac{54}{13}$ gives $y=\dfrac{32}{13}\ne\dfrac{42}{13}$. So the diagonals are not concurrent; they form a small triangle (shaded in sketch (a)).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V={(2,0),(9,0),(9,3),(6,6),(0,6),(0,2)};
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(V[0]--V[3],blue);draw(V[1]--V[4],blue);draw(V[2]--V[5],blue);
pair P1=extension(V[0],V[3],V[1],V[4]),P2=extension(V[0],V[3],V[2],V[5]),P3=extension(V[1],V[4],V[2],V[5]);
fill(P1--P2--P3--cycle,red);
for(int i=0;i<6;++i)label("$V_"+string(i+1)+"$",V[i],2*unit(V[i]-(4.5,3)),fontsize(8pt));
pair d=(16,3);
pair[] W={(-3,-1.5),(1,-2.5),(3.4,0.2),(3,1.5),(-1,2.5),(-3.4,-0.2)};
draw(shift(d)*(W[0]--W[1]--W[2]--W[3]--W[4]--W[5]--cycle),linewidth(0.9));
draw(shift(d)*(W[0]--W[3]),blue);draw(shift(d)*(W[1]--W[4]),blue);draw(shift(d)*(W[2]--W[5]),blue);dot(d);label("$M$",d,S,fontsize(8pt));
label("(a)",(4.5,-1.6));label("(b)",d+(0,-4.6));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} Going around a convex polygon, the directions of the sides turn steadily the same way, through $360^\circ$ in total, with each turn strictly between $0^\circ$ and $180^\circ$. From side $V_1V_2$ to the opposite side $V_4V_5$ the direction turns through three of these angles, and back through the other three. Both amounts are positive and add up to $360^\circ$, and the sides are parallel, so each amount is $180^\circ$: the sides $V_1V_2$ and $V_4V_5$, traversed in order, point in opposite directions. As they have equal lengths, $V_5-V_4=-\left(V_2-V_1\right)$. Hence
\[V_2-V_1=V_4-V_5\quad\Longrightarrow\quad V_1+V_4=V_2+V_5,\]
so $V_1V_4$ and $V_2V_5$ have the same midpoint (they are the diagonals of the parallelogram $V_1V_2V_4V_5$). In the same way $V_3-V_2=V_5-V_6$ gives $V_2+V_5=V_3+V_6$. So all three main diagonals have the same midpoint $M$, and they pass through it, as in sketch (b). $\blacksquare$

Either the two cards are different or the same card is chosen twice.
\begin{itemize}
\item Two different cards, order ignored: $\dbinom{52}{2}=\dfrac{52\cdot51}{2}=1326$ ways.
\item The same card twice: $52$ ways.
\end{itemize}
The total is $1326+52=\boxed{1378}$. (Equivalently, this is the number of multisets of size $2$ from $52$ types, $\dbinom{53}{2}=1378$.)

question:
A triangle has a $60^\circ$ angle and two sides of lengths $6$ and $7$, and the $60^\circ$ angle is not between these two sides.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find all such triangles: give the length of the third side and the other two angles (to the nearest tenth of a degree).
\item[(b)] When an angle is found from its sine using the law of sines, there are in general two candidates, $\theta$ and $180^\circ-\theta$. Explain why, and explain what happens to the candidates here.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $\angle A=60^\circ$. Since the $60^\circ$ angle is not between the sides $6$ and $7$, one of them is the side $BC$ opposite $A$ and the other is adjacent to $A$, say $AC$. Let $c=AB$.

\textbf{Case 1: $BC=7$, $AC=6$.} By the law of cosines,
\[7^2=6^2+c^2-2\cdot6\cdot c\cos60^\circ\iff c^2-6c-13=0\iff c=3\pm\sqrt{22}.\]
Only $c=3+\sqrt{22}\approx7.69$ is positive, so there is exactly one triangle. By the law of sines,
\[\sin B=\dfrac{6\sin60^\circ}{7}=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{7}\approx0.7423,\]
so $B\approx47.9^\circ$ or $B\approx132.1^\circ$. The second candidate is impossible, since $60^\circ+132.1^\circ>180^\circ$. Hence $B\approx47.9^\circ$ and $C\approx180^\circ-60^\circ-47.9^\circ=72.1^\circ$.

\textbf{Case 2: $BC=6$, $AC=7$.} Then $\sin B=\dfrac{7\sin60^\circ}{6}=\dfrac{7\sqrt3}{12}>1$ (because $49\cdot3=147>144$), which is impossible. Geometrically, the distance from $C$ to line $AB$ is $7\sin60^\circ=\dfrac{7\sqrt3}{2}\approx6.06$, so a segment of length $6$ from $C$ cannot reach that line. (The law of cosines gives $c^2-7c+13=0$, whose discriminant $49-52$ is negative.)

\textbf{(a)} There is exactly one triangle: its third side is $\boxed{3+\sqrt{22}\approx7.69}$, the angle opposite the side $6$ is $\boxed{\approx47.9^\circ}$ and the angle opposite the third side is $\boxed{\approx72.1^\circ}$.

\textbf{(b)} The points of the unit circle at angles $\theta$ and $180^\circ-\theta$ are mirror images in the $y$-axis, so they have the same $y$-coordinate: $\sin(180^\circ-\theta)=\sin\theta$. So an equation $\sin B=s$ with $0<s<1$ has two solutions between $0^\circ$ and $180^\circ$, one acute and one obtuse. Both give a triangle only if both leave room for the known angle. Here the obtuse candidate $132.1^\circ$ fails in Case 1, and Case 2 gives no triangle at all, so the data determine a unique triangle. (In general, for an acute given angle $A$ with the given adjacent side $AC=b$ and the given opposite side $a$, there are two triangles exactly when $b\sin A<a<b$.)

