question:
Consider an $8\times8$ chessboard.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many rectangles (of all sizes, with sides along the grid lines) can be formed?
\item[(b)] How many of them are squares?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Write $s=\sin\theta$ and $c=\cos\theta$. Rotating the point $(c,s)$ of the unit circle by $90^\circ$ gives $(-s,c)$, by $180^\circ$ gives $(-c,-s)$, by $270^\circ$ gives $(s,-c)$, and reflecting it in the $x$-axis gives the point at angle $-\theta$ (or $360^\circ-\theta$), namely $(c,-s)$. Hence
\begin{align*}
&\sin(180^\circ+\theta)=-s,\quad \cos(90^\circ+\theta)=-s,\quad \sin(360^\circ-\theta)=-s,\quad \cos(360^\circ+\theta)=c,\quad \csc(-\theta)=-\dfrac1s,\\
&\tan(270^\circ-\theta)=\dfrac{\sin(270^\circ-\theta)}{\cos(270^\circ-\theta)}=\dfrac{-c}{-s}=\dfrac cs,\qquad \cot(360^\circ-\theta)=\dfrac{c}{-s}=-\dfrac cs,\\
&\cot(270^\circ+\theta)=\dfrac{\cos(270^\circ+\theta)}{\sin(270^\circ+\theta)}=\dfrac{s}{-c}=-\dfrac sc,\qquad \sin(270^\circ+\theta)=-c.
\end{align*}
(For $270^\circ-\theta$: the point at angle $-\theta$ is $(c,-s)$, and rotating it by $270^\circ$ gives $(-s,-c)$.)

\textbf{(a)} The numerator is $(-s)(-s)\cdot\dfrac cs\cdot\left(-\dfrac cs\right)=-c^2$, and the denominator is
\[(-s)\cdot c\cdot\left(-\dfrac1s\right)\cdot\left(-\dfrac sc\right)=-s.\]
So the left-hand side equals $\boxed{\dfrac{\cos^2\theta}{\sin\theta}}$, which is not identically $1$: for $\theta=30^\circ$ it equals $\dfrac{3}{4}\div\dfrac12=\dfrac32$. The claim is false.

\textbf{(b)} With $\sin(270^\circ+\theta)=-c$ in place of $\cot(270^\circ+\theta)$, the denominator becomes $(-s)\cdot c\cdot\left(-\dfrac1s\right)\cdot(-c)=-c^2$, so the left-hand side is $\dfrac{-c^2}{-c^2}=1$ whenever it is defined. $\blacksquare$

