question:
Synthetic division divides a polynomial $p(x)=a_nx^n+a_{n-1}x^{n-1}+\cdots+a_1x+a_0$ ($n\ge1$) by $x-r$ as follows: bring down $b_{n-1}=a_n$, and then repeatedly multiply by $r$ and add the next coefficient:
\[b_{k-1}=a_k+r\,b_k\quad(k=n-1,\dots,1),\qquad R=a_0+r\,b_0.\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $p(x)=(x-r)\left(b_{n-1}x^{n-1}+\cdots+b_1x+b_0\right)+R$, so that the numbers $b_k$ are the coefficients of the quotient and $R$ is the remainder.
\item[(b)] Use synthetic division to divide $2x^3-3x^2+4x-5$ by $x-2$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $O$ be the centre of the square.

\emph{If.} The half-turn (rotation by $180^\circ$) about $O$ maps the square onto itself. It also maps a line $\ell$ through $O$ onto itself, and it swaps the two sides of $\ell$ (a point $P$ goes to the point $P'$ with $O$ the midpoint of $PP'$). So it maps the part of the square on one side of $\ell$ onto the part on the other side. These parts are congruent, so they have equal areas.

\emph{Only if.} Suppose $\ell$ does not pass through $O$, and let $\ell'$ be the line through $O$ parallel to $\ell$. By the first part, $\ell'$ bisects the area. The part of the square on the side of $\ell$ that contains $O$ consists of the half of the square beyond $\ell'$ together with the part of the square inside the strip between $\ell'$ and $\ell$ (shaded). This strip part has positive area: $O$ is an interior point of the square, so a small disc around $O$ lies inside the square, and the strip cuts off a region of positive area from this disc. Hence that side of $\ell$ contains more than half of the area, and $\ell$ does not bisect it. $\blacksquare$

