Using the angle-sum formulas,
\[\sin\left(x+\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right)=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}(\sin x+\cos x),\qquad \cos\left(x+\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right)=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}(\cos x-\sin x),\]
so $\tan\left(x+\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right)=\dfrac{\sin x+\cos x}{\cos x-\sin x}$, defined when $\cos x\ne\sin x$. The equation also needs $\sin x+\cos x\ne0$. Under these conditions it becomes
\[\dfrac{1}{\cos x-\sin x}=1\iff\cos x-\sin x=1.\]
On the unit circle $(X,Y)=(\cos x,\sin x)$ this is the line $X-Y=1$, which meets the circle at $(1,0)$ and $(0,-1)$ (substituting $X=1+Y$ into $X^2+Y^2=1$ gives $Y(Y+1)=0$). So $x=2k\pi$ or $x=\dfrac{3\pi}{2}+2k\pi$. For these, $\sin x+\cos x=\pm1\ne0$ and $\cos x-\sin x=1\ne0$, and indeed $\dfrac{\tan\dfrac{\pi}{4}}{1}=1$ and $\dfrac{\tan\left(-\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right)}{-1}=1$.

The solutions are
\[\boxed{x=2k\pi\quad\text{or}\quad x=\dfrac{3\pi}{2}+2k\pi,\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.}\]

