\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the monic quadratic polynomial whose roots are $3+i$ and $3-i$.
\item[(b)] In general, find the monic quadratic polynomial whose roots are $a$ and $b$, in terms of $a$ and $b$.
\end{enumerate}

A rectangular area next to a straight river is to be fenced in; no fence is needed along the river. The enclosed area must be $1000$ square feet. Fencing for the side parallel to the river costs \$5 per foot, fencing for the two sides perpendicular to the river costs \$8 per foot, and each of the four corner posts costs \$25. Let $x$ be the length, in feet, of each side perpendicular to the river.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Write a function $C(x)$ giving the cost of the project in dollars.
\item[(b)] What is the domain of $C$?
\item[(c)] Find the dimensions of the cheapest enclosure and its cost.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The side parallel to the river has length $\dfrac{1000}{x}$. So
\[C(x)=5\cdot\dfrac{1000}{x}+8\cdot2x+4\cdot25=\boxed{\dfrac{5000}{x}+16x+100}.\]
\textbf{(b)} Any positive length works: the domain is $\boxed{x>0}$.

\textbf{(c)} By the AM--GM inequality,
\[16x+\dfrac{5000}{x}\ge2\sqrt{16x\cdot\dfrac{5000}{x}}=2\sqrt{80000}=400\sqrt2,\]
with equality when $16x=\dfrac{5000}{x}$, i.e. $x^2=\dfrac{625}{2}$, $x=\dfrac{25\sqrt2}{2}\approx17.68$. The cheapest enclosure has sides $\boxed{\dfrac{25\sqrt2}{2}\approx17.68\text{ ft}}$ perpendicular to the river and $\dfrac{1000}{x}=\boxed{40\sqrt2\approx56.57\text{ ft}}$ along it, and costs $100+400\sqrt2\approx\$665.69$.

