The volume of a frustum with base radii $R$, $r$ and height $h$ is $\dfrac{\pi h}{3}\left(R^2+Rr+r^2\right)$, so each cup holds
\[\dfrac{7\pi}{3}\left(36+18+9\right)=147\pi\approx461.8\ \text{cm}^3.\]
The juice is $115.5$ litres $=115500$ cm$^3$. Now $250\cdot147\pi=36750\pi$, and $\dfrac{115500}{36750}=\dfrac{22}{7}>\pi$, so $250$ cups use less than $115500$ cm$^3$. On the other hand $251\cdot147\pi>36897\cdot3.14>115800>115500$. So $\boxed{250}$ cups can be filled completely. (With the approximation $\pi\approx\dfrac{22}{7}$, the $250$ cups use up exactly all the juice.)

