question:
Six mutually exclusive outcomes cover all possibilities. Outcomes (i)--(v) have the following probabilities:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $\dfrac1x$, where $x$ is the smaller root of $x^2-36x+323=0$;
\item[(ii)] $\dfrac1x$, where $x$ is the smallest positive integer divisible by both $2$ and $5$;
\item[(iii)] $\dfrac1x$, where $x$ is the number of ordered pairs of nonzero integers $(a,b)$ with $\dfrac1a+\dfrac1b=\dfrac1{60}$;
\item[(iv)] $\dfrac1x$, where $x$ is the fourth smallest positive integer with exactly $4$ positive divisors;
\item[(v)] $\dfrac{1}{(2+5)^2+4^2\cdot20+60\cdot10+x}$, where $x$ is the eleventh prime.
\end{enumerate}
Outcome (vi) is that none of (i)--(v) happens. What is the probability of (vi)?

solution:
Let $\omega$ be the circumcircle of $ABD$, with centre $O$ and radius $8$. Since $CD=4$ and $C$ lies on line $BD$ outside the segment $BD$, the power of $C$ with respect to $\omega$ is
\[CD\cdot CB=4\cdot9=36=CA^2.\]
The power also equals $CO^2-8^2$, so $CO^2=CA^2+OA^2$. By the converse of Pythagoras, $\angle CAO=90^\circ$: the line $CA$ is tangent to $\omega$ at $A$, and $CO=\sqrt{36+64}=10$.

Let $N$ be the midpoint of $BD$. Then $ON\perp BC$ and $CN=4+\dfrac52=\dfrac{13}{2}$, so
\[\cos\angle OCB=\dfrac{13}{20},\qquad\sin\angle OCB=\sqrt{1-\dfrac{169}{400}}=\dfrac{\sqrt{231}}{20}.\]
In the right triangle $CAO$, $\cos\angle OCA=\dfrac{6}{10}=\dfrac35$ and $\sin\angle OCA=\dfrac45$.

The points at distance $6$ from $C$ on $\omega$ are exactly the two points of tangency from $C$, which lie on either side of line $CO$. So $\angle ACB=\angle OCB+\angle OCA$ for one of them and $\left|\angle OCB-\angle OCA\right|$ for the other ($A'$ in the sketch). (The circle through $B$ and $D$ could also have its centre on the other side of $BC$; by reflection this gives the same two angles.) The larger angle is $\angle OCB+\angle OCA$, so
\[\cos\angle ACB=\dfrac{13}{20}\cdot\dfrac35-\dfrac{\sqrt{231}}{20}\cdot\dfrac45=\boxed{\dfrac{39-4\sqrt{231}}{100}}\approx-0.218,\]
i.e. $\angle ACB\approx102.6^\circ$.

