question:
John plays a one-player game with a fair $46$-sided die whose faces show $1,2,\dots,46$. He rolls repeatedly. If he rolls a $46$, he loses all his points and the game ends. Otherwise he gains the number of points shown (a roll of $45$ gives $45$ points) and may roll again. He may stop at any time and keep his points.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Suppose he must decide before the game how many times $n$ he will roll (stopping earlier only if a $46$ ends the game). Which $n$ maximises his expected score, and what is that expected score?
\item[(b)] Suppose instead that after each roll he may decide whether to continue. Find a strategy that maximises his expected score, and prove that it is optimal.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the circles have centres $O_1$ (small, radius $r$) and $O$ (large, radius $R>r$). Since the circles are tangent at $A$, the points $A$, $O_1$, $O$ are collinear with $AO=\dfrac Rr\,AO_1$. Let line $AD$ meet the large circle again at $M$.

Consider the homothety (enlargement) $h$ with centre $A$ and ratio $k=\dfrac Rr>1$. It maps $O_1$ to $O$ and multiplies all distances by $k$, so it maps the small circle onto the large circle. It maps $D$ to the point of ray $AD$ at distance $k\cdot AD$ from $A$, which lies on the large circle; this point is $M$. A homothety maps each line to a parallel line, so the tangent line to the small circle at $D$, namely line $BC$, is mapped to the tangent line to the large circle at $M$, which is therefore parallel to $BC$.

The radius $OM$ is perpendicular to that tangent, hence perpendicular to $BC$. So $M$ lies on the perpendicular from $O$ to the chord $BC$, which is the perpendicular bisector of $BC$; thus $MB=MC$, and $M$ is the midpoint of one of the two arcs $BC$.

The small circle touches line $BC$ only at $D$, so all its other points, including $A$, lie on one side of $BC$. Since $AM=k\cdot AD>AD$, the point $D$ lies between $A$ and $M$, so $M$ is on the other side of $BC$. Hence $M$ is the midpoint of the arc $BC$ not containing $A$. The inscribed angles $\angle BAM$ and $\angle MAC$ stand on the equal arcs $BM$ and $MC$, so they are equal. As $D$ lies on segment $AM$, the line $AD$ bisects $\angle BAC$. $\blacksquare$

