Let the third side be $x$. By the triangle inequality $0<x<20$, so $x\in\{1,2,\dots,19\}$ (for $x=10$ the triangle is equilateral). The altitude to the side $x$ bisects it, so its length is $h=\sqrt{100-\dfrac{x^2}{4}}$ and the area is
\[A(x)=\dfrac x2\sqrt{100-\dfrac{x^2}{4}}=\dfrac14\sqrt{x^2\left(400-x^2\right)}.\]
With $t=x^2$, the product $t(400-t)=20000-(t-200)^2$ increases for $t<200$ and decreases for $t>200$. Among the integers, $14^2=196$ and $15^2=225$ are the squares nearest $200$ on either side, and $196\cdot204=39984>225\cdot175=39375$; so the maximum is at $x=14$, with $A=\dfrac{\sqrt{39984}}4=\sqrt{2499}\approx49.990$. The minimum is at an end: $x=1$ gives $1\cdot399=399$ and $x=19$ gives $361\cdot39=14079$, so the minimum is $A(1)=\dfrac{\sqrt{399}}4\approx4.994$.

The difference is $\sqrt{2499}-\dfrac{\sqrt{399}}{4}\approx44.996$, which rounds to $\boxed{45}$.

