Put $M=(0,0)$, $A=(1,0)$, $T=(1,1)$, $H=(0,1)$; both circles have centre $Q=\left(\dfrac12,\dfrac12\right)$, with radii $\dfrac12$ and $\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}$.

\emph{The point $I$.} For $P=(x,y)$ on the incircle let $p=\left|x-\dfrac12\right|$, $q=\left|y-\dfrac12\right|$, so $p^2+q^2=\dfrac14$. The distances from $P$ to the four sides are $\dfrac12-p$, $\dfrac12+p$, $\dfrac12-q$ and $\dfrac12+q$, so the two nearest sides are at distances $\dfrac12-p$ and $\dfrac12-q$. Their sum is $1-(p+q)$, and $p+q\le\sqrt{2\left(p^2+q^2\right)}=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}$, with equality iff $p=q=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{4}$. Of the four such points, the one nearest $H$ is $I=\left(\dfrac12-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{4},\ \dfrac12+\dfrac{\sqrt2}{4}\right)$.

\emph{The point $Z$.} A point of the circumcircle outside side $HM$ (with $x\le0$) has distance $-x$ to that side, its nearest one; this is largest at the midpoint of the arc, $\left(\dfrac12-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2},\dfrac12\right)$, at distance $\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}-\dfrac12$. By symmetry the four arc midpoints are the maximisers. Only for the one beyond side $HM$ is $MATHZ$ not self-intersecting (otherwise the edges $HZ$ and $ZM$ would cross the square's boundary), so $Z=\left(\dfrac12-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2},\dfrac12\right)$.

\emph{Areas.} $I$ lies inside the square, so $MATHI$ is the square minus triangle $HIM$, whose base $HM=1$ has height equal to the $x$-coordinate of $I$:
\[[MATHI]=1-\dfrac12\left(\dfrac12-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{4}\right)=\dfrac34+\dfrac{\sqrt2}{8}.\]
$Z$ lies outside, so $MATHZ$ is the square plus triangle $HZM$:
\[[MATHZ]=1+\dfrac12\left(\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}-\dfrac12\right)=\dfrac34+\dfrac{\sqrt2}{4}.\]
The sum is $\dfrac32+\dfrac{3\sqrt2}{8}=\dfrac{12+3\sqrt2}{8}$, so $a+b+c+d=12+3+2+8=\boxed{25}$.

