By the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality,
\[(2x+y)^2\le\left(2^2+1^2\right)\left(x^2+y^2\right)=5,\]
so $2x+y\le\sqrt5$, with equality when $(x,y)$ is proportional to $(2,1)$, i.e. $(x,y)=\left(\dfrac2{\sqrt5},\dfrac1{\sqrt5}\right)$. The maximum is $\boxed{\sqrt5}$.

Geometrically, $(x,y)$ lies on the unit circle, and the lines $2x+y=k$ are parallel. The largest $k$ for which the line still meets the circle is the one for which the line is tangent, i.e. at distance $\dfrac{k}{\sqrt5}=1$ from the origin, again $k=\sqrt5$.

