question:
Use convenient groupings to compute each of the following without a calculator.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $1+2+3+\cdots+100$, by pairing the first and last terms, the second and second-to-last terms, and so on.
\item[(b)] $72\cdot87$, by splitting $72=70+2$.
\item[(c)] $4000-129$, by breaking $129$ into $100+29$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $x=\dfrac{BD}{DC}$, $y=\dfrac{CE}{EA}$, $z=\dfrac{AF}{FB}$. By Ceva's theorem $xyz=1$.

\emph{The ratios at $X$.} Put masses $1$ at $A$, $z$ at $B$ and $xz$ at $C$. Then $F$ is the balance point of $A$ and $B$ (since $\dfrac{AF}{FB}=\dfrac z1$), $D$ that of $B$ and $C$ (since $\dfrac{BD}{DC}=\dfrac{xz}{z}$), and $E$ that of $C$ and $A$ (since $\dfrac{CE}{EA}=\dfrac{1}{xz}=y$). So $X$ is the centre of mass, and
\[\dfrac{AX}{XD}=\dfrac{z+xz}{1}=z+\dfrac1y,\qquad \dfrac{BX}{XE}=\dfrac{1+xz}{z}=\dfrac1z+x,\qquad \dfrac{CX}{XF}=\dfrac{1+z}{xz}=y+\dfrac1x,\]
using $xz=\dfrac1y$. Hence, with $s=x+y+z$ and $q=xy+yz+zx$,
\[\dfrac{AX}{XD}+\dfrac{BX}{XE}+\dfrac{CX}{XF}=s+\dfrac1x+\dfrac1y+\dfrac1z=s+\dfrac{q}{xyz}=s+q.\]

\emph{Finding $q$.} Since $x,y,z\in\left[\dfrac15,5\right]$,
\[(5-x)(5-y)(5-z)\ge0\quad\text{and}\quad\left(x-\dfrac15\right)\left(y-\dfrac15\right)\left(z-\dfrac15\right)\ge0.\]
Expanding with $xyz=1$, these say $124-25s+5q\ge0$ and $124-25q+5s\ge0$ (after multiplying the second by $125$). Hence
\[25s\le124+5q\le124+\dfrac{124+5s}{5},\qquad\text{so}\qquad 120s\le744,\quad s\le\dfrac{31}{5}.\]
We are given $s=\dfrac{31}{5}$, so equality holds throughout; in particular $25s=124+5q$, which gives $q=\dfrac{155-124}{5}=\dfrac{31}{5}$. (Equality forces one ratio to be $5$, one to be $\dfrac15$ and the third to be $1$, which is realised by the sketch.)

Therefore the requested sum is $s+q=\dfrac{62}{5}$, and $m+n=\boxed{67}$.

