A circle centred at $O$ with radius $r$ is not crossed by the line exactly when $r$ is at most the distance $d$ from $O$ to the line (for $r=d$ the line is tangent). So the largest area is $\pi d^2$ (in the sketch, $d=OF$).

To find $d$, drop the perpendicular from $O$ to the line. The line has direction $(1,m)$, so the perpendicular has direction $(m,-1)$, and its foot is $F=t(m,-1)$ for the $t$ with $-t=m\cdot tm+b$, i.e. $t=-\dfrac{b}{m^2+1}$. Hence
\[d=|t|\sqrt{m^2+1}=\dfrac{|b|}{\sqrt{m^2+1}},\qquad\text{and the largest area is}\qquad\boxed{\dfrac{\pi b^2}{m^2+1}}.\]

