Use vectors with origin $B$: let $\vec a=\overrightarrow{BA}$ and $\vec c=\overrightarrow{BC}$, and let $\rho$ be the rotation by $90^\circ$ about $B$ in the direction that takes $\vec a$ to $\overrightarrow{BD}$ (the square $ABDE$ lies on the side of $AB$ away from $C$). The square $BCFG$ lies on the other side of $BC$, so $\overrightarrow{BG}$ is $\vec c$ rotated by $90^\circ$ the opposite way: $\overrightarrow{BG}=-\rho(\vec c)$. (Concretely, with $B=(0,0)$, $C$ on the positive $x$-axis and $A$ above it, $\rho$ is the counterclockwise rotation and $G$ lies below $B$.) Rotations are linear, so
\[\overrightarrow{GD}=\overrightarrow{BD}-\overrightarrow{BG}=\rho(\vec a)+\rho(\vec c)=\rho\left(\vec a+\vec c\right).\]
Let $N$ be the midpoint of $AC$. Then $\vec a+\vec c=2\overrightarrow{BN}\ne\vec 0$ (as $A,B,C$ are not collinear), so $GD$ is the median $BN$ rotated by $90^\circ$ and doubled; in particular $DG\perp BN$.

Hence $DG\parallel AC$ exactly when $BN\perp AC$. Using dot products,
\[\left(\vec a+\vec c\right)\cdot\left(\vec c-\vec a\right)=|\vec c|^2-|\vec a|^2,\]
so $BN\perp AC$ exactly when $BC=BA$. Therefore, if $DG\parallel AC$, then $BA=BC$ and triangle $ABC$ is isosceles (and conversely). $\blacksquare$

