question:
Call a positive integer \emph{orz} if all of the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item it is a perfect cube;
\item its last digit equals the number of its other digits that are perfect squares (that is, equal to $0$, $1$, $4$ or $9$);
\item it is not a palindrome;
\item it is at most $9000$.
\end{enumerate}
Find the unique four-digit orz number.

solution:
Let $A_1A_2\cdots A_n$ be a regular polygon, with all sides equal and all interior angles equal to $\alpha<180^\circ$. Let the bisectors of the angles at $A_1$ and $A_2$ meet at $O$; they meet inside the polygon, because together they make angles $\dfrac\alpha2+\dfrac\alpha2=\alpha<180^\circ$ with the side $A_1A_2$.

Triangle $OA_1A_2$ has equal base angles $\dfrac\alpha2$, so $OA_1=OA_2$. Next compare triangles $OA_2A_1$ and $OA_2A_3$: they share $OA_2$, have $A_2A_1=A_2A_3$, and $\angle OA_2A_1=\angle OA_2A_3=\dfrac\alpha2$ because $OA_2$ bisects the angle at $A_2$. By SAS they are congruent, so $OA_3=OA_1$ and $\angle OA_3A_2=\angle OA_1A_2=\dfrac\alpha2$; that is, $OA_3$ bisects the angle at $A_3$. Repeating the argument around the polygon gives
\[OA_1=OA_2=\cdots=OA_n.\]
A point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on its perpendicular bisector. Since $O$ is equidistant from the endpoints of every side, all the perpendicular bisectors pass through $O$. $\blacksquare$ ($O$ is the centre of the circumscribed circle.)

