Let $x_k$ be the measure of the arc $A_kA_{k+1}$ that contains no other vertex (indices modulo $2m$), so $x_1+x_2+\cdots+x_{2m}=360^\circ$.

The angle at $A_k$ is the inscribed angle $\angle A_{k-1}A_kA_{k+1}$. It stands on the arc from $A_{k+1}$ to $A_{k-1}$ not containing $A_k$, which is the whole circle except the arcs $x_{k-1}$ and $x_k$. By the inscribed angle theorem,
\[\angle A_k=\dfrac{360^\circ-x_{k-1}-x_k}{2}.\]
Add these for $k=1,3,\dots,2m-1$. The pairs $(x_{2m},x_1),(x_2,x_3),\dots,(x_{2m-2},x_{2m-1})$ use every arc exactly once, so
\[\angle A_1+\angle A_3+\cdots+\angle A_{2m-1}=\dfrac{m\cdot360^\circ-360^\circ}{2}=(m-1)\cdot180^\circ.\ \blacksquare\]
The same holds for the even-numbered angles. With $n=2m$ vertices this is $90n-180$ degrees, as conjectured.

