question:
Tim has square tiles of various sizes. Each tile has an etched quarter circle, centred at a corner of the tile, whose radius equals the tile's side length. Tim arranges eight tiles as shown, so that the etchings join into one spiral. The two smallest tiles have side length $10$ cm, and each of the other tiles has side length equal to the sum of the side lengths of the two tiles placed just before it. What is the length of the spiral? Express your answer in terms of $\pi$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int[] sd={1,1,2,3,5,8,13,21};
pair[] lo={(0,0),(1,0),(0,1),(-3,0),(-3,-5),(2,-5),(-3,3),(-24,-5)};
pair[] cen={(1,1),(1,1),(0,1),(0,0),(2,0),(2,3),(-3,3),(-3,-5)};
real[] a0={180,270,0,90,180,270,0,90};
for(int k=0;k<8;++k){draw(shift(lo[k])*scale(sd[k])*unitsquare,gray(0.4));
 draw(arc(cen[k],sd[k],a0[k],a0[k]+90),linewidth(1));}
\end{asy}

solution:
The side lengths are $10,10,20,30,50,80,130,210$ cm, each the sum of the two before (Fibonacci numbers times $10$). A quarter circle of radius $s$ has length $\dfrac{2\pi s}{4}=\dfrac{\pi s}{2}$, so the spiral has length
\[\dfrac\pi2\left(10+10+20+30+50+80+130+210\right)=\dfrac\pi2\cdot540=\boxed{270\pi}\text{ cm}.\]

