question:
Jane and Jenny plan to meet at a restaurant between $1$ pm and $2$ pm. Each of them arrives at a uniformly random time in that hour, independently of the other, waits $20$ minutes, and then leaves. Lily arrives at $1{:}30$ pm and stays until $2$ pm.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the probability that Jane and Jenny meet?
\item[(b)] What is the probability that Jane and Jenny meet each other, but neither of them meets Lily?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let Jane and Jenny arrive $x$ and $y$ minutes after $1$ pm; the point $(x,y)$ is uniform in the square $[0,60]\times[0,60]$ of area $3600$.

\textbf{(a)} They meet exactly when $|x-y|\le20$. The complement consists of two right triangles with legs $40$, of total area $40^2=1600$. So the probability is $\dfrac{3600-1600}{3600}=\boxed{\dfrac59}$.

\textbf{(b)} Jane is present during $[x,x+20]$ and Lily during $[30,60]$. These overlap exactly when $x+20\ge30$, so Jane avoids Lily exactly when $x<10$; similarly Jenny avoids Lily exactly when $y<10$. If $x,y<10$ then $|x-y|<10\le20$, so Jane and Jenny certainly meet. Hence the required region is the square $[0,10)\times[0,10)$, and the probability is
\[\dfrac{10^2}{60^2}=\boxed{\dfrac1{36}}.\]

