question:
Let $n$ be a positive integer. For a positive integer $N$, split its decimal digits into blocks of $n$ digits starting from the right (the leftmost block may be shorter), and let $B(N)$ be the sum of the numbers formed by the blocks. For example, with $n=2$, $B(12345)=1+23+45$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $N\equiv B(N)\pmod{10^n-1}$. In particular, $10^n-1$ divides $N$ if and only if it divides $B(N)$.
\item[(b)] Use (a) to find the remainder when $123456789$ is divided by $999$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
We need $x\ne0$. Since $\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{27}}=3^{-\dfrac32}=\left(\dfrac13\right)^{\dfrac32}$ and $t\mapsto\left(\dfrac13\right)^t$ is decreasing, the inequality is equivalent to
\[x+\dfrac12-\dfrac2x<\dfrac32\iff x-1-\dfrac2x<0\iff\dfrac{x^2-x-2}{x}<0\iff\dfrac{(x-2)(x+1)}{x}<0.\]
The sign changes at $-1$, $0$ and $2$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $x<-1$: the signs of $x-2$, $x+1$, $x$ are $-,-,-$, so the quotient is negative;
\item $-1<x<0$: $-,+,-$, positive;
\item $0<x<2$: $-,+,+$, negative;
\item $x>2$: $+,+,+$, positive.
\end{itemize}
At $x=-1$ and $x=2$ the two sides are equal. The solution set is
\[\boxed{x<-1\ \text{ or }\ 0<x<2}.\]

question:
Two spheres of radius $1$ are placed so that each passes through the centre of the other.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the volume of the region that lies inside both spheres.
\item[(b)] Find the total surface area of this region.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The centres $O_1$, $O_2$ are at distance $1$. By symmetry the two spheres meet in a circle lying in the plane perpendicular to $O_1O_2$ through its midpoint, at distance $\dfrac12$ from each centre. The common region is made of two spherical caps, one from each sphere, each of height $h=1-\dfrac12=\dfrac12$ (the cross-section through the centres is shown).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O1=(0,0),O2=(1,0);
path c1=circle(O1,1),c2=circle(O2,1);
pair P=(0.5,sqrt(3)/2),Q=(0.5,-sqrt(3)/2);
fill(arc(O2,1,120,240)--arc(O1,1,-60,60)--cycle,mediumgray+opacity(0.5));
draw(c1);draw(c2);
draw(P--Q,dashed);
dot(O1);dot(O2);label("$O_1$",O1,W);label("$O_2$",O2,E);
draw(O1--O2);
label("$\dfrac12$",(0.25,0),S,fontsize(8pt));label("$\dfrac12$",(0.75,0),S,fontsize(8pt));
label("$1$",(O1+P)/2,SE,fontsize(8pt));draw(O1--P,dotted);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} A cap of height $h$ of a sphere of radius $r$ has volume $\dfrac{\pi h^2(3r-h)}{3}$. With $r=1$, $h=\dfrac12$:
\[\dfrac{\pi\cdot\dfrac14\cdot\dfrac52}{3}=\dfrac{5\pi}{24}.\]
(Equivalently, slicing perpendicular to $O_1O_2$, the cap is $\displaystyle\int_{\dfrac12}^{1}\pi\left(1-t^2\right)dt=\pi\left(\dfrac12-\dfrac{7}{24}\right)=\dfrac{5\pi}{24}$.) The common volume is $2\cdot\dfrac{5\pi}{24}=\boxed{\dfrac{5\pi}{12}}$.

\textbf{(b)} The curved surface of a cap of height $h$ on a sphere of radius $r$ has area $2\pi rh$ (Archimedes). Each cap contributes $2\pi\cdot1\cdot\dfrac12=\pi$, so the surface area is $\boxed{2\pi}$.

