Bob wants to send a $1000$ kg payload into orbit. Carrying mass to orbit uses fuel at the rate of $4$ kg of fuel for every $5$ kg of mass carried. But the fuel has mass too.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Suppose all the fuel had to be carried the whole way (as if it were burned only on arrival). How much fuel would be needed?
\item[(b)] In reality fuel is used up during the flight. Model this by dividing the flight into $k$ equal stages: carrying a mass $m$ through one stage uses $\dfrac{4m}{5k}$ kg of fuel, which is burned at the start of that stage, so only the mass $m$ continues. Find the fuel needed in terms of $k$, and its limit as $k\to\infty$ (to the nearest kilogram).
\end{enumerate}

For an integer $a\ge2$, say that $a$ is its own inverse modulo $m$ if $a^2\equiv1\pmod m$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many integers $m>101$ are there for which $101$ is its own inverse modulo $m$?
\item[(b)] How many integers $m>102$ are there for which $102$ is its own inverse modulo $m$?
\item[(c)] How many integers $m>201$ are there for which $201$ is its own inverse modulo $m$?
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why a positive integer $N$ has exactly $\lfloor\log_{10}N\rfloor+1$ digits.
\item[(b)] Given that $\log_{10}3=0.47712$ to five decimal places, find the number of digits of $3^{87}$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $h$ be the distance between the lines $AB$ and $DC$, and let $\angle DAB=2\alpha$, $\angle ABC=2\beta$ (so $0<\alpha,\beta<90^\circ$). As $AB\parallel DC$, $\angle CDA=180^\circ-2\alpha$ and $\angle BCD=180^\circ-2\beta$.

\emph{$W$ and $X$ lie on the midline.} A point on the bisector of $\angle DAB$ is equidistant from lines $AB$ and $AD$; on the bisector of $\angle CDA$, from $DA$ and $DC$. So $W$ is equidistant from the parallel lines $AB$ and $DC$, i.e. it lies on the midline, at distance $\dfrac h2$ from both. The same holds for $X$. Hence $WABX$ and $WDCX$ are trapezoids of height $\dfrac h2$, and
\[(AB+WX)\dfrac h4=24,\qquad(DC+WX)\dfrac h4=36.\]

\emph{Lengths in terms of the angles.} Going from $A$ down the bisector to the midline moves $\dfrac h2\cot\alpha$ along the direction of $AB$, and similarly from $B$; so with $p=\cot\alpha+\cot\beta$,
\[WX=AB-\dfrac{hp}{2}.\]
In triangle $ABZ$ the angles at $A$ and $B$ are $\alpha$ and $\beta$; if $Z$ is at distance $t$ from $AB$, then $AB=tp$ and $[ABZ]=\dfrac{AB\cdot t}{2}=\dfrac{AB^2}{2p}=25$, so $p=\dfrac{AB^2}{50}$.

\emph{Solving.} Since $AB+WX=2AB-\dfrac{hp}2$, the first area gives $\left(2AB-\dfrac{hp}2\right)h=96$. With $p=\dfrac{AB^2}{50}$ and $s=AB\cdot h$ this becomes
\[2s-\dfrac{s^2}{100}=96\iff s^2-200s+9600=0\iff s=80\ \text{or}\ s=120.\]
Then $WX\cdot h=s-\dfrac{s^2}{100}$ is $16$ or $-24$. As $WABX$ is a genuine quadrilateral, $W$ and $X$ are in the same order as $A$ and $B$, so $WX>0$. Hence $AB\cdot h=80$, $WX\cdot h=16$, and from the second area, $DC\cdot h=144-16=128$.

\emph{Triangle $CYD$.} Its angles at $D$ and $C$ are $90^\circ-\alpha$ and $90^\circ-\beta$. With $q=\tan\alpha+\tan\beta$, the same reasoning gives $[CYD]=\dfrac{DC^2}{2q}$ and $WX=DC-\dfrac{hq}2$ (from $D$ the bisector moves $\dfrac h2\tan\alpha$ along $DC$). So $qh=2(DC-WX)$, and
\[[CYD]=\dfrac{DC^2h}{4(DC-WX)}=\dfrac{(DC\cdot h)^2}{4(DC\cdot h-WX\cdot h)}=\dfrac{128^2}{4\cdot112}=\dfrac{256}{7}.\]
Thus $m+n=256+7=\boxed{263}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=sqrt(83);
real q=224/h^2;real dd=sqrt(q^2-7);
real ta=(q+dd)/2,tb=(q-dd)/2;
real al=atan(ta),be=atan(tb);
real ab=80/h,dc=128/h;
pair A=(0,h),B=(ab,h);
pair D=(h/tan(2*al),0),C=(ab-h/tan(2*be),0);
pair Wp=(h/2/ta,h/2),Xp=(ab-h/2/tb,h/2);
real z=ab/(1/ta+1/tb);pair Zp=A+z*(1/ta,-1);
real y=dc/(ta+tb);pair Yp=D+y*(ta,1);
fill(A--B--Zp--cycle,lightgray+opacity(0.5));
fill(C--D--Yp--cycle,lightgray+opacity(0.5));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw((D.x-0.5,h/2)--(C.x+0.5,h/2),dashed);
draw(A--Zp--B,gray);draw(D--Yp--C,gray);
draw(A--Wp--D);draw(B--Xp--C);
dot(Wp);dot(Xp);dot(Yp);dot(Zp);
label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,NE);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,SW);
label("$W$",Wp,1.4*W,Fill(white));label("$X$",Xp,1.4*E,Fill(white));label("$Y$",Yp,N);label("$Z$",Zp,S);
\end{asy}

The sketch is drawn to scale with $h^2=83$ (then $AB=\dfrac{80}{h}$, $DC=\dfrac{128}{h}$ and $\tan\alpha\tan\beta=\dfrac74$); the dashed line is the midline, which contains $W$ and $X$. Such trapezoids exist, so the data are consistent.

We have $QA=QP$ and $QR=QB$, and $\angle AQP=\angle RQB=60^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Q=(0,0),P=(-3,-1.2),R=(2.6,-1.6);
pair outer(pair X,pair Y,pair O){pair c1=rotate(60,X)*Y,c2=rotate(-60,X)*Y;
 return (cross(Y-X,c1-X)*cross(Y-X,O-X)<0)?c1:c2;}
pair A=outer(Q,P,R),B=outer(Q,R,P);
draw(P--Q--R--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--P--Q--cycle);draw(B--Q--R--cycle);
draw(A--R,dashed+blue);draw(P--B,dashed+red);
label("$P$",P,SW);label("$Q$",Q,S);label("$R$",R,SE);label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,NE);
\end{asy}

Consider the rotation about $Q$ through $60^\circ$ that takes $A$ to $P$ (possible because $QA=QP$ and $\angle AQP=60^\circ$). Since both equilateral triangles are drawn outside $PQR$, the turn from ray $QA$ to ray $QP$ and the turn from ray $QR$ to ray $QB$ are in the same direction: going around $Q$ we meet the rays in the order $QA$, $QP$, $QR$, $QB$, with $60^\circ$ from $QA$ to $QP$ and $60^\circ$ from $QR$ to $QB$. So the same rotation takes $R$ to $B$ (as $QR=QB$).

A rotation preserves distances, and it takes the segment $AR$ to the segment $PB$. Hence $AR=PB$. $\blacksquare$

(Equivalently, triangles $AQR$ and $PQB$ are congruent by SAS: $QA=QP$, $QR=QB$, and the angles $AQR$ and $PQB$ are both obtained by adding $60^\circ$ to $\angle PQR$, or both equal $300^\circ-\angle PQR$ if that sum exceeds $180^\circ$.) The hypothesis that the triangle is obtuse with longest side $PR$ is not needed.

Let $AH$ be the altitude from $A$ to line $BC$. Triangles $ABM$ and $AMC$ both have height $AH$, and their bases $BM$ and $MC$ are equal, so they have equal areas.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(6,0),A=(2,3.5),M=(3,0),H=(2,0);
fill(A--M--C--cycle,mediumgray+opacity(0.4));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(A--M);draw(A--H,dashed);
draw((2,0)--(2,0.25)--(2.25,0.25)--(2.25,0));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$M$",M,S);label("$H$",H,S);
