The figure therefore has area $4\cdot10=\boxed{40\text{ cm}^2}$.

question:
Points $A$, $B$, $I$, $E$, $F$ lie on a line in this order, with $AB=5\text{ cm}$, $BI=12\text{ cm}$, $IE=5\text{ cm}$ and $EF=12\text{ cm}$. Squares $ABCD$ and $EFGH$ are drawn on the same side of the line, and $CIHJ$ is also a square. Squares $LDJM$ and $JGON$ are drawn on segments $DJ$ and $JG$, on the sides away from square $CIHJ$, as shown. What is the area of triangle $MNJ$?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-5,0),B=(0,0),C=(0,5),D=(-5,5),I=(12,0),Ep=(17,0),F=(29,0),G=(29,12),H=(17,12),J=(5,17);
pair L=(-17,15),M=(-7,27),Np=(10,41),O=(34,36);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(Ep--F--G--H--cycle);draw(C--I--H--J--cycle);
draw(L--D--J--M--cycle);draw(J--G--O--Np--cycle);
draw((-8,0)--(32,0));
label("$A$",A,S);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,W);label("$I$",I,S);label("$E$",Ep,S);label("$F$",F,S);
label("$G$",G,E);label("$H$",H,SE);label("$J$",J,E);label("$L$",L,W);label("$M$",M,NW);label("$N$",Np,N);label("$O$",O,E);
\end{asy}

solution:
Put $B$ at the origin with the line as the $x$-axis and the squares above it. Then $A=(-5,0)$, $C=(0,5)$, $D=(-5,5)$, $I=(12,0)$, $E=(17,0)$, $F=(29,0)$, $G=(29,12)$ and $H=(17,12)$.

Now $\overrightarrow{CI}=(12,-5)$ and $\overrightarrow{IH}=(5,12)$ are perpendicular and both have length $13$ (right triangles $CBI$ and $IEH$ are $5$-$12$-$13$ triangles), so $CIHJ$ is indeed a square, and
\[J=C+\overrightarrow{IH}=(5,17).\]
Then $\overrightarrow{JD}=(-10,-12)$ and $\overrightarrow{JG}=(24,-5)$. The square on $DJ$ lies on the side away from $CIHJ$ (up and to the left), so $\overrightarrow{JM}=(-12,10)$, which is $\overrightarrow{JD}$ turned through $90^\circ$; similarly $\overrightarrow{JN}=(5,24)$. Hence
\[[MNJ]=\dfrac12\left|(-12)(24)-(10)(5)\right|=\dfrac12\cdot338=\boxed{169\text{ cm}^2}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-5,0),B=(0,0),C=(0,5),D=(-5,5),I=(12,0),Ep=(17,0),F=(29,0),G=(29,12),H=(17,12),J=(5,17);
pair L=(-17,15),M=(-7,27),Np=(10,41),O=(34,36);
filldraw(M--J--Np--cycle,mediumgray+opacity(0.5));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);draw(Ep--F--G--H--cycle);draw(C--I--H--J--cycle);
draw(L--D--J--M--cycle);draw(J--G--O--Np--cycle);
draw(D--G,dashed);
draw((-8,0)--(32,0));
label("$A$",A,S);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,SE,Fill(white));label("$D$",D,W);label("$I$",I,S);label("$E$",Ep,S);label("$F$",F,S);
label("$G$",G,E);label("$H$",H,SE,Fill(white));label("$J$",J,E);label("$L$",L,W);label("$M$",M,NW);label("$N$",Np,N);label("$O$",O,E);
label("$5$",(-2.5,0),S,fontsize(8pt));label("$12$",(6,0),S,fontsize(8pt));label("$5$",(14.5,0),S,fontsize(8pt));label("$12$",(23,0),S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Remark: $[MNJ]=[DJG]$ (the dashed segment is $DG$). Indeed, rotating triangle $MJN$ about $J$ through $90^\circ$ takes $M$ to $D$ and $N$ to the reflection $G'$ of $G$ in $J$; triangles $DJG'$ and $DJG$ have equal bases $JG'=JG$ on one line and the same height from $D$.

We use Burnside's lemma: the number of essentially different colourings is the average, over the symmetries, of the number of colourings each symmetry leaves unchanged. A symmetry fixes a colouring exactly when the colouring is constant on each cycle of the permutation of vertices, so it fixes $6^c$ colourings, where $c$ is its number of cycles.

\emph{Rotations} ($4$ of them):
\begin{itemize}
\item identity: $4$ cycles, $6^4=1296$;
\item $90^\circ$ and $270^\circ$: one $4$-cycle each, $6$ each;
\item $180^\circ$: two $2$-cycles, $6^2=36$.
\end{itemize}
Number of colourings: $\dfrac{1296+6+6+36}{4}=\dfrac{1344}{4}=\boxed{336}$.

\emph{With reflections} ($8$ symmetries), add the four reflections:
\begin{itemize}
\item the $2$ reflections in the diagonals fix two vertices and swap the other two: $3$ cycles, $6^3=216$ each;
\item the $2$ reflections in lines through midpoints of opposite sides swap two pairs: $2$ cycles, $36$ each.
\end{itemize}
Number of colourings: $\dfrac{1344+2\cdot216+2\cdot36}{8}=\dfrac{1848}{8}=\boxed{231}$.

We use Burnside's lemma with the $24$ rotations of the cube. A rotation fixes $3^c$ colourings, where $c$ is the number of cycles in which it permutes the $8$ vertices. The rotation axes are of three kinds (one of each is sketched).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair o=(0.55,0.4);
void cb(pair s){
 pair a=s,b=s+(1,0),c=s+(1,1),d=s+(0,1);
 draw(a--b--c--d--cycle);draw(d--d+o--c+o--c);draw(b--b+o--c+o);
 draw(a--a+o,dashed);draw(a+o--b+o,dashed);draw(a+o--d+o,dashed);}
cb((0,0));cb((2.4,0));cb((4.8,0));
pair f1=(0.5,0)+o/2,f2=(0.5,1)+o/2;
draw((f1+(0,-0.45))--(f2+(0,0.45)),red+linewidth(0.8));
pair v1=(2.4,0),v2=(3.4,1)+o;
draw((v1-0.25*(v2-v1))--(v2+0.25*(v2-v1)),red+linewidth(0.8));
pair e1=(4.8,0)+o/2,e2=(5.8,1)+o/2;
draw((e1-0.25*(e2-e1))--(e2+0.25*(e2-e1)),red+linewidth(0.8));
dot(e1,red);dot(e2,red);
label("face axis",(0.75,-0.65),fontsize(8pt));label("vertex axis",(3.15,-0.65),fontsize(8pt));label("edge axis",(5.55,-0.65),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\begin{itemize}
\item The identity: $8$ cycles, $3^8=6561$.
\item Through centres of opposite faces ($3$ axes): rotations by $90^\circ$ or $270^\circ$ ($6$ rotations) give two $4$-cycles, $3^2=9$ each; rotations by $180^\circ$ ($3$ rotations) give four $2$-cycles, $3^4=81$ each.
\item Through opposite vertices ($4$ axes, rotations by $120^\circ$ or $240^\circ$, $8$ rotations): the two vertices on the axis are fixed and the other six form two $3$-cycles, so $4$ cycles, $81$ each.
\item Through midpoints of opposite edges ($6$ axes, $180^\circ$): four $2$-cycles, $81$ each.
\end{itemize}
These are $1+6+3+8+6=24$ rotations. The number of colourings is
\[\dfrac{6561+6\cdot9+3\cdot81+8\cdot81+6\cdot81}{24}=\dfrac{7992}{24}=\boxed{333}.\]

question:
A bank pays $5\%$ annual interest, compounded quarterly (every $3$ months), on a deposit of $\$1000$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain what "compounded quarterly" means, and find the balance after one year, to the nearest cent.
\item[(b)] What annual interest rate, compounded once a year, would give the same balance after one year?
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $x+\dfrac1x\ge2$ for every positive real number $x$, with equality only when $x=1$.
\item[(b)] Prove that $x+\dfrac1x\le-2$ for every negative real number $x$, so that the inequality in (a) fails for every negative $x$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $x$ be the side of the square. Since $RS$ lies on $BC$ and $PQ\parallel RS$, we have $PQ\parallel BC$, and $PQ$ is at distance $x$ from $BC$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(6,0),A=(2.2,4);
real s=6*4/10;
pair P=B+(A-B)*(s/4),Q=C+(A-C)*(s/4),R=(Q.x,0),S0=(P.x,0),Hf=(A.x,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
filldraw(P--Q--R--S0--cycle,lightgray);
draw(A--Hf,dashed);
draw((Hf+(0.18,0))--(Hf+(0.18,0.18))--(Hf+(0,0.18)));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$P$",P,W);label("$Q$",Q,E);label("$R$",R,S);label("$S$",S0,S);
label("$h_a$",(A.x,3.3),E);label("$x$",(Q+R)/2,E);
\end{asy}

So triangle $APQ$ is similar to triangle $ABC$ (corresponding angles are equal), and its altitude from $A$ is $h_a-x$. Corresponding lengths are in the same ratio:
\[\dfrac{PQ}{BC}=\dfrac{h_a-x}{h_a}\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad\dfrac xa=\dfrac{h_a-x}{h_a}.\]
Hence $xh_a=ah_a-ax$, and
\[\boxed{x=\dfrac{ah_a}{a+h_a}}.\]
(Conversely, the segment $PQ$ parallel to $BC$ at this height has length $x$, and since the angles at $B$ and $C$ are acute, the feet $S$ and $R$ of the perpendiculars from $P$ and $Q$ lie on side $BC$, so the square exists.)

question:
Define words in the letters $0$ and $1$ by $W_1=0$ and $W_{k+1}=W_k\overline{W_k}$, where $\overline{W}$ is $W$ with every $0$ replaced by $1$ and every $1$ by $0$. So $W_2=01$, $W_3=0110$, $W_4=01101001$, and so on. Each $W_k$ is the beginning of $W_{k+1}$, so together they define an infinite sequence $t_0t_1t_2\ldots=0110100110010110\ldots$ (the Thue--Morse sequence).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $t_{2n}=t_n$ and $t_{2n+1}=1-t_n$ for all $n\ge0$. In particular, the terms $t_0,t_2,t_4,\dots$ reproduce the whole sequence.
\item[(b)] Prove that the sequence contains neither $000$ nor $111$ as three consecutive terms.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Take a right triangle $ACB$ with the right angle at $C$, $\angle ABC=30^\circ$, $AC=1$, $CB=\sqrt3$ and $AB=2$. Extend $CB$ beyond $B$ to $D$ with $BD=BA=2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r3=sqrt(3);
pair C=(0,0),A=(0,1),B=(r3,0),D=(2+r3,0);
draw(C--D--A--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(A--B);
draw((0.12,0)--(0.12,0.12)--(0,0.12));
label("$C$",C,SW);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,S);label("$D$",D,SE);
label("$1$",(0,0.5),W);label("$\sqrt3$",(r3/2,0),S);label("$2$",(r3+1,0),S);label("$2$",(A+B)/2,NE);
draw(arc(B,0.35,150,180));label("$30^\circ$",B+0.4*dir(165),W,fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc(D,0.9,180-15,180));label("$15^\circ$",D+0.95*dir(172),W,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Triangle $ABD$ is isosceles with $\angle ABD=180^\circ-30^\circ=150^\circ$, so $\angle BDA=\angle BAD=15^\circ$. In the right triangle $ACD$, $CD=2+\sqrt3$ and $AC=1$, so
\[AD^2=1+\left(2+\sqrt3\right)^2=8+4\sqrt3=\left(\sqrt6+\sqrt2\right)^2,\qquad AD=\sqrt6+\sqrt2.\]
Therefore
\[\sin15^\circ=\dfrac{AC}{AD}=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt6+\sqrt2}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt6-\sqrt2}{4}},\qquad\cos15^\circ=\dfrac{CD}{AD}=\dfrac{2+\sqrt3}{\sqrt6+\sqrt2}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt6+\sqrt2}{4}}.\]
(For the last step, $(2+\sqrt3)\left(\sqrt6-\sqrt2\right)=2\sqrt6-2\sqrt2+3\sqrt2-\sqrt6=\sqrt6+\sqrt2$.) The same values follow from $\sin(45^\circ-30^\circ)=\sin45^\circ\cos30^\circ-\cos45^\circ\sin30^\circ$. In general, exact values come from special triangles and such formulas; calculators use other methods (such as series) to approximate the rest.

Let $I$ be the incentre and $a,b,c$ the side lengths. The radii from $I$ to the points of tangency are perpendicular to the sides and have length $x$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(5,0),C=(1.5,3);
real a=abs(B-C),b=abs(C-A),c=abs(A-B);
pair I=(a*A+b*B+c*C)/(a+b+c);
pair ft(pair P,pair U,pair V){return U+dot(P-U,unit(V-U))*unit(V-U);}
real r=I.y;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(circle(I,r));
draw(I--A,gray);draw(I--B,gray);draw(I--C,gray);
draw(I--ft(I,A,B),dashed);draw(I--ft(I,B,C),dashed);draw(I--ft(I,C,A),dashed);
dot(I);label("$I$",I,1.3*E,fontsize(9pt));
label("$x$",(I+ft(I,A,B))/2,W,fontsize(8pt));
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);
\end{asy}

Joining $I$ to the vertices splits the triangle into three triangles with bases $a,b,c$ and common height $x$, so
\[\text{Area}=\dfrac{ax}{2}+\dfrac{bx}{2}+\dfrac{cx}{2}=\dfrac{(a+b+c)x}{2}.\]
Thus $3x=\dfrac{(a+b+c)x}{2}$, and since $x>0$ the perimeter is $a+b+c=\boxed{6}$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] (Vieta's formulas.) Let $P(x)=x^n+c_{n-1}x^{n-1}+\cdots+c_1x+c_0$ have roots $r_1,\dots,r_n$ (listed with multiplicity, complex allowed), and let $e_k$ be the sum of the products of the roots taken $k$ at a time. Prove that $e_k=(-1)^kc_{n-k}$ for $k=1,\dots,n$.
\item[(b)] (Newton's sums.) Let $u,v,w$ be the roots of $x^3+ax^2+bx+c$ and $s_k=u^k+v^k+w^k$. Prove that $s_1+a=0$, $s_2+as_1+2b=0$, and $s_k+as_{k-1}+bs_{k-2}+cs_{k-3}=0$ for $k\ge3$ (with $s_0=3$).
\item[(c)] If $u,v,w$ are the roots of $x^3-x-1$, find $u^5+v^5+w^5$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let $m=m_A+m_B\ne0$. Then
\[\vec P-\vec A=\dfrac{m_A\vec A+m_B\vec B-m\vec A}{m}=\dfrac{m_B}{m}\left(\vec B-\vec A\right),\]
so $P$ lies on line $AB$. Also
\[m_A\left(\vec A-\vec P\right)+m_B\left(\vec B-\vec P\right)=m_A\vec A+m_B\vec B-m\vec P=\vec0,\]
which is $m_A\overrightarrow{PA}+m_B\overrightarrow{PB}=\vec0$. If $m_A,m_B\ne0$, dividing by $m_A$ gives
\[\overrightarrow{PA}=-\dfrac{m_B}{m_A}\,\overrightarrow{PB}.\]
Taking lengths, $|m_A|\cdot PA=|m_B|\cdot PB$, i.e. $PA:PB=|m_B|:|m_A|$. If $m_A$ and $m_B$ have the same sign, the factor $-\dfrac{m_B}{m_A}$ is negative, so $\overrightarrow{PA}$ and $\overrightarrow{PB}$ point in opposite directions and $P$ lies between $A$ and $B$. If they have opposite signs, the factor is positive, the two vectors point the same way, and $P$ lies on line $AB$ outside the segment (nearer to the point with the larger absolute mass). $\blacksquare$

So negative masses are allowed, as long as the masses being combined do not add up to $0$: they describe points that divide a segment externally.

\textbf{(b)} With masses $-1$, $2$, $2$ at $A$, $B$, $C$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $B$ and $C$ balance at $\dfrac{2\vec B+2\vec C}{4}$, the midpoint $D$ (total mass $4$);
\item $A$ and $B$ balance at $\dfrac{-\vec A+2\vec B}{1}=\vec B+\left(\vec B-\vec A\right)$, which is the point beyond $B$ with $BF=AB$, i.e. $F$ (total mass $1$);
\item similarly $A$ and $C$ balance at $\vec E=2\vec C-\vec A$ (total mass $1$).
\end{itemize}
Let $\vec G=\dfrac{-\vec A+2\vec B+2\vec C}{3}$, the balance point of all three masses. Grouping the terms in three ways,
\[\vec G=\dfrac{-\vec A+4\vec D}{3}=\dfrac{2\vec B+\vec E}{3}=\dfrac{2\vec C+\vec F}{3},\]
since $4\vec D=2\vec B+2\vec C$, $\vec E=-\vec A+2\vec C$ and $\vec F=-\vec A+2\vec B$. So $G$ is the balance point of $-1$ at $A$ and $4$ at $D$, of $2$ at $B$ and $1$ at $E$, and of $2$ at $C$ and $1$ at $F$. By (a), $G$ lies on each of the lines $AD$, $BE$ and $CF$, so the three lines pass through $G$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(2,4),B=(0,0),C=(5,0),D=(2.5,0),F=(-2,-4),Ep=(8,-4),G=(8/3,-4/3);
draw(B--F,gray);draw(C--Ep,gray);draw(B--Ep);draw(C--F);
draw(A--D);draw(D--G,dashed);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((1.25,-0.12)--(1.25,0.12));draw((3.75,-0.12)--(3.75,0.12));
dot(G);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,NE);label("$E$",Ep,SE);label("$F$",F,SW);label("$G$",G,S);
label("$-1$",A,1.8*E,fontsize(8pt));label("$2$",B,1.8*dir(295),fontsize(8pt));label("$2$",C,1.8*S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

On line $AD$ the masses $-1$ and $4$ have opposite signs, so by (a) $G$ lies outside segment $AD$, beyond $D$ (the larger absolute mass), with
\[AG:GD=4:1.\]
(Check: $B$ and $C$ are the midpoints of $AF$ and $AE$, so $BE$ and $CF$ are medians of triangle $AFE$ and $G$ is its centroid, at $\dfrac23$ of the median from $A$; and $D$, the midpoint of the midline $BC$, lies halfway along that median. So $AG=\dfrac23\cdot2AD=\dfrac43AD$ and $GD=\dfrac13AD$.) The answer is $\boxed{AG:GD=4:1}$, with $G$ beyond $D$.

Since $AB=AC$, the base angles are equal, so
\[\angle B=\dfrac{180^\circ-\left(\dfrac{900}{7}\right)^\circ}{2}=\dfrac12\cdot\left(\dfrac{360}{7}\right)^\circ=\left(\dfrac{180}{7}\right)^\circ.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real t=180/7;
pair B=(-1,0),C=(1,0),A=(0,Tan(t));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);
draw(arc(A,0.1,180+t,360-t));label("$\left(\dfrac{900}{7}\right)^\circ$",A-(0,0.1),S,fontsize(7pt));
draw(arc(B,0.3,0,t));label("$\left(\dfrac{180}{7}\right)^\circ$",B+0.56*dir(t/2),fontsize(7pt));
pair m1=B+0.78*(A-B),m2=C+0.78*(A-C);
draw((m1+0.05*dir(90+t))--(m1-0.05*dir(90+t)));draw((m2+0.05*dir(90-t))--(m2-0.05*dir(90-t)));
\end{asy}

As $\gcd(180,7)=1$, $m+n=180+7=\boxed{187}$.

Let the right angle be at $C$. The legs are $\dfrac{36}{7}\cdot3$ and $\dfrac{36}{7}\cdot4$, so the hypotenuse is $\dfrac{36}{7}\cdot5=\dfrac{180}{7}$.

The midpoint $M$ of the hypotenuse $AB$ is the centre of the circle through $A$, $B$ and $C$ (the angle at $C$ is a right angle, so $AB$ is a diameter of that circle). Hence the median $CM$ equals $MA=\dfrac12AB=\dfrac{90}{7}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),A=(0,108/7),B=(144/7,0),M=(A+B)/2;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(C--M);
draw(circle(M,90/7),dashed+gray);
draw((0.8,0)--(0.8,0.8)--(0,0.8));
dot(M);label("$M$",M,NE);label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,SW);
label("$\dfrac{108}{7}$",(0,54/7),E,fontsize(8pt));label("$\dfrac{144}{7}$",(72/7,0),S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

As $\gcd(90,7)=1$, $2m+n=180+7=\boxed{187}$.

The interior angles of a heptagon add up to $(7-2)\cdot180^\circ=900^\circ$, so each interior angle of a regular heptagon is $\left(\dfrac{900}{7}\right)^\circ$. (Equivalently, the exterior angle is $\dfrac{360^\circ}{7}$ and the interior angle is $180^\circ-\dfrac{360^\circ}{7}$.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V;for(int i=0;i<7;++i) V.push(dir(90+360*i/7));
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--V[6]--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
pair c=V[4];pair bis=unit(unit(V[5]-c)+unit(V[3]-c));
draw(arc(c,0.15,degrees(V[5]-c),degrees(V[3]-c)));
label("$\left(\dfrac{900}{7}\right)^\circ$",c+0.42*bis,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Dividing by $5$ gives $\left(\dfrac{180}{7}\right)^\circ$, so $m+n=180+7=\boxed{187}$.

Let $O$ be the centre. Joining $O$ to the vertices divides the polygon into $n$ congruent isosceles triangles, each with base $s$ and apex angle $\dfrac{360^\circ}{n}$ (shown for $n=6$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V;for(int i=0;i<6;++i) V.push(dir(60*i));
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
pair O=(0,0),Mf=(V[4]+V[5])/2;
filldraw(O--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,lightgray);
draw(O--Mf,dashed);
pair u=unit(V[5]-V[4]),v=unit(O-Mf);
draw((Mf+0.07*u)--(Mf+0.07*u+0.07*v)--(Mf+0.07*v));
dot(O);label("$O$",O,N);label("$a$",(O+Mf)/2,E,fontsize(9pt));label("$s$",Mf,S);
draw(arc(O,0.2,240,300));
\end{asy}

The altitude from $O$ (the apothem $a$) bisects the base and the apex angle, giving a right triangle with legs $\dfrac s2$ and $a$ and angle $\dfrac{180^\circ}{n}$ at $O$. So
\[\tan\dfrac{180^\circ}{n}=\dfrac{s}{2a},\qquad a=\dfrac{s}{2\tan\dfrac{180^\circ}{n}}.\]
Each triangle has area $\dfrac{sa}{2}$, so the polygon has area
\[\boxed{A=\dfrac{nsa}{2}=\dfrac{ns^2}{4\tan\dfrac{180^\circ}{n}}}.\]
Checks: for $n=4$, $\tan45^\circ=1$ gives $s^2$; for $n=6$, $\tan30^\circ=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}$ gives $\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}s^2$, six equilateral triangles of area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2$.

Long division of $4x^4+0x^3+0x^2+2x-3$ by $2x+4$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $4x^4\div2x=2x^3$; subtract $2x^3(2x+4)=4x^4+8x^3$, leaving $-8x^3+2x-3$;
\item $-8x^3\div2x=-4x^2$; subtract $-8x^3-16x^2$, leaving $16x^2+2x-3$;
\item $16x^2\div2x=8x$; subtract $16x^2+32x$, leaving $-30x-3$;
\item $-30x\div2x=-15$; subtract $-30x-60$, leaving $57$.
\end{itemize}
So
\[\dfrac{4x^4+2x-3}{2x+4}=\boxed{2x^3-4x^2+8x-15+\dfrac{57}{2x+4}}.\]
Check: by the remainder theorem the remainder is the value of $4x^4+2x-3$ at $x=-2$, namely $64-4-3=57$.

Since $D$ lies on side $AB$, the ray $BD$ is the ray $BA$, so $\angle DBC=\angle ABC=90^\circ$. The condition $2\angle DBC=3\angle BAC$ gives $\angle BAC=60^\circ$.

Let $AB=c$. Then $BC=c\tan60^\circ=c\sqrt3$, and since $AD=1$, $BD=c-1$. In the right triangle $DBC$,
\[CD^2=BD^2+BC^2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad4=(c-1)^2+3c^2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad4c^2-2c-3=0.\]
The positive root is
\[c=\dfrac{2+\sqrt{4+48}}{8}=\boxed{\dfrac{1+\sqrt{13}}{4}}\approx1.151.\]
It exceeds $1$, so $D$ indeed lies on side $AB$ (close to $B$, as in the sketch, which is to scale).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real c=(1+sqrt(13))/4;
pair B=(0,0),A=(c,0),C=(0,c*sqrt(3)),D=(c-1,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(C--D);
draw((0,0.07)--(0.07,0.07)--(0.07,0));
label("$A$",A,SE);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,S);
label("$1$",(D+A)/2,S);label("$2$",(C+D)/2,E);
draw(arc(A,0.22,120,180));label("$60^\circ$",A+0.25*dir(150),NW,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

In an equilateral triangle the incentre $O$ is also the centroid and lies on the altitude $AD$, with $OD=r=\dfrac13AD$. The circle touches $BC$ at $D$, and $AD$ passes through $O$, so $DE$ is a diameter: $DE=2r$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s3=sqrt(3);
pair A=(0,3),B=(-s3,0),C=(s3,0),D=(0,0),O=(0,1),Ep=(0,2);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(circle(O,1));draw(A--D);
draw((0,0.13)--(0.13,0.13)--(0.13,0));
dot(O);dot(Ep);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$O$",O,E);label("$E$",Ep,NE);
label("$1$",(0,2.5),1.2*W);label("$r$",(0,0.5),1.2*W);label("$r$",(0,1.5),1.2*W);
\end{asy}

Hence $AE=AD-2r=\dfrac13AD=1$, so $AD=3$. Since $AD=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}AB$,
\[AB=\dfrac{6}{\sqrt3}=2\sqrt3,\]
so $a=2$, $b=3$ and $a+b=\boxed{5}$.

"$30^\circ$ north of east" means the direction obtained by turning $30^\circ$ from due east towards north. Let the detour start at $P$, the turn be at $Q$ and the rejoining point be $R$, so $PR=1000$ km, $\angle QPR=30^\circ$ and $\angle QRP=45^\circ$. Let $F$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $Q$ to $PR$, and $QF=x$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real x=500*(sqrt(3)-1)/100;
pair P=(0,0),R=(10,0),Q=(x*sqrt(3),x),F=(x*sqrt(3),0);
draw(P--R,dashed);draw(P--Q,linewidth(0.8),MidArrow);draw(Q--R,linewidth(0.8),MidArrow);
draw(Q--F,dotted);draw((F+(0.25,0))--(F+(0.25,0.25))--(F+(0,0.25)));
draw(arc(P,1.4,0,30));label("$30^\circ$",P+1.5*dir(15),E,fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc(R,1.1,135,180));label("$45^\circ$",R+1.2*dir(157),W,fontsize(8pt));
label("$P$",P,W);label("$R$",R,E);label("$Q$",Q,N);label("$F$",F,S);
label("$1000$ km",(5,-0.6),S);
draw((11.5,0.2)--(11.5,1.4),Arrow);label("N",(11.5,1.4),N,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Triangle $PFQ$ is a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle, so $PF=x\sqrt3$ and $PQ=2x$; triangle $RFQ$ is a $45$-$45$-$90$ triangle, so $FR=x$ and $QR=x\sqrt2$. From $PF+FR=1000$,
\[x=\dfrac{1000}{\sqrt3+1}=500\left(\sqrt3-1\right)\approx366.03.\]
The detour has length $PQ+QR=x\left(2+\sqrt2\right)\approx1249.69$ km, compared with $1000$ km along the original route. The extra distance is about $249.69$, i.e. $\boxed{250}$ km.

The angles of a quadrilateral add up to $360^\circ$, so the other three add up to $360^\circ-138^\circ=222^\circ$. With common difference $d$,
\[70+(70+d)+(70+2d)=222\quad\Longrightarrow\quad3d=12\quad\Longrightarrow\quad d=4.\]
The angles are $70^\circ$, $74^\circ$, $78^\circ$, and the last one is $\boxed{\textbf{(A) }78}$. Such a quadrilateral exists (all four angles are less than $180^\circ$):

\begin{asy}
