draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
label("$70^\circ$",A+0.7*dir(35),fontsize(8pt));
label("$138^\circ$",B+0.75*dir(111),fontsize(8pt));
label("$74^\circ$",C+0.85*dir(185),fontsize(8pt));
label("$78^\circ$",D+0.8*dir(289),fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc(A,0.35,0,70));draw(arc(B,0.35,42,180));draw(arc(C,0.35,148,222));draw(arc(D,0.35,250,328));
\end{asy}

The area of a rhombus is half the product of its diagonals: $\dfrac{7\cdot24}{2}=84$. The diagonals of a rhombus bisect each other at right angles, so a side is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs $3.5$ and $12$:
\[\sqrt{3.5^2+12^2}=\sqrt{12.25+144}=\sqrt{156.25}=12.5.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-12,0),B=(0,3.5),C=(12,0),D=(0,-3.5);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(A--C,dashed);draw(B--D,dashed);
draw((0,0.6)--(0.6,0.6)--(0.6,0));
label("$12$",(6,0),S,fontsize(8pt));label("$3.5$",(0,1.75),W,fontsize(8pt));label("$12.5$",(6,1.75),NE,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The sum is $84+12.5=96.5$, answer $\boxed{\textbf{(B) }96.5}$.

We use induction on $n$.

\emph{Base case $n=1$.} A $2\times2$ board with one square removed is itself an L-tromino.

\emph{Inductive step.} Suppose every $2^n\times2^n$ board with one square removed can be tiled, and take a $2^{n+1}\times2^{n+1}$ board with one square removed. Cut it into four $2^n\times2^n$ quadrants. The removed square lies in one of them. Place one L-tromino on the three centre squares that belong to the other three quadrants (one square in each), as in the sketch for $n=2$ (the removed square is black and the central tromino is shaded).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<=8;++i){draw((i,0)--(i,8),gray(0.6));draw((0,i)--(8,i),gray(0.6));}
fill((1,6)--(2,6)--(2,7)--(1,7)--cycle,black);
fill((4,4)--(5,4)--(5,3)--(3,3)--(3,4)--(4,4)--cycle,mediumgray);
fill((4,4)--(5,4)--(5,5)--(4,5)--cycle,mediumgray);
fill((3,3)--(5,3)--(5,4)--(3,4)--cycle,mediumgray);
draw((4,3)--(5,3)--(5,5)--(4,5)--(4,4)--(3,4)--(3,3)--(4,3),linewidth(1));
draw((0,0)--(8,0)--(8,8)--(0,8)--cycle,linewidth(1.2));
draw((4,0)--(4,8),linewidth(1.2));draw((0,4)--(8,4),linewidth(1.2));
\end{asy}

Now each of the four quadrants is a $2^n\times2^n$ board with exactly one square removed or already covered. By the induction hypothesis each can be tiled, and together with the central tromino this tiles the whole board.

By induction the statement holds for all $n\ge1$. $\blacksquare$ (A count confirms that this is possible: $4^n-1$ is divisible by $3$.)

question:
For sets $X$ and $Y$, let $X\cup Y$ be the set of elements in $X$ or $Y$, and let $X\setminus Y$ be the set of elements of $X$ that are not in $Y$. Let $S$ be the set of positive integers with $1$, $2$ or $3$ digits that are palindromes and whose digits are all $0$ or $9$. Define
\begin{itemize}
\item $A$: the elements of $S$ having the digit $0$;
\item $B$: the elements of $S$ having only the digit $9$;
\item $C$: the elements of $S$ having $3$ digits;
\item $D$: the elements of $S$ divisible by $11$.
\end{itemize}
How many distinct nonempty sets can be obtained from $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$ using the operations $\cup$ and $\setminus$ (any number of times)?

solution:
An integer that is both a square and a cube is a sixth power; the smallest one greater than $1$ is $2^6=64$. So the shorter side is $\sqrt[3]{64}=4$.

Let the kite be $PQTR$ with $PQ=PR=4$, $\angle QPR=90^\circ$, $TQ=TR=x$ and $\angle QTR=60^\circ$. Triangle $PQR$ is an isosceles right triangle, so $QR=4\sqrt2$. Triangle $TQR$ is isosceles with apex angle $60^\circ$, so its base angles are also $60^\circ$ and it is equilateral. Hence $x=QR=\boxed{4\sqrt2}$, answer $\textbf{(A)}$.

