$R$ is defined for $x\ne\pm2$. It has a zero at $x=1$, vertical asymptotes $x=-2$ and $x=2$, and $R(x)\to0$ as $x\to\pm\infty$ (horizontal asymptote $y=0$); also $R(0)=\dfrac14$.

The sign of $R(x)=\dfrac{x-1}{(x-2)(x+2)}$ changes only at $-2$, $1$ and $2$:
\[\begin{array}{c|cccc}
&x<-2&-2<x<1&1<x<2&x>2\\\hline
x-1&-&-&+&+\\
x^2-4&+&-&-&+\\
R(x)&-&+&-&+
\end{array}\]
Including the zero at $x=1$ (but not $\pm2$, where $R$ is undefined),
\[R(x)\ge0\iff\boxed{-2<x\le1\ \text{ or }\ x>2}.\]
In particular $R(x)\ge0$ does \emph{not} hold for every $x$: for example $R(-3)=-\dfrac45$.

In the sketch, the solution set is marked in red on the $x$-axis.

