question:
The perfect squares $0,1,4,9,16,25,36,\dots$ increase by the odd numbers $1,3,5,7,9,11,\dots$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that for every integer $n\ge0$, $(n+1)^2-n^2=2n+1$; that is, the step from $n^2$ to $(n+1)^2$ is the $(n+1)$st odd number.
\item[(b)] Deduce that $1+3+5+\cdots+(2n-1)=n^2$ for every positive integer $n$.
\end{enumerate}

A polygon is called \emph{tangential} (or circumscribable about a circle) if there is a circle inside it touching every side.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that a convex polygon is tangential if and only if the bisectors of all its interior angles (drawn as rays from the vertices into the polygon) pass through one point.
\item[(b)] Let $A_1A_2\cdots A_{2m}$ be a tangential polygon with an even number of sides, and let $a_i=A_iA_{i+1}$ (with $A_{2m+1}=A_1$). Prove that
\[a_1+a_3+\cdots+a_{2m-1}=a_2+a_4+\cdots+a_{2m}.\]
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Suppose a circle with centre $I$ and radius $r$ lies inside the polygon and touches every side. Then $I$ is at distance $r$ from the two side lines through each vertex $V$, and lies inside the angle at $V$. A point inside an angle that is equidistant from its two arms lies on the bisector of the angle. So every interior angle bisector passes through $I$.

Conversely, suppose all the interior angle bisectors pass through a point $I$. A point on the bisector of the angle at $V$ is equidistant from the two sides at $V$. Going round the polygon, the distances from $I$ to the lines of consecutive sides are equal, so $I$ is at the same distance $r$ from every side line. Moreover $I$ lies on the bisector ray at each vertex, so it lies inside every angle of the polygon, i.e. on the inner side of every side line; for a convex polygon the intersection of these half-planes is the polygon itself, so $I$ is inside it. The disc with centre $I$ and radius $r$ lies on the inner side of every side line, hence inside the polygon, and it touches each side line at the foot of the perpendicular from $I$. That point lies in the polygon and on the side line, so it lies on the side itself (for a convex polygon, the points of the polygon on a side line form exactly that side). Hence the circle touches every side, and the polygon is tangential.

\textbf{(b)} The two tangent segments from a vertex to the circle are equal; call their common length $t_i$ for vertex $A_i$. Each side is made of two tangent segments, so $a_i=t_i+t_{i+1}$ (with $t_{2m+1}=t_1$). Then
\[a_1-a_2+a_3-a_4+\cdots-a_{2m}=(t_1+t_2)-(t_2+t_3)+(t_3+t_4)-\cdots-(t_{2m}+t_1)=0,\]
since every $t_i$ appears once with each sign. This is the required equality. $\blacksquare$

For a quadrilateral, (b) is the familiar condition $AB+CD=BC+DA$, and there it is also sufficient. For more sides the side lengths alone do not decide the question. For example, an equilateral hexagon with angles $100^\circ,140^\circ,120^\circ,100^\circ,140^\circ,120^\circ$ exists (it is centrally symmetric, with exterior angles adding up to $360^\circ$) and satisfies (b). It is not tangential: the tangent lengths would satisfy $t_i=r\cot\dfrac{A_i}{2}$ and $t_i+t_{i+1}=s$ (the common side length), forcing $t_1=t_3$ and hence $A_1=A_3$, but $100^\circ\ne120^\circ$. The general test is the concurrence in (a). In the sketch, the dashed angle bisectors meet at the incentre $I$, and the blue points are the points of tangency.

