question:
A student wants to show that, apart from quadrilaterals, no polygon has a pair of supplementary opposite angles. The argument is:

``Suppose a polygon with $n\ne4$ sides ($n$ even) has two opposite angles that are supplementary. There are $\dfrac n2$ pairs of opposite angles, so the angle sum is $180^\circ\cdot\dfrac n2$. But the angle sum is $180^\circ(n-2)$, and $180\cdot\dfrac n2\ne180(n-2)$ for $n\ne4$. Contradiction.''
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain what is wrong with this argument.
\item[(b)] Let $A_1A_2\cdots A_{2m}$ be a convex polygon inscribed in a circle, with $m\ge3$. The angles at $A_i$ and $A_{i+m}$ are called opposite. Prove that the sum of any two opposite angles is greater than $180^\circ$; in particular, no two opposite angles are supplementary.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The assumption is that \emph{one} pair of opposite angles is supplementary, but the computation uses that \emph{all} $\dfrac n2$ pairs are. The argument therefore only shows that not every pair can be supplementary when $n\ne4$; it says nothing about a single pair. It also never uses that the polygon is inscribed in a circle, and for general polygons a single supplementary pair is possible: a convex hexagon with angles $90^\circ,135^\circ,135^\circ,90^\circ,135^\circ,135^\circ$ exists (its exterior angles $90^\circ,45^\circ,45^\circ,90^\circ,45^\circ,45^\circ$ add up to $360^\circ$), and its opposite angles $90^\circ$ and $90^\circ$ are supplementary.

\textbf{(b)} Let $x_k$ be the measure of the arc $A_kA_{k+1}$ not containing the other vertices (indices mod $2m$), so $x_1+x_2+\cdots+x_{2m}=360^\circ$ and every $x_k>0$. The angle at $A_i$ is an inscribed angle standing on the arc from $A_{i+1}$ to $A_{i-1}$ that does not contain $A_i$, which measures $360^\circ-x_{i-1}-x_i$. Hence
\[\angle A_i=180^\circ-\dfrac{x_{i-1}+x_i}{2},\qquad\angle A_{i+m}=180^\circ-\dfrac{x_{i+m-1}+x_{i+m}}{2}.\]
Adding, and writing $R$ for the sum of the other $2m-4$ arcs,
\[\angle A_i+\angle A_{i+m}=360^\circ-\dfrac{360^\circ-R}{2}=180^\circ+\dfrac R2.\]
Since $m\ge3$, there are $2m-4\ge2$ other arcs (for instance $x_{i+1}$), each of positive measure, so $R>0$ and $\angle A_i+\angle A_{i+m}>180^\circ$. $\blacksquare$

(For $m=2$ there are no other arcs, $R=0$, and we recover that opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral are supplementary.) In the hexagon sketch, the opposite angles at $A_1$ and $A_4$ exceed $180^\circ$ in total by half of the blue arcs $x_2$ and $x_5$.

