Number the points $0,1,\dots,16$ around the circle. Going around the circle, the five chosen points cut it into five gaps; let the \emph{length} of a gap be the number of steps between consecutive chosen points, so the lengths add up to $17$. One step is $\dfrac{360^\circ}{17}$, so a gap of length $8$ is less than $180^\circ$ and a gap of length $9$ is more.

The inscribed pentagon contains the centre exactly when every gap is less than a semicircle; if some gap exceeds $180^\circ$, all five points lie on the opposite, smaller arc and the pentagon lies on one side of a chord that misses the centre. (No gap equals $180^\circ$, since $17$ is odd.)

Count the \emph{bad} choices, with a gap of length at least $9$. Two such gaps would need $18>17$ steps, so a bad choice has exactly one long gap. Number the points clockwise and let $V$ be the chosen point at which the long gap starts, going clockwise. The other four chosen points then lie on the remaining arc of at most $8$ steps that ends at $V$, i.e. among the $8$ points $V-1,V-2,\dots,V-8$ (mod $17$). Conversely, any choice of $V$ and of four of these $8$ points gives a bad set whose long gap starts at $V$. So there are $17\dbinom84=17\cdot70=1190$ bad sets out of $\dbinom{17}5=6188$.

The probability is
\[1-\dfrac{1190}{6188}=1-\dfrac5{26}=\boxed{\dfrac{21}{26}}.\]
In the sketch, the blue pentagon contains the centre; the red one has a gap of $9$ steps and does not.

