\end{asy}

Recall $\varphi^2=\varphi+1$ and $2\cos36^\circ=\varphi$. Let $X$ be the area of pentagon $PALIR$.

\emph{The heptagon.} Since $L$ and $I$ lie inside $MAREB$, the boundary $M\to A\to L\to I\to R\to E\to B$ cuts quadrilateral $LIRA$ out of pentagon $MAREB$:
\[[MALIREB]=[MAREB]-[LIRA],\qquad [LIRA]=X-[RAP]=X-\sqrt{2+\varphi}.\]
The pentagons are similar with ratio $\dfrac{2\varphi}{2}=\varphi$, so $[MAREB]=\varphi^2X$, and
\[[MALIREB]=\varphi^2X-X+\sqrt{2+\varphi}=\varphi X+\sqrt{2+\varphi}.\]

\emph{The small pentagon.} The diagonals from $A$ split $PALIR$ into triangles $ALI$, $AIR$ and $ARP$. Triangle $ALI$ has two sides $2$ with a $108^\circ$ angle between them, as does $RAP$, so $[ALI]=\sqrt{2+\varphi}$. Triangle $AIR$ has $AI=AR=2\varphi$ and apex angle $\angle IAR=36^\circ$, so
\[\dfrac{[AIR]}{[ALI]}=\dfrac{\dfrac12(2\varphi)^2\sin36^\circ}{\dfrac12\cdot2^2\sin108^\circ}=\dfrac{\varphi^2\sin36^\circ}{\sin72^\circ}=\dfrac{\varphi^2}{2\cos36^\circ}=\varphi.\]
Hence $X=(2+\varphi)\sqrt{2+\varphi}$, and
\[[MALIREB]=\left(\varphi(2+\varphi)+1\right)\sqrt{2+\varphi}=(3\varphi+2)\sqrt{2+\varphi}.\]

\emph{The form $\sqrt{m+n\varphi}$.} Using $\varphi^2=\varphi+1$,
\[(3\varphi+2)^2(2+\varphi)=(21\varphi+13)(\varphi+2)=21\varphi^2+55\varphi+26=76\varphi+47.\]
So the area is $\sqrt{47+76\varphi}\approx13.04$ m$^2$. (The integers are unique: $m+n\varphi=m'+n'\varphi$ with $n\ne n'$ would make $\varphi$ rational.) Thus $m=47$, $n=76$ and $m+2n=\boxed{199}$.

In the sketch, the heptagon is shaded, the small pentagon $PALIR$ is dashed, and the dotted segment is $AR$.

