The triangle is isosceles: $(2,1)$ is at distance $\sqrt2$ from both $(1,0)$ and $(3,0)$, while the base has length $2$. Since it is not equilateral, its only line of symmetry is the perpendicular bisector of the base, $x=2$. Rotations and reflections preserve distances, so they map this line to the line of symmetry of the image triangle.

The line $x=2$ is the set of points $p$ with $p\cdot(1,0)=2$. After a rotation by $30^\circ$ about the origin, the normal vector $(1,0)$ becomes $(\cos30^\circ,\sin30^\circ)$, so the image line is
\[\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}x+\dfrac12y=2.\]
Reflecting in $y=x$ swaps $x$ and $y$:
\[\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}y+\dfrac12x=2\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad y=-\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}x+\dfrac{4}{\sqrt3}.\]
So $m=-\dfrac1{\sqrt3}$, $b=\dfrac4{\sqrt3}$ and $\dfrac bm=\boxed{-4}$.

The sketch shows the original triangle (black), the rotated triangle (dashed), the final triangle (blue) and its line of symmetry (red).

