Let $b=CA$ and $c=AB$. Since the triangle is acute, $H$ lies between $A$ and $B$, and $AH=b\cos A$.

\emph{Using the concurrency.} By the angle bisector theorem $\dfrac{BD}{DC}=\dfrac cb$, and $\dfrac{CM}{MA}=1$. By Ceva's theorem for the concurrent cevians $AD$, $BM$, $CH$,
\[\dfrac{AH}{HB}\cdot\dfrac{BD}{DC}\cdot\dfrac{CM}{MA}=1,\qquad\text{so}\qquad\dfrac{AH}{HB}=\dfrac bc.\]
With $AH+HB=c$ this gives $AH=\dfrac{bc}{b+c}$, hence
\[\cos A=\dfrac{AH}{AC}=\dfrac{c}{b+c}.\]

\emph{Using that $\angle C$ is acute.} By the law of cosines, $a^2=b^2+c^2-2bc\cos A=b^2+c^2-\dfrac{2bc^2}{b+c}$. The condition $c^2<a^2+b^2$ becomes
\[c^2<2b^2+c^2-\dfrac{2bc^2}{b+c}\iff bc^2<b^2(b+c)\iff c^2<b^2+bc.\]
With $t=\dfrac bc$ this says $t^2+t-1>0$, i.e. $t>\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{2}$. Therefore
\[\cos A=\dfrac{1}{1+t}<\dfrac{1}{1+\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{2}}=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt5+1}=\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{2}\approx0.618<\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}=\cos45^\circ.\]
Since cosine is decreasing on $\left(0^\circ,180^\circ\right)$, $\angle BAC>45^\circ$. $\blacksquare$ (In fact the argument shows $\angle BAC>\arccos\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{2}\approx51.8^\circ$.)

