solution:
\emph{Smallest.} For lattice points, twice the area is $\left|(x_2-x_1)(y_3-y_1)-(x_3-x_1)(y_2-y_1)\right|$, a positive integer. So the area is at least $\dfrac12$, attained by $(0,0)$, $(1,0)$, $(0,1)$.

\emph{Largest.} Let the vertices have $y$-coordinates $y_1\le y_2\le y_3$. The horizontal line through the middle vertex cuts the triangle into two triangles (one may be degenerate) with a common horizontal side of length $\ell\le285$ (it lies in the board) and heights $h_1$, $h_2$ with $h_1+h_2=y_3-y_1\le200$. So the area is
\[\dfrac12\ell(h_1+h_2)\le\dfrac12\cdot285\cdot200=28500,\]
attained by $(0,0)$, $(285,0)$, $(0,200)$.

The difference is $28500-\dfrac12=\boxed{28499.5}$.

