\emph{Necessary conditions.} Putting $x=y=0$: $2f(0)=f(0)^2+1$, so $(f(0)-1)^2=0$ and $f(0)=1$. Putting $x=y=1$: $f(0)+f(1)=f(1)^2+1$, so $f(1)=f(1)^2$ and $a=f(1)\in\{0,1\}$. Putting $x=0$: $f(-y)+1=f(y)+1$, so $f$ is even. Putting $y=-1$ and using $f(-1)=a$:
\[f(x+1)+f(-x)=af(x)+1\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad f(x+1)=(a-1)f(x)+1.\]
This recursion and $f(0)=1$ determine $f(n)$ for $n\ge0$, and evenness determines the rest. So there are at most two functions:
\begin{itemize}
\item $a=1$: $f(x+1)=1$, so $f\equiv1$;
\item $a=0$: $f(x+1)=1-f(x)$, so $f(n)=1$ for even $n$ and $f(n)=0$ for odd $n$.
\end{itemize}

\emph{Both work.} For $f\equiv1$: $1+1=1\cdot1+1$. For the parity function, check the four parity cases of $(x,y)$:
\begin{itemize}
\item both even: $x-y$ and $xy$ even, $1+1=1\cdot1+1$;
\item exactly one odd: $x-y$ odd and $xy$ even, $0+1=0+1$;
\item both odd: $x-y$ even and $xy$ odd, $1+0=0+1$.
\end{itemize}
So there are exactly $\boxed{2}$ such functions.

We prove the identity
\[\sin^2A+\sin^2B+\sin^2C=2+2\cos A\cos B\cos C.\]
Using $\sin^2\theta=\dfrac{1-\cos2\theta}{2}$ and the sum-to-product formula,
\[\sin^2A+\sin^2B=1-\dfrac{\cos2A+\cos2B}{2}=1-\cos(A+B)\cos(A-B)=1+\cos C\cos(A-B),\]
since $A+B=180^\circ-C$ gives $\cos(A+B)=-\cos C$. Also $\sin^2C=1-\cos^2C=1+\cos C\cos(A+B)$. Adding,
\[\sin^2A+\sin^2B+\sin^2C=2+\cos C\left(\cos(A-B)+\cos(A+B)\right)=2+2\cos A\cos B\cos C.\]
Hence the sum equals $2$ exactly when $\cos A\cos B\cos C=0$, i.e. when one of the angles is $90^\circ$ (the angles lie strictly between $0^\circ$ and $180^\circ$, where the cosine vanishes only at $90^\circ$). $\blacksquare$

(The identity also shows the sum is greater than $2$ for an acute triangle and less than $2$ for an obtuse one.)

