question:
Solve each equation.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $20\times2-0.5\times y\times4=20.4$
\item[(b)] $20\times2-x\times9.8\times4=20.4$
\item[(c)] $20\times2-0.5\times9.8\times a=20.4$
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The parities of the terms depend only on the parities of the first two terms, and repeat with period $3$:
\begin{itemize}
\item odd, odd: $o,o,e,o,o,e,\dots$; $2020=3\cdot673+1$ terms give $1347$ odd and $673$ even;
\item odd, even: $o,e,o,o,e,o,\dots$; again $1347$ odd and $673$ even;
\item even, odd: $e,o,o,e,o,o,\dots$; $1346$ odd and $674$ even;
\item even, even: all terms even.
\end{itemize}
Since $1347>2\cdot673=1346$ but $1346<2\cdot674$, the condition holds exactly when the first term is odd.

First terms in $\{1,\dots,2m-1\}$ that are odd: $m$ choices; second terms: $2m-1$ choices. So
\[m(2m-1)=2415\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad2m^2-m-2415=0\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad m=\dfrac{1+\sqrt{1+19320}}{4}=\dfrac{1+139}{4}=35.\]
The answer is $m=\boxed{35}$, choice $\textbf{(D)}$. (Check: $35\cdot69=2415$.)

question:
$2310=2\cdot3\cdot5\cdot7\cdot11$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many ordered triples $(a,b,c)$ of positive integers satisfy $abc=2310$? (The number $1$ is allowed as a factor.)
\item[(b)] How many unordered triples $\{a,b,c\}$ (that is, ways to write $2310$ as a product of three positive integers, ignoring order) are there?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the side be $s$ and the centre $O$; every vertex $V$ is at distance $s$ from $O$. A point is at least as close to $O$ as to $V$ exactly when it lies on $O$'s side of the perpendicular bisector of $OV$, a line at distance $\dfrac s2$ from $O$.

So the points closer to $O$ than to every vertex form the region bounded by the six perpendicular bisectors: a regular hexagon with centre $O$ and apothem $\dfrac s2$ (red in the sketch). Its vertices are at distance $\dfrac{s}{\sqrt3}<\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s$ from $O$, so it lies inside the original hexagon, whose apothem is $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s$.

The two hexagons are similar with ratio of apothems $\dfrac{s}{2}:\dfrac{\sqrt3 s}{2}=1:\sqrt3$, so the area ratio is $\dfrac13$. The probability that $P$ is closer to some vertex than to the centre is
\[1-\dfrac13=\boxed{\dfrac23}.\]

