The parabola is $y=(x-3)^2-1$. Write its points as $(3+u,\ u^2-1)$. The squared distance from $(3,1)$ is
\[D(u)=u^2+\left(u^2-2\right)^2=u^4-3u^2+4=\left(u^2-\dfrac32\right)^2+\dfrac74.\]
The circle of radius $r$ meets the parabola where $D(u)=r^2$, i.e. $\left(u^2-\dfrac32\right)^2=r^2-\dfrac74$, and it is tangent at a point exactly when the corresponding root $u$ is a repeated root of this quartic equation.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $r^2<\dfrac74$, there are no common points.
\item If $r^2=\dfrac74$, then $u^2=\dfrac32$ is a double root, so $u=\pm\sqrt{\dfrac32}$ are both double roots: the circle touches the parabola at the two points $\left(3\pm\sqrt{\dfrac32},\ \dfrac12\right)$, and every other point of the parabola is farther away.
\item If $r^2>\dfrac74$, then $u^2=\dfrac32\pm\sqrt{r^2-\dfrac74}$; a value $u^2>0$ gives two simple roots $\pm u$ (crossing points), and only $u^2=0$, which happens for $r^2=4$, gives a double root, at the single point $u=0$ (the vertex).
\end{itemize}
So the circle is tangent at two points exactly when $r^2=\boxed{\dfrac74}$.

