$\textbf{(A) } 6 \qquad \textbf{(B) } 12 \qquad \textbf{(C) } 18 \qquad \textbf{(D) } 24 \qquad \textbf{(E) } 36$

(A student argued: ``$\overline{AB}$ is parallel to $3$ other edges, and then $\overline{EF}$ gives $2$ new pairs, so there are $5$ pairs in each direction and $15$ in all.'' Find the error.)

solution:
The $12$ edges fall into $3$ directions, with $4$ parallel edges in each. In the direction of $\overline{AB}$ these are $\overline{AB}$, $\overline{EF}$, $\overline{DC}$, $\overline{HG}$ (red in the sketch). Any two of the four form a pair, giving $\dbinom42=6$ pairs: $3$ pairs containing $\overline{AB}$, then $2$ new pairs containing $\overline{EF}$, then $1$ more, $\overline{DC}$ with $\overline{HG}$. The student stopped after $3+2$ and forgot this last pair.

So there are $3\cdot6=\boxed{18}$ pairs, answer $\textbf{(C)}$. (Equivalently: each of the $12$ edges is parallel to $3$ others, and each pair is counted twice: $\dfrac{12\cdot3}{2}=18$.)

