\end{asy}

question:
For any angle $\theta$, let the point of the unit circle reached by turning $\theta$ counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis be $(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$; this defines sine and cosine for all angles, not just acute ones, and $\tan\theta=\dfrac{\sin\theta}{\cos\theta}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $\sin\left(180^\circ-\theta\right)=\sin\theta$ and $\cos\left(180^\circ-\theta\right)=-\cos\theta$.
\item[(b)] Find the exact values of $\sin150^\circ$, $\cos150^\circ$, $\sin225^\circ$, $\cos300^\circ$ and $\tan120^\circ$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Reflecting in the $y$-axis maps the unit circle to itself and sends $(x,y)$ to $(-x,y)$. It maps the positive $x$-axis to the negative $x$-axis and reverses the sense of turning, so the ray at angle $\theta$ goes to the ray making angle $\theta$ with the negative $x$-axis, measured clockwise; that is the ray at angle $180^\circ-\theta$. (In the sketch, the red radius makes the angle $\theta$ with the negative $x$-axis.) Hence the point $(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$ goes to $\left(\cos\left(180^\circ-\theta\right),\sin\left(180^\circ-\theta\right)\right)$, and this equals $(-\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(b)} In the same way, a half-turn about the origin gives $(\cos(180^\circ+\theta),\sin(180^\circ+\theta))=(-\cos\theta,-\sin\theta)$, and reflection in the $x$-axis gives $(\cos(360^\circ-\theta),\sin(360^\circ-\theta))=(\cos\theta,-\sin\theta)$. Using the values for $30^\circ$, $45^\circ$, $60^\circ$:
\[\sin150^\circ=\sin30^\circ=\boxed{\dfrac12},\qquad\cos150^\circ=-\cos30^\circ=\boxed{-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}},\]
\[\sin225^\circ=-\sin45^\circ=\boxed{-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}},\qquad\cos300^\circ=\cos60^\circ=\boxed{\dfrac12},\]
\[\tan120^\circ=\dfrac{\sin60^\circ}{-\cos60^\circ}=\dfrac{\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}}{-\dfrac12}=\boxed{-\sqrt3}.\]

