question:
Numerals in a negative base work like those in a positive base: in base $-7$, the numeral $243$ means $2\cdot(-7)^2+4\cdot(-7)+3$, and in base $-2$ the digits are $0$ and $1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the value of $243_{(-7)}$.
\item[(b)] Write $10$ and $-10$ in base $-2$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the dividend be $N$ and the divisor $d$. A remainder of $1$ needs $d\ge2$, and $N\equiv1\pmod d$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $N$ is even (it ends in $2$), so $d$ cannot be even.
\item $N\equiv2\pmod5$, so $d\ne5$.
\item $N\equiv1\pmod9$ implies $N\equiv1\pmod3$.
\end{itemize}
So $N$ works exactly when $N\equiv1\pmod3$ or $N\equiv1\pmod7$. Combined with $N\equiv2\pmod{10}$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $N\equiv22\pmod{30}$: $112,142,\dots,982$, which is $30$ numbers;
\item $N\equiv22\pmod{70}$: $162,232,\dots,932$, which is $12$ numbers;
\item both, $N\equiv22\pmod{210}$: $232,442,652,862$, which is $4$ numbers.
\end{itemize}
By inclusion--exclusion there are $30+12-4=\boxed{38}$ values.

