\end{asy}

question:
$ABCD$ is a rhombus. $E,F,G,H$ are the midpoints of $AB$, $BC$, $CD$, $DA$, and $I,J,K,L$ are the midpoints of $EF$, $FG$, $GH$, $HE$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $EFGH$ is a rectangle.
\item[(b)] Prove that $IJKL$ is a rhombus similar to $ABCD$: it is the image of $ABCD$ under the dilation with centre $O$, the centre of the rhombus, and ratio $\dfrac12$ (with $A,B,C,D$ mapped to $L,I,J,K$). In particular, its area is $\dfrac14$ of the area of $ABCD$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The diagonals of a rhombus are perpendicular and bisect each other, so we may take coordinates with $O$ at the origin and
\[A=(p,0),\quad B=(0,q),\quad C=(-p,0),\quad D=(0,-q)\qquad(p,q>0).\]

\textbf{(a)} The midpoints are
\[E=\left(\dfrac p2,\dfrac q2\right),\quad F=\left(-\dfrac p2,\dfrac q2\right),\quad G=\left(-\dfrac p2,-\dfrac q2\right),\quad H=\left(\dfrac p2,-\dfrac q2\right).\]
Its sides are horizontal and vertical, so $EFGH$ is a rectangle (with sides $p$ and $q$, parallel to the diagonals of $ABCD$).

\textbf{(b)} The midpoints of its sides are
\[I=\left(0,\dfrac q2\right)=\dfrac12B,\quad J=\left(-\dfrac p2,0\right)=\dfrac12C,\quad K=\left(0,-\dfrac q2\right)=\dfrac12D,\quad L=\left(\dfrac p2,0\right)=\dfrac12A.\]
So $L,I,J,K$ are the images of $A,B,C,D$ under the dilation with centre $O$ and ratio $\dfrac12$. A dilation maps a rhombus to a similar rhombus with parallel sides, scaled by $\dfrac12$; hence $IJKL$ is a rhombus similar to $ABCD$ with half the side length and $\left(\dfrac12\right)^2=\dfrac14$ of its area. $\blacksquare$

