\end{asy}

question:
Zan's rule for generating sequences of whole numbers is:
\begin{itemize}
\item if a number is $25$ or less, double it;
\item if a number is greater than $25$, subtract $12$ from it.
\end{itemize}
Let $F$ be the first number of a sequence generated by this rule. $F$ is called \emph{sweet} if $16$ is not a term of the sequence that starts with $F$. How many of the whole numbers $1$ through $50$ are sweet? Prove your answer.

solution:
A chord whose (minor) central angle is $\theta$, with $0\le\theta\le180^\circ$, has length $2r\sin\dfrac\theta2$, which is at most $r$ exactly when $\sin\dfrac\theta2\le\dfrac12$, i.e. $\theta\le60^\circ$.

The three points cut the circle into three arcs $\alpha_1,\alpha_2,\alpha_3$ with $\alpha_1+\alpha_2+\alpha_3=360^\circ$, and each side of the triangle is the chord of one of these arcs. Its minor central angle is $\min\left(\alpha_i,360^\circ-\alpha_i\right)$. So every side is at most $r$ exactly when each $\alpha_i$ is at most $60^\circ$ or at least $300^\circ$. They cannot all be at most $60^\circ$ (their sum is $360^\circ$), so one arc is at least $300^\circ$, and then the other two automatically total at most $60^\circ$. Hence:
\[\text{no side exceeds } r\iff\text{the three points lie on some arc of } 60^\circ.\]
For $i=1,2,3$ let $A_i$ be the event that the other two points lie on the $60^\circ$ arc going counterclockwise from $P_i$. Each other point lands there with probability $\dfrac{60}{360}=\dfrac16$, independently, so $P(A_i)=\dfrac1{36}$. The points lie on some $60^\circ$ arc exactly when one of the $A_i$ occurs (take $P_i$ to be the first point of that arc). Two of these events cannot happen together: if $A_i$ and $A_j$ both held, going counterclockwise from $P_i$ to $P_j$ and back to $P_i$ would take at most $60^\circ+60^\circ<360^\circ$. Therefore the probability is
\[3\cdot\dfrac1{36}=\boxed{\dfrac1{12}}.\]

