question:
A tetrahedron $OABC$ is called \emph{trirectangular} if its three face angles at $O$ are right angles: $\angle AOB=\angle BOC=\angle COA=90^\circ$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Let $OA=p$, $OB=q$ and $OC=r$. Prove that the face $ABC$ has area $\dfrac12\sqrt{p^2q^2+q^2r^2+r^2p^2}$, so that
\[[ABC]^2=[OAB]^2+[OBC]^2+[OCA]^2.\]
\item[(b)] Prove that there are infinitely many pairwise non-similar trirectangular tetrahedra all four of whose faces have integer areas.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let $H$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $O$ to $AB$. Computing the area of the right triangle $OAB$ in two ways, $OH\cdot AB=pq$, so $OH=\dfrac{pq}{\sqrt{p^2+q^2}}$.

The line $OC$ is perpendicular to $OA$ and $OB$, hence to the plane $OAB$; so $OC\perp AB$ and $OC\perp OH$. As also $OH\perp AB$, the line $AB$ is perpendicular to the plane $OCH$, so $CH\perp AB$: $CH$ is the altitude of triangle $ABC$ from $C$. In the right triangle $COH$,
\[CH^2=r^2+\dfrac{p^2q^2}{p^2+q^2}.\]
Therefore
\[[ABC]=\dfrac12\,AB\cdot CH=\dfrac12\sqrt{p^2+q^2}\,\sqrt{r^2+\dfrac{p^2q^2}{p^2+q^2}}=\dfrac12\sqrt{r^2\left(p^2+q^2\right)+p^2q^2},\]
which is the stated formula. Squaring, $[ABC]^2=\left(\dfrac{pq}2\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{qr}2\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{rp}2\right)^2=[OAB]^2+[OBC]^2+[OCA]^2$. $\blacksquare$

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),A=(3,0),B=1.5*dir(215),C=(0,2.4);
pair H=(A+B)/2;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(C--H);
draw(O--A,dashed);draw(O--B,dashed);draw(O--C,dashed);draw(O--H,dashed);
dot(O);dot(H);
label("$O$",O,NE);label("$A$",A,E);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,N);label("$H$",H,S);
label("$p$",(O+A)/2,N);label("$q$",(O+B)/2,NW);label("$r$",(O+C)/2,W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} Write $X=[OAB]=\dfrac{pq}2$, $Y=[OBC]=\dfrac{qr}2$, $Z=[OCA]=\dfrac{rp}2$. Conversely, for any positive numbers $X,Y,Z$ the lengths
\[p=\sqrt{\dfrac{2XZ}{Y}},\qquad q=\sqrt{\dfrac{2XY}{Z}},\qquad r=\sqrt{\dfrac{2YZ}{X}}\]
satisfy $pq=2X$, $qr=2Y$, $rp=2Z$, so they give a trirectangular tetrahedron with these three face areas. By (a) its fourth face has area $\sqrt{X^2+Y^2+Z^2}$. So we need positive integers $X,Y,Z$ with $X^2+Y^2+Z^2$ a perfect square. For every positive integer $k$,
\[1^2+(2k)^2+\left(2k^2\right)^2=4k^4+4k^2+1=\left(2k^2+1\right)^2,\]
so $(X,Y,Z)=\left(1,2k,2k^2\right)$ gives a tetrahedron with face areas $1$, $2k$, $2k^2$ and $2k^2+1$, all integers.

These tetrahedra are pairwise non-similar: a similarity multiplies all four face areas by the same factor, so it preserves the ratio of the largest face area to the smallest, which here is $2k^2+1$ and is different for different $k$. Hence there are infinitely many. $\blacksquare$

(For example, $k=1$ gives $p=q=\sqrt2$, $r=2\sqrt2$ with face areas $1,2,2,3$.)

Let the legs be $a\le b$ and the hypotenuse $c$, so that $a^2+b^2=c^2$ and $a+b+c=150$. Substituting $c=150-a-b$,
\[a^2+b^2=(150-a-b)^2=22500-300a-300b+a^2+2ab+b^2,\]
so $ab-150a-150b+11250=0$, i.e.
\[(150-a)(150-b)=22500-11250=11250.\]
Put $u=150-a$ and $v=150-b$. As $a,b>0$, both $u$ and $v$ are less than $150$; since $uv=11250$, each is then greater than $\dfrac{11250}{150}=75$. Conversely, any such factorisation gives $c=150-a-b=u+v-150>0$ and a genuine right triangle.

Now $11250=2\cdot3^2\cdot5^4$, and its divisors strictly between $75$ and $150$ are only $90$ and $125$ (the divisors near this range are $75,90,125,150$). So $\{u,v\}=\{90,125\}$, giving $\{a,b\}=\{60,25\}$ and $c=150-85=65$. Check: $25^2+60^2=625+3600=4225=65^2$.

There is exactly one such triangle, with sides $\boxed{25,\ 60,\ 65}$ (five times the $5$-$12$-$13$ triangle).

