Let $\angle A=\alpha$ and let $s=AD=DE=EB=BC$.

Triangle $ADE$ is isosceles with $DA=DE$, so $\angle DEA=\angle DAE=\alpha$, and $\angle ADE=180^\circ-2\alpha$. Its base is $AE=2s\cos\alpha$, so
\[AB=AE+EB=s\left(2\cos\alpha+1\right).\]
In the isosceles triangle $ABC$ the base is $BC=2\,AB\sin\dfrac\alpha2$, so $s=2s\left(2\cos\alpha+1\right)\sin\dfrac\alpha2$, i.e.
\[2\sin\dfrac\alpha2\left(1+2\cos\alpha\right)=1.\]
Using $\sin3x=\sin x\left(3-4\sin^2x\right)=\sin x\left(1+2\cos2x\right)$ with $x=\dfrac\alpha2$, this says $2\sin\dfrac{3\alpha}2=1$, so $\dfrac{3\alpha}{2}=30^\circ$ or $150^\circ$, i.e. $\alpha=20^\circ$ or $\alpha=100^\circ$. The angle $\alpha=100^\circ$ is impossible, because triangle $ADE$ has two angles equal to $\alpha$. So $\alpha=20^\circ$ and
\[\angle ADE=180^\circ-40^\circ=\boxed{140^\circ}.\]
(Conversely, for $\alpha=20^\circ$ the points $D$ and $E$ with $AD=EB=BC$ do lie on the sides, since $AB=s(2\cos20^\circ+1)>s$, and then $DE=s$ by the same computation, so the configuration exists.)

