Write $x=\sin\theta$ with $0^\circ<\theta<45^\circ$. Then $\cos\theta>0$ and $\cos2\theta>0$, so
\[f(x)=2\sin\theta\cos\theta=\sin2\theta,\qquad f(f(x))=2\sin2\theta\cos2\theta=\sin4\theta.\]
Hence $a:b:c=\sin\theta:\sin2\theta:\sin4\theta$.

\emph{Lemma.} If in a triangle $B=2A$ (angles opposite $b$ and $a$), then $b^2=a(a+c)$. Indeed, by the law of sines $\dfrac ba=\dfrac{\sin2A}{\sin A}=2\cos A$ and, as $C=180^\circ-3A$,
\[\dfrac{a+c}{a}=1+\dfrac{\sin3A}{\sin A}=1+\left(3-4\sin^2A\right)=4\cos^2A=\left(\dfrac ba\right)^2.\]
Applying the lemma with $a=\sin\theta$, $b=\sin2\theta$, $c=\sin4\theta$ (scaling does not matter):
\[\sin^22\theta=\sin^2\theta+\sin\theta\sin4\theta.\]
Since $\sin^22\theta-\sin^2\theta=\sin3\theta\sin\theta$ (because $\sin^2u-\sin^2v=\sin(u+v)\sin(u-v)$), dividing by $\sin\theta>0$ gives $\sin3\theta=\sin4\theta$. With $0^\circ<3\theta<4\theta<180^\circ$, this forces $3\theta+4\theta=180^\circ$, so $\theta=\dfrac{180^\circ}{7}$.

Then $\theta+2\theta+4\theta=180^\circ$, so there is a triangle with angles $\theta,2\theta,4\theta$; by the law of sines its sides are proportional to $\sin\theta,\sin2\theta,\sin4\theta$, which are our sides. By SSS similarity our triangle has angles $\theta$, $2\theta$, $4\theta$ opposite $a$, $b$, $c$. The smallest angle is
\[\boxed{\dfrac{180^\circ}{7}=\dfrac\pi7}.\]
The angles $\dfrac{180^\circ}7,\dfrac{360^\circ}7,\dfrac{720^\circ}7$ form a geometric sequence with ratio $2$, and the largest, $\dfrac{720^\circ}{7}\approx102.9^\circ$, is obtuse. $\blacksquare$

