Call the expression $F$. Each side is less than $\dfrac12$, since for example $a<b+c=1-a$.

\textbf{Step 1.} For $0<t<\dfrac12$, $\dfrac{t^3}{1+t^3}<\dfrac49t^2$. Indeed, this is equivalent to $9t<4\left(1+t^3\right)$, i.e. $4t^3-9t+4>0$, and
\[4t^3-9t+4=(2t-1)\left(2t^2+t-4\right),\]
where both factors are negative for $0<t<\dfrac12$.

\textbf{Step 2.} Hence $\dfrac{a}{1+b^3}=a-\dfrac{ab^3}{1+b^3}>a-\dfrac49ab^2$, and similarly for the other two terms. Adding,
\[F>(a+b+c)-\dfrac49\left(ab^2+bc^2+ca^2\right)=1-\dfrac49\left(ab^2+bc^2+ca^2\right).\]
\textbf{Step 3.} $ab^2+bc^2+ca^2\le\dfrac18$. Write $a=y+z$, $b=z+x$, $c=x+y$, where $x,y,z>0$ are the tangent lengths from $A$, $B$, $C$ to the incircle (see the sketch); then $x+y+z=\dfrac12$. Expanding shows the identity
\[\dfrac{(a+b+c)^3}{8}-\left(ab^2+bc^2+ca^2\right)=x^2z+xy^2+yz^2>0,\]
and the left side is $\dfrac18-\left(ab^2+bc^2+ca^2\right)$.

Combining, $F>1-\dfrac49\cdot\dfrac18=\dfrac{17}{18}$ for every triangle of perimeter $1$. So $n=\dfrac{17}{18}$ works.

\textbf{No larger $n$ works.} Take $a=b=\dfrac{1-\varepsilon}{2}$ and $c=\varepsilon$ with small $\varepsilon>0$ (a valid triangle). As $\varepsilon\to0$,
\[F\to\dfrac{\dfrac12}{\dfrac18+1}+\dfrac{\dfrac12}{1}+0=\dfrac49+\dfrac12=\dfrac{17}{18},\]
so $F$ takes values arbitrarily close to $\dfrac{17}{18}$. The largest $n$ is $\boxed{\dfrac{17}{18}}$.

