Let the triangle have angles $A$, $B$, $C$. By the extended law of sines with circumradius $1$, its sides are $2\sin A$, $2\sin B$, $2\sin C$, so the perimeter is
\[P=2\left(\sin A+\sin B+\sin C\right).\]
\textbf{Upper bound.} For $0<u,v<\pi$,
\[\sin u+\sin v=2\sin\dfrac{u+v}2\cos\dfrac{u-v}2\le2\sin\dfrac{u+v}2,\]
with equality when $u=v$. Apply this three times, using the extra angle $60^\circ$:
\[\sin A+\sin B+\sin C+\sin60^\circ\le2\sin\dfrac{A+B}2+2\sin\dfrac{C+60^\circ}2\le4\sin\dfrac{A+B+C+60^\circ}{4}=4\sin60^\circ.\]
Hence $\sin A+\sin B+\sin C\le3\sin60^\circ=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}2$ and $P\le3\sqrt3$, with equality for the equilateral triangle ($A=B=C=60^\circ$).

\textbf{All values in between occur.} Put the vertices at angles $0$, $t$, $2t$ on the circle, with $0<t\le\dfrac{2\pi}{3}$. The sides are chords of central angles $t,t,2t$, so the perimeter is
\[g(t)=4\sin\dfrac t2+2\sin t.\]
This is continuous, tends to $0$ as $t\to0$, and $g\left(\dfrac{2\pi}3\right)=3\sqrt3$. By the intermediate value theorem every value in $\left(0,3\sqrt3\right]$ is a perimeter. A triangle has positive perimeter, so $0$ is not attained.

The set of possible perimeters is $\boxed{\left(0,\,3\sqrt3\,\right]}$.

