Since $2x^2+5x+2=(2x+1)(x+2)$, the inequality is defined for $x\ne-\dfrac12,-2,-1$. We must not multiply by the denominators, whose signs vary; instead bring everything to one side:
\[\dfrac{2x}{(2x+1)(x+2)}-\dfrac1{x+1}=\dfrac{2x(x+1)-(2x+1)(x+2)}{(2x+1)(x+2)(x+1)}=\dfrac{-(3x+2)}{(2x+1)(x+2)(x+1)}>0.\]
So we need
\[\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+2)(x+1)(2x+1)}<0.\]
The critical points are $-2<-1<-\dfrac23<-\dfrac12$. The sign of the left side on each interval:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|c|c|c|c|c}
& $x<-2$ & $-2<x<-1$ & $-1<x<-\dfrac23$ & $-\dfrac23<x<-\dfrac12$ & $x>-\dfrac12$\\ \hline
sign & $+$ & $-$ & $+$ & $-$ & $+$
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
(For $x>-\dfrac12$ all four factors are positive, and each critical point is a simple zero, so the sign alternates.) At $x=-\dfrac23$ the expression is $0$, which is not allowed. The solution set is
\[\boxed{-2<x<-1\quad\text{or}\quad-\dfrac23<x<-\dfrac12}.\]

From $x^2<25$ we get $-4\le x\le4$, and likewise $-4\le y\le4$. For each $x$, the second condition says $y\ge2-x$, and the first says $y^2<25-x^2$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $x=-4$ or $x=-3$: we would need $y\ge5$ or $y\ge6$, impossible since $|y|\le4$ (for $x=-3$, in fact $|y|\le3$).
\item $x=-2$: $y^2<21$, so $|y|\le4$, and $y\ge4$: $y=4$.
\item $x=-1$: $|y|\le4$ and $y\ge3$: $y=3,4$.
\item $x=0$: $|y|\le4$ and $y\ge2$: $y=2,3,4$.
\item $x=1$: $|y|\le4$ and $y\ge1$: $y=1,2,3,4$.
\item $x=2$: $|y|\le4$ and $y\ge0$: $y=0,1,2,3,4$.
\item $x=3$: $y^2<16$, so $|y|\le3$, and $y\ge-1$: $y=-1,0,1,2,3$.
\item $x=4$: $y^2<9$, so $|y|\le2$, and $y\ge-2$: $y=-2,-1,0,1,2$.
\end{itemize}
So the solutions are
\[(-2,4),\quad(-1,3),\ (-1,4),\quad(0,2),\ (0,3),\ (0,4),\]
\[(1,y)\ (1\le y\le4),\quad(2,y)\ (0\le y\le4),\quad(3,y)\ (-1\le y\le3),\quad(4,y)\ (-2\le y\le2),\]
which is $1+2+3+4+5+5+5=\boxed{25}$ pairs.

