\end{enumerate}

solution:
Two triangles are \emph{defined} to be congruent, $\triangle ABC\cong\triangle DEF$, when there is a correspondence $A\leftrightarrow D$, $B\leftrightarrow E$, $C\leftrightarrow F$ under which all three pairs of corresponding sides and all three pairs of corresponding angles are congruent. So once two triangles are known to be congruent (for example by SSS or SAS, which are postulates or theorems), the congruence of every pair of corresponding parts is simply what ``congruent'' means. The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(A) } \text{Definition}}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(3,0),C=(1,2);
pair sh=(4.8,0);pair A2=A+sh,B2=B+sh,C2=C+sh;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(A2--B2--C2--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);
label("$D$",A2,SW);label("$E$",B2,SE);label("$F$",C2,N);
void tk(pair P,pair Q,int k){pair M=(P+Q)/2,u=unit(Q-P),v=rotate(90)*u*0.12;for(int i=0;i<k;++i){pair c=M+(i-(k-1)/2)*0.1*u;draw(c-v--c+v);}}
tk(A,B,1);tk(A2,B2,1);tk(B,C,2);tk(B2,C2,2);tk(C,A,3);tk(C2,A2,3);
draw(arc(A,0.35,0,degrees(C-A)));draw(arc(A2,0.35,0,degrees(C2-A2)));
\end{asy}

(The marks show the six pairs of corresponding parts: matching ticks for sides, and one pair of corresponding angles marked; the other two angle pairs correspond in the same way.)

Let $P=(x,0)$, $A=(0,4)$ and $B=(5,8)$. By the distance formula the expression is $PA+PB$. The reflection of $A$ in the $x$-axis is $A'=(0,-4)$, and $PA'=PA$ for every $P$ on the axis. By the triangle inequality,
\[PA+PB=PA'+PB\ge A'B=\sqrt{5^2+12^2}=13,\]
with equality exactly when $P$ lies on the segment $A'B$. That segment crosses the $x$-axis where $y=-4+\dfrac{12}{5}x=0$, i.e. at $x=\dfrac53$. Check: $\sqrt{16+\dfrac{25}9}+\sqrt{64+\dfrac{100}9}=\dfrac{13}3+\dfrac{26}3=13$. The minimum is $\boxed{13}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,4),A2=(0,-4),B=(5,8),P=(5/3,0);
draw((-1.5,0)--(8,0),Arrow(4));draw((0,-5)--(0,9),Arrow(4));
draw(A--P--B,linewidth(0.8));draw(A2--P,dashed);
dot(A);dot(A2);dot(B);dot(P);
label("$A(0,4)$",A,W);label("$A'(0,-4)$",A2,W);label("$B(5,8)$",B,E);label("$P\left(\dfrac53,0\right)$",P,SE);
label("$x$",(8,0),S);label("$y$",(0,9),W);
\end{asy}

(The same idea handles the general type $\sqrt{a^2+u^2}+\sqrt{b^2+v^2}$: it is the length of a broken line, and is at least $\sqrt{(a+b)^2+(u+v)^2}$.)

question:
Prove that $f(n)=1-n$ is the only function $f$ from the integers to the integers that satisfies all of the following:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $f(f(n))=n$ for all integers $n$;
\item[(ii)] $f(f(n+2)+2)=n$ for all integers $n$;
\item[(iii)] $f(0)=1$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Measure times in minutes after 12:00: the dog arrives at $s$ and the person at $m$, with $(s,m)$ uniform in the square $[0,60]\times[0,60]$. They overlap exactly when $|s-m|\le20$. The complementary region consists of two right triangles with legs $40$, of total area $40^2=1600$, out of $3600$. So on one day
\[P(\text{play})=1-\dfrac{1600}{3600}=\dfrac59.\]
The dog fails to play on both days with probability $\left(\dfrac49\right)^2=\dfrac{16}{81}$, so the answer is $1-\dfrac{16}{81}=\boxed{\dfrac{65}{81}}$.

\begin{asy}
