Let $G$ be the centre of the base and $M$ the midpoint of $BC$. The base circumradius is $AG=\dfrac{\sqrt6}{\sqrt3}=\sqrt2$, so the height is $SG=\sqrt{6-2}=2$.

By symmetry the centre $O$ of the ball lies on the axis $SG$. The ball touches the base, so $O$ is at height $r$ above $G$ and touches the base at $G$; then $SO=2-r$. Let $T$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $O$ to the edge $SA$; the ball touches $SA$ exactly when $OT=r$. In the right triangle $SGA$, $\sin\angle GSA=\dfrac{AG}{SA}=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{\sqrt6}=\dfrac1{\sqrt3}$, so
\[OT=SO\sin\angle GSA=\dfrac{2-r}{\sqrt3}.\]
Setting $OT=r$: $2-r=\sqrt3\,r$, so
\[r=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3+1}=\boxed{\sqrt3-1\ \text{cm}}\approx0.73\text{ cm}.\]
The point $T$ lies on the edge itself, since $ST=SO\cos\angle GSA=(3-\sqrt3)\sqrt{\dfrac23}\approx1.04<\sqrt6$; by symmetry the ball touches $SB$ and $SC$ in the same way.

