The centres of the four lower balls are at height $r$ and form a square of side $2r$ (neighbouring balls touch), whose diagonal is $2\sqrt2\,r$. By symmetry the centre of the top ball is directly above the centre of the square, at horizontal distance $\sqrt2\,r$ from each lower centre. It touches each lower ball, so its distance to each lower centre is $2r$, and its height above the lower centres is
\[\sqrt{(2r)^2-\left(\sqrt2\,r\right)^2}=\sqrt2\,r.\]
So the top centre is at height $r+\sqrt2\,r$, and the farthest point of the top ball is at height
\[\boxed{\left(2+\sqrt2\right)r}.\]

