question:
A $12$-hour analogue clock shows $h$ hours and $m$ minutes, where $0\le h\le11$ (twelve o'clock counts as $h=0$) and $0\le m<60$. Let
\[\theta=\left|30h-5.5m\right|\quad\text{(in degrees)}.\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $\theta$ is one of the two angles formed by the hour hand and the minute hand. Deduce that the angle between the hands (the one that is at most $180^\circ$) is $\theta$ if $\theta\le180^\circ$ and $360^\circ-\theta$ otherwise.
\item[(b)] Find the angle between the hands at $9{:}40$ and at $2{:}50$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The displayed numbers strictly decrease. For $n\ge100$, let $p(n)$ be the probability that $99$ appears at some point when the display starts at $n$. We prove by strong induction that $p(n)=\dfrac{1}{100}$ for every $n\ge100$.

From $n$, the next number $j$ is uniform on $\{0,1,\dots,n-1\}$:
\begin{itemize}
\item if $j<99$, then $99$ never appears;
\item if $j=99$, it appears;
\item if $99<j<n$, it appears with probability $p(j)$.
\end{itemize}
Hence
\[p(n)=\dfrac1n\left(1+\sum_{j=100}^{n-1}p(j)\right).\]
For $n=100$ the sum is empty and $p(100)=\dfrac1{100}$. If $p(j)=\dfrac1{100}$ for $100\le j<n$, then
\[p(n)=\dfrac1n\left(1+\dfrac{n-100}{100}\right)=\dfrac1n\cdot\dfrac{n}{100}=\dfrac{1}{100}.\]
So $p(200)=\boxed{\dfrac{1}{100}}$.

(Equivalently: the first displayed number below $100$ is uniformly distributed on $\{0,1,\dots,99\}$, and $99$ appears exactly when that number is $99$.)

