solution:
No. Place the cylinder as $K=\{(x,y,z):x^2+y^2\le r^2,\ 0\le z\le h\}$, with the $z$-axis as its axis, and let $\Pi$ be the cutting plane. Suppose the cross-section $Q=\Pi\cap K$ is a polygon; it is convex, being an intersection of convex sets. Its sides lie on the boundary of $K$, which consists of the curved surface $x^2+y^2=r^2$ and the two discs $z=0$ and $z=h$.

\textbf{Case 1: $\Pi$ is parallel to the axis.} Then $\Pi$ has an equation $ax+by=c$, so a point lies in $Q$ exactly when its $(x,y)$ lies on the chord $\{ax+by=c\}$ of the disc $x^2+y^2\le r^2$ and $0\le z\le h$. So $Q$ is a rectangle (or a segment, or empty). A rectangle has two pairs of parallel sides, so it is not a trapezoid with exactly one pair.

\textbf{Case 2: $\Pi$ is not parallel to the axis.} Then $\Pi$ contains no vertical segment.

Let $s$ be a side of $Q$, with points $X+tv$ ($0\le t\le1$). If $s$ does not lie in one of the planes $z=0$, $z=h$, then it meets each of them in at most one point, so all other points of $s$ lie on the curved surface. Then the polynomial
\[\left(X_x+tv_x\right)^2+\left(X_y+tv_y\right)^2-r^2\]
of degree at most $2$ in $t$ has infinitely many zeros, so it is identically zero. Its $t^2$-coefficient $v_x^2+v_y^2$ is then $0$, so $v$ is vertical. This contradicts that $\Pi$ contains no vertical segment.

Hence every side of $Q$ lies in the plane $z=0$ or in the plane $z=h$. If $\Pi$ is one of these two planes, then $Q$ is a disc, not a polygon. Otherwise $\Pi$ meets each of them in at most one line, and a convex polygon has at most one side on any line. So $Q$ has at most two sides, which is impossible for a polygon.

So the only polygonal cross-sections are rectangles, and no cross-section is a trapezoid with exactly one pair of parallel sides. $\blacksquare$

