The altitude from $A$ is perpendicular to $BC$, and the altitude from $B$ is perpendicular to $CA$. Rotating both lines through $90^\circ$ turns them into lines parallel to $BC$ and $CA$. So the angles between the two altitude lines are the same as the angles between the lines $BC$ and $CA$, namely $\angle C$ and $180^\circ-\angle C$. The acute one is $80^\circ$, so $\angle C=80^\circ$ or $\angle C=100^\circ$.

Both values occur.
\begin{itemize}
\item $\angle C=80^\circ$: in the acute triangle with angles $60^\circ,40^\circ,80^\circ$ at $A,B,C$, the altitudes $AD$ and $BE$ meet at the orthocentre $H$ inside the triangle. From quadrilateral $CDHE$, $\angle DHE=360^\circ-90^\circ-90^\circ-80^\circ=100^\circ$, so the acute angle between the altitudes, $\angle BHD=\angle AHE$, is $80^\circ$.
\item $\angle C=100^\circ$: in the triangle with angles $45^\circ,35^\circ,100^\circ$, the feet $D$ and $E$ lie on the extensions of $BC$ and $AC$ beyond $C$, and the altitude lines meet at $H$ beyond $C$. In quadrilateral $HDCE$ the angles at $D$ and $E$ are right and the angle at $C$ is $\angle DCE=\angle ACB=100^\circ$ (vertical angles), so $\angle AHB=80^\circ$.
\end{itemize}

