question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] For events $A$ and $B$ with $P(B)>0$, the \emph{conditional probability} of $A$ given $B$ is defined by $P(A\mid B)=\dfrac{P(A\cap B)}{P(B)}$. Explain why, when all outcomes are equally likely, $P(A\mid B)$ is the fraction of the outcomes in $B$ that are also in $A$.
\item[(b)] Two fair dice are rolled. Let $A$ be the event that at least one die shows a $6$, and $B$ the event that the sum is $8$. Find $P(A)$, $P(A\mid B)$ and $P(B\mid A)$. Are $A$ and $B$ independent?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Count the possible intersection points pair by pair:
\begin{itemize}
\item the two circles meet in at most $2$ points;
\item each of the $3\cdot2=6$ line--circle pairs meets in at most $2$ points, at most $12$ in all;
\item each of the $\dbinom32=3$ pairs of lines meets in at most $1$ point, at most $3$ in all.
\end{itemize}
So there are at most $2+12+3=17$ such points.

All $17$ can be distinct. For example, take the circles of radius $1.5$ centred at $(\pm0.8,0)$ and the lines
\[y=-0.9x-0.36,\qquad y=0.9x-0.55,\qquad y=-0.075x+0.63.\]
Each line passes within distance $1.5$ of both centres, so it cuts each circle twice; no two lines are parallel; and all the intersection points are different, as the sketch shows.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O1=(-0.8,0), O2=(0.8,0); real r=1.5;
path c1=circle(O1,r), c2=circle(O2,r);
real[] m={-0.9,0.9,-0.075}; real[] q={-0.36,-0.55,0.63};
path[] L;
for(int i=0;i<3;++i){ real x0=-2.75,x1=2.75; L.push((x0,m[i]*x0+q[i])--(x1,m[i]*x1+q[i])); }
draw(c1,linewidth(0.8)); draw(c2,linewidth(0.8));
for(int i=0;i<3;++i) draw(L[i],blue);
pair[] P;
P.append(intersectionpoints(c1,c2));
for(int i=0;i<3;++i){ P.append(intersectionpoints(L[i],c1)); P.append(intersectionpoints(L[i],c2)); }
for(int i=0;i<3;++i) for(int j=i+1;j<3;++j) P.append(intersectionpoints(L[i],L[j]));
for(int k=0;k<P.length;++k) dot(P[k],red);
label("$\ell_1$",(-2.75,-0.9*(-2.75)-0.36),W,blue);
label("$\ell_2$",(-2.75,0.9*(-2.75)-0.55),W,blue);
label("$\ell_3$",(2.75,-0.075*2.75+0.63),E,blue);
\end{asy}

The maximum is $\boxed{17}$.

Let $c_1,\dots,c_5$ be the carries from the units, tens, hundreds, thousands and ten-thousands columns. The columns give
\begin{align*}
E+E&=R+10c_1, & V+R+c_1&=E+10c_2, & L+O+c_2&=V+10c_3,\\
O+M+c_3&=E+10c_4, & S+c_4&=L+10c_5, & c_5&=C.
\end{align*}
Since $SOLVE+MORE<100000+10000$, $C=1=c_5$. Then $S+c_4=L+10\ge10$ forces $S=9$, $c_4=1$, $L=0$.

The hundreds column becomes $O+c_2=V+10c_3$. As $O\le8$, $O+c_2\le9$, so $c_3=0$ and $V=O+c_2$; since $V\ne O$, $c_2=1$ and $V=O+1$. The thousands column gives $O+M=E+10$, and the tens column gives $O+1+R+c_1=E+10$. Subtracting, $M=R+1+c_1$. Finally $2E=R+10c_1$. The remaining letters $O,V,E,M,R$ take distinct values in $\{2,\dots,8\}$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $c_1=0$: $R=2E$ and $M=2E+1$, so $E\in\{2,3\}$ (as $M\le8$), and $O=E+10-M=9-E$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $E=2$: $R=4$, $M=5$, $O=7$, $V=8$: all distinct.
\item $E=3$: $R=6$, $M=7$, $O=6=R$: impossible.
\end{itemize}
\item $c_1=1$: $R=2E-10\ge2$, so $E\ge6$, and $M=R+2=2E-8$. Then $O=E+10-M=18-E\ge10$ for $E\le8$: impossible.
\end{itemize}
So the only solution is
\[\boxed{97082+5742=102824}.\]

Since $6^2+8^2=10^2$, each end is a right triangle with the right angle at the bottom and the hypotenuse of length $10$ on top. Its area is $\dfrac12\cdot6\cdot8=24$, and its height from the right angle to the hypotenuse is $\dfrac{2\cdot24}{10}=4.8$ inches.

In $10$ hours, $0.06\cdot10=0.6$ inches of rain fall on the $10\times100$ opening, a volume of $0.6\cdot1000=600$ cubic inches. The water forms a prism of length $100$, so its triangular cross-section has area $\dfrac{600}{100}=6$ square inches.

The water surface is horizontal, parallel to the hypotenuse, so the water's cross-section is a triangle similar to the end triangle. The ratio of areas is $\dfrac{6}{24}=\dfrac14$, so the ratio of lengths is $\dfrac12$, and the water height is
\[h=\dfrac12\cdot4.8=\boxed{2.4\text{ inches}}.\]

