question:
A student argues: ``The disc of radius $2$ contains infinitely many points. The disc of radius $1$ with the same centre contains infinitely many points, and the ring between the two circles also contains infinitely many points. Therefore $\infty-\infty=\infty$.''
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain what is wrong with this conclusion.
\item[(b)] Let $D$ be the closed disc of radius $2$ centred at the origin. Give infinite subsets $X\subseteq D$ for which the set $D\setminus X$ of remaining points is (i) infinite, (ii) a set of exactly $5$ points, (iii) empty.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(b)}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $X$ = the disc of radius $1$: the remaining ring is infinite (this is the student's example).
\item[(ii)] Choose $5$ points $P_1,\dots,P_5$ of $D$ and let $X=D\setminus\{P_1,\dots,P_5\}$. It is infinite, and exactly $5$ points remain.
\item[(iii)] $X=D$: nothing remains.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{(a)} In all three cases an infinite set is removed from an infinite set, yet what remains is infinite, finite with $5$ points, or empty. (Using any number $k$ instead of $5$, any finite size can occur.) So ``infinity minus infinity'' has no well-defined value: the result depends on \emph{which} infinite set is removed, not only on the fact that both are infinite. The student's argument correctly shows that removing the small disc from the large disc leaves infinitely many points, but it says nothing about a number $\infty-\infty$. This is why $\infty-\infty$ is left undefined (an ``indeterminate form''), while statements about specific sets are fine.

Let $O_1,O_2,O$ be the centres, $r_1=961$, $r_2=625$, $r$ the radius of $\omega$, and $d=O_1O_2$. Put $O_1=(0,0)$ and $O_2=(d,0)$.

\textbf{Line $AB$.} A point $X=(x,y)$ lies on both circles only if $XO_1^2-r_1^2=XO_2^2-r_2^2$ (both sides are $0$). Since $XO_1^2-XO_2^2=x^2-(x-d)^2=2dx-d^2$, the points $A$ and $B$ satisfy $2dx-d^2=r_1^2-r_2^2$. So line $AB$ is the vertical line $x=x_0$ with $2dx_0-d^2=r_1^2-r_2^2$. (It is the radical axis of the two circles.)

\textbf{Distance from $O$ to $AB$.} External tangency gives $OO_1=r+r_1$ and $OO_2=r+r_2$. If $O=(x_O,y_O)$, then
\[2dx_O-d^2=OO_1^2-OO_2^2=(r+r_1)^2-(r+r_2)^2=(r_1-r_2)(2r+r_1+r_2).\]
Subtracting $2dx_0-d^2=r_1^2-r_2^2=(r_1-r_2)(r_1+r_2)$ gives
\[2d\left(x_O-x_0\right)=2r\left(r_1-r_2\right),\qquad\text{so the distance from }O\text{ to }AB\text{ is }\dfrac{r\left(r_1-r_2\right)}{d}.\]

\textbf{The chord $PQ$.} The minor arc $PQ$ is $120^\circ$, so $\angle POQ=120^\circ$ and the distance from $O$ to the chord $PQ$ is $r\cos60^\circ=\dfrac r2$. Therefore
\[\dfrac{r\left(r_1-r_2\right)}{d}=\dfrac r2,\qquad d=2\left(r_1-r_2\right)=2\cdot336=\boxed{672}.\]

