question:
Let $a\ne0$ be a real number and $f(x)=a\ln x+\sqrt{1+x}$ for $x>0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(I)] When $a=-\dfrac34$, find the intervals on which $f$ is increasing and decreasing.
\item[(II)] Suppose that $f(x)\le\dfrac{\sqrt x}{2a}$ for every $x\in\left[\dfrac{1}{e^2},+\infty\right)$. Find the range of values of $a$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
By the sum-to-product formula,
\[\sin3x+\sin x=2\sin\dfrac{3x+x}{2}\cos\dfrac{3x-x}{2}=2\sin2x\cos x.\]
So we need $\sin2x=0$ or $\cos x=0$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $\sin2x=0$ means $x=\dfrac{k\pi}{2}$; in the interval this gives $x=-\dfrac\pi2,0,\dfrac\pi2$.
\item $\cos x=0$ gives $x=\pm\dfrac\pi2$ in the interval.
\end{itemize}
The solutions are $\boxed{x=-\dfrac{\pi}{2},\ 0,\ \dfrac{\pi}{2}}$.

