In spherical geometry on a sphere with centre $O$, the ``lines'' are the great circles (the circles on the sphere whose centre is $O$), and the angle between two such lines at a point where they cross is defined to be the angle between the planes of the two great circles. Let $N$ be the north pole and let $A$ and $B$ be points on the equator.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the angle at $N$ between the great circles through $N$ and $A$ and through $N$ and $B$ equals $\angle AOB$, and that it is also the angle between the tangent lines to these two circles at $N$. (So the definition gives the angle you would see between the two lines when standing at $N$.)
\item[(b)] Suppose $\angle AOB=90^\circ$. Find the three angles of the spherical triangle $NAB$, whose sides are the arcs $NA$, $NB$ and $AB$ of these great circles and of the equator, and find their sum.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Recall that the angle between two planes that meet along a line $\ell$ is the angle between two lines, one in each plane, that are perpendicular to $\ell$ at the same point.

\textbf{(a)} The planes of the great circles $NA$ and $NB$ are the planes $NOA$ and $NOB$; they meet along the line $ON$. The equatorial plane is perpendicular to the axis $ON$, so $OA\perp ON$ and $OB\perp ON$. Thus $OA$ and $OB$ are lines in the two planes perpendicular to the common line $ON$ at $O$, and the angle between the planes is $\angle AOB$.

The tangent line to the great circle $NA$ at $N$ lies in the plane $NOA$ and is perpendicular to the radius $ON$. In that plane, $OA$ is also perpendicular to $ON$, so the tangent line is parallel to $OA$. Likewise the tangent to the circle $NB$ at $N$ is parallel to $OB$. Hence the angle between the two tangent lines is also $\angle AOB$.

\textbf{(b)} \emph{At $N$:} by (a), the angle is $\angle AOB=90^\circ$.

\emph{At $A$:} the two sides through $A$ lie in the plane $NOA$ and in the equatorial plane, which meet along $OA$. In the plane $NOA$, $ON\perp OA$; in the equatorial plane, $OB\perp OA$ because $\angle AOB=90^\circ$. So the angle between the planes is $\angle NOB=90^\circ$.

\emph{At $B$:} in the same way (using $ON\perp OB$ and $OA\perp OB$) the angle is $\angle NOA=90^\circ$.

