Measure time $t$ in hours from the moment our Houston-bound bus leaves Dallas, and measure position as the fraction of the trip from Dallas ($0$ at Dallas, $1$ at Houston). Our bus is at position $\dfrac t5$ for $0\le t\le5$.

A Dallas-bound bus leaving Houston at time $s$, where $s$ is a half hour ($s=k+\dfrac12$ for an integer $k$), is at position $1-\dfrac{t-s}{5}$ for $s\le t\le s+5$. The two positions are equal when
\[\dfrac t5=1-\dfrac{t-s}{5}\iff t=\dfrac{5+s}{2}.\]
This meeting happens on the highway exactly when both buses are strictly between the cities at that moment, i.e. $0<t<5$ and $0<t-s<5$. The first condition gives $-5<s<5$, and then $t-s=\dfrac{5-s}{2}$ also lies strictly between $0$ and $5$.

The half-hour departure times with $-5<s<5$ are $-4.5,-3.5,\dots,3.5,4.5$, which is $10$ buses. In the time-position graph below (our bus in red), these are the $10$ marked crossings; the buses leaving at $s=-5.5$ and $s=5.5$ are in a station when our bus is on the road.

