question:
In mathematical logic, the statement ``$P$ implies $Q$'' (written $P\Rightarrow Q$) is false only when $P$ is true and $Q$ is false; in every other case it is true. The statements $P$ and $Q$ need not be related in meaning.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Decide whether each statement is true or false:
(i) ``$1+1=2$'' $\Rightarrow$ ``the word \emph{apple} begins with the letter a'';
(ii) ``$1+1=3$'' $\Rightarrow$ ``the word \emph{apple} begins with the letter b'';
(iii) ``$1+1=2$'' $\Rightarrow$ ``the word \emph{apple} begins with the letter b''.
\item[(b)] Prove that if $Q$ is true, then $P\Rightarrow Q$ is true for every statement $P$. In particular, does a true statement imply every other true statement?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)}
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] Both parts are true, so it is not the case ``$P$ true and $Q$ false''. The implication is \textbf{true}.
\item[(ii)] The hypothesis $1+1=3$ is false, so the implication is \textbf{true} (a false hypothesis implies anything).
\item[(iii)] The hypothesis is true and the conclusion is false, so the implication is \textbf{false}.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{(b)} The only way for $P\Rightarrow Q$ to be false is that $Q$ is false. If $Q$ is true, this cannot happen, whatever $P$ is, so $P\Rightarrow Q$ is true. In particular, if $P$ and $Q$ are both true, then $P\Rightarrow Q$ is true: \textbf{yes}, in this logical sense every true statement implies every true statement, as in (i).

\[\boxed{\text{(i) true,\quad (ii) true,\quad (iii) false}}\]

This is a statement about truth values only. It does not mean that $Q$ can be \emph{proved from} $P$ or that $P$ is a reason for $Q$; for that, an argument connecting them is needed.

Let $H$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $A$ to the line $BC$. Since $ABC$ is a triangle, $A$ is not on the line $BC$, so $AH>0$.

\textbf{Two basic inequalities.} If $H\ne B$, then triangle $AHB$ has a right angle at $H$, and by the Pythagorean theorem $AB^2=AH^2+HB^2>HB^2$, so $AB>HB$. If $H=B$, then $AB>0=HB$. Either way $AB>HB$, and in the same way $AC>HC$.

\textbf{Case 1: $H$ lies on the segment $BC$.} Then $BC=BH+HC<AB+AC$.

\textbf{Case 2: $H$ lies outside the segment $BC$.} Say $C$ lies between $B$ and $H$ (the case where $B$ lies between $C$ and $H$ is the same with $B$ and $C$ swapped). Then
\[BC<BH<AB<AB+AC.\]

