A small cube has icing on one face for each iced face of the cake that it touches. A cube touching two iced faces of the cake lies along an edge where two iced faces meet. The edges of the cake between two iced faces are the $4$ edges of the top face and the $4$ vertical edges; the $4$ bottom edges meet the un-iced bottom.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Top edges.} Each has $4$ cubes. The $2$ end cubes are top corners, touching the top and two sides ($3$ iced faces); the $2$ middle ones touch exactly the top and one side. This gives $4\cdot2=8$ cubes.
\item \textbf{Vertical edges.} Each has $4$ cubes. The top one is a top corner ($3$ iced faces). The other $3$ touch exactly two side faces (the bottom one also touches the bottom, which has no icing). This gives $4\cdot3=12$ cubes.
\end{itemize}
No other cube touches two iced faces, so the answer is $8+12=\boxed{20}$.

