question:
There are $30$ people in a room. Assume each person's birthday is equally likely to be any of $365$ days (ignore February $29$), independently of the others.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the probability that at least two of them share a birthday, to three decimal places.
\item[(b)] A student reasons: ``Two given people share a birthday with probability $\dfrac{1}{365}$. There are $\dbinom{30}{2}=435$ pairs, so the probability that no pair shares a birthday is $\left(\dfrac{364}{365}\right)^{435}$, and the answer is $1-\left(\dfrac{364}{365}\right)^{435}\approx0.697$.'' Explain the error.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Here $\angle BAC=180^\circ-40^\circ-80^\circ=60^\circ$ and $\angle ABD=\angle DBC=20^\circ$.

\textbf{Where $E$ lies.} In triangle $ABD$, $\angle ADB=180^\circ-60^\circ-20^\circ=100^\circ>60^\circ=\angle BAD$, so $AB>BD$ (the larger side is opposite the larger angle). Hence $E$, with $BE=BA$, lies on the ray $BD$ beyond $D$.

\textbf{$A$, $B$, $C$, $E$ are concyclic.} Triangle $ABE$ is isosceles with $BA=BE$ and apex angle $20^\circ$, so $\angle BEA=\angle BAE=80^\circ$. Thus $\angle AEB=80^\circ=\angle ACB$. The segments $AC$ and $BE$ cross at $D$ (as $D$ lies between $B$ and $E$), so $ABCE$ is a convex quadrilateral with its vertices in this order; in particular $E$ and $C$ lie on the same side of $AB$, and $B$ and $C$ lie on the same side of $AE$. So $E$ and $C$ see the segment $AB$ under equal angles from the same side, and $A,B,C,E$ lie on one circle.

\textbf{$CE=AE$.} Since $\angle BAE=80^\circ>60^\circ=\angle BAC$, the ray $AC$ lies between $AB$ and $AE$, and $\angle CAE=80^\circ-60^\circ=20^\circ$. On the circle, the inscribed angles $\angle ACE$ and $\angle ABE$ stand on the same arc $AE$ (as $B$ and $C$ lie on the same side of $AE$), so $\angle ACE=\angle ABE=20^\circ$. Triangle $ACE$ has equal angles at $A$ and $C$, so $CE=AE$.

\textbf{$AD=AE$.} In triangle $ADE$: $\angle DAE=\angle CAE=20^\circ$, $\angle AED=\angle AEB=80^\circ$, and $\angle ADE=180^\circ-\angle ADB=80^\circ$. The angles at $D$ and $E$ are equal, so $AD=AE$.

\begin{asy}
