For a two-digit number $n=10q+r$, where $q$ is its tens digit and $r$ its units digit, say that $n$ is \emph{nice} if there are positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b=n$ such that $n$ divides both $q-ar$ and $q+br$. For example, $13$ is nice with $a=9$, $b=4$ (as $1-9\cdot3=-26$ and $1+4\cdot3=13$), and $17$ is nice with $a=5$, $b=12$ (as $1-5\cdot7=-34$ and $1+12\cdot7=85$). A student checked the primes from $11$ to $29$, found that they are all nice, and conjectured that a nice two-digit number must be prime.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Let $n>1$ be an integer with $\gcd(n,10)=1$, and let $a$ be a positive integer with $10a\equiv-1\pmod n$. Prove that for every integer $N=10Q+R$, we have $n\mid N$ if and only if $n\mid Q-aR$.
\item[(b)] Deduce that every two-digit $n$ with $\gcd(n,10)=1$ is nice, and disprove the conjecture.
