solution:
\textbf{At most $4$.} Let the circle be $(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2=\rho^2$. A common point $(x,y)$ has $y=ax^2+bx+c$, so its $x$-coordinate satisfies
\[(x-h)^2+\left(ax^2+bx+c-k\right)^2-\rho^2=0.\]
The left side is a polynomial in $x$ of degree $4$ with leading coefficient $a^2\ne0$, so it has at most $4$ real roots. Each intersection point is determined by its $x$-coordinate (because $y=ax^2+bx+c$), so there are at most $4$ intersection points.

\textbf{Four can occur.} Take the parabola $y=2x^2-5$, which contains $(\pm1,-3)$ and $(\pm2,3)$, and the circle with centre $\left(0,\dfrac14\right)$ and radius $\dfrac{\sqrt{185}}{4}$:
\[1^2+\left(-3-\dfrac14\right)^2=1+\dfrac{169}{16}=\dfrac{185}{16},\qquad 2^2+\left(3-\dfrac14\right)^2=4+\dfrac{121}{16}=\dfrac{185}{16},\]
and by symmetry the circle also passes through $(-1,-3)$ and $(-2,3)$. So these four points are common to both curves.

