question:
$ABCD$ is an isosceles trapezoid with $AB\parallel CD$ and $AD=BC$, and its diagonals $AC$ and $BD$ meet at $P$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many circles are tangent to both of the lines $AC$ and $BD$?
\item[(b)] Describe the set of centres of these circles in terms of the trapezoid, and explain how to construct such a circle.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} A circle with centre $O$ and radius $\rho>0$ is tangent to a line exactly when the distance from $O$ to the line equals $\rho$. So a circle is tangent to both lines $AC$ and $BD$ exactly when its centre $O$ is at the same positive distance from both lines and $\rho$ is that distance. The points equidistant from two lines that cross at $P$ form the two bisectors of the angles at $P$: two perpendicular lines through $P$. Every point $O\ne P$ on them is the centre of exactly one such circle, so there are \textbf{infinitely many} circles.

\textbf{(b)} Let $m$ be the axis of symmetry of the trapezoid, the common perpendicular bisector of $AB$ and $CD$. Reflection in $m$ swaps $A$ with $B$ and $D$ with $C$, so it swaps the lines $AC$ and $BD$; hence it fixes their intersection $P$, so $P$ lies on $m$, and the reflection carries each angle between the two lines to the angle on the other side of $m$. So $m$ makes equal angles with $AC$ and $BD$: it is one bisector. The other bisector is the line through $P$ perpendicular to $m$, which is parallel to $AB$ and $CD$.

So the centres are exactly the points other than $P$ on the axis of symmetry $m$ and on the line through $P$ parallel to the bases.

\emph{Construction.} Choose a point $O\ne P$ on one of these two lines, drop the perpendicular from $O$ to $AC$ with foot $T$, and draw the circle with centre $O$ through $T$. It touches $AC$ at $T$, and since $O$ lies on a bisector of the angles between the two lines, it is equally far from $BD$, so the circle also touches $BD$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-3,0), B=(3,0), C=(1.6,2.2), D=(-1.6,2.2);
pair P=extension(A,C,B,D);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A-0.25*(C-A)--A+1.55*(C-A),gray); draw(B-0.25*(D-B)--B+1.55*(D-B),gray);
draw(A--C,linewidth(0.8)); draw(B--D,linewidth(0.8));
draw((0,-1.2)--(0,3.4),dashed+blue); draw((-4.2,P.y)--(4.2,P.y),dashed+blue);
pair dAC=unit(C-A);
pair O1=(0,P.y+1.3); real r1=abs(cross(dAC,O1-A));
pair O2=(-3.0,P.y); real r2=abs(cross(dAC,O2-A));
draw(circle(O1,r1),red); draw(circle(O2,r2),red);
dot(O1); dot(O2); dot(P);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N); label("$D$",D,N); label("$P$",P,1.8*dir(300),fontsize(9pt));
label("$m$",(0,-1.2),S,blue);
\end{asy}

The sketch shows the two bisector lines (dashed) and one circle centred on each.
\[\boxed{\text{infinitely many; centres on the axis of symmetry or on the line through }P\text{ parallel to the bases}}\]

Let $s=10\sqrt{15}$ and use coordinates $A=(0,0)$, $B=(s,0)$, $C=(s,s)$, $D=(0,s)$. Then
\[E=\left(s,\dfrac s3\right),\quad F=\left(\dfrac{2s}3,s\right),\quad G=\left(0,\dfrac{2s}3\right),\quad H=\left(\dfrac s3,0\right).\]
The four lines are
\[AE:\ x-3y=0,\qquad CG:\ x-3y+2s=0,\qquad BF:\ 3x+y-3s=0,\qquad DH:\ 3x+y-s=0.\]
So $AE\parallel CG$, $BF\parallel DH$, and the two directions are perpendicular (the normal vectors $(1,-3)$ and $(3,1)$ are perpendicular). The bounded region is therefore a rectangle whose sides are the distances between the parallel lines:
\[\dfrac{|2s|}{\sqrt{1^2+3^2}}=\dfrac{2s}{\sqrt{10}}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\dfrac{|-3s-(-s)|}{\sqrt{3^2+1^2}}=\dfrac{2s}{\sqrt{10}}.\]
It is a square of area
\[\left(\dfrac{2s}{\sqrt{10}}\right)^2=\dfrac{4s^2}{10}=\dfrac{4\cdot1500}{10}=\boxed{600}.\]

