Group the pairs by their product $n=ab$, where $2\le n\le99$ (the product $1$ only comes from $1\cdot1$, which is not two different numbers).

\textbf{Pairs with a given product.} If $\gcd(a,b)=1$ and $ab=n$, then for each prime $p$ the whole power of $p$ in $n$ goes to $a$ or to $b$. So if $n$ has $\omega(n)$ distinct prime factors, there are $2^{\omega(n)}$ ordered pairs $(a,b)$. They never have $a=b$ (that would force $a=b=1$), so there are $2^{\omega(n)-1}$ unordered pairs.

\textbf{Counting $n$ by $\omega(n)$}, for $2\le n\le99$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\omega=1$ (prime powers): the $25$ primes below $100$ and $4,8,16,32,64,9,27,81,25,49$, so $35$ numbers;
\item $\omega=3$: $30,42,60,66,70,78,84,90$, so $8$ numbers (four distinct primes would give at least $210$);
\item $\omega=2$: the remaining $98-35-8=55$ numbers.
\end{itemize}
The number of pairs is
\[35\cdot1+55\cdot2+8\cdot4=35+110+32=\boxed{177}.\]

