\textbf{Estimate $\sqrt5+\sqrt[3]7$.} Since $2.2^2=4.84<5<5.0625=2.25^2$, we have $2.2<\sqrt5<2.25$. Since $1.9^3=6.859<7<7.077888=1.92^3$, we have $1.9<\sqrt[3]7<1.92$. So
\[4.1<\sqrt5+\sqrt[3]7<4.17.\]
Using $3.14<\pi<3.15$: $\pi<3.15<4.1$ and $\dfrac{3\pi}{2}>4.71>4.17$. Hence
\[\pi<\sqrt5+\sqrt[3]7<\dfrac{3\pi}2,\qquad\text{so}\qquad-\dfrac{3\pi}2<t<-\pi.\]

\textbf{Locate the point.} Turning by $t$ is the same as turning by $t+2\pi$, and $\dfrac\pi2<t+2\pi<\pi$. So $P_t$ lies in the $\boxed{\text{second quadrant}}$ ($x<0$, $y>0$).

