question:
Compare the following pairs of numbers (angles in radians):
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $\sin1$ and $\sin\left(1+\dfrac{2\pi}{5}\right)$;
\item[(b)] $\cos\left(1+\dfrac{2\pi}{5}\right)$ and $\cos\left(1+\dfrac{4\pi}{5}\right)$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
We use $3<\pi<\dfrac{10}{3}$, that sine is increasing on $\left[0,\dfrac\pi2\right]$, and that cosine is decreasing on $[0,\pi]$.

\textbf{(a)} $\sin\left(1+\dfrac{2\pi}5\right)=\sin\left(\pi-1-\dfrac{2\pi}5\right)=\sin\left(\dfrac{3\pi}5-1\right)$. Here $0<\dfrac{3\pi}{5}-1<1<\dfrac\pi2$, because $\dfrac{3\pi}5<2$ (as $\pi<\dfrac{10}3$). Since sine is increasing on $\left[0,\dfrac\pi2\right]$,
\[\sin\left(1+\dfrac{2\pi}5\right)=\sin\left(\dfrac{3\pi}5-1\right)<\sin1.\]

\textbf{(b)} $\cos\left(1+\dfrac{4\pi}{5}\right)=\cos\left(2\pi-1-\dfrac{4\pi}5\right)=\cos\left(\dfrac{6\pi}5-1\right)$. Both $1+\dfrac{2\pi}5$ and $\dfrac{6\pi}5-1$ lie in $(0,\pi)$ (the second because $\dfrac\pi5<1$), and
\[\left(\dfrac{6\pi}5-1\right)-\left(1+\dfrac{2\pi}5\right)=\dfrac{4\pi}5-2>0\]
since $\pi>2.5$. Cosine is decreasing on $[0,\pi]$, so
\[\cos\left(1+\dfrac{2\pi}5\right)>\cos\left(\dfrac{6\pi}5-1\right)=\cos\left(1+\dfrac{4\pi}5\right).\]

