\textbf{($\Rightarrow$)} Suppose $BD$ bisects $\angle ABC$. Let $E$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $D$ to $BC$. The triangles $BAD$ and $BED$ have right angles at $A$ and $E$, equal angles $\angle ABD=\angle EBD$, and the common side $BD$, so they are congruent (AAS). Hence
\[BE=BA\qquad\text{and}\qquad DE=DA.\]
Since $BE=BA<BC$ (the hypotenuse is the longest side), $E$ lies between $B$ and $C$. In triangle $DEC$, $\angle DEC=90^\circ$ and $\angle ECD=\angle ACB=45^\circ$, so it is an isosceles right triangle and $EC=DE=AD$. Therefore
\[BC=BE+EC=AB+AD.\]

\begin{asy}
