\end{asy}

\textbf{($\Leftarrow$)} Let $D'$ be the point where the bisector of $\angle ABC$ meets side $AC$. By the first part, $BC=AB+AD'$. If also $BC=AB+AD$, then $AD=AD'$. Both $D$ and $D'$ lie on the segment $AC$, at the same distance from $A$, so $D=D'$, and $BD$ is the bisector. $\blacksquare$

(With $AB=AC=1$, both conditions say $AD=\sqrt2-1$.)

Let $D=PQ$ be the diameter of the large circle. Then $PX=16$ and $XQ=D-16$. A half-disc of diameter $d$ has area $\dfrac12\pi\left(\dfrac d2\right)^2=\dfrac{\pi d^2}{8}$. The shaded region is the large half-disc minus the half-disc on $XQ$, plus the half-disc on $PX$ (which lies on the other side of the line and does not overlap the others), so its area is
\[\dfrac\pi8\left(D^2-(D-16)^2+16^2\right)=\dfrac\pi8\left(32D-256+256\right)=4\pi D.\]

