question:
A fair six-sided die, with faces numbered $1$ to $6$, is thrown three times, independently. Find the probability that the product of the three numbers is
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] even;
\item[(b)] a multiple of $10$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove the inclusion--exclusion principle for three finite sets:
\[|A\cup B\cup C|=|A|+|B|+|C|-|A\cap B|-|B\cap C|-|C\cap A|+|A\cap B\cap C|.\]
\item[(b)] How many integers from $1$ to $100$ are divisible by at least one of $2$, $3$ and $5$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{The proof is correct.} In detail:
\begin{itemize}
\item The bisector of $\angle BAC$ is a ray inside the angle, so it meets the opposite side $BC$ at a point $Z$ between $B$ and $C$.
\item Triangles $ABZ$ and $ACZ$, with the correspondence $A\leftrightarrow A$, $B\leftrightarrow C$, $Z\leftrightarrow Z$, have $AB=AC$ (given), $\angle BAZ=\angle CAZ$ (the bisector), and the common side $AZ=AZ$. The equal angles lie between the pairs of equal sides, so SAS applies and $\triangle ABZ\cong\triangle ACZ$.
\item Corresponding angles are equal: $\angle ABZ=\angle ACZ$. Since $Z$ lies on segment $BC$, $\angle ABZ=\angle ABC$ and $\angle ACZ=\angle ACB$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,3), B=(-1.6,0), C=(1.6,0), Z=(0,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8)); draw(A--Z,blue);
draw(arc(A,0.55,degrees(B-A),270),red); draw(arc(A,0.55,270,degrees(C-A)),red);
void tick(pair P,pair Q){ pair m=(P+Q)/2, v=rotate(90)*unit(Q-P); draw(m-0.1*v--m+0.1*v,linewidth(0.7)); }
tick(A,B); tick(A,C);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$Z$",Z,S);
\end{asy}

Hence $\angle ABC=\angle ACB$. $\blacksquare$

The argument differs from other textbook proofs (for example, applying SAS to triangle $ABC$ and its mirror image $ACB$, or using the midpoint of $BC$ with SSS), but it is equally valid; the only extra fact it uses is that the angle bisector from $A$ meets the segment $BC$.

question:
A soldier has stepped on a mine that will explode as soon as he lifts his foot, and he cannot stay on it forever. An explosives expert can stay and try to disarm the mine while everyone else moves out of range. She succeeds with probability $p$, and then nobody dies; if she fails, the mine explodes and both she and the soldier die. If she does not try, everyone else (including her) moves out of range, and the soldier dies when he lifts his foot. The commander wants to minimise the expected number of deaths.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If $p=\dfrac12$, should the expert try?
\item[(b)] What if $p=0.4$?
\item[(c)] For which success rates $p$ is trying worse than not trying? Give the answer as an interval.
\end{enumerate}

Let $\mathbb N=\{1,2,3,\dots\}$, and let $\mathcal P(\mathbb N)$ be the set of all subsets of $\mathbb N$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find a one-to-one function from $\mathbb N$ to $\mathcal P(\mathbb N)$.
\item[(b)] Prove that there is no bijection between $\mathbb N$ and $\mathcal P(\mathbb N)$.
\item[(c)] Explain why this shows that there are infinite sets ``bigger'' than $\mathbb N$ (the size of $\mathbb N$ is called $\aleph_0$), and that there is no largest size of infinite set.
\end{enumerate}

For a fraction $\dfrac ab$ with positive integers $a$ and $b$, two decimal numbers can be formed by writing the numerator and the denominator next to each other, separated by a decimal point: ``$a.b$'' and ``$b.a$''. (For example, for $\dfrac74$ these are $7.4$ and $4.7$.)
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that the decimal ``$a.b$'' is never equal to $\dfrac ab$.
\item[(b)] Find all fractions $\dfrac ab$, where $a$ and $b$ are digits from $1$ to $9$, such that the decimal ``$b.a$'' equals $\dfrac ab$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The base of the pyramid is an $s\times s$ square. The apex lies above the centre of the base, at height $h$, and the midpoint of a base edge is at distance $\dfrac s2$ from the centre, so by the Pythagorean theorem the slant height is
\[\ell=\sqrt{h^2+\dfrac{s^2}{4}}.\]
The diagonal of the base is $s\sqrt2$. So the conditions are
\[h>s\qquad\text{and}\qquad h^2+\dfrac{s^2}{4}<2s^2\iff h^2<\dfrac{7s^2}{4}\iff h<\dfrac{\sqrt7}{2}\,s.\]

\begin{asy}
