draw(A0--A1,linewidth(0.8)); draw(B0--B1,linewidth(0.8)); draw(C0--C1,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A1--B1--C1,linewidth(0.8)); draw(A1--D1--C1,dashed);
draw(T--A1,linewidth(0.8)); draw(T--B1,linewidth(0.8)); draw(T--C1,linewidth(0.8)); draw(T--D1,dashed);
draw(T--O,dashed+blue); draw(T--M,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(T); dot(O); dot(M);
label("$h$",T+0.5*(O-T),W,blue);
label("$\ell$",(T+M)/2,NE,red);
label("$s$",(A0+B0)/2,S);
label("$s$",(A0+A1)/2,W);
label("$\dfrac{s}{2}$",M+(0.05,-0.68),blue+fontsize(7pt));
draw(M+(-0.07,-0.45)--(O+M)/2+(0.12,-0.04),blue+linewidth(0.3),EndArrow(3));
draw(O--M,blue+dotted);
\end{asy}

Since $\sqrt7$ is irrational, $\dfrac{\sqrt7}{2}s$ is never an integer, so the allowed heights are the integers $h$ with $s<h\le\left\lfloor\dfrac{\sqrt7}{2}s\right\rfloor$, and
\[N(s)=\left\lfloor\dfrac{\sqrt7}{2}s\right\rfloor-s\le\left(\dfrac{\sqrt7}{2}-1\right)s.\]
Now $\dfrac{\sqrt7}{2}-1\approx0.32288$, so for $s\le309$ we get $N(s)\le0.32288\cdot309<99.8$, i.e. $N(s)\le99$.

For $s=310$: $\dfrac{\sqrt7}{2}\cdot310=155\sqrt7=\sqrt{168175}$, and $410^2=168100<168175<168921=411^2$, so $\left\lfloor155\sqrt7\right\rfloor=410$ and
\[N(310)=410-310=100.\]
The smallest such cube has edge length $s=\boxed{310}$.

An isosceles triangle has two equal sides (legs) of length $a$ and a third side (base) of length $b$, where $b=a$ is allowed. Different pairs $(a,b)$ give non-congruent triangles, because the side lengths $\{a,a,b\}$ determine $a$ (the repeated length) and $b$.

