draw(m2+0.12*dir(degrees(A-C)+90)*(-1)--m2+0.12*dir(degrees(A-C)+90));
\end{asy}

The conditions are:
\begin{itemize}
\item triangle inequality: $a+a>b$, i.e. $b\le2a-1$ (the other inequality $a+b>a$ is automatic);
\item perimeter: $2a+b\le19$, i.e. $b\le19-2a$.
\end{itemize}
So for each $a\ge1$ the number of bases is $\min(2a-1,\ 19-2a)$ (when positive):
\[
\begin{array}{c|ccccccccc}
a&1&2&3&4&5&6&7&8&9\\\hline
\#b&1&3&5&7&9&7&5&3&1
\end{array}
\]
For $a\ge10$ there is no base. The total is
\[1+3+5+7+9+7+5+3+1=\boxed{41}.\]

question:
A student computes $\left(\dfrac{1}{1000}\right)^{\dfrac{1}{1000}}\approx0.993$ and concludes: ``$x^x$ gets close to $1$ as $x$ gets close to $0$, so $0^0=1$.''
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $x^x\to1$ as $x\to0^+$.
\item[(b)] Let $f(x)=e^{-\dfrac1x}$ for $x>0$. Show that $f(x)\to0$ as $x\to0^+$, but $f(x)^x\to\dfrac1e$.
\item[(c)] Explain why the student's argument does not determine a value for $0^0$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} We can write
\[P_k=\left(a+\dfrac kn(c-a),\ b+\dfrac kn(d-b)\right).\]
So $P_k$ is reached from $A$ by moving $\dfrac kn$ of the horizontal change $c-a$ and $\dfrac kn$ of the vertical change $d-b$. Hence consecutive points differ by the same step $\left(\dfrac{c-a}{n},\dfrac{d-b}{n}\right)$. By the distance formula,
\[P_kP_{k+1}=\sqrt{\left(\dfrac{c-a}{n}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{d-b}{n}\right)^2}=\dfrac1n\sqrt{(c-a)^2+(d-b)^2}=\dfrac{AB}{n}.\]
All the steps are the same vector, so $P_0=A,P_1,\dots,P_n=B$ lie on one line, in this order, and the $n$ pieces are equal. (This is the midpoint formula for $n=2$.) For $n=3$, $k=1,2$ gives the stated trisection points.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(6,4.5);
