In triangle $QRS$, the law of sines gives
\[\dfrac{RS}{\sin\angle RQS}=\dfrac{QR}{\sin\angle QSR}.\]
Since $R$ lies on line $PS$, the angle $\angle QSR$ is either equal to $\angle QSP$ (if $R$ is on ray $SP$) or supplementary to it (if $R$ is on the other side of $S$); in both cases $\sin\angle QSR=\sin\angle QSP$. In the right triangle $PQS$,
\[\sin\angle QSP=\dfrac{PQ}{PS}.\]
Substituting, with $\sin\angle RQS=\sin30^\circ=\dfrac12$:
\[2\,RS=\dfrac{QR\cdot PS}{PQ},\qquad\text{so}\qquad PS\cdot QR=2\cdot PQ\cdot RS=2\cdot7\cdot4=\boxed{56}.\]
Both positions of $R$ occur: $R$ beyond $S$ (left sketch, $\angle PQR=120^\circ$) and $R$ between $P$ and $S$ (right sketch, $\angle PQR=60^\circ$), and the argument covers both.

