Let $P=(x_1,y_1)$ and $Q=(x_2,y_2)$, so $0\le x_1,y_1\le2$, $5\le x_2\le7$ and $1\le y_2\le3$. The run is always positive,
\[x_2-x_1\ge5-2=3,\]
so the slope $m=\dfrac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}$ is defined, and the rise satisfies $-1=1-2\le y_2-y_1\le3-0=3$.

\textbf{Greatest slope.} If the rise is $\le0$, then $m\le0$. If the rise is positive, then $m\le\dfrac{3}{x_2-x_1}\le\dfrac33=1$: make the rise as large and the run as small as possible. Equality needs $y_2-y_1=3$ and $x_2-x_1=3$, i.e. $P=(2,0)$ and $Q=(5,3)$.

\textbf{Least slope.} If the rise is $\ge0$, then $m\ge0$. If it is negative, then $m\ge\dfrac{-1}{x_2-x_1}\ge-\dfrac13$, with equality exactly for $P=(2,2)$, $Q=(5,1)$.

