\textbf{Reduction to triples of diagonals.} A formed triangle determines the three diagonals containing its sides (two distinct diagonals are never collinear, as no three vertices of a convex polygon are collinear). Conversely, three diagonals give a triangle exactly when each two of them meet (at an interior crossing point or at a common endpoint) and the three meeting points are distinct.

In a convex octagon, two diagonals without a common endpoint cross exactly when their endpoints alternate around the octagon. So which pairs of diagonals meet depends only on the labels of their endpoints, not on the shape. A triple of pairwise meeting diagonals then fails to give a triangle only if all three pass through one point. Hence the count is largest when no three diagonals pass through a common interior point. This happens for suitable octagons (e.g. after slightly moving the vertices of a regular octagon), and we count the triangles in that case. Classify them by the number of octagon vertices among their vertices.

\textbf{3 octagon vertices.} The triangle $PQR$ has sides $PQ,QR,RP$, which must be diagonals, so no two of $P,Q,R$ are adjacent. Going around the octagon from a chosen vertex, the numbers of unchosen vertices in the three gaps are $g_1,g_2,g_3\ge1$ with $g_1+g_2+g_3=5$, which has $\dbinom42=6$ solutions. With $8$ choices of the starting vertex this gives $48$, and each triangle is counted $3$ times (once from each vertex), so there are $16$ such triangles.

\textbf{2 octagon vertices $P,Q$, one crossing point $X$.} The sides $PX$ and $QX$ lie on diagonals $PR$ and $QS$ that cross at $X$, so their endpoints alternate: in the cyclic order of the four vertices $P,Q,R,S$, the vertices $P$ and $Q$ are neighbours. So each $4$-set of vertices, together with one of its $4$ cyclically consecutive pairs, gives one triangle (the pair and the crossing of the $4$-set's two diagonals), provided that pair is a diagonal and not a side of the octagon. There are $4\dbinom84=280$ such choices. A side of the octagon is a consecutive pair in each of the $\dbinom62=15$ four-sets containing it, which excludes $8\cdot15=120$. This leaves $160$ triangles.

\textbf{1 octagon vertex $P$, two crossing points.} The sides at $P$ lie on diagonals $PR$, $PS$, and the third side lies on a diagonal $QT$ crossing both. This forces $Q,T\ne P,R,S$ and the cyclic order $P,Q,R,S,T$: $Q$ between $P$ and $R$, and $T$ between $S$ and $P$. Conversely, every $5$-set of vertices and every choice of $P$ in it gives such a triangle. All three segments are then diagonals of the octagon, since each joins two vertices separated by chosen vertices on both sides. This gives $5\dbinom85=280$ triangles.

\textbf{No octagon vertex.} Three diagonals with $6$ distinct endpoints that cross pairwise: for $6$ vertices $V_1,\dots,V_6$ in cyclic order, the only way is $V_1V_4$, $V_2V_5$, $V_3V_6$. This gives $\dbinom86=28$ triangles.

Every formed triangle is of exactly one type and is counted once, so the maximum is
\[16+160+280+28=\boxed{484}.\]

\begin{asy}
