question:
Sequences that tend to $+\infty$ show why $\infty-\infty$ is not given a value.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] For every real number $c$, find sequences $a_n\to+\infty$ and $b_n\to+\infty$ with $a_n-b_n\to c$.
\item[(b)] Find sequences $a_n,b_n\to+\infty$ with $a_n-b_n\to+\infty$; with $a_n-b_n\to-\infty$; and with $a_n-b_n$ having no limit at all.
\item[(c)] Explain why no value can be assigned to $\infty-\infty$ consistently with limits.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Take $a_n=n+c$ and $b_n=n$. Both tend to $+\infty$, and $a_n-b_n=c$ for every $n$.

\textbf{(b)}
\begin{itemize}
\item $a_n=2n$, $b_n=n$: $a_n-b_n=n\to+\infty$.
\item $a_n=n$, $b_n=2n$: $a_n-b_n=-n\to-\infty$.
\item $a_n=n+(-1)^n$, $b_n=n$: both tend to $+\infty$, and $a_n-b_n=(-1)^n$ alternates between $-1$ and $1$, so it has no limit.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{(c)} If $\infty-\infty$ had a value $v$, then the rule ``if $a_n\to\infty$ and $b_n\to\infty$ then $a_n-b_n\to\infty-\infty$'' would force every such difference to tend to $v$. But by (a) and (b) the difference can tend to any real number, to $+\infty$, to $-\infty$, or to nothing. So $\infty-\infty$ is an \emph{indeterminate form}: the limit depends on how fast the two sequences grow, not on the fact that both tend to infinity. (Also, $\infty$ is not a real number, so the expression is not an ordinary subtraction.)

$\boxed{\text{e.g. }(n+c)-n\to c,\ 2n-n\to\infty,\ n-2n\to-\infty,\ (n+(-1)^n)-n\text{ diverges}}$

question:
Consider the graph of $y=x^2$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that it is symmetric about the $y$-axis, and that it lies on or above the $x$-axis, touching it only at the origin.
\item[(b)] Show that a point $P$ lies on the graph if and only if $P$ is equidistant from the point $F\left(0,\dfrac14\right)$ and the line $y=-\dfrac14$. (So the graph is the parabola with focus $F$ and directrix $y=-\dfrac14$.)
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} If $(x,y)$ is on the graph then so is $(-x,y)$, since $(-x)^2=x^2$: the graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. Also $y=x^2\ge0$ with equality only at $x=0$, so the graph lies on or above the $x$-axis and meets it only at $(0,0)$, its lowest point (the vertex).

\textbf{(b)} Let $P=(x,y)$. Its distance to $F$ is $\sqrt{x^2+\left(y-\dfrac14\right)^2}$, and its distance to the line $y=-\dfrac14$ is $\left|y+\dfrac14\right|$. Both are nonnegative, so they are equal if and only if their squares are:
\[x^2+y^2-\dfrac y2+\dfrac1{16}=y^2+\dfrac y2+\dfrac1{16}\iff x^2=y.\]
So $P$ is equidistant from $F$ and the directrix exactly when $P$ lies on $y=x^2$. $\blacksquare$

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return x^2;}
draw((-2.2,0)--(2.4,0),Arrow(5)); draw((0,-0.7)--(0,3.6),Arrow(5));
label("$x$",(2.4,0),E); label("$y$",(0,3.6),N);
draw(graph(f,-1.85,1.85),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-2.2,-0.25)--(2.3,-0.25),red+dashed); label("$y=-\dfrac14$",(-1.6,-0.25),S,red+fontsize(8pt));
pair Fc=(0,0.25), P=(1.5,2.25), H=(1.5,-0.25);
dot(Fc); label("$F$",Fc,W,fontsize(8pt));
dot(P); label("$P(x,x^2)$",P,E,fontsize(8pt));
draw(P--Fc,deepgreen); draw(P--H,deepgreen); dot(H);
label("$y=x^2$",(-1.8,f(-1.8)),W,blue);
\end{asy}

$\boxed{y=x^2\text{ is the parabola with vertex }(0,0),\ \text{focus }\left(0,\dfrac14\right),\ \text{directrix }y=-\dfrac14}$

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] The parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ ($a\ne0$) crosses the $x$-axis at $x=r$ and $x=s$. Prove that its vertex has $x$-coordinate $\dfrac{r+s}{2}$.
\item[(b)] Use (a) to find the vertex of $y=2x^2-8x-10$.
\item[(c)] Explain why the method in (a) cannot be used directly for $y=x^2+2x+5$, and find its vertex in another way.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Since $r$ and $s$ are the roots, $ax^2+bx+c=a(x-r)(x-s)$. Let $m=\dfrac{r+s}{2}$ and $d=\dfrac{s-r}{2}$, so $r=m-d$ and $s=m+d$. Then
\[a(x-r)(x-s)=a\bigl((x-m)+d\bigr)\bigl((x-m)-d\bigr)=a(x-m)^2-ad^2.\]
Since $(x-m)^2\ge0$ with equality only at $x=m$, the function has its extreme value (minimum if $a>0$, maximum if $a<0$) exactly at $x=m$. So the vertex is at $x=m=\dfrac{r+s}{2}$, the average of the $x$-intercepts, and the parabola is symmetric about the line $x=m$.

\textbf{(b)} $2x^2-8x-10=2\left(x^2-4x-5\right)=2(x-5)(x+1)$, so $r=-1$, $s=5$ and the vertex has $x=2$; then $y=8-16-10=-18$. The vertex is $\boxed{(2,-18)}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return 2x^2-8x-10;}
pair T(real x,real y){return (x,0.12*y);}
draw(T(-2.3,0)--T(6.4,0),Arrow(5)); draw(T(0,-21)--T(0,8),Arrow(5));
label("$x$",T(6.4,0),E); label("$y$",T(0,8),N);
guide g; for(int i=0;i<=100;++i){real x=-1.6+7.2*i/100; g=g--T(x,f(x));}
draw(g,blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(T(-1,0)); dot(T(5,0)); dot(T(2,-18),red);
label("$(-1,0)$",T(-1,0),SW,fontsize(8pt)); label("$(5,0)$",T(5,0),SE,fontsize(8pt));
label("$(2,-18)$",T(2,-18),S,red+fontsize(8pt));
draw(T(2,-18)--T(2,6),dashed+red); label("$x=2$",T(2,6),N,red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(c)} The discriminant of $x^2+2x+5$ is $4-20<0$, so the parabola has no $x$-intercepts and there is nothing to average. Completing the square, $x^2+2x+5=(x+1)^2+4$, so the vertex is $\boxed{(-1,4)}$. (The idea of (a) still works with any horizontal line that meets the parabola twice: $y=5$ meets it where $x^2+2x=0$, at $x=0$ and $x=-2$, whose average is $-1$.)

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $mn+m+n+1=(m+1)(n+1)$ (this is ``Simon's Favourite Factoring Trick'').
\item[(b)] Find all ordered pairs of positive integers $(m,n)$ with $mn+m+n=59$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $D$ be the foot of the altitude from $C$ to $AB$. The angles at $A$ and $B$ are acute (they add up to $90^\circ$), so $D$ lies strictly between $A$ and $B$, and $AD+DB=c$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), A=(0,3), B=(4,0);
pair D=A+dot(C-A,unit(B-A))*unit(B-A);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(C--D,dashed+blue);
real s=0.22;
draw((s,0)--(s,s)--(0,s));
draw(D+s*unit(B-D)--D+s*unit(B-D)+s*unit(C-D)--D+s*unit(C-D),blue);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$D$",D,NE);
label("$b$",(A+C)/2,W); label("$a$",(B+C)/2,S); label("$c$",(A+B)/2+(0.25,0.2),NE);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Triangles $ACD$ and $ABC$ are similar:} both have a right angle ($\angle ADC=\angle ACB=90^\circ$) and they share the angle at $A$. Matching $A\to A$, $D\to C$, $C\to B$,
\[\dfrac{AD}{AC}=\dfrac{AC}{AB},\qquad\text{so}\qquad b^2=AC^2=c\cdot AD.\]
\textbf{Triangles $BCD$ and $BAC$ are similar} in the same way (right angles at $D$ and $C$, common angle at $B$), so
\[\dfrac{BD}{BC}=\dfrac{BC}{BA},\qquad\text{so}\qquad a^2=BC^2=c\cdot BD.\]
Adding,
\[a^2+b^2=c\,(AD+BD)=c\cdot c=c^2.\qquad\blacksquare\]

\textbf{Effect of one gluing.} Gluing a cube of side $t$ face-to-face onto the centre of a larger face covers a $t\times t$ square of that face and the glued face of the small cube, and exposes the other $5$ faces of the small cube. So the surface area changes by
\[5t^2-t^2=4t^2,\]
as long as the new cube touches nothing else.

\textbf{No other contacts.} Put the big cube at $[0,8]^3$. The side-$4$ cube on its top face is $[2,6]\times[2,6]\times[8,12]$. The side-$2$ cubes on its four vertical faces, such as $[3,5]\times[0,2]\times[9,11]$, lie at heights $9\le z\le11$, and the one on its top is $[3,5]\times[3,5]\times[12,14]$. So everything glued over the top face lies in the block $0\le x,y\le8$, $z\ge8$, and touches the big cube only along the glued $4\times4$ square. By symmetry the same holds for each of the six faces. The blocks over different faces meet only along edges of the big cube, and no small cube reaches an edge, so cubes glued over different faces never touch.

\textbf{Total.} The big cube has area $6\cdot8^2=384$. There are $6$ side-$4$ cubes, each adding $4\cdot4^2=64$, and each has $5$ exposed faces, so there are $30$ side-$2$ cubes, each adding $4\cdot2^2=16$:
\[384+6\cdot64+30\cdot16=384+384+480=\boxed{1248}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
void sq(real x0,real y0,real t,pen p){fill(box((x0,y0),(x0+t,y0+t)),p); draw(box((x0,y0),(x0+t,y0+t)),linewidth(0.7));}
pen p8=rgb(0.85,0.9,1), p4=rgb(1,0.88,0.8), p2=rgb(0.85,1,0.85);
// front view: big cube [0,8]^2; 4-cubes on the left, right, top, bottom and front faces; 2-cubes on their outer and side faces
sq(0,0,8,p8);
sq(2,8,4,p4); sq(2,-4,4,p4); sq(-4,2,4,p4); sq(8,2,4,p4);
sq(3,12,2,p2); sq(0,9,2,p2); sq(6,9,2,p2);
sq(3,-6,2,p2); sq(0,-3,2,p2); sq(6,-3,2,p2);
sq(-6,3,2,p2); sq(-3,0,2,p2); sq(-3,6,2,p2);
sq(12,3,2,p2); sq(9,0,2,p2); sq(9,6,2,p2);
sq(2,2,4,p4); sq(3,3,2,p2);
sq(0,3,2,p2); sq(6,3,2,p2); sq(3,0,2,p2); sq(3,6,2,p2);
sq(3,9,2,p2); sq(3,-3,2,p2); sq(-3,3,2,p2); sq(9,3,2,p2);
label("$8$",(1,1),fontsize(8pt)); label("$4$",(4,2),N,fontsize(8pt)); label("$2$",(4,3),N,fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

The sketch is the front view of the object: the big square is the cube of side $8$, the side-$4$ cubes on its left, right, top and bottom faces stick out, and the side-$4$ cube on the front face appears in the middle, with its five side-$2$ cubes (one in front, four around it). The cubes on the back face are hidden.

Convert each to bundles:
\begin{itemize}
\item $4$ bindles $=4\cdot3=12$ bundles;
\item $8$ bandles $=8\cdot4=32$ bindles $=96$ bundles;
\item $6$ boondles $=6\cdot6=36$ bundles;
\item $1$ bendle $=6$ boondles $=36$ bundles.
\end{itemize}
The total is $12+96+36+36=\boxed{180}$ bundles.

question:
Five centimetres more than three times the length of a rectangle is at least $20$ cm and at most $44$ cm. The width of the rectangle is $10$ cm.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the positive difference between the maximum and the minimum possible area of the rectangle?
\item[(b)] A student argues: ``the length of a rectangle is always at least its width, so $\ell\ge10$, and the answer is $130-100=30$.'' Explain why the intended answer is the one in (a).
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} With length $\ell$,
\[20\le3\ell+5\le44\iff15\le3\ell\le39\iff5\le\ell\le13.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(box((0,0),(6.5,2.6)),linewidth(0.9));
label("$\ell$",(3.25,0),S); label("$10$",(0,1.3),W);
label("$5\le\ell\le13$",(3.25,1.3));
\end{asy}

The area $10\ell$ ranges from $10\cdot5=50$ to $10\cdot13=130$ square centimetres, so the difference is $130-50=\boxed{80}$ square centimetres.

\textbf{(b)} ``Length'' and ``width'' are just names for the two side lengths of the rectangle; the definition of a rectangle does not require the side called the length to be the longer one, and the problem states no such condition. So every $\ell$ with $5\le\ell\le13$ is allowed, including $\ell<10$. The student's answer $30$ solves a different problem, with the extra condition $\ell\ge10$ added.

Let $XN=d$, so $YN=d+5$, $XY=d+10$ and $BC=d+15$. Triangle $XNY$ has a right angle at $N$, so
\[d^2+(d+5)^2=(d+10)^2\iff d^2-10d-75=0\iff(d-15)(d+5)=0,\]
so $d=15$: $XN=15$, $YN=20$, $XY=25$, $BC=30$.

The altitude of the right triangle $XNY$ from $N$ is $\dfrac{XN\cdot YN}{XY}=\dfrac{15\cdot20}{25}=12$. Since $N$ lies on $BC$ and $XY\parallel BC$, this is the distance between the lines $XY$ and $BC$.

Since $XY\parallel BC$, triangle $AXY$ is similar to triangle $ABC$ with ratio $\dfrac{XY}{BC}=\dfrac{25}{30}=\dfrac56$. If $h$ is the altitude from $A$ to $BC$, the altitude from $A$ to $XY$ is $h-12$, so
\[h-12=\dfrac56h\iff h=72.\]
The area is $\dfrac12\cdot30\cdot72=\boxed{1080}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(30,0), A=(12,72);
pair X=B+(A-B)/6, Y=C+(A-C)/6;
fill(A--X--Y--cycle,rgb(0.92,0.92,0.92));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(X--Y,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((A.x,0)--A,dashed);
label("$A$",A,N,fontsize(8pt)); label("$B$",B,SW,fontsize(8pt)); label("$C$",C,SE,fontsize(8pt)); label("$X$",X,W,fontsize(8pt)); label("$Y$",Y,E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$72$",(A.x,40),E,fontsize(7pt)); label("$30$",(B+C)/2+(6,0),S,fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(30,0), A=(12,72);
pair X=B+(A-B)/6, Y=C+(A-C)/6, Np=(11,0);
draw(B--C,linewidth(0.9)); draw(B--(B+0.3*(A-B)),linewidth(0.9)); draw(C--(C+0.3*(A-C)),linewidth(0.9));
draw(X--Y,blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(X--Np--Y,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(Np--(Np.x,12),dashed);
real s=1.2;
draw(Np+s*unit(X-Np)--Np+s*unit(X-Np)+s*unit(Y-Np)--Np+s*unit(Y-Np));
