Both sides must be defined, so $x\ne\dfrac\pi4+k\pi$. If $\tan x$ is undefined, i.e. $x=\dfrac\pi2+k\pi$, the equation reads $-1=-9$, which is false. Otherwise put $t=\tan x$, with $t\ne\pm1$. By the addition formulas,
\[\tan\left(\dfrac\pi4+x\right)=\dfrac{1+t}{1-t},\qquad\tan\left(\dfrac\pi4-x\right)=\dfrac{1-t}{1+t},\]
so the equation becomes
\[(1+t)^2=9(1-t)^2\iff1+t=\pm3(1-t)\iff t=\dfrac12\ \text{or}\ t=2.\]
So the solutions are $x=\arctan\dfrac12+k\pi$ and $x=\arctan2+k\pi$. Both $\arctan\dfrac12\approx0.4636$ and $\arctan2\approx1.1071$ lie in $\left(0,\dfrac\pi2\right)$, and every other positive solution exceeds $\pi$. So the two smallest positive solutions are
\[\boxed{x_1=\arctan\dfrac12,\qquad x_2=\arctan2}.\]
Since $\tan x_2=2=\dfrac{1}{\tan x_1}=\tan\left(\dfrac\pi2-x_1\right)$, and both $x_2$ and $\dfrac\pi2-x_1$ lie in $\left(0,\dfrac\pi2\right)$, where $\tan$ is one-to-one, we get $x_2=\dfrac\pi2-x_1$, i.e. $x_1+x_2=\dfrac\pi2$. (Their product, $\arctan\dfrac12\cdot\arctan2\approx0.5133$, has no simpler closed form.)

