In the right triangle $ABE$ (right angle at $E$), the angle at $A$ is $\angle BAE=\angle BAC=A$, because $E$ lies on segment $AC$. Hence $AE=AB\cos A$. Similarly, in the right triangle $ACF$, $AF=AC\cos A$. Therefore
\[\dfrac{AE}{AB}=\dfrac{AF}{AC}=\cos A,\]
and the triangles $AEF$ and $ABC$ have the same angle at $A$ (since $E$ is on ray $AC$ and $F$ on ray $AB$). By SAS similarity, $\triangle AEF\sim\triangle ABC$ with $A\to A$, $E\to B$, $F\to C$. In particular $\angle AEF=\angle ABC$ and $\angle AFE=\angle ACB$.

The same argument at $B$ gives $BD=BA\cos B$ and $BF=BC\cos B$, so $\dfrac{BD}{BA}=\dfrac{BF}{BC}=\cos B$ and $\triangle BDF\sim\triangle BAC$ ($D\to A$, $F\to C$). At $C$, $CD=CA\cos C$ and $CE=CB\cos C$, so $\triangle CDE\sim\triangle CAB$ ($D\to A$, $E\to B$). $\blacksquare$

