question:
Find all pairs of sets $A$ and $B$, each consisting of three positive integers, such that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the largest element of $A$ is $12$;
\item[(ii)] the largest element of $B$ is $6$;
\item[(iii)] $A\cap B$ has at least one element;
\item[(iv)] for every $a\in A$ there is $b\in B$ such that $a+b$ is a perfect square;
\item[(v)] for every $x\in A$ there is $y\in B$ such that $xy$ is a perfect square.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
One solution is $(x,y)=(-1,1)$. If $(x,y)$ is any solution, then $4(x+1)=-7(y-1)$, and since $\gcd(4,7)=1$, $7\mid x+1$. Writing $x+1=7t$ gives $y-1=-4t$. So the integer solutions are exactly
\[x=-1+7t,\qquad y=1-4t\qquad(t\in\mathbb Z).\]
The conditions become:
\begin{itemize}
\item $-99\le-1+7t\le99\iff-14\le t\le14$ (since $7t$ must lie in $[-98,100]$);
\item $-99\le1-4t\le99\iff-24\le t\le25$ (since $4t$ must lie in $[-98,100]$).
\end{itemize}
Both hold exactly for $-14\le t\le14$, which gives $\boxed{29}$ pairs.

