Po-Shen Loh's method for quadratics writes the two roots of $x^2+bx+c=0$ symmetrically about their average, as $-\dfrac b2\pm u$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Use this idea and Vieta's formulas to derive the quadratic formula $x=-\dfrac b2\pm\sqrt{\dfrac{b^2}{4}-c}$.
\item[(b)] For a cubic $x^3+px^2+qx+r$, show that the substitution $x=y-\dfrac p3$ (centring at the average of the roots) gives a cubic in $y$ with no $y^2$ term.
\item[(c)] Use (b) to solve $x^3-6x^2+11x-6=0$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $\gamma=\angle ACB$ and let the bisector meet $AB$ at $D$, with $CD=6$.

\textbf{The half-angle.} Triangles $ACD$ and $BCD$ make up triangle $ABC$, so comparing areas,
\[\dfrac12\cdot5\cdot8\sin\gamma=\dfrac12\cdot5\cdot6\sin\dfrac\gamma2+\dfrac12\cdot8\cdot6\sin\dfrac\gamma2.\]
Using $\sin\gamma=2\sin\dfrac\gamma2\cos\dfrac\gamma2$ and dividing by $\sin\dfrac\gamma2>0$: $80\cos\dfrac\gamma2=78$, so
\[\cos\dfrac\gamma2=\dfrac{39}{40},\qquad\sin\dfrac\gamma2=\sqrt{1-\dfrac{1521}{1600}}=\dfrac{\sqrt{79}}{40}.\]
Hence
\[\cos\gamma=2\cdot\dfrac{1521}{1600}-1=\dfrac{721}{800},\qquad\sin\gamma=2\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt{79}}{40}\cdot\dfrac{39}{40}=\dfrac{39\sqrt{79}}{800}.\]

\textbf{The third side.} By the law of cosines,
\[AB^2=25+64-80\cdot\dfrac{721}{800}=89-\dfrac{721}{10}=\dfrac{169}{10},\qquad AB=\dfrac{13}{\sqrt{10}}=\dfrac{13\sqrt{10}}{10}.\]

\textbf{The circumradius.} By the extended law of sines, $R=\dfrac{AB}{2\sin\gamma}$:
\[R=\dfrac{13}{\sqrt{10}}\cdot\dfrac{400}{39\sqrt{79}}=\dfrac{400}{3\sqrt{790}}=\dfrac{40\sqrt{790}}{237}\approx4.744.\]
So $AB=\boxed{\dfrac{13\sqrt{10}}{10}}$ and $R=\boxed{\dfrac{40\sqrt{790}}{237}}$. (This avoids Heron's formula: the bisector gives the half-angle directly, and the law of sines gives $R$.)

