draw(arc((-1,0),1,180,0)--arc((1,0),1,180,0),linewidth(0.8));
draw(arc(A,2,0,-45),linewidth(0.8)); draw(arc(A,2,225,180),linewidth(0.8));
draw(D--C--B,linewidth(0.8)); draw(D--A--B);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(E1);dot(F);
label("$A$",A,S);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,S);label("$D$",D,W);label("$E$",E1,W);label("$F$",F,E);
\end{asy}

$\textbf{(A) }4+2\pi\qquad\textbf{(B) }4\pi\qquad\textbf{(C) }1+4\pi\qquad\textbf{(D) }2+4\pi\qquad\textbf{(E) }1+6\pi$

solution:
\textbf{The shape.} The diameters are $AE=AF=2$. Two circles tangent at $A$ have a common tangent line at $A$, which is perpendicular to both diameters $AE$ and $AF$; since the semicircles are externally tangent, $E$ and $F$ are on opposite sides of $A$, so $E$, $A$, $F$ lie on one line. The arc $ED$ is centred at $A$, so $AD=AE=2$, and similarly $AB=AF=2$: the square has side $2$. The angles at $A$ on the side of line $EF$ containing the square add up to $180^\circ$:
\[\angle EAD+\angle DAB+\angle BAF=180^\circ,\qquad\angle DAB=90^\circ,\]
and the congruent arcs $ED$ and $FB$ (same radius $2$) have equal central angles, so $\angle EAD=\angle BAF=45^\circ$.

