draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(C--L,dashed);
draw((0.25,0)--(0.25,0.25)--(0,0.25));
dot(v[1]);dot(v[2]);dot(v[3]); filldraw(circle(L,0.06),white,black);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,SW);
label("$v_1$",v[1],NE,fontsize(8pt));label("$v_2$",v[2],NE,fontsize(8pt));label("$v_3$",v[3],NE,fontsize(8pt));label("$L$",L,SW,fontsize(8pt));
label("$3$",(0,1.5),W);label("$4$",(2,0),S);
\end{asy}

Triangles $v_nAC$ and $BAC$ have the same height from $C$ to line $AB$, so their areas are in the ratio $Av_n:AB=t_n$. The area of $ABC$ is $\dfrac12\cdot3\cdot4=6$, hence
\[[v_nAC]=6t_n=4+2\left(-\dfrac12\right)^n,\qquad[v_{4912}AC]=4+\dfrac{1}{2^{4911}}.\]
The closest integer is $\boxed{4}$.

The areas are $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}t^2$ and $s^2$.

