Setting them equal gives
\[\left(\dfrac st\right)^2=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4},\qquad\dfrac st=\dfrac{\sqrt[4]{3}}{2},\qquad\left(\dfrac st\right)^n=\dfrac{\left(\sqrt[4]{3}\right)^n}{2^n}.\]
This is rational exactly when $\left(\sqrt[4]{3}\right)^n$ is rational. Write $n=4k+r$ with $r\in\{0,1,2,3\}$; then $\left(\sqrt[4]{3}\right)^n=3^k\left(\sqrt[4]{3}\right)^r$. If $r\ne0$ and $\left(\sqrt[4]{3}\right)^r=\dfrac ab$ with positive integers $a,b$, then $3^rb^4=a^4$; the exponent of $3$ is $\equiv r\pmod4$ on the left and $\equiv0\pmod4$ on the right, a contradiction. So the expression is rational exactly when $4\mid n$, and the smallest such $n$ is $4$:
\[\left(\dfrac st\right)^4=\dfrac{3}{16}.\]
Thus $n+p+q=4+3+16=\boxed{23}$.

The edge lengths are positive integers whose product is $2^{100}$, so each is a power of $2$: $2^a$, $2^b$, $2^c$ with $a+b+c=100$.

