The condition $2^a\ge512=2^9$ means $a\ge9$, and similarly $b,c\ge9$. Put $a'=a-9$, $b'=b-9$, $c'=c-9$; then $a',b',c'$ are nonnegative integers with
\[a'+b'+c'=100-27=73.\]
By stars and bars, the number of ordered solutions is $\dbinom{73+2}{2}=\dbinom{75}{2}=\boxed{2775}$.

question:
Call a real number $n$ \emph{impossible} if all of the following hold:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\dfrac{2n}{3}$ is an integer;
\item $0\le n\le150$;
\item when the integer $\dfrac{2n}{3}$ is divided by $4$, the sum of the quotient and the remainder is greater than $25$.
\end{itemize}
Find the sum of all impossible numbers.

solution:
Let $k=\dfrac{2n}{3}$. The first two conditions say that $k$ is an integer with $0\le k\le100$. Write $k=4q+r$ with $0\le r\le3$; we need $q+r\ge26$.

Since $k\le100$, $q\le25$. If $q=25$, then $k\ge100$ forces $k=100$, $r=0$ and $q+r=25$: not enough. If $q\le24$, then $q+r\le q+3$, so we need $q\ge23$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $q=24$: $r\ge2$, giving $k=98$ or $99$;
\item $q=23$: $r=3$, giving $k=95$.
\end{itemize}
So $k\in\{95,98,99\}$ and $n=\dfrac{3k}{2}\in\{142.5,\ 147,\ 148.5\}$, all in $[0,150]$. The sum is
\[142.5+147+148.5=\boxed{438}.\]

question:
A square has its four vertices and the midpoints of its four sides marked, $8$ points in all.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many segments join two of the marked points? (A segment is counted once: the segment from $C$ to $E$ is the same as the segment from $E$ to $C$.)
\item[(b)] How many different lines pass through at least two of the marked points?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} A segment is determined by an unordered pair of the $8$ points, so there are $\dbinom82=\boxed{28}$ segments.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(2,0),C=(2,2),D=(0,2);
pair[] P={A,B,C,D,(1,0),(2,1),(1,2),(0,1)};
for(int i=0;i<8;++i) for(int j=i+1;j<8;++j) draw(P[i]--P[j],gray(0.7)+linewidth(0.4));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
for(int i=0;i<8;++i) dot(P[i]);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} All marked points lie on the boundary of the square. A line that is not one of the four side lines meets the boundary of the (convex) square in at most two points, so it contains at most two marked points. Each side line contains exactly three marked points (two vertices and a midpoint), and the $\dbinom32=3$ segments joining them lie on this one line. So the $28$ segments lie on
\[28-4\cdot(3-1)=\boxed{20}\]
different lines.

question:
Let $S_n=\dfrac14+\dfrac1{4^2}+\dfrac1{4^3}+\cdots+\dfrac1{4^n}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $S_n=\dfrac13\left(1-\dfrac1{4^n}\right)$, and deduce that
\[\dfrac14+\dfrac1{4^2}+\dfrac1{4^3}+\cdots=\dfrac13.\]
\item[(b)] A unit square is cut into four equal quarter-squares and the bottom-left quarter is shaded; the top-right quarter is then cut into four equal squares in the same way, its bottom-left one is shaded, and so on forever. Explain, using the picture, why the shaded area $\dfrac14+\dfrac1{4^2}+\cdots$ is $\dfrac13$ of the square.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Multiply by $4$ and subtract:
\[4S_n-S_n=\left(1+\dfrac14+\cdots+\dfrac1{4^{n-1}}\right)-\left(\dfrac14+\cdots+\dfrac1{4^n}\right)=1-\dfrac1{4^n},\]
so $S_n=\dfrac13\left(1-\dfrac1{4^n}\right)$. As $n$ grows, $\dfrac1{4^n}\to0$, so the partial sums approach $\dfrac13$, which is what the infinite sum means:
\[\dfrac14+\dfrac1{4^2}+\dfrac1{4^3}+\cdots=\boxed{\dfrac13}.\]

\textbf{(b)}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=4;
void sq(pair o,real w,int lev){
  fill(box(o,o+(w/2,w/2)),rgb(0.45,0.65,1));
  draw(box(o,o+(w,w)));
  draw((o.x+w/2,o.y)--(o.x+w/2,o.y+w)); draw((o.x,o.y+w/2)--(o.x+w,o.y+w/2));
  if(lev>0) sq(o+(w/2,w/2),w/2,lev-1);
}
sq((0,0),s,5);
draw(box((0,0),(s,s)),linewidth(1));
label("$\dfrac14$",(s/4,s/4)); label("$\dfrac1{16}$",(5s/8,5s/8),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

At the first stage, the square minus its top-right quarter is an L-shape made of three equal quarter-squares, one of which is shaded: exactly $\dfrac13$ of the L-shape is shaded. The same happens inside the top-right quarter at the next stage, and so on. These L-shapes fill the whole square except the ever smaller top-right corner square, whose area $\dfrac1{4^n}$ shrinks to $0$. Since every L-shape is one-third shaded, the shaded area, which is $\dfrac14+\dfrac1{4^2}+\cdots$, is $\dfrac13$ of the area of the square, i.e. $\dfrac13$.

question:
In mathematics, an implication ``if $P$, then $Q$'' is false only when $P$ is true and $Q$ is false; in every other case it is true.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Decide whether each implication is true or false:
(i) If $2+2=3$, then $2+2=4$. \quad (ii) If $2+2=4$, then $2+2=3$. \quad (iii) If $2+2=3$, then $2+2=5$. \quad (iv) If $2+2=4$, then $2\cdot2=4$.
\item[(b)] Explain why this convention is the right one, using the statement ``for every integer $n$, if $n$ is divisible by $4$, then $n$ is even'' with $n=2$ and $n=3$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)}
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] The hypothesis $2+2=3$ is false, so the implication is \textbf{true} (whatever the conclusion).
\item[(ii)] The hypothesis is true and the conclusion $2+2=3$ is false, so the implication is \textbf{false}.
\item[(iii)] The hypothesis is false, so the implication is \textbf{true}.
\item[(iv)] Hypothesis and conclusion are both true, so the implication is \textbf{true}.
\end{itemize}
\[\boxed{\text{(i) true,\quad(ii) false,\quad(iii) true,\quad(iv) true}}\]

\textbf{(b)} The statement ``for every integer $n$, if $4\mid n$ then $n$ is even'' is a true theorem, so each of its instances must be a true implication. For $n=2$ the hypothesis is false and the conclusion true; for $n=3$ both are false. These instances are true only because an implication with a false hypothesis counts as true. An implication only promises something when its hypothesis holds; if the hypothesis fails, the promise cannot be broken.

question:
Let $X$ be a point inside triangle $ABC$. Through $X$ draw three lines parallel to the sides:
\begin{itemize}
\item the line parallel to $BC$ meets $AB$ at $U$ and $AC$ at $R$;
\item the line parallel to $CA$ meets $AB$ at $T$ and $BC$ at $Q$;
\item the line parallel to $AB$ meets $CA$ at $S$ and $BC$ at $P$.
\end{itemize}
Prove that
\[\dfrac{PQ}{BC}+\dfrac{RS}{CA}+\dfrac{TU}{AB}=1.\]

solution:
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(7,0),C=(2.2,4.6);
pair X=(2.9,1.5);
pair U=extension(X,X+(C-B),A,B), R=extension(X,X+(C-B),A,C);
pair T=extension(X,X+(C-A),A,B), Q=extension(X,X+(C-A),B,C);
pair Sp=extension(X,X+(B-A),A,C), P=extension(X,X+(B-A),B,C);
fill(X--P--Q--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1)); fill(X--R--Sp--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.95,0.85)); fill(X--T--U--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.85));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(U--R,blue); draw(T--Q,red); draw(Sp--P,deepgreen);
dot(X); dot(U);dot(R);dot(T);dot(Q);dot(Sp);dot(P);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$X$",X,1.5*dir(-65));
label("$T$",T,S);label("$U$",U,S);label("$Q$",Q,NE);label("$P$",P,NE);label("$S$",Sp,W);label("$R$",R,W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Three small similar triangles.} In triangle $XTU$ (with $T$, $U$ on $AB$), the side $XT$ is parallel to $CA$ and $XU$ is parallel to $CB$, so its angles at $T$ and $U$ equal the angles $A$ and $B$ of triangle $ABC$. Hence $\triangle TUX\sim\triangle ABC$ with ratio $t=\dfrac{TU}{AB}$, and the side $UX$ corresponds to $BC$:
\[UX=t\cdot BC.\]
Likewise, in triangle $SRX$ (with $S$, $R$ on $CA$), $XS\parallel AB$ and $XR\parallel BC$, so $\triangle SXR\sim\triangle ABC$ (with $S,X,R$ corresponding to $A,B,C$) with ratio $s=\dfrac{RS}{CA}$, and
\[XR=s\cdot BC.\]

\textbf{Along $BC$.} Quadrilateral $UXPB$ has $UX\parallel BP$ and $XP\parallel UB$, so it is a parallelogram and $BP=UX$. Quadrilateral $XRCQ$ has $XR\parallel QC$ and $XQ\parallel RC$, so it is a parallelogram and $QC=XR$.

Since $X$ is inside the triangle, it lies between $U$ and $R$ on segment $UR$, and $UR<BC$ (triangle $AUR$ is a smaller copy of $ABC$). So $BP+QC=UX+XR=UR<BC$: the points lie in the order $B,P,Q,C$, and
\[BC=BP+PQ+QC=t\cdot BC+PQ+s\cdot BC.\]
Dividing by $BC$:
\[\dfrac{PQ}{BC}+\dfrac{RS}{CA}+\dfrac{TU}{AB}=\dfrac{PQ}{BC}+s+t=1.\ \blacksquare\]

question:
Let $A$ be a positive integer.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find $\gcd(A,0)$, the greatest integer that divides both $A$ and $0$.
\item[(b)] Show that $A$ and $0$ have no positive common multiple, so that ``the least positive common multiple'' of $A$ and $0$ does not exist.
\item[(c)] Explain why it is reasonable to define $\operatorname{lcm}(A,0)=0$, by checking the identity $\gcd(a,b)\cdot\operatorname{lcm}(a,b)=ab$ and the property that every common multiple of $a$ and $b$ is a multiple of $\operatorname{lcm}(a,b)$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Every integer $d$ divides $0$, since $0=d\cdot0$. So the common divisors of $A$ and $0$ are exactly the divisors of $A$, and the greatest of them is $A$: $\gcd(A,0)=\boxed{A}$.

\textbf{(b)} The multiples of $0$ are the numbers $0\cdot k=0$, so the only multiple of $0$ is $0$ itself. Hence the only common multiple of $A$ and $0$ is $0$, which is not positive, and there is no least positive common multiple.

\textbf{(c)} With $\operatorname{lcm}(A,0)=0$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\gcd(A,0)\cdot\operatorname{lcm}(A,0)=A\cdot0=0=A\cdot0$, so the identity still holds;
\item the only common multiple of $A$ and $0$ is $0$, which is a multiple of $0$, so every common multiple is a multiple of the lcm.
\end{itemize}
So the convention $\operatorname{lcm}(A,0)=0$ keeps the usual properties: $0$ is the ``least'' common multiple in the sense of divisibility, though not in size.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Suppose the ordered pair $(x_0,y_0)$ satisfies both equations $a_1x+b_1y=c_1$ and $a_2x+b_2y=c_2$. Prove that it also satisfies the equation obtained by adding them,
\[(a_1+a_2)x+(b_1+b_2)y=c_1+c_2.\]
\item[(b)] Show by an example that the converse is false: a pair satisfying the sum of two equations need not satisfy either of them.
\item[(c)] Use the idea of (a) to solve the system $x+y=7$, $x-y=1$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{Why ordinary mass points get stuck.} The transversal $EF$ cuts two sides through $A$, so $A$ would need one mass to balance $B$ about $F$ (where $AF:FB=1:3$ needs mass $3$ at $A$ for mass $1$ at $B$) and another to balance $C$ about $E$ (where $AE:EC=1:1$ needs equal masses). The fix is to \emph{split} the mass at $A$.

\textbf{Split masses.} Put mass $1$ at $B$ and $1$ at $C$, so $D$, the midpoint of $BC$, gets mass $2$.
\begin{itemize}
\item On $AB$: $AF:FB=1:3$, so the part of $A$'s mass used with $B$ is $3$, and $F$ gets $3+1=4$.
\item On $AC$: $E$ is the midpoint, so the part of $A$'s mass used with $C$ is $1$, and $E$ gets $1+1=2$.
\end{itemize}
So $A$ has total mass $3+1=4$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(6,0),A=(1.6,4.2);
pair D=(B+C)/2, E1=(A+C)/2, F=A+(B-A)/4;
pair X=extension(E1,F,A,D);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,blue); draw(E1--F,red);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(E1);dot(F);dot(X);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$E$",E1,NE);label("$F$",F,W);label("$X$",X,dir(-20));
label("$3+1$",A+(0.7,0.1),fontsize(8pt)+deepgreen); label("$1$",B+(-0.3,0.35),fontsize(8pt)+deepgreen); label("$1$",C+(0.3,0.35),fontsize(8pt)+deepgreen);
label("$2$",D+(0.35,0.3),fontsize(8pt)+deepgreen); label("$2$",E1+(0.1,-0.45),fontsize(8pt)+deepgreen); label("$4$",F+(-0.2,0.5),fontsize(8pt)+deepgreen);
\end{asy}

The centre of mass of the whole system lies on $AD$ (mass $4$ at $A$, mass $2$ at $D$) and on $EF$ (mass $2$ at $E$, mass $4$ at $F$), so it is $X$. On $AD$ the distances are inversely proportional to the masses:
\[AX:XD=2:4=1:2,\qquad\dfrac{AX}{AD}=\boxed{\dfrac13}.\]
(Also $EX:XF=4:2=2:1$.)

\textbf{Check with vectors.} Take $A$ as origin and write $\vec b=\overrightarrow{AB}$, $\vec c=\overrightarrow{AC}$. Then $D=\dfrac{\vec b+\vec c}{2}$, $E=\dfrac{\vec c}2$, $F=\dfrac{\vec b}4$. A point of $AD$ is $\lambda\dfrac{\vec b+\vec c}{2}$ and a point of $EF$ is $(1-\mu)\dfrac{\vec c}2+\mu\dfrac{\vec b}4$. Comparing coefficients of $\vec b$ and $\vec c$: $\dfrac\lambda2=\dfrac\mu4$ and $\dfrac\lambda2=\dfrac{1-\mu}2$, so $\mu=\dfrac23$ and $\lambda=\dfrac13$, confirming $\dfrac{AX}{AD}=\dfrac13$.

In a parallelogram the opposite sides are parallel and equal: $AB\parallel CD$ and $AD=BC=10$. Since $E$ lies on line $CD$, segment $DE$ is parallel to $AB$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(16,0),C=B+10*dir(60),D=A+(C-B),E1=D+(-4,0);
pair F=extension(B,E1,A,D);
fill(A--B--F--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1)); fill(D--E1--F--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.85));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(D--E1,dashed); draw(B--E1,red);
draw(arc(B,1.6,60,180)); label("$120^\circ$",B+2.6*dir(120),fontsize(8pt));
dot(F); dot(E1);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,N);label("$E$",E1,NW);label("$F$",F,dir(160));
label("$16$",(A+B)/2,S);label("$10$",(B+C)/2,E);label("$4$",(D+E1)/2,N);
\end{asy}

In triangles $FAB$ and $FDE$, the angles at $F$ are vertical angles, and $\angle FAB=\angle FDE$ because they are alternate angles for the parallel lines $AB$ and $DE$. So $\triangle FAB\sim\triangle FDE$ with ratio $\dfrac{AB}{DE}=\dfrac{16}{4}=4$, hence $AF=4\,FD$. Since $F$ lies on segment $AD$,
\[AF+FD=AD=10\implies5\,FD=10\implies FD=2.\]
So $FD$ is exactly $2$, answer $\boxed{\textbf{(B) }2}$. (The angle $120^\circ$ is not needed.)

question:
In the figure, $A$, $B$, $C$ lie on a line with $B$ between $A$ and $C$, point $D$ lies on segment $BE$, and segments $EA$ and $CD$ are drawn. If $\angle A=60^\circ$, $\angle E=40^\circ$ and $\angle C=30^\circ$ (that is, $\angle EAB=60^\circ$, $\angle AEB=40^\circ$ and $\angle BCD=30^\circ$), then $\angle BDC=$

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(2.2,0);
pair E1=extension(A,A+dir(60),B,B+dir(100));
pair C=(5.2,0);
pair D=extension(C,C+dir(150),B,E1);
draw(A--C,linewidth(0.8)); draw(A--E1--B,linewidth(0.8)); draw(C--D,linewidth(0.8));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(E1);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,NE);label("$E$",E1,N);
\end{asy}

$\textbf{(A) }40^\circ\qquad\textbf{(B) }50^\circ\qquad\textbf{(C) }60^\circ\qquad\textbf{(D) }70^\circ\qquad\textbf{(E) }80^\circ$

solution:
In triangle $ABE$, the angles add up to $180^\circ$, so
\[\angle ABE=180^\circ-60^\circ-40^\circ=80^\circ.\]
Since $A$, $B$, $C$ are collinear and $D$ lies on ray $BE$, the angle $\angle DBC$ is supplementary to $\angle ABE$:
\[\angle DBC=180^\circ-80^\circ=100^\circ.\]
In triangle $BDC$,
\[\angle BDC=180^\circ-100^\circ-30^\circ=50^\circ.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(2.2,0);
pair E1=extension(A,A+dir(60),B,B+dir(100));
pair C=(5.2,0);
pair D=extension(C,C+dir(150),B,E1);
draw(A--C,linewidth(0.8)); draw(A--E1--B,linewidth(0.8)); draw(C--D,linewidth(0.8));
draw(arc(A,0.5,0,60)); label("$60^\circ$",A+0.85*dir(30),fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc(E1,0.45,240,280)); label("$40^\circ$",E1+0.42*dir(236),W,fontsize(8pt));
