The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(B) }50^\circ}$.

Each row consists of $5$ circles of diameter $6$, tangent to each other and to two opposite sides, so the side of the square is $5\cdot6=30$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<5;++i) for(int j=0;j<5;++j) filldraw(circle((6i+3,6j+3),3),rgb(0.85,0.92,1),black);
draw(box((0,0),(30,30)),linewidth(1));
draw((3,3)--(6,3),red); label("$3$",(4.5,3),S,red+fontsize(8pt));
draw((0,-2)--(30,-2),Arrows(4)); label("$30$",(15,-2),S);
\end{asy}

The circles cover $25\cdot\pi\cdot3^2=225\pi$ square inches, and the square has area $30^2=900$. The ratio is
\[\dfrac{225\pi}{900-225\pi}=\boxed{\textbf{(C) }\dfrac{\pi}{4-\pi}}.\]
(The answer does not depend on the radius or on the number of circles: each circle of radius $r$ sits in its own $2r\times2r$ cell, and covers $\dfrac{\pi r^2}{4r^2}=\dfrac\pi4$ of it. A single circle inscribed in the square gives the same ratio.)

Place the cube as $[-6,6]^3$. The square on the face $z=6$ has vertices $(\pm5,\pm5,6)$, and similarly for the other faces; the $24$ vertices are the points with one coordinate $\pm6$ and the other two $\pm5$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
triple V=unit((2.0,1.1,0.9));
triple e1=unit(cross((0,0,1),V)); triple e2=cross(V,e1);
pair pr(triple p){return (dot(p,e1),dot(p,e2));}
triple[][] faces;
for(int k=0;k<3;++k) for(int s=-1;s<=1;s+=2){
  triple[] f; real[][] q={{5,5},{-5,5},{-5,-5},{5,-5}};
  for(int i=0;i<4;++i){real[] c=new real[3]; c[k]=6s; c[(k+1)%3]=q[i][0]; c[(k+2)%3]=q[i][1]; f.push((c[0],c[1],c[2]));}
  faces.push(f);}
for(int k=0;k<3;++k) for(int si=-1;si<=1;si+=2) for(int sj=-1;sj<=1;sj+=2){
  int i=k, j=(k+1)%3, m=(k+2)%3;
  triple mk(real a,real bb,real c){real[] r=new real[3]; r[i]=a; r[j]=bb; r[m]=c; return (r[0],r[1],r[2]);}
  faces.push(new triple[]{mk(6si,5sj,5),mk(6si,5sj,-5),mk(5si,6sj,-5),mk(5si,6sj,5)});}
for(int sx=-1;sx<=1;sx+=2) for(int sy=-1;sy<=1;sy+=2) for(int sz=-1;sz<=1;sz+=2)
  faces.push(new triple[]{(6sx,5sy,5sz),(5sx,6sy,5sz),(5sx,5sy,6sz)});
real[] cb={-6,6};
for(int a=0;a<2;++a) for(int c=0;c<2;++c){
  draw(pr((cb[a],cb[c],-6))--pr((cb[a],cb[c],6)),gray(0.7)+dashed);
  draw(pr((cb[a],-6,cb[c]))--pr((cb[a],6,cb[c])),gray(0.7)+dashed);
  draw(pr((-6,cb[a],cb[c]))--pr((6,cb[a],cb[c])),gray(0.7)+dashed);}
for(triple[] f:faces){
  triple g=(0,0,0); for(triple p:f) g+=p;
  if(dot(g,V)>0){
    path pth=pr(f[0]); for(int i=1;i<f.length;++i) pth=pth--pr(f[i]); pth=pth--cycle;
    bool sqf=(f.length==4) && ((f[0].x==f[1].x && f[1].x==f[2].x) || (f[0].y==f[1].y && f[1].y==f[2].y) || (f[0].z==f[1].z && f[1].z==f[2].z));
    pen fc=(f.length==3)?rgb(1,0.85,0.75):(sqf?rgb(0.8,0.88,1):rgb(0.85,0.95,0.85));
    filldraw(pth,fc,black+linewidth(0.7));}}
label("$10$",pr((6,0,-5)),N,fontsize(8pt));
pair qq=pr((5.5,-5,5.5)); draw(qq--qq+(-1.6,1.6),linewidth(0.4)); label("$\sqrt2$",qq+(-1.6,1.6),NW,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{The faces.} Every vertex satisfies $|x|,|y|,|z|\le6$, $|x|+|y|\le11$ (and similarly for the other pairs of coordinates), and $|x|+|y|+|z|\le16$. So the polyhedron lies on one side of each of the following planes, and each plane contains at least three non-collinear vertices, so it meets the polyhedron in a face:
\begin{itemize}
\item the $6$ planes such as $z=6$: the drawn $10\times10$ squares, total area $6\cdot100=600$;
\item the $12$ planes such as $y+z=11$ (one for each edge of the cube): the vertices $(\pm5,5,6)$ and $(\pm5,6,5)$ form a rectangle with sides $10$ and $\sqrt{(6-5)^2+(5-6)^2}=\sqrt2$, total area $12\cdot10\sqrt2=120\sqrt2$;
\item the $8$ planes such as $x+y+z=16$ (one for each corner): the vertices $(6,5,5)$, $(5,6,5)$, $(5,5,6)$ form an equilateral triangle with side $\sqrt2$, area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot2=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$, total $4\sqrt3$.
\end{itemize}
These $26$ polygons fit together edge to edge (each side of a square is a long side of a rectangle, and each short side of a rectangle is a side of a triangle), so they form the whole surface; as a check, $V-E+F=24-48+26=2$. So the surface area is $600+4\sqrt3+120\sqrt2$, while the cube's surface area is $6\cdot12^2=864$. The ratio is
\[\dfrac{600+4\sqrt3+120\sqrt2}{864}=\dfrac{150+\sqrt3+30\sqrt2}{216},\]
and $150,1,30,216$ have no common factor. (Since $1$, $\sqrt2$, $\sqrt3$ are linearly independent over the rationals, this representation is unique.) Hence $a+b+c+d=150+1+30+216=\boxed{397}$.

Two facts about a regular hexagon:
\begin{itemize}
\item A regular hexagon inscribed in a circle of radius $r$ has side $r$, since it splits into six equilateral triangles with a common vertex at the centre.
\item The circle inscribed in a regular hexagon with side $s$ has radius equal to the height of one of these equilateral triangles, $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] H1,H2;
for(int k=0;k<6;++k){H1.push(dir(60k)); H2.push((sqrt(3)/2)*dir(60k+30));}
draw(H1[0]--H1[1]--H1[2]--H1[3]--H1[4]--H1[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle((0,0),sqrt(3)/2),blue);
draw(H2[0]--H2[1]--H2[2]--H2[3]--H2[4]--H2[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.7));
draw(circle((0,0),3/4),red);
pair O=(0,0); dot(O); label("$O$",O,SW,fontsize(8pt));
draw(O--(sqrt(3)/2)*dir(-90),blue); label("$r_1$",(sqrt(3)/2)*dir(-90)*0.55,E,blue+fontsize(8pt));
draw(O--(3/4)*dir(0),red); label("$r_2$",(3/8,0),N,red+fontsize(8pt));
label("$1$",(H1[4]+H1[5])/2,S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

So $r_1=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\cdot1$, and the next hexagon has side $r_1$, so $r_2=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}r_1$. In general $r_{k+1}=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}r_k$, and
\[r_n=\left(\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)^n,\qquad r_{10}=\dfrac{\left(\sqrt3\right)^{10}}{2^{10}}=\dfrac{3^5}{2^{10}}=\boxed{\dfrac{243}{1024}}.\]

Let the area of $ABC$ be $1$. We have $AP=\dfrac13AB$, $BQ=\dfrac13BC$, $CR=\dfrac13CA$, so $PB=\dfrac23AB$, $QC=\dfrac23BC$, $RA=\dfrac23CA$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(3,0),C=(1.5,1.5*sqrt(3));
pair P=A+(B-A)/3,Q=B+(C-B)/3,R=C+(A-C)/3;
fill(P--Q--R--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
fill(P--B--Q--cycle,rgb(1,0.9,0.85)); fill(Q--C--R--cycle,rgb(1,0.9,0.85)); fill(R--A--P--cycle,rgb(1,0.9,0.85));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(P--Q--R--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.8));
for(int k=1;k<=2;++k){dot(A+(B-A)*k/3); dot(B+(C-B)*k/3); dot(C+(A-C)*k/3);}
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$P$",P,S);label("$Q$",Q,NE);label("$R$",R,W);
label("$\dfrac29$",(P+B+Q)/3+(0.12,-0.05),fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

Triangle $PBQ$ shares the angle at $B$ with triangle $ABC$, so its area is
\[\dfrac{PB}{AB}\cdot\dfrac{BQ}{BC}\cdot[ABC]=\dfrac23\cdot\dfrac13=\dfrac29.\]
In the same way, triangles $QCR$ and $RAP$ each have area $\dfrac29$. These three corner triangles and triangle $PQR$ fill triangle $ABC$, so
\[[PQR]=1-3\cdot\dfrac29=\dfrac13,\]
and the ratio is $\boxed{\dfrac13}$.

question:
A positive number is in \emph{scientific notation} when it is written as $a\times10^n$ with $1\le a<10$ and $n$ an integer, and in \emph{engineering notation} when it is written as $b\times10^m$ with $1\le b<1000$ and $m$ an integer multiple of $3$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Write $47\,300\,000$ and $0.00052$ in both notations.
\item[(b)] Explain how to convert a number from scientific notation to engineering notation, and why engineering notation fits the metric prefixes kilo ($10^3$), mega ($10^6$), milli ($10^{-3}$) and micro ($10^{-6}$).
\end{enumerate}

Let $m$ and $n$ be positive integers, and define $x$ by
\[(2m-1)^2=8n^2+x.\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $x$ always leaves remainder $1$ when divided by $8$.
\item[(b)] Show that the value of $x$ itself is not determined by this information.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Work from the inside out:
\begin{itemize}
\item $2<5$, so $f(2)=3\cdot2+6=12$;
\item $12\ge5$, so $f(12)=7\cdot12-20=64$;
\item $64\ge5$, so $f(64)=7\cdot64-20=428$.
\end{itemize}
So $f(f(f(2)))=\boxed{428}$.

Let $AB=BC=s$, so the triangle has area $\dfrac{s^2}2$. The part of a circle centred at $B$ that lies inside the right angle $ABC$ is a quarter-disc, and it is inside the triangle as long as its radius is at most the distance $\dfrac{s}{\sqrt2}$ from $B$ to $AC$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1; real s=sqrt(5*pi)*r;
pair B=(0,0),A=(0,s),C=(s,0);
fill(A--B--C--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
fill(B--arc(B,2r,0,90)--cycle,rgb(1,0.95,0.8));
fill(B--arc(B,r,0,90)--cycle,rgb(1,0.85,0.75));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(B,r,0,90),red); draw(arc(B,2r,0,90),red);
draw((0.18,0)--(0.18,0.18)--(0,0.18));
draw(B--r*dir(30),dashed); label("$r$",0.5r*dir(30),NW,fontsize(8pt));
draw(B--2r*dir(60),dashed); label("$2r$",1.4r*dir(60),NW,fontsize(8pt));
draw(B--(s/2,s/2),dotted); label("$\dfrac{s}{\sqrt2}$",(0.38*s,0.38*s),SE,fontsize(7pt));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,E);label("$s$",(0,s/2),W);label("$s$",(s/2,0),S);
\end{asy}

So the two areas are
\[\dfrac{s^2}{2}-\dfrac{\pi(2r)^2}{4}=\dfrac{s^2}2-\pi r^2,\qquad\dfrac{s^2}{2}-\dfrac{\pi r^2}{4},\]
and
\[3\left(\dfrac{s^2}2-\pi r^2\right)=2\left(\dfrac{s^2}2-\dfrac{\pi r^2}4\right)\iff\dfrac{s^2}{2}=\dfrac{5}{2}\pi r^2\iff s=r\sqrt5\cdot\sqrt\pi.\]
(Check: $\dfrac{s}{\sqrt2}=r\sqrt{\dfrac{5\pi}2}\approx2.80r\ge2r$, so the quarter-disc of radius $2r$ is indeed inside the triangle.) Since $s=n\sqrt\pi$, we get $n=r\sqrt5$ and
\[\dfrac nr=\boxed{\sqrt5}.\]

Let $O$, $P$, $Q$ be the centres of the circles with radii $6$, $2$, $3$.

\textbf{Distances.} Internal tangency puts $B$ on ray $OP$ with $OP=6-2=4$ and $OB=6$, and $C$ on ray $OQ$ with $OQ=6-3=3$ and $OC=6$. External tangency puts $A$ on segment $PQ$ with $PA=2$, $AQ=3$, $PQ=5$. Since $OP^2+OQ^2=16+9=25=PQ^2$, the angle $POQ$ is right, so $\angle BOC=90^\circ$ and
\[BC=6\sqrt2.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),P=(4,0),Q=(0,3),B=(6,0),C=(0,6),O2=(6,6);
pair A=P+(2/5)*(Q-P);
draw(circle(O,6),linewidth(0.8)); draw(circle(P,2),blue); draw(circle(Q,3),red);
draw(arc(O2,6,180,270),dashed+deepgreen); draw(B--O2,deepgreen+dotted); label("$6$",(6,3),E,deepgreen+fontsize(8pt));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--B,gray(0.6)); draw(O--C,gray(0.6)); draw(P--Q,gray(0.6));
draw((0.4,0)--(0.4,0.4)--(0,0.4));
draw(arc(A,0.45,degrees(B-A)-360,degrees(C-A)));
dot(O);dot(P);dot(Q);dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(O2);
label("$O$",O,SW);label("$P$",P,S);label("$Q$",Q,W);label("$A$",A,1.3*S);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,NW);label("$O'$",O2,NE);
label("$135^\circ$",A+0.95*unit((0.6,1.8)),fontsize(7pt));
label("$4$",(2,0),S,fontsize(8pt));label("$3$",(0,1.5),W,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{The angle at $A$.} Triangle $PAB$ is isosceles ($PA=PB=2$), so $\angle PAB=\angle PBA=\angle OBA$; similarly $QA=QC=3$ gives $\angle QAC=\angle OCA$. Since $P,A,Q$ are collinear, with $B$ and $C$ on the same side of line $PQ$,
\[\angle BAC=180^\circ-\left(\angle OBA+\angle OCA\right).\]
On the other hand, $A$ lies on segment $PQ$, inside the isosceles right triangle $OBC$, whose base angles are $45^\circ$. So $\angle ABC=45^\circ-\angle OBA$ and $\angle ACB=45^\circ-\angle OCA$, and the angle sum in triangle $ABC$ gives
\[\angle BAC=180^\circ-90^\circ+\left(\angle OBA+\angle OCA\right).\]
Adding the two expressions, $2\angle BAC=270^\circ$, so $\angle BAC=135^\circ$.

\textbf{The circumradius.} By the extended law of sines,
\[2R=\dfrac{BC}{\sin\angle BAC}=\dfrac{6\sqrt2}{\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}}=12,\qquad R=\boxed{6}.\]
(Check with coordinates $O=(0,0)$, $B=(6,0)$, $C=(0,6)$, $P=(4,0)$, $Q=(0,3)$, $A=\left(\dfrac{12}5,\dfrac65\right)$: the point $O'=(6,6)$ is at distance $6$ from $A$, $B$ and $C$, so the circumcircle is the reflection of the big circle in $BC$.)

Let the path have $r$ right, $l$ left, $u$ up and $d$ down steps. Then
\[r-l=7,\qquad u-d=7,\qquad r+l+u+d=18,\]
so $2l+2d+14=18$ and $l+d=2$. A path is an arrangement of its $18$ steps, so each case gives a multinomial coefficient:
\begin{itemize}
\item $(l,d)=(2,0)$: $r=9$, $u=7$: $\dfrac{18!}{9!\,2!\,7!}=1750320$ paths;
\item $(l,d)=(0,2)$: $u=9$, $r=7$: also $1750320$ paths;
\item $(l,d)=(1,1)$: $r=u=8$: $\dfrac{18!}{8!\,1!\,8!\,1!}=3938220$ paths.
\end{itemize}
The total is $2\cdot1750320+3938220=\boxed{7438860}$.

\textbf{Right angle.} $AB^2+BC^2=80+20=100=AC^2$, so $\angle ABC=90^\circ$ and the bisector $BD$ makes $45^\circ$ with both legs.

\textbf{Length of $BD$.} The areas of triangles $ABD$ and $DBC$ add up to the area of $ABC$:
\[\dfrac12\cdot AB\cdot BD\sin45^\circ+\dfrac12\cdot BC\cdot BD\sin45^\circ=\dfrac12\cdot AB\cdot BC,\]
so
\[BD=\dfrac{\sqrt2\cdot AB\cdot BC}{AB+BC}=\dfrac{\sqrt2\cdot40}{6\sqrt5}=\dfrac{4\sqrt{10}}{3}.\]
(By the angle bisector theorem $D$ divides $AC$ with $AD:DC=AB:BC=2:1$, which the sketch shows.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real sx=2*sqrt(5), sy=4*sqrt(5);
pair B=(0,0),C=(sx,0),A=(0,sy);
pair D=A+(2/3)*(C-A);
pair E1=C+(4*sqrt(10)/3)*unit(C-A);
pair H=(A*sx*sx+C*sy*sy)/(sx*sx+sy*sy);
fill(B--C--E1--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(C--E1,linewidth(0.9)); draw(B--E1); draw(B--D,red);
draw(B--H,dashed); label("$4$",B+0.55*(H-B),SE,fontsize(8pt));
draw((0.35,0)--(0.35,0.35)--(0,0.35));
draw(arc(B,0.9,0,45),red); draw(arc(B,1.1,45,90),red);
dot(D);dot(E1);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,S);label("$D$",D,NE);label("$E$",E1,SE);
label("$4\sqrt5$",(0,sy/2),W);label("$2\sqrt5$",(0.62*sx,-0.5));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Area of $BCE$.} The distance from $B$ to line $AC$ is $\dfrac{AB\cdot BC}{AC}=\dfrac{40}{10}=4$ (twice the area of $ABC$ divided by $AC$). Triangle $BCE$ has base $CE=BD=\dfrac{4\sqrt{10}}3$ on line $AC$ and this height, so
\[[BCE]=\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac{4\sqrt{10}}{3}\cdot4=\dfrac{8\sqrt{10}}{3}.\]
Thus $a=8$, $b=10$, $c=3$ and $a+b+c=\boxed{21}$.

question:
Euler's totient function $\varphi(n)$ counts the integers $k$ with $1\le k\le n$ and $\gcd(k,n)=1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find $\varphi(1)$.
\item[(b)] Some books define $\varphi(n)$ as ``the number of positive integers less than $n$ that are relatively prime to $n$''. What does this give for $n=1$, and why would that conflict with the rule $\varphi(mn)=\varphi(m)\varphi(n)$ for relatively prime $m$ and $n$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Write $f(x)=8x^{100}-\dfrac1x+5x^2+c$. The translation moves each point $P(x_0,y_0)$ to $P'(x_0-3,\,y_0+2)$.

So a point $(X,Y)$ lies on the new graph exactly when the point it came from, $(X+3,\,Y-2)$, lies on the old graph, that is, when $Y-2=f(X+3)$. Hence the new graph is $y=f(x+3)+2$:
\[\boxed{y=8(x+3)^{100}-\dfrac{1}{x+3}+5(x+3)^2+c+2}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return 0.25*(x-2)^2+1.5;}
real g(real x){return f(x+3)+2;}
draw((-4.9,0)--(5.8,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-0.4)--(0,6.9),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(5.8,0),E); label("$y$",(0,6.9),N);
draw(graph(f,-0.5,5),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(graph(g,-4.3,1.5),red+linewidth(0.9));
pair P=(4,f(4)), Q=(1,f(4)+2);
draw(P--(P.x-3,P.y),dashed,Arrow(5)); draw((P.x-3,P.y)--Q,dashed,Arrow(5));
dot(P); dot(Q);
label("$3$ left",(2.5,P.y),N,fontsize(8pt)); label("$2$ up",(P.x-3,P.y+1),E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$P$",P,SE,fontsize(9pt)); label("$P'$",Q,NW,fontsize(9pt));
label("$y=f(x)$",(5,f(5)),E,blue+fontsize(8pt));
label("$y=f(x+3)+2$",(-4.3,g(-4.3)),W,red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

(The sketch uses a generic curve to show the rule. A common slip is to replace $x$ by $x-3$, which moves the graph $3$ units to the \emph{right}: the point that lands at $x$ comes from $x+3$.)

question:
Let $ABCD$ be a convex quadrilateral inscribed in a circle. Let $K$ be the point on the diagonal $AC$ such that $\angle ABK=\angle DBC$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that triangles $ABK$ and $DBC$ are similar, and that triangles $CBK$ and $DBA$ are similar.
\item[(b)] Deduce Ptolemy's theorem: $AB\cdot CD+AD\cdot BC=AC\cdot BD$.
\item[(c)] Prove that $K$ is the intersection point of the diagonals $AC$ and $BD$ if and only if $BD$ bisects $\angle ABC$. Does this mean that the proof in (b) only works when $BD$ bisects $\angle ABC$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Because $ABCD$ is convex, the diagonal $BD$ lies inside $\angle ABC$, so $\angle DBC<\angle ABC$. Hence the ray from $B$ that makes the angle $\angle DBC$ with $BA$ (on the same side as $C$) lies inside $\angle ABC$ and meets the segment $AC$ at a point $K$ strictly between $A$ and $C$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
pair A=dir(200), B=dir(110), C=dir(15), D=dir(300);
real aA=degrees(A-B), aC=degrees(C-B), aD=degrees(D-B);
pair K=extension(B,B+dir(aA+(aC-aD)),A,C);
draw(unitcircle,gray);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--C); draw(B--D,blue); draw(B--K,red);
draw(arc(B,0.22,aA,aA+(aC-aD)),red); draw(arc(B,0.25,aA,aA+(aC-aD)),red); draw(arc(B,0.22,aD,aC),red); draw(arc(B,0.25,aD,aC),red);
pair Pq=extension(A,C,B,D);
dot(K); dot(Pq);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,N); label("$C$",C,E); label("$D$",D,S); label("$K$",K,S); label("$P$",Pq,NE);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} Inscribed angles on the same arc are equal: $\angle BAC=\angle BDC$ (both on arc $BC$) and $\angle BCA=\angle BDA$ (both on arc $AB$).
\begin{itemize}
\item In triangles $ABK$ and $DBC$: $\angle ABK=\angle DBC$ by the choice of $K$, and $\angle BAK=\angle BAC=\angle BDC$. So $\triangle ABK\sim\triangle DBC$, and $\dfrac{AK}{DC}=\dfrac{AB}{DB}$.
\item In triangles $CBK$ and $DBA$: $\angle KBC=\angle ABC-\angle ABK=\angle ABC-\angle DBC=\angle ABD$, and $\angle BCK=\angle BCA=\angle BDA$. So $\triangle CBK\sim\triangle DBA$, and $\dfrac{CK}{DA}=\dfrac{BC}{BD}$.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{(b)} From (a), $AK=\dfrac{AB\cdot CD}{BD}$ and $CK=\dfrac{AD\cdot BC}{BD}$. Since $K$ lies between $A$ and $C$,
\[AC=AK+KC=\dfrac{AB\cdot CD+AD\cdot BC}{BD},\]
which is $\boxed{AB\cdot CD+AD\cdot BC=AC\cdot BD}$.

\textbf{(c)} If $K$ lies on $BD$, then the rays $BK$ and $BD$ coincide, so $\angle ABD=\angle ABK=\angle DBC$ and $BD$ bisects $\angle ABC$. Conversely, if $\angle ABD=\angle DBC$, then the rays $BD$ and $BK$ both lie inside $\angle ABC$ and make the same angle with $BA$, so they coincide; then $K$ lies on both $AC$ and $BD$ and is their intersection point.

This does \emph{not} restrict the theorem. The proof never needs $K$ to be on $BD$: $K$ is simply the point of $AC$ defined by the angle condition, and it exists for every convex cyclic quadrilateral. In general $K$ is a different point of $AC$ from the intersection $P$ of the diagonals (as in the figure); the two points coincide only in the special case when $BD$ bisects $\angle ABC$. So Ptolemy's theorem holds for every convex cyclic quadrilateral. $\blacksquare$

question:
Three congruent circles lie in a row, each externally tangent to the circle next to it. Rectangle $ABCD$ is drawn so that its sides $\overline{AB}$ and $\overline{CD}$ are tangent to all three circles and its sides $\overline{BC}$ and $\overline{DA}$ are tangent to the two end circles, as shown. The rectangle has area $128$. What is the area of the shaded region, the part of the rectangle outside the three circles?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1;
filldraw(box((0,-r),(6r,r)),rgb(0.75,0.85,1),black+linewidth(0.8));
for(int i=0;i<3;++i) filldraw(circle((r+2r*i,0),r),white,black);
label("$A$",(0,r),NW); label("$B$",(6r,r),NE); label("$C$",(6r,-r),SE); label("$D$",(0,-r),SW);
\end{asy}

$\textbf{(A) }32-8\pi \qquad \textbf{(B) }32\pi \qquad \textbf{(C) }32+16\pi \qquad \textbf{(D) }64-8\pi \qquad \textbf{(E) }128-32\pi$

solution:
Let $r$ be the common radius. The centres lie on the line midway between $AB$ and $CD$, at distance $r$ from each, so $BC=2r$. Along that line, from side $DA$ we pass $r$ to the first centre, $2r$ to the second, $2r$ to the third and $r$ to side $BC$, so $AB=6r$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1;
filldraw(box((0,-r),(6r,r)),rgb(0.75,0.85,1),black+linewidth(0.8));
for(int i=0;i<3;++i) {filldraw(circle((r+2r*i,0),r),white,black); dot((r+2r*i,0));}
draw((r,0)--(r,r),red); label("$r$",(r,r/2),E,red+fontsize(8pt));
draw((3r,0)--(3r,-r),red); label("$r$",(3r,-r/2),E,red+fontsize(8pt));
draw((0,-1.35r)--(6r,-1.35r),Arrows(4)); label("$6r$",(3r,-1.35r),S,fontsize(8pt));
draw((6.35r,-r)--(6.35r,r),Arrows(4)); label("$2r$",(6.35r,0),E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$A$",(0,r),NW); label("$B$",(6r,r),NE); label("$C$",(6r,-r),SE); label("$D$",(0,-r),SW);
\end{asy}

The area of the rectangle is $6r\cdot2r=12r^2=128$, so $r^2=\dfrac{32}{3}$. The three circles have total area
\[3\pi r^2=3\pi\cdot\dfrac{32}{3}=32\pi,\]
and the shaded region has area $128-32\pi$. The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(E)}\ 128-32\pi}$.

question:
Segment $AB$ has endpoint $A=(x_1,y_1)$ and midpoint $M=(m_1,m_2)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the other endpoint is $B=(2m_1-x_1,\;2m_2-y_1)$.
\item[(b)] If $A=(-3,5)$ and $M=(2,-1)$, find $B$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let $B=(x_2,y_2)$. The midpoint formula says
\[m_1=\dfrac{x_1+x_2}{2},\qquad m_2=\dfrac{y_1+y_2}{2},\]
so $x_2=2m_1-x_1$ and $y_2=2m_2-y_1$. Equivalently, the step from $A$ to $M$ is repeated from $M$ to $B$: $B=M+(M-A)$.

\textbf{(b)} $B=(2\cdot2-(-3),\;2\cdot(-1)-5)=\boxed{(7,-7)}$. Check: going from $A$ to $M$ adds $5$ to $x$ and $-6$ to $y$; doing the same again from $M$ reaches $(7,-7)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-3,5), M=(2,-1), B=(7,-7);
draw((-4.5,0)--(8.5,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-8.5)--(0,6.5),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(8.5,0),E); label("$y$",(0,6.5),N);
draw(A--B,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--(M.x,A.y),dashed); draw(M--(M.x,A.y),dashed); draw(M--(B.x,M.y),dashed); draw(B--(B.x,M.y),dashed);
label("$+5$",(1,A.y),N,fontsize(8pt)); label("$-6$",(M.x,(A.y+M.y)/2),E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$+5$",((M.x+B.x)/2,M.y),N,fontsize(8pt)); label("$-6$",(B.x,(M.y+B.y)/2),E,fontsize(8pt));
dot(A); dot(M); dot(B);
label("$A(-3,5)$",A,W); label("$M(2,-1)$",M,SW,Fill(white)); label("$B(7,-7)$",B,SW);
\end{asy}

question:
A random number generator outputs one integer each second: in the $n$-th second it outputs an integer chosen uniformly at random from $\{1,2,\dots,n\}$, independently of the earlier outputs. Let $S_n$ be the sum of the first $n$ outputs.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the expected value $E(S_n)$ in terms of $n$.
\item[(b)] For which positive integer $n$ is $E(S_n)=280$?
\end{enumerate}

A forum post claims to prove that $x=0.\overline{9}$ satisfies $x\ge4.705$, as follows.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[1.] The series $1-1+1-1+\cdots$ has sum $\dfrac12$.
\item[2.] We have $\dfrac{x}{11}=0.\overline{09}$ and $\dfrac{10x}{11}=0.\overline{90}$, so
\[\dfrac{9x}{11}=\dfrac{10x}{11}-\dfrac{x}{11}=\dfrac{9}{10}-\dfrac{9}{100}+\dfrac{9}{1000}-\cdots.\]
\item[3.] The Cauchy--Schwarz inequality in the form $\left(\sum a_i\right)\left(\sum b_i\right)\ge\left(\sum\sqrt{a_ib_i}\right)^2$, applied to $a_i=(-1)^{i+1}$ and $b_i=(-1)^{i+1}\dfrac{9}{10^i}$ (so that $a_ib_i=\dfrac{9}{10^i}$), gives
\[\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac{9x}{11}\ge\left(\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\sqrt{\dfrac{9}{10^i}}\right)^2=\dfrac{9}{\left(\sqrt{10}-1\right)^2},\]
hence $x\ge\dfrac{22}{\left(\sqrt{10}-1\right)^2}\approx4.705$.
\end{enumerate}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] For each of steps 1, 2 and 3, decide whether it is valid, and explain exactly what is wrong with each invalid step.
\item[(b)] Prove that $0.\overline{9}=1$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $\omega_{AB}$, $\omega_{BC}$, $\omega_{CA}$ be the circles with diameters $AB$, $BC$, $CA$, and let $H_A$, $H_B$, $H_C$ be the feet of the altitudes from $A$, $B$, $C$ (on the lines $BC$, $CA$, $AB$). We use Thales' theorem: a point $P\ne A,B$ lies on $\omega_{AB}$ if and only if $\angle APB=90^\circ$.

\textbf{All intersection points.}
\begin{itemize}
\item Two sides meet only at a vertex: $A$, $B$, $C$.
\item $\omega_{AB}$ meets line $AB$ only at $A$ and $B$. It meets line $BC$ at $B$ and at the point $P$ of line $BC$ with $\angle APB=90^\circ$, which is $H_A$; similarly it meets line $CA$ at $A$ and $H_B$. The same holds for the other two circles, so every circle--side intersection is a vertex or a foot of an altitude.
\item $\omega_{BC}$ and $\omega_{CA}$ both pass through $C$. A second common point $P\ne C$ would satisfy $PB\perp PC$ and $PA\perp PC$, so $A$, $P$, $B$ are collinear and $P$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $C$ to $AB$, i.e. $P=H_C$ (and $H_C$ is indeed on both circles). Similarly $\omega_{CA}\cap\omega_{AB}=\{A,H_A\}$ and $\omega_{AB}\cap\omega_{BC}=\{B,H_B\}$.
\end{itemize}
So the intersection points are exactly $A,B,C,H_A,H_B,H_C$, whether the feet lie on the sides or on their extensions.

\textbf{Counting them.} A foot is never the vertex it is drawn from. $H_A=B$ exactly when $\angle B=90^\circ$, and $H_A=C$ exactly when $\angle C=90^\circ$; two feet can coincide only at a vertex (e.g. $H_A=H_B$ lies on lines $BC$ and $CA$, so it is $C$, and then $\angle C=90^\circ$). Hence:
\begin{itemize}
\item if the triangle has no right angle, the six points are distinct and $f=6$;
\item if, say, $\angle C=90^\circ$, then $H_A=H_B=C$ and the points are $A,B,C,H_C$, so $f=4$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0.8,3.2), B=(0,0), C=(4,0);
pair ft(pair P,pair U,pair V){return U+dot(P-U,unit(V-U))*unit(V-U);}
pair HA=ft(A,B,C), HB=ft(B,C,A), HC=ft(C,A,B);
draw(circle((A+B)/2,abs(A-B)/2),blue); draw(circle((B+C)/2,abs(B-C)/2),red); draw(circle((C+A)/2,abs(C-A)/2),deepgreen);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--HA,dashed); draw(B--HB,dashed); draw(C--HC,dashed);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(HA,red); dot(HB,red); dot(HC,red);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,E);
label("$H_A$",HA,S); label("$H_B$",HB,NE); label("$H_C$",HC,W);
\end{asy}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), B=(2.1,0), A=(0,2.0);
pair ft(pair P,pair U,pair V){return U+dot(P-U,unit(V-U))*unit(V-U);}
pair HC=ft(C,A,B);
draw(circle((A+B)/2,abs(A-B)/2),blue); draw(circle((B+C)/2,abs(B-C)/2),red); draw(circle((C+A)/2,abs(C-A)/2),deepgreen);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(C--HC,dashed);
real s=0.15; draw((s,0)--(s,s)--(0,s));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(HC,red);
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C=H_A=H_B$",C,SW,fontsize(8pt)); label("$H_C$",HC,NE);
\end{asy}

\textbf{The triangles.} Any three distinct integers from $20$ to $30$ form a triangle, since the two smallest sum to at least $20+21=41>30$. So there are $\dbinom{11}{3}=165$ triangles. A right triangle with legs $a<b$ and hypotenuse $c$ here has $a\ge20$, $b\ge21$, so $c^2\ge841$ and $c\in\{29,30\}$. For $c=29$: $a^2+b^2=841$ with $20\le a<b$ forces $a=20$, $b=21$, which works. For $c=30$: $b^2=900-a^2\ge441$ forces $a\in\{20,21\}$, and neither $900-400=500$ nor $900-441=459$ is a square. So $(20,21,29)$ is the only right triangle.

The average is
\[\dfrac{164\cdot6+1\cdot4}{165}=\dfrac{988}{165},\]
in lowest terms since $988=2^2\cdot13\cdot19$ and $165=3\cdot5\cdot11$. Hence $x+y=\boxed{1153}$.

Use coordinates attached to the can: the axis is the $z$-axis, the bottom is $z=0$, the top is $z=12$, and the cross-sections are the disc $x^2+y^2\le9$. Tilt the can so that the side $x=3$ goes down.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real t=45;
transform T=rotate(-t);
pair P(real x,real z){return T*(x,z);}
path can=P(-3,0)--P(3,0)--P(3,12)--P(-3,12)--cycle;
pair Q=P(1,12);
path liq=P(-3,0)--P(3,0)--P(3,12)--P(1,12)--P(-3,8)--cycle;
fill(liq,rgb(0.75,0.85,1));
draw(can,linewidth(0.9));
draw(P(-1,12)--P(1,12),white+linewidth(2.5));
dot(P(-1,12)); dot(Q,red);
draw(P(-3,8)--Q,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(P(-3,8)-(1.5,0)--Q+(1.5,0),dashed+gray);
draw(P(0,0)--P(0,13.5),dotted);
draw(P(0,0)--P(0,0)+(0,4),dotted);
label("hole",P(0,12)+(0,1.25),fontsize(8pt));
label("$X$",Q,NE,red+fontsize(8pt));
label("surface $z=x+11$",(P(-3,8)+Q)/2,S,blue+fontsize(8pt));
label("axis",P(0,13.5),E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$12$",(P(3,0)+P(3,12))/2,SE,fontsize(8pt)); label("$6$",(P(-3,0)+P(3,0))/2,SW,fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc(P(0,0),3,90,90-t),Arrow(4)); label("$45^\circ$",P(0,0)+3.6*dir(90-t/2),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{The surface.} When the axis makes $45^\circ$ with the vertical, a horizontal plane makes $45^\circ$ with the cross-sections, and it is higher up the can (larger $z$) on the lowered side (larger $x$). So the surface has the form $z=x+k$. The lowest point of the hole is the point $X=(1,0,12)$ of its rim nearest the lowered side; the soda just reaches it, so $12=1+k$ and the surface is $z=x+11$.

Since $x+11\ge8>0$ on the disc, the bottom is covered, and the soda fills the region below $z=\min(12,\,x+11)$. (Where $x>1$ the soda presses against the lid; it touches the hole only at $X$.)

\textbf{The volume.} Slicing parallel to the $yz$-plane, the slice at $x$ has width $2\sqrt{9-x^2}$, so
\[V=\iint_{x^2+y^2\le9}\min(12,\,x+11)\,dA=\iint(x+11)\,dA-\iint_{x>1}(x-1)\,dA.\]
By symmetry $\iint x\,dA=0$, so the first integral is $11\cdot9\pi=99\pi$. For the second,
\[\iint_{x>1}(x-1)\,dA=\int_1^3 2x\sqrt{9-x^2}\,dx-\int_1^3 2\sqrt{9-x^2}\,dx.\]
\begin{itemize}
\item $\displaystyle\int_1^3 2x\sqrt{9-x^2}\,dx=\left[-\dfrac23\left(9-x^2\right)^{\dfrac32}\right]_1^3=\dfrac23\cdot8^{\dfrac32}=\dfrac{32\sqrt2}{3}$.
\item $\displaystyle\int_1^3 2\sqrt{9-x^2}\,dx$ is the area of the part of the disc with $x\ge1$, a circular segment: $9\arccos\dfrac13-1\cdot\sqrt{9-1}=9\arccos\dfrac13-2\sqrt2$.
\end{itemize}
So the second integral is $\dfrac{32\sqrt2}{3}+2\sqrt2-9\arccos\dfrac13=\dfrac{38\sqrt2}{3}-9\arccos\dfrac13$, and the soda left is
\[V=99\pi-\dfrac{38\sqrt2}{3}+9\arccos\dfrac13.\]

\textbf{The answer.} The full can held $\pi\cdot3^2\cdot12=108\pi$, so Bob drank
\[108\pi-V=\boxed{9\pi+\dfrac{38\sqrt2}{3}-9\arccos\dfrac13\approx35.11}\]
cubic units.

Reflect $E$ in $D$ to get $E'$, so that $D$ is the midpoint of both $BC$ and $EE'$. Then $BECE'$ is a parallelogram (its diagonals bisect each other), and opposite angles of a parallelogram are equal: $\angle BE'C=\angle BEC$. Hence
\[\angle BE'C+\angle BAC=180^\circ.\]
Since $E$ lies on segment $AD$ on the same side of $BC$ as $A$, its reflection $E'$ lies on the other side of $BC$. So $ABE'C$ is a convex quadrilateral with a pair of supplementary opposite angles, hence cyclic.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(sqrt(7),0), A=(0.9,2.3);
pair D=(B+C)/2;
real R=abs(A-D); real k=7/4/abs(A-D)/abs(A-D);
pair Ep=D+k*(A-D), E2=D-k*(A-D);
pair O=extension((A+B)/2,(A+B)/2+rotate(90)*(B-A),(B+C)/2,(B+C)/2+(0,1));
draw(circle(O,abs(O-A)),gray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--E2,blue);
draw(B--Ep--C,red); draw(B--E2--C,red+dashed);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(Ep); dot(E2);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,E); label("$D$",D,SE); label("$E$",Ep,W); label("$E'$",E2,S);
\end{asy}

The chords $AE'$ and $BC$ of this circle meet at $D$, so by the intersecting chords theorem (power of the point $D$),
\[DA\cdot DE'=DB\cdot DC=\left(\dfrac{\sqrt7}{2}\right)^2=\dfrac74.\]
As $DE'=DE$, we get $DE\cdot DA=\dfrac74$, so $m+n=7+4=\boxed{11}$.

Let the polygon have $n\ge3$ vertices. Every simple polygon, convex or not, can be cut by diagonals into $n-2$ triangles, so the sum of its interior angles is $(n-2)\cdot180^\circ$. If all $n$ interior angles are equal, each of them is
\[\dfrac{(n-2)\cdot180^\circ}{n}=180^\circ-\dfrac{360^\circ}{n}<180^\circ.\]
So no interior angle is reflex (or straight). A simple polygon all of whose interior angles are less than $180^\circ$ is convex. $\blacksquare$

So an equiangular polygon is automatically convex, and its angles are all $180^\circ-\dfrac{360^\circ}{n}$. It need not be regular: a rectangle is equiangular, and so is the hexagon below, with angles $120^\circ$ and unequal sides. A polygon mixing reflex and non-reflex angles cannot have all its interior angles equal, so it is not equiangular.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V; pair p=(0,0); real[] s={3,1.2,2,2,2.2,1};
for(int i=0;i<6;++i){V.push(p); p=p+s[i]*dir(60*i);}
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
for(int i=0;i<6;++i){
  pair P=V[i], Pm=V[(i+5)%6], Pp=V[(i+1)%6];
  real a1=degrees(Pp-P), a2=degrees(Pm-P); if(a2<a1) a2+=360;
  draw(arc(P,0.3,a1,a2),red);
  label("$120^\circ$",P+0.62*unit(unit(Pp-P)+unit(Pm-P)),red+fontsize(7pt));
}
\end{asy}

question:
The integers $1$ to $16$ are placed in the $16$ circles of the figure, one in each circle, each number used once. The figure consists of four regular hexagons, arranged as shown, with a circle at each vertex. The sum of the six numbers around each hexagon is the same number $S$. What is the greatest possible value of $S$?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=sqrt(3);
pair cen(int q,int r){return (h*(q+r/2),1.5*r);}
pair[] cs={cen(0,0),cen(1,0),cen(0,1),cen(1,1)};
pair[] V;
for(int k=0;k<4;++k){
  pair c=cs[k];
  draw(c+dir(90)--c+dir(150)--c+dir(210)--c+dir(270)--c+dir(330)--c+dir(30)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
  for(int j=0;j<6;++j){pair p=c+dir(90+60*j); bool nw=true; for(pair w:V) if(abs(w-p)<1e-6) nw=false; if(nw) V.push(p);}
}
for(pair p:V) filldraw(circle(p,0.2),white,black);
\end{asy}

solution:
Look at how many hexagons each circle belongs to: the two circles where three hexagons meet belong to $3$ hexagons, the four other circles on shared edges belong to $2$ hexagons, and the remaining ten circles belong to $1$ hexagon each. (Check: $2\cdot3+4\cdot2+10\cdot1=24=4\cdot6$.)

\textbf{Upper bound.} Adding the four hexagon sums counts each number as many times as its circle's multiplicity:
\[4S=3(t_1+t_2)+2(d_1+d_2+d_3+d_4)+(\text{the other ten numbers}),\]
where $t_1,t_2$ are the numbers in the triple circles and $d_1,\dots,d_4$ those in the double circles. Equivalently, $4S=136+2(t_1+t_2)+(d_1+d_2+d_3+d_4)$, since every number is counted once in $1+2+\cdots+16=136$. This is largest when $t_1+t_2$ is as large as possible and then the $d_i$ are the next largest, so
\[4S\le136+2(16+15)+(14+13+12+11)=136+62+50=248,\qquad S\le62.\]

\textbf{The bound is attained.} Put $16$ and $15$ in the triple circles, $11$ to $14$ in the double circles and $1$ to $10$ in the others as follows (each hexagon's sum is shown in its centre):

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=sqrt(3);
pair cen(int q,int r){return (h*(q+r/2),1.5*r);}
pair[] cs={cen(0,0),cen(1,0),cen(0,1),cen(1,1)};
pair[] V;
for(int k=0;k<4;++k){
  pair c=cs[k];
  draw(c+dir(90)--c+dir(150)--c+dir(210)--c+dir(270)--c+dir(330)--c+dir(30)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
  for(int j=0;j<6;++j){pair p=c+dir(90+60*j); bool nw=true; for(pair w:V) if(abs(w-p)<1e-6) nw=false; if(nw) V.push(p);}
}
int[] num={11,6,8,10,12,15,16,1,5,13,2,4,14,3,7,9};
for(int i=0;i<V.length;++i){filldraw(circle(V[i],0.24),white,black); label("$"+string(num[i])+"$",V[i],fontsize(7pt));}
for(int k=0;k<4;++k) label("$62$",cs[k],blue+fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

For example, the bottom-left hexagon contains $11,6,8,10,12,15$, with sum $62$, and the other three hexagons also have sum $62$. Hence the greatest possible value is $S=\boxed{62}$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Suppose $a_1\equiv b_1\pmod{c_1}$ and $a_2\equiv b_2\pmod{c_2}$. Is it always true that $a_1a_2\equiv b_1b_2\pmod{c_1c_2}$?
\item[(b)] Is the converse true: if $a_1a_2\equiv b_1b_2\pmod{c_1c_2}$, must $a_1\equiv b_1\pmod{c_1}$ and $a_2\equiv b_2\pmod{c_2}$?
\item[(c)] Prove that under the hypotheses of (a), $a_1a_2\equiv b_1b_2\pmod{d}$, where $d=\gcd(c_1,c_2)$, and that $c_1c_2$ divides $(a_1-b_1)(a_2-b_2)$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the side length be $s$. The surface area is $6s^2$, and the space diagonal is $\sqrt{s^2+s^2+s^2}=s\sqrt3$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=2; pair o=(0.9,0.6);
pair A=(0,0),B=(s,0),C=(s,s),D=(0,s);
pair A2=A+o,B2=B+o,C2=C+o,D2=D+o;
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8)); draw(B--B2--C2--C,linewidth(0.8)); draw(D--D2--C2,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--A2,dashed); draw(A2--B2,dashed); draw(A2--D2,dashed);
draw(A--C2,red+linewidth(0.9));
label("$s$",(A+B)/2,S); label("$s$",(B+B2)/2,SE); label("$s$",(A+D)/2,W);
label("$s\sqrt3$",A+0.42*(C2-A),SE,red);
\end{asy}

So $6s^2=12\sqrt3\cdot s\sqrt3=36s$, and since $s>0$, $s=\boxed{6}$.

The diagonals of a rhombus bisect each other at right angles, so half-diagonals $p$ and $q$ form a right triangle with a side as hypotenuse: $p^2+q^2=\left(2\sqrt{61}\right)^2=244$. If the diagonals are $d$ and $d+4$, then $p=\dfrac{d+4}{2}$ and $q=\dfrac d2$, so
\[\left(\dfrac{d+4}{2}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac d2\right)^2=244\iff d^2+4d-480=0\iff(d-20)(d+24)=0.\]
So the diagonals are $20$ and $24$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-12,0),B=(0,10),C=(12,0),D=(0,-10);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,dashed); draw(B--D,dashed);
real m=1.2; draw((m,0)--(m,m)--(0,m));
label("$12$",(-6,0),S); label("$10$",(0,5),E);
label("$2\sqrt{61}$",(A+B)/2,NW);
\end{asy}

The area is $\dfrac{20\cdot24}{2}=\boxed{240}$.

Let the radius be $r$, so $\pi r^2=150$. A diagonal of the inscribed square is a diameter of the circle, of length $2r$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1;
draw(unitcircle,linewidth(0.8));
pair[] V={dir(45),dir(135),dir(225),dir(315)};
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(V[0]--V[2],dashed);
dot((0,0)); label("$r$",(0,0)--V[0],NW); label("$r$",(0,0)--V[2],NW);
\end{asy}

A square with diagonal $2r$ has area $\dfrac{(2r)^2}{2}=2r^2=\dfrac{300}{\pi}\approx\boxed{95.5}$.

The letters are $B,B,A,T,O,Y$: five different letters, with $B$ available twice.
\begin{itemize}
\item Three different letters: $5\cdot4\cdot3=60$ arrangements.
\item Two $B$'s and one other letter: $4$ choices for the other letter and $3$ positions for it, $12$ arrangements.
\end{itemize}
The total is $60+12=\boxed{72}$.

The side of length $15$ is the leg opposite the $15^\circ$ angle, so the other leg (opposite $75^\circ$) is $15\tan75^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=15, bb=15*(2+sqrt(3));
pair C=(0,0), B=(bb,0), A=(0,a);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((2,0)--(2,2)--(0,2));
draw(arc(B,9,180-15,180),red); label("$15^\circ$",B+20*dir(173),red+fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc(A,4,270,270+75),red); label("$75^\circ$",A+6*dir(307.5),red+fontsize(8pt));
label("$15$",(A+C)/2,W); label("$15(2+\sqrt3)$",(B+C)/2,S);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,SW);
\end{asy}

Since $\tan75^\circ=\tan(45^\circ+30^\circ)=\dfrac{1+\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}}{1-\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}}=2+\sqrt3$, the area is
\[\dfrac12\cdot15\cdot15\left(2+\sqrt3\right)=225+\dfrac{225\sqrt3}{2}\approx\boxed{419.9}.\]

question:
Let $a$ and $b$ be distinct positive numbers, and let $h=\dfrac{2ab}{a+b}$ be their harmonic mean.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $\dfrac{h-a}{b-h}=\dfrac ab$. Deduce that $h$ lies between $a$ and $b$, and that $h=a+\dfrac{a}{a+b}(b-a)$. Use this to find the harmonic mean of $12$ and $24$.
\item[(b)] A runner covers a distance $d$ at speed $a$ and then another distance $d$ at speed $b$. Show that the average speed for the whole run is $h$, and explain the ratio in (a) in terms of the times spent at each speed.
\end{enumerate}

Let $ABCD$ be a convex cyclic quadrilateral with sides $AB=a$, $BC=b$, $CD=c$, $DA=d$ and diagonals $AC=e$, $BD=f$. Ptolemy's theorem states that $ac+bd=ef$; here $a,c$ and $b,d$ are pairs of \emph{opposite} sides.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show by an example that the ``adjacent'' version $ab+cd=ef$ is false in general.
\item[(b)] Prove that $ab+cd=ef$ holds if and only if $a=d$ or $b=c$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} A $3\times4$ rectangle is cyclic, with $a=c=3$, $b=d=4$ and diagonals $e=f=5$. Ptolemy's theorem checks: $ac+bd=9+16=25=ef$. But $ab+cd=12+12=24\ne25$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=dir(205), B=dir(120), C=dir(35), D=dir(285);
draw(unitcircle,gray);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,blue); draw(B--D,blue);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,N); label("$C$",C,E); label("$D$",D,S);
label("$a$",(A+B)/2,NW); label("$b$",(B+C)/2,NE); label("$c$",(C+D)/2,SE); label("$d$",(D+A)/2,SW);
label("$e$",A+0.3*(C-A),N,blue); label("$f$",B+0.25*(D-B),E,blue);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} By Ptolemy's theorem $ef=ac+bd$, so
\[ab+cd=ef\iff ab+cd-ac-bd=0\iff a(b-c)-d(b-c)=0\iff(a-d)(b-c)=0,\]
that is, $a=d$ or $b=c$. $\blacksquare$

So the pairing in Ptolemy's theorem matters: the products must be of opposite sides. Pairing adjacent sides gives the right value only when $AB=DA$ or $BC=CD$, for example in a kite inscribed in a circle.

question:
In number theory, $\tau(n)$ usually denotes the number of positive divisors of the positive integer $n$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that if $n=p_1^{e_1}p_2^{e_2}\cdots p_k^{e_k}$ with distinct primes $p_i$, then $\tau(n)=(e_1+1)(e_2+1)\cdots(e_k+1)$.
\item[(b)] Compute $\tau(2021)$ and $\tau(10!)$.
\item[(c)] Find the smallest positive integer $n$ with $\tau(n)=12$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Each interior angle of a regular pentagon is $\dfrac{3\cdot180^\circ}{5}=108^\circ$, and all sides are equal.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V; for(int i=0;i<5;++i) V.push(dir(90+72*i));
pair A=V[0],B=V[1],C=V[2],D=V[3],Ep=V[4],O=(0,0);
draw(A--B--C--D--Ep--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--A,blue); draw(O--B,blue); draw(O--C,red); draw(O--D,red); draw(O--Ep,red);
dot(O);
for(int i=0;i<5;++i){
  pair P=V[i], Pp=V[(i+1)%5], Pm=V[(i+4)%5];
  real a1=degrees(Pp-P), a2=degrees(O-P), a3=degrees(Pm-P);
  if(a2<a1) a2+=360; if(a3<a2) a3+=360;
  draw(arc(P,0.16,a1,a2),gray); draw(arc(P,0.2,a2,a3),gray);
}
label("$54^\circ$",B+0.42*dir(degrees(O-B)+25),fontsize(7pt)); label("$54^\circ$",B+0.42*dir(degrees(O-B)-25),fontsize(7pt));
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$D$",D,SE); label("$E$",Ep,E); label("$O$",O,S);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Triangle $OAB$.} Since $AO$ and $BO$ are bisectors, $\angle OAB=\angle OBA=54^\circ$, so triangle $OAB$ is isosceles with $OA=OB$; also $O$ lies inside the pentagon, since both bisectors run into its interior.

\textbf{$OC$ bisects $\angle C$.} Triangles $OBA$ and $OBC$ are congruent by SAS: $BA=BC$, $\angle OBA=\angle OBC=54^\circ$ (as $BO$ bisects $\angle B$), and $OB$ is common. Hence $\angle OCB=\angle OAB=54^\circ=\dfrac12\cdot108^\circ$, so $\angle OCD=108^\circ-54^\circ=54^\circ$ and $OC$ bisects $\angle C$. Also $OC=OA$.

\textbf{Repeat.} Now triangles $OCB$ and $OCD$ are congruent by SAS ($CB=CD$, $\angle OCB=\angle OCD$, $OC$ common), so $\angle ODC=\angle OBC=54^\circ$ and $OD$ bisects $\angle D$. In the same way triangles $ODC$ and $ODE$ are congruent, so $\angle OED=\angle OCD=54^\circ$ and $OE$ bisects $\angle E$. $\blacksquare$

(So $O$ is equidistant from all five vertices: it is the centre of the pentagon.)

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Is the following statement true? ``If an integer is divisible by $a$ and by $b$, then it is divisible by $ab$.''
\item[(b)] Prove that the statement is true when $a$ and $b$ are relatively prime positive integers.
\end{enumerate}

$ABCD$ is a trapezoid with $AB\parallel CD$, and its diagonals $AC$ and $BD$ meet at $M$. Write $[XYZ]$ for the area of triangle $XYZ$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $[AMB]\cdot[CMD]=[AMD]\cdot[BMC]$. (This holds in every convex quadrilateral.)
\item[(b)] Prove that $[AMD]=[BMC]$.
\item[(c)] Deduce that $[AMD]=\sqrt{[AMB]\cdot[CMD]}$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Triangles $AMB$ and $AMD$ have the same vertex $A$ and bases $MB$ and $MD$ on the line $BD$, so they have the same height and
\[\dfrac{[AMB]}{[AMD]}=\dfrac{MB}{MD}.\]
In the same way, triangles $CMB$ and $CMD$ give $\dfrac{[BMC]}{[CMD]}=\dfrac{MB}{MD}$. Hence $\dfrac{[AMB]}{[AMD]}=\dfrac{[BMC]}{[CMD]}$, which is $[AMB]\cdot[CMD]=[AMD]\cdot[BMC]$. Only the fact that the diagonals cross at $M$ was used, so this holds in every convex quadrilateral.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.2,2), B=(4,2), C=(6,0), D=(0,0);
pair M=extension(A,C,B,D);
fill(A--M--D--cycle,rgb(0.8,0.9,1)); fill(B--M--C--cycle,rgb(0.8,0.9,1));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--C); draw(B--D);
dot(M);
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,NE); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,SW); label("$M$",M,S);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} Triangles $ABD$ and $ABC$ have the common base $AB$, and since $CD\parallel AB$, the points $D$ and $C$ are at the same distance from line $AB$. So $[ABD]=[ABC]$. Subtracting the common part $[AMB]$ gives $[AMD]=[BMC]$ (the two shaded triangles).

\textbf{(c)} Substituting (b) into (a): $[AMD]^2=[AMB]\cdot[CMD]$, so $[AMD]=\sqrt{[AMB]\cdot[CMD]}$. $\blacksquare$

question:
Now person $A$ is $x$ times as old as person $B$, and in $y$ years ($y>0$) $A$ will be $z$ times as old as $B$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that if $x\ne z$, then $B$'s present age is $\dfrac{y(z-1)}{x-z}$ and $A$'s is $\dfrac{xy(z-1)}{x-z}$.
\item[(b)] Suppose $A$ is older than $B$, so $x>1$. Show that the ages are positive only if $1<z<x$.
\item[(c)] A mother is now $4$ times as old as her daughter, and in $20$ years she will be twice as old. Find both ages.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
A line through $A$ is either the vertical line $x=3$ or $y-5=k(x-3)$, i.e. $kx-y+5-3k=0$.
\begin{itemize}
\item The line $x=3$ is at distance $|3-2|=1$ from $B$, so it is tangent (at $(3,2)$).
\item For $kx-y+5-3k=0$, tangency means the distance from $B$ is $1$:
\[\dfrac{|2k-2+5-3k|}{\sqrt{k^2+1}}=1\iff(3-k)^2=k^2+1\iff9-6k=1\iff k=\dfrac43.\]
\end{itemize}
Since a point outside a circle has exactly two tangents, these are the two: $x=3$ and $y-5=\dfrac43(x-3)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
pair Bc=(2,2), A=(3,5);
draw((-1.6,0)--(4.4,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-0.6)--(0,5.8),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(4.4,0),E); label("$y$",(0,5.8),N);
draw(circle(Bc,1),linewidth(0.8));
draw((3,-0.4)--(3,5.6),blue); draw((-1.05,-0.4)--(3.45,5.6),blue);
pair T1=(3,2), T2=(1.2,2.6);
dot(Bc); dot(A); dot(T1); dot(T2); dot((3,0),red); dot((-0.75,0),red);
draw(Bc--T1,dashed); draw(Bc--T2,dashed);
label("$B(2,2)$",Bc,S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$A(3,5)$",A,E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$(3,0)$",(3,0),SE,red+fontsize(8pt)); label("$\left(-\dfrac34,0\right)$",(-0.75,0),SE,red+fontsize(8pt));
label("$x=3$",(3,4.2),E,blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$y-5=\dfrac43(x-3)$",(0.6,2.2),W,blue+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The first meets the $x$-axis at $(3,0)$. For the second, $y=0$ gives $x-3=-\dfrac{15}{4}$, so $x=-\dfrac34$. Hence $m+n=3-\dfrac34=\boxed{\dfrac94}$.

Scale the pentagon to side $1$ and let $F$ be the intersection of the diagonals $AC$ and $BE$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=dir(90), B=dir(162), C=dir(234), D=dir(306), Ep=dir(18);
pair M=(A+Ep)/2;
pair Fq=extension(A,C,B,Ep), Z=extension(A,C,B,M);
draw(A--B--C--D--Ep--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,blue); draw(B--Ep,gray); draw(B--M,red);
dot(M); dot(Fq); dot(Z);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$D$",D,SE); label("$E$",Ep,E);
label("$M$",M,NE); label("$F$",Fq,SE); label("$Z$",Z,NW);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Lengths in the pentagon.} Each interior angle is $108^\circ$, and the isosceles triangles $ABC$ and $EAB$ have base angles $36^\circ$, so $\angle BAC=\angle ABE=36^\circ$. In triangle $ABF$ these two angles are equal, so $AF=BF$, and $\angle AFB=108^\circ$. In triangle $BCF$, $\angle CBF=108^\circ-36^\circ=72^\circ$ and $\angle BFC=180^\circ-108^\circ=72^\circ$, so $CF=CB=1$. Let $d=AC=BE$ be the diagonal; then $AF=BF=d-1$ and $FE=BE-BF=1$.

Triangles $ABF$ and $ACB$ have the same angles ($36^\circ,36^\circ,108^\circ$), so $\dfrac{AF}{AB}=\dfrac{AB}{AC}$, i.e. $d-1=\dfrac1d$. Thus $d^2-d-1=0$ and $d=\varphi=\dfrac{1+\sqrt5}{2}$, with $AF=BF=\varphi-1=\dfrac1\varphi$ and $FE=1$.

\textbf{Mass points in triangle $ABE$.} In triangle $ABE$, the segment $BM$ is a median and $AF$ is a cevian with $\dfrac{BF}{FE}=\dfrac{1}{\varphi}$; they meet at $Z$. Mass points place weights at the vertices so that each point on a side is the balance point (centre of mass) of the weights at the ends of that side, like a see-saw: weights $w_1$, $w_2$ at the ends balance at a point that divides the segment in the ratio $w_2:w_1$. Then the intersection of two cevians is the balance point of all three weights, and it divides each cevian inversely to the weights at its ends.
\begin{itemize}
\item $M$ is the midpoint of $AE$, so put weight $1$ at $A$ and $1$ at $E$.
\item $F$ balances $B$ and $E$ with $\dfrac{BF}{FE}=\dfrac1\varphi$, so the weight at $B$ is $\varphi$ times the weight at $E$: weight $\varphi$ at $B$, and $F$ carries $\varphi+1=\varphi^2$.
\item On the cevian $AF$, $Z$ balances weight $1$ at $A$ against $\varphi^2$ at $F$, so $\dfrac{AZ}{ZF}=\varphi^2$.
\end{itemize}
(Menelaus' theorem in triangle $AFE$ with the line $B$--$Z$--$M$ confirms this: $\dfrac{AZ}{ZF}\cdot\dfrac{FB}{BE}\cdot\dfrac{EM}{MA}=1$ gives $\dfrac{AZ}{ZF}=\dfrac{BE}{FB}=\dfrac{\varphi}{\varphi-1}=\varphi^2$.)

\textbf{Finish.} Hence
\[AZ=\dfrac{\varphi^2}{\varphi^2+1}\,AF=\dfrac{\varphi^2}{\varphi^2+1}\cdot\dfrac1\varphi=\dfrac{\varphi}{\varphi^2+1}=\dfrac{\varphi}{\varphi+2}=\dfrac{1+\sqrt5}{5+\sqrt5}=\dfrac{(1+\sqrt5)(5-\sqrt5)}{20}=\dfrac{\sqrt5}{5}.\]
So in any regular pentagon $ZA=\dfrac{AB}{\sqrt5}$, and $ZA=3$ gives $AB=\boxed{3\sqrt5}$.

question:
One-inch squares are cut from the corners of a $5$-inch square. In the remaining region a square is placed as in the figure: each of its four vertices lies on a side of the $5$-inch square, and each of its sides passes through the inner corner of one of the cut-out squares.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=(5-sqrt(5))/2;
fill(box((0,0),(1,1)),gray(0.6)); fill(box((4,0),(5,1)),gray(0.6)); fill(box((4,4),(5,5)),gray(0.6)); fill(box((0,4),(1,5)),gray(0.6));
draw((1,0)--(4,0)--(4,1)--(5,1)--(5,4)--(4,4)--(4,5)--(1,5)--(1,4)--(0,4)--(0,1)--(1,1)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(box((0,0),(5,5)),dashed+gray);
filldraw((a,0)--(5,a)--(5-a,5)--(0,5-a)--cycle,rgb(0.8,0.9,1),blue+linewidth(0.8));
label("$1$",(0,0.5),W,fontsize(8pt)); draw((0,-0.45)--(5,-0.45),Arrows(4)); label("$5$",(2.5,-0.45),S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the area of the tilted square.
\item[(b)] One solution splits each corner triangle (between the tilted square and a corner of the $5$-inch square) into two triangles that meet at the inner corner of the cut-out, and says that both have height $1$. Explain why this is true even though the square is tilted.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Put the $5$-inch square at $0\le x,y\le5$ and look at the bottom-left corner $O$. The tilted square cuts off a right triangle at each corner; let the one at $O$ have legs $a$ (along the bottom side) and $5-a$ (along the left side), with hypotenuse a side of the tilted square passing through the inner corner $P=(1,1)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=(5-sqrt(5))/2;
pair O=(0,0), X1=(a,0), Y1=(0,5-a), P=(1,1);
fill(O--X1--P--cycle,rgb(1,0.85,0.85)); fill(O--P--Y1--cycle,rgb(0.85,1,0.85));
draw(box((0,0),(5,5)),gray);
draw((a,0)--(5,a)--(5-a,5)--(0,5-a)--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(box((0,0),(1,1)),gray+dashed);
draw(O--P);
draw(P--(1,0),red+dashed); draw(P--(0,1),deepgreen+dashed);
dot(P); label("$P(1,1)$",P,NE,fontsize(8pt));
label("$a$",(a/2,0),S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$5-a$",(0,(5-a)/2),W,fontsize(8pt));
label("$1$",(1,0.45),W,red+fontsize(8pt)); label("$1$",(0.5,1),N,deepgreen+fontsize(8pt));
label("$O$",O,SW,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} Join $O$ to $P$. This splits the corner triangle into a triangle with base $a$ on the bottom side of the big square and a triangle with base $5-a$ on its left side, both with apex $P$. The height of a triangle is the perpendicular distance from the apex to the line \emph{containing the base}. Here the bases lie on the sides of the big square, not on the tilted square, and $P$ is $1$ inch from the bottom side and $1$ inch from the left side (it is the corner of a $1$-inch square). So both heights are $1$, whatever the tilt.

\textbf{(a)} Hence the corner triangle has area
\[\dfrac12\cdot a\cdot1+\dfrac12\cdot(5-a)\cdot1=\dfrac52,\]
independently of $a$. The same holds at each of the four corners (the four corner triangles of a square inscribed in a square are congruent). So the tilted square has area
\[25-4\cdot\dfrac52=\boxed{15}\text{ square inches}.\]
(Check: the corner triangle's area is also $\dfrac{a(5-a)}{2}$, so $a(5-a)=5$, $a=\dfrac{5-\sqrt5}{2}$, and the side squared is $a^2+(5-a)^2=25-2a(5-a)=15$.)

This tilted square is the one in the figure of the original contest problem, which asks for the largest square that fits in the remaining space; the answer there is $15$.

Let the square have side $s$, let $AE=BF=CG=DH=t$, and let $X$ have distances $p$ from $AD$ and $q$ from $AB$ (so $0<p,q<s$). Split each quadrilateral by the segment from $X$ to the square's corner:
\begin{align*}
[XEAH]&=[XEA]+[XAH]=\dfrac12tq+\dfrac12(s-t)p,\\
[XGCF]&=[XGC]+[XCF]=\dfrac12t(s-q)+\dfrac12(s-t)(s-p),
\end{align*}
using bases $AE=t$, $AH=s-t$, $CG=t$, $CF=s-t$ and the distances from $X$ to the corresponding sides. Adding,
\[[XEAH]+[XGCF]=\dfrac12\big(tq+t(s-q)\big)+\dfrac12\big((s-t)p+(s-t)(s-p)\big)=\dfrac12ts+\dfrac12(s-t)s=\dfrac{s^2}{2}.\]
In the same way $[XFBE]+[XHDG]=\dfrac{s^2}{2}$. So opposite quadrilaterals have equal total area:
\[[XHDG]=[XEAH]+[XGCF]-[XFBE]=81+125-49=\boxed{157}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real L=sqrt(412), t=5.5; pair X=(9.92,2.77);
pair A=(0,0),B=(L,0),C=(L,L),D=(0,L);
pair Ep=(t,0),Fq=(L,t),G=(L-t,L),H=(0,L-t);
