\end{asy}

\textbf{Check.} The triangle with $\alpha=60^\circ$, $\beta=75^\circ$, $\gamma=45^\circ$ is acute and has $\gamma<\alpha$, so $E$ lies between $B$ and $C$; then $\angle DEB=\angle DAB=45^\circ$ and $\angle BDE=180^\circ-120^\circ-45^\circ=15^\circ$, as required.

So $\angle ACB=\boxed{45^\circ}$.

An isosceles triangle with perimeter $48$ has two equal sides $a$ and a third side $48-2a$, where $a$ is a positive integer.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(4,0), A=(2,3.2);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
label("$a$",(A+B)/2,NW); label("$a$",(A+C)/2,NE); label("$48-2a$",(B+C)/2,S);
draw(((A+B)/2+0.12*dir(-32))--((A+B)/2-0.12*dir(-32)));
draw(((A+C)/2+0.12*dir(32))--((A+C)/2-0.12*dir(32)));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);
\end{asy}

The triangle exists exactly when $48-2a>0$ and the triangle inequality holds. The only inequality that can fail is $a+a>48-2a$ (the others say $a+(48-2a)>a$, i.e. $48>2a$). So we need
\[4a>48\quad\text{and}\quad 2a<48,\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad 13\le a\le23.\]
Different values of $a$ give non-congruent triangles, because a non-equilateral isosceles triangle has only one pair of equal sides, and the equilateral triangle $16,16,16$ arises only once (from $a=16$). So the triangles are $(a,a,48-2a)$ for $a=13,14,\dots,23$, and there are $\boxed{11}$ of them.

