Use mass points. Put masses $m_A,m_B,m_C$ at the vertices so that $P$ and $Q$ are the balance points of $AB$ and $AC$:
\[m_A\cdot AP=m_B\cdot PB\iff 3m_A=9m_B,\qquad m_A\cdot AQ=m_C\cdot QC\iff 15m_A=6m_C.\]
Taking $m_A=6$ gives $m_B=2$ and $m_C=15$. Then $P$ carries mass $m_A+m_B=8$, and the centre of mass of the whole system lies on $CP$ (the balance point of the masses $8$ at $P$ and $15$ at $C$) and also on $BQ$. So it is $S$, and on segment $PC$
\[PS:SC=m_C:m_P=\boxed{15:8}.\]

