Write $p=n^2+2$ with $n\ge10$ (since $p\ge100$).
\begin{itemize}
\item If $n$ is even, $p$ is even and greater than $2$, so not prime.
\item If $3\nmid n$, then $n^2\equiv1\pmod3$ and $p\equiv0\pmod3$, with $p>3$, so not prime.
\end{itemize}
So $n$ is an odd multiple of $3$, and the smallest with $n\ge10$ is $n=15$, giving $p=227$. It is prime: it is not divisible by $2,3,5,7,11,13$, and $17^2>227$. The smallest nice prime is $\boxed{227}$.

question:
Five distinct baskets are given, and each basket can hold exactly one item of clothing. Items of the same kind are identical.
\begin{itemize}
\item Let $x$ be the number of ways to place a shirt, $2$ pants, a sweater and a hoodie in the $5$ baskets.
\item Let $y$ be the number of ways to place $3$ shirts and $2$ pants in the $5$ baskets.
\end{itemize}
Find $\dfrac{y}{x}\cdot100$.

solution:
Let $\angle BAC=\alpha$, $\angle ABC=\beta$, $\angle ACB=\gamma$.

\textbf{The angle at $I$.} $BI$ and $CI$ bisect the angles at $B$ and $C$, so
\[\angle BIC=180^\circ-\dfrac{\beta+\gamma}{2}=180^\circ-\dfrac{180^\circ-\alpha}{2}=90^\circ+\dfrac{\alpha}{2}.\]

\textbf{The angle at $H$.} Since $H\ne B$, line $BH$ is the line of the altitude from $B$, so $BH\perp AC$; similarly $CH\perp AB$. Turning both lines through $90^\circ$ turns them into lines parallel to $AC$ and $AB$, so the two angles between lines $HB$ and $HC$ are the angles between lines $AC$ and $AB$, namely $\alpha$ and $180^\circ-\alpha$. Hence $\angle BHC$ equals $\alpha$ or $180^\circ-\alpha$.

\textbf{Solving.} If $\angle BHC=\alpha$, then $\alpha=90^\circ+\dfrac\alpha2$ gives $\alpha=180^\circ$, impossible. So
\[180^\circ-\alpha=90^\circ+\dfrac{\alpha}{2},\qquad \alpha=60^\circ.\]
This value does occur: in any acute triangle with $\angle A=60^\circ$ (for example an equilateral triangle, where $H=I$), the orthocentre is inside and $\angle BHC=180^\circ-60^\circ=120^\circ=90^\circ+30^\circ=\angle BIC$. (Then $B$, $C$, $H$, $I$ lie on one circle, dotted in the sketch.)

