question:
The graphs of $\sin x$, $\cos x$ and $\tan x$ repeat the same shape over and over.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $\sin(x+2\pi)=\sin x$ and $\cos(x+2\pi)=\cos x$ for all real $x$, and that $\tan(x+\pi)=\tan x$ whenever $\tan x$ is defined.
\item[(b)] Show that $2\pi$ is the smallest positive number $T$ with $\sin(x+T)=\sin x$ for all $x$, that the same holds for $\cos$, and that $\pi$ is the smallest positive number $T$ with $\tan(x+T)=\tan x$ for all $x$ where both sides are defined.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Recall that $(\cos x,\sin x)$ is the point reached on the unit circle by turning through the angle $x$ from $(1,0)$.

\textbf{(a)} Turning through $x+2\pi$ is turning through $x$ and then one more full turn, which ends at the same point, so $\sin$ and $\cos$ are unchanged. Turning through $x+\pi$ ends at the diametrically opposite point $(-\cos x,-\sin x)$, so
\[\tan(x+\pi)=\dfrac{-\sin x}{-\cos x}=\dfrac{\sin x}{\cos x}=\tan x.\]

\textbf{(b)} Suppose $T>0$ and $\sin(x+T)=\sin x$ for all $x$. Putting $x=\dfrac\pi2$ gives $\sin\left(\dfrac\pi2+T\right)=1$. The only point of the unit circle with second coordinate $1$ is $(0,1)$, so $\dfrac\pi2+T=\dfrac\pi2+2\pi k$ for an integer $k$; as $T>0$, $k\ge1$ and $T\ge2\pi$. For $\cos$, put $x=0$: $\cos T=1$ forces the point $(1,0)$, so again $T=2\pi k\ge2\pi$. For $\tan$: if $\tan T$ is defined, putting $x=0$ gives $\tan T=0$, so $\sin T=0$ and $T$ is a multiple of $\pi$; if $\tan T$ is not defined, then $T=\dfrac\pi2+k\pi$ and $x=\dfrac\pi4$ gives $\tan\left(\dfrac{3\pi}{4}+k\pi\right)=-1\ne1=\tan\dfrac\pi4$, which is impossible. So $T$ is a positive multiple of $\pi$ and $T\ge\pi$. Together with (a), the smallest periods are $2\pi$, $2\pi$ and $\pi$. $\blacksquare$

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real xs=0.5;
draw((-0.3,0)--(xs*4*pi+0.4,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-1.5)--(0,1.6),Arrow(6));
path s=graph(new pair(real t){return (xs*t,sin(t));},0,4*pi);
draw(s,blue+linewidth(0.9));
for(int k=1;k<=4;++k){draw((xs*k*pi,-0.06)--(xs*k*pi,0.06));}
label("$\pi$",(xs*pi,0),SW,fontsize(8pt)); label("$2\pi$",(xs*2*pi,0),SW,fontsize(8pt)); label("$3\pi$",(xs*3*pi,0),SW,fontsize(8pt)); label("$4\pi$",(xs*4*pi,0),SW,fontsize(8pt));
label("$y=\sin x$",(xs*3.5*pi,-1),S,blue+fontsize(8pt));
draw((xs*pi/2,1.2)--(xs*5*pi/2,1.2),red,Arrows(4)); label("$2\pi$",(xs*1.5*pi,1.2),N,red+fontsize(8pt));
real ys=0.25;
pair o=(0,-4.2);
draw(o+(-0.3,0)--o+(xs*2*pi+0.4,0),Arrow(6)); draw(o+(0,-1.5)--o+(0,1.6),Arrow(6));
for(int k=0;k<=2;++k){
  real a=k*pi-pi/2+0.25, c=k*pi+pi/2-0.25;
  if(k==0) a=0;
  if(k==2) c=2*pi;
  draw(graph(new pair(real t){return o+(xs*t,ys*tan(t));},a,c),heavygreen+linewidth(0.9));
}
draw(o+(xs*pi/2,-1.5)--o+(xs*pi/2,1.5),dashed+gray); draw(o+(xs*3*pi/2,-1.5)--o+(xs*3*pi/2,1.5),dashed+gray);
label("$\pi$",o+(xs*pi,0),SE,fontsize(8pt)); label("$2\pi$",o+(xs*2*pi,0),SE,fontsize(8pt));
label("$y=\tan x$",o+(xs*2*pi,1.2),E,heavygreen+fontsize(8pt));
draw(o+(xs*0,-1.8)--o+(xs*pi,-1.8),red,Arrows(4)); label("$\pi$",o+(xs*pi/2,-1.8),S,red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The graph of $\sin x$ repeats every $2\pi$; the graph of $\tan x$ (vertical scale compressed) repeats every $\pi$, between its vertical asymptotes.

question:
A prisoner digs a straight tunnel. He started at a point $H$ on the ground $10$ m east of the chief's office $O$, and digs westward and downward in the vertical plane through $O$ and $H$. He is now at the point $K$, $5$ m west of the office and $20$ m below ground level.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] At what angle below the horizontal does the tunnel run?
\item[(b)] A robot starts at the office, at ground level, and moves in a straight line to $K$. How far must it travel, and in what direction?
\end{enumerate}
Give angles to the nearest tenth of a degree.

solution:
Work in the vertical plane, with $O$ at the origin, east to the right and up positive: $H=(10,0)$ and $K=(-5,-20)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0), H=(10,0), C=(-5,-20);
draw((-9,0)--(13,0),linewidth(0.8));
label("ground",(-8,0),N,fontsize(8pt));
draw(H--C,brown+linewidth(1)); draw(O--C,red+dashed);
draw(C--(-5,0),dotted);
dot(O);dot(H);dot(C);
label("$O$",O,N); label("$H$",H,N); label("$K$",C,SW);
label("$10$",(5,0),N,fontsize(8pt)); label("$5$",(-2.5,0),N,fontsize(8pt)); label("$20$",(-5,-10),W,fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc(H,2.2,180,degrees(C-H)),gray); label("$53.1^\circ$",H+3.4*dir(200),fontsize(7pt));
draw(arc(O,2.4,180,degrees(C-O)),gray); label("$76.0^\circ$",O+3.6*dir(220),fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} From $H$ to $K$ the tunnel goes $15$ m west and $20$ m down, so its angle $\theta$ below the horizontal satisfies $\tan\theta=\dfrac{20}{15}=\dfrac43$, and $\theta\approx\boxed{53.1^\circ}$ (this is the angle $OHK$).

\textbf{(b)} From $O$ to $K$ the robot goes $5$ m west and $20$ m down, a distance of
\[\sqrt{5^2+20^2}=\sqrt{425}=\boxed{5\sqrt{17}\text{ m}}\approx20.6\text{ m},\]
heading west at an angle $\varphi$ below the horizontal with $\tan\varphi=\dfrac{20}{5}=4$, i.e. $\varphi\approx\boxed{76.0^\circ}$.

Since $\cos(2\pi)=1$, his speed with the wind is $v(t)=1+\sin t$ km per hour. This is never negative, so he never turns back and the distance travelled is
\[\int_0^{10\pi}(1+\sin t)\,dt=\Bigl[t-\cos t\Bigr]_0^{10\pi}=(10\pi-\cos10\pi)-(0-\cos0)=10\pi-1+1=10\pi\text{ km}.\]
Without the wind he travels $1\cdot10\pi=10\pi$ km. The difference is $\boxed{0}$ km: over the $5$ full periods of $\sin t$, the wind helps and hinders equally.

