For any real $\alpha$, the point $P=(\cos\alpha,\sin\alpha)$ is, by definition, the point of the unit circle (centre $O$, radius $1$) reached by turning through $\alpha$ from $(1,0)$.

Drop the perpendicular from $P$ to the $x$-axis, with foot $Q$. If $P$ is not on an axis, $OQP$ is a right triangle with legs $|\cos\alpha|$ and $|\sin\alpha|$ and hypotenuse $OP=1$, so by the Pythagorean theorem
\[\cos^2\alpha+\sin^2\alpha=|\cos\alpha|^2+|\sin\alpha|^2=1.\]
If $P$ is on an axis, one of $\cos\alpha,\sin\alpha$ is $0$ and the other is $\pm1$, and the identity holds directly. $\blacksquare$

