Hence $\sin^2\alpha+\cos^2\alpha+\sin^2\alpha+\cos^2\alpha=1+1$ for every $\alpha$, so the claim is correct (if roundabout).

Let the common radius be $r$; then $AB=r$, and $C$ (on the circle centred at $A$) and $D$ (on the circle centred at $B$) are the far ends of the line, with $CA=AB=BD=r$.

Since $E$ lies on both circles, $EA=EB=AB=r$, so triangle $ABE$ is equilateral and $\angle AEB=60^\circ$, $\angle EAB=\angle EBA=60^\circ$.

In triangle $CAE$, $AC=AE=r$ and $\angle CAE=180^\circ-60^\circ=120^\circ$, so $\angle AEC=\angle ACE=30^\circ$. In the same way $\angle BED=30^\circ$. Therefore
\[\angle CED=\angle CEA+\angle AEB+\angle BED=30^\circ+60^\circ+30^\circ=\boxed{120^\circ},\]
answer $\textbf{(C)}$.

