The median $m$ is the $5$th smallest element, so exactly $4$ elements of $R$ are smaller than $m$ and exactly $4$ are larger. Only $3$ elements are not among the six given ones.

\textbf{Bounds.} If $m\le2$, then $3,4,6,9,14$ are five elements larger than $m$, too many. If $m\ge10$, then $2,3,4,6,9$ are five elements smaller than $m$, too many. So $3\le m\le9$.

\textbf{All of these occur.} Writing the three new elements in brackets:
\begin{itemize}
\item $m=3$: $[-1],[0],[1],2,3,4,6,9,14$;
\item $m=4$: $[0],[1],2,3,4,6,9,14,[15]$;
\item $m=5$: $[1],2,3,4,[5],6,9,14,[15]$;
\item $m=6$: $2,3,4,[5],6,9,14,[15],[16]$;
\item $m=7$ and $m=8$: $2,3,4,6,[m],9,14,[15],[16]$;
\item $m=9$: $2,3,4,6,9,14,[15],[16],[17]$.
\end{itemize}
So there are $\boxed{7}$ possible medians, answer $\textbf{(D)}$.

question:
A student offers the following ``proof'' that $1+1=2$ (here $i^2=-1$):
\begin{align*}
1+1&=(2-1)+(2-1)=1(2-1)+1(2-1)=(1+1)(2-1)=\dfrac{(1+1)(2-1)}{1}=\dfrac{(1+1)(2-1)}{\sin^2\alpha+\cos^2\alpha}\\
&=\dfrac{\tan\alpha}{\tan\alpha}\cdot\dfrac{(1+1)(2-1)}{\sin^2\alpha+\cos^2\alpha}=\dfrac{i}{i}\cdot\dfrac{\tan\alpha\,(1+1)(2-1)}{\tan\alpha\left(\sin^2\alpha+\cos^2\alpha\right)}=\dfrac{i\tan\alpha\,(2-1+2-1)}{i\tan\alpha\sin^2\alpha+i\tan\alpha\cos^2\alpha}\\
&=\dfrac{2i(2\tan\alpha)-2i\tan\alpha}{i\tan\alpha\sin^2\alpha+i\tan\alpha\cos^2\alpha}=\dfrac{\pi\ln10}{\pi\ln10}\cdot\dfrac{2i(2\tan\alpha)-2i\tan\alpha}{i\tan\alpha\sin^2\alpha+i\tan\alpha\cos^2\alpha}\\
&=\dfrac{2i\pi\ln10\cdot2\cdot\dfrac{\sin\alpha}{\cos\alpha}-2i\pi\ln10\tan\alpha}{i\pi\ln10\tan\alpha\sin^2\alpha+i\pi\ln10\tan\alpha\cos^2\alpha}=2.
\end{align*}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] For which real numbers $\alpha$ is every expression in the chain defined?
\item[(b)] For those $\alpha$, check the last step: show that the final fraction equals $2$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The expressions involve $\tan\alpha=\dfrac{\sin\alpha}{\cos\alpha}$, which needs $\cos\alpha\ne0$, and they divide by $\tan\alpha$ (in $\dfrac{\tan\alpha}{\tan\alpha}$ and in the later denominators), which needs $\tan\alpha\ne0$, i.e. $\sin\alpha\ne0$. Everything else ($\sin^2\alpha+\cos^2\alpha=1$, $i\ne0$, $\pi\ln10\ne0$) is harmless. So the chain makes sense exactly when $\sin\alpha\ne0$ and $\cos\alpha\ne0$, that is,
\[\boxed{\alpha\ne\dfrac{k\pi}{2}\ \text{ for every integer }k}.\]
On the unit circle, these are all angles except those pointing along the axes (the four open dots).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),1),linewidth(0.8));
draw((-1.3,0)--(1.3,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-1.3)--(0,1.3),Arrow(6));
pair[] X={(1,0),(0,1),(-1,0),(0,-1)};
for(pair p: X){ filldraw(circle(p,0.06),white,red); }
label("$0$",(1,0),SE,fontsize(8pt)); label("$\dfrac{\pi}{2}$",(0,1),NE,fontsize(8pt)); label("$\pi$",(-1,0),SW,fontsize(8pt)); label("$\dfrac{3\pi}{2}$",(0,-1),SE,fontsize(8pt));
pair P=dir(50); draw((0,0)--P,blue); dot(P,blue); label("$\alpha$",0.3*dir(25),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} Let $t=\tan\alpha\ne0$ and $c=i\pi\ln10\ne0$. Since $2\cdot\dfrac{\sin\alpha}{\cos\alpha}=2t$, the numerator is $2c\cdot2t-2ct=2ct$, and the denominator is $ct\left(\sin^2\alpha+\cos^2\alpha\right)=ct$. So the fraction equals $\dfrac{2ct}{ct}=2$.

Every step is a correct identity for these $\alpha$, so the chain is a valid, if very roundabout, computation; but it is not a proof of $1+1=2$, since steps such as $(1+1)(2-1)=2-1+2-1$ already use ordinary arithmetic with these numbers.

