Write in slope-intercept form $y=mx+b$ the equation of
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] the line parallel to $y=3x-4$ that passes through $(0,6)$;
\item[(b)] the line perpendicular to $y=-\dfrac12x+3$ that passes through $(0,1)$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Parallel lines have equal slopes, so the slope is $3$. The point $(0,6)$ is the $y$-intercept, so the line is $\boxed{y=3x+6}$.

\textbf{(b)} Two non-vertical lines are perpendicular exactly when the product of their slopes is $-1$, so the slope is $-1\div\left(-\dfrac12\right)=2$. With $y$-intercept $1$, the line is $\boxed{y=2x+1}$. (It meets $y=-\dfrac12x+3$ at $\left(\dfrac45,\dfrac{13}{5}\right)$.)

