question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] In how many different ways can $15015$ be represented as the sum of two or more consecutive positive integers written in ascending order?
\item[(b)] A student lets the first term be $x$ and the number of terms be $n$, writes $x\cdot\dfrac{x+(x+n-1)}{2}=15015$, and obtains the answer $2^6-1=63$. Explain what went wrong.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] An integer $n$ is \emph{even} if $n=2k$ for some integer $k$. Is $-2$ even? Is $0$ even?
\item[(b)] Prove that an integer $n$ is even if and only if $-n$ is even.
\item[(c)] A \emph{prime number} is an integer greater than $1$ whose only positive divisors are $1$ and itself. Is $-5$ a prime number?
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why the lateral (curved) surface area of a right circular cone with base radius $r$ and slant height $\ell$ is $\pi r\ell$.
\item[(b)] A cone has base radius $3$ and slant height $5$. Find its lateral surface area, and the central angle of the sector obtained by cutting its lateral surface along a slant height and flattening it.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Cut the lateral surface along a slant height and unroll it flat. Every point of the base circle is at distance $\ell$ from the apex, so the surface becomes a sector of a circle of radius $\ell$, and the arc of the sector is the base circle, of length $2\pi r$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair T=(0,2);
draw((-1.5,0)..(0,-0.5)..(1.5,0),linewidth(0.8)); draw((-1.5,0)..(0,0.5)..(1.5,0),dashed);
draw((-1.5,0)--T--(1.5,0),linewidth(0.8));
draw((0,0)--(1.5,0),red); label("$r$",(0.7,0),N,red+fontsize(9pt));
label("$\ell$",(T+(1.5,0))/2,NE);
dot((0,0));
pair O2=(5.2,1.5); real R=2.5, th=216;
fill(O2--arc(O2,R,90-th/2,90+th/2)--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
draw(O2--arc(O2,R,90-th/2,90+th/2)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
label("$\ell$",O2+0.55*R*dir(90+th/2),dir(90+th/2+90));
label("arc $2\pi r$",O2+R*dir(90),N,fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc(O2,0.35,90-th/2,90+th/2),blue); label("$\theta$",O2+0.6*dir(90),blue);
\end{asy}

A full circle of radius $\ell$ has circumference $2\pi\ell$ and area $\pi\ell^2$. The sector is the fraction $\dfrac{2\pi r}{2\pi\ell}=\dfrac r\ell$ of the full circle (area is proportional to arc length), so its area is
\[\dfrac r\ell\cdot\pi\ell^2=\pi r\ell.\]
This is the same idea as the area of a triangle: the sector has ``base'' $2\pi r$ and ``height'' $\ell$, and area $\dfrac12\cdot2\pi r\cdot\ell$.

\textbf{(b)} The lateral area is $\pi\cdot3\cdot5=\boxed{15\pi}$. The sector is the fraction $\dfrac r\ell=\dfrac35$ of a full turn, so its central angle is $\theta=\dfrac35\cdot360^\circ=\boxed{216^\circ}$.

