The condition says that every animal has at least one neighbour of its own kind. Going around the table, the animals form blocks of consecutive animals of the same kind, alternating between kinds; the condition is exactly that every block has size at least $2$.

So the tortoises form blocks of sizes $\ge2$ adding up to $4$, namely one block of $4$ or two blocks of $2$, and the turtles form the same number of blocks. There are two patterns of kinds around the table:
\begin{itemize}
\item one block of each kind, $TTTTtttt$, which can be placed on the $8$ labelled seats in $8$ ways (by rotation);
\item two blocks of each kind, $TTttTTtt$, which can be placed in $4$ ways.
\end{itemize}
So there are $12$ patterns on labelled seats, and each can be filled with the distinct animals in $4!\cdot4!=576$ ways, giving $12\cdot576=6912$ seatings on labelled seats. Each seating up to rotation corresponds to exactly $8$ seatings on labelled seats (all animals are distinct, so no rotation fixes a seating). The answer is $\dfrac{6912}{8}=\boxed{864}$.

The altitude $AO$ has length $x$ and is perpendicular to $DC$, so triangle $AOC$ has a right angle at $O$ and
\[x^2+y^2=AC^2=169.\]
The only way to write $169$ as a sum of two positive squares is $25+144$, so $x=5$ and $y=12$. The base is $x+y=17$, so $DO=17-12=5>0$ and $O$ is indeed on side $DC$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair D=(0,0),O=(5,0),C=(17,0),A=(5,5),B=(22,5);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--O,dashed); draw(A--C,red);
