question:
Let $n=10a+b$ be a positive integer with units digit $b$, so that $a$ is the number formed by the other digits.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Let $p$ be a prime other than $2$ and $5$, and let $k$ be an integer with $10k\equiv1\pmod p$. Prove that $p\mid n$ if and only if $p\mid a+kb$.
\item[(b)] Find such a $k$ with $|k|\le10$ for each of $p=13,17,19,23,29$, and state the resulting divisibility tests.
\item[(c)] Use the tests to show that $17\mid2023$ and $29\mid1914$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
A strictly monotone number is determined by its set of digits, since the order is forced.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Increasing (including one-digit numbers).} The digit $0$ cannot appear: it would have to come first. Every nonempty subset of $\{1,\dots,9\}$ gives one number: $2^9-1=511$ numbers.
\item \textbf{Decreasing with at least two digits.} Any subset of $\{0,1,\dots,9\}$ with at least two elements gives one number; its first digit is its largest, which is nonzero. There are $2^{10}-1-10=1013$ such subsets.
\end{itemize}
The two lists do not overlap, since numbers with two or more digits cannot be both increasing and decreasing, and the one-digit numbers were counted only in the first list. The total is $511+1013=\boxed{\textbf{(B)}\ 1524}$.

question:
The $n$-th \emph{triangular number} $T_n$ is the number of dots in a triangular array with $1$ dot in the first row, $2$ dots in the second row, and so on up to $n$ dots in the $n$-th row; that is, $T_n=1+2+\cdots+n$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $T_n=\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}$.
\item[(b)] Find $T_{10}$.
\item[(c)] Which triangular number equals $210$?
\end{enumerate}

Four different people sit around a round table with $4$ seats.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Two seatings are considered the same if one is a rotation of the other. Explain why every seating is the same as exactly $4$ seatings on the $4$ labelled seats, and deduce the number of different seatings.
\item[(b)] Now two seatings are also considered the same if one is a reflection (mirror image) of the other. Explain why every seating now corresponds to exactly $8$ seatings on the labelled seats, and deduce the number of different seatings.
\end{enumerate}

An equilateral triangle is divided into small congruent equilateral triangles by lines parallel to its sides, so that there are $16$ rows of small triangles (each side of the big triangle is $16$ times the side of a small triangle), as in the figure.

\begin{asy}
