How many triangles of any size, whose sides lie along the lines of the figure, point upward (in the same direction as the big triangle)?

solution:
Let $n=10a+b$ with digits $a\ge1$ and $b$. The condition $ab=\dfrac{10a+b}{2}$ becomes $2ab=10a+b$, i.e. $b(2a-1)=10a$. Since $\gcd(2a-1,a)=1$ (a common divisor divides $2a-(2a-1)=1$), $2a-1$ must divide $10$; as $2a-1$ is odd, $2a-1\in\{1,5\}$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $2a-1=1$: $a=1$, $b=10$, not a digit.
\item $2a-1=5$: $a=3$, $b=6$.
\end{itemize}
So $n=36$ (check: $3\cdot6=18=\dfrac{36}{2}$), and the digit sum is $\boxed{9}$.

$AD$ and $BE$ are medians, so $F$ is the centroid and $AF:FD=2:1$. Write $[\,\cdot\,]$ for area and $S=[ABC]$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(7,0),C=(2.2,4.6);
pair Ee=(A+C)/2,D=(B+C)/2; pair Fp=extension(A,D,B,Ee);
fill(A--Ee--Fp--cycle,rgb(0.8,0.9,1)); fill(Ee--Fp--D--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.85));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8)); draw(A--D); draw(B--Ee); draw(Ee--D,dashed);
dot(Fp); dot(D); dot(Ee);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N); label("$D$",D,NE); label("$E$",Ee,NW); label("$F$",Fp,S);
label("$10$",(A+Ee+Fp)/3,fontsize(8pt)); label("$5$",(Ee+Fp+D)/3,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Since $D$ is the midpoint of $BC$, $[ADC]=\dfrac S2$; since $E$ is the midpoint of $AC$, $[ADE]=\dfrac12[ADC]=\dfrac S4$. Triangles $AEF$ and $FED$ have the same height from $E$ to line $AD$, so their areas are in the ratio $AF:FD=2:1$:
\[[AEF]=\dfrac23[ADE]=\dfrac S6,\qquad[EFD]=\dfrac13[ADE]=\dfrac S{12}.\]
From $[AEF]=10$ we get $S=60$, and $[EFD]=\boxed{5}$.

Work modulo $5$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $3\equiv-2$, so $2^x+3^x\equiv2^x+(-2)^x$. This is $0$ when $x$ is odd, and $2\cdot2^x\not\equiv0$ when $x$ is even. So $5\mid2^x+3^x$ exactly when $x$ is odd.
\item $7\equiv2$ and $9\equiv-1$, so $7^x+9^x\equiv2^x+(-1)^x$. The powers of $2$ modulo $5$ repeat with period $4$: $2,4,3,1$. For odd $x$, $2^x\equiv2$ or $3$ and $2^x-1\not\equiv0$. So $5\mid7^x+9^x$ only for even $x$ (in fact exactly for $x\equiv2\pmod4$, where $2^x+1\equiv4+1\equiv0$).
\end{itemize}
The first condition needs $x$ odd and the second needs $x$ even, so they never hold together. The probability is $\boxed{0}$.

Place the centre $O$ of the hexagon at the origin with
\[A=(2,0),\ B=(1,\sqrt3),\ C=(-1,\sqrt3),\ E=(-2,0),\ G=(-1,-\sqrt3),\ I=(1,-\sqrt3).\]
$AE$ and $BG$ are long diagonals through $O$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(2,0),B=(1,sqrt(3)),C=(-1,sqrt(3)),Ee=(-2,0),G=(-1,-sqrt(3)),I=(1,-sqrt(3)),O=(0,0);
pair P=(0,-2*sqrt(3)/3),Q=(-0.5,-sqrt(3)/2);
fill(A--O--Q--P--cycle,rgb(1,0.85,0.6));
draw(A--B--C--Ee--G--I--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(B--A--G--cycle,blue); draw(A--I--Ee--cycle,red);
dot(O); dot(P); dot(Q);
label("$A$",A,E); label("$B$",B,NE); label("$C$",C,NW); label("$E$",Ee,W); label("$G$",G,SW); label("$I$",I,SE);
label("$O$",O,N); label("$P$",P,S); label("$Q$",Q,W);
\end{asy}

Triangle $AIE$ lies on or below the line $AE$ (the $x$-axis), and the part of triangle $BAG$ below the $x$-axis is triangle $OAG$, because side $BG$ crosses the $x$-axis at $O$. So the common region is the part of triangle $OAG$ inside triangle $AIE$.
\begin{itemize}
\item Line $AI$ is $\sqrt3x-y=2\sqrt3$; at $O$ and $G$ the left side is $0$ and $-\sqrt3+\sqrt3=0$, both less than $2\sqrt3$, as at $E$. So triangle $OAG$ lies entirely on the same side of $AI$ as triangle $AIE$.
\item Line $IE$ is $y=-\dfrac{x+2}{\sqrt3}$. It meets $AG$ (that is, $y=\dfrac{x-2}{\sqrt3}$) at $P=\left(0,-\dfrac{2\sqrt3}{3}\right)$ and $OG$ (that is, $y=\sqrt3x$) at $Q=\left(-\dfrac12,-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$, and it separates $G$ from $O$ and $A$.
\end{itemize}
So the common region is the quadrilateral $AOQP$, which is triangle $OAG$ with triangle $GQP$ cut off. By the shoelace formula,
\[[OAG]=\dfrac12\left|2\cdot(-\sqrt3)-0\cdot(-1)\right|=\sqrt3,\qquad[GQP]=\dfrac12\left|\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{3}-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\cdot1\right|=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{6},\]
using the vectors $Q-G=\left(\dfrac12,\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$ and $P-G=\left(1,\dfrac{\sqrt3}{3}\right)$. The area is
\[\sqrt3-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{6}=\boxed{\dfrac{5\sqrt3}{6}}.\]

Complete the square: $y=x^2-6x+9-2=(x-3)^2-2$. So the graph is the parabola $y=x^2$ shifted $3$ to the right and $2$ down:
\begin{itemize}
\item the vertex is $\boxed{(3,-2)}$, the lowest point, since $(x-3)^2\ge0$;
\item the axis of symmetry is the line $\boxed{x=3}$;
\item the $x$-intercepts satisfy $(x-3)^2=2$, so $x=\boxed{3\pm\sqrt2}$ (about $1.59$ and $4.41$);
\item the $y$-intercept is $y=\boxed{7}$, at $(0,7)$.
\end{itemize}
The parabola opens upward.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return x^2-6x+7;}
real k=0.5;
pair T(real x,real y){return (x,k*y);}
draw(T(-1,0)--T(7,0),Arrow(4)); draw(T(0,-3)--T(0,11),Arrow(4));
label("$x$",T(7,0),E); label("$y$",T(0,11),N);
path g=graph(new pair(real x){return T(x,f(x));},-0.4,6.4);
draw(g,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(T(3,-2)--T(3,10.5),dashed);
dot(T(3,-2)); label("$(3,-2)$",T(3,-2),S,fontsize(8pt));
dot(T(0,7)); label("$(0,7)$",T(0,7),W,fontsize(8pt));
dot(T(3-sqrt(2),0)); dot(T(3+sqrt(2),0));
label("$3-\sqrt2$",T(3-sqrt(2),0),SW,fontsize(7pt)); label("$3+\sqrt2$",T(3+sqrt(2),0),SE,fontsize(7pt));
label("$x=3$",T(3,10.5),N,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

(The vertical axis is compressed by a factor of $2$.)

question:
Let $J(x)$ be a polynomial.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $J(1)$ equals the sum of the coefficients of $J$.
\item[(b)] Show that $1$ is the only number $t$ such that $J(t)$ equals the sum of the coefficients of $J$ for every polynomial $J$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The shortest path between two points is the straight segment joining them, so we need its length. If the points differ in all three coordinates, they are opposite corners of a box with edges parallel to the axes, of lengths $|x_2-x_1|$, $|y_2-y_1|$ and $|z_2-z_1|$. (If some coordinates agree, the box is flat and the same computation works.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair pr(real x,real y,real z){return (x+0.45*y,z+0.3*y);}
real a=4,bq=3,c=2.4;
pair P1=pr(0,0,0),P2=pr(a,bq,c),K=pr(a,bq,0);
draw(pr(0,0,0)--pr(a,0,0)--pr(a,0,c)--pr(0,0,c)--cycle);
draw(pr(a,0,0)--pr(a,bq,0)--pr(a,bq,c)--pr(a,0,c));
draw(pr(0,0,c)--pr(0,bq,c)--pr(a,bq,c));
draw(pr(0,0,0)--pr(0,bq,0)--pr(a,bq,0),dashed); draw(pr(0,bq,0)--pr(0,bq,c),dashed);
draw(P1--K,blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(P1--P2,red+linewidth(1));
dot(P1); dot(P2); dot(K);
label("$(x_1,y_1,z_1)$",P1,SW,fontsize(8pt)); label("$(x_2,y_2,z_2)$",P2,NE,fontsize(8pt));
label("$|x_2-x_1|$",pr(a/2,0,0),S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$|y_2-y_1|$",pr(a,bq/2,0),SE,fontsize(8pt)); label("$|z_2-z_1|$",pr(a,bq,c/2),E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$K$",K,SE,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Let $K=(x_2,y_2,z_1)$. The points $(x_1,y_1,z_1)$ and $K$ lie in the horizontal plane $z=z_1$, so by the Pythagorean theorem in that plane,
\[\left(\text{distance to }K\right)^2=(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2.\]
The segment from $K$ to $(x_2,y_2,z_2)$ is vertical, so it is perpendicular to the horizontal segment from $(x_1,y_1,z_1)$ to $K$. Applying the Pythagorean theorem to this right triangle,
\[d^2=(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2+(z_2-z_1)^2,\qquad d=\boxed{\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2+(z_2-z_1)^2}}.\ \blacksquare\]

The radius is $1.5$ miles $=1.5\cdot5280=7920$ feet.

