Each circle passes through the other two centres, so the centres $O_1,O_2,O_3$ are at mutual distance $x$: they form an equilateral triangle of side $x$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O1=(0,0),O2=(1,0),O3=(0.5,sqrt(3)/2);
path R=arc(O1,1,0,60)--arc(O2,1,120,180)--arc(O3,1,240,300)--cycle;
fill(R,rgb(1,0.85,0.6));
draw(circle(O1,1)); draw(circle(O2,1)); draw(circle(O3,1));
draw(O1--O2--O3--cycle,dashed);
dot(O1);dot(O2);dot(O3);
label("$O_1$",O1,SW); label("$O_2$",O2,SE); label("$O_3$",O3,N);
label("$x$",(0.5,-0.134),S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The common region is bounded by three arcs: the arc of the circle centred at $O_1$ from $O_2$ to $O_3$, and similarly for the other two. (Each centre lies on the other two circles, and every point of the region bounded by these arcs is within $x$ of all three centres.) The region consists of the triangle $O_1O_2O_3$ and three circular segments, each cut off from a $60^\circ$ sector of radius $x$ by a side of the triangle. The area of one segment is
\[\dfrac{\pi x^2}{6}-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}x^2,\]
so the common area is
\[\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}x^2+3\left(\dfrac{\pi x^2}{6}-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}x^2\right)=\boxed{\dfrac{\left(\pi-\sqrt3\right)x^2}{2}}.\]

Let the second circle have centre $(h,k)$. It lies inside the square exactly when $|h|\le7$ and $|k|\le7$, and it is tangent to a side when $|h|=7$ or $|k|=7$.

If the centres are at distance $c\ge2$, the circles are at distance $c-2$ (the closest points lie on the line of centres); otherwise the circles meet or one lies inside the other, and the distance is $0$. So we must maximise $c=\sqrt{h^2+k^2}$. Since $|h|,|k|\le7$, we have $c\le\sqrt{49+49}=7\sqrt2$, with equality at the corners $(\pm7,\pm7)$, where the circle is tangent to two sides.

