The largest possible distance is $\boxed{7\sqrt2-2}\approx7.90$.

question:
The line $x=a$ is a \emph{vertical asymptote} of the graph of a function $f$ if $|f(x)|$ becomes arbitrarily large as $x$ approaches $a$ (from at least one side).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $x=0$ is a vertical asymptote of $y=\dfrac5x$: for every $M>0$, find how close to $0$ the number $x$ must be to guarantee $\left|\dfrac5x\right|>M$.
\item[(b)] Find all vertical asymptotes of the graph of $y=\dfrac{x^2-1}{x^2-x}$.
\end{enumerate}

Lines $AE$ and $CF$ are parallel, with $E$ and $F$ on the same side of line $AC$. Point $D$ lies strictly between the two parallel lines.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,2),Ee=(6,2),C=(0,0),Fp=(6,0),D=(3,1.2);
draw((-1,2)--(7,2)); draw((-1,0)--(7,0));
draw(A--D--C,linewidth(0.9));
dot(A);dot(C);dot(D);dot(Ee);dot(Fp);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$E$",Ee,N); label("$C$",C,S); label("$F$",Fp,S); label("$D$",D,E);
\end{asy}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Suppose $D$ is on the same side of line $AC$ as $E$ and $F$, as in the figure. Prove in two different ways that $\angle ADC=\angle EAD+\angle FCD$.
\item[(b)] Suppose instead that $D$ is on the other side of line $AC$. Express $\angle ADC$ in terms of $\angle EAD$ and $\angle FCD$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a), first proof.} Through $D$ draw the line parallel to $AE$ and $CF$. It lies strictly between them, so it meets segment $AC$ at a point $G$, and ray $DG$ lies inside angle $ADC$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,2),Ee=(6,2),C=(0,0),Fp=(6,0),D=(3,1.2),G=(0,1.2);
draw((-1,2)--(7,2)); draw((-1,0)--(7,0));
draw(A--D--C,linewidth(0.9)); draw(D--G,blue+dashed); draw(A--C,gray+dotted);
draw(arc(A,1.1,degrees(D-A)-360,0),red+linewidth(0.9)); draw(arc(C,1.1,0,degrees(D-C)),deepgreen+linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(D,1.1,degrees(A-D),180),red+linewidth(0.9)); draw(arc(D,1.3,180,degrees(C-D)),deepgreen+linewidth(0.9));
dot(A);dot(C);dot(D);dot(Ee);dot(Fp);dot(G);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$E$",Ee,N); label("$C$",C,S); label("$F$",Fp,S); label("$D$",D,E); label("$G$",G,W);
\end{asy}

$E$ and $G$ are on opposite sides of line $AD$, and $AE\parallel DG$, so $\angle EAD=\angle ADG$ (alternate angles). Similarly $\angle FCD=\angle CDG$. Adding,
\[\angle ADC=\angle ADG+\angle GDC=\boxed{\angle EAD+\angle FCD}.\]
\textbf{(a), second proof.} Draw $AC$. Since $D$ lies inside angle $EAC$ and inside angle $FCA$, we have $\angle EAC=\angle EAD+\angle DAC$ and $\angle FCA=\angle FCD+\angle DCA$. The co-interior angles on the transversal $AC$ satisfy $\angle EAC+\angle FCA=180^\circ$, and the angle sum of triangle $ACD$ gives $\angle DAC+\angle DCA+\angle ADC=180^\circ$. Subtracting,
\[\angle EAD+\angle FCD-\angle ADC=0.\]

\textbf{(b)} Again draw through $D$ the line parallel to $AE$, meeting $AC$ at $G$; now ray $DG$ points towards $E$ and $F$ and lies inside angle $ADC$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,2),Ee=(6,2),C=(0,0),Fp=(6,0),D=(-2.5,1),G=(0,1);
draw((-3.5,2)--(7,2)); draw((-3.5,0)--(7,0));
draw(A--D--C,linewidth(0.9)); draw(D--G,blue+dashed); draw(A--C,gray+dotted);
draw(arc(A,0.35,degrees(D-A)-360,0),red); draw(arc(C,0.35,0,degrees(D-C)),deepgreen);
draw(arc(D,0.8,degrees(C-D)-360,degrees(A-D)),blue);
dot(A);dot(C);dot(D);dot(Ee);dot(Fp);dot(G);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$E$",Ee,N); label("$C$",C,S); label("$F$",Fp,S); label("$D$",D,W); label("$G$",G,E);
\end{asy}

Now $E$ and $G$ are on the same side of line $AD$, so $\angle EAD$ and $\angle ADG$ are co-interior angles: $\angle EAD+\angle ADG=180^\circ$. Similarly $\angle FCD+\angle CDG=180^\circ$. Adding and using $\angle ADG+\angle GDC=\angle ADC$,
\[\angle ADC=\boxed{360^\circ-\angle EAD-\angle FCD}.\]

question:
For a positive real number $x$, define $x^i=e^{i\ln x}=\cos(\ln x)+i\sin(\ln x)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Check that $x=e^{\dfrac{\pi}{2}}$ satisfies $x^i=i$.
\item[(b)] Find all positive real numbers $x$ with $x^i=i$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $\sigma(m)$ be the sum of all positive divisors of $m$; then $m$ is abundant exactly when $\sigma(m)>2m$. Check $n=1,2,\dots$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $n=1$: $\sigma(1)=1<2$;
\item $n=2$: $\sigma(4)=1+2+4=7<8$;
\item $n=3$: $\sigma(9)=1+3+9=13<18$;
\item $n=4$: $\sigma(16)=31<32$;
\item $n=5$: $\sigma(25)=31<50$;
\item $n=6$: $\sigma(36)=1+2+3+4+6+9+12+18+36=91>72$.
\end{itemize}
So the least such $n$ is $\boxed{6}$; indeed the proper divisors of $36$ sum to $55>36$.

\emph{Remark.} No prime power $n=p^k$ with $k\ge1$ works: $\dfrac{\sigma\left(p^{2k}\right)}{p^{2k}}=1+\dfrac1p+\cdots+\dfrac{1}{p^{2k}}<\dfrac{p}{p-1}\le2$. That is why $2,3,4,5$ fail, and $6$ is the smallest integer greater than $1$ that is not a prime power.

question:
Regular hexagon $ABCDEF$ has side length $6$. A circle with centre $F$ and radius $FC$ is drawn, and line $AF$ meets the circle at points $G$ and $H$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find $GC^2+HC^2$.
\item[(b)] Find $GC+HC$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
In a regular hexagon the long diagonal $FC$ passes through the centre $O$ and equals twice the side, so the radius is $FC=12$. The triangles $OAF$ and $OFE$ are equilateral, so $\angle AFC=60^\circ$.

\textbf{(a)} The line $AF$ passes through the centre $F$ of the circle, so $GH$ is a diameter, of length $24$. Since $C$ is on the circle, $\angle GCH=90^\circ$ (an angle in a semicircle). By Pythagoras,
\[GC^2+HC^2=GH^2=24^2=\boxed{576}.\]

\textbf{(b)} Name the points so that $G$ is on ray $FA$ (beyond $A$, since $FA=6<12$) and $H$ is on the opposite ray. In triangle $GFC$ we have $FG=FC=12$ and $\angle GFC=\angle AFC=60^\circ$, so it is equilateral and $GC=12$. In triangle $HFC$ we have $FH=FC=12$ and $\angle HFC=180^\circ-60^\circ=120^\circ$; it is isosceles with base angles $30^\circ$, so
\[HC=2\cdot12\cos30^\circ=12\sqrt3.\]
Hence $GC+HC=\boxed{12+12\sqrt3}$. (Check with (a): $144+432=576$.)

