\end{asy}

For any two points $(x_i)$ and $(y_i)$ of the hypercube, each coordinate difference satisfies $|x_i-y_i|\le7$, so their distance is at most
\[\sqrt{7^2+7^2+7^2+7^2}=\sqrt{196}=14.\]
Equality holds for the opposite corners $(0,0,0,0)$ and $(7,7,7,7)$, and the segment joining them lies in the hypercube (its points $(t,t,t,t)$ with $0\le t\le7$ satisfy all the inequalities). So the longest segment has length $\boxed{14}$.

(This continues the pattern of diagonals: a square of side $7$ has diagonal $7\sqrt2$, a cube $7\sqrt3$, and the hypercube $7\sqrt4=14$; each step applies Pythagoras once more.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
