for(int a=0;a<2;++a) for(int b=0;b<2;++b) for(int c=0;c<2;++c) for(int d=0;d<2;++d){
  if(a==0) draw(P(0,b,c,d)--P(1,b,c,d),gray);
  if(b==0) draw(P(a,0,c,d)--P(a,1,c,d),gray);
  if(c==0) draw(P(a,b,0,d)--P(a,b,1,d),gray);
  if(d==0) draw(P(a,b,c,0)--P(a,b,c,1),gray);
  dot(P(a,b,c,d),gray);
}
draw(P(0,0,0,0)--P(1,1,1,1),red+linewidth(1));
dot(P(0,0,0,0),red); dot(P(1,1,1,1),red);
label("$(0,0,0,0)$",P(0,0,0,0),SW,fontsize(8pt));
label("$(7,7,7,7)$",P(1,1,1,1),NE,fontsize(8pt));
label("$7$",(P(0,0,0,0)+P(1,0,0,0))/2,S,fontsize(8pt));
label("$14$",(0.2,0.62),red);
\end{asy}

(A projection of the hypercube: two cubes with corresponding vertices joined; the red segment is the long diagonal.)

The shortest side is not the hypotenuse, so it is a leg. Let the other leg be $b$ and the hypotenuse $c$, with integers $10<b<c$ (if $b=10$, then $c=10\sqrt2$ is not an integer). Then
\[c^2-b^2=100,\qquad (c-b)(c+b)=100.\]
The factors $c-b$ and $c+b$ are positive with the same parity (their sum $2c$ is even) and $c-b<c+b$. The factorisations of $100$ into two factors of the same parity with the first smaller are $2\cdot50$ only ($10\cdot10$ has equal factors, giving $b=0$). So $c-b=2$, $c+b=50$, giving $c=26$ and $b=24$.

The only such triangle is $10$--$24$--$26$, and its area is $\dfrac12\cdot10\cdot24=\boxed{120}$.

