draw(circle(O,2),linewidth(0.8));
draw((O.x-2,O.y)--(O.x+2,O.y),red);
dot(O);
label("$2x$",(O.x+0.9,O.y),N,red);
label("area $x^2$",(1,1));
label("area $\pi x^2$",(O.x,O.y-0.9));
\end{asy}

Since $8^2+15^2=64+225=289=17^2$, the triangle is right-angled, with hypotenuse $17$ (by the converse of Pythagoras' theorem).

\textbf{Circumradius.} The hypotenuse of a right triangle is a diameter of its circumcircle (the right angle is an angle in a semicircle), so $R=\dfrac{17}{2}$.

\textbf{Inradius.} The area is $K=\dfrac12\cdot8\cdot15=60$ and the semiperimeter is $s=\dfrac{8+15+17}{2}=20$. From $K=rs$ we get $r=3$.

Hence
\[R+r=\dfrac{17}{2}+3=\boxed{\dfrac{23}{2}}.\]

