label("$O$",O,S); label("$P$",P,N); label("$Q$",Q,E);
label("$R$",(O+Q)/2,SE,gray);
label("$\rho$",3.45*unit((P+Q)/2),red);
label("$h$",(0,R-h/2),W);
label("$R-h$",(0,(R-h)/2),W);
label("cap",(-1.2,R+0.15),NW,blue);
\end{asy}

(Cross-section, not to scale: the blue arc is the cap.)

\textbf{The probability.} With $\rho=2$, each patch has area $4\pi$, and the $12$ patches have total area $48\pi$. The sphere has area $4\pi\cdot42^2=7056\pi$. So the probability is
\[\dfrac{48\pi}{7056\pi}=\dfrac{1}{147},\]
and $a+b=1+147=\boxed{148}$.

\textbf{The quadrilateral is a square.} The congruent isosceles triangles $ADC$ and $AFC$ share the base $AC$, so all four base angles $\angle DAC,\angle DCA,\angle FAC,\angle FCA$ are equal, say to $\alpha$. Since $D$ and $F$ are on opposite sides of $AC$, $\angle DAF=2\alpha$, and $\angle DAF=90^\circ$ because $AF\perp AD$. So $\alpha=45^\circ$, the apex angles are $90^\circ$, and $AD=DC=CF=FA$. Hence $AFCD$ is a square; let its side be $s$.

\textbf{Coordinates.} Put $A=(0,0)$, $F=(s,0)$, $C=(s,s)$, $D=(0,s)$. Then $E=\left(\dfrac{4s}{9},0\right)$. Because segment $CB$ crosses side $AF$, the point $B$ lies below the line $AF$. Ray $AC$ makes $45^\circ$ with ray $AF$, so the ray $AB$, which makes $75^\circ$ with $AC$ and points below $AF$, makes $30^\circ$ with $AF$ on the other side:
\[B=\left(8\cos30^\circ,\,-8\sin30^\circ\right)=\left(4\sqrt3,\,-4\right).\]
\textbf{Finding $s$.} The points $C$, $E$, $B$ are collinear, so the vectors $E-C=\left(-\dfrac{5s}{9},-s\right)$ and $B-C=\left(4\sqrt3-s,\,-4-s\right)$ are parallel:
\[\left(-\dfrac{5s}{9}\right)(-4-s)-(-s)\left(4\sqrt3-s\right)=0.\]
Dividing by $s$: $\dfrac{5(4+s)}{9}+4\sqrt3-s=0$, i.e. $\dfrac{4s}{9}=\dfrac{20}{9}+4\sqrt3$, so
\[s=5+9\sqrt3.\]
\textbf{The length.} Now $s-4\sqrt3=5+5\sqrt3$ and $s+4=9+9\sqrt3$, so
\[CB=\sqrt{\left(5+5\sqrt3\right)^2+\left(9+9\sqrt3\right)^2}=\left(1+\sqrt3\right)\sqrt{25+81}=\boxed{\sqrt{106}+\sqrt{318}}\approx28.13,\]
which rounds to $\boxed{28}$. (Indeed $B$ is beyond $E$ on ray $CE$, since $B$ is below the line $AF$.)

