label("$A$",A,W); label("$F$",F,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,NW); label("$E$",pE,NE); label("$B$",B,S);
label("$75^\circ$",5.3*dir(12));
label("$8$",(A+B)/2,SW,red);
\end{asy}

question:
A student asks whether the ``width'' of a parabola extends from $-\infty$ to $\infty$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the domain and the range of the function $f(x)=2x^2-8x+3$.
\item[(b)] The parabola $x=y^2-4y+1$ opens to the right. Find the set of $x$-coordinates and the set of $y$-coordinates of its points.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The formula $2x^2-8x+3$ can be evaluated for every real $x$, so the domain is all real numbers: a parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ never ``ends'' horizontally. Completing the square,
\[f(x)=2\left(x^2-4x+4\right)-8+3=2(x-2)^2-5.\]
Since $(x-2)^2$ takes every value $\ge0$, $f(x)$ takes every value $\ge-5$, and no smaller value. So
\[\text{domain}=\boxed{\text{all real }x},\qquad\text{range}=\boxed{y\ge-5}.\]

