solution:
We construct a polygonal path through the points with no self-intersections.

\textbf{Choosing axes.} There are finitely many lines through two of the points. Choose a direction for the $x$-axis that is not perpendicular to any of these lines; this is possible since only finitely many directions are excluded. Then no two of the points lie on a common line perpendicular to the $x$-axis, so all the points have different $x$-coordinates.

\textbf{The path.} Label the points $P_1,P_2,\dots,P_n$ so that their $x$-coordinates satisfy $x_1<x_2<\cdots<x_n$, and join them by the segments $P_1P_2,\ P_2P_3,\ \dots,\ P_{n-1}P_n$. The segment $P_iP_{i+1}$ lies in the vertical strip $x_i\le x\le x_{i+1}$, and it meets each boundary line of the strip only at its endpoint ($P_i$ or $P_{i+1}$), because it is not vertical.

\textbf{No self-intersections.}
\begin{itemize}
\item If $j\ge i+2$, the strips of $P_iP_{i+1}$ and $P_jP_{j+1}$ are $[x_i,x_{i+1}]$ and $[x_j,x_{j+1}]$, which are disjoint since $x_{i+1}<x_j$. So these segments do not meet.
\item The consecutive segments $P_{i-1}P_i$ and $P_iP_{i+1}$ lie in the strips $[x_{i-1},x_i]$ and $[x_i,x_{i+1}]$, which share only the line $x=x_i$, and each segment meets that line only at $P_i$. So they meet only at their common endpoint $P_i$.
\end{itemize}
Hence the polygonal path $P_1P_2\dots P_n$ passes through all the points and does not intersect itself. (Its corners can be rounded off if a smooth curve is wanted.) $\blacksquare$

