Let $ABCD$ be a parallelogram. Its opposite sides are congruent: $AB=CD$ and $BC=DA$. (The diagonal $AC$ splits it into triangles $ABC$ and $CDA$, which are congruent by ASA, since $AB\parallel CD$ and $BC\parallel AD$ give equal alternate angles at $A$ and $C$, and $AC$ is common.)

\textbf{If.} Suppose two consecutive sides are congruent, say $AB=BC$ (any pair of consecutive sides can be named this way). Then
\[AB=BC=DA\quad\text{and}\quad CD=AB,\]
so all four sides are congruent and $ABCD$ is a rhombus.

\textbf{Only if.} A rhombus has all four sides congruent, so in particular any two consecutive sides are congruent.

Hence the statement is true. $\blacksquare$

