question:
Let $A=6+6^2+6^3+\cdots+6^{99}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the remainder when $A$ is divided by $6^{50}+1$.
\item[(b)] Show that there are at least three positive integers $n$ such that the remainder when $A$ is divided by $n+1$ is $n$.
\end{enumerate}

Let
\[S=\sum_{n=1}^{2017}(2018-n)\dbinom{n}{2}.\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $S=\dbinom{2019}{4}$ by counting the $4$-element subsets of $\{1,2,\dots,2019\}$ according to their third-smallest element.
\item[(b)] Hence evaluate $\dfrac12S$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let $\{a<b<c<d\}$ be a $4$-element subset of $\{1,\dots,2019\}$, and write $c=n+1$. Since two elements are below $c$ and one is above it, $2\le n\le2017$. For a fixed $n$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $a<b$ are chosen from $\{1,\dots,n\}$, in $\dbinom n2$ ways;
\item $d$ is chosen from $\{n+2,\dots,2019\}$, in $2019-(n+1)=2018-n$ ways.
\end{itemize}
Summing over $n$ counts every subset exactly once:
\[\dbinom{2019}{4}=\sum_{n=2}^{2017}(2018-n)\dbinom n2=S,\]
since the term $n=1$ of $S$ is $0$. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(b)}
\[\dfrac12S=\dfrac12\dbinom{2019}{4}=\dfrac{2019\cdot2018\cdot2017\cdot2016}{48}=2019\cdot1009\cdot2017\cdot84=\boxed{345153808188}.\]

Let the centres be $A,B,C$. Each centre lies on the other two circles, so $AB=BC=CA=1$ and $ABC$ is equilateral with side $1$.

Let $R$ be the region inside all three circles, i.e. the set of points at distance at most $1$ from each of $A,B,C$. It contains the triangle $ABC$, whose points are within $1$ of every vertex. Outside the triangle, consider the side beyond $BC$ (opposite $A$):
\begin{itemize}
\item a point of $R$ there lies in the disc about $A$ on the far side of chord $BC$, i.e. in the circular segment cut off by $BC$ from the circle about $A$;
\item conversely, every point of the $60^\circ$ arc $BC$ of the circle about $A$ is within $1$ of $B$ and of $C$ (a chord subtending at most $60^\circ$ in a unit circle has length at most $1$), so by convexity of the discs the whole segment lies in $R$.
\end{itemize}
So $R$ is the triangle together with three congruent circular segments (a Reuleaux triangle). Each segment is a $60^\circ$ sector of radius $1$ minus the triangle:
\[\dfrac{\pi}{6}-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}.\]
Hence
\[[R]=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}+3\left(\dfrac\pi6-\dfrac{\sqrt3}4\right)=\boxed{\dfrac{\pi-\sqrt3}{2}}\approx0.705.\]

