Let the edge lengths be integers $a\le b\le c$. Then
\[abc=720,\qquad 2(ab+bc+ca)=484\iff ab+bc+ca=242.\]
Dividing the second equation by the first,
\[\dfrac1a+\dfrac1b+\dfrac1c=\dfrac{242}{720}=\dfrac{121}{360}.\]
Since $a$ is the smallest edge, $\dfrac3a\ge\dfrac{121}{360}$, so $a\le\dfrac{1080}{121}<9$; and $\dfrac1a<\dfrac{121}{360}$, so $a>\dfrac{360}{121}>2$. Thus $3\le a\le8$, and $a$ divides $720$, so $a\in\{3,4,5,6,8\}$. For each $a$, $bc=\dfrac{720}a$ and $b+c=\dfrac{242-bc}{a}$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $a=3$: $bc=240$, $b+c=\dfrac23$; \quad $a=4$: $bc=180$, $b+c=\dfrac{62}4$;
\item $a=5$: $bc=144$, $b+c=\dfrac{98}5$; \quad $a=6$: $bc=120$, $b+c=\dfrac{122}6$;
\item $a=8$: $bc=90$, $b+c=19$.
\end{itemize}
Only $a=8$ gives an integer $b+c$, and then $b,c$ are the roots of $t^2-19t+90=(t-9)(t-10)$. So the edges are $8,9,10$ (check: $8\cdot9\cdot10=720$ and $72+90+80=242$).

The longest segment joining two vertices is a space diagonal. By the Pythagorean theorem, a face diagonal is $\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$, and it is perpendicular to the third edge, so the space diagonal is
\[\sqrt{a^2+b^2+c^2}=\sqrt{(a+b+c)^2-2(ab+bc+ca)}=\sqrt{27^2-484}=\sqrt{245}=\boxed{7\sqrt5\text{ cm}}.\]

