\textbf{(a)} Measure positions in degrees clockwise from the top.
\begin{itemize}
\item The minute hand turns $360^\circ$ in $60$ minutes, so it is at $6m$ degrees.
\item The hour hand turns $\dfrac{360}{H}$ degrees per hour, i.e. $\dfrac{360}{60H}=\dfrac6H$ degrees per minute, so it is at $\dfrac{360h}{H}+\dfrac{6m}{H}$ degrees.
\end{itemize}
Both positions lie in $[0,360)$, because $\dfrac{360h}H+\dfrac{6m}H<\dfrac{360(H-1)}H+\dfrac{360}H=360$. The hands split the full turn into two angles: the difference of the positions,
\[\left|\dfrac{360h}{H}+\dfrac{6m}{H}-6m\right|=\left|\dfrac{360h}{H}-\dfrac{6(H-1)m}{H}\right|=\theta,\]
and $360^\circ-\theta$. The angle between the hands is the smaller one, which is $\theta$ when $\theta\le180^\circ$ and $360^\circ-\theta$ otherwise. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(b)}
\begin{itemize}
\item $H=10$: $\theta=|36h-5.4m|$;
\item $H=6$: $\theta=|60h-5m|$;
\item $H=5$: $\theta=|72h-4.8m|$;
\item $H=24$: $\theta=|15h-5.75m|$.
\end{itemize}
(For $H=12$ this gives the familiar $|30h-5.5m|$.)

\textbf{(c)} At $7{:}30$ on a $10$-hour clock, $\theta=|252-162|=90$, so the angle is $\boxed{90^\circ}$.

At $17{:}45$ on a $24$-hour clock, $\theta=|255-258.75|=3.75$, so the angle is $\boxed{3.75^\circ}$. (Indeed the hour hand is at $17.75\cdot15=266.25^\circ$ and the minute hand at $270^\circ$.)

question:
A student reduces exponents modulo the modulus. For example: ``since $100\equiv0\pmod{100}$, we get $2^{100}-2\equiv2^0-2\equiv-1\equiv99\pmod{100}$'', and ``since $102\equiv1\pmod{101}$, we get $3^{102}-5\equiv3^1-5\equiv-2\equiv99\pmod{101}$''.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show by a small counterexample that this rule is not valid.
\item[(b)] Find the correct remainders of $2^{100}-2$ upon division by $100$ and of $3^{102}-5$ upon division by $101$.
\end{enumerate}

The remainder theorem says that the remainder when a polynomial $f(x)$ is divided by $x-a$ is $f(a)$. Here is a version for a quadratic divisor.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Let $f(x)$ be a polynomial and let $a\ne b$. Prove that the remainder when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-a)(x-b)$ is
\[r(x)=f(a)\cdot\dfrac{x-b}{a-b}+f(b)\cdot\dfrac{x-a}{b-a}.\]
\item[(b)] Find the remainder when $x^{2021}+2x^{10}$ is divided by $x^2-1$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $r$ be the common radius and $O$ the centre of the middle circle. Tangent circles of radius $r$ have centres $2r$ apart, so the centres $P_1,\dots,P_6$ of the outer circles are at distance $2r$ from $O$, and neighbouring outer centres are $2r$ apart. Thus $O$ and two neighbouring outer centres form an equilateral triangle of side $2r$.

Such a triangle $OP_1P_2$ contains exactly one purple region: its sides pass through the three points of tangency, and the parts of the triangle inside the circles are three $60^\circ$ sectors of radius $r$. So each purple region has area
\[\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}(2r)^2-3\cdot\dfrac{\pi r^2}{6}=\sqrt3\,r^2-\dfrac{\pi r^2}{2},\]
and the six together have area $\left(6\sqrt3-3\pi\right)r^2$. Setting this equal to $6\sqrt3-3\pi$ gives $r=1$.

The centre $Z$ of the large circle, of radius $R$, is at distance $R-r$ from each $P_i$ (internal tangency). The points $P_1,\dots,P_6$ lie on the circle with centre $O$ and radius $2r$, and the only point equidistant from three points of a circle is its centre, so $Z=O$ and $R=2r+r=3r=3$. The large circle has area
\[\pi\cdot3^2=\boxed{9\pi}.\]

