Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral are supplementary, so $\angle BAC+\angle BDC=180^\circ$. Since $P,A,B$ are collinear, $\angle PAC=180^\circ-\angle BAC=\angle BDC=\angle PDB$. Triangles $PAC$ and $PDB$ also share the angle at $P$, so they are similar, and again $\dfrac{PA}{PD}=\dfrac{PC}{PB}$, i.e. $PA\cdot PB=PC\cdot PD$.

\textbf{(c) Tangent.} Let $A$ lie between $P$ and $B$.

