By the tangent--chord angle theorem, the angle between the tangent $TP$ and the chord $TA$ equals the inscribed angle on the arc $TA$: $\angle PTA=\angle TBA=\angle TBP$. Triangles $PTA$ and $PBT$ share the angle at $P$, so they are similar, and
\[\dfrac{PT}{PB}=\dfrac{PA}{PT},\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad PT^2=PA\cdot PB.\ \blacksquare\]

question:
How many $5$-letter strings of the $26$ letters $A$ to $Z$ have at least two consecutive letters that are the same? A student argues by cases:
\begin{itemize}
\item exactly $2$ consecutive letters the same: $4\cdot(26\cdot1\cdot25\cdot25\cdot25)=1625000$;
\item $3$ consecutive letters the same: $3\cdot(26\cdot1\cdot1\cdot25\cdot25)=48750$;
\item $4$ consecutive letters the same: $2\cdot(26\cdot1\cdot1\cdot1\cdot25)=1300$;
\item all $5$ letters the same: $26$;
\end{itemize}
for a total of $1675076$. Find the correct count, and determine exactly which strings the student's cases miss.

solution:
\textbf{The count.} There are $26^5=11881376$ strings. A string with no two equal neighbours has $26$ choices for the first letter and $25$ for each later letter (anything except the previous letter): $26\cdot25^4=10156250$ strings. So
\[26^5-26\cdot25^4=11881376-10156250=\boxed{1725126}\]
strings have two equal consecutive letters.

\textbf{What the cases miss.} Record for a string which of its $4$ neighbouring pairs (positions $12,23,34,45$) are equal, as a pattern of four bits ($1$ = equal). A pattern with $j$ zeros is shared by $26\cdot25^{j}$ strings: $26$ choices for the first letter, and each later letter is either forced (equal to the previous one) or one of $25$.

The student's cases count exactly the patterns whose $1$s form a single block: $1000,0100,0010,0001$ (case 1, each $26\cdot25^3$), $1100,0110,0011$ (case 2), $1110,0111$ (case 3) and $1111$ (case 4). These counts are right, but some patterns are missing: those with two separate blocks of equal letters,
\begin{itemize}
\item $1010$, $1001$, $0101$ (e.g. $AABBC$, $AABCC$, $ABBCC$): $3\cdot26\cdot25^2=48750$ strings;
\item $1101$, $1011$ (e.g. $AAABB$, $AABBB$): $2\cdot26\cdot25=1300$ strings.
\end{itemize}
In total the student misses $48750+1300=50050$ strings, and indeed $1675076+50050=1725126$.

Use coordinates $B=(0,0)$, $C=(14,0)$, $A=(5,12)$ (indeed $5^2+12^2=13^2$ and $9^2+12^2=15^2$).

\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Orthocentre.} The altitude from $A$ is the line $x=5$, with foot $K=(5,0)$. The altitude from $B$ is perpendicular to $AC$, whose direction is $(-9,12)\parallel(3,-4)$, so it is the line $3x-4y=0$. At $x=5$ it gives $y=\dfrac{15}4$, so $H=\left(5,\dfrac{15}4\right)$ and $H'=\left(5,-\dfrac{15}4\right)$.
\item \emph{Angle bisector.} By the angle bisector theorem $\dfrac{BD}{DC}=\dfrac{AB}{AC}=\dfrac{13}{15}$, so $BD=14\cdot\dfrac{13}{28}=\dfrac{13}2$ and $D=\left(\dfrac{13}2,0\right)$.
\item \emph{Incircle.} With semiperimeter $s=21$, the tangent lengths give $AE=s-BC=7$ and $CE=8$. So $E=C+\dfrac8{15}(A-C)=\left(14-\dfrac{72}{15},\dfrac{96}{15}\right)=\left(\dfrac{46}5,\dfrac{32}5\right)$.
\end{itemize}

