The side $H'H$ of the pentagon crosses $BC$ at $K$, and the pentagon is the union of the quadrilateral $HECK$ above $BC$ and the triangle $KDH'$ below it (it is concave at $D$). Splitting $HECK$ along $KE$:
\[[KCE]=\dfrac12\cdot KC\cdot y_E=\dfrac12\cdot9\cdot\dfrac{32}5=\dfrac{144}5,\qquad[KEH]=\dfrac12\cdot KH\cdot(x_E-5)=\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac{15}4\cdot\dfrac{21}5=\dfrac{63}8,\]
\[[KDH']=\dfrac12\cdot KD\cdot KH'=\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac32\cdot\dfrac{15}4=\dfrac{45}{16}.\]
Hence
\[[HECDH']=\dfrac{144}5+\dfrac{63}8+\dfrac{45}{16}=\dfrac{2304+630+225}{80}=\boxed{\dfrac{3159}{80}}=39.4875.\]

Since $CD=17=BD+BC$, the point $B$ lies between $D$ and $C$. Let $X$ be a point on ray $CA$ beyond $A$; the external bisector satisfies $\angle BAD=\angle DAX$.

