\textbf{$E$ and the length $AC$.} As $BE\parallel AD$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\angle ABE=\angle BAD$ (alternate angles, transversal $AB$);
\item $\angle AEB=\angle XAD$ (corresponding angles, transversal $CA$).
\end{itemize}
Since $\angle BAD=\angle XAD$, triangle $ABE$ is isosceles with $AE=AB=4$. (This is the proof of the external angle bisector theorem.) Also $BE\parallel DA$ gives triangle $CBE\sim$ triangle $CDA$, so
\[\dfrac{CE}{CA}=\dfrac{CB}{CD}=\dfrac5{17}.\]
With $CE=CA-4$: $17(CA-4)=5\,CA$, so $CA=\dfrac{17}3$ and $CE=\dfrac53$.

