\textbf{The points exist.} If $AB\parallel CD$, the arcs between the parallel chords are equal and $ABCD$ is an isosceles trapezoid with $AD=BC$, contrary to $AD<BC$; so $E$ exists. The tangents at $A$ and $D$ are parallel only if $AD$ is a diameter; but no chord is longer than a diameter and $AD<BC$, so $AD$ is not a diameter and $G$ exists. The diagonals of a convex quadrilateral meet, so $O$ exists.

\textbf{Collinearity.} We use Pascal's theorem: if $P_1,\dots,P_6$ lie on a circle, then the three points $P_1P_2\cap P_4P_5$, $P_2P_3\cap P_5P_6$ and $P_3P_4\cap P_6P_1$ are collinear; this remains true when two consecutive points coincide, the line through them being the tangent at that point.

Apply it to the hexagon $A,A,C,D,D,B$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $P_1P_2$ is the tangent at $A$ and $P_4P_5$ is the tangent at $D$; they meet at $G$;
