Two sets have the same \emph{size} (cardinality) if there is a bijection between them, and a set is \emph{countably infinite} if it has the same size as $\mathbb N=\{1,2,3,\dots\}$; its size is written $\aleph_0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Give a bijection from $\mathbb N$ to the set $\mathbb Z$ of all integers. (Since $\mathbb Z$ is the union of the two disjoint countably infinite sets $\{1,2,3,\dots\}$ and $\{0,-1,-2,\dots\}$, this shows ``$\aleph_0+\aleph_0=\aleph_0$''.)
\item[(b)] Prove that $f(m,n)=2^{m-1}(2n-1)$ is a bijection from the set of pairs $(m,n)$ of positive integers to $\mathbb N$. (This shows ``$\aleph_0\cdot\aleph_0=\aleph_0$''.)
\item[(c)] Show that ``$\aleph_0-\aleph_0$'' cannot be defined in the same way: give three countably infinite subsets $A$ of $\mathbb N$ such that $\mathbb N\setminus A$ is, respectively, empty, a set with exactly $5$ elements, and countably infinite.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let $g(n)=\dfrac n2$ for even $n$ and $g(n)=-\dfrac{n-1}{2}$ for odd $n$, so $g$ lists $\mathbb Z$ as $0,1,-1,2,-2,\dots$. It is a bijection, with inverse $z\mapsto2z$ for $z>0$ and $z\mapsto1-2z$ for $z\le0$ (check: $g(2z)=z$, $g(1-2z)=z$, and these inverse values cover each $n\in\mathbb N$ exactly once).

\textbf{(b)} Every positive integer $N$ can be written as $N=2^{k}q$ with $k\ge0$ and $q$ odd: divide by $2$ as long as possible. This form is unique, because $k$ must be the exponent of $2$ in the prime factorisation of $N$, and then $q=\dfrac{N}{2^k}$. Writing $k=m-1$ and $q=2n-1$ with positive integers $m,n$, each $N$ equals $f(m,n)$ for exactly one pair $(m,n)$. So $f$ is a bijection. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(c)}
\begin{itemize}
\item $A=\mathbb N$: then $\mathbb N\setminus A=\varnothing$.
\item $A=\{6,7,8,\dots\}$, which is countably infinite via $n\mapsto n+5$: then $\mathbb N\setminus A=\{1,2,3,4,5\}$ has $5$ elements.
\item $A=\{2,4,6,\dots\}$, countably infinite via $n\mapsto2n$: then $\mathbb N\setminus A=\{1,3,5,\dots\}$ is countably infinite via $n\mapsto2n-1$.
\end{itemize}
In each case a set of size $\aleph_0$ is removed from a set of size $\aleph_0$, but the size of what is left can be $0$, $5$ or $\aleph_0$. So subtraction of infinite cardinals has no well-defined answer (and division fails for the same reason), while addition and multiplication behave as in (a) and (b).

Let $D$ be the disc and $Q$ the square, and let $I$ be their overlap. Then
\[[D\text{ outside }Q]=[D]-[I],\qquad[Q\text{ outside }D]=[Q]-[I].\]
These are equal exactly when $[D]=[Q]$, that is,
\[\pi r^2=1,\qquad r=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt\pi}\approx0.564,\]
which is $\boxed{0.56}$ to the nearest hundredth.

(With this $r<1$, the overlap is a quarter disc of area $\dfrac14$, the part of the disc outside the square is $\dfrac34$ of the disc, of area $\dfrac34$, and the part of the square outside the disc also has area $1-\dfrac14=\dfrac34$.)

