question:
A hotel-rating website works as follows. As long as every rating a hotel has received is five stars, the site sends the hotel exactly one new customer after each rating; as soon as the hotel receives a one-star rating, the site removes it and no further new customers come.

Every guest of Sarah's hotel would honestly rate it five stars. However, each new customer, independently of the others, gives a one-star rating (to ``game the system'') with probability $\dfrac{1}{10}$, and otherwise gives five stars. Billy, the first guest, is not counted as a new customer; he rates the hotel five stars and so starts the chain of new customers.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the expected number of new customers who visit Sarah's hotel?
\item[(b)] Let $x$ be the answer to (a). What is the probability that exactly $x$ new customers visit the hotel?
\end{enumerate}

