question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] A triangle has side lengths $a,b,c$, semiperimeter $s=\dfrac{a+b+c}{2}$ and area $K$. Prove that the radius of its inscribed circle is $r=\dfrac Ks$.
\item[(b)] Find the inradius of the triangle with side lengths $13$, $14$, $15$.
\item[(c)] Prove that a right triangle with legs $a,b$ and hypotenuse $c$ has inradius $r=\dfrac{a+b-c}{2}$, and check both formulas on the $3$-$4$-$5$ triangle.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let $I$ be the incentre of triangle $ABC$, with $a=BC$, $b=CA$, $c=AB$. The incircle touches each side, and the radius to a point of tangency is perpendicular to that side, so the distance from $I$ to each side is $r$. Joining $I$ to the vertices splits the triangle into triangles $IBC$, $ICA$, $IAB$ with bases $a,b,c$ and common height $r$:
\[K=\dfrac12ar+\dfrac12br+\dfrac12cr=r\cdot\dfrac{a+b+c}{2}=rs,\qquad\text{so}\qquad r=\dfrac Ks.\ \blacksquare\]

\textbf{(b)} Here $s=21$, and by Heron's formula $K=\sqrt{21\cdot8\cdot7\cdot6}=\sqrt{7056}=84$. So $r=\dfrac{84}{21}=\boxed{4}$.

