Everyone faces inwards, so each person's right hand holds the left hand of the neighbour on one fixed side (say clockwise), and the left hand holds the right hand of the neighbour on the other side. Going once around the circle clockwise, read the sequence of boys and girls.
\begin{itemize}
\item A boy gives his right hand to a girl exactly when the next person clockwise is a girl: a change from boy to girl.
\item A boy gives his left hand to a girl exactly when the previous person is a girl: a change from girl to boy.
\end{itemize}
Around a circle the changes alternate: after a change from boys to girls, the next change must be from girls back to boys, and so on. So the two kinds of change occur equally often (each block of consecutive girls starts with one change and ends with the other). Hence the number of boys giving their left hand to a girl is also $18$. The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(A)}\ 18}$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] \emph{Rational root theorem.} Let $P(x)=a_nx^n+a_{n-1}x^{n-1}+\cdots+a_1x+a_0$ have integer coefficients with $a_n\ne0$ and $a_0\ne0$. Prove that if $\dfrac pq$ is a root of $P$, where $p,q$ are integers with $q>0$ and $\gcd(p,q)=1$, then $p\mid a_0$ and $q\mid a_n$.
\item[(b)] Use the theorem to find all rational roots of $2x^3+x^2-13x+6$, and factor the polynomial completely over the integers.
\end{enumerate}

