Trapezoid $ABED$ has bases $AB$ and $DE$, legs $AD$ and $BE$, and diagonals $AE$ and $BD$. Their slopes are
\[AD:\ \dfrac{-\sqrt3}{-1}=\sqrt3,\qquad BE:\ \dfrac{-\sqrt3}{1}=-\sqrt3,\qquad AE:\ \dfrac{-\sqrt3}{3},\qquad BD:\ \dfrac{-\sqrt3}{-3}=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{3}.\]
The positive ones are the leg $AD$ and the diagonal $BD$, and
\[\sqrt3+\dfrac{\sqrt3}{3}=\dfrac{4\sqrt3}{3}.\]
So $a=4$, $b=3$, $c=3$ and $abc=\boxed{36}$.

There are $\dbinom92=36$ equally likely pairs of cards for each draw, and the two draws are independent.

\textbf{Alice.} The prime sums possible are $3,5,7,11,13,17$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $3$: $\{1,2\}$;\quad $5$: $\{1,4\},\{2,3\}$;\quad $7$: $\{1,6\},\{2,5\},\{3,4\}$;
\item $11$: $\{2,9\},\{3,8\},\{4,7\},\{5,6\}$;\quad $13$: $\{4,9\},\{5,8\},\{6,7\}$;\quad $17$: $\{8,9\}$.
\end{itemize}
That is $14$ pairs, so $P(\text{Alice wins})=\dfrac{14}{36}=\dfrac7{18}$.

\textbf{Bob.} Sums $12$: $\{3,9\},\{4,8\},\{5,7\}$; $13$: $3$ pairs (above); $14$: $\{5,9\},\{6,8\}$; $15$: $\{6,9\},\{7,8\}$; $16$: $\{7,9\}$; $17$: $\{8,9\}$. That is $12$ pairs, so $P(\text{Bob wins})=\dfrac{12}{36}=\dfrac13$.

By independence, the probability that both win is
\[\dfrac7{18}\cdot\dfrac13=\boxed{\dfrac{7}{54}}.\]

Adjacent points among five equally spaced points subtend a central angle of $\dfrac{360^\circ}{5}=72^\circ$, so sector $AOB$ is $\dfrac15$ of its disc. Among eight points the angle is $45^\circ$, so sector $CPD$ is $\dfrac18$ of its disc. Each disc has area $400\pi$, so the difference is
\[\dfrac{400\pi}{5}-\dfrac{400\pi}{8}=80\pi-50\pi=\boxed{30\pi}.\]

