question:
Charlie has a rectangular sheet of paper whose horizontal sides have length $x$ and whose vertical sides have length $\dfrac43x$. He makes three creases, unfolding the sheet after each fold:
\begin{itemize}
\item first he folds the sheet in half so that the top edge lies on the bottom edge (a horizontal crease);
\item then he folds along a diagonal of the sheet;
\item finally he folds the bottom edge of the sheet (a side of length $x$) up so that it lies on the first crease.
\end{itemize}
The three creases divide the sheet into regions. The smallest region that is a triangle has area $\dfrac abx^2$, where $a$ and $b$ are relatively prime positive integers. Find $(a+b)^2$.

solution:
Place the sheet with corners $(0,0)$, $(x,0)$, $(x,\dfrac43x)$, $(0,\dfrac43x)$ and take the diagonal from $(0,0)$ to $\left(x,\dfrac43x\right)$, the line $Y=\dfrac43X$. (The other diagonal gives the mirror image, with the same areas.) The first crease is the line $Y=\dfrac23x$. Folding the bottom edge onto it creates the crease halfway between them, $Y=\dfrac13x$.

The diagonal meets $Y=\dfrac13x$ at $\left(\dfrac x4,\dfrac x3\right)$ and $Y=\dfrac23x$ at $\left(\dfrac x2,\dfrac23x\right)$. The two horizontal creases cut the sheet into three strips, and the diagonal cuts each strip in two:
\begin{itemize}
\item bottom strip: the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(0,\dfrac13x)$, $(\dfrac14x,\dfrac13x)$, and a quadrilateral;
\item middle strip: two quadrilaterals (trapezoids);
\item top strip: a quadrilateral, and the triangle with vertices $(\dfrac12x,\dfrac23x)$, $(x,\dfrac23x)$, $(x,\dfrac43x)$.
\end{itemize}

