Let $O$ be the centre and $R$ the distance from $O$ to each vertex.

\textbf{The circumradius.} A regular octagon of side $4$ is a square with its corners cut off by isosceles right triangles with legs $\dfrac{4}{\sqrt2}=2\sqrt2$, so the distance between opposite sides is $4+4\sqrt2$ and the distance from $O$ to a side is $2+2\sqrt2$. The foot of that perpendicular is the midpoint of the side, so
\[R^2=2^2+\left(2+2\sqrt2\right)^2=4+12+8\sqrt2=16+8\sqrt2.\]

\textbf{The triangle.} $F$ and $B$ are opposite vertices, so $FB$ is a diameter through $O$. The vertices $G$ and $E$ are symmetric about line $OF$, so $GE\perp FB$ and $J$ is the midpoint of $GE$; since $\angle FOE=45^\circ$,
\[OJ=R\cos45^\circ=\dfrac{R}{\sqrt2},\qquad JE=R\sin45^\circ=\dfrac{R}{\sqrt2}.\]
In the same way $A$ and $C$ are symmetric about line $OB$, so $AC\perp FB$ at its midpoint $I$, with $OI=\dfrac{R}{\sqrt2}$ on the other side of $O$. Hence $JI=OJ+OI=\sqrt2\,R$, and triangle $JEI$ has a right angle at $J$.

