question:
A right triangle has legs of lengths $A$ and $B$ and hypotenuse of length $C$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Is it possible that $A+B=C+1$? If so, give an example.
\item[(b)] Show that this is impossible if $A$, $B$, $C$ are all integers.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Yes. Take the $3$-$4$-$5$ right triangle, for which $3+4-5=2$, and scale it by $\dfrac12$: the triangle with legs $\dfrac32$, $2$ and hypotenuse $\dfrac52$ is a right triangle (since $\dfrac94+4=\dfrac{25}{4}$), and
\[\dfrac32+2=\dfrac72=\dfrac52+1.\]
More generally, scaling any right triangle by the factor $\dfrac{1}{A+B-C}$ (which is positive, by the triangle inequality) gives an example. Geometrically, $A+B-C$ is the diameter of the inscribed circle, so the examples are exactly the right triangles with inradius $\dfrac12$.

