pair A=(-sqrt(5),-2), B=(sqrt(21),-2), C=(sqrt(21),sqrt(28)), D=(-sqrt(5),sqrt(28));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(X--A,blue); draw(X--B,blue); draw(X--C,blue); draw(X--D,red+dashed);
dot(X);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,NW); label("$X$",X,W);
label("$3$",(X+A)/2,NW,blue); label("$5$",(X+B)/2,N,blue); label("$7$",(X+C)/2,NW,blue); label("$\sqrt{33}$",(X+D)/2,NE,red);
\end{asy}

Let the other vertices be $P=\left(p,p^2\right)$ and $Q=\left(q,q^2\right)$ with $p\ne q$, both nonzero. From $OP=OQ$,
\[p^2+p^4=q^2+q^4\iff\left(p^2-q^2\right)\left(1+p^2+q^2\right)=0,\]
and the second factor is positive, so $p^2=q^2$, i.e. $q=-p$. Then $PQ=2|p|$, and $PQ=OP$ gives
\[4p^2=p^2+p^4\iff p^2=3.\]
So the triangle is unique: its vertices are $O$, $\left(\sqrt3,3\right)$, $\left(-\sqrt3,3\right)$, and its side length is $\boxed{2\sqrt3}$. (Check: $OP=\sqrt{3+9}=2\sqrt3$.)

