Let $(x,y)$ be on the graph. Since both squares are at most $1$, we have $|x|\le1$, and
\[\left(y-\sqrt{|x|}\right)^2=1-x^2\le1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad y-\sqrt{|x|}\ge-1.\]
So $y\ge\sqrt{|x|}-1$ and
\[x+y\ge x+\sqrt{|x|}-1\ge-|x|+\sqrt{|x|}-1\ge-1,\]
because $\sqrt{|x|}\ge|x|$ when $0\le|x|\le1$.

Equality holds at $(x,y)=(0,-1)$, which lies on the graph: $0^2+(-1-0)^2=1$. So the least value of $x+y$ is $\boxed{-1}$.

\begin{asy}
