The heart-shaped graph lies above the line $x+y=-1$ and touches it only at its bottom point $(0,-1)$.

The vertex is $V=(4,3)$. A point of the parabola is $\left(4+s,3+s^2\right)$, at distance $\sqrt{s^2+s^4}$ from $V$; this distance is $5$ exactly when
\[s^4+s^2-25=0\iff s^2=\dfrac{\sqrt{101}-1}{2}\]
(the other root of the quadratic in $s^2$ is negative). So the two base vertices must be $P=\left(4-t,3+t^2\right)$ and $Q=\left(4+t,3+t^2\right)$ with $t=\sqrt{\dfrac{\sqrt{101}-1}{2}}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return (x-4)^2+3;}
real t=sqrt((sqrt(101)-1)/2);
draw((-0.3,0)--(7.4,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-0.3)--(0,10),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(7.4,0),E); label("$y$",(0,10),N);
draw(graph(f,1.4,6.6),blue+linewidth(0.9));
pair V=(4,3), P=(4-t,f(4-t)), Q=(4+t,f(4+t));
fill(V--P--Q--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.7)); draw(V--P--Q--cycle,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(V--(4,f(4+t)),dashed);
dot(V); dot(P); dot(Q);
label("$V(4,3)$",V,S); label("$P$",P,W); label("$Q$",Q,E);
label("$5$",(V+P)/2+(-0.5,0),W,red); label("$5$",(V+Q)/2+(0.5,0),E,red);
label("$t^2$",(4,(3+f(4+t))/2),E); label("$2t$",(4,f(4+t)),N);
\end{asy}

The base $PQ$ is horizontal with length $2t$, and the height from $V$ is $t^2$. The area is
\[\dfrac12\cdot2t\cdot t^2=t^3=\boxed{\left(\dfrac{\sqrt{101}-1}{2}\right)^{\dfrac32}}\approx9.63.\]

question:
Ash, May, Pikachu, Mudkip and Treeko stand in a circle in that order and count aloud in turn, starting with Ash saying $0$, May $1$, Pikachu $2$, Mudkip $3$, Treeko $4$, Ash $5$, and so on around the circle. The rules are:
\begin{itemize}
\item whoever calls out a multiple of $69$ gains an extra life;
\item whoever calls out a number divisible by none of the first five primes $2,3,5,7,11$ must leave the circle, unless they have an extra life, in which case they use up the extra life and stay.
\end{itemize}
After someone leaves, the counting continues with the next person still in the circle. The game ends when four people have left. Who is the last one left?

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that for all real numbers $a,b,c,d$,
\[\left(a^2+b^2\right)\left(c^2+d^2\right)-(ac+bd)^2=(ad-bc)^2,\]
and deduce that $(ac+bd)^2\le\left(a^2+b^2\right)\left(c^2+d^2\right)$, with equality exactly when $ad=bc$.
\item[(b)] Ash's machine opens $a^2+b^2$ wormholes, where $a,b,c,d$ are positive integers. Looking through one wormhole takes him $c^2+d^2$ years, and he has $(ac+bd)^2$ years to live. Show that he can look through all the wormholes (and so is guaranteed to find the one leading to you) only when $\dfrac ab=\dfrac cd$.
\end{enumerate}

The lid of Ash's box is a regular hexagon with side length $69$. For each vertex $V$ of the hexagon, the arc of the circle with centre $V$ and radius $69$ that lies inside the hexagon is drawn; it runs from one neighbouring vertex of $V$ through the centre of the hexagon to the other neighbouring vertex. These six arcs bound six congruent leaf-shaped regions (Ash's tea leaves), each joining the centre of the hexagon to a vertex, as in the figure.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1;
pair[] V; for(int k=0;k<6;++k) V.push(r*dir(60k));
for(int i=0;i<6;++i){
  path pet=arc(V[(i+5)%6],r,60i+60,60i+120)--arc(V[(i+1)%6],r,60i+240,60i+300)--cycle;
  fill(pet,rgb(0.7,0.9,0.7));}
for(int k=0;k<6;++k) draw(arc(V[k],r,60k+120,60k+240),darkgreen);
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot((0,0)); label("$69$",(V[4]+V[5])/2,S);
\end{asy}

Find the area of the part of the lid not covered by the tea leaves.

solution:
Let $r=69$, let $O$ be the centre and $V_0,\dots,V_5$ the vertices in order. Each triangle $OV_kV_{k+1}$ is equilateral with side $r$.

\textbf{One leaf.} The circle with centre $V_0$ and radius $r$ passes through $O$ and $V_1$ (both at distance $r$ from $V_0$), and its arc from $V_1$ to $O$ subtends $\angle V_1V_0O=60^\circ$. The region between this arc and the chord $OV_1$ is a circular segment of area
\[\text{sector}-\text{triangle}=\dfrac{60}{360}\pi r^2-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}r^2=r^2\left(\dfrac{\pi}{6}-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\right).\]
It bulges away from $V_0$, into triangle $OV_1V_2$. Likewise the arc of the circle centred at $V_2$ from $O$ to $V_1$ cuts off a congruent segment on the other side of $OV_1$. These two segments form the leaf from $O$ to $V_1$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1;
pair[] V; for(int k=0;k<6;++k) V.push(r*dir(60k));
pair O=(0,0);
for(int i=0;i<6;++i){
  path pet=arc(V[(i+5)%6],r,60i+60,60i+120)--arc(V[(i+1)%6],r,60i+240,60i+300)--cycle;
  fill(pet,rgb(0.85,0.95,0.85));}
path seg1=arc(V[0],r,120,180)--cycle;
path seg2=arc(V[2],r,300,360)--cycle;
fill(seg1,rgb(0.5,0.8,0.5)); fill(seg2,rgb(0.6,0.75,0.95));
for(int k=0;k<6;++k) draw(arc(V[k],r,60k+120,60k+240),darkgreen);
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--V[1],red); draw(V[0]--O,dashed); draw(V[0]--V[1],dashed); draw(V[2]--O,dashed);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,dir(255));
label("$V_1$",V[1],N); label("$V_0$",V[0],E); label("$V_2$",V[2],N);
label("$60^\circ$",V[0]+0.28*dir(150),fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

so each leaf has area $2r^2\left(\dfrac{\pi}{6}-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\right)=r^2\left(\dfrac{\pi}{3}-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$.

\textbf{No overlaps.} Inside triangle $OV_1V_2$ lie two segments: the one on chord $OV_1$ (arc centred at $V_0$) and, by symmetry, the one on chord $OV_2$ (arc centred at $V_3$). The axis of symmetry of this triangle is the line through $O$ perpendicular to $V_0O$, so it is tangent at $O$ to the circle centred at $V_0$; hence the segment on $OV_1$ lies on $V_1$'s side of the axis, touching it only at $O$, and by symmetry the segment on $OV_2$ lies on $V_2$'s side. So the segments meet only at $O$. Hence the six leaves do not overlap, and together they cover $6r^2\left(\dfrac{\pi}{3}-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)=r^2\left(2\pi-3\sqrt3\right)$.

\textbf{Uncovered area.} The hexagon has area $6\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}r^2=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}r^2$, so the uncovered area is
\[\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}r^2-\left(2\pi-3\sqrt3\right)r^2=\left(\dfrac{9\sqrt3}{2}-2\pi\right)r^2.\]
With $r^2=4761$ this is
\[\boxed{\dfrac{42849\sqrt3}{2}-9522\pi}\approx7194.1.\]

Triangle $OAD$ has base $AD=50$ (on the line $x=-25$) and height $25$ (the distance from $O$ to that line), so its area is $\dfrac12\cdot50\cdot25=625$.

The vectors $\overrightarrow{OA}=(-25,25)$ and $\overrightarrow{OD}=(-25,-25)$ have dot product $625-625=0$, so $\angle AOD=90^\circ$. Since the distance from $O$ to $AD$ is $25>4$, the part of the disc inside the triangle is exactly a quarter of the disc, of area $\dfrac14\pi\cdot4^2=4\pi$.

