So one triangle portion has area $\boxed{625-4\pi}$. (By symmetry, triangle $OBC$ gives the same.)

Each interior angle of an equiangular hexagon is $120^\circ$. Extend sides $AB$, $CD$ and $EF$. Lines $AB$ and $CD$ meet at a point $Q$ beyond $B$ and $C$, and triangle $BQC$ has angles $180^\circ-120^\circ=60^\circ$ at $B$ and at $C$, so it is equilateral with side $BC=4$. In the same way lines $CD$ and $EF$ meet at $R$, with triangle $DRE$ equilateral of side $DE=3$, and lines $EF$ and $AB$ meet at $P$, with triangle $FPA$ equilateral of side $FA=5$. So the three lines form a large equilateral triangle $PQR$ with side
\[PQ=PA+AB+BQ=FA+AB+BC=5+3+4=12.\]

