The hexagon is the large triangle minus the three corner triangles, so its area is
\[\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\left(12^2-5^2-4^2-3^2\right)=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot94=\boxed{\dfrac{47\sqrt3}{2}}.\]

Let $P$, $Q$ be the centres of the circles of radius $5$ and $R$ the centre of the circle of radius $8$. Then $PQ=10$ and $PR=QR=13$. Place $P=(-5,0)$, $Q=(5,0)$; then $R$ is on the $y$-axis at height $\sqrt{13^2-5^2}=12$, so $R=(0,12)$.

Let the big circle have centre $Z$ and radius $\rho$. Internal tangency means
\[ZP=\rho-5,\qquad ZQ=\rho-5,\qquad ZR=\rho-8.\]
From $ZP=ZQ$, $Z$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $PQ$, the $y$-axis: $Z=(0,k)$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $k\le12$, then $ZR=12-k=\rho-8$, so $\rho=20-k$, and $ZP=\sqrt{25+k^2}=15-k$. Squaring, $25+k^2=225-30k+k^2$, so $k=\dfrac{20}{3}$ and $\rho=\dfrac{40}{3}$.
\item If $k>12$, then $k-12=\rho-8$, so $\rho=k-4$ and $\sqrt{25+k^2}=k-9$, which gives $25=-18k+81$, $k=\dfrac{28}{9}<12$: a contradiction.
\end{itemize}
Check: $ZP=\sqrt{25+\dfrac{400}{9}}=\dfrac{25}{3}=\rho-5$ and $ZR=12-\dfrac{20}{3}=\dfrac{16}{3}=\rho-8$. So the radius is $\boxed{\dfrac{40}{3}}$.

