Three positive numbers $\alpha<\beta<\gamma$ are the angles of a triangle exactly when $\alpha+\beta+\gamma=180$. Substituting $\gamma=180-\alpha-\beta$, the conditions $0<\alpha<\beta<\gamma$ become
\[\alpha>0,\qquad\beta>\alpha,\qquad\alpha+2\beta<180.\]
In the $(\alpha,\beta)$-plane these describe the inside of the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(60,60)$ and $(0,90)$ (without its boundary).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=0.04;
pair O=(0,0), P=s*(60,60), Q=s*(0,90);
fill(O--P--Q--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.9,1));
draw(O--P,blue+dashed); draw(P--Q,blue+dashed); draw(O--Q,blue+dashed);
draw((0,0)--s*(105,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,0)--s*(0,105),Arrow(6));
label("$\alpha$",s*(105,0),E); label("$\beta$",s*(0,105),N);
draw(s*(60,0)--P,dotted); draw(s*(0,60)--P,dotted);
label("$60$",s*(60,0),S); label("$90$",Q,W); label("$60$",s*(0,60),W);
label("$(60,60)$",P,E); label("$\beta=\alpha$",s*(40,40),SE,blue+fontsize(8pt));
label("$\alpha+2\beta=180$",s*(32,76),NE,blue+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Necessary bounds.}
\begin{itemize}
\item $3\alpha<\alpha+\beta+\gamma=180$, so $0<\alpha<60$.
\item $2\beta<\beta+\gamma=180-\alpha<180$, so $0<\beta<90$.
\item $3\gamma>\alpha+\beta+\gamma=180$, so $60<\gamma<180$.
\end{itemize}
\textbf{Every such value occurs.}
\begin{itemize}
\item Given $0<\alpha<60$, take $\beta=45+\dfrac{\alpha}{4}$ and $\gamma=135-\dfrac{5\alpha}{4}$. The angles add up to $180$, and $\alpha<\beta<\gamma$ both reduce to $\alpha<60$.
\item Given $0<\beta<90$, let $\alpha=\dfrac12\min(\beta,180-2\beta)>0$ and $\gamma=180-\alpha-\beta$. Then $\alpha<\beta$, and $\gamma>\beta$ because $\alpha<180-2\beta$.
\item Given $60<\gamma<180$, put $m=\dfrac{180-\gamma}{2}$, so $0<m<\gamma$; let $\delta=\dfrac12\min(m,\gamma-m)>0$, $\alpha=m-\delta$ and $\beta=m+\delta$. Then $0<\alpha<\beta<\gamma$ and $\alpha+\beta+\gamma=180$.
\end{itemize}
Hence
\[\boxed{0^\circ<\alpha<60^\circ,\qquad0^\circ<\beta<90^\circ,\qquad60^\circ<\gamma<180^\circ}.\]

Let $g=\gcd(96,105)=3$. The lattice points on the segment are the points $(32k,35k)$ for $k=0,1,2,3$: a point $(x,y)$ of the segment has $y=\dfrac{35}{32}x$, which is an integer only when $32\mid x$, since $\gcd(32,35)=1$. These points cut the segment into $3$ translated copies of the segment from $(0,0)$ to $(32,35)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=1/16;
pair O=(0,0), T=s*(96,105);
draw(box(O,T),linewidth(0.8));
for(int k=1;k<3;++k){draw(s*(32k,0)--s*(32k,105),dashed+gray); draw(s*(0,35k)--s*(96,35k),dashed+gray);}
draw(O--T,red+linewidth(1));
for(int k=0;k<=3;++k) dot(s*(32k,35k));
label("$(0,0)$",O,SW); label("$(96,105)$",T,NE); label("$(32,35)$",s*(32,35),SE,fontsize(8pt)); label("$(64,70)$",s*(64,70),SE,fontsize(8pt));
label("$96$",s*(48,0),S); label("$105$",s*(0,52.5),W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{One piece.} The segment from $(0,0)$ to $(32,35)$ crosses each of the $31$ vertical lines $x=1,\dots,31$ and each of the $34$ horizontal lines $y=1,\dots,34$ exactly once, and no two of these crossings coincide, because the segment passes through no lattice point strictly between its ends. It starts in one square, and each crossing takes it into a new square, so it passes through $1+31+34=66$ squares.

\textbf{Whole segment.} The three pieces lie in different blocks of squares (the dashed $32\times35$ rectangles), so the total is $3\cdot66=\boxed{198}$. This agrees with the formula $96+105-\gcd(96,105)=198$.

question:
A student found the following way to add two two-digit numbers $\overline{ab}$ and $\overline{cd}$ (where $a,c$ are the tens digits and $b,d$ the units digits):
\begin{itemize}
\item compute $a+c$ and $a+b$, and form the number $10(a+c)+(a+b)$ (``write $a+b$ after $a+c$'', with a carry if $a+b\ge10$);
\item then add $d-a$.
\end{itemize}
For example, $51+28$: $5+2=7$ and $5+1=6$ give $76$, and $76+(8-5)=79$. For $88+73$: $8+7=15$ and $8+8=16$ give $150+16=166$, and $166+(3-8)=161$. Prove that the method always gives the correct sum.

solution:
The vertex on the line is $(x,4-2x)$ with $0<x<2$, so the rectangle has width $x$, height $4-2x$ and area
\[x(4-2x)=-2x^2+4x=2-2(x-1)^2\le2,\]
with equality exactly when $x=1$. The largest rectangle is $1$ by $2$, with perimeter $2(1+2)=\boxed{6}$.

