Let $M$ be the midpoint of the hypotenuse $AC$. Then $MA=MB=MC$ (the midpoint of the hypotenuse is the circumcentre), and since $\angle A=60^\circ$, triangle $ABM$ is equilateral. Let $s=AB=AM=BM=MC$. Also $\angle MBC=\angle MCB=30^\circ$; since $\angle DBC=10^\circ<30^\circ$, the point $D$ lies strictly between $M$ and $C$.

\textbf{Step 1.} Put $x=MD$, so $0<x<s$ and $CD=s-x$. Then $BE=CD=s-x<s=AB$, so $E$ lies on segment $AB$ with $AE=AB-BE=x$.

\textbf{Step 2.} Let $F$ be the point of segment $AM$ with $AF=x$. Triangle $AEF$ has $AE=AF=x$ and $\angle A=60^\circ$, so it is equilateral: $EF=x$ and $\angle AFE=60^\circ$, hence $\angle EFD=180^\circ-60^\circ=120^\circ$. Moreover $FD=FM+MD=(s-x)+x=s$.

\textbf{Step 3.} Compare triangles $EFD$ and $DMB$:
\[EF=x=DM,\qquad FD=s=MB,\qquad\angle EFD=120^\circ=\angle DMB,\]
where $\angle DMB=180^\circ-\angle AMB=120^\circ$. By SAS they are congruent, so $ED=DB$.

