label("$10^\circ$",B+0.9*dir(5),fontsize(7pt));
draw(arc(D,0.35,degrees(B-D),degrees(Ep-D)),red); label("$20^\circ$",D+0.62*unit(unit(B-D)+unit(Ep-D)),red+fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 4.} Triangle $DBE$ is isosceles with $DB=DE$, and $\angle DBE=\angle ABC-\angle DBC=90^\circ-10^\circ=80^\circ$. So $\angle DEB=80^\circ$ and
\[\angle BDE=180^\circ-80^\circ-80^\circ=\boxed{20^\circ}.\]

Let $M$ be the midpoint of $AB$. Since $F$ is on line $EM$, which joins the midpoints of opposite sides of the square, $FM\perp AB$. Since $\angle AFB=90^\circ$, $F$ lies on the circle with diameter $AB$ (Thales), so $MF=MA=1$.

So triangle $AFB$ has base $AB=2$ and height $MF=1$, and area $\dfrac12\cdot2\cdot1=1$. It lies outside the square (on the other side of $AB$), so the pentagon $AFBCD$ is the square together with this triangle:
\[\text{area}=4+1=\boxed{5}.\]

