We have $k=2^4\cdot3^3\cdot5^2\cdot7\cdot11\cdot13\cdot17\cdot19\cdot23\cdot29$. Let $d$ be a divisor of $k$. A number $m\in S$ fails to divide $d$ exactly when $v_p(m)>v_p(d)$ for some prime $p$. For each prime $p$ with $v_p(d)<v_p(k)$, the numbers in $S$ that fail \emph{because of $p$} are the multiples of $p^{v_p(d)+1}$ in $S$, and there is at least one of them. Their number is:
\begin{itemize}
\item $p=2$: $1$ (only $16$) if $v_2(d)=3$, and at least $3$ ($8,16,24$) if $v_2(d)\le2$;
\item $p=3$: $1$ (only $27$) if $v_3(d)=2$, and at least $3$ ($9,18,27$) if $v_3(d)\le1$;
\item $p=5$: $1$ (only $25$) if $v_5(d)=1$, and $6$ if $v_5(d)=0$;
\item $p=7$: $4$ ($7,14,21,28$); $p=11$: $2$ ($11,22$); $p=13$: $2$ ($13,26$);
\item $p=17,19,23,29$: $1$ each.
\end{itemize}
We need exactly $2$ numbers of $S$ to fail. A prime contributing $3$ or more failures is impossible. If a prime contributes $2$ failures ($p=11$ or $13$, with $p\nmid d$), no other prime may be deficient: any other prime would add a failure outside $\{11,22\}$ or $\{13,26\}$. That gives $2$ divisors, $\dfrac{k}{11}$ and $\dfrac{k}{13}$. Otherwise exactly two primes are deficient, each contributing $1$ failure; they are chosen from the $7$ primes $2,3,5,17,19,23,29$ (each lowered by exactly one factor), and their failing numbers ($16,27,25,17,19,23,29$) are distinct. That gives $\dbinom72=21$ divisors.

In total there are $21+2=\boxed{23}$ such divisors.

Use vectors from $A$: let $\mathbf b=\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\mathbf c=\overrightarrow{AC}$, which are not parallel. Then
\[\overrightarrow{AP}=\dfrac25\mathbf b,\qquad\overrightarrow{AQ}=\dfrac34\mathbf c,\qquad\overrightarrow{AM}=\dfrac12(\mathbf b+\mathbf c).\]
Since $N$ is on $AM$, $\overrightarrow{AN}=t\,\overrightarrow{AM}=\dfrac t2\mathbf b+\dfrac t2\mathbf c$ for some $t$. Since $N$ is on $PQ$, $\overrightarrow{AN}=(1-u)\dfrac25\mathbf b+u\cdot\dfrac34\mathbf c$ for some $u$. Comparing coefficients,
\[\dfrac t2=\dfrac25(1-u),\qquad\dfrac t2=\dfrac34u.\]
So $\dfrac25(1-u)=\dfrac34u$, i.e. $\dfrac25=\dfrac{23}{20}u$, $u=\dfrac{8}{23}$, and $t=\dfrac32u=\dfrac{12}{23}$. Hence $AN=\dfrac{12}{23}AM$ and
\[AM:AN=\boxed{23:12}.\]

