draw((0.5,0.2)--(0.5,-5.6)--(6.3,-5.6)--cycle,gray);
\end{asy}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] In how many ways can the pegs be placed so that no (horizontal) row and no (vertical) column contains two pegs of the same colour? (Pegs of the same colour are identical.)
\item[(b)] How many placements would there be if all $15$ pegs were distinguishable (for example, numbered)? Explain why (a) is the intended reading.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Number the rows $1$ to $5$ from the top and the columns $1$ to $5$ from the left; row $r$ has holes in columns $1,\dots,r$, and all $15$ holes are filled.

\textbf{(a)} The $5$ yellow pegs must lie in $5$ different rows, so there is one in each row. Row $1$ has only the hole $(1,1)$, so a yellow peg is there. Row $2$ has holes in columns $1,2$, and column $1$ already has a yellow peg, so the yellow peg is at $(2,2)$. Continuing, the yellow pegs fill the diagonal $(r,r)$.

The $4$ red pegs go into the remaining holes, which lie in rows $2$ to $5$ (row $r$ now has columns $1,\dots,r-1$ free); they need $4$ different rows, so one in each. Row $2$ has only $(2,1)$ free, so red is there; then row $3$ must use $(3,2)$, and so on: red fills $(r,r-1)$. In the same way green is forced into $(r,r-2)$, blue into $(r,r-3)$ and orange into $(5,1)$. This placement satisfies the conditions (each colour occupies distinct rows and distinct columns), so there is exactly $\boxed{1}$ way:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
string[] nm={"Y","R","G","B","O"};
pen[] cl={rgb(1,0.9,0.2),rgb(1,0.4,0.4),rgb(0.4,0.8,0.4),rgb(0.45,0.6,1),rgb(1,0.65,0.2)};
for(int r=1;r<=5;++r) for(int c=1;c<=r;++c){
  filldraw(circle((c,-r),0.3),cl[r-c]); label(nm[r-c],(c,-r),fontsize(8pt));}
draw((0.5,0.2)--(0.5,-5.6)--(6.3,-5.6)--cycle,gray);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} The positions of the colours are forced as in (a); if the pegs were distinguishable, the yellow pegs could be permuted among the yellow holes in $5!$ ways, and so on, giving $5!\cdot4!\cdot3!\cdot2!\cdot1!=34560$ placements. But the problem only describes pegs by their colour and asks for placements of colours; the only thing that matters is which colour is in each hole, so the pegs of one colour are interchangeable and the answer to the problem is $1$.

By the sum of cubes, $s^3+c^3=(s+c)\left(s^2-sc+c^2\right)$. With $s=\sin x$, $c=\cos x$ and $s+c\ne0$,
\[\dfrac{\sin^3x+\cos^3x}{\sin x+\cos x}=\sin^2x-\sin x\cos x+\cos^2x=\boxed{1-\sin x\cos x}=1-\dfrac12\sin2x.\]
The expression is undefined where $\sin x+\cos x=0$, i.e. $\tan x=-1$, $x=-\dfrac{\pi}{4}+k\pi$; there the simplified formula would give $1-\dfrac12\sin\left(-\dfrac{\pi}{2}+2k\pi\right)=\dfrac32$, but the original expression has no value.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return 1-0.5*sin(2x);}
real sx=0.9;
draw((-pi*sx-0.3,0)--(pi*sx+0.4,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-0.3)--(0,2.0),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(pi*sx+0.4,0),E); label("$y$",(0,2.0),N);
draw(xscale(sx)*graph(f,-pi,pi),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-pi*sx,1.5)--(pi*sx,1.5),dotted); draw((-pi*sx,0.5)--(pi*sx,0.5),dotted);
label("$\dfrac32$",(-pi*sx,1.5),W,fontsize(7pt)); label("$\dfrac12$",(-pi*sx,0.5),W,fontsize(7pt));
for(real t: new real[]{-pi/4,3pi/4}){filldraw(circle((t*sx,f(t)),0.07),white,blue);}
label("$-\dfrac{\pi}{4}$",(-pi/4*sx,0),S,fontsize(7pt)); label("$\dfrac{3\pi}{4}$",(3pi/4*sx,0),S,fontsize(7pt));
draw((-pi/4*sx,0)--(-pi/4*sx,1.5),dashed+gray); draw((3pi/4*sx,0)--(3pi/4*sx,1.5),dashed+gray);
label("$y=1-\sin x\cos x$",(pi*sx,f(pi)),SE,blue+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The graph on $[-\pi,\pi]$ oscillates between $\dfrac12$ and $\dfrac32$, with holes (open circles) at the excluded points $x=-\dfrac{\pi}{4}$ and $x=\dfrac{3\pi}{4}$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the volume of a tetrahedron is not determined by the lengths of three of its edges: find two tetrahedra in which the three edges at one vertex all have length $1$, but whose volumes are different.
\item[(b)] Prove that if the three edges at a vertex of a tetrahedron are mutually perpendicular, with lengths $a$, $b$ and $c$, then its volume is $\dfrac{abc}{6}$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(b)} Let the edges $OA=a$, $OB=b$, $OC=c$ be mutually perpendicular. Take triangle $OAB$ as the base: it is a right triangle with area $\dfrac{ab}{2}$. The edge $OC$ is perpendicular to both $OA$ and $OB$, hence to the plane $OAB$, so the height from $C$ is $OC=c$. Therefore
\[V=\dfrac13\cdot\dfrac{ab}{2}\cdot c=\dfrac{abc}{6}.\ \blacksquare\]

\textbf{(a)} By (b), the tetrahedron whose three edges at $O$ are mutually perpendicular with length $1$ has volume $\dfrac16$.

The regular tetrahedron with edge $1$ also has three edges of length $1$ at each vertex. Its base is an equilateral triangle with area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}$; the foot of the height from the top vertex is the centre of the base, at distance $\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}$ from each base vertex, so the height is $\sqrt{1-\dfrac13}=\sqrt{\dfrac23}$. Its volume is
\[\dfrac13\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot\sqrt{\dfrac23}=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{12}\approx0.118,\]
which differs from $\dfrac16\approx0.167$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair pr(real x,real y,real z){return (x+0.75*y,z+0.45*y);}
pair O=pr(0,0,0), A=pr(1,0,0), B=pr(0,1,0), C=pr(0,0,1);
draw(O--A--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8)); draw(A--B--C,linewidth(0.8)); draw(O--B,dashed);
draw(pr(0.12,0,0)--pr(0.12,0,0.12)--pr(0,0,0.12));
label("$1$",(O+A)/2,S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$1$",(O+C)/2,W,fontsize(8pt)); label("$1$",(O+B)/2,NW,fontsize(8pt));
label("$V=\dfrac16$",(0.5,-0.35),fontsize(8pt));
pair sh=(2.0,0);
pair P0=sh+pr(0,0,0), P1=sh+pr(1,0,0), P2=sh+pr(0.5,sqrt(3)/2,0), P3=sh+pr(0.5,sqrt(3)/6,sqrt(2/3));
draw(P0--P1--P3--cycle,linewidth(0.8)); draw(P1--P2--P3,linewidth(0.8)); draw(P0--P2,dashed);
label("$1$",(P0+P1)/2,S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$1$",(P0+P3)/2,W,fontsize(8pt)); label("$1$",(P0+P2)/2,NW,fontsize(8pt));
label("$V=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{12}$",sh+(0.5,-0.35),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

So three edge lengths do not determine the volume. (In general all six edges are needed; then the volume can be computed, for example with coordinates.)

Put $O_2$ at the origin, so that $X=(0,2)$ and $O_1=(0,3)$, and roll the small circle clockwise (rolling the other way gives the mirror image). Suppose the centre of the small circle has turned through an angle $\theta$ about $O_2$, to the position $O_1'$. The new point of tangency $Y$ lies on the segment $O_2O_1'$, so $\angle XO_2Y=\theta$ and the arc $XY$ of the large circle has length $2\theta$.

\textbf{How far has the small circle turned?} At the start, the radius of the small circle from $O_1$ to the point of contact points straight down, towards $O_2$. Now the radius from $O_1'$ to the point of contact $Y$ again points towards $O_2$, so the direction of the radius to the point of contact has turned clockwise through $\theta$. Because there is no slipping, the arc of the small circle that has rolled along the large circle also has length $2\theta$. So the point of the small circle that started at $X$ is now an arc of length $2\theta$ behind $Y$, which (as the radius is $1$) is an angle of $2\theta$ at the centre $O_1'$, measured clockwise from $O_1'Y$. Hence the radius to this point has turned clockwise through $\theta+2\theta=3\theta$. The small circle moves as a rigid body, so every segment fixed in it, in particular $O_1P$, has turned through the same angle $3\theta$.

\textbf{When is the line parallel again?} The line $PO_1$ is parallel to its original position (or coincides with it, which happens for one special direction of $O_1P$) exactly when the segment $O_1P$ has turned through a multiple of $180^\circ$ (turning through $180^\circ$ reverses the direction of the segment but gives a parallel line). The first time is $3\theta=180^\circ$, that is $\theta=60^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O2=(0,0), O1=(0,3), X=(0,2);
real th=60;
pair O1p=3*dir(90-th), Y=2*dir(90-th);
pair v=(0.3,0.5);
pair P=O1+v, Pp=O1p-v;
draw(circle(O2,2),linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O1,1),dashed);
draw(circle(O1p,1),linewidth(0.9));
draw(O2--O1,gray); draw(O2--O1p,gray);
draw(arc(O2,0.55,90-th,90),Arrow(4));
label("$60^\circ$",0.55*dir(60),dir(60),fontsize(8pt));
draw((O1-1.1*v)--(O1+1.6*v),blue);
draw((O1p-1.6*v)--(O1p+1.5*v),blue);
draw(X--Y,red+linewidth(1));
dot(O2); dot(O1); dot(O1p); dot(X); dot(Y); dot(P,blue); dot(Pp,blue);
label("$X$",X,dir(228)); label("$Y$",Y+0.4*dir(181));
label("$O_1$",O1,W); label("$O_1'$",O1p,SE);
label("$2$",(X+Y)/2,dir(240),red);
label("$P$",P,NW,blue); label("$P'$",Pp,SE,blue);
label("$O_2$",O2,SW);
\end{asy}

At that moment triangle $XO_2Y$ has $O_2X=O_2Y=2$ and $\angle XO_2Y=60^\circ$, so it is equilateral and $XY=2$. Therefore $XY^2=4$, answer $\boxed{\textbf{(A)}\ 4}$.

(The original contest problem waits until the marked segment points in its original direction again, which happens when $3\theta=360^\circ$, $\theta=120^\circ$; that would give $XY^2=\left(2\sqrt3\right)^2=12$. Forgetting the extra turn $\theta$ and using only $2\theta$ would give $\theta=90^\circ$ or $180^\circ$, that is $XY^2=8$ or $16$.)

question:
Prove that $2^n>2n$ for every integer $n\ge3$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] by mathematical induction;
\item[(b)] using the binomial theorem, $2^n=\dbinom n0+\dbinom n1+\cdots+\dbinom nn$.
\item[(c)] A student argues: ``$\dbinom n1=\dbinom n{n-1}=n$, and there are more than two terms, so $2^n>2n$.'' Where does this argument need $n\ge3$? Is the inequality true for $n=1$ and $n=2$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The six years have $6\cdot365+1=2191$ days and $6\cdot12=72$ months.
\begin{itemize}
\item The daily patrols cover $1200\cdot2191=2629200$ yards.
\item Each monthly trip out to sea adds $600+600=1200$ yards, so these add $1200\cdot72=86400$ yards.
\end{itemize}
In total Bertha travels $2629200+86400=2715600$ yards. A yard is $3$ feet, so this is $3\cdot2715600=\boxed{8146800}$ feet.

Work one prime at a time. For a prime $p$, let $p^\alpha$, $p^\beta$, $p^\gamma$ be the highest powers of $p$ dividing $a$, $b$, $c$. In a gcd the exponent of $p$ is the \emph{smallest} of the exponents and in an lcm it is the \emph{largest}; in a product or quotient of positive integers the exponents add or subtract. Two positive rational numbers are equal when every prime has the same exponent in both.

\textbf{(a)} The exponent of $p$ on the right is $\max\left(\max(\alpha,\beta),\max(\beta,\gamma)\right)=\max(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$, which is its exponent in $\operatorname{lcm}(a,b,c)$. This holds for every prime, so the two sides are equal. $\blacksquare$

For (b) and (c), both sides are unchanged when $a,b,c$ are permuted, so for the prime $p$ we may assume (relabelling if necessary) that $\alpha\le\beta\le\gamma$. Then
\begin{itemize}
\item $\operatorname{lcm}(a,b,c)$ has exponent $\gamma$ and $\gcd(a,b,c)$ has exponent $\alpha$;
\item $\operatorname{lcm}(a,b)$, $\operatorname{lcm}(b,c)$, $\operatorname{lcm}(c,a)$ have exponents $\beta,\gamma,\gamma$, with sum $\beta+2\gamma$;
\item $\gcd(a,b)$, $\gcd(b,c)$, $\gcd(c,a)$ have exponents $\alpha,\beta,\alpha$, with sum $2\alpha+\beta$;
\item $abc$ has exponent $\alpha+\beta+\gamma$.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{(b)} The exponent of $p$ on the right is $(\beta+2\gamma)+\alpha-(\alpha+\beta+\gamma)=\gamma$, the same as on the left. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(c)} The exponent of $p$ on the left is $2\gamma-(\beta+2\gamma)=-\beta$, and on the right it is $2\alpha-(2\alpha+\beta)=-\beta$. $\blacksquare$

So $\operatorname{lcm}(a,b,c)$ is the lcm of any two of the pairwise lcms, but a formula built from products of the pairwise lcms also needs gcds, as in (b) and (c). (For example, with $a=b=c=2$ all three pairwise lcms are $2$ and their product is $8$, while $\operatorname{lcm}(a,b,c)=2$.)

There are $6^5=7776$ equally likely ordered outcomes.

\textbf{Symmetry.} Replacing every number $r$ rolled by $7-r$ is a one-to-one correspondence between outcomes. It changes the median $m$ to $7-m$ and the mean $\mu$ to $7-\mu$, so it turns ``median greater than mean'' into ``median less than mean'' and back. Hence these two events contain the same number of outcomes, namely $\dfrac{7776-T}{2}$, where $T$ is the number of outcomes in which the median equals the mean.

\textbf{Counting the ties.} Write the rolled numbers in increasing order as $s_1\le s_2\le m\le s_4\le s_5$, with median $m$. The mean equals $m$ exactly when the sum is $5m$, that is when
\[(m-s_1)+(m-s_2)=(s_4-m)+(s_5-m):\]
the two smaller numbers fall short of $m$ by the same total amount as the two larger numbers exceed it. A sorted list in which the values occur $k_1,k_2,\dots$ times comes from $\dfrac{5!}{k_1!\,k_2!\cdots}$ ordered outcomes. We go through the possible medians.
\begin{itemize}
\item $m=1$: then $s_1=s_2=1$, the shortfall is $0$, so all five numbers are $1$: $1$ outcome. Likewise $m=6$ gives $1$ outcome.
\item $m=2$: the shortfall is at most $2$. The sorted lists are $22222$ ($1$ outcome), $12223$ ($20$), $11224$ ($30$) and $11233$ ($30$), in total $81$. By the symmetry $r\mapsto7-r$, $m=5$ also gives $81$.
\item $m=3$: the shortfall is at most $4$ and each excess is at most $3$. By the size of the shortfall:
\begin{itemize}
\item $0$: $33333$ ($1$);
\item $1$: $23334$ ($20$);
\item $2$: $22335$ ($30$), $22344$ ($30$), $13335$ ($20$), $13344$ ($30$);
\item $3$: $12336$ ($60$), $12345$ ($120$);
\item $4$: $11346$ ($60$), $11355$ ($30$).
\end{itemize}
In total $1+20+110+180+90=401$. By symmetry, $m=4$ also gives $401$.
\end{itemize}
So $T=1+81+401+401+81+1=966$, and the number of outcomes with median greater than mean is
\[\dfrac{7776-966}{2}=3405.\]
The probability is
\[\dfrac{3405}{7776}=\boxed{\dfrac{1135}{2592}}\approx0.438.\]

question:
A positive integer $N$ leaves remainder $1$ when divided by $10$ and remainder $3$ when divided by $11$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that these conditions do not determine the remainder of $N$ on division by $12$: find all the possible values of this remainder.
\item[(b)] What is the remainder when the smallest such $N$ is divided by $12$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The diameters are $9$, $18$ and $36$ inches, so the radii are $\dfrac92$, $9$ and $18$ inches. The snowballs are stacked along the axis of the cylinder, so the cylinder has radius $18$ and height $9+18+36=63$ inches.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=18, H=63, h=73/16, sh=58;
path cyl=(-R,0)--(R,0)--(R,H)--(-R,H)--cycle;
draw(cyl,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle((0,18),18)); draw(circle((0,45),9)); draw(circle((0,58.5),4.5));
draw((-18,18)--(18,18),dashed); label("$36$",(0,18),N);
draw((-9,45)--(9,45),dashed); draw((-4.5,58.5)--(4.5,58.5),dashed);
label("$18$",(13.5,45)); label("$9$",(11,58.5));
draw((-R-5,0)--(-R-5,H),Arrows(4)); label("$63$",(-R-5,H/2),W);
label("before",(0,-3),S);
fill(shift(sh,0)*((-R,0)--(R,0)--(R,h)--(-R,h)--cycle),rgb(0.6,0.8,1));
draw(shift(sh,0)*cyl,linewidth(0.9));
draw((sh-R,h)--(sh+R,h),blue);
draw((sh+R+5,0)--(sh+R+5,h),Arrows(3)); label("$\dfrac{73}{16}$",(sh+R+5,h/2),E);
draw((sh+R+5,h)--(sh+R+5,H),Arrows(4)); label("$\dfrac{935}{16}$",(sh+R+5,(h+H)/2),E);
draw((sh-R,H+3)--(sh+R,H+3),Arrows(4)); label("$36$",(sh,H+3),N);
label("after melting",(sh,-3),S);
\end{asy}

The volume of snow is
\[\dfrac43\pi\left(\left(\dfrac92\right)^3+9^3+18^3\right)=\dfrac43\pi\cdot\dfrac{729}{8}\left(1+8+64\right)=\dfrac43\pi\cdot\dfrac{729\cdot73}{8}=\dfrac{17739\pi}{2}\text{ cubic inches},\]
so the water has volume $\dfrac16\cdot\dfrac{17739\pi}{2}=\dfrac{5913\pi}{4}$ cubic inches. The water fills the bottom of the cylinder to a depth $h$ with
\[\pi\cdot18^2\cdot h=\dfrac{5913\pi}{4},\qquad h=\dfrac{5913}{4\cdot324}=\dfrac{5913}{1296}=\dfrac{73}{16}\text{ inches}\]
(since $5913=81\cdot73$ and $1296=81\cdot16$). The surface of the water is therefore
\[63-\dfrac{73}{16}=\boxed{\dfrac{935}{16}=58\dfrac{7}{16}\text{ inches}}\]
below the top of the cylinder.

The year 2021 has $365$ days, and the odd-numbered days are days $1,3,5,\dots,365$: there are $\dfrac{365+1}{2}=183$ of them. A holiday adds a day only if it falls on an even-numbered day. The months before the holidays have $31,28,31,30,31,30,31,31,30,31$ days, so the holidays are on these days of the year:
\begin{itemize}
\item 1 January: day $1$ (odd);
\item 4 April: day $31+28+31+4=94$ (even);
\item 4 July: day $181+4=185$ (odd), since January to June have $181$ days;
\item 31 October: day $273+31=304$ (even), since January to September have $273$ days;
\item 25 November: day $304+25=329$ (odd);
\item 25 December: day $304+30+25=359$ (odd).
\end{itemize}
Only Easter and Halloween are extra days, so Bobby cooked for his parents on $183+2=\boxed{185}$ days.

question:
The identity $(n+1)^3=n^3+3n^2+3n+1$ lets you find each cube from the previous one.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove the identity.
\item[(b)] Given that $17^3=4913$, use the identity twice to find $18^3$ and $19^3$.
\item[(c)] A student did the same computation and wrote $19^3=5832+972+54+1=6853$. Find the error.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
With single-digit factors, $24=3\cdot8=4\cdot6$ and $12=2\cdot6=3\cdot4$ (the products $1\cdot24$, $2\cdot12$ and $1\cdot12$ use a two-digit number). The four ages must be different:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\{3,8\}$ with $\{2,6\}$: all different;
\item $\{3,8\}$ with $\{3,4\}$: $3$ repeats;
\item $\{4,6\}$ with $\{2,6\}$: $6$ repeats;
\item $\{4,6\}$ with $\{3,4\}$: $4$ repeats.
\end{itemize}
So the ages are $2,3,6,8$ and their sum is $\boxed{19}$.

question:
Two angles of triangle $ABC$ are $\angle A=68^\circ$ and $\angle B=41^\circ$, and one of its sides has length $4$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find $\angle C$.
\item[(b)] For each of the three possibilities $BC=4$, $CA=4$ and $AB=4$, find the other two side lengths, to two decimal places.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} $\angle C=180^\circ-68^\circ-41^\circ=\boxed{71^\circ}$.

\textbf{(b)} Write $a=BC$, $b=CA$, $c=AB$ for the sides opposite $A$, $B$, $C$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(4,0);
pair A=extension(B,B+dir(41),C,C+dir(109));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(B,0.55,0,41)); draw(arc(C,0.5,109,180)); draw(arc(A,0.45,221,289));
label("$41^\circ$",B+0.62*dir(20),dir(20),fontsize(8pt));
label("$71^\circ$",C+0.58*dir(145),dir(145),fontsize(8pt));
label("$68^\circ$",A+0.52*dir(255),dir(255),fontsize(8pt));
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$a$",(B+C)/2,S); label("$b$",(C+A)/2,NE); label("$c$",(A+B)/2,NW);
\end{asy}

By the law of sines,
\[\dfrac{a}{\sin68^\circ}=\dfrac{b}{\sin41^\circ}=\dfrac{c}{\sin71^\circ},\]
with $\sin68^\circ\approx0.92718$, $\sin41^\circ\approx0.65606$ and $\sin71^\circ\approx0.94552$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $BC=a=4$: $CA=\dfrac{4\sin41^\circ}{\sin68^\circ}\approx\boxed{2.83}$ and $AB=\dfrac{4\sin71^\circ}{\sin68^\circ}\approx\boxed{4.08}$.
\item If $CA=b=4$: $BC=\dfrac{4\sin68^\circ}{\sin41^\circ}\approx\boxed{5.65}$ and $AB=\dfrac{4\sin71^\circ}{\sin41^\circ}\approx\boxed{5.76}$.
\item If $AB=c=4$: $BC=\dfrac{4\sin68^\circ}{\sin71^\circ}\approx\boxed{3.92}$ and $CA=\dfrac{4\sin41^\circ}{\sin71^\circ}\approx\boxed{2.78}$.
\end{itemize}
(In each case the longest side is opposite the largest angle, $71^\circ$, and the shortest is opposite $41^\circ$.)

Let $\omega$ be the incircle, with radius $r$. We prove the statement for triangle $ADF$; the other two triangles are handled in exactly the same way, at the vertices $B$ and $C$.

\textbf{The candidate point.} The tangent segments from $A$ are equal, $AD=AF$, and $ID=IF=r$. So $A$ and $I$ both lie on the perpendicular bisector of $DF$, and this bisector is the line $AI$; it is also the bisector of the angle $DAF$. The point $A$ is outside $\omega$, so the segment $AI$ meets $\omega$ in exactly one point; call it $K$. As $K$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of the chord $DF$, we have $KD=KF$, and $K$ is the midpoint of the arc $DF$ of $\omega$ on the side of $A$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.2,4), B=(0,0), C=(5.2,0);
real a=abs(B-C), bb=abs(C-A), c=abs(A-B);
pair Ic=(a*A+bb*B+c*C)/(a+bb+c);
real r=Ic.y;
pair foot(pair P, pair Q, pair X){pair u=unit(Q-P); return P+dot(X-P,u)*u;}
pair D=foot(A,B,Ic), Ep=foot(B,C,Ic), Fp=foot(C,A,Ic);
pair KA=Ic+r*unit(A-Ic), KB=Ic+r*unit(B-Ic), KC=Ic+r*unit(C-Ic);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(Ic,r));
draw(A--Ic,dashed); draw(B--Ic,gray+dashed); draw(C--Ic,gray+dashed);
draw(D--Fp,gray); draw(D--KA,blue); draw(Fp--KA,blue);
dot(Ic); dot(D); dot(Ep); dot(Fp);
dot(KA,red); dot(KB,red); dot(KC,red);
label("$K$",KA,dir(160),red);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$D$",D,W); label("$E$",Ep,S); label("$F$",Fp,NE); label("$I$",Ic,S);
\end{asy}

\textbf{$DK$ bisects angle $ADF$.} The line $DA$ is tangent to $\omega$ at $D$. By the tangent-chord angle theorem, the angle between the tangent $DA$ and the chord $DK$ equals half the arc $DK$ that lies inside this angle:
\[\angle ADK=\dfrac12\,\overset{\frown}{DK}.\]
The inscribed angle $\angle KDF$ stands on the arc $KF$ (the one not containing $D$), so $\angle KDF=\dfrac12\,\overset{\frown}{KF}$. Since $K$ is the midpoint of the arc $DF$, the arcs $DK$ and $KF$ are equal, and therefore $\angle ADK=\angle KDF$.

\textbf{Conclusion.} The point $K$ lies on the bisector $AI$ of angle $DAF$ and on the bisector $DK$ of angle $ADF$ of triangle $ADF$, so it is the incentre of triangle $ADF$. Since $K$ lies on $\omega$, the incentre of $ADF$ lies on the incircle of $ABC$. In the same way the incentres of $BED$ and $CFE$ are the points where $BI$ and $CI$ meet $\omega$ (the red points in the figure). $\blacksquare$

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $n^5$ and $n$ have the same last digit for every positive integer $n$.
\item[(b)] A friend picks an integer $n$ with $1\le n\le99$ and tells you that $n^5=3{,}939{,}040{,}643$. Using (a) and the values $80^5=3{,}276{,}800{,}000$ and $90^5=5{,}904{,}900{,}000$, find $n$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
If $z<0$, then $\dfrac3z$ and $\dfrac4z$ are negative, so both brackets are negative and the sum cannot be $5$. So $z>0$; put $u=\dfrac1z>0$. Making $z$ as large as possible means making $u$ as small as possible subject to
\[g(u)=[3u]+[4u]=5.\]
The function $g$ does not decrease as $u$ increases. For $u<\dfrac34$:
\begin{itemize}
\item if $u<\dfrac23$, then $3u<2$ and $4u<\dfrac83<3$, so $g(u)\le1+2=3$;
\item if $\dfrac23\le u<\dfrac34$, then $2\le3u<\dfrac94$ and $\dfrac83\le4u<3$, so $g(u)=2+2=4$.
\end{itemize}
At $u=\dfrac34$: $[3u]+[4u]=\left[\dfrac94\right]+[3]=2+3=5$. So the smallest admissible $u$ is $\dfrac34$, the largest $z$ is $\dfrac43$, and
\[21z=21\cdot\dfrac43=\boxed{28}.\]

By Fermat's little theorem, $5^p\equiv5\pmod p$ for every prime $p$ (for $p=5$ both sides are $0$). So if $p\mid5^p+1$, then $p\mid5+1=6$, and $p=2$ or $p=3$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $p=2$: $5^2+1=26$ is not divisible by $4$.
\item $p=3$: $5^3+1=126=9\cdot14$ is divisible by $9$.
\end{itemize}
So the only such prime is $p=\boxed{3}$.

We need $x\ne0$, $y\ne0$ and $y\ne-3$. For such $x,y$, multiplying by $xy(y+3)$ shows that the equation is equivalent to
\[y(y+3)+2x^2y=(x+1)(y+3),\qquad\text{that is}\qquad y^2+\left(2x^2-x+2\right)y-3(x+1)=0.\tag{$*$}\]
Regard $(*)$ as a quadratic equation in $y$ and put $c=2x^2-x+2$, a positive integer. If $y$ is an integer root, the discriminant
\[D=c^2+12(x+1)\]
must be a perfect square. We show that this fails unless $-1\le x\le3$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $x\ge4$: then $0<12(x+1)<2c+1$, because $2c+1-12(x+1)=4x^2-14x-7=2x(2x-7)-7\ge8-7>0$. So $c^2<D<(c+1)^2$, and $D$ is not a square.
\item If $x\le-2$: then $12(x+1)<0$ and $12(x+1)>-(2c-1)$, because $2c-1+12(x+1)=4x^2+10x+15>0$ for every $x$ (its discriminant $100-240$ is negative). So $(c-1)^2<D<c^2$, and $D$ is not a square.
\end{itemize}
It remains to check $x\in\{-1,1,2,3\}$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $x=-1$: $y^2+5y=0$, so $y=0$ (not allowed) or $y=-5$;
\item $x=1$: $y^2+3y-6=0$, with $D=33$, no integer root;
\item $x=2$: $y^2+8y-9=(y-1)(y+9)=0$, so $y=1$ or $y=-9$;
\item $x=3$: $y^2+17y-12=0$, with $D=337$, not a square.
\end{itemize}
None of these has $y=-3$. The solutions are
\[\boxed{(x,y)=(-1,-5),\ (2,1),\ (2,-9)}.\]
(Check: for $(2,1)$ both sides equal $\dfrac32$; for $(2,-9)$ both sides equal $-\dfrac16$; for $(-1,-5)$ both sides equal $0$.)

question:
A student notices that even $+$ even and odd $+$ odd are even, while even $+$ odd is odd, and concludes: ``there are more ways to make an even number than an odd number, so there should be more even integers than odd integers.''
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $n\mapsto n+1$ is a one-to-one correspondence between the even integers and the odd integers.
\item[(b)] Let $N$ be a positive integer. Show that exactly half of the $(2N)^2$ ordered pairs $(a,b)$ with $a,b\in\{1,2,\dots,2N\}$ have an even sum $a+b$.
\item[(c)] Explain what is wrong with the student's reasoning.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $c$ be the number of correct answers and $k$ the number of blanks, with $c,k\ge0$ and $c+k\le25$; the number of wrong answers $25-c-k$ is then determined. The score is $S=6c+1.5k$.

\textbf{When do two combinations give the same score?} $(c,k)$ and $(c',k')$ have the same score exactly when $6(c-c')=1.5(k'-k)$, i.e. $k'-k=4(c-c')$. So the combinations with the same score as $(c,k)$ are $(c-t,\,k+4t)$ for the integers $t$ with
\[c-t\ge0,\qquad k+4t\ge0,\qquad (c-t)+(k+4t)=c+k+3t\le25.\]
Each condition is a linear inequality in $t$, so the admissible values of $t$ form a block of consecutive integers containing $t=0$. Hence $(c,k)$ is the only combination with its score exactly when neither $t=1$ nor $t=-1$ is admissible.
\begin{itemize}
\item $t=1$ is not admissible exactly when $c=0$ or $c+k\ge23$.
\item $t=-1$ is not admissible exactly when $k\le3$ (the other two conditions, $c+1\ge0$ and $c+k-3\le25$, always hold).
\end{itemize}
So the score is achieved by a unique combination exactly when $k\le3$ and ($c=0$ or $c+k\ge23$):
\begin{itemize}
\item $c=0$, $k=0,1,2,3$: scores $0$, $1.5$, $3$, $4.5$;
\item $k=0$, $c=23,24,25$: scores $138$, $144$, $150$;
\item $k=1$, $c=22,23,24$: scores $133.5$, $139.5$, $145.5$;
\item $k=2$, $c=21,22,23$: scores $129$, $135$, $141$;
\item $k=3$, $c=20,21,22$: scores $124.5$, $130.5$, $136.5$.
\end{itemize}
So there are $16$ such scores:
\[\boxed{0,\ 1.5,\ 3,\ 4.5,\ 124.5,\ 129,\ 130.5,\ 133.5,\ 135,\ 136.5,\ 138,\ 139.5,\ 141,\ 144,\ 145.5,\ 150}.\]

Count the complement: the positive integers below $100$ with no prime factor greater than $4$, i.e. those of the form $2^a3^b$ (including $1=2^03^0$):
\begin{itemize}
\item $b=0$: $1,2,4,8,16,32,64$ ($7$ numbers);
\item $b=1$: $3,6,12,24,48,96$ ($6$);
\item $b=2$: $9,18,36,72$ ($4$);
\item $b=3$: $27,54$ ($2$);
\item $b=4$: $81$ ($1$).
\end{itemize}
That is $20$ numbers, so $99-20=\boxed{79}$ of the numbers $1,\dots,99$ have a prime factor greater than $4$.

Use position vectors $\vec A,\vec B,\vec C,\vec D$ of the vertices. Since $M$ and $N$ are midpoints, $\vec M=\dfrac{\vec A+\vec D}{2}$ and $\vec N=\dfrac{\vec B+\vec C}{2}$, so
\[\overrightarrow{MN}=\vec N-\vec M=\dfrac{\left(\vec B-\vec A\right)+\left(\vec C-\vec D\right)}{2}=\dfrac{\overrightarrow{AB}+\overrightarrow{DC}}{2}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(6,0), C=(4.4,3), D=(1.4,3);
pair M=(A+D)/2, Nn=(B+C)/2;
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--B,blue+linewidth(1.1),Arrow(9));
draw(D--C,blue+linewidth(1.1),Arrow(9));
draw(M--Nn,red+linewidth(1.1),Arrow(9));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(M); dot(Nn);
label("$a$",(A+B)/2,S,blue); label("$b$",(D+C)/2,N,blue);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$M$",M,W); label("$N$",Nn,E);
\end{asy}

The bases $AB$ and $DC$ are parallel, and in the trapezoid $ABCD$ (vertices in this order) the vectors $\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\overrightarrow{DC}$ point in the same direction; if they pointed in opposite directions, the legs $AD$ and $BC$ would cross. Their lengths are $a$ and $b$, so $\overrightarrow{DC}=\dfrac ba\,\overrightarrow{AB}$. Hence
\[\overrightarrow{MN}=\dfrac12\left(1+\dfrac ba\right)\overrightarrow{AB}=\dfrac{a+b}{2a}\,\overrightarrow{AB},\]
and, using $\overrightarrow{AB}=\dfrac ab\,\overrightarrow{DC}$ instead,
\[\overrightarrow{MN}=\dfrac12\left(\dfrac ab+1\right)\overrightarrow{DC}=\dfrac{a+b}{2b}\,\overrightarrow{DC}.\qquad\blacksquare\]
(In particular $MN\parallel AB$ and $MN=\dfrac{a+b}{2}$: the midline of a trapezoid is parallel to the bases and equal to half their sum.)

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many ordered triples $(a,b,c)$ of positive integers satisfy $\operatorname{lcm}(a,b,c)=100$?
\item[(b)] How many such triples are there if the order of $a,b,c$ does not matter (that is, how many with $a\le b\le c$)?
\end{enumerate}

A sequence is defined by $a_1=a_2=1$ and
\[a_n=\dfrac{a_{n-1}^2+2}{a_{n-2}}\qquad(n\ge3).\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $a_n=4a_{n-1}-a_{n-2}$ for all $n\ge3$; in particular every $a_n$ is a positive integer.
\item[(b)] Find a closed formula for $a_n$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Each of the $4$ balls independently chooses one of the $3$ boxes: $3^4=81$ outcomes.

\textbf{(b)} Now an outcome is only the list of how many balls each box gets: nonnegative integers $x_A+x_B+x_C=4$. By stars and bars there are $\dbinom{4+2}{2}=15$ outcomes.

\textbf{(c)} Now an outcome is a way of splitting the set $\{1,2,3,4\}$ into at most $3$ nonempty groups (the boxes have no names, so only the grouping matters):
\begin{itemize}
\item one group: $1$ way;
\item two groups: sizes $3+1$ in $4$ ways, sizes $2+2$ in $\dfrac12\dbinom42=3$ ways, so $7$ ways;
\item three groups: sizes $2+1+1$, determined by the pair that is together: $\dbinom42=6$ ways.
\end{itemize}
In total $1+7+6=14$ outcomes.

\textbf{(d)} Now an outcome is only the multiset of box sizes, a partition of $4$ into at most $3$ parts: $4$, $3+1$, $2+2$, $2+1+1$. So there are $4$ outcomes.

So the answers are $\boxed{81,\ 15,\ 14,\ 4}$.

\textbf{Why not just divide.} Making objects identical merges outcomes that differ only by permuting those objects, but different outcomes merge in groups of different sizes. For example, in (a) the outcome ``all four balls in box $A$'' corresponds to only $1$ arrangement of the numbered balls, while ``balls $1,2$ in $A$, balls $3,4$ in $B$'' is one of $\dbinom42=6$ labelled outcomes with the same counts $(2,2,0)$. So $81$ is not $4!\cdot15$, and similarly $81$ is not $3!\cdot14$, because an outcome with empty boxes is unchanged by some relabellings of the boxes. Each model has to be counted on its own terms.

Let $V$ be the volume of the cone, and fix a positive integer $n$. Cut the cone by planes parallel to the base at distances $\dfrac hn,\dfrac{2h}{n},\dots,\dfrac{(n-1)h}{n}$ from the apex, into $n$ slices of thickness $\dfrac hn$.

\textbf{Cross-sections.} By similar triangles (in the cross-section through the axis), the cross-section at distance $d$ from the apex is a disc of radius $\dfrac{rd}{h}$. So the $k$-th slice, between distances $\dfrac{(k-1)h}{n}$ and $\dfrac{kh}{n}$ from the apex, has circular faces of radii $\dfrac{r(k-1)}{n}$ and $\dfrac{rk}{n}$, and every cross-section in between has a radius between these two.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=2, h=4; int n=5;
pair V=(0,h);
for(int k=1;k<=n;++k){
  real y0=h-k*h/n, y1=h-(k-1)*h/n;
  real ro=r*k/n, ri=r*(k-1)/n;
  draw((-ro,y0)--(ro,y0)--(ro,y1)--(-ro,y1)--cycle,blue+dashed);
  if(k>1) fill((-ri,y0)--(ri,y0)--(ri,y1)--(-ri,y1)--cycle,rgb(1,0.85,0.6));
}
for(int k=2;k<=n;++k){ real y0=h-k*h/n, y1=h-(k-1)*h/n; real ri=r*(k-1)/n; draw((-ri,y0)--(ri,y0)--(ri,y1)--(-ri,y1)--cycle,orange);}
draw((-r,0)--V--(r,0)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0)--V,dotted);
dot(V); label("apex",V,N);
label("$r$",(r/2,0),S); draw((-r-0.4,0)--(-r-0.4,h),Arrows(4)); label("$h$",(-r-0.4,h/2),W);
draw((r+0.4,h-2*h/n)--(r+0.4,h-3*h/n),Arrows(4)); label("$\dfrac{h}{n}$",(r+0.4,h-2.5*h/n),E);
\end{asy}

Hence the $k$-th slice \emph{contains} the cylinder of radius $\dfrac{r(k-1)}{n}$ and height $\dfrac hn$ (shaded) and \emph{is contained in} the cylinder of radius $\dfrac{rk}{n}$ and height $\dfrac hn$ (dashed). Adding over the slices,
\[L_n=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\pi\left(\dfrac{r(k-1)}{n}\right)^2\dfrac hn\ \le\ V\ \le\ \sum_{k=1}^{n}\pi\left(\dfrac{rk}{n}\right)^2\dfrac hn=U_n.\]

\textbf{The two sums.} Using the formula for the sum of squares,
\[U_n=\dfrac{\pi r^2h}{n^3}\cdot\dfrac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6}=\dfrac{\pi r^2h}{6}\cdot\dfrac{2n^2+3n+1}{n^2},\qquad L_n=\dfrac{\pi r^2h}{n^3}\cdot\dfrac{(n-1)n(2n-1)}{6}=\dfrac{\pi r^2h}{6}\cdot\dfrac{2n^2-3n+1}{n^2}.\]
Since $2n^2-3n+1<2n^2<2n^2+3n+1$, we get $L_n<\dfrac13\pi r^2h<U_n$. Both $V$ and $\dfrac13\pi r^2h$ lie between $L_n$ and $U_n$, so
\[\left|V-\dfrac13\pi r^2h\right|\le U_n-L_n=\dfrac{\pi r^2h}{6}\cdot\dfrac{6n}{n^2}=\dfrac{\pi r^2h}{n}.\]
This holds for every positive integer $n$, and $\dfrac{\pi r^2h}{n}$ can be made smaller than any positive number. Therefore $V=\dfrac13\pi r^2h$. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{Yes.} Recall that triangle $XYZ$ is similar to triangle $X'Y'Z'$ (with the vertices corresponding in this order) when the corresponding angles are equal and the corresponding sides are in a constant ratio $\dfrac{X'Y'}{XY}=\dfrac{Y'Z'}{YZ}=\dfrac{Z'X'}{ZX}$.

Let $\mathcal{A}=XYZ$. Since $\mathcal{A}\sim\mathcal{B}$, we can name the vertices of $\mathcal{B}$ as $X_1Y_1Z_1$ so that $\angle X_1=\angle X$, $\angle Y_1=\angle Y$, $\angle Z_1=\angle Z$ and
\[X_1Y_1=k\cdot XY,\quad Y_1Z_1=k\cdot YZ,\quad Z_1X_1=k\cdot ZX\]
for some $k>0$. Similarly name the vertices of $\mathcal{C}$ as $X_2Y_2Z_2$ with the angles of $XYZ$ and ratio $m>0$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair X=(0,0),Y=(2,0),Z=(0.6,1.2);
pair X1=(3.6,0),Y1=(5.1,0),Z1=(4.05,0.9);
pair X2=(7,0),Y2=(10,0),Z2=(7.9,1.8);
draw(X--Y--Z--cycle,linewidth(0.8)); draw(X1--Y1--Z1--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.8)); draw(X2--Y2--Z2--cycle,red+linewidth(0.8));
label("$X$",X,SW);label("$Y$",Y,SE);label("$Z$",Z,N);
label("$X_1$",X1,SW,blue);label("$Y_1$",Y1,SE,blue);label("$Z_1$",Z1,N,blue);
label("$X_2$",X2,SW,red);label("$Y_2$",Y2,SE,red);label("$Z_2$",Z2,N,red);
label("$\mathcal{A}$",(1,-0.9));label("$\mathcal{B}$, ratio $k$",(4.35,-0.9),blue);label("$\mathcal{C}$, ratio $m$",(8.5,-0.9),red);
\end{asy}

Then $\angle X_2=\angle X=\angle X_1$, and likewise $\angle Y_2=\angle Y_1$ and $\angle Z_2=\angle Z_1$. For the sides,
\[\dfrac{X_2Y_2}{X_1Y_1}=\dfrac{m\cdot XY}{k\cdot XY}=\dfrac mk,\qquad\dfrac{Y_2Z_2}{Y_1Z_1}=\dfrac mk,\qquad\dfrac{Z_2X_2}{Z_1X_1}=\dfrac mk.\]
So the angles correspond and the sides are in the constant ratio $\dfrac mk$: triangle $X_1Y_1Z_1$ is similar to triangle $X_2Y_2Z_2$, i.e. $\boxed{\mathcal{B}\sim\mathcal{C}}$. $\blacksquare$

(In the same way, $\mathcal{A}\sim\mathcal{B}$ implies $\mathcal{B}\sim\mathcal{A}$ with ratio $\dfrac1k$; similarity behaves like equality.)

Factor out $\dfrac12$ from the terms with $x$ and complete the square:
\[H(x)=\dfrac12\left(x^2+4x\right)-3=\dfrac12\left(x^2+4x+4\right)-2-3=\boxed{\dfrac12(x+2)^2-5}.\]
Since $\dfrac12(x+2)^2\ge0$, with equality only at $x=-2$, the least value of $H$ is $-5$:
\begin{itemize}
\item the graph is a parabola opening upwards with vertex $(-2,-5)$ and axis of symmetry $x=-2$;
\item the $y$-intercept is $H(0)=-3$, i.e. the point $(0,-3)$;
\item the $x$-intercepts satisfy $\dfrac12(x+2)^2=5$, i.e. $(x+2)^2=10$, so $x=-2\pm\sqrt{10}$ (about $1.16$ and $-5.16$).
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real H(real x){return 0.5*x^2+2*x-3;}
draw((-7,0)--(3,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-6.2)--(0,4.5),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(3,0),E); label("$y$",(0,4.5),N);
draw(graph(H,-6.6,2.6),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-2,-5)--(-2,4.2),dashed);
pair Vx=(-2,-5); dot(Vx); label("$(-2,-5)$",Vx,S);
dot((0,-3)); label("$(0,-3)$",(0,-3),E);
real r1=-2+sqrt(10), r2=-2-sqrt(10);
dot((r1,0)); dot((r2,0));
label("$-2+\sqrt{10}$",(r1,0),SE); label("$-2-\sqrt{10}$",(r2,0),SW);
label("$x=-2$",(-2,4.2),W);
\end{asy}

A central angle of $120^\circ$ is $\dfrac{120}{360}=\dfrac13$ of the full angle, so the sector is one third of the disc.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); real R=2;
fill(O--arc(O,R,30,150)--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
draw(circle(O,R),gray); draw(O--R*dir(30),linewidth(0.8)); draw(O--R*dir(150),linewidth(0.8));
draw(arc(O,R,30,150),blue+linewidth(1));
draw(arc(O,0.4,30,150)); label("$120^\circ$",0.4*dir(90),N);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,S); label("$r$",R/2*dir(30),SE);
label("area $48\pi$",(0,1.25));
\end{asy}

If the radius is $r$, then
\[\dfrac13\pi r^2=48\pi\quad\Longrightarrow\quad r^2=144\quad\Longrightarrow\quad r=\boxed{12}.\]

We use one fact repeatedly: two triangles with equal bases on the same line and a common opposite vertex have the same height, hence the same area.

Since $M$ is the midpoint of $BC$, triangles $MPB$ and $CPM$ have equal bases $BM=MC$ and the common vertex $P$, so $[MPB]=[CPM]=x$ say. In the same way $[APN]=[NPC]=y$ (as $AN=NC$) and $[BPO]=[OPA]=z$ (as $AO=OB$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0.8,3.2),B=(0,0),C=(4.4,0);
pair M=(B+C)/2,Nn=(A+C)/2,Oo=(A+B)/2,P=(A+B+C)/3;
fill(B--P--M--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1)); fill(C--P--M--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
fill(A--P--Nn--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.75)); fill(C--P--Nn--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.75));
fill(A--P--Oo--cycle,rgb(0.85,1,0.85)); fill(B--P--Oo--cycle,rgb(0.85,1,0.85));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--M); draw(B--Nn); draw(C--Oo);
dot(P); dot(M); dot(Nn); dot(Oo);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$M$",M,S);label("$N$",Nn,NE);label("$O$",Oo,W);label("$P$",P,dir(65));
label("$x$",(B+P+M)/3); label("$x$",(C+P+M)/3); label("$y$",(A+P+Nn)/3); label("$y$",(C+P+Nn)/3); label("$z$",(A+P+Oo)/3); label("$z$",(B+P+Oo)/3);
\end{asy}

The median $AM$ also splits triangle $ABC$ into two triangles of equal area: $[ABM]=[ACM]$, since $BM=MC$ and $A$ is a common vertex. Now $ABM$ consists of the triangles $OPA$, $BPO$, $MPB$, and $ACM$ consists of $APN$, $NPC$, $CPM$, so
\[2z+x=2y+x,\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad z=y.\]
Likewise the median $BN$ gives $[BCN]=[BAN]$, i.e. $2x+y=2z+y$, so $x=z$. Hence $x=y=z$, and since the six triangles fill triangle $ABC$, each has area $\dfrac16[ABC]$. $\blacksquare$

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find all angles $\theta$ with $0^\circ<\theta<360^\circ$ such that rotating a regular pentagon by $\theta$ about its centre maps the pentagon onto itself.
\item[(b)] Show that the pentagon with vertices $(0,0)$, $(2,0)$, $(2,1)$, $(1,2)$, $(0,1)$ has no rotational symmetry other than a full turn.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let $O$ be the centre and $V_1,\dots,V_5$ the vertices in order. The vertices lie on a circle about $O$, and consecutive vertices are $\dfrac{360^\circ}{5}=72^\circ$ apart as seen from $O$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] P; for(int k=0;k<5;++k) P.push(1.3*dir(90+72k));
draw(P[0]--P[1]--P[2]--P[3]--P[4]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
pair O=(0,0); dot(O); label("$O$",O,S);
draw(O--P[0],dashed); draw(O--P[1],dashed);
draw(arc(O,0.3,90,162),Arrow(4)); label("$72^\circ$",0.3*dir(126),dir(126),fontsize(8pt));
for(int k=0;k<5;++k) label("$V_{"+string(k+1)+"}$",P[k],P[k]);
pair sh=(2.6,-1.1);
pair A=sh+(0,0),B=sh+(2,0),C=sh+(2,1),D=sh+(1,2),Ee=sh+(0,1);
draw(A--B--C--D--Ee--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
label("$(0,0)$",A,SW,fontsize(8pt)); label("$(2,0)$",B,SE,fontsize(8pt)); label("$(2,1)$",C,E,fontsize(8pt)); label("$(1,2)$",D,N,fontsize(8pt)); label("$(0,1)$",Ee,W,fontsize(8pt));
label("$2$",(A+B)/2,S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

A rotation about $O$ by $72^\circ$ sends each $V_i$ to the next vertex, so it maps the pentagon onto itself; so do its repeats by $144^\circ$, $216^\circ$ and $288^\circ$. Conversely, a rotation about $O$ mapping the pentagon onto itself must send $V_1$ to some vertex $V_j$, so its angle is a multiple of $72^\circ$. The answer is
\[\boxed{\theta=72^\circ,\ 144^\circ,\ 216^\circ,\ 288^\circ},\]
so a regular pentagon has rotational symmetry of order $5$.

\textbf{(b)} A rotation preserves lengths and maps sides of the polygon onto sides. The sides have lengths $2,1,\sqrt2,\sqrt2,1$, so the only side of length $2$, from $(0,0)$ to $(2,0)$, must be mapped onto itself. A rotation that is not a full turn and maps a segment onto itself must swap its endpoints: it is the half-turn about the midpoint $(1,0)$, i.e. $(x,y)\mapsto(2-x,-y)$. But this sends the vertex $(1,2)$ to $(1,-2)$, which is not a vertex. So the only rotation mapping this pentagon onto itself is the full turn. (It does have a mirror symmetry, in the line $x=1$.) $\blacksquare$

So a \emph{regular} pentagon has rotational symmetry, but a pentagon in general need not.

question:
Rectangles $R_1$ and $R_2$, and squares $S_1$, $S_2$, and $S_3$, shown below, combine to form a rectangle that is $3322$ units wide and $2020$ units high. What is the side length of $S_2$ in units?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,3)--(0,3)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((3,0)--(3,1)--(0,1),linewidth(0.9));
draw((3,1)--(3,2)--(5,2),linewidth(0.9));
draw((3,2)--(2,2)--(2,1)--(2,3),linewidth(0.9));
label("$R_1$",(1.5,0.5)); label("$S_3$",(4,1)); label("$S_2$",(2.5,1.5)); label("$S_1$",(1,2)); label("$R_2$",(3.5,2.5));
\end{asy}

$\textbf{(A) }651\qquad\textbf{(B) }655\qquad\textbf{(C) }656\qquad\textbf{(D) }662\qquad\textbf{(E) }666$

solution:
Let the squares $S_1$, $S_2$, $S_3$ have sides $s_1$, $s_2$, $s_3$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill((0,1)--(2,1)--(2,3)--(0,3)--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1)); fill((2,1)--(3,1)--(3,2)--(2,2)--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.7)); fill((3,0)--(5,0)--(5,2)--(3,2)--cycle,rgb(0.85,1,0.85));
draw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,3)--(0,3)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((3,0)--(3,1)--(0,1),linewidth(0.9));
draw((3,1)--(3,2)--(5,2),linewidth(0.9));
draw((3,2)--(2,2)--(2,1)--(2,3),linewidth(0.9));
label("$R_1$",(1.5,0.5)); label("$S_3$",(4,0.7)); label("$S_2$",(2.5,1.65)); label("$S_1$",(1,2.1)); label("$R_2$",(3.6,2.5));
draw((0,1.3)--(5,1.3),red+dashed);
label("$s_1$",(1,3),N); label("$s_2$",(2.5,2),N,fontsize(9pt)); label("$s_3$",(4,0),S); label("$s_3$",(5,1),E);
label("$s_1-s_2$",(5,2.5),E);
draw((0,-0.55)--(5,-0.55),Arrows(4)); label("$3322$",(2.5,-0.55),S);
draw((-0.6,0)--(-0.6,3),Arrows(4)); label("$2020$",(-0.6,1.5),W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Across.} The squares $S_1$, $S_2$, $S_3$ stand side by side and span the whole width (the red dashed line crosses $S_1$, $S_2$, $S_3$ and nothing else), so
\[s_1+s_2+s_3=3322.\]
\textbf{Up the right side.} $S_1$ and $S_2$ both stand on top of $R_1$, so their bottom sides are at the same height; the top of $S_1$ is the top of the big rectangle, and $R_2$ lies between the top of $S_2$ and the top of the big rectangle. So $R_2$ has height $s_1-s_2$. Along the right side of the big rectangle we meet $S_3$ and then $R_2$, so
\[s_3+(s_1-s_2)=2020.\]
Subtracting the second equation from the first gives $2s_2=3322-2020=1302$, so $s_2=651$. The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(A) }651}$.

(Such a tiling exists, for example with $s_1=s_3=\dfrac{2671}{2}$: then $R_1$ is $\dfrac{3973}{2}$ by $\dfrac{1369}{2}$ and $R_2$ is $\dfrac{3973}{2}$ by $\dfrac{1369}{2}$; the data determine $s_2$ but not $s_1$ and $s_3$ separately.)

question:
Rectangle $ABCD$ is inscribed in a semicircle with diameter $FE$: the vertices $D$ and $A$ lie on the diameter, with $F,D,A,E$ in this order, and $B$ and $C$ lie on the arc, as shown. Let $DA=16$ and $FD=AE=9$. What is the area of $ABCD$?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); real R=17;
pair Fp=(-17,0),D=(-8,0),A=(8,0),Ep=(17,0),C=(-8,15),B=(8,15);
draw(arc(O,R,0,180),linewidth(0.9)); draw(Fp--Ep,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--B--C--D,linewidth(0.9));
dot(Fp);dot(D);dot(A);dot(Ep);dot(B);dot(C);
label("$F$",Fp,S);label("$D$",D,S);label("$A$",A,S);label("$E$",Ep,S);label("$B$",B,NE);label("$C$",C,NW);
label("$9$",(Fp+D)/2,S);label("$16$",(D+A)/2,S);label("$9$",(A+Ep)/2,S);
\end{asy}

$\textbf{(A) }240\qquad\textbf{(B) }248\qquad\textbf{(C) }256\qquad\textbf{(D) }264\qquad\textbf{(E) }272$

solution:
The diameter is $FE=9+16+9=34$, so the radius is $17$. Since $FD=AE$, the centre $O$ of the semicircle is the midpoint of $DA$, so $OD=8$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); real R=17;
pair Fp=(-17,0),D=(-8,0),A=(8,0),Ep=(17,0),C=(-8,15),B=(8,15);
draw(arc(O,R,0,180),linewidth(0.9)); draw(Fp--Ep,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--B--C--D,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--C,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(Fp);dot(D);dot(A);dot(Ep);dot(B);dot(C);dot(O);
label("$F$",Fp,S);label("$D$",D,S);label("$A$",A,S);label("$E$",Ep,S);label("$B$",B,NE);label("$C$",C,NW);label("$O$",O,S);
label("$8$",(D+O)/2,S);label("$17$",(O+C)/2,E,red);label("$15$",(C+D)/2,W);
\end{asy}

The vertex $C$ is on the arc, so $OC=17$, and $CD\perp FE$. By the Pythagorean theorem in right triangle $ODC$,
\[CD=\sqrt{17^2-8^2}=\sqrt{225}=15.\]
The area of $ABCD$ is $DA\cdot CD=16\cdot15=240$, so the answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(A) }240}$.

\textbf{When do two sides cross?} Each side of $DEF$ or $PQR$ joins points on two different sides of $ABC$, so apart from its endpoints it lies inside triangle $ABC$. Go once around the boundary of $ABC$; the six points appear in some cyclic order. For a side $UV$ of one triangle and a side $WZ$ of the other (four distinct points), the segments meet exactly when $W$ and $Z$ lie on different arcs of the boundary cut out by $U$ and $V$: the segment $UV$ divides triangle $ABC$ into two convex pieces, and $WZ$ crosses $UV$ exactly when $W$ and $Z$ lie in different pieces.

All common points are such crossings: the boundaries share no point on the boundary of $ABC$ (the six points are distinct), and a point inside $ABC$ lies on at most one side of each triangle (two sides of a triangle meet only at a vertex). So the number of common points is the number of pairs (side of $PQR$, side of $DEF$) that cross.

\textbf{Counting.} The points $P,Q,R$ cut the boundary of $ABC$ into three arcs: from $P$ to $Q$ through $B$, from $Q$ to $R$ through $C$, and from $R$ to $P$ through $A$. The side $PQ$ separates its arc from the rest. If that arc contains $j$ of the points $D,E,F$, then $PQ$ crosses the sides of $DEF$ that join one of these $j$ points to one of the other $3-j$, i.e. $j(3-j)$ sides: $0$ if $j=0$ or $3$, and $2$ if $j=1$ or $2$. The same holds for $QR$ and $RP$.

Each arc contains interior points of only two sides of $ABC$; for example the arc from $P$ to $Q$ through $B$ contains no interior point of $CA$, so it does not contain $F$. Hence no arc contains all three of $D,E,F$, so at least two arcs contain one or two of them, and at least two sides of $PQR$ cross the boundary of $DEF$ twice each. So there are at least $4$ common points.

\textbf{Four is possible.} Take, going around from $A$, the order $P,D$ on $AB$, then $E,Q$ on $BC$, then $F,R$ on $CA$ (left figure). The arc from $P$ to $Q$ contains $D$ and $E$, the arc from $Q$ to $R$ contains $F$, and the arc from $R$ to $P$ contains none, so there are exactly $2+2+0=4$ common points. (When the two triangles alternate, $D,P,E,Q,F,R$, every arc contains one point and there are $6$; these are the only two possibilities.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(12cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(10,0),C=(3,8);
pair pt(pair P,pair Q,real t){return P+t*(Q-P);}
picture p1,p2;
// left: 4 common points (P,D on AB; E,Q on BC; F,R on CA)
pair P=pt(A,B,0.3),D=pt(A,B,0.6),Ep=pt(B,C,0.25),Q=pt(B,C,0.6),F=pt(C,A,0.3),R=pt(C,A,0.65);
draw(p1,A--B--C--cycle,gray);
draw(p1,D--Ep--F--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(p1,P--Q--R--cycle,red+linewidth(0.9)); dot(p1,D,blue);dot(p1,Ep,blue);dot(p1,F,blue);dot(p1,P,red);dot(p1,Q,red);dot(p1,R,red);
label(p1,"$A$",A,SW);label(p1,"$B$",B,SE);label(p1,"$C$",C,N);
label(p1,"$D$",D,S,blue);label(p1,"$P$",P,S,red);label(p1,"$E$",Ep,E,blue);label(p1,"$Q$",Q,E,red);label(p1,"$F$",F,W,blue);label(p1,"$R$",R,W,red);
label(p1,"order $P,D,E,Q,F,R$: $4$ points",(5,-1.3));
// right: 6 common points (alternating)
pair D2=pt(A,B,0.3),P2=pt(A,B,0.65),Ep2=pt(B,C,0.3),Q2=pt(B,C,0.65),F2=pt(C,A,0.3),R2=pt(C,A,0.65);
draw(p2,A--B--C--cycle,gray);
draw(p2,D2--Ep2--F2--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(p2,P2--Q2--R2--cycle,red+linewidth(0.9)); dot(p2,D2,blue);dot(p2,Ep2,blue);dot(p2,F2,blue);dot(p2,P2,red);dot(p2,Q2,red);dot(p2,R2,red);
label(p2,"$A$",A,SW);label(p2,"$B$",B,SE);label(p2,"$C$",C,N);
label(p2,"$D$",D2,S,blue);label(p2,"$P$",P2,S,red);label(p2,"$E$",Ep2,E,blue);label(p2,"$Q$",Q2,E,red);label(p2,"$F$",F2,W,blue);label(p2,"$R$",R2,W,red);
label(p2,"order $D,P,E,Q,F,R$: $6$ points",(5,-1.3));
add(p1); add(shift(13,0)*p2);
\end{asy}

The least possible number is $\boxed{4}$.

The last digit must be even. Count by whether it is $0$.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Last digit $0$.} The first five digits are chosen in order from the $9$ remaining digits, and none of them is $0$: $9\cdot8\cdot7\cdot6\cdot5=15120$.
\item \textbf{Last digit $2$, $4$, $6$ or $8$} ($4$ choices). The first digit cannot be $0$ or the last digit: $8$ choices. The four middle digits are then chosen in order from the $8$ unused digits: $8\cdot7\cdot6\cdot5=1680$. This gives $4\cdot8\cdot1680=53760$.
\end{itemize}
In total there are $15120+53760=\boxed{68880}$ such numbers.

question:
Using $1^2+2^2+\cdots+m^2=\dfrac{m(m+1)(2m+1)}{6}$, find formulas for
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] the sum of the first $n$ even squares, $2^2+4^2+\cdots+(2n)^2$;
\item[(b)] the sum of the first $n$ odd squares, $1^2+3^2+\cdots+(2n-1)^2$.
\end{enumerate}

A traveller goes from $A$ to $B$ at a constant speed $k$ and returns from $B$ to $A$ along the same route at a constant speed $y$ (in the same units). A student conjectures that the average speed for the whole round trip is
\[\left|\dfrac{y-k}{3}\right|+k.\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the true average speed in terms of $k$ and $y$.
\item[(b)] Show that the conjecture is false in general, and find all pairs of positive speeds $(k,y)$ for which it gives the correct value.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let each woman have $n$ boxes. If a woman takes $3$ days for $t$ of her boxes and $2$ days for the other $n-t$, her total time is $2n+t$ with $0\le t\le n$, so it lies between $2n$ and $3n$.
\begin{itemize}
\item Mrs.\ Munch: $2n\le43\le3n$, so $15\le n\le21$.
\item Mrs.\ Crunch: $2n\le62\le3n$, so $21\le n\le31$.
\end{itemize}
Hence $n=\boxed{21}$. This works: Mrs.\ Munch takes $3$ days for $1$ box and $2$ days for $20$ ($3+40=43$), and Mrs.\ Crunch takes $3$ days for $20$ boxes and $2$ days for $1$ ($60+2=62$).

The condition says that $a$ is an integer strictly between $\dfrac{13b}{21}$ and $\dfrac{12b}{19}$. The gap between these is $\dfrac{12b}{19}-\dfrac{13b}{21}=\dfrac{5b}{399}<\dfrac14$ for $b\le19$, so for each $b$ there is at most one candidate, namely the least integer greater than $\dfrac{13b}{21}$. Checking $b=1,2,\dots,19$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $b=8$: $4.95<a<5.05$ gives $a=5$;
\item $b=16$: $9.90<a<10.11$ gives $a=10$, but $\dfrac{10}{16}$ is not in lowest terms;
\item every other $b$ gives no integer; for example $b=19$ gives $11.76<a<12$.
\end{itemize}
(The intervals for the other values of $b$ are $(0.62,0.63)$, $(1.24,1.26)$, $(1.86,1.89)$, $(2.48,2.53)$, $(3.10,3.16)$, $(3.71,3.79)$, $(4.33,4.42)$, $(5.57,5.68)$, $(6.19,6.32)$, $(6.81,6.95)$, $(7.43,7.58)$, $(8.05,8.21)$, $(8.67,8.84)$, $(9.29,9.47)$, $(10.52,10.74)$, $(11.14,11.37)$ for $b=1,\dots,7,9,\dots,15,17,18$.)

So $\dfrac ab=\dfrac58$ and $a+b=\boxed{13}$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What does it mean for a number $r$ to be a root (solution) of the equation $x^2-x-6=0$?
\item[(b)] Find all roots of this equation and check them.
\item[(c)] How do the roots appear on the graph of $y=x^2-x-6$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The number $r$ is a root if substituting $x=r$ makes the equation true: $r^2-r-6=0$. (Equivalently, $r$ is a zero of the polynomial $x^2-x-6$.)

\textbf{(b)} Factor: $x^2-x-6=(x-3)(x+2)$. A product is $0$ exactly when one of the factors is $0$, so the roots are $\boxed{x=3\text{ and }x=-2}$. Check: $9-3-6=0$ and $4+2-6=0$.

\textbf{(c)} A point of the graph lies on the $x$-axis exactly when $y=0$, i.e. when $x$ is a root. So the roots are the $x$-coordinates of the points where the parabola crosses the $x$-axis, $(-2,0)$ and $(3,0)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return x^2-x-6;}
draw((-3.6,0)--(4.6,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-7.2)--(0,5),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(4.6,0),E); label("$y$",(0,5),N);
draw(graph(f,-3.1,4.1),blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot((-2,0)); dot((3,0)); label("$(-2,0)$",(-2,0),SW); label("$(3,0)$",(3,0),SE);
label("$y=x^2-x-6$",(3.4,f(3.4)),E,blue);
\end{asy}

Let the old tiles have side $t$. Four of them cover the table, arranged $2$ by $2$, so the table has side $2t$. Decreasing $t$ by $75\%$ leaves $25\%$ of it: the new side is $\dfrac t4$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=4;
fill((0,0)--(s,0)--(s,s)--(0,s)--cycle,rgb(0.95,0.95,0.85));
for(int i=1;i<8;++i){ draw((i*s/8,0)--(i*s/8,s),gray(0.6)); draw((0,i*s/8)--(s,i*s/8),gray(0.6)); }
draw((s/2,0)--(s/2,s),linewidth(1.2)); draw((0,s/2)--(s,s/2),linewidth(1.2));
draw((0,0)--(s,0)--(s,s)--(0,s)--cycle,linewidth(1.2));
draw((0,-0.3)--(s/2,-0.3),Arrows(4)); label("old tile side $t$",(s/4,-0.3),S);
draw((s+0.3,0)--(s+0.3,s/8),Arrows(4)); label("$\dfrac t4$",(s+0.3,s/16),E);
\end{asy}

Along each side of the table fit $\dfrac{2t}{\dfrac t4}=8$ new tiles, so $8\cdot8=\boxed{64}$ new tiles are needed. (Equivalently, each old tile is replaced by $4\cdot4=16$ new ones, and $4\cdot16=64$.)

question:
A student notices that $\dfrac{\sin x}{x}$ gets closer and closer to $1$ as $x$ approaches $0$ ($x$ in radians), claims that $\dfrac{\tan x}{x}$ gets closer and closer to $0$, and asks whether this shows that $\dfrac00=1$ or $\dfrac00=0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $\dfrac{\sin x}{x}\to1$ as $x\to0$.
\item[(b)] Find the limit of $\dfrac{\tan x}{x}$ as $x\to0$.
\item[(c)] Explain why such limits cannot give a value to $\dfrac00$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let $0<x<\dfrac\pi2$. On the unit circle with centre $O$, let $A=(1,0)$, let $P$ be the point at angle $x$, and let $T$ be the point where line $OP$ meets the tangent to the circle at $A$, so $AT=\tan x$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real x=0.75;
pair O=(0,0),A=(1,0),P=dir(x*180/pi),T=(1,tan(x));
fill(O--A--T--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
fill(O--arc(O,1,0,x*180/pi)--cycle,rgb(1,0.85,0.7));
fill(O--A--P--cycle,rgb(0.8,1,0.8));
draw(arc(O,1,-10,100),gray);
draw(O--A--T--cycle,linewidth(0.8)); draw(O--P,linewidth(0.8)); draw(P--A,linewidth(0.8));
draw(P--(P.x,0),dashed);
dot(O);dot(A);dot(P);dot(T);
label("$O$",O,SW);label("$A$",A,SE);label("$P$",P,NW);label("$T$",T,NE);
label("$1$",(0.5,0),S); label("$\sin x$",(P.x,P.y/2),W); label("$\tan x$",(1,tan(x)/2),E);
draw(arc(O,0.22,0,x*180/pi)); label("$x$",0.3*dir(x*90/pi),E);
\end{asy}

Triangle $OAP$ lies inside the sector $OAP$, which lies inside triangle $OAT$. Comparing areas,
\[\dfrac12\cdot1\cdot\sin x<\dfrac12\cdot1^2\cdot x<\dfrac12\cdot1\cdot\tan x,\qquad\text{so}\qquad\sin x<x<\tan x.\]
Dividing by $\sin x>0$ gives $1<\dfrac{x}{\sin x}<\dfrac1{\cos x}$, i.e.
\[\cos x<\dfrac{\sin x}{x}<1.\]
As $x\to0^+$, $\cos x\to1$, so $\dfrac{\sin x}{x}$ is squeezed to $1$. Since $\dfrac{\sin(-x)}{-x}=\dfrac{\sin x}{x}$, the same holds as $x\to0^-$. So $\dfrac{\sin x}{x}\to\boxed{1}$.

\textbf{(b)} $\dfrac{\tan x}{x}=\dfrac{\sin x}{x}\cdot\dfrac{1}{\cos x}\to1\cdot1=\boxed{1}$. So the student's observation is wrong: $\dfrac{\tan x}{x}$ also approaches $1$ (for example $\dfrac{\tan0.1}{0.1}\approx1.0033$).

\textbf{(c)} Many expressions of the form $\dfrac{\text{(something tending to }0)}{\text{(something tending to }0)}$ have different limits: as $x\to0$,
\[\dfrac xx\to1,\qquad\dfrac{2x}{x}\to2,\qquad\dfrac{x^2}{x}\to0.\]
The limit depends on how the numerator and denominator approach $0$, not on the value ``$\dfrac00$''. Moreover, $\dfrac00=c$ would have to mean $c\cdot0=0$, which is true for every number $c$, so no single value can be assigned. Hence $\dfrac00$ stays undefined; it is an \emph{indeterminate form}.

Let $R$ be the radius of the circle, with centre $O$. Joining $O$ to the $12$ vertices splits the dodecagon into $12$ congruent isosceles triangles with legs $R$ and apex angle $\dfrac{360^\circ}{12}=30^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V; for(int k=0;k<12;++k) V.push(dir(30k));
draw(circle((0,0),1),blue);
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--V[6]--V[7]--V[8]--V[9]--V[10]--V[11]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
pair O=(0,0); dot(O); label("$O$",O,W);
fill(O--V[0]--V[1]--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.7)); draw(O--V[0]--V[1]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
pair Hh=(V[1].x,0); draw(V[1]--Hh,dashed);
label("$R$",V[0]/2,S); label("$30^\circ$",0.28*dir(15),E,fontsize(8pt)); draw((V[1]+Hh)/2+(0.04,0)--(1.25,0.35),gray); label("$BH=\dfrac R2$",(1.25,0.35),E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$A$",V[0],SE); label("$B$",V[1],NE); label("$H$",Hh,SW);
\end{asy}

In triangle $OAB$, drop the perpendicular $BH$ to $OA$. Triangle $OHB$ is a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle with hypotenuse $OB=R$, so $BH=\dfrac R2$, and
\[[OAB]=\dfrac12\cdot OA\cdot BH=\dfrac12\cdot R\cdot\dfrac R2=\dfrac{R^2}4.\]
The dodecagon has area $12\cdot\dfrac{R^2}{4}=3R^2$, and the circle has area $\pi R^2$. The ratio is
\[\dfrac{\pi R^2}{3R^2}=\boxed{\dfrac\pi3}\approx1.047.\]

