solution:
The side of the square is $\sqrt{961}=31$. The diagonal $AC$ is a diameter of the circle, since the inscribed angle $ABC$ is a right angle (or by symmetry about the centre). By the Pythagorean theorem $AC=31\sqrt2$, so the radius is $\dfrac{31\sqrt2}{2}$ and the circle has area
\[\pi\left(\dfrac{31\sqrt2}{2}\right)^2=\dfrac{961\pi}{2}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=31; real R=s/sqrt(2);
pair O=(0,0),A=(-s/2,s/2),B=(s/2,s/2),C=(s/2,-s/2),D=(-s/2,-s/2);
draw(circle(O,R),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,red+dashed);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,NE);
label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,NE);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,SW);
label("$31$",(A+B)/2,S); label("$31\sqrt2$",(O+C)/2+(2,3),red+fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

The ratio of the areas is
\[\dfrac{961}{\dfrac{961\pi}{2}}=\boxed{\dfrac{2}{\pi}}.\]

\textbf{Bounding boxes.} Let a square with vertices at grid points have, going around it anticlockwise, the side vectors $u$, $u$ turned by $90^\circ$, $-u$, and so on. Exactly one of these four vectors has the form $(p,q)$ with $p>0$ and $q\ge0$; then the smallest axis-parallel square containing our square (its \emph{box}) has side $k=p+q$, and our square touches each side of the box at one vertex, at distance $q$ from a corner (measured the same way round on every side).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int k=3;
for(int i=0;i<=k;++i){ draw((i,0)--(i,k),gray(0.7)); draw((0,i)--(k,i),gray(0.7)); }
for(int i=0;i<=k;++i) for(int j=0;j<=k;++j) dot((i,j),gray(0.4));
draw((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(0,3)--cycle,black+linewidth(1.1));
draw((1,0)--(3,1)--(2,3)--(0,2)--cycle,blue+linewidth(1.1));
draw((2,0)--(3,2)--(1,3)--(0,1)--cycle,red+linewidth(1.1));
label("$q=0$",(1.5,-0.25),S); label("$q=1$",(1,0),SW,blue); label("$q=2$",(2,0),SE,red);
\end{asy}

Conversely, a $k\times k$ box with corners at grid points contains exactly $k$ such squares, one for each $q=0,1,\dots,k-1$ (the figure shows $k=3$): the square with vertices at distance $q$ from the corners, going around. Each square is counted exactly once, by its own box.

\textbf{Counting.} A $k\times k$ box fits in $(n+1-k)(m+1-k)$ positions, for $1\le k\le n$. So the number of squares is
\[S(n,m)=\sum_{k=1}^{n}k(n+1-k)(m+1-k).\]

\textbf{Closed form.} Write $m+1-k=(n+1-k)+(m-n)$. With $N=n+1$ and the standard sums of $k$, $k^2$, $k^3$ for $k=1,\dots,n$,
\[\sum_{k=1}^{n}k(N-k)=\dfrac{n(n+1)(n+2)}{6},\qquad\sum_{k=1}^{n}k(N-k)^2=N^2\sum k-2N\sum k^2+\sum k^3=\dfrac{n(n+1)^2(n+2)}{12}.\]
Hence
\[S(n,m)=\dfrac{n(n+1)^2(n+2)}{12}+(m-n)\dfrac{n(n+1)(n+2)}{6}=\boxed{\dfrac{n(n+1)(n+2)(2m-n+1)}{12}}.\]
For example, a $1\times1$ grid has $1$ square, a $2\times2$ grid has $6$ ($4+1$ axis-parallel and $1$ tilted), and an $8\times8$ chessboard has $540$. (The product $\dbinom{n+1}{2}\dbinom{m+1}{2}$ counts axis-parallel rectangles, not squares.)

From $(-20,0)$ to $(0,10)$ the line rises $10$ while running $20$, so its slope is $\dfrac{10}{20}=\dfrac12$. It crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,10)$, so its $y$-intercept is $10$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-23,0)--(6,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-2)--(0,14),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(6,0),E); label("$y$",(0,14),N);
draw((-22,-1)--(4,12),blue+linewidth(0.9));
pair P=(0,10),Q=(-20,0); dot(P); dot(Q);
label("$(0,10)$",P,E); label("$(-20,0)$",Q,S+0.6*W);
draw(Q--(0,0),red,Arrow(5)); draw((0,0)--P,red,Arrow(5));
label("run $20$",(-10,0),S,red); label("rise $10$",(0,5),E,red);
\end{asy}

The product is $\dfrac12\cdot10=\boxed{5}$. (The line is $y=\dfrac12x+10$.)

The side of the square is $\sqrt{36}=6$ cm. If the circle has radius $r$, then $2\pi r=6$, so $r=\dfrac3\pi$ cm.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(6,0)--(6,6)--(0,6)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
label("$6$",(3,0),S); label("area $36$",(3,3));
real r=3/pi; pair O=(9,r);
draw(circle(O,r),blue+linewidth(0.9)); dot(O); draw(O--O+(r,0),blue); label("$r$",O+(r/2,0),N,blue);
label("circumference $6$",O+(0,-r),S,blue);
\end{asy}

The area of the circle is
\[\pi r^2=\pi\cdot\dfrac{9}{\pi^2}=\boxed{\dfrac9\pi\text{ cm}^2}\approx2.86\text{ cm}^2.\]

The diagonal is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs are the width and the length $\ell$, so by the Pythagorean theorem
\[\ell=\sqrt{15^2-9^2}=\sqrt{144}=12.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(12,0)--(12,9)--(0,9)--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw((0,0)--(12,9),red);
label("$12$",(6,0),S); label("$9$",(0,4.5),W); label("$15$",(6,4.5),NW,red);
\end{asy}

The area is $9\cdot12=108\text{ cm}^2$ and the perimeter is $2(9+12)=42$ cm. The sum of the numbers is $108+42=\boxed{150}$.

The radius is $2$ inches, so the disc has area $4\pi$. Joining the centre $O$ to the six vertices splits the hexagon into six equilateral triangles of side $2$ (each has two sides equal to the radius and a $60^\circ$ angle at $O$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); real R=2;
pair[] V; for(int k=0;k<6;++k) V.push(R*dir(60k));
fill(circle(O,R),rgb(0.85,0.92,1)); fill(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,white);
draw(circle(O,R),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--V[0],dashed); draw(O--V[1],dashed); dot(O); label("$O$",O,S);
label("$2$",(V[0])/2,S); label("$2$",V[1]/2,W); label("$2$",(V[0]+V[1])/2,NE);
\end{asy}

An equilateral triangle of side $2$ has area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot2^2=\sqrt3$, so the hexagon has area $6\sqrt3$. The shaded region has area
\[\boxed{4\pi-6\sqrt3}\approx2.17\text{ square inches}.\]

question:
A circle of radius $2$ is cut into four congruent arcs. The four arcs are joined to form the star figure shown (each arc bends inwards, and neighbouring arcs meet at the four points of the star). What is the ratio of the area of the star figure to the area of the original circle?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=2;
picture c; draw(c,circle((0,0),r),linewidth(0.9));
for(int k=0;k<4;++k) draw(c,(0.85*r*dir(45+90k))--(1.15*r*dir(45+90k)),linewidth(0.9));
label(c,"$2$",(0,0)--r*dir(0),N); draw(c,(0,0)--(r,0)); dot(c,(0,0));
picture s;
draw(s,arc((2,2),2,180,270),linewidth(0.9)); draw(s,arc((-2,2),2,270,360),linewidth(0.9));
draw(s,arc((-2,-2),2,0,90),linewidth(0.9)); draw(s,arc((2,-2),2,90,180),linewidth(0.9));
add(c); add(shift(6.5,0)*s);
\end{asy}

\[\textbf{(A)}\ \dfrac{4-\pi}{\pi}\qquad\textbf{(B)}\ \dfrac1\pi\qquad\textbf{(C)}\ \dfrac{\sqrt2}{\pi}\qquad\textbf{(D)}\ \dfrac{\pi-1}{\pi}\qquad\textbf{(E)}\ \dfrac3\pi\]

solution:
Each arc is a quarter of a circle of radius $2$. Draw the square whose corners are the centres of the four arcs: its side is $2+2=4$, and the four points of the star are the midpoints of its sides.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill((-2,-2)--(2,-2)--(2,2)--(-2,2)--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
fill(arc((2,2),2,180,270)--(2,2)--cycle,white); fill(arc((-2,2),2,270,360)--(-2,2)--cycle,white);
fill(arc((-2,-2),2,0,90)--(-2,-2)--cycle,white); fill(arc((2,-2),2,90,180)--(2,-2)--cycle,white);
draw((-2,-2)--(2,-2)--(2,2)--(-2,2)--cycle,dashed);
draw(arc((2,2),2,180,270),linewidth(0.9)); draw(arc((-2,2),2,270,360),linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc((-2,-2),2,0,90),linewidth(0.9)); draw(arc((2,-2),2,90,180),linewidth(0.9));
draw((2,2)--(2,0),gray); label("$2$",(2,1),E); label("$4$",(0,-2),S);
label("star",(0,0));
\end{asy}

The square consists of the star and four quarter-discs of radius $2$, which together make up one disc of radius $2$. So
\[[\text{star}]=4^2-\pi\cdot2^2=16-4\pi,\qquad\dfrac{[\text{star}]}{[\text{circle}]}=\dfrac{16-4\pi}{4\pi}=\dfrac{4-\pi}{\pi}.\]
The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(A)}\ \dfrac{4-\pi}{\pi}}$.

question:
A square with area $4$ is inscribed in a square with area $5$, with one vertex of the smaller square on each side of the larger square. A vertex of the smaller square divides a side of the larger square into two segments, one of length $a$ and the other of length $b$. What is the value of $ab$?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=(sqrt(5)-sqrt(3))/2, b=(sqrt(5)+sqrt(3))/2, s=sqrt(5);
pair A=(0,0),B=(s,0),C=(s,s),D=(0,s);
pair P=(a,0),Q=(s,a),R=(s-a,s),S2=(0,s-a);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(P--Q--R--S2--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(P);dot(Q);dot(R);dot(S2);
label("$a$",(a/2,0),S); label("$b$",(a+b/2,0),S);
\end{asy}

\[\textbf{(A)}\ \dfrac15\qquad\textbf{(B)}\ \dfrac25\qquad\textbf{(C)}\ \dfrac12\qquad\textbf{(D)}\ 1\qquad\textbf{(E)}\ 4\]

solution:
The four corner triangles are right triangles whose hypotenuses are the sides of the small square, of length $2$. At each vertex of the small square, the two acute angles of the neighbouring corner triangles add up to $180^\circ-90^\circ=90^\circ$, so all four corner triangles have the same angles and the same hypotenuse: they are congruent. Hence every side of the large square is split into the same two lengths $a$ and $b$, and each corner triangle has legs $a$ and $b$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=(sqrt(5)-sqrt(3))/2, b=(sqrt(5)+sqrt(3))/2, s=sqrt(5);
pair A=(0,0),B=(s,0),C=(s,s),D=(0,s);
pair P=(a,0),Q=(s,a),R=(s-a,s),S2=(0,s-a);
fill(A--P--S2--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.7)); fill(B--Q--P--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.7)); fill(C--R--Q--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.7)); fill(D--S2--R--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.7));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(P--Q--R--S2--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
label("$a$",(a/2,0),S); label("$b$",(a+b/2,0),S); label("$a$",(s,a/2),E); label("$b$",(s,a+b/2),E);
label("$2$",(P+Q)/2,NW); label("area $4$",(s/2,s/2));
\end{asy}

Comparing areas, the large square is the small square plus the four triangles:
\[5=4+4\cdot\dfrac{ab}{2},\qquad\text{so}\qquad ab=\dfrac12.\]
(Equivalently, $a+b=\sqrt5$ and $a^2+b^2=2^2=4$ give $2ab=5-4=1$.) The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ \dfrac12}$.

The sides are $x$, $3x$ and $4x$ for some positive integer $x$, so the volume is $12x^3$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real x=1; pair o=(0,0); pair dx=(3.2,0), dy=(0,1.1), dz=(0.9,0.6);
path f1=o--o+dx--o+dx+dy--o+dy--cycle;
draw(f1,linewidth(0.9)); draw(o+dy--o+dy+dz--o+dx+dy+dz--o+dx+dy,linewidth(0.9)); draw(o+dx--o+dx+dz--o+dx+dy+dz,linewidth(0.9));
draw(o--o+dz,dashed); draw(o+dz--o+dz+dx,dashed); draw(o+dz--o+dz+dy,dashed);
label("$4x$",o+dx/2,S); label("$x$",o+dy/2,W); label("$3x$",o+dx+dz/2,SE);
\end{asy}

Dividing the choices by $12$ gives $4$, $\dfrac{14}3$, $\dfrac{16}3$, $8$ and $12$; only $8=2^3$ is a perfect cube. So the volume could be $12\cdot2^3=96$ (a $2\times6\times8$ box): the answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(D)}\ 96}$.

\textbf{Face sums.} The labels add up to $36$ and every vertex lies on $3$ faces, so the six face sums add up to $108$ and each face sum is $18$.

\textbf{Opposite edges.} Take coordinates $\{0,1\}^3$ for the vertices and write $L(v)$ for the label of $v$ and $v'$ for the vertex opposite $v$ (through the centre). Two faces sharing an edge both have sum $18$, so the two remaining vertices of one face have the same sum as the two remaining vertices of the other. For example, the faces $z=0$ and $y=0$ share the edge from $000$ to $100$, so
\[L(010)+L(110)=L(001)+L(101).\]
The edges $\{010,110\}$ and $\{001,101\}$ are opposite edges of the cube. In the same way, for every edge $uv$ we get $L(u)+L(v)=L(u')+L(v')$, i.e. $L(u)-L(u')=-\left(L(v)-L(v')\right)$.

\textbf{A constant difference.} Let $d(u)=L(u)-L(u')$. Along every edge $d$ changes sign. Colour the vertices with an even coordinate sum red and the others blue; every edge joins a red and a blue vertex, and the opposite of a red vertex is blue. So all four red vertices have the same value $d(u)=\delta\ne0$: the labels form four opposite pairs (red, blue) whose difference is the same number $\delta$.

Conversely, if the four opposite pairs all have the same difference $\delta$ (red minus blue), then for each pair of opposite faces, say $z=0$ and $z=1$, each face has two red and two blue vertices whose opposites lie on the other face, so the two face sums differ by $2\delta-2\delta=0$. They add up to $36$, so each is $18$. Hence the arrangements are exactly the labelings in which the opposite pairs have a common difference.

\textbf{Counting.} The numbers $1,\dots,8$ split into four pairs with a common difference $|\delta|$ only for
\[|\delta|=1:\ \{1,2\},\{3,4\},\{5,6\},\{7,8\};\qquad|\delta|=2:\ \{1,3\},\{2,4\},\{5,7\},\{6,8\};\qquad|\delta|=4:\ \{1,5\},\{2,6\},\{3,7\},\{4,8\}\]
(for $|\delta|=3$, $5$, $6$ or $7$ the number $1$ or $8$ cannot be paired, or two numbers are left over). So a labeling is fixed by choosing the pairing ($3$ ways), which colour gets the larger numbers ($2$ ways), and which red vertex gets each of the four larger numbers ($4!$ ways): $3\cdot2\cdot24=144$ labelings. One of them is shown, with red and blue vertices.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=2.2; pair dx=(s,0), dy=(0.95,0.65), dz=(0,s);
pair P(int i,int j,int k){return i*dx+j*dy+k*dz;}
int L(int i,int j,int k){ int[][][] t={{{8,1},{3,6}},{{2,7},{5,4}}}; return t[i][j][k];}
bool even(int i,int j,int k){return (i+j+k)%2==0;}
// edges (back ones dashed)
for(int i=0;i<2;++i) for(int j=0;j<2;++j) for(int k=0;k<2;++k){
  if(i==0){ pen p=(j==1 && k==0)?dashed:solid; draw(P(0,j,k)--P(1,j,k),p);}
  if(j==0){ pen p=(i==0 && k==0)?dashed:solid; draw(P(i,0,k)--P(i,1,k),p);}
  if(k==0){ pen p=(i==0 && j==1)?dashed:solid; draw(P(i,j,0)--P(i,j,1),p);}
}
for(int i=0;i<2;++i) for(int j=0;j<2;++j) for(int k=0;k<2;++k){
  pair q=P(i,j,k); pen c=even(i,j,k)?red:blue;
  dot(q,c);
  pair d=unit(q-P(0,0,0)/2-(P(1,1,1))/2);
  label("$"+string(L(i,j,k))+"$",q,d,c);
}
\end{asy}

A rotation other than the identity moves some vertex, so it changes every labeling (all labels are different). Hence the $24$ rotations of the cube turn each labeling into $24$ different labelings, and the number of arrangements up to rotation is $\dfrac{144}{24}=6$. The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ 6}$.

The side lengths $\ell$ and $25-\ell$ are integers with $1\le\ell\le24$, and the area is
\[\ell(25-\ell)=\dfrac{625}{4}-\left(\ell-\dfrac{25}2\right)^2.\]
It is largest when $\ell$ is as close as possible to $\dfrac{25}{2}$, i.e. $\ell=12$ or $13$ (area $156$), and smallest when $\ell$ is as far as possible, i.e. $\ell=1$ or $24$ (area $24$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(13,0)--(13,12)--(0,12)--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); label("$13$",(6.5,0),S); label("$12$",(0,6),W); label("area $156$",(6.5,6));
draw((16,5)--(40,5)--(40,6)--(16,6)--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); label("$24$",(28,5),S); label("$1$",(40,5.5),E); label("area $24$",(28,6),N);
\end{asy}

The difference is $156-24=132$: the answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(D)}\ 132}$.

\textbf{Counting directly.} Of each of the $12$ edges of the big cube, only the middle third survives: $12$ edges. At each of the $8$ corners, removing the small cube leaves a notch.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair dx=(1,0), dy=(0.45,0.3), dz=(0,1);
pair P(real x,real y,real z){return x*dx+y*dy+z*dz;}
// visible faces of the solid: front y=0, right x=3, top z=3 (cross shapes) and the notch faces
path cross(pair o, pair u, pair v){ return o+1u--o+2u--o+2u+1v--o+3u+1v--o+3u+2v--o+2u+2v--o+2u+3v--o+1u+3v--o+1u+2v--o+2v--o+1v--o+1u+1v--cycle; }
// painter's order: the back notches first, then the front notches, then the outer cross-shaped faces
// notch at the back-top-right corner: faces x=2, z=2 (y=2 hidden); partly hidden behind the edge cubes in front of it
filldraw(P(2,2,2)--P(3,2,2)--P(3,3,2)--P(2,3,2)--cycle,rgb(1,0.85,0.6),linewidth(0.8));
filldraw(P(2,2,2)--P(2,3,2)--P(2,3,3)--P(2,2,3)--cycle,rgb(1,0.9,0.7),linewidth(0.8));
// notch at the back-bottom-right corner: face x=2; notch at the back-top-left corner: face z=2
filldraw(P(2,2,0)--P(2,3,0)--P(2,3,1)--P(2,2,1)--cycle,rgb(1,0.9,0.7),linewidth(0.8));
filldraw(P(0,2,2)--P(1,2,2)--P(1,3,2)--P(0,3,2)--cycle,rgb(1,0.85,0.6),linewidth(0.8));
// notch at the front-top-right corner (cube [2,3]x[0,1]x[2,3] removed): faces x=2, y=1, z=2
filldraw(P(2,0,2)--P(3,0,2)--P(3,1,2)--P(2,1,2)--cycle,rgb(1,0.85,0.6),linewidth(0.8));
filldraw(P(2,1,2)--P(3,1,2)--P(3,1,3)--P(2,1,3)--cycle,rgb(1,0.75,0.45),linewidth(0.8));
filldraw(P(2,0,2)--P(2,1,2)--P(2,1,3)--P(2,0,3)--cycle,rgb(1,0.9,0.7),linewidth(0.8));
// notch at the front-top-left corner: faces x=1 (hidden), y=1, z=2
filldraw(P(0,0,2)--P(1,0,2)--P(1,1,2)--P(0,1,2)--cycle,rgb(1,0.85,0.6),linewidth(0.8));
filldraw(P(0,1,2)--P(1,1,2)--P(1,1,3)--P(0,1,3)--cycle,rgb(1,0.75,0.45),linewidth(0.8));
// notch at the front-bottom-right corner: faces x=2, y=1 (z=1 hidden)
filldraw(P(2,1,0)--P(3,1,0)--P(3,1,1)--P(2,1,1)--cycle,rgb(1,0.75,0.45),linewidth(0.8));
filldraw(P(2,0,0)--P(2,1,0)--P(2,1,1)--P(2,0,1)--cycle,rgb(1,0.9,0.7),linewidth(0.8));
// notch at the front-bottom-left corner: face y=1 (x=1 and z=1 hidden)
filldraw(P(0,1,0)--P(1,1,0)--P(1,1,1)--P(0,1,1)--cycle,rgb(1,0.75,0.45),linewidth(0.8));
filldraw(cross(P(0,0,0),dx,dz),rgb(0.9,0.9,1),linewidth(0.8));
filldraw(cross(P(3,0,0),dy,dz),rgb(0.8,0.85,1),linewidth(0.8));
filldraw(cross(P(0,0,3),dx,dy),rgb(0.95,0.95,1),linewidth(0.8));
// highlight the 9 edges created at the front-top-right corner
pen hp=red+linewidth(1.4);
draw(P(2,1,2)--P(3,1,2),hp); draw(P(2,1,2)--P(2,1,3),hp); draw(P(2,1,2)--P(2,0,2),hp);
draw(P(3,0,2)--P(3,1,2)--P(3,1,3),hp); draw(P(2,0,3)--P(2,1,3)--P(3,1,3),hp); draw(P(2,0,2)--P(3,0,2),hp); draw(P(2,0,2)--P(2,0,3),hp);
\end{asy}

The notch has three new square faces meeting at the inner corner, which gives $3$ edges there, and on each of the three old faces around the corner the boundary of the notch consists of $2$ unit segments, which gives $6$ more edges ($9$ edges per corner, shown in red for one corner). So the solid has
\[12+8\cdot9=84\]
edges. The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(D)}\ 84}$.

\textbf{Check with Euler's formula.} Each corner contributes $7$ vertices (the inner corner, $3$ points on old edges and $3$ points on old faces), so $V=56$; the faces are the $6$ old faces and $3$ new ones per corner, $F=30$. Indeed $V-E+F=56-84+30=2$.

question:
In the figure, $ABCD$ is a square, and $EFGH$ is a square inside it such that $E$ lies on segment $BH$, $F$ on $CE$, $G$ on $DF$ and $H$ on $AG$, where $ABH$, $BCE$, $CDF$ and $DAG$ are right triangles with the right angles at $H$, $E$, $F$ and $G$. The length of side $AB$ is $\sqrt{50}$ and $BE=1$. What is the area of the inner square $EFGH$?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,1),B=(7,0),C=(6,-7),D=(-1,-6),H=(0,0),Ep=(6,0),Fp=(6,-6),G=(0,-6);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--G); draw(B--H); draw(C--Ep); draw(D--Fp);
draw(H--Ep--Fp--G--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(H);dot(Ep);dot(Fp);dot(G);
label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,NE);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,SW);
label("$H$",H,NE);label("$E$",Ep,dir(-45));label("$F$",Fp,SW);label("$G$",G,dir(135));
\end{asy}

\[\textbf{(A)}\ 25\qquad\textbf{(B)}\ 32\qquad\textbf{(C)}\ 36\qquad\textbf{(D)}\ 40\qquad\textbf{(E)}\ 42\]

solution:
The four right triangles $ABH$, $BCE$, $CDF$, $DAG$ have equal hypotenuses (the sides of $ABCD$) and equal angles (their acute angles at $A$ and $B$ add up to $90^\circ$, and so on around the square), so they are congruent. In particular the short legs are equal: $AH=BE=1$. The long leg of triangle $ABH$ is $HB=HE+EB=HE+1$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,1),B=(7,0),C=(6,-7),D=(-1,-6),H=(0,0),Ep=(6,0),Fp=(6,-6),G=(0,-6);
fill(A--B--H--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.7));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--G); draw(B--H); draw(C--Ep); draw(D--Fp);
draw(H--Ep--Fp--G--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(H--(0.6,0)--(0.6,0.6)--(0,0.6));
label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,NE);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,SW);
label("$H$",H,dir(200));label("$E$",Ep,dir(-135));label("$F$",Fp,SW);label("$G$",G,dir(135));
label("$1$",(A+H)/2,W); label("$1$",(Ep+B)/2,S); label("$HE$",(H+Ep)/2,S); label("$\sqrt{50}$",(A+B)/2,N);
\end{asy}

By the Pythagorean theorem in triangle $ABH$,
\[1^2+(HE+1)^2=50,\qquad(HE+1)^2=49,\qquad HE=6.\]
So the inner square has area $6^2=36$: the answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ 36}$.

Let $O$ be the centre and $B$ the bottom of the wheel, and let $C$ be the first point of the rider's path $10$ feet above $B$. Let $M$ be the point of $OB$ at the height of $C$, so $OM=20-10=10$ and $CM\perp OB$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); real R=20;
draw(circle(O,R),linewidth(0.9));
pair Bt=(0,-R), C=R*dir(-30), M=(0,-10);
draw((-23,-10)--(23,-10),gray+dotted);
draw(O--Bt,dashed); draw(O--C,linewidth(0.9));
dot(O); dot(Bt); dot(C); dot(M);
label("$O$",O,N); label("$B$",Bt,S); label("$C$",C,dir(-20)); label("$M$",M,dir(200));
label("$20$",(O+C)/2,NE); label("$10$",(O+M)/2,W); label("$10$",(M+Bt)/2,W);
draw(arc(O,5,-90,-30)); label("$60^\circ$",6*dir(-60),dir(-60),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

In right triangle $OMC$, the hypotenuse is $OC=20$ and $OM=10=\dfrac{OC}{2}$, so $\angle MOC=60^\circ$ (a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle). The rider has turned through $60^\circ$, i.e. $\dfrac16$ of a revolution, which takes $\dfrac{60}{6}=10$ seconds. The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(D)}\ 10}$.

\textbf{Upper bound.} Two distinct lines meet in at most one point, so $N\le\dbinom42=6$.

\textbf{Possible values.} The configurations below give $N=0$ (four parallel lines), $1$ (four lines through one point), $3$ (three parallel lines and a transversal), $4$ (two pairs of parallel lines), $5$ (two parallel lines and two lines crossing each other between them) and $6$ (no two parallel, no three through a point).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
picture[] p;
for(int i=0;i<6;++i) p.push(new picture);
// N=0
for(int k=0;k<4;++k) draw(p[0],(-1,-1+0.6k)--(1,-0.4+0.6k));
label(p[0],"$N=0$",(0,-1.6));
// N=1
for(int k=0;k<4;++k) draw(p[1],1.1*dir(20+45k)--1.1*dir(200+45k)); dot(p[1],(0,0));
label(p[1],"$N=1$",(0,-1.6));
// N=3: three parallel, one transversal
for(int k=0;k<3;++k) draw(p[2],(-1,-0.9+0.7k)--(1,-0.9+0.7k)); draw(p[2],(-0.6,-1.2)--(0.6,1.0));
label(p[2],"$N=3$",(0,-1.6));
// N=4: two pairs of parallel lines
draw(p[3],(-1,-0.4)--(1,-0.4)); draw(p[3],(-1,0.4)--(1,0.4)); draw(p[3],(-0.7,-1)--(-0.2,1)); draw(p[3],(0.2,-1)--(0.7,1));
label(p[3],"$N=4$",(0,-1.6));
// N=5: two parallel, two others crossing between them
draw(p[4],(-1,-0.6)--(1,-0.6)); draw(p[4],(-1,0.6)--(1,0.6)); draw(p[4],(-0.8,-1)--(0.8,1)); draw(p[4],(0.8,-1)--(-0.8,1));
label(p[4],"$N=5$",(0,-1.6));
// N=6: general position
draw(p[5],(-1,-0.8)--(1,0.2)); draw(p[5],(-1,0.5)--(1,-0.9)); draw(p[5],(-0.7,-1.1)--(0.2,1.1)); draw(p[5],(0.9,-1.1)--(0.1,1.1));
label(p[5],"$N=6$",(0,-1.6));
for(int i=0;i<6;++i) add(shift(2.6*(i%3),-3.2*floor(i/3))*p[i]);
\end{asy}

\textbf{$N=2$ is impossible.} Suppose the only intersection points are $P$ and $Q$.
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Some line $\ell$ passes through both.} At least one other line $m$ passes through $P$ and at least one other line $n$ passes through $Q$; $m\ne n$, since otherwise this line would pass through $P$ and $Q$ and equal $\ell$. If $m$ and $n$ met, they would meet at $P$ or $Q$, and again one of them would equal $\ell$; so $m\parallel n$. The fourth line $k$ is not parallel to $m$: otherwise it would cross $\ell$ at $P$ or $Q$ and coincide with $m$ or $n$. So $k$ meets $m$ and $n$, necessarily at $P$ and at $Q$, and then $k=\ell$, a contradiction.
\item \emph{No line passes through both.} Then exactly two lines pass through $P$ and the other two through $Q$. A line through $P$ and a line through $Q$ cannot meet (their common point would be $P$ or $Q$), so they are parallel. Then both lines through $P$ are parallel to the same line through $Q$, hence to each other, although they meet at $P$: a contradiction.
\end{itemize}
So the possible values are $0,1,3,4,5,6$, with sum $19$. The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(D)}\ 19}$.

The lines are $y=\dfrac x2+1$ and $y=2x-2$ (both pass through $(2,2)$). They meet $x+y=10$ where $\dfrac x2+1=10-x$, i.e. at $(6,4)$, and where $2x-2=10-x$, i.e. at $(4,6)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-0.5,0)--(10.5,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-0.5)--(0,10.5),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(10.5,0),E); label("$y$",(0,10.5),N);
draw((0,1)--(9,5.5),blue); draw((1,0)--(5.5,9),red); draw((0.5,9.5)--(9,1),darkgreen);
pair P=(2,2),Q=(6,4),R=(4,6);
fill(P--Q--R--cycle,rgb(1,0.9,0.7)); draw(P--Q--R--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(P);dot(Q);dot(R);
label("$(2,2)$",P,SE); label("$(6,4)$",Q,E); label("$(4,6)$",R,NW);
label("$y=\dfrac x2+1$",(9,5.5),E,blue); label("$y=2x-2$",(5.5,9),N,red); label("$x+y=10$",(9,1),NE,darkgreen);
\end{asy}

By the shoelace formula, the triangle with vertices $(2,2)$, $(6,4)$, $(4,6)$ has area
\[\dfrac12\left|2(4-6)+6(6-2)+4(2-4)\right|=\dfrac12|-4+24-8|=6.\]
The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ 6}$.

Let $R$ be the radius of the circle; it is the circumradius of each of the triangles $ABD$, $ABC$, $ACD$.

\textbf{A distance formula.} In a triangle $PQS$ with circumradius $R$, the distance from $P$ to the line $QS$ is $PQ\sin\angle Q$, and by the law of sines $PS=2R\sin\angle Q$. Hence
\[\text{dist}(P,QS)=\dfrac{PQ\cdot PS}{2R}.\]
Applying this to triangles $ABD$, $ABC$ and $ACD$ (all with circumradius $R$):
\[x=\dfrac{AB\cdot AD}{2R},\qquad y=\dfrac{AB\cdot AC}{2R},\qquad z=\dfrac{AC\cdot AD}{2R}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
pair A=dir(105),B=dir(195),C=dir(250),D=dir(350);
pair ft(pair X,pair U,pair V){pair d=V-U; return U+dot(X-U,d)/dot(d,d)*d;}
pair X1=ft(A,B,D),Y1=ft(A,B,C),Z1=ft(A,C,D);
draw(circle(O,1),gray);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(B--D,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,gray+dashed);
draw(B--Y1,dotted); draw(D--Z1,dotted);
draw(A--X1,red); draw(A--Y1,blue); draw(A--Z1,darkgreen);
dot(X1,red); dot(Y1,blue); dot(Z1,darkgreen);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,S);label("$D$",D,E);
label("$x$",(A+X1)/2,E,red); label("$y$",(A+Y1)/2,W,blue); label("$z$",(A+Z1)/2,NE,darkgreen);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Reduction to Ptolemy.} Substituting,
\[\dfrac{BD}{x}=\dfrac{2R\cdot BD}{AB\cdot AD},\qquad\dfrac{BC}{y}=\dfrac{2R\cdot BC}{AB\cdot AC},\qquad\dfrac{CD}{z}=\dfrac{2R\cdot CD}{AC\cdot AD}.\]
Multiplying the required identity by $\dfrac{AB\cdot AC\cdot AD}{2R}>0$, it becomes
\[AC\cdot BD=AD\cdot BC+AB\cdot CD,\]
which is Ptolemy's theorem for the cyclic quadrilateral $ABCD$. Since every step is reversible, the identity holds. $\blacksquare$

question:
$101$ dalmatians stand in a line, all facing the front. Each dog sees all the dogs in front of it and none behind it: the first dog sees no one, and the last dog sees the other $100$. Each dog is given a blue or a pink hat, but cannot see its own. Starting from the back (the last dog), each dog in turn says one colour aloud, which all the dogs hear. A dog that says the colour of its own hat goes free; otherwise it is silently taken away (the others do not learn whether it was right). The dogs may agree on a strategy beforehand.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find a strategy that guarantees that at least $100$ dogs go free, whatever the hats.
\item[(b)] Show that no strategy guarantees that all $101$ dogs go free.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{The statement is false.} Take the rectangle $A=(-2,1)$, $B=(2,1)$, $C=(2,-1)$, $D=(-2,-1)$, with sides $4$ and $2$. All four angles are $90^\circ$, and all four vertices are at distance $\sqrt{2^2+1^2}=\sqrt5$ from the centre $O=(0,0)$, so $ABCD$ is cyclic. But it is not a square, since $AB=4\ne2=BC$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); pair A=(-2,1),B=(2,1),C=(2,-1),D=(-2,-1);
draw(circle(O,sqrt(5)),blue);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--C,dashed); draw(B--D,dashed);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,S);
label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,NE);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,SW);
label("$4$",(A+B)/2,N); label("$2$",(B+C)/2,E);
real m=0.25;
draw(A+(m,0)--A+(m,-m)--A+(0,-m)); draw(B+(-m,0)--B+(-m,-m)--B+(0,-m)); draw(C+(-m,0)--C+(-m,m)--C+(0,m)); draw(D+(m,0)--D+(m,m)--D+(0,m));
\end{asy}

In fact every quadrilateral with four right angles is a rectangle, and every rectangle is cyclic (its diagonals are equal and bisect each other, so their midpoint is equidistant from the four vertices). So ``cyclic with four right angles'' just means ``rectangle'', and a rectangle is a square only when two adjacent sides are equal. $\boxed{\text{False}}$

question:
A student proposes the following proof of the quadratic formula.

\emph{Let $r$ and $s$ be the roots of $ax^2+bx+c=0$, where $a\ne0$. By Vieta's formulas $r+s=-\dfrac ba$ and $rs=\dfrac ca$. Then}
\[r-s=\sqrt{(r-s)^2}=\sqrt{(r+s)^2-4rs}=\sqrt{\dfrac{b^2}{a^2}-\dfrac{4c}{a}}=\sqrt{\dfrac{b^2-4ac}{a^2}}=\dfrac{\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{a}.\]
\emph{Adding and subtracting the equations $r+s=-\dfrac ba$ and $r-s=\dfrac{\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{a}$ gives $r=\dfrac{-b+\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}$ and $s=\dfrac{-b-\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}$.}

Point out the gaps in this argument and give a corrected proof.

solution:
Join the midpoints of the three sides. This divides the triangle into $4$ equilateral triangles of side $6$ (closed regions, including their boundaries), which together cover the whole triangle.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(12,0),C=(6,6*sqrt(3));
pair Mab=(A+B)/2,Mbc=(B+C)/2,Mca=(C+A)/2;
fill(A--Mab--Mca--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1)); fill(Mab--B--Mbc--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.7)); fill(Mca--Mbc--C--cycle,rgb(0.85,1,0.85)); fill(Mab--Mbc--Mca--cycle,rgb(0.95,0.85,1));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(Mab--Mbc--Mca--cycle);
label("$6$",(A+Mab)/2,S); label("$6$",(Mab+B)/2,S);
label("$1$",(A+Mab+Mca)/3); label("$2$",(Mab+B+Mbc)/3); label("$3$",(Mca+Mbc+C)/3); label("$4$",(Mab+Mbc+Mca)/3);
\end{asy}

By the pigeonhole principle, two of the $5$ points lie in the same small triangle. Any two points $X$, $Y$ of a triangle of side $6$ are at distance at most $6$: for fixed $X$, the distance $XY$ is greatest when $Y$ is a vertex (the distance from $X$ increases along a straight line away from the foot of the perpendicular, so on each side it is largest at an endpoint), so $XY\le\max_V XV$; and likewise $XV$ is at most the largest distance between two vertices, which is $6$. Hence those two points are at most $6$ apart. $\blacksquare$

question:
A standard Catan board is made of $19$ regular hexagonal tiles arranged in the shape of a large hexagon with $3$ tiles along each edge (rows of $3,4,5,4,3$ tiles), as shown. How many tiles does such a hexagonal board with $N$ tiles along each edge have?

\begin{asy}
