solution:
If all lengths are multiplied by a factor $k$, all volumes are multiplied by $k^3$; with the same material, the weight is proportional to the volume.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair o=(0,0); real k=1;
path box(real w,real h,real d){ return (0,0)--(w,0)--(w,h)--(0,h)--cycle; }
void solid(pair o,real w,real h,real d){
  pair dz=(0.5*d,0.35*d);
  draw(shift(o)*box(w,h,d),linewidth(0.9));
  draw(o+(0,h)--o+(0,h)+dz--o+(w,h)+dz--o+(w,h),linewidth(0.9));
  draw(o+(w,0)--o+(w,0)+dz--o+(w,h)+dz,linewidth(0.9));
}
solid((0,0),3,4,2); solid((6.2,0),1.5,2,1);
draw((-0.4,0)--(-0.4,4),Arrows(4)); label("$4$ ft",(-0.4,2),W);
draw((5.8,0)--(5.8,2),Arrows(4)); label("$x$",(5.8,1),W);
label("$96$ lb",(1.5,-0.1),S); label("$12$ lb",(6.95,-0.1),S);
\end{asy}

Here the weight ratio is $\dfrac{12}{96}=\dfrac18$, so $k^3=\dfrac18$ and $k=\dfrac12$. Hence $x=4k=\boxed{2}$.

From each department the committee takes two professors: two men ($1$ way), two women ($1$ way), or one man and one woman ($2\cdot2=4$ ways). The three departments together must give $3$ men.
\begin{itemize}
\item Every department gives one man and one woman: $4^3=64$ committees.
\item One department gives two men, another two women and the third one of each: $3!=6$ ways to assign these roles, and $1\cdot1\cdot4$ choices of people, so $24$ committees.
\end{itemize}
(No other pattern gives exactly $3$ men: the numbers of men per department are $0$, $1$ or $2$ and add up to $3$, so they are $1,1,1$ or $0,1,2$ in some order.) In total there are $64+24=\boxed{88}$ committees.

question:
In the polygon shown, each side is perpendicular to its adjacent sides, and all $28$ of the sides are congruent. The perimeter of the polygon is $56$. What is the area of the region bounded by the polygon?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V={(0,0),(1,0),(1,-1),(2,-1),(2,-2),(3,-2),(3,-3),(4,-3),(4,-2),(5,-2),(5,-1),(6,-1),(6,0),(7,0),(7,1),(6,1),(6,2),(5,2),(5,3),(4,3),(4,4),(3,4),(3,3),(2,3),(2,2),(1,2),(1,1),(0,1)};
path p=V[0]; for(int k=1;k<V.length;++k) p=p--V[k]; p=p--cycle;
draw(p,linewidth(0.9));
\end{asy}

solution:
The $28$ congruent sides have total length $56$, so each side has length $\dfrac{56}{28}=2$.

The sides are horizontal and vertical segments of length $2$, so the horizontal and vertical lines through the vertices form a grid of squares of side $2$, and the region bounded by the polygon is made of whole squares of this grid. Counting them row by row from the top:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V={(0,0),(1,0),(1,-1),(2,-1),(2,-2),(3,-2),(3,-3),(4,-3),(4,-2),(5,-2),(5,-1),(6,-1),(6,0),(7,0),(7,1),(6,1),(6,2),(5,2),(5,3),(4,3),(4,4),(3,4),(3,3),(2,3),(2,2),(1,2),(1,1),(0,1)};
path p=V[0]; for(int k=1;k<V.length;++k) p=p--V[k]; p=p--cycle;
int[] lo={3,2,1,0,1,2,3}; int[] hi={3,4,5,6,5,4,3};
for(int i=0;i<7;++i){ int y=i-3; for(int x=lo[i];x<=hi[i];++x) filldraw(box((x,y),(x+1,y+1)),rgb(0.88,0.92,1),gray(0.55)+dashed);
  label("$"+string(hi[i]-lo[i]+1)+"$",(8.2,y+0.5)); }
draw(p,linewidth(1));
label("$2$",(0.5,0),S); label("$2$",(0,0.5),W);
label("squares",(8.2,4.3),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\[1+3+5+7+5+3+1=25\text{ squares}.\]
Each square has area $2^2=4$, so the region has area $25\cdot4=\boxed{100}$.

question:
A circle of radius $1$ is surrounded by $4$ circles of radius $r$ as shown: each of the four circles is tangent to the circle of radius $1$ and to two of the other three, and their centres form a square. What is $r$?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1+sqrt(2);
draw(circle((0,0),1),linewidth(0.9));
for(int k=0;k<4;++k) draw(circle((1+r)*dir(45+90k),r),linewidth(0.9));
dot((0,0)); draw((0,0)--(1,0)); label("$1$",(0.5,0),S);
pair c=(1+r)*dir(45); dot(c); draw(c--c+r*dir(45)); label("$r$",c+r/2*dir(45),NW);
\end{asy}

solution:
Let $O$ be the centre of the small circle and $P,Q,R,S$ the centres of the four circles, in order.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1+sqrt(2);
draw(circle((0,0),1),linewidth(0.9));
pair[] Cc; for(int k=0;k<4;++k){ Cc.push((1+r)*dir(45+90k)); draw(circle(Cc[k],r),gray); dot(Cc[k]); }
draw(Cc[0]--Cc[1]--Cc[2]--Cc[3]--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(Cc[0]--Cc[2],red+linewidth(0.9));
dot((0,0)); label("$O$",(0,0),SE);
