Tangent circles have centres at distance equal to the sum of the radii. So $PQ=2r$ (adjacent circles are tangent), and $OP=OR=1+r$, with $P$, $O$, $R$ on a line (the diagonal of the square $PQRS$ passes through its centre $O$). The diagonal of a square of side $2r$ is $2r\sqrt2$, so
\[2(1+r)=2r\sqrt2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad r\left(\sqrt2-1\right)=1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad r=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2-1}=\boxed{1+\sqrt2}.\]

\textbf{Construction.} Let $AB$ be the given segment, $AB=1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Extend $AB$ beyond $B$ and draw the circle with centre $B$ and radius $AB$; it meets the extension at $C$, so $BC=1$ and $AC=2$.
\item Erect the perpendicular to line $AC$ at $A$: the circle with centre $A$ and radius $AB$ meets line $AC$ again at a point $P$ on the other side of $A$, so $AP=AB$; the circles with centres $P$ and $B$ and radius $PB$ meet at a point $X$ above the line; draw line $AX$.
\item The circle with centre $A$ and radius $AB$ meets line $AX$ at $D$, so $AD=1$.
\item Draw the segment $CD$.
