If Harry has folded $a$ roses and $b$ lilies when the hour ends exactly, then $6a+8b=60$, i.e. $3a+4b=30$. Then $b$ is a multiple of $3$, and the solutions in nonnegative integers are
\[(a,b)=(10,0),\ (6,3),\ (2,6),\]
with $10$, $9$ and $8$ flowers.

The event ``he finishes a flower exactly at minute $60$'' happens when his first $a+b$ choices consist of $a$ roses and $b$ lilies for one of these pairs. A particular sequence of $n$ choices has probability $\left(\dfrac12\right)^n$, and different such sequences describe disjoint events (the finishing times of a sequence increase, so it cannot reach $60$ in two ways). So, in units of $\dfrac{1}{1024}$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $(10,0)$: $1$ sequence of length $10$: $1$;
\item $(6,3)$: $\dbinom93=84$ sequences of length $9$: $84\cdot2=168$;
\item $(2,6)$: $\dbinom82=28$ sequences of length $8$: $28\cdot4=112$.
\end{itemize}
The total is $\dfrac{281}{1024}$, and the first two cases ($9$ or $10$ flowers) give $\dfrac{169}{1024}$. Hence
\[P(\text{at least }9\text{ flowers}\mid\text{a flower ends at }60)=\boxed{\dfrac{169}{281}}\approx0.60.\]

The displacement is $(215-44,\,482-83)=(171,399)$. Since $171=3\cdot57$ and $399=7\cdot57$ with $\gcd(3,7)=1$, we have $\gcd(171,399)=57$.

The points of the segment are $(44+171t,\ 83+399t)$ for $0\le t\le1$. Such a point is a lattice point exactly when $171t$ and $399t$ are integers. If $t=\dfrac{k}{57}$ with $k$ an integer, then $171t=3k$ and $399t=7k$ are integers. Conversely, if both are integers then so is $399t-2\cdot171t=57t$, so $t=\dfrac{k}{57}$.

