question:
A flower has the shape of a regular hexagon with side length $\sqrt3$, with $6$ congruent petals around it, one on each side, as shown. Each petal is a circular segment: the region outside the hexagon bounded by a side of the hexagon and an arc of a circle through the two endpoints of that side. Adjacent petals are tangent to each other: the circles of two petals on adjacent sides are tangent at the common endpoint of those sides. Compute the area of the entire flower.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=sqrt(3);
pair[] V; for(int k=0;k<6;++k) V.push(s*dir(30+60k));
for(int k=0;k<6;++k){ pair c=2*dir(60k); filldraw(arc(c,1,60k-120,60k+120)--cycle,rgb(1,0.85,0.9),linewidth(0.9)); }
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
label("$\sqrt3$",(V[0]+V[1])/2,dir(240));
\end{asy}

solution:
Let $O$ be the centre of the hexagon, let $AB$ and $BC$ be two adjacent sides, and let $\omega_1$ and $\omega_2$ be the circles of the petals on $AB$ and $BC$, with centres $O_1$ and $O_2$. The petals are congruent, so $\omega_1$ and $\omega_2$ have the same radius $\rho$.

\textbf{Locating the centres.} The circles $\omega_1$ and $\omega_2$ are tangent at $B$, so $O_1$, $B$, $O_2$ lie on a line. The centres are not on the same side of $B$: otherwise both would be the point at distance $\rho$ from $B$ on the same ray, and the two circles would coincide. Hence $B$ is the midpoint of $O_1O_2$.

Since $O_1A=O_1B$, the centre $O_1$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$, which passes through $O$ (as $OA=OB$) and through the midpoint $M$ of $AB$; likewise $O_2$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $BC$. Put $O=(0,0)$, $B=\left(0,\sqrt3\right)$, $A=\left(\dfrac32,\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$, $C=\left(-\dfrac32,\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$. Then $M=\left(\dfrac34,\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{4}\right)$, the two bisectors are the lines $y=\sqrt3\,x$ and $y=-\sqrt3\,x$, and
\[O_1=\left(t,\sqrt3\,t\right),\qquad O_2=\left(u,-\sqrt3\,u\right).\]
The midpoint condition gives $t+u=0$ and $\sqrt3\,t-\sqrt3\,u=2\sqrt3$, so $t=1$, $u=-1$:
\[O_1=\left(1,\sqrt3\right),\qquad O_2=\left(-1,\sqrt3\right),\qquad\rho=O_1B=1,\]
and indeed $O_1A=\sqrt{\left(\dfrac12\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)^2}=1$. By symmetry every petal circle has radius $1$ and its centre at distance $2$ from $O$ on the ray through the midpoint of its side; adjacent centres are $2=1+1$ apart, so adjacent circles really are tangent, at the common vertex.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=sqrt(3);
pair[] V; for(int k=0;k<6;++k) V.push(s*dir(30+60k));
pair O=(0,0);
for(int k=0;k<6;++k){ pair c=2*dir(60k); filldraw(arc(c,1,60k-120,60k+120)--cycle,rgb(1,0.9,0.93),linewidth(0.6)); }
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
pair A=V[0],B=V[1],C=V[2],O1=2*dir(60),O2=2*dir(120),M=(A+B)/2;
draw(circle(O1,1),gray+dashed); draw(circle(O2,1),gray+dashed);
draw(O--O1,dashed); draw(O1--O2,red+linewidth(0.9)); draw(O1--A,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(M+0.15*dir(150)--M+0.15*dir(150)+0.15*dir(60)--M+0.15*dir(60));
dot(O);dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(O1,red);dot(O2,red);dot(M);
label("$O$",O,S); label("$A$",A,dir(-20)); label("$B$",B,N); label("$C$",C,dir(200)); label("$M$",M,dir(290));
label("$O_1$",O1,dir(45),red); label("$O_2$",O2,dir(210),red);
label("$1$",(O1+A)/2,E,blue); label("$1$",(O1+B)/2,N,red);
\end{asy}

\textbf{One petal.} The distance from $O$ to the side $AB$ is $OM=\dfrac32$, while $OO_1=2$; so $O_1$ lies outside the hexagon, at distance $\dfrac12$ beyond the line $AB$. The petal on $AB$ is the part of the disc of $\omega_1$ beyond the line $AB$, which contains the centre: it is the larger of the two pieces into which the chord $AB$ cuts the disc. In triangle $AO_1B$ we have $O_1A=O_1B=1$ and $AB=\sqrt3$, so by the law of cosines $\cos\angle AO_1B=\dfrac{1+1-3}{2}=-\dfrac12$ and $\angle AO_1B=120^\circ$. The smaller piece (inside the hexagon) is a $120^\circ$ sector minus triangle $AO_1B$:
\[\dfrac13\pi-\dfrac12\cdot1\cdot1\cdot\sin120^\circ=\dfrac\pi3-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4},\qquad\text{so one petal has area}\qquad\pi-\left(\dfrac\pi3-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\right)=\dfrac{2\pi}{3}+\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}.\]

\textbf{The flower.} Each petal lies outside the hexagon, beyond its own side; two adjacent petal discs only touch at a vertex, and the other petal discs are farther apart (centres at distance $2\sqrt3$ or $4$, more than $2$). So the hexagon and the six petals do not overlap. The hexagon consists of six equilateral triangles of side $\sqrt3$, with area $6\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot3=\dfrac{9\sqrt3}{2}$. The flower has area
\[\dfrac{9\sqrt3}{2}+6\left(\dfrac{2\pi}{3}+\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\right)=\boxed{4\pi+6\sqrt3}\approx22.96.\]
(If two adjacent petals were allowed to lie on one and the same circle, namely the circle through all six vertices, they would also meet smoothly at $B$; but then their circles coincide instead of being tangent, and the ``flower'' would just be a disc of area $3\pi$.)

Let the painting be $a\times b$. The framed picture is $(a+2)\times(b+2)$, so the frame has area
\[(a+2)(b+2)-ab=2a+2b+4.\]

\begin{asy}
