There are $9000$ integers from $1000$ to $9999$. Count the odd ones with distinct digits, choosing the digits in a convenient order:
\begin{itemize}
\item the units digit is odd: $5$ choices ($1,3,5,7,9$);
\item the thousands digit is not $0$ and differs from the units digit: $8$ choices;
\item the hundreds digit differs from those two: $8$ choices;
\item the tens digit differs from those three: $7$ choices.
\end{itemize}
That gives $5\cdot8\cdot8\cdot7=2240$ integers, so the probability is
\[\dfrac{2240}{9000}=\boxed{\dfrac{56}{225}}.\]

In the $3\times3\times3$ cube, the centre cube shows no faces, each of the $6$ face-centre cubes shows exactly $1$ face, each of the $12$ edge cubes shows $2$ faces and each of the $8$ corner cubes shows $3$ faces.

Only $7$ positions show at most one face, and at most one of them (the centre) shows none. So the $6$ white cubes show at least $0+1+1+1+1+1=5$ unit squares, and $5$ is achieved by putting one white cube in the centre and the other five in face centres.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair pr(real x, real y, real z){return (x+0.42*y, z+0.32*y);}
pen rd=rgb(0.93,0.45,0.42), wt=white;
void sq(pair a, pair b2, pair c, pair d, pen p){filldraw(a--b2--c--d--cycle,p,black+0.6);}
for(int i=0;i<3;++i) for(int k=0;k<3;++k){
  pen p=(i==1&&k==1)?wt:rd;
  sq(pr(i,0,k),pr(i+1,0,k),pr(i+1,0,k+1),pr(i,0,k+1),p);
}
for(int j=0;j<3;++j) for(int k=0;k<3;++k){
  pen p=(j==1&&k==1)?wt:rd;
  sq(pr(3,j,k),pr(3,j+1,k),pr(3,j+1,k+1),pr(3,j,k+1),p);
}
for(int i=0;i<3;++i) for(int j=0;j<3;++j){
  pen p=(i==1&&j==1)?wt:rd;
  sq(pr(i,j,3),pr(i+1,j,3),pr(i+1,j+1,3),pr(i,j+1,3),p);
}
draw(pr(0,0,0)--pr(3,0,0)--pr(3,3,0)--pr(3,3,3)--pr(0,3,3)--pr(0,0,3)--cycle,black+1.1);
\end{asy}

The whole surface consists of $6\cdot9=54$ unit squares, so the white fraction is $\boxed{\dfrac{5}{54}}$.

Triangle $BDC$ is isosceles with $BD=DC$, so its base angles are equal: $\angle DBC=\angle DCB=70^\circ$. Hence
\[\angle BDC=180^\circ-70^\circ-70^\circ=40^\circ.\]
Since $D$ lies on segment $AC$, the angles $\angle ADB$ and $\angle BDC$ are supplementary, so
\[\angle ADB=180^\circ-40^\circ=140^\circ.\]

