Each of the $2^4=16$ patterns of boys and girls is equally likely. Counting patterns:
\begin{itemize}
\item all boys: $1$, so probability $\dfrac{1}{16}$; all girls: also $\dfrac{1}{16}$;
\item two of each: $\dbinom42=6$, so $\dfrac{6}{16}=\dfrac38$;
\item three of one gender and one of the other: $\dbinom41+\dbinom43=8$, so $\dfrac{8}{16}=\dfrac12$.
\end{itemize}
The most likely outcome is $\boxed{\textbf{(D)}}$, three of one gender and one of the other.

question:
A straight one-mile stretch of highway, $40$ feet wide, is closed. Robert rides his bike on a path composed of semicircles: their diameters lie end to end along the centre line of the highway, they lie alternately on the two sides of the centre line, and each one just reaches an edge of the highway, as in the figure.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real w=40, r=20; int n=6;
fill((0,-w/2)--(n*w,-w/2)--(n*w,w/2)--(0,w/2)--cycle,rgb(0.9,0.9,0.9));
draw((0,-w/2)--(n*w,-w/2),linewidth(1)); draw((0,w/2)--(n*w,w/2),linewidth(1));
draw((0,0)--(n*w,0),dashed+gray);
for(int i=0;i<n;++i){
  pair c=(i*w+r,0);
  if(i%2==0) draw(arc(c,r,180,0),red+linewidth(1)); else draw(arc(c,r,180,360),red+linewidth(1));
}
draw((n*w+12,-w/2)--(n*w+12,w/2),Arrows(4)); label("$40$ ft",(n*w+12,0),E);
\end{asy}

If he rides at $5$ miles per hour, how many hours will it take to cover the one-mile stretch? (Note: $1$ mile $=5280$ feet.)

$\textbf{(A) }\dfrac{\pi}{11}\qquad\textbf{(B) }\dfrac{\pi}{10}\qquad\textbf{(C) }\dfrac{\pi}{5}\qquad\textbf{(D) }\dfrac{2\pi}{5}\qquad\textbf{(E) }\dfrac{2\pi}{3}$

solution:
Each semicircle reaches from the centre line to an edge, so its radius is half the width, $20$ feet, and its diameter, $40$ feet, lies along the centre line.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real w=40, r=20; int n=6;
fill((0,-w/2)--(n*w,-w/2)--(n*w,w/2)--(0,w/2)--cycle,rgb(0.9,0.9,0.9));
draw((0,-w/2)--(n*w,-w/2),linewidth(1)); draw((0,w/2)--(n*w,w/2),linewidth(1));
draw((0,0)--(n*w,0),dashed+gray);
for(int i=0;i<n;++i){
  pair c=(i*w+r,0);
  if(i%2==0) draw(arc(c,r,180,0),red+linewidth(1)); else draw(arc(c,r,180,360),red+linewidth(1));
}
draw((w+r,0)--(w+r,-r),blue,Arrow(4)); label("$20$",(w+r,-r/2),E,blue); draw((0,4)--(w,4),blue,Arrows(4)); label("$40$",(r,4),N,blue);
\end{asy}

The diameters cover the mile, so there are $\dfrac{5280}{40}=132$ semicircles, each of length $\pi\cdot20=20\pi$ feet. The path has length
\[132\cdot20\pi=2640\pi\text{ feet}=\dfrac{2640\pi}{5280}\text{ mile}=\dfrac{\pi}{2}\text{ mile},\]
and at $5$ miles per hour it takes $\dfrac{\pi}{2}\div5=\boxed{\textbf{(B) }\dfrac{\pi}{10}}$ hours.

The grid is $60$ squares long and $32$ squares wide.
\begin{itemize}
\item Horizontal toothpicks lie on $32+1=33$ horizontal lines, each made of $60$ toothpicks: $33\cdot60=1980$.
\item Vertical toothpicks lie on $60+1=61$ vertical lines, each made of $32$ toothpicks: $61\cdot32=1952$.
\end{itemize}
Altogether $1980+1952=\boxed{\textbf{(E) }3932}$ toothpicks are used.

The reflection $(x,y)\mapsto(16-x,y)$ sends the triangle $T$ with vertices $(6,5)$, $(8,-3)$, $(9,1)$ to the triangle $T'$ with vertices $(10,5)$, $(8,-3)$, $(7,1)$.

\textbf{Area of $T$.} By the shoelace formula,
\[[T]=\dfrac12\left|6(-3)-8\cdot5+8\cdot1-9(-3)+9\cdot5-6\cdot1\right|=\dfrac12|{-58}+35+39|=8,\]
and $[T']=8$ too.

\textbf{The overlap.} The sides of $T$ lie on the lines $y=-4x+29$ (through $(6,5)$ and $(8,-3)$), $y=4x-35$ (through $(8,-3)$ and $(9,1)$) and $y=-\dfrac43x+13$. Reflecting, the sides of $T'$ lie on $y=4x-35$, $y=-4x+29$ and $y=\dfrac43x-\dfrac{25}{3}$. So both triangles lie above the two lines through $(8,-3)$, and
\[T\cap T'=\left\{(x,y):\ y\ge-4x+29,\ y\ge4x-35,\ y\le-\dfrac43x+13,\ y\le\dfrac43x-\dfrac{25}{3}\right\}.\]
This is the quadrilateral with vertices $(8,-3)$, $(9,1)$ (where $y=4x-35$ meets $y=-\dfrac43x+13$), $\left(8,\dfrac73\right)$ (where the two top lines meet) and $(7,1)$. It is a kite with diagonals of lengths $\dfrac73+3=\dfrac{16}{3}$ and $2$, so its area is $\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac{16}{3}\cdot2=\dfrac{16}{3}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P1=(6,5), Q1=(8,-3), R1=(9,1);
pair P2=(10,5), R2=(7,1), K=(8,7/3);
fill(Q1--R1--K--R2--cycle,rgb(0.8,0.9,0.8));
draw(P1--Q1--R1--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(P2--Q1--R2--cycle,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw((8,-3)--(8,6.2),dashed); label("$x=8$",(8,6.2),N);
dot(P1); dot(Q1); dot(R1); dot(P2); dot(R2); dot(K);
label("$(6,5)$",P1,W,blue); label("$(9,1)$",R1,E,blue);
label("$(10,5)$",P2,E,red); label("$(7,1)$",R2,W,red);
label("$(8,-3)$",Q1,S); label("$\left(8,\dfrac{7}{3}\right)$",(5.6,2.33),W,fontsize(8pt)); draw((5.65,2.33)--(7.88,2.33),gray+0.4,Arrow(3));
\end{asy}

\textbf{The union} has area
\[8+8-\dfrac{16}{3}=\boxed{\textbf{(E) }\dfrac{32}{3}}.\]

Number the days of the period $1,\dots,365$, counting from today. Alice comes on the multiples of $3$, Beatrix on the multiples of $4$ and Claire on the multiples of $5$. All three come on the multiples of $60$, and there are $\left\lfloor\dfrac{365}{60}\right\rfloor=6$ of them.
\begin{itemize}
\item Alice and Beatrix: multiples of $12$, $\left\lfloor\dfrac{365}{12}\right\rfloor=30$, of which $30-6=24$ are not multiples of $5$;
\item Alice and Claire: multiples of $15$, $\left\lfloor\dfrac{365}{15}\right\rfloor=24$, of which $24-6=18$ are not multiples of $4$;
\item Beatrix and Claire: multiples of $20$, $\left\lfloor\dfrac{365}{20}\right\rfloor=18$, of which $18-6=12$ are not multiples of $3$.
\end{itemize}
Exactly two friends come on $24+18+12=\boxed{\textbf{(B) }54}$ days.

By the shoelace formula,
\[[ABCD]=\dfrac12\left|(0\cdot2-1\cdot0)+(1\cdot3-3\cdot2)+(3\cdot0-4\cdot3)+(4\cdot0-0\cdot0)\right|=\dfrac12|0-3-12+0|=\dfrac{15}{2}.\]
Let the line meet $\overline{CD}$ at $P=(x,y)$. The piece $APD$ is a triangle with base $AD=4$ on the $x$-axis and height $y$, so it has half the area exactly when
\[\dfrac12\cdot4\cdot y=\dfrac{15}{4}\iff y=\dfrac{15}{8}.\]
The line $CD$ is $y=-3(x-4)$, i.e. $x=4-\dfrac{y}{3}$, so $x=4-\dfrac58=\dfrac{27}{8}$, and $P=\left(\dfrac{27}{8},\dfrac{15}{8}\right)$ (between $C$ and $D$, as $0<\dfrac{15}{8}<3$).

\begin{asy}
