question:
Let $f(x)=x^x$ for $x>0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to0^+}x^x=1$.
\item[(b)] Find the minimum value of $f$ on $(0,\infty)$. Is it true that, as $x$ decreases from $1$ towards $0$, the values $x^x$ first get smaller and then get larger than $1$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Write $x^x=e^{x\ln x}$.

\textbf{(a)} For $0<x<1$ put $x=\dfrac1t$ with $t>1$; then $x\ln x=-\dfrac{\ln t}{t}$. Using $\ln u\le u-1$ for $u>0$ with $u=\sqrt t$,
\[0<\ln t=2\ln\sqrt t\le2\left(\sqrt t-1\right)<2\sqrt t,\qquad\text{so}\qquad0<\dfrac{\ln t}{t}<\dfrac{2}{\sqrt t}.\]
As $x\to0^+$, $t\to\infty$ and $\dfrac{2}{\sqrt t}\to0$, so $x\ln x\to0$. Since $e^u$ is continuous, $x^x=e^{x\ln x}\to e^0=1$.

\textbf{(b)} The exponent $g(x)=x\ln x$ has $g'(x)=\ln x+1$, which is negative for $x<\dfrac1e$ and positive for $x>\dfrac1e$. Since $e^u$ is increasing, $x^x$ decreases on $\left(0,\dfrac1e\right]$ and increases on $\left[\dfrac1e,\infty\right)$, so its minimum is
\[f\left(\dfrac1e\right)=\boxed{e^{-\dfrac{1}{e}}}\approx0.6922.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return exp(x*log(x));}
draw((0,0)--(1.35,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,0)--(0,1.35),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(1.35,0),E); label("$y$",(0,1.35),N);
draw(graph(f,0.0005,1.25,400),blue+linewidth(1));
draw((0,1)--(1.3,1),dashed+gray); label("$1$",(0,1),W);
real m=exp(-1); pair M=(m,f(m)); dot(M); draw(M--(m,0),dotted);
label("$\dfrac{1}{e}$",(m,0),S); label("$e^{-\dfrac{1}{e}}$",M+(0.04,-0.02),SE,fontsize(8pt));
filldraw(circle((0,1),0.018),white,blue); dot((1,1)); label("$y=x^x$",(1.25,f(1.25)),E,blue);
\end{asy}

So as $x$ decreases from $1$ to $0$, $x^x$ first decreases from $1$ to $e^{-\dfrac1e}$ (reached at $x=\dfrac1e\approx0.368$) and then increases back towards $1$. But it never becomes larger than $1$: for $0<x<1$ we have $x\ln x<0$, so $x^x<1$. The values only approach $1$ from below.

(The limit does not give $0^0$ a value by itself: for instance $0^x=0$ for every $x>0$, while $x^0=1$. The value $0^0=1$ is a convention.)

Since $1337=7\cdot191$ with $7$ and $191$ prime, a number is leet exactly when it is divisible by $7$ or by $191$.

A $3$-digit palindrome is $\overline{aba}=101a+10b$ with $1\le a\le9$, $0\le b\le9$. Since $101=98+3$ and $10=7+3$,
\[101a+10b\equiv3a+3b=3(a+b)\pmod7,\]
so $7\mid\overline{aba}$ exactly when $7\mid a+b$, i.e. $a+b=7$ or $a+b=14$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $a+b=7$: $a=1,\dots,7$, giving $7$ palindromes ($161,252,\dots,707$);
\item $a+b=14$: $a=5,\dots,9$ (so that $b\le9$), giving $5$ palindromes ($595,686,777,868,959$).
\end{itemize}
The $3$-digit multiples of $191$ are $191,382,573,764,955$, and only $191$ is a palindrome; it is not a multiple of $7$.

So there are $12+1=\boxed{13}$ leet $3$-digit palindromes.

