solution:
Each jump lands on a square not visited before, and there are only $15$ squares other than the starting one, so the frog makes at most $15$ jumps.

Fifteen jumps are possible. Number the rows $1$ to $4$ from the top and the columns $1$ to $4$ from the left, and start at (row $1$, column $1$). The route
\[(1,1)\to(1,3)\to(3,3)\to(3,1)\to(3,4)\to(1,4)\to(4,4)\to(4,1)\to(4,3)\to(2,3)\to(2,1)\to(2,4)\to(2,2)\to(4,2)\to(1,2)\to(3,2)\]
moves along a row or a column every time, always over at least one square (each jump changes a coordinate by $2$ or $3$), and visits all $16$ squares. In the grid below each square shows the jump after which the frog lands on it.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int[][] ord={{0,14,1,5},{10,12,9,11},{3,15,2,4},{7,13,8,6}};
fill(box((0,3),(1,4)),rgb(0.85,0.95,0.85));
for(int i=0;i<=4;++i){draw((0,i)--(4,i)); draw((i,0)--(i,4));}
for(int r=0;r<4;++r) for(int c=0;c<4;++c) label("$"+string(ord[r][c])+"$",(c+0.5,3.5-r));
\end{asy}

The maximum number of jumps is $\boxed{15}$.

question:
Let $\varphi(m)$ denote the number of positive integers less than or equal to $m$ that are relatively prime to $m$, let $x!!$ denote the double factorial and $\lfloor x\rfloor$ the greatest integer less than or equal to $x$. For positive integers $x$ define
\begin{align*}
f(x)&=29^{5924x^3+|\lfloor x!!\rfloor|!}+\dfrac{52+29429|\varphi(x)|}{1728-|\varphi(x)^3|}+\varphi(x+24)+52x^4+26x^3-34x^2+18x+1,\\
g(x)&=29^{5924x^3+|\lfloor x!!\rfloor|!}+\dfrac{52+29429|\varphi(x)|}{1728-|\varphi(x)^3|}+\varphi(|x|+24)+52x^4+25x^3+17x^2-668x+1177,
\end{align*}
wherever these expressions are defined. Find the largest $x$ at which the graphs of $f$ and $g$ intersect.

solution:
A two-digit number has at least $2$ factors, so $n\ge2$ and $A+n\ge12$. Check the smallest two-digit numbers:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|cccccc}
$A$ & $10$ & $11$ & $12$ & $13$ & $14$ & $15$\\ \hline
$n$ & $4$ & $2$ & $6$ & $2$ & $4$ & $4$\\
digit sum & $1$ & $2$ & $3$ & $4$ & $5$ & $6$\\
$10-n$ & $6$ & $8$ & $4$ & $8$ & $6$ & $6$
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The first match is $A=15$ (factors $1,3,5,15$, so $n=4$, and $1+5=6=10-4$), giving $A+n=19$. Any $A$ with $A+n<19$ has $A\le16$, and $A=16$ ($n=5$, $A+n=21$) and $A\le14$ fail. So the least value is $\boxed{19}$. (It is also attained by $A=17$: $n=2$ and $1+7=8$.)

