solution:
Since the pyramids are similar and corresponding altitudes are in the ratio $1:5$, every length of $B$ is $5$ times the corresponding length of $A$ (scale factor $5$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair pr(triple t){return (t.x+0.4*t.y, t.z+0.25*t.y);}
void pyramid(real s, real h, pair sh, pen p){
  triple A=(0,0,0), B=(s,0,0), C=(s,s,0), D=(0,s,0), T=(s/2,s/2,h);
  draw(shift(sh)*(pr(A)--pr(B)--pr(C)),p); draw(shift(sh)*(pr(C)--pr(D)--pr(A)),p+dashed);
  draw(shift(sh)*(pr(A)--pr(T)),p); draw(shift(sh)*(pr(B)--pr(T)),p); draw(shift(sh)*(pr(C)--pr(T)),p); draw(shift(sh)*(pr(D)--pr(T)),p+dashed);
  draw(shift(sh)*(pr(T)--pr((s/2,s/2,0))),gray+dotted);
}
pyramid(1,1.2,(0,0),blue); pyramid(5,6,(2.5,0),red);
label("$A$",(0.6,-0.4),blue); draw((-0.3,0.125)--(-0.3,1.325),blue,Arrows(3)); label("$h$",(-0.3,0.725),W,blue);
label("$B$",(5.2,-0.4),red); draw((2.1,0.625)--(2.1,6.625),red,Arrows(4)); label("$5h$",(2.1,3.6),W,red);
\end{asy}

Areas scale by the square of the scale factor, so every face of $B$ has $25$ times the area of the corresponding face of $A$, and the surface area of $B$ is $25$ times that of $A$. Hence the surface area of $A$ is $\dfrac{1}{25}$ of that of $B$, and $x=\boxed{\dfrac{1}{25}}$.

Count the odd numbers by their number of digits:
\begin{itemize}
\item one digit: $1,3,5,7,9$, so $5$ numbers and $5$ digits;
\item two digits: $11$ to $99$, so $45$ numbers and $90$ digits;
\item three digits: $101$ to $999$, so $450$ numbers and $1350$ digits;
