solution:
In the isosceles triangle $TUV$ the base angles are $\angle TUV=\angle TVU=90^\circ-\dfrac a2$. The rectangle has $\angle VUP=90^\circ$. So the interior angle of the pentagon at the shoulder $U$ is
\[\angle TUP=\angle TUV+\angle VUP=\left(90^\circ-\dfrac a2\right)+90^\circ=180^\circ-\dfrac a2.\]
Reflection in $TU$ preserves angles, so the second pentagon also has $\angle TUP'=180^\circ-\dfrac a2$, on the other side of $TU$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=40; real w=1, L=3.2;
real h=w/Tan(a/2);
pair T=(0,0), U=rotate(-a/2)*(h/Cos(a/2)*(1,0)), V=rotate(a/2)*(h/Cos(a/2)*(1,0));
// pentagon 1: tip T, shoulders U,V, rectangle U,P,Q,V with long sides along the axis
pair ax=(1,0), P=U+L*ax, Q=V+L*ax;
path p1=T--U--P--Q--V--cycle;
transform rf=reflect(T,U);
path p2=rf*p1;
fill(p1,rgb(1,0.92,0.6)); fill(p2,rgb(0.75,0.88,1));
draw(p1,linewidth(0.9)); draw(p2,linewidth(0.9));
pair P2=rf*P;
dot(T); dot(U); dot(V); dot(P); dot(P2);
label("$T$",T,W); label("$U$",U,dir(95)); label("$V$",V,N); label("$P$",P,E); label("$P'$",P2,S);
draw(arc(T,0.6,-a/2,a/2)); label("$a$",T+0.75*dir(0),fontsize(8pt));
real l3=h/Cos(a/2);
draw(U--U+l3*dir(0),red+dashed); draw(U--U+l3*dir(-a),red+dashed); draw(U+l3*dir(0)--U+l3*dir(-a),red+dashed);
draw(arc(U,0.45,-a,0),red); label("$a$",U+0.62*dir(-a/2),red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The three angles $\angle TUP$, $\angle TUP'$ and $\angle PUP'$ fill the full angle around $U$, so
\[\angle PUP'=360^\circ-2\left(180^\circ-\dfrac a2\right)=a.\]
A third pencil's tip has apex angle $a$; placing its apex at $U$ with its slanted sides along $UP$ and $UP'$, it fills this angle exactly. $\blacksquare$

(This is the poster's angle count: two angles $90^\circ-\dfrac a2$, two right angles and the desired angle add up to $360^\circ$. For real pencils the argument is applied in the plane through the axes of the two pencils.)

By linearity of expectation, each section's expected score is $800-10E[n]$.
\begin{itemize}
\item English: $E[n]=\dfrac{0+1+2+3+4+5}{6}=\dfrac52$, so the expected score is $800-25=775$.
\item Math: $E[n]=\dfrac16$, so the expected score is $800-\dfrac{10}{6}=\dfrac{2395}{3}$.
\end{itemize}
The expected overall score is $775+\dfrac{2395}{3}=\boxed{\dfrac{4720}{3}}\approx1573.3$.

Write $[\cdot]$ for area and $T=[ABC]$. The ratios give $AZ=\dfrac78AB$, $ZB=\dfrac18AB$, $BX=\dfrac78BC$, $XC=\dfrac18BC$, $CY=\dfrac78CA$, $YA=\dfrac18CA$.

