Two triangles sharing an angle have areas in the ratio of the products of the sides enclosing that angle. So
\[[AZY]=\dfrac{AZ}{AB}\cdot\dfrac{AY}{AC}\,T=\dfrac78\cdot\dfrac18\,T=\dfrac{7}{64}T,\]
and likewise $[BXZ]=\dfrac78\cdot\dfrac18T=\dfrac{7}{64}T$ and $[CYX]=\dfrac78\cdot\dfrac18T=\dfrac{7}{64}T$. Removing these three corner triangles leaves $XYZ$:
\[\dfrac{[XYZ]}{[ABC]}=1-3\cdot\dfrac{7}{64}=\boxed{\dfrac{43}{64}}.\]

Take the cross-section through the axis of the cone. Let $V$ be the vertex and $\theta$ the half-angle of the cone. A ball of radius $r$ tangent to the sides has its centre on the axis at distance $d=\dfrac{r}{\sin\theta}$ from $V$ (the radius to the point of tangency is perpendicular to the side).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=1/3, c=sqrt(8)/3; real t=s/c;   // half-angle theta with sin=1/3
pair O=(0,0);
real H=8;
draw(O--(H*t,H)); draw(O--(-H*t,H)); draw((-H*t,H)--(H*t,H),linewidth(0.9));
real[] r={0.5,1,2};
for(int k=0;k<3;++k){ real d=3*r[k]; draw(circle((0,d),r[k]),blue); dot((0,d),blue); }
draw((0,0)--(0,8),dashed+gray);
pair P2=(0,6), T2=(0,6)+2*dir(180+asin(1/3)*180/pi);
draw(P2--T2,red); label("$2$",(P2+T2)/2,N,red+fontsize(8pt));
draw((3.3,0)--(3.3,6),Arrows(3)); label("$6$",(3.3,3),E,fontsize(8pt)); draw((3.3,6)--(3.3,8),Arrows(3)); label("$2$",(3.3,7),E,fontsize(8pt));
draw((0,6)--(3.4,6),dotted); draw((0,0)--(3.4,0),dotted);
draw(arc(O,0.75,90,90+asin(1/3)*180/pi)); label("$\theta$",O+0.8*dir(90+asin(1/3)*180/pi),W,fontsize(8pt));
label("$V$",O,S); label("rim",(-H*t,H),W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{The angle.} For consecutive scoops of radii $r$ and $2r$ that touch, the distance between the centres is $r+2r=3r$, and it is also $\dfrac{2r}{\sin\theta}-\dfrac{r}{\sin\theta}=\dfrac{r}{\sin\theta}$. So $\sin\theta=\dfrac13$.

\textbf{The height.} The largest scoop has radius $2$, so its centre is $\dfrac{2}{\sin\theta}=6$ inches above $V$, and its top is $6+2=8$ inches above $V$. (It touches the sides at height $6\cos^2\theta=\dfrac{16}{3}<8$, so inside the cone.) Since the top of this scoop is level with the rim, the height of the cone is $\boxed{8\text{ inches}}$.

The diameter is $FE=9+16+9=34$, so the radius is $17$, and the centre $O$ is the midpoint of $FE$, which is also the midpoint of $DA$. So $OA=8$, and in the right triangle $OAB$,
\[AB=\sqrt{OB^2-OA^2}=\sqrt{17^2-8^2}=15.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0), F=(-17,0), Ep=(17,0), D=(-8,0), A=(8,0), B=(8,15), C=(-8,15);
fill(arc(O,17,0,180)--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.9,1)); fill(A--B--C--D--cycle,white);
draw(arc(O,17,0,180)--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(O); dot(F); dot(Ep); dot(D); dot(A); dot(B); dot(C);
label("$F$",F,S); label("$E$",Ep,S); label("$D$",D,S); label("$A$",A,S); label("$B$",B,NE); label("$C$",C,NW); label("$O$",O,S);
draw(O--B,dashed); label("$17$",(4,7.5),E,fontsize(8pt)); label("$15$",(8,7.5),E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$9$",(-12.5,0),S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$9$",(12.5,0),S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$8$",(4,0),S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The rectangle has area $16\cdot15=240$ and the semicircle has area $\dfrac12\pi\cdot17^2=\dfrac{289\pi}{2}$. The required area is
\[\boxed{\dfrac{289\pi}{2}-240}\approx214.0.\]

question:
Rectangle $ABCD$ is inscribed in a semicircle with diameter $\overline{FE}$: the side $DA$ lies on $\overline{FE}$ (with $F$, $D$, $A$, $E$ in this order) and $B$, $C$ lie on the arc. Let $DA=16$ and $FD=AE=9$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the area of the region bounded by $\overline{BC}$ and the arc of the semicircle?
\item[(b)] What is the area of the region bounded by $\overline{AB}$, $\overline{AE}$ and the arc of the semicircle?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
As in the computation of the rectangle, the radius is $17$, the centre $O$ is the midpoint of $DA$, $OA=8$ and $AB=15$. Put $O=(0,0)$, so $A=(8,0)$, $B=(8,15)$, $C=(-8,15)$, $E=(17,0)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0), F=(-17,0), Ep=(17,0), D=(-8,0), A=(8,0), B=(8,15), C=(-8,15);
real al=aSin(8/17), be=aCos(8/17);
fill(arc(O,17,90-al,90+al)--cycle,rgb(1,0.85,0.6));
fill(A--Ep--arc(O,17,0,be)--cycle,rgb(0.8,0.92,0.8));
draw(arc(O,17,0,180)--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--B,dashed); draw(O--C,dashed);
dot(O); dot(Ep); dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D);
label("$F$",F,S); label("$E$",Ep,S); label("$D$",D,S); label("$A$",A,S); label("$B$",B,NE); label("$C$",C,NW); label("$O$",O,S);
draw(arc(O,3,be,180-be),red); label("$2\alpha$",(0,3),N,red+fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc(O,4.2,0,be),heavygreen); label("$\beta$",4.4*dir(be/2),E,heavygreen+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} Let $\angle BOC=2\alpha$; the chord $BC=16$ has half-length $8$, so $\sin\alpha=\dfrac{8}{17}$ and $\cos\alpha=\dfrac{15}{17}$. The region is a circular segment: the sector $BOC$ minus triangle $BOC$:
\[\dfrac12\cdot17^2\cdot2\alpha-\dfrac12\cdot16\cdot15=\boxed{289\arcsin\dfrac{8}{17}-120}\approx21.6.\]
\textbf{(b)} Let $\beta=\angle EOB$, so $\cos\beta=\dfrac{8}{17}$. The region is the sector $EOB$ minus triangle $OAB$:
\[\dfrac12\cdot17^2\cdot\beta-\dfrac12\cdot8\cdot15=\boxed{\dfrac{289}{2}\arccos\dfrac{8}{17}-60}\approx96.2.\]
(Check: since $2\alpha+2\beta=180^\circ$, the region in (a) plus twice the region in (b) is $289\cdot\dfrac{\pi}{2}-240$, the part of the semicircle outside the rectangle.)

An equilateral triangle with side $s$ has height $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s$. Here the height is $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\cdot\dfrac{4\sqrt3}{3}=2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(4/sqrt(3),0), C=rotate(60)*B;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); pair M=(A+B)/2; draw(C--M,dashed);
label("$\dfrac{4\sqrt3}{3}$",M,S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$2$",(C+M)/2,E,fontsize(8pt));
draw(M--M+(0,0.15)--M+(0.15,0.15)--M+(0.15,0));
\end{asy}

So the area is $\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac{4\sqrt3}{3}\cdot2=\boxed{\dfrac{4\sqrt3}{3}}$. (Equivalently $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot\dfrac{16}{3}$.)

Put the hexagon's centre at the origin with side $1$: $A=(1,0)$, $B=\left(\dfrac12,\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$, $C=\left(-\dfrac12,\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$, $D=(-1,0)$, $E=\left(-\dfrac12,-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$, $F=\left(\dfrac12,-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$.

The configuration is unchanged by a rotation of $60^\circ$ about the centre (which swaps the two triangles), so the overlap is a regular hexagon with the same centre. One of its sides lies on side $CE$ (the line $x=-\dfrac12$) of triangle $ACE$, between the points where $BD$ and $DF$ cross it. The segment $BD$ consists of the points $\left(\dfrac12-\dfrac32u,\ \dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}(1-u)\right)$, $0\le u\le1$, which has $x=-\dfrac12$ at $u=\dfrac23$, i.e. at $X=\left(-\dfrac12,\dfrac{\sqrt3}{6}\right)$; by symmetry $DF$ crosses it at $Y=\left(-\dfrac12,-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{6}\right)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V; for(int k=0;k<6;++k) V.push(dir(60k));
string[] nm={"$A$","$B$","$C$","$D$","$E$","$F$"};
pair[] Wv; for(int k=0;k<6;++k) Wv.push((1/sqrt(3))*dir(60k+30));
fill(Wv[0]--Wv[1]--Wv[2]--Wv[3]--Wv[4]--Wv[5]--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.9,1));
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(V[0]--V[2]--V[4]--cycle,red); draw(V[1]--V[3]--V[5]--cycle,blue);
for(int k=0;k<6;++k){dot(V[k]); label(nm[k],V[k],dir(60k));}
dot(Wv[2]); dot(Wv[3]); label("$X$",Wv[2],1.3*dir(-30)); label("$Y$",Wv[3],1.3*dir(30));
\end{asy}

So the inner hexagon has side $XY=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{3}$, while $ABCDEF$ has side $1$. Regular hexagons are similar, so the area ratio is the square of the ratio of sides:
\[\dfrac{[A'B'C'D'E'F']}{[ABCDEF]}=\left(\dfrac{\sqrt3}{3}\right)^2=\boxed{\dfrac13}.\]

The triangle with $CA=15$, $CB=13$ and the one with $CA=13$, $CB=15$ are mirror images (swap $A$ and $B$), so the ratio is the same; take $CA=15$, $CB=13$.

\textbf{Coordinates.} Put $A=(0,0)$, $B=(14,0)$ and $C=(x,y)$ with $y>0$. From $x^2+y^2=225$ and $(x-14)^2+y^2=169$ we get $28x=252$, so $x=9$ and $y=12$. The area is $\dfrac12\cdot14\cdot12=84$ and the semiperimeter is $21$, so the inradius is $\dfrac{84}{21}=4$. The tangent length from $A$ is $s-BC=21-13=8$, so $E=(8,0)$, $I=(8,4)$ and $H=(8,8)$. Also $F=(7,0)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(14,0), C=(9,12), I=(8,4), Ep=(8,0), H=(8,8), Fm=(7,0), G=(8,6);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(circle(I,4),blue);
draw(C--Fm,red); draw(Ep--H,heavygreen+linewidth(0.9));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(I); dot(Ep); dot(H); dot(Fm); dot(G);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N); label("$I$",I,E); label("$E$",Ep,SE); label("$F$",Fm,SW); label("$H$",H,W); label("$G$",G,E);
label("$15$",(A+C)/2,NW); label("$13$",(B+C)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

\textbf{The intersection.} $EH$ is the line $x=8$. The line $CF$ passes through $F=(7,0)$ and $C=(9,12)$: $y=6(x-7)$, which gives $y=6$ at $x=8$. So $G=(8,6)$, and
\[\dfrac{HG}{GE}=\dfrac{8-6}{6-0}=\boxed{\dfrac13}.\]

\textbf{Angles of the contact triangle.} Tangent segments from a point to a circle are equal, so $BD=BF$ and $CD=CE$. Triangle $BDF$ is isosceles, so $\angle BDF=90^\circ-\dfrac{B}{2}$, and likewise $\angle CDE=90^\circ-\dfrac C2$. Hence
\[\angle EDF=180^\circ-\left(90^\circ-\dfrac B2\right)-\left(90^\circ-\dfrac C2\right)=\dfrac{B+C}{2}=90^\circ-\dfrac A2,\]
and in the same way $\angle DEF=90^\circ-\dfrac B2$ and $\angle EFD=90^\circ-\dfrac C2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(7,0), C=(2,5);
real a=abs(B-C), bb=abs(C-A), c=abs(A-B), s=(a+bb+c)/2;
pair I=(a*A+bb*B+c*C)/(a+bb+c); real r=sqrt((s-a)*(s-bb)*(s-c)/s);
pair D=B+(s-bb)*unit(C-B), Ep=C+(s-c)*unit(A-C), Fp=A+(s-a)*unit(B-A);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(circle(I,r),blue); draw(D--Ep--Fp--cycle,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--I,dashed+gray);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(Ep); dot(Fp); dot(I);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N); label("$D$",D,NE); label("$E$",Ep,NW); label("$F$",Fp,S); label("$I$",I,W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{The triangle is equilateral.} The similarity gives $A=\angle EDF=90^\circ-\dfrac A2$, so $A=60^\circ$; likewise $B=C=60^\circ$. (Even without the order of the vertices this holds: if $A\le B\le C$, the angles of $DEF$ in increasing order are $90^\circ-\dfrac C2\le90^\circ-\dfrac B2\le90^\circ-\dfrac A2$, so $A=90^\circ-\dfrac C2$ and $C=90^\circ-\dfrac A2$; subtracting gives $A-C=\dfrac{A-C}{2}$, so $A=C$ and all angles are $60^\circ$.)

\textbf{The length.} In an equilateral triangle the incircle touches the sides at their midpoints, so $DEF$ is the medial triangle and $DF=\dfrac12AC=\dfrac12\cdot14=\boxed{7}$.

Extend $BD$ beyond $D$ to meet the circle again at $G$. Since $D$ is inside the circle and $C$ is between $B$ and $D$, the line meets the circle at $C$ and $G$ with $B$, $C$, $D$, $G$ in this order, and $BD=6$.

\textbf{Power of $B$:} $BA^2=BC\cdot BG$, so $36=3\cdot BG$ and $BG=12$. Hence $DG=BG-BD=6$.

\textbf{Power of $D$:} for a point inside a circle of radius $r$, $DC\cdot DG=r^2-OD^2$ (draw the chord through $D$ perpendicular to $OD$, or use the diameter through $D$). So
\[3\cdot6=r^2-4\iff r^2=22,\]
and the radius is $\boxed{\sqrt{22}}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=sqrt(22); pair O=(0,0);
real k=sqrt(22-20.25);
pair C=(-4.5,-k), G=(4.5,-k), D=(-1.5,-k), B=(-7.5,-k);
pair[] T=intersectionpoints(circle(O,r),circle(B,6));
pair A=(T[0].y>T[1].y)?T[0]:T[1];
draw(circle(O,r),linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--G,linewidth(0.9)); draw(B--A,blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(O--A,dashed); draw(O--D,red);
dot(O); dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(G);
label("$O$",O,E); label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$D$",D,S); label("$G$",G,SE);
label("$6$",(A+B)/2,NW,blue); label("$3$",(B+C)/2,S); label("$3$",(C+D)/2,S); label("$6$",(D+G)/2,S); label("$2$",(O+D)/2,SE,red);
\end{asy}

(Such a configuration exists: with $r=\sqrt{22}$ the chord $CG=9$ is at distance $\sqrt{22-4.5^2}=\dfrac{\sqrt7}{2}$ from $O$, and $D$, the point of the chord $1.5$ from its midpoint, has $OD^2=\dfrac74+\dfrac94=4$. Then $OB^2=\dfrac74+7.5^2=58$, and the tangent length is $\sqrt{58-22}=6$, as required.)

question:
Let $p(x)=a_0x^n+a_1x^{n-1}+\cdots+a_n$ be a polynomial, and let $C$ be the graph of $y=p(x)$. Find an equation of the image of $C$ under
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] reflection in the $x$-axis;
\item[(b)] reflection in the $y$-axis;
\item[(c)] rotation by $180^\circ$ about the origin;
\item[(d)] rotation by $90^\circ$ counterclockwise about the origin.
\end{enumerate}
Illustrate with $p(x)=x^2-2x$.

solution:
A point $(X,Y)$ lies on the image exactly when the point it came from lies on $C$, i.e. satisfies $y=p(x)$.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] Reflection in the $x$-axis sends $(x,y)\mapsto(x,-y)$, so $(X,Y)$ comes from $(X,-Y)$: the image is $-Y=p(X)$, i.e. $\boxed{y=-p(x)}$ (every coefficient changes sign).
\item[(b)] Reflection in the $y$-axis sends $(x,y)\mapsto(-x,y)$: the image is $\boxed{y=p(-x)}$. In $p(-x)$ the coefficients of the odd powers of $x$ change sign and the others stay.
\item[(c)] The half-turn sends $(x,y)\mapsto(-x,-y)$: the image is $-Y=p(-X)$, i.e. $\boxed{y=-p(-x)}$ (the coefficients of the even powers, including the constant, change sign).
\item[(d)] The quarter-turn sends $(x,y)\mapsto(-y,x)$, so $(X,Y)$ comes from $(Y,-X)$: the image is $-X=p(Y)$, i.e. $\boxed{x=-p(y)}$. It is in general not the graph of a function of $x$.
\end{itemize}
For $p(x)=x^2-2x$: (a) $y=-x^2+2x$; (b) $y=x^2+2x$; (c) $y=-x^2-2x$; (d) $x=-y^2+2y$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real p(real x){return x^2-2*x;}
draw((-3.6,0)--(3.6,0),Arrow(5)); draw((0,-3.2)--(0,3.6),Arrow(5));
label("$x$",(3.6,0),E); label("$y$",(0,3.6),N);
draw(graph(p,-0.9,2.9),blue+linewidth(1));
draw(graph(new real(real x){return -p(x);},-0.9,2.9),red+linewidth(1));
draw(graph(new real(real x){return p(-x);},-2.9,0.9),heavygreen+linewidth(1));
label("$y=x^2-2x$",(2.9,p(2.9)),E,blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$y=-x^2+2x$",(2.9,-p(2.9)),E,red+fontsize(8pt)); label("$y=x^2+2x$",(-2.9,p(2.9)),W,heavygreen+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real p(real x){return x^2-2*x;}
draw((-3.6,0)--(3.6,0),Arrow(5)); draw((0,-3.6)--(0,3.6),Arrow(5));
label("$x$",(3.6,0),E); label("$y$",(0,3.6),N);
draw(graph(p,-0.9,2.9),blue+linewidth(1));
draw(graph(new real(real x){return -p(-x);},-2.9,0.9),red+linewidth(1));
path q=graph(p,-0.9,2.9); draw(rotate(90)*q,heavygreen+linewidth(1));
label("$y=x^2-2x$",(2.9,p(2.9)),E,blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$y=-x^2-2x$",(-2.9,-p(2.9)),W,red+fontsize(8pt)); label("$x=-y^2+2y$",rotate(90)*(2.9,p(2.9)),W,heavygreen+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Let $O$ be the centre and $r=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{3}\approx0.577$. Each side of the square is at distance $\dfrac12<r$ from $O$, and the half-diagonal is $\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}>r$, so the circle crosses every side and the corners are outside the circle. The region inside the circle and outside the square consists of four congruent circular segments, one beyond each side.

Consider the right side, met by the circle at $P$ and $Q$. The foot of the perpendicular from $O$ to that side is at distance $\dfrac12$, so $\cos\dfrac{\angle POQ}{2}=\dfrac{1}{2r}=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$, hence $\angle POQ=60^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=sqrt(3)/3;
pair O=(0,0);
fill(circle(O,r),rgb(1,0.85,0.6)); fill(box((-0.5,-0.5),(0.5,0.5)),white);
draw(circle(O,r),linewidth(0.9)); draw(box((-0.5,-0.5),(0.5,0.5)),linewidth(0.9));
real h=sqrt(r^2-0.25);
pair P=(0.5,h), Q=(0.5,-h);
draw(O--P,dashed); draw(O--Q,dashed); draw((0.3,0)--(0.5,0),dotted);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,W); label("$P$",P,NE); label("$Q$",Q,SE);
draw(arc(O,0.1,-30,30)); label("$60^\circ$",O+0.11*dir(0),E,fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

One segment has area
\[\dfrac12r^2\left(\dfrac{\pi}{3}-\sin60^\circ\right)=\dfrac16\left(\dfrac{\pi}{3}-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)=\dfrac{\pi}{18}-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{12},\]
so the four segments have area $\dfrac{2\pi}{9}-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{3}$: answer $\boxed{\textbf{(B) }\dfrac{2\pi}{9}-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{3}}$.

Let the circles have radius $r$. Along each side of the square two circles fit side by side, so the side is $s=4r$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=5, r=s/4;
fill(box((0,0),(s,s)),rgb(1,0.88,0.7));
for(int i=0;i<2;++i) for(int j=0;j<2;++j) filldraw(circle((r+2r*i,r+2r*j),r),white,blue);
draw(box((0,0),(s,s)),linewidth(0.9));
draw((r,r)--(r,0),red); label("$r$",(r,r/2),E,red);
draw((0,-0.35)--(s,-0.35),Arrows(4)); label("$s=4r$",(s/2,-0.35),S);
\end{asy}

The area outside the circles is
\[s^2-4\pi r^2=s^2-4\pi\cdot\dfrac{s^2}{16}=\dfrac{s^2(4-\pi)}{4}.\]
Setting this equal to $\dfrac{100-25\pi}{4}=\dfrac{25(4-\pi)}{4}$ gives $s^2=25$, so the side length is $\boxed{5}$.

question:
A median of a triangle joins a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side, and an angle bisector of a triangle is the segment from a vertex to the opposite side that bisects the angle at that vertex.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why a median and an angle bisector drawn from the \emph{same} vertex can never be perpendicular.
\item[(b)] In triangle $ABC$, the median $AM$ (with $M$ the midpoint of $BC$) is perpendicular to the bisector of angle $B$. Prove that $BC=2AB$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let the median $AM$ and the bisector $AD$ both start at $A$. The bisector makes the angle $\dfrac12\angle BAC$ with each of the rays $AB$ and $AC$, and the ray $AM$ lies between the rays $AB$ and $AC$. So the angle between $AM$ and $AD$ is at most $\dfrac12\angle BAC<90^\circ$, and the two segments cannot be perpendicular. (So in a problem like (b), the median and the bisector come from different vertices.)

\textbf{(b)} Let the bisector of angle $B$ be $BD$, with $D$ on $AC$. The line $BD$ separates the ray $BA$ from the ray $BC$, so $A$ and $M$ (which lies on $BC$) are on opposite sides of it, and the segment $AM$ crosses $BD$ at a point $P$. By assumption $BP\perp AM$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(4,0), M=(2,0);
pair A=2*dir(70);
pair u=dir(35);
pair P=extension(B,B+u,A,M);
pair D=extension(B,B+u,A,C);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--M,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(B--D,red+dashed);
pair e1=unit(A-P), e2=unit(B-P); real s=0.14;
draw(P+s*e1--P+s*e1+s*e2--P+s*e2);
draw(arc(B,0.55,0,35)); draw(arc(B,0.62,35,70));
void tick(pair X, pair Y){pair m=(X+Y)/2; pair n=0.09*dir(degrees(Y-X)+90); draw(m-n--m+n);}
tick(B,A); tick(B,M); tick(M,C);
dot(M); dot(P); dot(D);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$M$",M,S); label("$P$",P,E); label("$D$",D,NE);
\end{asy}

Compare triangles $ABP$ and $MBP$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\angle ABP=\angle MBP$, because $BP$ bisects angle $ABC$ and $M$ lies on $BC$;
\item they share the side $BP$;
\item $\angle BPA=\angle BPM=90^\circ$.
\end{itemize}
By ASA they are congruent, so $AB=MB$. Since $M$ is the midpoint of $BC$, $MB=\dfrac12BC$, and therefore
\[\boxed{BC=2AB}.\ \blacksquare\]
In other words, in triangle $ABM$ the bisector from $B$ is also an altitude, which forces $ABM$ to be isosceles with $BA=BM$.

question:
Let $n$ be a positive integer that is not a perfect square.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $\sqrt n$ is irrational.
\item[(b)] Explain why it follows that the decimal expansion of $\sqrt n$ neither terminates nor eventually repeats.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Suppose, for contradiction, that $\sqrt n=\dfrac pq$ with positive integers $p,q$ that have no common factor greater than $1$. Squaring gives
\[p^2=nq^2.\]
If $q>1$, let $r$ be a prime factor of $q$. Then $r$ divides $nq^2=p^2$, and since $r$ is prime it divides $p$. So $r$ is a common factor of $p$ and $q$, a contradiction. Hence $q=1$ and $n=p^2$ is a perfect square, contradicting the assumption. Therefore $\sqrt n$ is irrational. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(b)} Every terminating or eventually repeating decimal is rational:
\begin{itemize}
\item a terminating decimal with $k$ digits after the point equals $\dfrac{N}{10^k}$ for an integer $N$;
\item if $x$ has a block of $m$ digits that repeats forever after the first $k$ digits after the point, then $10^{k+m}x$ and $10^kx$ have the same digits after the point, so $10^{k+m}x-10^kx=N$ is an integer and $x=\dfrac{N}{10^k\left(10^m-1\right)}$.
\end{itemize}
Since $\sqrt n$ is irrational by (a), its decimal expansion can be neither terminating nor eventually repeating. For example, $\sqrt2=1.41421356\ldots$ never settles into a repeating block.

Let $a,m,p,c$ be the numbers of art, music, photography and coding classes chosen, with $a+m+p+c=4$ and $a,m,p\ge1$. Then exactly one of the four classes is ``extra'', and the cases are:
\begin{itemize}
\item $(a,m,p,c)=(1,1,1,1)$: $3\cdot2\cdot2\cdot5=60$ ways;
\item $(2,1,1,0)$: $\dbinom32\cdot2\cdot2=12$ ways;
\item $(1,2,1,0)$: $3\cdot\dbinom22\cdot2=6$ ways;
\item $(1,1,2,0)$: $3\cdot2\cdot\dbinom22=6$ ways.
\end{itemize}
The total is $60+12+6+6=\boxed{84}$.

Check by inclusion--exclusion over the $\dbinom{12}{4}=495$ choices, subtracting those with no art, no music or no photography class:
\[495-\left(\dbinom94+\dbinom{10}4+\dbinom{10}4\right)+\left(\dbinom74+\dbinom74+\dbinom84\right)-\dbinom54=495-546+140-5=84.\]
(The tempting count $3\cdot2\cdot2\cdot9=108$ is wrong: it counts a choice such as two art classes twice, once for each art class being ``the'' required one.)

\textbf{Construction 1 (circles only).} Draw the circle with centre $A$ through $B$ and the circle with centre $B$ through $A$. Both have radius $AB$, and the distance between their centres is $AB$, which is less than the sum $2AB$ of the radii, so they meet in two points; let $C$ be one of them. Then
\[AC=AB\quad(\text{radius of the first circle}),\qquad BC=BA\quad(\text{radius of the second circle}),\]
so $AB=BC=CA$ and triangle $ABC$ is equilateral.

\textbf{Construction 2 (perpendicular bisector).} Mark the midpoint $M$ of $AB$ and draw the line through $M$ perpendicular to $AB$. Draw the circle with centre $A$ through $B$; it meets this line at a point $C$ (the line passes through $M$, which is inside the circle). Then $AC=AB$. Also triangles $AMC$ and $BMC$ are congruent (SAS: $AM=BM$, $\angle AMC=\angle BMC=90^\circ$, common side $MC$), so $CB=CA$. Hence $AB=AC=BC$ and triangle $ABC$ is equilateral.

\begin{asy}
