size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(2,0), C=(1,sqrt(3)), M=(1,0);
draw(circle(A,2),gray); draw(circle(B,2),gray);
draw((1,-2.2)--(1,2.2),dashed);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
real s=0.13; draw(M+(s,0)--M+(s,s)--M+(0,s));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(M);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$M$",M,SE);
\end{asy}

Both constructions give the same point $C$ (or its mirror image below $AB$): the two circles meet exactly on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$.

Let $O$ be the centre and $R$ the circumradius. Each side subtends a central angle of $30^\circ$, so by the law of cosines in triangle $Oa_1a_2$,
\[10^2=2R^2-2R^2\cos30^\circ=R^2\left(2-\sqrt3\right),\qquad R^2=\dfrac{100}{2-\sqrt3}=100\left(2+\sqrt3\right).\]
Going from $a_8$ through $a_9,a_{10},a_{11},a_{12}$ to $a_1$ uses $5$ sides, and going the other way uses $7$, so the smaller piece is the hexagon $a_8a_9a_{10}a_{11}a_{12}a_1$. It does not contain $O$, and together with triangle $Oa_8a_1$ it makes up the region $Oa_8a_9\ldots a_{12}a_1$, which consists of $5$ triangles $Oa_ka_{k+1}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=10/(2*sin(pi/12));
pair[] V; for(int k=0;k<12;++k) V.push(R*dir(90-30*k));
fill(V[7]--V[8]--V[9]--V[10]--V[11]--V[0]--cycle,rgb(1,0.86,0.6));
