draw(O--V[0],dashed);
for(int k=0;k<12;++k) label("$a_{"+string(k+1)+"}$",V[k],dir(90-30*k));
label("$10$",(V[3]+V[4])/2,dir(90-105));
\end{asy}

Each triangle $Oa_ka_{k+1}$ has area $\dfrac12R^2\sin30^\circ=\dfrac{R^2}{4}$, and triangle $Oa_8a_1$ has central angle $5\cdot30^\circ=150^\circ$, so its area is $\dfrac12R^2\sin150^\circ=\dfrac{R^2}{4}$ too. Hence the smaller piece has area
\[5\cdot\dfrac{R^2}{4}-\dfrac{R^2}{4}=R^2=200+100\sqrt3\approx\boxed{373.21\text{ square feet}}.\]
(This is exactly one third of the whole dodecagon, whose area is $12\cdot\dfrac{R^2}{4}=3R^2$.)

Let the park have radius $r$. Joining the centre $O$ to the $12$ vertices cuts the dodecagon into $12$ congruent isosceles triangles with two sides $r$ and apex angle $\dfrac{360^\circ}{12}=30^\circ$.

