Each has area $\dfrac12r^2\sin30^\circ=\dfrac{r^2}{4}$, so the dodecagon has area $3r^2$. The orb is uniform in the disc of area $\pi r^2$, so the probability is
\[\dfrac{3r^2}{\pi r^2}=\boxed{\dfrac3\pi}\approx0.955.\]

Use coordinates with $O=(0,0)$, $A=(-1,0)$, $B=(1,0)$.

\textbf{The points $C$ and $P$.} The points at distance $1$ from both $O$ and $A$ are $\left(-\dfrac12,\pm\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$; by the symmetry in line $AB$ we may take $C=\left(-\dfrac12,\dfrac{\sqrt3}2\right)$ (the other choice gives the mirror image and the same length $BH$). Since $AC=1$, the two points at distance $1$ from both $A$ and $C$ are the apexes of the two equilateral triangles on $AC$: one is $O$, and the other is its reflection in line $AC$,
\[P=A+C-O=\left(-\dfrac32,\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right).\]
(Check: $PA=\left|\left(-\dfrac12,\dfrac{\sqrt3}2\right)\right|=1$ and $PC=|(-1,0)|=1$.)

\textbf{The points $G$ and $H$.} The tangent at $A$ is perpendicular to the radius $OA$, so $L$ is the line $x=-1$. Line $PO$ is $y=-\dfrac{x}{\sqrt3}$, so $G=\left(-1,\dfrac1{\sqrt3}\right)$. The two points of $L$ at distance $3$ from $G$ are $\left(-1,\dfrac1{\sqrt3}\pm3\right)$, at distances $3\pm\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}$ from $A$; the condition $AH<3$ picks $H=\left(-1,\dfrac1{\sqrt3}-3\right)$.

