\textbf{The length.}
\[BH^2=2^2+\left(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}-3\right)^2=4+\dfrac13-2\sqrt3+9=\dfrac{40}{3}-2\sqrt3=\dfrac{40-6\sqrt3}{3}.\]
So $BH=\sqrt{\dfrac{40-6\sqrt3}{3}}$. This form is the only one allowed: $c$ squarefree forces $c=3$ (since $\dfrac{b}{d}\sqrt c=2\sqrt3$), and then $\dfrac ad=\dfrac{40}{3}$, $\dfrac bd=2$ give $(a,b,d)=(40k,6k,3k)$, with $k=1$ by the common-factor condition. Hence
\[a+b+c+d=40+6+3+3=\boxed{52}.\]

The ratio does not depend on the size of the square, so let the side be $4$: $A=(0,0)$, $B=(4,0)$, $C=(4,4)$, $D=(0,4)$, $M=(4,2)$.

\textbf{Locating $S$.} Since $\angle AMS=90^\circ$, $\angle AMB+\angle SMC=90^\circ=\angle AMB+\angle BAM$, so $\angle SMC=\angle BAM$. The right triangles $ABM$ and $MCS$ are therefore similar, and
\[\dfrac{CS}{MC}=\dfrac{BM}{AB}=\dfrac12,\qquad CS=1,\qquad DS=3.\]

\textbf{The two right triangles.} Triangle $AMS$ has a right angle at $M$, legs $AM=\sqrt{4^2+2^2}=2\sqrt5$, $MS=\sqrt{2^2+1^2}=\sqrt5$ and hypotenuse $AS=\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5$. Triangle $ADS$ has a right angle at $D$, legs $4$ and $3$, hypotenuse $5$.

