In a right triangle with legs $u,v$ and hypotenuse $w$, the inradius is $\dfrac{u+v-w}{2}$ (the tangent lengths from the right-angle vertex both equal the inradius). So
\[r_O=\dfrac{2\sqrt5+\sqrt5-5}{2}=\dfrac{3\sqrt5-5}{2},\qquad r_R=\dfrac{4+3-5}{2}=1.\]
The ratio of the areas is
\[\dfrac{\pi r_O^2}{\pi r_R^2}=\left(\dfrac{3\sqrt5-5}{2}\right)^2=\dfrac{70-30\sqrt5}{4}=\boxed{\dfrac{35-15\sqrt5}{2}}\approx0.729.\]

\textbf{Area, inradius, circumradius.} With $a=BC=21$, $b=CA=26$, $c=AB=20$ the semiperimeter is $s=\dfrac{67}{2}$, and $s-a=\dfrac{25}{2}$, $s-b=\dfrac{15}{2}$, $s-c=\dfrac{27}{2}$. Heron's formula gives
\[K^2=\dfrac{67\cdot25\cdot15\cdot27}{16}=\dfrac{678375}{16},\qquad K=\dfrac{45\sqrt{335}}{4}.\]
Let $I$, $\rho=\dfrac Ks$ be the incentre and inradius, and $R=\dfrac{abc}{4K}$ the circumradius.

\textbf{The three corner circles.} A circle of radius $r$ tangent to sides $AB$ and $AC$ inside the triangle has its centre $P_A$ on the bisector $AI$, at distance $r$ from both sides. Along the segment $AI$ the distance to $AB$ grows in proportion from $0$ at $A$ to $\rho$ at $I$, so $P_A$ is the image of $A$ under the dilation with centre $I$ and ratio $k=\dfrac{\rho-r}{\rho}$. The same holds for $P_B$ and $P_C$, so triangle $P_AP_BP_C$ is the image of triangle $ABC$ under this dilation, and its circumradius is $kR$.

\textbf{The fourth circle.} Its centre $X$ is at distance $2r$ from each of $P_A,P_B,P_C$ (externally tangent congruent circles), so $X$ is the circumcentre of $P_AP_BP_C$ and
\[2r=kR=\dfrac{(\rho-r)R}{\rho}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad r=\dfrac{R\rho}{R+2\rho}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(21,0);
real ax=(20^2-26^2+21^2)/(2*21); pair A=(ax,sqrt(400-ax^2));
real a=21, bb=26, c=20; real s=(a+bb+c)/2; real K=21*A.y/2; real rho=K/s;
pair I=(a*A+bb*B+c*C)/(a+bb+c);
real d=2*(A.x*(B.y-C.y)+B.x*(C.y-A.y)+C.x*(A.y-B.y));
pair O=((abs(A)^2*(B.y-C.y)+abs(B)^2*(C.y-A.y)+abs(C)^2*(A.y-B.y))/d,(abs(A)^2*(C.x-B.x)+abs(B)^2*(A.x-C.x)+abs(C)^2*(B.x-A.x))/d);
real R=abs(O-A); real r=R*rho/(R+2*rho); real k=(rho-r)/rho;
pair PA=I+k*(A-I), PB=I+k*(B-I), PC=I+k*(C-I), X=I+k*(O-I);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--I,gray+dashed); draw(B--I,gray+dashed); draw(C--I,gray+dashed);
draw(PA--PB--PC--cycle,gray+dotted);
draw(circle(PA,r),blue); draw(circle(PB,r),blue); draw(circle(PC,r),blue); draw(circle(X,r),red);
draw(X--PC,red);
dot(I); dot(PA); dot(PB); dot(PC); dot(X);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$I$",I,W,fontsize(8pt)); label("$X$",X,N,red);
label("$2r$",X+0.3*(PC-X),NE,red);
label("$20$",(A+B)/2,W); label("$21$",(B+C)/2,S); label("$26$",(A+C)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

\textbf{The value of $rK$.} Substituting $R=\dfrac{abc}{4K}$ and $\rho=\dfrac Ks$ and simplifying,
\[r=\dfrac{\dfrac{abc}{4s}}{\dfrac{abc}{4K}+\dfrac{2K}{s}}=\dfrac{abc\,K}{abc\,s+8K^2},\qquad rK=\dfrac{abc\,K^2}{abc\,s+8K^2}.\]
Here $abc=10920$, $abc\,s=365820$ and $8K^2=\dfrac{678375}{2}$, so
\[rK=\dfrac{10920\cdot\dfrac{678375}{16}}{365820+\dfrac{678375}{2}}=\dfrac{1365\cdot678375}{1410015}=\boxed{\dfrac{921375}{1403}}\approx656.72.\]
(Numerically $K\approx205.91$ and $r\approx3.189$. The triangle is acute, and one checks that the circles do not overlap and that the fourth circle stays at distance more than $r$ from every side, so the configuration exists.)

Place $B=(0,0)$ and $C=(20,0)$. If $A=(p,q)$ with $q>0$, then $p^2+q^2=784$ and $(p-20)^2+q^2=576$; subtracting gives $40p-400=208$, so $p=\dfrac{76}{5}$ and $q^2=784-\dfrac{5776}{25}=\dfrac{13824}{25}$, $q=\dfrac{48\sqrt6}{5}$.

\textbf{The circumcentre.} $O$ lies on the perpendicular bisector $x=10$ of $BC$. Writing $O=(10,y)$, the condition $OB=OA$ gives
\[100+y^2=\left(10-\dfrac{76}5\right)^2+\left(y-\dfrac{48\sqrt6}{5}\right)^2\iff\dfrac{96\sqrt6}{5}y=\dfrac{676+13824}{25}-100=480,\]
so $y=\dfrac{25}{\sqrt6}=\dfrac{25\sqrt6}{6}$ and $R=OB=\sqrt{100+\dfrac{625}{6}}=\dfrac{35\sqrt6}{6}$. (Check: $R=\dfrac{abc}{4K}=\dfrac{28\cdot20\cdot24}{4\cdot96\sqrt6}=\dfrac{35}{\sqrt6}$, since the area is $K=\dfrac12\cdot20\cdot\dfrac{48\sqrt6}5=96\sqrt6$.)

