\textbf{The hexagon.} The line $AG$ makes $30^\circ$ with $AB$, so $G$ lies strictly between the two trisectors from $A$, and likewise for $B$ and $C$. Hence the hexagon is the set of points lying between the two trisectors from each vertex. The figure is symmetric under the rotations by $120^\circ$ about $G$ (which permute $A$, $B$, $C$) and under the reflection in the altitude from $C$, the vertical line through $G$.

On this altitude, the $20^\circ$ trisectors from $A$ and from $B$ meet at $V_1=\left(\dfrac x2,\dfrac x2\tan20^\circ\right)$, and the $40^\circ$ trisectors meet at $V_4=\left(\dfrac x2,\dfrac x2\tan40^\circ\right)$. Both points lie on the bisector from $C$, hence between the trisectors from $C$; $V_1$ is below the two $40^\circ$ lines and $V_4$ above the two $20^\circ$ lines. So $V_1$ and $V_4$ lie in the hexagon, each on two of its six boundary lines, hence they are vertices of it, on opposite sides of $G$, at distances
\[d_1=GV_1=\dfrac x2\left(\tan30^\circ-\tan20^\circ\right),\qquad d_2=GV_4=\dfrac x2\left(\tan40^\circ-\tan30^\circ\right).\]
Rotating by $\pm120^\circ$ gives four more vertices. The hexagon is bounded by six lines, so it has at most six vertices, and these are all of them: they lie on the six half-lines from $G$ along the axes of symmetry, $60^\circ$ apart, alternately at distances $d_1$ and $d_2$.

\begin{asy}
