The expression is defined when $\sin a\ne0$, $\cos a\ne0$ (so that $\sec a$ and $\csc a$ exist) and $\sec a+\csc a\ne0$. Multiplying the numerator and the denominator by $\sin a\cos a$,
\[\dfrac{\sec a-\csc a}{\sec a+\csc a}=\dfrac{\sin a-\cos a}{\sin a+\cos a}.\]
(The condition $\sec a+\csc a\ne0$ is the same as $\sin a+\cos a\ne0$.) Dividing the numerator and the denominator by $\cos a$ and using $\tan\dfrac\pi4=1$,
\[\dfrac{\sin a-\cos a}{\sin a+\cos a}=\dfrac{\tan a-1}{1+\tan a}=\boxed{\tan\left(a-\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right)}.\]
So the expression equals $\tan\left(a-\dfrac\pi4\right)$ for every $a$ that is not a multiple of $\dfrac\pi2$ and not of the form $\dfrac{3\pi}{4}+k\pi$. The graph below shows it on $[-\pi,\pi]$, with holes at the multiples of $\dfrac\pi2$ and asymptotes at $a=-\dfrac\pi4$ and $a=\dfrac{3\pi}4$.

