Since $b>0$, we have $c^2=a^2+b^2>a^2$, so $c>a$; in the same way $c>b$. Therefore
\[a+c>c>b\qquad\text{and}\qquad b+c>c>a.\]
For the third inequality,
\[(a+b)^2=a^2+2ab+b^2=c^2+2ab>c^2,\]
and since $a+b$ and $c$ are positive, $a+b>c$. $\blacksquare$

