\textbf{Two area equations.} Triangles $ABP$ and $ACQ$ have equal bases $BP=QC$ on line $BC$ and the same height $h$, so they have equal areas. Using the formula $\dfrac12xy\sin\theta$ for each,
\[\dfrac12AB\cdot AP\sin\angle BAP=\dfrac12AC\cdot AQ\sin\angle CAQ\quad\Longrightarrow\quad AB\cdot AP=AC\cdot AQ,\]
since the two angles are equal. In the same way triangles $ABQ$ and $ACP$ have equal bases $BQ=PC$ and the same height, and $\angle BAQ=\angle CAP$, so
\[AB\cdot AQ=AC\cdot AP.\]
\textbf{Conclusion.} Multiplying the two equations gives $AB^2\cdot AP\cdot AQ=AC^2\cdot AP\cdot AQ$, so $AB^2=AC^2$ and $\boxed{AB=AC}$: the triangle is isosceles with apex $A$. $\blacksquare$

(Conversely, in any triangle with $AB=AC$, points $P$ and $Q$ symmetric about the midpoint of $BC$ satisfy both conditions, so nothing more can be said: the triangle need not be equilateral.)

