A regular hexagon has side length $s$ and centre $O$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that a straight line divides the hexagon into two pieces of equal area if and only if it passes through $O$.
\item[(b)] Find the shortest and the longest possible length of the segment that such a line cuts out of the hexagon.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} \emph{Lines through $O$.} The half-turn about $O$ maps the hexagon onto itself and maps a line $\ell'$ through $O$ onto itself, swapping its two sides. So it maps one piece onto the other, and the two pieces have equal areas.

\emph{Lines not through $O$.} Let $\ell$ be a line not through $O$ that cuts the hexagon, and let $\ell'$ be the line through $O$ parallel to $\ell$. Let $U$ be the piece cut off by $\ell$ on the side away from $O$, and $U'$ the piece cut off by $\ell'$ on the side containing $\ell$. Then $U$ is contained in $U'$, and $U'$ also contains every point of the hexagon strictly between $\ell'$ and $\ell$, which is not in $U$. Since $O$ lies on $\ell'$ and is an interior point of the hexagon, all points strictly between the two lines and close enough to $O$ belong to the hexagon, so this part has positive area (shaded below). Hence
\[\text{area}(U)<\text{area}(U')=\dfrac12\cdot\text{area of the hexagon},\]
so $\ell$ does not bisect the area.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); pair[] V; for(int k=0;k<6;++k) V.push(dir(60k));
path H=V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle;
pair d=dir(20), n=dir(110); real e=0.28;
pair P1=O-1.4*d, P2=O+1.4*d, Q1=O+e*n-1.4*d, Q2=O+e*n+1.4*d;
fill(H,rgb(0.92,0.92,0.92));
pair[] a=intersectionpoints(P1--P2,H), bq=intersectionpoints(Q1--Q2,H);
fill(a[0]--a[1]--bq[1]--bq[0]--cycle,rgb(1,0.8,0.5));
draw(H,linewidth(0.8));
draw(P1--P2,blue); draw(Q1--Q2,red);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,S);
label("$\ell'$",P2,E,blue); label("$\ell$",Q2,E,red);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} By (a), the bisecting lines are exactly the lines through $O$. For such a line, the half-turn about $O$ maps the segment it cuts out onto itself, so $O$ is the midpoint of the segment, and its length is $2\cdot OX$, where $X$ is the point where a ray from $O$ meets the boundary.

The boundary point $X$ lies on some side. The distance from $O$ to points of a side is smallest at the midpoint of the side, where it equals the apothem $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s$ (the height of an equilateral triangle of side $s$), and largest at its endpoints, the vertices, where it equals $s$. Hence
\[\sqrt3\,s\le\text{length}\le2s.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); pair[] V; for(int k=0;k<6;++k) V.push(dir(60k));
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
pair m1=(V[0]+V[1])/2, m2=(V[3]+V[4])/2;
draw(V[1]--V[4],red+linewidth(0.9)); draw(m1--m2,blue+linewidth(0.9));
pair u=dir(15); pair X1=extension(O,O+u,V[0],V[1]), X2=extension(O,O-u,V[3],V[4]);
draw(X1--X2,deepgreen+dashed);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,NW);
label("$2s$",(V[1]+O)/2,W,red); label("$s\sqrt3$",m2,W,blue);
dot(m1); dot(m2); dot(X1); dot(X2); label("$X$",X1,E,deepgreen);
label("$s$",(V[4]+V[5])/2,SE);
\end{asy}

The shortest bisecting segment, of length $\boxed{\sqrt3\,s}$, joins the midpoints of two opposite sides, and the longest, of length $\boxed{2s}$, joins two opposite vertices. Every length in between also occurs, for the lines through $O$ in between.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the last three digits of
\[N=5^{2017^{2016^{\cdots^{2^1}}}}.\]
\item[(b)] A student argues: ``Since $\gcd(5,8)=1$, Fermat's little theorem gives $5^7\equiv1\pmod8$.'' Explain what is wrong, and state a correct result of this kind for the modulus $8$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $P=(-14,2)$. The line $4x+3y=12$ has slope $-\dfrac43$, so a perpendicular line has slope $\dfrac34$. The perpendicular through $P$ is
\[y-2=\dfrac34(x+14),\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad y=\dfrac34x+\dfrac{25}{2}.\]
\textbf{The foot of the perpendicular.} Substituting into $4x+3y=12$:
\[4x+\dfrac94x+\dfrac{75}{2}=12\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\dfrac{25}{4}x=-\dfrac{51}{2}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad x=-\dfrac{102}{25},\]
and then $y=\dfrac34\cdot\left(-\dfrac{102}{25}\right)+\dfrac{25}{2}=\dfrac{236}{25}$. So the foot is $F=\left(-\dfrac{102}{25},\dfrac{236}{25}\right)$.

\textbf{The reflection.} $F$ is the midpoint of $P$ and its image $P'$, so
\[P'=2F-P=\left(-\dfrac{204}{25}+14,\ \dfrac{472}{25}-2\right)=\boxed{\left(\dfrac{146}{25},\dfrac{422}{25}\right)}=(5.84,\,16.88).\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real k=0.3;
pair P=(-14,2), F=(-102/25,236/25), Q=(146/25,422/25);
draw((-16,0)--(9,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-4)--(0,19),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(9,0),E); label("$y$",(0,19),N);
draw((-9,16)--(6,-4),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--Q,red+dashed);
pair e1=unit(Q-F), e2=unit((6,-4)-F); real s=0.8;
draw(F+s*e1--F+s*e1+s*e2--F+s*e2);
dot(P); dot(F); dot(Q);
label("$P(-14,2)$",P,NW); label("$F$",F,W); label("$P'$",Q,E);
label("$4x+3y=12$",(6,-4),E,blue);
\end{asy}

Check: the midpoint of $P$ and $P'$ is $(-4.08,9.44)$, and $4(-4.08)+3(9.44)=12$; the segment $PP'$ has slope $\dfrac{16.88-2}{5.84+14}=\dfrac{14.88}{19.84}=\dfrac34$, perpendicular to the line.

We use Menelaus' theorem twice (all lengths unsigned).

\textbf{Step 1: the ratio $BN:NC$.} In triangle $ABN$, the line through $C$, $P$, $M$ meets side $AB$ at $M$, side $AN$ at $P$ and the extension of side $BN$ at $C$. Menelaus' theorem gives
\[\dfrac{AM}{MB}\cdot\dfrac{BC}{CN}\cdot\dfrac{NP}{PA}=1,\qquad\text{so}\qquad 1\cdot\dfrac{BC}{CN}\cdot\dfrac37=1,\qquad\dfrac{BC}{CN}=\dfrac73.\]
Hence $BN:NC=4:3$.

\textbf{Step 2: the length $PM$.} In triangle $BCM$, the line through $A$, $P$, $N$ meets side $BC$ at $N$, side $CM$ at $P$ and the extension of side $MB$ at $A$. Menelaus' theorem gives
\[\dfrac{BN}{NC}\cdot\dfrac{CP}{PM}\cdot\dfrac{MA}{AB}=1,\qquad\text{so}\qquad\dfrac43\cdot\dfrac{5}{PM}\cdot\dfrac12=1,\qquad PM=\dfrac{10}{3}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(0,0), A=7*dir(200), Nn=3*dir(20), C=5*dir(100), M=(10/3)*dir(280), B=2*M-A;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--Nn,blue); draw(C--M,blue);
pair u=unit(B-A), v=rotate(90)*u;
for(pair m : new pair[]{(A+M)/2,(M+B)/2}) draw((m-0.3v)--(m+0.3v));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(M); dot(Nn); dot(P);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N); label("$M$",M,S); label("$N$",Nn,NE); label("$P$",P,dir(150));
label("$7$",(A+P)/2,dir(290),blue); label("$3$",(P+Nn)/2,dir(290),blue);
label("$5$",(P+C)/2,dir(10),blue); label("$\dfrac{10}{3}$",(P+M)/2,dir(10),red);
label("$4k$",(B+Nn)/2,dir(30)); label("$3k$",(Nn+C)/2,dir(30));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Check with mass points.} Put masses $1$ at $A$ and $1$ at $B$ (so $M$, the midpoint, has mass $2$), and mass $\dfrac43$ at $C$, so that $N$ with $BN:NC=4:3$ is the balance point of $B$ and $C$ (mass $\dfrac73$). Then $AP:PN=\dfrac73:1=7:3$, which agrees with $PA=7$, $PN=3$, and $CP:PM=2:\dfrac43=3:2$, so $PM=\dfrac23\cdot5=\dfrac{10}{3}$.

Such a triangle exists: for example, with $P$ at the origin, $A=7(\cos200^\circ,\sin200^\circ)$, $C=5(\cos100^\circ,\sin100^\circ)$, $M=-\dfrac{2}{3}C$ and $B=2M-A$, the point $N=-\dfrac37A$ lies on $BC$ (the figure is drawn this way). So $PM=\boxed{\dfrac{10}{3}}$.

question:
Six people, Amanda, George, Timothy, Hortense, Oliver and Riley, sit in six seats arranged in two rows (an upper row and a lower row) of three seats each, so that the seats form three columns of two. We know that:
\begin{itemize}
\item Amanda sits directly above Timothy;
\item Timothy sits to the left of Riley, in the same row;
\item Riley sits two seats to the right of Oliver, in the same row;
\item Hortense sits directly above Oliver.
\end{itemize}
In which seat does George sit?

solution:
Riley is two seats to the right of Oliver in the same row, and a row has only three seats, so Oliver is in the left seat and Riley in the right seat of some row. Hortense sits directly above Oliver, so Oliver is in the lower row: Oliver is lower left, Riley is lower right and Hortense is upper left.

Amanda sits directly above Timothy, so Timothy is also in the lower row. The only free seat there is the middle one, so Timothy is lower middle (and indeed to the left of Riley), and Amanda is upper middle. George takes the last seat:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
Hortense & Amanda & George\\
\hline
Oliver & Timothy & Riley\\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
George sits in the \boxed{\text{upper row, right-hand seat}}, directly above Riley. All four clues hold, and each step was forced, so this is the only arrangement.

Each unit cube is chosen with probability $\dfrac{1}{n^3}$, and then each of its faces comes out on top with probability $\dfrac16$. So each of the $6n^3$ pairs (unit cube, face of that cube) occurs with the same probability $\dfrac{1}{6n^3}$, and we only have to count the pairs whose face is red.

A face of a unit cube is red exactly when it lies on the surface of the big cube; faces inside the big cube were never painted. Each of the $6$ faces of the big cube is divided into $n^2$ unit squares, and each of these unit squares is a face of exactly one unit cube. So there are exactly $6n^2$ red unit faces.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair v=(1.2,0.8); real n=3;
pen f1=rgb(1,0.55,0.55), f2=rgb(1,0.7,0.7), f3=rgb(0.95,0.45,0.45);
fill(box((0,0),(n,n)),f1);
fill((0,n)--(n,n)--(n,n)+v--(0,n)+v--cycle,f2);
fill((n,0)--(n,0)+v--(n,n)+v--(n,n)--cycle,f3);
for(int i=1;i<3;++i){
 draw((i,0)--(i,n)); draw((0,i)--(n,i));
 draw((i,n)--(i,n)+v); draw((0,n)+i/n*v--(n,n)+i/n*v);
 draw((n,i)--(n,i)+v); draw((n,0)+i/n*v--(n,n)+i/n*v);}
draw(box((0,0),(n,n)),linewidth(0.9)); draw((0,n)--(0,n)+v--(n,n)+v--(n,0)+v--(n,0),linewidth(0.9)); draw((n,n)--(n,n)+v,linewidth(0.9));
label("$n=3$",(n/2,0),S);
label("$6n^2=54$ red unit faces",(n/2+0.6,-0.9));
\end{asy}

Therefore
\[P(\text{top face red})=\dfrac{6n^2}{6n^3}=\dfrac1n.\qquad\blacksquare\]
For example, for $n=3$ there are $54$ red faces among the $6\cdot27=162$ faces of the small cubes, and $\dfrac{54}{162}=\dfrac13$. (Grouping by the number of painted faces gives the same result: $\dfrac{1}{27}\left(8\cdot\dfrac36+12\cdot\dfrac26+6\cdot\dfrac16\right)=\dfrac13$.)

question:
Let $A\le B$ be integers.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why there are exactly $B-A+1$ integers $n$ with $A\le n\le B$.
\item[(b)] How many integers are there from $1$ to $10$ inclusive? What does the number $B-A=9$ count in this case?
\end{enumerate}

Let $n\ge2$ be an integer. An integer $a$ is called \emph{invertible modulo $n$} if there is an integer $x$ with $ax\equiv1\pmod n$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $a$ is invertible modulo $n$ if and only if $\gcd(a,n)=1$.
\item[(b)] Which of $9$ and $13$ are invertible modulo $26$? Find an inverse of each one that is.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{Coordinates.} Put $A=(0,0)$ and $C=(14,0)$, and let $B=(x,y)$ with $y>0$. From $x^2+y^2=169$ and $(x-14)^2+y^2=225$ we get $-28x+196=56$, so $x=5$ and $y=12$. Thus $B=(5,12)$, the foot of the altitude is $M=(5,0)$, $BM=12$, $AM=5$ and $MC=9$.

\textbf{The circle.} The line $BM$ is $x=5$, and the foot of the perpendicular from $C$ to it is $M$. So the circle centred at $C$ tangent to $BM$ has radius $CM=9$ and touches $BM$ at $M$; in particular $M$ lies on the circle. The point $N$ on $BC$ with $CN=9$ is
\[N=C+\dfrac{9}{15}(B-C)=(14,0)+\dfrac35(-9,12)=\left(\dfrac{43}{5},\dfrac{36}{5}\right).\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=9; pair A=(0,0), C=(14,0), B=(5,12), M=(5,0), Nn=(43/5,36/5);
real aN=degrees(Nn-C); pair Z=C+r*dir((180+aN)/2);
fill(C--M--Z--Nn--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
draw(arc(C,r,105,185),gray+dashed);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(B--M);
draw(C--M--Z--Nn--C,blue); draw(C--Z,blue+dashed); draw(M--Nn,red+dashed);
draw((M+(0,0.7))--(M+(0.7,0.7))--(M+(0.7,0)));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(M); dot(Nn); dot(Z);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,N); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$M$",M,S); label("$N$",Nn,NE); label("$Z$",Z,W);
label("$5$",(A+M)/2,S); label("$9$",(M+C)/2,S); label("$12$",(B+M)/2,W);
label("$9$",(C+Nn)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

\textbf{The kite.} $CM=CN=CZ=9$ (radii), and $ZM=ZN$ because the arcs $MZ$ and $ZN$ are equal. So $CMZN$ is a kite with axis of symmetry $CZ$; it is convex because $Z$ lies on the minor arc. The diagonals of a kite are perpendicular, so its area is $\dfrac12\cdot CZ\cdot MN$. Here
\[MN=\sqrt{\left(\dfrac{43}{5}-5\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{36}{5}\right)^2}=\sqrt{\left(\dfrac{18}{5}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{36}{5}\right)^2}=\dfrac{18}{5}\sqrt{1+4}=\dfrac{18\sqrt5}{5},\]
so
\[[MZNC]=\dfrac12\cdot9\cdot\dfrac{18\sqrt5}{5}=\boxed{\dfrac{81\sqrt5}{5}}\approx36.2.\]

The base angles are $\angle A=\angle B=\dfrac{180^\circ-120^\circ}{2}=30^\circ$. Let $H$ be the midpoint of $AB$. Since $AC=BC$, the segment $CH$ is perpendicular to $AB$. Since $AD=EB$, $H$ is also the midpoint of $DE$.

Let $AB=6t$, so $AD=DE=EB=2t$, $AH=3t$ and $DH=t$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s3=sqrt(3);
pair A=(-3,0), B=(3,0), C=(0,s3), D=(-1,0), E=(1,0), H=(0,0);
fill(C--D--E--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(C--D); draw(C--E); draw(C--H,dashed);
draw((0,0.18)--(0.18,0.18)--(0.18,0));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(E); dot(H);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N); label("$D$",D,S); label("$E$",E,S); label("$H$",H,S);
label("$30^\circ$",(-2.3,0.15),fontsize(8pt)); label("$60^\circ$",(-0.62,0.19),fontsize(8pt));
label("$t$",(D+H)/2,S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$2t$",(A+D)/2,S,fontsize(8pt));
label("$CH=\sqrt3\,t$",(1.5,1.45),E,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Triangle $AHC$ is a $30^\circ$-$60^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle with the right angle at $H$, so $CH=\dfrac{AH}{\sqrt3}=\sqrt3\,t$. In the right triangle $CHD$,
\[CD^2=CH^2+DH^2=3t^2+t^2=4t^2,\qquad CD=2t=2\,DH.\]
So $CHD$ is also a $30^\circ$-$60^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle, with the $60^\circ$ angle at $D$ (opposite the longer leg $CH$). Since $H$ lies on segment $DE$,
\[\angle CDE=\angle CDH=\boxed{60^\circ}.\]
(In fact $CD=CE=DE=2t$, so triangle $CDE$ is equilateral.)

question:
Solve each congruence for the integer $x$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $2x\equiv5\pmod{13}$;
\item[(b)] $2x\equiv6\pmod{13}$.
\end{enumerate}
Is there an integer that satisfies both congruences?

solution:
\textbf{Lemma 1.} If a point $X$ lies in a triangle $UVW$ (inside or on its boundary) and $X\ne W$, then $UX+XV<UW+WV$.

\emph{Proof.} If $X$ is on side $UV$, then $UX+XV=UV<UW+WV$. Otherwise the ray from $U$ through $X$ leaves the triangle at a point $T$ of side $VW$. By the triangle inequality, $UX+XT=UT\le UW+WT$ and $XV\le XT+TV$. Adding, $UX+XV\le UW+WT+TV=UW+WV$, and equality would need $T=W$ and $X$ on segment $TV$, i.e. $X=W$. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{Lemma 2.} If the interior angle at a vertex $X$ of the quadrilateral is at least $180^\circ$, then $X$ lies in the triangle formed by the other three vertices.

\emph{Proof.} The quadrilateral lies inside the convex hull of its four vertices. At a corner of the convex hull, the interior angle of the quadrilateral is at most the angle of the hull there, which is less than $180^\circ$. So $X$ is not a corner of the hull. The convex hull of four points one of which is not a corner is the triangle formed by the other three, and $X$ lies in that triangle. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{Applying the lemmas.} If the angle at a vertex $X$ is at least $180^\circ$, and $U$, $W$ are the neighbours of $X$ and $O$ the opposite vertex, then by the two lemmas $UX+XW<UO+OW$. Check each vertex:
\begin{itemize}
\item at $P$: $SP+PQ=4015$ and $SR+RQ=4015$, and $4015<4015$ is false;
\item at $R$: $QR+RS=4015$ and $QP+PS=4015$: false;
\item at $S$: $RS+SP=4016$ and $RQ+QP=4014$: false;
\item at $Q$: $PQ+QR=4014$ and $PS+SR=4016$: possible.
\end{itemize}
So the angles at $P$, $R$ and $S$ are certainly less than $180^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(0,0), R=(6,0), Ss=(3.4,4), Q=(2.8,1.6);
pair T=extension(P,Q,R,Ss);
fill(P--Q--R--Ss--cycle,rgb(0.88,0.94,1));
draw(P--R,dashed);
draw(P--Q--R--Ss--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(Q--T,dotted);
dot(P); dot(Q); dot(R); dot(Ss); dot(T);
label("$P$",P,SW); label("$Q$",Q,S); label("$R$",R,SE); label("$S$",Ss,N); label("$T$",T,NE);
label("(not to scale)",(3,-0.7));
\end{asy}

\textbf{The angle at $Q$ can be reflex.} Take $P=(0,0)$ and $R=(4000,0)$. The point $S$ above $PR$ with $SP=2009$, $SR=2007$ is $S\approx(2001.0,\ 179.1)$, and the point $Q$ above $PR$ with $QP=2006$, $QR=2008$ is $Q\approx(1999.0,\ 167.5)$. Then $Q$ lies inside triangle $PRS$ (it is above $PR$ and below the line $PS$, which has height about $178.9$ at $x=1999.0$). So $PQRS$ is triangle $PRS$ with the notch $PQR$ cut out, as in the sketch, and its angle at $Q$ is greater than $180^\circ$. (Of course a convex quadrilateral with these sides also exists, so no angle is forced to be reflex.)

Hence the vertices whose angles must be less than $180^\circ$ are exactly $\boxed{P,\ R\text{ and }S}$.

Move everything to one side and multiply by $2$:
\[2x^2+2y^2-2xy-4x-4y=0.\]
Adding $8$ to both sides and grouping,
\[(x-y)^2+(x-2)^2+(y-2)^2=8,\]
because $(x-y)^2+(x-2)^2+(y-2)^2=2x^2+2y^2-2xy-4x-4y+8$.

The only squares that are at most $8$ are $0$, $1$ and $4$, and the only way to write $8$ as a sum of three of them is $4+4+0$. So two of the numbers $x-y$, $x-2$, $y-2$ are $\pm2$ and the third is $0$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $x-y=0$ and $x-2=y-2=\pm2$: $(x,y)=(4,4)$ or $(0,0)$;
\item $x-2=0$, so $x=2$, and $y-2=\pm2$ (then $x-y=\mp2$ as needed): $(2,4)$ or $(2,0)$;
\item $y-2=0$, so $y=2$, and $x-2=\pm2$: $(4,2)$ or $(0,2)$.
\end{itemize}
All six pairs satisfy the equation (for example $16+4=2\cdot6+8$ for $(4,2)$), so there are $\boxed{6}$ ordered pairs.

question:
Six different beads are threaded onto a circular bracelet, equally spaced. Two bracelets count as the same if one can be turned (rotated) or flipped over (reflected) to give the other.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many different bracelets can be made?
\item[(b)] A student argues: ``Turning the bracelet through $180^\circ$ is the same as flipping it over, so I should not divide both by the number of rotations and by the number of reflections.'' Explain what is wrong with this argument.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Fix six positions around the circle. There are $6!=720$ ways to put the beads into the positions. The positions have $12$ symmetries: $6$ rotations (including turning by $0^\circ$) and $6$ reflections (flips). Applying the $12$ symmetries to one placement gives $12$ different placements: since the beads are all different, a symmetry that gives back the same placement must leave every position where it is, and only the identity does that. So each bracelet corresponds to exactly $12$ placements, and the number of bracelets is
\[\dfrac{720}{12}=\boxed{60}.\]
(Equivalently: fix one bead and arrange the other five in $5!=120$ ways around it, then divide by $2$ for flipping: $\dfrac{(6-1)!}{2}=60$.)

\textbf{(b)} Turning through $180^\circ$ and flipping over are different symmetries, so they give different placements. Read clockwise from the top:
\begin{itemize}
\item the start is $A,B,C,D,E,F$;
\item after turning through $180^\circ$ it is $D,E,F,A,B,C$;
\item after flipping over (about the vertical axis) it is $A,F,E,D,C,B$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
void hexa(pair c, string[] L, string cap){
 draw(circle(c,1),gray);
 for(int k=0;k<6;++k){pair p=c+dir(90-60k); dot(p); label(L[k],c+1.32*dir(90-60k));}
 label(cap,c+(0,-1.9));}
hexa((0,0),new string[]{"$A$","$B$","$C$","$D$","$E$","$F$"},"start");
hexa((4,0),new string[]{"$D$","$E$","$F$","$A$","$B$","$C$"},"turned $180^\circ$");
hexa((8,0),new string[]{"$A$","$F$","$E$","$D$","$C$","$B$"},"flipped over");
\end{asy}

A rotation keeps the clockwise order of the beads; a flip reverses it. (A $180^\circ$ turn of a flat figure is a reflection in a \emph{point}; flipping a bracelet over is a reflection in a \emph{line}, which is a different motion.) So all $12$ symmetries are different, and dividing by $6$ for the rotations and then by $2$ for flipping is correct.

question:
A student conjectures that $2^n-7$ is never a prime number for integers $n\ge3$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that if $n\ge3$ and $2^n-7$ is prime, then $n\equiv3\pmod4$.
\item[(b)] Check the conjecture for $3\le n\le20$.
\end{enumerate}

For positive integers $x$ let
\[f(x)=\prod_{n=1}^{x}\dfrac{n}{n+1}=\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac23\cdot\dfrac34\cdots\dfrac{x}{x+1}.\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find a simple formula for $f(x)$.
\item[(b)] A student writes the infinite product $\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac23\cdot\dfrac34\cdots$, cancels every denominator against the next numerator, and concludes that the infinite product equals $1$. Explain the error, and find the correct value of the infinite product, defined as the limit of $f(x)$ as $x\to\infty$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the width be $w$; then the length is $3w$, and $w+3w=16$ gives $w=4$. The rectangle is $4$ by $12$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(box((0,0),(12,4)),linewidth(0.9));
label("$w$",(12,2),E); label("$3w$",(6,0),S); label("$w+3w=16$",(6,2));
\end{asy}

Its area is $4\cdot12=\boxed{48}$ square units.

Every side is horizontal or vertical, so each length is a difference of coordinates:
\[AB=4,\quad BC=2,\quad CD=2,\quad DE=2,\quad EF=2,\quad FA=4.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<=5;++i){draw((i,-0.3)--(i,5),gray+0.3); draw((-0.3,i)--(5,i),gray+0.3);}
draw((-0.3,0)--(5.2,0),Arrow(5)); draw((0,-0.3)--(0,5.2),Arrow(5));
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(4,2),D=(2,2),E2=(2,4),F=(0,4);
draw(A--B--C--D--E2--F--cycle,blue+linewidth(1));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(E2);dot(F);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,S); label("$C$",C,E); label("$D$",D,NE); label("$E$",E2,NE); label("$F$",F,W);
label("$4$",(2,0),S,blue); label("$2$",(4,1),E,blue); label("$2$",(3,2),N,blue); label("$2$",(2,3),E,blue); label("$2$",(1,4),N,blue); label("$4$",(0,2),W,blue);
\end{asy}

The perimeter is $4+2+2+2+2+4=\boxed{16}$. (It is also the perimeter of the $4$ by $4$ square, since the notch at $C$, $D$, $E$ only moves two sides inward.)

Let the original rectangle be $w$ by $2w$. The new rectangle is $(w+3)$ by $(2w+3)$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(box((0,0),(6,3)),linewidth(0.9)); draw(box((0,0),(9,6)),dashed);
label("$w$",(6,1.5),W); label("$2w$",(3,0),N);
label("$w+3$",(9,3),E); label("$2w+3$",(4.5,6),N);
\end{asy}

The condition on the areas is
\[(w+3)(2w+3)=3\cdot2w^2\iff2w^2+9w+9=6w^2\iff4w^2-9w-9=0\iff(4w+3)(w-3)=0.\]
Since $w>0$, $w=3$. The new rectangle is $6$ by $9$, so its length is $\boxed{9}$ feet. (Check: $6\cdot9=54=3\cdot(3\cdot6)$.)

Let the legs be $a$, $b$ and the hypotenuse $c$, so $a^2+b^2=c^2$ and $a+b+c=36$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(12,0),C=(0,9);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0.8)--(0.8,0.8)--(0.8,0));
label("$a$",(0,4.5),W); label("$b$",(6,0),S); label("$c$",(6,4.5),NE);
label("$a+b+c=36$",(4,-2));
\end{asy}

Substituting $c=36-a-b$:
\[a^2+b^2=(36-a-b)^2=1296-72a-72b+a^2+2ab+b^2,\]
so $2ab-72a-72b+1296=0$, which factors as
\[(36-a)(36-b)=648.\]
Both factors are positive integers (as $a,b<36$) and less than $36$. Writing $648=2^3\cdot3^4$, the factorisations of $648$ into two factors less than $36$ are only $24\cdot27$ (the next candidates $18\cdot36$ and $36\cdot18$ have a factor $36$). So $\{36-a,36-b\}=\{24,27\}$, i.e. $\{a,b\}=\{12,9\}$, and $c=15$.

The triangle is the $9$-$12$-$15$ right triangle, with area $\dfrac12\cdot9\cdot12=\boxed{54}$ square units.

question:
Positive quantities $a$, $b$, $c$ are related so that $a$ is determined by $b$ and $c$. When $c$ is held fixed, $a$ is directly proportional to $b$; when $b$ is held fixed, $a$ is directly proportional to $c$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] A student argues: ``$\dfrac ab=d$ and $\dfrac ac=e$ for constants $d$ and $e$. Let $k=d+e$; adding the equations gives $\dfrac ab+\dfrac ac=k$, so $kbc=a(b+c)$.'' This does not lead to $a=kbc$. What is wrong with the argument?
\item[(b)] Prove that $a=kbc$ for some constant $k$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the lengths be $a_1<a_2<\cdots<a_7$. Three lengths $x<y<z$ form a triangle exactly when $x+y>z$ (the other two triangle inequalities hold automatically, since $z$ is the largest).

Suppose that no three of the segments form a triangle. Then in particular, for $i=1,2,\dots,5$, the lengths $a_i<a_{i+1}<a_{i+2}$ do not, so
\[a_{i+2}\ge a_i+a_{i+1}.\]
Starting from $a_1\ge200$ and $a_2\ge200$, this gives
\[a_3\ge400,\quad a_4\ge600,\quad a_5\ge1000,\quad a_6\ge1600,\quad a_7\ge2600,\]
the lower bounds growing like the Fibonacci numbers $1,1,2,3,5,8,13$ times $200$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real[] m={200,200,400,600,1000,1600,2600}; real s=0.002;
for(int i=0;i<7;++i){ real y=-0.45*i; fill(box((0,y-0.15),(s*m[i],y+0.15)),rgb(0.75,0.85,1)); draw(box((0,y-0.15),(s*m[i],y+0.15)));
 label("$a_"+string(i+1)+"\ge"+string(m[i])+"$",(0,y),W,fontsize(8pt)); }
draw((s*2009,0.4)--(s*2009,-3.05),red+dashed); label("$2009$",(s*2009,0.4),N,red);
\end{asy}

But $a_7\le2009$, a contradiction. Hence some three of the segments (in fact, three with consecutive lengths) form a triangle. $\blacksquare$

question:
The first ten terms of a sequence are $8,3,4,9,3,9,8,3,4,3$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Assuming that the $n$th term is given by a polynomial in $n$ of degree at most $9$, find the eleventh term.
\item[(b)] A student claims that the method ``take differences until one number is left, assume that it stays constant, and work back'' always finds the next term of any sequence. In what sense is this true, and in what sense is it wrong?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{Upper bound for each pair of shapes.} A line meets another line in at most $1$ point, a circle in at most $2$ points, and the boundary of a rectangle in at most $2$ points (a line meets the boundary of a convex region in at most two points unless it contains a side, which is excluded). Two circles meet in at most $2$ points. A circle or the boundary of a rectangle meets each side of another rectangle (a segment) in at most $2$ points, so in at most $4\cdot2=8$ points in all. Hence any two shapes share at most $8$ points, and the number of points on at least two shapes is at most
\[8\dbinom{19}{2}=8\cdot171=1368.\]

\textbf{Construction.} Take $19$ congruent squares with a common centre $O$ and half-side $1$, rotated through $19$ different angles $\theta_1,\dots,\theta_{19}$ in $[0^\circ,90^\circ)$. Two of them, rotated relative to each other by an angle strictly between $0^\circ$ and $90^\circ$, meet in exactly $8$ points:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real[] th={0,25,55}; pen[] cl={blue,red,deepgreen};
for(int k=0;k<3;++k) draw(rotate(th[k])*box((-1,-1),(1,1)),cl[k]+linewidth(0.9));
for(int i=0;i<3;++i) for(int j=i+1;j<3;++j){
 path p=rotate(th[i])*box((-1,-1),(1,1)), q=rotate(th[j])*box((-1,-1),(1,1));
 pair[] X=intersectionpoints(p,q); for(pair x : X) dot(x,black+3pt);}
dot((0,0)); label("$O$",(0,0),S);
\end{asy}

No point lies on three of the squares. Indeed, a point at distance $\rho$ from $O$ in direction $\varphi$ lies on the square rotated by $\theta$ exactly when $\rho\cos\delta=1$, where $\delta\in[0^\circ,45^\circ]$ is the distance from $\varphi-\theta$ to the nearest multiple of $90^\circ$. For a fixed point, $\delta$ is determined by $\rho$, and then $\theta\equiv\varphi\pm\delta\pmod{90^\circ}$ leaves only two possible rotations. So the $8\cdot171$ intersection points are all different, and the bound is attained.

The greatest possible number is $\boxed{1368}$.

No. If $a+b=c$, then squaring gives
\[c^2=(a+b)^2=a^2+2ab+b^2,\]
and together with $a^2+b^2=c^2$ this gives $2ab=0$, so $a=0$ or $b=0$, which is impossible for the sides of a triangle.

(Even without the Pythagorean condition, $a+b=c$ is impossible in a triangle: the triangle inequality requires $a+b>c$. In a right triangle with legs $a$, $b$ the hypotenuse is strictly shorter than the path along the legs.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(0,3);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw((0,0.3)--(0.3,0.3)--(0.3,0));
label("$a$",(0,1.5),W); label("$b$",(2,0),S); label("$c$",(2,1.5),NE);
label("$c<a+b$",(2,-0.9));
\end{asy}

So the answer is $\boxed{\text{no}}$.

Use coordinates $B=(0,0)$, $C=(16,0)$, $A=(0,12)$; the line $AC$ is $3x+4y=48$. The foot of the perpendicular from $B$ to it is
\[N=\dfrac{48}{25}(3,4)=\left(\dfrac{144}{25},\dfrac{192}{25}\right),\qquad\text{and}\qquad L=(8,0).\]
Since $\angle BNC=90^\circ$, $N$ lies on the circle with diameter $BC$, so $LN=LB=LC=8$. Thus triangle $BNL$ is isosceles with apex $L$, and the foot $T$ of the perpendicular from $L$ is the midpoint of $BN$: $T=\left(\dfrac{72}{25},\dfrac{96}{25}\right)$. Then
\[M=\dfrac{T+L}{2}=\left(\dfrac{136}{25},\dfrac{48}{25}\right),\quad E=\dfrac{N+L}{2}=\left(\dfrac{172}{25},\dfrac{96}{25}\right),\quad F=\dfrac{L+E}{2}=\left(\dfrac{186}{25},\dfrac{48}{25}\right).\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(16,0),A=(0,12);
pair Nn=(144/25,192/25), L=(8,0), T=(72/25,96/25), M=(T+L)/2, E2=(Nn+L)/2, F=(L+E2)/2;
fill(A--B--L--Nn--cycle,rgb(0.9,0.95,1));
fill(M--F--E2--cycle,rgb(1,0.75,0.6));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(B--Nn); draw(Nn--L); draw(L--T,dashed);
draw(M--F--E2--cycle,red);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(Nn);dot(L);dot(T);dot(M);dot(E2);dot(F);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$N$",Nn,NE); label("$L$",L,S);
label("$T$",T,NW); label("$M$",M,W); label("$E$",E2,NE); label("$F$",F,E);
label("$8$",L+0.78*(Nn-L),E,fontsize(8pt)); label("$8$",(B+L)/2,S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Triangle $MFE$.} $M$ and $F$ have the same $y$-coordinate, so $MF=\dfrac{186-136}{25}=2$ is horizontal, and the height of $E$ above it is $\dfrac{96-48}{25}=\dfrac{48}{25}$. So $[MFE]=\dfrac12\cdot2\cdot\dfrac{48}{25}=\dfrac{48}{25}$, and
\[ME=\sqrt{\left(\dfrac{36}{25}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{48}{25}\right)^2}=\dfrac{12}{25}\sqrt{9+16}=\dfrac{12}{5}.\]

\textbf{Quadrilateral $ABLN$.} It is triangle $ABC$ minus triangle $NLC$. With $BN=\dfrac{12\cdot16}{20}=\dfrac{48}{5}$ and $NC=\dfrac{16^2}{20}=\dfrac{64}{5}$, $[NBC]=\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac{48}{5}\cdot\dfrac{64}{5}=\dfrac{1536}{25}$, and $L$ is the midpoint of $BC$, so $[NLC]=\dfrac{768}{25}$. Hence $[ABLN]=96-\dfrac{768}{25}=\dfrac{1632}{25}$.

\textbf{The answer.}
\[\dfrac{[MFE]\cdot ME}{[ABLN]}=\dfrac{\dfrac{48}{25}\cdot\dfrac{12}{5}}{\dfrac{1632}{25}}=\dfrac{576}{5\cdot1632}=\boxed{\dfrac{6}{85}}.\]
(The quantity is a length, so the answer is in the units of the given lengths.)

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] The plane is cut into regions by finitely many lines and curves. Show by an example that it is not always possible to colour each region black or white so that any two regions sharing a piece of boundary (more than a single point) get different colours.
\item[(b)] Prove that if the plane is cut into regions by finitely many straight lines, then such a colouring always exists.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Take a circle and one of its diameters. They cut the plane into three regions: the upper half-disc $1$, the lower half-disc $2$ and the outside $3$. Regions $1$ and $2$ share the diameter, and region $3$ shares an arc of the circle with each of them. So the three regions are pairwise adjacent, and two colours cannot work (two of the three regions would get the same colour).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill(arc((0,0),1,0,180)--cycle,rgb(0.6,0.6,0.6)); fill(arc((0,0),1,180,360)--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.85,1));
draw(circle((0,0),1),linewidth(0.9)); draw((-1,0)--(1,0),linewidth(0.9));
label("$1$",(0,0.45)); label("$2$",(0,-0.45)); label("$3$",(1.15,0.9));
draw(box((-1.5,-1.3),(1.5,1.3)),gray+dotted);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} Let the lines be $\ell_1,\dots,\ell_k$, and for each line call one of its two sides positive. For a point $X$ on none of the lines, let $c(X)$ be the number of lines for which $X$ is on the positive side. A region does not meet any line and is connected, so it lies on one side of each line, and $c$ is constant on it. Colour a region black if $c$ is even and white if $c$ is odd.

Suppose two regions share a boundary segment. The segment lies on one of the lines, say $\ell_i$ (it cannot lie on two different lines, which share at most one point). Choose a point $Y$ inside the segment that is not on any other line; this is possible because the other lines meet $\ell_i$ in finitely many points. Points close to $Y$ on the two sides of $\ell_i$ lie in the two regions and are on the same side of every line except $\ell_i$. So the values of $c$ in the two regions differ by exactly $1$, and the regions have different colours. $\blacksquare$

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real L=10; pair[] P={(0,0.3),(0,-0.2),(0.4,0)}; pair[] D={dir(20),dir(125),dir(80)};
path hp(pair p, pair d){ pair n=rotate(90)*d; return (p-L*d)--(p+L*d)--(p+L*d+L*n)--(p-L*d+L*n)--cycle; }
fill(hp(P[0],D[0])^^hp(P[1],D[1])^^hp(P[2],D[2]),evenodd+rgb(0.6,0.6,0.6));
for(int i=0;i<3;++i) draw((P[i]-L*D[i])--(P[i]+L*D[i]),linewidth(0.9));
clip(box((-1.5,-1.3),(1.5,1.3)));
draw(box((-1.5,-1.3),(1.5,1.3)),gray+dotted);
\end{asy}

(The figure shows such a colouring for three lines: the shaded regions are on the positive side of an odd number of lines.)

For each positive integer $m$ let
\[a=2m+1,\qquad b=2m^2+2m,\qquad c=2m^2+2m+1.\]
Then $a^2+b^2=c^2$, because
\[(2m+1)^2+\left(2m^2+2m\right)^2=4m^4+8m^3+8m^2+4m+1=\left(2m^2+2m+1\right)^2.\]
Let $T_m$ be the triangle with vertices $(-a,0)$, $(a,0)$ and $(0,b)$; these are lattice points. By the distance formula its sides are $2a$, $\sqrt{a^2+b^2}=c$ and $c$, all integers. So $T_m$ is an isosceles triangle with rational sides. For example, $T_1$ has vertices $(-3,0)$, $(3,0)$, $(0,4)$ and sides $6,5,5$, and $T_2$ has sides $10,13,13$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=-6;i<=6;++i) for(int j=0;j<=12;++j) dot((i,j),gray+1.5pt);
draw((-3,0)--(3,0)--(0,4)--cycle,blue+linewidth(1));
draw((-5,0)--(5,0)--(0,12)--cycle,red+linewidth(1));
label("$5$",(-1.5,2),W,blue); label("$5$",(1.5,2),E,blue); label("$6$",(0,0),S,blue);
label("$13$",(-2.5,6),W,red); label("$13$",(2.5,6),E,red);
label("$m=1$",(0,1.3),blue,Fill(white)); label("$m=2$",(0,7),red,Fill(white));
\end{asy}

\textbf{No two are similar.} Dividing, $\dfrac ca=m+\dfrac{m+1}{2m+1}$, and $\dfrac12<\dfrac{m+1}{2m+1}<1$ for $m\ge1$. So $\dfrac{c}{a}$ lies strictly between $m+\dfrac12$ and $m+1$; these intervals do not overlap for different $m$, so the ratios $\dfrac{c}{2a}$ (leg to base) are all different. They are also never equal to $1$, so no $T_m$ is equilateral. Two isosceles triangles that are not equilateral are similar only if their ratios of leg to base are equal. Hence the triangles $T_1,T_2,T_3,\dots$ are pairwise non-similar, and there are infinitely many of them. $\blacksquare$

(Infinitely many non-congruent examples would be easier: just scale $T_1$ by any positive integer.)

The image $A'$ is the point for which the line $2x-5y=20$ is the perpendicular bisector of $AA'$. So $AA'$ is twice the distance from $A$ to the line:
\[AA'=2\cdot\dfrac{\left|2\cdot3-5\cdot3-20\right|}{\sqrt{2^2+5^2}}=2\cdot\dfrac{29}{\sqrt{29}}=2\sqrt{29}.\]
Concretely, moving from $A$ along the normal vector $(2,-5)$ of the line: $A'=A+2(2,-5)=(7,-7)$. The midpoint $M=(5,-2)$ of $AA'$ lies on the line ($10+10=20$), and $AA'$ is parallel to the normal vector, so it is perpendicular to the line.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real f(real x){return (2x-20)/5;}
draw((-1,0)--(11,0),gray,Arrow(5)); draw((0,-8)--(0,4.5),gray,Arrow(5));
label("$x$",(11,0),E,gray); label("$y$",(0,4.5),N,gray);
draw((-1,f(-1))--(11,f(11)),blue+linewidth(0.9)); label("$2x-5y=20$",(11,f(11)),N,blue);
pair A=(3,3), A2=(7,-7), M=(5,-2);
pair u=unit((5,2)), n=unit((2,-5));
draw(A--A2,red+dashed); draw(M+0.5u--M+0.5u-0.5n--M-0.5n);
dot(A); dot(A2); dot(M);
label("$A(3,3)$",A,NE); label("$A'(7,-7)$",A2,E); label("$M(5,-2)$",M+(0.6,-0.9),E);
\end{asy}

Hence $AA'=\sqrt{4^2+10^2}=\sqrt{116}=2\sqrt{29}\approx\boxed{10.77}$ units.

A point $(-kx,-ky)$ is in quadrant III exactly when both coordinates are negative, i.e. $-kx<0$ and $-ky<0$. Since $k>0$, this happens exactly when $x>0$ and $y>0$, that is, when the original point is in quadrant I. Hence the image of a figure lies in quadrant III exactly when the figure lies in quadrant I.

For example, with $k=2$ the triangle with vertices $(1,1)$, $(2,1)$, $(1,2.2)$ in quadrant I goes to the triangle $(-2,-2)$, $(-4,-2)$, $(-2,-4.4)$ in quadrant III; but the point $Q(-1.5,2)$ in quadrant II goes to $Q'(3,-4)$ in quadrant IV.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-5,0)--(5,0),gray,Arrow(5)); draw((0,-5.2)--(0,5),gray,Arrow(5));
label("$x$",(5,0),E,gray); label("$y$",(0,5),N,gray);
pair[] T={(1,1),(2,1),(1,2.2)};
draw(T[0]--T[1]--T[2]--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-2)*T[0]--(-2)*T[1]--(-2)*T[2]--cycle,blue+dashed+linewidth(0.9));
for(pair t : T) draw(t--(-2)*t,gray+dotted);
pair Q=(-1.5,2); dot(Q,red); dot((-2)*Q,red); draw(Q--(-2)*Q,red+dotted);
label("$Q$",Q,NW,red); label("$Q'$",(-2)*Q,E,red);
label("I",(3.5,3.5)); label("II",(-3.5,3.5)); label("III",(-3.5,-3.5)); label("IV",(4.3,-2.3));
label("$k=2$",(2.2,-1.0));
\end{asy}

So the statement is $\boxed{\text{sometimes true}}$: true for figures in quadrant I and false for all others.

The distance from the shop to the river is the length of the perpendicular from $(3,3)$ to the line. Write the line as $x-4y+32=0$. By the point-to-line distance formula,
\[d=\dfrac{\left|3-4\cdot3+32\right|}{\sqrt{1^2+4^2}}=\dfrac{23}{\sqrt{17}}\approx5.5783\text{ units}.\]
(The foot of the perpendicular is $F=\left(\dfrac{28}{17},\dfrac{143}{17}\right)$: it lies on the line, and $F-(3,3)=-\dfrac{23}{17}(1,-4)$ is perpendicular to the direction $(4,1)$ of the line.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real f(real x){return x/4+8;}
draw((-3,0)--(9,0),gray,Arrow(5)); draw((0,-0.5)--(0,11),gray,Arrow(5));
label("$x$",(9,0),E,gray); label("$y$",(0,11),N,gray);
draw((-3,f(-3))--(9,f(9)),blue+linewidth(0.9)); label("river $y=\dfrac{x}{4}+8$",(9,f(9)),SE,blue);
pair S=(3,3), F=(28/17,143/17);
draw(S--F,red+linewidth(0.9));
pair u=unit((4,1)), n=unit((1,-4));
draw(F+0.45u--F+0.45u+0.45n--F+0.45n);
dot(S); dot(F);
label("shop $(3,3)$",S,SE); label("$F$",F,NW);
label("$\dfrac{23}{\sqrt{17}}$",(S+F)/2,E,red);
\end{asy}

At $100$ feet per unit, the distance is about $557.83$ feet, i.e. $\boxed{558\text{ feet}}$.

All three solids have radius $5$ feet.

