So the graph has three branches: the left one rises from $-\infty$ along $y=x-1$, crosses the axis at $-2$ and climbs up the asymptote $x=-1$; the middle one comes up from $-\infty$ next to $x=-1$, passes through $(0,-3)$ and $(1,0)$ and goes up to $+\infty$ next to $x=2$; the right one comes up from $-\infty$ next to $x=2$, crosses the axis at $3$ and then follows $y=x-1$.

\textbf{The locus of $C$.} Put $A=(0,0)$, $B=(9,0)$ and $C=(x,y)$ with $y>0$. The condition $41\cdot BC=40\cdot AC$ means
\[1681\left((x-9)^2+y^2\right)=1600\left(x^2+y^2\right)\iff81x^2+81y^2-30258x+136161=0.\]
Dividing by $81$ and completing the square,
\[x^2-\dfrac{3362}{9}x+y^2+1681=0\iff\left(x-\dfrac{1681}{9}\right)^2+y^2=\left(\dfrac{1681}{9}\right)^2-1681=\dfrac{1681\cdot1600}{81}.\]
So $C$ lies on the circle with centre $O=\left(\dfrac{1681}{9},0\right)$ and radius $\dfrac{41\cdot40}{9}=\dfrac{1640}{9}$ (a circle of Apollonius).

\textbf{The maximum.} The area is $\dfrac12\cdot AB\cdot y=\dfrac92y$, and on this circle $y\le\dfrac{1640}{9}$, with equality at the top point $C=\left(\dfrac{1681}{9},\dfrac{1640}{9}\right)$, which gives a genuine triangle with $BC:AC=40:41$. Hence the largest area is
\[\dfrac92\cdot\dfrac{1640}{9}=\boxed{820},\]
and the sum of its digits is $8+2+0=\boxed{10}$.

