The figure is to scale: $AB=9$ is tiny compared with the circle, and the best triangle is long and thin ($AC=41k$, $BC=40k$ with $k=\dfrac{\sqrt{3281}}{9}\approx6.36$).

question:
Let $a$, $b$ and $c$ be positive integers, and let $A$ and $B$ be numbers with
\[A=\dfrac ab\qquad\text{and}\qquad A+B=\dfrac cb.\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Express $B$ in terms of $a$, $b$ and $c$.
\item[(b)] A student claims that $B=\dfrac ab\cdot\dfrac{a-c}{b}$. Show that the claim is false in general, and find exactly when it is true.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{Upper bound.} A line meets a circle in at most $2$ points, so each side of the square meets each circle in at most $2$ points, and each circle meets the square in at most $4\cdot2=8$ points. Two distinct circles meet in at most $2$ points. Hence there are at most $8+8+2=18$ intersection points.

\textbf{Construction.} Take the square with vertices $(\pm1,\pm1)$ and the circles of radius $\dfrac65$ centred at $\left(\pm\dfrac1{10},0\right)$. The circle centred at $\left(\dfrac1{10},0\right)$ meets
\begin{itemize}
\item the side $x=1$ where $y^2=\dfrac{36}{25}-\dfrac{81}{100}=\dfrac{63}{100}$, and the side $x=-1$ where $y^2=\dfrac{36}{25}-\dfrac{121}{100}=\dfrac{23}{100}$;
\item the sides $y=\pm1$ where $\left(x-\dfrac1{10}\right)^2=\dfrac{11}{25}$, i.e. $x=\dfrac1{10}\pm\sqrt{\dfrac{11}{25}}\approx0.76$ and $-0.56$.
\end{itemize}
All these points lie on the sides (their coordinates are between $-1$ and $1$), giving $8$ points; the other circle gives the $8$ mirror images in the $y$-axis, which are different points. The two circles meet at $\left(0,\pm\sqrt{\dfrac{143}{100}}\right)$, outside the square. So all $18$ points are distinct.

\begin{asy}
