size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(box((-1,-1),(1,1)),linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle((0.1,0),1.2),blue); draw(circle((-0.1,0),1.2),red);
real t=sqrt(0.44);
pair[] P={(1,sqrt(0.63)),(1,-sqrt(0.63)),(-1,sqrt(0.23)),(-1,-sqrt(0.23)),(0.1+t,1),(0.1-t,1),(0.1+t,-1),(0.1-t,-1),
 (-1,sqrt(0.63)),(-1,-sqrt(0.63)),(1,sqrt(0.23)),(1,-sqrt(0.23)),(-0.1+t,1),(-0.1-t,1),(-0.1+t,-1),(-0.1-t,-1),
 (0,sqrt(1.43)),(0,-sqrt(1.43))};
for(pair p:P) dot(p,linewidth(3));
\end{asy}

Therefore $N=18$ and $1000N=\boxed{18000}$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Let $P(x)$ and $D(x)$ be polynomials with real coefficients, where $D$ is not the zero polynomial. Prove that there are unique polynomials $Q(x)$ and $R(x)$ such that
\[P(x)=D(x)Q(x)+R(x),\qquad\text{where }R=0\text{ or }\deg R<\deg D.\]
\item[(b)] The condition in (a) compares the degree of the remainder with the degree of the divisor; nothing requires $\deg D<\deg P$. Illustrate this by dividing $P(x)=2x^2+3x+1$ by $D(x)=x^2+1$, which has the same degree.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The five cases do not overlap and cover all possibilities, so the last one has probability
\[1-\left(\dfrac14+\dfrac16+\dfrac18+\dfrac13\right)=1-\dfrac{6+4+3+8}{24}=\dfrac{3}{24}=\dfrac18.\]
In each case, multiply the probability of the case by the probability that Ron stays in that case:
\begin{itemize}
\item Hermione is furious: $\dfrac14\cdot\dfrac16=\dfrac1{24}$;
\item she sends him away: $\dfrac16\cdot\dfrac14=\dfrac1{24}$;
\item she forgives him outright: $\dfrac18\cdot1=\dfrac18$;
\item both tell him to leave: $\dfrac13\cdot\dfrac13=\dfrac19$;
\item she lets him stay: $\dfrac18\cdot1=\dfrac18$.
\end{itemize}
Adding these (the law of total probability), with common denominator $72$:
\[\dfrac{3}{72}+\dfrac{3}{72}+\dfrac{9}{72}+\dfrac{8}{72}+\dfrac{9}{72}=\dfrac{32}{72}=\boxed{\dfrac49}.\]

question:
Real numbers $b$ and $c$ are chosen independently and uniformly at random from the interval $[0,5]$, and the quadratic $x^2+bx+c=0$ is formed.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the probability that the product of the roots of the quadratic is greater than $3$?
\item[(b)] What is the probability that the quadratic has real roots and the product of its roots is greater than $3$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The point $(b,c)$ is uniformly distributed in the square $[0,5]\times[0,5]$ of area $25$, so each probability is an area divided by $25$. By Vieta's formulas the product of the roots of $x^2+bx+c=0$ is $c$ (this holds also when the roots are not real).

\textbf{(a)} The condition is just $c>3$, a strip of area $5\cdot2=10$ (light blue below). The probability is $\dfrac{10}{25}=\boxed{\dfrac25}$.

\textbf{(b)} The roots are real exactly when the discriminant is nonnegative: $b^2\ge4c$, i.e. $b\ge2\sqrt c$ (as $b,c\ge0$). So we need the area of the region $3<c\le5$, $2\sqrt c\le b\le5$ (orange below):

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return x^2/4;}
fill((0,3)--(5,3)--(5,5)--(0,5)--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
path cv=graph(f,2*sqrt(3),2*sqrt(5),100);
fill((2*sqrt(3),3)--(5,3)--(5,5)--(2*sqrt(5),5)--reverse(cv)--cycle,rgb(1,0.75,0.55));
draw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,5)--(0,5)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(graph(f,0,2*sqrt(5),200),red+linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,3)--(5,3),dashed);
draw((-0.3,0)--(5.7,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-0.3)--(0,5.7),Arrow(6));
label("$b$",(5.7,0),E); label("$c$",(0,5.7),N);
label("$5$",(5,0),S); label("$5$",(0,5),W); label("$3$",(0,3),W); label("$0$",(0,0),SW);
label("$b^2=4c$",(2.2,f(2.2)),SE,red);
label("$c=3$",(0.9,3),N);
\end{asy}

\[\int_3^5\left(5-2\sqrt c\right)dc=10-\dfrac43\left(5^{\dfrac32}-3^{\dfrac32}\right)=10-\dfrac{20\sqrt5}{3}+4\sqrt3.\]
Dividing by $25$, the probability is
\[\boxed{\dfrac{30-20\sqrt5+12\sqrt3}{75}}\approx0.0809.\]

Sam's score is $6$ times the number of correct answers, so it is $150$, $144$ or $138$ with $0$, $1$ or $2$ wrong answers. A score of $144$ or more always reaches the cutoff, and a score of $138$ reaches it with probability $\dfrac12$. All wrong answers come from the $10$ medium questions (each wrong with probability $0.2$) and the $5$ hard ones (each wrong with probability $0.5$).
\begin{itemize}
\item No wrong answer: $0.8^{10}\cdot0.5^5=0.0033554432$.
\item Exactly one wrong: $10\cdot0.2\cdot0.8^{9}\cdot0.5^5+0.8^{10}\cdot5\cdot0.5^5=0.008388608+0.016777216=0.025165824$.
\item Exactly two wrong: two medium, one of each, or two hard:
\begin{align*}
&\dbinom{10}{2}0.2^2\,0.8^8\,0.5^5+10\cdot0.2\cdot0.8^9\cdot5\cdot0.5^5+0.8^{10}\dbinom52 0.5^5\\
&\qquad=0.009437184+0.04194304+0.033554432=0.084934656.
\end{align*}
\end{itemize}
The probability is
\[0.0033554432+0.025165824+\dfrac12\cdot0.084934656=0.0709885952\approx\boxed{0.071}.\]

Number the vertices $0,1,\dots,2n-1$ around the polygon; vertex $k$ is opposite vertex $k+n$ (indices mod $2n$). Each move marks one pair of opposite vertices, one circled and one crossed out, so at the end every pair of opposite vertices has \emph{different} marks.

\emph{Necessity.} If no two adjacent vertices have the same mark, the marks alternate around the polygon (this is consistent because $2n$ is even), so vertex $k$ has the same mark as vertex $0$ exactly when $k$ is even. Vertices $0$ and $n$ are opposite, so they have different marks, which forces $n$ to be odd.

\emph{Sufficiency.} Let $n$ be odd. Circle the vertices $0,2,4,\dots,2n-2$ one at a time. Each of them is unmarked when chosen, and its opposite vertex $2j+n$ is odd, hence also unmarked; it gets crossed out. After $n$ moves the even vertices are circled and the odd ones are crossed out, so the marks alternate. The figure shows $n=3$.

\begin{asy}
