draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
for(int k=0;k<3;++k) draw(V[k]--V[k+3],gray+dashed);
for(int k=0;k<6;++k){
  if(k%2==0){ filldraw(circle(V[k],0.1),white,blue+linewidth(1)); }
  else { draw(V[k]+0.09*(-1,-1)--V[k]+0.09*(1,1),red+linewidth(1)); draw(V[k]+0.09*(-1,1)--V[k]+0.09*(1,-1),red+linewidth(1)); }
  label("$"+string(k)+"$",1.25*V[k]);
}
\end{asy}

So it can be done exactly when $\boxed{n\text{ is odd}}$, that is, when the number of sides is $6,10,14,\dots$ (twice an odd number).

Let $\ell$ be the line $O_1O_2$ and let $r_1,r_2$ be the radii, so $O_1T=r_1$ and $O_2T=r_2$.

Suppose, for contradiction, that $T$ is not on $\ell$. Let $T'$ be the reflection of $T$ in $\ell$; then $T'\ne T$. A reflection preserves distances and fixes every point of $\ell$, in particular $O_1$ and $O_2$. Hence
\[O_1T'=O_1T=r_1\qquad\text{and}\qquad O_2T'=O_2T=r_2,\]
so $T'$ lies on both circles. Then the circles have two common points $T$ and $T'$, contradicting the assumption that $T$ is the only one.

