draw((-1.9,0)--(3.8,0),dashed);
draw(O1--T--O2,gray); draw(O1--Tp--O2,gray);
draw(T--Tp,dotted);
dot(O1); dot(O2); dot(T); dot(Tp);
label("$O_1$",O1,SW); label("$O_2$",O2,SE); label("$T$",T,N); label("$T'$",Tp-(0,0.33));
label("$\ell$",(3.8,0),E);
\end{asy}

Therefore $T$ lies on $\ell$. $\blacksquare$

\emph{Remarks.} (1) The two cases are external tangency, with $T$ between the centres and $O_1O_2=r_1+r_2$, and internal tangency, with both centres on the same side of $T$ and $O_1O_2=\left|r_1-r_2\right|$.

