(2) The common tangent line at $T$ is perpendicular to $O_1T$ and to $O_2T$ (a tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact), which is another way to see that $O_1$, $T$, $O_2$ are collinear once one knows that tangent circles share a tangent line at $T$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why the sum of all the coefficients of a polynomial $P(x)$ equals $P(1)$.
\item[(b)] Find the sum of all the coefficients of the polynomial $\left(x^2-4x+1\right)^{2015}$ after it is expanded.
\end{enumerate}

For a positive real number $a$, the tetration ${}^{n}a$ is defined by ${}^{1}a=a$ and ${}^{n+1}a=a^{\left({}^{n}a\right)}$ for $n\ge1$; so ${}^{n}a$ is a tower of $n$ copies of $a$, evaluated from the top down.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Compute ${}^{3}2$ and ${}^{4}2$.
\item[(b)] Compare ${}^{3}3=3^{\left(3^3\right)}$ with the bottom-up tower $\left(3^3\right)^3$.
\item[(c)] Show that if $x={}^{n}2$, then applying $\log_2$ to $x$ exactly $n$ times gives $1$. Use this to find the $n$ with ${}^{n}2=65536$ (the ``super-logarithm'' of $65536$ to base $2$).
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $P(x)$ be the polynomial; the coefficient of $x^{1998-k}$ is $(-1)^k(k+1)$ for $k=0,1,\dots,1998$. We claim that
\[P(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{999}i\,x^{1998-2i}(x-1)^2+1000.\]
Indeed, $i\,x^{1998-2i}(x-1)^2=i\,x^{2000-2i}-2i\,x^{1999-2i}+i\,x^{1998-2i}$. Compare coefficients on the right:
\begin{itemize}
\item an odd power $x^{1999-2i}$ ($1\le i\le999$) comes only from the $i$-th term, with coefficient $-2i$; in $P$ its coefficient is $(-1)^{2i-1}\cdot2i=-2i$;
\item an even power $x^{1998-2j}$ with $1\le j\le998$ gets $j+1$ from the $(j+1)$-th term and $j$ from the $j$-th term, in total $2j+1$, as in $P$;
\item $x^{1998}$ gets $1$ (from $i=1$), and the constant term is $999+1000=1999$, as in $P$.
\end{itemize}
Every term of the sum is nonnegative for real $x$, because $x^{1998-2i}$ is an even power and $(x-1)^2\ge0$. Therefore
\[P(x)\ge1000>0\qquad\text{for all real }x,\]
so $P(x)\ne0$. $\blacksquare$

\emph{How to find the grouping.} For $x\le0$ every term of $P(x)$ is nonnegative and the constant is $1999$, so only $x>0$ is in question. Pairing terms as $x^{2m}-2x^{2m-1}+x^{2m-2}=x^{2m-2}(x-1)^2$ uses up the coefficients $1,-2,1$; the leftover $2x^{1996}$ starts the next group $2\left(x^{1996}-2x^{1995}+x^{1994}\right)$, and so on.

Let $X$ be a point of $t$ on the opposite side of line $AB$ from $C$.

\textbf{Step 1: $\angle ADE=\angle ACB$.} By the tangent-chord theorem, the angle between the tangent $t$ and the chord $AB$ equals the inscribed angle on the other side: $\angle XAB=\angle ACB$. Since $DE\parallel t$ and the line $AB$ crosses both, with $X$ and $E$ on opposite sides of $AB$, the angles $\angle XAD$ and $\angle ADE$ are alternate angles, so $\angle ADE=\angle XAB=\angle ACB$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real k=0.3;
pair A=(0,0), B=A+10k*dir(245), C=A+12k*dir(305);
pair cc(pair P1,pair P2,pair P3){ real d=2*(P1.x*(P2.y-P3.y)+P2.x*(P3.y-P1.y)+P3.x*(P1.y-P2.y));
  real ux=((P1.x^2+P1.y^2)*(P2.y-P3.y)+(P2.x^2+P2.y^2)*(P3.y-P1.y)+(P3.x^2+P3.y^2)*(P1.y-P2.y))/d;
  real uy=((P1.x^2+P1.y^2)*(P3.x-P2.x)+(P2.x^2+P2.y^2)*(P1.x-P3.x)+(P3.x^2+P3.y^2)*(P2.x-P1.x))/d; return (ux,uy);}
void angarc(pair V, pair P1, pair P2, real r, pen pn){
  real a1=degrees(P1-V), a2=degrees(P2-V); real d=a2-a1;
  while(d>180) d-=360; while(d<=-180) d+=360;
  if(d>=0) draw(arc(V,r,a1,a1+d),pn); else draw(arc(V,r,a1+d,a1),pn); }
real side(pair P){ return cross(B-A,P-A); }
pair O=cc(A,B,C); real R=abs(A-O);
pair D=A+6k*dir(245), E=A+5k*dir(305);
draw(circle(O,R),gray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
pair u=unit(rotate(90)*(A-O));
draw(A-2.2*u--A+2.2*u,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(D--E,red+linewidth(0.8));
pair X=A+1.3*u; if(side(X)*side(C)>0) X=A-1.3*u;
angarc(A,X,B,0.4,blue); angarc(D,A,E,0.4,red); angarc(C,A,B,0.5,heavygreen);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(E); dot(X,blue);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,W); label("$E$",E,NE);
label("$X$",X,N,blue); label("$t$",A+2.2*u,N,blue);
label("$6$",(A+D)/2,W); label("$5$",(A+E)/2,NE); label("$7$",(E+C)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 2: similar triangles.} Triangles $ADE$ and $ACB$ share the angle at $A$ and have $\angle ADE=\angle ACB$, so they are similar, with $D\leftrightarrow C$ and $E\leftrightarrow B$. Hence
\[\dfrac{AD}{AC}=\dfrac{AE}{AB}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad AD\cdot AB=AE\cdot AC.\]
(Equivalently, $B$, $C$, $E$, $D$ are concyclic and this is the power of the point $A$.)

\textbf{Step 3.} $AC=AE+EC=12$, so $6\cdot AB=5\cdot12$, $AB=10$, and
\[BD=AB-AD=10-6=\boxed{4}.\]
(The angle at $A$ is not determined, but the answer does not depend on it; the figure uses one possible triangle.)

Let $ABCD$ be the quadrilateral and $P,Q,R,S$ the midpoints of $AB,BC,CD,DA$.

\textbf{Midline lemma.} In triangle $XYZ$, if $M$ and $N$ are the midpoints of $XY$ and $YZ$, then $MN\parallel XZ$, $MN=\dfrac12XZ$, and the direction from $M$ to $N$ is the same as the direction from $X$ to $Z$. Proof: triangles $YMN$ and $YXZ$ share the angle at $Y$, and $\dfrac{YM}{YX}=\dfrac{YN}{YZ}=\dfrac12$, so they are similar (SAS) with ratio $\dfrac12$. Hence $MN=\dfrac12XZ$, and $\angle YMN=\angle YXZ$ gives $MN\parallel XZ$ (corresponding angles). Since $M$ and $X$ lie on the same ray from $Y$, and so do $N$ and $Z$, the segment $MN$ points the same way as $XZ$.

\textbf{The proof.} Draw the diagonal $AC$ (for a non-convex quadrilateral it may lie outside; this does not matter).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(5,-0.4), C=(4.2,2.6), D=(0.8,3.2);
