draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,gray+dashed);
draw(P--Q--R--Sx--cycle,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(P); dot(Q); dot(R); dot(Sx);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$P$",P,S); label("$Q$",Q,E); label("$R$",R,N); label("$S$",Sx,W);
label("convex",(2.5,-1.3));
\end{asy}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(5,0), C=(2.4,1.2), D=(1.6,3.6);
pair P=(A+B)/2, Q=(B+C)/2, R=(C+D)/2, Sx=(D+A)/2;
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,gray+dashed);
draw(P--Q--R--Sx--cycle,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(P); dot(Q); dot(R); dot(Sx);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,S); label("$D$",D,N);
label("$P$",P,S); label("$Q$",Q,NE); label("$R$",R,E); label("$S$",Sx,W);
label("non-convex",(2.5,-1.0));
\end{asy}

In triangle $BAC$ (with $P$ on $BA$ and $Q$ on $BC$) the lemma says that $PQ$ is parallel to $AC$, half as long, and points from the $A$-side to the $C$-side. In triangle $DAC$ (with $S$ on $DA$ and $R$ on $DC$) it says the same about $SR$. So the segments $PQ$ and $SR$ are parallel, equal and point the same way: moving from $P$ to $Q$ is the same translation as moving from $S$ to $R$. Hence $PQRS$ has a pair of opposite sides that are equal and parallel, so it is a parallelogram. (In the same way, using the diagonal $BD$, $QR$ and $PS$ are parallel and equal to $\dfrac12BD$.) $\blacksquare$

\emph{Non-degeneracy.} $PQRS$ could only collapse to a segment if $P,Q,R,S$ were collinear, which would force $AC\parallel BD$ (as $PQ\parallel AC$ and $QR\parallel BD$). That cannot happen when the sides of $ABCD$ do not cross: put $A,C$ on the line $y=0$ and $B,D$ on the line $y=1$, with $A$ to the left of $C$. If $B$ is left of $D$, the sides $BC$ and $DA$ cross; if $B$ is right of $D$, the sides $AB$ and $CD$ cross.

Let the base have sides $AB=EF=p$ and $BC=FG=q$; the volume gives $2pq=32$, so $pq=16$. At $F$ the three edges $FB=2$, $FE=p$, $FG=q$ are mutually perpendicular, so put $F$ at the origin with these edges along the axes: $B=(0,0,2)$, $E=(p,0,0)$, $G=(0,q,0)$. The plane $BEG$ is
\[\dfrac xp+\dfrac yq+\dfrac z2=1,\]
and the distance $d$ from $F=(0,0,0)$ to it satisfies
\[\dfrac{1}{d^2}=\dfrac{1}{p^2}+\dfrac{1}{q^2}+\dfrac14.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real p=5, q=3, h=2;
pair P(real x,real y,real z){return (x-0.55*y, z+0.4*y);}
pair A=P(0,0,0), B=P(p,0,0), C=P(p,q,0), D=P(0,q,0), E=P(0,0,h), F=P(p,0,h), G=P(p,q,h), H=P(0,q,h);
fill(B--E--G--cycle,rgb(1,0.82,0.62));
draw(A--B--F--E--cycle,linewidth(0.8)); draw(E--H--G--F,linewidth(0.8)); draw(A--D--H,linewidth(0.8));
draw(D--C,dashed); draw(C--B,dashed); draw(C--G,dashed);
draw(B--E--G--cycle,red+linewidth(0.9));
real a1=1/p, a2=1/q, a3=1/h; real tt=1/(a1^2+a2^2+a3^2);
pair K=P(p-tt*a1,tt*a2,h-tt*a3);
draw(F--K,blue+linewidth(0.9)); dot(K,blue); dot(F);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NW); label("$D$",D,W);
label("$E$",E,W); label("$F$",F,E); label("$G$",G,NE); label("$H$",H,NW);
label("$2$",(B+F)/2,E); label("$p$",(E+F)/2,S); label("$q$",(F+G)/2,NE);
label("$d$",(F+K)/2,N,blue);
\end{asy}

By AM-GM, $\dfrac1{p^2}+\dfrac1{q^2}\ge\dfrac{2}{pq}=\dfrac18$, with equality when $p=q=4$. Hence
\[\dfrac1{d^2}\ge\dfrac18+\dfrac14=\dfrac38,\qquad d\le\sqrt{\dfrac83}=\dfrac{2\sqrt6}{3},\]
and this maximum is attained for the $4\times4\times2$ prism. So $a=2$, $b=6$, $c=3$ and $abc=\boxed{36}$.

\emph{Distance to the plane or to the triangle?} They are the same here. The triangle $BEG$ is acute (for instance $BE^2+BG^2-EG^2=(p^2+4)+(q^2+4)-(p^2+q^2)=8>0$, and similarly at $E$ and $G$), and the foot of the perpendicular from $F$ is the orthocentre of $BEG$: the perpendicular from $F$ is orthogonal to the plane, and $FB$ is orthogonal to the plane $FEG$, so the plane through $FB$ and the perpendicular is orthogonal to $EG$. The orthocentre of an acute triangle lies inside it, so the closest point of the triangle to $F$ is this foot.

With $x$ movies and $y$ games ($x,y\ge0$ integers) the constraints are
\[2x+20y\le180\iff x+10y\le90\quad(\text{time}),\qquad10x+40y\le600\iff x+4y\le60\quad(\text{money}),\]
and the number of new friends is $x+3y$.

The point $(90,0)$ satisfies the time constraint but not the money constraint: $90$ movies cost $\$900$. The best point must satisfy \emph{both} constraints, so the corners of the feasible region are $(0,0)$, $(60,0)$, $(40,5)$ and $(0,9)$, where $(40,5)$ is the intersection of $x+10y=90$ and $x+4y=60$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real sx=0.1;
pair O=(0,0), P1=(60*sx,0), P2=(40*sx,5*0.4), P3=(0,9*0.4);
fill(O--P1--P2--P3--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
draw((90*sx,0)--(0,9*0.4),red); draw((60*sx,0)--(0,15*0.4),blue);
draw((-0.2,0)--(10.4,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-0.2)--(0,6.6),Arrow(6));
label("$x$ (movies)",(10.4,0),E); label("$y$ (games)",(0,6.6),N);
dot(P1); dot(P2); dot(P3); dot((90*sx,0),red);
label("$(60,0)$",P1,S); label("$(40,5)$",P2,NE); label("$(0,9)$",P3,W); label("$(90,0)$",(90*sx,0),S,red);
label("time: $x+10y=90$",(7.0,1.6),NE,red); label("money: $x+4y=60$",(1.6,4.6),E,blue);
\end{asy}

At the corners $x+3y$ equals $0$, $60$, $55$ and $27$. In fact the maximum can be seen directly: for every feasible point,
\[x+3y\le x+4y\le60,\]
with equality only when $y=0$ and $x=60$. So I should watch $\boxed{60\text{ movies}}$ and buy no games, making $60$ new friends.

question:
Row $1$ starts with $2$ light switches, all off. The following process is applied to a row whose switches are all off:
\begin{enumerate}
\item add one switch, turned off, to the next row;
\item in the current row, turn on the rightmost switch that is off, and turn off all switches to its right;
\item repeat steps 1 and 2 until all switches of the current row are on; then add one more switch to the next row and apply the whole process to the next row.
\end{enumerate}
For example, row $2$ ends up with $4$ switches. The number of switches in row $6$ can be written as $a^b$, where $a$ is a prime and $b$ is a positive integer. Find the last two digits of $a+b$.

solution:
For each colour, its two faces are either opposite or adjacent. If two colours each occupy a pair of opposite faces, the remaining two faces are opposite as well, so the number of colours with opposite faces is $3$, $1$ or $0$.

\textbf{All three colours on opposite pairs.} Rotate so that red is on top and bottom; a quarter turn about the vertical axis puts green in front and back. So there is exactly $1$ such cube.

\textbf{Exactly one colour on opposite faces.} Choose that colour ($3$ ways) and put it on top and bottom. The four side faces form a ring, and each of the other two colours occupies two \emph{adjacent} faces of the ring; so the ring reads $XXYY$ for the two other colours $X,Y$, starting at one of $4$ places. Quarter turns about the vertical axis move the starting place around the ring, so all these cubes are the same. This gives $3$ cubes.

\textbf{No colour on opposite faces.} Rotate so that red is on top and front. The other faces are bottom, back, left, right, where left and right are opposite. Green and blue must split them into two adjacent pairs, so the split is $\{\text{bottom},\text{left}\}\cup\{\text{back},\text{right}\}$ or $\{\text{bottom},\text{right}\}\cup\{\text{back},\text{left}\}$. The only rotations that keep the red pair $\{\text{top},\text{front}\}$ in place are the identity and the half turn $h$ about the axis through the midpoints of the top-front and bottom-back edges, which swaps top with front, bottom with back and left with right. The rotation $h$ swaps the two pairs of each split (for example $\{\text{bottom},\text{left}\}\leftrightarrow\{\text{back},\text{right}\}$), so the choice of which pair is green does not matter. But neither the identity nor $h$ turns one split into the other, so the two splits give different cubes (they are mirror images). This gives $2$ cubes.

In total there are $1+3+2=\boxed{6}$ cubes. The nets below show one cube of each kind (top face above the middle face, bottom face below it; the long row is left, front, right, back).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pen cR=rgb(0.95,0.45,0.45), cG=rgb(0.5,0.85,0.5), cB=rgb(0.5,0.65,1);
pen col(string c){ if(c=="R") return cR; if(c=="G") return cG; return cB; }
// faces: top, left, front, right, back, bottom
pair[] pos={(1,2),(0,1),(1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(1,0)};
void net(pair o, string s, string name){
  for(int i=0;i<6;++i){
    pair c=o+pos[i];
    filldraw(shift(c)*unitsquare,col(substr(s,i,1)),black);
    label(substr(s,i,1),c+(0.5,0.5),fontsize(8pt));
  }
  label(name,o+(2,-0.45),fontsize(9pt));
}
net((0,4.6),"RBGBGR","1: all opposite");
net((5,4.6),"RBGGBR","2: R opposite");
net((10,4.6),"GBRRBG","3: G opposite");
net((0,0),"BGRRGB","4: B opposite");
net((5,0),"RGRBBG","5: none opposite");
net((10,0),"RBRGBG","6: none opposite");
\end{asy}

\emph{Check with Burnside's lemma.} There are $\dfrac{6!}{2!\,2!\,2!}=90$ colourings. The $24$ rotations fix: the identity, $90$; the $6$ quarter turns about face axes, $0$; the $3$ half turns about face axes, $6$ each (the two faces on the axis share one colour and the two swapped pairs take the other two colours); the $8$ turns about vertex axes, $0$; the $6$ half turns about edge axes, $3!=6$ each. The number of distinct cubes is $\dfrac{90+18+36}{24}=6$.

\textbf{Which sides form the obtuse angle?} Say the obtuse angle is formed by sides of lengths $u$ and $w$; the right triangle has legs $u,w$ and area $\dfrac{uw}2=75$, so $uw=150$. Suppose the angle were not the one between the sides $10$ and $15$. Then one of $u,w$ is the third side $x$ and the other is $10$ or $15$, and $uw=150$ gives $x=15$ or $x=10$ respectively. So the triangle would have sides $10,15,15$ with the angle opposite a side $15$, or sides $10,10,15$ with the angle opposite a side $10$. But an obtuse angle lies opposite the strictly longest side of a triangle (its square exceeds the sum of the squares of the other two), and in neither case is the opposite side strictly longest. So the obtuse angle is the angle between the sides $10$ and $15$.

\textbf{The area is not determined.} Put the side of length $15$ along $PQ$ and the side of length $10$ along $PA$, with obtuse angle $\angle APQ$. The area is $\dfrac12\cdot15\cdot h$, where $h$ is the height of $A$ above the line $PQ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(10cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
void panel(pair o, real ang, pen pn, string lab){
  pair Pv=o, Q=o+(5,0), A=o+(10/3)*dir(ang), A0=o+(10/3)*dir(90);
  pair Fo=(A.x,o.y);
  draw(Fo--Pv,gray+dashed);
  draw(Pv--A0--Q,gray+dashed);
  draw(A--Fo,dotted);
  draw(Pv--Q,linewidth(0.9)); draw(Pv--A--Q,pn+linewidth(0.9));
  draw(arc(Pv,0.45,0,ang),pn);
  dot(Pv); dot(Q); dot(A); dot(A0,gray);
  label("$P$",Pv,S); label("$Q$",Q,S); label("$15$",(Pv+Q)/2,S); label("$A$",A,W,pn); label("$A_0$",A0,N,gray);
  label("$10$",(ang<135 ? Pv+0.35*(A-Pv) : (Pv+A)/2),dir(ang+90),pn); label("$h$",(A+Fo)/2,W);
  label(lab,Pv+0.95*dir(38),pn+fontsize(8pt));
}
panel((0,0),120,blue,"$120^\circ$");
panel((9.6,0),150,red,"$150^\circ$");
\end{asy}

\begin{itemize}
\item If $\angle APQ=120^\circ$, then $A$ is $10\sin60^\circ=5\sqrt3$ above $PQ$ (a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle), and the area is $\dfrac{75\sqrt3}{2}\approx64.95$.
\item If $\angle APQ=150^\circ$, then $h=10\sin30^\circ=5$ and the area is $\dfrac{75}{2}=37.5$.
\end{itemize}
Two different triangles fit the description, so the area \textbf{cannot be determined}. In general the area is $\dfrac12\cdot10\cdot15\sin\angle APQ=75\sin\angle APQ$, and as the angle runs over all obtuse values ($90^\circ<\angle APQ<180^\circ$) the sine takes every value strictly between $0$ and $1$. So the possible areas are exactly the numbers in the open interval $\boxed{(0,75)}$.

Take the rectangular hyperbola $xy=c^2$ ($c>0$), whose asymptotes are the coordinate axes, and the point $P=\left(ct,\dfrac ct\right)$ with $t\ne0$.

\textbf{Tangent.} The line $\dfrac{x}{2ct}+\dfrac{y}{\dfrac{2c}{t}}=1$, i.e. $y=\dfrac{2c}{t}-\dfrac{x}{t^2}$, passes through $P$. Substituting into $xy=c^2$ gives $\dfrac{2cx}{t}-\dfrac{x^2}{t^2}=c^2$, i.e. $(x-ct)^2=0$: the line meets the hyperbola only at $P$, with a double root, so it is the tangent at $P$. Its slope is $-\dfrac1{t^2}$, and its intercepts are $a_1=2ct$ on the $x$-axis and $b_1=\dfrac{2c}{t}$ on the $y$-axis.

\textbf{Normal.} It is perpendicular to the tangent, so it has slope $t^2$: $y-\dfrac ct=t^2(x-ct)$. Setting $y=0$ and $x=0$:
\[a_2=ct-\dfrac{c}{t^3}=\dfrac{c\left(t^4-1\right)}{t^3},\qquad b_2=\dfrac ct-ct^3=\dfrac{c\left(1-t^4\right)}{t}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real c=1, t=1.4;
real f(real x){return c^2/x;}
pair Pp=(c*t,c/t);
draw((-0.6,0)--(4.4,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-2.7)--(0,3.6),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(4.4,0),E); label("$y$",(0,3.6),N);
draw(graph(f,0.3,4.2),black+linewidth(0.9));
real tl(real x){return 2*c/t-x/t^2;}
draw((-0.3,tl(-0.3))--(3.7,tl(3.7)),blue+linewidth(0.8));
real m=t^2;
real nl(real x){return c/t+m*(x-c*t);}
draw((-0.25,nl(-0.25))--(2.25,nl(2.25)),red+linewidth(0.8));
pair a1=(2*c*t,0), b1=(0,2*c/t), a2=(c*t-c/t^3,0), b2=(0,c/t-c*t^3);
dot(Pp); dot(a1,blue); dot(b1,blue); dot(a2,red); dot(b2,red);
label("$P$",Pp+(0.32,0.2));
label("$a_1$",a1,N,blue); label("$b_1$",b1,W,blue); label("$a_2$",a2,NW,red); label("$b_2$",b2,W,red);
label("$xy=c^2$",(4.2,f(4.2)),N);
label("tangent",(3.7,tl(3.7)),S,blue); label("normal",(2.25,nl(2.25)),E,red);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Products.}
\[a_1a_2=2ct\cdot\dfrac{c\left(t^4-1\right)}{t^3}=\dfrac{2c^2\left(t^4-1\right)}{t^2},\qquad b_1b_2=\dfrac{2c}{t}\cdot\dfrac{c\left(1-t^4\right)}{t}=\dfrac{2c^2\left(1-t^4\right)}{t^2}.\]
So $a_1a_2+b_1b_2=0$ for every point $P$, and the answer is $\boxed{(4)}$. The relation is symmetric in the two axes, so it does not matter which axis carries the $a$'s. Statement (1) holds only when $t^4=1$ (then the normal passes through the origin, $a_2=b_2=0$, and both products are $0$), and (2) and (3) fail in general (for example at $t=2$, $c=1$: $a_1a_2=\dfrac{15}{2}$, $b_1b_2=-\dfrac{15}{2}$, $a_1b_1+a_2b_2=4-\dfrac{225}{16}\ne0$).

\emph{Remark.} For the other standard form $x^2-y^2=k^2$, at a point $(x_0,y_0)$ with $x_0y_0\ne0$ the tangent $x_0x-y_0y=k^2$ has intercepts $\dfrac{k^2}{x_0}$ and $-\dfrac{k^2}{y_0}$, and the normal has intercepts $2x_0$ and $2y_0$; again $a_1a_2+b_1b_2=2k^2-2k^2=0$.

The line $3x+2y+4=0$ is $y=-\dfrac32x-2$, with slope $-\dfrac32$. A perpendicular line has slope $\dfrac23$ (the product of the slopes is $-1$). Through $(2,3)$:
\[y-3=\dfrac23(x-2)\iff3y-9=2x-4\iff\boxed{2x-3y+5=0}.\]
(Check: $2\cdot2-3\cdot3+5=0$, and the normal vectors $(3,2)$ and $(2,-3)$ are orthogonal.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
draw((-3.2,0)--(5.2,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-3.6)--(0,5.4),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(5.2,0),E); label("$y$",(0,5.4),N);
real g(real x){return (-3*x-4)/2;}
real h(real x){return (2*x+5)/3;}
draw(graph(g,-3,0.8),blue+linewidth(0.8)); draw(graph(h,-3,5),red+linewidth(0.8));
pair Q=(2,3); dot(Q); label("$(2,3)$",Q,SE);
label("$3x+2y+4=0$",(0.8,g(0.8)),E,blue); label("$2x-3y+5=0$",(4.9,h(4.9)),SE,red);
pair X=intersectionpoint(graph(g,-3,0.8),graph(h,-3,5));
dot(X);
path sq=X+0.3*unit((1,2/3))--X+0.3*unit((1,2/3))+0.3*unit((-2,3))--X+0.3*unit((-2,3));
draw(sq);
\end{asy}

The third angle is $180^\circ-15^\circ-30^\circ=135^\circ$. By the law of sines the sides opposite $15^\circ$, $30^\circ$, $135^\circ$ are proportional to
\[\sin15^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt6-\sqrt2}{4},\qquad\sin30^\circ=\dfrac12,\qquad\sin135^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2},\]
that is, to $\sqrt6-\sqrt2,\ 2,\ 2\sqrt2$. These add up to $\sqrt6+\sqrt2+2=\sqrt2\left(\sqrt3+1+\sqrt2\right)$, while the perimeter is $\sqrt{10}+\sqrt{20}+\sqrt{30}=\sqrt{10}\left(1+\sqrt2+\sqrt3\right)$. So the scale factor is $\dfrac{\sqrt{10}}{\sqrt2}=\sqrt5$ and the sides are
\[\sqrt5\left(\sqrt6-\sqrt2\right)=\sqrt{30}-\sqrt{10},\qquad2\sqrt5=\sqrt{20},\qquad2\sqrt{10}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real k=1.2;
pair B=(0,0), C=(2*sqrt(10)*k,0);
pair A=B+sqrt(20)*k*dir(15);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,E);
label("$15^\circ$",B+(1.35,0.17),fontsize(8pt));
label("$30^\circ$",C+(-1.3,0.3),fontsize(8pt));
label("$135^\circ$",A+(0,-0.45),fontsize(8pt));
label("$2\sqrt{10}$",(B+C)/2,S); label("$\sqrt{20}$",(A+B)/2,N); label("$\sqrt{30}-\sqrt{10}$",(A+C)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

The angle between the sides $\sqrt{20}$ and $2\sqrt{10}$ is the $15^\circ$ angle, so the area is
\[\dfrac12\cdot\sqrt{20}\cdot2\sqrt{10}\cdot\sin15^\circ=10\sqrt2\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt6-\sqrt2}{4}=\dfrac{10\sqrt{12}-20}{4}=5\sqrt3-5.\]
So $a=-5$, $b=5$, $c=3$, and $abc=\boxed{-75}$. (Check with another pair of sides: $\dfrac12\left(\sqrt{30}-\sqrt{10}\right)\cdot2\sqrt5\cdot\sin135^\circ=5\sqrt3-5$.)

question:
On any given workday, Methelagona the miner finds iron with probability $\dfrac{1}{1.125}$ and finds diamonds with probability $\dfrac{1}{2.325}$, and these two events are independent. On a randomly selected day, what is the probability that he finds
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] iron but no diamonds;
\item[(b)] diamonds but no iron;
\item[(c)] both iron and diamonds;
\item[(d)] neither iron nor diamonds?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
First simplify the probabilities:
\[P(I)=\dfrac{1}{1.125}=\dfrac{1000}{1125}=\dfrac89,\qquad P(D)=\dfrac{1}{2.325}=\dfrac{1000}{2325}=\dfrac{40}{93},\]
so $P(\text{no iron})=\dfrac19$ and $P(\text{no diamonds})=\dfrac{53}{93}$. By independence (which also holds for the complementary events):
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $\dfrac89\cdot\dfrac{53}{93}=\boxed{\dfrac{424}{837}}$;
\item[(b)] $\dfrac19\cdot\dfrac{40}{93}=\boxed{\dfrac{40}{837}}$;
\item[(c)] $\dfrac89\cdot\dfrac{40}{93}=\boxed{\dfrac{320}{837}}$;
\item[(d)] $\dfrac19\cdot\dfrac{53}{93}=\boxed{\dfrac{53}{837}}$.
\end{enumerate}
As a check, the four cases cover all possibilities and $424+40+320+53=837$.

question:
Consider the integers $n$ with $-2021<n<2021$. Here a perfect square means the square of an integer and a perfect cube means the cube of an integer (so $0$ is both).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many of these integers are perfect squares?
\item[(b)] How many are perfect cubes?
\item[(c)] How many are perfect squares or perfect cubes (or both)?
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Is it true that $\sqrt[8]{x^8}=x$ for every real number $x$? If not, what is $\sqrt[8]{x^8}$?
\item[(b)] For an integer $n\ge2$, is it true that $\sqrt[n]{ab}=\sqrt[n]a\cdot\sqrt[n]b$? Determine for which real numbers $a,b$ it holds, with real $n$-th roots.
\end{enumerate}

Methuselah takes $3$ tubs of pecan ice cream and scoops $8$ equally sized scoops from each tub. He counts the pecans in every scoop and finds that the average number of pecans per scoop is a whole number that is
\begin{itemize}
\item divisible by two distinct primes that have at least one prime between them (such as $2$ and $5$, with $3$ between them), and
\item divisible by a perfect number (a positive integer equal to the sum of its divisors smaller than itself, such as $6=1+2+3$).
\end{itemize}
What is the smallest possible total number of pecans in all the scoops?

solution:
Let the polyhedron have $t$ triangular and $p$ pentagonal faces, so $t+p=32$.

\textbf{Counting corners.} Each triangle has $3$ corners and each vertex lies on $T$ triangles, so $3t=VT$; similarly $5p=VP$.

\textbf{Counting edges.} Each edge lies on two faces, so $2E=3t+5p=V(T+P)$.

\textbf{Euler.} $V-E+F=2$ becomes $V-\dfrac{V(T+P)}{2}+32=2$, i.e.
\[V(T+P-2)=60.\]
Also $t+p=32$ gives $\dfrac{VT}{3}+\dfrac{VP}{5}=32$, i.e. $V(5T+3P)=480$. Dividing the two equations,
\[\dfrac{5T+3P}{T+P-2}=8\iff3T+5P=16.\]
In nonnegative integers the only solution is $T=P=2$ ($P=0,1,3$ give $3T=16,11,1$). Then $V=\dfrac{60}{2}=30$, $t=20$, $p=12$, $E=60$, and indeed $30-60+32=2$. (This is the icosidodecahedron: around each vertex two triangles and two pentagons alternate, using $2\cdot60^\circ+2\cdot108^\circ=336^\circ$, as in the sketch.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair V=(0,0);
path regpoly(pair O, real alpha, int n){
  pair P=O; path g=P; real d=alpha;
  for(int i=0;i<n-1;++i){ P=P+dir(d); g=g--P; d+=360/n; }
  return g--cycle; }
filldraw(regpoly(V,0,3),rgb(0.75,0.85,1),black);
filldraw(regpoly(V,60,5),rgb(1,0.85,0.6),black);
filldraw(regpoly(V,168,3),rgb(0.75,0.85,1),black);
filldraw(regpoly(V,228,5),rgb(1,0.85,0.6),black);
dot(V);
label("$T$",V+0.6*dir(30)); label("$P$",V+1.25*dir(114)); label("$T$",V+0.6*dir(198)); label("$P$",V+1.25*dir(282));
draw(arc(V,0.45,336,360),red); label("$24^\circ$",V+0.75*dir(348),red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

So $100P+10T+V=200+20+30=\boxed{250}$.

