Count the bars by direction. The bars parallel to an edge of length $L$ run the full length $L$ along the four faces that are parallel to that edge, and they cross the rectangular cross-section perpendicular to them exactly at the lattice points of its boundary (a bar along an edge of the tank is one bar, shared by two faces).
\begin{itemize}
\item Bars of length $8$: the cross-section is a $5\times6$ rectangle, whose boundary contains $2(5+6)=22$ lattice points, so there are $22$ bars: $22\cdot8=176$ inches.
\item Bars of length $6$: the cross-section is $5\times8$, with $2(5+8)=26$ boundary lattice points: $26\cdot6=156$ inches.
\item Bars of length $5$: the cross-section is $6\times8$, with $2(6+8)=28$ boundary lattice points: $28\cdot5=140$ inches.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=8, bb=5, c=6;
pair P(real x,real y,real z){return (x+0.5*y, z+0.35*y);}
pen g=gray(0.45)+linewidth(0.5);
for(int i=0;i<=8;++i) draw(P(i,0,0)--P(i,0,c),g);
for(int k=0;k<=6;++k) draw(P(0,0,k)--P(a,0,k),g);
for(int i=0;i<=8;++i) draw(P(i,0,c)--P(i,bb,c),g);
for(int j=0;j<=5;++j) draw(P(0,j,c)--P(a,j,c),g);
for(int j=0;j<=5;++j) draw(P(a,j,0)--P(a,j,c),g);
for(int k=0;k<=6;++k) draw(P(a,0,k)--P(a,bb,k),g);
draw(P(0,0,0)--P(a,0,0)--P(a,bb,0)--P(a,bb,c)--P(0,bb,c)--P(0,0,c)--cycle,black+linewidth(0.9));
draw(P(0,0,c)--P(a,0,c)--P(a,0,0),black+linewidth(0.9)); draw(P(a,0,c)--P(a,bb,c),black+linewidth(0.9));
label("$8$",P(a/2,0,0),S); label("$6$",P(0,0,c/2),W); label("$5$",P(a,bb/2,0),SE);
\end{asy}

The total is $176+156+140=\boxed{472\text{ inches}}$.

\emph{Check.} Adding up the grids face by face, an $m\times n$ face has $(m+1)$ bars of length $n$ and $(n+1)$ of length $m$, giving $2(71+93+110)=548$; this counts each of the $12$ edges twice, and the edges have total length $4(5+6+8)=76$, so the total is $548-76=472$.

