Five unit squares are placed in the plane without overlapping to form a cross: a central square with one square attached to each of its four sides.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
path sq(pair p){return shift(p)*((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle);}
pair[] P={(0,0),(0,1),(0,-1),(1,0),(-1,0)};
for(int i=0;i<5;++i) {fill(sq(P[i]),rgb(0.88,0.92,1)); draw(sq(P[i]),gray);}
draw((0,-1)--(1,-1)--(1,0)--(2,0)--(2,1)--(1,1)--(1,2)--(0,2)--(0,1)--(-1,1)--(-1,0)--(0,0)--cycle,linewidth(1));
\end{asy}

Two points are chosen independently and uniformly at random inside the cross. What is the probability that the segment joining them crosses the perimeter (the outer boundary) of the cross?

solution:
Put the central square $C$ at $[0,1]\times[0,1]$, with arms $N=[0,1]\times[1,2]$, $S=[0,1]\times[-1,0]$, $E=[1,2]\times[0,1]$ and $W=[-1,0]\times[0,1]$. The cross has area $5$, so each point lies in each of the five squares with probability $\dfrac15$, independently. Boundary cases (a point on an edge, a segment through a corner) have probability $0$ and are ignored. A segment between two points of the cross crosses the perimeter exactly when it leaves the cross.

\textbf{Pairs that never leave the cross.} If both points lie in the same square, or one lies in $C$ and the other in an arm, or they lie in opposite arms, then both points lie in one rectangle ($C$ with one arm is a $1\times2$ rectangle; $N$, $C$, $S$ together form the $1\times3$ rectangle $[0,1]\times[-1,2]$, and likewise $W$, $C$, $E$). A rectangle is convex, so the segment stays inside it.

\textbf{Adjacent arms.} The remaining case is two adjacent arms, for example $P\in N$ and $Q\in E$. Write
\[P=(1-u,\,1+s),\qquad Q=(1+t,\,1-v),\]
where $u,s,t,v$ are independent and uniform on $[0,1]$: $u$ and $t$ are the horizontal distances of $P$ and $Q$ from the line $x=1$, and $s$ and $v$ their vertical distances from the line $y=1$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
path sq(pair p){return shift(p)*((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle);}
fill(sq((0,0)),rgb(0.88,0.92,1)); fill(sq((0,1)),rgb(0.88,0.92,1)); fill(sq((1,0)),rgb(0.88,0.92,1));
fill(sq((0,-1)),rgb(0.95,0.95,0.95)); fill(sq((-1,0)),rgb(0.95,0.95,0.95));
draw((0,-1)--(1,-1)--(1,0)--(2,0)--(2,1)--(1,1)--(1,2)--(0,2)--(0,1)--(-1,1)--(-1,0)--(0,0)--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle,gray);
draw((1,1)--(2,1)--(2,2)--(1,2),dotted);
label("missing",(1.5,1.6),fontsize(8pt)); label("corner",(1.5,1.4),fontsize(8pt));
draw((1,-1.25)--(1,2.25),dashed);
label("$x=1$",(1,-1.25),S,fontsize(9pt));
pair Pp=(0.35,1.7), Qp=(1.75,0.55);
draw(Pp--Qp,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(Pp); dot(Qp);
label("$P$",Pp,NW); label("$Q$",Qp,SE);
draw(Pp--(1,1.7),dotted); label("$u$",(0.675,1.7),N,fontsize(9pt));
draw(Pp--(0.35,1),dotted); label("$s$",(0.35,1.35),W,fontsize(9pt));
draw(Qp--(1,0.55),dotted); label("$t$",(1.375,0.55),S,fontsize(9pt));
draw(Qp--(1.75,1),dotted); label("$v$",(1.75,0.78),E,fontsize(9pt));
label("$N$",(0.15,1.12),fontsize(8pt)); label("$E$",(1.12,0.12),fontsize(8pt)); label("$C$",(0.12,0.12),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The segment $PQ$ crosses the line $x=1$ at the point dividing $PQ$ in the ratio $u:t$, namely $\dfrac{tP+uQ}{u+t}$, whose height is
\[y^*=\dfrac{t(1+s)+u(1-v)}{u+t}=1+\dfrac{st-uv}{u+t}.\]
\begin{itemize}
\item If $y^*\le1$: the part of the segment with $x\le1$ has heights between $1-v\ge0$ and $1+s\le2$, so it lies in the column $C\cup N$; the part with $x\ge1$ has heights between $y^*$ and $1-v$, both in $[0,1]$, so it lies in $E$. The segment stays inside the cross.
\item If $y^*>1$: just to the right of the line $x=1$ the segment is still above the line $y=1$, so it passes through the missing corner square $(1,2)\times(1,2)$ and leaves the cross.
\end{itemize}
So, given $P\in N$ and $Q\in E$, the segment leaves the cross exactly when $st>uv$. The products $st$ and $uv$ are independent and have the same distribution, so by symmetry $P(st>uv)=P(uv>st)$; and $P(st=uv)=0$. Hence this conditional probability is $\dfrac12$.

By symmetry the same holds for each of the $4$ pairs of adjacent arms $\{N,E\},\{E,S\},\{S,W\},\{W,N\}$, with either point in either arm. The probability that the two points lie in a given pair of adjacent arms (in either order) is $2\cdot\dfrac15\cdot\dfrac15=\dfrac{2}{25}$. Therefore the required probability is
\[4\cdot\dfrac{2}{25}\cdot\dfrac12=\boxed{\dfrac{4}{25}}.\]

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that if $n$ is a positive integer and $2^n+n^{2020}$ is prime, then $n=1$ or $n$ is an odd multiple of $3$.
\item[(b)] Find all positive integers $n$ for which $2^n+n^{2016}$ is prime.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Write $G,I,N,T$ for the four words. Build a sentence one word at a time from the left. A prefix can be completed to a sentence only if every $I$ in it, except possibly the last word, already has an $N$ next to it. Sort the prefixes of length $L$ by their last word:
\begin{itemize}
\item $g_L$, $t_L$, $n_L$: prefixes ending in $G$, $T$, $N$;
\item $s_L$: prefixes ending in an $I$ that already has $N$ on its left;
\item $u_L$: prefixes ending in an $I$ that does not have $N$ on its left (it will need $N$ as the next word).
\end{itemize}
Appending a word: $G$ may follow $G,T,N$ (not $I$); $T$ may follow $G,T,N$ or a satisfied $I$; $N$ may follow anything; an $I$ after $N$ is satisfied, an $I$ after $T$ or after a satisfied $I$ is unsatisfied, and nothing except $N$ may follow an unsatisfied $I$ (and $I$ may never follow $G$). Hence
\[g_{L+1}=g_L+t_L+n_L,\quad t_{L+1}=g_L+t_L+n_L+s_L,\quad n_{L+1}=g_L+t_L+n_L+s_L+u_L,\]
\[s_{L+1}=n_L,\qquad u_{L+1}=t_L+s_L,\]
starting from $(g_1,t_1,n_1,s_1,u_1)=(1,1,1,0,1)$. A prefix is a complete sentence exactly when it does not end in an unsatisfied $I$, so the number of sentences of length $L$ is $g_L+t_L+n_L+s_L$.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|ccccc|c}
$L$ & $g_L$ & $t_L$ & $n_L$ & $s_L$ & $u_L$ & sentences\\ \hline
1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 3\\
2 & 3 & 3 & 4 & 1 & 1 & 11\\
3 & 10 & 11 & 12 & 4 & 4 & 37\\
4 & 33 & 37 & 41 & 12 & 15 & 123\\
5 & 111 & 123 & 138 & 41 & 49 & 413\\
6 & 372 & 413 & 462 & 138 & 164 & 1385
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
(For example, the $11$ two-word sentences are the $9$ sentences made of $G,T,N$ only, together with $NI$ and $IN$.) In total there are
\[3+11+37+123+413+1385=\boxed{1972}\]
sentences.

question:
Let $y$ be a positive real number.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Write $y\cdot y^{\dfrac23}$ as a single power of $y$.
\item[(b)] Show that there is no fixed number $k$ such that $y^4+y^2+y^{\dfrac34}=y^k$ for all $y>0$; so this sum cannot be simplified to a single power of $y$.
\item[(c)] Factor $y^4+y^{\dfrac14}+y^{\dfrac54}$ by taking out the lowest power of $y$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $a>0$ and let $m,n$ be positive integers. Here $\sqrt[n]{a}$ denotes the unique positive real number whose $n$-th power is $a$, and $a^{\dfrac mn}$ is defined as $\left(\sqrt[n]{a}\right)^m$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $a^{\dfrac mn}=\sqrt[n]{a^m}$, that is, $\left(\sqrt[n]{a}\right)^m=\sqrt[n]{a^m}$.
\item[(b)] Prove that the definition does not depend on how the fraction is written: if $\dfrac mn=\dfrac pq$ with positive integers $p,q$, then $\left(\sqrt[n]{a}\right)^m=\left(\sqrt[q]{a}\right)^p$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Describe and sketch the set of points $(x,y)$ in the coordinate plane with $x=\sqrt y$.
\item[(b)] Let $n\ge2$ be an integer. Describe the graph of $x=\sqrt[n]{y}$, treating even and odd $n$ separately. (For odd $n$, $\sqrt[n]{y}$ is the real $n$-th root, defined for every real $y$; for even $n$ it is the nonnegative $n$-th root, defined for $y\ge0$.)
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The square root $\sqrt y$ is defined for $y\ge0$ and is the nonnegative number whose square is $y$. Hence
\[x=\sqrt y\iff x\ge0\ \text{and}\ x^2=y .\]
(If $x\ge0$ and $y=x^2$, then $y\ge0$ and $\sqrt y=x$.) So the graph is the right half of the parabola $y=x^2$, including its vertex $(0,0)$: the points $(x,x^2)$ with $x\ge0$, such as $(0,0)$, $(1,1)$, $(2,4)$. In other words, $x=\sqrt y$ describes the same points as $y=x^2$ with the restriction $x\ge0$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return x^2;}
draw((-2.6,0)--(3.0,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-0.5)--(0,5.2),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(3.0,0),E); label("$y$",(0,5.2),N);
draw(graph(f,-2.15,0),gray+dashed);
draw(graph(f,0,2.15),blue+linewidth(1.1));
dot((0,0),blue);
label("$x=\sqrt{y}$",(2.15,f(2.15)),E,blue+fontsize(9pt));
label("$y=x^2,\ x<0$",(-2.0,f(-2.0)),W,gray+fontsize(8pt));
dot((1,1)); label("$(1,1)$",(1,1),E,fontsize(8pt));
dot((2,4)); label("$(2,4)$",(2,4),E,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} \emph{Even $n$.} As in (a), $x=\sqrt[n]{y}\iff x\ge0$ and $y=x^n$, so the graph is the right half ($x\ge0$) of the curve $y=x^n$.

\emph{Odd $n$.} The function $x\mapsto x^n$ is increasing on all real numbers and takes every real value, and $\sqrt[n]{y}$ is the unique real number whose $n$-th power is $y$. So $x=\sqrt[n]{y}\iff y=x^n$, with no restriction on $x$: the graph is the whole curve $y=x^n$.

So in every case the graph of $x=\sqrt[n]{y}$ is $\boxed{\text{the curve } y=x^n, \text{ restricted to } x\ge0 \text{ when } n \text{ is even}}$. The figure shows $n=3$ (whole curve) and $n=4$ (right half).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return x^3;}
real g(real x){return x^4;}
draw((-2.2,0)--(2.3,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-3.6)--(0,4.0),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(2.3,0),E); label("$y$",(0,4.0),N);
draw(graph(f,-1.5,1.5),red+linewidth(1.1));
draw(graph(g,0,1.38),blue+linewidth(1.1));
draw(graph(g,-1.38,0),gray+dashed);
label("$x=\sqrt[3]{y}$",(1.5,f(1.5)),E,red+fontsize(9pt));
label("$x=\sqrt[4]{y}$",(1.38,g(1.38)),NE,blue+fontsize(9pt));
dot((1,1)); dot((-1,-1),red);
label("$(-1,-1)$",(-1,-1),W,red+fontsize(8pt)); label("$(1,1)$",(1,1),E,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

question:
A student claims: ``If $a$ and $b$ are relatively prime positive integers, then the largest integer that cannot be written as $an+bm$ with \emph{positive} integers $n$ and $m$ is $ab-a-b$.''
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the claim is false for $a=2$, $b=3$.
\item[(b)] Prove the correct statement: for relatively prime positive integers $a,b$, the largest integer that cannot be written as $an+bm$ with positive integers $n,m$ is $ab$.
\end{enumerate}

If Mr.\ Bird drives to work at an average speed of $40$ miles per hour, he arrives $3$ minutes late. If he drives at an average speed of $60$ miles per hour, he arrives $3$ minutes early.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that driving at $50$ miles per hour, the average of the two speeds, does not get him to work exactly on time.
\item[(b)] At what average speed must he drive to arrive exactly on time, and why is it not the average of $40$ and $60$?
\end{enumerate}

The number $i$ is defined by $i^2=-1$, and for a positive real number $r$ the principal square root of $-r$ is defined as $\sqrt{-r}=i\sqrt r$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find all complex numbers whose square is $-9$, and all whose square is $-7$. Is $i$ a square root of every negative number?
\item[(b)] Find the error in the computation $\sqrt{-4}\cdot\sqrt{-9}=\sqrt{(-4)(-9)}=\sqrt{36}=6$, and find the correct value of $\sqrt{-4}\cdot\sqrt{-9}$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $\alpha=\angle BAD=\angle DAC$.

\textbf{Two inequalities.} Triangle $ADC$ is isosceles with $AD=AC$, so $\angle ADC=\angle ACD<90^\circ$. Hence $\angle ADB=180^\circ-\angle ADC>90^\circ$ is the largest angle of triangle $ABD$, and $AB>AD$. Also $AE<AD$, since $E$ lies on side $AD$ (and $E\ne D$, as the angle $CED$ must be defined).

\textbf{A congruent copy of triangle $AEC$.} Let $E'$ be the point on side $AB$ with $AE'=AE$; it lies strictly between $A$ and $B$ because $AE<AD<AB$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real al=25, Bg=90-1.5*al, Cg=90-al/2;
real a=6; real c=a*Sin(Cg)/Sin(2al), bb=a*Sin(Bg)/Sin(2al);
pair B=(0,0), C=(a,0), A=c*dir(Bg);
pair D=(a*c/(bb+c),0);
real ae=bb*Sin(Bg-al)/Sin(Bg);
pair Ep=A+ae*unit(D-A), Fp=A+ae*unit(B-A);
void angmark(pair V, pair P, pair Q, real r, pen p=black){
  real a1=degrees(P-V), a2=degrees(Q-V); real d=a2-a1;
  while(d<=-180) d+=360; while(d>180) d-=360;
  draw(arc(V,r,a1,a1+d),p);}
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,linewidth(0.9));
draw(C--Ep,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(D--Fp,red+dashed+linewidth(0.9));
angmark(B,C,A,0.55); angmark(Ep,C,D,0.4,blue); angmark(Fp,B,D,0.4,red);
angmark(A,B,D,1.3); angmark(A,B,D,1.42); angmark(A,D,C,1.5); angmark(A,D,C,1.62);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(Ep); dot(Fp);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,S);
label("$E$",Ep,E); label("$E'$",Fp,W);
\end{asy}

Triangles $AE'D$ and $AEC$ are congruent (SAS): $AE'=AE$, $\angle E'AD=\angle BAD=\alpha=\angle EAC$, and $AD=AC$. Therefore
\[DE'=CE\qquad\text{and}\qquad\angle AE'D=\angle AEC .\]

\textbf{The isosceles triangle $BDE'$.} Since $E$ lies between $A$ and $D$, $\angle AEC=180^\circ-\angle CED=180^\circ-\angle ABD$. Since $E'$ lies between $A$ and $B$,
\[\angle BE'D=180^\circ-\angle AE'D=180^\circ-\angle AEC=\angle ABD=\angle E'BD .\]
So triangle $BDE'$ has equal angles at $B$ and $E'$, and $DB=DE'$.

Combining, $\boxed{BD=DE'=CE}$. $\blacksquare$

\emph{Remark.} From $\angle ADC=\angle ABD+\alpha$ and $\angle ADC=\angle ACB$ one finds $\angle ABC=90^\circ-\dfrac{3\alpha}{2}$. As $E$ moves from $A$ to $D$, the angle $CED$ increases from $\alpha$ to $90^\circ+\dfrac{\alpha}{2}$, so a point $E$ as in the problem exists exactly when $90^\circ-\dfrac{3\alpha}{2}>\alpha$, i.e. when $\angle BAC<72^\circ$. The figure uses $\angle BAC=50^\circ$.

The boundary is a closed path of length $4\cdot24=96$ m. Going once around it, the pots divide it into pieces of length $3$ m, and on a closed path there are as many pots as pieces (each pot starts one piece). So there are
\[\dfrac{96}{3}=\boxed{32}\]
pots.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(24,0)--(24,24)--(0,24)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
for(int i=0;i<8;++i){ dot((3i,0),darkgreen); dot((24,3i),darkgreen); dot((24-3i,24),darkgreen); dot((0,24-3i),darkgreen);}
label("$24$ m",(12,0),S); label("$3$ m",(1.5,25.8),N,fontsize(8pt));
draw((0,25.8)--(3,25.8),Arrows(3));
\end{asy}

(Check by sides: each side has $\dfrac{24}{3}+1=9$ pots including both corners; the $4$ corner pots are each counted on two sides, so the total is $4\cdot9-4=32$.)

Let $c=AB$, $b=AC$ and $\alpha=\dfrac12\angle BAC$. Since $L\ne D$, we have $b\ne c$ (the bisector meets $BC$ at its midpoint exactly when $b=c$, by the angle bisector theorem).

\textbf{Coordinates along the bisector.} Put $A$ at the origin with the bisector $AL$ along the positive $x$-axis, $B$ above it and $C$ below it:
\[B=(c\cos\alpha,\ c\sin\alpha),\qquad C=(b\cos\alpha,\ -b\sin\alpha).\]
Then
\[D=\dfrac{B+C}{2}=\left(\dfrac{(b+c)\cos\alpha}{2},\ \dfrac{(c-b)\sin\alpha}{2}\right).\]
By the angle bisector theorem $BL:LC=c:b$, so $L=\dfrac{bB+cC}{b+c}=\left(\dfrac{2bc\cos\alpha}{b+c},\,0\right)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real al=35, c=3, bb=7;
pair A=(0,0), B=c*dir(al), C=bb*dir(-al);
pair D=(B+C)/2, L=(2*bb*c*Cos(al)/(bb+c),0);
pair M=(2c/(bb+c))*D, Nn=(2bb/(bb+c))*D, P=(B.x,0), Q=(C.x,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--(6.6,0),blue); draw(A--Nn,red);
draw(B--M,gray); draw(C--Q,gray);
draw(L--M,linewidth(0.9)+dashed); draw(L--Nn,linewidth(0.9)+dashed);
draw(P+(0,0.22)--P+(0.22,0.22)--P+(0.22,0),gray); draw(Q+(0,-0.22)--Q+(-0.22,-0.22)--Q+(-0.22,0),gray);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(L); dot(M); dot(Nn); dot(P); dot(Q);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,N); label("$C$",C,S); label("$D$",D,SW);
label("$L$",L,NE); label("$M$",M,SW); label("$N$",Nn,SW); label("$P$",P,NW); label("$Q$",Q,NE);
label("bisector",(6.6,0),E,blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("median",Nn,E,red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{The points $M$ and $N$.} The perpendicular from $B$ to $AL$ is the vertical line $x=c\cos\alpha$ (it meets $AL$ at $P=(c\cos\alpha,0)$). A point of line $AD$ is $tD$ for a real $t$, and its $x$-coordinate is $c\cos\alpha$ exactly when $t=\dfrac{2c}{b+c}$. So $M=\dfrac{2c}{b+c}D$, and in the same way $N=\dfrac{2b}{b+c}D$.

\textbf{$LM\parallel AB$.} Compute
\[M-L=\left(c\cos\alpha-\dfrac{2bc\cos\alpha}{b+c},\ \dfrac{c(c-b)\sin\alpha}{b+c}\right)=\dfrac{c-b}{b+c}\,\bigl(c\cos\alpha,\ c\sin\alpha\bigr)=\dfrac{c-b}{b+c}\,(B-A).\]
Since $b\ne c$, $M\ne L$, and the vector $\overrightarrow{LM}$ is a nonzero multiple of $\overrightarrow{AB}$. So $LM\parallel AB$.

\textbf{$LN\parallel AC$.} Similarly
\[N-L=\left(b\cos\alpha-\dfrac{2bc\cos\alpha}{b+c},\ \dfrac{b(c-b)\sin\alpha}{b+c}\right)=\dfrac{b-c}{b+c}\,\bigl(b\cos\alpha,\ -b\sin\alpha\bigr)=\dfrac{b-c}{b+c}\,(C-A),\]
so $LN\parallel AC$. $\blacksquare$

(The figure shows the case $c<b$: then $M$ lies between $A$ and $D$ and $N$ lies beyond $D$; for $c>b$ the roles of $M$ and $N$ are exchanged, and the computation covers both cases.)

question:
Three circles of radius $2$ are externally tangent to each other in pairs. A first triangle has its vertices at the centres of the three circles. A second triangle has its vertices at the three points of tangency between the circles.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the area of the second (smaller) triangle.
\item[(b)] Inside the smaller triangle there is a region that lies inside none of the three circles. Find its area.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the centres be $A,B,C$. Externally tangent circles of radius $2$ have centres $2+2=4$ apart, so $ABC$ is equilateral with side $4$. The point of tangency of two circles lies on the segment joining their centres, at distance $2$ from each, so the tangency points $X$, $Y$, $Z$ are the midpoints of $AB$, $BC$, $CA$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(4,0), C=(2,2*sqrt(3));
pair Mab=(A+B)/2, Mbc=(B+C)/2, Mca=(C+A)/2;
fill(arc(A,2,0,60)--arc(C,2,240,300)--arc(B,2,120,180)--cycle,rgb(1,0.8,0.5));
draw(circle(A,2)); draw(circle(B,2)); draw(circle(C,2));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,dashed);
draw(Mab--Mbc--Mca--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(Mab); dot(Mbc); dot(Mca);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N);
label("$X$",Mab,1.6*S,Fill(white)); label("$Y$",Mbc,1.6*dir(30),Fill(white)); label("$Z$",Mca,1.6*dir(150),Fill(white));
label("$2$",(A+Mab)/2,S); label("$2$",(Mab+B)/2,S);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} $XYZ$ is the triangle of midpoints, so each side is half a side of $ABC$ (midpoint theorem): it is equilateral with side $2$, and its area is
\[\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot2^2=\boxed{\sqrt3}.\]

\textbf{(b)} Inside triangle $ABC$, the three discs cover three $60^\circ$ sectors (one at each vertex), which overlap only at the tangency points. The rest of $ABC$ is the curved region $R$ between the circles (shaded), of area
\[[ABC]-3\cdot\dfrac{60}{360}\pi\cdot2^2=4\sqrt3-2\pi .\]
This region $R$ is exactly the part of triangle $XYZ$ outside the circles:
\begin{itemize}
\item Triangle $XYZ$ lies inside $ABC$, so its points outside all circles lie in $R$.
\item Conversely, the part of $ABC$ on $A$'s side of the line $XZ$ is the triangle $AXZ$, whose vertices are at distance at most $2$ from $A$; it lies inside the disc around $A$ (a disc is convex). The same holds at $B$ and at $C$. So every point of $R$ lies on the far side of all three lines $XZ$, $XY$, $YZ$, i.e. inside triangle $XYZ$.
\end{itemize}
Hence the required area is $\boxed{4\sqrt3-2\pi}\approx0.645$.

The centres are $O_1O_2=3+3=6$ apart, and $T$ is the midpoint of $O_1O_2$. The large circle with centre $T$ and radius $R$ is internally tangent to the circle with centre $O_1$ and radius $3$ exactly when $R-3=TO_1=3$, so $R=6$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair T=(0,0), O1=(-3,0), O2=(3,0), P=(0,6), Q=(0,-6);
draw(circle(O1,3)); draw(circle(O2,3)); draw(circle(T,6),linewidth(0.9));
draw(O1--P--O2--Q--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(O1--O2,dashed); draw(P--Q,dashed);
dot(T); dot(O1); dot(O2); dot(P); dot(Q);
label("$T$",T,dir(-45)); label("$O_1$",O1,W); label("$O_2$",O2,E); label("$P$",P,N); label("$Q$",Q,S);
label("$3$",(-1.5,0),N); label("$6$",(0,3),E);
\end{asy}

The diagonals of a rhombus are perpendicular and bisect each other. One diagonal is $O_1O_2$, with midpoint $T$; so the other diagonal $PQ$ lies on the line through $T$ perpendicular to $O_1O_2$, with $T$ as its midpoint. Since $P$ and $Q$ lie on the large circle, $TP=TQ=6$ and $PQ=12$. The area of a rhombus is half the product of its diagonals:
\[\dfrac12\cdot6\cdot12=\boxed{36}.\]

question:
Let $\varphi$ be Euler's totient function.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the smallest positive integer $n$ with $\varphi(n)\varphi(6)=8$.
\item[(b)] Is there a positive integer $m$ with $\varphi(m)\varphi(6)=10$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{Lemma.} If $X$ lies on side $PQ$ and $Y$ on side $PR$ of triangle $PQR$, then $\dfrac{[PXY]}{[PQR]}=\dfrac{PX}{PQ}\cdot\dfrac{PY}{PR}$, because both areas are $\dfrac12\cdot(\text{two sides})\cdot\sin\angle P$.

Let $t=\dfrac{FA}{AC}$, so $\dfrac{CF}{CA}=1-t$. Triangle $DEF$ is triangle $ABC$ with three corner triangles removed:
\[\dfrac{[ADF]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac12\cdot t,\qquad\dfrac{[BDE]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac34=\dfrac38,\qquad\dfrac{[CEF]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac14(1-t).\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(10,0), C=(3,7);
pair D=(A+B)/2, Ep=C+(B-C)/4, Fp=A+(11/50)*(C-A);
fill(D--Ep--Fp--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.9,1));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(D--Ep--Fp--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(D); dot(Ep); dot(Fp);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N);
label("$D$",D,S); label("$E$",Ep,NE); label("$F$",Fp,W);
\end{asy}

Hence
\[\dfrac{[DEF]}{[ABC]}=1-\dfrac t2-\dfrac38-\dfrac{1-t}{4}=\dfrac38-\dfrac t4 .\]
Setting this equal to $\dfrac{8}{25}$ gives $\dfrac t4=\dfrac38-\dfrac{8}{25}=\dfrac{11}{200}$, so
\[\dfrac{FA}{AC}=t=\boxed{\dfrac{11}{50}}.\]

Since $\sin C=1$ and $0^\circ<C<180^\circ$, the angle at $C$ is $90^\circ$. So $AB$ is the hypotenuse, and $AC$ is the leg opposite $B$:
\[\sin B=\dfrac{AC}{AB}=\dfrac35\quad\Longrightarrow\quad AB=10.\]
By the Pythagorean theorem $BC=\sqrt{10^2-6^2}=8$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), A=(0,6), B=(8,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0.5)--(0.5,0.5)--(0.5,0));
label("$C$",C,SW); label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SE);
label("$6$",(A+C)/2,W); label("$8$",(B+C)/2,S); label("$10$",(A+B)/2,NE);
draw(arc(B,1.2,180-degrees(atan(6/8)),180));
\end{asy}

The area is $\dfrac12\cdot AC\cdot BC=\dfrac12\cdot6\cdot8=\boxed{24}$.

question:
How many positive integers less than $1000$ satisfy both of the following conditions?
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(1)] When divided by $3$, it leaves a remainder of $1$.
\item[(2)] When multiplied by $3$, the result is odd.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Since $D$ lies on segment $BC$ and $CD=\dfrac12CB$, also $BD=CB-CD=\dfrac12CB$, so $BD=CD$ and $D$ is the midpoint of $BC$.

Let $H$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $A$ to line $BC$ and $h=AH$. Triangles $ABD$ and $ACD$ both have their bases $BD$ and $DC$ on line $BC$, so both have height $h$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(8,0), A=(3.1,5), D=(B+C)/2, H=(3.1,0);
fill(A--B--D--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.9,1)); fill(A--D--C--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.75));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--D,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--H,dashed);
draw(H+(0.3,0)--H+(0.3,0.3)--H+(0,0.3));
draw((2,-0.2)--(2,0.2)); draw((6,-0.2)--(6,0.2));
dot(D); dot(H);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,S); label("$H$",H,S); label("$h$",(A+H)/2,W);
\end{asy}

\[[ABD]=\dfrac12\cdot BD\cdot h=\dfrac12\cdot DC\cdot h=[ACD].\]
So $\boxed{[ACD]=[ABD]}$: a median divides a triangle into two triangles of equal area. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{Lemma.} If $X$ lies on side $PQ$ and $Y$ on side $PR$ of triangle $PQR$, then $\dfrac{[PXY]}{[PQR]}=\dfrac{PX}{PQ}\cdot\dfrac{PY}{PR}$ (both areas equal $\dfrac12\cdot(\text{two sides})\cdot\sin\angle P$).

\textbf{Triangle $DEF$.} The three corner triangles of $ABC$ have
\[\dfrac{[AFE]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac25\cdot\dfrac35,\qquad\dfrac{[BDF]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac25\cdot\dfrac35,\qquad\dfrac{[CED]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac25\cdot\dfrac35,\]
(for example at $A$: $\dfrac{AF}{AB}=\dfrac25$ and $\dfrac{AE}{AC}=1-\dfrac{CE}{CA}=\dfrac35$), so
\[\dfrac{[DEF]}{[ABC]}=1-3\cdot\dfrac{6}{25}=\dfrac{7}{25}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(10,0), C=(4,8);
pair Fp=A+(2/5)*(B-A), D=B+(2/5)*(C-B), Ep=C+(2/5)*(A-C);
pair G=Ep+(1/3)*(Fp-Ep), H=Fp+(1/4)*(D-Fp), I=D+(3/4)*(Ep-D);
fill(G--H--I--cycle,rgb(1,0.8,0.6));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(D--Ep--Fp--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.8)); draw(G--H--I--cycle,red+linewidth(0.8));
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N);
label("$D$",D,NE); label("$E$",Ep,NW); label("$F$",Fp,S);
label("$G$",G,W); label("$H$",H,dir(-30)); label("$I$",I,N);
dot(D); dot(Ep); dot(Fp); dot(G); dot(H); dot(I);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Triangle $GHI$.} In triangle $DEF$: $\dfrac{FG}{FE}=1-\dfrac13=\dfrac23$, $\dfrac{DH}{DF}=1-\dfrac14=\dfrac34$ and $\dfrac{EI}{ED}=1-\dfrac34=\dfrac14$. The corner triangles of $DEF$ have
\[\dfrac{[FGH]}{[DEF]}=\dfrac23\cdot\dfrac14=\dfrac16,\qquad\dfrac{[DHI]}{[DEF]}=\dfrac34\cdot\dfrac34=\dfrac{9}{16},\qquad\dfrac{[EIG]}{[DEF]}=\dfrac14\cdot\dfrac13=\dfrac{1}{12},\]
which add up to $\dfrac{8+27+4}{48}=\dfrac{13}{16}$. So $\dfrac{[GHI]}{[DEF]}=\dfrac{3}{16}$, and
\[\dfrac{[GHI]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac{7}{25}\cdot\dfrac{3}{16}=\boxed{\dfrac{21}{400}}.\]

question:
The binomial coefficient $\dbinom nk$ is defined as the number of $k$-element subsets of an $n$-element set.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is $\dbinom56$? Is it $0$ or undefined?
\item[(b)] Check that this value agrees with Pascal's rule $\dbinom66=\dbinom55+\dbinom56$ and with $\dbinom50+\dbinom51+\cdots+\dbinom55+\dbinom56=2^5$.
\item[(c)] Why can the formula $\dbinom nk=\dfrac{n!}{k!\,(n-k)!}$ not be used to compute $\dbinom56$?
\end{enumerate}

A square has side length $6$. A semicircle is drawn inside the square, with the bottom side of the square as its diameter. A line tangent to the semicircle at a point $P$ meets the left side of the square at $X$ and the right side at $Y$, and $XY=4\sqrt3$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=6; pair A=(0,0), B=(s,0), C=(s,s), D=(0,s), O=(3,0);
real t=60; pair P=O+3*dir(t);
pair X=(0,3*sqrt(3)), Y=(6,sqrt(3));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(O,3,0,180)); draw(A--B);
draw(X--Y,blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(X); dot(Y); dot(P);
label("$X$",X,W); label("$Y$",Y,E); label("$P$",P,dir(t));
label("$6$",(3,0),S);
\end{asy}

Find $XP^2+PY^2$.

solution:
Let $A$ and $B$ be the bottom left and bottom right corners and $O$ the midpoint of $AB$, the centre of the semicircle (radius $3$).

\textbf{Equal tangents.} The left side $AX$ is perpendicular to the diameter $AB$ at its endpoint $A$, so it is tangent to the circle at $A$. Thus $XA$ and $XP$ are the two tangent segments from $X$, and $XP=XA$. Likewise $YP=YB$. Put $a=XA=XP$ and $b=YB=YP$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=6; pair A=(0,0), B=(s,0), C=(s,s), D=(0,s), O=(3,0);
real t=60; pair P=O+3*dir(t);
pair X=(0,3*sqrt(3)), Y=(6,sqrt(3));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(O,3,0,180));
draw(X--Y,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--P,dashed); draw(O--A,dashed);
draw(P+0.35*dir(t+180)--P+0.35*dir(t+180)+0.35*dir(t+90)--P+0.35*dir(t+90),gray);
dot(X); dot(Y); dot(P); dot(O); dot(A); dot(B);
label("$X$",X,W); label("$Y$",Y,E); label("$P$",P,dir(t)); label("$O$",O,S);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE);
label("$a$",(0,X.y/2),W,red); label("$b$",(6,Y.y/2),E,red);
label("$a$",(X+P)/2,N,red); label("$b$",(P+Y)/2,N,red);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Two equations.} Since $P$ lies between $X$ and $Y$,
\[a+b=XP+PY=XY=4\sqrt3 .\]
With $A$ as origin, $X=(0,a)$ and $Y=(6,b)$, so
\[XY^2=6^2+(a-b)^2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad48=36+(a-b)^2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad(a-b)^2=12 .\]

\textbf{The answer.}
\[XP^2+PY^2=a^2+b^2=\dfrac{(a+b)^2+(a-b)^2}{2}=\dfrac{48+12}{2}=\boxed{30}.\]
(Indeed $\{a,b\}=\{3\sqrt3,\sqrt3\}$, both less than $6$, so such a tangent exists; the figure shows $a=3\sqrt3$, $b=\sqrt3$.)

question:
A mental-arithmetic trick squares a number by rounding it to the nearest ten: for example
\[24^2=(20+4)^2=20^2+2\cdot20\cdot4+4^2=576,\qquad26^2=(30-4)^2=30^2-2\cdot30\cdot4+4^2=676.\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why the trick always works.
\item[(b)] Use the same idea to compute $347^2$ in your head.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the edge be $a$. Place the octahedron with centre $O$ at the origin and vertices $(\pm c,0,0)$, $(0,\pm c,0)$, $(0,0,\pm c)$; adjacent vertices are $c\sqrt2$ apart, so $c=\dfrac{a}{\sqrt2}$. By symmetry the inscribed sphere is centred at $O$, and its radius $r$ is the distance from $O$ to a face, say the face in the plane $x+y+z=c$:
\[r=\dfrac{c}{\sqrt{1^2+1^2+1^2}}=\dfrac{c}{\sqrt3}=\dfrac{a}{\sqrt6}.\]
The figure shows the cross-section through two opposite vertices $V,V'$ and the midpoints $M,M'$ of two opposite edges: a rhombus with half-diagonals $OV=\dfrac{a}{\sqrt2}$ and $OM=\dfrac a2$, whose sides are altitudes of faces, and the great circle of the sphere touching them.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=3; pair V=(0,s), Vp=(0,-s), M=(s/sqrt(2),0), Mp=(-s/sqrt(2),0), O=(0,0);
real r=s/sqrt(3);
draw(V--M--Vp--Mp--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O,r),blue);
draw(V--Vp,dashed); draw(Mp--M,dashed);
pair Tt=(r*sqrt(2/3),r/sqrt(3));
draw(O--Tt,red); dot(Tt); label("$r$",(O+Tt)/2,NW,red);
dot(O); dot(V); dot(Vp); dot(M); dot(Mp);
label("$O$",O,SW); label("$V$",V,N); label("$V'$",Vp,S); label("$M$",M,E); label("$M'$",Mp,W);
label("$\dfrac{a}{\sqrt2}$",(0,0.75*s),E,fontsize(7pt)); label("$\dfrac{a}{2}$",(s/(2*sqrt(2)),0),S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

(Check in the cross-section: the side $VM=\sqrt{\dfrac{a^2}{2}+\dfrac{a^2}{4}}=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}a$, and the distance from $O$ to it is $\dfrac{OV\cdot OM}{VM}=\dfrac{\dfrac{a}{\sqrt2}\cdot\dfrac a2}{\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}a}=\dfrac{a}{\sqrt6}$.)

The surface area is
\[4\pi r^2=\dfrac{4\pi a^2}{6}=\dfrac{2\pi a^2}{3}=\dfrac{2\pi\cdot10816}{3}=\dfrac{21632\pi}{3}\approx22652.98,\]
which rounds to $\boxed{22653}$.

The angles of the triangle are $\angle A=80^\circ$, $\angle B=60^\circ$, $\angle C=40^\circ$.

\textbf{Step 1: $AB=BD=DC$.} Let line $BP$ meet side $AC$ at $D$; as $P$ is inside the triangle, $P$ lies between $B$ and $D$. In triangle $DBC$, $\angle DBC=40^\circ=\angle DCB$, so $DB=DC$, and the exterior angle is $\angle BDA=40^\circ+40^\circ=80^\circ$. In triangle $ABD$, $\angle BAD=80^\circ=\angle BDA$, so $BA=BD$. Hence $AB=BD=DC$, and $\angle BDC=100^\circ$.

\textbf{Step 2: the circumcentre $O$ of triangle $ABP$.} We have $\angle APB=180^\circ-10^\circ-20^\circ=150^\circ$. This inscribed angle stands on the arc $AB$ not containing $P$, so that arc measures $300^\circ$ and the arc $AB$ through $P$ measures $60^\circ$. Hence the central angle $\angle AOB=60^\circ$, and since $OA=OB$, triangle $ABO$ is equilateral: $OA=OB=OP=AB$. Also $O$ lies on the opposite side of line $AB$ from $P$ (because $P$ is on the minor arc).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair d(real a){return dir(a);}
pair B=(0,0), C=(10,0);
real ab=10*Sin(40)/Sin(80);
pair A=ab*dir(60);
pair P=extension(B,B+dir(40),A,A+dir(250));
pair D=extension(B,P,A,C);
pair O=ab*dir(120);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--P,linewidth(0.8)); draw(B--D,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--O--B,gray); draw(O--P,blue); draw(O--D,blue+dashed); draw(P--C,red+linewidth(0.8));
draw(arc(O,ab,300,360),gray+dotted);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(P); dot(D); dot(O);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$P$",P,dir(-95)); label("$D$",D,NE); label("$O$",O,W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 3: two isosceles triangles.} Since $\angle OBA=60^\circ$, $\angle ABP=20^\circ$ and $O$, $P$ are on opposite sides of $AB$, we get $\angle OBP=80^\circ$. In the isosceles triangle $OBP$ ($OB=OP$) this gives $\angle OPB=80^\circ$. In triangle $OBD$ we have $OB=AB=BD$ and $\angle OBD=\angle OBP=80^\circ$, so $\angle BDO=\dfrac{180^\circ-80^\circ}{2}=50^\circ$.

\textbf{Step 4: a parallelogram.} Turning from ray $BD$ towards ray $BA$ by $20^\circ$ and then by $60^\circ$ more reaches ray $BO$; as $80^\circ<180^\circ$, the points $A$ and $O$ lie on the same side of line $BD$. The rays $PO$ and $DA$ make the same angle $\angle OPB=80^\circ=\angle ADB$ with the rays $PB$ and $DB$, which point the same way along line $BD$, and lie on the same side of it. Hence $PO\parallel DA$, with the same direction. As $D$ lies between $A$ and $C$, ray $DA$ is opposite to ray $DC$, so $\overrightarrow{PO}$ has the direction of $\overrightarrow{CD}$; and $PO=AB=DC$. Therefore $\overrightarrow{PO}=\overrightarrow{CD}$, and $PODC$ is a parallelogram. In particular $PC\parallel OD$.

\textbf{Step 5: the angle.} The vertices $O$ and $C$ of the parallelogram lie on opposite sides of its diagonal $PD$, so the alternate angles at $P$ and $D$ are equal: $\angle DPC=\angle PDO=\angle BDO=50^\circ$. In triangle $PDC$, $\angle PDC=\angle BDC=100^\circ$, so $\angle PCD=180^\circ-100^\circ-50^\circ=30^\circ$. Finally, since $D$ lies on $CA$,
\[\angle PCB=\angle ACB-\angle PCA=40^\circ-30^\circ=\boxed{10^\circ}.\]

question:
Let $p$ be a prime. You may use Wilson's theorem, $(p-1)!\equiv-1\pmod p$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that for every integer $n$ with $0\le n\le p-1$,
\[n!\,(p-1-n)!\equiv(-1)^{n+1}\pmod p .\]
\item[(b)] Use (a) to find the remainder when $18!$ is divided by $23$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The number $7+30N$ leaves remainder $7$ when divided by $30$, so it is divisible by none of $2$, $3$, $5$. For $N\le5$ we have $7+30N\le157<169=13^2$, so if such a number were composite it would have a prime factor less than $13$ other than $2,3,5$, that is, $7$ or $11$.
\begin{itemize}
\item Modulo $7$: $7+30N\equiv2N$, which is divisible by $7$ only when $7\mid N$.
\item Modulo $11$: $7+30N\equiv7+8N$, which is divisible by $11$ exactly when $8N\equiv4$, i.e. $2N\equiv1$, i.e. $N\equiv6\pmod{11}$.
\end{itemize}
So $7+30N$ is prime for $N=1,2,3,4,5$ (the numbers $37,67,97,127,157$), while for $N=6$ it is $187=11\cdot17$. The answer is $\boxed{N=6}$.

question:
For a nonzero number $x$, let $t=x+\dfrac1x$ and $P_n=x^n+\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ for $n=0,1,2,\dots$ (so $P_0=2$ and $P_1=t$).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $P_n=t\,P_{n-1}-P_{n-2}$ for every $n\ge2$.
\item[(b)] Prove by induction that for every $n\ge1$, $P_n$ is a polynomial in $t$ of degree $n$ with integer coefficients and leading coefficient $1$.
\item[(c)] Express $x^5+\dfrac{1}{x^5}$ as a polynomial in $t$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Since $m\le\sqrt x<n$, the longest side is $n$, so it is the hypotenuse:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(2.6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(3,0), A=(0,4);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0.3,0)--(0.3,0.3)--(0,0.3));
label("$m$",(B+C)/2,S); label("$\sqrt{x}$",(A+B)/2,W); label("$n$",(A+C)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

\[n^2=m^2+x,\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad x=n^2-m^2.\]
The condition $m\le\sqrt x$ means $m^2\le x=n^2-m^2$, i.e. $n^2\ge2m^2$; and $x\le100$ means $n^2\le100+m^2$. Conversely, any positive integers $m,n$ with $2m^2\le n^2\le100+m^2$ give a valid $x=n^2-m^2\ge m^2\ge1$ (and then $\sqrt x<n$ automatically). Also $m^2\le x\le100$ forces $m\le10$, and $m=10$ would need $n^2=200$, which is impossible.

We list, for each $m$, the values $x=n^2-m^2$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $m=1$, $2\le n\le10$: $x=3,8,15,24,35,48,63,80,99$;
\item $m=2$, $3\le n\le10$: $x=5,12,21,32,45,60,77,96$;
\item $m=3$, $5\le n\le10$: $x=16,27,40,55,72,91$;
\item $m=4$, $6\le n\le10$: $x=20,33,48,65,84$;
\item $m=5$, $8\le n\le11$: $x=39,56,75,96$;
\item $m=6$, $9\le n\le11$: $x=45,64,85$;
\item $m=7$, $10\le n\le12$: $x=51,72,95$;
\item $m=8$, $n=12$: $x=80$;
\item $m=9$, $n=13$: $x=88$.
\end{itemize}
This is $9+8+6+5+4+3+3+1+1=40$ entries, but $48$, $96$, $45$, $72$ and $80$ each appear twice. So there are $40-5=\boxed{35}$ values of $x$.

question:
Each time a player defeats a certain monster in a video game, the monster drops a rod with probability $\dfrac12$, independently of all other times. A player defeats $1000$ of these monsters and collects at least $900$ rods.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the probability of collecting at least $900$ rods from $1000$ monsters is less than $10^{-80}$.
\item[(b)] What is this probability as a percentage, rounded to the nearest millionth of a percent?
\end{enumerate}

The figure below shows a square and four equilateral triangles, with each triangle having a side lying on a side of the square, such that each triangle has side length $2$ and the third vertices of the triangles meet at the center of the square. The region inside the square but outside the triangles is shaded. What is the area of the shaded region?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=sqrt(3);
fill((-s,-s)--(s,-s)--(s,s)--(-s,s)--cycle,gray(0.7));
pair O=(0,0);
for(int k=0;k<4;++k){ path t=rotate(90k)*((-1,-s)--(1,-s)--O--cycle); fill(t,white); draw(t,linewidth(0.8)); }
draw((-s,-s)--(s,-s)--(s,s)--(-s,s)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
\end{asy}

$\textbf{(A) } 4 \qquad \textbf{(B) } 12-4\sqrt{3} \qquad \textbf{(C) } 3\sqrt{3}\qquad \textbf{(D) } 4\sqrt{3} \qquad \textbf{(E) } 16-4\sqrt{3}$

solution:
Let $O$ be the centre of the square. Each triangle is equilateral with side $2$, so its height is $\sqrt{2^2-1^2}=\sqrt3$. The apex of the triangle is $O$ and its base lies on a side of the square, so the distance from $O$ to that side is $\sqrt3$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=sqrt(3);
fill((-s,-s)--(s,-s)--(s,s)--(-s,s)--cycle,gray(0.75));
pair O=(0,0);
for(int k=0;k<4;++k){ path t=rotate(90k)*((-1,-s)--(1,-s)--O--cycle); fill(t,white); draw(t,linewidth(0.8)); }
draw((-s,-s)--(s,-s)--(s,s)--(-s,s)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--(0,-s),red+dashed); dot(O); label("$O$",(0,0.62));
label("$\sqrt{3}$",(0.02,-s/2-0.3),E,red+fontsize(8pt));
label("$2$",(0,-s),S);
draw((-s-0.35,-s)--(-s-0.35,s),Arrows(4)); label("$2\sqrt{3}$",(-s-0.35,0),W);
\end{asy}

Hence the square has side $2\sqrt3$ and area $12$. Each triangle has area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot2^2=\sqrt3$, and the four triangles meet only at $O$ (each lies in the quarter of the square cut off by the diagonals next to its own side), so they cover $4\sqrt3$. The shaded area is
\[12-4\sqrt3,\]
answer $\boxed{\textbf{(B)}}$.

question:
Identical cubes with edge $1$ cm are stacked in a single column on a horizontal table: there is exactly one cube on each level, the bottom cube stands on the table, every other cube stands on the cube below it, and the cubes may be shifted sideways towards the edge of the table. The stack balances when, for every $k$, the centre of mass of the top $k$ cubes lies above the cube directly below them (above the table, for the whole stack); lying exactly above an edge is allowed. The \emph{overhang} is the distance by which the stack reaches beyond the edge of the table. Let $H_n=1+\dfrac12+\dfrac13+\cdots+\dfrac1n$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that a balanced stack of $n$ cubes has overhang at most $\dfrac{H_n}{2}$ cm, and that this overhang can be reached.
\item[(b)] Deduce that an overhang of $1$ metre needs more than $2^{200}$ (which is more than $10^{60}$) cubes.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Measure lengths in cm along the table, with the table edge at $0$ and the table on the side of negative numbers. Number the cubes $1,2,\dots,n$ from the top, let $x_i$ be the position of the centre of cube $i$ (so cube $i$ covers $\left[x_i-\dfrac12,x_i+\dfrac12\right]$), and let $c_k=\dfrac{x_1+\cdots+x_k}{k}$ be the centre of mass of the top $k$ cubes. Balancing means
\[x_{k+1}-\dfrac12\le c_k\le x_{k+1}+\dfrac12\quad(1\le k\le n-1),\qquad c_n\le0.\]

\textbf{(a) The bound.} For $k\ge2$, using $x_k\ge c_{k-1}-\dfrac12$,
\[c_k=\dfrac{(k-1)c_{k-1}+x_k}{k}\ge\dfrac{(k-1)c_{k-1}+c_{k-1}-\dfrac12}{k}=c_{k-1}-\dfrac{1}{2k}.\tag{1}\]
Fix a cube $j$; we show that its right face $x_j+\dfrac12$ is at most $\dfrac{H_n}{2}$. If $j=1$, then $c_1=x_1$. If $j\ge2$, then $c_{j-1}\ge x_j-\dfrac12$ gives $c_j=\dfrac{(j-1)c_{j-1}+x_j}{j}\ge x_j-\dfrac{j-1}{2j}$. In both cases $c_j\ge x_j-\dfrac12+\dfrac1{2j}$. Applying (1) for $k=j+1,\dots,n$,
\[0\ge c_n\ge c_j-\dfrac12\left(\dfrac1{j+1}+\cdots+\dfrac1n\right)\ge x_j-\dfrac12+\dfrac1{2j}-\dfrac{H_n-H_j}{2}.\]
Hence
\[x_j+\dfrac12\le\dfrac{H_n-H_j}{2}+1-\dfrac1{2j}\le\dfrac{H_n}{2},\]
because $H_j\ge2-\dfrac1j$ for every $j\ge1$: the numbers $f(j)=H_j+\dfrac1j-2$ satisfy $f(1)=0$ and $f(j+1)-f(j)=\dfrac2{j+1}-\dfrac1j=\dfrac{j-1}{j(j+1)}\ge0$. So no point of the stack is more than $\dfrac{H_n}{2}$ beyond the edge.

\textbf{(a) Reaching it (the harmonic stack).} Let $R_k=\dfrac12\left(\dfrac1k+\dfrac1{k+1}+\cdots+\dfrac1n\right)$ for $1\le k\le n$ and $R_{n+1}=0$, and place cube $k$ with its right face at $R_k$, i.e. $x_k=R_k-\dfrac12$. We claim $c_k=R_{k+1}$ for every $k$. For $k=1$: $c_1=R_1-\dfrac12=R_2$. If $c_{k-1}=R_k$, then
\[c_k=\dfrac{(k-1)R_k+R_k-\dfrac12}{k}=R_k-\dfrac1{2k}=R_{k+1}.\]
So the centre of mass of the top $k$ cubes lies exactly above the right face of cube $k+1$ (for $k=n$: exactly above the table edge), every balance condition holds, and the top cube reaches $R_1=\dfrac{H_n}{2}$. For $n=4$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real[] r={25/24,13/24,7/24,1/8,0};
fill((-2.2,-0.6)--(0,-0.6)--(0,0)--(-2.2,0)--cycle,gray(0.8));
draw((-2.2,0)--(0,0)--(0,-0.6),linewidth(0.9));
for(int k=0;k<4;++k){ real y=3-k; draw((r[k]-1,y)--(r[k],y)--(r[k],y+1)--(r[k]-1,y+1)--cycle,linewidth(0.8)); label("$"+string(k+1)+"$",(r[k]-0.75,y+0.5)); }
draw((0,0)--(0,4.6),gray+dashed);
draw((0,4.35)--(r[0],4.35),Arrows(4)); label("$\dfrac{H_4}{2}$",(r[0]/2,4.35),N);
label("table",(-1.1,-0.3));
string[] lb={"$\dfrac12$","$\dfrac14$","$\dfrac16$","$\dfrac18$"};
for(int k=0;k<4;++k){ real y=3-k-0.15; draw((r[k+1],y)--(r[k],y),red,Arrows(3)); label(lb[k],(r[k],y),E,red+fontsize(7pt)); }
\end{asy}


\textbf{(b)} An overhang of $100$ cm needs $\dfrac{H_n}{2}\ge100$, i.e. $H_n\ge200$. Group the terms of $H_{2^m-1}$ in blocks $\dfrac1{2^i}+\cdots+\dfrac1{2^{i+1}-1}$ for $i=0,1,\dots,m-1$: each block has $2^i$ terms, each at most $\dfrac1{2^i}$, so each block is at most $1$. The block with $i=1$ is $\dfrac12+\dfrac13=\dfrac56$, so for $m\ge3$
\[H_{2^m}=H_{2^m-1}+\dfrac1{2^m}\le m-\dfrac16+\dfrac1{2^m}<m.\]
With $m=200$: every $n\le2^{200}$ has $H_n\le H_{2^{200}}<200$, so a balanced stack of at most $2^{200}$ cubes has overhang less than $100$ cm. Hence more than $2^{200}$ cubes are needed, and $2^{200}=1024^{20}>10^{60}$. $\blacksquare$

(In fact, since $H_n\approx\ln n+0.577$, about $4\times10^{86}$ cubes are needed in a single column.)

Let $\theta=\angle ABE=\angle BCF=\angle CAD$, and write $A$, $B$, $C$ also for the angles of the triangle.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(5,0), C=(3.2594,4.8338), D=(3.4101,2.9388), E=(1.202,1.0358), F=(3.6139,0.378);
fill(D--E--F--cycle,rgb(1,0.85,0.6));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,blue); draw(B--E,blue); draw(C--F,blue);
dot(D); dot(E); dot(F);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N);
label("$D$",D,NE); label("$E$",E,NW); label("$F$",F,NE);
draw(arc(B,0.9,180-15.25,180),red); draw(arc(C,0.9,degrees(B-C)-15.25,degrees(B-C)),red); draw(arc(A,0.9,degrees(C-A)-15.25,degrees(C-A)),red);
label("$\theta$",B+1.15*dir(180-7.6),red+fontsize(8pt)); label("$\theta$",C+1.15*dir(degrees(B-C)-7.6),red+fontsize(8pt)); label("$\theta$",A+1.15*dir(degrees(C-A)-7.6),red+fontsize(8pt));
label("$30$",(A+B+E)/3); label("$35$",(B+C+F)/3); label("$40$",(C+A+D)/3);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Triangle $DEF$ is similar to triangle $ABC$.} In triangle $ABE$ the angle at $A$ is $\angle BAD=A-\theta$ and the angle at $B$ is $\theta$, so $\angle AEB=180^\circ-A$. Since $E$ lies between $A$ and $D$, and $F$ between $B$ and $E$, the angle $\angle DEF$ is the supplement of $\angle AEB$:
\[\angle DEF=180^\circ-\angle AEB=A.\]
In the same way, triangle $BCF$ has angles $B-\theta$ at $B$ and $\theta$ at $C$, so $\angle EFD=180^\circ-\angle BFC=B$, and then $\angle FDE=C$. Hence triangle $EFD\sim$ triangle $ABC$ with $E\leftrightarrow A$, $F\leftrightarrow B$, $D\leftrightarrow C$. The side $FD$ corresponds to $BC$, so the ratio of similarity is $\dfrac{FD}{BC}=\dfrac12$, and
\[[DEF]=\left(\dfrac12\right)^2[ABC]=\dfrac14[ABC].\]

\textbf{The pieces.} The segments $AD$, $BE$ and $CF$ cut triangle $ABC$ into the four triangles $ABE$, $BCF$, $CAD$ and $DEF$ (each of the three segments is a side of two of these pieces, and they meet only along these sides). Therefore
\[[ABC]=30+35+40+\dfrac14[ABC]\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\dfrac34[ABC]=105\quad\Longrightarrow\quad[ABC]=\boxed{140}.\]

(Such a triangle exists: for example, a triangle with angles about $56.0^\circ$, $70.2^\circ$, $53.8^\circ$ and $\theta\approx15.25^\circ$, scaled to the right size, has exactly these areas and $BC=2DF$; the figure is drawn to scale for it.)

question:
Let $\ell$ be the line $Ax+By+C=0$, where $A$ and $B$ are not both $0$, and let $P=(x_0,y_0)$ be a point. Prove that the shortest distance from $P$ to a point of $\ell$ is
\[\dfrac{\left|Ax_0+By_0+C\right|}{\sqrt{A^2+B^2}},\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] by finding the foot of the perpendicular from $P$ to $\ell$;
\item[(b)] by using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality $(pu+qv)^2\le\left(p^2+q^2\right)\left(u^2+v^2\right)$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Write $k=\sqrt{A^2+B^2}>0$ and $d=Ax_0+By_0+C$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair L=(-0.6,2.0), u=unit((2,-1)), P=(1,3.3);
pair Q=L+dot(P-L,u)*u, R=Q+2.4*u;
draw((Q-2.4*u)--(Q+3.6*u),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--Q,red); draw(P--R,dashed);
pair n=unit(P-Q);
draw((Q+0.22*u)--(Q+0.22*u+0.22*n)--(Q+0.22*n));
dot(P); dot(Q); dot(R);
label("$P(x_0,y_0)$",P,N); label("$Q$",Q,SW); label("$R$",R,SW);
label("$Ax+By+C=0$",Q+3.6*u,E,blue);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} The line $\ell$ has direction vector $(B,-A)$, and $(A,B)\cdot(B,-A)=0$, so $(A,B)$ is perpendicular to $\ell$. The points on the perpendicular through $P$ are $Q_t=(x_0-tA,\ y_0-tB)$. Such a point lies on $\ell$ when
\[A(x_0-tA)+B(y_0-tB)+C=0\iff d=t\left(A^2+B^2\right)\iff t=\dfrac{d}{k^2}.\]
For this $t$, $Q=Q_t$ is the foot of the perpendicular, and
\[PQ=|t|\sqrt{A^2+B^2}=\dfrac{|d|}{k^2}\cdot k=\dfrac{|d|}{k}.\]
For any other point $R$ of $\ell$, triangle $PQR$ has a right angle at $Q$, so $PR^2=PQ^2+QR^2\ge PQ^2$. Hence $PQ=\dfrac{|Ax_0+By_0+C|}{\sqrt{A^2+B^2}}$ is the shortest distance.

\textbf{(b)} Let $R=(x,y)$ be any point of $\ell$, so $Ax+By+C=0$. Then
\[d=Ax_0+By_0+C-(Ax+By+C)=A(x_0-x)+B(y_0-y),\]
and by Cauchy-Schwarz
\[d^2\le\left(A^2+B^2\right)\left((x_0-x)^2+(y_0-y)^2\right)=k^2\cdot PR^2,\]
so $PR\ge\dfrac{|d|}{k}$ for every point $R$ of $\ell$. Equality holds when $(x_0-x,\,y_0-y)$ is a multiple of $(A,B)$, which is exactly the foot $Q$ found in (a), and that point lies on $\ell$. So the minimum distance is $\dfrac{|Ax_0+By_0+C|}{\sqrt{A^2+B^2}}$. $\blacksquare$

Collect like terms:
\begin{itemize}
\item $x$-terms: $93x+9x+x=103x$;
\item $y$-terms: $7y+9y+6y=22y$;
\item $u$-terms: $8u$;
\item constants: $5000+3+7+5+i^2=5015-1=5014$;
\item the term $i$.
\end{itemize}
So the expression equals $\boxed{103x+22y+8u+5014+i}$.

Since $2018\equiv18\equiv-2\pmod{20}$, we need $(-2)^{2018}=2^{2018}\pmod{20}$. Work modulo $4$ and $5$:
\begin{itemize}
\item modulo $4$: $2^{2018}\equiv0$, since $2018\ge2$;
\item modulo $5$: $2^4=16\equiv1$, and $2018=4\cdot504+2$, so $2^{2018}\equiv2^2=4$.
\end{itemize}
The number $r$ with $0\le r<20$, $r\equiv0\pmod4$ and $r\equiv4\pmod5$ is $r=4$ (the candidates $0,4,8,12,16$ have remainders $0,4,3,2,1$ modulo $5$). So the remainder is $\boxed{4}$.

(Alternatively, the powers $2^2,2^3,2^4,2^5,2^6,\dots$ have remainders $4,8,16,12,4,\dots$ modulo $20$, repeating with period $4$; since $2018-2$ is a multiple of $4$, $2^{2018}\equiv2^2=4$.)

Put the centre at $O=(0,0)$ and $L=(r,0)$. If he swims in the direction $u=(\cos\varphi,\sin\varphi)$, he reaches the shore after the distance $d>0$ with $|L+du|=1$, i.e.
\[d^2+2rd\cos\varphi+r^2-1=0,\qquad d=-r\cos\varphi+\sqrt{1-r^2\sin^2\varphi}\]
(the positive root, since the product of the roots is $r^2-1<0$). He survives exactly when $d\le1$:
\[\sqrt{1-r^2\sin^2\varphi}\le1+r\cos\varphi.\]
The right side is positive (as $r<1$), so we may square:
\[1-r^2\sin^2\varphi\le1+2r\cos\varphi+r^2\cos^2\varphi\iff0\le2r\cos\varphi+r^2\iff\cos\varphi\ge-\dfrac r2.\]
Geometrically: the circle of radius $1$ about $L$ meets the shore at $X$ and $Y$, with $OX=LX=1$, and he survives exactly when he swims towards the arc of the shore inside that circle; the losing directions point into the angle $XLY$ around the direction $LO$, and $\angle OLX=\arccos\dfrac r2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0), L=(0.55,0);
real r=0.55; real a=degrees(acos(r/2));
pair X=L+dir(180-a), Y=L+dir(180+a);
fill(L--arc(L,0.35,-(180-a),180-a)--cycle,rgb(0.8,1,0.8));
draw(circle(O,1),blue+linewidth(1)); draw(circle(L,1),gray+dashed);
draw(O--L); draw(O--X,dashed); draw(L--X,red); draw(L--Y,red);
dot(O); dot(L); dot(X); dot(Y);
label("$O$",O,W); label("$L$",L,SE); label("$X$",X,NW); label("$Y$",Y,SW);
label("$r$",(O+L)/2,S); label("$1$",(L+X)/2,NE,red); label("$1$",(O+X)/2,W);
label("shore",dir(130),NW,blue);
\end{asy}

So the surviving directions are those with $|\varphi|\le\pi-\arccos\dfrac r2$ (measuring $\varphi\in(-\pi,\pi]$ from the direction $OL$), and
\[\mathcal P(r)=\dfrac{2\left(\pi-\arccos\dfrac r2\right)}{2\pi}=1-\dfrac1\pi\arccos\dfrac r2.\]
As $r$ increases from $0$ to $1$, $\arccos\dfrac r2$ decreases strictly from $\dfrac\pi2$ to $\dfrac\pi3$, so $\mathcal P$ increases strictly from $\dfrac12$ to $\dfrac23$, taking every value strictly between them and neither endpoint. Hence $\mathcal A=\dfrac12$ and $\mathcal B=\dfrac23$, and
\[77\mathcal B-7\mathcal A=\dfrac{154}{3}-\dfrac72=\boxed{\dfrac{287}{6}}.\]

question:
In triangle $ABC$ let $BC=a$, $CA=b$ and $AB=c$, and let $m_a$ be the length of the median from $A$ to $BC$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that
\[m_a=\sqrt{\dfrac{b^2}{2}+\dfrac{c^2}{2}-\dfrac{a^2}{4}}=\dfrac12\sqrt{2b^2+2c^2-a^2}.\]
\item[(b)] Show that the formula $m_a=\sqrt{\dfrac a4+\dfrac b2+\dfrac c2}$ is wrong.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let $M$ be the midpoint of $BC$, so $BM=MC=\dfrac a2$, and let $\theta=\angle AMC$. Then $\angle AMB=180^\circ-\theta$, and $\cos\angle AMB=-\cos\theta$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(6,0), A=(1.6,3.6), M=(3,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--M,blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(M);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$M$",M,S);
label("$c$",(A+B)/2,W); label("$b$",(A+C)/2,NE);
label("$\dfrac a2$",(B+M)/2,S); label("$\dfrac a2$",(M+C)/2,S);
label("$m_a$",(A+M)/2,E,blue);
draw(arc(M,0.45,0,degrees(A-M)),red); label("$\theta$",M+0.7*dir(degrees(A-M)/2),red);
\end{asy}

The law of cosines in triangles $AMC$ and $AMB$ gives
\begin{align*}
b^2&=m_a^2+\dfrac{a^2}{4}-a\,m_a\cos\theta,\\
c^2&=m_a^2+\dfrac{a^2}{4}+a\,m_a\cos\theta.
\end{align*}
Adding, $b^2+c^2=2m_a^2+\dfrac{a^2}{2}$, so
\[m_a^2=\dfrac{b^2}{2}+\dfrac{c^2}{2}-\dfrac{a^2}{4}\quad\text{and}\quad m_a=\dfrac12\sqrt{2b^2+2c^2-a^2}.\quad\blacksquare\]
(If $\theta=90^\circ$ the same equations hold with $\cos\theta=0$.)

\textbf{(b)} In an equilateral triangle with side $1$ the median is the height $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\approx0.866$, which agrees with (a): $\sqrt{\dfrac12+\dfrac12-\dfrac14}=\dfrac{\sqrt3}2$. The other formula gives $\sqrt{\dfrac14+\dfrac12+\dfrac12}=\dfrac{\sqrt5}{2}\approx1.118$, which is wrong. (Nor can it hold for all triangles of any one shape: scaling a triangle by $2$ doubles the median, but multiplies $\sqrt{\dfrac a4+\dfrac b2+\dfrac c2}$ only by $\sqrt2$.)

Split the $12$ positions into three blocks: positions $1$--$3$ (only B's and C's allowed), positions $4$--$7$ (only A's and C's), and positions $8$--$12$ (only A's and B's).

Let $k$ be the number of A's in the last block. Then:
\begin{itemize}
\item the last block has $k$ A's and $5-k$ B's;
\item the middle block has the other $3-k$ A's and $4-(3-k)=k+1$ C's;
\item the first block has the other $5-(k+1)=4-k$ C's and $3-(4-k)=k-1$ B's.
\end{itemize}
The B count checks: $(k-1)+(5-k)=4$. All counts must be nonnegative, so $1\le k\le3$. Within each block the letters can be placed in any order, so the number of arrangements is
\[\sum_{k=1}^{3}\dbinom{3}{k-1}\dbinom{4}{3-k}\dbinom{5}{k}=1\cdot6\cdot5+3\cdot4\cdot10+3\cdot1\cdot10=30+120+30=\boxed{180}.\]

question:
For nonzero polynomials $f$ and $g$ (with real coefficients) we have the rule $\deg(fg)=\deg f+\deg g$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove this rule.
\item[(b)] Show that it is impossible to give the zero polynomial an integer degree $d$ so that the rule holds for all polynomials $f$ and $g$, including the zero polynomial.
\item[(c)] Explain why the convention $\deg0=-\infty$, with $-\infty+n=-\infty$ for every $n$ and $-\infty+(-\infty)=-\infty$, keeps the rule true for all polynomials.
\end{enumerate}

In trapezoid $ABCD$ with $\overline{AB}\parallel\overline{DC}$, let $AB=a$ and $DC=b$ with $a\ne b$, and let $E$ be the intersection of the diagonals $\overline{AC}$ and $\overline{BD}$. Four segments parallel to the bases are defined, each with one endpoint on $\overline{AD}$ and the other on $\overline{BC}$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\overline{LL'}$ divides the trapezoid into two parts of equal area, $[ABL'L]=[LL'CD]$;
\item $\overline{MM'}$ joins the midpoints $M$ and $M'$ of $\overline{AD}$ and $\overline{BC}$;
\item $\overline{NN'}$ divides the trapezoid into two similar trapezoids, $ABN'N\sim NN'CD$ (with the vertices corresponding in this order);
\item $\overline{OO'}$ passes through $E$.
\end{itemize}
Prove that $LL'>MM'>NN'>OO'$.

solution:
\textbf{Lengths of parallel cross-sections.} Let the height be $h$ and put $A=(0,0)$, $B=(a,0)$, $D=(p,h)$, $C=(p+b,h)$. The segment parallel to the bases at height $th$ ($0<t<1$) joins $(tp,th)$ on $AD$ to $\left(a+t(p+b-a),th\right)$ on $BC$, so its length is
\[\ell(t)=a+t(b-a),\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad t=\dfrac{\ell-a}{b-a}.\]
It cuts off from $ABCD$ the trapezoid with parallel sides $a$, $\ell$ and height $th$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(7,0), D=(2,4), C=(4,4);
real a=7, bb=2;
real[] ell={sqrt((a^2+bb^2)/2),(a+bb)/2,sqrt(a*bb),2*a*bb/(a+bb)};
string[] nm={"L","M","N","O"};
pen[] pc={red,blue,deepgreen,magenta};
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,gray+dashed); draw(B--D,gray+dashed);
for(int i=0;i<4;++i){ real t=(a-ell[i])/(a-bb); pair P=A+t*(D-A), Q=B+t*(C-B); draw(P--Q,pc[i]+linewidth(0.8)); label("$"+nm[i]+"$",P,W,pc[i]); label("$"+nm[i]+"'$",Q,E,pc[i]); }
pair E=A+7/9*(C-A); dot(E); label("$E$",E,N,fontsize(8pt));
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$a$",(A+B)/2,S); label("$b$",(C+D)/2,N);
\end{asy}

\textbf{$LL'$.} Equal areas means $\dfrac{(a+\ell)th}{2}=\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac{(a+b)h}{2}$, i.e. $(a+\ell)\cdot\dfrac{\ell-a}{b-a}=\dfrac{a+b}{2}$, so $\ell^2-a^2=\dfrac{b^2-a^2}{2}$ and
\[LL'=\sqrt{\dfrac{a^2+b^2}{2}}.\]
\textbf{$MM'$.} The midpoints correspond to $t=\dfrac12$, so $MM'=\dfrac{a+b}{2}$.

\textbf{$NN'$.} In $ABN'N\sim NN'CD$, the side $AB$ corresponds to $NN'$ and $N'N$ corresponds to $DC$, so $\dfrac{a}{\ell}=\dfrac{\ell}{b}$ and $\ell^2=ab$. Conversely, the cross-section with $\ell=\sqrt{ab}$ works: it has $t=\dfrac{\sqrt{ab}-a}{b-a}=\dfrac{\sqrt a}{\sqrt a+\sqrt b}$, so the legs are divided in the ratio $t:(1-t)=\sqrt a:\sqrt b=a:\ell$, which is also the ratio of the parallel sides and of the heights; and corresponding angles are equal because $NN'$ is parallel to the bases. So
\[NN'=\sqrt{ab}.\]
\textbf{$OO'$.} Triangles $ABE$ and $CDE$ are similar (as $AB\parallel DC$) with ratio $a:b$, so $E$ divides the height in the ratio $a:b$, i.e. $t=\dfrac{a}{a+b}$. Then
\[OO'=a+\dfrac{a(b-a)}{a+b}=\dfrac{2ab}{a+b}.\]
\textbf{The inequalities.} With $a\ne b$ and $a,b>0$:
\begin{align*}
\dfrac{a^2+b^2}{2}-\left(\dfrac{a+b}{2}\right)^2&=\dfrac{(a-b)^2}{4}>0,\\
\dfrac{a+b}{2}-\sqrt{ab}&=\dfrac{\left(\sqrt a-\sqrt b\right)^2}{2}>0,\\
\sqrt{ab}-\dfrac{2ab}{a+b}&=\dfrac{\sqrt{ab}\left(\sqrt a-\sqrt b\right)^2}{a+b}>0.
\end{align*}
So $LL'>MM'>NN'>OO'$: these are the quadratic, arithmetic, geometric and harmonic means of $a$ and $b$. $\blacksquare$ (If $a=b$ all four lengths would be equal, which is why the weak inequalities $\ge$ are often stated.)

Let $N$ be the midpoint of $CA$ and let $B'$ be the reflection of $B$ in the point $N$. The diagonals $BB'$ and $AC$ of $ABCB'$ bisect each other, so $ABCB'$ is a parallelogram with sides $8$ and $9$ and diagonals $AC=10$ and $BB'=2\,BN$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), C=(10,0);
real x=(64-81+100)/20; pair B=(x,sqrt(64-x^2));
pair Nm=(A+C)/2, B2=2*Nm-B;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(B--B2,blue); draw(A--B2--C,dashed);
dot(Nm);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,N); label("$C$",C,E); label("$N$",Nm,SE); label("$B'$",B2,S);
label("$8$",(A+B)/2,NW); label("$9$",(B+C)/2,NE); label("$5$",(A+Nm)/2,S); label("$5$",(Nm+C)/2,S);
\end{asy}

In a parallelogram the sum of the squares of the diagonals equals the sum of the squares of the four sides (by the law of cosines in two adjacent triangles, whose angles at a common side are supplementary). Hence
\[(2BN)^2+10^2=2\cdot8^2+2\cdot9^2=290,\qquad 4BN^2=190,\qquad BN=\dfrac{\sqrt{190}}{2}.\]
Since $190=2\cdot5\cdot19$ is squarefree, $a=190$, $b=2$ and $a+b=\boxed{192}$.

question:
Let $I$ and $G$ be the incentre and the centroid of $\triangle ABC$, let $\overline{AD}$ be the angle bisector from $A$ (with $D$ on $\overline{BC}$) and $\overline{AE}$ the median from $A$. The side lengths $AB$, $BC$, $CA$ form an arithmetic sequence, in that order.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $\dfrac{AI}{ID}=\dfrac{AG}{GE}$, and deduce that $IG\parallel BC$.
\item[(b)] Show that $\dfrac{ID}{AI}=\dfrac{AG}{GE}$ is false for every such triangle.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $a=BC$, $b=CA$, $c=AB$. The arithmetic sequence condition is $2a=b+c$.

\textbf{(a)} By the angle bisector theorem in triangle $ABC$, $\dfrac{BD}{DC}=\dfrac cb$, so $BD=\dfrac{ac}{b+c}$. In triangle $ABD$ the segment $BI$ bisects the angle at $B$, so by the angle bisector theorem again
\[\dfrac{AI}{ID}=\dfrac{AB}{BD}=\dfrac{c}{\dfrac{ac}{b+c}}=\dfrac{b+c}{a}=\dfrac{2a}{a}=2.\]
The centroid divides each median in the ratio $2:1$ from the vertex, so $\dfrac{AG}{GE}=2$. Hence $\dfrac{AI}{ID}=\dfrac{AG}{GE}$. $\blacksquare$

Since $\dfrac{AI}{AD}=\dfrac{AG}{AE}=\dfrac23$, triangle $AIG$ is the image of triangle $ADE$ under the dilation from $A$ with factor $\dfrac23$, so $IG\parallel DE$, i.e. $IG\parallel BC$ (if $D=E$, i.e. $b=c$, then $I$ and $G$ both lie on the median and coincide).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(6,0), A=(1,sqrt(24));
pair I=(6*A+7*B+5*C)/18, G=(A+B+C)/3, D=(2.5,0), Em=(3,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,blue); draw(A--Em,red);
draw(B--I,gray+dashed); draw(I--G,deepgreen+dashed);
dot(I); dot(G); dot(D); dot(Em);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$D$",D,S,blue); label("$E$",Em,S,red); label("$I$",I,W); label("$G$",G,E);
label("$5$",(A+B)/2,W); label("$6$",(B+C)/2+(0.8,0),S); label("$7$",(A+C)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

(The figure shows the triangle with $AB=5$, $BC=6$, $CA=7$.)

\textbf{(b)} By (a), $\dfrac{ID}{AI}=\dfrac12$ while $\dfrac{AG}{GE}=2$, so they are never equal.

