Let the triangle be $ABC$; we show that the external bisectors at $B$ and $C$ and the internal bisector at $A$ pass through one point. We use the fact that a point on the bisector of an angle is equidistant from the two lines forming it (the two right triangles formed with the feet of the perpendiculars are congruent by AAS), and conversely that a point inside an angle that is equidistant from its two sides lies on its bisector (congruent right triangles by hypotenuse and leg).

\textbf{The external bisectors meet.} The external angle at $B$ has as its sides the ray $BC$ and the extension of $AB$ beyond $B$. That extension lies on the opposite side of line $BC$ from $A$, and the ray $BC$ lies on $C$'s side of line $AB$; so the inside of this angle, and with it the external bisector at $B$ (a ray from $B$), lies on the opposite side of $BC$ from $A$ and on $C$'s side of $AB$. Similarly the external bisector at $C$ is a ray from $C$ on the opposite side of $BC$ from $A$ and on $B$'s side of $AC$. These two rays make angles $\dfrac{180^\circ-B}{2}$ and $\dfrac{180^\circ-C}{2}$ with segment $BC$, which add up to $180^\circ-\dfrac{B+C}{2}<180^\circ$, so they meet at a point $J$ (forming a triangle $BCJ$).

\textbf{$J$ is on the internal bisector at $A$.} Let $P$, $R$, $Q$ be the feet of the perpendiculars from $J$ to the lines $AB$, $BC$, $CA$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,3.2), B=(-1.4,0), C=(2.2,0);
real a=abs(B-C), bb=abs(C-A), c=abs(A-B);
pair J=(-a*A+bb*B+c*C)/(-a+bb+c);
real r=-J.y;
draw(circle(J,r),gray+dashed);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--(A+1.75*(B-A)),gray); draw(A--(A+1.75*(C-A)),gray);
draw(B--J,blue); draw(C--J,blue); draw(A--J,red);
pair Pp=B+dot(J-B,unit(B-A))*unit(B-A), Qp=C+dot(J-C,unit(C-A))*unit(C-A), Rp=(J.x,0);
draw(J--Pp,dashed); draw(J--Qp,dashed); draw(J--Rp,dashed);
dot(J); dot(Pp); dot(Qp); dot(Rp);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,E); label("$J$",J,S);
label("$P$",Pp,W); label("$Q$",Qp,E); label("$R$",Rp,NE,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Since $J$ is on the external bisector at $B$, $JP=JR$; since it is on the external bisector at $C$, $JR=JQ$. Hence $JP=JQ$: $J$ is equidistant from the lines $AB$ and $AC$. Moreover $J$ is on $C$'s side of line $AB$ and on $B$'s side of line $AC$, so $J$ lies inside the angle $BAC$. A point inside an angle that is equidistant from its sides lies on its bisector, so $J$ lies on the internal bisector of angle $A$.

Hence the three bisectors pass through $J$. $\blacksquare$ (The point $J$ is the excentre opposite $A$: the circle with centre $J$ and radius $JP=JQ=JR$ touches line $BC$ and the extensions of $AB$ and $AC$.)

question:
Four circles of radius $1$ are each tangent to two sides of a square and externally tangent to a circle of radius $2$, as shown. What is the area of the square?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=2+3*sqrt(2); real h=s/2; real a=h-1;
draw((-h,-h)--(h,-h)--(h,h)--(-h,h)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle((0,0),2));
draw(circle((a,a),1)); draw(circle((-a,a),1)); draw(circle((a,-a),1)); draw(circle((-a,-a),1));
\end{asy}

$\textbf{(A) }32\qquad\textbf{(B) }22+12\sqrt2\qquad\textbf{(C) }16+16\sqrt3\qquad\textbf{(D) }48\qquad\textbf{(E) }36+16\sqrt2$

solution:
Let the square have side $s$ and centre $O$, and use coordinates with $O$ at the origin and sides parallel to the axes. A circle of radius $1$ tangent to the two sides at a corner has its centre at distance $1$ from both sides, e.g. $P=\left(\dfrac s2-1,\dfrac s2-1\right)$. The four such centres form a square centred at $O$, and the circle of radius $2$, being at distance $1+2=3$ from all four of them, is centred at $O$ (as in the figure).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=2+3*sqrt(2); real h=s/2; real a=h-1;
draw((-h,-h)--(h,-h)--(h,h)--(-h,h)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle((0,0),2));
draw(circle((a,a),1)); draw(circle((-a,a),1)); draw(circle((a,-a),1)); draw(circle((-a,-a),1));
pair O=(0,0), P=(a,a);
draw(O--P,red); draw(P--(h,a),blue); draw(P--(a,h),blue);
dot(O); dot(P);
label("$O$",O,SW); label("$P$",P,NW,fontsize(8pt));
label("$1$",(a+0.5,a),S,blue+fontsize(8pt));
label("$OP=3$",(0.55*a,0.55*a),NW,red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

External tangency gives $OP=1+2=3$, and $OP=\sqrt2\left(\dfrac s2-1\right)$. Hence
\[\dfrac s2-1=\dfrac{3}{\sqrt2}=\dfrac{3\sqrt2}{2},\qquad s=2+3\sqrt2,\]
and the area is
\[s^2=4+12\sqrt2+18=22+12\sqrt2.\]
The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(B) }22+12\sqrt2}$.

question:
A laser pointer is held at height $h>0$ above a straight line $\ell$, first pointing straight down at the point $F$ of $\ell$. It is then rotated so that the beam makes an angle $\theta$ with the vertical, and the beam hits $\ell$ at the point $P$ with $FP=p$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Express $\theta$ in terms of $p$ and $h$.
\item[(b)] A student argues: ``As $p\to\infty$ we have $\theta\to90^\circ$. But a beam at $90^\circ$ is parallel to $\ell$. So two lines can be parallel and intersect at the same time.'' What is wrong with this reasoning?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The laser, $F$ and $P$ form a right triangle with the right angle at $F$, legs $h$ and $p$, and angle $\theta$ at the laser:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair L=(0,2.2), Fp=(0,0), P=(3.2,0);
draw((-0.6,0)--(7.4,0),linewidth(0.9)); label("$\ell$",(7.4,0),E);
draw(L--Fp,dashed); dot(L); label("laser",L,W);
draw(L--(1.4,0),gray+dotted); draw(L--(6.6,0),gray+dotted);
draw(L--P,red); dot(P,red);
label("$P$",P,S,red); label("$F$",Fp,S);
label("$h$",(0,1.1),W); draw((0,-0.75)--(3.2,-0.75),Arrows(4)); label("$p$",(1.6,-0.75),S);
draw(arc(L,0.6,-90,-34.51),blue); label("$\theta$",L+0.85*dir(-62),blue);
draw(L--(7.4,2.2),gray+dashed); label("$\theta=90^\circ$",(7.2,2.2),N,gray);
\end{asy}

so $\tan\theta=\dfrac ph$ and $\boxed{\theta=\arctan\dfrac ph}$.

\textbf{(b)} For every actual hitting point $P$ (every finite $p\ge0$) we have $\theta=\arctan\dfrac ph<90^\circ$, so the beam is never parallel to $\ell$. As $p$ grows, $\theta$ gets as close to $90^\circ$ as we like, which is all that $\lim\theta=90^\circ$ means; but the limit value is never attained. The beam at exactly $90^\circ$ is horizontal, parallel to $\ell$, and it meets $\ell$ nowhere: there is no point $P$ for it.

So the argument wrongly treats the limit as if it were one of the values: ``$\theta\to90^\circ$'' does not mean that some beam with $\theta=90^\circ$ hits $\ell$. (Compare $\dfrac1n\to0$, although $\dfrac1n$ is never $0$.) No line is both parallel to $\ell$ and intersecting it.

The instrument does not measure distance directly: it counts wheel turns and multiplies by the circumference of the original wheel, $2\pi\cdot15$ inches.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),1.5),linewidth(0.9)); draw((0,0)--(1.5,0),blue); dot((0,0));
label("$15$",(0.75,0),N,blue); label("original",(0,-1.5),S);
draw(circle((4.1,0),1.53),linewidth(0.9)); draw((4.1,0)--(5.63,0),red); dot((4.1,0));
label("$R$",(4.85,0),N,red); label("snow tire",(4.1,-1.53),S);
\end{asy}

The route is $450$ miles long (the reading with the original tires is accurate). With wheels of radius $R$ inches, each turn covers $2\pi R$ inches, so over the same route the wheels turn $\dfrac{450\text{ mi}}{2\pi R}$ times, and the instrument shows
\[\dfrac{450}{2\pi R}\cdot2\pi\cdot15=\dfrac{450\cdot15}{R}\text{ miles}.\]
(The unit conversion between miles and inches cancels.) Setting this equal to $440$:
\[R=\dfrac{450\cdot15}{440}=\dfrac{675}{44}\approx15.3409\text{ inches}.\]
So the radius increased by about $15.3409-15=0.3409$, i.e. $\boxed{0.34\text{ inch}}$ to the nearest hundredth.

\textbf{Why inverse proportion.} For a fixed true distance, the reading is proportional to $\dfrac1R$: bigger wheels turn fewer times. The proportion $\dfrac{15}{440}=\dfrac{R}{450}$ (reading $\times$ radius is constant) gives the new radius $R\approx15.34$, but the question asks for the \emph{increase}, $R-15\approx0.34$.

The radius is $\dfrac{76.2}{2}=38.1$ cm.

