question:
A student proposes a shortcut for spotting extraneous solutions of equations with square roots. For $\sqrt{x+5}=-3$, squaring gives $x+5=9$, so $x=4$; the student writes the side condition $x\ge5$ next to the work, sees that $x=4$ fails it, and rejects it.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why $x\ge5$ is not the right side condition, and state the condition that must be checked when an equation $\sqrt{A}=B$ is squared.
\item[(b)] Use the correct condition to solve $\sqrt{x+4}=x-2$ without substituting the candidates back.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $\angle ACB=x$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $CD=DA$, so triangle $ACD$ is isosceles and $\angle DAC=\angle DCA=x$.
\item $AD$ bisects angle $A$, so $\angle DAB=x$ and $\angle CAB=2x$.
\item $\angle ADB$ is an exterior angle of triangle $ACD$, so $\angle ADB=\angle DAC+\angle DCA=2x$.
\item $AB=AD$, so triangle $ABD$ is isosceles and $\angle ABD=\angle ADB=2x$.
\end{itemize}
The angles of triangle $ABD$ give $x+2x+2x=180^\circ$, so $x=36^\circ$.

