Then $\angle CAB=72^\circ=\angle ABC$, which agrees with the last given condition (it is not needed, but it is consistent). So $\angle ACB=\boxed{36^\circ}$.

Since $AB<DC$, the lines $DA$ and $CB$ meet at a point $P$ beyond $AB$. Because $AB\parallel EF\parallel DC$, the triangles $PAB$, $PEF$ and $PDC$ are similar, so their areas are proportional to the squares of $AB$, $EF$ and $DC$: say $[PAB]=k\cdot AB^2$, $[PEF]=k\cdot EF^2$, $[PDC]=k\cdot DC^2$ for some $k>0$.

