question:
Bob makes tacos. He has three meats (beef, pork, chicken), four vegetables (cucumbers, tomatoes, peppers, lettuce), three sauces (salsa, sour cream, a secret sauce) and two kinds of shell (whole wheat, white).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many different tacos can he make with exactly one meat, two different vegetables, one sauce and one shell?
\item[(b)] How many of the tacos in (a) can a customer have who eats no pork and never has sour cream together with meat?
\item[(c)] For a vegetarian customer, the meat is replaced by one of two options, cheese or fake meat; everything else is as in (a). How many tacos are possible?
\end{enumerate}

What is printed when the following program is run? (The operation \texttt{\textasciicircum} is exponentiation and is done before \texttt{*} and \texttt{/}, which are done from left to right; \texttt{int(x)} is the integer part of \texttt{x}.)
