solution:
\textbf{At most $3$.} At each vertex of a convex polygon the interior and exterior angles add up to $180^\circ$, and the exterior angles add up to $360^\circ$. An acute interior angle (less than $90^\circ$) has an exterior angle greater than $90^\circ$. If there were $4$ or more acute angles, their exterior angles alone would add up to more than $4\cdot90^\circ=360^\circ$, which is impossible.

\textbf{$3$ is possible.} Start with a triangle whose three angles are acute, and push five points of its sides slightly outwards (say two points on each of two sides and one point on the third side). The triangle becomes a convex octagon whose five new angles are slightly less than $180^\circ$, while the three original angles grow only slightly and stay acute. The figure shows such an octagon: its exterior angles are $100^\circ,12^\circ,12^\circ,100^\circ,12^\circ,12^\circ,100^\circ,12^\circ$ in order (sum $360^\circ$), so its interior angles are $80^\circ$ at three vertices and $168^\circ$ at the other five.

\begin{asy}
