A $5$-digit palindrome is $\overline{abcba}$ with $a\ne0$. It must be divisible by $5$ and by $9$.
\begin{itemize}
\item Divisible by $5$: the last digit $a$ is $0$ or $5$, and $a\ne0$, so $a=5$.
\item Divisible by $9$: the digit sum $2a+2b+c=10+2b+c$ is a multiple of $9$. Since $0\le2b+c\le27$, we need $2b+c\in\{8,17,26\}$.
\end{itemize}
Count the digit pairs $(b,c)$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $2b+c=8$: $b=0,1,2,3,4$ (with $c=8,6,4,2,0$), $5$ pairs;
\item $2b+c=17$: $b=4,5,6,7,8$ (with $c=9,7,5,3,1$), $5$ pairs;
\item $2b+c=26$: $b=9$, $c=8$, $1$ pair.
\end{itemize}
So there are $5+5+1=\boxed{11}$ such palindromes (from $50805$ to $59895$).

