\textbf{Lower bound.} The total weight is $100+100+40+30+70+30=370$ pounds, and each trip to the far side carries at most $170$ pounds. Two trips carry at most $340<370$ pounds, so at least $3$ trips to the far side are needed. After each of these trips except the last, someone must bring the boat back. So at least $3$ forward trips and $2$ return trips are needed: at least $5$ crossings.

\textbf{Five crossings suffice.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Caroll and Grayson cross ($110$ pounds).
\item Grayson returns.
\item Lucky, Grayson and Vice cross ($170$ pounds).
\item Grayson returns.
\item Tarune, Grayson and Whitnie cross ($170$ pounds).
\end{enumerate}
Now everyone is on the far side, so the least number of crossings is $\boxed{5}$.

\textbf{Method 1 (Pythagoras twice).} Let $J$ be the midpoint of edge $BF$. In the face $ABFE$, triangle $ABJ$ has a right angle at $B$, so $AJ=\sqrt{AB^2+BJ^2}=\sqrt{2^2+1^2}=\sqrt5$. In the face $BCGF$, the segment $JI$ joins the midpoint of $BF$ to the centre of the face, so $JI\parallel BC$ and $JI=1$. Since $BC$ is perpendicular to the whole face $ABFE$, $JI$ is perpendicular to $AJ$, and triangle $AJI$ has a right angle at $J$:
\[AI=\sqrt{AJ^2+JI^2}=\sqrt{\left(\sqrt5\right)^2+1^2}=\sqrt6.\]

