\textbf{Method 2 (coordinates).} Put $A=(0,0,0)$, $B=(2,0,0)$, $D=(0,2,0)$, $E=(0,0,2)$. The face $BCGF$ is the face $x=2$, with centre $I=(2,1,1)$, so
\[AI=\sqrt{2^2+1^2+1^2}=\sqrt6.\]
Both methods give $AI=\boxed{\sqrt6}$.

Take $r=1$, centre $O$, the two points $A,B$ on the unit circle and $C$ on the circle of radius $\sqrt2$. Let $\theta\in[0,\pi]$ be the angle $AOB$; it is uniformly distributed on $[0,\pi]$. Events of probability $0$ (such as $A=B$ or a right angle) are ignored.

\textbf{The angle at $C$ is never obtuse.} Since $|A+B|=2\cos\dfrac\theta2$ and $A\cdot B=\cos\theta=2\cos^2\dfrac\theta2-1$,
\[(A-C)\cdot(B-C)=A\cdot B-C\cdot(A+B)+|C|^2\ge2c^2-1-\sqrt2\cdot2c+2=\left(\sqrt2\,c-1\right)^2\ge0,\qquad c=\cos\dfrac\theta2.\]
(Equivalently, the circle with diameter $AB$, dotted in the figure, never reaches outside the circle of radius $\sqrt2$.)

\textbf{The angle at $A$.} It is obtuse exactly when $(C-A)\cdot(B-A)<0$, i.e. when $C$ lies beyond the line $\ell_A$ through $A$ perpendicular to $AB$, on the side away from $B$. With $u$ the unit vector from $A$ towards $B$, we have $A\cdot u=-\sin\dfrac\theta2$, so $\ell_A$ is at distance $d=\sin\dfrac\theta2$ from $O$, and $O$ is on the same side as $B$. The line cuts off from the circle of radius $\sqrt2$ an arc (thick in the figure) of central angle $2\arccos\dfrac{d}{\sqrt2}$, so, for fixed $A,B$,
\[P(\angle A\text{ obtuse})=\dfrac1\pi\arccos\left(\dfrac{\sin\dfrac\theta2}{\sqrt2}\right).\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
