By symmetry the angle at $B$ is obtuse with the same conditional probability, and the two events are disjoint (a triangle has at most one obtuse angle). Averaging over $\theta$, with $t=\dfrac\theta2$ uniform on $\left[0,\dfrac\pi2\right]$:
\begin{align*}
P(\text{obtuse})&=\dfrac{2}{\pi}\cdot\dfrac{2}{\pi}\int_0^{\dfrac\pi2}\arccos\left(\dfrac{\sin t}{\sqrt2}\right)dt\\
&=\boxed{\dfrac{4}{\pi^2}\int_0^{\dfrac\pi2}\arccos\left(\dfrac{\sin t}{\sqrt2}\right)dt\approx0.694}.
\end{align*}
Expanding $\arcsin$ in a power series and integrating term by term, the integral equals $\dfrac{\pi^2}{4}-\chi$, where
\[\chi=\sum_{n\ge0}\dfrac{1}{2^{n}\sqrt2\,(2n+1)^2},\]
so the probability is $1-\dfrac{4}{\pi^2}\chi\approx0.69356$. (The sum $\chi$ is a value of Legendre's chi function, $\chi_2\left(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}\right)$; no simpler closed form for it is known.)

\textbf{Counting the choices.} First choose the $6$ endpoints, in $\dbinom96=84$ ways, and then pair them up. Six points can be split into $3$ pairs in $5\cdot3\cdot1=15$ ways (the first point has $5$ possible partners, the first remaining point then has $3$, and the last two are paired). So there are $84\cdot15=1260$ equally likely choices.

\textbf{Non-crossing pairings.} Six points on a circle are in convex position; number them $1$ to $6$ around the circle. Two chords cross exactly when their endpoints alternate around the circle. If point $1$ is joined to point $j$, the points on each side of the chord $1j$ must be paired among themselves, so each side must contain an even number of points: $j=2,4$ or $6$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $j=2$: points $3,4,5,6$ must be paired without crossing: $\{34,56\}$ or $\{36,45\}$, $2$ ways;
\item $j=4$: the pairs $23$ and $56$ are forced, $1$ way;
\item $j=6$: points $2,3,4,5$: $\{23,45\}$ or $\{25,34\}$, $2$ ways.
\end{itemize}
So $5$ of the $15$ pairings are non-crossing (this is the Catalan number $C_3=5$):

