void hexpic(pair c, int[] m){
  draw(circle(c,1),gray);
  pair[] P; for(int k=0;k<6;++k) P.push(c+dir(90-60k));
  for(int k=0;k<3;++k) draw(P[m[2k]]--P[m[2k+1]],blue+linewidth(0.9));
  for(int k=0;k<6;++k){dot(P[k]); label("$"+string(k+1)+"$",c+1.25*dir(90-60k),fontsize(7pt));}
}
hexpic((0,0),new int[]{0,1,2,3,4,5});
hexpic((2.8,0),new int[]{1,2,3,4,5,0});
hexpic((5.6,0),new int[]{0,1,2,5,3,4});
hexpic((1.4,-2.8),new int[]{0,3,1,2,4,5});
hexpic((4.2,-2.8),new int[]{0,5,1,4,2,3});
\end{asy}

Hence the number of favourable choices is $84\cdot5$, and the probability is
\[\dfrac{84\cdot5}{84\cdot15}=\boxed{\dfrac13}.\]
(The number $9$ does not matter: whichever $6$ points are used, $5$ of the $15$ pairings are non-crossing.)

question:
Twenty-five students sit around a round table. Three of them are chosen, all choices of three students being equally likely.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the probability that at least two of the three chosen students were sitting next to each other.
\item[(b)] A posted solution lets $a,b,c$ be the numbers of unchosen students in the three gaps between consecutive chosen students (going around the table), so that $a+b+c=22$. It says that $a+b+c=22$ has $\dbinom{21}{2}$ solutions with $a,b,c\ge1$ and $\dbinom{24}{2}$ solutions with $a,b,c\ge0$, and that the probability that no two chosen students are neighbours is $\dfrac{\dbinom{21}{2}}{\dbinom{24}{2}}$. Explain where $\dbinom{21}{2}$ and $\dbinom{24}{2}$ come from, and why this ratio is correct even though a choice of three students does not correspond to just one triple $(a,b,c)$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{Slopes of chords.} The chord through $\left(s,s^2\right)$ and $\left(t,t^2\right)$ has slope $\dfrac{t^2-s^2}{t-s}=s+t$. So the sides $PQ$, $QR$, $RP$ have slopes $p+q$, $q+r$, $r+p$.

\textbf{Directions.} $PQ$ has slope $\sqrt3$, so it makes an angle of $60^\circ$ with the $x$-axis. In an equilateral triangle the other two sides make angles of $60^\circ$ with $PQ$, on either side, so (as lines) they make angles $0^\circ$ and $120^\circ$ with the $x$-axis, and they are different lines. Their slopes are $0$ and $-\sqrt3$, so $\{q+r,\ r+p\}=\{0,-\sqrt3\}$.

\textbf{Solving.} In either case, adding the three slopes gives $2(p+q+r)=\sqrt3+0-\sqrt3=0$, so $p+q+r=0$ and $r=-(p+q)=-\sqrt3$. Then $\{q,p\}=\{\sqrt3,0\}$, and the vertices are $(0,0)$, $\left(\sqrt3,3\right)$, $\left(-\sqrt3,3\right)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
