The side length is the distance from $\left(-\sqrt3,3\right)$ to $\left(\sqrt3,3\right)$:
\[QR=2\sqrt3,\qquad\text{and indeed}\quad PQ=\sqrt{3+9}=2\sqrt3.\]
So the side length is $\boxed{2\sqrt3}$.

\textbf{Condition for tangency.} Write the parabola as $F(x,y)=y+x^2+1=0$ and the circle as $G(x,y)=x^2+(y+a)^2-1=0$. At a common point the curves have the same tangent line exactly when the normal vectors $(2x,1)$ and $(2x,2(y+a))$ are parallel:
\[2x\cdot2(y+a)-2x\cdot1=0\iff x\bigl(2(y+a)-1\bigr)=0.\]

\textbf{Case $x=0$.} Then $y=-1$ on the parabola, and the circle passes through $(0,-1)$ when $(a-1)^2=1$, i.e. $a=0$ or $a=2$.

\textbf{Case $x\ne0$.} Then $y=\dfrac12-a$, and the parabola gives $x^2=-y-1=a-\dfrac32$, which must be positive. The circle gives $x^2+(y+a)^2=1$, i.e. $a-\dfrac32+\dfrac14=1$, so $a=\dfrac94$. Then $y=-\dfrac74$ and $x=\pm\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$: the curves touch at two symmetric points.

So the values are $\boxed{a=0,\ a=2,\ a=\dfrac94}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(10cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return -x^2-1;}
void panel(pair c, real a, string s){
  draw(shift(c)*((-1.8,0)--(1.8,0)),gray); draw(shift(c)*((0,-3.6)--(0,0.8)),gray);
  draw(shift(c)*graph(f,-1.6,1.6),blue+linewidth(0.9));
  draw(circle(c+(0,-a),1),red+linewidth(0.9));
  dot(c+(0,-a),red);
  label(s,c+(0,-3.6),S,fontsize(8pt));
}
panel((0,0),0,"$a=0$"); dot((0,-1));
panel((4,0),2,"$a=2$"); dot((4,-1)); dot((4+1,-2),heavygreen); dot((4-1,-2),heavygreen);
panel((8,0),9/4,"$a=\dfrac94$"); dot((8+sqrt(3)/2,-7/4)); dot((8-sqrt(3)/2,-7/4));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Other common points.} Substituting $x^2=-y-1$ into the circle gives $y^2+(2a-1)y+a^2-2=0$, and each root $y\le-1$ gives the common points with that $y$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $a=0$: $y^2-y-2=0$, $y=-1$ or $y=2$; only $y=-1$ is possible, so $(0,-1)$ is the only common point.
\item $a=2$: $y^2+3y+2=0$, $y=-1$ or $y=-2$. Besides the tangency at $(0,-1)$, the curves meet at $(\pm1,-2)$, where they cross: there the parabola has slope $\mp2$, while the circle has a vertical tangent, because $(\pm1,-2)$ is level with its centre $(0,-2)$.
\item $a=\dfrac94$: $y^2+\dfrac72y+\dfrac{49}{16}=\left(y+\dfrac74\right)^2=0$, a double root, so the only common points are the two points of tangency.
\end{itemize}
So only for $\boxed{a=2}$ do the curves also cross at other points.

question:
Are there more positive integers than positive even integers?
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the rule $n\mapsto2n$ pairs every positive integer with exactly one positive even integer, and that every positive even integer is used exactly once.
\item[(b)] Explain in what sense the two sets have ``the same size'', even though every even number is a positive integer but not the other way round.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{Setting.} Let $O$ be the centre. Since the circle is convex, the random points are the vertices, in order around it, of a convex polygon, namely their convex hull. Ignore events of probability $0$ (two points equal, or two points on a line through $O$).

\textbf{Step 1.} $O$ lies outside the polygon exactly when all the points lie in an open half-plane bounded by a line through $O$. Indeed, if all points lie in such a half-plane, so does their convex hull; and if $O$ is outside the convex polygon, a line separating $O$ from the polygon, moved parallel to itself until it passes through $O$, leaves the polygon strictly on one side.

\textbf{Step 2.} For each point $X_i$, let $H_i$ be the open half-plane obtained by turning the ray $OX_i$ counterclockwise through angles strictly between $0^\circ$ and $180^\circ$, and let $E_i$ be the event that all the other points lie in $H_i$. If all points lie in an open half-plane through $O$, that half-plane is swept out by a ray from $O$ turning counterclockwise through $180^\circ$ from one of its bounding rays; let $X_i$ be the first point the ray meets. All the other points are met after turning less than $180^\circ$ further, so $E_i$ holds. Conversely, if $E_i$ holds, turning the line $OX_i$ slightly clockwise gives an open half-plane containing all the points. And two of the events cannot both hold: $X_j\in H_i$ and $X_i\in H_j$ would mean that going counterclockwise from $X_i$ to $X_j$ and from $X_j$ back to $X_i$ both take less than $180^\circ$, although together they make $360^\circ$.

\textbf{Step 3.} The reflection in $O$ maps the circle onto itself and preserves the uniform distribution on it, and it swaps $H_i$ with the opposite open half-plane. So, whatever $X_i$ is, each other point lies in $H_i$ with probability $\dfrac12$, independently of the others, and $P(E_i)=\left(\dfrac12\right)^{m-1}$, where $m$ is the number of points.

\textbf{Conclusion.} Hence
\[P(O\text{ outside the polygon})=P(E_1)+\cdots+P(E_m)=\dfrac{m}{2^{m-1}}.\]

