A knight move goes from one corner square of a $2\times3$ or $3\times2$ block of squares to the opposite corner square of that block, and each block contains exactly $2$ such pairs of opposite corners (its two diagonals), each usable in $2$ directions. Conversely, every knight move lies in exactly one such block.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int k=0;k<=8;++k){draw((k,0)--(k,8),gray); draw((0,k)--(8,k),gray);}
fill(box((1,1),(4,3)),rgb(0.85,0.92,1)); draw(box((1,1),(4,3)),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((1.5,1.5)--(3.5,2.5),red,Arrow(5)); draw((1.5,2.5)--(3.5,1.5),red,Arrow(5));
fill(box((5,3),(7,6)),rgb(0.85,0.92,1)); draw(box((5,3),(7,6)),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((5.5,3.5)--(6.5,5.5),red,Arrow(5)); draw((6.5,3.5)--(5.5,5.5),red,Arrow(5));
\end{asy}

An $8\times8$ board contains $7\cdot6=42$ blocks of size $2\times3$ (width $3$, height $2$) and $6\cdot7=42$ blocks of size $3\times2$, so the number of (start, end) pairs of knight moves is $84\cdot2\cdot2=336$. The expected number of moves from a random square is
\[\dfrac{336}{64}=\boxed{\dfrac{21}{4}}=5.25.\]

Write $n=[x]$ and $f=\{x\}$, so $x=n+f$ with $n$ an integer and $0\le f<1$. All three sides must be positive, so $n\ge1$ and $0<f<1$. The triangle inequalities are:
\begin{itemize}
\item $n+f>\dfrac{n+f}{2}$: always true.
\item $\dfrac{n+f}{2}+n>f$, i.e. $3n>f$: always true, since $n\ge1>f$.
\item $\dfrac{n+f}{2}+f>n$, i.e. $3f>n$.
\end{itemize}
Since $f<1$, the last condition needs $n<3$, so $n=1$ or $n=2$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $n=1$: $f>\dfrac13$, so $\dfrac43<x<2$;
\item $n=2$: $f>\dfrac23$, so $\dfrac83<x<3$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair tri(pair c, real a, real b, real cc){ // side cc on the base, side b at the left vertex
  real x=(b^2-a^2+cc^2)/(2cc); real y=sqrt(b^2-x^2); return c+(x,y);}
void pic(pair c, real X, string s){
  int n=floor(X); real f=X-n;
  real a=X/2, b=f, cc=n, k=2.5;
  pair P=tri(c,k*a,k*b,k*cc); cc=k*cc;
  draw(c--c+(cc,0)--P--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
  label("$["+"x]="+string(n)+"$",c+(cc/2,0),S,fontsize(8pt));
  label("$\{x\}="+format("%.1f",f)+"$",(c+P)/2,NW,fontsize(8pt));
  label("$\dfrac x2="+format("%.2f",a)+"$",(c+(cc,0)+P)/2,NE,fontsize(8pt));
  label(s,c+(cc/2,-0.75),S,fontsize(8pt));
}
pic((0,0),1.8,"$x=1.8$");
pic((6,0),2.9,"$x=2.9$");
\end{asy}

The answer is $\boxed{\dfrac43<x<2\ \text{ or }\ \dfrac83<x<3}$. (The figure shows one triangle from each case.)

question:
A grade has $52$ students. The school day has $4$ periods. In each period the students are split into two sections of $26$ students each, and each section has a class together; the split may be different in different periods.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Can the splits be arranged so that every two students are in the same section in at least one period?
\item[(b)] What is the least number of periods for which such an arrangement exists?
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] In how many ways can the letters of the word SUNDAY be arranged so that the two vowels U and A are not next to each other? (Treat Y as a consonant.)
\item[(b)] How many arrangements are there if Y is also counted as a vowel, so that no two of U, A, Y may be next to each other?
\end{enumerate}

In trapezoid $ABCD$ with $AB\parallel CD$, write $[XYZ]$ for the area of triangle $XYZ$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $[ACD]=[BDC]$.
\item[(b)] The diagonals meet at $O$. Deduce that $[AOD]=[BOC]$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Triangles $ACD$ and $BDC$ have the same base $CD$. Their heights are the distances from $A$ and from $B$ to the line $CD$. Since $AB\parallel CD$, every point of line $AB$ is at the same distance $h$ from line $CD$, so both heights equal $h$:
\[[ACD]=\dfrac12\cdot CD\cdot h=[BDC].\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.2,2.2), B=(4.2,2.2), C=(6,0), D=(0,0);
pair O=extension(A,C,B,D);
fill(A--O--D--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1)); fill(B--O--C--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.85));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,blue); draw(B--D,red);
pair Ha=(A.x,0), Hb=(B.x,0);
draw(A--Ha,dashed); draw(B--Hb,dashed);
draw((Ha+(0.18,0))--(Ha+(0.18,0.18))--(Ha+(0,0.18))); draw((Hb-(0.18,0))--(Hb+(-0.18,0.18))--(Hb+(0,0.18)));
label("$h$",(A+Ha)/2,W); label("$h$",(B+Hb)/2,E);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,N);
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,NE); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,SW);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} Triangle $ACD$ is made up of triangles $AOD$ and $OCD$, and triangle $BDC$ is made up of triangles $BOC$ and $OCD$. Subtracting the common part from the equal areas in (a),
\[[AOD]=[ACD]-[OCD]=[BDC]-[OCD]=[BOC].\ \blacksquare\]

\textbf{Legs.} From each vertex, the two tangent segments to the circle are equal. Splitting every side at its point of tangency and adding,
\[AB+DC=AD+BC,\]
so $AD+BC=40$ and, since the trapezium is isosceles, $AD=BC=20$.

\textbf{Height.} Drop the perpendicular from $A$ to $DC$. Because the trapezium is symmetric, it cuts off $\dfrac{30-10}{2}=10$ from $DC$, so the height is
\[h=\sqrt{20^2-10^2}=\sqrt{300}=10\sqrt3.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=10*sqrt(3);
pair D=(-15,0), C=(15,0), B=(5,h), A=(-5,h), O=(0,h/2);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O,h/2),blue);
pair Fa=(-5,0);
draw(A--Fa,dashed); draw((Fa+(0.9,0))--(Fa+(0.9,0.9))--(Fa+(0,0.9)));
draw(O--(0,h),red); dot(O); label("$O$",O,E);
label("$r$",(0,0.75*h),E,red);
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,NE); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,SW);
label("$10$",(A+B)/2,N); label("$30$",(4,0),S); label("$20$",(B+C)/2,NE); label("$20$",(A+D)/2,NW);
label("$10$",(D+Fa)/2,S); label("$h$",(A+Fa)/2,E);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Circle.} The circle touches the two parallel sides, so its diameter is the distance between them: $2r=10\sqrt3$. Hence
\[r=\boxed{5\sqrt3\text{ cm}},\qquad \text{area}=\pi r^2=\boxed{75\pi\text{ cm}^2}\approx235.6\text{ cm}^2.\]

question:
Call a positive integer \emph{special} if it equals the sum of its digits multiplied by the product of its digits. For example, $15$ is not special, because $(1+5)(1\cdot5)=30\ne15$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $1$, $135$ and $144$ are special.
\item[(b)] Show that a special number has no digit $0$, and that no two-digit number is special.
\item[(c)] Show that a special number has fewer than $70$ digits, so that there are only finitely many special numbers.
\end{enumerate}

The digits of $2021$ are rearranged to form four-digit numbers.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] In how many of the arrangements does the $0$ lie (somewhere) between the two $2$s?
\item[(b)] In how many does the $0$ lie directly between the two $2$s?
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] A circle is tangent to all four sides of a convex quadrilateral $ABCD$. Prove that $AB+CD=BC+DA$.
\item[(b)] Use (a) to show that a rectangle has a circle tangent to all four of its sides only if it is a square.
\item[(c)] A \emph{kite} is a convex quadrilateral $ABCD$ with $AB=AD$ and $CB=CD$. Prove that every kite has a circle tangent to all four of its sides.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The two tangent segments from a point outside a circle to the circle have equal lengths. Let the tangent segments from $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$ have lengths $w$, $x$, $y$, $z$. Each side is split by its point of tangency into two such segments:
\[AB=w+x,\quad BC=x+y,\quad CD=y+z,\quad DA=z+w.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
real[] t={80,190,265,350};
pair V(real a, real c){return dir((a+c)/2)/Cos((c-a)/2);}
pair A=V(t[0],t[1]), B=V(t[1],t[2]), C=V(t[2],t[3]), D=V(t[3],t[0]+360);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O,1),blue);
pair P=dir(t[0]), Q=dir(t[1]), R=dir(t[2]), T=dir(t[3]);
dot(P,blue); dot(Q,blue); dot(R,blue); dot(T,blue);
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,NE);
label("$w$",(A+Q)/2,W,red); label("$w$",(A+P)/2,N,red);
label("$x$",(B+Q)/2,W,red); label("$x$",(B+R)/2,S,red);
label("$y$",(C+R)/2,S,red); label("$y$",(C+T)/2,E,red);
label("$z$",(D+T)/2,E,red); label("$z$",(D+P)/2,N,red);
\end{asy}

Hence $AB+CD=w+x+y+z=BC+DA$. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(b)} For a rectangle with sides $p$ and $q$, (a) requires $p+p=q+q$, so $p=q$ and the rectangle is a square. (Conversely, a square has such a circle: the circle centred at its centre with radius half the side.)

\textbf{(c)} Triangles $ABC$ and $ADC$ are congruent (SSS, with the common side $AC$), so the diagonal $AC$ bisects the angles of the kite at $A$ and at $C$. Let the bisector of angle $ABC$ meet the diagonal $AC$ at $I$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $I$ lies on the bisector of angle $B$, so it is equally far from the lines $BA$ and $BC$.
\item $I$ lies on $AC$, the bisector of angle $A$, so it is equally far from $AB$ and $AD$, and on the bisector of angle $C$, so it is equally far from $CB$ and $CD$.
\end{itemize}
So $I$ is at the same distance $r$ from all four side lines.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,3.2), B=(1.7,0.9), C=(0,-1.1), D=(-1.7,0.9);
pair I=extension(B,B+unit(A-B)+unit(C-B),A,C);
pair ft(pair P,pair U,pair W){return U+dot(P-U,unit(W-U))*unit(W-U);}
real r=abs(I-ft(I,A,B));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,dashed); draw(B--I,dashed+red);
draw(circle(I,r),blue); dot(I); label("$I$",I,1.6*W);
pair F1=ft(I,A,B),F2=ft(I,B,C),F3=ft(I,C,D),F4=ft(I,D,A);
draw(I--F1,blue); draw(I--F2,blue); draw(I--F3,blue); draw(I--F4,blue);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,S); label("$D$",D,W);
\end{asy}

The feet of the perpendiculars from $I$ lie on the sides themselves: for example, in triangle $IAB$ the angles at $A$ and $B$ are halves of angles of the kite, hence acute, so the foot of the perpendicular from $I$ lies between $A$ and $B$. Therefore the circle with centre $I$ and radius $r$ touches all four sides. $\blacksquare$

(In general, a convex quadrilateral has a circle tangent to all four sides exactly when $AB+CD=BC+DA$; part (a) is one direction of this, Pitot's theorem.)

question:
Balls numbered $1,2,3,\dots$ (one for every positive integer) are processed in order. When ball $n$ is processed, it is put into a box, and then, if the box contains the ball numbered $\sqrt n$ (this can happen only when $n$ is a perfect square), that ball is taken out of the box and put into a drawer. For $n=1$ this means that ball $1$ is moved to the drawer right after it is put in, since $\sqrt1=1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many balls are in the box after balls $1,2,\dots,N$ have been processed?
\item[(b)] Show that every ball eventually ends up in the drawer, even though the number of balls in the box grows without bound.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Place $Y=(-6,0)$ and $Z=(6,0)$. The foot of the height is the midpoint $Q=(0,0)$ of the base (the triangle is isosceles), so $X=(0,12)$. Then $R$, the midpoint of $ZX$, is $\left(\dfrac{6+0}{2},\dfrac{0+12}{2}\right)=(3,6)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Y=(-6,0), Z=(6,0), X=(0,12), P=(Y+X)/2, Q=(Y+Z)/2, R=(Z+X)/2;
draw(X--Y--Z--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(X--Q,dashed); draw((0,0.7)--(0.7,0.7)--(0.7,0));
draw(Y--R,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(R--(3,0),dotted);
dot(P); dot(Q); dot(R);
label("$X$",X,N); label("$Y$",Y,SW); label("$Z$",Z,SE); label("$P$",P,W); label("$Q$",Q,S); label("$R$",R,NE);
label("$12$",(0,6),W); label("$6$",(-3,0),S); label("$6$",(4.5,0),S);
label("$9$",(-1.5,0),N,blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$6$",(3,2.3),E,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

By the distance formula (the legs of the right triangle are $3-(-6)=9$ and $6$),
\[YR^2=9^2+6^2=81+36=\boxed{117}.\]

question:
Using a club $8$, Tom hits the ball an average distance of $100$ metres; using a club $7$, $108$ metres; using a club $6$, $114$ metres. Suppose that each time the club number goes down by $1$, the gain in distance is $2$ metres less than the previous gain (the gains are $8$, then $6$, and so on).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How far does the ball travel, on average, with a club $5$?
\item[(b)] Show that, as long as this pattern holds, the average distance with club $8-m$ is $100+9m-m^2$ metres ($m=0,1,2,\dots$).
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $O$ be the centre of the circle. Both tangent lines at $A$ are perpendicular to the radius $OA$ at $A$:
\[AB\perp OA\qquad\text{and}\qquad AC\perp OA.\]
Through a given point $A$ there is only one line perpendicular to the line $OA$. Hence the lines $AB$ and $AC$ are the same line, so $B$, $A$, $C$ all lie on it. $\blacksquare$

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0), A=(0,-1), B=(-1.7,-1), C=(1.5,-1);
draw(circle(O,1));
draw((-2.1,-1)--(1.9,-1),linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--A,dashed);
draw((A+(0.12,0))--(A+(0.12,0.12))--(A+(0,0.12)));
dot(O); dot(A); dot(B); dot(C);
label("$O$",O,N); label("$A$",A,S); label("$B$",B,S); label("$C$",C,S);
\end{asy}

The angle in each corner of the square is a right angle inscribed in the circle, so each diagonal of the square is a diameter, of length $20$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
pair A=10*dir(45), B=10*dir(135), C=10*dir(225), D=10*dir(315);
draw(circle(O,10));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,blue);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,SE);
label("$20$",(A+C)/2+(1.5,3.4),blue);
label("$s$",(A+B)/2,N); label("$s$",(B+C)/2,W);
\end{asy}

If the side is $s$, the Pythagorean theorem in the right isosceles triangle formed by two sides and a diagonal gives $s^2+s^2=20^2$, so $s^2=200$ and
\[s=\boxed{10\sqrt2}.\]

Let the triangle be $ABC$ and the centre $O$. The three sides cut the circle into three equal arcs, so the central angle $\angle BOC$ is $\dfrac{360^\circ}{3}=120^\circ$, and $OB=OC=6$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0), A=6*dir(90), B=6*dir(210), C=6*dir(330);
draw(circle(O,6));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--B,dashed); draw(O--C,dashed);
draw(arc(O,1.1,210,330),blue); label("$120^\circ$",O+1.9*dir(270),blue+fontsize(8pt));
dot(O); label("$O$",O,N);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$6$",(O+B)/2,NW); label("$6$",(O+C)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

By the law of cosines in triangle $OBC$,
\[BC^2=6^2+6^2-2\cdot6\cdot6\cos120^\circ=36+36+36=108,\]
so $BC=\boxed{6\sqrt3}$. (Alternatively, $O$ is the centroid, so the altitude is $\dfrac32\cdot6=9$ and the side is $\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3}\cdot9=6\sqrt3$.)

By the intersecting chords theorem (power of the point $M$),
\[MX\cdot MY=MP\cdot MQ=4\cdot81=324.\]
Since $M$ is the midpoint of $XY$, $MX=MY$, so $MX^2=324$, $MX=18$, and
\[XY=\boxed{36}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=42.5; pair O=(0,0), P=(R,0), Q=(-R,0), M=(R-4,0);
pair X=(R-4,18), Y=(R-4,-18);
draw(circle(O,R));
draw(P--Q,linewidth(0.9)); draw(X--Y,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((M+(-2.5,0))--(M+(-2.5,2.5))--(M+(0,2.5)));
dot(O); dot(M); dot(X); dot(Y); dot(P); dot(Q);
label("$O$",O,N); label("$M$",M+(-2.5,-0.5),SW); label("$X$",X,NE); label("$Y$",Y,SE); label("$P$",P,E); label("$Q$",Q,W);
draw((R-2,0.8)--(R+4,8),linewidth(0.3)); label("$4$",(R+4,8),NE,fontsize(8pt)); label("$81$",(-20,0),S);
label("$18$",(R-4,13),W,blue); label("$18$",(R-4,-13),W,blue);
\end{asy}

(Check: the radius is $\dfrac{85}{2}$ and $OM=\dfrac{85}{2}-4=\dfrac{77}{2}$; since the diameter bisects the chord, $OM\perp XY$ and $MX=\sqrt{\left(\dfrac{85}{2}\right)^2-\left(\dfrac{77}{2}\right)^2}=\sqrt{4\cdot81}=18$.)

Since $OT\perp PT$, the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle $OTP$ gives
\[PO=\sqrt{10^2+6^2}=\sqrt{136}=2\sqrt{34}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); real d=2*sqrt(34); pair P=(-d,0);
real th=aCos(10/d); pair Tt=10*dir(180-th);
pair X=(-10,0), Y=(10,0);
draw(circle(O,10));
draw(P--Tt,linewidth(0.9)); draw(P--Y,blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(O--Tt,dashed);
pair u=unit(P-Tt), v=unit(O-Tt); draw((Tt+1.2*u)--(Tt+1.2*u+1.2*v)--(Tt+1.2*v));
dot(O); dot(P); dot(Tt); dot(X); dot(Y);
label("$O$",O,S); label("$P$",P,W); label("$T$",Tt,N); label("$X$",X,SE); label("$Y$",Y,E);
label("$6$",(P+Tt)/2,NW); label("$10$",(O+Tt)/2,NE); label("$10$",(O+Y)/2,S);
\end{asy}

Hence $PY=PO+OY=\boxed{10+2\sqrt{34}}\approx21.66$. (Also $PX=2\sqrt{34}-10$, and as a check $PX\cdot PY=136-100=36=PT^2$, the power of $P$.)

Let $O$ be the centre. The radii $OA$ and $OB$ are perpendicular to the tangents, and by symmetry $PO$ bisects $\angle APB$, so $\angle APO=60^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); real d=24/Sin(60); pair P=(-d,0);
pair A=24*dir(150), B=24*dir(210);
draw(circle(O,24));
draw(P--A,linewidth(0.9)); draw(P--B,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--A,dashed); draw(O--B,dashed); draw(O--P,dotted);
draw(arc(P,4,-60,60),blue); label("$120^\circ$",P+(8.5,3.2),blue+fontsize(7pt));
pair u=unit(P-A), v=unit(O-A); draw((A+2.5*u)--(A+2.5*u+2.5*v)--(A+2.5*v));
dot(O); dot(P); dot(A); dot(B);
label("$O$",O,E); label("$P$",P,W); label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,S); label("$24$",(O+A)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

In the right triangle $OAP$ (right angle at $A$), the leg $OA=24$ is opposite the $60^\circ$ angle, so
\[PA=\dfrac{OA}{\tan60^\circ}=\dfrac{24}{\sqrt3}=\boxed{8\sqrt3}.\]

For any point $Q$ on the circle, the triangle inequality (in triangle $OPQ$, or on a line when $O,P,Q$ are collinear) gives
\[PQ\ge OQ-OP=6-2=4\qquad\text{and}\qquad PQ\le OQ+OP=6+2=8.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0), P=(2,0), Q=6*dir(115);
draw(circle(O,6));
draw((-6,0)--(6,0),dashed);
draw(P--Q,blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(O--Q,dotted);
dot(O); dot(P); dot(Q); dot((6,0)); dot((-6,0));
label("$O$",O,S); label("$P$",P,S); label("$Q$",Q,NW);
label("$4$",(4,0),N); label("$8$",(-2,0),S);
\end{asy}

The value $4$ is attained at the point of the circle on ray $OP$, and $8$ at the point on the opposite ray. As $Q$ moves along the circle from one of these points to the other, $PQ$ changes continuously, so it takes every value in between. The possible lengths form the interval $\boxed{[4,8]}$.

Let $O$ (radius $12$) and $O'$ (radius $3$) be the centres; since the circles are internally tangent, $OO'=12-3=9$. Let the chord be $AB$ with midpoint $M$ and point of tangency $T$, so $O'T\perp AB$ and $O'T=3$.

\textbf{Coordinates.} The distance from $O$ to the chord is $OM=\sqrt{12^2-\left(\dfrac{21}{2}\right)^2}=\sqrt{\dfrac{135}{4}}=\dfrac{3\sqrt{15}}{2}$. Put $O=(0,0)$ and the chord on the line $y=d$ with $d=\dfrac{3\sqrt{15}}2$, so $A=(-10.5,d)$, $B=(10.5,d)$ and $T=(t,d)$. Then $O'=(t,d+3)$ or $O'=(t,d-3)$: the small circle lies on the far side of the chord from $O$, or on the same side. Then $p=10.5+t$, $q=10.5-t$ and
\[p^2+q^2=2\cdot10.5^2+2t^2=\dfrac{441}{2}+2t^2.\]
From $OO'^2=81$:
\begin{itemize}
\item far side: $t^2=81-(d+3)^2=72-d^2-6d=\dfrac{153}{4}-9\sqrt{15}\approx3.39$;
\item same side: $t^2=81-(d-3)^2=\dfrac{153}{4}+9\sqrt{15}\approx73.11$.
\end{itemize}
In both cases $0<t^2<10.5^2$, so $T$ lies inside the chord and both configurations really occur:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real d=3*sqrt(15)/2; real sg=1;
real b0=d+3*sg; real t=sqrt(81-b0^2);
pair O=(0,0), Op=(t,b0), Tt=(t,d), A=(-10.5,d), B=(10.5,d), K=12*unit(Op);
draw(circle(O,12)); draw(circle(Op,3),blue);
draw(A--B,linewidth(0.9)); draw(Op--Tt,dashed+blue); draw(O--K,dotted);
dot(O); dot(Op,blue); dot(Tt,red); dot(A); dot(B); dot(K);
label("$O$",O,S); label("$O'$",Op,E,blue); label("$T$",Tt,SE,red); label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,E); label("$K$",K,N);
label("$p$",(A+Tt)/2,S); label("$q$",(Tt+B)/2+(sg>0 ? (0,0) : (-0.25,0)),S,fontsize(sg>0 ? 10pt : 8pt));
\end{asy}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real d=3*sqrt(15)/2; real sg=-1;
