The $90^\circ$ arc $AB$ belongs to a circle with centre $C$ and $\angle ACB=90^\circ$, with $C$ on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$ on the side away from the bulge of the arc. Triangle $ACB$ is right isosceles with hypotenuse $AB=120$, so its legs are $CA=CB=60\sqrt2$ (the radius of the arc) and its area is $\dfrac12(60\sqrt2)^2=3600$. Its height from $C$ is $60$, so $C$ is the point of the original circle opposite the arc, and the arc rises $60\sqrt2-60\approx24.9<60$ above $AB$: it stays inside the semicircle on its side.

