Count the palindromes by number of digits:
\begin{itemize}
\item one digit: $9$; two digits ($\overline{aa}$): $9$; three digits ($\overline{aba}$): $9\cdot10=90$; four digits ($\overline{abba}$): $90$.
\end{itemize}
That makes $9+9+90+90=198$ palindromes with at most four digits. So the $1000$th palindrome is the $802$nd five-digit palindrome. Five-digit palindromes $\overline{abcba}$ are in increasing order exactly when their first three digits $\overline{abc}$ are, and $\overline{abc}$ runs through $100,101,\dots,999$. The $802$nd value of $\overline{abc}$ is $100+801=901$, so the answer is $\boxed{90109}$.

question:
A trapezoid $ABCD$ has $AB\parallel CD$ and $AB\ne CD$. A single straight cut along a line divides it into two pieces.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that if both pieces are triangles, then the cut is along a diagonal.
\item[(b)] Show that an isosceles trapezoid (with legs $AD=BC$) can never be cut into two acute triangles in this way.
\item[(c)] Show that the trapezoid with vertices $A(2,3)$, $B(7,3)$, $C(6,0)$, $D(0,0)$ can be cut into two acute triangles by a single straight cut.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The cut enters and leaves the trapezoid at two boundary points, each either a vertex or an interior point of a side. Count the corners of the two pieces together: every vertex of the trapezoid is a corner of one piece, and each end of the cut is a corner of both pieces. An end at a vertex therefore adds $1$ extra corner, and an end inside a side adds $2$. So the total is $4+1+1=6$ only when both ends are vertices; otherwise it is at least $7$. Two triangles have $6$ corners, so the cut joins two vertices. It is not a side, so it is a diagonal.

\textbf{(b)} In an isosceles trapezoid $\angle A=\angle B$ and $\angle C=\angle D$, and $\angle A+\angle D=180^\circ$ (co-interior angles between the parallel sides). So either $\angle A=\angle B\ge90^\circ$ or $\angle C=\angle D\ge90^\circ$. The cut along diagonal $AC$ leaves the angles at $B$ and $D$ whole, as angles of the triangles $ABC$ and $ACD$; the cut along $BD$ leaves the angles at $A$ and $C$ whole. In each case one of these whole angles is at least $90^\circ$ (it is $\angle B$ or $\angle D$ for $AC$, and $\angle A$ or $\angle C$ for $BD$), so one triangle is not acute.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(2,3), B=(7,3), C=(6,0), D=(0,0);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--C,red+linewidth(0.9));
label("$A(2,3)$",A,N); label("$B(7,3)$",B,N); label("$C(6,0)$",C,S); label("$D(0,0)$",D,S);
pair s=(10,0);
pair A2=(1.2,3)+s, B2=(4.8,3)+s, C2=(6,0)+s, D2=(0,0)+s;
draw(A2--B2--C2--D2--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A2--C2,dashed); draw(B2--D2,dashed);
label("$A$",A2,N); label("$B$",B2,N); label("$C$",C2,S); label("$D$",D2,S);
draw(arc(A2,0.5,-111.8,0),blue); draw(arc(B2,0.5,180,291.8),blue);
label("obtuse",(A2+B2)/2+(0,0.9),blue+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(c)} Here $AB\parallel CD$ (both horizontal), $AB=5\ne CD=6$, and the legs $AD$, $BC$ have slopes $\dfrac32$ and $3$, so $ABCD$ is a trapezoid. Cut along $AC$, with $AC^2=4^2+3^2=25$.
\begin{itemize}
\item Triangle $ABC$: $AB^2=25$, $BC^2=1+9=10$, $AC^2=25$; the largest square, $25$, is less than the sum of the other two ($25+10$), so the triangle is acute.
\item Triangle $ACD$: $CD^2=36$, $AC^2=25$, $AD^2=4+9=13$; the largest square satisfies $36<25+13=38$, so this triangle is acute too.
\end{itemize}
So the cut along $AC$ splits this trapezoid into two acute triangles. $\blacksquare$

Let one of the triangles have a side of length $c$. Place it as $T_1$ with vertices $(0,0)$, $(c,0)$, $(p,h)$, where $h>0$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $T_2$ has vertices $(c,0)$, $(p+c,h)$, $(p,h)$. It is the image of $T_1$ under the half-turn about the midpoint of the segment from $(c,0)$ to $(p,h)$, so it is congruent to $T_1$.
\item $T_3$ has vertices $(c,0)$, $(2c,0)$, $(p+c,h)$; it is $T_1$ moved $c$ to the right.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real c=4, p=1.3, h=2.2;
pair P0=(0,0), P1=(c,0), P2=(2c,0), Q0=(p,h), Q1=(p+c,h);
fill(P0--P1--Q0--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1)); fill(P1--Q1--Q0--cycle,rgb(1,0.9,0.8)); fill(P1--P2--Q1--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
draw(P0--P2--Q1--Q0--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(P1--Q0); draw(P1--Q1);
label("$T_1$",(P0+P1+Q0)/3); label("$T_2$",(P1+Q1+Q0)/3); label("$T_3$",(P1+P2+Q1)/3);
label("$c$",(P0+P1)/2,S); label("$c$",(P1+P2)/2,S); label("$c$",(Q0+Q1)/2,N);
\end{asy}

The segments from $(c,0)$ to $(p,h)$ and to $(p+c,h)$ cut the quadrilateral with vertices $(0,0)$, $(2c,0)$, $(p+c,h)$, $(p,h)$ into exactly these three triangles, so they do not overlap and together form this quadrilateral. Its sides on $y=0$ and $y=h$ are parallel, with lengths $2c$ and $c$. The other two sides are not parallel: they point in the directions $(p,h)$ and $(p-c,h)$, which are parallel only if $c=0$. So the three triangles form a trapezoid with parallel sides $2c$ and $c$. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{Key fact.} In any triangle $ABC$, the excircle opposite $A$ touches the rays $AB$ and $AC$ (beyond $B$ and $C$) at points $T_B$, $T_C$ with $AT_B=AT_C=s$. Indeed, if it touches $BC$ at $X$, equal tangent segments give $BT_B=BX$ and $CT_C=CX$, so
\[AT_B+AT_C=AB+BX+AC+CX=AB+BC+CA=2s,\]
and $AT_B=AT_C$.

\textbf{Construction.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Draw the angle $\alpha$ with vertex $A$, and mark $T_B$, $T_C$ on its two rays with $AT_B=AT_C=s$.
\item The perpendiculars to the rays at $T_B$ and $T_C$ meet at $J$; draw the circle $\omega$ with centre $J$ through $T_B$ and $T_C$. Its radius is $\rho=s\tan\dfrac{\alpha}{2}$, and $AJ=\dfrac{s}{\cos\dfrac\alpha2}$.
\item Draw the circle $\gamma$ with centre $A$ and radius $h$.
\item Draw a common internal tangent $\ell$ of $\gamma$ and $\omega$ (a line touching both, with the two circles on opposite sides). For example: draw the circle with centre $J$ and radius $\rho+h$, a tangent $AK$ to it from $A$, the point $P$ on $JK$ with $JP=\rho$, and the line through $P$ parallel to $AK$.
\item Let $\ell$ meet the two rays at $B$ and $C$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real al=70, s=6, h=2.4;
pair A=(0,0);
pair TB=s*dir(al/2), TC=s*dir(-al/2);
real AJ=s/Cos(al/2), rho=s*Tan(al/2);
pair J=(AJ,0);
real th=aSin((rho+h)/AJ);
pair u=dir(-th);
pair K=sqrt(AJ^2-(rho+h)^2)*u;
pair P=J+rho*unit(K-J);
pair B=extension(P,P+u,A,TB), C=extension(P,P+u,A,TC);
pair nv=rotate(90)*u; pair H=A+dot(P-A,nv)*nv;
draw(A--1.15*TB,gray); draw(A--1.15*TC,gray);
draw(circle(J,rho),blue); draw(circle(A,h),red+dashed);
draw((B+0.25*(B-C))--(C+0.12*(C-B)),linewidth(0.6));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--H,red); draw(J--TB,dotted); draw(J--TC,dotted);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(J,blue); dot(TB,blue); dot(TC,blue); dot(H,red);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,dir(165)); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$J$",J,E,blue);
label("$T_B$",TB,NW,blue); label("$T_C$",TC,dir(-110),blue); label("$h$",(A+H)/2,dir(-64),red);
label("$s$",(A+TB)/2+(0,0.35),NW); label("$s$",(A+TC)/2-(0,0.35),SW);
draw(arc(A,0.9,-al/2,al/2)); label("$\alpha$",A+0.62*dir(-10),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Proof.} The line $\ell$ separates $A$ from $\omega$, and $T_B$, $T_C$ lie on $\omega$ but not on $\ell$, so $\ell$ crosses the segments $AT_B$ and $AT_C$ at interior points $B$ and $C$. Then $\angle BAC=\alpha$. The distance from $A$ to $\ell$ is the radius $h$ of $\gamma$, so the altitude from $A$ is $h$. The circle $\omega$ touches the lines $AB$, $AC$, $BC$ and lies on the far side of $BC$ from $A$, so it is the excircle opposite $A$; by the key fact the semiperimeter is $AT_B=s$.

\textbf{Existence.} Conversely, in any such triangle, $BC$ is a common internal tangent of $\gamma$ and the excircle, which is the circle $\omega$ of step 2 (it touches the rays at distance $s$ from $A$). A common internal tangent exists exactly when the circles do not overlap, that is, when $AJ\ge h+\rho$:
\[\dfrac{s}{\cos\dfrac\alpha2}\ge h+s\tan\dfrac\alpha2\iff h\le s\cdot\dfrac{1-\sin\dfrac\alpha2}{\cos\dfrac\alpha2}.\]
When the inequality is strict there are two internal tangents, symmetric about $AJ$, giving two mirror-image (congruent) triangles; in the case of equality there is one, and the triangle is isosceles. $\blacksquare$

The semiperimeter is $s=\dfrac{6+9+12}{2}=\dfrac{27}{2}$, and by Heron's formula the area is
\[K=\sqrt{\dfrac{27}{2}\cdot\dfrac{15}{2}\cdot\dfrac{9}{2}\cdot\dfrac{3}{2}}=\dfrac{\sqrt{10935}}{4}=\dfrac{27\sqrt{15}}{4}.\]
The incentre $I$ splits the triangle into three triangles with bases $6$, $9$, $12$ and common height $r$, so $K=rs$ and
\[r=\dfrac Ks=\dfrac{27\sqrt{15}}{4}\cdot\dfrac{2}{27}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt{15}}{2}}\approx1.94.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(12,0); real ax=99/24; pair A=(ax,sqrt(36-ax^2));
pair I=(12*A+9*B+6*C)/27; real r=sqrt(15)/2;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(circle(I,r),blue); draw(I--(I.x,0),blue);
dot(I); label("$I$",I,N,blue); label("$r$",(I.x,r/2),E,blue);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$6$",(A+B)/2,NW); label("$9$",(A+C)/2,NE); label("$12$",(9,0),S);
\end{asy}

question:
Consider the list of numbers $1,2,3,3,5,6,8$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many different values can the sum of three \emph{different} numbers from the list take?
\item[(b)] How many different values can the sum of three of the seven entries of the list take, if the two $3$s may both be used?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $F$ be the midpoint of $AC$, so $BF$ is another median and $E$ is the intersection of $AD$ and $BF$.

Since $D$ and $F$ are the midpoints of $CB$ and $CA$, the segment $DF$ is a midline of triangle $ABC$: $DF\parallel AB$ and $DF=\dfrac12AB$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.6,3.4), B=(0,0), C=(5,0);
pair D=(B+C)/2, F=(A+C)/2, E=(A+B+C)/3;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,blue); draw(B--F,blue); draw(D--F,red+dashed);
dot(D); dot(F); dot(E);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,S); label("$F$",F,NE); label("$E$",E,NW);
\end{asy}

Because $DF\parallel AB$, the alternate angles are equal: $\angle EDF=\angle EAB$ and $\angle EFD=\angle EBA$. So triangles $EDF$ and $EAB$ are similar, with ratio $\dfrac{DF}{AB}=\dfrac12$. Hence $\dfrac{ED}{EA}=\dfrac12$, that is,
\[\dfrac{AE}{ED}=2.\ \blacksquare\]

question:
Two money machines are compared. Time is measured in whole seconds, and at the end of every second all machines act at once.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Option 1.} You start with one machine $a_{10000}$. Every second, each machine $a_n$ with $n>1$ produces one new machine $a_{n-1}$, and each machine $a_1$ pays you $\$1$.
\item \textbf{Option 2.} You start with one machine $b$ and one machine $c$. Every second, each $b$ produces one new $c$ and pays you $\$1$, and each $c$ produces one new $b$.
\end{itemize}
Machines produced at the end of a second start working in the next second. Find the total amount of money after $T$ seconds for each option, and decide which option gives more money in the long run.

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that for integers $x$ and $y$, $x\equiv y\pmod{14}$ if and only if $x\equiv y\pmod2$ and $x\equiv y\pmod7$.
\item[(b)] An even integer $x$ leaves remainder $3$ when divided by $7$. Find the remainder when $x$ is divided by $14$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Since $I$, $C$, $M$ are collinear, $IM$ is a diameter. An angle inscribed in a semicircle is a right angle, so $\angle ILM=90^\circ$ and $\angle IOM=90^\circ$. By the Pythagorean theorem in both right triangles,
\[IM^2=LI^2+LM^2=529+1369=1898,\qquad OM^2=IM^2-IO^2=1898-1089=809.\]
So $OM=\sqrt{809}$, answer $\boxed{\textbf{(D)}}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=sqrt(1898)/2;
pair I=(-R,0), M=(R,0);
real fl=2*aSin(23/(2*R)), fo=2*aSin(33/(2*R));
pair L=R*dir(180-fl), O=R*dir(180+fo), K=(-O.x,O.y);
draw(circle((0,0),R));
draw(K--I--L--M--O--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(I--M,blue); draw(I--O,dashed);
dot((0,0)); label("$C$",(0,0),N);
dot(K); dot(I); dot(L); dot(M); dot(O);
label("$K$",K,SW); label("$I$",I,W); label("$L$",L,N); label("$M$",M,E); label("$O$",O,SE);
label("$23$",(I+L)/2,2.2*dir(150)); label("$37$",(L+M)/2,NE); label("$33$",(I+O)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

(The configuration exists: with $K$ the mirror image of $O$ in the perpendicular bisector of $IM$, $OK\parallel IM$ and $MOKI$ is an isosceles trapezoid; since $OM=\sqrt{809}<33=OI$, the order $K,I,L,M,O$ around the circle is right.)

question:
Two equilateral triangles $PAB$ and $PCD$, with side lengths $a$ and $b$, have the common vertex $P$ and no other common point. The four points $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$ lie on a circle of radius $r$, and $P$ is not the centre of this circle.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $a^2+ab+b^2=3r^2$.
\item[(b)] Show by an example that the condition ``no other common point'' cannot be dropped.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let $O$ be the centre. Since $PA=PB$ and $OA=OB$, both $P$ and $O$ lie on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$; as $P\ne O$, this bisector is the line $OP$. In the same way the perpendicular bisector of $CD$ is $OP$. So both triangles are symmetric about the line $OP$: the sides $PA$, $PB$ make angles of $30^\circ$ with this line, on its two sides, and so do $PC$, $PD$.

If both triangles pointed the same way along $OP$ from $P$, they would share all points of that line close to $P$. Since they share only $P$, they point in opposite directions. Name the vertices so that $A$ and $D$ are on opposite sides of the line $OP$; then the rays $PA$ and $PD$ are opposite, so $A,P,D$ are collinear with $P$ between $A$ and $D$, and likewise $B,P,C$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=3,b=2; real t=(a-b)/sqrt(3); real r=sqrt((a^2+a*b+b^2)/3);
pair P=(0,0), Oc=(t,0);
pair A=(sqrt(3)/2*a,a/2), B=(sqrt(3)/2*a,-a/2), C=(-sqrt(3)/2*b,b/2), D=(-sqrt(3)/2*b,-b/2);
draw(circle(Oc,r));
fill(P--A--B--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1)); fill(P--C--D--cycle,rgb(1,0.9,0.8));
draw(P--A--B--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(P--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,dashed); draw(B--C,dashed); draw(B--D,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(P); dot(Oc); label("$P$",P,N); label("$O$",Oc,SE);
label("$A$",A,NE); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NW); label("$D$",D,SW);
label("$a$",(P+A)/2,N); label("$b$",(P+C)/2,N);
\end{asy}

Now $\angle BAD=\angle BAP=60^\circ$ is an inscribed angle on the chord $BD$, so (extended law of sines in triangle $ABD$)
\[BD=2r\sin60^\circ=r\sqrt3.\]
In triangle $BPD$, $PB=a$, $PD=b$ and $\angle BPD=180^\circ-\angle APB=120^\circ$, so by the law of cosines
\[BD^2=a^2+b^2-2ab\cos120^\circ=a^2+ab+b^2.\]
Hence $a^2+ab+b^2=3r^2$. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(b)} Take the unit circle ($r=1$), $P=(-\sqrt3,0)$, $A=(0,1)$, $B=(0,-1)$, $C=\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2},\dfrac12\right)$, $D=\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2},-\dfrac12\right)$. Then $PA=PB=AB=2$ and $PC=PD=CD=1$, all four points are on the circle, but triangle $PCD$ lies inside triangle $PAB$, and
\[a^2+ab+b^2=4+2+1=7\ne3=3r^2.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=2,b=1; pair Oc=(0,0), P=(-sqrt(3),0);
pair A=(0,1), B=(0,-1), C=(-sqrt(3)/2,0.5), D=(-sqrt(3)/2,-0.5);
draw(circle(Oc,1));
fill(P--A--B--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1)); fill(P--C--D--cycle,rgb(1,0.9,0.8));
draw(P--A--B--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(P--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(P); dot(Oc); label("$P$",P,W); label("$O$",Oc,E);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,S); label("$C$",C,NW); label("$D$",D,SW);
\end{asy}

(In this nested case $A,C,P$ and $B,D,P$ are collinear, $\angle BPC=60^\circ$, and the same argument gives $a^2-ab+b^2=3r^2$ instead: $4-2+1=3$.)

Point at one of the paths, say path $X$, and ask either twin:
\begin{center}
``If I asked your twin whether path $X$ leads to the other side, would your twin say yes?''
\end{center}
Then take path $X$ if the answer is ``no'', and the other path if the answer is ``yes''.

\textbf{Why it works.} Suppose path $X$ is the right one.
\begin{itemize}
\item If you asked the truth-teller: the liar would say ``no'' to the inner question, and the truth-teller reports this honestly: ``no''.
\item If you asked the liar: the truth-teller would say ``yes'', and the liar reports the opposite: ``no''.
\end{itemize}
If path $X$ is the wrong one, both cases give ``yes'' in the same way. So the answer is always the opposite of the truth about path $X$ (exactly one liar is involved in the chain), and you choose correctly. $\blacksquare$

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] On a game show there are $n\ge1$ people on stage, of whom $k$ are mathematicians; $n$ is announced, but $k$ is not. The contestant eliminates $n-1$ of the people, one at a time and in any order he likes (he cannot tell who is a mathematician), and each eliminated person says truthfully whether he or she is a mathematician. Then one person is left, and the contestant must decide whether to play for a big prize, which he wins only if this person is a mathematician. The rules of the show are:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(1)] $k\ge1$;
\item[(2)] whatever order of elimination the contestant chooses, when one person is left he can never be certain whether that person is a mathematician.
\end{itemize}
The contestant knows these rules and reasons perfectly; in particular he knows exactly which values of $k$ the rules allow. For which pairs $(n,k)$ can the game be played?
\item[(b)] On Friday a teacher announces that there will be a quiz on exactly one of the five school days of next week, and that on the morning of the quiz day the students will not be able to deduce that the quiz is that day. Suppose the students know exactly the set $D$ of days on which the teacher could give such a quiz. Show that $D$ is empty.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that for every integer $b\ge2$, the number $b$ written in base $b$ is $10$.
\item[(b)] Write the number four and the number ten in base $4$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
By the distance formula the four legs have lengths
\[\sqrt{4^2+0^2}=4,\quad\sqrt{2^2+9^2}=\sqrt{85},\quad\sqrt{2^2+5^2}=\sqrt{29},\quad\sqrt{8^2+15^2}=17.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] Q={(1,7),(5,7),(3,-2),(5,3),(13,18)};
draw((-1,0)--(14,0),Arrow(5)); draw((0,-3)--(0,19),Arrow(5));
draw(Q[0]--Q[1]--Q[2]--Q[3]--Q[4],blue+linewidth(0.9));
for(int i=0;i<5;++i) dot(Q[i]);
label("$(1,7)$",Q[0],SE,fontsize(8pt)); label("$(5,7)$",Q[1],N,fontsize(8pt)); label("$(3,-2)$",Q[2],S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$(5,3)$",Q[3],SE,fontsize(8pt)); label("$(13,18)$",Q[4],W,fontsize(8pt));
label("$4$",(2.5,7),N,fontsize(8pt)); label("$\sqrt{85}$",(Q[1]+Q[2])/2,W,fontsize(8pt));
label("$\sqrt{29}$",(3.6,-1),E,fontsize(8pt)); label("$17$",(Q[3]+Q[4])/2,SE,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The total length is $\boxed{21+\sqrt{85}+\sqrt{29}}\approx35.6$.

Compare the squared distances from $(-2,6)$:
\[(5,7):\ 7^2+1^2=50,\qquad(3,-2):\ 5^2+8^2=89,\qquad(5,3):\ 7^2+3^2=58.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair R0=(-2,6); pair[] Q={(5,7),(3,-2),(5,3)};
draw((-3,0)--(7,0),Arrow(5)); draw((0,-3)--(0,9),Arrow(5));
draw(R0--Q[0],red+linewidth(0.9)); draw(R0--Q[1],dashed); draw(R0--Q[2],dashed);
dot(R0); dot(Q[0],red); dot(Q[1]); dot(Q[2]);
label("$(-2,6)$",R0,NW); label("$(5,7)$",Q[0],NE); label("$(3,-2)$",Q[1],SE); label("$(5,3)$",Q[2],E);
label("$\sqrt{50}$",(R0+Q[0])/2,N,red+fontsize(8pt)); label("$\sqrt{89}$",R0+0.55*(Q[1]-R0),NE,fontsize(8pt)); label("$\sqrt{58}$",R0+0.7*(Q[2]-R0),NE,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The nearest point is $\boxed{(5,7)}$, at distance $\sqrt{50}=5\sqrt2\approx7.07$.

question:
Five players wear the numbers $4$, $6$, $8$, $9$ and $10$, and each votes for one player by writing that player's number:
\begin{itemize}
\item player $6$ votes for $4$, and player $4$ votes for $6$;
\item player $8$ votes for $x$, where $x^3-12x^2+48x=64$;
\item player $9$ votes for $y$, where $2y=20-2(y-2)$;
\item player $10$ votes for $z$, where $4z+xy=(4^2+y^2)\cdot\dfrac{17}{13}-\dfrac{180}{21-3x}$.
\end{itemize}
Which player receives the most votes?

solution:
\begin{itemize}
\item $x^3-12x^2+48x-64=(x-4)^3$, so $x=4$.
\item $2y=20-2y+4$, so $4y=24$ and $y=6$.
\item With $x=4$, $y=6$: $4z+24=52\cdot\dfrac{17}{13}-\dfrac{180}{9}=68-20=48$, so $z=6$.
\end{itemize}
The votes are: for $4$ from players $6$ and $8$; for $6$ from players $4$, $9$ and $10$. Player $\boxed{6}$ receives the most votes ($3$ out of $5$, a majority).

In $t$ hours, $P$ can reach exactly the points within $\dfrac52t$ of the origin, so $P$ can be within $1$ unit of a player at time $t$ exactly when that player's distance from the origin is at most $\dfrac52t+1$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $C$ is at $(10+t,0)$: $10+t\le\dfrac52t+1\iff t\ge6$.
\item $Q$ is at $(0,-7-\dfrac32t)$: $7+\dfrac32t\le\dfrac52t+1\iff t\ge6$.
\item $B$ is at $(2t,5)$: $\sqrt{4t^2+25}\le\dfrac52t+1\iff4t^2+25\le\dfrac{25}{4}t^2+5t+1\iff9t^2+20t-96\ge0$, that is, $t\ge\dfrac{-10+2\sqrt{241}}{9}\approx2.34$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real ts=(2*sqrt(241)-10)/9;
pair P=(0,0), Bt=(2*ts,5);
draw((-1,0)--(17,0),gray); draw((0,-11)--(0,7),gray);
draw((10,0)--(16,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-7)--(0,-10.5),Arrow(6)); draw((0,5)--(6,5),Arrow(6));
draw(P--(Bt-unit(Bt)),red+linewidth(0.9)); draw(circle(Bt,1),red+dashed);
dot(P); dot((10,0)); dot((0,-7)); dot((0,5)); dot(Bt,red);
label("$P$",P,SW); label("$C$",(10,0),N); label("$Q$",(0,-7),W); label("$B$",(0,5),NW);
label("$B$ at time $t$",Bt+(1.2,0.9),NE,red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

So $P$ should go for $\boxed{B}$, whom he can reach (within $1$ unit) after $\dfrac{2\sqrt{241}-10}{9}\approx2.34$ hours, by walking straight towards $B$'s position at that time.

A small cube has exactly two faces on the outside exactly when it lies on an edge of the big cube but not at a corner. Each of the $12$ edges of the big cube consists of $3$ small cubes: $2$ corner pieces and $1$ edge piece in the middle (shown in red below).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real k=0.45; pair dz=(k*cos(pi/6),k*sin(pi/6));
pair p(real x,real y,real z){return (x,y)+z*dz;}
void E(real x1,real y1,real z1,real x2,real y2,real z2,bool hid){
  pair a=p(x1,y1,z1), c=p(x2,y2,z2);
  draw(a--c, hid ? dashed+gray : black);
  draw((a+(c-a)/3)--(a+2*(c-a)/3), red+linewidth(2.2));
}
E(0,0,0,3,0,0,false); E(3,0,0,3,3,0,false); E(3,3,0,0,3,0,false); E(0,3,0,0,0,0,false);
E(0,3,3,3,3,3,false); E(3,3,3,3,0,3,false); E(0,0,3,3,0,3,true); E(0,0,3,0,3,3,true);
E(0,0,0,0,0,3,true); E(3,0,0,3,0,3,false); E(3,3,0,3,3,3,false); E(0,3,0,0,3,3,false);
\end{asy}

So there are $\boxed{12}$ edge pieces, one for each edge of the cube.

A cube of side $k$ ($1\le k\le5$) is determined by the position of its corner with the smallest coordinates, which can be chosen in $6-k$ ways in each of the three directions. So there are $(6-k)^3$ such cubes (for example, the red square below is the front face of one of the $3^3=27$ cubes of side $3$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real k=0.5; pair dz=(k*cos(pi/6),k*sin(pi/6));
pair p(real x,real y,real z){return (x,y)+z*dz;}
for(int i=0;i<=5;++i){ draw(p(i,0,0)--p(i,5,0),gray); draw(p(0,i,0)--p(5,i,0),gray);
  draw(p(0,5,i)--p(5,5,i),gray); draw(p(i,5,0)--p(i,5,5),gray);
  draw(p(5,0,i)--p(5,5,i),gray); draw(p(5,i,0)--p(5,i,5),gray); }
draw(p(0,0,0)--p(5,0,0)--p(5,5,0)--p(0,5,0)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(p(0,5,0)--p(0,5,5)--p(5,5,5)--p(5,0,5)--p(5,0,0),linewidth(0.9)); draw(p(5,5,0)--p(5,5,5),linewidth(0.9));
draw(p(1,1,0)--p(4,1,0)--p(4,4,0)--p(1,4,0)--cycle,red+linewidth(1.2));
label("$5$",p(2.5,0,0),S); label("$5$",p(0,2.5,0),W); label("$5$",p(5,0,2.5),SE);
\end{asy}

The total is
\[5^3+4^3+3^3+2^3+1^3=125+64+27+8+1=\boxed{225}.\]

A point $(x,y)$ is on the parabola when its distance to the focus equals its distance to the directrix:
\[\sqrt{(x-1)^2+(y-1)^2}=\dfrac{|x+y|}{\sqrt2}.\]
Squaring and multiplying by $2$:
\[2(x-1)^2+2(y-1)^2=(x+y)^2\iff x^2-2xy+y^2-4x-4y+4=0\iff\boxed{(x-y)^2=4(x+y-1)}.\]
The axis is the line through the focus perpendicular to the directrix, which is the line $\boxed{y=x}$, and the vertex is midway between $F$ and the foot $(0,0)$ of the perpendicular from $F$ to the directrix: $V=\left(\dfrac12,\dfrac12\right)$ (indeed $(0)^2=4(1-1)$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
pair Fp=(1,1);
pair f(real t){ real u=(t^2+2)/(2*sqrt(2)); return ((u+t)/sqrt(2),(u-t)/sqrt(2)); }
draw((-1.6,0)--(4.2,0),Arrow(5)); draw((0,-1.6)--(0,4.2),Arrow(5));
draw(graph(f,-2.5,2.5),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-1.5,1.5)--(1.5,-1.5),red+dashed); label("$x+y=0$",(-1.5,1.5),W,red+fontsize(8pt));
draw((-0.8,-0.8)--(3.6,3.6),dotted); label("axis $y=x$",(3.6,3.6),NE,fontsize(8pt));
dot(Fp); label("$F(1,1)$",Fp,NE,fontsize(8pt)); dot((0.5,0.5)); label("$V$",(0.5,0.5),1.5*dir(-80),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

So \textbf{yes}: this parabola's axis, $y=x$, is parallel to neither coordinate axis. (In general, a parabola can have any line as its axis; its equation then contains an $xy$ term.)

All sides must be positive: $x+1>0$ and $3x-2>0$, so $x>\dfrac23$. By the triangle inequality, the two legs together must be longer than the base:
\[2(x+1)>3x-2\iff x<4.\]
(The other two inequalities, $(x+1)+(3x-2)>x+1$, say, hold automatically when $3x-2>0$.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(4,0), A=(2,sqrt(5));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
label("$x+1$",(A+B)/2,NW); label("$x+1$",(A+C)/2,NE); label("$3x-2$",(B+C)/2,S);
void tick(pair P,pair Q){pair M=(P+Q)/2,v=rotate(90)*unit(Q-P); draw((M-0.12*v)--(M+0.12*v));}
tick(A,B); tick(A,C);
\end{asy}

So the possible values are $\boxed{\dfrac23<x<4}$. (For $x=\dfrac32$ all three sides equal $\dfrac52$; an equilateral triangle is also isosceles.)

\textbf{Angles.} $\angle C=35^\circ$, and $BE$ bisects $\angle B$, so $\angle ABE=\angle EBC=40^\circ$ and $\angle AEB=180^\circ-65^\circ-40^\circ=75^\circ$. At each reflection the ray leaves the side at the same angle as it arrived:
\begin{itemize}
\item at $E$: $\angle FEC=\angle AEB=75^\circ$, so in triangle $EFC$, $\angle EFC=180^\circ-75^\circ-35^\circ=70^\circ$;
\item at $F$: $\angle GFB=70^\circ$, so in triangle $FBG$, $\angle FGB=180^\circ-70^\circ-80^\circ=30^\circ$;
\item at $G$: $\angle HGA=30^\circ$, so in triangle $AGH$, $\angle AHG=180^\circ-30^\circ-65^\circ=85^\circ$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(6,0);
real AC=6*Sin(80)/Sin(35); pair C=AC*dir(65);
real a=abs(B-C), b2=abs(A-C), c=abs(A-B);
pair D=(a*A+b2*B+c*C)/(a+b2+c);
pair E=extension(B,D,A,C);
pair w1=reflect((0,0),C-A)*(E-B); pair F=extension(E,E+w1,B,C);
pair w2=reflect((0,0),C-B)*(F-E); pair G=extension(F,F+w2,A,B);
pair w3=reflect((0,0),B-A)*(G-F); pair H=extension(G,G+w3,A,C);
fill(E--F--G--H--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--E,dashed); draw(E--F--G--H,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(D); dot(E); dot(F); dot(G); dot(H);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N); label("$D$",D,N);
label("$E$",E,W); label("$F$",F,dir(10)); label("$G$",G,SE); label("$H$",H,NW);
label("$75^\circ$",E+0.9*dir(30),fontsize(7pt)); label("$70^\circ$",F+0.75*dir(150),fontsize(7pt));
label("$30^\circ$",G+1.15*dir(14),fontsize(7pt)); label("$40^\circ$",B+1.1*dir(160),fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Lengths} (law of sines), taking $BC=1$:
\begin{align*}
CE&=\dfrac{\sin40^\circ}{\sin105^\circ}\approx0.66546, & CF&=CE\cdot\dfrac{\sin75^\circ}{\sin70^\circ}\approx0.68404, & BF&=1-CF\approx0.31596,\\
BG&=BF\cdot\dfrac{\sin70^\circ}{\sin30^\circ}\approx0.59381, & AB&=\dfrac{\sin35^\circ}{\sin65^\circ}\approx0.63287, & AG&=AB-BG\approx0.03906,\\
AH&=AG\cdot\dfrac{\sin30^\circ}{\sin85^\circ}\approx0.01961.
\end{align*}
Since $AH<AE=AC-CE\approx0.42115$, the point $H$ lies between $A$ and $E$, and $EFGH$ is the triangle with its three corner triangles $EFC$, $FBG$, $AGH$ removed.

\textbf{Areas.}
\begin{align*}
x&=\dfrac12\cdot BC\cdot AB\sin80^\circ\approx0.311628,\qquad [EFC]=\dfrac12\,CE\cdot CF\sin35^\circ\approx0.130547,\\
[FBG]&=\dfrac12\,BF\cdot BG\sin80^\circ\approx0.092385,\qquad [AGH]=\dfrac12\,AG\cdot AH\sin65^\circ\approx0.000347,
\end{align*}
so $y\approx0.311628-0.130547-0.092385-0.000347=0.088350$ and
\[\dfrac xy\approx\boxed{3.527}.\]

\textbf{A fixed point.} The lines meet where $7x+4(2x-3)=-12$, that is, $15x=0$: at $X=(0,-3)$. This point is its own reflection, so it lies on the image.

\textbf{A second point.} The point $P=(4,-10)$ lies on $7x+4y=-12$ ($28-40=-12$). The perpendicular to $y=2x-3$ through $P$ has slope $-\dfrac12$: $y+10=-\dfrac12(x-4)$. It meets $y=2x-3$ where $2x-3=-\dfrac12x-8$, that is, at $M=(-2,-7)$. The reflection of $P$ is $P'=2M-P=(-8,-4)$.

