solution:
\textbf{Two right triangles.} Since $3^2+4^2=5^2$, triangle $ABC$ has a right angle at $C$, and triangle $ABD$ has a right angle at $D$. Let $E$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $C$ to $AB$. In the right triangle $ABC$, $CE=\dfrac{3\cdot4}{5}=\dfrac{12}{5}$ and $AE=\dfrac{AC^2}{AB}=\dfrac95$. Triangle $ABD$ is congruent to $ABC$ with the same placement of the sides $3$ and $4$ (as $AD=AC=3$ and $BD=BC=4$), so the foot of the perpendicular from $D$ to $AB$ is the same point $E$ and $DE=\dfrac{12}{5}$.

\textbf{A right angle at $E$.} Now $CE^2+DE^2=2\cdot\dfrac{144}{25}=\dfrac{288}{25}=CD^2$, so $\angle CED=90^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
