label("$AB=5$",(3.6,0),N,fontsize(8pt)); label("$\dfrac{12}{5}$",(D+Ep)/2,E,blue+fontsize(8pt));
void tick(pair P,pair Q){pair M=(P+Q)/2, v=0.09*unit(rotate(90)*(Q-P)); draw((M-v)--(M+v),blue+linewidth(0.8));}
tick(C,Ep); tick(D,Ep);
pair u1=0.22*unit(D-Ep), u2=0.22*unit(C-Ep), u3=0.22*unit(B-Ep); draw((Ep+u1)--(Ep+u1+u2)--(Ep+u2),blue); draw((Ep+u1)--(Ep+u1+u3)--(Ep+u3),blue);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Volume.} The line $AB$ is perpendicular to both $EC$ and $ED$, hence to the plane $CED$. So the tetrahedron is made of two pyramids with base $CED$ and heights $AE$ and $EB$, which add up to $AB=5$:
\[V=\dfrac13\cdot[CED]\cdot AB=\dfrac13\cdot\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac{12}{5}\cdot\dfrac{12}{5}\cdot5=\boxed{\dfrac{24}{5}}.\]

question:
A prize wheel is $99\%$ red and $1\%$ blue, so each spin lands on blue with probability $\dfrac{1}{100}$, independently of the other spins.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the probability of getting blue on one spin?
\item[(b)] What is the probability of getting blue at least once in two spins? Explain why the answer is not $2\%$.
\item[(c)] What is the probability of getting blue at least once in $100$ spins? Give it to three decimal places, and explain why it is not $100\%$.
\end{enumerate}

Twenty-four chairs are evenly spaced around a circular table, and there is a name card for each of $24$ guests in front of the chairs. The guests sit down without noticing the cards, and it turns out that no one is sitting in front of his or her own card.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that the table can be rotated so that at least two guests are simultaneously sitting in front of their own cards.
\item[(b)] Is it always possible to rotate the table so that at least three guests are simultaneously sitting in front of their own cards? Prove your answer.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{Different choices give different values.} For each $k$, the largest possible contribution of the terms below $k!$ is
\[1\cdot1!+2\cdot2!+\cdots+(k-1)(k-1)!=\bigl(2!-1!\bigr)+\bigl(3!-2!\bigr)+\cdots+\bigl(k!-(k-1)!\bigr)=k!-1<k!,\]
using $j\cdot j!=(j+1)!-j!$. If two choices of $(a,b,c,d,e)$ differed, look at the largest factorial $k!$ where their coefficients differ: there the values differ by at least $k!$, while all lower terms together can make up at most $k!-1$. So the two values of $n$ would be different.

\textbf{The sum.} With $c=0$ there are $2\cdot3\cdot5\cdot6=180$ choices, giving $180$ different values. Add up each term over all choices:
\begin{itemize}
\item $a$: each value of $a$ occurs in $3\cdot5\cdot6=90$ choices, contributing $(0+1)\cdot1!\cdot90=90$;
\item $b$: $(0+1+2)\cdot2!\cdot(2\cdot5\cdot6)=3\cdot2\cdot60=360$;
\item $d$: $(0+1+2+3+4)\cdot4!\cdot(2\cdot3\cdot6)=10\cdot24\cdot36=8640$;
\item $e$: $(0+1+\cdots+5)\cdot5!\cdot(2\cdot3\cdot5)=15\cdot120\cdot30=54000$.
\end{itemize}
The sum of all possible values of $n$ is $90+360+8640+54000=\boxed{63090}$.

question:
A banquet room was a square with side $x$ feet. It is being rebuilt so that one side is doubled in length, while the other side is decreased by $5$ feet. Molding will be placed along the whole perimeter of the new room, and the budget allows a perimeter of at least $55$ feet and at most $90$ feet. The molding costs \$2.60 per linear foot to buy and install.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Should this situation be described by an equation or by an inequality? Write it in terms of $x$ and solve it.
\item[(b)] What are the least and the greatest possible costs of the molding?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The new room is a rectangle with sides $2x$ and $x-5$ (so $x>5$), and its perimeter is
\[2\bigl(2x+(x-5)\bigr)=6x-10.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real x=13;
draw((0,0)--(x,0)--(x,x)--(0,x)--cycle,dashed+gray);
draw((0,0)--(2x,0)--(2x,x-5)--(0,x-5)--cycle,linewidth(1));
label("$x$",(0,x/2+2),W,gray); label("$x$",(x/2,x),N,gray);
label("$2x$",(x,0),S); label("$x-5$",(2x,(x-5)/2),E);
label("old room",(x/2,x-2),gray+fontsize(8pt)); label("new room",(1.5*x,(x-5)/2),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The budget does not fix the perimeter; it only gives a range of allowed values, so the situation is described by a (compound) \textbf{inequality}:
\[55\le6x-10\le90.\]
Adding $10$ gives $65\le6x\le100$, so
\[\boxed{\dfrac{65}{6}\le x\le\dfrac{50}{3}},\]
that is, the original side was between about $10.83$ and $16.67$ feet (and the condition $x>5$ holds automatically). If ``between $55$ and $90$'' is meant to exclude the end values, the inequalities are strict: $55<6x-10<90$.

\textbf{(b)} The cost is $2.60(6x-10)$ dollars, which is least when the perimeter is $55$ feet and greatest when it is $90$ feet: from $2.60\cdot55=\boxed{\$143}$ to $2.60\cdot90=\boxed{\$234}$.

question:
In a right triangle $ABC$ with the right angle at $C$, the \emph{altitude to the hypotenuse} is the segment $CD$ from the vertex $C$ of the right angle perpendicular to the hypotenuse $AB$, with $D$ on $AB$. Let $a=BC$, $b=AC$ and $c=AB$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $CD=\dfrac{ab}{c}$, $AD=\dfrac{b^2}{c}$ and $DB=\dfrac{a^2}{c}$.
\item[(b)] Find $CD$, $AD$ and $DB$ when $a=6$ and $b=8$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The altitude to the hypotenuse is the height of the triangle when the hypotenuse is taken as the base. The two legs are also a base and a height, so computing the area in two ways gives
\[\dfrac12ab=\dfrac12c\cdot CD,\qquad CD=\dfrac{ab}{c}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), B=(6,0), A=(0,8);
pair u=unit(B-A); pair D=A+dot(C-A,u)*u;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(C--D,blue+linewidth(0.9));
real m=0.45; pair v=unit(C-D);
draw((m,0)--(m,m)--(0,m)); draw(D+m*u--D+m*u+m*v--D+m*v);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$D$",D,NE);
label("$a=6$",(3,0),S); label("$b=8$",(0,4),W); label("$c=10$",(A+B)/2+(0.3,0.5),NE);
label("$h$",(C+D)/2,NW,blue);
\end{asy}

Triangles $ADC$ and $ACB$ both have a right angle (at $D$ and at $C$) and share the angle at $A$, so they are similar, and
\[\dfrac{AD}{AC}=\dfrac{AC}{AB},\qquad AD=\dfrac{b^2}{c}.\]
In the same way triangles $BDC$ and $BCA$ are similar, so $DB=\dfrac{a^2}{c}$. (Check: $AD+DB=\dfrac{a^2+b^2}{c}=\dfrac{c^2}{c}=c$.)

\textbf{(b)} By the Pythagorean theorem $c=\sqrt{36+64}=10$, so
\[CD=\dfrac{6\cdot8}{10}=\boxed{\dfrac{24}{5}},\qquad AD=\dfrac{64}{10}=\boxed{\dfrac{32}{5}},\qquad DB=\dfrac{36}{10}=\boxed{\dfrac{18}{5}}.\]

question:
A student's homework grade is (points earned) divided by (points possible). Because of extra credit, a few days ago his grade was $\dfrac{254.50}{251.00}$, and now, after he has turned in several assignments that were all scored $10$ out of $10$, it is $\dfrac{274.50}{271.00}$: the difference between his points and the points possible has stayed $3.5$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Write both grades as percentages to two decimal places and check that the grade has gone down.
\item[(b)] Prove that if $p>t>0$, then $\dfrac{p+10}{t+10}<\dfrac pt$. Explain why every perfect $10$ out of $10$ lowers the grade of a student whose points exceed the points possible.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $t=BE$ and use coordinates $A=(0,0)$, $B=(1,0)$, $C=(1,1)$, $D=(0,1)$. Then
\[E=(1-t,0),\quad F=(1,1-t),\quad G=(t,1),\quad H=(0,t).\]
The rotation by $90^\circ$ about the centre of the square, $(x,y)\mapsto(1-y,x)$, maps $A\to B\to C\to D\to A$ and $F\to G\to H\to E\to F$, so it maps the segments $AF\to BG\to CH\to DE\to AF$. Hence the region they enclose is a square (drawn below with a larger $t$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real t=0.3;
pair A=(0,0), B=(1,0), C=(1,1), D=(0,1);
pair Ept=(1-t,0), F=(1,1-t), G=(t,1), H=(0,t);
pair P1=extension(A,F,B,G), P2=extension(B,G,C,H), P3=extension(C,H,D,Ept), P4=extension(D,Ept,A,F);
fill(P1--P2--P3--P4--cycle,lightgray);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--F,blue); draw(B--G,blue); draw(C--H,blue); draw(D--Ept,blue);
dot(Ept); dot(F); dot(G); dot(H);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$E$",Ept,S); label("$F$",F,E); label("$G$",G,N); label("$H$",H,W);
label("$t$",(1-t/2,0),S,red); label("$t$",(1,1-t/2),E,red); label("$t$",(t/2,1),N,red); label("$t$",(0,t/2),W,red);
\end{asy}

The segments $AF$ and $CH$ are parallel (both have direction $(1,1-t)$), and two opposite sides of the small square lie on them, so the side of the small square is the distance between these lines. The line $AF$ is $(1-t)x-y=0$, and $H=(0,t)$ lies on $CH$, so the side is
\[\dfrac{|0-t|}{\sqrt{(1-t)^2+1}}=\dfrac{t}{\sqrt{1+(1-t)^2}}.\]
The area condition $\dfrac{t^2}{1+(1-t)^2}=\dfrac1n$ becomes $nt^2=t^2-2t+2$, that is,
\[(n-1)t^2+2t-2=0,\qquad t=\dfrac{-1+\sqrt{2n-1}}{n-1}=\dfrac{2}{1+\sqrt{2n-1}}\]
(taking the positive root; the two forms agree because $(\sqrt{2n-1}-1)(\sqrt{2n-1}+1)=2(n-1)$). This $t$ lies between $0$ and $1$. For $n=6519$, $2n-1=13037$ and
\[BE=\boxed{\dfrac{2}{1+\sqrt{13037}}=\dfrac{\sqrt{13037}-1}{6518}}\approx0.0174.\]

The segment between the houses is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs $13$ and $21$ along the axis directions, so by the Pythagorean theorem (the distance formula) the distance is
\[\sqrt{13^2+21^2}=\sqrt{169+441}=\boxed{\sqrt{610}}\approx24.7.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
draw((-1,0)--(15,0),Arrow(5)); draw((0,-1)--(0,23),Arrow(5));
pair M=(0,0), T=(13,21);
draw(M--(13,0)--T,dashed); draw(M--T,blue+linewidth(1));
dot(M); dot(T);
label("$x$",(15,0),E); label("$y$",(0,23),N);
label("$(0,0)$",M,SW); label("$(13,21)$",T,NE);
label("$13$",(6.5,0),S); label("$21$",(13,10.5),E); label("$\sqrt{610}$",(6.5,10.5),NW,blue);
\end{asy}

Join the centre $O$ to the eight vertices. This cuts the octagon into $8$ congruent isosceles triangles with two sides $5$ and apex angle $\dfrac{360^\circ}{8}=45^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
draw(circle(O,5),gray);
pair[] V; for(int k=0;k<8;++k) V.push(5*dir(22.5+45*k));
fill(O--V[0]--V[1]--cycle,paleblue);
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--V[6]--V[7]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
for(int k=0;k<8;++k) draw(O--V[k],dotted);
draw(O--V[0],linewidth(0.8)); draw(O--V[1],linewidth(0.8));
pair Fo=dot(V[1],unit(V[0]))*unit(V[0]);
draw(V[1]--Fo,red+dashed);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,W);
label("$5$",(O+V[0])/2,S); label("$5$",(O+V[1])/2,NW);
draw(arc(O,1.1,22.5,67.5)); label("$45^\circ$",O+1.7*dir(45),fontsize(8pt));
label("$h$",(V[1]+Fo)/2,E,red);
\end{asy}

In one of them, take one radius as the base. The height $h$ from the opposite vertex is a leg of a $45^\circ$-$45^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle with hypotenuse $5$, so $h=\dfrac{5}{\sqrt2}=\dfrac{5\sqrt2}{2}$. Each triangle has area $\dfrac12\cdot5\cdot\dfrac{5\sqrt2}{2}=\dfrac{25\sqrt2}{4}$, and the octagon has area
\[8\cdot\dfrac{25\sqrt2}{4}=\boxed{50\sqrt2}.\]

The spacing condition puts no lower bound on the area, because the points may lie on a line. Put the students at the points $P_k=(6(k-1),0)$ for $k=1,2,\dots,100$; any two are at least $6$ feet apart, and they fill a segment of length $6\cdot99=594$ feet. The thin rectangle
\[0\le x\le594,\qquad -\dfrac w2\le y\le\dfrac w2\]
contains all of them and has area $594w$ (the picture is not to scale).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
