Choosing $w=\dfrac{1}{594}$ gives a classroom of area exactly $1$ square foot. A polygon always has positive area, so no smaller positive integer is possible, and the answer is $\boxed{1}$. (Indeed every positive area, however small, is possible. A real lower bound only appears with extra conditions, for example if each student needed a disc of radius $3$ feet inside the room.)

We must show that the equation $0\cdot q=n$ does not have exactly one solution $q$. Since $0\cdot q=0$ for every number $q$, there are two cases.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $n\ne0$, then $0\cdot q=0\ne n$ for every $q$, so there is \emph{no} solution.
\item If $n=0$, then $0\cdot q=0$ holds for \emph{every} $q$, so the solution is not unique, and no single value could be chosen.
\end{itemize}
In both cases there is no unique $q$, so $\dfrac n0$ is undefined. $\blacksquare$

Every minute the clock moves to the next time whose minutes have no digit $0$. The allowed minute values in each hour are
\[11\text{--}19,\quad21\text{--}29,\quad31\text{--}39,\quad41\text{--}49,\quad51\text{--}59.\]
Count the $60$ steps from $5{:}25$:
\begin{itemize}
\item steps $1$--$4$: $5{:}26$ to $5{:}29$; steps $5$--$13$: $5{:}31$ to $5{:}39$; steps $14$--$22$: $5{:}41$ to $5{:}49$; steps $23$--$31$: $5{:}51$ to $5{:}59$;
\item step $32$: $6{:}11$ (the times $6{:}00$ to $6{:}10$ all contain a $0$); steps $32$--$40$: $6{:}11$ to $6{:}19$;
\item steps $41$--$49$: $6{:}21$ to $6{:}29$; steps $50$--$58$: $6{:}31$ to $6{:}39$; step $59$: $6{:}41$; step $60$: $6{:}42$.
\end{itemize}
So after $60$ minutes the clock shows $\boxed{6{:}42}$.

The can holds $\pi r^2h=\pi\cdot3^2\cdot10=90\pi$ cubic inches, and each hemispherical bowl holds half of a sphere of radius $3$:
\[\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac43\pi\cdot3^3=18\pi\text{ cubic inches}.\]

