So the can fills exactly $\dfrac{90\pi}{18\pi}=\boxed{5}$ bowls.

\textbf{Driving time.} The speed drops after the second rest station (mile $200$) and after the fourth (mile $400$):
\begin{itemize}
\item miles $0$--$200$ at $60$ mph: $\dfrac{200}{60}$ hours $=200$ minutes;
\item miles $200$--$400$ at $50$ mph: $4$ hours $=240$ minutes;
\item miles $400$--$600$ at $40$ mph: $5$ hours $=300$ minutes.
\end{itemize}
Total driving: $740$ minutes.

\textbf{Stops.} Five rest stations at $4\cdot5=20$ minutes each: $100$ minutes. Six gas stops at $15$ minutes each: $90$ minutes.

The trip takes $740+100+90=930$ minutes $=15$ hours $30$ minutes. Leaving at $6{:}20$ a.m.\ Eastern Time, they arrive at $9{:}50$ p.m.\ Eastern Time, which is $\boxed{8{:}50\text{ p.m. Central Time}}$.

The stopwatch must be started and stopped at moments $9$ minutes apart, and the only moments the turkeys can recognise are when timers are started or ring. Since $10+10=20=11+9$, one way is:
\begin{itemize}
\item at time $0$, start the $11$-minute timer and one $10$-minute timer;
\item when the first $10$-minute timer rings (time $10$), start the second $10$-minute timer;
\item when the $11$-minute timer rings (time $11$), start the stopwatch;
\item when the second $10$-minute timer rings (time $20$), stop the stopwatch.
\end{itemize}
The stopwatch runs from time $11$ to time $20$, that is, exactly $\boxed{9\text{ minutes}}$, and the pause is not even needed. (Another way, since $8+11=19=10+9$: start the $8$- and a $10$-minute timer at time $0$; when the $8$ rings, start the $11$; run the stopwatch from the ring of the $10$ at time $10$ to the ring of the $11$ at time $19$. A way that uses the pause, since $7+(10-8)=9$: start the $7$-, $8$- and a $10$-minute timer together with the stopwatch at time $0$; pause the stopwatch when the $7$ rings, restart it when the $8$ rings and stop it when the $10$ rings. It runs $7+2=9$ minutes.)

question:
Six turkeys are put on $5$ different plates so that every plate has at least one turkey. In how many ways can this be done
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] if the turkeys are identical;
\item[(b)] if the turkeys are all different?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Extend $AC$ beyond $C$ to the point $F$ with $CF=CE$. (In the figure, the lines through $D$ and $C$ perpendicular to $AE$ are red.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), A=(0,4), B=(4,0);
real d=1.5;
pair D=(0,d), Ept=(d,0), F=(0,-d);
pair u=unit(Ept-A), n=(u.y,-u.x);
pair K=extension(C,C+n,A,B), L=extension(D,D+n,A,B);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--Ept,blue); draw(C--F,dashed); draw(B--F,red+dashed);
draw(C--K,red); draw(D--L,red);
pair M1=extension(C,K,A,Ept), M2=extension(D,L,A,Ept);
real m=0.18;
draw(M1+m*n--M1+m*n-m*u--M1-m*u); draw(M2+m*n--M2+m*n-m*u--M2-m*u);
draw((m,0)--(m,m)--(0,m));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(Ept); dot(F); dot(K); dot(L);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,W); label("$D$",D,W); label("$E$",Ept,S); label("$F$",F,W);
label("$K$",K,NE); label("$L$",L,NE);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 1: $\angle CAE=\angle CBF$.} Triangles $ACE$ and $BCF$ have $AC=BC$, $CE=CF$ and right angles $\angle ACE=\angle BCF=90^\circ$, so they are congruent (SAS), and $\angle CAE=\angle CBF$.

\textbf{Step 2: $CK\parallel BF$.} Since $CK\perp AE$, in the right triangle formed by $A$, $C$ and the foot of the perpendicular from $C$ to $AE$ we get $\angle ACK=90^\circ-\angle CAE$. Hence
\[\angle KCB=90^\circ-\angle ACK=\angle CAE=\angle CBF.\]
The points $K$ (on the same side of $BC$ as $A$) and $F$ (on the extension of $AC$ beyond $C$) lie on opposite sides of the line $BC$, so $\angle KCB$ and $\angle CBF$ are alternate angles for the lines $CK$ and $BF$ cut by $BC$. They are equal, so $CK\parallel BF$.

\textbf{Step 3.} The lines $DL$ and $CK$ are both perpendicular to $AE$, so $DL\parallel CK\parallel BF$. These three parallel lines cut the line $AF$ at $D$, $C$, $F$ and the line $AB$ at $L$, $K$, $B$. Since $DC=CE=CF$, the intercept theorem gives $LK=KB$. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{Step 1: $AF^2=AC_1\cdot AB$.} In triangle $AFB$ the angle at $F$ is right, and $F$ lies on $CC_1$, which is perpendicular to $AB$ at $C_1$. So $FC_1$ is the altitude from the right angle of triangle $AFB$. The right triangles $AC_1F$ and $AFB$ share the angle at $A$, so they are similar, and
\[\dfrac{AC_1}{AF}=\dfrac{AF}{AB},\qquad AF^2=AC_1\cdot AB.\]

\textbf{Step 2: $AE^2=AB_1\cdot AC$.} In the same way, triangle $AEC$ has a right angle at $E$, and $EB_1$ is perpendicular to $AC$, so $AE^2=AB_1\cdot AC$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.2,3), B=(0,0), C=(4,0);
pair B1=A+dot(B-A,unit(C-A))*unit(C-A), C1=A+dot(C-A,unit(B-A))*unit(B-A);
pair Ept=B1+sqrt(abs(dot(A-B1,C-B1)))*unit(B-B1);
pair F=C1+sqrt(abs(dot(A-C1,B-C1)))*unit(C-C1);
draw(circle((B+C)/2,abs(C-B)/2),gray+dashed);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--B1,gray); draw(C--C1,gray);
draw(A--Ept,blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(Ept--C,blue); draw(A--F,red+linewidth(0.9)); draw(F--B,red);
real m=0.15;
pair u1=unit(C-Ept), v1=unit(A-Ept); draw(Ept+m*u1--Ept+m*u1+m*v1--Ept+m*v1,blue);
pair u2=unit(B-F), v2=unit(A-F); draw(F+m*u2--F+m*u2+m*v2--F+m*v2,red);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(B1); dot(C1); dot(Ept); dot(F);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$B_1$",B1,NE); label("$C_1$",C1,NW);
label("$E$",Ept,S); label("$F$",F,S);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 3: $AC_1\cdot AB=AB_1\cdot AC$.} The right triangles $AC_1C$ (right angle at $C_1$) and $AB_1B$ (right angle at $B_1$) share the angle at $A$, so they are similar, and $\dfrac{AC_1}{AC}=\dfrac{AB_1}{AB}$, that is, $AC_1\cdot AB=AB_1\cdot AC$. (Equivalently, $B_1$ and $C_1$ lie on the circle with diameter $BC$, dashed in the figure, and this is the power of the point $A$ with respect to that circle.)

Combining the three steps, $AF^2=AE^2$, so $AE=AF$. $\blacksquare$

question:
For any three points $A$, $B$, $C$ in the plane, the triangle inequality says that $AB+AC\ge BC$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove this inequality.
\item[(b)] Prove that equality $AB+AC=BC$ holds exactly when $A$ lies on the segment $BC$. (So a ``triangle'' with $AB+AC=BC$ is flat: its vertices lie on one line.)
\end{enumerate}

solution:
We may assume $B\ne C$. Let $A'$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $A$ to the line $BC$ (so $A'=A$ if $A$ is on that line). The figure shows the two possible positions of $A'$: on the segment $BC$, or outside it.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(5,0), A=(1.8,2.2), Ap=(A.x,0);
draw(B--C,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--B,blue); draw(A--C,blue); draw(A--Ap,dashed);
real m=0.2; draw(Ap+(m,0)--Ap+(m,m)--Ap+(0,m));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(Ap);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$A'$",Ap,S);
pair B2=(7,0), C2=(10.5,0), A2=(12.2,2.2), Ap2=(A2.x,0);
draw(B2--Ap2,linewidth(0.9)+gray); draw(B2--C2,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A2--B2,blue); draw(A2--C2,blue); draw(A2--Ap2,dashed);
draw(Ap2+(-m,0)--Ap2+(-m,m)--Ap2+(0,m));
dot(A2); dot(B2); dot(C2); dot(Ap2);
label("$A$",A2,N); label("$B$",B2,SW); label("$C$",C2,S); label("$A'$",Ap2,SE);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 1.} If $A\ne A'$, triangle $AA'B$ has a right angle at $A'$, so $AB^2=AA'^2+A'B^2>A'B^2$. Hence $AB\ge A'B$, with equality exactly when $A=A'$. In the same way $AC\ge A'C$, with equality exactly when $A=A'$.

\textbf{Step 2.} The points $A'$, $B$, $C$ lie on one line. If $A'$ is on the segment $BC$, then $A'B+A'C=BC$. If $A'$ is outside it, say beyond $C$ (right picture), then $A'B=A'C+CB$, so $A'B+A'C=BC+2A'C>BC$; the case beyond $B$ is the same. So $A'B+A'C\ge BC$, with equality exactly when $A'$ is on the segment $BC$.

\textbf{(a)} Combining, $AB+AC\ge A'B+A'C\ge BC$. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(b)} Equality $AB+AC=BC$ holds only if both inequalities are equalities, that is, $A=A'$ (so $A$ is on the line $BC$) and $A'$ is on the segment $BC$: together, $A$ is on the segment $BC$. Conversely, if $A$ is on the segment $BC$, then $AB+AC=BC$ directly. $\blacksquare$

This is why the inequality is often written with $\ge$ for arbitrary points: for the three vertices of a genuine (non-flat) triangle it is strict, $AB+AC>BC$.

\textbf{Step 1.} Since $i^2=-1$, the first equation is $10y^2-25=15y$, that is, $2y^2-3y-5=0$, or $(2y-5)(y+1)=0$. So $y=\dfrac52$ or $y=-1$.

\textbf{Step 2.}
\begin{itemize}
\item For $y=\dfrac52$: $50x^2-10x+\dfrac52=0$, that is, $20x^2-4x+1=0$, so $x=\dfrac{4\pm\sqrt{16-80}}{40}=\dfrac{4\pm8i}{40}$, giving $\boxed{x=\dfrac{1\pm2i}{10}}$.
\item For $y=-1$: $50x^2-10x-1=0$, so $x=\dfrac{10\pm\sqrt{100+200}}{100}=\dfrac{10\pm10\sqrt3}{100}$, giving $\boxed{x=\dfrac{1\pm\sqrt3}{10}}$.
\end{itemize}

question:
Let $\theta$ be an acute angle of a right triangle, and let $o$, $a$ and $h$ be the lengths of the side opposite $\theta$, the side adjacent to $\theta$ and the hypotenuse. Then
\[\sin\theta=\dfrac oh,\quad\cos\theta=\dfrac ah,\quad\tan\theta=\dfrac oa,\quad\csc\theta=\dfrac ho,\quad\sec\theta=\dfrac ha,\quad\cot\theta=\dfrac ao.\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] By substituting these ratios, prove the identities $\sin^2\theta+\cos^2\theta=1$ and $\tan\theta+\cot\theta=\sec\theta\csc\theta$.
\item[(b)] Explain why verifying a true identity in this way always comes down to the Pythagorean theorem $o^2+a^2=h^2$, and why it proves the identity only for acute angles $\theta$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Label the right triangle as in the figure.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(0,0), Q=(4,0), R=(4,3);
draw(P--Q--R--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
real m=0.3; draw(Q+(-m,0)--Q+(-m,m)--Q+(0,m));
draw(arc(P,0.8,0,degrees(atan2(3,4)))); label("$\theta$",P+1.15*dir(18));
label("$a$",(2,0),S); label("$o$",(4,1.5),E); label("$h$",(2,1.5),NW);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} By the Pythagorean theorem $o^2+a^2=h^2$. Then
\[\sin^2\theta+\cos^2\theta=\dfrac{o^2}{h^2}+\dfrac{a^2}{h^2}=\dfrac{o^2+a^2}{h^2}=\dfrac{h^2}{h^2}=1,\]
and
\[\tan\theta+\cot\theta=\dfrac oa+\dfrac ao=\dfrac{o^2+a^2}{ao}=\dfrac{h^2}{ao}=\dfrac ha\cdot\dfrac ho=\sec\theta\csc\theta.\]

\textbf{(b)} After the substitution, both sides of an identity become expressions in $o$, $a$, $h$. For any positive $o$ and $a$ there is a right triangle with these legs, and its hypotenuse is $h=\sqrt{o^2+a^2}$; so $o$ and $a$ can be anything, and the only relation that always holds between the three lengths is $o^2+a^2=h^2$. Hence a true identity, after clearing denominators, must follow from this one relation, and the computation keeps arriving at it. This is not a sign of an error; it is exactly the reason the identity holds.

The method uses a right triangle with angle $\theta$, which exists only for $0^\circ<\theta<90^\circ$. For other angles the trigonometric functions are defined with the unit circle, so this argument does not cover them; the same algebra works there with the point $(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$ on the unit circle and the relation $x^2+y^2=1$ in place of $o^2+a^2=h^2$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that for every integer $k\ge0$, each integer from $1$ to $2^{k+1}-1$ can be written in exactly one way as a sum of distinct powers of $2$ chosen from $2^0,2^1,\dots,2^k$.
\item[(b)] Use (a) to explain why the expansion of $(x+1)\left(x^2+3\right)\left(x^4+5\right)$ has exactly one term for each of the powers $x^0,x^1,\dots,x^7$, and find the coefficient of $x^5$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{The two maps.} Rotating $(x,y)$ by $180^\circ$ about the origin gives $(-x,-y)$. Reflecting $(x,y)$ across the line $y=-x$ gives $(-y,-x)$: the midpoint $\left(\dfrac{x-y}{2},\dfrac{y-x}{2}\right)$ of $(x,y)$ and $(-y,-x)$ lies on $y=-x$, and the segment joining them has direction $(-y-x,-x-y)$, a multiple of $(1,1)$, which is perpendicular to the line $y=-x$ (direction $(1,-1)$).

\textbf{Comparison.} The two images agree exactly when $(-y,-x)=(-x,-y)$, that is, when $x=y$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
draw((-4.5,0)--(4.5,0),Arrow(5)); draw((0,-4.5)--(0,4.5),Arrow(5));
draw((-4.3,4.3)--(4.3,-4.3),gray); label("$y=-x$",(-4.3,4.3),W,gray);
pair P=(-1,-3), Rf=(3,1), Ro=(1,3), Q=(-2,-2), Q2=(2,2);
draw(P--Rf,blue+dashed); draw(P--Ro,red+dashed);
dot(P); dot(Rf,blue); dot(Ro,red); dot(Q); dot(Q2,purple);
label("$P(-1,-3)$",P,SW); label("$(3,1)$",Rf,E,blue); label("$(1,3)$",Ro,N,red);
label("$Q(-2,-2)$",Q,W); label("$(2,2)$",Q2,E,purple);
label("$x$",(4.5,0),E); label("$y$",(0,4.5),N);
\end{asy}

In quadrant III ($x<0$, $y<0$) some points have $x=y$ and some do not. For $Q=(-2,-2)$ both maps give $(2,2)$, but for $P=(-1,-3)$ the reflection gives $(3,1)$ while the rotation gives $(1,3)$. So the claim holds exactly for the points of quadrant III on the line $y=x$, and the answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(B) }\text{sometimes true}}$.

From $a+d=b+c$ we get $d=b+c-a$, so
\[bc-ad=bc-a(b+c-a)=bc-ab-ac+a^2=(b-a)(c-a)=93.\]
Here $0<b-a<c-a$ and $93=3\cdot31$, so $(b-a,c-a)=(1,93)$ or $(3,31)$. Then $d=b+c-a$ gives $d-c=b-a>0$, so the order $a<b<c<d$ holds automatically, and only $d<500$ restricts $a$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $(1,93)$: $b=a+1$, $c=a+93$, $d=a+94<500$, so $1\le a\le405$: $405$ tuples;
\item $(3,31)$: $b=a+3$, $c=a+31$, $d=a+34<500$, so $1\le a\le465$: $465$ tuples.
\end{itemize}
In total there are $405+465=\boxed{870}$ four-tuples.

\textbf{Step 1: the length $PF$.} Triangles $PBC$ and $ABC$ have the same base $BC$, and their heights are in the ratio $PD:AD$ (the heights are measured along parallel perpendiculars, and $P$, $A$ lie on the line $AD$). So $\dfrac{[PBC]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac{PD}{AD}$, and similarly $\dfrac{[PCA]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac{PE}{BE}$ and $\dfrac{[PAB]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac{PF}{CF}$. The three small triangles fill triangle $ABC$, so
\[\dfrac{PD}{AD}+\dfrac{PE}{BE}+\dfrac{PF}{CF}=1,\qquad\dfrac{6}{12}+\dfrac{3}{12}+\dfrac{PF}{20}=1,\]
which gives $PF=5$ and $CP=15$. Moreover $[PBC]=\dfrac12[ABC]$ and $[PCA]=[PAB]=\dfrac14[ABC]$.

\textbf{Step 2: $D$ is the midpoint of $BC$.} Triangles $ABD$, $ACD$ (and $PBD$, $PCD$) have the same heights from $A$ (and from $P$), so $\dfrac{BD}{DC}=\dfrac{[ABD]-[PBD]}{[ACD]-[PCD]}=\dfrac{[PAB]}{[PCA]}=1$.

\textbf{Step 3: the area of $PBC$.} Extend $PD$ beyond $D$ to $P'$ with $DP'=PD=6$. The diagonals of $PBP'C$ bisect each other at $D$, so it is a parallelogram and $BP'=PC=15$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,-6), B=(9,0), C=(-9,12), P=(0,0);
pair D=(B+C)/2, Ept=(2*A+C)/3, F=(2*A+B)/3, Pp=2*D-P;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D); draw(B--Ept); draw(C--F);
draw(D--Pp,dashed); draw(B--Pp,dashed+blue); draw(C--Pp,dashed+gray);
draw(P--B,blue+linewidth(1)); draw(P--Pp,blue+linewidth(1));
real m=0.9; draw(P+m*unit(B-P)--P+m*unit(B-P)+m*unit(Pp-P)--P+m*unit(Pp-P),blue);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(P); dot(D); dot(Ept); dot(F); dot(Pp);
label("$A$",A,S); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,NW); label("$P$",P,SW); label("$D$",D,NE); label("$E$",Ept,W); label("$F$",F,SE); label("$P'$",Pp,N);
label("$6$",(A+P)/2,W); label("$6$",(P+D)/2+(0,-0.6),W); label("$9$",(P+B)/2,S); label("$3$",(P+Ept)/2,N); label("$15$",(B+Pp)/2,E);
\end{asy}

Triangle $PBP'$ has sides $PB=9$, $PP'=12$ and $BP'=15$, and $9^2+12^2=15^2$, so it has a right angle at $P$ and area $\dfrac12\cdot9\cdot12=54$. Since $D$ is the midpoint of both $BC$ and $PP'$, we have $[PBC]=2[PBD]=[PBP']=54$.

\textbf{Step 4.} Therefore $[ABC]=2[PBC]=\boxed{108}$.

Classify the words by the set of positions holding $O$; each of the other positions holds one of the $19$ other letters. The set of $O$-positions must contain two adjacent positions and must not contain three consecutive positions:
\begin{itemize}
\item two $O$'s: positions $\{1,2\}$, $\{2,3\}$ or $\{3,4\}$, each with $19^2=361$ fillings: $3\cdot361=1083$ words;
\item three $O$'s: positions $\{1,2,4\}$ or $\{1,3,4\}$ (not $\{1,2,3\}$ or $\{2,3,4\}$), each with $19$ fillings: $38$ words;
\item four $O$'s: $OOOO$ contains $OOO$, so none.
\end{itemize}
In total there are $1083+38=\boxed{1121}$ words.

Let $t=\dfrac{AX}{AB}=\dfrac{BY}{BC}=\dfrac{CZ}{CA}$, with $0<t<1$. Then $AZ=(1-t)\,AC$, and since triangles $AXZ$ and $ABC$ share the angle at $A$,
\[\dfrac{[AXZ]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac{AX}{AB}\cdot\dfrac{AZ}{AC}=t(1-t).\]
The same holds for the corner triangles at $B$ and at $C$. Removing the three corners,
\[\dfrac{[XYZ]}{[ABC]}=1-3t(1-t)=\dfrac{7}{12},\qquad t(1-t)=\dfrac{5}{36},\]
so $36t^2-36t+5=0$ and $t=\dfrac16$ or $t=\dfrac56$. The two cases are mirror images of each other (shown below); in both,
\[|AX-BX|=|7t-7(1-t)|=7|2t-1|=7\cdot\dfrac23=\boxed{\dfrac{14}{3}}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(10cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(7,0), C=(2.5,24/7);
real[] ts={1/6,5/6};
for(int i=0;i<2;++i){
  real t=ts[i]; pair s=(i*9.5,0);
  pair X=A+t*(B-A), Y=B+t*(C-B), Z=C+t*(A-C);
  fill(shift(s)*(X--Y--Z--cycle),paleblue);
  draw(shift(s)*(A--B--C--cycle),linewidth(0.9)); draw(shift(s)*(X--Y--Z--cycle),blue);
  dot(shift(s)*X); dot(shift(s)*Y); dot(shift(s)*Z);
  label("$A$",shift(s)*A,SW); label("$B$",shift(s)*B,SE); label("$C$",shift(s)*C,N);
  label("$X$",shift(s)*X,S); label("$Y$",shift(s)*Y,NE); label("$Z$",shift(s)*Z,NW);
  label(i==0 ? "$t=\dfrac16$" : "$t=\dfrac56$",shift(s)*(3.5,-1.1));
}
\end{asy}

