Through $E$ draw the line parallel to $AB$; it meets the line $BC$ at $G$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(1,0), A=(0.5,sqrt(3)/2);
pair D=2*A-B, Ept=2*C-A, F=(4/3,0), G=(2,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,dashed); draw(C--Ept,dashed); draw(C--G,dashed);
draw(D--Ept,blue); draw(Ept--G,red);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(Ept); dot(F); dot(G);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$D$",D,N); label("$E$",Ept,S); label("$F$",F,NE); label("$G$",G,SE);
label("$1$",(A+D)/2,W); label("$1$",(C+Ept)/2,W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 1.} Triangles $CEG$ and $CAB$ have vertical angles at $C$ and $EG\parallel AB$, so they are similar, with ratio $\dfrac{CE}{CA}=1$: they are congruent. Hence $CG=CB=1$ and $EG=AB=1$, and $G$ lies on the extension of $BC$ beyond $C$, with $BG=2$.

\textbf{Step 2.} $EG\parallel BD$, so triangles $FEG$ and $FDB$ are similar, and
\[\dfrac{FG}{FB}=\dfrac{EG}{DB}=\dfrac{1}{2}.\]
The point $F$ lies on the segment $BG$ (the line $DE$ crosses from $D$, above line $BG$, to $E$, below it), so $FB=2FG$ and $FB+FG=BG=2$. Thus $FG=\dfrac23$ and $FB=\dfrac43$, so $F$ lies beyond $C$ and
\[CF=FB-BC=\dfrac43-1=\boxed{\dfrac13}.\]
(Check with Menelaus' theorem for triangle $ABC$ and the line $DFE$: $\dfrac{AD}{DB}\cdot\dfrac{BF}{FC}\cdot\dfrac{CE}{EA}=\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac{4}{1}\cdot\dfrac12=1$.)

\textbf{Coordinates.} Let $M$ be the midpoint of $AC$. Since $BA=BC$, $BM\perp AC$ and $BM=\sqrt{7^2-5^2}=2\sqrt6$. Put $M=(0,0)$, $A=(-5,0)$, $C=(5,0)$ and $B=\left(0,2\sqrt6\right)$.

\textbf{Where $D$ can be.} Since $\angle ADC=90^\circ$, $D$ lies on the circle with diameter $AC$ (angle in a semicircle): $x^2+y^2=25$. Since $BD=1$: $x^2+\left(y-2\sqrt6\right)^2=1$. Subtracting the second equation from the first,
\[4\sqrt6\,y-24=24,\qquad y=\dfrac{48}{4\sqrt6}=2\sqrt6,\]
and then $x^2=25-24=1$. So $D=\left(-1,2\sqrt6\right)$ or $D'=\left(1,2\sqrt6\right)$; both are at the height of $B$ and different from $B$, so both lie outside the triangle.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=2*sqrt(6);
pair A=(-5,0), C=(5,0), B=(0,h), M=(0,0), D=(-1,h), D2=(1,h);
fill(A--D--C--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
draw(circle(M,5),dashed+gray(0.5));
draw(circle(B,1),dotted);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D--C,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D2,dashed+red);
draw(B--M,dotted);
pair u=unit(A-D), v=unit(C-D);
draw(D+0.45*u--D+0.45*u+0.45*v--D+0.45*v,blue);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D,blue); dot(D2,red); dot(M);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$C$",C,E); label("$B$",B,N); label("$D$",D,NW,blue); label("$D'$",D2,NE,red);
label("$M$",M,S); label("$1$",(B+D)/2,N,fontsize(8pt)); label("$2\sqrt{6}$",(0,1.6),E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$5$",(-2.5,0),S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$5$",(2.5,0),S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Which point is allowed.} The line $BC$ is $y=2\sqrt6\left(1-\dfrac x5\right)$, and the segment $BC$ consists of its points with $0\le x\le5$.
\begin{itemize}
\item The segment $AD'$ lies on $y=2\sqrt6\cdot\dfrac{x+5}{6}$ with $-5\le x\le1$. It meets the line $BC$ where $\dfrac{x+5}{6}=1-\dfrac x5$, that is, $x=\dfrac5{11}$, which lies in both ranges. So $AD'$ crosses $BC$, and $D'$ is not allowed.
\item The segment $AD$ lies on $y=2\sqrt6\cdot\dfrac{x+5}{4}$ with $-5\le x\le-1$. It meets the line $BC$ only where $\dfrac{x+5}{4}=1-\dfrac x5$, that is, $x=-\dfrac59$, which is not in the range $0\le x\le5$. So $AD$ does not meet $BC$, and it does not pass through $B$ (all its points have $x\le-1$).
\end{itemize}
So such a triangle exists, and $D=\left(-1,2\sqrt6\right)$ is the only possible position. Its distance from $AC$ is $2\sqrt6$, so
\[[ADC]=\dfrac12\cdot10\cdot2\sqrt6=\boxed{10\sqrt6}\approx24.49.\]
Since there is only one possible triangle $ADC$, this is also the smallest possible area. (The other point $D'$ would give the same area.)

question:
Let $f(x)=\dfrac{8}{x^{-2}}$ and $g(x)=8x^2$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $f(x)=g(x)$ for every real number $x\ne0$.
\item[(b)] Is there a real number at which $f$ and $g$ differ? Use this to explain in what sense the expression $\dfrac{8}{x^{-2}}$ is, and is not, a polynomial.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Put $C=(0,0)$, $A=(3,0)$ and $B=(0,4)$. The blue circle has centre $\left(\dfrac32,0\right)$ and radius $\dfrac32$, the green circle centre $(0,2)$ and radius $2$, and the pink circle centre $\left(\dfrac32,2\right)$ and radius $\dfrac52$. Let the orange circle have centre $(x,y)$ and radius $r$. External tangency to the blue and green circles and internal tangency to the pink circle mean
\begin{align*}
\left(x-\dfrac32\right)^2+y^2&=\left(\dfrac32+r\right)^2,\\
x^2+(y-2)^2&=(2+r)^2,\\
\left(x-\dfrac32\right)^2+(y-2)^2&=\left(\dfrac52-r\right)^2.
\end{align*}
Subtracting the third equation from the first: $4y-4=\left(\dfrac94+3r\right)-\left(\dfrac{25}4-5r\right)=8r-4$, so $y=2r$. Subtracting the third from the second: $3x-\dfrac94=\left(4+4r\right)-\left(\dfrac{25}4-5r\right)=9r-\dfrac94$, so $x=3r$. Substituting into the third equation,
\[\left(3r-\dfrac32\right)^2+(2r-2)^2=\left(\dfrac52-r\right)^2\iff13r^2-17r+\dfrac{25}4=r^2-5r+\dfrac{25}4\iff12r(r-1)=0.\]
Since $r>0$, $r=1$, and the centre is $O=(3,2)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), A=(3,0), B=(0,4);
draw(circle((1.5,2),2.5),magenta+linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle((1.5,0),1.5),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle((0,2),2),heavygreen+linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle((3,2),1),orange+linewidth(1.1));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((3,2)--(4,2),orange); draw((1.5,0)--(3,2),dashed+gray(0.4)); draw((0,2)--(3,2),dashed+gray(0.4));
dot(C); dot(A); dot(B); dot((3,2),orange); dot((1.5,0),blue); dot((0,2),heavygreen); dot((1.5,2),magenta);
label("$C$",C,SW); label("$A$",A,SE); label("$B$",B,NW); label("$O$",(3,2),N,orange);
label("$1$",(3.5,2),N,orange+fontsize(8pt));
label("$3$",(0.8,0),S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$4$",(0,3.1),W,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Check: $O$ is at distance $\dfrac52=\dfrac32+1$ from the blue centre, $3=2+1$ from the green centre and $\dfrac32=\dfrac52-1$ from the pink centre; it lies beyond the hypotenuse, in the region of the pink disc not covered by the other two discs. The radius is $\boxed{1}$.

\textbf{The bisector.} By the angle bisector theorem, $\dfrac{CK}{KB}=\dfrac{AC}{AB}$, so $\dfrac{AC}{AB}=\dfrac13$.

\textbf{The altitude.} Triangles $CHA$ and $CAB$ are similar (they share the angle at $C$, and both have a right angle, at $H$ and at $A$), so $\dfrac{CH}{CA}=\dfrac{CA}{CB}$, that is, $CH=\dfrac{AC^2}{BC}$. In the same way $HB=\dfrac{AB^2}{BC}$. Hence
\[\dfrac{CH}{HB}=\left(\dfrac{AC}{AB}\right)^2=\dfrac19.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), B=(10,0), H=(1,0), A=(1,3), K=(2.5,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--H,blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--K,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(H+(0,0.35)--H+(0.35,0.35)--H+(0.35,0),blue);
pair u=unit(B-A), v=unit(C-A);
draw(A+0.5*u--A+0.5*u+0.5*v--A+0.5*v);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(H,blue); dot(K,red);
label("$H$",H,S,blue); label("$K$",K,S,red); label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,SW);
\end{asy}

(In the figure $AC=\sqrt{10}$, $AB=3\sqrt{10}$, $BC=10$, $CK=2.5$, $CH=1$ and $HB=9$.) The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(B) }1:9}$.

\textbf{Three small equilateral triangles.} Draw through $P$ the lines parallel to the sides of the triangle. The lines through $P$ parallel to $AB$ and to $AC$ meet $BC$ at points $V$ and $U$. Triangle $PVU$ has its sides parallel to those of $ABC$, so it is equilateral, with apex $P$ and base $VU$ on $BC$; let $x$ be its side. In the same way we get an equilateral triangle with apex $P$ and base on $CA$, with side $y$, and one with base on $AB$, with side $z$ (dashed below).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(1,0), A=(0.5,sqrt(3)/2), P=(0.42,0.24);
pair D=extension(A,P,B,C), E=extension(B,P,C,A), F=extension(C,P,A,B);
pair V=extension(P,P+(B-A),B,C), U=extension(P,P+(C-A),B,C);
pair Y1=extension(P,P+(A-B),C,A), Y2=extension(P,P+(C-B),C,A);
pair Z1=extension(P,P+(B-C),A,B), Z2=extension(P,P+(A-C),A,B);
fill(P--V--U--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1)); fill(P--Y1--Y2--cycle,rgb(1,0.9,0.8)); fill(P--Z1--Z2--cycle,rgb(0.88,1,0.88));
draw(P--V--U--cycle,dashed+blue); draw(P--Y1--Y2--cycle,dashed+orange); draw(P--Z1--Z2--cycle,dashed+heavygreen);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D); draw(B--E); draw(C--F);
draw(P--D,red+linewidth(1)); draw(P--E,red+linewidth(1)); draw(P--F,red+linewidth(1));
dot(P); dot(D); dot(E); dot(F);
label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$P$",P+0.1*dir(-41));
label("$D$",D,S); label("$E$",E,NE); label("$F$",F,NW);
label("$V$",V,S,blue); label("$U$",U,S,blue);
label("$x$",(0.7*V+0.3*U)+(0,0.035),blue+fontsize(8pt));
label("$A$",A,N);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 1: $x+y+z=1$.} The height of triangle $PVU$ from $P$ is the distance $d_a$ from $P$ to $BC$, and an equilateral triangle of side $s$ has height $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s$, so $x=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3}d_a$. Similarly $y=\dfrac2{\sqrt3}d_b$ and $z=\dfrac2{\sqrt3}d_c$, where $d_b$, $d_c$ are the distances from $P$ to $CA$ and $AB$. The triangles $PBC$, $PCA$, $PAB$ fill triangle $ABC$, so
\[\dfrac12\left(d_a+d_b+d_c\right)\cdot1=[ABC]=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4},\qquad d_a+d_b+d_c=\dfrac{\sqrt3}2,\]
and therefore $x+y+z=\dfrac2{\sqrt3}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}=1$.

\textbf{Step 2: $PD<x$.} Since $P$ is inside the triangle, $P=A+s(B-A)+t(C-A)$ with $s,t>0$. The ray from $P$ away from $A$, along line $AP$, has direction $P-A=s(B-A)+t(C-A)$. The rays $PV$ and $PU$ have directions $B-A$ and $C-A$ (they are parallel to $AB$ and $AC$ and point towards $BC$). A positive combination of the two directions points strictly inside the angle $VPU$, so this ray meets the segment $VU$ at a point strictly between $V$ and $U$. That point lies on line $AP$ and on $BC$, so it is $D$.

Now let $M$ be the midpoint of $VU$. Then $PM\perp VU$ and $MD<\dfrac x2$, so
\[PD^2=PM^2+MD^2<PM^2+\left(\dfrac x2\right)^2=PV^2=x^2,\]
that is, $PD<x$.

\textbf{Step 3.} In the same way $PE<y$ and $PF<z$. Adding,
\[PD+PE+PF<x+y+z=1.\qquad\blacksquare\]

Write $A=\overline{abcd}=1000a+100b+10c+d$. The new number is
\[1000(a+n)+100(b-n)+10(c+n)+(d-n)=A+909n.\]
So $A+909n=nA$, that is, $(n-1)A=909n$. Here $n\ne1$ (otherwise $909=0$), so $A=\dfrac{909n}{n-1}$. Since $n$ and $n-1$ have no common factor, $n-1$ must divide $909=3^2\cdot101$, so $n-1\in\{1,3,9,101,303,909\}$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $n=2$: $A=1818$;
\item $n=4$: $A=1212$, but its second digit $2$ cannot be decreased by $4$;
\item $n=10$: $A=1010$, but its first digit $1$ cannot be increased by $10$;
\item $n=102$, $304$, $910$: $A=918$, $912$, $910$, which have only three digits.
\end{itemize}
So $A=1818$ and $n=2$: indeed the digits become $3,6,3,6$, and $3636=2\cdot1818$. The sum of the digits of $A$ is $1+8+1+8=\boxed{18}$.

Notice that $AB^2+BC^2=17+99=116=19+97=CD^2+DA^2$.

In a cyclic quadrilateral the opposite angles $B$ and $D$ add up to $180^\circ$, so $\cos D=-\cos B$. By the law of cosines in triangles $ABC$ and $ADC$,
\[AC^2=17+99-2\sqrt{17\cdot99}\cos B=19+97+2\sqrt{19\cdot97}\cos B.\]
Since $17+99=19+97$, this gives $\left(\sqrt{17\cdot99}+\sqrt{19\cdot97}\right)\cos B=0$, so $\cos B=0$ and $\angle B=\angle D=90^\circ$. Then $AC$ subtends a right angle at $B$, so $AC$ is a diameter, with
\[AC^2=AB^2+BC^2=116.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=sqrt(29);
pair O=(0,0), A=(-R,0), C=(R,0);
real tb=aCos(sqrt(17/116)), td=aCos(sqrt(97/116));
pair B=A+sqrt(17)*dir(tb), D=A+sqrt(97)*dir(-td);
draw(circle(O,R),gray(0.4));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,dashed+blue);
pair u=unit(A-B), v=unit(C-B); draw(B+0.5*u--B+0.5*u+0.5*v--B+0.5*v);
pair u2=unit(A-D), v2=unit(C-D); draw(D+0.5*u2--D+0.5*u2+0.5*v2--D+0.5*v2);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(O);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,N); label("$C$",C,E); label("$D$",D,S); label("$O$",O,S);
label("$\sqrt{17}$",(A+B)/2,NW,fontsize(8pt)); label("$\sqrt{99}$",(B+C)/2,NE,fontsize(8pt));
label("$\sqrt{19}$",(C+D)/2,SE,fontsize(8pt)); label("$\sqrt{97}$",(D+A)/2,SW,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The radius is $r=\dfrac{\sqrt{116}}2$, so $r^2=\dfrac{116}4=29$ and the area of the circle is $\boxed{29\pi}$.

question:
Which of the following graphs contains at least three distinct points that all lie on a single line?
\begin{enumerate}
\item[A.] $y=2\sin x$
\item[B.] $y=\ln(x-1)$
\item[C.] $y=x^2+4$
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{A works.} The points $(0,0)$, $(\pi,0)$ and $(2\pi,0)$ lie on $y=2\sin x$ and on the line $y=0$.

\textbf{C does not.} For $x_1<x_2<x_3$, the slope of the segment joining the points of $y=x^2+4$ above $x_1$ and $x_2$ is
\[\dfrac{(x_2^2+4)-(x_1^2+4)}{x_2-x_1}=x_1+x_2,\]
and the slope for $x_2,x_3$ is $x_2+x_3>x_1+x_2$. Two segments from the same point with different slopes are not on one line.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return 2*sin(x);}
draw((-0.6,0)--(7,0),Arrow(5)); draw((0,-2.4)--(0,2.6),Arrow(5));
draw(graph(f,-0.5,6.9),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-0.6,0)--(7,0),red+linewidth(0.8));
dot((0,0),red); dot((pi,0),red); dot((2*pi,0),red);
label("$y=2\sin x$",(1.6,2),N,blue+fontsize(8pt));
label("$\pi$",(pi,0),S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$2\pi$",(2*pi,0),S,fontsize(8pt));
real g(real x){return 0.3*(x^2+4);}
pair o=(11.6,-2.4);
draw(o+(-2.2,0)--o+(2.4,0),Arrow(5)); draw(o+(0,0)--o+(0,3.6),Arrow(5));
draw(shift(o)*graph(g,-2.1,2.4),heavygreen+linewidth(0.9));
pair P1=o+(-1.5,g(-1.5)), P2=o+(0.5,g(0.5)), P3=o+(2,g(2));
draw(P1--P2--P3,dashed);
dot(P1); dot(P2); dot(P3);
label("$y=x^2+4$",o+(-0.2,3.3),W,heavygreen+fontsize(8pt)); label("(vertical scale reduced)",o+(0.1,-0.35),S,fontsize(7pt));
