\textbf{B does not.} Take $1<x_1<x_2<x_3$ and put $u_k=x_k-1>0$. We use $\ln t<t-1$ for $t>0$, $t\ne1$, and its consequence $\ln t=-\ln\dfrac1t>1-\dfrac1t$. The slope of the segment for $x_1,x_2$ is
\[\dfrac{\ln u_2-\ln u_1}{u_2-u_1}=\dfrac{\ln\dfrac{u_2}{u_1}}{u_2-u_1}>\dfrac{1-\dfrac{u_1}{u_2}}{u_2-u_1}=\dfrac1{u_2},\]
and the slope for $x_2,x_3$ is
\[\dfrac{\ln\dfrac{u_3}{u_2}}{u_3-u_2}<\dfrac{\dfrac{u_3}{u_2}-1}{u_3-u_2}=\dfrac1{u_2}.\]
So the two slopes differ, and no three points of this graph are on a line.

The answer is $\boxed{\text{A}}$.

question:
Runner $A$ runs at $10$ meters per second and runner $B$ at $5$ meters per second, in the same direction along a straight track. They start at the same moment, with $B$ starting $50$ meters ahead of $A$, at a point $C_0$. When $A$ reaches $C_0$, $B$ has moved on to a point $C_1$; when $A$ reaches $C_1$, $B$ is at a point $C_2$; and so on. Call ``stage $1$'' the time $A$ needs to reach $C_0$, and ``stage $k$'' (for $k\ge2$) the time $A$ needs to run from $C_{k-2}$ to $C_{k-1}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the length of stage $k$, and the total length of all the stages.
\item[(b)] A student argues: ``$B$ is ahead of $A$ at the end of every stage, and there are infinitely many stages, so $B$ is always ahead.'' Explain the error, and find when and where $A$ catches up with $B$.
\end{enumerate}

A student solves the equation $ax+by=c$ in integers by reducing it modulo $b$, which gives $ax\equiv c\pmod b$, and then multiplying by an inverse of $a$ modulo $b$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Use this method to find \emph{all} integer solutions $(x,y)$ of $3x+4y=7$.
\item[(b)] When does $a$ have an inverse modulo $b$? Explain how to adapt the method when it does not, and use it to find all integer solutions of $6x+10y=14$. Does $6x+10y=15$ have integer solutions?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Label the vertices $A_1,A_2,\dots,A_{2n}$ in order (indices are taken modulo $2n$), and let $a_i=A_iA_{i+1}$, so $a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_{2n}=1$. Call $A_iA_{i+k}$ a diagonal of \emph{step} $k$ ($2\le k\le n$). There are $2n$ diagonals of step $k$ for $2\le k\le n-1$ (one for each starting vertex $i$; they are all different since $k\ne n$) and $n$ diagonals of step $n$ (the ``long'' diagonals $A_iA_{i+n}$, $1\le i\le n$). In total this is $2n(n-2)+n=n(2n-3)$ diagonals, which is all of them.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] P={(0,0),(3,-0.6),(5.2,0.8),(5.6,3),(3.4,4.6),(0.6,3.6)};
draw(P[0]--P[1]--P[2]--P[3]--P[4]--P[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(P[0]--P[1]--P[2],blue+linewidth(1.6));
draw(P[0]--P[2],red+linewidth(1));
draw(P[0]--P[3],heavygreen+dashed+linewidth(1));
string[] nm={"$A_1$","$A_2$","$A_3$","$A_4$","$A_5$","$A_6$"};
pair[] dr={SW,S,E,E,N,W};
for(int i=0;i<6;++i){dot(P[i]); label(nm[i],P[i],dr[i]);}
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step $k$ with $2\le k\le n-1$.} By the triangle inequality, the diagonal $A_iA_{i+k}$ is shorter than the broken line $A_iA_{i+1}\dots A_{i+k}$ (strictly, because the vertices of a convex polygon are not collinear):
\[A_iA_{i+k}<a_i+a_{i+1}+\cdots+a_{i+k-1}.\]
Adding these $2n$ inequalities for $i=1,\dots,2n$, each side $a_j$ appears exactly $k$ times on the right, so the diagonals of step $k$ have total length less than $k(a_1+\cdots+a_{2n})=k$. (In the figure, $n=3$: the red diagonal $A_1A_3$ is shorter than the blue path $A_1A_2A_3$.)

\textbf{Step $n$.} The long diagonal $A_iA_{i+n}$ splits the boundary into two broken lines, each longer than the diagonal, and their lengths add up to $1$. So $A_iA_{i+n}<\dfrac12$, and the $n$ long diagonals (green in the figure) have total length less than $\dfrac n2$.

\textbf{Total.} The sum of all diagonals is less than
\[\sum_{k=2}^{n-1}k+\dfrac n2=\left(\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2}-1\right)+\dfrac n2=\dfrac{n^2}{2}-1.\qquad\blacksquare\]
(For $n=2$ the sum over $k$ is empty and the bound says that the two diagonals of a quadrilateral add up to less than its perimeter.) The bound cannot be improved: for a very thin convex polygon with $n$ vertices crowded near each end of a segment of length $\dfrac12$, the $n^2-2$ diagonals joining the two groups have lengths close to $\dfrac12$ and the others are close to $0$, so the sum is close to $\dfrac{n^2-2}{2}$.

Let the circle (radius $r$) touch $AD$, $CD$, $BC$ at $W$, $Z$, $Y$. Tangent segments from a point are equal, so write $AX=AW=5t$, $BX=BY=9t$, $DW=DZ=p$ and $CZ=CY=q$. Then
\[5t+p=125,\qquad9t+q=169.\]

\textbf{The right angle at $O$.} Since $AB\parallel CD$, $\angle DAB+\angle ADC=180^\circ$. The centre $O$ lies on the bisectors of these angles, so $\angle OAD+\angle ODA=90^\circ$ and $\angle AOD=90^\circ$. In the right triangle $AOD$, $OW$ is the altitude to the hypotenuse, so (by the similar triangles $AWO$ and $OWD$) $OW^2=AW\cdot WD$, that is, $r^2=5t\cdot p$. In the same way, from the right angle $BOC$, $r^2=9t\cdot q$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
