\textbf{Surface area:}
\[2\pi r^2+2\pi rh=2\pi\cdot25+2\pi\cdot5\cdot34=50\pi+340\pi=\boxed{390\pi\text{ cm}^2}\approx1225.2\text{ cm}^2.\]
\textbf{Volume:}
\[\pi r^2h=\pi\cdot25\cdot34=\boxed{850\pi\text{ cm}^3}\approx2670.4\text{ cm}^3.\]

\textbf{A correct formula.} Triangles $AGC$ and $ADC$ have the common vertex $C$ and their bases $AG$, $AD$ on the same line, so $\dfrac{[AGC]}{[ADC]}=\dfrac{AG}{AD}$. Triangles $ADC$ and $ABC$ have the common vertex $A$ and bases on $BC$, so $\dfrac{[ADC]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac{DC}{BC}$. Hence
\[\dfrac{[AGC]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac{AG}{AD}\cdot\dfrac{DC}{BC}.\]
(In the same way, using $CE$, it also equals $\dfrac{CG}{CE}\cdot\dfrac{AE}{AB}$.)

\textbf{The claim is false in general.} Take $B=(0,0)$, $C=(4,0)$, $A=(0,6)$, the midpoint $D=(2,0)$ of $BC$ and $E=(0,4)$, so $\dfrac{AE}{AB}=\dfrac13$. The lines $AD$ ($3x+y=6$) and $CE$ ($x+y=4$) meet at $G=(1,3)$, the midpoint of $AD$, so $\dfrac{AG}{AD}=\dfrac12$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(4,0), A=(0,6), D=(2,0), E=(0,4), G=(1,3);
fill(A--G--C--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(C--E,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(D--E,dotted);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(E); dot(G);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,S); label("$E$",E,W); label("$G$",G,dir(20));
\end{asy}

Then $[ABC]=12$ and $[AGC]=\dfrac12\left|0\cdot(3-0)+1\cdot(0-6)+4\cdot(6-3)\right|=3$, so the true ratio is $\dfrac3{12}=\dfrac14=\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac12$, while the claimed formula gives $\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac13=\dfrac16$. So the statement is \textbf{false}.

\textbf{When it holds.} Comparing with the correct formula, the claim holds exactly when $\dfrac{DC}{BC}=\dfrac{AE}{AB}$, that is, $\dfrac{BD}{BC}=\dfrac{BE}{BA}$. By similar triangles (and the converse of the basic proportionality theorem) this happens exactly when $\boxed{DE\parallel AC}$.

Let $O$ be the centre. If one of the points is $O$, its distance to any other point is at most $1<\sqrt2$, and if two points coincide we are done; so assume all four points differ from $O$.

Draw the rays from $O$ through the four points. Going once around $O$, these rays cut the full angle into four angles between consecutive rays (an angle may be $0^\circ$ if two rays coincide), and these four angles add up to $360^\circ$. So one of them is at most $90^\circ$: there are two of the points, $P$ and $Q$, with $\theta=\angle POQ\le90^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
draw(circle(O,1),gray(0.4));
real[] ang={20,100,205,290}; real[] rad={0.9,0.7,1,0.85};
pair[] P;
for(int i=0;i<4;++i){P.push(rad[i]*dir(ang[i])); draw(O--1.0*dir(ang[i]),dashed+gray(0.5)); dot(P[i]);}
draw(arc(O,0.25,20,100),red); label("$\theta\le90^\circ$",0.42*dir(58),red+fontsize(8pt));
draw(P[0]--P[1],blue+linewidth(1));
dot(O); label("$O$",O,dir(152));
label("$P$",P[0],S); label("$Q$",P[1],W);
\end{asy}

Let $OP=p\le1$ and $OQ=q\le1$. By the law of cosines (also valid for $\theta=0^\circ$), and since $\cos\theta\ge0$,
\[PQ^2=p^2+q^2-2pq\cos\theta\le p^2+q^2\le2.\]
Hence $PQ\le\sqrt2$. $\blacksquare$

(The bound is sharp: the four vertices of a square inscribed in the circle are pairwise at distance at least $\sqrt2$.)

question:
Bill sold his motor scooter to Tom for $\$100$. After driving it around for a few days, Tom discovered it was in such a broken-down condition that he sold it back to Bill for $\$80$. The next day Bill sold it to Herman for $\$90$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How much more money does Bill have after these three sales than before them?
\item[(b)] Bill had originally bought the scooter for $c$ dollars. What is his total profit on the scooter? Explain why the question ``What is Bill's total profit?'' cannot be answered without knowing $c$.
\end{enumerate}

You wait in a room for an event that may happen at any time, with no limit on how long you might have to wait, but it is certain to happen eventually. Say that the event happens ``in minute $k$'' if it happens during the $k$-th minute of waiting ($k=1,2,3,\dots$).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that it is impossible for the event to be equally likely to happen in each of the minutes $1,2,3,\dots$.
\item[(b)] Suppose instead that the event happens in minute $k$ with probability $p_k=q(1-q)^{k-1}$, where $0<q<1$ is fixed. Show that these probabilities add up to $1$, and that for every whole number $t\ge0$: if the event has not happened during the first $t$ minutes, the probability that it happens in the next minute is $q$.
\item[(c)] What do (a) and (b) say about the question ``If you have already waited $t$ minutes, what is the chance that the event happens within the next minute?''
\end{enumerate}

