The point $(8,9)$ is on the line, since $24+36=60$. The radius to the point of tangency is perpendicular to the tangent line, and $(3,4)$ is perpendicular to the line $3x+4y=60$. So the centre is $(8+3t,\ 9+4t)$ for some number $t$, and the radius is its distance from $(8,9)$, namely $5|t|$.

A circle tangent to both axes has centre $(\pm r,\pm r)$, so we need $|8+3t|=|9+4t|=5|t|$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $8+3t=9+4t$, then $t=-1$: the centre is $(5,5)$, and indeed $|5|=5=5|t|$, so $r=5$.
\item If $8+3t=-(9+4t)$, then $t=-\dfrac{17}{7}$: the centre is $\left(\dfrac57,-\dfrac57\right)$, at distance $\dfrac57$ from the axes but $5|t|=\dfrac{85}7$ from $(8,9)$, so this fails.
\end{itemize}

