question:
A student takes the AMC 8, which has $25$ questions, each with $5$ answer choices of which exactly one is correct; the score is the number of correct answers. He cannot solve any of the problems. On each question, independently of the others, he skips the question with probability $40\%$, and otherwise he guesses one of the $5$ choices uniformly at random. Find the probability that his score is
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $10$;
\item[(b)] $15$;
\item[(c)] $0$.
\end{enumerate}
Give exact expressions and decimal approximations.

solution:
On each question he answers correctly with probability
\[p=(1-0.4)\cdot\dfrac15=\dfrac35\cdot\dfrac15=\dfrac{3}{25},\]
independently of the other questions, so his score is binomial with $n=25$ and $p=\dfrac3{25}$:
\[P(\text{score}=k)=\dbinom{25}{k}\left(\dfrac{3}{25}\right)^k\left(\dfrac{22}{25}\right)^{25-k}.\]
With $\dbinom{25}{10}=\dbinom{25}{15}=3268760$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $P(10)=\dbinom{25}{10}\left(\dfrac{3}{25}\right)^{10}\left(\dfrac{22}{25}\right)^{15}\approx\boxed{2.97\times10^{-4}}$;
\item[(b)] $P(15)=\dbinom{25}{15}\left(\dfrac{3}{25}\right)^{15}\left(\dfrac{22}{25}\right)^{10}\approx\boxed{1.40\times10^{-8}}$;
\item[(c)] $P(0)=\left(\dfrac{22}{25}\right)^{25}\approx\boxed{0.0409}$.
\end{enumerate}

Let the sides be $2$, $2r$ and $2r^2$ with ratio $r>1$; the hypotenuse is the longest side, $2r^2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), B=(0,2), A=(sqrt(2+2*sqrt(5)),0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0.15,0)--(0.15,0.15)--(0,0.15));
label("$A$",A,SE); label("$B$",B,N); label("$C$",C,SW);
label("$2$",(B+C)/2,W); label("$2r$",(A+C)/2,S); label("$2r^2$",(A+B)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

By the Pythagorean theorem,
\[2^2+(2r)^2=\left(2r^2\right)^2\iff r^4-r^2-1=0\iff r^2=\dfrac{1+\sqrt5}{2}\]
(the other root of $u^2-u-1=0$ is negative). So the hypotenuse is
\[2r^2=\boxed{1+\sqrt5}.\]
Check: the middle side is $2r=\sqrt{2+2\sqrt5}$, and $2^2+\left(2+2\sqrt5\right)=6+2\sqrt5=\left(1+\sqrt5\right)^2$.

Let $M$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $O$ to $AB$, so $OM=7$. The triangles $OMA$ and $OMB$ are congruent right triangles ($OA=OB$, common leg $OM$), so $M$ is the midpoint of $AB$ and $MB=6$.

