By the Pythagorean theorem, $AM=\sqrt{7^2-\left(\dfrac72\right)^2}=\dfrac{7\sqrt3}{2}$, so $r=\dfrac{7\sqrt3}{6}$ and the area is
\[\pi r^2=\pi\cdot\dfrac{49\cdot3}{36}=\boxed{\dfrac{49\pi}{12}}.\]

All three lengths are positive because $n>1$. The longest is $n^2+1$ (it exceeds $n^2-1$, and $n^2+1-2n=(n-1)^2>0$), and
\[(n^2-1)+2n-(n^2+1)=2n-2>0,\]
so the sum of the two shorter sides exceeds the longest side, and a triangle with these sides exists. Moreover
\[\left(n^2-1\right)^2+(2n)^2=n^4-2n^2+1+4n^2=n^4+2n^2+1=\left(n^2+1\right)^2,\]
so by the converse of the Pythagorean theorem the angle opposite the side $n^2+1$ is a right angle. $\blacksquare$

