Palindromes between $1$ and $10000$ have $1$ to $4$ digits ($10000$ is not one). A palindrome is determined by the first half of its digits (with the middle digit when the length is odd), and its first digit is not $0$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $1$ digit: $9$ (the digits $1$ to $9$);
\item $2$ digits $\overline{aa}$: $9$;
\item $3$ digits $\overline{aba}$: $9\cdot10=90$;
\item $4$ digits $\overline{abba}$: $9\cdot10=90$.
\end{itemize}
In total there are $9+9+90+90=\boxed{198}$ palindromes.

Let $D$, $E$, $F$ be the midpoints of $BC$, $CA$, $AB$, and let the medians $BE$ and $CF$ meet at $G$ (they cross inside the triangle).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.3,3.4), B=(0,0), C=(5.2,0);
pair Dm=(B+C)/2, Em=(C+A)/2, Fm=(A+B)/2, G=(A+B+C)/3;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--Dm,blue); draw(B--Em,blue); draw(C--Fm,blue);
draw(Fm--Em,red+dashed);
dot(Dm); dot(Em); dot(Fm); dot(G);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$D$",Dm,S); label("$E$",Em,NE); label("$F$",Fm,W); label("$G$",G,SE);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 1: $G$ cuts $BE$ in the ratio $2:1$.} By the midline theorem, $FE\parallel BC$ and $FE=\dfrac12BC$. Hence $\angle GFE=\angle GCB$ and $\angle GEF=\angle GBC$ (alternate angles), so $\triangle GFE\sim\triangle GCB$ with ratio $FE:CB=1:2$. Therefore $GE=\dfrac12GB$, that is, $BG:GE=2:1$.

\textbf{Step 2.} The same argument for the medians $AD$ and $BE$ (now with the midline $DE\parallel AB$, $DE=\dfrac12AB$) shows that $AD$ meets $BE$ at the point $G'$ of $BE$ with $BG':G'E=2:1$.

There is only one point of the segment $BE$ dividing it in the ratio $2:1$, so $G'=G$. Thus all three medians pass through $G$. $\blacksquare$

(The proof also shows that the centroid divides each median in the ratio $2:1$ from the vertex.)

For a point $P$ and a line through a point $Q$, the distance from $P$ to the line is $QP\sin\theta$, where $\theta$ is the angle between $QP$ and the line.

Let the bisectors of the angles at $A$ and $B$ meet at $I$. Each bisector runs from a vertex to a point of the opposite side, so they cross at a point $I$ inside the triangle. In the figure the dashed segments are the perpendiculars from $I$ to the sides.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.5,2.6), B=(0,0), C=(5.2,0);
real a=abs(B-C), bb=abs(C-A), c=abs(A-B);
pair I=(a*A+bb*B+c*C)/(a+bb+c);
pair Dm=(bb*B+c*C)/(bb+c), Em=(a*A+c*C)/(a+c), Fm=(a*A+bb*B)/(a+bb);
pair pr(pair P,pair U,pair V){return U+dot(P-U,V-U)/dot(V-U,V-U)*(V-U);}
pair X=pr(I,B,C), Y=pr(I,C,A), Z=pr(I,A,B);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--Dm,blue); draw(B--Em,blue); draw(C--Fm,blue);
draw(I--X,red+dashed); draw(I--Y,red+dashed); draw(I--Z,red+dashed);
dot(I);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$I$",I,dir(-120));
\end{asy}

Since $\angle IAB=\angle IAC$,
\[\operatorname{dist}(I,AB)=AI\sin\angle IAB=AI\sin\angle IAC=\operatorname{dist}(I,AC),\]
and in the same way $\operatorname{dist}(I,BA)=\operatorname{dist}(I,BC)$. Hence $\operatorname{dist}(I,CA)=\operatorname{dist}(I,CB)$, that is,
\[CI\sin\angle ICA=CI\sin\angle ICB,\qquad\text{so}\qquad \sin\angle ICA=\sin\angle ICB.\]
Two angles between $0^\circ$ and $180^\circ$ with equal sines are equal or add up to $180^\circ$. Since $I$ is inside the triangle, $\angle ICA+\angle ICB=\angle ACB<180^\circ$, so $\angle ICA=\angle ICB$: the line $CI$ is the bisector of the angle at $C$. Thus all three bisectors pass through $I$. $\blacksquare$

(Since $I$ is equally far from all three sides, it is the centre of the circle tangent to all three sides, the incircle.)

\textbf{Lemma.} A point $P$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of a segment $XY$ if and only if $PX=PY$.

\emph{Proof.} Let $M$ be the midpoint of $XY$. If $P$ is on the perpendicular bisector and $P\ne M$, then triangles $PMX$ and $PMY$ are congruent (SAS: $MX=MY$, right angles at $M$, common side $PM$), so $PX=PY$; if $P=M$ this is clear. Conversely, if $PX=PY$ and $P\ne M$, then triangles $PMX$ and $PMY$ are congruent (SSS), so $\angle PMX=\angle PMY$; these angles add up to $180^\circ$, so both are right angles and $P$ lies on the perpendicular bisector. $\square$

Let $K$, $M$, $L$ be the midpoints of $AB$, $BC$, $CA$. The perpendicular bisectors of $AB$ and $BC$ are perpendicular to the non-parallel lines $AB$ and $BC$, so they are not parallel and meet at a point $O$.

