By the lemma, $OA=OB$ (as $O$ is on the bisector of $AB$) and $OB=OC$ (as $O$ is on the bisector of $BC$). So $OC=OA$, and by the lemma again $O$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $CA$. Thus all three perpendicular bisectors pass through $O$. $\blacksquare$

(Since $OA=OB=OC$, the circle with centre $O$ through $A$ passes through all three vertices: it is the circumcircle.)

Each great meridian is a full circle through both poles, made of two half-circles from pole to pole. So $12$ great meridians give $24$ half-meridians, which cut the sphere into $24$ slices from pole to pole. The $12$ parallels cut each slice into $13$ parts. (The figure shows a globe with $3$ great meridians and $4$ parallels: $6$ slices, each cut into $5$ parts.)

