Let the third side be $x$. By the triangle inequality, $x<5+19=24$ and $x+5>19$, so $14<x<24$, and every such real $x$ gives a triangle.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(19,0), A=B+5*dir(121);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,S); label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$5$",(A+B)/2,W); label("$19$",(B+C)/2,S); label("$x$",(A+C)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

The perimeter $24+x$ therefore takes exactly the values strictly between $38$ and $48$. A whole number exceeds all of them exactly when it is at least $48$: the number $48$ works because $24+x<48$ always, while $47$ fails because $x=23.5$ gives the perimeter $47.5$. The answer is $\boxed{48}$.

\textbf{Argument (i)} looks for the \emph{smallest} perimeter, but the question needs a number above \emph{every} perimeter, so the largest possible perimeters matter. (It also assumes a whole-number side, and the final step from $39$ to $43$ has no justification.)

\textbf{Argument (ii)} reaches the right number for a wrong reason: side lengths need not be whole numbers (the third side can be $23.9$, with perimeter $47.9$), and the range $15\le x\le23$ is not the full range $14<x<24$. The correct reason is the bound $x<24$, i.e. perimeter $<48$, together with perimeters as close to $48$ as we like.

question:
Let $XYZ$ be an angle smaller than $180^\circ$, and let $P$ be a point other than $Y$ on its bisector. Let $Q$ and $R$ be the feet of the perpendiculars from $P$ to the lines $YX$ and $YZ$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $PQ=PR$ and $YQ=YR$.
\item[(b)] Prove that $PQ'>PQ$ for every point $Q'\ne Q$ on the line $YX$. (So ``$P$ is equidistant from the two sides'' refers to the feet of the perpendiculars: $PQ$ is the distance from $P$ to the line $YX$.)
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Since $\angle PYX=\angle PYZ=\dfrac12\angle XYZ<90^\circ$, the feet $Q$ and $R$ lie on the rays $YX$ and $YZ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Y=(0,0), X=(6.5,0), Z=6.5*dir(64), P=4.2*dir(32);
pair Q=(P.x,0), R=dot(P,dir(64))*dir(64), Q2=(5.6,0);
draw(Y--X,linewidth(0.9)); draw(Y--Z,linewidth(0.9)); draw(Y--P,blue);
draw(P--Q,red); draw(P--R,red); draw(P--Q2,dotted);
draw(Q+(-0.25,0)--Q+(-0.25,0.25)--Q+(0,0.25));
pair u=unit(Y-R), v=unit(P-R); draw(R+0.25*u--R+0.25*u+0.25*v--R+0.25*v);
dot(P); dot(Q); dot(R); dot(Q2);
label("$Y$",Y,SW); label("$X$",X,E); label("$Z$",Z,N); label("$P$",P,E);
label("$Q$",Q,S); label("$R$",R,NW); label("$Q'$",Q2,S);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} The triangles $YQP$ and $YRP$ have right angles at $Q$ and $R$, equal angles at $Y$ (since $YP$ is the bisector), and the common side $YP$. By AAS they are congruent, so $PQ=PR$ and $YQ=YR$. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(b)} If $Q'\ne Q$ is on the line $YX$, then $PQQ'$ is a right triangle with hypotenuse $PQ'$, so by the Pythagorean theorem $PQ'^2=PQ^2+QQ'^2>PQ^2$. $\blacksquare$

So the two points on the sides that are ``equally far'' from $P$ are not arbitrary: they are the feet $Q$ and $R$ of the perpendiculars, which are the closest points of the two sides to $P$, and they are also equally far from the vertex $Y$. Points $Q'$ on $YX$ and $R'$ on $YZ$ chosen in another way are in general not equally far from $P$.

\textbf{Tangency condition.} Let a circle have centre $(0,k)$ and radius $r$. The squared distance from $(0,k)$ to the point $\left(x,x^2\right)$ of the parabola is, with $t=x^2\ge0$,
\[x^2+\left(x^2-k\right)^2=t^2-(2k-1)t+k^2=\left(t-\left(k-\dfrac12\right)\right)^2+k-\dfrac14 .\]
For $k\ge\dfrac12$ its minimum over $t\ge0$ is $k-\dfrac14$, attained at the two points with $x^2=k-\dfrac12$. The circle lies inside the parabola and touches it exactly when this minimum distance equals the radius, i.e.
\[k=r^2+\dfrac14 .\]
\textbf{The radii.} For the first circle, $k_1-r_1=2$, so $r_1^2-r_1+\dfrac14=2$, i.e. $\left(r_1-\dfrac12\right)^2=2$ and $r_1=\dfrac12+\sqrt2$ (the radius must exceed $\dfrac12$). For consecutive circles, external tangency with both centres on the axis means $k_{n+1}-k_n=r_n+r_{n+1}$, so
\[r_{n+1}^2-r_n^2=r_{n+1}+r_n\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad r_{n+1}-r_n=1 .\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return x^2;}
draw(graph(f,-4.3,4.3),linewidth(0.9));
real r1=0.5+sqrt(2);
for(int n=0;n<2;++n){ real r=r1+n, k=r^2+0.25;
 draw(circle((0,k),r),blue); dot((0,k),blue);
 label("$r_"+string(n+1)+"$",(0,k)+(r/2,0),N,blue+fontsize(8pt)); draw((0,k)--(r,k),blue+dashed);}
dot((0,0)); label("$(0,0)$",(0,0),SE,fontsize(8pt));
draw((-1.6,0)--(-1.6,2),Arrows(3)); label("$2$",(-1.6,1),W);
draw((-1.8,0)--(0,0),dotted); draw((-1.8,2)--(0,2),dotted);
draw((-4.6,0)--(4.6,0),gray); draw((0,-0.8)--(0,18.8),gray);
\end{asy}

Thus $r_n=n-\dfrac12+\sqrt2$, and $r_{243}=\dfrac{485}{2}+\sqrt2$. The centre of the $243$rd circle is at height
\[k_{243}=r_{243}^2+\dfrac14=\dfrac{485^2}{4}+485\sqrt2+2+\dfrac14=\dfrac{235226}{4}+2+485\sqrt2=\boxed{\dfrac{117617}{2}+485\sqrt2},\]
which is the required distance, since the centre is on the $y$-axis above the vertex.

Since $\angle B$ is right, the angles at $A$ and $C$ are acute, so $D$ lies between $A$ and $C$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), A=(0,3), C=(4,0);
pair D=A+dot(B-A,C-A)/dot(C-A,C-A)*(C-A);
