question:
For a positive integer $n$, let $f(n)$ be the number obtained by writing the digits of $n$ in reverse order, dropping any leading zeros; for example $f(144)=441$, $f(1)=1$ and $f(10)=1$. Note that $n=12$ satisfies $f\left(n^2\right)=f(n)^2$, since $f(144)=441=21^2$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that infinitely many positive integers $n$ satisfy $f\left(n^2\right)=f(n)^2$.
\item[(b)] Find all positive integers $n<100$ with $f\left(n^2\right)=f(n)^2$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that a point $(x,y)$ lies on the line with slope $m$ through the point $\left(x_0,y_0\right)$ if and only if
\[y-y_0=m\left(x-x_0\right).\]
\item[(b)] Find the equation of the line with slope $-\dfrac23$ through the point $(3,5)$, in the form $y=mx+b$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The line is not vertical (it has a slope). The point $\left(x_0,y_0\right)$ is on the line and satisfies the equation. A point $(x,y)$ with $x=x_0$ and $y\ne y_0$ is not on the line and does not satisfy the equation. Any other point $(x,y)$ has $x\ne x_0$, and it lies on the line exactly when the slope from $\left(x_0,y_0\right)$ to $(x,y)$ is $m$:
\[\dfrac{y-y_0}{x-x_0}=m\iff y-y_0=m\left(x-x_0\right).\]
So the line is exactly the set of solutions of $y-y_0=m\left(x-x_0\right)$ (the \emph{point-slope form}). $\blacksquare$

