Let $O$ be the centre of the large disc. A disc of radius $3$ with centre $X$ lies strictly inside it exactly when $OX<5$. So Ben's centre $P$ is uniform in the open disc of radius $5$ about $O$, and you need a centre $Q$ with $OQ<5$ and $PQ\ge6$ (two discs of radius $3$ do not overlap exactly when their centres are at least $6$ apart).

If $OP>1$, choose $Q$ on the ray from $P$ through $O$, beyond $O$, with $\max(0,6-OP)<OQ<5$; then $PQ=OP+OQ\ge6$, and you win. If $OP\le1$, then for every allowed $Q$, $PQ\le PO+OQ<1+5=6$, and you lose.

