question:
Let $f(x)=ax^2+bx+c$ with $a\ne0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the graph of $y=-f(x)$ is the reflection of the graph of $y=f(x)$ in the $x$-axis, so that one parabola opens upward exactly when the other opens downward.
\item[(b)] Show that the equations $f(x)=0$ and $-f(x)=0$ have the same solutions.
\item[(c)] For $f(x)=x^2-2x-3$, find the roots and the vertices of both parabolas $y=f(x)$ and $y=-f(x)$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The reflection in the $x$-axis sends $(x,y)$ to $(x,-y)$. A point $(x,y)$ is on the graph of $f$ exactly when $y=f(x)$, i.e. when $-y=-f(x)$, i.e. when $(x,-y)$ is on the graph of $-f$. So the reflection maps one graph onto the other. The leading coefficients are $a$ and $-a$, which have opposite signs, so one parabola opens upward and the other downward.

\textbf{(b)} $f(x)=0$ holds exactly when $-f(x)=0$ (multiply or divide by $-1$). So multiplying an \emph{equation} by $-1$ does not change its solutions, even though it flips the \emph{graph} of $y=f(x)$.

\textbf{(c)} $f(x)=x^2-2x-3=(x-3)(x+1)=(x-1)^2-4$. Both parabolas cross the $x$-axis at $x=-1$ and $x=3$. The vertex of $y=f(x)$ is $(1,-4)$, and the vertex of $y=-f(x)=-(x-1)^2+4$ is its mirror image $(1,4)$.

