label("$y=f(x)$",(3.9,f(3.9)),E,blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$y=-f(x)$",(3.9,g(3.9)),E,red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

question:
Bill has a plus-sign-shaped figure made of nine unit squares: a central unit square with an arm of two unit squares attached to each of its four sides, as shown.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(2.2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=-2;i<=2;++i){ draw(shift(i-0.5,-0.5)*unitsquare,linewidth(0.8)); if(i!=0) draw(shift(-0.5,i-0.5)*unitsquare,linewidth(0.8)); }
\end{asy}

He wishes to place the figure, possibly rotated, inside a $7$ by $7$ square. Find the area of the region consisting of all points $P$ such that there is a placement of the figure inside the square with the centre of the ``$+$'' at $P$.

$\textbf{(A) }4\qquad\textbf{(B) }67-42\sqrt2\qquad\textbf{(C) }9\qquad\textbf{(D) }\dfrac{123-70\sqrt2}{2}\qquad\textbf{(E) }\dfrac{107-42\sqrt2}{2}$

solution:
Centre the figure at the origin: it is the union of the rectangles $\left[-\dfrac52,\dfrac52\right]\times\left[-\dfrac12,\dfrac12\right]$ and $\left[-\dfrac12,\dfrac12\right]\times\left[-\dfrac52,\dfrac52\right]$. A rectangle with half-sides $p$ and $q$, turned through an angle $\theta$, reaches a horizontal distance $p\left|\cos\theta\right|+q\left|\sin\theta\right|$ from its centre. So the figure turned through $\theta$ reaches horizontally to the distance
\[w(\theta)=\max\left(\dfrac52\left|\cos\theta\right|+\dfrac12\left|\sin\theta\right|,\ \dfrac12\left|\cos\theta\right|+\dfrac52\left|\sin\theta\right|\right)\]
on both sides of its centre, and, because the figure is unchanged by a quarter turn, also vertically up and down.

Put the square at $[0,7]\times[0,7]$. The figure turned through $\theta$ with centre $(x,y)$ fits exactly when $w(\theta)\le x\le7-w(\theta)$ and $w(\theta)\le y\le7-w(\theta)$. For each $\theta$ these centres form a square of side $7-2w(\theta)$ centred at $\left(\dfrac72,\dfrac72\right)$. These squares are nested, so the region is the largest of them, the one with the smallest $w(\theta)$.

By symmetry it suffices to take $0\le\theta\le45^\circ$, where $\cos\theta\ge\sin\theta$ and $w(\theta)=\dfrac52\cos\theta+\dfrac12\sin\theta=\dfrac{\sqrt{26}}{2}\cos(\theta-\alpha)$ with $\tan\alpha=\dfrac15$. This increases on $[0,\alpha]$ and decreases on $[\alpha,45^\circ]$, so its minimum is at an endpoint:
\[w(0)=\dfrac52,\qquad w(45^\circ)=\dfrac{3}{\sqrt2}=\dfrac{3\sqrt2}{2}\approx2.12.\]
So the smallest reach is $\dfrac{3\sqrt2}{2}$, attained for the figure turned through $45^\circ$, and the region is a square of side $7-3\sqrt2$ (shaded below; the figure is drawn with its centre $P$ at a corner of the region).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real w=3/sqrt(2);
draw(box((0,0),(7,7)),linewidth(1));
filldraw(box((w,w),(7-w,7-w)),lightblue+opacity(0.5),blue+dashed);
path pl=(2.5,0.5)--(0.5,0.5)--(0.5,2.5)--(-0.5,2.5)--(-0.5,0.5)--(-2.5,0.5)--(-2.5,-0.5)--(-0.5,-0.5)--(-0.5,-2.5)--(0.5,-2.5)--(0.5,-0.5)--(2.5,-0.5)--cycle;
draw(shift(w,w)*rotate(45)*pl,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot((w,w),red); label("$P$",(w,w),NW,red);
draw((w,w)--(w,-0.7),dotted); draw((0,-0.7)--(w,-0.7),Arrows(3)); label("$\dfrac{3\sqrt2}{2}$",(w/2,-0.7),S,fontsize(7pt));
label("$7$",(4.6,0),S); label("$7$",(7,3.5),E);
\end{asy}

Its area is
\[\left(7-3\sqrt2\right)^2=49-42\sqrt2+18=\boxed{\textbf{(B) }67-42\sqrt2}.\]

Let $d=OP$. The bad points are those with $2020\le OQ<2021$ (the shaded ring). As $Q$ runs around the circle of radius $r$ about $P$, the distance $OQ$ takes exactly the values from $\left|r-d\right|$ to $r+d$ (the extremes occur on the line $OP$). So the condition fails for a given $r$ exactly when
\[2020\le\left|r-d\right|\quad\text{and}\quad r+d<2021.\]
\textbf{$d<\dfrac12$ fails:} take $r=2020+d$; then $\left|r-d\right|=2020$ and $r+d=2020+2d<2021$, so every point at distance $r$ from $P$ is bad.

\textbf{$d=\dfrac12$ works:} if $r<\dfrac12$, then $\left|r-d\right|<\dfrac12<2020$; if $r\ge\dfrac12$, the two conditions would give $2020\le r-\dfrac12$ and $r+\dfrac12<2021$, i.e. $2020.5\le r<2020.5$, which is impossible. So for every $r$ there is a good point.

