\end{asy}

By the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle $GCH$,
\[GH=\sqrt{20^2+21^2}=\sqrt{841}=\boxed{29}.\]

\textbf{$F$ is the midpoint of $AM$.} Since $DE=EC$, $E$ is the midpoint of $DC$, and $M$ is the midpoint of $BC$. By the midline theorem in triangle $CDB$, $ME\parallel BD$ and $ME=\dfrac12BD$. Now in triangle $AME$, the segment $DF$ lies on $BD$, so $DF\parallel EM$, and $D$ is the midpoint of $AE$ (as $AD=DE$). Hence $F$ is the midpoint of $AM$, and $DF=\dfrac12EM=\dfrac14BD$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.2,3.6), B=(0,0), C=(5.2,0);
pair M=(B+C)/2, Dd=A+(C-A)/3, Ee=A+2*(C-A)/3;
pair F=extension(B,Dd,A,M), G=extension(B,Ee,A,M);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--M,blue); draw(B--Dd); draw(B--Ee);
draw(M--Ee,red+dashed);
dot(M); dot(Dd); dot(Ee); dot(F); dot(G);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$M$",M,S);
label("$D$",Dd,NE); label("$E$",Ee,NE); label("$F$",F,W); label("$G$",G,dir(200));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Where $G$ cuts $FM$.} We have $FB=BD-DF=\dfrac34BD$. Since $FB\parallel ME$, the triangles $GFB$ and $GME$ are similar (vertical angles at $G$ and alternate angles), so
\[\dfrac{FG}{GM}=\dfrac{FB}{ME}=\dfrac{\dfrac34BD}{\dfrac12BD}=\dfrac32 .\]
\textbf{Conclusion.} Taking $AM=10$: $AF=FM=5$, and $FM$ is split as $FG=3$, $GM=2$. Hence
\[AF:FG:GM=\boxed{5:3:2}.\]

Let $P=AD\cap BE$, $Q=BE\cap CF$, $R=CF\cap AD$. The conditions say that $P$, $Q$, $R$ are the midpoints of $AD$, $BE$, $CF$. Let
\[x=\dfrac{BD}{BC},\qquad y=\dfrac{CE}{CA},\qquad z=\dfrac{AF}{AB}.\]
\textbf{The ratios.} By Menelaus' theorem in triangle $ADC$ with the line $BPE$ (which meets $DC$ extended at $B$),
\[\dfrac{AP}{PD}\cdot\dfrac{DB}{BC}\cdot\dfrac{CE}{EA}=1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad x\cdot\dfrac{y}{1-y}=1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad y=\dfrac1{1+x}.\]
In the same way, triangle $BEA$ with the line $CQF$ gives $z=\dfrac{1}{1+y}$, and triangle $CFB$ with the line $ARD$ gives $x=\dfrac1{1+z}$. With $h(t)=\dfrac1{1+t}$ we get $x=h(h(h(x)))$. Since $h$ is decreasing for $t>0$, so is $h\circ h\circ h$, which therefore has at most one fixed point; the fixed point of $h$ itself, the positive root of $t^2+t-1=0$, is one. Hence
\[x=y=z=\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{2}.\]
\textbf{The area.} Since $P$ is the midpoint of $AD$, $[ABP]=\dfrac12[ABD]=\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac{BD}{BC}[ABC]=\dfrac x2$. Likewise $[BCQ]=\dfrac12[BCE]=\dfrac y2$ and $[CAR]=\dfrac12[CAF]=\dfrac z2$. The triangles $ABP$, $BCQ$, $CAR$ and $PQR$ fill triangle $ABC$ without overlapping (see the figure, where $PQR$ is shaded), so
\[[PQR]=1-\dfrac{x+y+z}{2}=1-\dfrac{3\left(\sqrt5-1\right)}{4}=\boxed{\dfrac{7-3\sqrt5}{4}}\approx0.073.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.3,3.8), B=(0,0), C=(5.2,0);
real t=(sqrt(5)-1)/2;
pair Dd=B+t*(C-B), Ee=C+t*(A-C), F=A+t*(B-A);
pair P=(A+Dd)/2, Q=(B+Ee)/2, R=(C+F)/2;
filldraw(P--Q--R--cycle,orange+opacity(0.7),red);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--Dd,blue); draw(B--Ee,blue); draw(C--F,blue);
dot(Dd); dot(Ee); dot(F); dot(P); dot(Q); dot(R);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$D$",Dd,S); label("$E$",Ee,NE); label("$F$",F,W);
label("$P$",P,W,fontsize(8pt)); label("$Q$",Q,S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$R$",R,NE,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} From the given ratios,
\[BD=\dfrac{r}{r+1}BC,\ DC=\dfrac{1}{r+1}BC,\quad CE=\dfrac{r}{r+1}CA,\ EA=\dfrac1{r+1}CA,\quad AF=\dfrac{r}{r+1}AB,\ FB=\dfrac1{r+1}AB.\]
Two triangles with a common angle have areas in the ratio of the products of the sides enclosing that angle. So the corner triangles (grey in the figure, drawn for $r=2$) satisfy
\[\dfrac{[AFE]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac{AF}{AB}\cdot\dfrac{AE}{AC}=\dfrac{r}{(r+1)^2},\quad\dfrac{[BDF]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac{BD}{BC}\cdot\dfrac{BF}{BA}=\dfrac{r}{(r+1)^2},\quad\dfrac{[CED]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac{CE}{CA}\cdot\dfrac{CD}{CB}=\dfrac{r}{(r+1)^2}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.3,3.8), B=(0,0), C=(5.2,0);
real r=2;
pair Dd=B+r/(r+1)*(C-B), Ee=C+r/(r+1)*(A-C), F=A+r/(r+1)*(B-A);
fill(A--F--Ee--cycle,lightgray); fill(B--Dd--F--cycle,lightgray); fill(C--Ee--Dd--cycle,lightgray);
filldraw(Dd--Ee--F--cycle,lightblue+opacity(0.7),blue);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(Dd); dot(Ee); dot(F);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$D$",Dd,S); label("$E$",Ee,NE); label("$F$",F,W);
\end{asy}

Triangle $DEF$ is what remains of $ABC$ after removing the three corner triangles, so
\[\dfrac{[DEF]}{[ABC]}=1-\dfrac{3r}{(r+1)^2}=\dfrac{r^2+2r+1-3r}{(r+1)^2}=\dfrac{r^2-r+1}{(r+1)^2}.\qquad\blacksquare\]
(For $r=1$ this gives $\dfrac14$, the medial triangle; for $r=2$ it gives $\dfrac13$.)

\textbf{(b)} $r^2-r+1=\left(r-\dfrac12\right)^2+\dfrac34>0$ for every real $r$, and $(r+1)^2>0$ for $r\ne-1$. So the fraction is always positive and the absolute value bars change nothing. (They appear in versions of the theorem that allow signed ratios, i.e. points on the extensions of the sides, where $r$ may be negative.)

An equilateral triangle with side $s$ has height $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s$ (Pythagorean theorem in half of it), so its area is $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(2,0), C=(1,sqrt(3));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(C--(1,0),dashed); draw((1,0)+(0.08,0)--(1,0)+(0.08,0.08)--(1,0)+(0,0.08));
label("$s$",(A+B)/2+(0.45,0),S); label("$s$",(A+C)/2,NW); label("$s$",(B+C)/2,NE);
label("$\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s$",(1,0.75),E,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

From $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2=5^7$,
\[s^2=\dfrac{4\cdot5^7}{\sqrt3},\qquad s=\dfrac{2\cdot5^3\sqrt5}{\sqrt[4]{3}}=\boxed{\dfrac{250\sqrt5}{\sqrt[4]3}=\dfrac{250}{3}\sqrt[4]{675}}\approx424.8 .\]
(For the last form, $\dfrac{\sqrt5}{\sqrt[4]3}=\sqrt[4]{\dfrac{25}{3}}=\dfrac{\sqrt[4]{25\cdot27}}{3}$.)

Two triangles with a common angle have areas in the ratio of the products of the sides enclosing that angle. Here $\dfrac{AF}{AB}=\dfrac34$, $\dfrac{AE}{AC}=\dfrac14$, and similarly at $B$ and $C$, so each corner triangle (grey) has area
\[[AFE]=\dfrac34\cdot\dfrac14\cdot16=3,\qquad[BDF]=\dfrac34\cdot\dfrac14\cdot16=3,\qquad[CED]=\dfrac34\cdot\dfrac14\cdot16=3.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.3,3.8), B=(0,0), C=(5.2,0);
pair Dd=B+0.75*(C-B), Ee=C+0.75*(A-C), F=A+0.75*(B-A);
fill(A--F--Ee--cycle,lightgray); fill(B--Dd--F--cycle,lightgray); fill(C--Ee--Dd--cycle,lightgray);
filldraw(Dd--Ee--F--cycle,lightblue+opacity(0.7),blue);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(Dd); dot(Ee); dot(F);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$D$",Dd,S); label("$E$",Ee,NE); label("$F$",F,W);
label("$3$",(A+F)/2,W,fontsize(8pt)); label("$1$",(F+B)/2,W,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Therefore $[DEF]=16-3\cdot3=\boxed{7}$. (This agrees with the general formula $\dfrac{[DEF]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac{r^2-r+1}{(r+1)^2}=\dfrac{7}{16}$ for $r=3$.)

The solid is a cone of radius $6$ and height $10$ with a hemisphere of radius $6$ on its base.

