\[V_{\text{hemisphere}}=\dfrac12\cdot\dfrac43\pi\cdot6^3=144\pi,\qquad V_{\text{cone}}=\dfrac13\pi\cdot6^2\cdot10=120\pi,\]
so the total volume is $144\pi+120\pi=\boxed{264\pi}\approx829.4$ cubic inches.

Let the side be $s$. The altitude $CH$ meets the opposite side at its midpoint, so the right triangle $AHC$ has legs $\dfrac s2$ and $6\sqrt3$ and hypotenuse $s$. It is a $30^\circ$-$60^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle, so the altitude is $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s$, and $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s=6\sqrt3$ gives $s=12$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(12,0), C=(6,6*sqrt(3)), H=(6,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(C--H,dashed);
draw(H+(0.5,0)--H+(0.5,0.5)--H+(0,0.5));
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N); label("$H$",H,S);
label("$6\sqrt3$",(6,2.6),W); label("$6$",(3,0),S); label("$12$",(A+C)/2,NW);
\end{asy}

The area is $\dfrac12\cdot12\cdot6\sqrt3=\boxed{36\sqrt3}$ square metres $\approx62.4$ m$^2$.

If each small circle has radius $r$, the large diameter is $2r+2r=4r$, so the large radius is $R=2r$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),2),linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle((-1,0),1),blue); draw(circle((1,0),1),blue);
draw((-2,0)--(2,0),dashed);
dot((0,0)); dot((-1,0),blue); dot((1,0),blue);
label("$r$",(-1.5,0),N,blue); label("$r$",(-0.5,0),N,blue); label("$R=2r$",(0,-2),S);
\end{asy}

The ratio of the areas is
\[\dfrac{\pi r^2}{\pi(2r)^2}=\boxed{\dfrac14}.\]

Let $x=\dfrac{n^2-1}{2}$. Since $n$ is odd, $n^2$ is odd and $x$ is an integer, and $x\ge\dfrac{9-1}2=4$. Then
\[n^2+x^2=(2x+1)+x^2=(x+1)^2 .\]
The lengths $n$, $x$, $x+1$ form a triangle, because the largest one, $x+1$, is less than $n+x$ (as $n>1$). By the converse of the Pythagorean theorem the triangle has a right angle opposite the side $x+1$. $\blacksquare$

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), A=(4,0), B=(0,3);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0.2,0)--(0.2,0.2)--(0,0.2));
label("$n$",(B+C)/2,W); label("$x=\dfrac{n^2-1}{2}$",(A+C)/2,S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$x+1$",(A+B)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

For example, $n=3,5,7$ give the triangles $3,4,5$; $5,12,13$; $7,24,25$.

question:
Let $ABCD$ be a trapezoid with $AB\parallel CD$, and let $E$ be the intersection of its diagonals $AC$ and $BD$. The product of the areas of triangles $ABE$ and $CDE$ is $67$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the areas of triangles $ADE$ and $BCE$.
\item[(b)] Show that the area of $ABCD$ is not determined by this information.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Write $[XYZ]$ for the area of triangle $XYZ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(7,0), C=(5,3), D=(1,3);
pair X=extension(A,C,B,D);
fill(A--B--X--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1)); fill(C--D--X--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
fill(A--X--D--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.85)); fill(B--C--X--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.85));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--C); draw(B--D);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(X);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,NW); label("$E$",X,2*S);
label("$[ABE]$",(A+B+X)/3+(0,-0.25),fontsize(8pt)); label("$[CDE]$",(C+D+X)/3+(0,0.15),fontsize(8pt));
label("$[ADE]$",(A+D+X)/3+(0.12,0.05),fontsize(8pt)); label("$[BCE]$",(B+C+X)/3+(-0.12,0.05),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} Triangles $ABD$ and $ABC$ have the same base $AB$ and the same height (the distance between the parallel lines $AB$ and $CD$), so $[ABD]=[ABC]$. Removing the common part $ABE$ gives $[ADE]=[BCE]$.

Triangles $ADE$ and $ABE$ have the same height from $A$ to the line $BD$, so $\dfrac{[ADE]}{[ABE]}=\dfrac{DE}{EB}$. In the same way, with the height from $C$, $\dfrac{[CDE]}{[CBE]}=\dfrac{DE}{EB}$. Hence
\[\dfrac{[ADE]}{[ABE]}=\dfrac{[CDE]}{[BCE]}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad[ADE]\cdot[BCE]=[ABE]\cdot[CDE]=67.\]
Since $[ADE]=[BCE]$, each of them is $\boxed{\sqrt{67}}$.

\textbf{(b)} Let $r=\dfrac{AB}{CD}$. Triangles $ABE$ and $CDE$ are similar with ratio $r$ (vertical angles at $E$ and alternate angles), so $[ABE]=r^2[CDE]$, and by (a) $[ADE]=\dfrac{DE}{EB}[ABE]=\dfrac1r[ABE]=r[CDE]$. With $[ADE]=\sqrt{67}$ this gives $[CDE]=\dfrac{\sqrt{67}}{r}$ and $[ABE]=r\sqrt{67}$, so
\[[ABCD]=\sqrt{67}\left(r+2+\dfrac1r\right).\]
Every trapezoid with any ratio $r$ can be scaled so that $[ADE]=\sqrt{67}$, and then the product $[ABE]\cdot[CDE]$ is $67$; but the total area changes with $r$: for $r=2$ it is $\dfrac92\sqrt{67}$, and for $r=3$ it is $\dfrac{16}{3}\sqrt{67}$. So the area of $ABCD$ is not determined (it can be any number greater than $4\sqrt{67}$, the value for $r=1$ that a trapezoid with unequal bases never reaches).

Moving a line parallel to itself changes neither its slope nor the angles it makes with other lines, so we may assume both lines pass through the origin $O$: they are $y=m_1x$ and $y=m_2x$. If $m_1=m_2$ the lines coincide, they are not perpendicular, and $m_1m_2=m_1^2\ne-1$; so assume $m_1\ne m_2$. The points $P=(1,m_1)$ and $Q=(1,m_2)$ lie on the two lines and are different from $O$ and from each other.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0), P=(1,2), Q=(1,-0.5);
draw((-1.2,0)--(2,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-1.2)--(0,2.6),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(2,0),E); label("$y$",(0,2.6),N);
draw((-0.6,-1.2)--(1.3,2.6),blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw((-1.2,0.6)--(2,-1),red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--Q,dashed);
pair u=unit(P), v=unit(Q);
draw(0.25*u--0.25*(u+v)--0.25*v);
dot(O); dot(P); dot(Q);
label("$O$",O+0.3*dir(207)); label("$P(1,m_1)$",P,E); label("$Q(1,m_2)$",Q,NE);
label("$y=m_1x$",(1.3,2.6),E,blue); label("$y=m_2x$",(2,-1),E,red);
\end{asy}

The lines are perpendicular exactly when $\angle POQ=90^\circ$, which by the Pythagorean theorem and its converse happens exactly when $OP^2+OQ^2=PQ^2$. By the distance formula,
\[OP^2=1+m_1^2,\qquad OQ^2=1+m_2^2,\qquad PQ^2=(m_1-m_2)^2.\]
So the condition is
\[2+m_1^2+m_2^2=m_1^2-2m_1m_2+m_2^2\iff2=-2m_1m_2\iff m_1m_2=-1.\]
Hence the lines are perpendicular if and only if $m_1m_2=-1$, that is, if and only if each slope is the negative reciprocal of the other. $\blacksquare$

Let $C_1$ have centre $O_1$ and radius $r_1$, and $C_2$ have centre $O_2$ and radius $r_2$. First translate the plane by the vector $\overrightarrow{O_1O_2}$; this moves $C_1$ to the circle with centre $O_2$ and radius $r_1$ (dashed). Then apply the dilation with centre $O_2$ and factor $k=\dfrac{r_2}{r_1}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O1=(0,0), O2=(4.2,0); real r1=0.8, r2=1.6;
draw(circle(O1,r1),linewidth(0.9)); draw(circle(O2,r2),linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O2,r1),dashed+gray);
pair P=O1+r1*dir(50), P2=O2+r1*dir(50), P3=O2+r2*dir(50);
draw(O1--P,blue); draw(O2--P3,red); draw(O1--O2,dotted);
dot(O1); dot(O2); dot(P); dot(P3); dot(P2,gray);
label("$O_1$",O1,S); label("$O_2$",O2,S); label("$P$",P,NE); label("$P'$",P3,NE);
label("$r_1$",(O1+P)/2,NW,blue); label("$r_2$",O2+1.3*dir(50),dir(140),red);
label("$C_1$",O1+r1*dir(200),W); label("$C_2$",O2+r2*dir(330),SE);
\end{asy}

Together, a point $P$ goes to
\[P'=O_2+\dfrac{r_2}{r_1}\left(P-O_1\right).\]
If $P$ is on $C_1$, then $O_2P'=\dfrac{r_2}{r_1}\cdot O_1P=\dfrac{r_2}{r_1}\cdot r_1=r_2$, so $P'$ is on $C_2$. Conversely, every point $X$ of $C_2$ is the image of the point $P=O_1+\dfrac{r_1}{r_2}\left(X-O_2\right)$, which is at distance $r_1$ from $O_1$, so it is on $C_1$. Hence the map sends $C_1$ exactly onto $C_2$. A translation preserves distances and a dilation multiplies all distances by $k$, so the composition is a similarity. $\blacksquare$

In a (non-degenerate) triangle each side is shorter than the sum of the other two (triangle inequality, strict). Here the longest side is $2n+1$, and
\[n+(n+1)=2n+1,\]
which is not greater than $2n+1$. So the strict triangle inequality fails, and no triangle has these side lengths. $\blacksquare$

What happens instead: three points $X$, $Y$, $Z$ with $XY=n$, $YZ=n+1$ and $XZ=2n+1$ must lie on one line, with $Y$ between $X$ and $Z$, because $XY+YZ=XZ$ is the equality case of the triangle inequality. The ``triangle'' collapses to a segment.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair X=(0,0), Y=(2,0), Z=(5,0);
draw(X--Z,linewidth(0.9));
dot(X); dot(Y); dot(Z);
label("$X$",X,W); label("$Y$",Y,S); label("$Z$",Z,E);
draw(X{dir(40)}..{dir(-40)}Y,blue,Arrows(5)); label("$n$",(1,0.45),N,blue);
draw(Y{dir(40)}..{dir(-40)}Z,red,Arrows(5)); label("$n+1$",(3.5,0.6),N,red);
draw(X{dir(-35)}..{dir(35)}Z,dashed,Arrows(5)); label("$2n+1$",(2.5,-0.85),S);
\end{asy}

question:
Let $x\ge y\ge0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find $a$ and $b$ in terms of $x$ and $y$ such that $\sqrt{a-2\sqrt b}=\sqrt x-\sqrt y$.
\item[(b)] Conversely, given $a\ge0$ and $b\ge0$ with $a^2\ge4b$, find $x\ge y\ge0$ in terms of $a$ and $b$ with $\sqrt{a-2\sqrt b}=\sqrt x-\sqrt y$.
\item[(c)] Use this to simplify $\sqrt{7-2\sqrt{10}}$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{If $mn$ is odd, it is impossible.} Colour the grid like a chessboard. Each move goes to a square of the other colour, so along a closed route the colours alternate and the route has an even number of moves. A route that visits each of the $mn$ squares exactly once and returns to the start consists of exactly $mn$ moves. So $mn$ must be even.

\textbf{If $mn$ is even, it is possible.} Say the number of rows $m$ is even (otherwise $n$ is even, and we turn the grid on its side). Number the rows $1$ to $m$ from the top and the columns $1$ to $n$ from the left, and let the ant start in row $1$, column $1$.
\begin{itemize}
\item Go along row $1$ to column $n$.
\item Snake through rows $2,3,\dots,m$, using only columns $2$ to $n$: row $2$ from column $n$ back to column $2$, row $3$ from column $2$ to column $n$, and so on. Since $m$ is even, row $m$ is traversed from column $n$ to column $2$.
\item Step left to row $m$, column $1$, and go up column $1$ to row $2$; the next step returns to the start.
\end{itemize}
Row $1$ is covered by the first step, columns $2$ to $n$ of the other rows by the snake, and column $1$ of the other rows on the way back, each square exactly once, and every step goes to a neighbouring square. The figure shows the route for $m=4$, $n=5$ (the small grey squares mark one colour of the chessboard colouring).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int m=4, n=5;
for(int i=0;i<=n;++i) draw((i,0)--(i,m),gray);
for(int j=0;j<=m;++j) draw((0,j)--(n,j),gray);
pair c(int row,int col){return (col-0.5,m-row+0.5);}
path p=c(1,1);
for(int col=2;col<=n;++col) p=p--c(1,col);
for(int row=2;row<=m;++row){
  if(row%2==0) for(int col=n;col>=2;--col) p=p--c(row,col);
  else for(int col=2;col<=n;++col) p=p--c(row,col);
}
for(int row=m;row>=2;--row) p=p--c(row,1);
p=p--cycle;
draw(p,blue+linewidth(1.1));
dot(c(1,1),red+5); label("start",c(1,1),N,red+fontsize(8pt));
for(int row=1;row<=m;++row) for(int col=1;col<=n;++col) if((row+col)%2==1) fill(shift(col-0.95,m-row+0.05)*scale(0.18)*unitsquare,gray);
\end{asy}

Hence such a route exists if and only if $mn$ is even. $\blacksquare$

Two circles meet in at most $2$ points, two lines in at most $1$ point, and a line and a circle in at most $2$ points. Counting pairs, the number of intersection points is at most
\[2\dbinom52+1\cdot\dbinom42+2\cdot5\cdot4=20+6+40=\boxed{66}.\]
The figure shows the same count for $2$ circles and $2$ lines: $2+1+8=11$ points.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair c1=(0,0), c2=(1.3,0.3); real r1=1.4, r2=1.1;
path C1=circle(c1,r1), C2=circle(c2,r2);
path L1=(-2.2,-0.9)--(3.2,0.99), L2=(-1.8,2.1)--(2.9,-1.425);
draw(C1,linewidth(0.9)); draw(C2,linewidth(0.9)); draw(L1,blue); draw(L2,red);
pair[] P;
P.append(intersectionpoints(C1,C2)); P.append(intersectionpoints(L1,L2));
P.append(intersectionpoints(C1,L1)); P.append(intersectionpoints(C1,L2));
P.append(intersectionpoints(C2,L1)); P.append(intersectionpoints(C2,L2));
for(int i=0;i<P.length;++i) dot(P[i],linewidth(4));
\end{asy}

The bound $66$ is attained. Take five circles of radius $1$ whose centres are distinct points on a short segment; any two of them meet in two points. Choose the centres so that no three circles pass through a common point (possible, since each of the finitely many intersection points rules out only finitely many positions for a further centre). Then draw four lines through the region common to all five discs, so each line crosses each circle twice; choose them with different slopes, no three through a point, and avoiding all previously found intersection points (again only finitely many directions or positions are excluded each time). Then all $66$ intersection points are distinct.

In each round, ``three heads'' ($H$) has probability $p=\dfrac18$ and ``a six'' ($X$) has probability $q=\dfrac16$, independently. What matters after a round is which of $H$, $X$ happened in it. Let $u$, $a$, $b$, $c$ be the probabilities that the coins win, starting after a round with neither event, with $H$ only, with $X$ only, and with both, respectively. (The start is like ``neither''.) The four outcomes of a round have probabilities
\[\Pr(H\text{ only})=\dfrac{5}{48},\quad\Pr(X\text{ only})=\dfrac{7}{48},\quad\Pr(\text{both})=\dfrac1{48},\quad\Pr(\text{neither})=\dfrac{35}{48}.\]
Conditioning on the next round:
\begin{itemize}
\item after neither: $u=\dfrac5{48}a+\dfrac7{48}b+\dfrac1{48}c+\dfrac{35}{48}u$;
\item after $H$ only: an $H$ wins at once (a six cannot complete a pair yet), so $a=\dfrac18+\dfrac7{48}b+\dfrac{35}{48}u$;
\item after $X$ only: an $X$ loses at once, so $b=\dfrac5{48}a+\dfrac{35}{48}u$;
\item after both: $H$ without $X$ wins, $X$ loses (with $H$ it is a tie, which is not a win), so $c=\dfrac{5}{48}+\dfrac{35}{48}u$.
\end{itemize}
Substituting $b$ into the equation for $a$ gives $\dfrac{2269}{2304}a=\dfrac{288+1925u}{2304}$, so $a=\dfrac{288+1925u}{2269}$ and then $b=\dfrac{30+1855u}{2269}$. The first equation reads $13u=5a+7b+c$; multiplying by $2269\cdot48$,
\[1415856u=48(1650+22610u)+2269(5+35u)=90545+1164695u,\]
so $251161u=90545$ and
\[u=\boxed{\dfrac{90545}{251161}}\approx0.3605.\]
(A tie, with both pairs completed in the same round, has probability $\dfrac{2269}{251161}$, and the die wins first with probability $\dfrac{158347}{251161}$.)

First count the patterns of letters $AAAABBCCC$ with no $B$ next to a $C$. Place the four $A$'s; they create $5$ gaps (including the two ends). Inside a gap the letters are consecutive, so a gap may not contain both a $B$ and a $C$: each gap holds only $B$'s, only $C$'s, or nothing. Choose $k$ gaps for the $B$'s and $l$ other gaps for the $C$'s, and split the $2$ $B$'s into $k$ nonempty groups ($\dbinom{1}{k-1}$ ways) and the $3$ $C$'s into $l$ nonempty groups ($\dbinom{2}{l-1}$ ways):
\begin{itemize}
\item $k=1$: $5$ choices of the $B$-gap, then $\dbinom41\cdot1+\dbinom42\cdot2+\dbinom43\cdot1=4+12+4=20$, giving $100$;
\item $k=2$: $\dbinom52=10$ choices of the $B$-gaps, then $\dbinom31\cdot1+\dbinom32\cdot2+\dbinom33\cdot1=3+6+1=10$, giving $100$.
\end{itemize}
So there are $200$ patterns. The two $B$ books can be ordered in $2!$ ways and the three $C$ books in $3!$ ways, so the number of arrangements is
\[200\cdot2\cdot6=\boxed{2400}.\]

