\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] A $45^\circ$-$45^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle has legs of length $a$. Find its hypotenuse.
\item[(b)] A $30^\circ$-$60^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle has shorter leg $a$ (opposite the $30^\circ$ angle). Prove that its hypotenuse is $2a$ and its longer leg is $a\sqrt3$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The two legs are equal (the base angles are equal), so by the Pythagorean theorem the hypotenuse $h$ satisfies $h^2=a^2+a^2=2a^2$, and $h=\boxed{a\sqrt2}$.

\textbf{(b)} Let $PQR$ have the right angle at $P$, $\angle Q=30^\circ$, $\angle R=60^\circ$ and $PR=a$. Reflect $R$ in the line $PQ$ to $R'$ (dashed), so $PR'=a$ and $R,P,R'$ are collinear.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(2.2,0), C=(0,2.2);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw((0.2,0)--(0.2,0.2)--(0,0.2));
label("$a$",(1.1,0),S); label("$a$",(0,1.1),W); label("$a\sqrt2$",(1.1,1.1),NE);
label("$45^\circ$",B+(-0.55,0.15),fontsize(8pt)); label("$45^\circ$",C+(0.17,-0.6),fontsize(8pt));
pair P=(4.2,0), Q=(4.2+2*sqrt(3),0), R=(4.2,2), R2=(4.2,-2);
draw(P--Q--R--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(P--R2--Q,dashed);
draw(P+(0.2,0)--P+(0.2,0.2)--P+(0,0.2));
label("$a$",(P+R)/2,W); label("$a$",(P+R2)/2,W); label("$a\sqrt3$",(P+Q)/2+(-0.3,0),S); label("$2a$",(Q+R)/2,NE);
label("$30^\circ$",Q+(-0.85,0.2),fontsize(8pt)); label("$60^\circ$",R+(0.2,-0.45),fontsize(8pt));
label("$R$",R,N); label("$P$",P,W); label("$Q$",Q,E); label("$R'$",R2,S);
\end{asy}

Triangle $RQR'$ has $\angle R=60^\circ$ and, by symmetry, $\angle R'=60^\circ$, so it is equilateral. Its side is $RR'=2a$, so the hypotenuse is $QR=\boxed{2a}$. By the Pythagorean theorem, the longer leg is $PQ=\sqrt{(2a)^2-a^2}=\sqrt{3a^2}=\boxed{a\sqrt3}$.

So the sides of a $45$-$45$-$90$ triangle are in the ratio $1:1:\sqrt2$, and those of a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle in the ratio $1:\sqrt3:2$. $\blacksquare$

For every $x$, $|2x+5|\ge0$ and $-|3x-2|\le0$. So the graphs can meet only where both values are $0$, which would need $2x+5=0$ and $3x-2=0$ at the same time, i.e. $x=-\dfrac52$ and $x=\dfrac23$. That is impossible.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return abs(2x+5);} real g(real x){return -abs(3x-2);}
draw((-5,0)--(3,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-5)--(0,5),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(3,0),E); label("$y$",(0,5),N);
draw(graph(f,-4.8,-0.3),blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(graph(g,-0.3,2.3),red+linewidth(0.9));
dot((-2.5,0),blue); dot((2/3,0),red);
label("$y=|2x+5|$",(-4.6,4.4),E,blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$y=-|3x-2|$",(1.6,-2.2),E,red+fontsize(8pt));
label("$-\dfrac52$",(-2.5,0),S,blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$\dfrac23$",(2/3,0),N,red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

So the graphs have $\boxed{0}$ points of intersection: the first lies on or above the $x$-axis and touches it only at $x=-\dfrac52$, and the second lies on or below it and touches it only at $x=\dfrac23$.

Surface area scales with the square of the edge, so the edges are in the ratio $\sqrt9=3$; volume scales with the cube of the edge, so the volumes are in the ratio $3^3=27$.

