\textbf{Why $BD=CD$.} The inscribed angles $\angle BAD$ and $\angle DAC$ are equal, and they subtend the chords $BD$ and $DC$ of the same circle. Equal inscribed angles subtend equal chords, so $BD=CD$. ($D$ is the midpoint of the arc $BC$ not containing $A$.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(9,0); real ax=11/3; pair A=(ax,sqrt(49-ax^2));
real k=(A.x^2+A.y^2-9*A.x)/(2*A.y); pair O=(4.5,k); real R=abs(O-B);
pair D=(4.5,k-R);
draw(circle(O,R),gray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--D,blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(B--D,red); draw(C--D,red);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,E); label("$D$",D,S);
label("$7$",(A+B)/2,NW); label("$8$",(A+C)/2,NE); label("$9$",(B+C)/2+(1.2,0),N);
draw(arc(A,0.9,degrees(B-A),degrees(D-A)),blue); draw(arc(A,1.05,degrees(D-A),degrees(C-A)),blue);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Ptolemy's theorem.} The points $A,B,D,C$ lie on the circle in this order, so for the cyclic quadrilateral $ABDC$ the product of the diagonals equals the sum of the products of opposite sides:
\[AD\cdot BC=AB\cdot CD+AC\cdot BD.\]
With $BD=CD$ this gives $9\cdot AD=7\cdot CD+8\cdot CD=15\cdot CD$, so
\[\dfrac{AD}{CD}=\dfrac{15}{9}=\boxed{\dfrac53}.\]

Measure time in grid units. The first ant at time $t$ is at a point $(x_1,y_1)$ with $x_1+y_1=t$; the second at a point $(x_2,y_2)$ with $(5-x_2)+y_2=t$. If they meet, then $x_1=x_2$ and $y_1=y_2$, so $x_1+y_1=5-x_1+y_1$, i.e. $x_1=\dfrac52$. So they can only meet on the line $x=\dfrac52$, which each ant crosses exactly once, on a horizontal unit segment between $x=2$ and $x=3$ at some height $j\in\{0,\dots,5\}$. The first ant crosses at height $j$ at time $\dfrac52+j$, and so does the second; hence they collide exactly when they cross at the same height.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<=5;++i){draw((i,0)--(i,5),gray); draw((0,i)--(5,i),gray);}
draw((2.5,-0.4)--(2.5,5.4),dashed);
path p1=(0,0)--(1,0)--(1,2)--(2,2)--(3,2)--(3,4)--(4,4)--(4,5)--(5,5);
path p2=(5,0)--(5,1)--(4,1)--(4,2)--(3,2)--(2,2)--(2,3)--(1,3)--(1,4)--(0,4)--(0,5);
draw(p1,blue+linewidth(1.2)); draw(p2,red+linewidth(1.2)+dashed);
dot((2.5,2),black+5);
dot((0,0),blue+5); dot((5,5),blue+5); dot((5,0),red+5); dot((0,5),red+5);
label("$(0,0)$",(0,0),SW,blue); label("$(5,5)$",(5,5),NE,blue); label("$(5,0)$",(5,0),SE,red); label("$(0,5)$",(0,5),NW,red);
label("$x=\dfrac52$",(2.5,5.4),N);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Crossing heights.} Each ant has $\dbinom{10}{5}=252$ shortest routes. The first ant crosses at height $j$ if its route goes from $(0,0)$ to $(2,j)$, then to $(3,j)$, then to $(5,5)$: $\dbinom{j+2}{2}\dbinom{7-j}{2}$ routes. For $j=0,\dots,5$ these numbers are $21,45,60,60,45,21$ (total $252$). By symmetry the second ant has the same distribution. So the probability of a collision is
\[\sum_j\left(\dfrac{n_j}{252}\right)^2=\dfrac{21^2+45^2+60^2+60^2+45^2+21^2}{252^2}=\dfrac{12132}{63504}=\dfrac{337}{1764},\]
and the probability of no collision is
\[1-\dfrac{337}{1764}=\boxed{\dfrac{1427}{1764}}\approx0.809.\]

question:
The area of a triangle is $\dfrac12\cdot\text{base}\cdot\text{height}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] In triangle $ABC$ with $a=BC$ and $b=CA$, let $h_a$ and $h_b$ be the altitudes from $A$ and from $B$. Prove directly (using similar triangles) that $a\cdot h_a=b\cdot h_b$, so that it does not matter which side is used as the base, as long as the height is measured to that side.
\item[(b)] A triangle has sides $13$, $14$ and $15$, and the altitude to the side of length $14$ is $12$. Find the area of the triangle and the altitudes to the other two sides.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let $H_a$ and $H_b$ be the feet of the altitudes from $A$ and $B$. The right triangles $AH_aC$ and $BH_bC$ have the same angle at $C$ (if $C$ is obtuse, the angles at $C$ are both supplementary to $\angle ACB$; if $C$ is a right angle, $h_a=b$ and $h_b=a$ directly), so they are similar, and
\[\dfrac{AH_a}{AC}=\dfrac{BH_b}{BC},\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad\dfrac{h_a}{b}=\dfrac{h_b}{a}.\]
Hence $a\,h_a=b\,h_b$. In the same way $b\,h_b=c\,h_c$. So any side may be taken as the base: $\dfrac12\cdot\text{side}\cdot(\text{altitude to that side})$ is the same for all three sides. $\blacksquare$

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(14,0), A=(5,12);
pair Ha=(5,0); pair Hb=C+dot(B-C,unit(A-C))*unit(A-C);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--Ha,blue); draw(B--Hb,red);
draw(Ha+(0,0.6)--Ha+(0.6,0.6)--Ha+(0.6,0),blue);
pair u=unit(C-Hb), v=unit(B-Hb); draw(Hb+0.6*u--Hb+0.6*(u+v)--Hb+0.6*v,red);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(Ha); dot(Hb);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$H_a$",Ha,S); label("$H_b$",Hb,NE);
label("$h_a$",(A+Ha)/2,W,blue); label("$h_b$",(B+Hb)/2,NW,red);
label("$a$",(B+C)/2+(2,0),S); label("$b$",A+0.75*(C-A),NE);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} With base $14$ and height $12$, the area is $\dfrac12\cdot14\cdot12=\boxed{84}$. The altitude to the side $13$ is $\dfrac{2\cdot84}{13}=\boxed{\dfrac{168}{13}}$, and the altitude to the side $15$ is $\dfrac{2\cdot84}{15}=\boxed{\dfrac{56}{5}}$.

question:
Let $p,q,r,s$ be the roots (with multiplicity) of $ax^4+bx^3+cx^2+dx+e=0$, where $a\ne0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Express $p+q+r+s$, $pq+pr+ps+qr+qs+rs$, $pqr+pqs+prs+qrs$ and $pqrs$ in terms of $a,b,c,d,e$, and explain why these formulas hold.
\item[(b)] Check the formulas for the second and third sums on $x^4-2x^3-7x^2+8x+12=0$, whose roots are $-2,-1,2,3$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{Hexagon.} It splits into $6$ equilateral triangles of side $s$, so its area is $6\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}s^2$.

\textbf{Octagon.} Cutting off the four corner triangles of the surrounding square shows that its area is $(1+\sqrt2)^2s^2-4\cdot\dfrac12\left(\dfrac{s}{\sqrt2}\right)^2=(3+2\sqrt2)s^2-s^2=2(1+\sqrt2)s^2$. (Alternatively: a central square of side $s$, four $s\times\dfrac{s}{\sqrt2}$ rectangles and four corner triangles; the square drawn dashed below is the central one.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] H; for(int i=0;i<6;++i) H.push(dir(60*i));
pair[] G; real R=1/(2*Sin(22.5)); for(int i=0;i<8;++i) G.push((3.4,0)+R*dir(22.5+45*i));
draw(H[0]--H[1]--H[2]--H[3]--H[4]--H[5]--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(G[0]--G[1]--G[2]--G[3]--G[4]--G[5]--G[6]--G[7]--cycle,red+linewidth(0.9));
for(int i=0;i<6;++i) draw((0,0)--H[i],blue+dashed);
draw(shift(3.4,0)*box((-0.5,-0.5),(0.5,0.5)),red+dashed);
label("$s$",(H[4]+H[5])/2,S,blue); label("$s$",(G[5]+G[6])/2,S,red);
label("$\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}s^2$",(0,-1.75),S,blue); label("$2(1+\sqrt2)s^2$",(3.4,-1.75),S,red);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Equation.} We need
\[2(1+\sqrt2)s^2-\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}s^2=1\iff s^2\cdot\dfrac{4+4\sqrt2-3\sqrt3}{2}=1,\]
so
\[s=\boxed{\sqrt{\dfrac{2}{4+4\sqrt2-3\sqrt3}}}\approx0.670.\]
(The denominator $4+4\sqrt2-3\sqrt3\approx4.461$ is positive, so this is a real number.)

Mark the angles $\angle1=\angle CAB$, $\angle2=\angle DAC$ and $\angle3=\angle DCA$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real t=70; pair A=(0,0), D=dir(t), C=D+(1,0), B=(1+2*Cos(t),0);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--C,blue);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,NW);
void tick(pair P, pair Q){pair M=(P+Q)/2, u=unit(Q-P), v=rotate(90)*u; draw(M-0.04*v--M+0.04*v,linewidth(0.8));}
tick(A,D); tick(D,C); tick(B,C);
real a1=degrees(C-A);
draw(arc(A,0.25,0,a1),red); draw(arc(A,0.3,a1,t),red);
draw(arc(C,0.25,180,180+a1),red);
label("$1$",A+0.42*dir(a1/2),red+fontsize(7pt)); label("$2$",A+0.48*dir((a1+t)/2),red+fontsize(7pt)); label("$3$",C+0.42*dir(180+a1/2),red+fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

\begin{itemize}
\item Since $AD=DC$, triangle $ADC$ is isosceles with apex $D$, so its base angles are equal: $\angle2=\angle DAC=\angle DCA=\angle3$.
\item Since $DC\parallel AB$, the alternate angles made by the transversal $AC$ are equal: $\angle3=\angle DCA=\angle CAB=\angle1$.
\end{itemize}
Hence $\angle DAC=\angle CAB$, so $AC$ bisects $\angle DAB$. $\blacksquare$ (By symmetry, $BD$ bisects $\angle ABC$. Only $AD=DC$ and $AB\parallel CD$ were used.)

The pair $(x,y)$ is uniform in the square $[0,6]^2$ of area $36$, and we need the area of the part with $x^2+y^2<48$, inside the circle of radius $\sqrt{48}=4\sqrt3\approx6.93$ about the origin. The circle meets the right side $x=6$ at $y=\sqrt{48-36}=2\sqrt3$, and the top side at $x=2\sqrt3$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=4*sqrt(3), q=2*sqrt(3);
pair P1=(q,6), P2=(6,q);
fill((0,0)--(6,0)--P2--arc((0,0),r,30,60)--P1--(0,6)--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
draw(box((0,0),(6,6)),linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc((0,0),r,0,90),red);
draw((0,0)--P1,dashed); draw((0,0)--P2,dashed);
dot(P1); dot(P2);
label("$O$",(0,0),SW); label("$(2\sqrt3,6)$",P1,N,fontsize(8pt)); label("$(6,2\sqrt3)$",P2,E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$6$",(3,0),S); label("$6$",(0,3),W); label("$x^2+y^2=48$",r*dir(80),NE,red+fontsize(8pt));
label("$30^\circ$",2.4*dir(45),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The region splits into two right triangles and a circular sector:
\begin{itemize}
\item the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(6,0)$, $(6,2\sqrt3)$: area $\dfrac12\cdot6\cdot2\sqrt3=6\sqrt3$;
\item the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(0,6)$, $(2\sqrt3,6)$: area $6\sqrt3$;
\item the sector between the rays to $(6,2\sqrt3)$ and to $(2\sqrt3,6)$, which make angles $30^\circ$ and $60^\circ$ with the $x$-axis: angle $30^\circ$, area $\dfrac{30}{360}\pi\cdot48=4\pi$.
\end{itemize}
The probability is
\[\dfrac{12\sqrt3+4\pi}{36}=\boxed{\dfrac{3\sqrt3+\pi}{9}}\approx0.926.\]

question:
In two-column proofs, the \emph{symmetric property} of equality says: if $a=b$, then $b=a$. A relation $R$ is called \emph{reflexive} if $a\,R\,a$ for every $a$, \emph{symmetric} if $a\,R\,b$ always implies $b\,R\,a$, and \emph{transitive} if $a\,R\,b$ and $b\,R\,c$ always imply $a\,R\,c$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the relation ``is taller than'' (among people of different heights) is not symmetric. In fact, show that $a$ is taller than $b$ and $b$ is taller than $a$ can never both be true.
\item[(b)] For real numbers, a step ``$a>b$, so $b<a$'' is often used in proofs. Explain why this step is correct, and why it is not an application of a symmetric property.
\item[(c)] Which of the three properties does the relation $>$ on the real numbers have? Justify your answers.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} If Abe is taller than Bob, then Bob is not taller than Abe, so ``taller than'' is not symmetric: applying a ``symmetric property'' to it would turn a true statement into a false one. More precisely, if $h(x)$ is the height of $x$, then ``$a$ is taller than $b$'' means $h(a)>h(b)$. If also $h(b)>h(a)$, then $h(a)>h(b)>h(a)$, so $h(a)>h(a)$, which is impossible. So the two statements are never both true.

\textbf{(b)} The symbol $<$ is defined from $>$: ``$b<a$'' means exactly the same as ``$a>b$'' (both say that $a-b$ is positive). So the step only rewrites the same statement in the other notation, reading it from right to left; it is justified by the definition of $<$. A symmetric property of $>$ would instead conclude $b>a$, with the same symbol and the order of the letters reversed, and that is false whenever $a>b$. (In the same way, ``$b$ is shorter than $a$'' is just another way of saying ``$a$ is taller than $b$''.)

\textbf{(c)}
\begin{itemize}
\item Not reflexive: $a>a$ is false for every $a$.
\item Not symmetric: $2>1$, but $1>2$ is false. As in (a), $a>b$ and $b>a$ are never both true, since together they would give $a>a$.
\item Transitive: if $a>b$ and $b>c$, then $a-b>0$ and $b-c>0$, so $a-c=(a-b)+(b-c)>0$, that is, $a>c$.
\end{itemize}
So $>$ is $\boxed{\text{transitive, but neither reflexive nor symmetric}}$.

question:
A square floor is covered with congruent square tiles. The total number of tiles that lie on the two diagonals is $37$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many tiles cover the floor?
\item[(b)] A student argues: ``A diagonal has $19$ tiles, and the area of a square with diagonal $d$ is $\dfrac{d^2}{2}$, so the floor has $\dfrac{19^2}{2}=180.5$ tiles.'' Explain the error.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let the floor be $n$ tiles by $n$ tiles. Each diagonal contains $n$ tiles. If $n$ were even, the diagonals would share no tile and there would be $2n$ diagonal tiles, an even number; since $37$ is odd, $n$ is odd and the two diagonals share the centre tile, giving $2n-1=37$, so $n=19$. The floor has $19^2=\boxed{361}$ tiles.

\textbf{(b)} The formula $\dfrac{d^2}{2}$ needs the \emph{length} of the diagonal, but $19$ is the \emph{number of tiles} along it. A tile has side $1$ (taking the tile side as the unit), so its own diagonal has length $\sqrt2$, and the $19$ tiles along the diagonal of the floor span a length $d=19\sqrt2$. Then $\dfrac{d^2}{2}=\dfrac{(19\sqrt2)^2}{2}=361$, which agrees with (a). (Also, $180.5$ cannot be a number of tiles.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int n=19;
for(int i=0;i<n;++i){ fill(shift(i,i)*unitsquare,rgb(0.6,0.75,1)); fill(shift(i,n-1-i)*unitsquare,rgb(0.6,0.75,1)); }
for(int i=0;i<=n;++i){ draw((i,0)--(i,n),gray+0.3); draw((0,i)--(n,i),gray+0.3); }
draw(box((0,0),(n,n)),linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0)--(n,n),red+linewidth(0.8));
label("$19$ tiles",(n/2,0),S); label("diagonal $19\sqrt2$",(n/2,n),N,red);
\end{asy}

Choose a zombie $a$ who shot as many skeletons as possible (there are finitely many zombies).

Since no zombie shot every skeleton, there is a skeleton $y$ that $a$ did not shoot. Every skeleton was shot by somebody, so some zombie $b$ shot $y$; then $b\ne a$.

Now compare the sets of skeletons shot by $a$ and by $b$. If $b$ had shot every skeleton that $a$ shot, then $b$, who also shot $y$, would have shot more skeletons than $a$, contradicting the choice of $a$. So there is a skeleton $x$ that $a$ shot and $b$ did not shoot; $x\ne y$ because $a$ shot $x$ but not $y$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair a=(0,1.6), bb=(0,0), x=(3,1.6), y=(3,0);
draw(a--x,blue+linewidth(1),Arrow(7)); draw(bb--y,blue+linewidth(1),Arrow(7));
draw(a--y,red+dashed); draw(bb--x,red+dashed);
dot(a); dot(bb); dot(x); dot(y);
label("zombie $a$",a,W); label("zombie $b$",bb,W); label("skeleton $x$",x,E); label("skeleton $y$",y,E);
label("shot",(a+x)/2,N,blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("shot",(bb+y)/2,S,blue+fontsize(8pt));
label("not shot",(1.5,0.8),red+fontsize(8pt),Fill(white));
\end{asy}

Then $a$ shot $x$, $b$ shot $y$, $a$ did not shoot $y$, and $b$ did not shoot $x$, as required. $\blacksquare$

Ratios of areas, and ratios of lengths along a line, do not change under an affine map, and any triangle can be mapped to any other by an affine map. So we may take $A=(0,0)$, $B=(5,0)$, $C=(0,5)$, with area $\dfrac{25}{2}$. Then
\[D=(2,0),\qquad E=B+\dfrac25(C-B)=(3,2),\qquad F=C+\dfrac25(A-C)=(0,3).\]
The cevians are
\[AE:\ y=\dfrac23x,\qquad BF:\ \dfrac x5+\dfrac y3=1,\qquad CD:\ \dfrac x2+\dfrac y5=1.\]
Solving in pairs,
\[AE\cap BF=\left(\dfrac{45}{19},\dfrac{30}{19}\right),\quad BF\cap CD=\left(\dfrac{20}{19},\dfrac{45}{19}\right),\quad CD\cap AE=\left(\dfrac{30}{19},\dfrac{20}{19}\right).\]

