question:
Jo starts at a point $A$ in space and teleports in three jumps, from $A$ to $B$, from $B$ to $C$ and from $C$ to $D$, in unknown directions. Bo's device records the lengths of the jumps, in meters, as $AB=1\pm0.02$, $BC=2\pm0.11$ and $CD=3\pm0.10$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Taking the recorded lengths $1$, $2$ and $3$ as exact, find the volume of the set of all possible positions of $D$.
\item[(b)] Use the rules of Gaussian error propagation: the uncertainty of a sum of independent measurements is the square root of the sum of the squares of their uncertainties, and if a length $r$ has a small uncertainty $\sigma$, then a quantity $V(r)$ has uncertainty about $V'(r)\,\sigma$ (for $V=\dfrac43\pi r^3$, this is $4\pi r^2\sigma$). Find the uncertainty of the radius of the set in (a), and give its volume with its uncertainty.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} By the triangle inequality, $AD\le AB+BC+CD=6$. Conversely, every distance $r$ with $0\le r\le6$ occurs: after the first two jumps, $AC$ can be any number from $2-1=1$ to $1+2=3$ (vary the angle at $B$). For $AC=c$, the distance $AD$ can be any number from $|3-c|$ to $3+c$; for $c=3$ this covers $[0,6]$. Since the directions are arbitrary, rotating the whole path about $A$ shows that every point at a distance $r\le6$ from $A$ is possible.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),3),linewidth(0.9)); draw(ellipse((0,0),3,0.8),dashed);
pair A=(0,0), B=A+0.5*dir(30), C=B+1*dir(150), D=C+1.5*dir(20);
draw(A--B--C--D,blue+linewidth(0.9),Arrow(5)); dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D);
label("$A$",A,S); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,NW); label("$D$",D,E);
draw(A--3*dir(-30),red,Arrow(5)); label("$6$",1.5*dir(-30),SW,red);
\end{asy}

So the possible positions fill the ball of radius $6$ about $A$, of volume
\[\dfrac43\pi\cdot6^3=\boxed{288\pi}\approx904.8\text{ cubic meters}.\]

\textbf{(b)} The radius is the total length $r=AB+BC+CD=6$, a sum of three independent measurements, so its uncertainty is
\[\sigma=\sqrt{0.02^2+0.11^2+0.10^2}=\sqrt{0.0004+0.0121+0.0100}=\sqrt{0.0225}=0.15\text{ m}.\]
The uncertainty of the volume is then about $4\pi r^2\sigma=4\pi\cdot36\cdot0.15=21.6\pi\approx67.9$ cubic meters. So the volume is
\[\boxed{288\pi\pm21.6\pi\approx905\pm68\text{ cubic meters}}.\]
(Under Gaussian propagation the uncertainties are not hard bounds: if the three intervals were taken as hard bounds, the set would be the ball of radius $6.23$.)

$10$ quarts is $320$ fluid ounces, so we count the nonnegative integer solutions of $5a+2b+c=320$. For each $a$ and $b$ with $5a+2b\le320$, $c$ is determined, so for fixed $a$ there are $\left\lfloor\dfrac{320-5a}{2}\right\rfloor+1$ choices of $b$, where $0\le a\le64$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $a=2j$ even ($j=0,\dots,32$): $\dfrac{320-10j}{2}+1=161-5j$, total $33\cdot161-5\cdot\dfrac{32\cdot33}{2}=5313-2640=2673$.
\item $a=2j+1$ odd ($j=0,\dots,31$): $\left\lfloor\dfrac{315-10j}{2}\right\rfloor+1=158-5j$, total $32\cdot158-5\cdot\dfrac{31\cdot32}{2}=5056-2480=2576$.
\end{itemize}
The number of ways is $2673+2576=\boxed{5249}$.

An equilateral triangle with side $2$ has area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot2^2=\sqrt3$, and a semicircle of radius $3$ has area $\dfrac12\pi\cdot3^2=\dfrac{9\pi}{2}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
path tri=(0,0)--(2,0)--(1,sqrt(3))--cycle;
filldraw(shift(-3,1.2)*tri,rgb(1,0.85,0.4)); filldraw(shift(1,1.2)*tri,rgb(1,0.85,0.4));
filldraw(arc((0,-0.5),3,180,360)--cycle,rgb(1,0.85,0.4));
label("$2$",(-2,1.2),S); label("$2$",(2,1.2),S);
draw((0,-0.5)--(3,-0.5),red); label("$3$",(1.5,-0.5),N,red); dot((0,-0.5));
\end{asy}

The total is $2\sqrt3+\dfrac{9\pi}{2}=\boxed{\textbf{(A) }\dfrac{4\sqrt3+9\pi}{2}}$.

Draw the line through $N$ parallel to $CA$ and $DY$, and let $X$ be a point on it inside the pentagon (on the side of $C$ and $Y$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), A=(4,0), D=(3,3.5), Y=(-0.5,3.5);
pair M=(A+D)/2; real r=abs(D-A)/2; pair Nn=M+r*unit(rotate(-90)*(D-A));
draw(C--A--Nn--D--Y--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((Nn.x-2.2,Nn.y)--Nn,dashed);
pair u=unit(A-Nn), v=unit(D-Nn); draw(Nn+0.3*u--Nn+0.3*(u+v)--Nn+0.3*v);
dot(C); dot(A); dot(Nn); dot(D); dot(Y);
label("$C$",C,SW); label("$A$",A,S); label("$N$",Nn,E); label("$D$",D,N); label("$Y$",Y,NW);
draw((1,0)--(1.15,0.1)--(1,0.2)); draw((1.2,3.5)--(1.35,3.6)--(1.2,3.7));
label("$X$",(Nn.x-2.2,Nn.y),W);
\end{asy}

The lines $CA$ and $NX$ are parallel, and $A$ and $N$ are consecutive vertices, so $\angle CAN$ and $\angle ANX$ are co-interior angles: $\angle CAN+\angle ANX=180^\circ$. In the same way $\angle NDY+\angle DNX=180^\circ$. Since the pentagon is convex, $X$ lies inside the angle $AND$, so $\angle ANX+\angle DNX=\angle AND=90^\circ$. Adding,
\[\angle CAN+\angle NDY=360^\circ-90^\circ=\boxed{\textbf{(A) }270^\circ}.\]
(Convexity matters: if the pentagon were dented at $N$, with interior angle $270^\circ$ there, the sum would be $90^\circ$.)

\textbf{Shape of the triangle.} Let $AC=x$, so $AB=2x$. By the law of cosines, $BC^2=4x^2+x^2-2\cdot2x\cdot x\cdot\dfrac12=3x^2$, so $BC=x\sqrt3$ and $AC^2+BC^2=4x^2=AB^2$: the angle at $C$ is right (a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle).

\textbf{Coordinates.} Put $C=(0,0)$, $A=(x,0)$, $B=\left(0,x\sqrt3\right)$ and $P=(p,q)$. Then
\[p^2+q^2=4,\qquad(p-x)^2+q^2=3,\qquad p^2+\left(q-x\sqrt3\right)^2=25.\]
Subtracting the first equation from the other two gives $x^2-2px=-1$ and $3x^2-2\sqrt3\,qx=21$, so
\[p=\dfrac{x^2+1}{2x},\qquad q=\dfrac{3x^2-21}{2\sqrt3\,x}.\]
Substituting into $p^2+q^2=4$ and multiplying by $12x^2$:
\[3(x^2+1)^2+(3x^2-21)^2=48x^2\iff12x^4-168x^2+444=0\iff x^4-14x^2+37=0,\]
so $x^2=7\pm2\sqrt3$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real x=sqrt(7+2*sqrt(3));
pair C=(0,0), A=(x,0), B=(0,x*sqrt(3));
real p=(x^2+1)/(2x), q=(3x^2-21)/(2*sqrt(3)*x); pair P=(p,q);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(P--A,blue); draw(P--B,red); draw(P--C,dashed);
draw((0.25,0)--(0.25,0.25)--(0,0.25));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(P);
label("$A$",A,SE); label("$B$",B,N); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$P$",P,dir(75));
label("$\sqrt3$",P+0.4*(A-P),SW,blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$5$",(P+B)/2,E,red); label("$2$",(P+C)/2,NW);
label("$x$",(C+A)/2,S); label("$x\sqrt3$",(C+B)/2,W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Choosing the root.} If $x^2=7-2\sqrt3\approx3.54$, then $AB=2x\approx3.76$ and $BC\approx3.26$; but every point of the triangle is within $\max(BA,BC)$ of $B$, while $PB=5$. So $x^2=7+2\sqrt3$. (Then $p\approx1.77$, $q\approx0.93$ and $\dfrac px+\dfrac{q}{x\sqrt3}\approx0.71<1$, so $P$ is indeed inside.)

\textbf{Area.} The legs are $x$ and $x\sqrt3$, so
\[[ABC]=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}x^2=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\left(7+2\sqrt3\right)=\boxed{\dfrac{6+7\sqrt3}{2}}\approx9.06.\]

question:
Seven friends sit at a round table; three of them are dressed as ghosts and four as other monsters. All friends are distinguishable, and seatings that differ by a rotation are considered the same.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] In how many seatings do at least two ghosts sit next to each other?
\item[(b)] In how many seatings do exactly two of the ghosts sit next to each other (and the third ghost next to neither of them)?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{Which sides are parallel.} If $AB\parallel CD$, then since also $AB=CD$, the quadrilateral $ABCD$ would be a parallelogram, forcing $BC=AD$; but $BC=1\ne2=AD$. Hence $BC\parallel AD$.

\textbf{Height.} Drop perpendiculars $BP$ and $CQ$ to $AD$. Then $PQ=BC=1$ and $BP=CQ$, so the right triangles $ABP$ and $DCQ$ have equal hypotenuses $AB=CD=1$ and equal legs, hence $AP=QD$. As $AP+PQ+QD=AD=2$, we get $AP=QD=\dfrac12$, and
\[h=BP=\sqrt{1-\dfrac14}=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), D=(2,0), B=(0.5,sqrt(3)/2), C=(1.5,sqrt(3)/2), P=(0.5,0), Q=(1.5,0);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,blue); draw(B--D,blue);
draw(B--P,dashed); draw(C--Q,dashed);
draw(P+(0,0.08)--P+(0.08,0.08)--P+(0.08,0)); draw(Q+(0,0.08)--Q+(-0.08,0.08)--Q+(-0.08,0));
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$D$",D,SE); label("$B$",B,NW); label("$C$",C,NE);
label("$P$",P,S); label("$Q$",Q,S);
label("$1$",(A+B)/2,W); label("$1$",(B+C)/2,N); label("$1$",(C+D)/2,E);
label("$\dfrac12$",(A+P)/2,S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$\dfrac12$",(Q+D)/2,S,fontsize(8pt));
label("$h$",(0.5,0.12),W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Area.} \[\dfrac{BC+AD}{2}\cdot BP=\dfrac{1+2}{2}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}=\boxed{\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{4}}.\]

\textbf{Diagonals.} With $A=(0,0)$, $D=(2,0)$, $B=\left(\dfrac12,\dfrac{\sqrt3}2\right)$ and $C=\left(\dfrac32,\dfrac{\sqrt3}2\right)$,
\[AC=\sqrt{\dfrac94+\dfrac34}=\sqrt3,\qquad BD=\sqrt{\dfrac94+\dfrac34}=\sqrt3,\]
so both diagonals have length $\boxed{\sqrt3}$. (The trapezoid is half of a regular hexagon of side $1$ cut along a long diagonal.)

The vertex of $y=ax^2+k$ is $(0,k)$, so $k=-4$. Substituting $(2,5)$:
\[5=a\cdot2^2-4\quad\Longrightarrow\quad 4a=9\quad\Longrightarrow\quad a=\dfrac94.\]
The equation is $\boxed{y=\dfrac94x^2-4}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return 9/4*x^2-4;}
draw((-2.6,0)--(2.6,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-5)--(0,7),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(2.6,0),E); label("$y$",(0,7),N);
draw(graph(f,-2.35,2.35),blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot((0,-4)); dot((2,5),red);
label("$(0,-4)$",(0,-4),SE); label("$(2,5)$",(2,5),E,red);
label("$y=\dfrac94x^2-4$",(-0.8,-2.6),W,blue);
\end{asy}

Substituting the three points gives
\[a+b+c=3,\qquad 4a+2b+c=5,\qquad 36a+6b+c=7.\]
Subtracting the first equation from the second and the second from the third:
\[3a+b=2,\qquad 32a+4b=2.\]
Four times the first minus the second gives $12a-32a=8-2$, so $a=-\dfrac{3}{10}$. Then $b=2-3a=\dfrac{29}{10}$ and $c=3-a-b=3+\dfrac3{10}-\dfrac{29}{10}=\dfrac25$. The parabola is
\[\boxed{y=-\dfrac{3}{10}x^2+\dfrac{29}{10}x+\dfrac25}.\]
Check at $x=6$: $-\dfrac{108}{10}+\dfrac{174}{10}+\dfrac{4}{10}=7$.

