Three friends wake up at a whole number of minutes after $7{:}00$ am, independently, as follows.
\begin{itemize}
\item The first wakes up at $7{:}00$ with probability $90\%$, and at each of the minutes $7{:}01,7{:}02,\dots,7{:}10$ with probability $1\%$.
\item The second wakes up at $7{:}00$ with probability $50\%$, and at each of the minutes $7{:}01,\dots,7{:}25$ with probability $2\%$.
\item The third wakes up at each of the minutes $7{:}01,\dots,7{:}20$ with probability $5\%$.
\end{itemize}
Each friend leaves as soon as they wake up, takes exactly $30$ minutes to reach the meeting place, waits there $5$ minutes, and then leaves. Two friends \emph{meet} if their arrival times differ by at most $5$ minutes. Find the probability that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(1)] at least two of the friends meet;
\item[(2)] all three friends are at the meeting place at the same time.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Write the number as $\overline{abcd}$ with $a\ge1$. Put $a'=a-1$; then $a'+b+c+d=11$ with $0\le a'\le8$ and $0\le b,c,d\le9$.

Without the upper bounds there are $\dbinom{14}{3}=364$ solutions. Remove the bad ones (at most one variable can be too big, since $9+10>11$):
\begin{itemize}
\item $a'\ge9$: put $a''=a'-9$, then $a''+b+c+d=2$: $\dbinom53=10$ solutions;
\item $b\ge10$ (similarly $c$ or $d$): the rest add up to $1$: $\dbinom43=4$ solutions each, $12$ in all.
\end{itemize}
So there are $364-10-12=\boxed{342}$ such integers.

