\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] A line has slope $m$ and passes through the point $(x_1,y_1)$. Show that its equation is $y-y_1=m(x-x_1)$.
\item[(b)] Find the equation, in the form $y=mx+c$, of the line with slope $3$ through the point $(2,-1)$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} A point $(x,y)\ne(x_1,y_1)$ lies on the line exactly when the slope between $(x_1,y_1)$ and $(x,y)$ equals $m$:
\[\dfrac{y-y_1}{x-x_1}=m,\]
that is, $y-y_1=m(x-x_1)$; and the point $(x_1,y_1)$ itself also satisfies this equation. (For $x=x_1$ and $y\ne y_1$ the slope would be undefined, and indeed the equation fails, so no such point is on the line.)

\textbf{(b)} By (a), $y-(-1)=3(x-2)$, so $y+1=3x-6$ and $\boxed{y=3x-7}$. Check: $x=2$ gives $y=-1$.

