question:
In the cube $ABCDEFGH$ with opposite vertices $C$ and $E$, $J$ and $I$ are the midpoints of the edges $\overline{FB}$ and $\overline{HD}$, respectively. (The faces are $ABCD$ on top, $EFGH$ on the bottom, with $A$ above $E$, $B$ above $F$, $C$ above $G$ and $D$ above $H$.) Let $R$ be the ratio of the area of the cross-section $EJCI$ to the area of one face of the cube.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find $R^2$.

$\textbf{(A) }\dfrac54\qquad\textbf{(B) }\dfrac43\qquad\textbf{(C) }\dfrac32\qquad\textbf{(D) }\dfrac{25}{16}\qquad\textbf{(E) }\dfrac94$
\item[(b)] All four sides of $EJCI$ have the same length. Explain why $EJCI$ is nevertheless not a square.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the cube have edge $1$, with $E=(0,0,0)$, $F=(1,0,0)$, $G=(1,1,0)$, $H=(0,1,0)$ and $A,B,C,D$ directly above them at height $1$. Then $C=(1,1,1)$, $J=\left(1,0,\dfrac12\right)$ and $I=\left(0,1,\dfrac12\right)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Ep=(0,0), F=(3,0), G=(4.2,1), H=(1.2,1), A=(0,3), B=(3,3), C=(4.2,4), D=(1.2,4);
pair J=(F+B)/2, I=(H+D)/2;
draw(Ep--F--G); draw(Ep--A); draw(F--B); draw(G--C); draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);
draw(Ep--H--G,dashed); draw(H--D,dashed);
filldraw(Ep--J--C--I--cycle,lightblue+opacity(0.4),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(Ep--C,red+dashed); draw(J--I,red+dashed);
dot(J); dot(I);
label("$E$",Ep,SW); label("$F$",F,SE); label("$G$",G,E); label("$H$",H,NW); label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,N);
label("$J$",J,E); label("$I$",I,W);
\end{asy}

Each side has length $\sqrt{1+0+\dfrac14}=\dfrac{\sqrt5}{2}$ (e.g. $EJ$ goes $1$ along an edge and $\dfrac12$ up), so $EJCI$ is a rhombus. (It is planar: $E+C=J+I$, so its diagonals bisect each other.) Its diagonals are
\[EC=\sqrt{1+1+1}=\sqrt3\quad\text{(a space diagonal)},\qquad JI=\sqrt{1+1+0}=\sqrt2\quad\text{(a face diagonal)}.\]

\textbf{(a)} The area of a rhombus is half the product of its diagonals: $\dfrac{\sqrt3\cdot\sqrt2}{2}=\dfrac{\sqrt6}{2}$. A face has area $1$, so $R=\dfrac{\sqrt6}2$ and $R^2=\dfrac64=\boxed{\textbf{(C) }\dfrac32}$.

\textbf{(b)} A square has equal diagonals, but here $EC=\sqrt3\ne\sqrt2=JI$. Equivalently, its angles are not right angles: $\vec{EJ}\cdot\vec{EI}=\left(1,0,\dfrac12\right)\cdot\left(0,1,\dfrac12\right)=\dfrac14\ne0$. So $EJCI$ is a rhombus but $\boxed{\text{not a square}}$. (In the usual picture it looks like a square because the drawing is a projection.)

The edges have length $1$, so we count edge traversals. Let the bottom face be $ABCD$ and the top face $EFGH$, with $E,F,G,H$ above $A,B,C,D$.

\textbf{Lower bound.} For each vertex $v$, count how many times the walk arrives at $v$ along an edge and how many times it leaves along an edge (the start counts as a departure and the end as an arrival). Since the walk is closed, arrivals and departures at $v$ are equal, so the total number of traversals at $v$ (counting both ends) is even. Each vertex has $3$ edges, each walked at least once, so the total at $v$ is at least $3$, hence at least $4$. Each traversal has two ends, so if the walk has length $L$,
\[2L=\sum_{v}(\text{traversals at }v)\ge8\cdot4=32,\qquad L\ge16.\]

\textbf{A walk of length $16$.} Walk the four vertical edges twice (red in the figure):
