The angle bisectors $AF$ and $CE$ meet at the incentre $I$, so $I$ lies on $BD$. The bisector of $\angle ABC$ also passes through $B$ and $I$; since $I\ne B$, it is the line $BI=BD$. So the altitude $BD$ bisects $\angle ABC$.

Then the right triangles $ABD$ and $CBD$ have the common side $BD$, right angles at $D$ and equal angles at $B$, so they are congruent (ASA). Hence $BA=BC$ and $AD=DC$: the triangle is isosceles and $D$ is the midpoint of $AC$, so $BD$ is a median.

