question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $24$ cannot be made from the numbers $3$, $4$ and $1$, each used exactly once, with $+,-,\times,\div$ and parentheses.
\item[(b)] Make $24$ using four numbers, each equal to $3$, $4$ or $1$, with each of $3$, $4$, $1$ used at least once, and the same operations.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Any such expression first combines two of the numbers and then combines the result with the third. The possible results of the first step are:
\begin{itemize}
\item from $3,4$: $7,\ \pm1,\ 12,\ \dfrac34,\ \dfrac43$; combining with $1$ gives at most $12+1=13$ in absolute value;
\item from $3,1$: $4,\ \pm2,\ 3,\ \dfrac13$; combining with $4$ gives at most $4\cdot4=16$;
\item from $4,1$: $5,\ \pm3,\ 4,\ \dfrac14$; combining with $3$ gives at most $5\cdot3=15$.
\end{itemize}
(In the three cases the first-step results have absolute value at most $12$, $4$ and $5$. Combining such a result $x$ with the third number $y$ by $+$, $-$ or $\times$ gives at most $|x|+|y|$ or $|x||y|$ in absolute value, that is at most $13$, $16$ and $15$. Dividing gives at most $12$: $\dfrac xy$ is at most $|x|$, and the only divisions by a number less than $1$ in absolute value are $1\div\dfrac34=\dfrac43$, $4\div\dfrac13=12$ and $3\div\dfrac14=12$.) Every result has absolute value at most $16<24$, so $24$ is impossible.

\textbf{(b)} For example, $\boxed{3\times4\times(1+1)=24}$, or $4\times(3+3)\times1=24$.

The allowed pairs in the boat are cellist--pianist, cellist--mathematician and flutist--mathematician. A plan with $5$ crossings:
\begin{enumerate}
\item cellist and pianist cross;
\item the cellist returns;
\item cellist and mathematician cross;
\item the mathematician returns;
\item mathematician and flutist cross.
\end{enumerate}
Every boat trip uses an allowed pair or one person, and at the end all four are across.

\textbf{No plan is shorter.} Crossings alternate between going over and coming back, starting and ending with going over, so a plan has $2k-1$ crossings for some $k$: $k$ trips over and $k-1$ back. Each trip over carries at most $2$ people and each trip back at least $1$, so at most $2k-(k-1)=k+1$ people end up across. We need $k+1\ge4$, so $k\ge3$ and at least $\boxed{5}$ crossings are needed.

question:
A book has $225$ pages, and I want to read $\dfrac6{15}$ of it by Friday.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] With probability $80\%$ I start following my plan, and then with probability $20\%$ I still fail to finish. If I do not start following it, then with probability $\dfrac1{15}$ I go back to it later, and then I finish with probability $\dfrac1{10}$. What is the probability that I reach my Friday goal?
\item[(b)] If I reach the Friday goal, I then want to have read $\dfrac9{15}$ of the book by Sunday. I manage this with probability $\dfrac56$; if I do not, then with probability $\dfrac1{10}$ I go back and still manage it by Sunday. What is the probability that I reach both goals?
\end{enumerate}

When I am frustrated while practising the cello, one of three things happens:
\begin{itemize}
\item with probability $\dfrac27$ I stop playing; in that case, with probability $\dfrac{1}{11}$ I later go back and finish the piece (otherwise I do not finish it);
\item with probability $\dfrac37$ I force myself to keep playing; in that case, with probability $90\%$ I put the cello down and give up before the end of the piece, and otherwise I finish the piece;
\item with probability $\dfrac27$ I give up completely and do not finish the piece.
\end{itemize}
What is the probability that I finish the piece?

solution:
\textbf{Paths.} A path from $(0,0)$ to $(a,b)$ made of unit steps right ($R$) and up ($U$) has $a+b$ steps, of which $b$ are $U$; choosing the positions of the $U$ steps gives $\dbinom{a+b}{b}$ such paths.

\textbf{The right side.} Let $T=(n-r,\,r+1)$. The paths from $O=(0,0)$ to $T$ have $n+1$ steps, $r+1$ of them up, so there are $\dbinom{n+1}{r+1}$ of them.

\textbf{Classify by the last up-step.} Every such path has a last up-step. It goes from some point $(j,r)$ to $(j,r+1)$, where $0\le j\le n-r$, and after it the path can only go right, along the top row to $T$. Conversely, for each $j$, any path from $O$ to $(j,r)$, followed by the up-step to $(j,r+1)$ and then $n-r-j$ right steps, is a path from $O$ to $T$ whose last up-step is in column $j$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int a=4, h=3;
for(int i=0;i<=a;++i) draw((i,0)--(i,h),gray(0.8));
for(int j=0;j<=h;++j) draw((0,j)--(a,j),gray(0.8));
for(int i=0;i<=a;++i) draw((i,h-1)--(i,h),dashed+gray(0.5));
path p=(0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(1,2)--(2,2)--(2,3)--(4,3);
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(1,2)--(2,2),blue+linewidth(1));
draw((2,2)--(2,3),red+linewidth(1.6));
draw((2,3)--(4,3),blue+linewidth(1));
dot((0,0)); dot((4,3)); dot((2,2),red); dot((2,3),red);
label("$O=(0,0)$",(0,0),SW); label("$T=(n-r,\,r+1)$",(4,3),NE);
label("$(j,r)$",(2,2),SE,red,Fill(white)); label("$(j,r+1)$",(2,3),N,red);
label("red: the last up-step",(2,-0.25),S,red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

So the paths whose last up-step is in column $j$ correspond exactly to the paths from $O$ to $(j,r)$, and there are $\dbinom{j+r}{r}$ of them. Each path to $T$ is counted for exactly one $j$, so
\[\dbinom{n+1}{r+1}=\sum_{j=0}^{n-r}\dbinom{j+r}{r}=\dbinom rr+\dbinom{r+1}{r}+\cdots+\dbinom nr.\]
$\blacksquare$

(In Pascal's triangle, the entries $\dbinom rr,\dots,\dbinom nr$ lie along a diagonal and $\dbinom{n+1}{r+1}$ is just below the end of it, on the other side: the shape of a hockey stick.)

question:
Mark has $n$ identical blue toys and $n$ identical red toys, and puts them into the $2n$ spaces around a circle, one toy per space.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If the $2n$ spaces are numbered (so that two arrangements are different whenever some space gets a different colour), how many arrangements are there? Express your answer in terms of $n$.
\item[(b)] Now suppose that two arrangements are considered the same if one is a rotation of the other. A student says that the answer is the answer to (a) divided by $2n$. Show that this is wrong for $n=2$ and for $n=3$, and find the correct numbers of arrangements for $n=2$ and $n=3$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the following claim is false: ``a quadratic equation $ax^2+bx+c=0$ with integer coefficients has integer roots if and only if its discriminant $b^2-4ac$ is a perfect square''.
\item[(b)] Prove the corrected statement: if $b$ and $c$ are integers, then both roots of $x^2+bx+c=0$ are integers if and only if $b^2-4c$ is a perfect square.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
With radius $r=\dfrac d2$, the volume of a sphere is
\[V=\dfrac43\pi r^3=\dfrac43\pi\cdot\dfrac{d^3}{8}=\dfrac{\pi d^3}{6}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),1),linewidth(0.9));
draw(yscale(0.32)*arc((0,0),1,0,180),dashed);
draw(yscale(0.32)*arc((0,0),1,180,360));
draw((-1,0)--(1,0),red+linewidth(0.9),Arrows(5));
label("$d$",(0,0),N,red);
label("$V=\dfrac{\pi d^3}{6}=137250$",(0,-1),S);
\end{asy}

So
\[d^3=\dfrac{6V}{\pi}=\dfrac{6\cdot137250}{\pi}=\dfrac{823500}{\pi}\approx262128.\]
Since $64^3=262144$, $d\approx64$. More precisely, $63.5^3\approx256048$ and $64.5^3\approx268336$, and $262128$ lies between them, so $d$ rounds to $\boxed{64\text{ cm}}$.

Since $3^2+4^2=5^2$, the triangle is right-angled, with legs $3$ and $4$. Its area is $\dfrac12\cdot3\cdot4=6$.

Let $I$ be the centre and $r$ the radius of the inscribed circle. Joining $I$ to the three vertices splits the triangle into three triangles with heights $r$ on the three sides, so
\[6=\dfrac12r(3+4+5)=6r,\]
and $r=\boxed{1}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,3), B=(0,0), C=(4,0), I=(1,1);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(I,1),blue);
pair T3=(0,1), T4=(1,0), T5=I+(3,4)/5;
draw(I--T3,blue+dashed); draw(I--T4,blue+dashed); draw(I--T5,blue+dashed);
dot(I,blue); dot(T3); dot(T4); dot(T5);
draw((0,0.25)--(0.25,0.25)--(0.25,0));
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$I$",I,SW,blue);
label("$3$",(0,2.1),W); label("$4$",(2.6,0),S); label("$5$",(2.3,1.75),NE);
label("$r$",(1,0.5),E,blue);
label("$r$",(0.5,1),N,blue);
label("$r$",(1.45,1.3),SE,blue);
\end{asy}

(Equivalently, for a right triangle the inradius is $\dfrac{a+b-c}{2}=\dfrac{3+4-5}{2}=1$.)

Let $O$ be the centre and $A_0,\dots,A_4$ the vertices, so $OA_k=2$. You may use $\cos36^\circ=\dfrac{1+\sqrt5}{4}$ and $\cos72^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{4}$.

\textbf{The inner vertices.} Let $Q$ be the crossing point of the diagonals $A_0A_2$ and $A_1A_4$, the inner vertex of the star between the points $A_0$ and $A_1$. The reflection in the perpendicular bisector of $A_0A_1$ swaps $A_0\leftrightarrow A_1$ and $A_2\leftrightarrow A_4$, so it swaps the two diagonals and fixes $Q$: hence $Q$ lies on the ray from $O$ through the midpoint of $A_0A_1$, which makes an angle of $36^\circ$ with $OA_0$. The chord $A_1A_4$ subtends the angle $144^\circ$ at $O$, so its distance from $O$ is $2\cos72^\circ$, measured along $OA_0$ (which is perpendicular to $A_1A_4$ by symmetry). Since $OQ$ makes the angle $36^\circ$ with this perpendicular,
\[OQ\cos36^\circ=2\cos72^\circ,\qquad OQ=\dfrac{2\cos72^\circ}{\cos36^\circ}=\dfrac{2(\sqrt5-1)}{\sqrt5+1}=\dfrac{2(\sqrt5-1)^2}{4}=3-\sqrt5.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=2, rho=3-sqrt(5);
pair[] P; pair[] Q;
for(int k=0;k<5;++k){ P.push(R*dir(90+72k)); Q.push(rho*dir(126+72k)); }
path st=P[0]--Q[0]--P[1]--Q[1]--P[2]--Q[2]--P[3]--Q[3]--P[4]--Q[4]--cycle;
fill(st,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
draw(circle((0,0),R),gray(0.6));
draw(P[0]--P[1]--P[2]--P[3]--P[4]--cycle,gray(0.4));
draw(P[0]--P[2]--P[4]--P[1]--P[3]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
pair O=(0,0);
fill(O--P[0]--Q[0]--cycle,rgb(1,0.8,0.7));
draw(O--P[0],red); draw(O--Q[0],red); draw(P[0]--Q[0],red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--0.95*R*dir(126),dashed+gray(0.4));
dot(O); label("$O$",O,S);
for(int k=0;k<5;++k) dot(P[k]);
label("$A_0$",P[0],N); label("$A_1$",P[1],W); label("$A_2$",P[2],SW); label("$A_3$",P[3],SE); label("$A_4$",P[4],E);
dot(Q[0],red); label("$Q$",Q[0],dir(222),red);
label("$2$",(O+P[0])/2,E,red);
draw(arc(O,0.28,90,126),red);
label("$\angle A_0OQ=36^\circ$",(0,-2.05),S,fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{The area.} By symmetry the star consists of $10$ triangles congruent to $OA_0Q$, each with sides $OA_0=2$, $OQ=3-\sqrt5$ and the angle $36^\circ$ between them. Also
\[\sin36^\circ=\sqrt{1-\left(\dfrac{1+\sqrt5}{4}\right)^2}=\sqrt{\dfrac{10-2\sqrt5}{16}}=\dfrac{\sqrt{10-2\sqrt5}}{4}.\]
So the area is
\[10\cdot\dfrac12\cdot2\,(3-\sqrt5)\sin36^\circ=\dfrac52(3-\sqrt5)\sqrt{10-2\sqrt5}.\]
Since $(3-\sqrt5)^2(10-2\sqrt5)=(14-6\sqrt5)(10-2\sqrt5)=200-88\sqrt5$, this equals
\[\dfrac52\sqrt{200-88\sqrt5}=\boxed{5\sqrt{50-22\sqrt5}}\approx4.49.\]

For an equilateral triangle with side $s$, the centre $O$ divides each median in the ratio $2:1$, and the median has length $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s$. So the inradius is $\dfrac13\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s$ and the circumradius is $\dfrac23\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s=\dfrac{s}{\sqrt3}$.

For the large triangle, $s=2\sqrt3$, so the inscribed circle has radius $\dfrac13\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\cdot2\sqrt3=1$. The small triangle has circumradius $1$, so its side is $\sqrt3\cdot1=\sqrt3$ (in fact it can be taken to be the triangle joining the three points of tangency, the midpoints of the sides).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,2), B=(-sqrt(3),-1), C=(sqrt(3),-1), O=(0,0);
pair D=(B+C)/2, Ep=(C+A)/2, F=(A+B)/2;
fill(D--Ep--F--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.8));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O,1),blue);
draw(D--Ep--F--cycle,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--D,blue+dashed); draw(O--A,dashed+gray(0.5));
dot(O); label("$O$",O,E);
label("$1$",(0,-0.5),E,blue);
label("$2$",(0,1.45),W,gray(0.3));
label("$2\sqrt3$",D,S);
label("$\sqrt3$",(D+F)/2,dir(210),red);
\end{asy}

The areas are
\[\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\left(2\sqrt3\right)^2=3\sqrt3\qquad\text{and}\qquad\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\left(\sqrt3\right)^2=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{4},\]
and their sum is $\boxed{\dfrac{15\sqrt3}{4}}$.

question:
In a game, a diamond throne consists of $75$ diamond blocks, and each diamond block is made of $9$ diamonds. A trader gives $5$ diamonds for every $2$ stacks of mycelium (a stack is $64$ blocks).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many stacks of mycelium are needed to get enough diamonds for all $75$ diamond blocks?
\item[(b)] A shulker box holds $27$ stacks. How many shulker boxes are needed to carry this mycelium?
\end{enumerate}

