The angles of a linear pair add up to $180^\circ$. With $\angle A=\angle B+16^\circ$,
\[\angle B+(\angle B+16^\circ)=180^\circ,\qquad 2\angle B=164^\circ,\qquad \angle B=82^\circ.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
draw((-3,0)--(3,0),linewidth(0.9),Arrows(5));
draw(O--2.6*dir(82),linewidth(0.9),Arrow(5));
draw(arc(O,0.7,0,82),blue); draw(arc(O,0.9,82,180),red);
label("$\angle B=82^\circ$",0.75*dir(41),NE,blue);
label("$\angle A=98^\circ$",0.95*dir(140),NW,red);
dot(O);
\end{asy}

So $\boxed{\angle A=98^\circ\text{ and }\angle B=82^\circ}$. (Check: $98^\circ+82^\circ=180^\circ$ and $98^\circ-82^\circ=16^\circ$.)

$\boxed{\text{False}}$. Adjacent angles share a vertex and a side, and have no interior points in common. Take rays $OX$, $OY$, $OZ$ with $\angle XOY=\angle YOZ=30^\circ$. The angles $\angle XOY$ and $\angle YOZ$ are adjacent and congruent, but together they make only $60^\circ$, so $OX$ and $OZ$ are not opposite rays and the angles do not form a linear pair.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
