\textbf{Lemma.} If $Z$ is a point of the segment $XY$ other than $X$ and $Y$, then for every point $P$,
\[PZ<\max\{PX,PY\}.\]
\emph{Proof.} If $P$ lies on the line $XY$, then $P$ is not on both sides of $Z$: if $P$ is on the same side of $Z$ as $X$ (or $P=Z$), then $PY=PZ+ZY>PZ$, and otherwise $PX=PZ+ZX>PZ$. If $P$ is not on the line $XY$, then $\angle PZX+\angle PZY=180^\circ$, so one of these angles, say $\angle PZY$, is at least $90^\circ$. In triangle $PZY$ it is then the largest angle, so the side opposite it is the longest: $PY>PZ$. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{Distances from a point to an interior point.} Since $D$ is inside the triangle, the ray $AD$ meets the side $BC$ at a point $D'$, and $D$ lies strictly between $A$ and $D'$. For any point $P$, the lemma (applied twice) gives
\[PD<\max\{PA,PD'\}\le\max\{PA,PB,PC\},\]
where the second step holds because $D'$ lies strictly between $B$ and $C$ (if $D'$ were $B$ or $C$, then $D$ would lie on a side), so that $PD'<\max\{PB,PC\}$ by the lemma.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(7,0), C=(2.2,4.6);
pair D=(2.6,1.6), Ep=(4.6,1.1);
pair D1=extension(A,D,B,C);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D1,dashed+blue);
draw(Ep--A,dotted+gray(0.4)); draw(Ep--B,dotted+gray(0.4)); draw(Ep--C,dotted+gray(0.4)); draw(Ep--D1,dotted+blue);
draw(D--Ep,red+linewidth(1));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D,red); dot(Ep,red); dot(D1,blue);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N);
label("$D$",D,NW,red); label("$E$",Ep,S,red); label("$D'$",D1,NE,blue);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Conclusion.} Let $M=\max\{AB,BC,CA\}$. With $P=E$,
\[DE<\max\{EA,EB,EC\}.\]
Now apply the same fact to the interior point $E$ and $P=A$: $AE<\max\{AA,AB,AC\}=\max\{AB,AC\}\le M$ (as $AA=0<AE$, the maximum is not $AA$). In the same way $BE<\max\{BA,BC\}\le M$ and $CE<\max\{CA,CB\}\le M$. Hence
\[DE<\max\{EA,EB,EC\}<M=\max\{AB,BC,CA\}.\qquad\blacksquare\]

question:
Harry Potter has lost his glasses. Exactly one of the following happens:
\begin{itemize}
\item with probability $50\%$, Hermione tries a spell to find them; the spell fails with probability $10\%$ (and then he does not get them back) and finds them with probability $90\%$;
\item with probability $25\%$, Harry finds them by himself;
\item with probability $25\%$, someone steps on them; then with probability $20\%$ that person breaks them and never gives them back, with probability $20\%$ breaks them and gives them back (and Hermione repairs them), and with probability $60\%$ gives them back unbroken.
\end{itemize}
What is the probability that Harry gets his (working) glasses back?

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that among any $52$ integers there are two whose sum or difference is divisible by $100$.
\item[(b)] Is the same true for any $51$ integers? Justify your answer.
\end{enumerate}

A cube $ABCDEFGH$ has edge length $10$. Its bottom face is the square $ABCD$, and $E$, $F$, $G$, $H$ lie directly above $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$ respectively. Let $M$ be the midpoint of the edge $\overline{HD}$, let $P$ be a point of the edge $\overline{AB}$, and let $T$ be a point of the edge $\overline{AE}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Suppose that $AT<5$. Show that there is exactly one point $P$ on $\overline{AB}$ for which the lines $MT$, $CP$ and $DA$ are concurrent (pass through one common point), and find $AP$ in terms of $AT$.
\item[(b)] Show that if $5\le AT\le10$, then there is no point $P$ on $\overline{AB}$ for which these three lines are concurrent.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
A common point of the three lines lies on the line $DA$. The line $MT$ lies in the plane of the face $ADHE$, and the line $CP$ lies in the plane of the face $ABCD$; neither is the line $DA$ itself, so each meets the line $DA$ in at most one point. Hence the three lines are concurrent exactly when $MT$ and $CP$ meet the line $DA$, and at the same point.

\textbf{Where $MT$ meets $DA$.} In the face $ADHE$, the segments $AT$ and $DM$ are both perpendicular to $AD$, so they are parallel, and $DM=5$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $AT=5$, then $ATMD$ is a rectangle and $MT\parallel DA$: no common point.
\item If $AT<5$, the line $MT$ meets the line $DA$ at a point $X$ on the extension beyond $A$ (with $X=A$ when $AT=0$). Since $AT\parallel DM$, the triangles $XAT$ and $XDM$ are similar, so $\dfrac{XA}{XD}=\dfrac{AT}{DM}=\dfrac{AT}{5}$.
\item If $AT>5$, then $DM<AT$ and the line $MT$ meets the line $DA$ on the extension beyond $D$.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Where $CP$ meets $DA$.} In the face $ABCD$, the segments $AP$ and $DC$ are parallel and $DC=10$. If $P=B$, then $CP=CB\parallel DA$: no common point. Otherwise $AP<10=DC$, and the line $CP$ meets the line $DA$ at a point $Y$ on the extension beyond $A$ (with $Y=A$ when $P=A$), and by the similar triangles $YAP$ and $YDC$, $\dfrac{YA}{YD}=\dfrac{AP}{DC}=\dfrac{AP}{10}$. In particular $Y$ is never beyond $D$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair o=(0.5,0.35);
pair Pr(real x,real y,real z){ return (x,z)+y*o; }
real t=2, p=4, xa=10*t/(5-t);
pair A=Pr(0,0,0), B=Pr(10,0,0), C=Pr(10,10,0), D=Pr(0,10,0), E_=Pr(0,0,10), F=Pr(10,0,10), G=Pr(10,10,10), H=Pr(0,10,10);
pair M=Pr(0,10,5), T=Pr(0,0,t), P=Pr(p,0,0), X=Pr(0,-xa,0);
draw(D--C,dashed+gray(0.55)); draw(D--H,dashed+gray(0.55));
draw(A--B--F--E_--cycle,gray(0.35)); draw(B--C--G--F,gray(0.35)); draw(E_--H--G,gray(0.35));
draw(X--D,heavygreen+linewidth(0.9));
draw(X--M,red+linewidth(0.9)); draw(X--C,blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(M); dot(T); dot(P); dot(X); dot(A); dot(D);
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,E); label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$E$",E_,NW); label("$F$",F,N); label("$G$",G,NE); label("$H$",H,N);
label("$M$",M,E); label("$T$",T,E); label("$P$",P,S); label("$X$",X,SW);
label("$t$",Pr(0,0,1),E,fontsize(8pt)); label("$2t$",Pr(2,0,0),N,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} Let $AT<5$. Points $Z$ on the extension of $DA$ beyond $A$ (including $A$) are determined by the ratio $\dfrac{ZA}{ZD}=\dfrac{ZA}{ZA+10}$, which increases with $ZA$. So $Y=X$ exactly when
\[\dfrac{AP}{10}=\dfrac{AT}{5},\qquad\text{that is,}\qquad AP=2\,AT.\]
Since $AT<5$, this gives $AP<10$, a point of $\overline{AB}$ other than $B$, and it is the only one. The answer is $\boxed{AP=2\,AT}$.

\textbf{(b)} If $AT=5$, then $MT\parallel DA$ and the lines have no common point. If $5<AT\le10$, the line $MT$ meets the line $DA$ beyond $D$, while $CP$ never does (or is parallel to $DA$). So for $5\le AT\le10$ there is no such point $P$. $\blacksquare$

(The segments $\overline{MT}$, $\overline{CP}$ and $\overline{DA}$ themselves have a common point only in the extreme case $T=P=A$, since $X$ lies on the segment $\overline{DA}$ only when $X=A$.)

question:
$2n$ people of different heights pose for a group photo in $2$ rows of $n$ people each, so that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] in each of the $n$ columns, the person in the back row is taller than the person in the front row, and
\item[(b)] in each row, the heights increase strictly from left to right.
