For example, for $n=4$ and people $P_1,\dots,P_8$ of heights $1,\dots,8$, a possible formation has $P_2,P_5,P_7,P_8$ in the back row and $P_1,P_3,P_4,P_6$ in the front row. In how many ways can the $2n$ people pose? Give the answer in terms of $n$.

A student solves the congruence $7x\equiv5\pmod4$ as follows:
\[7x\equiv5+4\cdot4=21\pmod4,\qquad 7x\cdot7^{-1}\equiv21\cdot7^{-1}\pmod4,\qquad x\equiv3\pmod4.\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Is the answer correct? Check it, and solve the congruence in another way.
\item[(b)] When is it allowed to cancel a factor $k$ from both sides of a congruence $ka\equiv kb\pmod m$? Give an example in which cancelling leads to a false statement.
\end{enumerate}

Does the statement $\dfrac15+\dfrac95\equiv0\pmod2$ make sense?
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Interpret $\dfrac15$ and $\dfrac95$ modulo $2$ as $1\cdot5^{-1}$ and $9\cdot5^{-1}$, where $5^{-1}$ is the inverse of $5$ modulo $2$, and check the congruence.
\item[(b)] Check that this agrees with first computing $\dfrac15+\dfrac95$ as a rational number.
\item[(c)] Explain why $\dfrac12$ has no meaning modulo $2$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Put the big cube at $0\le x,y,z\le5$ with the two edges on the lines $x=y=0$ and $x=y=5$, so that the cutting plane is $x=y$. The unit cubes are $[i,i+1]\times[j,j+1]\times[k,k+1]$ with $i,j,k\in\{0,1,2,3,4\}$.

The plane passes through the interior of such a cube exactly when some interior point has $x=y$, that is, when the open intervals $(i,i+1)$ and $(j,j+1)$ overlap, which happens exactly when $i=j$. If $|i-j|=1$ the plane only touches the cube along an edge, and if $|i-j|\ge2$ it misses it.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<5;++i) fill(shift(i,i)*unitsquare,rgb(1,0.85,0.75));
for(int i=0;i<=5;++i){ draw((i,0)--(i,5),gray(0.6)); draw((0,i)--(5,i),gray(0.6)); }
draw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,5)--(0,5)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0)--(5,5),red+linewidth(1.2));
label("$x$",(5,0),SE); label("$y$",(0,5),NW); label("$0$",(0,0),SW);
label("top view: $x=y$",(2.5,-0.6),S,red);
transform t=shift(9.8,0.2);
pair o=(-0.45,0.3);
pair P(real x,real y,real z){ return t*((x,z)+y*o); }
fill(P(0,0,0)--P(5,5,0)--P(5,5,5)--P(0,0,5)--cycle,rgb(1,0.85,0.75)+opacity(0.75));
draw(P(5,5,0)--P(0,5,0),dashed+gray(0.5)); draw(P(5,5,0)--P(5,0,0),dashed+gray(0.5));
draw(P(0,0,0)--P(5,0,0)--P(5,0,5)--P(0,0,5)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(P(0,0,0)--P(0,5,0)--P(0,5,5)--P(0,0,5)); draw(P(0,5,5)--P(5,5,5)--P(5,0,5));
draw(P(0,0,0)--P(5,5,0),red+dashed); draw(P(0,0,5)--P(5,5,5),red);
draw(P(0,0,0)--P(0,0,5),red+linewidth(1.2)); draw(P(5,5,0)--P(5,5,5),red+dashed+linewidth(1.2));
label("cutting plane",P(5,0,2.5),E,red+fontsize(8pt));
draw(P(5,0,2.5)+(0.1,0)--P(2.5,2.5,2.2),red+linewidth(0.4),EndArrow(3));
\end{asy}

In the top view, the plane crosses the $5$ diagonal squares, and each of them is the top of a column of $5$ unit cubes. So the number of cut cubes is $5\cdot5=25$: $\boxed{\textbf{(B) }25}$.

