solution:
Call the original face $O$, the opposite face $P$, and each of the four faces adjacent to $O$ an $A$-face. From $O$ the ant always moves to an $A$-face, and from $P$ also. From an $A$-face, of its four neighbours one is $O$, one is $P$, and two are $A$-faces.

