fill(P--Q--R--cycle,paleyellow);
draw(arc(M1,4,180,360),gray); draw(arc(M2,3,90,270),gray);
real t=degrees(B-A); draw(arc(M3,5,t,t+180),gray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(P--Q--R--cycle,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(M1--P,dashed); draw(M2--R,dashed); draw(M3--Q,dashed);
draw((0.4,0)--(0.4,0.4)--(0,0.4));
dot(P,red); dot(Q,red); dot(R,red);
label("$C$",C,SW); label("$B$",B,E); label("$A$",A,NW);
label("$P(4,-4)$",P,S,red); label("$Q(7,7)$",Q,NE,red); label("$R(-3,3)$",R,W,red);
label("$8$",(2.2,0),N,fontsize(9pt)); label("$6$",(0,1.6),E,fontsize(9pt)); label("$10$",(2.3,4.3),SW,fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

By the shoelace formula,
\[[PQR]=\dfrac12\left|4(7-3)+7(3-(-4))+(-3)(-4-7)\right|=\dfrac12\,|16+49+33|=\boxed{49}.\]
(In general, for legs $a$ and $b$ the same computation gives $\dfrac{(a+b)^2}{4}$; here $\dfrac{14^2}{4}=49$.)

In a triangle, each side is shorter than the sum of the other two (the triangle inequality). Here
\[x+2x=3x,\]
so the side $3x$ is \emph{not} shorter than the sum of the other two sides, and no triangle has these side lengths. $\blacksquare$

Geometrically: if $PQ=3x$, the circle of radius $x$ about $P$ and the circle of radius $2x$ about $Q$ meet only at the point $R$ of segment $PQ$ with $PR=x$, so the ``triangle'' $PQR$ collapses onto a segment.

