Count the $2$-element subsets of the set $\{1,2,\dots,n+1\}$; there are $\dbinom{n+1}{2}$ of them. Split them according to whether they contain the element $n+1$:
\begin{itemize}
\item subsets not containing $n+1$ are the $2$-element subsets of $\{1,\dots,n\}$: there are $\dbinom n2$;
\item subsets containing $n+1$ are determined by their other element, chosen from $\{1,\dots,n\}$: there are $\dbinom n1$.
\end{itemize}
Every subset is in exactly one of the two groups, so $\dbinom n2+\dbinom n1=\dbinom{n+1}2$. $\blacksquare$

Write each of the numbers $0,1,\dots,1999$ as a four-digit string $d_1d_2d_3d_4$ with leading zeros, where $d_1\in\{0,1\}$; the number $0$ contains no $9$, so it does not matter that it is included. A $9$ can only appear among $d_2,d_3,d_4$.
\begin{itemize}
\item Exactly one $9$: $2$ choices for $d_1$, $3$ choices for the position of the $9$, and $9$ choices for each of the other two digits: $2\cdot3\cdot81=486$.
\item Exactly three $9$'s: $d_2d_3d_4=999$, giving $999$ and $1999$: $2$ numbers.
\end{itemize}
The total is $486+2=\boxed{488}$.

In an equilateral triangle the bisector from $A$ is also the median and the altitude, so $D$ is the midpoint of $BC$ and $\angle ADB=90^\circ$. Let $AB=s$; then $BD=\dfrac s2$.

