question:
A student claims that when a clock shows $h$ hours and $m$ minutes, the two angles between the hands are
\[6m-30h-\dfrac{m}{12}\quad\text{degrees and}\quad360-\left(6m-30h-\dfrac m{12}\right)\text{ degrees}.\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find a correct formula, and show that the student's formula is wrong by testing it at $3{:}30$.
\item[(b)] Find the two angles between the hands at $3{:}40$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] The angles of a triangle are $45^\circ$, $60^\circ$ and $75^\circ$. Find the ratio of the side lengths, in simplest form, listing the sides opposite these angles in this order.
\item[(b)] The side lengths of a triangle are $3$, $5$ and $7$. Find its largest angle.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} By the law of sines, the sides are proportional to the sines of the opposite angles. Since
\[\sin75^\circ=\sin(45^\circ+30^\circ)=\sin45^\circ\cos30^\circ+\cos45^\circ\sin30^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt6+\sqrt2}{4},\]
the ratio is
\[\sin45^\circ:\sin60^\circ:\sin75^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}:\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}:\dfrac{\sqrt6+\sqrt2}{4}=2\sqrt2:2\sqrt3:\left(\sqrt6+\sqrt2\right).\]
Dividing by $\sqrt2$ gives $\boxed{2:\sqrt6:\left(1+\sqrt3\right)}$. (Check: $\dfrac{2}{\sin45^\circ}=\dfrac{\sqrt6}{\sin60^\circ}=\dfrac{1+\sqrt3}{\sin75^\circ}=2\sqrt2$.)

\textbf{(b)} The largest angle $\gamma$ is opposite the longest side $7$. By the law of cosines,
\[\cos\gamma=\dfrac{3^2+5^2-7^2}{2\cdot3\cdot5}=\dfrac{-15}{30}=-\dfrac12,\]
so $\gamma=\boxed{120^\circ}$.

