label("$45^\circ$",B,(3,1.2),fontsize(8pt)); label("$60^\circ$",C,(-3.2,1.2),fontsize(8pt)); label("$75^\circ$",A,(0.2,-2.6),fontsize(8pt));
label("$1+\sqrt3$",(B+C)/2,S,blue); label("$2$",(A+C)/2,NE,blue); label("$\sqrt6$",(A+B)/2,NW,blue);
label("(a)",(1.4,-1.0));
picture q;
pair P=(0,0), Q=(5,0), R=(6.5,3*sqrt(3)/2);
draw(q,P--Q--R--cycle,linewidth(1));
label(q,"$5$",(P+Q)/2,S,blue); label(q,"$3$",(Q+R)/2,SE,blue); label(q,"$7$",(P+R)/2,NW,blue);
draw(q,arc(Q,0.5,60,180),red); label(q,"$120^\circ$",Q+0.6*dir(120),NW,red+fontsize(8pt));
label(q,"(b)",(3.2,-1.0));
add(shift(4.5,0)*scale(0.5)*q);
\end{asy}

In general, the angles determine the shape but not the size, so they give only the ratio of the sides (law of sines); the three sides determine the angles through the law of cosines.

question:
There are $m$ boxes, each with $n\ge1$ storage slots. A box may be placed in a slot of another box, each slot holds at most one box, and no box may contain itself, directly or through other boxes. All the boxes are packed into a single box: every box except one (the outermost box) lies in a slot of some box.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that the number of vacant slots is the same however the boxes are arranged.
\item[(b)] Find this number in terms of $m$ and $n$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{Lemma.} If $Z$ is a point of a segment $XY$ and $P$ is any point, then $PZ\le\max(PX,PY)$.

\emph{Proof.} If $Z=X$ or $Z=Y$ there is nothing to prove. If $P$ lies on the line $XY$, the distance from $P$ to points of the segment is largest at an endpoint. Otherwise, the angles $\angle PZX$ and $\angle PZY$ add up to $180^\circ$, so one of them, say $\angle PZY$, is at least $90^\circ$. Then it is the largest angle of triangle $PZY$, so the opposite side $PY$ is longer than $PZ$. Hence $PZ\le\max(PX,PY)$.

\textbf{Main proof.}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(6,0), A=(2.2,3.2);
pair D=(3.8,0), E=C+0.55*(A-C);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(D--E,red+linewidth(1)); draw(D--A,dashed+blue);
dot(D); dot(E);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,S); label("$E$",E,NE);
\end{asy}

Since $E$ lies on segment $CA$, the lemma (with $P=D$) gives $DE\le\max(DC,DA)$. Now $DC\le BC$ because $D$ lies on segment $BC$. And since $D$ lies on segment $BC$, the lemma (with $P=A$) gives $DA\le\max(AB,AC)\le BC$, because $BC$ is a longest side. Therefore
\[DE\le\max(DC,DA)\le BC.\]
$\blacksquare$

\textbf{With an equation.} Put the origin at the middle of the base. The ellipse is
\[\dfrac{x^2}{50^2}+\dfrac{y^2}{40^2}=1.\]
A point $5$ feet from the edge has $x=45$, so
\[y=40\sqrt{1-\dfrac{45^2}{50^2}}=40\sqrt{1-\dfrac{81}{100}}=40\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt{19}}{10}=\boxed{4\sqrt{19}}\approx17.4\text{ feet}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real k=0.06;
path e=scale(50k,40k)*arc((0,0),1,0,180);
draw(e,blue+linewidth(1));
draw((-55k,0)--(55k,0));
draw((45k,0)--(45k,4*sqrt(19)*k),red+dashed); dot((45k,4*sqrt(19)*k),red);
label("$h$",(45k,2*sqrt(19)*k),W,red);
draw((0,0)--(0,40k),dashed); label("$40$",(0,20k),W);
draw((-50k,-0.35)--(50k,-0.35),Arrows(4)); label("$100$",(0,-0.35),S);
draw((45k,-0.9)--(50k,-0.9),Arrows(3)); label("$5$",(47.5k,-0.9),S,fontsize(8pt));
label("$O$",(0,0),SW,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Another way: squash a semicircle.} The arch is the semicircle of radius $50$ squeezed vertically by the factor $\dfrac{40}{50}=\dfrac45$. At $45$ feet from the centre, the semicircle has height $\sqrt{50^2-45^2}=\sqrt{475}=5\sqrt{19}$, so the arch has height $\dfrac45\cdot5\sqrt{19}=4\sqrt{19}$ feet.

Every turn is a right turn, so the directions repeat in the cycle east, south, west, north (say she starts walking east); stretch $k$ goes east, south, west or north according as $k$ leaves remainder $1$, $2$, $3$ or $0$ on division by $4$. Consecutive odd integers differ by $2$, so $x_{k+2}=x_k+4$.

\textbf{East-west.} The eastward stretches are $x_1,x_5,\dots,x_{97}$ and the westward ones $x_3,x_7,\dots,x_{99}$. Pairing them, the net eastward distance is
\[(x_1-x_3)+(x_5-x_7)+\cdots+(x_{97}-x_{99})=25\cdot(-4)=-100.\]

\textbf{North-south.} The southward stretches are $x_2,x_6,\dots,x_{98}$ and the northward ones $x_4,x_8,\dots,x_{100}$, so the net northward distance is
\[(x_4-x_2)+(x_8-x_6)+\cdots+(x_{100}-x_{98})=25\cdot4=100.\]

So she ends $100$ yards west and $100$ yards north of home, and
\[d=\sqrt{100^2+100^2}=\boxed{100\sqrt2}.\]
The answer does not depend on $x_1$. The path is a spiral that turns right every time; after every $4$ stretches she is $4$ yards further west and $4$ yards further north (the figure shows $x_k=2k-1$ for $k\le12$):

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] dirs={(1,0),(0,-1),(-1,0),(0,1)};
pair p=(0,0);
for(int k=1;k<=12;++k){pair q=p+(2k-1)*dirs[(k-1)%4]; draw(p--q,k<=4?blue:gray,Arrow(5)); p=q;}
dot((0,0)); label("home",(0,0),SW,fontsize(8pt));
dot((-4,4),blue); label("after $4$",(-4,4),W,blue+fontsize(8pt));
dot((-12,12)); label("after $12$",(-12,12),NE,fontsize(8pt));
draw((0,0)--(-12,12),dashed+red);
label("$x_1$",(0.5,0),N,fontsize(8pt)); label("$x_2$",(1,-1.5),E,fontsize(8pt)); label("$x_3$",(-1.5,-3),S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$x_4$",(-4,0.5),W,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The pole, the vertical drop and the ground form a right triangle with hypotenuse $5$ m and an angle of $20^\circ$ at the base of the pole. The honey lands at the foot of the vertical through the top, at distance $d$ from the base:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
