\end{itemize}
Substituting, $m\angle APQ+m\angle BQP=180^\circ$, so the two angles are supplementary. $\blacksquare$

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that
\[\dfrac{\log_8 18}{\log_9 5}=\dfrac{2\log3\,(\log2+2\log3)}{3\log2\,\log5},\]
where $\log$ denotes the logarithm to any fixed base.
\item[(b)] Using $\log_{10}2\approx0.3010$ and $\log_{10}3\approx0.4771$, estimate $\dfrac{\log_8 18}{\log_9 5}$ to three decimal places, without a calculator for logarithms.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The bisector meets side $BC$ at a point $D$ between $B$ and $C$, and continues to $K$ on the other side of line $BC$ from $A$. So $K$ lies on the arc $BC$ not containing $A$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=dir(115), B=dir(205), C=dir(335);
real aA=degrees(B-A), aC=degrees(C-A);
pair K=dir(270);
draw(circle((0,0),1),blue);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
pair D=extension(A,K,B,C);
draw(A--K,red); draw(B--K,deepgreen); draw(K--C,deepgreen);
dot(D); dot(K);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,E); label("$K$",K,S); label("$D$",D,NE);
draw(arc(A,0.3,degrees(B-A),degrees(K-A)),red); draw(arc(A,0.36,degrees(K-A),degrees(C-A)),red);
pair m1=(B+K)/2, m2=(K+C)/2; draw(m1+0.05*unit(rotate(90)*(K-B))*(-1)--m1+0.05*unit(rotate(90)*(K-B)),deepgreen); draw(m2+0.05*unit(rotate(90)*(C-K))*(-1)--m2+0.05*unit(rotate(90)*(C-K)),deepgreen);
\end{asy}

The inscribed angle $\angle BAK$ subtends the arc $BK$ (not containing $A$), and the inscribed angle $\angle KAC$ subtends the arc $KC$ (not containing $A$). An inscribed angle is half of its arc, and $\angle BAK=\angle KAC$ because $AK$ bisects angle $A$. Hence the arcs $BK$ and $KC$ are equal, and together they make up the arc $BC$ not containing $A$. So $K$ is the midpoint of that arc. $\blacksquare$ (Equivalently, the chords $KB$ and $KC$ are equal.)

Write $a=BC$, $b=CA$, $c=AB$, and let the angles at $A$ and $B$ be $2\alpha$ and $2\beta$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(7,0), C=(2.4,4);
real a=abs(B-C), b0=abs(C-A), c=abs(A-B);
pair X=C+(b0,0), Y=C-(a,0);
pair D=extension(A,X,B,C), E=extension(B,Y,A,C);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(Y-(0.6,0)--X+(0.6,0));
draw(A--X,blue); draw(B--Y,red);
draw(D--X,blue+linewidth(1.6)); draw(E--Y,red+linewidth(1.6));
dot(D); dot(E); dot(X); dot(Y);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N); label("$D$",D,E); label("$E$",E,dir(200)); label("$X$",X,N); label("$Y$",Y,N);
draw(arc(A,0.7,0,degrees(X-A)),blue); draw(arc(A,0.8,degrees(X-A),degrees(C-A)),blue);
draw(arc(B,0.7,degrees(Y-B),180),red); draw(arc(B,0.8,degrees(C-B),degrees(Y-B)),red);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Isosceles triangles.} Since $XY\parallel AB$, $\angle CXA=\angle XAB=\alpha=\angle XAC$ (alternate angles, then the bisector). So triangle $ACX$ is isosceles with $CX=CA=b$, and its base is $AX=2b\cos\alpha$ (drop the perpendicular from $C$ to $AX$). In the same way $CY=CB=a$ and $BY=2a\cos\beta$.

\textbf{The segments $XD$ and $YE$.} The triangles $DCX$ and $DBA$ are similar ($CX\parallel BA$), so $\dfrac{DX}{DA}=\dfrac{CX}{BA}=\dfrac bc$, and $D$ lies between $A$ and $X$. Hence $XD=\dfrac{b}{b+c}AX$, and similarly $YE=\dfrac{a}{a+c}BY$:
\[XD=\cos\alpha\cdot\dfrac{2b^2}{b+c},\qquad YE=\cos\beta\cdot\dfrac{2a^2}{a+c}.\]

\textbf{Comparison.} Suppose $a>b$. The larger side is opposite the larger angle, so $2\alpha>2\beta$, and since cosine decreases on $(0^\circ,90^\circ)$, $\cos\alpha<\cos\beta$. Also $\dfrac{t^2}{t+c}=t\cdot\dfrac{t}{t+c}$ increases for $t>0$, being the product of the positive increasing factors $t$ and $\dfrac{t}{t+c}=1-\dfrac{c}{t+c}$; so $\dfrac{b^2}{b+c}<\dfrac{a^2}{a+c}$. Multiplying the two inequalities between positive numbers, $XD<YE$, which contradicts $YE=XD$. In the same way $a<b$ gives $XD>YE$. Therefore $a=b$, that is, $AC=BC$. $\blacksquare$

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] For nonzero complex numbers $z$ and $w$, prove that $\arg(zw)=\arg z+\arg w$ and $\arg\dfrac zw=\arg z-\arg w$, where the equalities hold up to a multiple of $360^\circ$.
\item[(b)] Use (a) with $z=2+i$ and $w=3+i$ to prove that $\arctan\dfrac12+\arctan\dfrac13=45^\circ$.
\item[(c)] Similarly prove that $\arctan3-\arctan\dfrac12=45^\circ$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Write $z=r(\cos\alpha+i\sin\alpha)$ and $w=s(\cos\beta+i\sin\beta)$ with $r,s>0$, where $\alpha=\arg z$ and $\beta=\arg w$. Then
\[zw=rs\big[(\cos\alpha\cos\beta-\sin\alpha\sin\beta)+i(\sin\alpha\cos\beta+\cos\alpha\sin\beta)\big]=rs\big[\cos(\alpha+\beta)+i\sin(\alpha+\beta)\big]\]
by the angle addition formulas, so $zw$ has argument $\alpha+\beta$ (up to a multiple of $360^\circ$). Applying this to $\dfrac zw\cdot w=z$ gives $\arg\dfrac zw+\arg w=\arg z$, so $\arg\dfrac zw=\arg z-\arg w$. So, to add or subtract arguments, multiply or divide the numbers and take one argument of the result.

\textbf{(b)} $2+i$ and $3+i$ lie in the first quadrant, with arguments $\alpha=\arctan\dfrac12$ and $\beta=\arctan\dfrac13$. Their product is
\[(2+i)(3+i)=6+2i+3i+i^2=5+5i,\]
whose argument is $45^\circ$. By (a), $\alpha+\beta=45^\circ+k\cdot360^\circ$; since $0^\circ<\alpha+\beta<180^\circ$, $k=0$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-0.4,0)--(5.8,0),Arrow(5)); draw((0,-0.4)--(0,5.6),Arrow(5));
label("$\mathrm{Re}$",(5.8,0),E); label("$\mathrm{Im}$",(0,5.6),N);
pair z=(2,1), w=(3,1), p=(5,5);
draw((0,0)--z,blue); draw((0,0)--w,red); draw((0,0)--p,deepgreen);
dot(z,blue); dot(w,red); dot(p,deepgreen);
label("$z=2+i$",z,NW,blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$w=3+i$",w,SE,red+fontsize(8pt)); label("$zw=5+5i$",p,E,deepgreen+fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc((0,0),1.0,0,degrees(z)),blue); label("$\alpha$",1.2*dir(degrees(z)/2),blue+fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc((0,0),1.6,0,degrees(w)),red); label("$\beta$",1.85*dir(degrees(w)/2)+(0.15,0),red+fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc((0,0),2.6,0,45),deepgreen); label("$\alpha+\beta=45^\circ$",2.75*dir(30),E,deepgreen+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(c)} $\arg(1+3i)=\arctan3$ and $\arg(2+i)=\arctan\dfrac12$, and
\[\dfrac{1+3i}{2+i}=\dfrac{(1+3i)(2-i)}{(2+i)(2-i)}=\dfrac{2-i+6i+3}{5}=1+i,\]
whose argument is $45^\circ$. Since $\arctan3-\arctan\dfrac12$ lies between $-90^\circ$ and $90^\circ$, it equals $45^\circ$. $\blacksquare$

Let $P=(x,y)$. The line $CD$ is $y=4$; reflecting over it keeps $x$ and replaces $y$ by $8-y$, so $Q=(x,8-y)$. The line $BC$ is $x=4$; reflecting over it replaces $x$ by $8-x$, so $R=(8-x,8-y)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(4,0), C=(4,4), D=(0,4), P=(1.5,1), Q=(1.5,7), R=(6.5,7);
draw((0,4)--(7.5,4),dashed+gray); draw((4,0)--(4,8),dashed+gray);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--Q,dotted+blue); draw(Q--R,dotted+blue);
draw(P--R,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C,red); dot(D); dot(P); dot(Q); dot(R);
label("$A(0,0)$",A,SW); label("$B(4,0)$",B,S); label("$C(4,4)$",C,SE,red); label("$D(0,4)$",D,W);
label("$P(x,y)$",P,SE); label("$Q(x,8-y)$",Q,NW); label("$R(8-x,8-y)$",R,NE);
label("line $CD$",(7.5,4),N,gray); label("line $BC$",(4,8),E,gray);
\end{asy}

The midpoint of $PR$ is
\[\left(\dfrac{x+(8-x)}{2},\ \dfrac{y+(8-y)}{2}\right)=\boxed{(4,4)},\]
the vertex $C$, whatever $P$ is. (Two reflections in perpendicular lines through $C$ amount to a half-turn about $C$, so $C$ is always the midpoint of $PR$.)

The proof is correct; the only step that needs justification is $h_1+h_2=h$.

Draw the line through $E$ perpendicular to $AB$. Since $CD\parallel AB$, it is also perpendicular to $CD$; let it meet line $AB$ at $F$ and line $CD$ at $G$. Then $EF=h_1$, $EG=h_2$ and $FG=h$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(6,0), C=(8,3.5), D=(2,3.5), X=(4.4,1.3), F=(4.4,0), G=(4.4,3.5);
fill(A--X--B--cycle,rgb(0.8,0.88,1)); fill(C--X--D--cycle,rgb(1,0.88,0.75));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--X--B,blue); draw(C--X--D,orange);
draw(F--G,dashed);
real t=0.22;
draw((F.x,t)--(F.x+t,t)--(F.x+t,0)); draw((G.x,3.5-t)--(G.x+t,3.5-t)--(G.x+t,3.5));
dot(X); dot(F); dot(G);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$E$",(3.97,1.36)); label("$F$",F,S); label("$G$",G,N);
label("$h_1$",(F.x,0.62),E); label("$h_2$",(F.x,2.45),E);
label("$x$",(1.6,0),S); label("$x$",(6.4,3.5),N);
\end{asy}

Because $E$ is inside the parallelogram, it lies in the strip between the parallel lines $AB$ and $CD$, so $E$ lies on the segment $FG$. Hence $FE+EG=FG$, that is, $h_1+h_2=h$.

The rest is the area formula for a triangle: triangle $AEB$ has base $AB=x$ and height $h_1$, and triangle $CED$ has base $CD=x$ and height $h_2$. So
\[[AEB]+[CED]=\dfrac12xh_1+\dfrac12xh_2=\dfrac12xh=\dfrac12[ABCD].\]
The student's argument is a complete proof. (The textbook proof, which draws the line through $E$ parallel to $AB$ and splits $ABCD$ into two parallelograms, each containing one of the triangles as half of it, proves the same thing with more steps.) $\blacksquare$

question:
A \emph{Y-piece} consists of five unit squares: four in a row, and a fifth attached below the second square of the row.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(2.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int c=0;c<4;++c) filldraw(shift(c,1)*unitsquare,rgb(0.72,0.84,1),linewidth(0.9));
filldraw(shift(1,0)*unitsquare,rgb(0.72,0.84,1),linewidth(0.9));
\end{asy}

A student claims that a $15\times15$ table of unit squares cannot be completely covered, without overlaps, by Y-pieces (each piece covering exactly five squares of the table; pieces may be rotated and turned over). Prove or disprove the claim.

solution:
The claim is false: the table can be tiled.

First, there is no area obstruction: $15\cdot15=225=45\cdot5$, so a tiling would use $45$ pieces. To disprove the claim it is enough to exhibit one tiling. The figure shows a tiling by $45$ Y-pieces (thick lines separate the pieces; the colours only help to tell neighbouring pieces apart).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
string[] g={"AAAAMNNNNODDDDC","BAMMMMNQOOPDICC","BLLLLQQQQOPIIJC","BBLUTTTTSOPPIJC","BUUUUTSSSSPRIJJ","VVVVkkkkjRRRRJK","WVqqqqkjjYYYYKK","WWrqppppjlmYZXK","WrrrrponjlmZZXK","WissssonllmmZXX","iihsgoonnlmbZXH","EihggfonebbbbHH","EihhgfeeeeaaaaH","EEhFgffdccccaGH","EFFFFfddddcGGGG"};
string L="ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrs";
string cm="313123300210202311032022011221020102113310212";
pen[] pal={rgb(0.72,0.84,1),rgb(1,0.82,0.62),rgb(0.78,0.94,0.7),rgb(0.98,0.76,0.84)};
for(int r=0;r<15;++r) for(int c=0;c<15;++c){
  int k=find(L,substr(g[r],c,1));
  int ci=(int)substr(cm,k,1);
  fill(shift(c,14-r)*unitsquare,pal[ci]);
}
for(int i=0;i<=15;++i){draw((i,0)--(i,15),gray(0.6)+linewidth(0.3)); draw((0,i)--(15,i),gray(0.6)+linewidth(0.3));}
for(int r=0;r<15;++r) for(int c=0;c<15;++c){
  if(c<14 && substr(g[r],c,1)!=substr(g[r],c+1,1)) draw((c+1,14-r)--(c+1,15-r),linewidth(1.1));
  if(r<14 && substr(g[r],c,1)!=substr(g[r+1],c,1)) draw((c,14-r)--(c+1,14-r),linewidth(1.1));
}
draw((0,0)--(15,0)--(15,15)--(0,15)--cycle,linewidth(1.4));
\end{asy}

Each of the $45$ outlined regions consists of four squares in a straight line together with one square attached to the second square of that line, so each is a Y-piece in some position (rotated and possibly turned over). The pieces do not overlap and cover all $225$ squares. Therefore the claim is $\boxed{\text{false}}$: a $15\times15$ table can be tiled with Y-pieces. $\blacksquare$

(Finding such a tiling by hand takes patience. A natural first attempt, splitting the table into blocks that are easy to tile, such as $5\times10$ rectangles, does not work, because $225$ is not a multiple of $50$; the pieces in the figure interlock across the whole table.)

question:
Let $a,b,c$ be positive real numbers, each different from $1$ where it is used as a base. Recall that $\log_ax=y$ means $a^y=x$. Using only this definition and the laws of exponents, prove:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $\log_ab\cdot\log_bc=\log_ac$;
\item[(b)] $\log_ba=\dfrac{1}{\log_ab}$;
\item[(c)] $\log_{\dfrac1a}b=-\log_ab$;
\item[(d)] $\log_{a^m}a^n=\dfrac{n}{m}$ for real numbers $m\ne0$ and $n$.
\end{enumerate}
Also explain why the property $\log_a1=0$ holds for every base $a>0$ with $a\ne1$, and why the condition ``$a\ne0$'' is not the right condition for it.

solution:
\textbf{All solutions.} List the five numbers in increasing order, $x_1\le x_2\le x_3\le x_4\le x_5$. Then
\[x_1x_2x_3x_4x_5=x_1+\cdots+x_5\le5x_5,\]
so $x_1x_2x_3x_4\le5$. Going through the possibilities:
\begin{itemize}
\item $(1,1,1,1)$: $x_5=4+x_5$, impossible.
\item $(1,1,1,2)$: $2x_5=5+x_5$, so $x_5=5$.
\item $(1,1,1,3)$: $3x_5=6+x_5$, so $x_5=3$.
\item $(1,1,1,4)$: $4x_5=7+x_5$, no integer solution; $(1,1,2,2)$: $4x_5=6+x_5$, so $x_5=2$.
\item $(1,1,1,5)$: $5x_5=8+x_5$ gives $x_5=2<x_4$, impossible.
\end{itemize}
So $\{a,b,c,d,e\}$ is one of the multisets $\{1,1,1,2,5\}$, $\{1,1,1,3,3\}$, $\{1,1,2,2,2\}$, with common value $P=abcde$ equal to $10$, $9$, $8$ respectively.

\textbf{Maximising.} Since $abcd=\dfrac Pe$, we have $abcd+e=\dfrac Pe+e$, where $e$ is one of the five numbers:
\begin{itemize}
\item $P=10$: $e=1,2,5$ give $11,\ 7,\ 7$;
\item $P=9$: $e=1,3$ give $10,\ 6$;
\item $P=8$: $e=1,2$ give $9,\ 6$.
\end{itemize}
The maximum is $\boxed{11}$, attained for example by $(a,b,c,d,e)=(1,1,2,5,1)$: $1\cdot1\cdot2\cdot5\cdot1=10=1+1+2+5+1$ and $abcd+e=10+1=11$.

\textbf{With the theorem.} Here $k_1=1$, $k_2=\dfrac12$, $k_3=\dfrac13$, so
\[k_1+k_2+k_3=\dfrac{11}{6},\qquad k_1k_2+k_2k_3+k_3k_1=\dfrac12+\dfrac16+\dfrac13=1,\]
and $k_4=\dfrac{11}{6}\pm2$, that is, $k_4=\dfrac{23}{6}$ or $k_4=-\dfrac16$. The positive value is a small circle in the gap between the three circles, of radius $\boxed{\dfrac{6}{23}}$; the negative value is a circle enclosing all three, of radius $\boxed{6}$.

\textbf{Check with coordinates.} The distances between the centres are $1+2=3$, $1+3=4$ and $2+3=5$, so the centres form a $3$-$4$-$5$ right triangle; put them at $A=(0,0)$, $B=(3,0)$, $C=(0,4)$ (radii $1,2,3$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(3,0),C=(0,4),O=(3,4),I=(21/23,20/23);
real rho=6/23;
draw(circle(O,6),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(A,1),linewidth(0.9)); draw(circle(B,2),linewidth(0.9)); draw(circle(C,3),linewidth(0.9));
filldraw(circle(I,rho),rgb(1,0.8,0.6),red);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,dashed+gray);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(O,blue);
label("$A$",A,SE); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$(3,4)$",O,SE,blue);
draw(A--(-1,0)); label("$1$",(-0.5,0),N);
draw(B--(5,0)); label("$2$",(4,0),N);
draw(C--(0,7)); label("$3$",(0,5.5),E);
draw(O--(O+6*dir(20)),blue); label("$6$",O+3*dir(20),N,blue);
draw((1.6,-2.05)--I,red,Arrow(4)); label("$\dfrac{6}{23}$",(1.6,-2.05),S,red);
\end{asy}

\emph{Enclosing circle.} The point $(3,4)$ is at distance $5,4,3$ from $A,B,C$, which equal $6-1$, $6-2$, $6-3$; so the circle with centre $(3,4)$ and radius $6$ is internally tangent to all three circles.

\emph{Small circle.} Let its centre be $(x,y)$ and radius $\rho$, with distances $1+\rho$, $2+\rho$, $3+\rho$ to $A,B,C$:
\[x^2+y^2=(1+\rho)^2,\quad(x-3)^2+y^2=(2+\rho)^2,\quad x^2+(y-4)^2=(3+\rho)^2.\]
Subtracting the first equation from the second gives $-6x+9=3+2\rho$, so $x=1-\dfrac\rho3$; subtracting it from the third gives $-8y+16=8+4\rho$, so $y=1-\dfrac\rho2$. Substituting into the first equation:
\[\left(1-\dfrac\rho3\right)^2+\left(1-\dfrac\rho2\right)^2=(1+\rho)^2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad23\rho^2+132\rho-36=0,\]
so $\rho=\dfrac{-132\pm144}{46}$, that is, $\rho=\dfrac{6}{23}$ (the root $-6$ corresponds to the enclosing circle, with $1+\rho=-5$ and so on). The small circle has centre $\left(\dfrac{21}{23},\dfrac{20}{23}\right)$ and radius $\dfrac{6}{23}$, in agreement with the theorem.

The divisor $10_6$ is $6$, and the number is $2\cdot216+36X+30+Y\equiv Y\pmod6$, so it is divisible by $6$ exactly when $Y\equiv0\pmod 6$. So far the argument is right. The errors are about digits: in base six the digits are only $0,1,2,3,4,5$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $Y=6$ is not a base-six digit, so $Y=0$ is the only choice.
\item $X$ has $6$ possible values ($0$ to $5$), not $10$.
\end{itemize}
Hence there are $1\cdot6=\boxed{6}$ such numbers: $2050_6,2150_6,\dots,2550_6$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] At $h$ hours and $m$ minutes ($0\le h\le11$, $0\le m<60$), show that the minute hand of a clock is $6m$ degrees and the hour hand is $30h+\dfrac m2$ degrees clockwise from the $12$. Deduce that the angle between the hands is $\left|30h-\dfrac{11m}{2}\right|$ degrees, or $360^\circ$ minus this if it exceeds $180^\circ$.
\item[(b)] Find the angle between the hands at $9{:}48$.
\item[(c)] At what time between $2{:}00$ and $3{:}00$ do the two hands point in the same direction?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Multiply by $4$ and complete the squares:
\[(2x-31)^2-961=(2y-41)^2-1681,\qquad\text{so}\qquad(2y-41)^2-(2x-31)^2=720.\]
Let $u=2y-41$ and $v=2x-31$; both are odd. Then $(u-v)(u+v)=720$ with both factors even, so $u-v=2s$ and $u+v=2t$ with integers $s,t$ and $st=180$. Conversely, $u=s+t$ and $v=t-s$ are odd exactly when $s$ and $t$ have opposite parity; since $180=4\cdot45$, this means one of $s,t$ is odd and the other a multiple of $4$.

The odd divisors of $180$ are $1,3,5,9,15,45$. Taking $s$ or $t$ odd and both positive gives $12$ pairs $(s,t)$, and both negative gives $12$ more, so there are $24$ solutions, with
\[x=\dfrac{v+31}2=\dfrac{t-s+31}{2},\qquad y=\dfrac{u+41}{2}=\dfrac{s+t+41}{2}.\]
For example $(s,t)=(1,180)$ gives $(x,y)=(105,111)$ and $(s,t)=(5,36)$ gives $(31,41)$. Changing the signs of $s,t$ replaces $(x,y)$ by $(31-x,41-y)$, and swapping $s$ and $t$ replaces $x$ by $31-x$. Grouping the solutions by the common value $x(x-31)=y(y-41)$:
\begin{itemize}
\item value $0$: $x\in\{0,31\}$, $y\in\{0,41\}$;
\item value $-210$: $x\in\{10,21\}$, $y\in\{6,35\}$;
\item value $-238$: $x\in\{14,17\}$, $y\in\{7,34\}$;
\item value $180$: $x\in\{-5,36\}$, $y\in\{-4,45\}$;
\item value $572$: $x\in\{-13,44\}$, $y\in\{-11,52\}$;
\item value $7770$: $x\in\{-74,105\}$, $y\in\{-70,111\}$.
\end{itemize}
The solutions are the $\boxed{24}$ pairs $(x,y)$ with $x$ and $y$ chosen from the two sets in the same line.

question:
