\item[(b)] Through how many degrees does the hour hand turn from $1{:}15$ to $3{:}45$ on the same afternoon?
\item[(c)] What is the angle between the hour hand and the minute hand at $10{:}15$?
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the smallest positive integer that leaves a remainder of $2$ when divided by $4$ and also when divided by $21$?
\item[(b)] What is the smallest such integer greater than $2$?
\end{enumerate}

A thief stole between $44$ and $60$ fruits, inclusive, and touched between $70$ and $89$ fruits, inclusive. The police know that:
\begin{itemize}
\item the number of fruits stolen is even, is a factor of $8008$, and is a multiple of $13$;
\item the number of fruits touched is even, is a factor of $5976$, and is a multiple of $12$.
\end{itemize}
A thief's clumsiness rating is $\dfrac{F_T-F_S}{F_T}$, where $F_T$ is the number of fruits touched and $F_S$ the number stolen. Find the thief's clumsiness rating. (No calculators.)

Alice and Bob are each thinking of a quadratic polynomial in $x$. Their polynomials take the same value at $x=1$, the same value at $x=2$, and the same value at $x=3$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Must their polynomials be the same?
\item[(b)] Would equal values at $x=1$ and $x=2$ alone be enough?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{Probability of success on one day.} Farley's letters can be arranged in $\dfrac{6!}{3!}=120$ different ways. A match needs the same letter in both arrangements. The fixed arrangement has one A, one G, one N and three C's; Farley's has one A, one C, one N and three G's. So there is at most one matching A, at most one matching N, at most one matching C (Farley has only one C) and at most one matching G (the fixed arrangement has only one G): at most $4$ matches in all. Thus ``at least $4$'' means exactly these four matches:
\begin{itemize}
\item Farley's A and N are at the positions of the fixed A and N;
\item Farley's C is at one of the $3$ positions of the fixed C's;
\item the three remaining positions, which include the position of the fixed G, receive Farley's three G's.
\end{itemize}
So exactly $3$ of the $120$ arrangements succeed, whatever the fixed arrangement is, and the probability of success on a given day is $p=\dfrac{3}{120}=\dfrac1{40}$.

\textbf{Expected number of days.} Let $E$ be the expected number of days. On the first day she succeeds with probability $p$; otherwise the situation starts afresh after one day. Hence
\[E=p\cdot1+(1-p)(1+E),\qquad\text{so}\qquad E=\dfrac1p=\boxed{40}.\]

Since $196=4\cdot49$ with $\gcd(4,49)=1$, we need $4\mid x(x-3)$ and $49\mid x(x-3)$.

\textbf{Modulo $4$.} Checking $x=0,1,2,3$: $x(x-3)\equiv0,2,2,0\pmod4$. So $x\equiv0$ or $3\pmod4$.

\textbf{Modulo $49$.} A common divisor of $x$ and $x-3$ divides $3$, so $7$ cannot divide both. Hence if $49\mid x(x-3)$, both factors of $7$ come from the same factor: $49\mid x$ or $49\mid x-3$. So $x\equiv0$ or $3\pmod{49}$.

\textbf{Combining} (Chinese remainder theorem), there are four residues modulo $196$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $x\equiv0\pmod4$, $x\equiv0\pmod{49}$: $x\equiv0$;
\item $x\equiv3\pmod4$, $x\equiv3\pmod{49}$: $x\equiv3$;
\item $x\equiv0\pmod4$, $x\equiv3\pmod{49}$: $x=3+49k$ with $3+k\equiv0\pmod4$, so $k=1$ and $x\equiv52$;
\item $x\equiv3\pmod4$, $x\equiv0\pmod{49}$: $x=49k$ with $k\equiv3\pmod4$, so $x\equiv147$.
\end{itemize}
The answer is $\boxed{x\equiv0,\ 3,\ 52,\ 147\pmod{196}}$.

The first line is right: $2^{-2}=\dfrac14$ and $\left(\dfrac52\right)^{-3}=\left(\dfrac25\right)^3$. There are two errors:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\left(\dfrac25\right)^3=\dfrac{2^3}{5^3}=\dfrac{8}{125}$, not $\dfrac{6}{125}$ (the cube was computed as $2\cdot3$);
\item $32\times35=1120$, not $1620$.
\end{itemize}
The later steps are arithmetic on these wrong numbers. Correctly,
\[32\times35\times\dfrac14\times\dfrac{8}{125}=1120\times\dfrac{8}{500}=\dfrac{8960}{500}=\boxed{\dfrac{448}{25}}=17\dfrac{23}{25}=17.92.\]

question:
A student writes two columns of six consecutive integers, $10,11,12,13,14,15$ and $15,16,17,18,19,20$, so that each number in the second column is $5$ more than the number beside it. The student finds the column sums $60$ and $85$, ``a difference of $25$''.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the correct column sums and their difference, and explain where the numbers $60$ and $85$ come from.
\item[(b)] Prove that if each of $n$ numbers is increased by $d$, their sum increases by $nd$.
\item[(c)] Find a formula for the sum of the $n$ consecutive integers $a,a+1,\dots,a+n-1$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] The \emph{circumcircle} of a triangle is the circle through its three vertices. Prove that the perpendicular bisectors of the three sides of any triangle $ABC$ pass through one point $O$, and that the circle with centre $O$ through $A$ also passes through $B$ and $C$.
\item[(b)] The \emph{incircle} is the circle inside the triangle tangent to all three sides. Prove that the three angle bisectors of triangle $ABC$ pass through one point $I$, and that a circle with centre $I$ is tangent to all three sides.
\item[(c)] For the right triangle with legs $6$ and $8$, find the circumradius $R$ and the inradius $r$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} A point lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$ exactly when it is equally far from $A$ and $B$. The perpendicular bisectors of $AB$ and $BC$ are not parallel (since $AB$ and $BC$ are not parallel), so they meet at a point $O$ with $OA=OB$ and $OB=OC$. Then $OA=OC$, so $O$ also lies on the perpendicular bisector of $CA$. The circle with centre $O$ and radius $OA=OB=OC$ passes through all three vertices: it is the circumcircle, and $O$ is the circumcentre.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(11cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0),C=(2,4.6);
real dd=2*(A.x*(B.y-C.y)+B.x*(C.y-A.y)+C.x*(A.y-B.y));
pair O=((dot(A,A)*(B.y-C.y)+dot(B,B)*(C.y-A.y)+dot(C,C)*(A.y-B.y))/dd,(dot(A,A)*(C.x-B.x)+dot(B,B)*(A.x-C.x)+dot(C,C)*(B.x-A.x))/dd);
draw(circle(O,abs(O-A)),blue);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
pair[] mids={(A+B)/2,(B+C)/2,(C+A)/2};
for(pair m:mids){ draw(m--O,dashed+blue); }
dot(O,blue); label("$O$",O,dir(-45),blue);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N);
pair sh=(9.5,0);
pair A2=A+sh,B2=B+sh,C2=C+sh;
real a=abs(B2-C2), b=abs(C2-A2), c=abs(A2-B2);
pair I=(a*A2+b*B2+c*C2)/(a+b+c);
pair F=B2+dot(I-B2,unit(C2-B2))*unit(C2-B2);
real r=abs(I-F);
draw(circle(I,r),red);
draw(A2--B2--C2--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A2--I,dashed+red); draw(B2--I,dashed+red); draw(C2--I,dashed+red);
draw(I--F,red); dot(I,red); dot(F); label("$F$",F,NE);
label("$I$",I,dir(150),red); label("$r$",(I+F)/2,S,red);
label("$A$",A2,SW); label("$B$",B2,SE); label("$C$",C2,N);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} A point inside angle $A$ lies on its bisector exactly when it is equally far from the lines $AB$ and $AC$. The bisector of angle $A$ meets side $BC$ at a point $A'$, and the bisector of angle $B$, a ray inside angle $ABA'$, crosses segment $AA'$ at a point $I$ inside the triangle. Then $I$ is equally far from $AB$ and $AC$, and from $BA$ and $BC$, so it is equally far, say at distance $r$, from $CA$ and $CB$; hence it lies on the bisector of angle $C$ too. The circle with centre $I$ and radius $r$ is tangent to each of the three lines (each is at distance $r$ from the centre). It touches line $BC$ at the foot $F$ of the perpendicular from $I$, and $F$ lies between $B$ and $C$ because the angles $IBC=\dfrac{B}{2}$ and $ICB=\dfrac C2$ are acute; similarly for the other sides. So it is the incircle, and $I$ is the incentre.

\textbf{(c)} The hypotenuse is $\sqrt{6^2+8^2}=10$. In a right triangle the midpoint $M$ of the hypotenuse is equally far from all three vertices (the right angle is inscribed in the circle with the hypotenuse as diameter), so $R=\dfrac{10}{2}=\boxed{5}$. For the inradius, split the triangle into the three triangles $IBC$, $ICA$, $IAB$, each of height $r$:
\[\dfrac12\cdot6\cdot8=\dfrac r2(6+8+10),\qquad24=12r,\qquad r=\boxed{2}.\]

Clue (a) names four different people: Mike, Ms.\ Mosby (a girl), the dancer and the programmer.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Names.} The only possible matching initials are Mike Mosby and Wendy Williams; Mike is not Mosby by (a). So by (b), Wendy's last name is Williams and she is the dentist or the programmer. Then Ms.\ Mosby is Annie. Since Wendy is not the chef, (c) gives Mike Richardson, and Carlos is Parker.
\item \textbf{Dancer and programmer.} By (a), neither is Mike or Annie (Ms.\ Mosby), so they are Wendy and Carlos in some order. If Wendy were the dentist, the programmer would be Carlos and the dancer Wendy, contradicting (f). So Wendy is the programmer and Carlos the dancer.
\item \textbf{Careers.} Annie is the surgeon (g). Of chef and dentist, Carlos (the dancer) cannot be the dentist by (f), so Carlos is the chef and Mike the dentist.
\item \textbf{Ambitions.} Annie, Wendy and Mike wanted to be clown, teacher and firefighter. Wendy is not the clown (e) and not the teacher (d), so she wanted to be a firefighter. Mike, the dentist, was not the teacher (f), so he was the clown, and Annie wanted to be a teacher.
\end{itemize}
Every step was forced, so the solution is unique:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\textbf{Person} & \textbf{Childhood ambition} & \textbf{Career}\\
Annie Mosby & teacher & surgeon\\
Wendy Williams & firefighter & computer programmer\\
Carlos Parker & dancer & chef\\
Mike Richardson & clown & dentist
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
(All clues check: for example, (b) holds through the programmer Wendy Williams.)

Let $O$ be the centre and let $A$, $B$ be adjacent vertices, so $OA=OB=1$ and $AB=s(n)$. The $n$ triangles formed by $O$ and the sides are congruent and fill the full angle at $O$, so $\angle AOB=\dfrac{360^\circ}{n}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int n=7; pair O=(0,0);
pair V(int k){return dir(90+360*k/n);}
draw(circle(O,1),gray+dashed);
for(int k=0;k<n;++k) draw(V(k)--V(k+1));
pair A=V(3), B=V(4), M=(A+B)/2;
draw(O--A,blue); draw(O--B,blue); draw(O--M,red+dashed);
pair u=unit(A-M), w=unit(O-M); draw(M+0.07*u--M+0.07*u+0.07*w--M+0.07*w);
draw(arc(O,0.22,degrees(A),270),red);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,N);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$M$",M,SE);
label("$1$",(O+A)/2,W,blue);
label("$\theta$",O+0.33*dir(257),red+fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

Triangle $AOB$ is isosceles, so the line from $O$ to the midpoint $M$ of $AB$ is perpendicular to $AB$ and bisects $\angle AOB$; thus $\theta=\angle AOM=\dfrac{180^\circ}{n}$. In the right triangle $AOM$,
\[AM=OA\sin\theta=\sin\dfrac{180^\circ}{n},\qquad\text{so}\qquad \boxed{s(n)=2\sin\dfrac{180^\circ}{n}}.\]
(Equivalently, by the law of cosines in triangle $AOB$, $s(n)=\sqrt{2-2\cos\dfrac{360^\circ}{n}}$.)

Checks: $s(3)=2\sin60^\circ=\sqrt3$, $s(4)=2\sin45^\circ=\sqrt2$ (the diagonal of the square is the diameter $2$, so the side is $\dfrac{2}{\sqrt2}=\sqrt2$), and $s(6)=2\sin30^\circ=1$ (the hexagon is made of six equilateral triangles of side $1$).

question:
A student claims: \emph{if an equilateral triangle is placed in the coordinate plane with one side parallel to the $x$-axis, then its other two sides have slopes $1$ and $-1$.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the claim is false.
\item[(b)] Find, with proof, the slopes of the other two sides.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the horizontal side be $PR$ with $P=(p,q)$ and $R=(p+2a,q)$, $a>0$, and let $T$ be the third vertex. Since $TP=TR$, the vertex $T$ lies on the perpendicular bisector $x=p+a$ of $PR$. The height of an equilateral triangle of side $2a$ is $\sqrt{(2a)^2-a^2}=a\sqrt3$, so $T=(p+a,\ q\pm a\sqrt3)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(0.6,0.5), R=(2.6,0.5), T=(1.6,0.5+sqrt(3));
draw((-0.3,0)--(3.4,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-0.3)--(0,2.7),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(3.4,0),E); label("$y$",(0,2.7),N);
draw(P--R--T--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((1.6,0.5)--T,dashed);
label("$P(p,q)$",P,SW); label("$R(p+2a,q)$",R,SE); label("$T(p+a,q+a\sqrt3)$",T,N);
draw(arc(P,0.3,0,60)); label("$60^\circ$",P+0.5*dir(30),fontsize(9pt));
label("$a$",(1.1,0.5),S); label("$a\sqrt3$",(1.6,1.3),E,fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} If $T$ is above $PR$, the slopes are
\[\text{slope }PT=\dfrac{a\sqrt3}{a}=\sqrt3,\qquad \text{slope }RT=\dfrac{a\sqrt3}{-a}=-\sqrt3;\]
if $T$ is below, they are $-\sqrt3$ and $\sqrt3$. In every case the other two sides have slopes $\boxed{\sqrt3\text{ and }-\sqrt3}$. (This also follows from the $60^\circ$ angles at $P$ and $R$: the slopes are $\pm\tan60^\circ$.)

\textbf{(a)} Since $\sqrt3\ne1$, the claim is false; for example, the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(2,0)$, $(1,\sqrt3)$ is equilateral with a horizontal side, and its other sides have slopes $\sqrt3$ and $-\sqrt3$. Slopes $1$ and $-1$ would make $45^\circ$ angles with the horizontal side, and the triangle would be the right isosceles triangle with angles $45^\circ,45^\circ,90^\circ$, not an equilateral one.

question:
A right triangle has hypotenuse $50$ feet, and the leg forming its height is $30$ feet.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the other leg (the base).
\item[(b)] Someone also claims that the angle between the height and the hypotenuse is $60^\circ$. Show that this is impossible.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the right angle be at $B$, with height $BT=30$, hypotenuse $TC=50$ and base $BC=x$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(4,0), T=(0,3);
draw(B--C--T--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0.25)--(0.25,0.25)--(0.25,0));
label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$T$",T,N);
label("$30$",(B+T)/2,W); label("$50$",(C+T)/2,NE); label("$x$",(B+C)/2,S);
draw(arc(T,0.55,270,degrees(C-T)),blue); label("$\theta$",T+0.75*dir(290),blue);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} By the Pythagorean theorem, $x^2=50^2-30^2=2500-900=1600$, so the base is $\boxed{40}$ feet (a $3$-$4$-$5$ triangle scaled by $10$).

\textbf{(b)} The angle $\theta$ at $T$ between the height and the hypotenuse satisfies $\cos\theta=\dfrac{TB}{TC}=\dfrac{30}{50}=\dfrac{3}{5}$. But $\cos60^\circ=\dfrac{1}{2}\ne\dfrac{3}{5}$, so $\theta\ne60^\circ$ (in fact $\theta\approx53.1^\circ$). If the angle at $T$ were $60^\circ$ with hypotenuse $50$, the height would have to be $50\cos60^\circ=25$, not $30$ (and the base would be $25\sqrt3$).

The largest sphere is the inscribed sphere, tangent to all four faces. Let its centre be $I$ and its radius $r$. Joining $I$ to the four vertices cuts the tetrahedron into four pyramids with apex $I$, one on each face, each of height $r$. Adding their volumes,
\[V=\dfrac{1}{3} rS,\qquad r=\dfrac{3V}{S},\]
where $V$ is the volume and $S$ the total area of the faces. The figure shows the cross-section by the plane $x=y$, through the edge $OZ$ and the midpoint $M$ of the opposite edge; it contains the centre $I$ and is perpendicular to the faces $z=0$ and $x+y+z=1$, so the circle of radius $r$ touches the sides $OM$ and $MZ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=(3-sqrt(3))/6, h=sqrt(2)/2;
pair O=(0,0), M=(h,0), Z=(0,1), I=(r*sqrt(2),r);
draw(O--M--Z--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(I,r),blue);
dot(I,blue); label("$I$",I,W,blue);
pair T1=(r*sqrt(2),0); pair n=unit((1,h)); pair T2=I+r*n;
draw(I--T1,blue+dashed); draw(I--T2,blue+dashed);
label("$r$",(I+T1)/2,E,blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$r$",(I+T2)/2,N,blue+fontsize(8pt));
label("$O$",O,SW); label("$M$",M,SE); label("$Z(0,0,1)$",Z,N);
label("$\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}$",(O+M)/2,S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$1$",(O+Z)/2,W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Volume.} The base right triangle in the plane $z=0$ has area $\dfrac{1}{2}$ and the height is $1$, so $V=\dfrac{1}{3}\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot1=\dfrac{1}{6}$.

\textbf{Surface area.} The three faces in the coordinate planes have area $\dfrac{1}{2}$ each. The fourth face is equilateral with side $\sqrt2$ (the distance between any two of $(1,0,0)$, $(0,1,0)$, $(0,0,1)$), so its area is $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot2=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$. Thus $S=\dfrac{3}{2}+\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}=\dfrac{3+\sqrt3}{2}$.

Hence
\[r=\dfrac{3\cdot\dfrac{1}{6}}{\dfrac{3+\sqrt3}{2}}=\dfrac{1}{3+\sqrt3}=\boxed{\dfrac{3-\sqrt3}{6}}\approx0.211 .\]
Check with coordinates: the centre is $I=(r,r,r)$ (distance $r$ from the three coordinate planes), and its distance to the plane $x+y+z=1$ is $\dfrac{1-3r}{\sqrt3}$, which equals $r$ exactly when $r=\dfrac{1}{3+\sqrt3}$.

question:
Let $ABCD$ be a convex quadrilateral in which the perpendicular bisectors of the diagonals $AC$ and $BD$ meet at a single point $Q$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that if $AC\perp BD$, then $\angle AQB+\angle CQD=180^\circ$.
\item[(b)] Prove that if $Q$ lies inside $ABCD$ and $\angle AQB+\angle CQD=180^\circ$, then $AC\perp BD$.
\item[(c)] Let $A=(-5,0)$, $B=(-4,-3)$, $C=(-3,-4)$, $D=(0,5)$. Show that $ABCD$ is convex, find $Q$, and show that $\angle AQB+\angle CQD=180^\circ$ although $AC$ is not perpendicular to $BD$. (So the hypothesis that $Q$ lies inside cannot be dropped in (b).)
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Since $Q$ lies on both perpendicular bisectors, $QA=QC=r$ and $QB=QD=s$, with $r,s>0$ (if $Q=A$ then $A=C$). Put $Q$ at the origin and write $\vec a,\vec b,\vec c,\vec d$ for the position vectors of $A,B,C,D$.

\textbf{A criterion.} For angles $\varphi,\psi$ between $0^\circ$ and $180^\circ$, $\varphi+\psi=180^\circ$ if and only if $\cos\varphi=-\cos\psi=\cos(180^\circ-\psi)$, because cosine is one-to-one on $[0^\circ,180^\circ]$. As $\cos\angle AQB=\dfrac{\vec a\cdot\vec b}{rs}$ and $\cos\angle CQD=\dfrac{\vec c\cdot\vec d}{rs}$,
\[\angle AQB+\angle CQD=180^\circ\iff\vec a\cdot\vec b+\vec c\cdot\vec d=0. \tag{1}\]

\textbf{(a)} Expanding gives the identity
\[2(\vec a\cdot\vec b+\vec c\cdot\vec d)=(\vec a+\vec c)\cdot(\vec b+\vec d)+(\vec a-\vec c)\cdot(\vec b-\vec d). \tag{2}\]
Because $|\vec a|=|\vec c|$, we have $(\vec a+\vec c)\cdot(\vec a-\vec c)=|\vec a|^2-|\vec c|^2=0$; similarly $(\vec b+\vec d)\cdot(\vec b-\vec d)=0$. Now suppose $AC\perp BD$, that is, $(\vec a-\vec c)\cdot(\vec b-\vec d)=0$. In the plane, the vectors $\vec a+\vec c$ and $\vec b-\vec d$ are both perpendicular to the nonzero vector $\vec a-\vec c$, so $\vec a+\vec c=\lambda(\vec b-\vec d)$ for some real $\lambda$. Then
\[(\vec a+\vec c)\cdot(\vec b+\vec d)=\lambda(\vec b-\vec d)\cdot(\vec b+\vec d)=0,\]
so both terms on the right of (2) vanish, and by (1), $\angle AQB+\angle CQD=180^\circ$.

\textbf{(b)} Now let $Q$ be inside $ABCD$. The segments $QA,QB,QC,QD$ cut the quadrilateral into the triangles $QAB,QBC,QCD,QDA$, so the rays $QA,QB,QC,QD$ occur in this order around $Q$ (say counterclockwise), and the angles $x=\angle AQB$, $y=\angle BQC$, $z=\angle CQD$, $w=\angle DQA$ between consecutive rays satisfy $x+y+z+w=360^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=3, s=2.4; pair Q=(0,0);
pair A=r*dir(200), B=s*dir(245), C=r*dir(330), D=s*dir(105);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,gray); draw(B--D,gray);
draw(Q--A,blue); draw(Q--B,blue); draw(Q--C,blue); draw(Q--D,blue);
draw(1.15*r*dir(265)--1.15*r*dir(85),red+dashed);
draw(1.15*r*dir(355)--1.15*r*dir(175),orange+dashed);
dot(Q); label("$Q$",Q,dir(60));
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,E); label("$D$",D,N);
label("$x$",0.75*dir(222.5),fontsize(9pt)); label("$y$",0.75*dir(287.5),fontsize(9pt));
label("$z$",0.75*dir(37.5),fontsize(9pt)); label("$w$",0.75*dir(152.5),fontsize(9pt));
label("$\ell_1$",1.15*r*dir(85),E,red); label("$\ell_2$",1.15*r*dir(355),N,orange);
\end{asy}

Triangle $QAC$ is isosceles, and its axis of symmetry is the perpendicular bisector $\ell_1$ of $AC$. The reflection in $\ell_1$ swaps the rays $QA$ and $QC$, so $\ell_1$ is the line through $Q$ in the direction obtained by turning ray $QA$ counterclockwise by $\dfrac{x+y}{2}$. In the same way the perpendicular bisector $\ell_2$ of $BD$ is obtained by turning ray $QB$ by $\dfrac{y+z}{2}$, that is, ray $QA$ by $x+\dfrac{y+z}{2}$. So the angle from $\ell_1$ to $\ell_2$ is
\[x+\dfrac{y+z}{2}-\dfrac{x+y}{2}=\dfrac{x+z}{2},\qquad 0^\circ<\dfrac{x+z}{2}<180^\circ.\]
Since $AC\perp\ell_1$ and $BD\perp\ell_2$, the diagonals are perpendicular exactly when $\ell_1\perp\ell_2$, that is, when $\dfrac{x+z}{2}=90^\circ$, i.e. $x+z=180^\circ$. In particular, $\angle AQB+\angle CQD=180^\circ$ implies $AC\perp BD$. (This argument proves both directions when $Q$ is inside.)

\textbf{(c)} All four points are at distance $5$ from the origin, so the origin lies on both perpendicular bisectors; these are different lines (they are perpendicular to $AC$ and $BD$, which are not parallel), so $Q=(0,0)$. The points lie on the circle of radius $5$ in the order $D$ (at $90^\circ$), $A$ ($180^\circ$), $B$ (about $216.9^\circ$), $C$ (about $233.1^\circ$), so $ABCD$ is a convex quadrilateral inscribed in this circle.

