Now $\vec a\cdot\vec b=20$ and $\vec c\cdot\vec d=-20$, so $\vec a\cdot\vec b+\vec c\cdot\vec d=0$ and, by (1), $\angle AQB+\angle CQD=180^\circ$ (indeed $\cos\angle AQB=\dfrac{4}{5}$ and $\cos\angle CQD=-\dfrac{4}{5}$). But $\overrightarrow{AC}=(2,-4)$ and $\overrightarrow{BD}=(4,8)$ have dot product $8-32=-24\ne0$, so $AC$ is not perpendicular to $BD$. Here $Q$ is outside $ABCD$: $Q$ and $B$ lie on opposite sides of the line $CD$, $y=3x+5$ (at $Q$, $y<3x+5$; at $B$, $y>3x+5$). The turn from $QA$ to $QB$ and the turn from $QC$ to $QD$ go in opposite directions, which is what breaks the argument of (b).

Since $B$ is acute, $\cos B=\sqrt{1-\left(\dfrac{84}{85}\right)^2}=\dfrac{13}{85}$.

\textbf{Triangle $ABC$.} Its area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot5\cdot17\cdot\dfrac{84}{85}=42$, and by the law of cosines
\[AC^2=5^2+17^2-2\cdot5\cdot17\cdot\dfrac{13}{85}=25+289-26=288,\qquad AC=12\sqrt2.\]

\begin{asy}
