\textbf{Triangle $ACD$.} By the law of cosines,
\[\cos D=\dfrac{13^2+25^2-288}{2\cdot13\cdot25}=\dfrac{506}{650}=\dfrac{253}{325},\qquad \sin D=\dfrac{\sqrt{325^2-253^2}}{325}=\dfrac{\sqrt{72\cdot578}}{325}=\dfrac{204}{325}.\]
So its area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot13\cdot25\cdot\dfrac{204}{325}=102$. (Heron's formula gives the same.)

Since the quadrilateral is convex, the diagonal $AC$ splits it into these two triangles, and the area is $42+102=\boxed{144}$.

(Without the condition that $B$ is acute, $\cos B=-\dfrac{13}{85}$ is also possible; then $AC^2=340$, and the quadrilateral, again convex, has area $42+14\sqrt{69}\approx158.3$.)

