question:
You write a poem. Sayori likes it with probability $97\%$, Yuri with probability $64\%$, Natsuki with probability $34\%$ and Monika with probability $50\%$, independently of each other. What is the probability that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Sayori and Yuri both like the poem;
\item[(b)] Yuri and Monika both like the poem;
\item[(c)] Natsuki does not like the poem;
\item[(d)] all four like the poem;
\item[(e)] none of the four likes the poem?
\end{enumerate}

A principal of $P$ dollars is invested at an annual interest rate $r$ (written as a decimal), compounded $n$ times per year: the year is divided into $n$ equal periods, and at the end of each period interest equal to $\dfrac {r}{n}$ times the current balance is added to the balance.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that after $t$ years (that is, $nt$ periods, $t$ a positive integer) the balance is
\[A=P\left(1+\dfrac {r}{n}\right)^{nt}.\]
\item[(b)] Find the balance, to the nearest cent, when $\$1000$ is invested at $6\%$ per year compounded quarterly for $2$ years.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{$n+1$ elements are possible.} Take $B=\{n+1,n+2,\ldots,2n+1\}$. The sum of two different elements is at least $(n+1)+(n+2)=2n+3>2n+1$, so it is never in $B$. (The odd numbers $\{1,3,\ldots,2n+1\}$ also work: the sum of two odd numbers is even.)

\textbf{At most $n+1$ elements.} Let $m$ be the largest element of $B$. For every other element $x\in B$, the number $m-x$ is not a different element of $B$ (otherwise $x+(m-x)=m$ with three different elements). Pair up the numbers $1,2,\ldots,m-1$ as $\{x,m-x\}$:
\begin{itemize}
\item if $m=2j+1$, there are $j$ pairs $\{1,2j\},\ldots,\{j,j+1\}$, each containing at most one element of $B$, so $|B|\le j+1$; and $m\le2n+1$ gives $j\le n$;
\item if $m=2j$, there are $j-1$ pairs and the single number $j$, so $|B|\le(j-1)+1+1=j+1$; and $m\le2n+1$ gives $j\le n$.
\end{itemize}
In both cases $|B|\le n+1$. The maximum is $\boxed{n+1}$.

