\begin{itemize}
\item The curved parts of the four perimeters together make up the whole circumference, $28\pi$.
\item Each cut is divided by the crossing point into two pieces, and each piece is a side of exactly two slices (one on each side of the cut). So the straight parts of the perimeters add up to twice the total length $L$ of the cuts.
\end{itemize}
Hence $4\cdot45=28\pi+2L$, and
\[L=\dfrac{180-28\pi}{2}=\boxed{90-14\pi}\approx46.0\ \text{inches}.\]
(This is possible: each cut is a chord, at most $28$ inches long, and $46<56$.)

question:
Let $r,s,t$ be the roots of $3x^3+x^2+4x+2=0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find $r+s+t$, $rs+st+tr$ and $rst$.
\item[(b)] Find $r^2+s^2+t^2$ and $\dfrac{1}{r}+\dfrac{1}{s}+\dfrac{1}{t}$.
\item[(c)] Explain why the three roots cannot all be real.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $\omega$ be the circumcircle of $ABC$ (its centre is the midpoint of $BC$, since $\angle A=90^\circ$) and let $\gamma$ be the circle with centre $A$ and radius $AD$. The line $XY$ is the radical axis of $\gamma$ and $\omega$: the set of points with equal power with respect to both circles.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(4,0), C=(0,3);
real t=dot(A-B,C-B)/dot(C-B,C-B); pair D=B+t*(C-B);
pair M=(B+C)/2; real R=abs(B-M), h=abs(D-A);
path c1=circle(A,h), c2=circle(M,R);
pair[] I=intersectionpoints(c1,c2); pair X=I[0], Y=I[1];
pair A2=2*D-A, N=(A+D)/2;
draw(c2,gray); draw(c1,blue);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--A2,dashed); draw(X--Y,red);
draw(D+0.15*unit(A-D)--D+0.15*unit(A-D)+0.15*unit(B-D)--D+0.15*unit(B-D)); draw((0,0.22)--(0.22,0.22)--(0.22,0));
dot(D); dot(N,red); dot(X); dot(Y); dot(A2);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NW); label("$D$",D,dir(95));
label("$N$",N,dir(170),red); label("$X$",X,dir(X-A)); label("$Y$",Y,dir(Y-A)); label("$A'$",A2,NE);
\end{asy}

\textbf{The second intersection of line $AD$ with $\omega$.} Let line $AD$ meet $\omega$ again at $A'$. Since $D$ is inside $\omega$ on the chords $BC$ and $AA'$, the power of a point gives $DA\cdot DA'=DB\cdot DC$. In a right triangle, the altitude to the hypotenuse satisfies $AD^2=DB\cdot DC$ (triangles $ABD$ and $CAD$ are similar). Hence $DA'=AD$, and $AA'=2AD$.

\textbf{Powers of a point $P$ on segment $AD$.} Let $P$ be on segment $AD$ with $AP=p$, $0\le p\le AD$. Then
\[\operatorname{pow}_\gamma(P)=AP^2-AD^2=p^2-AD^2,\qquad \operatorname{pow}_\omega(P)=-PA\cdot PA'=-p(2AD-p)=p^2-2p\cdot AD .\]
These are equal exactly when $AD^2=2p\cdot AD$, i.e. $p=\dfrac{AD}{2}$. So the midpoint $N$ of $AD$ has equal powers with respect to the two circles, which means it lies on the radical axis $XY$. Hence $XY$ passes through the midpoint of $AD$.

\textbf{(b)} The centre $(x,y)$ is in the pixelated circle when $x^2+y^2\le r^2$. This requires $|x|\le r$, and for a fixed integer $x$ with $|x|\le r$ the allowed $y$ are the integers with $|y|\le\sqrt{r^2-x^2}$, that is, $|y|\le\left\lfloor\sqrt{r^2-x^2}\right\rfloor$. There are $2\left\lfloor\sqrt{r^2-x^2}\right\rfloor+1$ of them. Adding over $x$ gives the formula. (There is no simple closed form; $N(r)$ is close to the area $\pi r^2$ of the circle.)

\textbf{(a)} For $r=3$ the columns $x=0,\pm1,\pm2,\pm3$ contribute $7$, $5$, $5$, $1$ pixels ($\lfloor\sqrt9\rfloor=3$, $\lfloor\sqrt8\rfloor=2$, $\lfloor\sqrt5\rfloor=2$, $\lfloor\sqrt0\rfloor=0$), so
\[N(3)=7+2\cdot5+2\cdot5+2\cdot1=\boxed{29}.\]
The list misses the four pixels centred at $(\pm2,\pm2)$ (orange in the figure): $2^2+2^2=8\le9$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int x=-3;x<=3;++x) for(int y=-3;y<=3;++y) if(x*x+y*y<=9){
  pen f=(abs(x)==2 && abs(y)==2) ? orange : gray(0.82);
  filldraw(shift(x-0.5,y-0.5)*unitsquare,f,gray(0.4)); dot((x,y),black+2pt);}
draw(circle((0,0),3),blue+dashed);
draw((-4,0)--(4,0),Arrow(5)); draw((0,-4)--(0,4),Arrow(5));
label("$x$",(4,0),E); label("$y$",(0,4),N);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(c)} For $r=5$ and $x=0,\pm1,\pm2,\pm3,\pm4,\pm5$, the values of $\left\lfloor\sqrt{25-x^2}\right\rfloor$ are $5,4,4,4,3,0$, giving columns of $11,9,9,9,7,1$ pixels. So
\[N(5)=11+2(9+9+9+7+1)=\boxed{81},\]
compared with $\pi\cdot25\approx78.5$.

Let the first (slower) boat have speed $v$ km per hour. Two hours after it left, it has travelled $2v$ km; the second boat has sailed for one hour at $v+5$ km per hour, so it has travelled $v+5$ km.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(0,0), S1=(10,0), S2=10*dir(60);
draw(P--S1,blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(P--S2,red+linewidth(0.9)); draw(S1--S2,dashed);
draw(arc(P,1.5,0,60)); label("$60^\circ$",P+2.4*dir(30),fontsize(9pt));
dot(P); dot(S1,blue); dot(S2,red);
label("port",P,SW); label("$2v$",(P+S1)/2,S,blue); label("$v+5$",(P+S2)/2,NW,red); label("$10$",(S1+S2)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

By the law of cosines in the triangle formed by the port and the two boats,
\[10^2=(2v)^2+(v+5)^2-2(2v)(v+5)\cos60^\circ=4v^2+v^2+10v+25-2v^2-10v=3v^2+25.\]
So $3v^2=75$, $v^2=25$, and $v=\boxed{5}$ km per hour (exactly). Then both boats are $10$ km from the port, and the triangle is equilateral.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Solve $x^3+x^2+x=-1$ over the complex numbers, expressing the roots as powers of $i$.
\item[(b)] Use Vieta's formulas to find the sum of the roots, and compare the result with the equation in (a).
\end{enumerate}

solution:
From the first equation $c=19-|a+b|$.

\textbf{Case $c\ge0$, i.e. $|a+b|\le19$.} Then $ab-|a+b|=78$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $a+b\ge0$: $ab-a-b=78$, so $(a-1)(b-1)=79$, a prime. Then $\{a-1,b-1\}$ is $\{1,79\}$ or $\{-1,-79\}$. This gives $\{a,b\}=\{2,80\}$ (but then $|a+b|=82>19$) or $\{0,-78\}$ (but then $a+b<0$). No solutions.
\item If $a+b<0$: $ab+a+b=78$, so $(a+1)(b+1)=79$. This gives $\{a,b\}=\{0,78\}$ (but then $a+b>0$) or $\{-2,-80\}$ (but then $|a+b|>19$). No solutions.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Case $c<0$, i.e. $|a+b|>19$.} Then $ab+|a+b|=116$ (using $|c|=|a+b|-19$).
\begin{itemize}
\item If $a+b>0$: $(a+1)(b+1)=117=3^2\cdot13$. If both factors were negative, $a,b\le-2$ and $a+b<0$; so both are positive, and $\{a+1,b+1\}$ is $\{1,117\}$, $\{3,39\}$ or $\{9,13\}$. This gives $\{a,b\}=\{0,116\}$, $\{2,38\}$ or $\{8,12\}$, with $a+b=116,40,20$, all greater than $19$: $6$ ordered pairs.
\item If $a+b<0$: $(a-1)(b-1)=117$, and similarly both factors are negative. This gives $\{a,b\}=\{0,-116\}$, $\{-2,-38\}$ or $\{-8,-12\}$, with $|a+b|=116,40,20>19$: another $6$ ordered pairs.
\end{itemize}
Each pair determines $c=19-|a+b|$. There are $\boxed{12}$ ordered triples, for example $(8,12,-1)$: $|20|-1=19$ and $96+1=97$.

question:
Two terms are \emph{like terms} if they contain the same variables raised to the same powers; only like terms can be combined into a single term.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Are $x^n$ and $x$ like terms when $n>1$ is an integer?
\item[(b)] Prove that $x^2+x$ cannot be written as a single term: there are no constants $c$ and nonnegative integer $k$ such that $x^2+x=cx^k$ for every real number $x$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
We have $QA=8-2=6$ and $PB=6-2=4$.

\textbf{Locating $S$ (Ceva's theorem).} The cevians $AP$, $BQ$, $CS$ are concurrent at $R$, so
\[\dfrac{AS}{SB}\cdot\dfrac{BP}{PC}\cdot\dfrac{CQ}{QA}=1,\qquad\text{so}\qquad\dfrac{AS}{SB}=\dfrac{PC}{BP}\cdot\dfrac{QA}{CQ}=\dfrac{2}{4}\cdot\dfrac{6}{2}=\dfrac{3}{2}.\]
With $AS+SB=10$ this gives $AS=6$ and $SB=4$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),B=(6,0),A=(0,8),Q=(0,2),P=(2,0),R=(18/11,16/11),Sp=(18/5,16/5);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--P,blue); draw(B--Q,blue); draw(C--Sp,blue);
draw((0,0.4)--(0.4,0.4)--(0.4,0));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(P);dot(Q);dot(R,blue);dot(Sp);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$Q$",Q,W); label("$P$",P,S);
label("$R$",R,dir(200),blue); label("$S$",Sp,NE);
label("$2$",(0,1),W,fontsize(9pt)); label("$6$",(0,5),W,fontsize(9pt));
label("$2$",(1,0),S,fontsize(9pt)); label("$4$",(4,0),S,fontsize(9pt));
label("$6$",(A+Sp)/2,NE,fontsize(9pt)); label("$4$",(Sp+B)/2,NE,fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Locating $T$ (Menelaus' theorem).} The line through $Q$, $P$, $T$ crosses the sides of triangle $ABC$ (two of them extended), so
\[\dfrac{AT}{TB}\cdot\dfrac{BP}{PC}\cdot\dfrac{CQ}{QA}=1,\qquad\text{so}\qquad\dfrac{AT}{TB}=\dfrac{3}{2}\]
as well, with $T$ outside segment $AB$. Since $AT>TB$, the point $T$ lies beyond $B$, so $AT=TB+10$. Then $\dfrac{TB+10}{TB}=\dfrac{3}{2}$ gives $TB=20$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),B=(6,0),A=(0,8),Q=(0,2),P=(2,0),Sp=(18/5,16/5),T=(18,-16);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(B--T,dashed); draw(Q--T,red);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(P);dot(Q);dot(Sp);dot(T);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,NE); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$Q$",Q,W); label("$P$",P,SW); label("$S$",Sp,NE); label("$T$",T,SE);
label("$4$",(Sp+B)/2,NE,fontsize(9pt)); label("$20$",(B+T)/2,NE,fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

Therefore
\[TS=TB+BS=20+4=\boxed{24}.\]

\textbf{Check with coordinates.} Put $C=(0,0)$, $B=(6,0)$, $A=(0,8)$, so $P=(2,0)$ and $Q=(0,2)$. Line $QP$ is $x+y=2$ and line $AB$ is $4x+3y=24$; they meet at $T=(18,-16)$. Lines $AP$ ($y=8-4x$) and $BQ$ ($y=2-\dfrac {x}{3}$) meet at $R=\left(\dfrac{18}{11},\dfrac{16}{11}\right)$, and line $CR$ ($y=\dfrac{8}{9}x$) meets $AB$ at $S=\left(\dfrac{18}{5},\dfrac{16}{5}\right)$. Then $TS^2=\left(\dfrac{72}{5}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{96}{5}\right)^2=576$, so $TS=24$.

\textbf{Remark.} The given information must include the legs. In general $S$ and $T$ divide $AB$ internally and externally in the same ratio $k=\dfrac{AS}{SB}$, and $TS=\dfrac{2k\cdot AB}{\left|k^2-1\right|}$; if $AC=BC$ then $k=1$, line $QP$ is parallel to $AB$ and $T$ does not exist.

question:
A closet holds $20$ pairs of red shoes, $20$ pairs of green shoes and $20$ pairs of blue shoes; each pair consists of a left shoe and a right shoe. Shoes are taken out in the dark, one at a time.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many shoes must be taken out to be sure of having two shoes of the same colour?
\item[(b)] How many shoes must be taken out to be sure of having a wearable pair, that is, a left shoe and a right shoe of the same colour?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Since $3^2+4^2=5^2$, triangle $AXY$ has a right angle at $X$ (converse of the Pythagorean theorem).

Let $s$ be the side of the square. Because $\angle AXY=90^\circ$, the angles $\angle AXB$ and $\angle YXC$ add up to $90^\circ$, so $\angle YXC=90^\circ-\angle AXB=\angle BAX$. The right triangles $ABX$ and $XCY$ therefore have equal acute angles and are similar, with ratio $AX:XY=4:3$. Hence
\[XC=\dfrac{3}{4}AB=\dfrac{3s}{4},\qquad\text{so}\qquad BX=s-\dfrac{3s}{4}=\dfrac {s}{4}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=16/sqrt(17);
pair A=(0,0),B=(s,0),C=(s,s),D=(0,s),X=(s,s/4),Y=(13*s/16,s);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--X--Y--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.8));
pair u=unit(A-X),v=unit(Y-X); real m=0.22;
draw(X+m*u--X+m*u+m*v--X+m*v);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(X);dot(Y);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,NW); label("$X$",X,E); label("$Y$",Y,N);
label("$4$",(A+X)/2,SE,blue); label("$3$",(X+Y)/2,W,blue); label("$5$",(A+Y)/2,NW,blue);
label("$\dfrac{s}{4}$",(B+X)/2,E,fontsize(8pt)); label("$\dfrac{3s}{4}$",(X+C)/2,E,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

By the Pythagorean theorem in triangle $ABX$,
\[s^2+\dfrac{s^2}{16}=AX^2=16,\qquad\text{so}\qquad s^2=\dfrac{256}{17}.\]
The area of $ABCD$ is $\boxed{\dfrac{256}{17}}$.

Check: $CY=\dfrac{3}{4}BX=\dfrac{3s}{16}$, so $DY=\dfrac{13s}{16}$ and $AY^2=s^2+\dfrac{169s^2}{256}=\dfrac{425}{256}\cdot\dfrac{256}{17}=25$, as required.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many ordered triples $(a,b,c)$ of nonzero real numbers have the property that each number is the product of the other two?
\item[(b)] A student claims that there are infinitely many such triples, for example $\left(x,\dfrac{1}{x},1\right)$ for every nonzero $x$, ``because the three numbers multiply to $1$''. Explain the error.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many integers are in the list $25,26,27,\ldots,97$?
\item[(b)] How many numbers are in the list $7,11,15,\ldots,403$?
\item[(c)] Explain why a list $a,\ a+d,\ a+2d,\ \ldots,\ b$ (with common difference $d>0$) contains $\dfrac{b-a}{d}+1$ numbers.
\end{enumerate}

The notation $A'=D_{P,k}(A)$ means that $A'$ is the image of the point $A$ under the \emph{dilation} (homothety) with centre $P$ and scale factor $k$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] For $k>0$, describe where $A'$ lies in terms of $P$ and $A$. Is it enough to say that $A'$ is twice as far from $P$ as $A$ is, when $k=2$?
\item[(b)] Let $P=(1,2)$, $A=(4,-1)$ and $B=(3,5)$. Find $A'=D_{P,2}(A)$ and $B'=D_{P,2}(B)$.
\item[(c)] Show that $A'B'$ is parallel to $AB$ and twice as long.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} For $k>0$, the image $A'$ lies on the ray from $P$ through $A$, at distance $PA'=k\cdot PA$. In vector form, $A'=P+k(A-P)$. (The centre $P$ itself is fixed.) Saying only ``$A'$ is twice as far from $P$ as $A$ is'' is not enough: all points of the circle with centre $P$ and radius $2\cdot PA$ have that property. The direction matters too: $A'$ is on the same ray from $P$ as $A$. (For $k<0$ the image is on the opposite ray, at distance $|k|\cdot PA$.)

\textbf{(b)} Using $A'=P+2(A-P)$:
\[A'=(1,2)+2(3,-3)=\boxed{(7,-4)},\qquad B'=(1,2)+2(2,3)=\boxed{(5,8)}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(1,2),A=(4,-1),A2=(7,-4),B=(3,5),B2=(5,8);
draw((-0.5,0)--(8,0),gray,Arrow(4)); draw((0,-5)--(0,9),gray,Arrow(4));
draw(P--A2,dashed); draw(P--B2,dashed);
draw(A--B,linewidth(0.8)); draw(A2--B2,blue+linewidth(0.8));
dot(P);dot(A);dot(B);dot(A2,blue);dot(B2,blue);
label("$P(1,2)$",P,W); label("$A(4,-1)$",A,SW); label("$B(3,5)$",B,W);
label("$A'(7,-4)$",A2,E,blue); label("$B'(5,8)$",B2,E,blue);
label("$x$",(8,0),S); label("$y$",(0,9),W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(c)} $B'-A'=\left(P+2(B-P)\right)-\left(P+2(A-P)\right)=2(B-A)$. Here $B-A=(-1,6)$ and $B'-A'=(-2,12)=2(-1,6)$. So the segment $A'B'$ has the same direction as $AB$, hence is parallel to it, and $A'B'=2\cdot AB=2\sqrt{37}$.

Use vectors with origin $A$: let $\vec b=\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\vec c=\overrightarrow{AC}$, which are not parallel. Write the signed ratios
\[x=\dfrac{AM}{AB},\qquad y=\dfrac{AN}{AC},\]
so that $\overrightarrow{AM}=x\vec b$ and $\overrightarrow{AN}=y\vec c$. Since the line does not pass through $A$, $x\ne0$ and $y\ne0$. The centroid is $\overrightarrow{AG}=\dfrac{\vec b+\vec c}{3}$ (it lies two thirds of the way from $A$ to the midpoint $D$ of $BC$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1,4),B=(0,0),C=(6,0),G=(7/3,4/3),D=(3,0),M=(3/7,12/7),Nn=(5,4/5);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--D,dashed);
draw((M-0.25*(Nn-M))--(Nn+0.2*(Nn-M)),blue);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(G,red);dot(D);dot(M,blue);dot(Nn,blue);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$G$",G,SW,red); label("$D$",D,S);
label("$M$",M,NW,blue); label("$N$",Nn,NE,blue);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 1: $\dfrac{AB}{AM}+\dfrac{AC}{AN}=3$.} Since $G$ lies on line $MN$, there is a number $t$ with $\overrightarrow{AG}=(1-t)\overrightarrow{AM}+t\overrightarrow{AN}$, that is,
\[\dfrac{1}{3}\vec b+\dfrac{1}{3}\vec c=(1-t)x\,\vec b+ty\,\vec c.\]
As $\vec b$ and $\vec c$ are not parallel, the coefficients agree: $(1-t)x=\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $ty=\dfrac{1}{3}$. Hence
\[\dfrac{1}{3x}+\dfrac{1}{3y}=(1-t)+t=1,\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad\dfrac{AB}{AM}+\dfrac{AC}{AN}=3.\]

\textbf{Step 2: the identity.} Multiplying by $AM\cdot AN$ gives $AB\cdot AN+AC\cdot AM=3\,AM\cdot AN$. With the signed lengths $MB=AB-AM$ and $NC=AC-AN$,
\[AN\cdot MB+AM\cdot NC=AN\cdot AB-AN\cdot AM+AM\cdot AC-AM\cdot AN=3\,AM\cdot AN-2\,AM\cdot AN=AM\cdot AN.\]
This holds with signs, whether $M$ and $N$ lie on the sides or on their extensions. $\blacksquare$

(For example, the line through $G$ parallel to $BC$ has $AM=\dfrac{2}{3}AB$, $AN=\dfrac{2}{3}AC$, and indeed $\dfrac{3}{2}+\dfrac{3}{2}=3$.)

question:
An IMO problem set has problems $1,2,3$ on day one and $4,5,6$ on day two. Each problem is in one of the four categories A, C, G, N, and a \emph{combination} is the list of categories of problems $1,\ldots,6$ in order. The rules are:
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems $1,2,4,5$ are of four different categories;
\item the three problems of each day are of three different categories;
\item problems $3$ and $6$ are of different categories.
\end{enumerate}
How many combinations are possible?

For integers $n\ge k\ge1$, let $S(n,k)$ be the number of ways to put $n$ distinguishable objects into $k$ indistinguishable boxes so that no box is empty. Also put $S(n,1)=S(n,n)=1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $S(n,k)=S(n-1,k-1)+k\,S(n-1,k)$ for $1<k<n$.
\item[(b)] Prove that $S(n,k)=\dfrac{1}{k!}\displaystyle\sum_{j=0}^{k}(-1)^j\dbinom kj(k-j)^n$.
\item[(c)] In how many ways can $7$ distinguishable objects be put into $4$ indistinguishable boxes if boxes may be left empty?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} List the partitions by their largest part:
\begin{itemize}
\item largest part $7$: $7$;
\item $6$: $6+1$;
\item $5$: $5+2$, $5+1+1$;
\item $4$: $4+3$, $4+2+1$, $4+1+1+1$;
\item $3$: $3+3+1$, $3+2+2$, $3+2+1+1$, $3+1+1+1+1$;
\item $2$: $2+2+2+1$, $2+2+1+1+1$, $2+1+1+1+1+1$;
\item $1$: $1+1+1+1+1+1+1$.
\end{itemize}
That is $1+1+2+3+4+3+1=\boxed{15}$ partitions.

\textbf{(b)} Write $7$ as seven $1$s in a row. An ordered sum is obtained by choosing which of the $6$ gaps between neighbouring $1$s get a bar (stars and bars), so there are $2^6=\boxed{64}$ ordered sums. Stars and bars distinguishes the order of the parts: the partition $4+2+1$ alone corresponds to $3!=6$ ordered sums, while $3+3+1$ corresponds to only $3$. Because different partitions correspond to different numbers of ordered sums, there is no simple division that turns $64$ into the number of partitions; partitions are counted by casework (or by recursion), not by stars and bars. (For $4$: $2^3=8$ ordered sums but $5$ partitions.)

question:
The number $4013$ is prime.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $(2006!)^2\equiv-1\pmod{4013}$.
\item[(b)] Find the remainder when $2\cdot(2006!)^2$ is divided by $4013$.
\end{enumerate}

Nine different points $C,O,M,P,U,T,E,R,S$ are placed at random on a circle, all orders of the nine points around the circle being equally likely.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the probability that triangles $COP$ and $SET$ intersect (have at least one common point)?
\item[(b)] A student argued: ``Place $C,O,P$ together and $S,E,T$ together, in $(3!)^2=36$ orders; there are $\dbinom63=20$ ways to place the other three letters, and $(9-1)!=8!$ circular arrangements in all, so the probability that the triangles do not intersect is $\dfrac{36\cdot20}{8!}$.'' Find the error.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Only the circular order of the six points $C,O,P,S,E,T$ matters, and all $5!=120$ circular orders of these six points are equally likely (each arises from the same number of arrangements of all nine points).

\textbf{When are the triangles disjoint?} If $C,O,P$ are consecutive among the six points, then $S,E,T$ are consecutive too, and a chord separating the two arcs puts the triangles on opposite sides of it: they do not meet. Otherwise, going around the circle we meet points of the two triangles alternately at least four times, so there are $X_1,X_2\in\{C,O,P\}$ and $Y_1,Y_2\in\{S,E,T\}$ in the circular order $X_1,Y_1,X_2,Y_2$. Then the chords $X_1X_2$ and $Y_1Y_2$ cross, and these chords are sides of the triangles, so the triangles intersect.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
void pts(pair c, real[] ang, string[] nm, pen[] col){
  for(int i=0;i<ang.length;++i){ pair p=c+dir(ang[i]); dot(p,col[i]); label("$"+nm[i]+"$",c+1.17*dir(ang[i]),col[i]); }
}
pair c1=(0,0), c2=(3,0);
draw(circle(c1,1)); draw(circle(c2,1));
draw(c1+dir(40)--c1+dir(90)--c1+dir(140)--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(c1+dir(220)--c1+dir(270)--c1+dir(320)--cycle,red+linewidth(0.8));
draw(c2+dir(90)--c2+dir(210)--c2+dir(330)--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(c2+dir(30)--c2+dir(150)--c2+dir(270)--cycle,red+linewidth(0.8));
pts(c1,new real[]{90,140,40,220,270,320},new string[]{"C","O","P","S","E","T"},new pen[]{blue,blue,blue,red,red,red});
pts(c2,new real[]{90,210,330,150,270,30},new string[]{"C","O","P","S","E","T"},new pen[]{blue,blue,blue,red,red,red});
label("disjoint",(0,-1.45)); label("intersecting",(3,-1.45));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Counting.} Circular orders with $C,O,P$ consecutive: arrange the block $\{C,O,P\}$ and the block $\{S,E,T\}$ around the circle (one way), then order inside each block: $3!\cdot3!=36$. So
\[P(\text{disjoint})=\dfrac{36}{120}=\dfrac{3}{10},\qquad P(\text{intersect})=1-\dfrac{3}{10}=\boxed{\dfrac{7}{10}}.\]

\textbf{(b)} The total $8!$ counts circular arrangements of all nine points, so the favourable count must also place $M$, $U$, $R$ as distinct points among the others. Once the six points $C,O,P,S,E,T$ are in a fixed circular order, the three other letters can be inserted in $6\cdot7\cdot8=336$ ways (several letters may go into the same gap, and their order matters), not $\dbinom63=20$. With $336$:
\[\dfrac{36\cdot336}{8!}=\dfrac{12096}{40320}=\dfrac{3}{10},\]
in agreement with (a). (The student's expression also gives the probability of \emph{not} intersecting, so the intersecting probability is $1$ minus it.)

question:
A regular hexagon and a square are inscribed in the same circle. Find
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] the ratio of the area of the square to the area of the hexagon;
\item[(b)] the ratio of the perimeter of the square to the perimeter of the hexagon.
\end{enumerate}
Give exact answers in simplest form.

solution:
Let the circle have centre $O$ and radius $r$.

\textbf{Hexagon.} The radii to the six vertices cut the regular hexagon into six equilateral triangles of side $r$. So its side is $r$, its perimeter is $6r$, and its area is $6\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}r^2=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}r^2$.

\textbf{Square.} Its diagonals are diameters of length $2r$, so its side is $\dfrac{2r}{\sqrt2}=r\sqrt2$, its perimeter is $4\sqrt2\,r$ and its area is $2r^2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(unitcircle);
pair[] h; for(int i=0;i<6;++i) h.push(dir(60*i));
pair[] q; for(int i=0;i<4;++i) q.push(dir(45+90*i));
draw(h[0]--h[1]--h[2]--h[3]--h[4]--h[5]--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(q[0]--q[1]--q[2]--q[3]--cycle,red+linewidth(0.8));
dot((0,0)); label("$O$",(0,0),SW);
draw((0,0)--h[0],dashed); label("$r$",(0.5,0),S);
label("$r$",(h[0]+h[1])/2,NE,blue); label("$r\sqrt2$",(q[1]+q[2])/2,E,red);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} $\dfrac{2r^2}{\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}r^2}=\dfrac{4}{3\sqrt3}=\boxed{\dfrac{4\sqrt3}{9}}\approx0.770$.

\textbf{(b)} $\dfrac{4\sqrt2\,r}{6r}=\boxed{\dfrac{2\sqrt2}{3}}\approx0.943$.

An equilateral triangle with side $x$ has area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}x^2$. So
\[[a]+[b]=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\left(a^2+b^2\right)=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}c^2=[c]\]
by the Pythagorean theorem $a^2+b^2=c^2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),B=(4,0),A=(0,3);
pair Pa=(2,-2*sqrt(3)), Pb=(-1.5*sqrt(3),1.5), Pc=(2+1.5*sqrt(3),1.5+2*sqrt(3));
filldraw(C--B--Pa--cycle,palegreen,black); filldraw(C--A--Pb--cycle,palegreen,black); filldraw(A--B--Pc--cycle,lightblue,black);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0.3)--(0.3,0.3)--(0.3,0));
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,SW);
label("$a$",(2,0),N); label("$b$",(0,1.5),E); label("$c$",(2,1.5),SW);
label("$[a]$",(C+B+Pa)/3); label("$[b]$",(C+A+Pb)/3); label("$[c]$",(A+B+Pc)/3);
\end{asy}

The ratio is $\boxed{1:1}$. (The same holds for any similar figures built on the three sides, such as semicircles, because their areas are all the same constant times the square of the side.)

question:
A $30^\circ$-$60^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle $ABP$ (right angle at $P$) has its hypotenuse $AB$ equal to a side of an equilateral triangle $ABC$ with side $3\sqrt3$ inches.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Suppose the two triangles lie on opposite sides of $AB$, so that together they form the quadrilateral $APBC$. Show that no circle passes through all four vertices $A$, $P$, $B$, $C$.
\item[(b)] Suppose instead that the two triangles lie on the same side of $AB$. Show that $P$ is then the midpoint of a side of triangle $ABC$, so the figure is just the triangle $ABC$, and find the area of the circle through $A$, $B$ and $C$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} In quadrilateral $APBC$ the opposite angles at $P$ and $C$ are $90^\circ$ and $60^\circ$. A quadrilateral is cyclic only if its opposite angles add up to $180^\circ$, and $90^\circ+60^\circ=150^\circ\ne180^\circ$. So no circle passes through all four vertices.

Equivalently: the circle through $A$, $B$, $C$ has centre $O$ and radius $3$ (see (b)). A point $X$ of this circle on the other side of $AB$ from $C$ sees $AB$ at the angle $\angle AXB=180^\circ-60^\circ=120^\circ$, but $\angle APB=90^\circ$; so $P$ lies outside the circle.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=3*sqrt(3);
pair A=(0,0),B=(s,0),C=(s/2,s*sqrt(3)/2),P=(3*s/4,-s*sqrt(3)/4),O=(s/2,s*sqrt(3)/6);
draw(circle(O,3),dashed);
draw(A--C--B--P--cycle,linewidth(0.8)); draw(A--B);
pair u=unit(A-P),v=unit(B-P); draw(P+0.35*u--P+0.35*u+0.35*v--P+0.35*v);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(P);dot(O);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,N); label("$P$",P,S); label("$O$",O,NE);
label("$60^\circ$",C,4*S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$30^\circ$",A,6*dir(-15),fontsize(8pt)); label("$60^\circ$",B,5*dir(200),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} Say the $60^\circ$ angle of triangle $ABP$ is at $A$ (the other case is symmetric). Then $\angle PAB=60^\circ=\angle CAB$ and $P$, $C$ are on the same side of $AB$, so $P$ lies on ray $AC$. In the $30^\circ$-$60^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle, the side opposite the $30^\circ$ angle is half the hypotenuse: $AP=\dfrac{1}{2}AB=\dfrac{1}{2}AC$. So $P$ is the midpoint of $AC$, and $BP$ is the altitude (and median) of the equilateral triangle. The right triangle is half of triangle $ABC$, and the figure is triangle $ABC$ itself.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=3*sqrt(3);
pair A=(0,0),B=(s,0),C=(s/2,s*sqrt(3)/2),P=(s/4,s*sqrt(3)/4),O=(s/2,s*sqrt(3)/6);
draw(circle(O,3),dashed);
filldraw(A--B--P--cycle,lightblue,black);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
pair u=unit(A-P),v=unit(B-P); draw(P+0.35*u--P+0.35*u+0.35*v--P+0.35*v);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(P);dot(O);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,N); label("$P$",P,NW); label("$O$",O,N);
label("$3$",(O+B)/2,N,fontsize(8pt)); draw(O--B,dotted);
\end{asy}

The centre $O$ of the circle through $A$, $B$, $C$ is the centroid of the equilateral triangle, two thirds of the way down the altitude $BP$:
\[R=OB=\dfrac{2}{3}\cdot BP=\dfrac{2}{3}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\cdot3\sqrt3=3.\]
The area of the circle is $\pi R^2=\boxed{9\pi}$ square inches.

question:
Three circles are externally tangent to each other in pairs, and their centres $A$, $B$, $C$ form a $30^\circ$-$60^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle with hypotenuse $4$ yards.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the total area of the three circles.
\item[(b)] Find the area of the region inside triangle $ABC$ but outside all three circles.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the right angle be at $A$ and the $30^\circ$ angle at $B$. Then $BC=4$, $AC=2$ (opposite the $30^\circ$ angle) and $AB=2\sqrt3$.

\textbf{Radii.} For externally tangent circles, the distance between centres is the sum of the radii:
\[r_A+r_B=2\sqrt3,\qquad r_A+r_C=2,\qquad r_B+r_C=4.\]
Adding, $r_A+r_B+r_C=3+\sqrt3$, so
\[r_A=\sqrt3-1,\qquad r_B=\sqrt3+1,\qquad r_C=3-\sqrt3,\]
all positive.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real ra=sqrt(3)-1, rb=1+sqrt(3), rc=3-sqrt(3);
pair A=(0,0),B=(2*sqrt(3),0),C=(0,2);
fill(A--B--C--cycle,mediumgray);
fill(circle(A,ra),white); fill(circle(B,rb),white); fill(circle(C,rc),white);
draw(circle(A,ra),blue); draw(circle(B,rb),blue); draw(circle(C,rc),blue);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw((0,0.25)--(0.25,0.25)--(0.25,0));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NW);
label("$2\sqrt3$",(1.2,0),S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$2$",(0,1.35),W,fontsize(8pt)); label("$4$",(B+C)/2,NE,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} The circles do not overlap, so the total area is
\[\pi\left[\left(\sqrt3-1\right)^2+\left(\sqrt3+1\right)^2+\left(3-\sqrt3\right)^2\right]=\pi\left[(4-2\sqrt3)+(4+2\sqrt3)+(12-6\sqrt3)\right]=\boxed{\left(20-6\sqrt3\right)\pi}\]
square yards (about $52.2$).

\textbf{(b)} Inside the triangle, each circle covers a sector whose angle is the triangle's angle at that centre. (Each radius is smaller than the distance from its centre to the opposite side, which is $\sqrt3$ from $A$, $2\sqrt3$ from $B$ and $2$ from $C$, so the sectors lie inside the triangle.) The sector areas are
\[\dfrac{1}{4}\pi\left(4-2\sqrt3\right)+\dfrac{1}{12}\pi\left(4+2\sqrt3\right)+\dfrac{1}{6}\pi\left(12-6\sqrt3\right)=\dfrac{\pi\left(20-8\sqrt3\right)}{6}=\dfrac{\left(10-4\sqrt3\right)\pi}{3}.\]
The triangle has area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot2\cdot2\sqrt3=2\sqrt3$. The shaded region has area
\[\boxed{2\sqrt3-\dfrac{\left(10-4\sqrt3\right)\pi}{3}}\approx0.247\text{ square yards}.\]

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $\tan\left(z+\dfrac{3\pi}{2}\right)=-\cot z$ for every real $z$ that is not an integer multiple of $\pi$.
\item[(b)] Explain why substituting $b=\dfrac{3\pi}{2}$ into the angle-sum formula $\tan(a+b)=\dfrac{\tan a+\tan b}{1-\tan a\tan b}$ does not work, and show how the formula can still be used to reach the same result.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Turning by $\dfrac{3\pi}{2}$ anticlockwise is the same as turning by $\dfrac{\pi}{2}$ clockwise, which maps a point $(x,y)$ of the unit circle to $(y,-x)$. So the point at angle $z+\dfrac{3\pi}{2}$ is $(\sin z,-\cos z)$:
\[\cos\left(z+\dfrac{3\pi}{2}\right)=\sin z,\qquad\sin\left(z+\dfrac{3\pi}{2}\right)=-\cos z.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real t=35;
pair O=(0,0),P=dir(t),Q=dir(t-90);
draw((-1.35,0)--(1.4,0),Arrow(4)); draw((0,-1.35)--(0,1.35),Arrow(4));
draw(unitcircle);
draw(O--P,blue+linewidth(0.8)); draw(O--Q,red+linewidth(0.8));
draw(arc(O,0.3,0,t),blue,Arrow(3)); draw(arc(O,0.45,t,t+270),red,Arrow(3));
dot(P,blue); dot(Q,red);
label("$(\cos z,\sin z)$",P,NE,blue); label("$(\sin z,-\cos z)$",Q,SE,red);
label("$z$",0.42*dir(t/2),blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$z+\dfrac{3\pi}{2}$",(-1.25,0.85),red+fontsize(8pt));
label("$x$",(1.4,0),S); label("$y$",(0,1.35),W);
\end{asy}

If $z$ is not a multiple of $\pi$, then $\sin z\ne0$ and
\[\tan\left(z+\dfrac{3\pi}{2}\right)=\dfrac{\sin\left(z+\dfrac{3\pi}{2}\right)}{\cos\left(z+\dfrac{3\pi}{2}\right)}=\dfrac{-\cos z}{\sin z}=-\cot z.\]
(If $z$ is a multiple of $\pi$, both sides are undefined.)

\textbf{(b)} The angle-sum formula for tangent is valid only when $\tan a$, $\tan b$ and $\tan(a+b)$ are all defined. Since $\cos\dfrac{3\pi}{2}=0$, $\tan\dfrac{3\pi}{2}$ is undefined, so the formula simply does not apply with $b=\dfrac{3\pi}{2}$; getting ``undefined'' says nothing about $\tan\left(z+\dfrac{3\pi}{2}\right)$, which is the domain issue the question suspects. The formula can still be used for $b$ \emph{near} $\dfrac{3\pi}{2}$: dividing numerator and denominator by $\tan b$,
\[\tan(a+b)=\dfrac{\dfrac{\tan a}{\tan b}+1}{\dfrac{1}{\tan b}-\tan a}=\dfrac{\tan a\cot b+1}{\cot b-\tan a}.\]
As $b\to\dfrac{3\pi}{2}$, $\cot b\to0$, and by continuity of tangent the left side tends to $\tan\left(a+\dfrac{3\pi}{2}\right)$; the right side tends to $\dfrac{1}{-\tan a}=-\cot a$, in agreement with (a) (for $a$ not a multiple of $\dfrac{\pi}{2}$). Alternatively, use the period: $\tan\left(z+\dfrac{3\pi}{2}\right)=\tan\left(z+\dfrac{\pi}{2}\right)$, and the cosine and sine sum formulas give $\tan\left(z+\dfrac{\pi}{2}\right)=\dfrac{\cos z}{-\sin z}$.

Let the chord be $AB$ and the centre $O$. The perpendicular from $O$ bisects the chord, so in the right triangle with hypotenuse $OA=6$ and leg $3\sqrt3$, the other leg (the distance from $O$ to the chord) is $\sqrt{36-27}=3$. This is a $30^\circ$-$60^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle, so $\angle AOB=2\cdot60^\circ=120^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); real r=6;
pair P=r*dir(210),Q=r*dir(330);
fill(arc(O,r,210,330)--cycle,lightblue);
draw(circle(O,r)); draw(P--Q,linewidth(0.8)); draw(O--P); draw(O--Q); draw(O--(0,-3),dashed);
dot(O);dot(P);dot(Q);
label("$O$",O,N); label("$A$",P,W); label("$B$",Q,E);
label("$6$",(O+P)/2,NW,fontsize(9pt)); label("$3$",(0,-2.2),E,fontsize(9pt)); label("$120^\circ$",1.6*dir(240),fontsize(8pt));
label("$6\sqrt3$",(0,-3),S,fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

The smaller region (shaded) is the $120^\circ$ sector minus triangle $AOB$:
\[\dfrac{120}{360}\cdot36\pi-\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot6\sqrt3\cdot3=12\pi-9\sqrt3.\]
The larger region is the rest of the disc: $36\pi-\left(12\pi-9\sqrt3\right)=24\pi+9\sqrt3$. The ratio is
\[\dfrac{12\pi-9\sqrt3}{24\pi+9\sqrt3}=\boxed{\dfrac{4\pi-3\sqrt3}{8\pi+3\sqrt3}}\approx0.248.\]

The radii are $8,4,2,1,\ldots$ cm, so the areas are $64\pi,16\pi,4\pi,\pi,\ldots$: each area is $\dfrac{1}{4}$ of the previous one (halving a length divides an area by $4$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real x=0; real d=16;
for(int i=0;i<6;++i){ draw(circle((x+d/2,0),d/2)); if(i<4) label("$"+string(d)+"$",(x+d/2,0)); x+=d; d/=2; }
label("$\cdots$",(x+0.8,0));
\end{asy}

This is an infinite geometric series with first term $64\pi$ and ratio $\dfrac{1}{4}$, so its sum is
\[\dfrac{64\pi}{1-\dfrac{1}{4}}=\dfrac{64\pi}{\dfrac{3}{4}}=\boxed{\dfrac{256\pi}{3}}\text{ cm}^2\approx268\text{ cm}^2.\]

question:
A sheet of paper has area $A$ and thickness $t$. It is folded in half again and again; each fold halves the area covered by the folded stack and doubles its thickness. Ignore the paper used up in the bends.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the base area, the thickness and the volume of the stack after $n$ folds.
\item[(b)] Someone argues that since the thickness doubles forever, folding infinitely often would give a stack of infinite height and therefore infinite volume. Explain the error.
\item[(c)] For a sheet of area $600$ cm$^2$ and thickness $0.01$ cm, what is the least number of folds that would make the stack more than $1$ metre thick, and what would its base area be then?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} After $n$ folds the base area is $\dfrac{A}{2^n}$ and the thickness is $2^n t$. The volume is
\[\dfrac{A}{2^n}\cdot2^nt=\boxed{At},\]
the same as for the flat sheet: folding only rearranges the paper. (The total area of paper, counted layer by layer, also stays $2^n\cdot\dfrac{A}{2^n}=A$.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real L=8, t=0.12;
draw(box((0,0),(L,t)),linewidth(0.8)); label("$0$ folds: base $A$, thickness $t$",(L/2,t),N,fontsize(9pt));
draw(box((0,-1.6),(L/2,-1.6+2t)),linewidth(0.8)); label("$1$ fold: $\dfrac {A}{2}$, $2t$",(L/2,-1.6+t),E,fontsize(9pt));
draw(box((0,-3.2),(L/4,-3.2+4t)),linewidth(0.8)); label("$2$ folds: $\dfrac {A}{4}$, $4t$",(L/4,-3.2+2t),E,fontsize(9pt));
draw(box((0,-4.8),(L/8,-4.8+8t)),linewidth(0.8)); label("$3$ folds: $\dfrac {A}{8}$, $8t$",(L/8,-4.8+4t),E,fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} The height $2^nt$ grows without bound, but the base $\dfrac{A}{2^n}$ shrinks at exactly the same rate, so the volume (height times base) stays $At$ for every $n$. Volume is not determined by height alone; an ever taller stack can have a constant, finite volume because it becomes ever thinner. (Physically, after a few folds the base becomes too small to fold again, and real paper cannot be folded more than a handful of times.)

\textbf{(c)} We need $0.01\cdot2^n>100$, that is $2^n>10000$. Since $2^{13}=8192$ and $2^{14}=16384$, the least number is $\boxed{14}$ folds, giving a thickness of $163.84$ cm. The base area is then $\dfrac{600}{16384}\approx0.037$ cm$^2$, about the size of a $2$ mm by $2$ mm square; the volume is still $6$ cm$^3$.

question:
A car costs $\$10{,}000$. The payment service charges the seller a fee of $2.5\%$ of the amount the buyer pays. To cover the fee, the seller raises the price by $2.5\%$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Does the seller still lose money compared with receiving $\$10{,}000$? If so, how much?
\item[(b)] What price should the seller charge so that exactly $\$10{,}000$ remains after the fee? What is the least price in whole cents that leaves the seller at least $\$10{,}000$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
As $|x|^5=1$, $|x|=1$; write $x=\cos\theta+i\sin\theta$. By De Moivre's theorem $5\theta=90^\circ+360^\circ m$, so
\[\theta=18^\circ,\ 90^\circ,\ 162^\circ,\ 234^\circ,\ 306^\circ.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
fill((0,0)--(1.35,0)--(1.35,1.35)--(0,1.35)--cycle,palegreen);
draw((-1.4,0)--(1.45,0),Arrow(4)); draw((0,-1.4)--(0,1.45),Arrow(4));
draw(unitcircle);
for(int k=0;k<5;++k){ pair z=dir(18+72*k); dot(z,(k==0)?red:black); draw((0,0)--z,(k==0)?red:gray); }
label("$x$",dir(18),E,red); label("$i$",dir(90),NW); draw(arc((0,0),0.3,0,18),red); label("$18^\circ$",0.3*dir(5),E,red+fontsize(7pt));
label("$\mathrm{Re}$",(1.45,0),S); label("$\mathrm{Im}$",(0,1.45),W);
\end{asy}

Only $\theta=18^\circ$ lies strictly inside the first quadrant; $\theta=90^\circ$ gives $x=i$, which is on the imaginary axis (its real part is $0$). So $x=\cos18^\circ+i\sin18^\circ$.

\textbf{Exact values.} Let $\alpha=18^\circ$. Then $2\alpha=90^\circ-3\alpha$, so $\sin2\alpha=\cos3\alpha$:
\[2\sin\alpha\cos\alpha=4\cos^3\alpha-3\cos\alpha.\]
Dividing by $\cos\alpha\ne0$: $2\sin\alpha=4\cos^2\alpha-3=1-4\sin^2\alpha$, so $4\sin^2\alpha+2\sin\alpha-1=0$ and, as $\sin\alpha>0$,
\[\sin18^\circ=\dfrac{-2+\sqrt{20}}{8}=\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{4},\qquad\cos18^\circ=\sqrt{1-\dfrac{6-2\sqrt5}{16}}=\dfrac{\sqrt{10+2\sqrt5}}{4}.\]
Hence the only solution is
\[x=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt{10+2\sqrt5}}{4}+\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{4}\,i}.\]

question:
Solve $3^x=2.5$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Express the solution with a logarithm, show that it lies strictly between $0$ and $1$, and explain why the equation has exactly one real solution.
\item[(b)] Someone suggests that an equation with ``no nice answer'' has the solution $x=10$. Check whether $x=10$ is a solution, and find $x$ to three decimal places.
\end{enumerate}

For a complex number $w$, the \emph{principal square root} $\sqrt w$ is the square root of $w$ with positive real part, or, if the real part is $0$, the one with nonnegative imaginary part (so $\sqrt4=2$ and $\sqrt{-1}=i$).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that no complex number has principal square root $-1$.
\item[(b)] Suppose we invent a new number $j$ and declare $\sqrt j=-1$, where $\sqrt j$ is a square root of $j$, keeping the usual rules of arithmetic. Show that $j=1$, and explain why this contradicts the definition of the principal square root, whereas inventing $i$ with $i^2=-1$ leads to no contradiction.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $h$ be the distance from $A$ to line $BC$. Triangles $PBC$ and $ABC$ share the base $BC$, so
\[[PBC]=[ABC]\iff\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot BC\cdot d(P,BC)=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot BC\cdot h\iff d(P,BC)=h,\]
where $d(P,BC)$ is the distance from $P$ to line $BC$.

The points at distance $h$ from line $BC$ form two lines parallel to $BC$, one on each side. On the side of $A$, this is the line $\ell$ through $A$ parallel to $BC$. Conversely, every point $P$ of $\ell$ is at distance $h$ from $BC$, is not on line $BC$, and gives $[PBC]=[ABC]$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(6,0),A=(1.6,3),P=(4.6,3);
draw((-1.5,3)--(7.5,3),blue+linewidth(0.8)); label("$\ell$",(7.5,3),E,blue);
draw((-1.5,0)--(7.5,0),gray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8)); draw(P--B,dashed); draw(P--C,dashed);
draw(A--(1.6,0),dotted); draw(P--(4.6,0),dotted);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(P,blue);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$P$",P,N,blue);
label("$h$",(1.6,1.5),W,fontsize(9pt)); label("$h$",(4.6,1.5),E,fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

So the locus is \textbf{the whole line through $A$ parallel to $BC$}. $\blacksquare$

Each decagon has $10$ sides. The common side $PQ$ lies inside the combined figure, so it is not part of the outline; every other side of the two decagons is. That leaves $10+10-2=18$ segments on the outline.

These $18$ segments are $18$ different sides only if no two neighbouring segments lie on one straight line. Inside each decagon the angles are $\dfrac{(10-2)\cdot180^\circ}{10}=144^\circ$. At $P$ (and likewise at $Q$) the outline turns through the two decagon angles together, $144^\circ+144^\circ=288^\circ$, which is not $180^\circ$; so the two outline segments at $P$ do not form a straight line, and $P$ and $Q$ remain corners. (Also $288^\circ<360^\circ$, so the decagons do not overlap.) All other corners are corners of one decagon.

Hence the outline is a (non-convex) polygon with $\boxed{18}$ sides.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=1/(2*Sin(18)), a=1/(2*Tan(18));
pair L=(-a,0), Rr=(a,0);
guide gl, gr;
for(int k=0;k<10;++k){ gl=gl--(L+R*dir(18+36k)); gr=gr--(Rr+R*dir(198+36k)); }
gl=gl--cycle; gr=gr--cycle;
fill(gl,paleblue); fill(gr,paleyellow);
draw(gl,linewidth(0.9)); draw(gr,linewidth(0.9));
pair P=(0,-0.5), Q=(0,0.5);
draw(P--Q,white+linewidth(1.6)); draw(P--Q,red+dashed+linewidth(1));
dot(P); dot(Q); label("$P$",P,S); label("$Q$",Q+(0,0.3));
draw(arc(Q,0.22,126,270),blue); draw(arc(Q,0.27,-90,54),blue);
label("$144^\circ$",Q+0.5*dir(200),blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$144^\circ$",Q+0.5*dir(-20),blue+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Remark: the angle check matters. Two squares sharing a side form a rectangle with $4$ sides, not $4+4-2=6$, because $90^\circ+90^\circ=180^\circ$.

question:
The Smith family has $4$ sons and $3$ daughters.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] In how many ways can the seven of them sit in a row of $7$ chairs so that at least $2$ boys sit next to each other?
\item[(b)] A student argues: ``Glue two of the boys together into one block; the two boys can be chosen in order in $4\cdot3$ ways. Then arrange the $6$ objects (the block and the other $5$ people) in $6!$ ways. So the answer is $4\cdot3\cdot6!=8640$.'' Explain what is wrong with this argument, and what the number $8640$ actually counts.
\end{enumerate}

A man sells radishes for $\$5$ a pound. Two customers arrive: one wants only the tops of the radishes and the other only the bottoms. The seller agrees to charge $\$3$ a pound for tops and $\$2$ a pound for bottoms. Suppose the tops make up a fraction $t$ of the weight of each radish, where $0<t<1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] For each pound of whole radishes that he cuts up and sells in this way, how much money does the seller receive? Show that this is less than $\$5$ whatever $t$ is.
\item[(b)] If tops and bottoms each make up half of the weight, how much does the seller lose by selling one pound of tops and one pound of bottoms this way, compared with selling the same radishes whole?
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the area of a trapezoid equals the area of a triangle whose side lengths are the two diagonals of the trapezoid and the sum of its two bases.
\item[(b)] A trapezoid has bases $4$ and $11$ and diagonals $9$ and $12$. Find its area.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let $ABCD$ be the trapezoid with $AB\parallel DC$, $AB=b$, $DC=a$, and diagonals $AC=p$, $BD=q$. Translate the diagonal $BD$ by the vector $\overrightarrow{DC}$: $D$ goes to $C$ and $B$ goes to a point $B'$ on line $AB$ beyond $B$ with $BB'=DC=a$ (because $DC\parallel AB$). Then $CB'=BD=q$ and $AB'=b+a$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(11,0), C=(5.4,7.2), D=(1.4,7.2), Bp=(15,0);
fill(A--B--C--D--cycle,paleblue);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,blue); draw(B--D,red);
draw(B--Bp,dashed); draw(C--Bp,red+dashed);
draw(C--(C.x,0),dotted);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,S); label("$C$",C,N); label("$D$",D,N); label("$B'$",Bp,SE);
label("$4$",(D+C)/2,N); label("$11$",(A+B)/2,S); label("$4$",(B+Bp)/2,S);
label("$9$",(A+C)/2,NW,blue); label("$12$",(B+D)*0.5+(-0.3,0.4),NE,red); label("$12$",(C+Bp)/2,NE,red);
label("$h$",(C.x,3.2),E);
\end{asy}

The triangle $ACB'$ has sides $p$, $q$, $a+b$. Its base $AB'$ lies on line $AB$ and its apex $C$ lies on line $DC$, so its height is the height $h$ of the trapezoid. Hence
\[[ACB']=\dfrac{1}{2}(a+b)h=[ABCD].\]
(So the area of a trapezoid can be found from its diagonals and bases by Heron's formula applied to the triangle with sides $p,q,a+b$.)

\textbf{(b)} Here the triangle has sides $9$, $12$ and $4+11=15$. Since $9^2+12^2=81+144=225=15^2$, it is a right triangle with legs $9$ and $12$, and its area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot9\cdot12=54$. Therefore the trapezoid has area $\boxed{54}$.

Such a trapezoid exists: with $A=(0,0)$, $B=(11,0)$, $C=\left(\dfrac{27}{5},\dfrac{36}{5}\right)$ and $D=\left(\dfrac{7}{5},\dfrac{36}{5}\right)$ we have $DC=4$, $AC=9$, $BD=12$, and the height is $\dfrac{36}{5}$; indeed $\dfrac{1}{2}(4+11)\cdot\dfrac{36}{5}=54$.

question:
Sam does not know the answers to $5$ questions of a multiple-choice test; each has $5$ choices. For each of these questions he can either
\begin{itemize}
\item guess at random, and then he is right with probability $\dfrac{1}{5}$; or
\item reason it out, and then he is right exactly when his reasoning is correct, which happens with probability $p$ (where $0\le p\le1$).
\end{itemize}
The results on different questions are independent. Sam wants to maximise the probability of getting all $5$ questions right.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If he reasons on $k$ of the questions and guesses on the other $5-k$, what is the probability that he gets all $5$ right?
\item[(b)] For which values of $p$ should he reason on all five questions, and for which should he guess on all five? Can a combination of the two methods ever give a strictly better chance than both pure strategies?
\end{enumerate}

Consider the equation $x=-\dfrac{1}{y-1}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that for each real number $y\ne1$ there is exactly one value of $x$, and that there is none for $y=1$.
\item[(b)] Show that different values of $y$ give different values of $x$. Which real numbers $x$ occur, and how can $y$ be expressed in terms of $x$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} By the remainder theorem, $V(c)$ is the remainder when $V(t)$ is divided by $t-c$. Synthetic division with the coefficients $1,-19,108,-180,0$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $c=1$: $1,\ -18,\ 90,\ -90,\ -90$, so $V(1)=-90$;
\item $c=5$: $1,\ -14,\ 38,\ 10,\ 50$, so $V(5)=50$;
\item $c=8$: $1,\ -11,\ 20,\ -20,\ -160$, so $V(8)=-160$.
\end{itemize}
The speeds are $200-90=\boxed{110}$ mph at $t=1$, $200+50=\boxed{250}$ mph at $t=5$, and $200-160=\boxed{40}$ mph at $t=8$.

\textbf{(b)} The speed is $200$ mph exactly when $V(t)=0$. Factor out $t$: $V(t)=t\,(t^3-19t^2+108t-180)$. Trying divisors of $180$, $t=3$ is a root of the cubic ($27-171+324-180=0$); synthetic division by $t-3$ gives $t^2-16t+60=(t-6)(t-10)$. So
\[V(t)=t(t-3)(t-6)(t-10).\]
The roots in $0<t<10.3$ are $t=3,6,10$, so his speed was $200$ mph \textbf{three times}, at $t=3$, $t=6$ and $t=10$ seconds.

\textbf{Each diagonal is parallel to a side.} Triangles $BCD$ and $BCA$ have the common base $BC$ and equal areas, so $A$ and $D$ are at the same distance from line $BC$; by convexity they are on the same side of it. Hence $AD\parallel BC$. In the same way $BE\parallel CD$, $CA\parallel DE$, $DB\parallel EA$ and $EC\parallel AB$.

\textbf{A parallelogram.} Let the diagonals $BD$ and $CE$ meet at $P$. Then $EP\parallel AB$ (as $EC\parallel AB$) and $BP\parallel AE$ (as $BD\parallel EA$), so $ABPE$ is a parallelogram, and its diagonal $BE$ splits it into two congruent triangles: $[BPE]=[EAB]=1$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=dir(90), B=dir(162), C=dir(234), D=dir(306), E=dir(18);
pair P=extension(B,D,C,E);
fill(A--B--P--E--cycle,paleyellow);
draw(A--B--C--D--E--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--D,blue); draw(C--E,blue); draw(B--E,dashed);
draw(A--D,gray+dotted);
dot(P);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$D$",D,SE); label("$E$",E,E); label("$P$",P,S);
label("$x$",(P+C+D)/3,blue); label("$1-x$",(B+C+P)/3+(0.04,0.02),fontsize(8pt)); label("$1-x$",(P+D+E)/3+(-0.04,0.02),fontsize(8pt));
label("$1$",(B+P+E)/3+(0,0.03));
\end{asy}

\textbf{An equation.} Let $x=[PCD]$. Since $[BCD]=[BCP]+[PCD]=1$ and $[CDE]=[PCD]+[PDE]=1$, we get $[BCP]=[PDE]=1-x$. Triangles $BCP$ and $PCD$ have the same height from $C$, so $\dfrac{[BCP]}{[PCD]}=\dfrac{BP}{PD}$; triangles $BPE$ and $PDE$ have the same height from $E$, so $\dfrac{[BPE]}{[PDE]}=\dfrac{BP}{PD}$. Therefore
\[\dfrac{1-x}{x}=\dfrac{1}{1-x},\qquad (1-x)^2=x,\qquad x^2-3x+1=0.\]
Since $0<x<1$, $x=\dfrac{3-\sqrt5}{2}$.

\textbf{The area.} The pentagon consists of triangle $EAB$ and quadrilateral $BCDE$, which its diagonals split into $BPE$, $BCP$, $PCD$, $PDE$. So
\[[ABCDE]=1+1+(1-x)+x+(1-x)=4-x=4-\dfrac{3-\sqrt5}{2}=\boxed{\dfrac{5+\sqrt5}{2}}.\]
This value does not depend on the pentagon, so all such pentagons have the same area. (Such pentagons exist: a regular pentagon scaled so that $[ABC]=1$ is one.)

Let the side of length $8$ be $AB$, with $A=(0,0)$ and $B=(8,0)$, and let $C$ be the third vertex. The area is $12$, so the distance from $C$ to line $AB$ is $\dfrac{2\cdot12}{8}=3$; by symmetry we may assume $C$ lies on the line $\ell$: $y=3$. We must minimise $AC+CB$ for $C$ on $\ell$.

Reflect $A$ in $\ell$ to get $A'=(0,6)$. For every point $X$ on $\ell$, $AX=A'X$, so
\[AX+XB=A'X+XB\ge A'B,\]
with equality exactly when $X$ lies on segment $A'B$. Since $\ell$ is halfway between the heights of $A'$ and $B$, it meets $A'B$ at its midpoint $(4,3)$, the point of $\ell$ directly above the midpoint of $AB$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(8,0), C=(4,3), X=(1.3,3), Ap=(0,6);
draw((-1,3)--(9,3),gray+dashed); label("$\ell$",(9,3),E,gray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--X--B,blue);
draw(A--Ap,dotted); draw(Ap--B,red+dashed);
dot(Ap); dot(X,blue);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N); label("$X$",X,N,blue); label("$A'$",Ap,W);
label("$8$",(A+B)/2,S); label("$5$",(A+C)/2,NW); label("$5$",(B+C)/2,NE); label("$3$",(0,1.5),W);
\end{asy}

Here $A'B=\sqrt{8^2+6^2}=10$. So the minimum of $AC+CB$ is $10$, attained by the isosceles triangle with $AC=BC=\sqrt{4^2+3^2}=5$, and the minimum perimeter is $8+10=\boxed{18}$.

Let the given side be $AB=c$ and the given area $K$. The third vertex $X$ must be at distance $h=\dfrac{2K}{c}$ from line $AB$; by symmetry we may take $X$ on the line $\ell$ parallel to $AB$ at distance $h$ on one fixed side. The perimeter is $c+AX+XB$, so we must show that $AX+XB$ is smallest when $AX=XB$.

Reflect $A$ in $\ell$ to get $A'$; then $AX=A'X$ for every $X$ on $\ell$, so by the triangle inequality
\[AX+XB=A'X+XB\ge A'B,\]
with equality exactly when $X$ lies on segment $A'B$. The line $\ell$ is at height $h$ while $A'$ is at height $2h$ and $B$ at height $0$, so segment $A'B$ crosses $\ell$ at its midpoint $M$, which lies directly above the midpoint of $AB$. Hence the minimum of $AX+XB$ is attained exactly at $X=M$, where $MA=MB$ ($M$ is on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(7,0), M=(3.5,2.2), X=(1.4,2.2), Ap=(0,4.4);
draw((-0.8,2.2)--(8,2.2),gray+dashed); label("$\ell$",(8,2.2),E,gray);
draw(A--B,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--M--B,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--X--B,blue);
draw(A--Ap,dotted); draw(Ap--B,red+dashed); draw(Ap--X,blue+dotted);
dot(Ap); dot(X,blue); dot(M);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$M$",M,N); label("$X$",X,N,blue); label("$A'$",Ap,W);
label("$c$",(A+B)/2,S); label("$h$",(0,1.1),W); label("$h$",(0,3.3),W);
\end{asy}

So the triangle with the smallest perimeter is the isosceles triangle $ABM$ with $MA=MB$, and its perimeter is $c+A'B=c+\sqrt{c^2+4h^2}$. Every other triangle with the same base and area has a strictly larger perimeter.

The prism has two bases that are equilateral triangles of side $\sqrt3$, and three lateral faces. The lateral edges have length $\sqrt3$ as well; we take the prism to be a right prism (lateral edges perpendicular to the bases), so the lateral faces are squares of side $\sqrt3$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(1.732,0), C=(0.866,1.5), v=(1.1,0.75);
pair Ap=A+v, Bp=B+v, Cp=C+v;
fill(A--B--C--cycle,paleblue);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(Bp--Cp,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--Ap,dashed); draw(B--Bp,linewidth(0.9)); draw(C--Cp,linewidth(0.9));
draw(Ap--Bp,dashed); draw(Ap--Cp,dashed);
label("$\sqrt3$",(A+B)/2,S); label("$\sqrt3$",(B+Bp)/2,SE); label("$\sqrt3$",(A+C)/2,W);
\end{asy}

An equilateral triangle of side $s=\sqrt3$ has area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{4}$, and each square has area $3$. So the surface area is
\[2\cdot\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{4}+3\cdot3=\boxed{9+\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}},\]
and the volume is (base area)$\times$(height)
\[\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{4}\cdot\sqrt3=\boxed{\dfrac{9}{4}}.\]

\textbf{The three points.} At $x=0$: $y=-\dfrac{3}{4}\cdot4+12=9$, so the $y$-intercept is $(0,9)$. For $y=0$: $|x-4|=16$, so $x=20$ or $x=-12$; the positive $x$-intercept is $(20,0)$. The vertex (where $x-4=0$) is $(4,12)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return -0.75*abs(x-4)+12;}
draw((-3,0)--(22,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-2)--(0,14),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(22,0),E); label("$y$",(0,14),N);
draw(graph(f,-2,21),blue);
pair P=(0,9), Q=(20,0), V=(4,12);
fill(P--Q--V--cycle,palered); draw(P--Q--V--cycle,red);
dot(P); dot(Q); dot(V);
label("$(0,9)$",P,W); label("$(20,0)$",Q,SW); label("$(4,12)$",V,N);
\end{asy}

\textbf{The area.} By the shoelace formula for $(0,9)$, $(20,0)$, $(4,12)$:
\[\text{Area}=\dfrac{1}{2}\left|0\cdot(0-12)+20\cdot(12-9)+4\cdot(9-0)\right|=\dfrac{1}{2}(60+36)=\boxed{48}.\]

\textbf{Two right angles.} In triangle $MAT$, $15^2+20^2=225+400=625=25^2$, so by the converse of the Pythagorean theorem $\angle MAT=90^\circ$. In triangle $ATH$, $8^2+15^2=64+225=289=17^2$, so $\angle ATH=90^\circ$.

\textbf{Shape.} Both $MA$ and $TH$ are perpendicular to $AT$, so $MA\parallel TH$. The points $M$ and $H$ are on the same side of line $AT$: otherwise the side $HM$ would cross the side $AT$ and $MATH$ would not be a (simple) quadrilateral. So $MATH$ is a right trapezoid with parallel sides $MA=20$ and $TH=8$ and height $AT=15$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), M=(0,20), T=(15,0), H=(15,8);
fill(M--A--T--H--cycle,paleblue);
draw(M--A--T--H--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(M--T,dashed); draw(A--H,dashed);
draw((0,1.2)--(1.2,1.2)--(1.2,0)); draw((13.8,0)--(13.8,1.2)--(15,1.2));
label("$M$",M,NW); label("$A$",A,SW); label("$T$",T,SE); label("$H$",H,NE);
label("$20$",(A+M)/2,W); label("$15$",(A+T)/2,S); label("$8$",(T+H)/2,E);
label("$25$",(M+T)/2+(0.6,0.4),NE); label("$17$",A+0.62*(H-A),NW);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Area.}
\[[MATH]=\dfrac{(20+8)}{2}\cdot15=\boxed{210}.\]
(Check: $[MAT]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot20\cdot15=150$, and with $A=(0,0)$, $M=(0,20)$, $T=(15,0)$, $H=(15,8)$, triangle $MTH$ has area $60$; $150+60=210$.)

There are $\dbinom83=56$ equally likely triangles. Count by the number of octagon sides they use (label the vertices $1,\dots,8$ around the octagon).
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Two sides:} three consecutive vertices; there are $8$ such triangles (one for each middle vertex).
\item \textbf{Exactly one side:} choose the side ($8$ ways) and a third vertex that is not one of its endpoints and not next to either endpoint: $8-2-2=4$ ways. That gives $32$ triangles (like the red triangle $1,2,5$ below).
\end{itemize}
So $8+32=40$ triangles use at least one side of the octagon (the other $16$, like the dashed triangle $3,5,7$, use none). The probability is
\[\dfrac{40}{56}=\boxed{\textbf{(D)}\ \dfrac{5}{7}}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V; for(int k=0;k<8;++k) V[k]=dir(90+45k);
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--V[6]--V[7]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
for(int k=0;k<8;++k) dot(V[k]);
fill(V[0]--V[1]--V[4]--cycle,palered+opacity(0.6)); draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[4]--cycle,red);
draw(V[2]--V[4]--V[6]--cycle,blue+dashed);
for(int k=0;k<8;++k) label("$"+string(k+1)+"$",1.15*V[k]);
\end{asy}

The midpoints of the sides of a right triangle cut it into four congruent triangles, each similar to it with ratio $\dfrac{1}{2}$, hence each with $\dfrac{1}{4}$ of its area. So each shaded triangle has $\dfrac{1}{4}$ of the area of the triangle it was cut from, and the next step works inside a triangle (the one at $G$) that also has $\dfrac{1}{4}$ of that area.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), A=(0,6), G=(6,0);
pair B=(0,3), D=(3,0), J=(3,3), K=(3,1.5), E=(4.5,0), I=(4.5,1.5), L=(4.5,0.75), F=(5.25,0), H=(5.25,0.75);
fill(B--C--D--cycle,lightgray); fill(K--D--E--cycle,lightgray); fill(L--E--F--cycle,lightgray);
draw(A--C--G--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--D--J--cycle); draw(K--E--I--cycle); draw(L--F--H--cycle);
draw((0,0.35)--(0.35,0.35)--(0.35,0));
label("$A$",A,N); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$G$",G,SE); label("$B$",B,W); label("$D$",D,S); label("$J$",J,NE);
label("$K$",K,W); label("$E$",E,S); label("$I$",I,NE); label("$L$",L,W,fontsize(8pt)); label("$F$",F,S,fontsize(8pt));
label("$6$",(0,4.5),W); label("$6$",(1.5,0),S);
\end{asy}

The big triangle has area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot6\cdot6=18$, so the shaded areas are $\dfrac{18}{4},\dfrac{18}{16},\dfrac{18}{64},\dots$, and after $100$ steps the total is
\[\dfrac{18}{4}\left(1+\dfrac{1}{4}+\cdots+\dfrac{1}{4^{99}}\right)=\dfrac{18}{4}\cdot\dfrac{1-\left(\dfrac{1}{4}\right)^{100}}{1-\dfrac{1}{4}}=6\left(1-\dfrac{1}{4^{100}}\right).\]
This is less than $6$ by an extremely small amount, so the total is nearest to $\boxed{\textbf{(A)}\ 6}$.

Since $8^2+15^2=64+225=289=17^2$, the triangle has a right angle opposite the side $17$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), B=(15,0), A=(0,8);
pair O=(A+B)/2, I=(3,3);
draw(circle(O,8.5),gray); draw(circle(I,3),blue);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0.8)--(0.8,0.8)--(0.8,0));
draw(O--B,red); draw(I--(3,0),blue+dashed);
dot(O,red); dot(I,blue);
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,SW);
label("$8$",(A+C)/2,W); label("$15$",(10,0),S); label("$17$",A+0.28*(B-A),NE);
label("$O$",O,N,red); label("$R$",(O+B)/2,N,red); label("$I$",I,N,blue); label("$r$",(3,1.5),E,blue);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} The hypotenuse of a right triangle is a diameter of its circumcircle, so $R=\dfrac{17}{2}$ and the area is $\pi R^2=\boxed{\dfrac{289\pi}{4}}$.

\textbf{(b)} The area of the triangle is $K=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot8\cdot15=60$ and its semiperimeter is $s=\dfrac{8+15+17}{2}=20$. From $K=rs$, $r=3$. The incircle has area $\boxed{9\pi}$.

\textbf{(c)} The difference is $\dfrac{289\pi}{4}-9\pi=\dfrac{289\pi-36\pi}{4}=\boxed{\dfrac{253\pi}{4}}$.

By Heron's formula with $s=\dfrac{13+14+15}{2}=21$, the area is
\[K=\sqrt{21\cdot8\cdot7\cdot6}=\sqrt{7056}=84.\]
The altitude to a side of length $a$ is $\dfrac{2K}{a}=\dfrac{168}{a}$:
\[h_{13}=\dfrac{168}{13},\qquad h_{14}=\dfrac{168}{14}=12,\qquad h_{15}=\dfrac{168}{15}=\dfrac{56}{5}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(14,0), A=(5,12);
pair Ha=(5,0);
pair Hb=A+dot(B-A,C-A)/dot(C-A,C-A)*(C-A);
pair Hc=A+dot(C-A,B-A)/dot(B-A,B-A)*(B-A);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--Ha,blue); draw(B--Hb,red); draw(C--Hc,heavygreen);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$13$",(A+B)/2+(-0.4,0.2),W); label("$15$",(A+C)/2+(0.3,0.2),NE); label("$14$",(B+C)/2+(2,0),S);
label("$12$",(5,2.5),E,blue); label("$\dfrac{56}{5}$",B+0.33*(Hb-B),SE,red); label("$\dfrac{168}{13}$",C+0.3*(Hc-C),N,heavygreen);
\end{asy}

The sum is
\[\dfrac{168}{13}+12+\dfrac{56}{5}=\dfrac{840+780+728}{65}=\dfrac{2348}{65}\approx36.12,\]
which rounds to $\boxed{36.1}$.

$|3x-5|\ge3$ means $3x-5\ge3$ or $3x-5\le-3$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $3x-5\ge3\iff 3x\ge8\iff x\ge\dfrac{8}{3}$;
\item $3x-5\le-3\iff 3x\le2\iff x\le\dfrac{2}{3}$.
\end{itemize}
The solution set is $\boxed{x\le\dfrac{2}{3}\ \text{or}\ x\ge\dfrac{8}{3}}$.

question:
An $18$-foot giraffe was the tallest animal Casey's family saw at the zoo. Casey is $4.5$ feet tall.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many times as tall as Casey is the giraffe?
\item[(b)] How many feet taller than Casey is the giraffe, and how many of Casey's heights is that difference?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{No.} Squaring $\sqrt a+\sqrt b=\sqrt c$ (both sides are positive) gives
\[c=a+b+2\sqrt{ab}>a+b,\]
because $a,b>0$. But in a triangle each side is shorter than the sum of the other two: $c<a+b$ by the triangle inequality. This contradiction shows that no such triangle exists.

Geometrically: if the side of length $c$ is the segment $AB$, the third vertex would have to be at distance $b$ from $A$ and $a$ from $B$, but the circles of radii $b$ and $a$ about $A$ and $B$ do not meet, since $a+b<c$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(10,0);
real a=3, b_=4;
draw(A--B,linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(A,b_,0,75),blue); draw(arc(B,a,105,180),red);
draw(A--(b_,0),blue+linewidth(1.6)); draw(B--(10-a,0),red+linewidth(1.6));
dot(A); dot(B);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE);
label("$b$",(b_/2,0),N,blue); label("$a$",(10-a/2,0),N,red); label("$c$",(5,-0.6),S);
label("gap",((b_+10-a)/2,0),S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Let $M$ be the midpoint of $AB$ and let $\ell$ be the perpendicular bisector of $AB$ (the line through $M$ perpendicular to $AB$).

\textbf{Claim.} A point $O$ is equidistant from $A$ and $B$ if and only if $O$ lies on $\ell$. If $O$ is on $\ell$, then triangles $OMA$ and $OMB$ are congruent (SAS: $MA=MB$, right angles at $M$, common side $OM$), so $OA=OB$; if $O=M$ this is clear. Conversely, if $OA=OB$ and $O\ne M$, then triangles $OMA$ and $OMB$ are congruent (SSS), so the angles at $M$ are equal and add up to $180^\circ$; they are right angles and $O$ lies on $\ell$.

\textbf{The circles.} A circle with centre $O$ passes through $A$ and $B$ exactly when $OA=OB$ (both equal to the radius), that is, exactly when $O\in\ell$. For every point $O$ of $\ell$, the circle with centre $O$ and radius $OA$ passes through $A$ and $B$; different centres give different circles. Since the line $\ell$ has infinitely many points, there are \textbf{infinitely many} such circles, and their centres form exactly the \textbf{perpendicular bisector of $AB$}.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-1,0), B=(1,0);
draw((0,-2.2)--(0,3.4),gray+dashed); label("$\ell$",(0,3.4),N,gray);
pair[] O={(0,-0.8),(0,0.6),(0,2)};
pen[] pc={heavygreen,blue,red};
for(int i=0;i<3;++i){ draw(circle(O[i],abs(O[i]-A)),pc[i]); dot(O[i],pc[i]); draw(O[i]--A,pc[i]+dotted); draw(O[i]--B,pc[i]+dotted);}
draw(A--B,linewidth(0.9)); dot(A); dot(B);
draw((0,0.12)--(0.12,0.12)--(0.12,0));
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,E); label("$M$",(0,0),SE);
label("$O_1$",O[0],E,heavygreen); label("$O_2$",O[1],E,blue); label("$O_3$",O[2],E,red);
\end{asy}

(The smallest of these circles is the one with centre $M$ and diameter $AB$.)

question:
Let $S$ be the set of integers between $1$ and $2^{40}$ whose binary expansions have exactly two $1$s.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] A number is chosen at random from $S$. The probability that it is divisible by $9$ is $\dfrac {p}{q}$, where $p$ and $q$ are relatively prime positive integers. Find $p+q$.
\item[(b)] A student argues: ``$9=1001_2$ and $18=10010_2$. Every sum of two distinct powers of $2$ has two $1$s in binary, and there are $39+38+\cdots+1=780$ such sums, so there are $780$ multiples of $9$ in $S$.'' Explain the mistake.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
In triangle $ABC$, $\angle ACB=180^\circ-30^\circ-140^\circ=10^\circ$.

\textbf{The circumcircle of $ABC$.} Let $O$ be its centre. The inscribed angle $\angle BAC=30^\circ$ is acute, so the central angle on the same arc is $\angle BOC=2\cdot30^\circ=60^\circ$. Since $OB=OC$, triangle $OBC$ is equilateral, and $OA=OB=BC$. Similarly the inscribed angle $\angle ACB=10^\circ$ gives $\angle AOB=20^\circ$. So triangle $AOB$ is isosceles with legs $OA=OB=BC$ and apex angle $20^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(1,0), A=2*Sin(10)*dir(140), O=(0.5,sqrt(3)/2), D=C+2*Sin(10)*dir(-100);
draw(circle(O,1),gray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--D--C,linewidth(0.9)+blue);
draw(O--A,red+dashed); draw(O--B,red+dashed); draw(O--C,red+dashed);
dot(O,red);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,S); label("$C$",C,E); label("$D$",D,S,blue); label("$O$",O,N,red);
label("$30^\circ$",A+0.2*dir(-25),fontsize(7pt)); draw(arc(C,0.3,170,180)); label("$10^\circ$",C+0.36*dir(160),fontsize(7pt));
label("$80^\circ$",C+0.2*dir(222),blue+fontsize(7pt)); label("$20^\circ$",B+0.38*dir(-10),blue+fontsize(7pt));
label("$20^\circ$",O+0.32*dir(230),red+fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{A comparison triangle.} On the side of line $BC$ where $D$ lies, take the point $D'$ with $BD'=BC$ and $\angle CBD'=20^\circ$. Triangle $D'BC$ is isosceles with apex angle $20^\circ$, so its base angles are $80^\circ$; in particular $\angle BCD'=80^\circ$, so $D'$ lies on ray $CD$. Triangles $AOB$ and $D'BC$ are congruent by SAS (legs equal to $BC$, apex angles $20^\circ$), so $CD'=AB=CD$.

Since $D$ and $D'$ lie on the same ray from $C$ at the same distance from $C$, they coincide. Therefore
\[\angle CBD=\angle CBD'=\boxed{20^\circ}.\]
(The argument is the same whichever side of line $BC$ the point $D$ is on.)

\textbf{The statement is true.} The hypotenuse is the longest side, so the sides are $n$, $m$ and $m+1$ with legs $n$ and $m$. By the Pythagorean theorem,
\[n^2+m^2=(m+1)^2\iff n^2=2m+1\iff m=\dfrac{n^2-1}{2}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(12,0), C=(0,5);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0.6)--(0.6,0.6)--(0.6,0));
label("$n$",(A+C)/2,W); label("$m=\dfrac{n^2-1}{2}$",(A+B)/2,S); label("$m+1=\dfrac{n^2+1}{2}$",(B+C)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

(Since $n$ is odd, $m$ is an integer. For $n$ to be the shortest side we need $m>n$, i.e. $n^2-2n-1>0$, which holds for odd $n\ge3$; $n=1$ would give $m=0$.)

The perimeter is
\[n+m+(m+1)=n+n^2=n(n+1),\]
and the area is
\[\dfrac{nm}{2}=\dfrac{n(n^2-1)}{4}=\dfrac{n(n-1)(n+1)}{4}=\dfrac{n-1}{4}\cdot n(n+1).\]
So the area is $\dfrac{n-1}{4}$ times the perimeter. This also explains the pattern in the examples: the product of the legs is $2\cdot$area $=\dfrac{n-1}{2}\cdot$perimeter, and $\dfrac{n-1}{2}=1,2,3,\dots$ for $n=3,5,7,\dots$.

question:
A detective has $100$ yards of police tape. He cuts it into two pieces: one piece is used to mark a circle around one spot, and the other to mark a square around another spot (each piece is used completely, as the boundary of its shape).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Where should the tape be cut if the two enclosed areas must be equal?
\item[(b)] Where should the tape be cut to make the total enclosed area as small as possible? What is that minimum area?
\end{enumerate}
Give exact answers and decimal approximations.

solution:
Let the circle use $x$ yards, $0<x<100$, and the square $100-x$ yards.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real L=10, x=4.4;
draw((0,0)--(x,0),blue+linewidth(1.5)); draw((x,0)--(L,0),red+linewidth(1.5));
draw((x,-0.25)--(x,0.25)); label("cut",(x,0.25),N,fontsize(8pt));
label("$x$",(x/2,0),S,blue); label("$100-x$",((x+L)/2,0),S,red);
real r=x/(2*pi), s=(L-x)/4;
draw(circle((2,-2.6),r),blue+linewidth(1)); label("radius $\dfrac{x}{2\pi}$",(2,-2.6-r),S,blue+fontsize(8pt));
draw(shift(5.4,-2.6-s/2)*scale(s)*unitsquare,red+linewidth(1)); label("side $\dfrac{100-x}{4}$",(5.4+s/2,-2.6-s/2),S,red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The circle has radius $\dfrac{x}{2\pi}$ and area $\pi\left(\dfrac{x}{2\pi}\right)^2=\dfrac{x^2}{4\pi}$; the square has side $\dfrac{100-x}{4}$ and area $\dfrac{(100-x)^2}{16}$.

\textbf{(a)} Equal areas: $\dfrac{x^2}{4\pi}=\dfrac{(100-x)^2}{16}$. Both $x$ and $100-x$ are positive, so we may take square roots: $\dfrac{x}{2\sqrt\pi}=\dfrac{100-x}{4}$, i.e. $4x=2\sqrt\pi(100-x)$, so
\[x=\dfrac{100\sqrt\pi}{2+\sqrt\pi}\approx46.98.\]
Cut the tape about $\boxed{46.98}$ yards from one end: about $46.98$ yards for the circle and $53.02$ yards for the square.

\textbf{(b)} The total area is
\[A(x)=\dfrac{x^2}{4\pi}+\dfrac{(100-x)^2}{16}=\left(\dfrac{1}{4\pi}+\dfrac{1}{16}\right)x^2-\dfrac{25}{2}x+625,\]
a quadratic with positive leading coefficient. Its minimum is at the vertex
\[x=\dfrac{\dfrac{25}{2}}{2\left(\dfrac{1}{4\pi}+\dfrac{1}{16}\right)}=\dfrac{100\pi}{4+\pi}\approx43.99,\]
which lies between $0$ and $100$. So cut about $\boxed{43.99}$ yards for the circle and $\dfrac{400}{4+\pi}\approx56.01$ yards for the square. (At this cut the circle's diameter $\dfrac{x}{\pi}=\dfrac{100}{4+\pi}$ equals the square's side.) The minimum total area is
\[A\left(\dfrac{100\pi}{4+\pi}\right)=\dfrac{2500}{4+\pi}\approx350.06\ \text{square yards}.\]

Since $V=\pi r^2h$, multiplying $r$ by a factor $u$ and $h$ by a factor $w$ multiplies the volume by $u^2w$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
