\begin{itemize}
\item (a) $0.8^2\cdot1=0.64$;
\item (b) $1\cdot0.8=0.8$;
\item (c) $0.9^2\cdot0.9=0.729$;
\item (d) $0.7^2\cdot1.1=0.49\cdot1.1=0.539$.
\end{itemize}
The factors are different, so (e) is false, and the smallest is $0.539V$: the answer is $\boxed{\text{(d)}}$.

question:
Let $m\ge2$ be an integer. An integer $b$ is called an inverse of the integer $a$ modulo $m$ if $ab\equiv1\pmod m$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that if $b$ and $c$ are both inverses of $a$ modulo $m$, then $b\equiv c\pmod m$. (So an integer has at most one inverse modulo $m$, up to congruence.)
\item[(b)] A student claims a counterexample: modulo $4$, both $1$ and $5$ are inverses of $1$. Explain why this does not contradict (a).
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $S$, $G$, $P$ be the sets of students studying Spanish, studying German and doing sports. We know $|S|=40$, $|G|=27$, $|P|=60$, $|S\cap P|=24$, $|S\cap G|=10$, $|G\cap P|=12$ and $|S\cap G\cap P|=4$. (The pairwise numbers include the $4$ students in all three.)

\textbf{Exactly two activities:} $24-4=20$ (Spanish and sports only), $10-4=6$ (Spanish and German only), $12-4=8$ (German and sports only), together $34$.

\textbf{Exactly one activity:}
\begin{itemize}
\item only Spanish: $40-20-6-4=10$;
\item only German: $27-6-8-4=9$;
\item only sports: $60-20-8-4=28$.
\end{itemize}
So $10+9+28=\boxed{47}$ students take part in exactly one activity.

\textbf{None:} by inclusion-exclusion, the number taking part in at least one activity is
\[40+27+60-24-10-12+4=85\]
(check: $47+34+4=85$), so $94-85=\boxed{9}$ students take part in none.

\textbf{Side lengths.} Triangle $OBP$ is a right isosceles triangle with legs $7$, so $OP=7\sqrt2$, and the square $OPXS'$ has side $7\sqrt2$. Then $PT=TX=XU=US'=\dfrac{7\sqrt2}{2}$. Triangle $TXU$ is right isosceles (the angle at $X$ is a corner of the square) with legs $\dfrac{7\sqrt2}{2}$, so $TU=\dfrac{7\sqrt2}{2}\cdot\sqrt2=7$, and the square $UTIM$ has side $7$.

\textbf{The heptagon.} Its sides are
\[OP=7\sqrt2,\quad PT=\dfrac{7\sqrt2}{2},\quad TI=IM=MU=7,\quad US'=\dfrac{7\sqrt2}{2},\quad S'O=7\sqrt2.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,14), B=(14,14), C=(14,0), D=(0,0);
pair O=(7,14), P=(14,7), X=(21,14), S1=(14,21), T=(17.5,10.5), U=(17.5,17.5), I=(24.5,10.5), M=(24.5,17.5);
fill(O--P--T--I--M--U--S1--cycle,paleyellow);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,gray);
draw(O--P--X--S1--cycle,dashed);
draw(U--T--I--M--cycle,dashed);
draw(O--P--T--I--M--U--S1--cycle,red+linewidth(1.2));
dot(O);dot(P);dot(T);dot(I);dot(M);dot(U);dot(S1);dot(X);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,NW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,SW);
label("$O$",O,NW); label("$P$",P,SE); label("$X$",X,E); label("$S'$",S1,N);
label("$T$",T,SE); label("$U$",U,N); label("$I$",I,SE); label("$M$",M,NE);
label("$14$",(D+C)/2,S); label("$7\sqrt2$",(O+P)/2,SW,red); label("$7$",(T+I)/2,S,red); label("$7$",(I+M)/2,E,red); label("$7$",(M+U)/2,N,red);
\end{asy}

The perimeter is
\[7\sqrt2+\dfrac{7\sqrt2}{2}+7+7+7+\dfrac{7\sqrt2}{2}+7\sqrt2=\boxed{21+21\sqrt2}.\]
(In coordinates: $A=(0,14)$, $B=(14,14)$, $C=(14,0)$, $D=(0,0)$ give $O=(7,14)$, $P=(14,7)$, $X=(21,14)$, $S'=(14,21)$, $T=\left(\dfrac{35}{2},\dfrac{21}{2}\right)$, $U=\left(\dfrac{35}{2},\dfrac{35}{2}\right)$, $I=\left(\dfrac{49}{2},\dfrac{21}{2}\right)$, $M=\left(\dfrac{49}{2},\dfrac{35}{2}\right)$, and the heptagon is simple.)

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that for numbers $a,c$ and nonzero numbers $b,d$,
\[\dfrac {a}{b}=\dfrac {c}{d}\quad\text{if and only if}\quad ad=bc.\]
\item[(b)] Use (a) to decide whether $\dfrac{73}{43}=\dfrac{511}{301}$, and explain the answer using $511=7\cdot73$ and $301=7\cdot43$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the triangle be $ABC$.

\textbf{Lemma.} A point $P$ inside an angle is equidistant from its two sides if it lies on the bisector of the angle. Indeed, if $P$ is on the bisector of $\angle A$ and $E$, $F$ are the feet of the perpendiculars from $P$ to the two sides, then the right triangles $AEP$ and $AFP$ have the common hypotenuse $AP$ and equal angles at $A$, so they are congruent (AAS) and $PE=PF$.

\textbf{The centre.} The bisector of $\angle A$ meets side $BC$ at a point $A_1$, and the bisector of $\angle B$ meets side $CA$ at a point $B_1$. The segment $BB_1$ divides the triangle into triangles $ABB_1$ and $B_1BC$; the point $A$ lies in the first and $A_1$ (on side $BC$) in the second, so segment $AA_1$ crosses $BB_1$ at a point $I$ inside the triangle.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(7,0), C=(2,5);
real a=abs(B-C), b_=abs(C-A), c=abs(A-B);
pair I=(a*A+b_*B+c*C)/(a+b_+c);
pair foot(pair P,pair Q,pair R){return Q+dot(P-Q,R-Q)/dot(R-Q,R-Q)*(R-Q);}
pair F1=foot(I,B,C), F2=foot(I,C,A), F3=foot(I,A,B);
real r=abs(I-F3);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--I,blue); draw(B--I,blue); draw(C--I,blue+dotted);
draw(circle(I,r),red);
draw(I--F1,dashed); draw(I--F2,dashed); draw(I--F3,dashed);
dot(I); dot(F1); dot(F2); dot(F3);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N); label("$I$",I,E);
label("$D$",F1,NE); label("$E$",F2,NW); label("$F$",F3,S);
label("$r$",(I+F3)/2,E,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Let $D$, $E$, $F$ be the feet of the perpendiculars from $I$ to the lines $BC$, $CA$, $AB$. Since $I$ is on the bisector of $\angle A$, $IE=IF$; since $I$ is on the bisector of $\angle B$, $ID=IF$. So $ID=IE=IF=r$.

\textbf{The circle.} Consider the circle with centre $I$ and radius $r$. At $D$ the radius $ID$ is perpendicular to line $BC$, so the line $BC$ is tangent to the circle at $D$; similarly $CA$ is tangent at $E$ and $AB$ at $F$. The disc lies on the same side of each of the three lines as $I$ (it only touches each line), so it lies inside the triangle; hence the touching points $D$, $E$, $F$ belong to the triangle and lie on its sides, not on their extensions. So the circle touches all three sides; it is the inscribed circle (incircle) of the triangle. (Moreover, the right triangles $CDI$ and $CEI$ have the common hypotenuse $CI$ and the equal legs $ID=IE$, so they are congruent and $\angle DCI=\angle ECI$: the point $I$ also lies on the bisector of $\angle C$, so the three angle bisectors are concurrent.)

question:
A math club is to have exactly $50$ members: one supervising teacher and $49$ students. Every student member is a 7th grader or an 8th grader (6th graders may not join, and each grade has more than $50$ students to choose from). At least $54\%$ of the $50$ members must be 8th graders, and at least $24\%$ of the $50$ members must be 7th graders.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the greatest possible number of 8th graders in the club?
\item[(b)] What is the greatest possible number of 7th graders in the club?
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Give an example of a figure that has a horizontal line of symmetry, has no vertical line of symmetry, and is unchanged by some rotation through an angle strictly between $0^\circ$ and $180^\circ$. Justify all three properties.
\item[(b)] A figure has a horizontal line of symmetry $\ell$ and is unchanged by the rotation through $90^\circ$ about a point $O$ on $\ell$. Prove that the vertical line through $O$ is also a line of symmetry of the figure. (So in (a) the angle of rotation cannot be $90^\circ$.)
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Take an equilateral triangle $PQR$ with centre $O$, drawn so that the vertex $P$ points to the right and the side $QR$ is vertical.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0), P=dir(0), Q=dir(120), R=dir(240);
draw(P--Q--R--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((-1.35,0)--(1.45,0),blue+dashed);
label("$\ell$",(1.45,0),E,blue);
draw((0,-1.15)--(0,1.15),red+dotted);
label("no symmetry",(0,1.15),N,red+fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc(O,0.32,0,120),Arrow(5));
label("$120^\circ$",0.47*dir(118),fontsize(8pt));
dot(O); label("$O$",O,dir(-115));
label("$P$",P,dir(60)); label("$Q$",Q,NW); label("$R$",R,SW);
\end{asy}

\begin{itemize}
\item The horizontal line $\ell$ through $P$ and $O$ is the perpendicular bisector of $QR$, so reflecting in $\ell$ fixes $P$ and swaps $Q$ and $R$: it is a line of symmetry.
\item The rotation through $120^\circ$ about $O$ takes $P\to Q\to R\to P$, so it maps the triangle onto itself, and $0^\circ<120^\circ<180^\circ$.
\item There is no vertical line of symmetry: a reflection in a vertical line exchanges ``rightmost'' and ``leftmost''. The rightmost part of the triangle is the single point $P$, while its leftmost part is the whole side $QR$. A reflection would map the one onto the other, which is impossible.
\end{itemize}
So the triangle has all three properties. (Any regular polygon with an odd number of sides, drawn with a vertex pointing to the right, works in the same way.)

\textbf{(b)} Put $O$ at the origin with $\ell$ as the $x$-axis. Reflection in $\ell$ is $H(x,y)=(x,-y)$, and the rotation through $90^\circ$ is $T(x,y)=(-y,x)$. Doing $T$ twice gives $(x,y)\mapsto(-x,-y)$, the rotation through $180^\circ$, so the figure is unchanged by it. Following $H$ by this half-turn gives
\[(x,y)\ \mapsto\ (x,-y)\ \mapsto\ (-x,y),\]
which is the reflection in the $y$-axis, the vertical line through $O$. Each step maps the figure onto itself, so the composition does too, and the vertical line through $O$ is a line of symmetry. $\blacksquare$

Hence a figure as in (a) cannot be unchanged by a $90^\circ$ rotation (about a point of $\ell$); in fact any rotational symmetry whose repetition gives the half-turn is ruled out, which is why the example uses $120^\circ$.

question:
In equilateral triangle $ABC$, $M$ is the midpoint of side $AB$, and $CMN$ is also an equilateral triangle.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the ratio of the area of triangle $CMN$ to the area of triangle $ABC$.
\item[(b)] What fraction of the area of triangle $ABC$ lies inside triangle $CMN$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $AB=2$, so $AM=MB=1$ and $[ABC]=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot2^2=\sqrt3$. The median $CM$ is also an altitude, so $CM\perp AB$ and $CM=\sqrt{2^2-1^2}=\sqrt3$.

\textbf{(a)} Equilateral triangles are similar, with area ratio equal to the square of the ratio of sides:
\[\dfrac{[CMN]}{[ABC]}=\left(\dfrac{CM}{AB}\right)^2=\left(\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)^2=\boxed{\dfrac{3}{4}}.\]

\textbf{(b)} By symmetry in the line $CM$ we may assume that $N$ is on the same side of $CM$ as $B$; then triangle $CMN$ does not meet triangle $CMA$ except along $CM$. Compare the angles at the common vertices:
\begin{itemize}
\item at $M$: $\angle CMN=60^\circ<\angle CMB=90^\circ$, so the ray $MN$ runs inside angle $CMB$, making $\angle NMB=30^\circ$;
\item at $C$: $\angle MCB=30^\circ<\angle MCN=60^\circ$, so the ray $CB$ runs inside angle $MCN$.
\end{itemize}
Let $MN$ meet $BC$ at $P$. Then the part of triangle $CMN$ inside triangle $ABC$ is exactly triangle $CMP$ (shaded).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(2,0), C=(1,sqrt(3)), M=(1,0), N=(2.5,sqrt(3)/2), P=(1.75,sqrt(3)/4);
fill(C--M--P--cycle,palecyan);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(C--M--N--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(rotate(30,P)*shift(P)*scale(0.09)*((-1,0)--(-1,1)--(0,1)), black+linewidth(0.4));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(M); dot(N); dot(P,red);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N); label("$M$",M,S); label("$N$",N,E,blue); label("$P$",P,dir(-30),red);
label("$1$",(1.5,0),S); label("$1$",(0.5,0),S);
label("$30^\circ$",M+0.42*dir(14),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

In triangle $MPB$, $\angle PMB=30^\circ$ and $\angle MBP=60^\circ$, so $\angle MPB=90^\circ$ and $MP=MB\cos30^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$. Since $\angle CMP=60^\circ$,
\[[CMP]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot CM\cdot MP\cdot\sin60^\circ=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\sqrt3\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{8}.\]
Hence the fraction of triangle $ABC$ inside triangle $CMN$ is
\[\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{8}\div\sqrt3=\boxed{\dfrac{3}{8}}.\]

The dilation multiplies every coordinate by $2$, so the image has vertices $A'(0,8)$, $B'(6,0)$ and $C'(8,14)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
pair A=(0,4), B=(3,0), C=(4,7);
xaxis("$x$",-1,9.5,Arrow(5)); yaxis("$y$",-1,15,Arrow(5));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(2A--2B--2C--cycle,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0)--2C,gray+dashed); draw((0,0)--2B,gray+dashed);
dot(A,blue); dot(B,blue); dot(C,blue); dot(2A,red); dot(2B,red); dot(2C,red); dot((0,0));
label("$A$",A,W,blue); label("$B$",B,SE,blue); label("$C$",C,NW,blue);
label("$A'(0,8)$",2A,W,red); label("$B'(6,0)$",2B,S,red); label("$C'(8,14)$",2C,E,red);
\end{asy}

By the shoelace formula, the original triangle has area
\[\dfrac{1}{2}\left|0(0-7)+3(7-4)+4(4-0)\right|=\dfrac{1}{2}(9+16)=\dfrac{25}{2}.\]
A dilation by factor $2$ multiplies all lengths by $2$ and hence all areas by $2^2=4$, so the dilated triangle has area
\[4\cdot\dfrac{25}{2}=\boxed{50}.\]
(Directly: $\dfrac{1}{2}\left|0(0-14)+6(14-8)+8(8-0)\right|=\dfrac{1}{2}(36+64)=50$.)

question:
A student claims to prove that positive integers do not exist.
\begin{quote}
Dividing by $2$ gives a one-to-one correspondence $m\mapsto\dfrac {m}{2}$ between the even positive integers and all positive integers, and subtracting $1$ gives a one-to-one correspondence $m\mapsto m-1$ between the even positive integers and the odd positive integers. So the even positive integers, the odd positive integers and all positive integers have the same number of elements, say $N$. Every positive integer is either even or odd, and not both, so $N+N=N$. Hence $N=0$: there are no positive integers.
\end{quote}
Check that the two correspondences are really one-to-one correspondences, and find the error in the argument.

solution:
Let $a_n$ be the number of strings of $n$ digits from $\{1,2,3\}$ with no two consecutive $1$s. Split by the last digit:
\begin{itemize}
\item if it is $2$ or $3$, the first $n-1$ digits form any valid string: $2a_{n-1}$ strings;
\item if it is $1$, the digit before it (if any) must be $2$ or $3$, and the first $n-2$ digits form any valid string: $2a_{n-2}$ strings.
\end{itemize}
So $a_n=2a_{n-1}+2a_{n-2}$ for $n\ge3$, with $a_1=3$ and $a_2=3^2-1=8$ (only $11$ is forbidden). Then
\[a_3=22,\quad a_4=60,\quad a_5=164,\quad a_6=448,\quad a_7=2\cdot448+2\cdot164=\boxed{1224}.\]

Since $AB=3$, the point $B$ is $3$ units to the left or right of $A$: its coordinate is $8-3=5$ or $8+3=11$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((3.5,0)--(13.5,0),Arrows(5));
for(int k=4;k<=13;++k){ draw((k,-0.12)--(k,0.12)); label("$"+string(k)+"$",(k,-0.12),S,fontsize(8pt)); }
dot((8,0)); dot((5,0),blue); dot((11,0),blue);
label("$A$",(8,0.15),N); label("$B$",(5,0.15),N,blue); label("$B$",(11,0.15),N,blue);
draw((5,0.75)--(8,0.75),blue,Bars(4)); draw((8,0.75)--(11,0.75),blue,Bars(4));
label("$3$",(6.5,0.75),N,blue); label("$3$",(9.5,0.75),N,blue);
dot((6.5,0),red); dot((9.5,0),red);
label("$6.5$",(6.5,-0.55),S,red); label("$9.5$",(9.5,-0.55),S,red);
draw((6.5,-0.6)--(6.5,-0.05),red+dotted); draw((9.5,-0.6)--(9.5,-0.05),red+dotted);
\end{asy}

The midpoint of a segment on a number line has the average of the endpoint coordinates as its coordinate:
\[\dfrac{8+5}{2}=6.5\qquad\text{or}\qquad\dfrac{8+11}{2}=9.5.\]
(Equivalently, the midpoint is $1.5$ units from $A$ on either side.) The possible midpoints are $\boxed{6.5\text{ and }9.5}$.

Let the dimensions be $a,b,c$. The prism has four edges of each length, so
\[4(a+b+c)=144,\qquad 2(ab+bc+ca)=826,\qquad abc=1470,\]
that is, $a+b+c=36$, $ab+bc+ca=413$ and $abc=1470$. By Vieta's formulas, the monic cubic with roots $a,b,c$ is
\[(x-a)(x-b)(x-c)=x^3-(a+b+c)x^2+(ab+bc+ca)x-abc=\boxed{x^3-36x^2+413x-1470}.\]
Checking divisors of $1470=2\cdot3\cdot5\cdot7^2$ gives the root $x=7$ ($343-1764+2891-1470=0$), and
\[x^3-36x^2+413x-1470=(x-7)(x^2-29x+210)=(x-7)(x-14)(x-15).\]
So the prism is $7\times14\times15$: indeed $4(7+14+15)=144$, $2(98+210+105)=826$ and $7\cdot14\cdot15=1470$.

