question:
A standard $52$-card deck, which contains $13$ spades, is shuffled and dealt into $13$ hands of $4$ cards each.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the expected number of hands that contain at least one spade?
\item[(b)] What are the least and the greatest possible numbers of hands that contain at least one spade?
\end{enumerate}

The figures below are built as follows. Level $1$ is an equilateral triangle. To get level $n+1$ from level $n$, take each of the smallest upright triangles of level $n$ (there are $3^{n-1}$ of them) and join the midpoints of its sides, which splits it into three smaller upright triangles and one upside-down triangle in the middle.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
void sier(pair a, pair b, pair c, int d){
  draw(a--b--c--cycle,linewidth(0.7));
  if(d>1){ pair ab=(a+b)/2, bc=(b+c)/2, ca=(c+a)/2;
    sier(a,ab,ca,d-1); sier(ab,b,bc,d-1); sier(ca,bc,c,d-1); }
}
for(int k=1;k<=3;++k){
  pair s=((k-1)*2.6,0);
  sier(s,s+(2,0),s+(1,sqrt(3)),k);
  label("Level "+string(k),s+(1,-0.1),S);
}
\end{asy}

A triangle \emph{in the figure} is any triangle, of any size and orientation, whose three sides lie along drawn segments. For example, level $1$ contains $1$ triangle and level $2$ contains $5$. How many triangles does level $n$ contain?

solution:
Let $T_n$ be the number of triangles in level $n$; $T_1=1$ and $T_2=5$.

\textbf{Structure.} Let the big triangle be $ABC$ and let $D,E,F$ be the midpoints of $BC,CA,AB$. Level $n$ ($n\ge2$) consists of three copies of level $n-1$, scaled by $\dfrac{1}{2}$, in the corner triangles $AFE$, $FBD$ and $EDC$. These copies meet only at the points $D$, $E$, $F$, and the drawn segments of each copy lie in its corner triangle. Every drawn segment is parallel to a side of $ABC$, so every triangle in the figure is equilateral and points either up (like $ABC$) or down.

For example, level $3$ consists of three copies of level $2$ (shaded) and the red middle triangle $DEF$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
void sier(pair a, pair b, pair c, int d, pen p){
  draw(a--b--c--cycle,p);
  if(d>1){ pair ab=(a+b)/2, bc=(b+c)/2, ca=(c+a)/2;
    sier(a,ab,ca,d-1,p); sier(ab,b,bc,d-1,p); sier(ca,bc,c,d-1,p); }
}
pair B=(0,0), C=(4,0), A=(2,2*sqrt(3)), D=(B+C)/2, E=(C+A)/2, F=(A+B)/2;
fill(A--F--E--cycle,paleyellow); fill(F--B--D--cycle,palecyan); fill(E--D--C--cycle,palegreen);
sier(A,F,E,2,black); sier(F,B,D,2,black); sier(E,D,C,2,black);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(D--E--F--cycle,red+linewidth(1.2));
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$D$",D,S,red); label("$E$",E,NE,red); label("$F$",F,NW,red);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Claim.} Every triangle in level $n$ other than $ABC$ and the middle triangle $DEF$ lies inside one of the three copies.

Let $T$ be a triangle in the figure that is not inside one copy. Walking around the boundary of $T$, we can pass from one copy to another only through $D$, $E$ or $F$, and the walk passes each point once. If the boundary used segments of only two copies, say those at $A$ and $B$, it would have to go from one to the other and back through $F$, the only common point of these copies, twice, which is impossible. So the boundary of $T$ uses segments of all three copies and passes through $D$, $E$ and $F$. Also $T$ lies inside $ABC$, since all drawn segments do.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $T$ points up, it has a bottom side parallel to $BC$ and two sides parallel to $AB$ and $AC$. As $T$ lies inside $ABC$ and contains the point $D$ of line $BC$, its bottom side lies on line $BC$. As $T$ lies inside $ABC$ and contains the point $E$ of line $AC$, its side parallel to $AC$ lies on line $AC$; in the same way (using $F$) its side parallel to $AB$ lies on line $AB$. So $T=ABC$.
\item If $T$ points down, the only point of $T$ at its lowest level is its bottom vertex. As $T$ lies inside $ABC$ and contains the point $D$ of line $BC$ (the lowest level of $ABC$), its bottom vertex is $D$. The two sides through $D$ are parallel to $AB$ and $AC$, so they lie on the midlines $DE$ and $DF$. These lines leave triangle $ABC$ at $E$ and $F$, so $T$ reaches no higher than the level of $EF$; as $T$ contains $E$, its top side lies on line $EF$. So $T=DEF$.
\end{itemize}
This proves the claim.

\textbf{Recursion.} Each copy contains $T_{n-1}$ triangles, the copies share no triangle, and we add $ABC$ and $DEF$:
\[T_n=3T_{n-1}+2.\]
Adding $1$ to both sides, $T_n+1=3(T_{n-1}+1)$, so $T_n+1=3^{n-1}(T_1+1)=2\cdot3^{n-1}$ and
\[T_n=\boxed{2\cdot3^{n-1}-1}.\]
This gives $1,5,17,53,161,\dots$ for $n=1,2,3,4,5,\dots$

Draw the ray from $A$ making $40^\circ$ with $AB$. The vertex $C$ lies on this ray at distance $10$ from $B$, so it is an intersection of the ray with the circle of radius $10$ about $B$. The distance from $B$ to the ray is $13\sin40^\circ\approx8.36<10$, and both intersection points $C_1$ (nearer to $A$) and $C_2$ lie on the ray beyond $A$, which gives the two triangles.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real t1=13*Cos(40)-sqrt(100-(13*Sin(40))^2), t2=13*Cos(40)+sqrt(100-(13*Sin(40))^2);
pair A=(0,0), B=(13,0), C1=t1*dir(40), C2=t2*dir(40);
draw(A--B,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--C2,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--C1,blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(B--C2,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(A,1.6,0,40)); label("$40^\circ$",A+2.3*dir(20),fontsize(8pt));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C1,blue); dot(C2,red);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C_1$",C1,NW,blue); label("$C_2$",C2,N,red);
label("$13$",(6.5,0),S); label("$10$",(B+C1)/2,dir(-60),blue); label("$10$",(B+C2)/2,NE,red);
draw(arc(C1,0.9,degrees(B-C1)-360,40)); draw(arc(C2,0.9,220,degrees(B-C2))); label("$x$",C1+1.3*dir(12),fontsize(8pt)); label("$x$",C2+1.2*dir(-115),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Since $BC_1=BC_2=10$, triangle $BC_1C_2$ is isosceles; let $x=\angle BC_1C_2=\angle BC_2C_1$.
\begin{itemize}
\item In triangle $ABC_2$, the angle at $C_2$ is $x$, so $\angle ABC_2=180^\circ-40^\circ-x=140^\circ-x$.
\item In triangle $ABC_1$, the angle at $C_1$ is $180^\circ-x$ (supplementary to $\angle BC_1C_2$), so $\angle ABC_1=180^\circ-40^\circ-(180^\circ-x)=x-40^\circ$.
\end{itemize}
The sum of the two values of $\angle B$ is $(140^\circ-x)+(x-40^\circ)=\boxed{100^\circ}$.

(With the law of sines: $\sin C=\dfrac{13\sin40^\circ}{10}\approx0.836$, so $C\approx56.7^\circ$ or $C\approx123.3^\circ$, giving $B\approx83.3^\circ$ or $B\approx16.7^\circ$; their sum is $280^\circ-180^\circ=100^\circ$.)

Put $AB$ on the $x$-axis with $A=(-4,0)$ and $B=(4,0)$. The centre $O$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of the chord, at distance $\sqrt{5^2-4^2}=3$ from $AB$; take $O=(0,3)$. The tangency point is $T=(-3,0)$, and since circle $C$ touches the $x$-axis at $T$ from below, its centre is $C=(-3,-r)$, where $r$ is its radius.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=7/16;
pair O=(0,3), A=(-4,0), B=(4,0), T=(-3,0), C=(-3,-r), K=(-3,3);
draw(circle(O,5),linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--B,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(C,r),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--(0,0),dashed); draw(O--K,dashed); draw(K--C,dashed); draw(O--C,red+linewidth(0.8));
draw(O--A,gray);
dot(O); dot(A); dot(B); dot(T); dot(C,blue); dot((0,0));
label("$O$",O,NE); label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,E); label("$T$",T,dir(60),fontsize(8pt));
label("$C$",C+(0.5,-0.25),blue);
label("$3$",(0,1.5),E,fontsize(8pt)); label("$5$",(-2,1.5),NW,gray+fontsize(8pt));
label("$3$",(-1.5,3),N,fontsize(8pt)); label("$3+r$",(-3,1.4),W,fontsize(8pt));
label("$5-r$",(-1.5,1.2),dir(-20),red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Circle $C$ lies in the small region of circle $O$ cut off by the chord $AB$, so it is internally tangent to circle $O$ and $OC=5-r$. (External tangency would need $OC=5+r$, i.e.\ $9+(3+r)^2=(5+r)^2$, which gives the impossible $r=-\dfrac{7}{4}$.) Using the right triangle with legs $3$ and $3+r$:
\[3^2+(3+r)^2=(5-r)^2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad18+6r+r^2=25-10r+r^2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad16r=7.\]
So the radius is $\boxed{\dfrac{7}{16}}$. (Check: $OC^2=9+\left(\dfrac{55}{16}\right)^2=\dfrac{5329}{256}=\left(\dfrac{73}{16}\right)^2$ and $5-\dfrac{7}{16}=\dfrac{73}{16}$.)

By the Pythagorean theorem, the hypotenuse $c$ satisfies
\[c^2=8^2+10^2=64+100=164,\qquad c=\sqrt{164}=\boxed{2\sqrt{41}}\approx12.8.\]

\begin{asy}
