question:
What can you conclude from the following two true statements?

\emph{If you want to buy the school lunch today, then you will need \$$2.50$. You brought \$$2.50$ to school today.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(A)] You wanted to buy the school lunch today.
\item[(B)] You will buy the school lunch today.
\item[(C)] The school lunch always costs \$$2.50$.
\item[(D)] None of the above.
\end{enumerate}

What can you conclude from the following two true statements?

\emph{If Karl runs $1$ mile, then he runs $1760$ yards. If Karl runs $1760$ yards, then he runs $5280$ feet.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(A)] Karl ran $1$ mile.
\item[(B)] Karl ran $5280$ feet.
\item[(C)] If Karl runs $1$ mile, then he runs $5280$ feet.
\item[(D)] None of the above.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $O$ be the centre, and measure positions on the circle by the counterclockwise angle from $A$, modulo $360^\circ$. Equal chords subtend equal arcs, so the points hit by the light are
\[A_0=A,\ A_1=B,\ A_2=C,\ \dots,\qquad A_k\ \text{at angle}\ k\theta\pmod{360^\circ}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real t=50*sqrt(2);
pair O=(0,0);
draw(circle(O,1),linewidth(0.9));
pair[] P; for(int k=0;k<5;++k) P.push(dir(-90+k*t));
for(int k=0;k<4;++k) draw(P[k]--P[k+1],blue);
string[] nm={"A","B","C","D","E"};
for(int k=0;k<5;++k){ dot(P[k],blue); label("$"+nm[k]+"$",P[k],P[k],blue); }
pair Q=dir(90); dot(Q,red); label("$Q$",Q,N,red);
draw(O--P[0],dotted); draw(O--Q,dotted); dot(O); label("$O$",O,W);
draw(arc(O,0.25,-90,-90+t),Arrow(4)); label("$\theta$",0.33*dir(-90+t/2),dir(-90+t/2),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{The light never touches $Q$.} The light travels along chords $A_kA_{k+1}$, and a chord meets the circle only at its endpoints, so the light touches the circle only at the points $A_k$. If $A_k=Q$ for some $k\ge1$, then $k\theta=180+360m$ for some integer $m$, so $\theta=\dfrac{180+360m}{k}$ would be rational, a contradiction. In the same way $A_j\ne A_k$ for $j\ne k$, since otherwise $(k-j)\theta$ would be a multiple of $360$.

\textbf{The light comes arbitrarily close to $Q$.} Let $N$ be a positive integer and cut the circle into $N$ arcs of $\dfrac{360^\circ}{N}$ each. Among the $N+1$ distinct points $A_0,A_1,\dots,A_N$, two lie in the same arc by the pigeonhole principle, say $A_j$ and $A_k$ with $j<k$. Let $s=k-j$. Then $A_k$ is obtained from $A_j$ by turning through $s\theta$, so $s\theta$ is congruent modulo $360^\circ$ to an angle $\delta$ with $0<|\delta|<\dfrac{360^\circ}{N}$ ($\delta\ne0$ because $A_j\ne A_k$).

Now look at $A_0,A_s,A_{2s},A_{3s},\dots$: each is obtained from the previous one by turning through $\delta$. These points march around the circle in one direction in steps shorter than $\dfrac{360^\circ}{N}$, so they cannot jump over an arc of length $\dfrac{360^\circ}{N}$; going once around, one of them lands in the arc of length $\dfrac{360^\circ}{N}$ that starts at $Q$ in the direction of motion. That point $A_{ms}$ is within $\dfrac{360^\circ}{N}$ of $Q$ (measured along the circle).

Since $N$ can be as large as we like, the light hits the circle arbitrarily close to $Q$, although it never hits $Q$ itself. $\blacksquare$

(The same argument works for every point of the circle that is not one of the $A_k$: the hitting points are dense on the circle.)

The boundary consists of the four corners and $n-2$ further points on each side, so it contains $4(n-1)=4n-4$ points.

Three boundary points fail to form a triangle exactly when they are collinear. A line that does not contain a side of the square meets its boundary in at most two points, so three collinear boundary points must lie on one side of the square. Each side contains $n$ boundary points, so there are $4\dbinom n3$ collinear triples. (In the figure, for $n=5$, the blue triangle counts, while the three red points on the bottom side do not form a triangle.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int n=5;
for(int i=0;i<n;++i) for(int j=0;j<n;++j){
  bool b=(i==0||j==0||i==n-1||j==n-1);
  if(b) dot((i,j),linewidth(4)); else dot((i,j),gray+linewidth(2.5));
}
draw((0,1)--(2,4)--(4,2)--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-0.25,0)--(4.25,0),red+dashed);
dot((1,0),red+linewidth(4.5)); dot((2,0),red+linewidth(4.5)); dot((4,0),red+linewidth(4.5));
dot((0,1),blue+linewidth(4.5)); dot((2,4),blue+linewidth(4.5)); dot((4,2),blue+linewidth(4.5));
\end{asy}

By complementary counting, the number of triangles is
\[\boxed{\dbinom{4n-4}{3}-4\dbinom{n}{3}}.\]
For example, $n=2$ gives $4$, $n=3$ gives $56-4=52$, and $n=5$ gives $560-40=520$.

question:
Points $A$, $B$, $C$ are fixed on a sphere $S_2$, and $S_1$ is a sphere with a smaller radius. A point $P$ on $S_1$ has distances $PA=a$, $PB=b$ and $PC=c$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove or disprove: the distances $a$, $b$, $c$ always determine $P$ uniquely.
\item[(b)] Prove that at most two points of space (on $S_1$ or not) have distances $a$, $b$, $c$ from $A$, $B$, $C$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(b)} First note that $A$, $B$, $C$ are not collinear: a line meets a sphere in at most two points. Suppose $X\ne Y$ both have distances $a,b,c$ from $A,B,C$. Then each of $A$, $B$, $C$ is equidistant from $X$ and $Y$, so it lies on the perpendicular bisector plane of $XY$. Three non-collinear points lie in only one plane, so this plane is the plane $\Pi$ through $A$, $B$, $C$, and $Y$ is the mirror image of $X$ in $\Pi$. Hence any point with the given distances is $X$ or its mirror image: there are \textbf{at most two} such points. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(a)} The statement is \textbf{false}. Let $S_1$ and $S_2$ be centred at the origin $O$ with radii $1$ and $2$, and choose $A$, $B$, $C$ on the circle of radius $2$ in the plane $z=0$ (a great circle of $S_2$), for example $A=(-2,0,0)$, $B=(0,2,0)$, $C=(2,0,0)$. The points $P=(0,0,1)$ and $P'=(0,0,-1)$ both lie on $S_1$, and each is at distance $\sqrt{4+1}=\sqrt5$ from every point of that circle, so
\[PA=PB=PC=P'A=P'B=P'C=\sqrt5.\]
Two different points of $S_1$ have the same distances, so $P$ is not determined uniquely. The figure is the cross-section through $A$, $C$, $P$, $P'$; the plane $\Pi$ of $A$, $B$, $C$ appears as the dashed line.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0), P=(0,1), P2=(0,-1), A=(-2,0), C=(2,0);
draw(circle(O,2),linewidth(0.9)); draw(circle(O,1),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-2.6,0)--(3.0,0),gray+dashed); label("$\Pi$",(3.0,0),E,gray);
draw(P--A,red); draw(P2--A,red); draw(P--C,red+dotted); draw(P2--C,red+dotted);
dot(O); dot(P,blue); dot(P2,blue); dot(A); dot(C);
label("$O$",O,dir(-60)); label("$P$",P,N,blue); label("$P'$",P2,S,blue);
label("$A$",A,dir(-150)); label("$C$",C,dir(-40));
label("$S_1$",dir(40),NE,blue); label("$S_2$",2*dir(130),NW);
label("$\sqrt5$",(A+P)/2,NW,red+fontsize(8pt)); label("$\sqrt5$",(A+P2)/2,SW,red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

By (b), this is the only way uniqueness can fail: the mirror image $P'$ of $P$ in the plane $\Pi$ must also lie on $S_1$. (For instance, if the plane of $A,B,C$ passes through the centre of $S_1$, as here, $P'$ always lies on $S_1$.)

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $d+2d+3d+\cdots+nd=\dfrac{dn(n+1)}{2}$ for every positive integer $n$ and every number $d$.
\item[(b)] Use (a) to compute $7+14+21+\cdots+700$.
\item[(c)] More generally, find the sum $a+(a+d)+(a+2d)+\cdots+\bigl(a+(n-1)d\bigr)$ of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic sequence.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $\dfrac{1}{n(n+1)}=\dfrac{1}{n}-\dfrac{1}{n+1}$ for every positive integer $n$.
\item[(b)] Use (a) to evaluate
\[\dfrac{1}{1\cdot2}+\dfrac{1}{2\cdot3}+\dfrac{1}{3\cdot4}+\cdots+\dfrac{1}{99\cdot100}.\]
\end{enumerate}

Let $n\ge2$ be an integer. Fix $2n$ points in space, no four of them in one plane. A \emph{triangle} is formed by three of the points when all three segments joining them are drawn.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that it is possible to draw $n^2$ segments joining pairs of the points without forming a triangle.
\item[(b)] Prove that any $n^2+1$ segments joining pairs of the points form at least one triangle.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the other leg be $b$ and the hypotenuse $c$, positive integers with $c>b$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(6,0), C=(0,3.2);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0.3,0)--(0.3,0.3)--(0,0.3));
label("$b$",(3,0),S); label("$20$",(0,1.6),W); label("$c$",(3,1.6),NE);
\end{asy}

By the Pythagorean theorem $c^2-b^2=400$, that is,
\[(c-b)(c+b)=400.\]
The factors $c-b$ and $c+b$ are positive, $c-b<c+b$, and they have the same parity (their sum $2c$ is even). As $400$ is even, both must be even. Writing $c-b=2u$ and $c+b=2v$ gives $uv=100$ with $u<v$:
\[(u,v)=(1,100),\ (2,50),\ (4,25),\ (5,20).\]
(The pair $u=v=10$ would give $b=0$.) Then $c=u+v$ and $b=v-u$:
\[(b,c)=(99,101),\quad(48,52),\quad(21,29),\quad(15,25).\]
So the possible hypotenuses are $\boxed{25,\ 29,\ 52,\ 101}$.

Let the ellipse have centre $O$, semi-major axis $a$ and semi-minor axis $b$; then $a=\dfrac{24}{2}=12$. The axes are perpendicular, so each side of the rhombus is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs $a$ and $b$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
draw(ellipse(O,12,5),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((12,0)--(0,5)--(-12,0)--(0,-5)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((-12,0)--(12,0),dashed); draw((0,-5)--(0,5),dashed);
draw((0.6,0)--(0.6,0.6)--(0,0.6));
dot(O); label("$O$",O,dir(-135));
label("$12$",(6,0),S); label("$b$",(0,2.5),W); label("$13$",(6,2.5),NE);
\end{asy}

Hence $a^2+b^2=13^2$, so $b^2=169-144=25$ and $b=5$. The area of an ellipse with semi-axes $a$ and $b$ is $\pi ab$, so the area is
\[\pi\cdot12\cdot5=\boxed{60\pi}.\]

Two kinds of coplanar sets are easy to find:
\begin{itemize}
\item the $6$ faces;
\item the $6$ ``diagonal rectangles'' through two opposite (parallel, non-adjacent) edges, such as $ABGH$ below. The $12$ edges form $6$ such pairs.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair d=(0.5,0.35);
pair A=(0,0), B=(2,0), C=(2,2), D=(0,2);
pair A2=A+2d, B2=B+2d, C2=C+2d, D2=D+2d;
fill(A--B--C2--D2--cycle,palecyan);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(B--B2--C2--C,linewidth(0.9)); draw(D--D2--C2,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--A2,dashed); draw(A2--B2,dashed); draw(A2--D2,dashed);
draw(A--B--C2--D2--cycle,blue+linewidth(1));
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,S); label("$C$",C,dir(-30)); label("$D$",D,W);
label("$E$",A2,dir(150)); label("$F$",B2,E); label("$G$",C2,NE); label("$H$",D2,N);
\end{asy}

\textbf{There are no others.} Let $S$ be a set of four coplanar vertices whose plane $\Pi$ is not a face plane. A plane different from a face's plane meets that face in at most a segment, so it contains at most $2$ vertices of each face. Apply this to the top face and the bottom face, which together contain all $8$ vertices: $S$ has exactly $2$ vertices on the top face and $2$ on the bottom face. The plane $\Pi$ cuts the two parallel planes of these faces in parallel lines, so the top pair and the bottom pair form parallel segments.
\begin{itemize}
\item If the top pair is an edge, the bottom pair is a parallel edge of the bottom face: either the edge directly below (then $S$ is a side face, excluded) or the opposite one (a diagonal rectangle).
\item If the top pair is a diagonal of the top face, the bottom pair is the parallel diagonal of the bottom face, the one directly below it (a diagonal rectangle).
\end{itemize}
So every coplanar set is a face or a diagonal rectangle, and the answer is $6+6=\boxed{12}$.

Two points $P$ and $Q$ on a circle split it into two arcs; the smaller is the \emph{minor arc} and the larger the \emph{major arc}, and together they make the whole circle, $360^\circ$. If the minor arc measures $m$ degrees and the major arc $M$ degrees, then
\[M+m=360,\qquad M-m=100.\]
Subtracting, $2m=260$, so $m=130$ (and $M=230$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); real a1=-65, a2=65;
draw(arc(O,1,a1,a2),red+linewidth(1.6));
draw(arc(O,1,a2,a1+360),blue+linewidth(0.9));
pair P=dir(a1), Q=dir(a2);
dot(P); dot(Q); dot(O);
draw(O--P,dashed); draw(O--Q,dashed);
label("$P$",P,SE); label("$Q$",Q,NE); label("$O$",O,W);
label("$130^\circ$",dir(0)*1.03,E,red);
label("$230^\circ$",dir(180)*1.03,W,blue);
\end{asy}

The answer is $\boxed{\text{(C)}\ 130}$.

The diagonals of a rectangle bisect each other, so $x_A+x_C=x_B+x_D$. The only way to split $\{1,2,3,4\}$ into two pairs with equal sums is $\{1,4\}$ and $\{2,3\}$. Relabelling if necessary, $x_A=1$, $x_C=4$, and $B$, $D$ have $x$-coordinates $2$ and $3$.

Let $\vec{AB}=(p,s)$ and $\vec{AD}=(q,t)$. Since $C=A+\vec{AB}+\vec{AD}$, we have $p+q=3$ with $\{p,q\}=\{1,2\}$; by symmetry take $p=1$, $q=2$. The sides are perpendicular, so
\[\vec{AB}\cdot\vec{AD}=1\cdot2+st=0,\qquad st=-2,\]
and in particular $s\ne0$. The area is
\[|AB|\cdot|AD|=\sqrt{1+s^2}\,\sqrt{4+t^2}=\sqrt{1+s^2}\,\sqrt{4+\dfrac{4}{s^2}}=\dfrac{2(1+s^2)}{|s|}=2\left(|s|+\dfrac{1}{|s|}\right).\]
By AM-GM, $|s|+\dfrac{1}{|s|}\ge2$, with equality for $|s|=1$. So the area is at least $4$, with equality for $s=1$, $t=-2$: the rectangle $A(1,0)$, $B(2,1)$, $C(4,-1)$, $D(3,-2)$ with sides $\sqrt2$ and $2\sqrt2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
pair A=(1,0), B=(2,1), C=(4,-1), D=(3,-2);
xaxis("$x$",-0.3,4.8,Arrow(5)); yaxis("$y$",-2.6,1.8,Arrow(5));
for(int k=1;k<=4;++k){ draw((k,-2.5)--(k,1.5),gray+dotted); label("$"+string(k)+"$",(k,-2.5),S,gray+fontsize(8pt)); }
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,blue+linewidth(1));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D);
label("$A$",A,dir(140)); label("$B$",B,N); label("$C$",C,E); label("$D$",D,S);
label("$\sqrt2$",(A+B)/2,NW,fontsize(8pt)); label("$2\sqrt2$",(A+D)/2,SW,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The minimum possible area is $\boxed{4}$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why the single equation $x+y=12$ does not determine the numbers $x$ and $y$.
\item[(b)] How many ordered pairs $(x,y)$ of positive integers satisfy $x+y=12$?
\item[(c)] Find $x$ and $y$ if, in addition to $x+y=12$, we know that $x-y=4$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
If $n=p_1^{e_1}\cdots p_k^{e_k}$, then $n$ has $(e_1+1)\cdots(e_k+1)$ divisors. Writing $12$ as such a product:
\begin{itemize}
\item $12$: $n=p^{11}\ge2^{11}$, too large;
\item $6\cdot2$: $n=p^5q$: $2^5\cdot3=96$ ($2^5\cdot5=160$ and $3^5\cdot2=486$ are too large);
\item $4\cdot3$: $n=p^3q^2$: $2^3\cdot3^2=72$ ($2^3\cdot5^2=200$, $3^3\cdot2^2=108$ are too large);
\item $3\cdot2\cdot2$: $n=p^2qr$: $2^2\cdot3\cdot5=60$, $2^2\cdot3\cdot7=84$, $3^2\cdot2\cdot5=90$ (the next ones, $2^2\cdot3\cdot11=132$, $2^2\cdot5\cdot7=140$, $3^2\cdot2\cdot7=126$, $5^2\cdot2\cdot3=150$, are too large).
\end{itemize}
The numbers are $60,72,84,90,96$, with sum $\boxed{402}$.

question:
A multiple-choice question reads:
\begin{quote}
If you choose an answer to this question at random, what is the chance that you will be correct?\\
(A) $25\%$\qquad (B) $0\%$\qquad (C) $50\%$\qquad (D) $25\%$
\end{quote}
``At random'' means that each of the four choices is picked with probability $\dfrac{1}{4}$. Show that, whichever value is declared to be the correct answer, the question contradicts itself, so the question has no consistent answer.

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that if $a=b$, then $a^6=b^6$.
\item[(b)] Show that the converse is false: find real numbers $a\ne b$ with $a^6=b^6$.
\item[(c)] Prove that for real numbers $a$ and $b$, $a^6=b^6$ holds exactly when $a=b$ or $a=-b$.
\end{enumerate}

The rows of Pascal's triangle, starting with row $0$, are $1$; $1,1$; $1,2,1$; $1,3,3,1$; $1,4,6,4,1$; $\dots$ Reading rows $0$ to $4$ as numbers gives $1,11,121,1331,14641$, which are $11^0,11^1,11^2,11^3,11^4$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why this is not a coincidence by proving that $\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{n}\dbinom nk10^{\,n-k}=11^n$ for every $n\ge0$.
\item[(b)] Row $5$ is $1,5,10,10,5,1$. Explain why reading it with carries (each entry in its own decimal place) still gives $11^5$, and use row $6$ to find $11^6$.
\end{enumerate}

A circle is inscribed in a square with side length $10$. In each corner of the square there is a small circle that is tangent to the two sides at that corner and to the big circle.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the area of the region inside the square but outside the four small circles.
\item[(b)] Answer the same question for a square of side length $2x$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Do (b) first, with side $2x$. Put the square with corners $(0,0)$ and $(2x,2x)$; the big circle has centre $O=(x,x)$ and radius $x$. The small circle in the corner at the origin touches both axes, so its centre is $(r,r)$, where $r$ is its radius; it lies on the diagonal from the corner to $O$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=10, R=5, r=5*(3-2*sqrt(2));
pair O=(R,R);
draw((0,0)--(s,0)--(s,s)--(0,s)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O,R),linewidth(0.9));
pair[] cs={(r,r),(s-r,r),(s-r,s-r),(r,s-r)};
for(pair c:cs) filldraw(circle(c,r),palecyan,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw((0,0)--O,red+dashed);
dot(O); dot((r,r),blue); label("$O$",O,NE);
label("$10$",(s/2,s),N);
draw((r,r)--(r,0),blue); label("$r$",(r,r/2),E,blue+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The two circles are tangent externally, so the distance between their centres is $x+r$:
\[\sqrt2\,(x-r)=x+r\quad\Longrightarrow\quad r=\dfrac{\sqrt2-1}{\sqrt2+1}\,x=(\sqrt2-1)^2x=(3-2\sqrt2)\,x.\]
Then $r^2=(17-12\sqrt2)\,x^2$, and the area inside the square but outside the four small circles is
\[(2x)^2-4\pi r^2=\boxed{4x^2-4\pi(17-12\sqrt2)\,x^2}=4x^2\bigl(1-(17-12\sqrt2)\pi\bigr).\]
This answers \textbf{(b)}. For \textbf{(a)}, $x=5$:
\[100-100\pi(17-12\sqrt2)=\boxed{100-(1700-1200\sqrt2)\pi}\approx90.75.\]
(Each small circle has radius $5(3-2\sqrt2)\approx0.858$.)

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the average of the two solutions of the quadratic equation $ax^2+bx+c=0$ (with $a\ne0$)? (If there is a repeated root, count it twice.)
\item[(b)] Explain how this fact gives a quick check of the solutions of a quadratic equation, and use it to check the claimed solutions $x=3$ and $x=\dfrac{1}{2}$ of $2x^2-7x+3=0$ and the claimed solutions $x=3$ and $x=-\dfrac{1}{2}$ of the same equation.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] A geometric sequence has first term $a$ and common ratio $r\ne1$. Find a formula for the arithmetic mean of its first $n$ terms $a,ar,\ldots,ar^{n-1}$.
\item[(b)] Use it to find the arithmetic mean of the ten numbers $3,6,12,24,\ldots,1536$.
\end{enumerate}

For two relatively prime positive integers $a$ and $b$, the largest integer that cannot be written as $ax+by$ with nonnegative integers $x,y$ is $ab-a-b$. For three numbers there is no comparably simple formula, but residues give a method.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] For each remainder $k=0,1,2,3,4$, find the smallest number of the form $7y+9z$ (with $y,z\ge0$) that leaves remainder $k$ when divided by $5$.
\item[(b)] Hence find the largest integer that cannot be written as $5x+7y+9z$ with nonnegative integers $x,y,z$.
\item[(c)] A student ignores the $9$ and answers $5\cdot7-5-7=23$. Explain why this is wrong.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Put the rectangle at $(0,0),(8,0),(8,7),(0,7)$. The lower-left triangle has legs from $(0,0)$ to $(4,0)$ and to $(0,3)$, so its hypotenuse lies on the line $3x+4y=12$. The upper-left triangle has legs from $(0,7)$ to $(3,7)$ and down to $(0,3)$, so its hypotenuse joins $(0,3)$ and $(3,7)$ and lies on the line $4x-3y+9=0$. The figure is symmetric about the line $x=4$, and so are the two remaining hypotenuses, so we look for the circle with centre $O=(4,k)$ on this line.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A0=(0,0),B0=(8,0),C0=(8,7),D0=(0,7);
draw(A0--B0--C0--D0--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
pair P1=(4,0),P2=(0,3),P3=(0,3),P4=(3,7),P5=(8,3),P6=(5,7);
fill(A0--(4,0)--(0,3)--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1)); fill(B0--(4,0)--(8,3)--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.92,1));
fill(D0--(3,7)--(0,3)--cycle,rgb(1,0.9,0.8)); fill(C0--(5,7)--(8,3)--cycle,rgb(1,0.9,0.8));
draw((4,0)--(0,3)); draw((4,0)--(8,3)); draw((3,7)--(0,3)); draw((5,7)--(8,3));
draw(A0--B0--C0--D0--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
pair O=(4,25/7); real r=20/7;
draw(circle(O,r),blue+linewidth(0.9)); dot(O,blue);
pair T1=(16/7,9/7), T2=(12/7,37/7);
draw(O--T1,dashed+blue); draw(O--T2,dashed+blue); dot(T1); dot(T2);
label("$O$",O,E,blue);
label("$4$",(2,0),S); label("$4$",(6,0),S); label("$3$",(0,1.5),W); label("$4$",(0,5),W);
label("$3$",(1.5,7),N); label("$3$",(6.5,7),N); label("$3$",(8,1.5),E); label("$4$",(8,5),E);
label("$\dfrac{20}{7}$",(O+T1)/2,NW,blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$\dfrac{20}{7}$",(O+T2)/2,SW,blue+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The distances from $O$ to the two lines are
\[\dfrac{|3\cdot4+4k-12|}{5}=\dfrac{4k}{5}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\dfrac{|16-3k+9|}{5}=\dfrac{|25-3k|}{5}.\]
Tangency to all four hypotenuses (by symmetry, the right-hand ones give the same equations) requires these to be equal to the radius. With $O$ inside the rectangle between the lines, $4k=25-3k$, so $k=\dfrac{25}{7}$ and the radius is $\dfrac{4k}{5}=\dfrac{20}{7}$.

The points of tangency are the feet of the perpendiculars from $O$, namely $\left(\dfrac{16}{7},\dfrac{9}{7}\right)$ on the lower-left hypotenuse and $\left(\dfrac{12}{7},\dfrac{37}{7}\right)$ on the upper-left one; both lie on the hypotenuse segments (their $x$-coordinates are between $0$ and $4$, and between $0$ and $3$), so the circle really touches the four hypotenuses.

The diameter is $\dfrac{40}{7}$, so $m+n=40+7=\boxed{47}$.

A sphere of radius $2$ lies inside the prism exactly when its centre $(x,y,z)$ is at distance at least $2$ from every face:
\[2\le x\le22,\qquad 2\le y\le10,\qquad 2\le z\le10 .\]
These centres fill a box of volume $20\cdot8\cdot8=1280$.

The point $P=(21,6,6)$ lies inside the sphere exactly when the centre lies within distance $2$ of $P$, i.e. in the ball of radius $2$ about $P$. In the $y$- and $z$-directions this ball stays inside the box of centres ($4\le y,z\le8$), but in the $x$-direction it reaches $x=23>22$, so the part beyond the plane $x=22$ is cut off. The sketch shows the cross-section $z=6$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(24,0)--(24,12)--(0,12)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
fill((2,2)--(22,2)--(22,10)--(2,10)--cycle,rgb(0.9,0.95,1));
draw((2,2)--(22,2)--(22,10)--(2,10)--cycle,blue+dashed);
pair Q=(21,6);
path ins=arc(Q,2,60,300)--cycle;
fill(ins,rgb(1,0.82,0.82)); draw(arc(Q,2,60,300),red); draw(arc(Q,2,-60,60),red+dashed);
draw((22,3.5)--(22,8.5),blue);
dot(Q,red); label("$P$",Q,W,red+fontsize(8pt)); label("cap cut off",(23.2,6),E,red+fontsize(7pt));
label("$x=22$",(22,10),N,blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$x=2$",(2,10),N,blue+fontsize(8pt));
label("$24$",(12,0),S); label("$12$",(0,6),W);
label("region of centres",(10,6),blue+fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

The cut-off part is a spherical cap of height $h=1$ of a ball of radius $R=2$, with volume
\[\dfrac{\pi h^2(3R-h)}{3}=\dfrac{\pi\cdot1\cdot5}{3}=\dfrac{5\pi}{3}.\]
So the favourable centres fill a volume
\[\dfrac{4}{3}\pi\cdot2^3-\dfrac{5\pi}{3}=\dfrac{32\pi}{3}-\dfrac{5\pi}{3}=9\pi,\]
and the probability is $\boxed{\dfrac{9\pi}{1280}}\approx0.0221$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real k=0.35;
// cylinder
path e1=shift(0,0)*scale(4,4*k)*unitcircle, e2=shift(0,7)*scale(4,4*k)*unitcircle;
draw(e2);
draw(shift(0,0)*scale(1,k)*arc((0,0),4,180,360)); draw(shift(0,0)*scale(1,k)*arc((0,0),4,0,180),dashed);
draw((-4,0)--(-4,7)); draw((4,0)--(4,7));
draw((0,0)--(4,0),blue); label("$4$",(2,0),N,blue); draw((0,0)--(0,7),dashed+blue); label("$7$",(0,3.5),W,blue);
// cone
pair c=(13,0);
draw(shift(c)*scale(1,k)*arc((0,0),5,180,360)); draw(shift(c)*scale(1,k)*arc((0,0),5,0,180),dashed);
draw(c+(-5,0)--c+(0,12)--c+(5,0));
draw(c--c+(5,0),red); label("$5$",c+(2.5,0),N,red); draw(c--c+(0,12),dashed+red); label("$12$",c+(0,6),W,red);
label("$13$",c+(2.5,6),E,red);
label("cylinder",(0,-2.6)); label("cone",c+(0,-2.6));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Cylinder.} $V=\pi\cdot4^2\cdot7=112\pi$ and $A=2\pi\cdot4^2+2\pi\cdot4\cdot7=32\pi+56\pi=88\pi$, so $\dfrac {V}{A}=\dfrac{112}{88}=\dfrac{14}{11}$.

\textbf{Cone.} The slant height is $\sqrt{5^2+12^2}=13$, so $V=\dfrac{1}{3}\pi\cdot5^2\cdot12=100\pi$ and $A=\pi\cdot5^2+\pi\cdot5\cdot13=25\pi+65\pi=90\pi$, giving $\dfrac {V}{A}=\dfrac{100}{90}=\dfrac{10}{9}$.

Since $\dfrac{14}{11}=\dfrac{126}{99}>\dfrac{110}{99}=\dfrac{10}{9}$, the cylinder has the greater ratio, and its volume is $\boxed{112\pi}$.

In a cyclic quadrilateral opposite angles are supplementary, so $\angle D=180^\circ-\angle B$ and $\cos D=-\cos B$. Apply the law of cosines to the diagonal $AC$ in triangles $ABC$ and $ADC$:
\[AC^2=17^2+13^2-2\cdot17\cdot13\cos B=7^2+13^2+2\cdot7\cdot13\cos B.\]
So $458-442\cos B=218+182\cos B$, giving $624\cos B=240$ and $\cos B=\dfrac{5}{13}$. Then
\[AC^2=458-442\cdot\dfrac{5}{13}=458-170=288,\qquad AC=12\sqrt2,\]
and $\sin B=\sqrt{1-\dfrac{25}{169}}=\dfrac{12}{13}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=13*sqrt(2)/2;
real t1=2*aSin(17/(2R)), t2=2*aSin(13/(2R)), t3=2*aSin(13/(2R)), t4=2*aSin(7/(2R));
pair A=R*dir(200), B=R*dir(200-t1), C=R*dir(200-t1-t2), D=R*dir(200-t1-t2-t3);
draw(circle((0,0),R)); draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8)); draw(A--C,dashed+blue);
dot((0,0)); label("$P$",(0,0),SW);
label("$A$",A,dir(A)); label("$B$",B,dir(B)); label("$C$",C,dir(C)); label("$D$",D,dir(D));
label("$17$",(A+B)/2,dir((A+B)/2)*(-1)); label("$13$",(B+C)/2,-dir((B+C)/2)); label("$13$",(C+D)/2,-dir((C+D)/2)); label("$7$",(D+A)/2,-dir((D+A)/2));
label("$12\sqrt2$",(A+C)/2,NW,blue);
\end{asy}

The circle is the circumcircle of triangle $ABC$, so by the extended law of sines its radius is
\[R=\dfrac{AC}{2\sin B}=\dfrac{12\sqrt2}{2\cdot\dfrac{12}{13}}=\dfrac{13\sqrt2}{2}.\]
The area of the circle is $\pi R^2=\pi\cdot\dfrac{169\cdot2}{4}=\boxed{\dfrac{169\pi}{2}}$.

A number is divisible by $6$ exactly when it is even and its digit sum is divisible by $3$. A palindrome's last digit equals its first digit, which is not $0$.
\begin{itemize}
\item One digit: only $6$.
\item Two digits $\overline{aa}$: $a$ even and $2a$ divisible by $3$, so $a=6$: the number $66$.
\item Three digits $\overline{aba}$: $a\in\{2,4,6,8\}$ and $2a+b\equiv0\pmod3$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $a=2$: $b\equiv2\pmod 3$, so $b\in\{2,5,8\}$ ($3$ numbers);
\item $a=4$: $b\equiv1$, so $b\in\{1,4,7\}$ ($3$);
\item $a=6$: $b\equiv0$, so $b\in\{0,3,6,9\}$ ($4$);
\item $a=8$: $b\equiv2$, so $b\in\{2,5,8\}$ ($3$).
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
The total is $1+1+13=\boxed{15}$.

An inscribed angle of $90^\circ$ subtends a semicircle, so the hypotenuse $AB$ is a diameter of the circle (equivalently, the midpoint of the hypotenuse is equidistant from all three vertices).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-3,0),B=(3,0),C=3*dir(62),N0=(0,0);
draw(circle(N0,3)); draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
dot(N0); label("$N$",N0,S);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,N);
draw(C+0.35*dir(C--A)--C+0.35*dir(C--A)+0.35*dir(C--B)--C+0.35*dir(C--B));
label("$AB=12$",(0,-1.3));
\end{asy}

So the diameter is $12$ inches and the circumference is $\boxed{12\pi}$ inches.

The list has $9$ numbers, so its median is the $5$th smallest.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $x\le10$, the five numbers $5,7,7,10,x$ are all at most $10$ and the other four are larger, so the $5$th smallest is $\max(10,x)=10$. This works.
\item If $x\ge11$, the four numbers $5,7,7,10$ are the smallest and the $5$th smallest is $\min(11,x)=11\ne10$.
\end{itemize}
So $x\in\{1,2,\ldots,10\}$, and the sum is $1+2+\cdots+10=\boxed{55}$.

Let the square have side $2$. By the converse of Thales' theorem, $\angle MSN$ is a right angle exactly when $S$ is on the circle with diameter $MN$, obtuse when $S$ is inside that circle, and acute when $S$ is outside it. Inside the square, the points with an obtuse (or right) angle form the half-disc on $MN$ (shaded; $S'$ is such a point), of area $\dfrac{1}{2}\pi\cdot1^2=\dfrac{\pi}{2}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair M=(0,0),N0=(4,0),P=(4,4),Q=(0,4);
fill(arc((2,0),2,0,180)--cycle,rgb(1,0.85,0.85));
draw(M--N0--P--Q--cycle,linewidth(0.8)); draw(arc((2,0),2,0,180),red);
pair S1=(2.9,2.9), S2=(1.4,1.0);
draw(M--S1--N0,blue); draw(M--S2--N0,deepgreen);
dot(S1,blue); dot(S2,deepgreen); label("$S$",S1,N,blue); label("$S'$",S2,N,deepgreen);
label("$M$",M,SW); label("$N$",N0,SE); label("$P$",P,NE); label("$Q$",Q,NW);
\end{asy}

So the probability that the angle is acute is
\[1-\dfrac{\pi}{2}\cdot\dfrac{1}{4}=1-\dfrac{\pi}{8}\approx1-0.3927=0.6073,\]
which is $\boxed{0.61}$ to the nearest hundredth.

In an isosceles triangle the median to the base is also an altitude, so triangle $ABD$ has a right angle at $D$, with $AD=5$. Let $AB=c$ and $BD=m$ (positive integers). Then
\[c^2-m^2=25,\qquad (c-m)(c+m)=25.\]
Since $0<c-m<c+m$, the only factorisation is $c-m=1$, $c+m=25$, so $c=13$ and $m=12$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-5,0),C=(5,0),B=(0,12),D=(0,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8)); draw(B--D,blue);
draw(D+(0,0.6)--D+(0.6,0.6)--D+(0.6,0));
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$B$",B,N); label("$D$",D,S);
label("$5$",(-2.5,0),S); label("$5$",(2.5,0),S); label("$12$",(0,6),E,blue); label("$13$",(-2.5,6),W); label("$13$",(2.5,6),E);
\end{asy}

So $BD=\boxed{12}$ (and $AB=BC=13$).

A diagonal of an inscribed rectangle subtends a right angle at the other vertices, so it is a diameter: the diagonal is $116$. Let the sides be $20k$ and $21k$. Then
\[(20k)^2+(21k)^2=(29k)^2=116^2,\]
so $k=4$ and the sides are $80$ and $84$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=5.8; pair O=(0,0);
pair A=(-4.2,-4),B=(4.2,-4),C=(4.2,4),D=(-4.2,4);
draw(circle(O,R)); draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8)); draw(A--C,dashed+blue);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,N);
label("$84$",(0,-4),S); label("$80$",(4.2,0),E); label("$116$",(A+C)/2+(1.2,-1.6),blue);
\end{asy}

The perimeter is $2(80+84)=\boxed{328}$.

The sides are $\dfrac{13}{4}$, $\dfrac{14}{4}$ and $\dfrac{15}{4}$, so the triangle is the $13$-$14$-$15$ triangle scaled by $\dfrac{1}{4}$.

The $13$-$14$-$15$ triangle has area $84$: by Heron's formula with $s=21$, the area is $\sqrt{21\cdot8\cdot7\cdot6}=\sqrt{7056}=84$. (Its altitude to the side $14$ is $12$, splitting that side into $5$ and $9$.) Scaling lengths by $\dfrac{1}{4}$ scales areas by $\dfrac{1}{16}$; in the scaled triangle the altitude to the side $\dfrac{14}{4}$ is $3$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(3.5,0),A=(1.25,3);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8)); draw(A--(1.25,0),dashed+blue);
draw((1.25,0)+(0,0.18)--(1.43,0.18)--(1.43,0),blue);
label("$\dfrac{13}{4}$",(A+B)/2,W); label("$\dfrac{15}{4}$",(A+C)/2,NE); label("$\dfrac{14}{4}$",(B+C)/2,S);
label("$3$",(1.25,1.5),E,blue);
\end{asy}

So the area is $\dfrac{84}{16}=\boxed{\dfrac{21}{4}}$.

The slice is a sector with $\dfrac{80}{360}=\dfrac{2}{9}$ of the area of the pizza: $\dfrac{2}{9}\cdot81\pi=18\pi$ square inches. One rectangular piece has area $\dfrac{12\cdot16}{20}=\dfrac{48}{5}=9.6$ square inches.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
