The difference is $\boxed{18\pi-\dfrac{48}{5}}\approx56.55-9.6=46.95$ square inches.

There are $4^3=64$ equally likely outcomes, with sums from $3$ to $12$. The prime sums are $3,5,7,11$. Count the ordered triples:
\begin{itemize}
\item sum $3$: $(1,1,1)$: $1$ way;
\item sum $5$: the triples $\{1,1,3\}$ ($3$ orders) and $\{1,2,2\}$ ($3$ orders): $6$ ways;
\item sum $7$: $\{1,2,4\}$ ($6$ orders), $\{1,3,3\}$ ($3$), $\{2,2,3\}$ ($3$): $12$ ways;
\item sum $11$: $\{3,4,4\}$: $3$ ways.
\end{itemize}
That is $1+6+12+3=22$ outcomes, so the probability is $\dfrac{22}{64}=\boxed{\dfrac{11}{32}}$.

The nap times are:
\begin{itemize}
\item Brien: $2\!:\!00$--$3\!:\!00$ and $5\!:\!00$--$6\!:\!00$;
\item Jill: $1\!:\!45$--$2\!:\!15$, $4\!:\!45$--$5\!:\!15$ and $5\!:\!30$--$6\!:\!00$;
\item Bob: $1\!:\!50$--$3\!:\!20$ and $5\!:\!10$--$6\!:\!40$.
\end{itemize}
All three are asleep during $2\!:\!00$--$2\!:\!15$ ($15$ minutes; Jill wakes at $2\!:\!15$), during $5\!:\!10$--$5\!:\!15$ ($5$ minutes; Bob falls asleep at $5\!:\!10$, Jill wakes at $5\!:\!15$) and during $5\!:\!30$--$6\!:\!00$ ($30$ minutes), and at no other time (between $2\!:\!15$ and $5\!:\!00$ Brien and Jill are never asleep together). The total is $15+5+30=\boxed{50}$ minutes.

The sphere has radius $25$. The centre $O$ of the sphere, the centre of the circle and any point of the circle form a right triangle (the segment from $O$ to the centre of the circle is perpendicular to the plane), with hypotenuse $25$ and one leg equal to the radius $7$ of the circle. The cross-section through $O$ perpendicular to the plane is shown.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); real R=2.5;
draw(box((-R,-R),(R,R)),gray); draw(circle(O,R),linewidth(0.8));
real d=R*24/25, h=R*7/25;
draw((-h,d)--(h,d),red); draw((0,d)--(h,d),red+linewidth(1.8)); draw(O--(0,d),blue); draw(O--(h,d),dashed);
dot(O); dot((0,d),red);
label("$24$",(0,d/2),W,blue); label("$25$",(h/2,d/2),E); label("$7$",(h/2,d),N,red);
label("$O$",O,S);
\end{asy}

The distance is $\sqrt{25^2-7^2}=\sqrt{576}=\boxed{24}$.

Coin A has radius $0.6$ and coin B has radius $0.8$. Every small square looks the same, so we may work in one $2\times2$ square, with the coin's centre uniform in it. A coin of radius $\rho$ touches no line exactly when its centre is more than $\rho$ from all four sides, i.e. in a central square of side $2-2\rho$ (shaded below; the boundary has probability $0$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(box((0,0),(2,2)),linewidth(0.8)); draw(box((3,0),(5,2)),linewidth(0.8));
fill(box((0.6,0.6),(1.4,1.4)),rgb(0.85,0.92,1)); draw(box((0.6,0.6),(1.4,1.4)),blue+dashed);
draw(circle((1.25,0.8),0.6),blue); dot((1.25,0.8),blue);
fill(box((3.8,0.8),(4.2,1.2)),rgb(1,0.85,0.85)); draw(box((3.8,0.8),(4.2,1.2)),red+dashed);
draw(circle((4,1),0.8),red); dot((4,1),red);
label("coin A",(1,0),S); label("coin B",(4,0),S);
label("$0.8$",(1,1.4),N,blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$0.4$",(4,1.2),N,red+fontsize(8pt));
label("$2$",(0,1),W); label("$2$",(3,1),W);
\end{asy}

\begin{itemize}
\item Coin A: the central square has side $2-1.2=0.8$, so coin A touches no line with probability $\dfrac{0.8^2}{4}=0.16$.
\item Coin B: the central square has side $2-1.6=0.4$, so coin B touches no line with probability $\dfrac{0.4^2}{4}=0.04$, and touches a line with probability $0.96$.
\end{itemize}
By independence the probability is $0.16\cdot0.96=\boxed{0.1536=\dfrac{96}{625}}$.

List the knight's-move neighbours of each key:
\[0:\{3,5\},\ 5:\{0\},\ 3:\{0,4,8\},\ 1:\{6,8\},\ 2:\{7,9\},\ 4:\{3,9\},\ 6:\{1,7\},\ 7:\{2,6\},\ 8:\{1,3\},\ 9:\{2,4\}.\]
The key $5$ has only one neighbour, $0$, so it must be the first or the last key typed, next to $0$; and $0$'s other neighbour is $3$. Reading the number so that it starts $5,0,3$, the remaining keys $1,2,4,6,7,8,9$ together with $3$ form the cycle
\[3-4-9-2-7-6-1-8-3,\]
and from $3$ we must visit all of them along this cycle, in one of its two directions:
\[5,0,3,4,9,2,7,6,1,8\qquad\text{or}\qquad5,0,3,8,1,6,7,2,9,4 .\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
void key(pair p,string s){draw(shift(p)*box((-0.4,-0.4),(0.4,0.4))); label(s,p);}
key((0,3),"$7$");key((1,3),"$8$");key((2,3),"$9$");key((0,2),"$4$");key((1,2),"$5$");key((2,2),"$6$");key((0,1),"$1$");key((1,1),"$2$");key((2,1),"$3$");key((0,0),"$0$");
pair P(int d){ pair[] q={(0,0),(0,1),(1,1),(2,1),(0,2),(1,2),(2,2),(0,3),(1,3),(2,3)}; return q[d];}
int[] path={5,0,3,8,1,6,7,2,9,4};
for(int i=0;i<9;++i) draw(P(path[i])--P(path[i+1]),red,Arrow(5),Margins);
\end{asy}

Each of these two paths can be typed in either direction, and none of the four numbers starts with $0$:
\[5034927618,\quad 5038167294,\quad 8167294305,\quad 4927618305 .\]
So $\boxed{4}$ numbers can be typed. (On a telephone keypad, where $0$ is below $8$, the key $5$ has no knight's move at all, and the answer would be $0$.)

question:
Using each of the ten digits $0,1,\ldots,9$ exactly once, write two positive integers (neither starting with $0$) with exactly one of the operation signs $+$, $-$, $\times$, $\div$ between them, so that the value is $99$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the signs $+$ and $\times$ cannot work.
\item[(b)] Suppose the sign is $\div$, so that the expression is $A\div B=99$. Show that $B$ has exactly four digits and $A$ six, and that the digit sums of $A$ and of $B$ are both multiples of $9$.
\item[(c)] Check that $496287\div5013$ is such an expression.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The fence is $2(60+20)=160$ feet long, so the square has side $40$ feet.

