By the shoelace formula,
\[[VPQ]=\dfrac{1}{2}\left|\dfrac{2}{3}\left(-\dfrac{7}{2}+\dfrac{7}{4}\right)-\dfrac{1}{2}\left(-\dfrac{7}{4}-0\right)+\dfrac{5}{4}\left(0+\dfrac{7}{2}\right)\right|=\dfrac{1}{2}\left|-\dfrac{7}{6}+\dfrac{7}{8}+\dfrac{35}{8}\right|=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\dfrac{49}{12}=\boxed{\dfrac{49}{24}}.\]

For $k\ne k'$, the lines $x=k$ and $x=k'$ are distinct vertical lines, hence parallel, and a point on both would have $x=k$ and $x=k'$ at the same time, which is impossible.

