So no point lies on two of the lines, and the answer is $\boxed{0}$. (The lines cover the whole plane, each point lying on exactly one of them.)

Three lengths $a<b<c$ form a (nondegenerate) triangle exactly when $a+b>c$; the other two triangle inequalities hold automatically. If $a+b=c$, the ``triangle'' is flat.

