Count by the largest number $c$, listing the pairs $a<b<c$ with $a+b>c$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $c\le3$: none (for $c=3$ the only pair $1,2$ has sum $3$);
\item $c=4$: $(2,3)$: $1$;
\item $c=5$: $(2,4),(3,4)$: $2$;
\item $c=6$: $(2,5),(3,4),(3,5),(4,5)$: $4$;
\item $c=7$: $(2,6),(3,5),(3,6),(4,5),(4,6),(5,6)$: $6$;
\item $c=8$: $(2,7),(3,6),(3,7),(4,5),(4,6),(4,7),(5,6),(5,7),(6,7)$: $9$.
\end{itemize}
The total is $1+2+4+6+9=\boxed{22}$.

A faster count: for a fixed $c$, the number of pairs is $\left\lfloor\dfrac{(c-2)^2}{4}\right\rfloor$ (for $b=c-1,c-2,\ldots$ there are $c-2,c-4,\ldots$ choices of $a$), giving $0,1,2,4,6,9$ for $c=3,\ldots,8$.

