Let $ABCD$ be a parallelogram that is neither a rectangle nor a rhombus.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the half-turn (rotation by $180^\circ$) about the intersection point $O$ of the diagonals maps $ABCD$ onto itself.
\item[(b)] Prove that $ABCD$ has no line of symmetry.
\end{enumerate}
(So a parallelogram of this kind has rotational symmetry but no reflection symmetry.)

solution:
Let $AB=CD=a$ and $BC=DA=b$; since $ABCD$ is not a rhombus, $a\ne b$. Since it is not a rectangle, its angles are not right angles, so the angles at $A$ and $C$ (which are equal) differ from those at $B$ and $D$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),Cc=(5.5,2),D=(1.5,2),O=(A+Cc)/2;
draw(A--B--Cc--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8)); draw(A--Cc,gray+dashed); draw(B--D,gray+dashed);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,2*dir(-75));
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",Cc,NE); label("$D$",D,NW);
draw(arc(O,0.55,40,220),blue,Arrow(5)); label("$180^\circ$",O+(-0.2,0.55),N,blue+fontsize(8pt));
label("$a$",(A+B)/2,S); label("$a$",(Cc+D)/2,N); label("$b$",(B+Cc)/2,E); label("$b$",(D+A)/2,W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} The diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other, so $O$ is the midpoint of $AC$ and of $BD$. The half-turn about $O$ therefore swaps $A$ and $C$ and swaps $B$ and $D$, so it maps each side onto the opposite side and the parallelogram onto itself.

\textbf{(b)} Suppose a reflection $\sigma$ in a line $\ell$ maps the parallelogram onto itself. A reflection preserves distances and angles and maps corners to corners, so it permutes $A,B,C,D$, maps sides to sides and keeps the angle at each vertex. Hence $\sigma$ maps $\{A,C\}$ onto itself and $\{B,D\}$ onto itself.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $\sigma$ fixes $A$ and $C$, then $\ell$ is the line $AC$ (a reflection fixes only the points of its axis). The point $B$ is not on $AC$, so $\sigma(B)=D$, and then $AB=\sigma(A)\sigma(B)=AD$, i.e. $a=b$: a contradiction.
\item If $\sigma$ swaps $A$ and $C$ but fixes $B$ and $D$, then $\ell$ is the line $BD$ and $AB=\sigma(A)\sigma(B)=CB$, i.e. $a=b$: a contradiction.
\item If $\sigma$ swaps $A$ and $C$ and swaps $B$ and $D$, then $\ell$ is the perpendicular bisector of $AC$ and also of $BD$. So $AC$ and $BD$ are both perpendicular to $\ell$, hence parallel; but they meet at $O$, so they lie on one line, and the four vertices would be collinear: a contradiction.
\end{itemize}
So no line of symmetry exists. $\blacksquare$

