A student claims: \emph{in every convex hexagon whose sides are all longer than $1$, some diagonal is longer than $2$.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Let $ACE$ be an equilateral triangle with side $\dfrac{49}{25}$, and let $B$, $D$, $F$ be the points outside the triangle at distance $\dfrac{1}{5}$ from the midpoints of $AC$, $CE$, $EA$ respectively, each on the perpendicular bisector of its side. Show that the hexagon $ABCDEF$ is convex, that all of its sides are longer than $1$, and that all of its diagonals are shorter than $2$. (So the claim is false.)
\item[(b)] Prove that every convex hexagon whose sides are all longer than $1$ has a diagonal longer than $\sqrt3$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Write $s=\dfrac{49}{25}$ and $h=\dfrac{1}{5}$, and let $O$ be the centre of triangle $ACE$. The figure is unchanged by the rotations through $120^\circ$ about $O$ (they permute $A,C,E$ and $B,D,F$), so it is enough to look at one side and one diagonal of each kind.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=49/25, h=1/5;
pair A=(0,0), C=(s,0), Ee=(s/2,s*sqrt(3)/2), O=(A+C+Ee)/3;
pair B=(s/2,0)+h*unit(rotate(-90)*(C-A));
pair D=(C+Ee)/2+h*unit(rotate(-90)*(Ee-C));
pair F=(Ee+A)/2+h*unit(rotate(-90)*(A-Ee));
draw(A--C--Ee--cycle,dashed+gray);
draw(A--D,dotted+blue); draw(B--D,dotted+red);
draw(A--B--C--D--Ee--F--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(O);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,S); label("$C$",C,E); label("$D$",D,NE); label("$E$",Ee,N); label("$F$",F,NW); label("$O$",O,SW,fontsize(8pt));
label("$s$",(A+C)/2-(0.42,0),N,gray+fontsize(9pt));
draw(B--(s/2,0),linewidth(0.6)); label("$h$",(B+(s/2,0))/2+(-0.04,0),W,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\emph{Sides.} $B$ is at distance $h$ from the midpoint of $AC$ on its perpendicular bisector, so
\[AB^2=BC^2=\left(\dfrac {s}{2}\right)^2+h^2=\dfrac{2401}{2500}+\dfrac{100}{2500}=\dfrac{2501}{2500}>1 .\]
By the symmetry all six sides have this length, so all are longer than $1$.

\emph{Convexity.} Let $\beta=\angle BAC$; then $\tan\beta=\dfrac{2h}{s}=\dfrac{10}{49}<\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}$, so $\beta<30^\circ$. The angle of the hexagon at $B$ is $180^\circ-2\beta<180^\circ$. The angle at $A$ is $\angle FAE+\angle EAC+\angle CAB=\beta+60^\circ+\beta<120^\circ$. By symmetry every angle is less than $180^\circ$, so the hexagon is convex.

\emph{Diagonals.} The nine diagonals are of three kinds.
\begin{itemize}
\item $AC=CE=EA=s=\dfrac{49}{25}<2$.
\item $AD$, $BE$, $CF$: the perpendicular bisector of $CE$ passes through $A$, so $D$ lies on the altitude from $A$, beyond the midpoint of $CE$. Hence $AD=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s+h=\dfrac{49\sqrt3}{50}+\dfrac{1}{5}<\dfrac{49\cdot1.74}{50}+0.2<1.91<2$.
\item $BD$, $DF$, $FB$: $B$, $D$, $F$ are at distance $\rho+h$ from $O$, where $\rho=\dfrac{s}{2\sqrt3}$ is the inradius of $ACE$, and they are $120^\circ$ apart around $O$. So $BD=\sqrt3(\rho+h)=\dfrac {s}{2}+\sqrt3h=\dfrac{49}{50}+\dfrac{\sqrt3}{5}<1.33<2$.
\end{itemize}
So every side is longer than $1$ while every diagonal is shorter than $2$: the claim is \textbf{false}.

\textbf{(b)} The interior angles of a hexagon add up to $(6-2)\cdot180^\circ=720^\circ$, so at least one of them is at least $\dfrac{720^\circ}{6}=120^\circ$. Call that vertex $B$ and its neighbours $A$ and $C$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), A=1.25*dir(205), C=1.35*dir(-25);
draw(A--B--C,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--C,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(B,0.3,205,335),blue);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,N); label("$C$",C,E);
label("$\ge120^\circ$",B+(0,-0.3),S,blue+fontsize(8pt));
label("$>1$",(A+B)/2,NW,fontsize(8pt)); label("$>1$",(B+C)/2,NE,fontsize(8pt));
label("$AC>\sqrt3$",(A+C)/2,S,red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Since $\cos\angle ABC\le\cos120^\circ=-\dfrac{1}{2}$, the law of cosines gives
\[AC^2=AB^2+BC^2-2\,AB\cdot BC\cos\angle ABC\ge AB^2+BC^2+AB\cdot BC>1+1+1=3 .\]
Hence $AC>\sqrt3$, and $AC$ is a diagonal of the hexagon (it joins two vertices that are not neighbours). $\blacksquare$

First, $d$ is not the square of a rational number either: if $d=\left(\dfrac {p}{q}\right)^2$ in lowest terms, then $dq^2=p^2$, and every prime factor of $q$ would divide $p$, so $q=1$ and $d=p^2$ would be a perfect square.

The pair $(-1,0)$ is a solution. Let $(x,y)$ be any other rational solution, so $x\ne-1$, and put $s=\dfrac{y}{x+1}$, a rational number (the slope of the line through $(-1,0)$ and $(x,y)$). Then $y=s(x+1)$ and
\[x^2-1=dy^2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad(x-1)(x+1)=ds^2(x+1)^2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad x-1=ds^2(x+1),\]
dividing by $x+1\ne0$. Hence $x(1-ds^2)=1+ds^2$. Here $1-ds^2\ne0$, since otherwise $d=\left(\dfrac{1}{s}\right)^2$ would be a rational square. Therefore
\[x=\dfrac{1+ds^2}{1-ds^2},\qquad y=s(x+1)=\dfrac{2s}{1-ds^2}.\]
Conversely, for every rational $s$ these numbers are rational and
\[(1+ds^2)^2-d(2s)^2=1-2ds^2+d^2s^4=(1-ds^2)^2,\]
so they satisfy $x^2-dy^2=1$.

\textbf{Answer.} The rational solutions are $(x,y)=(-1,0)$ and
\[(x,y)=\left(\dfrac{1+ds^2}{1-ds^2},\ \dfrac{2s}{1-ds^2}\right),\qquad s\text{ rational}\]
(for example $s=0$ gives $(1,0)$). Geometrically, every line with rational slope $s$ through $(-1,0)$ meets the curve in exactly one more point, and that point is rational. For $d=2$ and $s=\dfrac{1}{2}$ we get $(3,2)$: $9-2\cdot4=1$.

