\end{asy}

question:
Eight children, $4$ boys and $4$ girls, are split at random into two groups $A$ and $B$ of $4$ children each, all such splits being equally likely.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the probability that all four boys are in the same group?
\item[(b)] A student argues: ``Each boy goes to $A$ or to $B$, which gives $2^4=16$ patterns; only $AAAA$ and $BBBB$ work, so the probability is $\dfrac{2}{16}=\dfrac{1}{8}$.'' Explain the error.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Sort the numbers $1,\ldots,50$ by their remainders mod $7$. Since $50=7\cdot7+1$, the remainder $1$ occurs $8$ times ($1,8,\ldots,50$) and each of the remainders $0,2,3,4,5,6$ occurs $7$ times.

\textbf{Upper bound.} Two elements have a sum divisible by $7$ exactly when their remainders add up to $0$ or $7$. So $S$ contains
\begin{itemize}
\item at most one multiple of $7$;
\item numbers from at most one of the classes $1$ and $6$, so at most $8$ of them;
\item numbers from at most one of the classes $2$ and $5$, so at most $7$;
\item numbers from at most one of the classes $3$ and $4$, so at most $7$.
\end{itemize}
Hence $|S|\le1+8+7+7=23$.

\textbf{Construction.} Take all numbers with remainders $1$, $2$ and $3$, together with $7$. Remainders of two distinct elements add up to one of $2,3,4,5,6$ (two from $\{1,2,3\}$) or to $1,2,3$ (with the single multiple of $7$), never to $0$ or $7$. This set has $8+7+7+1=23$ elements.

So the largest size is $\boxed{23}$.

question:
On your birthday you eat a whole cake. The phrase ``every half birthday half a cake, and so on'' can be continued in two ways over the following year (up to, but not including, your next birthday).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Half a year after the birthday you eat $\dfrac{1}{2}$ of a cake, a quarter of a year after that $\dfrac{1}{4}$ of a cake, an eighth of a year after that $\dfrac{1}{8}$ of a cake, and so on, halving both the waiting time and the amount each time. How much cake do you eat during the year, the birthday cake included?
\item[(b)] Besides the birthday cake, at each moment that is a whole number of half years after the birthday you eat $\dfrac{1}{2}$ of a cake; at each moment that is a whole number of quarter years but not of half years, $\dfrac{1}{4}$ of a cake; at each moment that is a whole number of eighths of a year but not of quarters, $\dfrac{1}{8}$ of a cake; and so on, down to moments that are whole numbers of $\dfrac{1}{2^n}$ of a year. How much cake do you eat during the year? What happens if this goes on without end?
\end{enumerate}

Segments $\overline{AB}$ and $\overline{CD}$ cross at a point $P$ that lies inside both segments, with $AP=CP$ and $BP=DP$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Is it always true that $\overline{AD}\parallel\overline{BC}$? Prove your answer.
\item[(b)] Prove that $\overline{AC}\parallel\overline{BD}$ always.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $a=AP=CP$ and $b=BP=DP$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(0,0), u=dir(10), v=dir(120);
real a=2, bb=1;
pair A=a*u, B=-bb*u, C=a*v, D=-bb*v;
draw(A--B,linewidth(0.9)); draw(C--D,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,red); draw(B--C,red);
draw(A--C,blue+dashed); draw(B--D,blue+dashed);
dot(P);
label("$A$",A,E); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,N); label("$D$",D,S); label("$P$",P,NE);
label("$2$",(P+A)/2,S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$2$",(P+C)/2,E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$1$",(P+B)/2,N,fontsize(8pt)); label("$1$",(P+D)/2,E,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} \textbf{No}; it holds exactly when $a=b$. Triangles $APD$ and $CPB$ are congruent (SAS: $AP=CP$, $PD=PB$, and the vertical angles at $P$ are equal), so $AD=CB$, but this does not make the segments parallel.

Suppose $AD\parallel BC$. Since $A,P,B$ are collinear and $D,P,C$ are collinear, the alternate angles give $\angle PAD=\angle PBC$ and $\angle PDA=\angle PCB$, so triangles $PAD$ and $PBC$ are similar and
\[\dfrac{PA}{PB}=\dfrac{PD}{PC},\qquad\text{that is}\qquad\dfrac {a}{b}=\dfrac {b}{a},\]
so $a=b$. Conversely, if $a=b$, then $P$ is the midpoint of both $AB$ and $CD$, so the diagonals of quadrilateral $ADBC$ bisect each other; it is a parallelogram and $AD\parallel BC$.

For a concrete counterexample take (as in the figure) $a=2$, $b=1$, or in coordinates $A=(2,0)$, $B=(-1,0)$, $C=(0,2)$, $D=(0,-1)$: the slope of $AD$ is $\dfrac{1}{2}$ and the slope of $BC$ is $2$ (red segments).

\textbf{(b)} Triangles $APC$ and $BPD$ are isosceles, with apex angles $\angle APC$ and $\angle BPD$, which are equal vertical angles. So their base angles are equal:
\[\angle PAC=\dfrac{180^\circ-\angle APC}{2}=\dfrac{180^\circ-\angle BPD}{2}=\angle PBD .\]
These are alternate angles formed by the transversal $AB$ with the lines $AC$ and $BD$ ($C$ and $D$ lie on opposite sides of line $AB$). Hence $AC\parallel BD$ (blue dashed segments). $\blacksquare$

\textbf{Mass points.} Put masses at the vertices so that $D$ and $F$ are balance points: $BD:DC=1:3$ needs $m_B:m_C=3:1$, and $AF:FB=1:4$ needs $m_A:m_B=4:1$. So take $m_A=12$, $m_B=3$, $m_C=1$. Then $D$ carries mass $m_B+m_C=4$, and $G$ divides $AD$ in the ratio
\[AG:GD=m_D:m_A=4:12=1:3 .\]
(The same ratio follows from Menelaus' theorem for triangle $ABD$ and the line $F$--$G$--$C$: $\dfrac{AF}{FB}\cdot\dfrac{BC}{CD}\cdot\dfrac{DG}{GA}=1$, i.e. $\dfrac{1}{4}\cdot\dfrac{4}{3}\cdot\dfrac{DG}{GA}=1$, so $\dfrac{DG}{GA}=3$.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(2,6.5), B=(0,0), C=(8,0);
pair D=B+(C-B)/4, F=A+(B-A)/5;
pair G=A+(D-A)/4;
pair Ee=extension(B,G,A,C);
fill(G--D--C--cycle,lightgray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D); draw(B--Ee); draw(C--F);
dot(G);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,S); label("$E$",Ee,NE); label("$F$",F,W); label("$G$",G,2*dir(14));
label("$1$",(B+D)/2,S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$3$",(D+C)/2,S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Areas.} Triangles $ADC$ and $ABC$ have the same height from $A$, so $[ADC]=\dfrac{DC}{BC}\cdot144=\dfrac{3}{4}\cdot144=108$. Triangles $GDC$ and $ADC$ have the same base $DC$, and the height of $G$ is $\dfrac{GD}{AD}=\dfrac{3}{4}$ of the height of $A$. Hence
\[[GDC]=\dfrac{3}{4}\cdot108=\boxed{81}.\]

