solution:
Turning at a constant rate, the ship moves along a circle; $18^\circ$ is $\dfrac{18}{360}=\dfrac{1}{20}$ of a full turn.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1;
draw(circle((0,0),r));
draw(arc((0,0),r,70,88),red+linewidth(1.5));
draw((0,0)--dir(70),dashed); draw((0,0)--dir(88),dashed);
draw(arc((0,0),0.3,70,88),blue);
label("$18^\circ$",0.42*dir(64),E,blue+fontsize(8pt));
label("$25$ min",dir(79),N,red+fontsize(8pt));
draw((0,0)--dir(-30)); label("$r$",dir(-30)/2,N,fontsize(8pt));
dot((0,0));
\end{asy}

So the full circle takes $20\cdot25=500$ minutes $=\dfrac{25}{3}$ hours, and its circumference is
\[20\cdot\dfrac{25}{3}=\dfrac{500}{3}\ \text{nautical miles}.\]
The radius is $r=\dfrac{500}{3}\cdot\dfrac{1}{2\pi}=\dfrac{250}{3\pi}$, and the area is
\[\pi r^2=\pi\cdot\dfrac{62500}{9\pi^2}=\boxed{\dfrac{62500}{9\pi}}\approx2210\ \text{square nautical miles}.\]

question:
Let $A$, $B$, $C$ be real numbers, with $A$ and $B$ not both $0$, and consider the line $Ax+By+C=0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that if $A$, $B$, $C$ form an arithmetic progression (in this order), then the line passes through the point $(1,-2)$.
\item[(b)] Prove the converse: if the line passes through $(1,-2)$, then $A$, $B$, $C$ form an arithmetic progression.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The point $(1,-2)$ lies on the line exactly when
\[A\cdot1+B\cdot(-2)+C=0,\qquad\text{that is}\qquad A+C=2B .\]
And $A$, $B$, $C$ form an arithmetic progression exactly when $B-A=C-B$, which is the same condition $A+C=2B$.

\textbf{(a)} If $A,B,C$ is an arithmetic progression, then $A+C=2B$, so $A-2B+C=0$ and $(1,-2)$ is on the line.

\textbf{(b)} If $(1,-2)$ is on the line, then $A-2B+C=0$, so $B-A=C-B$ and $A,B,C$ is an arithmetic progression. $\blacksquare$

For example, $(A,B,C)=(1,1,1)$, $(1,2,3)$, $(2,1,0)$ and $(3,1,-1)$ give four lines through $(1,-2)$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pen[] pp={blue,red,heavygreen,orange};
real[][] L={{1,1,1},{1,2,3},{2,1,0},{3,1,-1}};
string[] nm={"$x+y+1=0$","$x+2y+3=0$","$2x+y=0$","$3x+y-1=0$"};
for(int i=0;i<4;++i){
  real a=L[i][0], bq=L[i][1], c=L[i][2];
  draw((-3,(-c+3a)/bq)--(4,(-c-4a)/bq),pp[i]);
}
clip(box((-2.5,-4.5),(3.6,2.6)));
draw((-2.5,0)--(3.6,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-4.5)--(0,2.6),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(3.6,0),S); label("$y$",(0,2.6),W);
for(int i=0;i<4;++i){
  real yy=2.2-0.6i;
  draw((0.8,yy)--(1.3,yy),pp[i]); label(nm[i],(1.3,yy),E,pp[i]+fontsize(8pt));
}
dot((1,-2)); label("$(1,-2)$",(1,-2),E,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

