\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] A triangle has one side twice as long as another side. Must it be a $30^\circ$-$60^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle?
\item[(b)] Prove that if a right triangle has its hypotenuse twice as long as one of its legs, then its angles are $30^\circ$, $60^\circ$ and $90^\circ$.
\item[(c)] Is a right triangle with one leg twice the other a $30^\circ$-$60^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} No. The triangle with sides $2$, $4$, $5$ exists ($2+4>5$) and has one side twice another, but it is not even a right triangle, since $2^2+4^2=20\ne25=5^2$. (Another example: the isosceles triangle with sides $1$, $2$, $2$.)

\textbf{(b)} Let the triangle be $ABC$ with the right angle at $C$, leg $BC=x$ and hypotenuse $AB=2x$. Reflect $B$ in the line $AC$ to $B'$. Since $BC\perp AC$, the point $B'$ lies on line $BC$ with $CB'=x$, so $BB'=2x$, and $AB'=AB=2x$ by the reflection.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(5,0), C=(3.7,sqrt(16-3.7^2));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
label("$5$",(A+B)/2,S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$4$",(A+C)/2,NW,fontsize(8pt)); label("$2$",(B+C)/2,NE,fontsize(8pt));
label("(a)",(2.5,-1.1));
pair C2=(8,0), B2=(8,1.6), A2=(8+1.6*sqrt(3),0), B3=(8,-1.6);
draw(A2--B2--C2--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A2--B3--C2,dashed);
draw((8,0.18)--(8.18,0.18)--(8.18,0));
label("$C$",C2,W); label("$B$",B2,N); label("$A$",A2,E); label("$B'$",B3,S);
label("$x$",(C2+B2)/2,W,fontsize(8pt)); label("$x$",(C2+B3)/2,W,fontsize(8pt));
label("$2x$",(A2+B2)/2,NE,fontsize(8pt)); label("$2x$",(A2+B3)/2,SE,fontsize(8pt));
label("(b)",(9.4,-2.4));
\end{asy}

So triangle $ABB'$ has all sides equal to $2x$: it is equilateral, and $\angle ABC=\angle ABB'=60^\circ$. Hence the angles of $ABC$ are $60^\circ$ at $B$, $90^\circ$ at $C$ and $180^\circ-90^\circ-60^\circ=30^\circ$ at $A$. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{(c)} No. With legs $1$ and $2$ the hypotenuse is $\sqrt5$, which is not twice a leg; in a $30^\circ$-$60^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle the legs are in the ratio $1:\sqrt3$, not $1:2$. What makes the special triangle is the hypotenuse being twice the shorter leg, as in (b).

Write the angles as $m-d$, $m$, $m+d$ with $d\ge0$. Their sum is $3m=180^\circ$, so the middle angle is $m=60^\circ$. The angle $100^\circ$ is not $m$, and it cannot be $m-d\le60^\circ$, so $m+d=100^\circ$ and $d=40^\circ$. The angles are $20^\circ$, $60^\circ$, $100^\circ$: the other two angles measure $\boxed{20^\circ\text{ and }60^\circ}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(5,0);
pair C=extension(A,A+dir(20),B,B+dir(120));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
label("$20^\circ$",A,10*dir(10),fontsize(8pt)); label("$60^\circ$",B,6*dir(150),fontsize(8pt)); label("$100^\circ$",C,7*dir(-90),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Place $A=(0,0)$, $B=(1,0)$, $C=\left(\dfrac{1}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$. The square $ABDE$ lies below $AB$, so $D=(1,-1)$ (the vertex next to $B$) and $E=(0,-1)$. The square $ACFG$ lies on the other side of $AC$ from $B$; turning $\overrightarrow{AC}$ through $90^\circ$ counterclockwise gives $G=\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2},\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$ (the vertex next to $A$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(1,0), C=(1/2,sqrt(3)/2);
pair D=(1,-1), Ee=(0,-1);
pair G=rotate(90)*(C-A)+A, Fq=G+(C-A);
fill(D--C--G--cycle,lightgray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--B--D--Ee--cycle); draw(A--C--Fq--G--cycle);
draw(D--C--G--cycle,blue);
label("$A$",A+0.13*dir(105)); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,SE); label("$E$",Ee,SW); label("$F$",Fq,N); label("$G$",G,W);
\end{asy}

By the shoelace formula, with $D=(1,-1)$, $C=\left(\dfrac{1}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$, $G=\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2},\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$:
\[[DCG]=\dfrac{1}{2}\left|1\left(\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}-\dfrac{1}{2}\right)+\dfrac{1}{2}\left(\dfrac{1}{2}+1\right)-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\left(-1-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)\right|=\dfrac{1}{2}\left(\sqrt3+1\right).\]
The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(D) } \dfrac{\sqrt3+1}{2}}$.

By Legendre's formula, $a_n=\left\lfloor\dfrac {n}{2}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\dfrac {n}{4}\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\dfrac {n}{8}\right\rfloor+\cdots$. So we add up $\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{100}\left\lfloor\dfrac{n}{2^i}\right\rfloor$ for each $i$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $i=1$: the values $0,1,1,2,2,\ldots,49,49,50$ add up to $2(1+2+\cdots+49)+50=2500$;
\item $i=2$: each of $1,\ldots,24$ occurs $4$ times and $25$ once: $4\cdot300+25=1225$;
\item $i=3$: each of $1,\ldots,11$ occurs $8$ times and $12$ five times: $8\cdot66+60=588$;
\item $i=4$: each of $1,\ldots,5$ occurs $16$ times and $6$ five times: $16\cdot15+30=270$;
\item $i=5$: $1$ occurs $32$ times, $2$ occurs $32$ times, $3$ five times: $32+64+15=111$;
\item $i=6$: $1$ occurs for $n=64,\ldots,100$: $37$.
\end{itemize}
The total is $2500+1225+588+270+111+37=4731$, answer $\boxed{\textbf{(B) } 4731}$.

(Check: $a_n=n-s_2(n)$, where $s_2(n)$ is the sum of the binary digits of $n$, and $\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{100}s_2(n)=319$, so the sum is $5050-319=4731$.)

Let $R$ be the radius. The segment from the centre to the midpoint of a chord is perpendicular to the chord, so triangle $OMC$ has a right angle at $M$, with $OM=1$ and $MC=\dfrac{CD}{2}=1$:
\[R^2=OC^2=OM^2+MC^2=2,\qquad R=\sqrt2 .\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=sqrt(2); pair O=(0,0), A=(-(1+R),0), B=(-R,0);
real th=degrees(asin(1/(1+R)));
pair u=dir(th); pair M=A+dot(O-A,u)*u; pair C=M-u, D=M+u;
real al=acos(R/(1+R)); pair Ee=R*dir(180+degrees(al));
draw(circle(O,R)); draw(A--O);
draw(A--D+0.4u); draw(A--Ee,red); draw(O--Ee,dashed); draw(O--M,dashed+blue);
dot(O); dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(M); dot(Ee);
label("$O$",O,SE); label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,N); label("$D$",D,NE); label("$M$",M,N); label("$E$",Ee,S);
label("$1$",(A+B)/2,S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$1$",(O+M)/2,E,blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$R$",(O+Ee)/2,E,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Since $B$ lies between $A$ and $O$, $AO=AB+BO=1+\sqrt2$. The tangent $AE$ is perpendicular to the radius $OE$, so
\[AE^2=AO^2-OE^2=\left(1+\sqrt2\right)^2-2=1+2\sqrt2 .\]
The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(A) } 2\sqrt2+1}$. (Such a configuration exists: the distance $OM=1$ from $O$ to the line $ACD$ is less than $AO$ and less than $R$.)

By the Pythagorean theorem $AD=\sqrt{5^2-4^2}=3$. Put $A=(0,0)$, $B=(4,0)$, $C=(4,3)$, $D=(0,3)$. Moving $2$ units from $B$ towards $D$ (that is, $\dfrac{2}{5}$ of the way),
\[M=B+\dfrac{2}{5}(D-B)=(4,0)+\dfrac{2}{5}(-4,3)=\left(\dfrac{12}{5},\dfrac{6}{5}\right).\]
Line $AM$ is $y=\dfrac {x}{2}$, which meets $BC$ ($x=4$) at $N=(4,2)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(4,0), C=(4,3), D=(0,3);
pair M=(12/5,6/5), N=(4,2);
fill(D--M--N--cycle,lightgray);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(B--D); draw(A--N);
draw(D--M--N--cycle,blue);
dot(M); dot(N);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,NW); label("$M$",M,S); label("$N$",N,E);
label("$4$",(A+B)/2,S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$3$",(A+D)/2,W,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

By the shoelace formula,
\[[DMN]=\dfrac{1}{2}\left|0\left(\dfrac{6}{5}-2\right)+\dfrac{12}{5}(2-3)+4\left(3-\dfrac{6}{5}\right)\right|=\dfrac{1}{2}\left(-\dfrac{12}{5}+\dfrac{36}{5}\right)=\dfrac{12}{5}.\]
The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(B) } \dfrac{12}{5}}$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove the \emph{remainder theorem}: when a polynomial $p(x)$ is divided by $x-a$, the remainder is $p(a)$. Deduce the \emph{factor theorem}: $x-a$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if and only if $p(a)=0$.
\item[(b)] Prove the \emph{rational root theorem}: if $p(x)=a_nx^n+a_{n-1}x^{n-1}+\cdots+a_1x+a_0$ has integer coefficients with $a_n\ne0$, and $\dfrac {r}{s}$ is a root written in lowest terms ($s>0$), then $r$ divides $a_0$ and $s$ divides $a_n$.
\item[(c)] Let $p(x)=3x^3-4x^2-5x+2$. Use (a) to find the remainder when $p(x)$ is divided by $x+2$, and use (a) and (b) to find all rational roots of $p$ and to factor it.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $\alpha=\angle PQZ=\angle PZQ$ and $x=\angle RQZ$, so $\angle PQR=\alpha+x$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Q=(0,0), R=(5,0);
pair P=extension(Q,Q+dir(82),R,R+dir(140));
pair Z=P+abs(Q-P)*unit(R-P);
draw(P--Q--R--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(Q--Z,blue);
label("$P$",P,N); label("$Q$",Q,SW); label("$R$",R,SE); label("$Z$",Z,NE);
real tz=degrees(Z-Q);
draw(arc(Q,0.9,0,tz),blue); draw(arc(Q,0.6,tz,82));
label("$\alpha$",Q+0.85*dir((tz+82)/2),fontsize(8pt));
label("$x$",Q+1.25*dir(tz/2),blue+fontsize(8pt));
pair bz=unit(unit(P-Z)+unit(Q-Z)); label("$\alpha$",Z+0.6*bz,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The angle $\angle PZQ$ is an exterior angle of triangle $QZR$, so it equals the sum of the two remote interior angles:
\[\alpha=x+\angle PRQ,\qquad\text{so}\qquad\angle PRQ=\alpha-x .\]
Then
\[42^\circ=\angle PQR-\angle PRQ=(\alpha+x)-(\alpha-x)=2x,\]
and $\angle RQZ=x=\boxed{21^\circ}$.

question:
A positive integer $n$ leaves remainder $3$ when divided by $19$ and remainder $7$ when divided by $53$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the remainder when $n$ is divided by $1007=19\cdot53$.
\item[(b)] Show that the remainder when $n$ is divided by $2014$ is not determined by this information, and find its two possible values.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{Reformulation.} Every block of $7$ consecutive days contains exactly one Sunday, so a run of $D$ consecutive days contains at least $\left\lfloor\dfrac {D}{7}\right\rfloor$ Sundays. Each month has at least $28$ days, hence at least $4$ Sundays. So the three months each have exactly $4$ Sundays if and only if the three months together contain exactly $12$ Sundays.

\textbf{Bound.} If the three months have $D\ge91=13\cdot7$ days, they contain at least $13$ Sundays. So $D\le90$.

\textbf{Which month triples are short enough.} The totals of three consecutive months are
\begin{itemize}
\item February--March--April: $89$ (common year) or $90$ (leap year);
\item January--February--March and December--January--February: $90$ (common year) or $91$ (leap year);
\item every other triple contains no February, so each of its months has at least $30$ days, and at most two of them have $30$ days (the $30$-day months April, June, September and November are never next to each other); so its total is at least $30+31+30=91$.
\end{itemize}
So $D$ is $89$ or $90$.

\textbf{Both happen.} $89=12\cdot7+5$: if February $1$ of a common year is a Monday (as in $2021$), the $89$ days are $12$ full weeks Monday--Sunday followed by Monday--Friday, so they contain $12$ Sundays; indeed February, March and April $2021$ each have $4$ Sundays. $90=12\cdot7+6$: if January $1$ of a common year is a Monday (as in $2018$), January--March contain $12$ Sundays, and January, February and March $2018$ each have $4$ Sundays.

The possible totals are $\boxed{89\text{ and }90}$.

Each five seconds the balloon moves along the same straight line through the anchor, by $\sqrt{7^2+24^2}=25$ ft. The cable becomes taut when the balloon is $2500$ ft from the anchor $O$, after $100$ steps, at the point $P$ that is $2400$ ft up and $700$ ft across.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair G=(0,0), Bl=(7,24), F=(7,0), Mp=(3.5,12);
draw(G--F,linewidth(0.9)); draw(G--Bl,linewidth(0.9)); draw(Bl--F,dashed);
draw(Mp--F,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((7,0)--(6.3,0)--(6.3,0.7)--(7,0.7));
dot(G); dot(Bl); dot(F); dot(Mp,blue);
label("anchor $O$",G,SW,fontsize(8pt)); label("balloon $P$",Bl,NE,fontsize(8pt)); label("$Q$",F,SE); label("$M$",Mp,W,blue);
label("$2400$",(7,12),E,fontsize(8pt)); label("$700$",(3.5,0),S,fontsize(8pt));
label("$2500$",(2.0,7.5),NW,fontsize(8pt)); label("$1250$",Mp+0.7*(F-Mp),W,blue+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Let $Q$ be the point on the ground below $P$. Triangle $OQP$ has a right angle at $Q$, and the cable $OP=2500$ is its hypotenuse. The midpoint $M$ of the hypotenuse of a right triangle is equidistant from all three vertices (it is the centre of the circle through them, since $OP$ is a diameter), so
\[MQ=\dfrac{OP}{2}=\boxed{1250\text{ ft}}.\]
(With coordinates $M=(350,1200)$ and $Q=(700,0)$: $MQ=\sqrt{350^2+1200^2}=\sqrt{1562500}=1250$.)

