For every positive integer $n$: the largest integer dividing $n^3-n$ for all $n$ is $6$; for $n^5-n$ it is $30$; for $n^7-n$ it is $42$; and for $n^9-n$ it is $30$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the largest integer that divides $n^{11}-n$ for all positive integers $n$.
\item[(b)] Find the largest integer that divides $n^{13}-n$ for all positive integers $n$.
\item[(c)] Let $k\ge3$ be odd. Prove that the largest integer dividing $n^k-n$ for all positive integers $n$ is the product of all primes $p$ with $(p-1)\mid(k-1)$. (You may use the fact that every prime $p$ has a primitive root, that is, an integer $g$ whose powers $g,g^2,\ldots,g^{p-1}$ are all different modulo $p$.)
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Use the prime factorisation $36=2^2\cdot3^2$ to find a divisibility test for $36$, and use it to decide whether $7\,416$ and $5\,724$ are divisible by $36$.
\item[(b)] A student says: ``$36=3\cdot12$, so a number is divisible by $36$ exactly when it is divisible by $3$ and by $12$.'' Give a counterexample and explain what goes wrong.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Since $36=4\cdot9$ and $\gcd(4,9)=1$, a number $N$ is divisible by $36$ exactly when it is divisible by both $4$ and $9$: if $4\mid N$ and $9\mid N$, then the prime powers $2^2$ and $3^2$ both appear in $N$, so $2^2\cdot3^2\mid N$. The tests for $4$ and $9$ give:
\begin{center}
$36\mid N$ $\iff$ the number formed by the last two digits of $N$ is divisible by $4$ \emph{and} the digit sum of $N$ is divisible by $9$.
\end{center}
For $7\,416$: $16$ is divisible by $4$ and $7+4+1+6=18$ is divisible by $9$, so $\boxed{36\mid7\,416}$ (indeed $7\,416=36\cdot206$). For $5\,724$: $24$ is divisible by $4$ and $5+7+2+4=18$, so $\boxed{36\mid5\,724}$ too ($5\,724=36\cdot159$).

\textbf{(b)} $N=12$ is divisible by $3$ and by $12$ but not by $36$. The problem is that $3$ and $12$ share the factor $3$: divisibility by $3$ and by $12$ only guarantees divisibility by $\operatorname{lcm}(3,12)=12$. The split must use coprime factors, i.e. whole prime powers, as in $36=2^2\cdot3^2$.

Let each of the arcs $AB$, $BC$, $CD$, $DE$ of the first circle measure $2\alpha$, and let each of the five equal arcs $EF$, $FG$, $GH$, $HI$, $IA$ of the second circle measure $\beta$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P(real deg){return dir(deg);}
pair A=P(110),B=P(90),C=P(70),D=P(50),pE=P(30);
draw(arc((0,0),1,15,125),gray);
real r=abs(A-C); real tE=degrees(pE-C), tA=degrees(A-C);
real dt=tA-tE; if(dt>180) dt-=360; if(dt<-180) dt+=360;
pair[] Q; for(int k=0;k<=5;++k) Q.push(C+r*dir(tE+dt*k/5));
draw(arc(C,r,tE,tE+dt),blue);
pair F=Q[1],G=Q[2],H=Q[3],I=Q[4];
draw(A--B--D,red); draw(A--H--G,deepgreen); draw(B--A--G,heavymagenta+linewidth(0.9));
draw(C--A,dashed); draw(C--G,dashed); draw(C--pE,dashed);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(pE);dot(F);dot(G);dot(H);dot(I);
label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NE);label("$E$",pE,E);
label("$F$",F,S);label("$G$",G,S);label("$H$",H,S);label("$I$",I,SW);
\end{asy}

\textbf{$\angle ABD$.} It is an inscribed angle subtending the arc $AD$ that does not contain $B$, which measures $360^\circ-6\alpha$. So $\angle ABD=180^\circ-3\alpha$.

\textbf{Relating $\alpha$ and $\beta$.} In the first circle, $\angle ACE$ subtends the arc $AE$ not containing $C$, of measure $360^\circ-8\alpha$, so $\angle ACE=180^\circ-4\alpha$. In the second circle this is a central angle, so $5\beta=180^\circ-4\alpha$.

\textbf{$\angle AHG$.} The arc $AG$ of the second circle containing $H$ consists of three of the small arcs ($AI,IH,HG$), so the arc not containing $H$ measures $360^\circ-3\beta$ and $\angle AHG=180^\circ-\dfrac{3\beta}{2}$.

\textbf{The condition.} $\left(180^\circ-3\alpha\right)-\left(180^\circ-\dfrac{3\beta}{2}\right)=12^\circ$ gives $\dfrac{3\beta}{2}-3\alpha=12^\circ$. Substituting $\beta=\dfrac{180^\circ-4\alpha}{5}$:
\[54^\circ-\dfrac{6\alpha}{5}-3\alpha=12^\circ\implies\alpha=10^\circ,\quad\beta=28^\circ .\]

\textbf{$\angle BAG$.} Triangle $CAG$ is isosceles ($CA=CG$) with apex angle $\angle ACG=3\beta=84^\circ$, so $\angle CAG=48^\circ$. Also $\angle BAC$ is inscribed in the first circle on the arc $BC$ of measure $2\alpha=20^\circ$, so $\angle BAC=10^\circ$. Since $B$ and $G$ lie on opposite sides of line $AC$ (the small arc of the second circle lies inside the first circle),
\[\angle BAG=\angle BAC+\angle CAG=10^\circ+48^\circ=\boxed{58^\circ}.\]

question:
A student finds a shortcut for $(10a+1)^2-(10a)^2$, where $a$ is a positive integer: ``take the number $a$ (all digits of $10a+1$ except the units digit), double it, and write a $1$ after it.'' For example, $41^2-40^2=81$ ($4\cdot2=8$, then append $1$) and $131^2-130^2=261$ ($13\cdot2=26$, then append $1$).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that the shortcut always works.
\item[(b)] Explain why checking a few examples does not prove it, and find the analogous shortcut for $(10a+6)^2-(10a+5)^2$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $P$ be the midpoint of the diagonal $BD$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(3,0); real L=2.2;
pair A=B+L*dir(150), D=C+L*dir(90);
pair M=(B+C)/2, Nn=(A+D)/2, P=(B+D)/2;
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--D,gray+dashed); draw(M--P--Nn,blue); draw(M--Nn,red+linewidth(1));
draw(C+(-0.2,0)--C+(-0.2,0.2)--C+(0,0.2));
dot(M);dot(Nn);dot(P);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,NE);
label("$M$",M,S);label("$N$",Nn,N);label("$P$",P,E);
label("$150^\circ$",B+0.5*dir(108),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

In triangle $BCD$, $MP$ joins the midpoints of $BC$ and $BD$, so $MP\parallel CD$ and $MP=\dfrac{1}{2}CD$. In triangle $DAB$, $PN$ joins the midpoints of $DB$ and $DA$, so $PN\parallel BA$ and $PN=\dfrac{1}{2}AB$. Since $AB=CD$, triangle $MPN$ is isosceles with $PM=PN$.

Direct $BC$ to the right. Then the ray $PM$ points straight down (opposite to $\overrightarrow{CD}$, which is perpendicular to $BC$), and the ray $PN$ has the direction of $\overrightarrow{BA}$, which makes $150^\circ$ with $\overrightarrow{BC}$. The direction straight down makes $270^\circ$, so $\angle MPN=270^\circ-150^\circ=120^\circ$, and the base angles of the isosceles triangle are $\angle PMN=\angle PNM=30^\circ$.

As $MP\perp BC$, the line $MN$ makes an angle of $90^\circ-30^\circ=\boxed{60^\circ}$ with $BC$.

(Without $AB=CD$ the angle is not determined: for $AB=2$, $BC=1$, $CD=\dfrac{1}{2}$ the acute angle is about $40.9^\circ$.)

