Then $x^2+h^2=81$ and $(10-x)^2+h^2=169$. Subtracting, $100-20x=88$, so $x=\dfrac{3}{5}$ and $h^2=81-\dfrac{9}{25}=\dfrac{2016}{25}$. Since $BD=5$,
\[AD^2=(5-x)^2+h^2=\dfrac{484}{25}+\dfrac{2016}{25}=\dfrac{2500}{25}=100,\]
so $AD=\boxed{10}$.

(Equivalently, by Apollonius' theorem $AB^2+AC^2=2AD^2+2BD^2$: $81+169=2AD^2+50$, so $AD^2=100$.)

