An \emph{arithmetico-geometric series} is a series whose terms are the products of the terms of an arithmetic sequence and a geometric sequence, $\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\bigl(a+(k-1)d\bigr)r^{k-1}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find $S=1+\dfrac{2}{2}+\dfrac{3}{4}+\dfrac{4}{8}+\dfrac{5}{16}+\cdots$.
\item[(b)] Show that for $|r|<1$,
\[\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\bigl(a+(k-1)d\bigr)r^{k-1}=\dfrac{a}{1-r}+\dfrac{dr}{(1-r)^2}.\]
