label("$s$",(O+V[0])/2,SE,red);
\end{asy}

The height of an equilateral triangle of side $s$ is $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s$, so $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s=1$ and $s=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3}$. The area of the hexagon is
\[6\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2=6\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot\dfrac{4}{3}=\boxed{2\sqrt3}.\]
The student is right. The key's $\dfrac{27\sqrt3}{2}$ is the area of a regular hexagon of side $3$ (inradius $\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}$), so it does not belong to this problem.

The three midsegments of a triangle cut it into four congruent triangles, each similar to the original with ratio $\dfrac{1}{2}$ (each midsegment is parallel to a side and half as long). So the medial triangle has $\dfrac{1}{4}$ of the area.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(8,0),C=(2.4,6);
path t(pair a,pair b,pair c){return a--b--c--cycle;}
pair a=A,bb=B,c=C;
draw(t(a,bb,c),linewidth(0.9));
pen[] col={blue,red,deepgreen,heavymagenta};
for(int k=0;k<4;++k){pair a2=(bb+c)/2,b2=(c+a)/2,c2=(a+bb)/2; draw(t(a2,b2,c2),col[k]); a=a2;bb=b2;c=c2;}
fill(t(a,bb,c),heavymagenta+opacity(0.4));
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);
\end{asy}

Repeating four times,
\[\dfrac{[MON]}{[ABC]}=\left(\dfrac{1}{4}\right)^4=\boxed{\dfrac{1}{256}}.\]

The angles of a quadrilateral add up to $360^\circ$, so
\[\angle DAB+\angle ADC=360^\circ-140^\circ-110^\circ=110^\circ .\]
$O$ lies on the bisectors of the angles at $A$ and $D$, so in triangle $AOD$
\[\angle AOD=180^\circ-\dfrac{1}{2}\left(\angle DAB+\angle ADC\right)=180^\circ-55^\circ=\boxed{125^\circ}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0);
pair B=2.6*dir(70); pair C=B+3*dir(30); real q=C.y/Sin(40); pair D=C+q*dir(-40);
pair O=extension(A,A+dir(35),D,D+dir(160));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--O,blue); draw(D--O,blue);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,N);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,NW);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,SE);
label("$140^\circ$",B+0.55*dir(320),fontsize(8pt)); label("$110^\circ$",C+0.6*dir(265),fontsize(8pt));
label("$35^\circ$",A+1.1*dir(17.5),blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$20^\circ$",D+1.5*dir(170),blue+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

(The sketch uses $\angle DAB=70^\circ$ and $\angle ADC=40^\circ$; only their sum matters.)

\textbf{Why the circle is impossible.} If $ABCD$ were inscribed in a circle with centre $O$, then $OA=OD$, so $\angle DAO=\angle ADO$, and the given conditions would force $\angle DAB=\angle ADC$. But a cyclic quadrilateral has $\angle DAB=180^\circ-\angle BCD=70^\circ$ and $\angle ADC=180^\circ-\angle ABC=40^\circ$, which are different. (The given conditions say that $O$ lies on two angle bisectors, as for the centre of a circle \emph{inscribed} in $ABCD$.)

Let $A_1A_2\ldots A_n$ be a regular polygon with side $s$ and interior angle $\theta$. Let $O$ be the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of $A_1A_2$ and $A_2A_3$ (they meet, because the adjacent sides $A_1A_2$ and $A_2A_3$ are not parallel). Then $OA_1=OA_2=OA_3$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int n=7; pair[] V; for(int k=0;k<n;++k) V.push(dir(90+360*k/n));
for(int k=0;k<n;++k) draw(V[k]--V[(k+1)%n],linewidth(0.9));
draw(unitcircle,blue+dashed);
pair O=(0,0);
for(int k=0;k<4;++k) draw(O--V[k],red);
pair m1=(V[0]+V[1])/2, m2=(V[1]+V[2])/2;
draw(O--m1,gray+dashed); draw(O--m2,gray+dashed);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,SE);
label("$A_1$",V[0],N); label("$A_2$",V[1],NW); label("$A_3$",V[2],W); label("$A_4$",V[3],SW);
\end{asy}

Triangles $OA_1A_2$ and $OA_3A_2$ are congruent (SSS: $OA_1=OA_3$, $OA_2$ common, $A_1A_2=A_2A_3=s$), so $OA_2$ bisects the angle at $A_2$: $\angle OA_2A_3=\dfrac{\theta}{2}$. Triangle $OA_2A_3$ is isosceles, so also $\angle OA_3A_2=\dfrac{\theta}{2}$, and therefore
\[\angle OA_3A_4=\theta-\dfrac{\theta}{2}=\dfrac{\theta}{2}=\angle OA_3A_2 .\]
Now triangles $OA_3A_2$ and $OA_3A_4$ are congruent (SAS: $OA_3$ common, $A_3A_2=A_3A_4=s$, equal included angles), so $OA_4=OA_2$. The same argument, applied at $A_4$, $A_5$, \ldots, shows step by step that $OA_5=OA_6=\cdots=OA_n=OA_2$. Hence all vertices are at the same distance from $O$, and they lie on the circle with centre $O$ and radius $OA_1$. $\blacksquare$

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $\dfrac{x^2+898}{x+1}=(x-1)+\dfrac{899}{x+1}$ for $x\ne-1$.
\item[(b)] For which positive integers $x$ is $\dfrac{x^2+898}{x+1}$ an integer?
\end{enumerate}

In a triangle $PQR$ that is not right-angled, let $p,q,r$ be the lengths of the sides opposite $P,Q,R$. The median from $R$ meets $PQ$ at $S$, the altitude from $P$ meets $QR$ at $E$, and $RS$ and $PE$ meet at $O$. Given $p=\sqrt3$, $q=1$ and circumradius $1$, find
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] the length $RS$;
\item[(b)] the area of $\triangle SOE$;
\item[(c)] the length $OE$;
\item[(d)] the inradius of $\triangle PQR$;
\end{enumerate}
and the sum of these four quantities.

solution:
\textbf{The triangle.} By the law of sines, $\sin P=\dfrac{p}{2\cdot1}=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$ and $\sin Q=\dfrac {q}{2}=\dfrac{1}{2}$, so $P\in\{60^\circ,120^\circ\}$ and $Q\in\{30^\circ,150^\circ\}$. Since $P+Q<180^\circ$, the options are $(60^\circ,30^\circ)$, which gives $R=90^\circ$ (excluded), and $(120^\circ,30^\circ)$. Hence $P=120^\circ$, $Q=R=30^\circ$ and $r=PQ=q=1$.

\textbf{Coordinates.} Put $P=(0,0)$, $Q=(1,0)$, $R=\left(-\dfrac{1}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$ (so $PR=1$, $\angle P=120^\circ$, $QR=\sqrt3$). Then $S=\left(\dfrac{1}{2},0\right)$. Line $QR$ has direction $\left(-\dfrac{3}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$, i.e. slope $-\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}$, so the altitude from $P$ is $y=\sqrt3x$ and meets $QR$ at $E=\left(\dfrac{1}{4},\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\right)$ (the midpoint of $QR$, as $PQ=PR$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(0,0), Q=(1,0), R=(-0.5,sqrt(3)/2);
pair Sm=(P+Q)/2; pair Ef=(0.25,sqrt(3)/4); pair O=(1/6,sqrt(3)/6);
draw(P--Q--R--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(R--Sm,blue); draw(P--Ef,red);
fill(Sm--O--Ef--cycle,lightgray); draw(Sm--O--Ef--cycle,gray);
real m=0.05; pair u=unit(R-Q), v=unit(P-Ef);
draw(Ef+m*u--Ef+m*u+m*v--Ef+m*v);
dot(Sm);dot(Ef);dot(O);
label("$P$",P,SW);label("$Q$",Q,SE);label("$R$",R,N);
label("$S$",Sm,S);label("$E$",Ef,NE);label("$O$",O,W);
label("$1$",(P+R)/2,W);label("$\sqrt3$",R+0.75*(Q-R),NE);draw(arc(P,0.07,0,120));label("$120^\circ$",P+0.15*dir(100),fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

Line $RS$: from $S=\left(\dfrac{1}{2},0\right)$ in direction $R-S=\left(-1,\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$, points $\left(\dfrac{1}{2}-t,\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}t\right)$. On $y=\sqrt3x$: $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}t=\sqrt3\left(\dfrac{1}{2}-t\right)$, so $t=\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $O=\left(\dfrac{1}{6},\dfrac{\sqrt3}{6}\right)$.

\textbf{(a)} $RS=\sqrt{1+\dfrac{3}{4}}=\dfrac{\sqrt7}{2}$.

\textbf{(b)} By the shoelace formula with $S\left(\dfrac{1}{2},0\right)$, $O\left(\dfrac{1}{6},\dfrac{\sqrt3}{6}\right)$, $E\left(\dfrac{1}{4},\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\right)$:
\[[SOE]=\dfrac{1}{2}\left|\left(\dfrac{1}{6}-\dfrac{1}{2}\right)\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}-\left(\dfrac{1}{4}-\dfrac{1}{2}\right)\dfrac{\sqrt3}{6}\right|=\dfrac{1}{2}\left|-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{12}+\dfrac{\sqrt3}{24}\right|=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{48}.\]

\textbf{(c)} $OE=\sqrt{\left(\dfrac{1}{12}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{\sqrt3}{12}\right)^2}=\dfrac{2}{12}=\dfrac{1}{6}$.

\textbf{(d)} The area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot1\cdot1\cdot\sin120^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}$ and the semiperimeter is $\dfrac{2+\sqrt3}{2}$, so the inradius is
\[\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot\dfrac{2}{2+\sqrt3}=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2(2+\sqrt3)}=\dfrac{\sqrt3(2-\sqrt3)}{2}=\sqrt3-\dfrac{3}{2} .\]

\textbf{Sum.}
\[\dfrac{\sqrt7}{2}+\dfrac{\sqrt3}{48}+\dfrac{1}{6}+\sqrt3-\dfrac{3}{2}=\boxed{\dfrac{24\sqrt7+49\sqrt3-64}{48}}\approx1.758 .\]

The diagonals of a rhombus bisect each other at right angles, so each side is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs $7$ and $24$: the side is $25$. The inscribed circle is centred at the intersection $O$ of the diagonals, and its radius $r$ is the distance from $O$ to a side, i.e. the altitude to the hypotenuse of the $7$-$24$-$25$ triangle:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-24,0),B=(0,7),C=(24,0),D=(0,-7),O=(0,0);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--C,gray+dashed); draw(B--D,gray+dashed);
real r=168/25; draw(circle(O,r),blue);
pair T=(B-A)*dot(-A,B-A)/dot(B-A,B-A)+A;
draw(O--T,red); dot(O); dot(T);
real m=1.0; pair u=unit(B-T), v=unit(O-T); draw(T+m*u--T+m*u+m*v--T+m*v);
label("$O$",O,SE); label("$r$",(O+T)/2,W,red);
label("$24$",(A+O)/2,S); label("$7$",(O+B)/2,E); label("$25$",(A+B)/2,NW);
\end{asy}

\[r=\dfrac{7\cdot24}{25}=\dfrac{168}{25}.\]
(Equivalently, the area $\dfrac{14\cdot48}{2}=336$ equals $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\text{perimeter}\cdot r=50r$.) Then
\[\pi r^2+2\pi r=\pi\cdot\dfrac{28224}{625}+\pi\cdot\dfrac{336}{25}=\dfrac{28224\pi+8400\pi}{625}=\boxed{\dfrac{36624\pi}{625}}.\]

question:
For a positive integer $n$, let $\sigma(n)$ be the sum of all positive divisors of $n$. Call $n$ \emph{perfect} if $\sigma(n)=2n$, \emph{abundant} if $\sigma(n)>2n$ and \emph{deficient} if $\sigma(n)<2n$. There is no known formula listing all numbers of each kind, but each kind has simple infinite families.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that if $2^p-1$ is prime, then $2^{p-1}\left(2^p-1\right)$ is perfect.
\item[(b)] Prove that every prime power $q^k$ ($q$ prime, $k\ge1$) is deficient.
\item[(c)] Prove that every multiple $kn$ ($k\ge2$) of a perfect number $n$ is abundant, and deduce that $12,18,24,30,\ldots$ are abundant.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
If $n=p_1^{e_1}\cdots p_k^{e_k}$, the number of divisors is $(e_1+1)\cdots(e_k+1)=24$. Writing $24$ as a product of factors greater than $1$ gives the exponent patterns
\[(23),\quad(11,1),\quad(7,2),\quad(5,3),\quad(5,1,1),\quad(3,2,1),\quad(2,1,1,1).\]
All prime factors must be at least $11$. For each pattern the smallest number uses the primes $11,13,17,19$ with the largest exponent on the smallest prime:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
$11^2\cdot13\cdot17\cdot19=508\,079$ & $11^3\cdot13^2\cdot17=3\,823\,963$\\
$11^5\cdot13\cdot17=35\,592\,271$ & $11^5\cdot13^3=353\,829\,047$\\
$11^7\cdot13^2\approx3.3\cdot10^{9}$ & $11^{11}\cdot13\approx3.7\cdot10^{12}$,\quad $11^{23}\approx9\cdot10^{23}$.
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The smallest is
\[\boxed{508\,079=11^2\cdot13\cdot17\cdot19}.\]

In an equilateral triangle $ABC$ the circumcentre is also the centroid and the incentre, and $OM$ is perpendicular to the side $AB$. In the right triangle $AMO$, $AM=\dfrac{15}{2}$ and $\angle OAM=30^\circ$ ($AO$ bisects the $60^\circ$ angle at $A$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(15,0),C=(7.5,15*sqrt(3)/2); pair O=(A+B+C)/3, M=(A+B)/2;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(circle(O,abs(O-A)),blue+dashed);
draw(O--A,red); draw(O--M,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(M+(0.6,0)--M+(0.6,0.6)--M+(0,0.6));
dot(O);dot(M); label("$O$",O,NE); label("$M$",M,S);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);
label("$30^\circ$",A+2.6*dir(15),fontsize(8pt));
label("$\dfrac{15}{2}$",(A+M)/2,S);
\end{asy}

Hence
\[OM=AM\tan30^\circ=\dfrac{15}{2}\cdot\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}=\boxed{\dfrac{5\sqrt3}{2}\ \text{cm}}.\]
(Equivalently, $OM$ is one third of the altitude $\dfrac{15\sqrt3}{2}$.)

Let the quadrilateral be $ABCD$ with diagonal $AC=l$ and sides $AB=a$, $BC=b$, $CD=c$, $DA=d$, so $a+b+c+d=10$. The diagonal splits the quadrilateral into triangles $ABC$ and $ACD$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(2.2,-1.3),C=(4.4,0),D=(2.2,1.3);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--C,red+linewidth(0.9));
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,N);
label("$l$",(A+C)/2,N,red);label("$a$",(A+B)/2,SW);label("$b$",(B+C)/2,SE);label("$c$",(C+D)/2,NE);label("$d$",(D+A)/2,NW);
\end{asy}

By the triangle inequality, $l<a+b$ and $l<c+d$; adding, $2l<10$, so $l\le4$.

Every $l\in\{1,2,3,4\}$ occurs: a rhombus with side $\dfrac{5}{2}$ (perimeter $10$) can have a diagonal of any length $l<5$, the other diagonal being $2\sqrt{\dfrac{25}{4}-\dfrac{l^2}{4}}$. Hence the sum of the possible values is $1+2+3+4=\boxed{10}$.

\textbf{The configuration.} A side of the rectangle containing two vertices of the hexagon contains the segment joining them, and since the hexagon lies inside the rectangle, this segment must be a side of the hexagon. With six vertices on four sides, at least two sides of the rectangle contain two vertices each, hence contain hexagon sides. Two sides of a regular hexagon are never perpendicular (their directions differ by multiples of $60^\circ$), so these rectangle sides are opposite, and they contain two opposite sides of the hexagon. The other two vertices lie on the other two sides of the rectangle.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=2; pair[] V; for(int k=0;k<6;++k) V.push(s*dir(60*k));
draw((-s,-s*sqrt(3)/2)--(s,-s*sqrt(3)/2)--(s,s*sqrt(3)/2)--(-s,s*sqrt(3)/2)--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(V[0]--V[3],gray+dashed); draw(V[1]--V[5],red);
for(int k=0;k<6;++k) dot(V[k]);
label("$2s$",(-0.6,0),N,gray); label("$s\sqrt3$",(V[1]+V[5])/2+(0,0.55),W,red);
label("$s$",(V[1]+V[2])/2,N);
\end{asy}

\textbf{The area.} If the hexagon has side $s$, the rectangle has width $2s$ (the long diagonal) and height $s\sqrt3$ (the distance between opposite sides), so its area is $2\sqrt3\,s^2$. The hexagon consists of six equilateral triangles of side $s$, so its area is $\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}s^2$. Hence
\[\dfrac{[\text{rectangle}]}{[\text{hexagon}]}=\dfrac{2\sqrt3\,s^2}{\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}s^2}=\dfrac{4}{3},\qquad[\text{rectangle}]=\dfrac{4}{3}\cdot24=\boxed{32}.\]

