A regular octahedron has $8$ triangular faces:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair T=(0,1.6), Bt=(0,-1.6), p1=(-1.4,-0.15), p2=(0.6,-0.45), p3=(1.4,0.15), p4=(-0.6,0.45);
draw(T--p1--Bt--p2--T--p3--Bt,linewidth(0.8)); draw(p1--p2--p3,linewidth(0.8));
draw(p3--p4--p1,dashed); draw(T--p4--Bt,dashed);
dot(T);dot(Bt);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Rotations.} A rotation of the octahedron is determined by where it sends one face ($8$ choices) and how that face is turned onto its image ($3$ choices), so there are $8\cdot3=24$ rotations.

\textbf{Counting.} There are $8!=40\,320$ ways to paint the faces with all colours different. A rotation other than the identity moves some face to a different face, so it changes such a colouring (the colours are all different). Hence the $24$ rotations of a colouring give $24$ different colourings, and the $40\,320$ colourings fall into groups of $24$ that count as the same. The number of distinguishable colourings is
\[\dfrac{8!}{24}=\boxed{1680}.\]

Since $8^2+15^2=64+225=289=17^2$, triangles $ABD$ and $ACD$ are right-angled at $A$ (converse of Pythagoras). So $AD\perp AB$ and $AD\perp AC$, and $AD$ is perpendicular to the plane $ABC$: it is the height of the tetrahedron over the base $ABC$.

\begin{asy}
