In the isosceles base triangle, the median $AM$ to $BC$ is an altitude, with $AM=\sqrt{15^2-9^2}=12$, so $[ABC]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot18\cdot12=108$. Therefore
\[V=\dfrac{1}{3}\cdot108\cdot8=\boxed{288}\ \text{cm}^3.\]

question:
An integer $m$ is a \emph{multiple} of an integer $n$ if $m=nk$ for some integer $k$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Is $0$ a multiple of every integer $n$?
\item[(b)] Which integers are multiples of $0$?
\item[(c)] How many multiples of $7$ lie in the range $-20\le m\le20$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{The sum is a single binomial coefficient.} Since $\dbinom{15}{k}=\dbinom{15}{15-k}$, the sum equals
\[\sum_{k=0}^{15}\dbinom{15}{k}\dbinom{15}{15-k}.\]
This counts the ways to choose $15$ people from $15$ boys and $15$ girls: choose $k$ boys and $15-k$ girls and add over $k$. Hence the sum is $\dbinom{30}{15}$ (Vandermonde's identity).

\textbf{Prime factorisation.} The exponent of a prime $p$ in $\dbinom{30}{15}=\dfrac{30!}{15!\,15!}$ is (exponent in $30!$) minus twice (exponent in $15!$):
\begin{itemize}
\item $p=2$: $(15+7+3+1)-2(7+3+1)=26-22=4$;
\item $p=3$: $(10+3+1)-2(5+1)=14-12=2$;
\item $p=5$: $(6+1)-2\cdot3=1$;
\item $p=7,11,13$: $4-2\cdot2=0$, $2-2\cdot1=0$, $2-2\cdot1=0$;
\item $p=17,19,23,29$: $1-0=1$ each.
\end{itemize}
So
\[\dbinom{30}{15}=2^4\cdot3^2\cdot5\cdot17\cdot19\cdot23\cdot29=720\cdot(17\cdot19\cdot23\cdot29).\]

\textbf{The last two digits.} Write $M=17\cdot19\cdot23\cdot29$. Modulo $5$, $M\equiv2\cdot4\cdot3\cdot4=96\equiv1$, so $M=5j+1$ for an integer $j$. Then
\[720M=720\cdot5j+720=3600j+720\equiv720\equiv20\pmod{100}.\]
The remainder is $\boxed{20}$. (Indeed $\dbinom{30}{15}=155117520$.)

question:
Let $p$ be a prime other than $2$ and $5$, so that the decimal expansion of $\dfrac{1}{p}$ is repeating.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the repeating block of $\dfrac{1}{p}$ has at most $p-1$ digits.
\item[(b)] A student claims: ``There are infinitely many primes, so there is no largest possible length of the repeating block.'' The conclusion is true, but the reason given is not enough. Explain why, and give a complete proof that the length of the repeating block of $\dfrac{1}{p}$ can be as large as we like.
\end{enumerate}

In triangle $ABC$ with $AB\ne AC$, the bisector of the exterior angle at $A$ meets the line $BC$ at $X$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $X$ lies outside the segment $BC$ and that $\dfrac{BX}{XC}=\dfrac{AB}{AC}$. With directed segments along line $BC$ this reads $\dfrac{BX}{XC}=-\dfrac{AB}{AC}$, which is why the exterior bisector is said to divide $BC$ \emph{externally} in the ratio $AB:AC$.
\item[(b)] What happens when $AB=AC$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Suppose $AB>AC$ (the other case is the same with $B$ and $C$ swapped). Let $P$ be a point on ray $BA$ beyond $A$; the exterior angle at $A$ is $\angle PAC$, and $AX$ bisects it.

Let $E$ be the point on segment $AB$ with $AE=AC$ (it exists because $AC<AB$). Then triangle $AEC$ is isosceles, so $\angle AEC=\angle ACE$. The exterior angle of this triangle at $A$ is $\angle PAC=\angle AEC+\angle ACE=2\angle AEC$, so
\[\angle PAX=\dfrac{1}{2}\angle PAC=\angle AEC.\]
These are corresponding angles for the lines $AX$ and $EC$ with transversal $BP$, so $EC\parallel AX$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(3,0), A=(3.5,1.9);
pair d=unit(A-B)+unit(C-A);
pair X=extension(A,A+d,B,C);
pair Ept=A+(B-A)*abs(C-A)/abs(B-A);
pair P=A+0.9*unit(A-B);
draw(B--X,gray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(B--P);
draw(A--X,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(C--Ept,red+dashed);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(X,blue);dot(Ept,red);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,S);label("$X$",X,S,blue);label("$E$",Ept,NW,red);label("$P$",P,N);
real tP=angle(P-A)*180/pi, tD=angle(d)*180/pi, tC=angle(C-A)*180/pi;
draw(arc(A,0.4,tD,tP),blue);
draw(arc(A,0.48,tC,tD),blue);
\end{asy}

Because $E$ lies between $B$ and $A$ and $EC\parallel AX$, the line $AX$ meets line $BC$ on the far side of $C$ from $B$: the triangles $BEC$ and $BAX$ are similar (parallel lines), with ratio $\dfrac{BA}{BE}>1$, so $X$ lies on ray $BC$ beyond $C$. In particular $X$ is outside segment $BC$, and
\[\dfrac{BX}{BC}=\dfrac{BA}{BE}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\dfrac{XC}{BC}=\dfrac{BX-BC}{BC}=\dfrac{BA-BE}{BE}=\dfrac{AE}{BE},\]
hence
\[\dfrac{BX}{XC}=\dfrac{BA}{BE}\cdot\dfrac{BE}{AE}=\dfrac{AB}{AE}=\dfrac{AB}{AC}.\]
Since $X$ is outside segment $BC$, the directed segments $BX$ and $XC$ point in opposite directions, so as a directed ratio $\dfrac{BX}{XC}=-\dfrac{AB}{AC}$. (For an internal division the ratio is positive; that is the internal angle bisector theorem.)

\textbf{(b)} If $AB=AC$, then $\angle B=\angle C$ and the exterior angle at $A$ equals $\angle B+\angle C=2\angle B$, so the exterior bisector makes the angle $\angle B$ with ray $AP$; it is parallel to $BC$ and \textbf{does not meet line $BC$ at all}. This matches the formula: the ratio $-1$ would require $BX=-XC$, i.e. $B=C$, which is impossible.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find all positive integers $n$ such that $n^2+10$ is a perfect square.
\item[(b)] Find all positive integers $n$ such that $n^2+56$ is a perfect square.
\item[(c)] Find all positive integers $n$ such that $n^2+45$ is a perfect square.
\item[(d)] For a general positive integer $x$, explain how the positive integers $n$ with $n^2+x$ a perfect square correspond to the factorisations of $x$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $f(x)=x^2$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] When we draw the graph of $y=f(x+1)$, is the horizontal coordinate of a point on the graph the input $x$ or the value $x+1$? Explain.
\item[(b)] Show that a point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$ if and only if the point $(a-1,b)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x+1)$. What does this say about the two graphs?
\item[(c)] Find the vertex of the graph of $y=f(x+1)$ and the points where this graph meets the line $y=4$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The graph of $y=f(x+1)$ is the set of points $(x,y)$ with $y=f(x+1)$. The horizontal coordinate is the variable $x$ itself; the number $x+1$ is only what we feed into $f$ to compute the height. For example, at $x=2$ the point is $(2,f(3))=(2,9)$, not $(3,9)$.

\textbf{(b)} The point $(a-1,b)$ is on the graph of $y=f(x+1)$ exactly when $b=f\big((a-1)+1\big)=f(a)$, which is exactly the condition for $(a,b)$ to be on the graph of $y=f(x)$. So every point of the graph of $y=f(x)$ moves $1$ unit to the \textbf{left} to give the graph of $y=f(x+1)$: the new graph is the old one translated by $1$ unit left (not right, even though we ``add $1$'').

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return x^2;}
real g(real x){return (x+1)^2;}
draw((-4.3,0)--(3.3,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-0.5)--(0,5.5),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(3.3,0),E); label("$y$",(0,5.5),N);
draw(graph(f,-2.3,2.3),gray+linewidth(0.9));
draw(graph(g,-3.3,1.3),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-3,4)--(1,4),dashed);
dot((2,4)); dot((1,4),blue); dot((-2,4)); dot((-3,4),blue); dot((0,0)); dot((-1,0),blue);
draw((2,4)--(1.12,4),Arrow(5)); draw((-2,4)--(-2.88,4),Arrow(5));
label("$y=x^2$",(2.2,5.1),E); label("$y=(x+1)^2$",(-3.4,5.3),W,blue);
label("$(1,4)$",(1,4),NW,blue); label("$(-3,4)$",(-3,4),W,blue); label("$(-1,0)$",(-1,0),SW,blue);
for(int i=-4;i<=3;++i) if(i!=0) draw((i,-0.08)--(i,0.08));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(c)} The graph of $y=x^2$ has vertex $(0,0)$ and meets $y=4$ at $(\pm2,4)$. Shifting $1$ unit left, the graph of $y=(x+1)^2$ has vertex $\boxed{(-1,0)}$ and meets $y=4$ at $\boxed{(1,4)\text{ and }(-3,4)}$. Directly: $(x+1)^2=4$ gives $x+1=\pm2$, so $x=1$ or $x=-3$.

question:
In base $2$ the number two is written $10_2$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why $\sqrt{10_2}$ (with $10_2$ read in base $2$) is the same number as $\sqrt2$, and why changing the base does not change the number $\sqrt2$, only the way it is written.
\item[(b)] Find the first six digits after the binary point of $\sqrt2$ written in base $2$, justifying each digit by squaring.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The centres of the three unit circles are at distance $2$ from each other, so they form an equilateral triangle of side $2$. Its centre $O$ is at distance $\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3}$ (the circumradius) from each vertex. By symmetry both required circles are centred at $O$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=2/sqrt(3);
pair O=(0,0);
pair P1=s*dir(90), P2=s*dir(210), P3=s*dir(330);
draw(circle(O,s+1),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(P1,1)); draw(circle(P2,1)); draw(circle(P3,1));
draw(circle(O,s-1),red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(P1--P2--P3--cycle,dashed+gray);
dot(P1);dot(P2);dot(P3);dot(O);
draw(P2--(P2+dir(210)),Arrow(4)); label("$1$",P2+0.5*dir(210),NW);
draw(O--(O+(s+1)*dir(20)),blue,Arrow(4)); label("$R$",(s+1)*dir(20)*0.75,N,blue);
\end{asy}

The small circle touches each unit circle from outside, so its radius is the distance from $O$ to a centre minus $1$:
\[r=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3}-1=\boxed{\dfrac{2\sqrt3-3}{3}}\approx0.155.\]
The large circle touches each unit circle from outside it (internally), so its radius is that distance plus $1$:
\[R=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3}+1=\boxed{\dfrac{2\sqrt3+3}{3}}\approx2.155.\]

\textbf{(b)} With $k_1=k_2=k_3=1$ the equation becomes $(3+k)^2=2(3+k^2)$, i.e. $k^2-6k-3=0$, so
\[k=3\pm2\sqrt3.\]
\begin{itemize}
\item $k=3+2\sqrt3>0$: then $\dfrac{1}{k}=\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt3+3}=\dfrac{2\sqrt3-3}{12-9}=\dfrac{2\sqrt3-3}{3}=r$. This is the small circle.
\item $k=3-2\sqrt3<0$: then $-\dfrac{1}{k}=\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt3-3}=\dfrac{2\sqrt3+3}{3}=R$. This is the enclosing circle, with curvature $-\dfrac{1}{R}$.
\end{itemize}
If instead we used $k_4=+\dfrac{1}{R}=2\sqrt3-3$, the left side would be $(3+2\sqrt3-3)^2=12$, while the right side would be $2\left(3+(2\sqrt3-3)^2\right)=2(24-12\sqrt3)=48-24\sqrt3\approx6.43$, so the formula fails.

The sign records the direction of tangency: the other circles touch the enclosing circle from the inside, where it bends around them instead of away from them, so its curvature counts as negative.

question:
A function $f$ has a \emph{local maximum} at $a$ if there is an open interval $I$ containing $a$ with $f(x)\le f(a)$ for all $x$ in $I$, and a \emph{local minimum} at $a$ if instead $f(x)\ge f(a)$ for all $x$ in $I$. The maximum (minimum) is \emph{strict} if moreover $f(x)<f(a)$ (respectively $f(x)>f(a)$) for all $x\ne a$ in $I$.

Let $f(x)=1$ for every real $x$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that every real number $a$ is both a local maximum and a local minimum of $f$.
\item[(b)] Show that $f$ has no strict local maximum and no strict local minimum.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{Tangent lengths.} Tangent segments from a point to a circle are equal. With $s=\dfrac{73+123+120}{2}=158$,
\[AE=AF=s-BC=35,\qquad BF=BD=s-CA=38,\qquad CD=CE=s-AB=85.\]

\textbf{Menelaus' theorem.} The line through $D$, $F$ and $G$ crosses the lines $BC$, $AB$, $CA$ of triangle $ABC$, so
\[\dfrac{AF}{FB}\cdot\dfrac{BD}{DC}\cdot\dfrac{CG}{GA}=1\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad\dfrac{35}{38}\cdot\dfrac{38}{85}\cdot\dfrac{CG}{GA}=1\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad\dfrac{CG}{GA}=\dfrac{85}{35}=\dfrac{17}{7}.\]
The line $DF$ meets the sides $BC$ and $AB$ at interior points, so it meets line $CA$ outside the segment $CA$; since $CG>GA$, the point $G$ lies beyond $A$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(123,0);
real ax=(73^2-120^2+123^2)/(2*123);
pair A=(ax,sqrt(73^2-ax^2));
pair D=(38,0);
pair F=B+(A-B)*38/73;
pair pE=C+(A-C)*85/120;
pair G=A+(A-C)*84/120;
pair I=(123*A+120*B+73*C)/(123+120+73);
real rr=abs(I-D);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(circle(I,rr));
draw(A--G,dashed);
draw(D--G,blue);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(F);dot(pE);dot(G,red);dot(I);
label("$A$",A,NE);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$F$",F,W);label("$E$",pE,NE);label("$G$",G,N,red);label("$I$",I,S);
label("$35$",(A+F)/2,W,fontsize(8pt));label("$38$",(F+B)/2,W,fontsize(8pt));
label("$38$",(B+D)/2,S,fontsize(8pt));label("$85$",(D+C)/2,S,fontsize(8pt));
label("$35$",(A+pE)/2,NE,fontsize(8pt));label("$85$",(pE+C)/2,NE,fontsize(8pt));
label("$84$",(G+A)/2,NE,red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Let $GA=x$. Then $CG=x+120$ and $\dfrac{x+120}{x}=\dfrac{17}{7}$, so $7x+840=17x$ and $x=84$. Finally, $E$ lies on segment $AC$ with $AE=35$, so
\[EG=GA+AE=84+35=\boxed{119}.\]

question:
The sum of the first $2011$ terms of a geometric sequence is $200$. The sum of the first $4022$ terms is $380$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the sum of the first $6033$ terms.
\item[(b)] A student reasons: ``The second block of $2011$ terms sums to $180$; multiplying by the common ratio, the next block sums to $162$, so the answer is $200+180+162=542$.'' Explain exactly why this shortcut is correct (in particular, what ratio is being used).
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Seen from above, the tart is the region between circles of radius $\dfrac{3}{2}$ and $\dfrac{1}{2}$ (outer diameter $3$, width $1$). Its cross-sections are discs of radius $\dfrac{1}{2}$ whose centres lie on the circle of radius $R=\dfrac{3}{2}-\dfrac{1}{2}=1$ (dashed).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
filldraw(circle(O,1.5),lightgray,black);
filldraw(circle(O,0.5),white,black);
draw(circle(O,1),dashed+blue);
draw((-1.5,0)--(1.5,0),Arrows(5));
label("$3$",(0.9,0),N);
draw((0.5,-0.4)--(1.5,-0.4),red,Arrows(4)); label("$1$",(1,-0.4),S,red);
draw(O--1*dir(130),blue,Arrow(4)); label("$1$",0.5*dir(130),SW,blue);
dot(O);
\end{asy}

The torus is swept out by rotating a disc of radius $\rho=\dfrac{1}{2}$ about an axis at distance $R=1$ from the disc's centre. By Pappus' theorem the volume is the area of the disc times the distance travelled by its centre:
\[V=\pi\rho^2\cdot2\pi R=\pi\cdot\dfrac{1}{4}\cdot2\pi\cdot1=\boxed{\dfrac{\pi^2}{2}}\ \text{cubic inches}\approx4.93\text{ cubic inches}.\]

On the unit circle, the point at angle $\theta$ (measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis) is $(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$; $\sin\theta$ is its height. Turning $90^\circ$ from $(1,0)$ brings us to the top of the circle, the point $(0,1)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-1.4,0)--(1.4,0),Arrow(5)); draw((0,-1.3)--(0,1.4),Arrow(5));
draw(unitcircle);
pair P=dir(90), Q=dir(35);
draw((0,0)--Q,gray); draw(Q--(Q.x,0),dashed+gray);
draw((0,0)--P,blue+linewidth(1));
dot(P,blue); dot(Q,gray);
label("$(0,1)$",P,NE,blue);
label("$(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$",Q,NE,gray+fontsize(8pt));
label("$\theta$",(0.25,0.08),E,gray+fontsize(8pt));
label("$x$",(1.4,0),E); label("$y$",(0,1.4),N);
\end{asy}

So $\sin90^\circ=\boxed{1}$ (and $\cos90^\circ=0$). Equivalently, $\sin\theta$ is the largest possible, $1$, when the angle is a right angle.

The ink sac holds
\[V=\pi r^2h=\pi\cdot3^2\cdot13=117\pi\ \text{mm}^3.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real rx=3, ry=1, h=13;
draw((-rx,0)--(-rx,h)); draw((rx,0)--(rx,h));
draw(ellipse((0,h),rx,ry));
path e=scale(rx,ry)*unitcircle;
draw(subpath(e,2,4));
draw(subpath(e,0,2),dashed);
draw((0,h)--(rx,h),blue,Arrow(4)); label("$3$",(rx/2,h+ry),N,blue);
draw((rx+1,0)--(rx+1,h),Arrows(5)); label("$13$",(rx+1,h/2),E);
\end{asy}

A full page uses $2\cdot3=6$ cubic millimetres, so the ink lasts for
\[\dfrac{117\pi}{6}=19.5\pi\approx61.26\]
pages. Since $19.5\pi>19.5\cdot3.14=61.23>61$ and $19.5\pi<19.5\cdot3.15=61.425<62$, the pen writes $\boxed{61}$ full pages (and about a quarter of another).

question:
Let $p(x)=a_nx^n+a_{n-1}x^{n-1}+\cdots+a_1x+a_0$ be a polynomial with real coefficients, $n\ge1$ and $a_n\ne0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Is it true that $p$ always has $n$ real roots, counted with multiplicity? Prove it or give a counterexample.
\item[(b)] Prove that $p$ has at most $n$ real roots, counted with multiplicity.
\end{enumerate}

Write the fraction $\dfrac{1}{12}$
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] in base $2$;
\item[(b)] in base $3$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the sphere have radius $r$; the cone then has base radius $r$, and let its height be $h$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1, h=3;
draw((-r,0)--(0,-h)--(r,0));
draw(circle((0,0),r),blue);
draw((-r,0)--(r,0),dashed);
draw((0,0)--(r,0),red,Arrow(4)); label("$r$",(r/2,0),N,red);
draw((0,0)--(0,-h),dashed);
draw((-r-0.3,0)--(-r-0.3,-h),Arrows(5)); label("$h$",(-r-0.3,-h/2),W);
dot((0,0));
\end{asy}

The melted ice cream has volume
\[\dfrac{3}{4}\cdot\dfrac{4}{3}\pi r^3=\pi r^3,\]
and it exactly fills the cone, whose volume is $\dfrac{1}{3}\pi r^2h$. So
\[\dfrac{1}{3}\pi r^2h=\pi r^3\quad\Longrightarrow\quad h=3r.\]
The ratio of height to radius is $3:1$, answer $\boxed{\textbf{(B)}}$.

Since $AB=AC$ and $\angle A=120^\circ$, the base angles of triangle $ABC$ are $\angle ABC=\angle ACB=30^\circ$. By the congruence, $FC=FD=AB=AC$ and $\angle FCD=\angle ACB=30^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=sqrt(6);
pair B=(0,0), C=(s,0), D=(s,s), Ept=(0,s);
pair A=(s/2,-s/(2*sqrt(3)));
pair F=(s-s/(2*sqrt(3)),s/2);
draw(B--C--D--Ept--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
filldraw(A--B--C--cycle,lightblue+opacity(0.4),blue);
filldraw(F--C--D--cycle,lightred+opacity(0.4),red);
draw(A--F,dashed);
pair u=unit(A-C), w=unit(F-C); draw(C+0.25*u--C+0.25*(u+w)--C+0.25*w);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(Ept);dot(F);
label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,NE);label("$E$",Ept,NW);label("$A$",A,S);label("$F$",F,W);
label("$120^\circ$",A+(0.33,0.33),blue+fontsize(8pt));label("$120^\circ$",F,E,red+fontsize(8pt));
label("$30^\circ$",C+0.75*dir(196),fontsize(7pt));label("$30^\circ$",C+0.75*dir(104),fontsize(7pt));
label("$2000$",(A+F)/2,NW);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Angle $ACF$.} The ray $CA$ makes $30^\circ$ with $CB$ on the outside of the square, and the ray $CF$ makes $30^\circ$ with $CD$ on the inside. Hence
\[\angle ACF=\angle ACB+\angle BCF=30^\circ+(90^\circ-30^\circ)=90^\circ.\]
So triangle $ACF$ is an isosceles right triangle with legs $AC=CF$ and hypotenuse $AF=2000$, and $AC=\dfrac{2000}{\sqrt2}=1000\sqrt2$.

\textbf{Side of the square.} Dropping the altitude from $A$ to $BC$ splits triangle $ABC$ into two $30$-$60$-$90$ triangles with hypotenuse $AC$, so $BC=2\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}AC=\sqrt3\,AC=1000\sqrt6$.

The area of the square is $BC^2=\left(1000\sqrt6\right)^2=\boxed{6\,000\,000}$.

question:
A \emph{block walk} goes from $(0,0)$ to a lattice point using unit steps, each either right (R) or up (U).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why the number of block walks from $(0,0)$ to $(k,n-k)$ is $\dbinom nk$.
\item[(b)] Use block walks to prove Pascal's identity $\dbinom nk=\dbinom{n-1}{k-1}+\dbinom{n-1}{k}$ for $1\le k\le n-1$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} A walk to $(k,n-k)$ has exactly $k$ R-steps and $n-k$ U-steps, $n$ steps in all, and it is determined by which $k$ of the $n$ steps are R. So there are $\dbinom nk$ walks.

\textbf{(b)} Look at the \emph{last} step of a walk to $(k,n-k)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int n=6,k=3;
for(int i=0;i<=k;++i) draw((i,0)--(i,n-k),gray);
for(int j=0;j<=n-k;++j) draw((0,j)--(k,j),gray);
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,2)--(2,2)--(2,3)--(3,3),blue+linewidth(1));
draw((0,0)--(0,1)--(2,1)--(3,1)--(3,2)--(3,3),red+linewidth(1)+dashed);
dot((0,0)); dot((k,n-k)); dot((k-1,n-k),blue); dot((k,n-k-1),red);
label("$(0,0)$",(0,0),SW); label("$(k,n-k)$",(k,n-k),E);
label("$(k-1,n-k)$",(k-1,n-k),NW,blue); label("$(k,n-k-1)$",(k,n-k-1),E,red);
\end{asy}

\begin{itemize}
\item If the last step is R, the walk passes through $(k-1,n-k)$, and before that it is any walk to $(k-1,n-k)$. These are walks of $n-1$ steps with $k-1$ R-steps: $\dbinom{n-1}{k-1}$ of them (blue in the figure).
\item If the last step is U, the walk passes through $(k,n-k-1)$, reached by any of the $\dbinom{n-1}{k}$ walks of $n-1$ steps with $k$ R-steps (red, dashed).
\end{itemize}
Every walk is in exactly one of these two groups (both points exist because $1\le k\le n-1$), so counting the walks to $(k,n-k)$ in two ways gives
\[\dbinom nk=\dbinom{n-1}{k-1}+\dbinom{n-1}{k}.\]
This is exactly the rule that each entry of Pascal's triangle is the sum of the two above it: the entries count walks, and every walk arrives from one of two neighbours.

Let the numbers be $x$ and $y$. The pair $(x,y)$ is uniform in the unit square, so probabilities are areas. The condition $\dfrac{x+y}{2}\le\dfrac{2}{3}$ means $x+y\le\dfrac{4}{3}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1/3)--(1/3,1)--(0,1)--cycle,lightblue);
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle);
draw((1/3,1)--(1,1/3),blue+linewidth(0.9));
label("$x+y=\dfrac{4}{3}$",(1,0.8),E,blue+fontsize(8pt));
label("$\dfrac{1}{3}$",(1,1/3),E,fontsize(8pt)); label("$\dfrac{1}{3}$",(1/3,1),N,fontsize(8pt));
label("$0$",(0,0),SW); label("$1$",(1,0),S); label("$1$",(0,1),W);
label("$x$",(0.5,0),S); label("$y$",(0,0.5),W);
\end{asy}

The line $x+y=\dfrac{4}{3}$ cuts off the corner triangle near $(1,1)$ with vertices $\left(\dfrac{1}{3},1\right)$, $(1,1)$, $\left(1,\dfrac{1}{3}\right)$. Its legs are $\dfrac{2}{3}$, so its area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\left(\dfrac{2}{3}\right)^2=\dfrac{2}{9}$. Everything else in the square satisfies the condition, so the probability is
\[1-\dfrac{2}{9}=\boxed{\dfrac{7}{9}}.\]

question:
In triangle $ABC$, $D$ and $E$ are the midpoints of $AB$ and $BC$, and $F$ is the intersection of $AE$ and $DC$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the ratio of the area of triangle $DEC$ to the area of triangle $ABC$.
\item[(b)] Find the ratio of the area of triangle $DEF$ to the area of triangle $ABC$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Write $[XYZ]$ for the area of triangle $XYZ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(4,0), C=(1.5,3.2);
pair D=(A+B)/2, Ept=(B+C)/2, F=(A+B+C)/3;
fill(D--Ept--C--cycle,lightblue+opacity(0.5));
fill(D--Ept--F--cycle,lightred+opacity(0.7));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--Ept); draw(D--C); draw(D--Ept,blue);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(Ept);dot(F);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,S);label("$E$",Ept,NE);label("$F$",F,W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} Triangles $DBC$ and $ABC$ have the same height from $C$, and $DB=\dfrac{1}{2}AB$, so $[DBC]=\dfrac{1}{2}[ABC]$. Triangles $DEC$ and $DBC$ have the same height from $D$, and $EC=\dfrac{1}{2}BC$, so $[DEC]=\dfrac{1}{2}[DBC]$. Hence
\[\dfrac{[DEC]}{[ABC]}=\boxed{\dfrac{1}{4}}.\]

\textbf{(b)} $AE$ and $CD$ are medians, so $F$ is the centroid, which divides each median in the ratio $2:1$ from the vertex: $CF:FD=2:1$, i.e. $DF=\dfrac{1}{3}DC$. Triangles $DEF$ and $DEC$ have the same height from $E$, so $[DEF]=\dfrac{1}{3}[DEC]$ and
\[\dfrac{[DEF]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac{1}{3}\cdot\dfrac{1}{4}=\boxed{\dfrac{1}{12}}.\]
These ratios hold in every triangle, in particular in an equilateral one.

Let the square have side $s$ and the circle radius $r$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1, s=pi*r/2;
draw(circle((0,0),r)); draw((0,0)--(r,0),Arrow(4)); label("$r$",(r/2,0),N); dot((0,0));
draw(shift(1.6,-s/2)*((0,0)--(s,0)--(s,s)--(0,s)--cycle));
label("$s$",(1.6+s/2,-s/2),S);
\end{asy}

Equal perimeters give $4s=2\pi r$, so $s=\dfrac{\pi r}{2}$. Then
\[\dfrac{\text{area of square}}{\text{area of circle}}=\dfrac{s^2}{\pi r^2}=\dfrac{\dfrac{\pi^2r^2}{4}}{\pi r^2}=\boxed{\dfrac{\pi}{4}}.\]
(As expected, the circle encloses more area than the square with the same perimeter, since $\dfrac{\pi}{4}<1$.)

\textbf{At least $4$ marks.} With $m$ marks there are $m+2$ points (marks and ends), so at most $\dbinom{m+2}{2}$ different distances can be measured. For $m=3$ this is $\dbinom52=10<12$, so $3$ marks (or fewer) cannot work.

\textbf{$4$ marks suffice.} Put marks at $1,2,3$ and $8$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real L=12;
fill((0,0)--(L,0)--(L,0.8)--(0,0.8)--cycle,lightyellow);
draw((0,0)--(L,0)--(L,0.8)--(0,0.8)--cycle);
int[] m={1,2,3,8};
for(int i=0;i<4;++i){draw((m[i],0)--(m[i],0.45),linewidth(0.9)); label("$"+string(m[i])+"$",(m[i],0),S,fontsize(8pt));}
label("$0$",(0,0),S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$12$",(L,0),S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The points are $0,1,2,3,8,12$, and
\[\begin{array}{llll}
1=1-0, & 2=2-0, & 3=3-0, & 4=12-8,\\
5=8-3, & 6=8-2, & 7=8-1, & 8=8-0,\\
9=12-3, & 10=12-2, & 11=12-1, & 12=12-0.
\end{array}\]
So the least number of marks is $\boxed{4}$.

\textbf{Move one diagonal.} Through $A$ draw the line parallel to $BD$; it meets line $CD$ at $P$, on the other side of $D$ from $C$. Then $ABDP$ is a parallelogram ($AB\parallel PD$ and $AP\parallel BD$), so $PD=AB=12$ and $AP=BD=13$. Hence $PC=PD+DC=12+28=40$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(0,0), D=(12,0), C=(40,0), A=(5,12), B=(17,12);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--C,blue); draw(B--D,red); draw(A--P,red+dashed); draw(P--D,dashed);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(P);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$P$",P,SW);
label("$12$",(A+B)/2,N);label("$28$",(D+C)/2,S);label("$12$",(P+D)/2,S);
label("$37$",(A+C)/2,NE,blue);label("$13$",(B+D)/2,E,red);label("$13$",(A+P)/2,W,red);
draw(A--(5,0),dotted); label("$h$",(5,6),E);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Equal areas.} Triangle $APC$ has base $PC=AB+CD$ and the same height $h$ as the trapezoid (the distance from $A$ to line $CD$), so
\[[APC]=\dfrac{1}{2}(AB+CD)h=[ABCD].\]

\textbf{Heron's formula.} Triangle $APC$ has sides $13,37,40$ and semiperimeter $s=45$:
\[[APC]=\sqrt{45\cdot(45-13)(45-37)(45-40)}=\sqrt{45\cdot32\cdot8\cdot5}=\sqrt{57600}=240.\]
The area of the trapezoid is $\boxed{240}$. (The height is $h=\dfrac{2\cdot240}{40}=12$; with coordinates $P=(0,0)$, $D=(12,0)$, $C=(40,0)$, $A=(5,12)$, $B=(17,12)$ all the given lengths check.)

Put the rectangle as $[0,X]\times[0,Y]$. Let $Q$ be the centre of the circle of radius $2$ and $P$ that of the circle of radius $1$; external tangency means $PQ=3$.

\textbf{Constraints.} The circles lie inside the rectangle exactly when $Q\in[2,X-2]\times[2,Y-2]$ and $P\in[1,X-1]\times[1,Y-1]$. In particular $X\ge4$ and $Y\ge4$, so that the large circle fits. Also the horizontal distance between the centres is at most $(X-1)-2=X-3$ (and likewise $(X-2)-1=X-3$ the other way), and the vertical distance at most $Y-3$. Put $a=X-3\ge1$ and $b=Y-3\ge1$. Then
\[9=PQ^2\le a^2+b^2.\]
Conversely, if $a,b\ge1$ and $a^2+b^2\ge9$, the circles fit: place $Q=(2,2)$ and move $P$ inside $[1,X-1]\times[1,Y-1]$ continuously from near $Q$ to the far corner $(X-1,Y-1)$, at distance $\sqrt{a^2+b^2}\ge3$; somewhere $PQ=3$.

\textbf{Minimise $(a+3)(b+3)$} over $a,b\ge1$ with $a^2+b^2\ge9$. If $a^2+b^2>9$, decrease the larger of $a,b$ until $a^2+b^2=9$; it stays above $\sqrt{\dfrac{9}{2}}>1$, so the constraints still hold, and the area does not increase. So it suffices to consider $a^2+b^2=9$ with $a,b\ge1$. On the circle $a^2+b^2=9$, writing $s=a+b$ and $ab=\dfrac{s^2-9}{2}$,
\[(a+3)(b+3)=ab+3s+9=\dfrac{s^2+6s+9}{2}=\dfrac{(s+3)^2}{2},\]
which increases with $s$, and $s^2=9+2ab$. Since $(ab)^2=a^2\left(9-a^2\right)$ is a concave function of $a^2\in[1,8]$, it is smallest at an endpoint: $a^2=1$ or $a^2=8$, i.e. $\{a,b\}=\{1,2\sqrt2\}$. Then $s=1+2\sqrt2$ and the area is
\[\dfrac{\left(4+2\sqrt2\right)^2}{2}=\dfrac{24+16\sqrt2}{2}=\boxed{12+8\sqrt2}\approx23.31.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real X=4, Y=3+2*sqrt(2);
pair Q=(2,2), P=(3,Y-1);
draw((0,0)--(X,0)--(X,Y)--(0,Y)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(circle(Q,2),blue); draw(circle(P,1),red);
draw(Q--P,dashed); dot(Q,blue); dot(P,red);
draw(Q--(P.x,Q.y),dotted); draw((P.x,Q.y)--P,dotted);
label("$Q$",Q,SW,blue); label("$P$",P,NE,red);
label("$3$",(Q+P)/2,W); label("$1$",((Q.x+P.x)/2,Q.y),S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$2\sqrt2$",(P.x,(Q.y+P.y)/2),E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$4$",(X/2,0),S); label("$3+2\sqrt2$",(0,Y/2),W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{It is attained} by the $4\times\left(3+2\sqrt2\right)$ rectangle with $Q=(2,2)$ and $P=\left(3,2+2\sqrt2\right)$: $PQ=\sqrt{1+8}=3$.

(The answer $18$ comes from the point $(X,Y)=(3,6)$ on $(X-3)^2+(Y-3)^2=9$, but a rectangle of width $3$ cannot hold a circle of radius $2$: the conditions $X\ge4$, $Y\ge4$ were forgotten.)

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find all integers $a$ such that $a(a-8)$ is a perfect square.
\item[(b)] Find all integers $a$ such that $a(a+3)$ is a perfect square.
\end{enumerate}
In each part, prove that there are no other values. (Here $0$ counts as a perfect square.)

solution:
\textbf{Groups by number of digits.} Each power of $2$ has at most one more digit than the previous one, so the numbers $2^0,2^1,\dots,2^{332}$ have every number of digits from $1$ to $100$ (since $2^{333}$, with $101$ digits and first digit $1$, is less than $2\cdot10^{100}$, the number $2^{332}$ is less than $10^{100}$). Group them by their number of digits: $100$ groups.

\textbf{Each group has $3$ or $4$ members, and contains a $4$ exactly when it has $4$.} Let $2^k$ be the smallest member of the group with $d$ digits. Then $2^{k-1}<10^{d-1}\le2^k$ (for $k\ge1$; for the group of $2^0=1$ this is clear), so $2^k=x\cdot10^{d-1}$ with $1\le x<2$. The group consists of the numbers $x,2x,4x,8x,\ldots$ (times $10^{d-1}$) that are below $10$:
\begin{itemize}
\item if $x<1.25$, then $8x<10\le16x$, so the group is $\{x,2x,4x,8x\}$ with $4x\in[4,5)$: four members, one of them starting with $4$;
\item if $1.25\le x<2$, then $4x<8<10\le8x$, so the group is $\{x,2x,4x\}$ with first digits $1$, then $2$ or $3$, then $5$, $6$ or $7$: three members, none starting with $4$.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Count.} If $g$ groups have four members, then $333=3\cdot100+g$, so $g=33$. Each of these groups contains exactly one power with first digit $4$, and it is never $2^0$. So $\boxed{33}$ of the numbers $2^1,\dots,2^{332}$ start with $4$.

Since $0^\circ<x<90^\circ$, $x$ is an acute angle of a right triangle whose legs opposite and adjacent to $x$ are $2ab$ and $a^2-b^2$ (both positive because $a>b>0$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0), R=(4,0), T=(4,3);
draw(O--R--T--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw((3.7,0)--(3.7,0.3)--(4,0.3));
label("$a^2-b^2$",(O+R)/2,S); label("$2ab$",(R+T)/2,E); label("$a^2+b^2$",(O+T)/2,NW);
label("$x$",O+(0.55,0.15),E);
draw(arc(O,0.45,0,degrees(T)));
\end{asy}

The hypotenuse is
\[\sqrt{\left(a^2-b^2\right)^2+(2ab)^2}=\sqrt{a^4+2a^2b^2+b^4}=a^2+b^2.\]
Hence
\[\sin x=\dfrac{\text{opposite}}{\text{hypotenuse}}=\boxed{\dfrac{2ab}{a^2+b^2}}.\]

For positive integers $a,b\le10$, the points $X$ with $XA=a$ and $XB=b$ are the common points of the circle of radius $a$ about $A$ and the circle of radius $b$ about $B$, whose centres are $AB=10$ apart.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $|a-b|<10<a+b$, the circles cross in two points, symmetric in the $x$-axis.
\item If $a+b=10$, they touch at one point of segment $AB$.
\item If $a+b<10$, they do not meet. ($|a-b|\ge10$ is impossible, since $|a-b|\le9$.)
\end{itemize}
Different pairs $(a,b)$ give different points, because a point determines its two distances.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-5,0), B=(5,0);
draw((-12,0)--(12,0),gray);
draw(circle(A,6),blue+dashed);
draw(circle(A,3),blue); draw(circle(B,7),red);
real x1=(36-49)/20; pair X1=(x1,sqrt(36-(x1+5)^2)), X2=(x1,-sqrt(36-(x1+5)^2));
pair Y=(-2,0);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(X1);dot(X2);dot(Y);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$X$",X1,N);label("$X'$",X2,S);label("$Y$",Y,SE);
\end{asy}

(The figure shows $a=6$, $b=7$ with two points $X,X'$, and $a=3$, $b=7$ with one point $Y$ on $AB$.)

\textbf{Count.} The pairs with $a+b\ge11$: for each $a$ there are $a$ values $b\in\{11-a,\dots,10\}$, so $1+2+\cdots+10=55$ pairs, giving $110$ points. The pairs with $a+b=10$ are $a=1,\dots,9$: $9$ points. In total
\[110+9=\boxed{119}.\]

\textbf{Step 1: every $2\times2$ block uses all four colours}, because its four squares are pairwise adjacent.

\textbf{Step 2: neighbouring columns.} Read a column from top to bottom; call it \emph{alternating} if it has the form $x,y,x,y$. Let $p=(p_1,\dots,p_4)$ be a column and $q$ the column next to it. By Step 1, for each $i$ the pair $\{q_i,q_{i+1}\}$ is the complement of $\{p_i,p_{i+1}\}$ in the set of four colours.

\emph{Case A: $p$ is alternating}, $x,y,x,y$. Then every pair $\{q_i,q_{i+1}\}$ is $\{z,w\}$ (the other two colours), so $q$ is $z,w,z,w$ or $w,z,w,z$: two choices, both valid, and $q$ is again alternating.

\emph{Case B: $p$ is not alternating}, so $p_i\ne p_{i+2}$ for some $i$. As $p_i,p_{i+1}$ and $p_{i+1},p_{i+2}$ are adjacent, $p_i,p_{i+1},p_{i+2}$ are three different colours. The square $q_{i+1}$ must avoid all three, so it is the fourth colour; then $q_i$ is the remaining colour of the complement of $\{p_i,p_{i+1}\}$, $q_{i+2}$ that of $\{p_{i+1},p_{i+2}\}$, and so on: $q$ is uniquely determined (if it exists). Moreover $q$ is not alternating (by Case A, an alternating column only has alternating neighbours), and the column after $q$ is again the unique neighbour of $q$, which is $p$.

\textbf{Step 3: the structure.} By Step 2, either all four columns are alternating, or none is; in the latter case the columns read $p,q,p,q$, so every \emph{row} has the form $u,v,u,v$. So in every valid colouring all columns alternate or all rows alternate.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
string[][] g1={{"1","2","1","2"},{"3","4","3","4"},{"2","1","2","1"},{"3","4","3","4"}};
string[][] g2={{"1","3","2","3"},{"2","4","1","4"},{"1","3","2","3"},{"2","4","1","4"}};
pen[] col={palered,palegreen,paleblue,paleyellow};
void grid(string[][] g, pair o){
  for(int i=0;i<4;++i) for(int j=0;j<4;++j){
    int c=(int)g[i][j]-1;
    filldraw(shift(o+(j,-i))*((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle),col[c],black);
    label("$"+g[i][j]+"$",o+(j+0.5,-i+0.5));
  }
}
grid(g1,(0,0)); grid(g2,(5.5,0));
label("rows alternate",(2,-3.5)); label("columns alternate",(7.5,-3.5));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 4: count.}
\begin{itemize}
\item All columns alternating: the first column $x,y,x,y$ can be chosen in $4\cdot3=12$ ways, and each of the other three columns in $2$ ways (Case A): $12\cdot2^3=96$ colourings.
\item All rows alternating: $96$ colourings, by symmetry.
\item Both: the colouring is determined by its top-left $2\times2$ block, which uses the four colours in any order: $4!=24$ colourings.
\end{itemize}
By inclusion-exclusion the number of colourings is $96+96-24=\boxed{168}$.

\textbf{(a)} The centre $O$ is equidistant from all vertices, so $OA=OB=R$ and triangle $OAB$ is isosceles. $X$ is the midpoint of $AB$, so $AX=XB=\dfrac {l}{2}$ by definition. In an isosceles triangle the median $OX$ to the base is also the altitude and the angle bisector, so $OX\perp AB$ and $OX$ bisects $\angle AOB$. The $n$ sides subtend $n$ equal central angles that together make $360^\circ$, so $\angle AOB=\dfrac{360^\circ}{n}$ and $\angle AOX=\dfrac{180^\circ}{n}$. The perpendicular distance from $O$ to a side is the inradius, so $OX=r$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int n=8; real R=3;
pair O=(0,0);
pair[] V; for(int i=0;i<n;++i) V.push(R*dir(90+180/n+360*i/n));
for(int i=0;i<n;++i) draw(V[i]--V[(i+1)%n],linewidth(0.9));
pair A=V[n-1], B=V[0], X=(A+B)/2;
draw(O--A,blue); draw(O--B,blue); draw(O--X,red+dashed);
draw(X+0.25*unit(O-X)--X+0.25*unit(O-X)+0.25*unit(B-X)--X+0.25*unit(B-X));
dot(O); dot(X,red);
label("$O$",O,S); label("$A$",A,NE); label("$B$",B,NW); label("$X$",X,N,red);
label("$R$",(O+A)/2,E,blue); label("$r$",(O+X)/2,W,red);
label("$\dfrac{180^\circ}{n}$",O+(0,0.7),E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$\dfrac{l}{2}$",(A+X)/2,S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} Triangle $OXA$ has a right angle at $X$, hypotenuse $OA=R$, the side $AX=\dfrac {l}{2}$ opposite the angle $\theta=\dfrac{180^\circ}{n}$ at $O$, and the side $OX=r$ adjacent to it. Hence
\[\sin\theta=\dfrac{AX}{OA}=\dfrac{l}{2R}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad R=\dfrac{l}{2\sin\theta},\qquad \tan\theta=\dfrac{AX}{OX}=\dfrac{l}{2r}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad r=\dfrac{l}{2\tan\theta}.\]
So sine is used because $R$ is the hypotenuse, and tangent because $r$ is the other leg.

\textbf{(c)} For $n=6$, $\theta=30^\circ$: $R=\dfrac{6}{2\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}}=\boxed{6}$ and $r=\dfrac{6}{2\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{3}}=\boxed{3\sqrt3}$. (Indeed a regular hexagon consists of six equilateral triangles of side $6$, whose heights are $3\sqrt3$.)

There are $\dbinom{k+3}{2}$ equally likely pairs, of which $\dbinom32+\dbinom k2=3+\dfrac{k(k-1)}{2}$ have the same colour. So
\[\dfrac{3+\dfrac{k(k-1)}{2}}{\dfrac{(k+3)(k+2)}{2}}=\dfrac{1}{2}\iff 2\left(6+k^2-k\right)=(k+3)(k+2)\iff k^2-7k+6=0\iff(k-1)(k-6)=0.\]
Both roots work:
\begin{itemize}
\item $k=1$: same colour only for two reds, $\dfrac{3}{6}=\dfrac{1}{2}$;
\item $k=6$: $\dfrac{3+15}{36}=\dfrac{1}{2}$.
\end{itemize}
So $k=\boxed{1\text{ or }6}$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Which is the simplified form of the expression: $(4x)^2$ or $16x^2$? Show that they are equal.
\item[(b)] Factor $8x^3+27$ completely over the integers, writing each factor in simplified form, and show that the quadratic factor cannot be factored further over the real numbers.
\end{enumerate}

In football a team can score $2$ points (a safety), $3$ points (a field goal), $6$ points (a touchdown without the extra point) or $7$ points (a touchdown with the extra point). Two scoring combinations are the same if they use the same number of each kind of score, in any order.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] In how many ways can a team score exactly $21$ points?
\item[(b)] In how many ways can a team score exactly $17$ points?
\item[(c)] The final score of a game is $21$ to $17$. How many combinations of scores for the two teams give this final score?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
We count solutions of $2a+3b+6c+7d=N$ in nonnegative integers, organised by the number $d$ of $7$s and $c$ of $6$s; the remainder $M=N-7d-6c$ must be written as $2a+3b$.

The number of ways to write $M\ge0$ as $2a+3b$ is the number of $b\in\{0,1,\dots\}$ with $3b\le M$ and $M-3b$ even. For $M=0,1,\dots,21$ this is
\[1,0,1,1,1,1,2,1,2,2,2,2,3,2,3,3,3,3,4,3,4,4.\]

\textbf{(a)} $N=21$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $d=0$: $c=0,1,2,3$ give $M=21,15,9,3$: $4+3+2+1=10$ ways;
\item $d=1$: $c=0,1,2$ give $M=14,8,2$: $3+2+1=6$ ways;
\item $d=2$: $c=0$ gives $M=7$: $1$ way;
\item $d=3$: $M=0$: $1$ way.
\end{itemize}
Total $\boxed{18}$.

\textbf{(b)} $N=17$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $d=0$: $c=0,1,2$ give $M=17,11,5$: $3+2+1=6$ ways;
\item $d=1$: $c=0,1$ give $M=10,4$: $2+1=3$ ways;
\item $d=2$: $M=3$: $1$ way.
\end{itemize}
Total $\boxed{10}$.

\textbf{(c)} The two teams' combinations are independent, so there are $18\cdot10=\boxed{180}$ combinations.

A shortcut for questions like this is the generating function
\[\dfrac{1}{(1-t^2)(1-t^3)(1-t^6)(1-t^7)},\]
whose coefficients of $t^{21}$ and $t^{17}$ are $18$ and $10$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that a real number $x$ satisfies $x^2-5x+6=0$ if and only if $x=2$ or $x=3$. Prove both directions, using only the factorisation and the fact that a product of two real numbers is $0$ exactly when one of them is $0$ (without the fundamental theorem of algebra).
\item[(b)] Solve $\sqrt{2x+7}=x+2$ over the real numbers. Identify the step in the usual solution that is not reversible, and explain how it produces an extraneous solution.
\end{enumerate}

A convex polygon has $17$ sides.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many diagonals does it have?
\item[(b)] All the diagonals are drawn. Does this change the sum of the interior angles of the polygon? Find that sum.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Each of the $17$ vertices is joined by a diagonal to the $17-3=14$ vertices other than itself and its two neighbours. This counts every diagonal twice (once from each end), so there are
\[\dfrac{17\cdot14}{2}=\boxed{119}\text{ diagonals}.\]

\textbf{(b)} Drawing diagonals does not change the polygon, so its interior angles and their sum are unchanged. The $14$ diagonals from one vertex $P$ cut the polygon into $17-2=15$ triangles whose angles together make up exactly the interior angles of the polygon:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int n=17; real R=3;
pair[] V; for(int i=0;i<n;++i) V.push(R*dir(90-360*i/n));
for(int i=2;i<n-1;++i) draw(V[0]--V[i],blue+0.6);
for(int i=0;i<n;++i) draw(V[i]--V[(i+1)%n],linewidth(0.9));
dot(V[0]); label("$P$",V[0],N);
\end{asy}

So the sum of the interior angles is
\[15\cdot180^\circ=\boxed{2700^\circ}.\]

Let $O$ be the centre of $\omega$ and let $m$ be the perpendicular bisector of $BC$; it passes through $O$ and is perpendicular to $BC$.

The reflection in $m$ maps $\omega$ to itself (it fixes the centre) and swaps $B$ and $C$. It maps the line through $A$ parallel to $BC$ to itself, because that line is perpendicular to $m$. Hence it maps $A$ to the other intersection of this line with $\omega$, which is $D$ (if $A$ lies on $m$, the line is tangent at $A$ and $D=A$ is the reflection of itself).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); real R=3;
pair B=R*dir(200), C=R*dir(-20), A=R*dir(120);
pair D=(-A.x,A.y);
draw(circle(O,R));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(D--C,red+linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--B,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,-3.4)--(0,3.4),dashed+gray);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(O);
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,NE); label("$O$",O,E);
label("$\omega$",R*dir(250),SW); label("$m$",(0,3.4),N,gray);
\end{asy}

A reflection preserves lengths and maps segment $AB$ to segment $DC$. Therefore $AB=DC$ in every case, and
\[\dfrac{AB}{DC}=\boxed{1}.\]
(In other words, $ABCD$ or $ADBC$ is an isosceles trapezoid inscribed in $\omega$; in the tangent case $AB=AC=DC$.)

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $\left(\dfrac{n^0+n^1}{2}\right)^n\ge n!$, that is, $\left(\dfrac{n+1}{2}\right)^n\ge n!$, for every positive integer $n$.
\item[(b)] A student argues: ``Taking the $n$th root of both sides gives $\dfrac{n+1}{2}\ge(n-1)!$; replacing $n$ by $x+1$, we get $\dfrac{x+2}{2}\ge x!$ for all positive integers $x$.'' Find the error, and show that the student's conclusion is false.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $a,b,c$ be the side lengths, $s=\dfrac{a+b+c}{2}$ the semiperimeter and $K$ the area.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0.3,2.6), B=(0,0), C=(5,0);
real a=abs(B-C), b=abs(C-A), c=abs(A-B);
pair I=(a*A+b*B+c*C)/(a+b+c);
real s=(a+b+c)/2; real K=abs(cross(B-A,C-A))/2; real r=K/s;
// circumcentre
pair mAB=(A+B)/2, mBC=(B+C)/2;
pair O=extension(mAB,mAB+rotate(90)*(B-A),mBC,mBC+rotate(90)*(C-B));
real R=abs(O-A);
draw(circle(O,R),blue); draw(circle(I,r),red);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(O,blue); dot(I,red);
draw(O--C,blue+dashed); draw(I--(I.x,0),red+dashed);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$O$",O,S,blue); label("$I$",I,NE,red);
label("$R$",(O+C)/2,S,blue); label("$r$",(I+(I.x,0))/2,W,red);
\end{asy}

We use three standard facts:
\begin{itemize}
\item $K=rs$ (split the triangle into three triangles with apex at the incentre $I$ and heights $r$);
\item $K=\dfrac{abc}{4R}$ (from $K=\dfrac{1}{2}bc\sin A$ and the extended law of sines $a=2R\sin A$);
\item Heron's formula $K^2=s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)$.
\end{itemize}
Then
\[\dfrac{R}{2r}=\dfrac{\dfrac{abc}{4K}}{\dfrac{2K}{s}}=\dfrac{abcs}{8K^2}=\dfrac{abc}{8(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}.\]
Put $x=s-a$, $y=s-b$, $z=s-c$; these are positive by the triangle inequality, and $a=y+z$, $b=z+x$, $c=x+y$. By AM-GM, $y+z\ge2\sqrt{yz}$, $z+x\ge2\sqrt{zx}$, $x+y\ge2\sqrt{xy}$, and multiplying,
\[abc=(y+z)(z+x)(x+y)\ge8xyz=8(s-a)(s-b)(s-c).\]
Hence $\dfrac{R}{2r}\ge1$, that is, $R\ge2r$. Equality requires $x=y=z$, i.e. $a=b=c$; conversely an equilateral triangle of side $a$ has $R=\dfrac{a}{\sqrt3}$ and $r=\dfrac{a}{2\sqrt3}$, so $R=2r$. $\blacksquare$

question:
Let $r$ and $s$ be the roots of $3x^2+4x+12=0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Use Vieta's formulas to find $r^2+s^2$.
\item[(b)] The answer to (a) is negative. Explain why this is not a contradiction, and confirm the value by finding $r$ and $s$ explicitly.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $a=BC$, $b=CA$, $c=AB$ (all rational).

Since $\angle A$ is the largest angle, $\angle B$ and $\angle C$ are acute: if, say, $\angle B\ge90^\circ$, then $\angle A\ge\angle B\ge90^\circ$ and the angle sum would exceed $180^\circ$. Hence the foot $D$ lies strictly between $B$ and $C$. Put $x=BD$, so $DC=a-x$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(7,0), A=(2.2,3.6), D=(A.x,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--D,blue+dashed);
draw(D+(0.25,0)--D+(0.25,0.25)--D+(0,0.25));
dot(D,blue);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,S,blue);
label("$c$",(A+B)/2,NW); label("$b$",(A+C)/2,NE); label("$x$",(B+D)/2,S); label("$a-x$",(D+C)/2,S);
\end{asy}

By the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangles $ABD$ and $ACD$,
\[AD^2=c^2-x^2=b^2-(a-x)^2.\]
Expanding, $c^2-x^2=b^2-a^2+2ax-x^2$, so
\[BD=x=\dfrac{a^2+c^2-b^2}{2a},\]
which is rational, since it is built from rational numbers by addition, multiplication and division by $2a\ne0$. $\blacksquare$

(Without the hypothesis on $\angle A$, $D$ might lie outside segment $BC$; then the same computation gives $BD=\left|\dfrac{a^2+c^2-b^2}{2a}\right|$, still rational.)

The handrail lies on a cylinder of radius $\dfrac{7}{\pi}$. Cut the cylinder along a vertical line and unroll it into a flat rectangle; the handrail becomes a straight segment. Its horizontal extent is the length of a $270^\circ$ arc:
\[\dfrac{270}{360}\cdot2\pi\cdot\dfrac{7}{\pi}=\dfrac{3}{4}\cdot14=\dfrac{21}{2}\text{ feet},\]
and its vertical extent is $10$ feet.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real w=10.5, h=10;
draw((0,0)--(w,0)--(w,h)--(0,h)--cycle,gray);
draw((0,0)--(w,h),blue+linewidth(1));
draw((w-0.5,0)--(w-0.5,0.5)--(w,0.5));
label("$\dfrac{21}{2}$",(w/2,0),S); label("$10$",(w,h/2),E);
label("handrail",(w/2,h/2),NW,blue);
label("unrolled side of the cylinder",(w/2,h),N,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

By the Pythagorean theorem the handrail has length
\[\sqrt{10^2+\left(\dfrac{21}{2}\right)^2}=\sqrt{\dfrac{400+441}{4}}=\dfrac{29}{2}=\boxed{14.5}\text{ feet}.\]

