solution:
The incentre and the $A$-excentre both lie on the bisector of angle $A$, which is line $AD$; $I$ lies between $A$ and $D$, and $I_A$ lies beyond $D$. Also, $BI$ bisects angle $ABC$ internally and $BI_A$ bisects it externally (the excentre lies on the external bisectors at $B$ and $C$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.2,4), B=(0,0), C=(5,0);
real a=abs(B-C), b=abs(C-A), c=abs(A-B);
pair I=(a*A+b*B+c*C)/(a+b+c);
pair J=(-a*A+b*B+c*C)/(-a+b+c);
pair D=extension(A,I,B,C);
real s=(a+b+c)/2; real K=abs(cross(B-A,C-A))/2; real r=K/s; real ra=K/(s-a);
draw(circle(I,r),gray); draw(circle(J,ra),gray+dashed);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--J,blue); draw(B--I,red); draw(B--J,red+dashed);
draw(B--B+1.2*(B-C),gray); draw(C--C+0.6*(C-B),gray);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(I,red);dot(J,red);dot(D,blue);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$I$",I,E); label("$I_A$",J,SE); label("$D$",D,NE,blue);
\end{asy}

\textbf{A ratio lemma.} If $P$ lies on line $AD$, triangles $ABP$ and $DBP$ have the same height from $B$, so
\[\dfrac{AP}{PD}=\dfrac{[ABP]}{[DBP]}=\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{2}\,BA\cdot BP\sin\angle ABP}{\dfrac{1}{2}\,BD\cdot BP\sin\angle DBP}=\dfrac{BA\sin\angle ABP}{BD\sin\angle DBP}.\]

\textbf{$P=I$.} Since $D$ lies on segment $BC$, $\angle ABD=\angle ABC$, and $BI$ bisects it, so $\angle ABI=\angle DBI$ and $\dfrac{AI}{ID}=\dfrac{BA}{BD}$.

\textbf{$P=I_A$.} The external bisector $BI_A$ is perpendicular to $BI$, so $\angle ABI_A=90^\circ+\angle ABI$ and $\angle DBI_A=90^\circ-\angle DBI$ (measured on the other side of $BI$). These two angles add up to $180^\circ$, so their sines are equal and $\dfrac{AI_A}{I_AD}=\dfrac{BA}{BD}$.

Therefore $\dfrac{AI}{ID}=\dfrac{BA}{BD}=\dfrac{AI_A}{I_AD}$. $\blacksquare$

question:
Four cards are drawn at random, without replacement, from a standard deck of $52$ cards.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the probability that exactly $1$ ace and $3$ kings are drawn.
\item[(b)] A student says: ``There are $4!=24$ orders in which the ace and the kings can come out, so the count $\dbinom41\dbinom43$ should be divided by $24$.'' Explain why this is wrong, and confirm the answer to (a) by counting ordered draws.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} All $\dbinom{52}{4}=270725$ sets of four cards are equally likely. A favourable set consists of one of the $4$ aces and $3$ of the $4$ kings: $\dbinom41\dbinom43=16$ sets. The probability is
\[\boxed{\dfrac{16}{270725}}.\]

\textbf{(b)} In (a) both the numerator and the denominator count \emph{unordered} sets of cards, so order never entered and there is nothing to divide by. If we do care about order, we must count ordered draws in both places:
\begin{itemize}
\item ordered draws: $52\cdot51\cdot50\cdot49=6497400$;
\item favourable ordered draws: choose the position of the ace ($4$ ways), the ace ($4$ ways) and the kings in the other three positions in order ($4\cdot3\cdot2=24$ ways), $4\cdot4\cdot24=384$ in all.
\end{itemize}
Then $\dfrac{384}{6497400}=\dfrac{16}{270725}$, the same answer: the factor $24$ appears in both counts and cancels. Dividing only the numerator by $24$ would mix an unordered numerator with an unordered denominator divided once more.

question:
Let $ABC$ be a triangle with $a=BC$, $b=CA$, $c=AB$. Let $\omega$ be the circle with centre $C$ through $B$ (radius $a$), and let line $AB$ meet $\omega$ again at $B'$ (with $B'=B$ if the line is tangent).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove the power of a point in signed form: if a line through $A$ meets $\omega$ at $X$ and $Y$, and lengths along the line are measured with signs (positive in one fixed direction), then $\overline{AX}\cdot\overline{AY}=b^2-a^2$.
\item[(b)] Use (a) with the line $AB$ to prove the law of cosines $b^2=a^2+c^2-2ac\cos B$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Put a coordinate on the line, with $A$ at $0$. Let $H$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $C$ to the line, at coordinate $h$; then $AH=|h|$ and $AH^2+CH^2=b^2$. Since $CX=CY=a$, the foot $H$ is the midpoint of the chord $XY$. If $XY=2t$, then $t^2=a^2-CH^2$ by the Pythagorean theorem, and the coordinates of $X$ and $Y$ are $h-t$ and $h+t$. Hence
\[\overline{AX}\cdot\overline{AY}=(h-t)(h+t)=h^2-t^2=\left(b^2-CH^2\right)-\left(a^2-CH^2\right)=b^2-a^2.\]
The product is positive when $A$ is outside $\omega$ ($b>a$), negative when $A$ is inside ($b<a$), and $0$ when $A$ is on $\omega$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0); real a=3;
pair B=a*dir(150);
pair A=(5.2,B.y);
pair H=(0,B.y);
pair B2=(-B.x,B.y);
draw(circle(C,a),gray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(C--H,dashed); draw(H+(0.25,0)--H+(0.25,-0.25)--H+(0,-0.25));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(H);dot(B2,blue);
label("$A$",A,E); label("$B$",B,NW); label("$C$",C,S); label("$H$",H,N); label("$B'$",B2,N,blue);
label("$a$",(B+C)/2,SW); label("$b$",(A+C)/2,SE); label("$\omega$",a*dir(250),SW,gray);
label("$A$ outside $\omega$",(1,-3.3),S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0); real a=3;
pair B=a*dir(145);
pair A=(1.6,1.45);
pair u=unit(A-B); pair H=B+dot(C-B,u)*u;
pair B2=2*H-B;
draw(circle(C,a),gray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--B2,blue); draw(C--H,dashed);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(H);dot(B2,blue);
label("$A$",A,S); label("$B$",B,NW); label("$C$",C,S); label("$H$",H,N); label("$B'$",B2,NE,blue);
label("$A$ inside $\omega$",(0,-3.3),S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} Apply (a) to line $AB$, with the positive direction from $A$ to $B$, so that $B$ has coordinate $c$; let $H$ (coordinate $h$) be the foot of the perpendicular from $C$ to line $AB$. The vector from $B$ to $H$ is the projection of $\overrightarrow{BC}$ onto line $AB$; it has signed length $a\cos B$ in the direction from $B$ towards $A$. So $h=c-a\cos B$ (if $B$ is obtuse, $\cos B<0$ and $H$ lies beyond $B$). Since $H$ is the midpoint of $BB'$, the coordinate of $B'$ is $2h-c=c-2a\cos B$. By (a),
\[\overline{AB}\cdot\overline{AB'}=c\left(c-2a\cos B\right)=b^2-a^2,\]
that is, $b^2=a^2+c^2-2ac\cos B$. Both pictures (and the cases $A$ on $\omega$, or $\angle B=90^\circ$ with $B'=B$) are covered by the same signed computation. $\blacksquare$

A sum of $k$ odd numbers has the parity of $k$, and $49$ is odd, so $k$ is odd. Write each element as $2y-1$ with $y\in\{1,\dots,10\}$; a $k$-element subset with sum $49$ corresponds to $k$ distinct $y$'s with $2\sum y-k=49$, i.e. $\sum y=\dfrac{49+k}{2}$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $k=1$: impossible ($49>19$). $k=9$: the smallest sum is $1+3+\cdots+17=81>49$.
\item $k=3$: three distinct $y\le10$ with sum $26$. The largest possible sum is $8+9+10=27$, so we must lower it by exactly $1$: only $\{7,9,10\}$. \quad $1$ subset ($\{13,17,19\}$).
\item $k=7$: seven distinct $y$ with sum $28=1+2+\cdots+7$: only $\{1,\dots,7\}$. \quad $1$ subset ($\{1,3,\dots,13\}$).
\item $k=5$: five distinct $y_1<\cdots<y_5\le10$ with sum $27$. Put $z_i=y_i-i$; then $0\le z_1\le\cdots\le z_5\le5$ and $\sum z_i=27-15=12$. So we count partitions of $12$ into at most $5$ parts, each at most $5$. By the largest part:
\begin{itemize}
\item largest part $5$: the rest is a partition of $7$ into at most $4$ parts, each at most $5$: $5+2$, $5+1+1$, $4+3$, $4+2+1$, $4+1+1+1$, $3+3+1$, $3+2+2$, $3+2+1+1$, $2+2+2+1$: $9$;
\item largest part $4$: a partition of $8$ into at most $4$ parts, each at most $4$: $4+4$, $4+3+1$, $4+2+2$, $4+2+1+1$, $3+3+2$, $3+3+1+1$, $3+2+2+1$, $2+2+2+2$: $8$;
\item largest part $3$: a partition of $9$ into at most $4$ parts, each at most $3$: $3+3+3$, $3+3+2+1$, $3+2+2+2$: $3$;
\item largest part at most $2$: at most $5\cdot2=10<12$, none.
\end{itemize}
That gives $9+8+3=20$ subsets.
\end{itemize}
In total $1+20+1=\boxed{22}$.

\textbf{Algebra.} Expanding, the left side is $5x^2-(2a+4b)x+a^2+b^2$ and the right side is $5x^2-6x+9$. These agree for all $x$ exactly when
\[2a+4b=6\quad\text{and}\quad a^2+b^2=9.\]
From the first, $a=3-2b$; then $(3-2b)^2+b^2=9$ gives $5b^2-12b=0$, so $b=0$ or $b=\dfrac{12}{5}$. The pairs are
\[(a,b)=\boxed{(3,0)\ \text{and}\ \left(-\dfrac{9}{5},\dfrac{12}{5}\right)}.\]

\textbf{Geometry.} The left side is the squared distance from the point $T=(x,2x)$ to $(a,b)$, and the right side is the squared distance from $T$ to $(3,0)$. As $x$ varies, $T$ runs over the whole line $y=2x$. So the condition says: every point of the line $y=2x$ is equidistant from $(a,b)$ and $(3,0)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
draw((-3,0)--(4,0),gray,Arrow(5)); draw((0,-1)--(0,4),gray,Arrow(5));
draw((-0.4,-0.8)--(1.9,3.8),blue+linewidth(0.9));
pair P=(3,0), Q=(-9/5,12/5), M=(3/5,6/5);
draw(P--Q,red+dashed); dot(P,red); dot(Q,red); dot(M);
pair T=(1.5,3);
draw(T--P,gray); draw(T--Q,gray); dot(T);
label("$(3,0)$",P,S,red); label("$\left(-\dfrac{9}{5},\dfrac{12}{5}\right)$",Q,W,red);
label("$y=2x$",(1.9,3.8),E,blue); label("$(x,2x)$",T,E); label("$M$",M,dir(110));
\end{asy}

If $(a,b)\ne(3,0)$, the points equidistant from $(a,b)$ and $(3,0)$ form the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining them, which is a line. It contains the line $y=2x$, so it \emph{is} the line $y=2x$, and $(a,b)$ is the reflection of $(3,0)$ in $y=2x$. Indeed, the foot of the perpendicular from $(3,0)$ to $y=2x$ is $M=\left(\dfrac{3}{5},\dfrac{6}{5}\right)$, and $2M-(3,0)=\left(-\dfrac{9}{5},\dfrac{12}{5}\right)$. The other answer is $(a,b)=(3,0)$ itself.

Let the sides be $a,b,c$ with $a+b+c=2s$, and let $K$ be the area.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(6,0), C=(2,3.5);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
pair Ae=(0.5,4.8), Be=(5.5,4.8);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N);
label("$c$",(A+B)/2,S); label("$a$",(B+C)/2,NE); label("$b$",(A+C)/2,NW);
label("$a+b+c=2s$ fixed",(3,-0.9));
\end{asy}

By Heron's formula, $K^2=s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)$, where $s-a$, $s-b$, $s-c$ are positive (triangle inequality) and
\[(s-a)+(s-b)+(s-c)=3s-2s=s.\]
By the AM-GM inequality for these three positive numbers,
\[(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)\le\left(\dfrac{(s-a)+(s-b)+(s-c)}{3}\right)^3=\dfrac{s^3}{27}.\]
Hence $K^2\le\dfrac{s^4}{27}$, i.e.
\[K\le\dfrac{s^2}{3\sqrt3}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt3\,s^2}{9}},\]
with equality exactly when $s-a=s-b=s-c$, that is, $a=b=c=\dfrac{2s}{3}$. So among triangles with perimeter $2s$, the equilateral one has the largest area. (Check: an equilateral triangle of side $\dfrac{2s}{3}$ has area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot\dfrac{4s^2}{9}=\dfrac{\sqrt3\,s^2}{9}$.) $\blacksquare$

question:
Delegates from $9$ countries, one from each country and including countries $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, are to be seated.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] In how many ways can they sit in a row if the delegates of $A$ and $B$ sit next to each other and the delegates of $C$ and $D$ do not?
\item[(b)] In how many ways can they sit at a round table under the same conditions, if seatings that differ by a rotation are the same?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the circles have areas $P$ (larger) and $Q$ (smaller), and let $x$ be the area of $X$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O1=(0,0), O2=(3.2,0); real r1=2.6, r2=1.7;
fill(circle(O1,r1),palegreen); fill(circle(O2,r2),paleblue);
picture p; fill(p,circle(O1,r1),gray(0.75)); clip(p,circle(O2,r2)); add(p);
draw(circle(O1,r1)); draw(circle(O2,r2));
label("$B$",(-1,0)); label("$C$",(4.2,0)); label("$X$",(2.05,0));
\end{asy}

Then $B=P-x$ and $C=Q-x$, so
\[B-C=(P-x)-(Q-x)=P-Q.\]
This does not depend on $x$. Since the sizes of the circles do not change, $B-C$ changes by $\boxed{0\text{\%}}$: it stays equal to $P-Q$ (which is nonzero, so the percent change is defined).

question:
The ten letters of MATHCOUNTS are arranged in a random order, all distinct arrangements being equally likely.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the probability that the word MATCH appears as five consecutive letters somewhere in the arrangement?
\item[(b)] A student computes $\dfrac{6!}{10!}=\dfrac{1}{5040}$. Find the error.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Colour the grid like a chessboard: $8$ dark and $8$ light squares.

\textbf{Necessary.} Each domino covers one dark and one light square, so the $14$ remaining squares must be $7$ dark and $7$ light. Hence the two removed squares have different colours: at most $8\cdot8=64$ pairs.

\textbf{Sufficient.} The $16$ squares can be visited by a closed path that moves between squares sharing a side and visits each square exactly once (red below). Along this path the colours alternate.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<4;++i) for(int j=0;j<4;++j) if((i+j)%2==0) fill(shift(i,j)*unitsquare,gray(0.8));
for(int i=0;i<=4;++i){ draw((i,0)--(i,4)); draw((0,i)--(4,i)); }
pair[] P={(0,0),(1,0),(2,0),(3,0),(3,1),(2,1),(1,1),(1,2),(2,2),(3,2),(3,3),(2,3),(1,3),(0,3),(0,2),(0,1)};
path c; for(int k=0;k<16;++k) c=c--(P[k]+(0.5,0.5));
draw(c--cycle,red+linewidth(1));
\end{asy}

Remove a dark and a light square. They are at positions of different parity along the cycle of $16$ squares, so each of the (at most two) arcs of the cycle between them contains an even number of squares. Each arc is a path of adjacent squares, which can be covered by dominoes on consecutive squares. So every one of the $64$ pairs works.

The answer is $\boxed{64}$.

\textbf{Numbers below $2^{11}=2048$.} A number with exactly $d$ binary digits starts with $1$, followed by $d-1$ free digits. If $k$ of the free digits are $1$, it has $k+1$ ones and $d-1-k$ zeros, so we need $k+1>d-1-k$, i.e. $k\ge\left\lfloor\dfrac {d}{2}\right\rfloor$. By the symmetry $\dbinom{d-1}{k}=\dbinom{d-1}{d-1-k}$ of the binomial coefficients:
\begin{itemize}
\item $d=2m+2$ even: $k\ge m+1$ is exactly half of the $2^{2m+1}$ strings, $2^{2m}$;
\item $d=2m+1$ odd: $k\ge m$ is half of the strings plus half of the middle term, $\dfrac{2^{2m}+\dbinom{2m}{m}}{2}$.
\end{itemize}
For $d=1,\dots,11$ this gives $1,1,3,4,11,16,42,64,163,256,638$, with sum $1199$.

\textbf{Remove $2004$ to $2047$.} Since $2003=11111010011_2$, these $44$ numbers are $11111$ followed by the six-digit strings from $010100_2=20$ to $111111_2=63$. Each has five $1$'s at the front and at least one more, hence at least $6$ ones among $11$ digits: more $1$'s than $0$'s. So all $44$ are counted above, and
\[N=1199-44=1155.\]
The remainder is $\boxed{155}$.

question:
A teacher runs four special lessons, one each in maths, music, English and science, for some of the children in her class. For the children attending these lessons:
\begin{itemize}
\item there are exactly $3$ children in each lesson;
\item every two of these children attend at least one lesson together.
\end{itemize}
What is the largest possible number of children attending the special lessons?

solution:
There are $\dfrac{5!}{2!}=60$ numbers. Count them by first digit:
\begin{itemize}
\item first digit $1$: the rest is an arrangement of $3,6,6,8$: $\dfrac{4!}{2!}=12$ numbers (positions $1$-$12$);
\item first digit $3$: arrangements of $1,6,6,8$: $12$ numbers ($13$-$24$);
\item first digit $6$: arrangements of $1,3,6,8$: $4!=24$ numbers ($25$-$48$);
\item first digit $8$: arrangements of $1,3,6,6$: $12$ numbers ($49$-$60$).
\end{itemize}
So the $52$nd number is the $4$th one starting with $8$. The arrangements of $1,3,6,6$ in increasing order begin $1366$, $1636$, $1663$, $3166$, so the answer is $\boxed{83166}$.

question:
George puts $30$ jellybeans into a jar, $3$, $4$ or $5$ at a time.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] In how many ways can he do it, if the order of the handfuls matters (for example, $3$ then $4$ is different from $4$ then $3$)?
\item[(b)] In how many ways if only the numbers of handfuls of each size matter?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The radius is $6$. The distance from $O$ to $CD$ is $\sqrt{6^2-\left(3\sqrt3\right)^2}=\sqrt{36-27}=3$, so in the right triangle with hypotenuse $OD=6$ and leg $3$ the angle at $O$ between $OD$ and the perpendicular is $60^\circ$, and $\angle COD=120^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); real R=6;
pair A=(-6,0), B=(6,0), C=(-3*sqrt(3),3), D=(3*sqrt(3),3);
fill(A--B--arc(O,R,0,30)--D--C--arc(O,R,150,180)--cycle,lightgray);
fill(A--B--D--C--cycle,lightgray);
draw(circle(O,R)); draw(A--B,linewidth(0.9)); draw(C--D,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--C,blue+dashed); draw(O--D,blue+dashed); draw(O--(0,3),red+dashed);
dot(O);dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,NW); label("$D$",D,NE); label("$O$",O,S);
label("$3$",(0,1.5),E,red); label("$6$",(O+D)/2,SE,blue);
\end{asy}

The region is the half disc on the side of $CD$ minus the circular segment beyond $CD$. The segment has area
\[\dfrac{120}{360}\cdot36\pi-\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot6\cdot6\sin120^\circ=12\pi-9\sqrt3.\]
So the region has area
\[18\pi-\left(12\pi-9\sqrt3\right)=6\pi+9\sqrt3,\]
and $a+b+c=6+9+3=\boxed{18}$.

question:
Cardano's formula gives the real root of $x^3+6x=20$ as
\[x=\sqrt[3]{10+\sqrt{108}}-\sqrt[3]{-10+\sqrt{108}}.\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $\left(1+\sqrt3\right)^3=10+\sqrt{108}$ and $\left(\sqrt3-1\right)^3=-10+\sqrt{108}$, and deduce that the expression equals $2$.
\item[(b)] Give a second proof: show that $x$ satisfies $x^3+6x-20=0$ and that $2$ is the only real root of this equation.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $r$ be the radius. The circle in the corner at $A$ is tangent to both sides at $A$, so its centre $O_1$ is on the bisector of the $60^\circ$ angle at $A$, at height $r$ above $AB$. In the right triangle formed by $A$, $O_1$ and the foot of the perpendicular from $O_1$ to $AB$, the angle at $A$ is $30^\circ$, so the foot is at distance $r\sqrt3$ from $A$. The same holds at $B$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=sqrt(3); real s=6+2*sqrt(3);
pair A=(0,0), B=(s,0), C=(s/2,s*sqrt(3)/2);
pair O1=(r*sqrt(3),r), O2=(s-r*sqrt(3),r), O3=(s/2,s*sqrt(3)/2-2*r);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O1,r),blue); draw(circle(O2,r),blue); draw(circle(O3,r),blue);
draw(O1--O2,red+dashed); draw(O1--(O1.x,0),red); draw(A--O1,gray+dashed);
dot(O1);dot(O2);dot(O3);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N);
label("$O_1$",O1,NW); label("$O_2$",O2,NE); label("$O_3$",O3,N);
label("$r$",(O1.x,r/2),E,red); label("$2r$",O1+0.3*(O2-O1),N,red);
label("$r\sqrt3$",(r*sqrt(3)/2,0),S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$r\sqrt3$",(s-r*sqrt(3)/2,0),S,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The centres $O_1$ and $O_2$ are both at height $r$ above $AB$, so $O_1O_2=AB-2r\sqrt3$, and tangency of the two circles means $O_1O_2=2r$. Hence
\[6+2\sqrt3=2r+2r\sqrt3=2r\left(1+\sqrt3\right)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad r=\dfrac{3+\sqrt3}{1+\sqrt3}=\dfrac{\sqrt3\left(\sqrt3+1\right)}{1+\sqrt3}=\sqrt3.\]
By symmetry the same $r$ works for the other two pairs. The total area is
\[3\cdot\pi\left(\sqrt3\right)^2=\boxed{9\pi}.\]

question:
Each of the two equal sides of an isosceles triangle is $7$ inches longer than its base. Two copies of this triangle are put together along their bases, and they form a rhombus with perimeter $136$ inches.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the perimeter of one of the isosceles triangles.
\item[(b)] The original version of this problem said that the two triangles form a \emph{square}. Explain why that is impossible.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} When the triangles are joined along their bases, the common base becomes a diagonal of the rhombus, and the four sides of the rhombus are the four equal sides (legs) of the two triangles.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=sqrt(34^2-13.5^2);
pair P=(-13.5,0), Q=(13.5,0), T=(0,h), U=(0,-h);
fill(P--T--Q--cycle,paleblue); fill(P--U--Q--cycle,paleyellow);
draw(P--T--Q--U--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--Q,red+dashed+linewidth(0.8));
label("$34$",(P+T)/2,NW); label("$34$",(Q+T)/2,NE); label("$34$",(P+U)/2,SW); label("$34$",(Q+U)/2,SE);
label("$27$",(4,0),N,red);
dot(P); dot(Q); dot(T); dot(U);
\end{asy}

So each leg is $\dfrac{136}{4}=34$ inches, the base is $34-7=27$ inches, and the perimeter of one triangle is
\[34+34+27=\boxed{95}\text{ inches}.\]
(The triangle exists, since $27<34+34$.)

\textbf{(b)} If the figure were a square with side $34$, the glued base would be a diagonal of that square, so by the Pythagorean theorem it would have length $\sqrt{34^2+34^2}=34\sqrt2\approx48.1$ inches. That is \emph{longer} than the legs, but the base must be $7$ inches \emph{shorter} than each leg. In general, two congruent isosceles triangles joined along their bases form a square only when they are right isosceles triangles, whose base is $\sqrt2$ times the leg; here the base is shorter than the leg, so the figure is a rhombus that is not a square.

Let Bo start at $A$ and walk along $AB$ and $BC$ (each $2$ miles) and then $1$ mile along the next side, to its midpoint $P$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V; V[0]=(0,0);
for(int k=1;k<8;++k) V[k]=V[k-1]+2*dir(45*(k-1));
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--V[6]--V[7]--cycle,gray);
pair Pp=(V[2]+V[3])/2;
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--Pp,blue+linewidth(1.2));
draw(V[0]--Pp,red+dashed+linewidth(0.9));
draw(V[2]--(V[2].x,0)--V[1],dotted);
draw(Pp--(Pp.x,0),dotted);
dot(V[0]); dot(V[1]); dot(V[2]); dot(Pp,red);
label("$A$",V[0],SW); label("$B$",V[1],S); label("$C$",V[2],E); label("$P$",Pp,E,red);
label("$2$",(V[0]+V[1])/2,S); label("$2$",(V[1]+V[2])/2,SE); label("$1$",(V[2]+Pp)/2,E);
label("$\sqrt2$",((V[1].x+V[2].x)/2,0),S,fontsize(8pt));
label("$45^\circ$",V[1]+(0.35,0.12),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Each exterior angle of a regular octagon is $\dfrac{360^\circ}{8}=45^\circ$. Put $A=(0,0)$ and $B=(2,0)$. Side $BC$ makes a $45^\circ$ angle with the $x$-axis, so $C=\left(2+\sqrt2,\ \sqrt2\right)$ (the legs of a $45$-$45$-$90$ triangle with hypotenuse $2$ are $\sqrt2$). The next side turns another $45^\circ$, so it is vertical, and $P=\left(2+\sqrt2,\ \sqrt2+1\right)$. Hence
\[AP^2=\left(2+\sqrt2\right)^2+\left(1+\sqrt2\right)^2=\left(6+4\sqrt2\right)+\left(3+2\sqrt2\right)=9+6\sqrt2 .\]
Since $\left(\sqrt3+\sqrt6\right)^2=9+2\sqrt{18}=9+6\sqrt2$, Bo is
\[AP=\sqrt{9+6\sqrt2}=\boxed{\sqrt3+\sqrt6}\approx4.18\text{ miles}\]
from his starting point.

Let Peppa start at $A$, walk along $AB$ and $BC$ (each $2$ miles) and then $1$ mile along $CD$, stopping at the midpoint $P$ of $CD$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V; V[0]=(0,0);
for(int k=1;k<6;++k) V[k]=V[k-1]+2*dir(60*(k-1));
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,gray);
pair Pp=(V[2]+V[3])/2;
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--Pp,blue+linewidth(1.2));
draw(V[0]--Pp,red+dashed+linewidth(0.9));
dot(V[0]); dot(V[1]); dot(V[2]); dot(V[3]); dot(Pp,red);
label("$A$",V[0],SW); label("$B$",V[1],SE); label("$C$",V[2],E); label("$D$",V[3],N); label("$P$",Pp,NE,red);
label("$2$",(V[0]+V[1])/2,S); label("$2$",(V[1]+V[2])/2,E); label("$1$",(V[2]+Pp)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

The exterior angles of a regular hexagon are $60^\circ$. Put $A=(0,0)$ and $B=(2,0)$. Then
\[C=B+\left(2\cos60^\circ,\ 2\sin60^\circ\right)=\left(3,\ \sqrt3\right),\]
and $CD$ points in the direction $120^\circ$, so
\[P=C+\left(\cos120^\circ,\ \sin120^\circ\right)=\left(3-\dfrac{1}{2},\ \sqrt3+\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)=\left(\dfrac{5}{2},\ \dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}\right).\]
Therefore
\[AP=\sqrt{\dfrac{25}{4}+\dfrac{27}{4}}=\boxed{\sqrt{13}}\approx3.61\text{ miles}.\]

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Treating $n$ as a positive constant, write $\dfrac{1}{mn(m+n+1)}$ in the form $\dfrac{A}{m}+\dfrac{B}{m+n+1}$, where $A$ and $B$ depend only on $n$.
\item[(b)] Use (a) to evaluate
\[\sum_{m=1}^{\infty}\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\dfrac{1}{mn(m+n+1)} .\]
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Check $n=1,2,3,\dots$ in turn:
\begin{itemize}
\item $n=1$: $3-2=1$, not divisible by $2$;
\item $n=2$: $9-4=5$, not divisible by $3$;
\item $n=3$: $27-8=19$, not divisible by $4$;
\item $n=4$: $81-16=65=5\cdot13$, divisible by $5$.
\end{itemize}
So the answer is $\boxed{4}$.

Measure angles clockwise from the $12$. The minute hand points at the $6$, at $180^\circ$. The hour hand moves $30^\circ$ per hour, and at $7{:}30$ it is halfway between the $7$ and the $8$, at $7.5\cdot30^\circ=225^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),1),linewidth(0.9));
for(int k=0;k<12;++k){ pair d=dir(90-30*k); draw(0.9*d--d); label("$"+string(k==0?12:k)+"$",0.78*d,fontsize(8pt)); }
pair hh=0.48*dir(90-225), mm=0.66*dir(90-180);
draw((0,0)--hh,linewidth(1.6)); draw((0,0)--mm,blue+linewidth(1.1));
draw(arc((0,0),0.22,-90,-135),red,Arrows(4));
label("$45^\circ$",0.36*dir(-112.5),red+fontsize(8pt));
dot((0,0));
\end{asy}

The smaller angle between the hands is $225^\circ-180^\circ=\boxed{45^\circ}$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that there is no integer $N>1$ that leaves remainder $1$ when divided by \emph{every} integer greater than $1$.
\item[(b)] Find the smallest integer $N>1$ that leaves remainder $1$ when divided by each of $2,3,4,\dots,10$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The painted small cubes are the ones lying along the edges of the big cube (shaded below on the three visible faces).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=5; pair ox=(1,0), oy=(0.45,0.32), oz=(0,1);
pair p(real x,real y,real z){return x*ox+y*oy+z*oz;}
// front face cells (y=0), top face (z=5), right face (x=5)
for(int i=0;i<5;++i) for(int k=0;k<5;++k){
  bool e=(i==0||i==4||k==0||k==4);
  path c=p(i,0,k)--p(i+1,0,k)--p(i+1,0,k+1)--p(i,0,k+1)--cycle;
  if(e) fill(c,paleblue); draw(c,gray(0.4));
}
for(int i=0;i<5;++i) for(int j=0;j<5;++j){
  bool e=(i==0||i==4||j==0||j==4);
  path c=p(i,j,5)--p(i+1,j,5)--p(i+1,j+1,5)--p(i,j+1,5)--cycle;
  if(e) fill(c,paleblue); draw(c,gray(0.4));
}
for(int j=0;j<5;++j) for(int k=0;k<5;++k){
  bool e=(j==0||j==4||k==0||k==4);
  path c=p(5,j,k)--p(5,j+1,k)--p(5,j+1,k+1)--p(5,j,k+1)--cycle;
  if(e) fill(c,paleblue); draw(c,gray(0.4));
}
draw(p(0,0,0)--p(5,0,0)--p(5,0,5)--p(0,0,5)--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(p(0,0,5)--p(0,5,5)--p(5,5,5)--p(5,0,5),linewidth(1));
draw(p(5,0,0)--p(5,5,0)--p(5,5,5),linewidth(1));
label("$5$",p(2.5,0,0),S); label("$5$",p(5,2.5,0),SE); label("$5$",p(0,0,2.5),W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Corners and edges.} Each of the $8$ corner cubes is painted. Each of the $12$ edges also contains $5-2=3$ non-corner cubes, which lie on that edge only. So the number of painted cubes is
\[8+12\cdot3=\boxed{44}.\]
(Equivalently, $12$ edges with $5$ cubes each give $60$, but each corner cube lies on $3$ edges and has been counted $3$ times, so we subtract $2\cdot8=16$: $60-16=44$.)

question:
Consider the sequence
\[0,\ 1,\ 4,\ 18,\ 96,\ 600,\ 4320,\ 35280,\ \dots\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Each term is a whole number times a factorial. Find a simple formula for the $k$th term that fits all eight given terms, and use it to find the $15$th term.
\item[(b)] Prove that, for this formula, the sum of the first $m$ terms is $m!-1$, and find the sum of the first $15$ terms.
\end{enumerate}

A polygon is \emph{equiangular} if all of its interior angles are equal.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Must an equiangular $17$-gon have all of its sides equal? Prove your answer.
\item[(b)] Suppose an equiangular $17$-gon has all side lengths rational. Prove that it is regular. You may use the fact that the polynomial $1+x+x^2+\cdots+x^{16}$ cannot be written as a product of two non-constant polynomials with rational coefficients.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} No. Start with a regular $17$-gon $P_0P_1\cdots P_{16}$ with side $1$. Move the line $P_0P_1$ parallel to itself a small distance into the polygon, so that it cuts the sides $P_{16}P_0$ and $P_1P_2$ at points $Q_0$ and $Q_1$, and cut off the strip between the two lines (shaded grey).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int n=17; real R=1/(2*sin(pi/n));
pair[] P; for(int k=0;k<n;++k) P[k]=R*dir(-90-180/n+360*k/n);
real hh=0.28;
pair Q0=extension(P[16],P[0],(0,P[0].y+hh),(1,P[0].y+hh));
pair Q1=extension(P[1],P[2],(0,P[1].y+hh),(1,P[1].y+hh));
path poly=Q0--Q1; for(int k=2;k<n;++k) poly=poly--P[k]; poly=poly--cycle;
fill(poly,paleyellow);
fill(P[0]--P[1]--Q1--Q0--cycle,lightgray);
draw(poly,linewidth(1));
draw(Q0--P[0]--P[1]--Q1,gray+dashed);
draw(Q0--Q1,blue+linewidth(1.3));
dot(P[0]); dot(P[1]); dot(Q0,red); dot(Q1,red); dot(P[2]); dot(P[16]);
label("$P_0$",P[0],S,fontsize(8pt)); label("$P_1$",P[1],S,fontsize(8pt));
label("$P_2$",P[2],E,fontsize(8pt)); label("$P_{16}$",P[16],W,fontsize(8pt));
label("$Q_0$",Q0,N,red+fontsize(8pt)); label("$Q_1$",Q1,N,red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

In the convex polygon $Q_0Q_1P_2P_3\cdots P_{16}$, every angle is formed by the same pair of lines as in the regular $17$-gon, except at $Q_0$ and $Q_1$, where the line $P_0P_1$ has been replaced by a parallel line; corresponding angles at parallel lines are equal, so all the angles are unchanged and the new $17$-gon is still equiangular. But $Q_1P_2<P_1P_2=1=P_3P_4$, so its sides are not all equal.

\textbf{(b)} Walk around the polygon counterclockwise. At each vertex the direction turns by the exterior angle $\dfrac{360^\circ}{17}$. Place the polygon in the complex plane and let $\omega=\cos\dfrac{2\pi}{17}+i\sin\dfrac{2\pi}{17}$. If the side lengths in order are $a_0,a_1,\dots,a_{16}$, the sides are the complex numbers $a_k\omega^k u$ for a fixed $u$ with $|u|=1$, and since the walk returns to its start,
\[a_0+a_1\omega+a_2\omega^2+\cdots+a_{16}\omega^{16}=0 .\]
Also $\omega^{17}=1$ and $\omega\ne1$, so $1+\omega+\cdots+\omega^{16}=\dfrac{\omega^{17}-1}{\omega-1}=0$. Hence the polynomial
\[P(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{15}\left(a_k-a_{16}\right)x^k,\]
which has rational coefficients and degree at most $15$, satisfies $P(\omega)=\sum_{k=0}^{16}a_k\omega^k-a_{16}\sum_{k=0}^{16}\omega^k=0$.

Let $\Phi(x)=1+x+\cdots+x^{16}$ and suppose $P$ is not the zero polynomial. The Euclidean algorithm gives the greatest common divisor $D$ of $P$ and $\Phi$ with rational coefficients, and $D(\omega)=0$ because $\omega$ is a root of both; so $D$ is not constant. Since $D$ divides $\Phi$ and $\Phi$ has no factorisation into two non-constant rational polynomials, $D$ is a constant multiple of $\Phi$. Then $\Phi$ divides $P$, which is impossible because $P\ne0$ has degree at most $15<16$. Therefore $P=0$, so $a_k=a_{16}$ for every $k$: all sides are equal, and the equiangular $17$-gon is regular.

\emph{Remark.} This is where $17$ being prime matters: for $6$ sides, the equiangular hexagon with sides $1,2,1,2,1,2$ has rational sides but is not regular.

