In the right triangle $AMX$, $AM=93$ million miles and $\angle AXM=\dfrac{\theta}{2}=2.1345\times10^{-4}$ degrees, so
\[XM=\dfrac{AM}{\tan\left(\dfrac{\theta}{2}\right)} .\]
In radians, $\dfrac{\theta}{2}=2.1345\times10^{-4}\cdot\dfrac{\pi}{180}\approx3.7254\times10^{-6}$. For an angle this small, $\tan x$ agrees with $x$ (in radians) to about $11$ significant figures, so
\[XM\approx\dfrac{9.3\times10^{7}}{3.7254\times10^{-6}}\approx2.4964\times10^{13}\text{ miles}\approx\dfrac{2.4964\times10^{13}}{5.879\times10^{12}}\text{ light-years}\approx\boxed{4.25}\text{ light-years}.\]

\emph{About the wording.} The distances from the star to $A$, to $M$ and to $B$ differ by less than $10^{-11}$ of their size ($XA=\dfrac{XM}{\cos(\theta\div2)}$), so it does not matter which point of the orbit we measure from. The assumption $XA=XB$ supplies the missing information: one side and one angle do not determine a general triangle, but they do determine an isosceles one.

The equation is unchanged if $(x,y,z)$ is replaced by $(y,z,x)$, so we may rotate any solution.

\textbf{Case 1: some variable is $1$.} Rotate so that $x=1$. Then $1+y^z+z=1230$, that is, $y^z+z=1229$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $y=1$, then $z=1228$.
\item If $y\ge2$ and $z=1$, then $y=1228$.
\item If $y\ge2$ and $z\ge2$, then $y^z=1229-z$, so $1229-z$ would have to be a perfect $z$th power. It is not, because it lies strictly between two consecutive $z$th powers: $35^2<1227<36^2$, $10^3<1226<11^3$, $5^4<1225<6^4$, $4^5<1224<5^5$, $3^6<1223<4^6$, and $2^z\le1024<1229-z<2187\le3^z$ for $7\le z\le10$. For $z\ge11$, $y^z\ge2^{11}=2048>1229$. So there is no solution here.
\end{itemize}
This gives $(1,1,1228)$ and $(1,1228,1)$ and their rotations, that is, $(1,1,1228)$, $(1,1228,1)$ and $(1228,1,1)$.

\textbf{Case 2: $x,y,z\ge2$.} Since $x^y\equiv x\pmod2$ and so on, $x+y+z\equiv1230\equiv0\pmod2$, so an even number of the variables are odd. If all three were even, each term would be divisible by $4$, but $1230\equiv2\pmod4$. So exactly one variable is even; rotate so that $y$ is even and $x,z$ are odd, hence $x,z\ge3$. Modulo $8$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $x^y$ is an odd square, so $x^y\equiv1$;
\item $y^z$ with $y$ even and $z\ge3$ is divisible by $8$;
\item $z^x=z\cdot\left(z^2\right)^{(x-1)\div2}\equiv z$, since odd squares are $\equiv1$.
\end{itemize}
So $1230\equiv1+0+z\pmod8$, giving $z\equiv5\pmod8$. As $z^3\le z^x<1230$, $z\le10$, so $z=5$. Then $5^x<1230$ forces $x\le4$, so $x=3$, and the equation becomes $3^y+y^5=1230-125=1105$. With $y$ even and $y^5<1105$, $y\in\{2,4\}$: $y=2$ gives $41$, and $y=4$ gives $81+1024=1105$. So $(x,y,z)=(3,4,5)$, together with its rotations $(4,5,3)$ and $(5,3,4)$.

\textbf{Answer.} The solutions are
\[(3,4,5),\ (4,5,3),\ (5,3,4),\ (1,1,1228),\ (1,1228,1),\ (1228,1,1).\]

