The circle with centre $P$ and radius $1$ is inscribed in the small square, so it lies inside the big square. Hence $P$ is at distance at least $1$ from each side of the big square: $1\le P_x\le2$ and $1\le P_y\le2$ ($P$ lies in the dashed green square). Then
\[PO\le\sqrt{\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^2}=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}<1,\]
so $O$ lies strictly inside the inscribed circle, and therefore in the interior of the small square.

So \emph{every} square of side $2$ inside the big square contains $O$ in its interior. Two such squares would share the point $O$ of their interiors, so they would overlap. Hence at most one fits, and one clearly fits. The answer is $\boxed{1}$, as the poster's intuition says.

