Call the four corner squares \emph{corners}, the four squares in the middle of the sides \emph{edges}, and the middle square the \emph{centre}. Turns and flips map corners to corners and edges to edges, and they can take any corner to any other corner and any edge to any other edge. Sort the pairs of shaded squares by type.
\begin{itemize}
\item Centre and a corner: $1$ pattern. Centre and an edge: $1$ pattern.
\item Two corners: they are on a common side or opposite: $2$ patterns.
\item Two edges: they are next to a common corner or opposite: $2$ patterns.
\item A corner and an edge: the edge touches the corner (shares a side with it) or does not: $2$ patterns. (A flip in the diagonal through the corner swaps the two touching edges, and also the two other edges.)
\end{itemize}
Patterns in different bullet points or with different descriptions cannot be matched, because turns and flips preserve the types of squares and whether two squares share a side, lie on a common side of the grid, or are opposite.

