\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that at most four points in space can all be at the same distance from one another. (So the $7.7$ billion people on Earth cannot all be the same distance from everyone else.)
\item[(b)] In 2019 the world population was about $7.7\times10^{9}$ and the surface area of the Earth is about $5.101\times10^{14}$ square metres. Suppose the surface were divided into $7.7\times10^9$ equal squares (ignoring the curvature of the Earth), with one person at the centre of each square. About how far apart would neighbouring people be?
