\textbf{(b)} Each square has area $\dfrac{5.101\times10^{14}}{7.7\times10^{9}}\approx6.62\times10^{4}$ square metres, so its side is about $\sqrt{66250}\approx257$ metres. Neighbouring people would be about $\boxed{257\text{ metres}}$ apart.

No. In a non-degenerate triangle each side is shorter than the sum of the other two (the triangle inequality is strict). Here the two shorter sides add up to exactly the longest:
\[(x-1)+1=x .\]
So if we try to build the triangle $ABC$ with $AB=x$, $AC=x-1$ and $CB=1$, the point $C$ must lie on the segment $AB$, and the ``triangle'' collapses into a segment, however large $x$ is.

