dot(P); dot(Q); label("$P$",P,NE,fontsize(8pt)); label("$Q$",Q,dir(10),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{The diagonal.} In triangle $ABP$, $\angle BAP=\angle ABP=36^\circ$, so it is similar to triangle $ACB$ (angles $36^\circ,36^\circ,108^\circ$). Hence $\dfrac{AP}{AB}=\dfrac{AB}{AC}$, so $AP=\dfrac{n^2}{d}$. In triangle $ABQ$, $\angle BAQ=36^\circ$ and $\angle ABQ=72^\circ$, so $\angle AQB=72^\circ$ and $AQ=AB=n$; by symmetry $CP=n$ as well. So $AP=d-n$, and $(d-n)d=n^2$, giving $d=\dfrac{1+\sqrt5}{2}\,n$.

\textbf{The inner side.} The inner side is $PQ=AQ-AP=n-(d-n)=2n-d=\dfrac{3-\sqrt5}{2}\,n$.

\textbf{The area.} The inner pentagon is regular, so it is similar to $ABCDE$ with ratio $\dfrac{3-\sqrt5}{2}$, and its area is $\left(\dfrac{3-\sqrt5}{2}\right)^2=\dfrac{7-3\sqrt5}{2}$ times the area of $ABCDE$. A regular pentagon with side $s$ has area $\dfrac{s^2}{4}\sqrt{25+10\sqrt5}$ (five triangles from the centre, each with base $s$ and height $\dfrac{s}{2}\cot36^\circ$). Therefore the inner pentagon has area
\[\dfrac{7-3\sqrt5}{2}\cdot\dfrac{n^2}{4}\sqrt{25+10\sqrt5}=\boxed{\dfrac{(7-3\sqrt5)\sqrt{25+10\sqrt5}}{8}\,n^2}=\dfrac{\sqrt{250-110\sqrt5}}{8}\,n^2\approx0.251\,n^2 .\]

The vertices of the cube lie on the sphere, so a space diagonal of the cube is a diameter, of length $10$. The picture shows the cross-section through two opposite edges of the cube: a rectangle with sides $s$ and $s\sqrt2$ (a face diagonal), whose diagonal is the space diagonal.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=5, s=10/sqrt(3);
draw(circle((0,0),R),linewidth(1));
pair A=(-s*sqrt(2)/2,-s/2), B=(s*sqrt(2)/2,-s/2), C=(s*sqrt(2)/2,s/2), D=(-s*sqrt(2)/2,s/2);
fill(A--B--C--D--cycle,paleblue); draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,blue+linewidth(1));
draw(A--C,red+dashed);
label("$s$",(B+C)/2,E,blue); label("$s\sqrt2$",(A+B)/2,S,blue);
label("$10$",(0,0.35),NW,red);
dot((0,0));
\end{asy}

By the Pythagorean theorem, $s^2+\left(s\sqrt2\right)^2=10^2$, so $3s^2=100$ and $s=\dfrac{10}{\sqrt3}$. The cube's volume is $s^3=\dfrac{1000}{3\sqrt3}=\dfrac{1000\sqrt3}{9}$, and the sphere's volume is $\dfrac{4}{3}\pi\cdot5^3=\dfrac{500\pi}{3}$. The required volume is
\[\boxed{\dfrac{500\pi}{3}-\dfrac{1000\sqrt3}{9}}\approx331.1 .\]

For fixed $x$ and $y$, the equation $w+z=r$ with $r=15-x-y\ge0$ has $r+1$ solutions in nonnegative integers. Write $y=2j+1$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $x=0$: $r=14-2j$ with $j=0,1,\dots,7$, giving $15+13+\cdots+1=64$ solutions.
\item $x=1$: $r=13-2j$ with $j=0,1,\dots,6$, giving $14+12+\cdots+2=56$ solutions.
\end{itemize}
In total there are $64+56=\boxed{120}$ solutions.

If the triangle has area $K$, the altitude to a side of length $a$ is $h=\dfrac{2K}{a}$. So the altitudes are inversely proportional to the sides: the longest altitude $h_2$ is the one to the shortest side (length $2$), and the shortest altitude $h_4$ is the one to the longest side (length $4$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(4,0), C=(11/8,sqrt(135)/8);
pair Fc=(C.x,0);
pair u=unit(C-A); pair Fb=A+dot(B-A,u)*u;
draw(C--Fb,gray+dashed);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(C--Fc,blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(B--Fb,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(Fc+(0.12,0)--Fc+(0.12,0.12)--Fc+(0,0.12),blue);
pair v=unit(B-Fb); draw(Fb+0.12*u*(-1)--Fb+0.12*(-u)+0.12*v--Fb+0.12*v,red);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NW);
label("$4$",(A+B)/2,S); label("$2$",(A+C)/2,NW); label("$3$",(B+C)/2,SW);
label("$h_4$",(Fc+C)/2,W,blue+fontsize(8pt)); label("$h_2$",(B+Fb)/2,NE,red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Their ratio is
\[\dfrac{h_2}{h_4}=\dfrac{2K\div2}{2K\div4}=\dfrac{4}{2}=\boxed{2}.\]
(The angle opposite the side $4$ is obtuse, since $4^2>2^2+3^2$, so the altitude to the side $2$ falls outside the triangle; this does not affect the argument.)

Let Alice's rectangle be $L$ by $W$. Its perimeter gives $L+W=7$. Bob's rectangle is $2L$ by $\dfrac {W}{3}$, so $2L\cdot\dfrac {W}{3}=8$ and $LW=12$. So $L$ and $W$ are the roots of $t^2-7t+12=(t-3)(t-4)=0$, namely $3$ and $4$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(4,0)--(4,3)--(0,3)--cycle,blue+linewidth(1));
label("$4$",(2,0),S); label("$3$",(4,1.5),E); label("Alice",(2,1.5),blue);
draw((6,0)--(14,0)--(14,1)--(6,1)--cycle,red+linewidth(1));
label("$8$",(10,0),S); label("$1$",(14,0.5),E); label("Bob",(10,0.5),red);
\end{asy}

Either way the sides of Alice's rectangle are $3$ and $4$, so its longer side is $\boxed{4}$. (With $L=4$, $W=3$, Bob's rectangle is $8$ by $1$, as shown.)

question:
A rectangular grid of unit squares is $m$ squares wide and $n$ squares tall. Consider rectangles whose sides lie on the grid lines.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many of them are $a$ units wide and $b$ units tall, where $1\le a\le m$ and $1\le b\le n$?
\item[(b)] How many rectangles of all sizes are there?
\item[(c)] How many $2\times3$ rectangles, in either orientation, are there on an $8\times8$ chessboard?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Number the vertical grid lines $0,1,\dots,m$ from left to right and the horizontal grid lines $0,1,\dots,n$ from bottom to top.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<=6;++i) draw((i,0)--(i,4),gray(0.5));
for(int j=0;j<=4;++j) draw((0,j)--(6,j),gray(0.5));
fill((1,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(1,3)--cycle,paleblue);
draw((1,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(1,3)--cycle,blue+linewidth(1.2));
draw((0,4)--(6,4)--(6,0)--(0,0)--cycle,linewidth(1));
label("$a$",(2,3),N,blue); label("$b$",(3,1.5),E,blue);
label("$m$",(3,0),(0,-3)); label("$n$",(0,2),W);
for(int i=0;i<=6;++i) label("$"+string(i)+"$",(i,0),S,fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} A rectangle $a$ wide has its left side on a line $i$ and its right side on line $i+a$, so $0\le i\le m-a$: there are $m-a+1$ choices. Similarly there are $n-b+1$ choices for the bottom side. The number of such rectangles is
\[\boxed{(m-a+1)(n-b+1)}.\]

\textbf{(b)} A rectangle is determined by choosing $2$ of the $m+1$ vertical lines and $2$ of the $n+1$ horizontal lines, so there are $\boxed{\dbinom{m+1}{2}\dbinom{n+1}{2}}$ rectangles.

\textbf{(c)} By (a) with $m=n=8$: there are $7\cdot6=42$ rectangles $2$ wide and $3$ tall and $6\cdot7=42$ rectangles $3$ wide and $2$ tall, so $\boxed{84}$ in all. (In general, when $a\ne b$, both orientations together give $(m-a+1)(n-b+1)+(m-b+1)(n-a+1)$, where a product is replaced by $0$ if the rectangle does not fit.)

Let the side length be $s$. At any moment during the walk, each person has covered the same distance $\lambda s$ along a side, where $0\le\lambda\le1$. Anna is at the point $X$ of $AB$ with $AX=\lambda s$, Bob at the point $Y$ of $BC$ with $BY=\lambda s$, and Carson at the point $Z$ of $CA$ with $CZ=\lambda s$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(6,0), C=6*dir(60);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
real l=0.3;
pair X=A+l*(B-A), Y=B+l*(C-B), Z=C+l*(A-C);
draw(X--Y--Z--cycle,deepgreen+dashed);
pair Xm=(A+B)/2, Ym=(B+C)/2, Zm=(C+A)/2;
draw(Xm--Ym--Zm--cycle,red+linewidth(1));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(X,deepgreen); dot(Y,deepgreen); dot(Z,deepgreen); dot(Xm,red); dot(Ym,red); dot(Zm,red);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N);
label("Anna",X,S,deepgreen+fontsize(7pt)); label("Bob",Y,E,deepgreen+fontsize(7pt)); label("Carson",Z,W,deepgreen+fontsize(7pt));
label("$\dfrac {s}{2}$",(Xm+Ym)/2,dir(150),red+fontsize(8pt));
label("$s$",(A+B)/2,(0,-3));
\end{asy}

In triangle $XBY$, $XB=(1-\lambda)s$, $BY=\lambda s$ and $\angle B=60^\circ$, so by the law of cosines
\[XY^2=(1-\lambda)^2s^2+\lambda^2s^2-(1-\lambda)\lambda s^2=\left(1-3\lambda+3\lambda^2\right)s^2=\left(3\left(\lambda-\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^2+\dfrac{1}{4}\right)s^2 .\]
A rotation by $120^\circ$ about the centre of the triangle maps $A\to B\to C\to A$ and $X\to Y\to Z\to X$, so $XYZ$ is equilateral and all three distances equal $XY$. The smallest distance occurs at $\lambda=\dfrac{1}{2}$, when everyone is at a midpoint, and it equals $\dfrac {s}{2}$ (the red triangle). So the condition holds exactly when $\dfrac {s}{2}\ge6$, and the smallest side length is $\boxed{12}$ feet.

(The given speed of $4$ miles per hour, about $5.9$ feet per second, does not matter: it only has to let the walk end before the next signal.)

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] In how many ways can you make $100$ cents using only nickels ($5$ cents) and pennies ($1$ cent)?
\item[(b)] In how many ways can you make $n$ cents using only nickels and pennies? Give a formula in terms of $n$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] In how many ways can you make $100$ cents using only dimes ($10$ cents), nickels ($5$ cents) and pennies ($1$ cent)?
\item[(b)] In how many ways can you make $n$ cents using only dimes, nickels and pennies? Give a formula in terms of $n$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $m=\left\lfloor\dfrac {n}{5}\right\rfloor$. A way is determined by the numbers $d$ of dimes and $k$ of nickels, with $10d+5k\le n$ (the pennies make up the rest), that is, $2d+k\le m$. For each $d=0,1,\dots,\left\lfloor\dfrac {m}{2}\right\rfloor$ there are $m-2d+1$ choices of $k$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $m=2t$: the count is $(2t+1)+(2t-1)+\cdots+1=(t+1)^2$.
\item If $m=2t+1$: the count is $(2t+2)+2t+\cdots+2=(t+1)(t+2)$.
\end{itemize}
Both cases are given by one formula, since $\dfrac{(2t+2)^2}{4}=(t+1)^2$ and $\left\lfloor\dfrac{(2t+3)^2}{4}\right\rfloor=\left\lfloor t^2+3t+\dfrac{9}{4}\right\rfloor=(t+1)(t+2)$:

\textbf{(b)} the number of ways is $\boxed{\left\lfloor\dfrac{(m+2)^2}{4}\right\rfloor}$ with $m=\left\lfloor\dfrac {n}{5}\right\rfloor$;

\textbf{(a)} for $100$ cents, $m=20$ and the count is $\dfrac{22^2}{4}=\boxed{121}$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] In how many ways can you make $100$ cents using only quarters ($25$ cents), dimes, nickels and pennies?
\item[(b)] In how many ways can you make $n$ cents using quarters, dimes, nickels and pennies? Give a formula in terms of $n$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The lines have only two directions, so each such parallelogram has two opposite sides on two of the horizontal lines and the other two sides on two of the vertical lines; conversely, any two horizontal and any two vertical lines bound exactly one parallelogram (here a rectangle).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<10;++i) draw((-0.5,i*0.6)--(8.0,i*0.6),gray(0.4));
for(int j=0;j<8;++j) draw((j,-0.4)--(j,5.8),gray(0.4));
fill((2,1.2)--(5,1.2)--(5,4.2)--(2,4.2)--cycle,paleblue);
draw((2,1.2)--(5,1.2)--(5,4.2)--(2,4.2)--cycle,blue+linewidth(1.2));
draw((-0.5,1.2)--(8,1.2),red+linewidth(1)); draw((-0.5,4.2)--(8,4.2),red+linewidth(1));
draw((2,-0.4)--(2,5.8),deepgreen+linewidth(1)); draw((5,-0.4)--(5,5.8),deepgreen+linewidth(1));
\end{asy}

So the number of parallelograms is
\[\dbinom{10}{2}\dbinom82=45\cdot28=\boxed{1260}.\]

Place the road on the \(x\)-axis, with Alex at \(A=(0,10)\) and base camp at \(B=(45,10)\). In an optimal route the jeep uses one straight desert leg to a point \(P\) on the road, then the road, then one straight desert leg from \(Q\) to \(B\). (A route touching the road more than once is shortened by staying on the faster road between the first entry and the last exit.) Clamping both \(P\) and \(Q\) to the interval between the perpendicular projections of \(A\) and \(B\) shortens each desert leg and cannot increase the road separation, so we may assume \(0\le P,Q\le45\). If \(P>Q\), replacing the desert leg \(AP\) and the road part \(PQ\) by the single desert leg \(AQ\) shortens the route; hence \(P\le Q\). Thus write \(P=(p,0)\) and \(Q=(45-q,0)\), where \(p,q\ge0\) and \(p+q\le45\). Let \(s=p+q\). The desert portions have lengths \(\sqrt{p^2+100}\) and \(\sqrt{q^2+100}\), so the total time is
\[
T=\dfrac{\sqrt{p^2+100}}{30}+\dfrac{\sqrt{q^2+100}}{30}+\dfrac{45-s}{50}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,10), B=(45,10), A0=(0,0), B0=(45,0), P=(7.5,0), Q=(37.5,0);
draw((-5,0)--(52,0),linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--P--Q--B,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--A0,dashed); draw(B--B0,dashed);
draw(P--Q,red+linewidth(1.2));
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle,linewidth(0.5));
draw((45,0)--(44,0)--(44,1)--(45,1)--cycle,linewidth(0.5));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(P); dot(Q);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,N); label("$P$",P,S); label("$Q$",Q,S);
label("$10$",(-1.2,5)); label("$10$",(46.2,5));
label("$7.5$",(3.75,-1.5)); label("$30$",(22.5,-1.5)); label("$7.5$",(41.25,-1.5));
draw((0,13.5)--(45,13.5),Arrows(5)); draw((0,12.7)--(0,14.3)); draw((45,12.7)--(45,14.3));
label("$45$",(22.5,13.5),N);
label("road",(51,0),S);
\end{asy}

For fixed \(s=p+q\),
\[
\sqrt{p^2+100}+\sqrt{q^2+100}\ge \sqrt{s^2+400},
\]
because squaring twice reduces the inequality to \((p-q)^2\ge0\). Hence
\[
T\ge F(s)=\dfrac{\sqrt{s^2+400}}{30}+\dfrac{45-s}{50}.
\]
Now
\[
F(s)-\dfrac{43}{30}
=\dfrac{5\sqrt{s^2+400}-3s-80}{150}.
\]
Since \(s\ge0\), \(3s+80>0\), so the numerator is nonnegative exactly when
\[
25(s^2+400)\ge(3s+80)^2,
\]
which is equivalent to
\[
16(s-15)^2\ge0.
\]
Thus \(T\ge \dfrac{43}{30}\). Equality requires \(s=15\) and \(p=q=\dfrac{15}{2}\). This is achievable: drive \(12.5\) km through the desert to a point \(7.5\) km past the first projection, drive \(30\) km on the road, then drive \(12.5\) km through the desert to base camp. The time is
\[
2\cdot\dfrac{12.5}{30}+\dfrac{30}{50}
=\dfrac{5}{6}+\dfrac{3}{5}
=\dfrac{43}{30}.
\]
The all-desert straight route takes \(\dfrac{45}{30}=\dfrac{3}{2}>\dfrac{43}{30}\), so the minimum is
\[
\boxed{\dfrac{43}{30}\text{ hours}}.
\]

Let \(C=(34,68,16)\). The closest point on the line to \(C\) is the perpendicular projection of \(C\) onto the line, so the vector \(P(t)-C\) must be perpendicular to the direction vector \(v=(-100,-50,-90)\) of the line.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(3,4), P=(3,0);
draw((-1,0)--(8.5,0),linewidth(0.8));
draw(C--P,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw((3,0)--(3.8,0)--(3.8,0.8)--(3,0.8)--cycle,linewidth(0.5));
dot(C); dot(P);
label("$C$",C,N); label("$P(t)$",P,S);
label("flight path",(7.0,0.7));
\end{asy}

Now
\[
P(t)-C=(966-100t,\ 432-50t,\ 884-90t).
\]
Thus
\[
(P(t)-C)\cdot v=0.
\]
That gives
\[
(966-100t)(-100)+(432-50t)(-50)+(884-90t)(-90)=0.
\]
Simplifying,
\[
-197760+20600t=0,
\]
so
\[
t=\dfrac{197760}{20600}=\dfrac{48}{5}.
\]
The squared distance is a quadratic in \(t\) with positive leading coefficient, so this perpendicular projection is indeed the minimum. Therefore
\[
\boxed{\dfrac{48}{5}}.
\]

Let \(O\) be the shore point closest to the yacht \(Y\), and let \(H\) be the hospital. Then \(YO=15\) and \(OH=60\). Landing west of \(O\) would increase both the boat distance and the ambulance distance compared with landing at \(O\), and landing east of \(H\) would increase both distances compared with landing at \(H\). Hence we may let the landing point be \(L\), \(x=OL\) miles east of \(O\), with \(0\le x\le60\). The total time is
\[
T(x)=\dfrac{\sqrt{x^2+225}}{20}+\dfrac{60-x}{90}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Y=(0,15), O=(0,0), L=(12,0), H=(60,0);
draw((-5,0)--(65,0),linewidth(0.8));
draw(Y--L,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(L--H,red+linewidth(1.2));
draw(Y--O,dashed);
draw((0,0)--(1.2,0)--(1.2,1.2)--(0,1.2)--cycle,linewidth(0.5));
dot(Y); dot(O); dot(L); dot(H);
label("$Y$",Y,N); label("$O$",O,SW); label("$L$",L,SE); label("$H$",H,S);
label("$15$",(0,7.5),W); label("$x$",(6,0),S); label("$60-x$",(36,0),S);
draw(arc(Y,4,-90,-90+degrees(atan(12/15))),linewidth(0.5)); label("$\theta$",Y+5*dir(-90+0.5*degrees(atan(12/15))));
label("not to scale",(44,13),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

We show the minimum occurs at \(x=\dfrac{30}{\sqrt{77}}\). Indeed,
\[
T(x)-\dfrac{8+\sqrt{77}}{12}
=\dfrac{9\sqrt{x^2+225}-2x-15\sqrt{77}}{180}.
\]
The numerator is nonnegative, since
\[
(9\sqrt{x^2+225})^2-(2x+15\sqrt{77})^2
=(\sqrt{77}x-30)^2\ge0,
\]
and \(2x+15\sqrt{77}>0\). Equality occurs when
\[
x=\dfrac{30}{\sqrt{77}}.
\]
At this landing point,
\[
\sqrt{x^2+225}=\dfrac{135}{\sqrt{77}}.
\]
Let \(\theta\) be the angle the boat's path makes east of due south. Then
\[
\sin\theta=\dfrac{x}{\sqrt{x^2+225}}
=\dfrac{30}{135}
=\dfrac{2}{9}.
\]
Therefore the motorboat should head
\[
\boxed{\theta=\arcsin\left(\dfrac{2}{9}\right)\text{ east of due south}}.
\]
Equivalently, it should aim for a point \(\dfrac{30}{\sqrt{77}}\) miles east of the shore point closest to the yacht.

Label the vertices \(V_0,V_1,\ldots,V_9\) in order. Let \(V_i\) be at angle
\[
\theta_i=\dfrac{\pi}{2}-\dfrac{2\pi i}{10}.
\]
The direction of chord \(V_iV_j\) is determined by
\[
\dfrac{\theta_i+\theta_j}{2}+\dfrac{\pi}{2}\pmod{\pi},
\]
so two chords are parallel exactly when
\[
i+j\equiv k+\ell\pmod{10}.
\]
Thus each residue class of \(i+j\pmod{10}\) is one direction class of chords.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V;
for(int i=0;i<10;++i){ real th=pi/2-2*pi*i/10; V[i]=(cos(th),sin(th)); }
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--V[6]--V[7]--V[8]--V[9]--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(V[1]--V[9],blue+linewidth(1.0));
draw(V[2]--V[8],blue+linewidth(1.0));
draw(V[3]--V[7],blue+linewidth(1.0));
draw(V[4]--V[6],blue+linewidth(1.0));
for(int i=0;i<10;++i){ dot(V[i]); label("$V_"+string(i)+"$",1.18*V[i]); }
\end{asy}

For a fixed residue \(s\):

\begin{itemize}
\item If \(s\) is odd, the equation \(i+j\equiv s\pmod{10}\) has \(5\) unordered chord pairs and no solution with \(i=j\). A side joins \(i\) and \(i+1\), whose sum is \(2i+1\), always odd; for each odd \(s\), the equation \(2i+1\equiv s\pmod{10}\) has exactly \(2\) solutions. Thus this direction class contains \(5-2=3\) diagonals.
\item If \(s\) is even, there are \(10\) ordered solutions, but two have \(i=j\), leaving \(8\) ordered solutions, hence \(4\) unordered chord pairs. No side has even sum, so all \(4\) are diagonals.
\end{itemize}

There are five odd and five even residue classes. A direction class with \(k\) diagonals contributes \(\binom{k}{2}\) unordered parallel pairs. Hence the total is
\[
5\binom{3}{2}+5\binom{4}{2}
=5\cdot3+5\cdot6
=45.
\]
Therefore the number of unordered pairs of parallel diagonals is
\[
\boxed{45}.
\]

Suppose, for contradiction, that three distinct points \(A,B,C\) lie both on a circle with center \(O\) and on a line \(L\). Let \(M\) be the foot of the perpendicular from \(O\) to \(L\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); real r=2; real h=0.6;
pair A=(-sqrt(r^2-h^2),h), B=(sqrt(r^2-h^2),h), M=(0,h);
draw(circle(O,r),linewidth(0.8));
draw((-3,h)--(3,h),linewidth(0.8));
draw(O--M,dashed); draw(O--A); draw(O--B);
draw((0,h)--(0.2,h)--(0.2,h-0.2)--(0,h-0.2)--cycle,linewidth(0.5));
dot(O); dot(A); dot(B); dot(M);
label("$O$",O,S); label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,E); label("$M$",M,N);
\end{asy}

Because \(O\) is equidistant from \(A\) and \(B\), it lies on the perpendicular bisector of chord \(AB\). That perpendicular bisector passes through \(O\) and is perpendicular to \(AB\), hence to \(L\). Therefore it meets \(L\) at the foot \(M\). Since the perpendicular bisector meets \(AB\) at its midpoint, \(M\) is the midpoint of \(AB\).

Similarly, \(O\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of chord \(AC\). The perpendicular bisector of \(AC\) also passes through \(O\) and is perpendicular to \(L\), so it is the same line and meets \(L\) at \(M\). Hence \(M\) is also the midpoint of \(AC\). Therefore \(B\) and \(C\) are both the reflection of \(A\) in \(M\), so \(B=C\), contradicting the assumption that \(A,B,C\) are distinct. Therefore no circle contains three distinct collinear points.

Let $O$ be the center, and let $P$ be the chosen point with $OP=r$, where $0\le r\le R$. The nearest point of the circumference to $P$ lies on the ray from $O$ through $P$, at distance $R-r$ from $P$. Hence $P$ is closer to the center than to the circle exactly when
\[r<R-r,\qquad\text{that is,}\qquad r<\dfrac{R}{2}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=2;
pair O=(0,0);
draw(circle(O,R),linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O,R/2),dashed+linewidth(0.7));
dot(O);
label("$O$",O+0.16*dir(215));
pair u=dir(35);
pair Pt=0.72*u;
pair Pb=R*u;
dot(Pt);
label("$P$",Pt+0.18*u);
draw(O--Pb,linewidth(0.7));
label("$r$",(O+Pt)/2+0.17*dir(125));
label("$R-r$",(Pt+Pb)/2+0.20*dir(125));
pair v=dir(140);
draw(O--(R/2)*v,linewidth(0.7));
label("$\dfrac{R}{2}$",(R/4)*v+0.16*dir(230));
\end{asy}

So the favourable region is precisely the disc of radius $\dfrac{R}{2}$ about $O$: a point of the annulus between radius $\dfrac{R}{2}$ and radius $R$ is farther from $O$ than from the circumference, while a point inside the small disc is nearer to $O$.

The point is chosen uniformly with respect to area, so the probability is the ratio of the two areas:
\[\dfrac{\pi\left(\dfrac{R}{2}\right)^{2}}{\pi R^{2}}=\dfrac{1}{4}.\]
Thus the reasoning with the concentric circle of half the radius is correct, and the answer is
\[\boxed{\dfrac{1}{4}}.\]

question:
The vertices of a regular octagon are labelled $A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H$ in order around the octagon. Prove that $ADEH$ is a rectangle whose area is one half of the area of the octagon $ABCDEFGH$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair pA=(0.92388,0.38268), pB=(0.38268,0.92388), pC=(-0.38268,0.92388), pD=(-0.92388,0.38268);
pair pE=(-0.92388,-0.38268), pF=(-0.38268,-0.92388), pG=(0.38268,-0.92388), pH=(0.92388,-0.38268);
fill(pA--pD--pE--pH--cycle, paleblue);
draw(pA--pB--pC--pD--pE--pF--pG--pH--cycle, linewidth(1));
draw(pA--pD--pE--pH--cycle, blue+linewidth(0.8));
label("$A$", pA, E); label("$B$", pB, N); label("$C$", pC, N); label("$D$", pD, W);
label("$E$", pE, W); label("$F$", pF, S); label("$G$", pG, S); label("$H$", pH, E);
\end{asy}

solution:
Let $O$ be the centre of the octagon and let $R$ be its circumradius, so that $OA=OB=\cdots=OH=R$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair pA=(0.92388,0.38268), pB=(0.38268,0.92388), pC=(-0.38268,0.92388), pD=(-0.92388,0.38268);
pair pE=(-0.92388,-0.38268), pF=(-0.38268,-0.92388), pG=(0.38268,-0.92388), pH=(0.92388,-0.38268);
pair O=(0,0);
fill(pA--pD--pE--pH--cycle, paleblue);
draw(pA--pB--pC--pD--pE--pF--pG--pH--cycle, linewidth(1));
draw(pA--pD--pE--pH--cycle, blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(O--pB, gray+dashed); draw(O--pC, gray+dashed); draw(O--pF, gray+dashed); draw(O--pG, gray+dashed);
draw(O--pA, blue+linewidth(1.1)); draw(O--pD, blue+linewidth(1.1));
draw(O--pE, blue+linewidth(1.1)); draw(O--pH, blue+linewidth(1.1));
dot(O);
label("$O$", O, dir(45));
label("$A$", pA, E); label("$B$", pB, N); label("$C$", pC, N); label("$D$", pD, W);
label("$E$", pE, W); label("$F$", pF, S); label("$G$", pG, S); label("$H$", pH, E);
\end{asy}


\textbf{Step 1: $ADEH$ is a rectangle with centre $O$.}
Because the octagon is regular, $E$ is the vertex diametrically opposite $A$, and $H$ is the vertex diametrically opposite $D$. Hence $AE$ and $DH$ are both diameters of the circumcircle: each passes through $O$, and
\[ OA=OE=R, \qquad OD=OH=R. \]
So the diagonals $AE$ and $DH$ of the quadrilateral $ADEH$ bisect each other at $O$, which makes $ADEH$ a parallelogram, and they have equal length $2R$, which makes that parallelogram a rectangle. Since its centre $O$ lies inside it, the rectangle is the union of the four triangles $OAD$, $ODE$, $OEH$, $OHA$, and
\[ [ADEH]=[OAD]+[ODE]+[OEH]+[OHA]. \tag{1} \]

\textbf{Step 2: each side of the octagon cuts off $\dfrac{1}{8}$ of the octagon.}
Consecutive vertices of a regular octagon are $45^\circ$ apart on the circumcircle, so $\angle DOE=45^\circ$. Rotating by multiples of $45^\circ$ about $O$ carries triangle $ODE$ onto the seven other triangles $OEF$, $OFG$, $\ldots$, $OAB$, $OBC$ cut off by the sides; hence all eight are congruent and
\[ [ODE]=\dfrac{1}{8}[ABCDEFGH]. \tag{2} \]

\textbf{Step 3: the other three triangles in (1) have the same area as $ODE$.}
Since $O$ is the midpoint of $AE$, the points $A$ and $E$ are exchanged by the half-turn about $O$; a half-turn about a point of a line preserves distances to that line, so $A$ and $E$ are at the same distance from line $OD$. Triangles $OAD$ and $ODE$ have the common base $OD$ and equal heights, so $[OAD]=[ODE]$.
Similarly $O$ is the midpoint of $DH$, so the half-turn about $O$ swaps $D$ and $H$, giving $[OEH]=[ODE]$ (common base $OE$); and it swaps $A$ and $E$, giving $[OHA]=[OEH]=[ODE]$ (common base $OH$).

\textbf{Step 4: conclusion.}
By (1) and Step 3,
\[ [ADEH]=4[ODE]=\dfrac{4}{8}[ABCDEFGH]=\dfrac{1}{2}[ABCDEFGH], \]
using (2). Thus the rectangle $ADEH$ has exactly half the area of the octagon, as required.

The cube has side length $s$ with $s^3=27$, so $s=3$. Place the cube as $0\le x,y,z\le 3$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair sh=(0.45,0.4);
pair V(real x, real y, real z) { return (x,y)+z*sh; }
pair p000=V(0,0,0), p100=V(1,0,0), p110=V(1,1,0), p010=V(0,1,0);
pair p001=V(0,0,1), p101=V(1,0,1), p111=V(1,1,1), p011=V(0,1,1);
draw(p000--p100--p101--p111--p011--p010--cycle, linewidth(1));
draw(p010--p110--p100, linewidth(1));
draw(p110--p111, linewidth(1));
draw(p001--p000, dashed); draw(p001--p101, dashed); draw(p001--p011, dashed);
pair fz0=V(0.5,0.5,0), fz1=V(0.5,0.5,1);
pair fx0=V(0,0.5,0.5), fx1=V(1,0.5,0.5), fy0=V(0.5,0,0.5), fy1=V(0.5,1,0.5);
pen octpen = rgb(0,0,0.8)+linewidth(1);
draw(fz0--fx0, octpen); draw(fz0--fx1, octpen); draw(fz0--fy0, octpen); draw(fz0--fy1, octpen);
draw(fz1--fx0, octpen); draw(fz1--fx1, octpen); draw(fz1--fy0, octpen); draw(fz1--fy1, octpen);
draw(fx0--fy0, octpen); draw(fx0--fy1, octpen); draw(fx1--fy0, octpen); draw(fx1--fy1, octpen);
dot(fz0, blue); dot(fz1, blue); dot(fx0, blue); dot(fx1, blue); dot(fy0, blue); dot(fy1, blue);
label("$3$", (p000+p100)/2, S);
\end{asy}


The six face centres are the vertices of the octahedron. The four side faces $x=0$, $x=3$, $y=0$, $y=3$ have centres
\[ \left(0,\dfrac{3}{2},\dfrac{3}{2}\right),\quad \left(3,\dfrac{3}{2},\dfrac{3}{2}\right),\quad \left(\dfrac{3}{2},0,\dfrac{3}{2}\right),\quad \left(\dfrac{3}{2},3,\dfrac{3}{2}\right). \]
All four have $z=\dfrac{3}{2}$, so they lie in one plane and form a square whose diagonals are the segments from $\left(0,\dfrac{3}{2}\right)$ to $\left(3,\dfrac{3}{2}\right)$ and from $\left(\dfrac{3}{2},0\right)$ to $\left(\dfrac{3}{2},3\right)$. These diagonals have length $3$, are perpendicular, and bisect each other, so the square has area
\[ \dfrac{1}{2}\cdot 3\cdot 3=\dfrac{9}{2}. \]
The remaining two vertices of the octahedron are the centres $\left(\dfrac{3}{2},\dfrac{3}{2},0\right)$ and $\left(\dfrac{3}{2},\dfrac{3}{2},3\right)$ of the bottom and top faces. Each lies on the line through the centre of the square perpendicular to its plane, at distance $\dfrac{3}{2}$ from that plane.

Hence the octahedron is the union of two congruent square pyramids with base area $\dfrac{9}{2}$ and height $\dfrac{3}{2}$. Each has volume
\[ \dfrac{1}{3}\cdot\dfrac{9}{2}\cdot\dfrac{3}{2}=\dfrac{9}{4}, \]
so the octahedron has volume
\[ 2\cdot\dfrac{9}{4}=\boxed{\dfrac{9}{2}}. \]
(In general, the octahedron formed by the face centres of a cube of side $s$ has volume $\dfrac{s^3}{6}$, here $\dfrac{27}{6}=\dfrac{9}{2}$.)

question:
In triangle $ABC$, $\angle ABC=\angle ACB=80^\circ$. The point $P$ lies on segment $AB$ and satisfies $\angle BPC=30^\circ$. Prove that $AP=BC$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Bv=(0,0), Cv=(1,0), Av=(0.5,2.835641);
real tt=0.347296;
pair Pv = Av + tt*(Bv-Av);
draw(Av--Bv--Cv--cycle, linewidth(1));
dot(Av); dot(Bv); dot(Cv); dot(Pv);
label("$A$", Av, N); label("$B$", Bv, SW); label("$C$", Cv, SE); label("$P$", Pv, W);
label("$80^\circ$", Bv+0.28*dir(40)); label("$80^\circ$", Cv+0.28*dir(140));
label("$30^\circ$", Pv+0.30*dir(277));
\end{asy}

solution:
Because $\angle ABC=\angle ACB$, the triangle is isosceles with $AB=AC$ and $\angle BAC=20^\circ$.

\textbf{Construction.} Let $Q$ be the point on the same side of $BC$ as $A$ for which triangle $BCQ$ is equilateral, so $QB=QC=BC$ and $\angle QBC=\angle QCB=60^\circ$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Bv=(0,0), Cv=(1,0), Av=(0.5,2.835641);
real tt=0.347296;
pair Pv = Av + tt*(Bv-Av);
pair Qv=(0.5,0.866025);
draw(circle(Qv,1), gray+dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(Av--Bv--Cv--cycle, linewidth(1));
draw(Qv--Bv, blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(Qv--Cv, blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(Qv--Pv, blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(Qv--Av, blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(Av); dot(Bv); dot(Cv); dot(Pv); dot(Qv);
label("$A$", Av, N); label("$B$", Bv, SW); label("$C$", Cv, SE);
label("$P$", Pv, W); label("$Q$", Qv, E);
\end{asy}


\textbf{Step 1: $AQ$ bisects $\angle BAC$.} Since $AB=AC$ and $QB=QC$, both $A$ and $Q$ lie on the perpendicular bisector of $BC$. Hence line $AQ$ is that perpendicular bisector, so it bisects $\angle BAC$ (and $Q$ lies inside triangle $ABC$, because $60^\circ<80^\circ$). Therefore
\[ \angle BAQ=\angle QAC=10^\circ . \]

\textbf{Step 2: $QP=QB$.} Let $O$ be the circumcentre of triangle $BPC$. The central angle theorem gives $\angle BOC=2\angle BPC=60^\circ$, and $OB=OC$, so triangle $BOC$ is equilateral and $OB=OC=BC$. Since $\angle BPC=30^\circ<90^\circ$, the circumcentre $O$ lies on the same side of $BC$ as $P$, that is, on the same side as $A$; and the point on that side with $OB=OC=BC$ is exactly $Q$. Hence $O=Q$, so $Q$ is the circumcentre of triangle $BPC$ and
\[ QP=QB=BC . \]

\textbf{Step 3: two isosceles triangles.}
First, $\angle QBP=\angle ABC-\angle QBC=80^\circ-60^\circ=20^\circ$. Since $QP=QB$, triangle $QBP$ is isosceles with apex $Q$, so its base angles are equal:
\[ \angle QPB=\angle QBP=20^\circ, \qquad \angle PQB=180^\circ-20^\circ-20^\circ=140^\circ . \]
Second, in triangle $AQB$ we have $\angle BAQ=10^\circ$ and $\angle ABQ=\angle QBP=20^\circ$, so
\[ \angle AQB=180^\circ-10^\circ-20^\circ=150^\circ . \]

\textbf{Step 4: finish.} Since $P$ lies on side $AB$ of triangle $AQB$, the ray $QP$ lies inside $\angle AQB$, hence
\[ \angle AQP=\angle AQB-\angle PQB=150^\circ-140^\circ=10^\circ . \]
But $\angle PAQ=\angle BAQ=10^\circ$ as well. So triangle $AQP$ has two equal angles, $\angle PAQ=\angle AQP=10^\circ$, and the sides opposite them are equal:
\[ AP=QP . \]
Together with $QP=QB=BC$ from Step 2, this gives $AP=BC$, as required.

Euler's formula is often written in the equivalent form $E+2=V+F$. We first prove Euler's formula for drawings in the plane, then transfer it to the polyhedron.

\textbf{Step 1 (Euler for connected plane graphs).} Suppose a graph is drawn in the plane with no crossing edges, with $V$ vertices, $E$ edges and $F$ regions (faces), where the unbounded outside region counts as one face. If the graph is connected, then $V-E+F=2$.

\emph{Proof.} Induct on $E$. A connected graph with $V$ vertices always has $E\ge V-1$, with equality exactly when it is a tree.

\emph{Base case} $E=V-1$: the graph is a tree. Such a drawing has exactly one face. Indeed, starting from one vertex (one face) and adding the edges of the tree one at a time, each new edge ends at a brand-new vertex, so it cannot close up a curve and cannot split a region. Hence $V-E+F=V-(V-1)+1=2$.

\emph{Induction step}: suppose $E\ge V$, so the drawing contains a cycle. Pick an edge $e$ lying on a cycle $C$. A cycle is a simple closed curve (the drawing has no crossings), so by the Jordan curve theorem its two sides lie in different regions of the plane. Points just off $e$ on its two sides are on opposite sides of $C$, hence in different regions and therefore in different faces of the graph, so $e$ borders two distinct faces. Also $e$ is not a bridge (bridges lie on no cycle), so deleting $e$ keeps the graph connected. Deleting the interior of $e$ therefore joins those two faces into one face and changes nothing else: the new drawing is a connected plane graph with $V$ vertices, $E-1$ edges and $F-1$ faces. By the induction hypothesis $V-(E-1)+(F-1)=2$, i.e. $V-E+F=2$. This completes Step 1.

\textbf{Step 2 (from the polyhedron to the plane).} The surface of a convex polyhedron is a sphere (project it radially from an interior point). The vertices and edges of the polyhedron form a graph drawn on that sphere without crossings, and the regions into which this graph cuts the sphere are exactly the interiors of the $F$ polygonal faces. The graph is connected: any two vertices can be joined by a path on the surface, and such a path meets only finitely many edges, giving a chain of edges between them.

Now delete a single point from the interior of one face. A sphere with one point removed can be spread out flat as a plane (stereographic projection), so this turns the drawing into a connected plane graph with the same $V$ vertices and $E$ edges and the same number $F$ of faces --- the punctured face simply becomes the unbounded face. Step 1 then gives
\[
V-E+F=2 ,
\]
which is Euler's formula for the polyhedron.


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair a=(0,0), b=(2,0), c=(2,2), d=(0,2);
pair a1=(1.0,0.7), b1=(3.0,0.7), c1=(3.0,2.7), d1=(1.0,2.7);
draw(a--b--c--d--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(b--b1,linewidth(0.9));
draw(c--c1,linewidth(0.9));
draw(d--d1,linewidth(0.9));
draw(b1--c1--d1,linewidth(0.9));
draw(a--a1,dashed+linewidth(0.7));
draw(a1--b1,dashed+linewidth(0.7));
draw(a1--d1,dashed+linewidth(0.7));
dot(a); label("$v$",a+(-0.3,-0.25));
dot((a+b)/2); label("$e$",(a+b)/2+(0,-0.3));
label("$f$",(1,1));
pair o1=(4.6,0), o2=(7.6,0), o3=(7.6,3), o4=(4.6,3);
pair i1=(5.5,0.9), i2=(6.7,0.9), i3=(6.7,2.1), i4=(5.5,2.1);
draw(o1--o2--o3--o4--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(i1--i2--i3--i4--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(o1--i1,linewidth(0.9));
draw(o2--i2,linewidth(0.9));
draw(o3--i3,linewidth(0.9));
draw(o4--i4,linewidth(0.9));
label("outer face",(6.1,3.5));
\end{asy}


\textbf{Why convexity is needed.} The proof uses that the surface is a sphere. For a solid whose surface is a torus --- for example a rectangular block with a square tunnel bored straight through it --- one counts $V=16$, $E=32$ and $F=16$, where each of the two flat annular end faces is split into four quadrilaterals (a single annulus is not a polygon, and polyhedral faces must be polygons). Then $V-E+F=16-32+16=0\ne 2$. Hence the formula is not valid for ``any 3D shape''; it holds precisely for polyhedra whose surface is a sphere, and every convex polyhedron is of that kind. This completes the proof.

question:
A student claimed that $n^{10}-10$ is divisible by $10$ for every integer $n$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the claim is false.
\item[(b)] Find all integers $n$ for which $10$ divides $n^{10}-10$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Write the four angles in increasing order as \(a\), \(a+d\), \(a+2d\), \(a+3d\), where \(a\ge1\) and \(d\ge0\) are integers (each angle of a convex quadrilateral is strictly between \(0^\circ\) and \(180^\circ\)). The first sketch shows a representative case \(a=60^\circ\), \(d=20^\circ\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0);
pair B=(6,0);
pair D=3.5*dir(60);
pair C=(6-D.y/tan(80*pi/180),D.y);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D);
label("$A$",A,SW,fontsize(8pt));
label("$B$",B,SE,fontsize(8pt));
label("$C$",C,NE,fontsize(8pt));
label("$D$",D,N,fontsize(8pt));
label("$a$",A+0.9*(cos(30*pi/180),sin(30*pi/180)),fontsize(8pt));
label("$a+d$",B+1.0*(cos(140*pi/180),sin(140*pi/180)),fontsize(8pt));
label("$a+2d$",C+1.0*(cos(230*pi/180),sin(230*pi/180)),fontsize(8pt));
label("$a+3d$",D+1.0*(cos(300*pi/180),sin(300*pi/180)),fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

The angles of a quadrilateral sum to \(360^\circ\), so
\[4a+6d=360,\qquad\text{that is}\qquad 2a+3d=180.\]
The largest angle is then
\[x=a+3d=a+(180-2a)=180-a,\]
so \(x\) is largest exactly when \(a\) is smallest. From \(2a+3d=180\) with \(d\ge0\) we get \(a\le90\), and \(3d=180-2a\) must be a multiple of \(3\); as \(180\) is a multiple of \(3\), this means \(2a\equiv0\pmod 3\), i.e. \(a\equiv0\pmod 3\). The smallest positive possibility is \(a=3\), which gives \(3d=174\), so \(d=58\) and
\[x=180-3=177.\]
The four angles would be \(3^\circ\), \(61^\circ\), \(119^\circ\) and \(177^\circ\); they add up to \(360^\circ\) and each lies strictly between \(0^\circ\) and \(180^\circ\). Such a convex quadrilateral really exists: take four points on a circle cutting off consecutive arcs of \(235^\circ\), \(3^\circ\), \(3^\circ\) and \(119^\circ\); the inscribed-angle theorem gives the angles \(\dfrac{3+3}{2}=3\), \(\dfrac{3+119}{2}=61\), \(\dfrac{119+235}{2}=177\) and \(\dfrac{235+3}{2}=119\) degrees. (The angle of $3^\circ$ is too narrow to draw usefully, so only the representative case above is sketched.)
Therefore the largest possible value of \(x\) is \(\boxed{177}\).

question:
Find constants \(A\) and \(B\) such that
\[
\dfrac{x+7}{x^2-2x-35}
=\dfrac{A}{x-7}+\dfrac{B}{x+5}
\]
for all \(x\) for which both sides are defined. A student clears denominators and substitutes \(x=7\) and \(x=-5\). Is that method valid?

solution:
Let the equal sides be \(a,a\) and the base be \(b\), with \(0<b<2a\). By Heron's formula,
\[
A^2=\dfrac{b^2(4a^2-b^2)}{16}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fontsize(9pt);
pair A=(0,4);
pair B=(-3,0);
pair C=(3,0);
pair F=(0,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--F,dashed);
draw((0,0)--(0.4,0)--(0.4,0.4)--(0,0.4)--cycle);
label("$A$",A,N);
label("$B$",B,SW);
label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$a$",(A+B)/2,NW);
label("$a$",(A+C)/2,NE);
label("$b$",(B+C)/2,S);
label("$h$",(A+F)/2+(0.2,0),E);
\end{asy}

The condition \(A=6(2a+b)\), together with \(4a^2-b^2=(2a-b)(2a+b)\), gives
\[
\dfrac{b^2(2a-b)(2a+b)}{16}=36(2a+b)^2.
\]
Since \(2a+b>0\), this is
\[
b^2(2a-b)=576(2a+b).
\]
Set \(d=2a-b>0\). Then \(2a=b+d\), so \(2a+b=2b+d\), and
\[
d(b^2-576)=1152b.
\]
In particular \(b>24\). Rearranging and multiplying by \(d\) gives
\[
(db-576)^2=576(d^2+576).
\]
Because \(db=\dfrac{1152b^2}{b^2-576}>576\), the number \(db-576\) is positive. Thus \(d^2+576\) is a perfect square; write \(d^2+576=m^2\). Then
\[
(m-d)(m+d)=576.
\]
Put \(m-d=2u\) and \(m+d=2v\), where \(u,v\) are positive integers and \(uv=144\). Hence \(d=v-u\), \(m=u+v\), and
\[
b=\dfrac{576+24m}{d}=\dfrac{24(24+u+v)}{v-u}.
\]
Checking the factor pairs \(u<v\) with \(uv=144\) gives the following possibilities for \(b\) and \(a\):
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
u & v & b & a\\ \hline
3&48&40&\dfrac{85}{2}\ \text{not integer}\\
4&36&48&40\\
6&24&72&45\\
8&18&120&65\\
9&16&168&\dfrac{175}{2}\ \text{not integer}
\end{array}
\]
Only the rows with integer \(a\) are valid. The remaining factor pairs of \(144\) do not give an integer \(b\) (the pair \(12,12\) gives \(d=0\), which is degenerate). Therefore the possible side lengths are
\[
(40,40,48),\qquad (45,45,72),\qquad (65,65,120).
\]
A direct check confirms them:
\[
\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot48\cdot32=768=6\cdot128,
\]
\[
\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot72\cdot27=972=6\cdot162,
\]
\[
\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot120\cdot25=1500=6\cdot250.
\]
Thus the complete list is \(\boxed{(40,40,48),\ (45,45,72),\ (65,65,120)}\).

Let the radius be \(1\). For every point \(O\) on the unit circle centered at \(P\), draw the circle with center \(O\) and radius \(1\). Since \(PO=1\), this circle passes through \(P\). As \(O\) moves around the unit circle, the centers are all different, so the circles are all different. There are infinitely many choices of \(O\) (indeed, one for every angle \(\theta\) with \(0\le \theta<2\pi\)). Hence infinitely many circles pass through the single point \(P\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
