First, $\angle ACD=90^\circ$ and $AC=DC=1$, so triangle $ACD$ is right isosceles and
\[\angle ADC=45^\circ.\]
Next, triangles $ACE$ and $ACB$ are right triangles with legs along $AC$ and $CB$, so
\[\tan\angle AEC=\dfrac{AC}{CE}=\dfrac{1}{2},\qquad \tan\angle ABC=\dfrac{AC}{CB}=\dfrac{1}{3}.\]
Both angles are strictly between $0^\circ$ and $90^\circ$, and $\tan\angle AEC\cdot\tan\angle ABC=\dfrac{1}{6}<1$. If two acute angles have product of tangents equal to $1$ their sum is $90^\circ$ (then one tangent is the reciprocal of the other), so here $\angle AEC+\angle ABC<90^\circ$. Hence the tangent addition formula applies:
\[\tan\left(\angle AEC+\angle ABC\right)=\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{2}+\dfrac{1}{3}}{1-\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\dfrac{1}{3}}=\dfrac{\dfrac{5}{6}}{\dfrac{5}{6}}=1.\]
An angle that is acute and has tangent $1$ equals $45^\circ$, so $\angle AEC+\angle ABC=45^\circ$. Adding the right-isosceles angle,
\[\angle ABC+\angle AEC+\angle ADC=45^\circ+45^\circ=90^\circ,\]
as required.

Write $z=\cos\theta+i\sin\theta$ where $\theta$ is uniformly distributed on $[0,2\pi)$. Then $z^2=\cos2\theta+i\sin2\theta$, so the condition is
\[\sin2\theta\ge 1-\cos2\theta.\]
Since $\sin2\theta+\cos2\theta=\sqrt2\sin\left(2\theta+\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right)$, this is equivalent to
\[\sin\left(2\theta+\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right)\ge \dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}.\]
On the circle, $\sin u\ge\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}$ holds exactly for $u\in\left[\dfrac{\pi}{4},\dfrac{3\pi}{4}\right]$ modulo $2\pi$. Hence
\[\dfrac{\pi}{4}\le 2\theta+\dfrac{\pi}{4}\le\dfrac{3\pi}{4}\pmod{2\pi}\iff 0\le 2\theta\le\dfrac{\pi}{2}\pmod{2\pi}\iff 0\le\theta\le\dfrac{\pi}{4}\pmod{\pi}.\]
As $\theta$ runs over $[0,2\pi)$, the good values are $\left[0,\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right]\cup\left[\pi,\dfrac{5\pi}{4}\right]$, a total length of $\dfrac{\pi}{2}$ out of $2\pi$.

Geometrically: put $w=z^2$, which also lies on the unit circle, with $w=(x,y)$. The condition is $x+y\ge1$, and the chord $x+y=1$ cuts off exactly the quarter arc of the circle from $w=1$ to $w=i$:


