Divide each side of the rhombus into \(5\) equal parts and draw the parallels to the sides, obtaining a \(5\times5\) grid of congruent small rhombi.

Take a corner \(3\times3\) block. Split it into four pieces as follows: split it into three \(3\times1\) strips, then split one of those strips into a \(2\times1\) piece and a \(1\times1\) piece. These four pieces will simply be reassembled to form a \(3\times3\) rhombus, similar to the original with scale \(\dfrac{3}{5}\).

The remaining L-shape consists of a \(5\times2\) strip and a \(2\times3\) block. Cut the \(5\times2\) strip into two \(4\times1\) bars and a \(1\times2\) domino; keep the \(2\times3\) block as one piece. Together with the four pieces from the \(3\times3\) block, this gives \(4+2+1+1=8\) pieces.

To form the second smaller rhombus, use a \(4\times4\) rhombus grid. Place the two \(4\times1\) bars as two full rows. The remaining \(4\times2\) region is filled by the \(1\times2\) domino together with the \(2\times3\) block turned into a \(3\times2\) block. This is allowed because in a rhombus a \(2\times3\) grid block and a \(3\times2\) grid block are congruent; one is obtained from the other by the permitted flip across an angle bisector. The result is a \(4\times4\) rhombus, similar to the original with scale \(\dfrac{4}{5}\). The eight piece areas are \(3,3,2,1\) from the \(3\times3\) block and \(4,4,2,6\) from the L-shape, and indeed
\[
3+3+2+1=9=\left(\dfrac{3}{5}\right)^2\cdot 25,
\qquad
4+4+2+6=16=\left(\dfrac{4}{5}\right)^2\cdot 25,
\]
so all eight pieces are used, with nothing left over.

