Write $P=R+t(S-R)$ for a real number $t$. Then
\[
PR=|t|\,RS,\qquad PS=|1-t|\,RS.
\]
The condition $PR=3PS$ becomes $|t|=3|1-t|$. Squaring gives
\[
t^2=9(1-t)^2=9-18t+9t^2,
\]
so $8t^2-18t+9=0$. Its roots are
\[
t=\dfrac{18\pm\sqrt{324-288}}{16}=\dfrac{18\pm6}{16},
\]
namely $t=\dfrac{3}{4}$ and $t=\dfrac{3}{2}$.

For $t=\dfrac{3}{4}$,
\[
P=R+\dfrac{3}{4}(S-R)=\left(3+\dfrac{15}{4},\,5-6\right)=\left(\dfrac{27}{4},-1\right).
\]
For $t=\dfrac{3}{2}$,
\[
P=R+\dfrac{3}{2}(S-R)=\left(3+\dfrac{15}{2},\,5-12\right)=\left(\dfrac{21}{2},-7\right).
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real sc=0.32;
pair offset=(6.75,-1);
pair Scaled(pair p){return sc*(p-offset);}
pair Rpt=Scaled((3,5));
pair Tpt=Scaled((8,-3));
pair P1pt=Scaled((6.75,-1));
pair P2pt=Scaled((10.5,-7));
draw(Rpt--P2pt,linewidth(0.9));
dot(Rpt); dot(Tpt); dot(P1pt); dot(P2pt);
label("$R=(3,5)$", Rpt, NW, fontsize(8pt));
label("$S=(8,-3)$", Tpt, NE, fontsize(8pt));
label("$P_1$", P1pt, W, fontsize(8pt));
label("$P_2$", P2pt, SE, fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Both points lie on the line and satisfy $PR=3PS$, so the required points are
\[
\boxed{\left(\dfrac{27}{4},-1\right)\ \text{and}\ \left(\dfrac{21}{2},-7\right)}.
\]

question:
A common argument for \(0.\overline{9}=1\) is: ``For every digit \(a\), \(0.\overline{a}=\dfrac{a}{9}\). Taking \(a=9\) gives \(0.\overline{9}=\dfrac{9}{9}=1\).'' A student says this is circular reasoning.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove, without assuming \(0.\overline{9}=1\), that \(0.\overline{a}=\dfrac{a}{9}\) for every digit \(a=1,2,\ldots,9\).
\item[(b)] Is the quoted argument necessarily circular? Explain what must be justified about an infinite decimal for the argument to be valid.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The condition $\alpha<45^\circ$ is not needed; the identity follows from the angle-sum formula.

In the unit circle, the angle $2\alpha$ is twice the angle $\alpha$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1;
pair O=(0,0);
real ang1=25*pi/180, ang2=50*pi/180;
pair A=(r*cos(ang1),r*sin(ang1)), B=(r*cos(ang2),r*sin(ang2));
draw(circle(O,r),linewidth(0.8));
draw((-1.1,0)--(1.15,0),linewidth(0.5));
draw((0,-1.1)--(0,1.15),linewidth(0.5));
draw(O--A,linewidth(0.8));
draw(O--B,linewidth(0.8));
dot(O); dot(A); dot(B);
label("$O$",O,SW);
label("$A$",A,NE);
label("$B$",B,N);
label("$\alpha$",(0.30,0.06));
label("$2\alpha$",(0.17,0.24));
\end{asy}

Using $\cos(u+v)=\cos u\cos v-\sin u\sin v$ with $u=v=\alpha$,
\[
\cos 2\alpha=\cos(\alpha+\alpha)=\cos^2\alpha-\sin^2\alpha.
\]
The Pythagorean identity gives $\sin^2\alpha=1-\cos^2\alpha$, so
\[
\cos 2\alpha=\cos^2\alpha-(1-\cos^2\alpha)=2\cos^2\alpha-1.
\]
Thus the required identity holds for every angle $\alpha$, and in particular for $\alpha<45^\circ$.

Place coordinates with $C=(0,0)$, $A=(a,0)$, and $B=(0,b)$, where $a,b>0$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair PtC=(0,0), PtA=(4,0), PtB=(0,3), PtM=(2,1.5);
draw(PtC--PtA--PtB--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(PtC--PtM,dashed+linewidth(0.8));
draw((0.4,0)--(0.4,0.4)--(0,0.4),linewidth(0.6));
dot(PtC); dot(PtM);
label("$C$",PtC,SW);
label("$A$",PtA,SE);
label("$B$",PtB,NW);
label("$M$",PtM,NE);
label("$a$",(2,0),S);
label("$b$",(0,1.5),W);
\end{asy}

Then
\[
AB=\sqrt{a^2+b^2}.
\]
Since $M$ is the midpoint of $AB$,
\[
M=\left(\dfrac{a}{2},\dfrac{b}{2}\right).
\]
Thus
\[
CM=\sqrt{\left(\dfrac{a}{2}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{b}{2}\right)^2}
=\sqrt{\dfrac{a^2+b^2}{4}}
=\dfrac{1}{2}\sqrt{a^2+b^2}
=\dfrac{AB}{2}.
\]
Therefore the median to the hypotenuse has length half the hypotenuse.

Let the rectangle have coordinates
\[
A=(0,0),\quad B=(w,0),\quad C=(w,h),\quad D=(0,h),
\]
where $w,h>0$. Then
\[
E=\left(0,\dfrac{h}{2}\right),\qquad F=\left(0,\dfrac{3h}{4}\right).
\]
The line $CE$ has slope $\dfrac{h}{2w}$ and meets the line $AB$ (which is $y=0$) at $x=-w$, so
\[
G=(-w,0).
\]
The line $BF$ has slope $-\dfrac{3h}{4w}$ and meets the line $CD$ (which is $y=h$) at $x=-\dfrac{w}{3}$, so
\[
H=\left(-\dfrac{w}{3},h\right).
\]
Here is the configuration:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real w=4, h=3;
pair ptA=(0,0), ptB=(w,0), ptC=(w,h), ptD=(0,h);
pair ptE=(0,h/2), ptF=(0,3*h/4);
pair ptG=(-w,0), ptH=(-w/3,h);
draw(ptA--ptB--ptC--ptD--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(ptC--ptG,linewidth(0.8));
draw(ptB--ptH,linewidth(0.8));
dot(ptA); dot(ptB); dot(ptC); dot(ptD);
dot(ptE); dot(ptF); dot(ptG); dot(ptH);
label("$A$",ptA,SW);
label("$B$",ptB,SE);
label("$C$",ptC,NE);
label("$D$",ptD,NW);
label("$E$",ptE,W);
label("$F$",ptF,W);
label("$G$",ptG,S);
label("$H$",ptH,N);
\end{asy}

Triangles $BCG$ and $BCH$ share the vertical base $BC$ of length $h$. The height of $\triangle BCG$ to $BC$ is the horizontal distance from $G$ to the line $x=w$, namely $2w$. The height of $\triangle BCH$ to $BC$ is the horizontal distance from $H$ to $x=w$, namely $\dfrac{4w}{3}$. Therefore
\[
[BCG]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot h\cdot 2w=wh,
\]
and
\[
[BCH]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot h\cdot \dfrac{4w}{3}=\dfrac{2wh}{3}.
\]
Hence
\[
\dfrac{[BCG]}{[BCH]}=\dfrac{wh}{\dfrac{2wh}{3}}=\dfrac{3}{2}.
\]
Thus $m=3$ and $n=2$, and
\[
10m+10n+mn=10(3)+10(2)+(3)(2)=30+20+6=\boxed{56}.
\]

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many subsets of \(\{1,2,3,4\}\) contain \(3\)?
\item[(b)] More generally, if \(n\ge 1\) and \(k\in\{1,2,\ldots,n\}\), how many subsets of \(\{1,2,\ldots,n\}\) contain \(k\)? Prove your formula.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let \(O\) be the center of the regular octagon. The longest diagonal of a regular octagon joins two opposite vertices, so it is a diameter of the circumcircle, and the circumradius is
\[
10\text{ cm}.
\]
The whole octagon lies inside its circumcircle of radius \(10\), because the octagon is the convex hull of eight points on that circle and the disk bounded by the circle is convex.

Now consider any square contained in the octagon. Its diagonal is a segment with both endpoints in the octagon, hence in the circumcircle, so the diagonal has length at most the diameter of that circle, namely \(20\). A square with diagonal \(d\) has side \(\dfrac{d}{\sqrt2}\) and area \(\dfrac{d^2}{2}\). Therefore the area of any square inside the octagon is at most
\[
\dfrac{20^2}{2}=200.
\]

It remains to show that area \(200\) is attained.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),10), gray+dashed+linewidth(0.6));
pair A=(10,0), B=(0,10), C=(-10,0), D=(0,-10);
pair V1=(5*sqrt(2),5*sqrt(2)), V3=(-5*sqrt(2),5*sqrt(2)), V5=(-5*sqrt(2),-5*sqrt(2)), V7=(5*sqrt(2),-5*sqrt(2));
draw(A--V1--B--V3--C--V5--D--V7--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle, blue+linewidth(1.0));
draw(A--C, red+dashed+linewidth(0.8));
draw(B--D, red+dashed+linewidth(0.8));
draw((0.8,0)--(0.8,0.8)--(0,0.8), linewidth(0.6));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot((0,0));
label("$A$", A, E);
label("$B$", B, N);
label("$C$", C, W);
label("$D$", D, S);
label("$O$", (0,0), SW);
label("$20$", (3.6,0), N);
\end{asy}

Take the square \(ABCD\) whose vertices are the four octagon vertices that are endpoints of two perpendicular longest diagonals of the octagon. Its diagonals \(AC\) and \(BD\) both have length \(20\), are perpendicular, and bisect each other at \(O\) (they are diameters of the same circle), so \(ABCD\) is a square of area
\[
\dfrac{20^2}{2}=200.
\]
Since the octagon is convex and all four vertices of this square are vertices of the octagon, the square, being their convex hull, lies inside the octagon. Thus the largest possible area is
\[
\boxed{200}.
\]

Let \([XYZ]\) denote the area of triangle \(XYZ\), and write \(S=[ABC]\). Each of the segments \(EF\), \(GH\), and \(MN\) lies inside the convex triangle, so their common point \(O\) is inside \(ABC\). Define
\[
u=\dfrac{[OBC]}{S},\qquad v=\dfrac{[OCA]}{S},\qquad w=\dfrac{[OAB]}{S}.
\]
Then \(u,v,w>0\) and \(u+v+w=1\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fontsize(8pt);
pair A=(0,0), B=(4,0), C=(0,4);
pair O=(1,2/3);
pair Ep=(1,0), F=(1,3), G=(10/3,2/3), H=(0,2/3), M=(5/3,0), Np=(0,5/3);
fill(O--M--Ep--cycle,paleblue);
fill(O--Np--H--cycle,paleyellow);
fill(O--G--F--cycle,rgb(0.88,0.95,0.88));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(Ep--F,red+dashed+linewidth(0.7));
draw(G--H,red+dashed+linewidth(0.7));
draw(M--Np,red+dashed+linewidth(0.7));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(O); dot(Ep); dot(F); dot(G); dot(H); dot(M); dot(Np);
label("$A$",A,SW);
label("$B$",B,SE);
label("$C$",C,NW);
label("$O$",O,NE);
label("$E$",Ep,S);
label("$M$",M,S);
label("$G$",G,E);
label("$F$",F,NE);
label("$H$",H,W);
label("$N$",Np,W);
label("$4$",(O+M+Ep)/3);
label("$9$",(O+Np+H)/3);
label("$49$",(O+G+F)/3);
\end{asy}

Because \(EF\parallel AC\), the points \(E\) and \(O\) have the same distance from line \(AC\); this distance is \(v\) times the altitude from \(B\) to \(AC\). Since \(E\in AB\), \(\dfrac{AE}{AB}=v\).

Because \(MN\parallel BC\), the distance from \(M\) to \(BC\) is \(u\) times the altitude from \(A\) to \(BC\), so \(\dfrac{AM}{AB}=1-u\). Hence
\[
\dfrac{ME}{AB}=(1-u)-v=w.
\]
The distance from \(O\) to \(AB\) is \(w\) times the altitude from \(C\) to \(AB\). Therefore
\[
[MOE]=S w^2.
\]
By the same argument on the other two sides,
\[
[GOF]=S u^2,\qquad [NOH]=S v^2.
\]
Using the three given areas,
\[
S u^2=49,\qquad S v^2=9,\qquad S w^2=4.
\]
Since \(u,v,w\) are positive,
\[
u=\dfrac{7}{\sqrt S},\qquad v=\dfrac{3}{\sqrt S},\qquad w=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt S}.
\]
Thus
\[
1=u+v+w=\dfrac{12}{\sqrt S},
\]
so \(\sqrt S=12\) and \(S=144\). Therefore
\[
[ABC]=\boxed{144}.
\]

question:
Five circles are shaded inside a larger circle of radius \(7\) units. The large shaded circle is concentric with the outer circle. The four small congruent shaded circles are tangent to the outer circle and to the large shaded circle. The radius of each small shaded circle is \(\dfrac{1}{5}\) of the radius of the large shaded circle.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=7;
real r0=5;
real rs=1;
draw(circle((0,0),R), linewidth(0.9));
fill(circle((0,0),r0), gray(0.75));
draw(circle((0,0),r0), linewidth(0.8));
for(int i=0; i<4; ++i) {
  pair Q=(6*cos(pi/2*i),6*sin(pi/2*i));
  fill(circle(Q,rs), gray(0.75));
  draw(circle(Q,rs), linewidth(0.8));
}
draw((0,0)--(7*cos(pi/6),7*sin(pi/6)), dashed+gray(0.4)+linewidth(0.6));
dot((0,0));
label("$7$",(6.2*cos(pi/6),6.2*sin(pi/6)), NE);
\end{asy}

What fraction of the area of the outer circle is shaded? Express your answer as a common fraction.

solution:
Let \(r\) be the radius of the large shaded circle, so each small shaded circle has radius \(\dfrac{r}{5}\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=7;
real r0=5;
real rs=1;
draw(circle((0,0),R), linewidth(0.9));
fill(circle((0,0),r0), gray(0.9));
draw(circle((0,0),r0), linewidth(0.8));
for(int i=0; i<4; ++i) {
  pair Q=(6*cos(pi/2*i),6*sin(pi/2*i));
  fill(circle(Q,rs), gray(0.9));
  draw(circle(Q,rs), linewidth(0.8));
}
pair O=(0,0);
pair Cc=(6,0);
draw(O--(7,0), linewidth(0.8));
draw(Cc--(6,1), linewidth(0.8));
dot(O);
dot(Cc);
dot((5,0));
dot((7,0));
label("$O$",O,SW);
label("$C$",Cc,S);
label("$r$",(2.5,0),N);
label("$\dfrac{r}{5}$",(6.3,0.5),E);
draw(O--(R*cos(2*pi/3),R*sin(2*pi/3)), dashed+gray(0.4)+linewidth(0.6));
label("$7$",(6.2*cos(2*pi/3),6.2*sin(2*pi/3)), NW);
\end{asy}

Let \(O\) be the common center of the outer circle and the large shaded circle, and let \(C\) be the center of one small circle. Because the small circle is tangent to the large shaded circle,
\[
OC=r+\dfrac{r}{5}=\dfrac{6r}{5}.
\]
Because the small circle is internally tangent to the outer circle of radius \(7\),
\[
OC=7-\dfrac{r}{5}.
\]
Equating these expressions gives
\[
\dfrac{6r}{5}=7-\dfrac{r}{5},
\]
so \(\dfrac{7r}{5}=7\), hence \(r=5\). Therefore each small circle has radius \(1\).

The shaded area is
\[
\pi\cdot 5^2+4\pi\cdot 1^2=25\pi+4\pi=29\pi.
\]
The area of the outer circle is
\[
\pi\cdot 7^2=49\pi.
\]
Thus the required fraction is
\[
\boxed{\dfrac{29}{49}}.
\]

Let \(E\) denote the left-hand side. Group the terms as
\[
\begin{aligned}
E&=(a^3-ab^2+2abc-ac^2)+(a^2b+a^2c-b^3+b^2c+bc^2-c^3)\\
&=a(a^2-b^2+2bc-c^2)+(b+c)(a^2-b^2+2bc-c^2)\\
&=(a+b+c)(a^2-(b-c)^2)\\
&=(a+b+c)(a-b+c)(a+b-c).
\end{aligned}
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
defaultpen(fontsize(8pt));
pair A=(0,0);
pair B=(4.2,0);
pair C=(1.2,2.5);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C);
label("$A$",A,SW);
label("$B$",B,SE);
label("$C$",C,N);
label("$c$",(A+B)/2,S);
label("$a$",(B+C)/2,NE);
label("$b$",(C+A)/2,NW);
\end{asy}

Since \(a,b,c\) are side lengths of a nondegenerate triangle, the triangle inequalities give
\[
a+b>c,\qquad a+c>b,
\]
so \(a+b-c>0\) and \(a-b+c>0\). Also \(a+b+c>0\). Hence the product is positive, and the original expression is nonnegative.

question:
A circle with center \(O\) has radius \(OU=5\). From an external point \(P\), tangents \(PQ\) and \(PR\) are drawn to the circle at \(Q\) and \(R\). A third tangent to the circle at \(U\) meets segments \(PQ\) and \(PR\) at \(S\) and \(T\), respectively. If \(PO=9\), what is the perimeter of triangle \(PTS\)?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
defaultpen(fontsize(8pt));
real r=5;
pair O=(9,0);
pair P=(0,0);
real xq=56/9;
real yq=10*sqrt(14)/9;
pair Q=(xq,yq);
pair R=(xq,-yq);
real ys=(4/xq)*yq;
pair SP=(4,ys);
pair TP=(4,-ys);
pair U=(4,0);
draw(circle(O,r),linewidth(0.8));
draw(P--Q,linewidth(0.8));
draw(P--R,linewidth(0.8));
draw(SP--TP,linewidth(0.8));
dot(P); dot(O); dot(Q); dot(R); dot(SP); dot(TP); dot(U);
label("$P$",P,W);
label("$O$",O,E);
label("$Q$",Q,NE);
label("$R$",R,SE);
label("$S$",SP,NW);
label("$T$",TP,SW);
label("$U$",U,E);
\end{asy}

solution:
Since \(OQ\perp PQ\), triangle \(POQ\) is right with hypotenuse \(PO=9\) and leg \(OQ=5\). Hence
\[
PQ=\sqrt{9^2-5^2}=\sqrt{56}=2\sqrt{14}.
\]
Similarly, \(PR=2\sqrt{14}\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
defaultpen(fontsize(8pt));
pair uv(pair v){ return v/sqrt(v.x^2+v.y^2); }
real r=5;
pair O=(9,0);
pair P=(0,0);
real xq=56/9;
real yq=10*sqrt(14)/9;
pair Q=(xq,yq);
pair R=(xq,-yq);
real ys=(4/xq)*yq;
pair SP=(4,ys);
pair TP=(4,-ys);
pair U=(4,0);
draw(circle(O,r),linewidth(0.8));
draw(P--Q,linewidth(0.8));
draw(P--R,linewidth(0.8));
draw(SP--TP,linewidth(0.8));
draw(O--Q,blue+dashed+linewidth(0.8));
draw(O--R,blue+dashed+linewidth(0.8));
draw(O--U,blue+dashed+linewidth(0.8));
draw(P--O,red+dashed+linewidth(0.8));
draw(SP--Q,red+linewidth(1.0));
draw(SP--U,red+linewidth(1.0));
draw(TP--R,blue+linewidth(1.0));
draw(TP--U,blue+linewidth(1.0));
real m=0.35;
pair uq=uv(P-Q);
pair vq=uv(O-Q);
draw(Q+m*uq--Q+m*uq+m*vq--Q+m*vq,linewidth(0.6));
pair ur=uv(P-R);
pair vr=uv(O-R);
draw(R+m*ur--R+m*ur+m*vr--R+m*vr,linewidth(0.6));
draw(U+(0,m)--U+(0,m)+(m,0)--U+(m,0),linewidth(0.6));
dot(P); dot(O); dot(Q); dot(R); dot(SP); dot(TP); dot(U);
label("$P$",P,W);
label("$O$",O,E);
label("$Q$",Q,NE);
label("$R$",R,SE);
label("$S$",SP,NW);
label("$T$",TP,SW);
label("$U$",U,NW);
label("$5$",(O+Q)/2,NE,blue+fontsize(8pt));
label("$5$",(O+U)/2,N,blue+fontsize(8pt));
label("$9$",(2,0),N,red+fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

From point \(S\), the two tangent segments to the circle are equal, so \(SQ=SU\). From point \(T\), \(TR=TU\). Because \(S,U,T\) are collinear with \(U\) between \(S\) and \(T\),
\[
ST=SU+UT=SQ+TR.
\]
Therefore
\[
\begin{aligned}
PS+ST+TP
&=PS+(SQ+TR)+TP\\
&=(PS+SQ)+(PT+TR)\\
&=PQ+PR\\
&=4\sqrt{14}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The perimeter is \(\boxed{4\sqrt{14}}\).

The line $x+2y=12$ meets the $x$-axis at $(12,0)$ and the $y$-axis at $(0,6)$, and $y=mx$ passes through the origin. The three lines cut off a triangle with vertices
\[(0,0),\qquad (12,0),\qquad \left(\dfrac{12}{1+2m},\ \dfrac{12m}{1+2m}\right),\]
the last being the intersection of $y=mx$ with $x+2y=12$ (substitute $y=mx$: $x(1+2m)=12$).

Whichever side of the $x$-axis that third vertex lies on, the triangle has base $12$ along the $x$-axis and height equal to the absolute value of its $y$-coordinate, so
\[\text{area}=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot 12\cdot\left|\dfrac{12m}{1+2m}\right|=\left|\dfrac{72m}{1+2m}\right|.\]
Setting this equal to $12$ gives two cases.

\textbf{Case 1: $m>0$.} Then $1+2m>0$ and the vertex $(8,2)$ lies in the first quadrant:
\[\dfrac{72m}{1+2m}=12\iff 72m=12+24m\iff m=\dfrac{1}{4}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real sc=0.33;
pair pp(real x, real y){return (sc*x,sc*y);}
fill(pp(0,0)--pp(12,0)--pp(8,2)--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.9,1));
draw(pp(-1,0)--pp(17.5,0),Arrow);
draw(pp(0,-3)--pp(0,7),Arrow);
draw(pp(-0.5,6.25)--pp(14,-1),red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(pp(-4,-1)--pp(16.5,4.125),blue+linewidth(0.9));
label("$x$",pp(17.5,0),E);
label("$y$",pp(0,7),N);
label("$x+2y=12$",pp(1.5,5.25),NW,red);
label("$y=\dfrac{1}{4}x$",pp(14.3,3.575),NE,blue);
label("$O$",pp(0,0),SW);
label("$(12,0)$",pp(12,0),SE);
label("$(8,2)$",pp(8,2),N);
label("$12$",pp(9,0.7),E);
dot(pp(0,0)); dot(pp(12,0)); dot(pp(8,2));
\end{asy}

\textbf{Case 2: $m<0$ with $1+2m>0$.} Now the third vertex lies below the $x$-axis, and the triangle is bounded by the $x$-axis above and the two lines below:
\[-\dfrac{72m}{1+2m}=12\iff -72m=12+24m\iff m=-\dfrac{1}{8}.\]
Here the vertex is $(16,-2)$, giving base $12$ and height $2$, area $12$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real sc=0.33;
pair pp(real x, real y){return (sc*x,sc*y);}
