\textbf{Case 3: $1+2m<0$, i.e. $m<-\dfrac{1}{2}$.} Then the intersection point has a negative $x$-coordinate and a positive $y$-coordinate, and the area is $\dfrac{72m}{1+2m}$ (positive because both numerator and denominator are negative). Setting it equal to $12$ again forces $72m=12+24m$, i.e. $m=\dfrac{1}{4}$, which contradicts $m<-\dfrac{1}{2}$. So there is no such case.

The only possible values are $m=\dfrac{1}{4}$ and $m=-\dfrac{1}{8}$, so the smallest possible value is $\boxed{-\dfrac{1}{8}}$, choice (B).

A string made from TEXAS uses distinct letters, so it only has to satisfy the availability in TENNESSEE. The letters of TEXAS are T, E, X, A, S, while the letters of TENNESSEE are T, E, N, S. Since X and A never appear in TENNESSEE, the only usable letters are
\[\text{T},\ \text{E},\ \text{S},\]
and any string of distinct letters chosen from these three is automatically an arrangement of some of the letters of TENNESSEE.

Count by length:
\begin{itemize}
\item length $1$: $3$ strings (T, E, S);
\item length $2$: $3\cdot 2=6$ strings (TE, ET, TS, ST, ES, SE);
\item length $3$: $3\cdot 2\cdot 1=6$ strings (TES, TSE, ETS, EST, STE, SET).
\end{itemize}
No longer strings are possible, since only three usable letters exist. The total is
\[3+6+6=\boxed{15}.\]

Write $k=10^{2021}$, so the side of $S_1$ is $a=k+1$. Suppose a square has side $a$ and that each side is divided in the ratio $1:k$; one side of the inner square is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs $\dfrac{a}{k+1}$ and $\dfrac{ak}{k+1}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(4,0), C=(4,4), Dd=(0,4);
pair P=(1,0), Q=(4,1), R=(3,4), U=(0,3);
fill(P--Q--R--U--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.9,1));
draw(A--B--C--Dd--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(P--Q--R--U--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((0.25,0)--(0.25,0.25)--(0,0.25),linewidth(0.6));
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",Dd,NW);
label("$P$",P,S); label("$Q$",Q,E); label("$R$",R,N); label("$U$",U,W);
label("$\dfrac{a}{k+1}$",(0.5,0),S);
label("$\dfrac{ak}{k+1}$",(0,1.5),W);
label("$s$",(0.5,1.5),NE);
\end{asy}

(The sketch uses $k=3$ so that the division points are visible; the labels are symbolic.) By the Pythagorean theorem the inner side is
\[s=\sqrt{\left(\dfrac{a}{k+1}\right)^{2}+\left(\dfrac{ak}{k+1}\right)^{2}}=\dfrac{a\sqrt{k^{2}+1}}{k+1},\]
so each new square has area $\dfrac{k^{2}+1}{(k+1)^{2}}$ times the previous one. Thus
\[A_1=(k+1)^{2},\qquad r=\dfrac{A_{n+1}}{A_n}=\dfrac{k^{2}+1}{(k+1)^{2}}<1 ,\]
and the series is geometric:
\[S=\dfrac{A_1}{1-r}=\dfrac{(k+1)^{2}}{1-\dfrac{k^{2}+1}{(k+1)^{2}}}=\dfrac{(k+1)^{4}}{(k+1)^{2}-k^{2}-1}=\dfrac{(k+1)^{4}}{2k}.\]
Expanding,
\[S=\dfrac{k^{4}+4k^{3}+6k^{2}+4k+1}{2k}=\dfrac{k^{3}}{2}+2k^{2}+3k+2+\dfrac{1}{2k}.\]
With $k=10^{2021}$, which is even, this is
\[S=5\cdot 10^{6062}+2\cdot 10^{4042}+3\cdot 10^{2021}+2+\dfrac{1}{2\cdot 10^{2021}}.\]
Since $0<\dfrac{1}{2\cdot 10^{2021}}<1$, the greatest integer less than $S$ is
\[5\cdot 10^{6062}+2\cdot 10^{4042}+3\cdot 10^{2021}+2 .\]
The nonzero digits sit in positions $6062$, $4042$, $2021$ and $0$, which are all different, so there are no carries and the digit sum is
\[5+2+3+2=\boxed{12},\]
choice (D).

Put the room in the coordinate plane as the square $0\le x\le 3$, $0\le y\le 3$ and put Carol at $O=(0,0)$. The pillar is the unit square $1\le x\le 2$, $1\le y\le 2$, so the floor has area $9-1=8$.


