label("hidden", (2.55,2.6), fontsize(7pt));
label("$\left(\dfrac{3}{2},3\right)$", (1.5,3), N, fontsize(7pt));
label("$\left(3,\dfrac{3}{2}\right)$", (3,1.5), E, fontsize(7pt));
\end{asy}


A floor point $X$ is invisible to Carol exactly when the segment $OX$ passes through the interior of the pillar.

From $O$ the pillar is seen between the rays through its two nearer corners $(1,2)$ and $(2,1)$, whose equations are $y=2x$ and $y=\dfrac{x}{2}$. Every point of the pillar lies in the cone
\[
K=\left\{(x,y):\dfrac{x}{2}\le y\le 2x\right\},
\]
and conversely a ray from $O$ meets the pillar if and only if its direction lies in $K$. Therefore every floor point outside $K$ is visible.

Inside the room, $K$ is the square with two right triangles removed: the part above $y=2x$ is the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(0,3)$, $\left(\dfrac{3}{2},3\right)$, of area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot 3\cdot\dfrac{3}{2}=\dfrac{9}{4}$, and the part below $y=\dfrac{x}{2}$ is the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(3,0)$, $\left(3,\dfrac{3}{2}\right)$, also of area $\dfrac{9}{4}$. Hence
\[
[K]=9-\dfrac{9}{4}-\dfrac{9}{4}=\dfrac{9}{2}.
\]

Now split $K$ into the visible part, the pillar, and the hidden part. A ray of slope $s$ with $\dfrac{1}{2}\le s\le 1$ enters the pillar through its bottom edge at the point $\left(\dfrac{1}{s},1\right)$, so the visible part of that ray is the segment from $O$ to $\left(\dfrac{1}{s},1\right)$; the union of these segments over $\dfrac{1}{2}\le s\le 1$ is the triangle $O(1,1)(2,1)$, of area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot 1\cdot 1=\dfrac{1}{2}$. Similarly, a ray of slope $s$ with $1\le s\le 2$ enters the pillar through its left edge at $(1,s)$, and the union of the visible pieces is the triangle $O(1,1)(1,2)$, also of area $\dfrac{1}{2}$. So the visible part of $K$ has area $1$.

The pillar itself has area $1$, so the hidden part of $K$ has area
\[
\dfrac{9}{2}-1-1=\dfrac{5}{2}.
\]
(As a check, the hidden region is the hexagon with vertices $(2,1)$, $(2,2)$, $(1,2)$, $\left(\dfrac{3}{2},3\right)$, $(3,3)$, $\left(3,\dfrac{3}{2}\right)$, whose area by the shoelace formula is $\dfrac{5}{2}$.)

Therefore Carol sees
\[
8-\dfrac{5}{2}=\dfrac{11}{2}
\]
square units of floor, which is the fraction
\[
\dfrac{11}{2}\cdot\dfrac{1}{8}=\boxed{\dfrac{11}{16}},
\]
so the answer is $\textbf{(C)}$.

question:
For a positive integer $n$ and a nonnegative integer $N$, let $S$ be the set of all multiples of $n$ lying between $0$ and $N$ inclusive; thus $0\in S$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find a formula, in terms of $n$ and $N$, for the average of the elements of $S$.
\item[(b)] Use the formula to compute the average of all multiples of $7$ between $0$ and $100$ inclusive.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Write $Q=(t,t^2)$ for a real number $t$. Then
\[
PQ^2=(t-10)^2+(t^2-10)^2=t^4-19t^2-20t+200=:f(t).
\]
Since $PQ\ge 0$, minimising $PQ$ is the same as minimising $f$.

Differentiating,
\[
f'(t)=4t^3-38t-20=2\,(2t^3-19t-10).
\]
Let $h(t)=2t^3-19t-10$. Then $h'(t)=6t^2-19$, which vanishes only at $t=\pm\sqrt{\dfrac{19}{6}}\approx\pm1.78$, so $h$ has at most three real roots. Since
\[
h(-3)=-7,\quad h(-2)=12,\quad h(-1)=7,\quad h(0)=-10,\quad h(3)=-13,\quad h(4)=42,
\]
$h$ has exactly three real roots $r_1\in(-3,-2)$, $r_2\in(-1,0)$ and $r_3\in(3,4)$. Thus $f$ decreases, increases, decreases and increases, with local minima at $r_1$ and $r_3$ (and a local maximum at $r_2$).

The local minimum at $r_1$ is not the global one: for $t<0$,
\[
f(t)=(t-10)^2+(t^2-10)^2\ge(t-10)^2>100,
\]
while $f(3)=(3-10)^2+(9-10)^2=49+1=50$. Also $f(t)\to\infty$ as $t\to+\infty$, and on $[0,\infty)$ the only critical point that can give a minimum is $r_3$ (the point $r_2$ gives a local maximum, and $f(0)=200$). Hence the global minimum of $f$ is $f(r_3)$.

The sketch below shows the parabola, the point $P$, the closest point $Q=(r_3,r_3^2)$, and the normal segment $PQ$; the small right angle at $Q$ records that $PQ$ is perpendicular to the tangent line there.

