Set \(a=x-p\) and \(b=y-q\). Then \(ab=r\), and
\[a+b=(x+y)-p-q=s-p-q.\]
Put
\[S=s-p-q,\qquad w=\sqrt{S^{2}-4r}.\]
Because such a point \(P\) exists, \(S^{2}-4r=(a-b)^{2}\ge 0\), so \(w\) is real. The numbers \(a\) and \(b\) are the roots of \(t^{2}-St+r=0\), so for one of the two choices of sign,
\[a=\dfrac{S+w}{2},\quad b=\dfrac{S-w}{2}
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
a=\dfrac{S-w}{2},\quad b=\dfrac{S+w}{2}.\]
In either case \(a-b=\pm w\). The two choices correspond to the two possible positions of \(P\) (labeled \(P\) and \(P'\) in the sketch when they are distinct).

