Now
\[x-y=(p+a)-(q+b)=p-q+(a-b).\]
Thus the possible values of \(x-y\) are \(p-q+w\) and \(p-q-w\) (these coincide when \(w=0\)). Taking absolute values, the possible values of the positive difference \(|x-y|\) are
\[\boxed{\left|p-q+\sqrt{(s-p-q)^{2}-4r}\right|\quad\text{and}\quad\left|p-q-\sqrt{(s-p-q)^{2}-4r}\right|.}\]
These two values may coincide (for example when \(p=q\) or \(w=0\)); in that case there is only one possible value.

question:
Let $A$, $D$, $E$ be points in the plane such that $\angle DAE$ is acute. Points $B$ and $C$ are chosen on rays $AD$ and $AE$ respectively so that triangle $ABC$ is acute. Let $AX$, $BY$, $CZ$ be the altitudes of triangle $ABC$, with $X$ on $BC$, $Y$ on $CA$, $Z$ on $AB$.

Prove that as $B$ and $C$ vary, the measure of $\angle YXZ$ stays the same, and determine that constant measure in terms of $\angle DAE$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(5,0), C=(2.5712,3.0642);
real t=((A.x-B.x)*(C.x-B.x)+(A.y-B.y)*(C.y-B.y))/((C.x-B.x)^2+(C.y-B.y)^2);
pair X=B+t*(C-B);
real s=((B.x-A.x)*(C.x-A.x)+(B.y-A.y)*(C.y-A.y))/((C.x-A.x)^2+(C.y-A.y)^2);
pair Y=A+s*(C-A);
pair Z=(C.x,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--X,dashed+linewidth(0.7));
draw(B--Y,dashed+linewidth(0.7));
draw(C--Z,dashed+linewidth(0.7));
pair u1=(A-X); u1=u1/sqrt(u1.x^2+u1.y^2);
pair v1=(B-X); v1=v1/sqrt(v1.x^2+v1.y^2);
draw(X--(X+0.24*u1)--(X+0.24*u1+0.24*v1)--(X+0.24*v1),linewidth(0.5));
pair u2=(B-Y); u2=u2/sqrt(u2.x^2+u2.y^2);
pair v2=(A-Y); v2=v2/sqrt(v2.x^2+v2.y^2);
draw(Y--(Y+0.24*u2)--(Y+0.24*u2+0.24*v2)--(Y+0.24*v2),linewidth(0.5));
draw(Z--(Z+(0,0.24))--(Z+(0.24,0.24))--(Z+(0.24,0)),linewidth(0.5));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(X);dot(Y);dot(Z);
label("$A$",(-0.32,-0.24));
label("$B$",(5.3,-0.24));
label("$C$",(2.38,3.45));
label("$X$",(3.55,2.78));
label("$Y$",(1.55,2.82));
label("$Z$",(2.62,-0.34));
\end{asy}

solution:
Let $\alpha=\angle DAE=\angle BAC$; since $ABC$ is acute, $0<\alpha<90^\circ$, and the feet $X$, $Y$, $Z$ lie in the interiors of $BC$, $CA$, $AB$ respectively.


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(5,0), C=(2.5712,3.0642);
real t=((A.x-B.x)*(C.x-B.x)+(A.y-B.y)*(C.y-B.y))/((C.x-B.x)^2+(C.y-B.y)^2);
pair X=B+t*(C-B);
real s=((B.x-A.x)*(C.x-A.x)+(B.y-A.y)*(C.y-A.y))/((C.x-A.x)^2+(C.y-A.y)^2);
pair Y=A+s*(C-A);
pair Z=(C.x,0);
draw(circle((A+B)/2,2.5),grey+linewidth(0.6));
draw(circle((A+C)/2,2.0),grey+linewidth(0.6));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(X--Y--Z--cycle,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--X,dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(B--Y,dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(C--Z,dashed+linewidth(0.6));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(X);dot(Y);dot(Z);
label("$A$",(-0.34,-0.26));
label("$B$",(5.3,-0.26));
label("$C$",(2.36,3.45));
label("$X$",(3.6,2.8));
label("$Y$",(1.5,2.85));
label("$Z$",(2.62,-0.36));
\end{asy}


\textbf{Angle $\angle YXA$.} Since $AX\perp BC$ and $BY\perp CA$,
\[\angle AXB=90^\circ=\angle AYB.\]
So $X$ and $Y$ both see the segment $AB$ under a right angle (Thales), i.e. $A$, $X$, $Y$, $B$ lie on the circle with diameter $AB$. In that circle $\angle YXA$ and $\angle YBA$ subtend the same chord $YA$, and $X$, $B$ lie on the same side of the line $YA$, which is the line $AC$ (because $X$ is an interior point of $BC$). Hence
\[\angle YXA=\angle YBA.\]
In the right triangle $ABY$ we have $\angle AYB=90^\circ$ and $\angle YAB=\alpha$, so $\angle YBA=90^\circ-\alpha$, giving
\[\angle YXA=90^\circ-\alpha.\]

\textbf{Angle $\angle AXZ$.} Similarly $CZ\perp AB$ and $AX\perp BC$ give $\angle AZC=90^\circ=\angle AXC$, so $A$, $Z$, $X$, $C$ lie on the circle with diameter $AC$. There $\angle AXZ$ and $\angle ACZ$ subtend the chord $AZ$, and $X$, $C$ lie on the same side of the line $AZ$, which is the line $AB$. Hence $\angle AXZ=\angle ACZ$, and in the right triangle $AZC$, with $\angle ZAC=\alpha$, we get
\[\angle AXZ=90^\circ-\alpha.\]

\textbf{Position of the ray $XA$.} Because $X$ is an interior point of $BC$, the points $B$ and $C$ lie on opposite sides of the line $AX$. Since $Z$ lies on segment $AB$ and $Y$ lies on segment $AC$, the point $Z$ is on the same side of line $AX$ as $B$, and $Y$ on the same side as $C$. Therefore $Y$ and $Z$ are on opposite sides of line $AX$, i.e. the rays $XY$ and $XZ$ lie on opposite sides of that line. Also $Y$ and $Z$ lie in the half-plane bounded by $BC$ that contains $A$, so the whole angle $\angle YXZ$ lies in that half-plane; the ray opposite to $XA$ points into the other half-plane and thus cannot lie inside $\angle YXZ$. Hence the ray $XA$ does lie inside $\angle YXZ$, and the two angles can be added:
\[\angle YXZ=\angle YXA+\angle AXZ=(90^\circ-\alpha)+(90^\circ-\alpha)=180^\circ-2\alpha.\]

Since this expression involves only the fixed angle $\alpha=\angle DAE$, the measure of $\angle YXZ$ is the same for every admissible choice of $B$ and $C$; and because $0<\alpha<90^\circ$, the value $180^\circ-2\alpha$ is indeed between $0^\circ$ and $180^\circ$. Thus
\[\angle YXZ=\boxed{180^\circ-2\angle DAE},\]
which does not change as $B$ and $C$ vary.

