Let $ABCD$ be the trapezoid with $AB\parallel DC$, $AB=b$, $DC=c$, and altitude $12$. Put
\[A=(0,0),\quad B=(b,0),\quad D=(p,12),\quad C=(p+c,12),\]
so that the two bases are horizontal and the distance between them is $12$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=12;
pair pA=(0,0), pB=(17,0), pC=(9,h), pD=(1,h);
draw(pA--pB--pC--pD--cycle, linewidth(1));
pair pX=pA+0.68*(pC-pA);
draw(pA--pC, red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(pB--pD, red+linewidth(0.9));
pair w1=unit(pA-pX), w2=unit(pB-pX);
real sm=1.7;
draw(pX+sm*w1--pX+sm*w1+sm*w2--pX+sm*w2, linewidth(0.7));
draw(pD--(1,0), dashed+linewidth(0.7));
dot(pA); dot(pB); dot(pC); dot(pD); dot(pX);
label("$A$",pA,SW); label("$B$",pB,SE); label("$C$",pC,NE); label("$D$",pD,NW);
label("$X$",pX,N);
label("$12$",(1,6),E);
label("$15$",(pA+pC)/2,W);
\end{asy}

Let $X$ be the intersection of the diagonals. Write $u=p+c$, the horizontal run of diagonal $AC$, and $v=p-b$, the horizontal run of diagonal $BD$. Then
\[\vec{AC}=(u,12),\qquad \vec{BD}=(v,12),\]
and the diagonals are perpendicular exactly when their dot product is $0$:
\[uv+12\cdot 12=0,\qquad\text{that is, } uv=-144.\]
So $u$ and $v$ have opposite signs, and $|u|\cdot|v|=144$.

On the other hand,
\[b+c=(p+c)-(p-b)=u-v.\]
Since $b+c>0$, we must have $u>0>v$, and therefore $b+c=u-v=|u|+|v|$. (Indeed the diagonals lean in opposite directions, so the two horizontal runs together make up exactly the sum of the bases.)

Hence the area is
\[[ABCD]=\dfrac{(b+c)\cdot 12}{2}=6\bigl(|u|+|v|\bigr).\]
Each diagonal has vertical component $12$, so a diagonal with horizontal run $w$ has length $\sqrt{w^2+144}$. Since one diagonal has length $15$, one of $|u|,|v|$ equals $w$ with
\[w^2+144=225,\qquad w^2=81,\qquad w=9.\]
The other run then satisfies $|u|\cdot|v|=144$, so it equals $\dfrac{144}{9}=16$. Therefore
\[[ABCD]=6(9+16)=\boxed{150}.\]

As a check, the diagonals have lengths $15$ and $\sqrt{16^2+12^2}=20$, and a quadrilateral with perpendicular diagonals has area $\dfrac{d_1d_2\sin\theta}{2}=\dfrac{15\cdot 20}{2}=150$. The configuration really exists, for example
\[A=(0,0),\quad B=(17,0),\quad C=(9,12),\quad D=(1,12),\]
whose diagonals $\vec{AC}=(9,12)$ and $\vec{BD}=(-16,12)$ are perpendicular, of lengths $15$ and $20$, with altitude $12$ and area $\dfrac{(17+8)\cdot 12}{2}=150$.

Let the circle with centre $S$ have radius $r$ and the circle with centre $T$ have radius $R$. Since the two circles are tangent to each other,
\[ST=r+R.\]
A radius drawn to a point of tangency is perpendicular to the tangent line, so $SA\perp AB$ and $TB\perp AB$. Hence $SA\parallel TB$, and the distance between these two parallel lines equals $AB$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=2, R=3;
pair pA=(0,0);
pair pB=(2*sqrt(r*R),0);
pair pS=(0,r);
pair pT=(pB.x,R);
pair pP=(0,2*r);
pair pK=(0,R);
pair dd=pT-pP; real LL=length(dd);
pair uu=dd/LL; pair nn=(-uu.y,uu.x);
pair pQ=pP+(4*r*r/LL)*uu+(2*r*R/LL)*nn;
draw(circle(pS,r), linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(pT,R), linewidth(0.9));
draw(pA--pB, linewidth(0.9));
draw(pA--pP, linewidth(0.9));
draw(pT--pB, linewidth(0.9));
draw(pP--pQ, linewidth(0.9));
draw(pQ--pT, linewidth(0.9));
draw(pT--pK, dashed+linewidth(0.7));
real sm=0.32;
draw((sm,0)--(sm,-sm)--(0,-sm), linewidth(0.6));
draw((pB.x-sm,0)--(pB.x-sm,-sm)--(pB.x,-sm), linewidth(0.6));
pair v1=unit(pP-pQ), v2=unit(pT-pQ);
draw(pQ+sm*v1--pQ+sm*v1+sm*v2--pQ+sm*v2, linewidth(0.6));
dot(pA); dot(pB); dot(pP); dot(pQ); dot(pS); dot(pT); dot(pK);
label("$A$",pA,SW); label("$B$",pB,SE); label("$P$",pP,W); label("$Q$",pQ,NE);
label("$S$",pS,W); label("$T$",pT,E); label("$K$",pK,W);
label("$r$",(pA+pS)/2,SW); label("$R$",(pB+pT)/2,E);
\end{asy}

Let $K$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $T$ to the line $SA$. Since $AB\perp SA$ and $AB\perp TB$, the quadrilateral $ABTK$ has right angles at $A$, $B$ and $K$, so it is a rectangle. Therefore
\[TK=AB,\qquad AK=TB=R.\]
In the right triangle $SKT$ (right angle at $K$) we have $SK=|R-r|$ and $ST=r+R$, so
\[AB^2=TK^2=ST^2-SK^2=(r+R)^2-(R-r)^2=4rR,\qquad AB=2\sqrt{rR}.\]
(Here $AB>0$, and the computation shows $AB=2\sqrt{rR}$.)

Because $AP$ is a diameter of the circle with centre $S$, the point $S$ is the midpoint of $AP$, so
\[AP=2r,\]
and $P$ lies on the line $SA$ on the side of $S$ away from $A$; in particular $PA\perp AB$ as well.

Now measure along the two perpendicular directions $AB$ and $AP$. The point $T$ lies on the line $TB$, which is parallel to $SA$ and at distance $AB$ from it, so the component of $\vec{PT}$ in the direction of $AB$ has length $AB$. Along the line $AP$ the point $T$ projects to $K$, and
\[PK=|AK-AP|=|R-2r|\]
(the point $K$ may lie on either side of $P$, which does not matter since only its square enters). Hence, by the Pythagorean theorem,
\[PT^2=AB^2+PK^2=4rR+(R-2r)^2=4rR+R^2-4rR+4r^2=R^2+4r^2.\]
In particular $PT^2-R^2=4r^2>0$, so $P$ lies outside the circle with centre $T$ and the tangent from $P$ to that circle exists.

Finally, because $PQ$ is tangent to the circle with centre $T$ at $Q$, the radius $TQ=R$ is perpendicular to $PQ$. Thus triangle $PQT$ is right-angled at $Q$, and
\[PQ^2=PT^2-TQ^2=(R^2+4r^2)-R^2=4r^2,\qquad PQ=2r.\]
Therefore $PQ=2r=AP$, which proves that $AP=PQ$.

Rewrite the first line as $y=-2x+2$, so its slope is $-2$. Rewrite the second line as $y=\dfrac{x}{2}$, so its slope is $\dfrac{1}{2}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
