The product of the slopes is
\[
(-2)\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}=-1.
\]
A product of slopes equal to $-1$ means the lines are perpendicular. Therefore the lines are $\boxed{\text{perpendicular}}$.

Let the width be $w$ meters. Then the length is $w+3$ meters. The area is $w(w+3)$, and the perimeter is
\[
2w+2(w+3)=4w+6.
\]
The condition says
\[
w(w+3)=4w+6.
\]
Expanding and simplifying,
\[
w^2+3w=4w+6,\qquad w^2-w-6=0,\qquad (w-3)(w+2)=0.
\]
Since a width must be positive, $w=3$. The length is $w+3=6$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(6,0), C=(6,3), D=(0,3);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0)--(0.3,0)--(0.3,0.3)--(0,0.3),linewidth(0.5));
draw((6,0)--(5.7,0)--(5.7,0.3)--(6,0.3),linewidth(0.5));
label("$6$ m",(A+B)/2,S);
label("$3$ m",(B+C)/2,E);
label("area $=18$",(3,1.5),N);
\end{asy}

Indeed, the area is $3\cdot6=18$ and the perimeter is $2(3+6)=18$. Thus the rectangle is
\[
\boxed{3\text{ meters by }6\text{ meters}}.
\]

