\textbf{Step 1: $\angle AFE=\angle ACB$, and the tangent to $\omega$ at $A$ is parallel to $EF$.}
Because $\angle BEC=\angle BFC=90^\circ$, the points $B,C,E,F$ lie on the circle with diameter $BC$. The triangle is acute, so $E$ and $F$ lie on the sides and the cyclic order of the four points is $C,E,F,B$; therefore the angles at the opposite vertices $C$ and $F$ of this cyclic quadrilateral are supplementary, that is, $\angle BFE=180^\circ-\angle BCE=180^\circ-\angle ACB$. Since $A,F,B$ are collinear,
\[\angle AFE=180^\circ-\angle BFE=\angle ACB.\]
By the tangent-chord theorem the tangent to $\omega$ at $A$ makes with the line $AB$ the angle $\angle ACB$; the line $EF$ makes with the line $AB$ the same directed angle, since $\angle AFE=\angle ACB$ and $AF$ lies on $AB$. Hence the tangent at $A$ is parallel to $EF$.

\textbf{Step 2: $AP=AP'$.}
The line $EF$ is not tangent to $\omega$: it is parallel to the tangent at $A$ but does not pass through $A$. So it meets $\omega$ in the two points $P,P'$. By Step 1 the radius $OA$ is perpendicular to $EF$, hence perpendicular to the chord $PP'$; a radius perpendicular to a chord bisects the chord and both arcs it cuts off. Thus $A$ is the midpoint of one of the arcs $PP'$, and equal arcs give equal chords:
\[AP=AP'.\]
Moreover, arcs $AP$ and $AP'$ are equal, so the inscribed angles that subtend them are equal:
\[\angle PBA=\angle ABP'.\]
Finally, $F$ lies inside $\omega$ (it is an interior point of the chord $AB$), so on the chord $PP'$ the point $F$ lies between $P$ and $P'$; hence $P$ and $P'$ lie on opposite sides of the line $AB$. Therefore the rays $BP$ and $BP'$ make equal angles with $BA$ on opposite sides, i.e. $\sigma$ maps the line $BP$ to the line $BP'$.

\textbf{Step 3: $\sigma$ maps the line $DF$ to the line $EF$.}
Since $\angle ADC=\angle AFC=90^\circ$, the points $A,D,F,C$ are concyclic (on the circle with diameter $AC$). So $\angle ADF=\angle ACF=90^\circ-\angle BAC$. The ray $DF$ lies inside the angle $ADB$ (as $F$ lies on the segment $AB$), and $\angle ADB=90^\circ$, hence
\[\angle BDF=\angle ADB-\angle ADF=90^\circ-(90^\circ-\angle BAC)=\angle BAC.\]
Together with $\angle DBF=\angle ABC$ (as $F$ lies on $BA$ and $D$ on $BC$) this gives $\triangle BDF\sim\triangle BAC$, so
\[\angle BFD=\angle BCA=\angle ACB.\]
Thus at the point $F$ of the line $AB$ the two distinct lines $FD$ and $FE$ make equal angles with $AB$ (namely $\angle BFD=\angle AFE$), so they are mirror images of each other: $\sigma$ maps the line $DF$ to the line $EF$.

\textbf{Step 4: conclusion.}
The reflection $\sigma$ maps points to points and lines to lines, and it is a bijection. By Step 2, $\sigma$ sends the line $BP$ to the line $BP'$; by Step 3 it sends the line $DF$ to the line $EF$. Hence
\[\sigma(Q)=\sigma\big(BP\cap DF\big)=BP'\cap EF=P',\]
because $P'$ lies both on $EF$ and on $BP'$ (and the two lines are distinct). Therefore $Q=\sigma(P')$, and since $\sigma$ fixes $A$ and preserves distances,
\[AQ=A\sigma(P')=AP'=AP.\]
This holds whichever of the two intersection points is called $P$, so $AP=AQ$, as required.

Throughout, think of $\cos x+i\sin x$ as the point of the unit circle at angle $x$; the sketch shows the angle $x$, the angle $y$ measured on from it, and the resulting angle $x+y$ for the product.


