\textbf{(a)} Expand and collect real and imaginary parts:
\[(\cos x+i\sin x)(\cos y+i\sin y)=\big(\cos x\cos y-\sin x\sin y\big)+i\big(\sin x\cos y+\cos x\sin y\big).\]
By the addition formulas for cosine and sine, the real part is $\cos(x+y)$ and the imaginary part is $\sin(x+y)$. Hence the product equals $\cos(x+y)+i\sin(x+y)$.

\textbf{(b)} We prove the statement by induction on $n$. For $n=1$ it is $\cos x+i\sin x=\cos x+i\sin x$, which is clear. Suppose it holds for some $n\ge 1$. Then
\[(\cos x+i\sin x)^{n+1}=(\cos(nx)+i\sin(nx))(\cos x+i\sin x),\]
and applying (a) with $y=nx$ gives
\[(\cos x+i\sin x)^{n+1}=\cos(nx+x)+i\sin(nx+x)=\cos\big((n+1)x\big)+i\sin\big((n+1)x\big).\]
This is the statement for $n+1$, so by induction it holds for all positive integers $n$.

\textbf{(c)} Write $f(x)=\cos x+i\sin x$. Part (a) says exactly that
\[f(x+y)=f(x)f(y)\qquad\text{and}\qquad f(0)=1,\]
which is the multiplication law $b^{x+y}=b^x\,b^y$ shared by all exponential functions $x\mapsto b^x$. So $f$ behaves like an exponential in some base.

To find the base, recall how $e$ is singled out among real bases: the rate of growth of $b^x$ at $0$ is $\ln b$, and $e$ is the unique base for which this rate equals $1$. Differentiating real and imaginary parts of $f$,
\[f'(x)=-\sin x+i\cos x=i(\cos x+i\sin x)=i\,f(x),\]
so $f'(0)=i$: the function $f$ grows at rate $i$ at the origin instead of rate $1$. The exponential that grows at rate $c$ at the origin is $x\mapsto (e^{c})^x=e^{cx}$, so here the base is $e^{i}$ and
\[f(x)=(e^{i})^{x}=e^{ix}.\]
The $i$ in the exponent records that $f$ moves perpendicular to the real axis at the start, that is, that multiplying by $f(x)$ rotates the plane through $x$ radians rather than stretching it; this is why the base must be the complex number $e^i$ and not a real number.

