A projection onto \(AB\) and \(AC\) gives
\[
F=\left(\dfrac{u(uv-h^2)}{u^2+h^2},\dfrac{uh(u+v)}{u^2+h^2}\right),\qquad
E=\left(\dfrac{v(h^2-uv)}{v^2+h^2},\dfrac{vh(u+v)}{v^2+h^2}\right).
\]
Substituting the coordinates of \(E\) and \(F\) (and of \(D\) and \(F\)) gives
\[
EF:\quad (h^2+uv)y-h(v-u)x-2uvh=0
\]
and
\[
DF:\quad y=-\dfrac{h(u+v)}{h^2-uv}x.
\]
Let \(S=u+v\), \(M=uv\), \(T=h^2-M>0\), and \(R=h^2+M\). The circumcircle of \(ABC\) has equation
\[
x^2+y^2+(u-v)x+\dfrac{uv-h^2}{h}y-uv=0.
\]
If \(BP\) is vertical, then the vertical line through \(B\) meets the circumcircle again at \(P=\left(-u,\dfrac{T}{h}\right)\), and \(Q=\left(-u,\dfrac{hSu}{T}\right)\). Since \(P\in EF\), we have \(h^2uS=TR\), so \(\dfrac{hSu}{T}=\dfrac{R}{h}=h+\dfrac{uv}{h}\). Hence
\[
AP^2=u^2+\left(\dfrac{T}{h}-h\right)^2=u^2+\left(\dfrac{uv}{h}\right)^2
=u^2+\left(\dfrac{hSu}{T}-h\right)^2=AQ^2.
\]
Thus assume \(BP\) is not vertical. Let the slope of \(BP\) be \(s\). A point on \(BP\) has the form \((-u+t,st)\). For \(P=(-u+t_P,st_P)\), substituting in \(EF\) gives
\[
t_P=\dfrac{huS}{Rs-h(v-u)}. \tag{1}
\]
Substituting in the circumcircle gives
\[
t_P=\dfrac{hS+Ts}{h(1+s^2)}. \tag{2}
\]
Let \(U=hS+Ts\) and \(V=Rs-h(v-u)\). Comparing (1) and (2),
\[
1+s^2=\dfrac{UV}{h^2uS}. \tag{3}
\]
For \(Q=(-u+t_Q,st_Q)\), substituting in the equation of \(DF\) gives
\[
t_Q=\dfrac{hSu}{U}. \tag{4}
\]
Now
\[
AP^2=(-u+t_P)^2+(st_P-h)^2,\qquad
AQ^2=(-u+t_Q)^2+(st_Q-h)^2.
\]
Subtracting,
\[
AP^2-AQ^2=(t_P-t_Q)\bigl((1+s^2)(t_P+t_Q)-2(u+sh)\bigr).
\]
Using (1), (3), and (4),
\[
(1+s^2)(t_P+t_Q)
=\dfrac{UV}{h^2uS}\cdot huS\left(\dfrac{1}{V}+\dfrac{1}{U}\right)
=\dfrac{U+V}{h}.
\]
But
\[
U+V=(hS+Ts)+(Rs-h(v-u))
=h(u+v)-h(v-u)+(T+R)s=2hu+2h^2s=2h(u+hs).
\]
Thus the second factor is zero, so \(AP^2-AQ^2=0\). Since both lengths are positive, \(AP=AQ\), as required.

Let $R$ be the radius of $\Gamma$, and put $\beta=\angle ABC=\angle BCA$ and $\alpha=\angle BAC$. Since $\alpha+2\beta=180^\circ$ we have $0<\beta<90^\circ$, hence $\cos\beta>0$.

First find a right angle. Because $CD$ is a diameter of $\Gamma$, the angle $\angle CAD$ is a right angle (angle in a semicircle). Thus $AD\perp AC$, and since $DE\parallel AC$ also $AD\perp DE$. So triangle $ADE$ is right-angled at $D$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
