dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(P); dot(O);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,NW); label("$E$",P,SW); label("$O$",O,NE);
\end{asy}

Because $E$ lies on the segment $AB$, the line $AE$ is the line $AB$, while $DE\parallel AC$. Hence $\angle AED$ equals $\alpha$ or $180^\circ-\alpha$, and in either case $\sin\angle AED=\sin\alpha$. In the right triangle $ADE$ the leg $AD$ is opposite the angle at $E$, so
\[ AD=AE\sin\alpha. \]

Now compute $AD$. The arc $AC$ not containing $B$ has measure $2\beta<180^\circ$, so the central angle $\angle AOC$ equals $2\beta$. Since $OA=OC$, the isosceles triangle $AOC$ gives $\angle ACO=\dfrac{180^\circ-2\beta}{2}=90^\circ-\beta$. Because $O$ lies on $CD$, we get $\angle ACD=\angle ACO=90^\circ-\beta$. In the right triangle $ACD$ the hypotenuse is $CD=2R$, hence
\[ AD=CD\sin\angle ACD=2R\sin(90^\circ-\beta)=2R\cos\beta. \]

Also, by the extended law of sines in triangle $ABC$ (where $\angle ACB=\beta$),
\[ AB=2R\sin\beta. \]

Since $E$ is between $A$ and $B$ and $AE:EB=2:1$, we have $AE=\dfrac{2}{3}AB=\dfrac{4R\sin\beta}{3}$. Substituting the expressions for $AD$ and $AE$ into $AD=AE\sin\alpha$ gives
\[ 2R\cos\beta=\dfrac{4R\sin\beta}{3}\cdot\sin\alpha, \qquad\text{so}\qquad 3\cos\beta=2\sin\alpha\sin\beta. \]
Now $\alpha=180^\circ-2\beta$, so $\sin\alpha=\sin 2\beta=2\sin\beta\cos\beta$, and therefore
\[ 3\cos\beta=4\sin^2\beta\cos\beta, \qquad\text{that is,}\qquad \cos\beta\,(3-4\sin^2\beta)=0. \]
Since $\cos\beta>0$ we obtain $\sin^2\beta=\dfrac{3}{4}$, and as $\sin\beta>0$, $\sin\beta=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$. With $0<\beta<90^\circ$ this forces $\beta=60^\circ$, hence $\alpha=180^\circ-120^\circ=60^\circ$ too. All three angles of $ABC$ are $60^\circ$, so $ABC$ is equilateral, as required.

Let $g=\gcd(x,y)$ and write $x=ga$, $y=gb$, where $\gcd(a,b)=1$. Then $\operatorname{lcm}(x,y)=gab$, so the condition becomes
\[ gab=10g \implies ab=10, \]
since $g>0$ may be cancelled.

Because $\gcd(a,b)=1$ and $ab=10=2\cdot 5$, the only possible ordered pairs $(a,b)$ are
\[ (1,10),\qquad (2,5),\qquad (5,2),\qquad (10,1). \]

For a fixed coprime pair $(a,b)$, the pair $(x,y)=(ga,gb)$ is valid exactly when both entries are at most $100$, that is, when $g\max(a,b)\le 100$; every positive integer $g$ satisfying this works.
\begin{itemize}
\item $(a,b)=(1,10)$: $10g\le 100$, so $g=1,2,\dots,10$, giving $10$ pairs;
\item $(a,b)=(2,5)$: $5g\le 100$, so $1\le g\le 20$, giving $20$ pairs;
\item $(a,b)=(5,2)$: again $20$ pairs;
\item $(a,b)=(10,1)$: again $10$ pairs.
\end{itemize}
Distinct choices of $(a,b)$ and $g$ give distinct ordered pairs $(x,y)$, because $g=\gcd(x,y)$ and $(a,b)$ can be recovered from $(x,y)$ as $a=\dfrac{x}{g}$ and $b=\dfrac{y}{g}$. Hence the cases do not overlap and the total number of ordered pairs is
\[ 10+20+20+10=\boxed{60}. \]

question:
Let $i$ be the imaginary unit, so that $i^2=-1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Simplify $3(3-i)+i(i+6)$.
\item[(b)] List the values of $i^n$ for $n=1,2,3,4$ and describe the pattern; use it to compute $i^{2015}$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Write $\ell_{AB}$ and $\ell_{AC}$ for the lines $AB$ and $AC$.

\textbf{Step 1 (two pairs of congruent right triangles).} Because $X$ lies on the internal angle bisector of $\angle A$, it is equidistant from $\ell_{AB}$ and $\ell_{AC}$, so $XM=XN$. Because $X$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $\overline{BC}$, $XB=XC$.

The right triangles $AXM$ and $AXN$ have the common hypotenuse $AX$ and the equal legs $XM=XN$, so they are congruent (RHS); in particular $AM=AN$.

The right triangles $XBM$ and $XCN$ have the equal hypotenuses $XB=XC$ and the equal legs $XM=XN$, so they are congruent (RHS); in particular $BM=CN$.


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real th=55*pi/180;
real lb=7, lc=4.5;
pair A=(0,0);
pair B=lc*(cos(th),-sin(th));
pair C=lb*(cos(th),sin(th));
real t=(lb+lc)/2/cos(th);
pair X=(t,0);
pair Mf=t*cos(th)*(cos(th),-sin(th));
pair Nf=t*cos(th)*(cos(th),sin(th));
void ram(pair O, pair U, pair V){real s=0.33; pair u=(U-O)/length(U-O); pair v=(V-O)/length(V-O); draw(O+s*u--O+s*u+s*v--O+s*v,linewidth(0.5));}
draw(A--Mf,linewidth(1));
draw(B--C,linewidth(1));
draw(A--C,linewidth(1));
draw(X--Mf,linewidth(0.8));
draw(X--Nf,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--X,dashed+linewidth(0.7));
draw(B--X,linewidth(0.6)+gray);
draw(C--X,linewidth(0.6)+gray);
ram(Mf,B,X); ram(Nf,C,X);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(X); dot(Mf); dot(Nf);
label("$A$",A,W);
label("$B$",B,W);
label("$C$",C,NW);
label("$X$",X,E);
label("$M$",Mf,S);
label("$N$",Nf,N);
\end{asy}


\textbf{Step 2 ($N$ lies on ray $AC$).} The angle at $A$ in the right triangle $AXN$ is $\angle XAN=\dfrac{1}{2}\angle A<90^\circ$, so the foot $N$ of the perpendicular from $X$ to $\ell_{AC}$ falls on the ray $AC$ rather than on the opposite ray.

\textbf{Step 3 ($N$ does not go past $C$).} Since $M$ lies beyond $B$ on ray $AB$, we have $AM=AB+BM$. As $AM=AN$,
\[AN=AM=AB+BM>AB.\]
Suppose $N$ is not in the interior of segment $AC$. Since $N$ lies on ray $AC$, either $N=C$ or $N$ lies beyond $C$.

If $N=C$, then $CN=0$, hence $BM=CN=0$, so $M=B$, contradicting the hypothesis that $M$ lies beyond $B$.

If $N$ lies beyond $C$, then $AN=AC+CN$, and since $CN=BM$,
\[AB+BM=AM=AN=AC+CN=AC+BM,\]
so $AB=AC$. But when $AB=AC$, the internal angle bisector of $\angle A$ is the same line as the perpendicular bisector of $\overline{BC}$, so those two lines do not meet in a single point $X$ outside the triangle, contrary to the hypothesis.

Therefore $N$ lies strictly between $A$ and $C$, that is, on segment $\overline{AC}$, as required. (The hypothesis $\angle A>90^\circ$ is in fact never used; the argument works for every triangle whose angle bisector and perpendicular bisector meet in exactly one point.)

question:
The minute hand of a clock turns through $360^\circ$ in one hour, and the hour hand turns through $360^\circ$ in $12$ hours. Suppose the time is $h$ hours and $m$ minutes after 12:00, where $h$ is an integer with $0\le h\le 11$ and $m$ is an integer with $0\le m\le 59$.

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find a formula, in terms of $h$ and $m$, for the smaller of the two angles formed by the two hands.
\item[(b)] Use the formula to find the angle between the hands at 3:40.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Measure every angle clockwise from the 12 o'clock direction.

The minute hand moves $360^\circ$ in $60$ minutes, that is $6^\circ$ per minute, so at $m$ minutes past the hour it points at
\[6m \text{ degrees.}\]
The hour hand moves $360^\circ$ in $12$ hours, that is $30^\circ$ per hour and $\dfrac{1}{2}^\circ$ per minute, so at $h$ hours and $m$ minutes it points at
\[30h+\dfrac{m}{2}\text{ degrees.}\]
The (unsigned) difference of the two directions is therefore
\[D=\left|\left(30h+\dfrac{m}{2}\right)-6m\right|=\left|30h-\dfrac{11m}{2}\right|=\dfrac{|60h-11m|}{2}.\]
This number lies between $0$ and $360$ only after reduction, so the smaller of the two angles formed by the hands is
\[\min\left(D,\ 360^\circ-D\right)=\min\left(\left|30h-\dfrac{11m}{2}\right|,\ 360^\circ-\left|30h-\dfrac{11m}{2}\right|\right).\]

\textbf{(b)} Here $h=3$ and $m=40$, so
\[30h-\dfrac{11m}{2}=90-220=-130.\]
Thus $D=130^\circ$, and since $130\le 180$ this is already the smaller angle:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
draw(circle((0,0),1),linewidth(0.9));
for(int i=0;i<12;++i){
  real a=i*30*pi/180;
  pair uu=(sin(a),cos(a));
  draw(0.90*uu--uu,linewidth(0.8));
  label("$"+string((i==0) ? 12 : i)+"$",1.18*uu);
}
pair hourp=0.52*(sin(110*pi/180),cos(110*pi/180));
pair minp=0.86*(sin(240*pi/180),cos(240*pi/180));
draw((0,0)--hourp,linewidth(1.5));
draw((0,0)--minp,linewidth(1));
dot((0,0));
draw(arc((0,0),0.34,-150,-20),linewidth(0.7));
label("$130^\circ$",0.58*(sin(175*pi/180),cos(175*pi/180)));
\end{asy}

Therefore the angle between the hands at 3:40 is $\boxed{130^\circ}$.

Because $Y$ lies on the segment $KT$, the distance from $K$ to the centre is
\[ KT=KY+YT=6+2=8. \]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=2;
pair T=(0,0), K=(-8,0), Y=(-2,0), Z=(2,0);
real th=acos(r/8);
pair P=(r*cos(pi-th), r*sin(pi-th));
draw(circle(T,r),linewidth(0.9));
draw(K--Z,linewidth(0.8));
draw(K--P,linewidth(0.8));
draw(T--P,dashed+linewidth(0.7));
pair u=(K-P)/abs(K-P);
pair v=(T-P)/abs(T-P);
real q=0.3;
draw(P+q*u--P+q*u+q*v--P+q*v,linewidth(0.6));
dot(T); dot(K); dot(Y); dot(P); dot(Z);
label("$6$",(-5,0),S);
label("$2$",(-1,0),N);
label("$K$",(-8.55,0.05));
label("$Y$",(-2,-0.55));
label("$T$",(0.1,-0.55));
label("$Z$",(2,-0.55));
label("$P$",P+0.45*(-0.25,0.97));
\end{asy}

Let the tangent from $K$ touch the circle at $P$. A tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact, so $\angle KPT=90^\circ$, and $TP=TY=2$ is a radius. The Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle $KPT$ gives
\[ KP^2=KT^2-TP^2=8^2-2^2=64-4=60, \qquad KP=\sqrt{60}=2\sqrt{15}. \]

The same value comes from power of a point: the line $KT$ meets the circle a second time at $Z$ on the far side of $T$, where $TZ=2$ and $KZ=KY+2\cdot TY=6+4=10$. Then
\[ KP^2=KY\cdot KZ=6\cdot 10=60, \qquad KP=2\sqrt{15}. \]
The posted guess $\sqrt{6\cdot 8}=4\sqrt3$ uses $KZ=8$, but $8$ is the distance from $K$ to the \emph{centre}, not to the second point where the line meets the circle.

Therefore the tangent segment has length $\boxed{2\sqrt{15}}$ (about $7.75$).

In one complete revolution a wheel of diameter $d$ rolls a distance equal to its circumference $\pi d$. So one revolution of the larger wheel covers $20\pi$ inches and one revolution of the smaller wheel covers $17\pi$ inches, and in each revolution the larger wheel gets ahead by
\[
20\pi-17\pi=3\pi\ \text{inches}.
\]
Now $30$ minutes is $30\cdot 60=1800$ seconds, so at $10$ revolutions per second each wheel makes
\[
10\cdot 1800=18000
\]
revolutions.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O1=(-1.9,0), O2=(1.9,0);
real r1=1.2, r2=1.02;
draw(circle(O1,r1),linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O2,r2),linewidth(0.9));
draw(O1+(-r1,0)--O1+(r1,0),blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(O2+(-r2,0)--O2+(r2,0),blue+linewidth(0.8));
dot(O1); dot(O2);
label("$20$ in",O1+(0,0.3));
label("$17$ in",O2+(0,0.3));
\end{asy}

Hence the larger wheel travels
\[
18000\cdot 3\pi=54000\pi
\]
inches farther than the smaller one. (This is $4500\pi$ feet, about $2.68$ miles.)
\[
\boxed{54000\pi}
\]

question:
Let $a$ and $n$ be positive integers.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the integer $x$ with $0<x<19$ such that $16x\equiv 1 \pmod{19}$.
\item[(b)] Prove that if $\gcd(a,n)=1$, then there is exactly one residue $x$ modulo $n$ with $ax\equiv 1 \pmod n$, and explain how the Euclidean algorithm produces this $x$ in only a few steps instead of testing residues one at a time.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $\Gamma$ be the circumcircle of $\triangle ABC$, and let $M\neq A$ be the second intersection of the internal angle bisector of $\angle A$ with $\Gamma$. We show that $M$ is exactly the point $K$.

\emph{Step 1: $M$ is the midpoint of arc $BC$ not containing $A$.} Since $M$ lies on the ray that bisects $\angle A$ and inside the angle, $M$ is on the arc $BC$ not containing $A$, and
\[
\angle BAM=\angle MAC .
\]
These inscribed angles subtend the arcs $BM$ and $MC$ that do not contain $A$, so those two arcs are equal.

\emph{Step 2: $MB=MC$.} Equal arcs cut off equal chords, so $MB=MC$. Hence $M$ is equidistant from $B$ and $C$, i.e. $M$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $\overline{BC}$.

\emph{Step 3: the two lines meet only at $M$.} The circumcentre $O$ also lies on the perpendicular bisector of $\overline{BC}$. So the perpendicular bisector of $\overline{BC}$ is the line $OM$, and the angle bisector is the line $AM$; both pass through $M$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Oo=(0,0);
pair Aa=dir(100);
pair Bb=dir(210);
pair Cc=dir(330);
pair Kk=(0,-1);
pair Md=(0,-0.5);
draw(circle(Oo,1),linewidth(0.7));
draw(Aa--Bb--Cc--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(Aa--Kk,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,1.15)--(0,-1.2),red+dashed+linewidth(0.8));
draw(Md+(0.09,0)--Md+(0.09,0.09)--Md+(0,0.09),linewidth(0.6));
draw(arc(Aa,0.22,245,275),linewidth(0.5));
draw(arc(Aa,0.22,275,305),linewidth(0.5));
dot(Oo); dot(Kk); dot(Md);
label("$A$",Aa,dir(100));
label("$B$",Bb,dir(210));
label("$C$",Cc,dir(330));
label("$K$",Kk,S);
label("$O$",Oo,NW);
\end{asy}

If the lines $AM$ and $OM$ were distinct, they would meet in the single point $M$, and then $K=M\in\Gamma$, as required.

It remains to rule out the case where the two lines coincide. The line $AM$ equals the perpendicular bisector of $\overline{BC}$ only if $A$ itself lies on that perpendicular bisector, which happens exactly when $AB=AC$. But $\triangle ABC$ is scalene, so $AB\neq AC$ and $A$ is not on the perpendicular bisector of $\overline{BC}$. Hence $AM$ and the perpendicular bisector of $\overline{BC}$ are two distinct lines through $M$, their intersection is $K=M$, and therefore $K$ lies on the circumcircle of $\triangle ABC$.

(Without the scalene hypothesis the statement can fail to make sense: if $AB=AC$, the angle bisector of $\angle A$ \emph{is} the perpendicular bisector of $\overline{BC}$, so the two lines have infinitely many common points.)

Place $D$ at the origin and $BC$ along the $x$-axis. Write $DC=t$, so $BD=2t$, $BC=3t$, and
\[
D=(0,0),\qquad B=(-2t,0),\qquad C=(t,0),\qquad A=(u,v).
\]
The three given lengths give three equations:
\[
u^2+v^2=36,\qquad (u+2t)^2+v^2=144,\qquad (u-t)^2+v^2=81 .
\]
Subtract the third equation from the second:
\[
(u+2t)^2-(u-t)^2=63
\;\Longrightarrow\;
3t\,(2u+t)=63
\;\Longrightarrow\;
2ut+t^2=21 .
\]
Subtract the first equation from the third:
\[
(u-t)^2-u^2=81-36=45
\;\Longrightarrow\;
t^2-2ut=45 .
\]
Adding the last two relations, the $2ut$ terms cancel and
\[
2t^2=66,\qquad t^2=33,\qquad t=\sqrt{33}.
\]
Hence $BC=3t=3\sqrt{33}$.

Such a triangle exists: then $u=-\dfrac{6}{\sqrt{33}}$, and $v^2=36-u^2=36-\dfrac{36}{33}=\dfrac{384}{11}>0$, so $A$ is a genuine point, and $BC=3\sqrt{33}\approx 17.23<12+9$.

\begin{asy}
