size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Bb=(-5.170,0), Cc=(2.585,0), Dd=(0,0), Aa=(-0.470,2.659);
draw(Aa--Bb--Cc--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(Aa--Dd,blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(Dd);
label("$A$",Aa,N);
label("$B$",Bb,SW);
label("$C$",Cc,SE);
label("$D$",Dd,S);
label("$12$",(Aa+Bb)/2,W);
label("$9$",(Aa+Cc)/2,E);
label("$6$",(Aa+Dd)/2,W);
draw((-2.75,-0.1)--(-2.75,0.1));
draw((-2.42,-0.1)--(-2.42,0.1));
draw((1.29,-0.1)--(1.29,0.1));
\end{asy}

Therefore $BC=\boxed{3\sqrt{33}}$.

question:
Let \(N\) be an \(n\)-digit positive integer whose leftmost digit is \(5\), and let \(M\) be the number obtained by moving that \(5\) to the rightmost position while keeping the order of the other digits.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that there is no \(N\) for which \(M=3N\).
\item[(b)] Find the least \(n\) for which such an \(N\) can satisfy \(N=3M\).
\end{enumerate}

Points \(K,L,M,N\) lie on a circle with center \(O\). Chords \(KM\) and \(LN\) intersect at \(P\), and the points \(L,P,O,N\) are collinear. Suppose that \(OP=2\), \(PL=8\), and \(PK=9\).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find \(MN\).
\item[(b)] Find \(NK\), and explain why the additional condition \(NK=18\) is impossible.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Since \(P\) lies on chord \(LN\) and \(O\) lies on line \(LN\), the chord \(LN\) is a diameter. Because \(P\) is on the chord, it is between \(L\) and \(N\). With \(OP=2\) and \(PL=8\), the radius \(OL\) is either \(OP+PL=10\) (if \(P\) is between \(O\) and \(L\)) or \(PL-OP=6\) (if \(O\) is between \(L\) and \(P\)). In the second case every point of the circle is at distance at most \(6+2=8\) from \(P\), contradicting \(PK=9\). Hence the radius is \(10\), and \(P\) lies between \(O\) and \(L\).

Thus \(OL=ON=OK=10\) and \(PN=PO+ON=2+10=12\). The rays \(OP\) and \(ON\) are opposite, so
\[
\angle KON=180^\circ-\angle KOP.
\]
In triangle \(OKP\), the law of cosines gives
\[
\cos\angle KOP=\dfrac{OK^2+OP^2-KP^2}{2\cdot OK\cdot OP}
=\dfrac{100+4-81}{2\cdot10\cdot2}
=\dfrac{23}{40}.
\]
Therefore
\[
\cos\angle KON=-\cos\angle KOP=-\dfrac{23}{40},
\]
and in triangle \(KON\),
\[
KN^2=10^2+10^2-2\cdot10\cdot10\cos\angle KON
=200-200\left(-\dfrac{23}{40}\right)
=315.
\]
So \(NK=3\sqrt{35}\approx17.75\), not \(18\); the additional condition \(NK=18\) is impossible.


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fontsize(8pt);
real R=10;
pair Op=(0,0), Lp=(10,0), Np=(-10,0), Pp=(2,0);
pair Kp=(23/4, 3*sqrt(119)/4);
pair Mp=(-22/9, -8*sqrt(119)/9);
draw(circle(Op,R), linewidth(0.8));
draw(Lp--Np, linewidth(0.9));
draw(Kp--Mp, linewidth(0.9));
draw(Lp--Kp, linewidth(0.7));
draw(Np--Mp, linewidth(0.7));
draw(Mp--Lp, linewidth(0.7));
draw(Np--Kp, linewidth(0.9));
draw(Op--Kp, dashed+linewidth(0.7));
dot(Op); dot(Lp); dot(Np); dot(Pp); dot(Kp); dot(Mp);
label("$O$", Op+(0.2,-0.3), SW);
label("$L$", Lp+(0.2,0.2), NE);
label("$N$", Np+(-0.2,0.2), NW);
label("$P$", Pp+(0,0.35), N);
label("$K$", Kp+(0.2,0.1), NE);
label("$M$", Mp+(-0.2,-0.2), SW);
label("$2$", (Op+Pp)/2+(0,0.25), N);
label("$8$", (Pp+Lp)/2+(0,0.25), N);
label("$9$", (Pp+Kp)/2+(-0.2,0.2), NW);
label("$3\sqrt{35}$", (Np+Kp)/2+(-0.3,0.4), NW);
\end{asy}

Now find \(MN\). By power of a point at \(P\),
\[
PK\cdot PM=PL\cdot PN=8\cdot12=96,
\]
so \(PM=\dfrac{96}{9}=\dfrac{32}{3}\) and
\[
KM=PK+PM=9+\dfrac{32}{3}=\dfrac{59}{3}.
\]
Also \(\angle KNP=\angle KNL\) and \(\angle LMP=\angle LMK\), since \(P\) lies on \(NL\) and on \(MK\). Both angles subtend arc \(KL\), so they are equal. Together with the vertical angles \(\angle KPN=\angle LPM\), this gives
\[
\triangle KNP\sim\triangle LMP.
\]
The ratio of similarity is \(\dfrac{KP}{LP}=\dfrac{9}{8}\), so
\[
LM=\dfrac{8}{9}KN=\dfrac{8}{9}\cdot3\sqrt{35}=\dfrac{8\sqrt{35}}{3}.
\]
Next, in triangle \(LOK\), the rays \(OL\) and \(OP\) coincide, so \(\angle LOK=\angle POK\). Thus
\[
LK^2=10^2+10^2-2\cdot10\cdot10\cdot\dfrac{23}{40}=200-115=85,
\]
so \(LK=\sqrt{85}\).

The points \(L,K,N,M\) occur in that order around the circle (the chords \(KM\) and \(LN\) cross at \(P\)), so Ptolemy's theorem on cyclic quadrilateral \(LKNM\) gives
\[
LN\cdot KM=LK\cdot NM+KN\cdot ML.
\]
Substitute:
\[
20\cdot\dfrac{59}{3}=\sqrt{85}\cdot NM+3\sqrt{35}\cdot\dfrac{8\sqrt{35}}{3}.
\]
Since \(3\sqrt{35}\cdot\dfrac{8\sqrt{35}}{3}=8\cdot35=280\),
\[
\sqrt{85}\cdot NM=\dfrac{1180}{3}-280=\dfrac{340}{3},
\]
and therefore
\[
NM=\dfrac{340}{3\sqrt{85}}=\dfrac{4\sqrt{85}}{3}.
\]
So \(MN=\dfrac{4\sqrt{85}}{3}\approx12.29\). The posted answer \(12\) does not match these data; it presumably came from a different set of given lengths.

\[
\boxed{MN=\dfrac{4\sqrt{85}}{3},\qquad NK=3\sqrt{35}}
\]

question:
For a complex number \(z\), consider the equations \(w^2=z\) and \(w^4=z\) in the complex variable \(w\).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If \(z\ne0\), how many distinct complex solutions does each equation have?
\item[(b)] How many distinct complex solutions are there when \(z=0\)?
\item[(c)] What is the greatest possible number of distinct fourth roots of a complex number?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
In a \(30^\circ-60^\circ-90^\circ\) triangle, the side opposite the \(30^\circ\) angle is half the hypotenuse, so it is \(6\). The other leg is \(\sqrt{3}\) times this shorter leg, so it is \(6\sqrt{3}\).

