Add the four equations. Each of $w,x,y,z$ appears once with coefficient $3$ and three times with coefficient $1$, so each appears with total coefficient $6$:
\[6(w+x+y+z)=12+15+18+21=66,\qquad w+x+y+z=11.\]
Now compare each equation with this sum:
\begin{itemize}
\item first: $(w+x+y+z)+2z=12$, so $11+2z=12$ and $z=\dfrac{1}{2}$;
\item second: $11+2y=15$, so $y=2$;
\item third: $11+2x=18$, so $x=\dfrac{7}{2}$;
\item fourth: $11+2w=21$, so $w=5$.
\end{itemize}
Check the sum: $5+\dfrac{7}{2}+2+\dfrac{1}{2}=11$, and for instance the third equation: $5+2+\dfrac{1}{2}+3\cdot\dfrac{7}{2}=\dfrac{21}{2}+\dfrac{21}{2}=18$.
\[\boxed{w=5,\ x=\dfrac{7}{2},\ y=2,\ z=\dfrac{1}{2}}\]

question:
A forum post proposed the system
\[a^2+b^2+c^2=10,\qquad 2ab+2bc+2ca=6,\qquad a+b+c=3.\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that no real numbers $a,b,c$ satisfy all three equations.
\item[(b)] Using only the first two equations, find every possible value of $a+b+c$, and show that each value you find really occurs.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the side lengths be $x,y,z$. Opposite faces of a rectangular box are congruent, so the six face areas consist of three values, each repeated twice: the areas of the faces are $xy$, $xy$, $xz$, $xz$, $yz$, $yz$. Matching with the given list (in some order),
\[xy=48,\qquad xz=72,\qquad yz=96.\]
Multiplying all three equations,
\[(xyz)^2=(xy)(xz)(yz)=48\cdot 72\cdot 96.\]
Now $48\cdot 72=3456$ and $3456\cdot 96=331776$, and $576^2=331776$, so $xyz=576$ (the side lengths are positive).

Dividing $xyz=576$ by each product:
\[z=\dfrac{576}{48}=12,\qquad y=\dfrac{576}{72}=8,\qquad x=\dfrac{576}{96}=6.\]
Check: $6\cdot 8=48$, $6\cdot 12=72$, $8\cdot 12=96$, and $6\cdot 8\cdot 12=576$.


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0), Px=(1.68,0), Py=(0,2.24), dz=(1.07,1.07);
pair A1=O+dz, A2=Px+dz, A3=Px+Py+dz, A4=Py+dz;
fill(Py--(Px+Py)--A3--A4--cycle,paleyellow);
fill(Px--A2--A3--(Px+Py)--cycle,paleblue);
draw(O--Px--(Px+Py)--Py--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(Py--A4--A3--(Px+Py),linewidth(0.9));
draw(Px--A2--A3,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--A1,dashed);
draw(A1--A2,dashed);
draw(A1--A4,dashed);
label("$x$",(O+Px)/2,S);
label("$y$",(O+Py)/2,W);
label("$z$",(Px+A2)/2,E);
label("$48$",(Px+Py)/2);
label("$72$",(Py+(Px+Py)+A3+A4)/4);
label("$96$",(Px+A2+A3+(Px+Py))/4);
\end{asy}


