Since $a+b+c=26$ and the triangle inequality gives $a+b>c$, we have
\[
26-c>c,
\]
so $c<13$, and therefore $c\le 12$. Also $3c\ge a+b+c=26$, so $c\ge 9$. Thus
\[
c\in\{9,10,11,12\}.
\]
For a fixed $c$, we need $a+b=26-c$ with $1\le a\le b\le c$. Count the possibilities:
\begin{itemize}
\item $c=9$: $a+b=17$ and $b\le 9$. Since $a\le b$, this forces $b=9$ and $a=8$. This gives $1$ triangle.
\item $c=10$: $a+b=16$ and $b\le 10$. Then $b=8,9,10$, giving $a=8,7,6$. This gives $3$ triangles.
\item $c=11$: $a+b=15$ and $b\le 11$. Then $b=8,9,10,11$, giving $a=7,6,5,4$. This gives $4$ triangles.
\item $c=12$: $a+b=14$ and $b\le 12$. Then $b=7,8,9,10,11,12$, giving $a=7,6,5,4,3,2$. This gives $6$ triangles.
\end{itemize}
The condition $c\le 12$ ensures $a+b=26-c>c$, so every listed triple is a genuine triangle. Therefore the total number of types is
\[
1+3+4+6=\boxed{14}.
\]

