solution:
Let $O$ be the centre of the stationary circle and $C$ the centre of the rolling circle. During one complete circuit, $C$ travels once around a circle of radius $R+r$, so the path of $C$ has length $2\pi(R+r)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real R=3, r=1;
pair O=(0,0), C=(0,R+r);
draw(circle(O,R), linewidth(1));
draw(circle(C,r), linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O,R+r), dashed+gray);
draw(O--C, dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(arc(O,R+r,25,65), Arrow);
dot(O); dot(C);
label("$O$", O, SW, fontsize(9pt));
label("$C$", C, N, fontsize(9pt));
label("$R$", (0,R/2), W, fontsize(9pt));
label("$r$", (0,R+r/2), E, fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

For rolling without slipping, the instantaneous velocity of the material point of the small circle that touches the large circle must be zero. If $\theta$ is the angle through which $OC$ has turned and $\phi$ is the angle through which the small circle has rotated about $C$, measured from a fixed direction, then
\[
(R+r)\dot\theta=r\dot\phi.
\]
Integrating over one full circuit, $\theta$ changes by $2\pi$, so
\[
\phi=\dfrac{R+r}{r}\cdot 2\pi=(n+1)2\pi.
\]
Thus the small circle makes $n+1$ complete rotations.

The bogus solution uses only the circumference of the large circle. That would be correct only if the tangent direction did not change, as for rolling along a straight line of length $2\pi R$. When the path is wrapped once around the large circle, the tangent direction itself rotates through one full turn, which adds one extra rotation. Equivalently, the centre of the rolling circle travels around radius $R+r$, not radius $R$.

Therefore the number of rotations is
\[
\boxed{n+1}.
\]

Both lines pass through the origin and point into the first quadrant. A direction vector for $y=\dfrac{4}{3}x$ is $(3,4)$, of length $5$, so the unit vector $\left(\dfrac{3}{5},\dfrac{4}{5}\right)$ points along this ray. A direction vector for $y=\dfrac{12}{5}x$ is $(5,12)$, of length $13$, so the unit vector $\left(\dfrac{5}{13},\dfrac{12}{13}\right)$ points along the second ray.

The sum of two unit vectors points along the bisector of the angle between them: the two unit vectors form a rhombus (all four sides have length $1$), and the diagonal of a rhombus bisects its angles. Here
\[\left(\dfrac{3}{5},\dfrac{4}{5}\right)+\left(\dfrac{5}{13},\dfrac{12}{13}\right)
=\left(\dfrac{39+25}{65},\dfrac{52+60}{65}\right)=\left(\dfrac{64}{65},\dfrac{112}{65}\right).\]
Since both original rays point to the right, this sum lies strictly between them, so it is the bisector of the acute angle. Its slope is
\[\dfrac{112}{64}=\dfrac{7}{4},\]
the common factor $\dfrac{1}{65}$ having cancelled.


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real t1=atan(4.0/3), t2=atan(12.0/5), tm=(t1+t2)/2;
pair O=(0,0);
pair A=3.0*(cos(t1),sin(t1));
pair C=3.4*(cos(t2),sin(t2));
pair B=4.2*(cos(tm),sin(tm));
guide arcc(pair Q, real r, real u, real v){
  guide g;
  for(int i=0;i<=30;++i){ real t=u+(v-u)*i/30; g=g..(Q+r*(cos(t),sin(t))); }
  return g;
}
draw((0,0)--(2.5,0),gray+linewidth(0.4));
draw((0,0)--(0,3.9),gray+linewidth(0.4));
draw(O--A,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--C,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--B,red+dashed+linewidth(0.9));
draw(arcc(O,1.0,t1,tm),linewidth(0.6));
draw(arcc(O,1.05,tm,t2),linewidth(0.6));
label("$O$",O,SW);
label("$y=\dfrac{4}{3}x$",A,E);
label("$y=\dfrac{12}{5}x$",C,NW);
label("$y=\dfrac{7}{4}x$",B,E);
dot(O);
\end{asy}


The answer is consistent: $\dfrac{4}{3}<\dfrac{7}{4}<\dfrac{12}{5}$, so the bisecting line really lies inside the acute angle, and the point $(4,7)$ on it has distance $\dfrac{|4\cdot 4-3\cdot 7|}{5}=1$ from the first line and $\dfrac{|12\cdot 4-5\cdot 7|}{13}=1$ from the second, i.e. it is equidistant from the two lines.

Hence the slope is
\[\boxed{\dfrac{7}{4}}.\]

Let the side length of the square be \(s\), and place coordinates
\[
A=(0,0),\quad B=(s,0),\quad C=(s,s),\quad D=(0,s).
\]
Write \(P=(u,v)\). The centroid of a triangle is the average of its three vertices, so
\[
G_1=\left(\dfrac{s+u}{3},\dfrac{v}{3}\right),\qquad
G_2=\left(\dfrac{2s+u}{3},\dfrac{s+v}{3}\right),
\]
\[
G_3=\left(\dfrac{s+u}{3},\dfrac{2s+v}{3}\right),\qquad
G_4=\left(\dfrac{u}{3},\dfrac{s+v}{3}\right).
\]
Now compute the side vectors:
\[
\overrightarrow{G_1G_2}
=\left(\dfrac{s}{3},\dfrac{s}{3}\right),
\qquad
\overrightarrow{G_2G_3}
=\left(-\dfrac{s}{3},\dfrac{s}{3}\right),
\]
\[
\overrightarrow{G_3G_4}
=\left(-\dfrac{s}{3},-\dfrac{s}{3}\right),
\qquad
\overrightarrow{G_4G_1}
=\left(\dfrac{s}{3},-\dfrac{s}{3}\right).
\]
Thus adjacent sides are perpendicular and all sides have length
\[
\sqrt{\left(\dfrac{s}{3}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{s}{3}\right)^2}
=\dfrac{s\sqrt2}{3}.
\]
Therefore \(G_1G_2G_3G_4\) is a square with side \(\dfrac{s\sqrt2}{3}\). Its area is
\[
\left(\dfrac{s\sqrt2}{3}\right)^2=\dfrac{2s^2}{9}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=6;
pair A=(0,0), B=(6,0), C=(6,6), D=(0,6);
pair Pp=(2,3);
pair g1=((A.x+B.x+Pp.x)/3,(A.y+B.y+Pp.y)/3);
pair g2=((B.x+C.x+Pp.x)/3,(B.y+C.y+Pp.y)/3);
pair g3=((C.x+D.x+Pp.x)/3,(C.y+D.y+Pp.y)/3);
pair g4=((D.x+A.x+Pp.x)/3,(D.y+A.y+Pp.y)/3);
fill(g1--g2--g3--g4--cycle, paleblue);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
draw(g1--g2--g3--g4--cycle, blue+linewidth(1.0));
draw(Pp--A, dashed+gray);
draw(Pp--B, dashed+gray);
draw(Pp--C, dashed+gray);
draw(Pp--D, dashed+gray);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(Pp); dot(g1); dot(g2); dot(g3); dot(g4);
label("$A$",A,SW);
label("$B$",B,SE);
label("$C$",C,NE);
label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$P$",Pp,NW);
label("$G_1$",g1,S);
label("$G_2$",g2,E);
label("$G_3$",g3,N);
label("$G_4$",g4,W);
\end{asy}

The formula contains no \(u\) or \(v\), so the area is independent of \(P\). Since the area of \(ABCD\) is \(s^2\), the constant area is
\[
\boxed{\dfrac{2}{9}[ABCD]}.
\]
That is, the quadrilateral \(G_1G_2G_3G_4\) always has area two ninths of the area of the original square.

question:
A hotel has \(100\) rooms numbered \(1\) through \(100\). Initially every door is blue. The manager first has all doors painted red. Then, for each \(k=2,3,\ldots,100\), the painter repaints every \(k\)-th door, beginning with door \(k\): door \(n\) is repainted exactly when \(k\mid n\), and each such repainting changes its color. Count the initial painting of every door red as a color change.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Which doors are red when the painter finishes?
\item[(b)] Which doors have had their color changed exactly twice in total, including the initial red painting?
\item[(c)] Which doors have been painted the greatest number of times?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
A rook attacks along its row and along its column, so the requirement is exactly:
\begin{itemize}
\item no two rooks share a row, and
\item no two rooks share a column.
\end{itemize}
With $8$ rooks and $8$ rows, ``no two in the same row'' forces exactly one rook in each row; similarly there is exactly one rook in each column.

Therefore a placement is completely described by recording the column used in each row. Let $c_i$ be the column of the rook in row $i$. The list $(c_1,c_2,\dots,c_8)$ must contain the numbers $1,2,\dots,8$ each exactly once, that is, it is a permutation of eight objects. Conversely, every permutation gives a legal placement, because the eight rows are distinct and the eight columns $c_i$ are distinct.


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<8;++i){for(int j=0;j<8;++j){fill(shift((i,j))*unitsquare,((i+j)%2==0)?white:heavygray);}}
for(int i=0;i<=8;++i){draw((i,0)--(i,8),linewidth(0.4));draw((0,i)--(8,i),linewidth(0.4));}
for(int i=0;i<8;++i){fill(circle((i+0.5,i+0.5),0.26),black);}
draw(box((0,0),(8,8)),linewidth(1.0));
\end{asy}


Hence the number of placements equals the number of permutations of $8$ objects:
\[8!=8\cdot 7\cdot 6\cdot 5\cdot 4\cdot 3\cdot 2\cdot 1=\boxed{40320}.\]
(Equivalently: $8$ choices for the rook in the first row, then $7$ free columns for the second row, then $6$, and so on.)

