The height of \(P\) above the \(x\)-axis is \(0\), so
\[
\sin(G^\circ)=\sin(0^\circ)=\boxed{0}.
\]

question:
Let \(PS\) be a segment of length \(4\), and let \(O\) be its midpoint. Draw the semicircle with diameter \(PS\) on one side. Let \(X\) be the midpoint of this semicircular arc. Points \(Q\) and \(R\) lie on the arc \(PXS\) with \(QR\parallel PS\). Draw the semicircle with diameter \(QR\) on the side of \(QR\) opposite \(X\); this semicircle is tangent to \(PS\). Find the area of the region bounded by the arc \(QXR\) of the first semicircle and the arc \(QOR\) of the second semicircle.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=sqrt(2);
pair pP=(-2,0), pS=(2,0), O=(0,0), X=(0,2), Q=(-a,a), R=(a,a), C=(0,a);
draw(arc(O,2,0,180), linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(C,a,180,360), linewidth(0.9));
draw(pP--pS, dashed);
draw(Q--R, dashed);
dot(pP); dot(pS); dot(O); dot(X); dot(Q); dot(R);
label("$P$", pP, SW);
label("$S$", pS, SE);
label("$O$", O, S);
label("$X$", X, N);
label("$Q$", Q, NW);
label("$R$", R, NE);
\end{asy}

solution:
Place \(O=(0,0)\), \(P=(-2,0)\), and \(S=(2,0)\). The first semicircle has equation
\[
x^2+y^2=4,\qquad y\ge0,
\]
and its midpoint is \(X=(0,2)\). Since \(QR\parallel PS\), write
\[
Q=(-a,b),\qquad R=(a,b),
\]
where \(a>0\), \(b>0\), and
\[
a^2+b^2=4.
\]
The semicircle with diameter \(QR\) has center \(C=(0,b)\) and radius \(a\). It is tangent to \(PS\), the \(x\)-axis, so the distance from \(C\) to the \(x\)-axis equals its radius:
\[
b=a.
\]
Together with \(a^2+b^2=4\), this gives
\[
2a^2=4,
\]
so
\[
a=b=\sqrt2.
\]
Thus
\[
Q=(-\sqrt2,\sqrt2),\qquad R=(\sqrt2,\sqrt2).
\]
The vectors \(OQ\) and \(OR\) have dot product
\[
(-\sqrt2)(\sqrt2)+(\sqrt2)(\sqrt2)=-2+2=0,
\]
so \(\angle QOR=90^\circ\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=sqrt(2);
pair pP=(-2,0), pS=(2,0), O=(0,0), X=(0,2), Q=(-a,a), R=(a,a), C=(0,a);
path bigpart=arc(O,2,45,135);
path smallpart=arc(C,a,180,360);
fill(bigpart--smallpart--cycle, paleblue);
draw(arc(O,2,0,180), linewidth(0.9));
draw(smallpart, linewidth(0.9));
draw(pP--pS, dashed);
draw(Q--R, dashed);
draw(O--Q, dashed);
draw(O--R, dashed);
draw(C--Q, dashed);
pair uq=Q/2;
pair ur=R/2;
real q=0.22;
draw((0,0)--q*uq--q*uq+q*ur--q*ur--cycle, linewidth(0.6));
label("$90^\circ$", (0,0.62), N);
label("$2$", (O+Q)/2, SW);
label("$\sqrt{2}$", (C+Q)/2, S);
dot(pP); dot(pS); dot(O); dot(X); dot(Q); dot(R); dot(C);
label("$P$", pP, SW);
label("$S$", pS, SE);
label("$O$", O, S);
label("$X$", X, N);
label("$Q$", Q, NW);
label("$R$", R, NE);
label("$C$", C, S);
\end{asy}

The region is the circular segment of the large circle lying above chord \(QR\) together with the small semicircle lying below \(QR\).

The area of sector \(QOR\) of the large circle is
\[
\dfrac{90}{360}\cdot\pi\cdot2^2=\pi.
\]
Triangle \(QOR\) has base \(QR=2\sqrt2\) and height \(\sqrt2\), so its area is
\[
\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot2\sqrt2\cdot\sqrt2=2.
\]
Hence the large circular segment has area
\[
\pi-2.
\]
The small semicircle has radius \(\sqrt2\), so its area is
\[
\dfrac{1}{2}\pi(\sqrt2)^2=\pi.
\]
Therefore the required area is
\[
(\pi-2)+\pi=\boxed{2\pi-2}.
\]

question:
Consider $f(x)=x^3+3x^2+4x-11$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] List all the possible rational roots of $f$ allowed by the rational root theorem, and check them, to show that $f$ has no rational root.
\item[(b)] A student graphs $f$, sees an intercept at $x=\dfrac{121}{100}$, and objects that $121$ does not divide $11$. Compute $f\left(\dfrac{121}{100}\right)$ exactly, and explain why the graph nevertheless appears to cross the axis there.
\item[(c)] Prove that $f$ has exactly one real root, and say between which two numbers it lies.
\end{enumerate}

Let $a$ and $b$ be nonzero integers, and suppose that integers $x$ and $y$ satisfy
\[ax+by=1.\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $by\equiv 1\pmod a$ and that $ax\equiv 1\pmod b$.
\item[(b)] The argument must still make sense when $a$ or $b$ is negative. Explain why congruence modulo a negative integer is well defined, and check the claim on the example $a=-3$, $b=2$, $x=1$, $y=2$.
\end{enumerate}

Line segment $SE$ has endpoints $S(3,15)$ and $E(-9,-1)$. It is rotated $270^\circ$ clockwise about $E$, and the image of $S$ is named $O$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the area of $\triangle SEO$.
\item[(b)] Find the sum of the coordinates of $S$, $E$, and $O$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The vector from $E$ to $S$ is
\[
\overrightarrow{ES}=(3-(-9),15-(-1))=(12,16),
\]
so $ES=\sqrt{12^2+16^2}=20$.

\begin{asy}
