Applying the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangles $XON$ and $YOM$ gives
\[XN^2=OX^2+ON^2=x^2+\dfrac{y^2}{4}=19^2=361,\qquad YM^2=OY^2+OM^2=y^2+\dfrac{x^2}{4}=22^2=484.\]
Adding these two equations,
\[\dfrac{5}{4}\left(x^2+y^2\right)=361+484=845,\qquad\text{so}\qquad x^2+y^2=\dfrac{4}{5}\cdot 845=676.\]
Now $XOY$ is right-angled at $O$, so the Pythagorean theorem gives $XY^2=OX^2+OY^2=x^2+y^2=676$, and therefore
\[XY=\sqrt{676}=\boxed{26}.\]
(For reference, solving the system gives $x^2=256$ and $y^2=420$, i.e. $OX=16$ and $OY=2\sqrt{105}$, and indeed $256+\dfrac{420}{4}=361$ and $420+\dfrac{256}{4}=484$, so such a triangle exists.)

