question:
In triangle $ABC$ the angle at $A$ is $30^\circ$, the side $AB$ has length $10$, and the side $BC$ has length $6$. These are the data of the ``side-side-angle'' (SSA) case.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain geometrically why more than one triangle can have exactly these measurements, so that the ambiguous case really does have to be checked.
\item[(b)] Find every possible value of $AC$, and hence the number of non-congruent triangles satisfying the conditions.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $b=AC$. Applying the law of cosines to the angle at $A$, whose opposite side is $BC$,
\[6^{2}=10^{2}+b^{2}-2\cdot 10\cdot b\cos 30^\circ=100+b^{2}-10\sqrt3\,b,\]
so $b^{2}-10\sqrt3\,b+64=0$ and
\[b=\dfrac{10\sqrt3\pm\sqrt{300-256}}{2}=\dfrac{10\sqrt3\pm 2\sqrt{11}}{2}=5\sqrt3\pm\sqrt{11}.\]


