\end{asy}


\textbf{(a)} Fix the ray from $A$ that makes $30^\circ$ with $AB$; the point $C$ must lie on this ray and satisfy $BC=6$, so $C$ is an intersection of the ray with the circle of centre $B$ and radius $6$. The distance from $B$ to the line carrying the ray is $10\sin 30^\circ=5$, which is less than $6$, so that circle meets the line in two distinct points. The foot of the perpendicular from $B$ to the line is at distance $10\cos 30^\circ=5\sqrt3$ from $A$, and each intersection point is $\sqrt{6^{2}-5^{2}}=\sqrt{11}$ away from that foot. Their distances from $A$ are therefore $5\sqrt3-\sqrt{11}$ and $5\sqrt3+\sqrt{11}$, both of which are positive because $\sqrt{11}<\sqrt{27}=3\sqrt3<5\sqrt3$. So both intersection points lie on the ray, and they give two non-congruent triangles with the same $AB$, $BC$ and $\angle A$.

\textbf{(b)} The two values found from the quadratic are
\[AC=5\sqrt3+\sqrt{11}\quad\text{or}\quad AC=5\sqrt3-\sqrt{11}.\]
Both are positive, and part (a) showed that each value is realized by an intersection point of the circle with the ray, so each gives a genuine non-degenerate triangle. Conversely, any triangle with the given data must have $AC$ equal to one of these two roots. Hence there are exactly two non-congruent triangles.

The moral is that SSA data must always be tested: depending on the data there may be no triangle, exactly one, or two, and the number of solutions is not automatic.

