Let the convex $n$-gon have vertices $A_1,A_2,\ldots,A_n$ and interior angles $I_1,I_2,\ldots,I_n$.

At the vertex $A_k$ the side $A_kA_{k+1}$ and the extension of the side $A_{k-1}A_k$ past $A_k$ are opposite rays, so the exterior angle $E_k$ and the interior angle $I_k$ form a linear pair:
\[E_k=180^\circ-I_k .\]
Convexity is exactly what makes this true at every vertex: each interior angle is less than $180^\circ$, so the extension of the neighbouring side really does leave the polygon on the outside at $A_k$, and the angle it makes with the next side is the supplement of the interior angle.


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,2), B=(-1.9021,0.618), C=(-1.1756,-1.618), D=(1.1756,-1.618), F=(1.9021,0.618);
pair[] V={A,B,C,D,F};
pair[] Bk={F,A,B,C,D};
pair[] Fw={B,C,D,F,A};
draw(A--B--C--D--F--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
for(int i=0;i<5;++i){
  pair Q=V[i];
  pair uin=(Q-Bk[i])/abs(Q-Bk[i]);
  pair uout=(Fw[i]-Q)/abs(Fw[i]-Q);
  draw(Q--Q+0.95*uin,red+dashed+linewidth(0.6));
  real a1=atan2(uin.y,uin.x)*180/pi;
  real a2=atan2(uout.y,uout.x)*180/pi;
  while(a2<a1) a2+=360;
  draw(arc(Q,0.32,a1,a2),linewidth(0.6)+blue);
}
label("$A$",A,N);
label("$B$",B,W);
label("$C$",C,SW);
label("$D$",D,SE);
label("$E$",F,E);
label("$72^\circ$",B+0.72*dir(252));
\end{asy}


Summing over all vertices,
\[\sum_{k=1}^{n}E_k=n\cdot 180^\circ-\sum_{k=1}^{n}I_k .\]
The interior angles of a convex $n$-gon add up to $(n-2)\cdot 180^\circ$: the $n-3$ diagonals drawn from $A_1$ stay inside the polygon and cut it into $n-2$ triangles, and the angles of those triangles are precisely the interior angles of the polygon. Hence
\[\sum_{k=1}^{n}E_k=n\cdot 180^\circ-(n-2)\cdot 180^\circ=2\cdot 180^\circ=360^\circ .\]
For a triangle, where $n=3$, this is the computation
\[3\cdot 180^\circ-180^\circ=540^\circ-180^\circ=360^\circ ,\]
which is the argument given in the original post.

The same fact has a direct ``walking'' proof: walk once around the polygon, and at each vertex turn through the exterior angle, always in the same direction (convexity guarantees the turn is never backwards). After one lap you face the original direction again, so the total turn, that is the sum of the exterior angles, is one full revolution.

Without convexity the statement needs care: for a non-convex polygon the angle between a side and the extension of the neighbouring side need not be $180^\circ$ minus the interior angle, and the sum of these angles is then not necessarily $360^\circ$.

Therefore the exterior angles of every convex polygon add up to $360^\circ$.

