\textbf{Area method.} Compute the area of triangle $ABC$ in two ways. Taking the legs as base and height,
\[[ABC]=\dfrac{1}{2}ab .\]
Taking the hypotenuse as base and the altitude $CD$ as height,
\[[ABC]=\dfrac{1}{2}ch .\]
Both expressions are the area of the same triangle, so $\dfrac{1}{2}ab=\dfrac{1}{2}ch$ and therefore
\[h=\dfrac{ab}{c}=\dfrac{CA\cdot CB}{AB} .\]

\textbf{Similar-triangle check.} The triangles $ACD$ and $ABC$ share the angle at $A$, and both have a right angle: $\angle ADC=90^\circ$ because $CD\perp AB$, and $\angle ACB=90^\circ$. So $\triangle ACD\sim\triangle ABC$, with $A$ corresponding to $A$, $D$ to $C$ and $C$ to $B$. The sides opposite the shared angle at $A$ are $CD$ and $CB$, and the sides opposite the right angles are $CA$ and $AB$, so
\[\dfrac{CD}{CB}=\dfrac{CA}{AB},\qquad\text{hence}\qquad CD=\dfrac{CA\cdot CB}{AB},\]
in agreement with the area computation.

For instance, a right triangle with legs $3$ and $4$ has hypotenuse $5$ and altitude $\dfrac{3\cdot 4}{5}=\dfrac{12}{5}$.

Hence the altitude from the right angle to the hypotenuse always equals the product of the legs divided by the hypotenuse.

question:
On a \(12\)-hour clock, let \(h\) be an integer from \(1\) to \(12\) and let \(m\) be a real number with \(0\le m<60\). The hour hand moves \(30^\circ\) per hour and \(0.5^\circ\) per minute, while the minute hand moves \(6^\circ\) per minute.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Derive a formula for the smaller angle between the two hands in terms of \(h\) and \(m\).
\item[(b)] Explain why the expression \(|30h-5.5m|\) by itself does not always give the smaller angle.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Measured clockwise from the \(12\) o'clock position, the hour hand is at
\[
30h+0.5m
\]
degrees and the minute hand is at
\[
6m
\]
degrees. Hence the absolute difference of their positions is
\[
D=\left|(30h+0.5m)-6m\right|=|30h-5.5m|.
\]
One of the two angles formed by the hands has measure \(D\) and the other has measure \(360^\circ-D\). Since the smaller angle is the minimum of these two, the answer to (a) is
\[
\boxed{\min\{D,360^\circ-D\}=\min\{|30h-5.5m|,\,360^\circ-|30h-5.5m|\}.}
\]
For (b), \(D\) can exceed \(180^\circ\). The sketch shows the \(2{:}30\) case, where
\[
D=|30\cdot2-5.5\cdot30|=|60-165|=105^\circ,
\]
which is less than \(180^\circ\). At \(1{:}59\), however, \(D\) is larger than \(180^\circ\); then the smaller angle is \(360^\circ-D\), not \(D\).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
unitsize(1cm);
real rad=2;
pair O=(0,0);
draw(circle(O,rad),linewidth(0.8));
for (int i=0; i<12; ++i) {
  real ang=90-i*30;
  pair p = rad*(cos(ang*pi/180), sin(ang*pi/180));
  pair q = (rad-0.12)*(cos(ang*pi/180), sin(ang*pi/180));
  draw(p--q,linewidth(0.6));
}
label("$12$",(0,rad+0.22),N);
label("$3$",(rad+0.22,0),E);
label("$6$",(0,-rad-0.22),S);
label("$9$",(-rad-0.22,0),W);
real hour=2, minute=30;
real ah=30*hour+0.5*minute;
real am=6*minute;
pair H=1.7*(sin(ah*pi/180), cos(ah*pi/180));
pair Mh=1.7*(sin(am*pi/180), cos(am*pi/180));
draw(O--H,linewidth(1.2)+red);
draw(O--Mh,linewidth(1.2)+blue);
dot(O);
label("hour hand",H,NE,red);
label("minute hand",Mh,S,blue);
real std_h=90-ah;
real std_m=90-am;
path arcpath;
for (int i=0; i<=30; ++i) {
  real a=(std_m+(std_h-std_m)*i/30)*pi/180;
  pair pt=0.7*(cos(a),sin(a));
  if (i==0) arcpath=pt; else arcpath=arcpath--pt;
}
draw(arcpath,linewidth(0.7));
label("$105^\circ$",0.95*(cos((std_m+std_h)/2*pi/180),sin((std_m+std_h)/2*pi/180)),black);
\end{asy}

In general, use \(D\) when \(D\le180^\circ\) and use \(360^\circ-D\) when \(D\ge180^\circ\).

A circle of diameter \(1\) has radius \(0.5\). Since every circle must lie inside the \(100\times100\) square, its center must lie at least \(0.5\) from each side. Thus all centers lie in a square of side
\[
100-2(0.5)=99.
\]
In the triangular packing, the distance between the centers of two tangent circles in the same row is \(1\). If two adjacent rows are shifted by \(0.5\), then the vertical distance between their rows of centers is the height of an equilateral triangle of side \(1\):
\[
\sqrt{1^2-\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^2}=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
unitsize(1cm);
real r=0.5;
real h=sqrt(3)/2;
for (int i=0; i<4; ++i) { draw(circle((i,0),r),linewidth(0.6)); dot((i,0)); }
for (int i=0; i<3; ++i) { draw(circle((i+0.5,h),r),linewidth(0.6)); dot((i+0.5,h)); }
for (int i=0; i<4; ++i) { draw(circle((i,2*h),r),linewidth(0.6)); dot((i,2*h)); }
pair A=(0,0), B=(1,0), C=(0.5,h);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,red+linewidth(0.9));
label("$1$",(0.5,-0.75),S,red);
draw((-0.8,0)--(-0.8,h),red+linewidth(0.7));
draw((-0.9,0)--(-0.7,0),red);
draw((-0.9,h)--(-0.7,h),red);
label("$\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$",(-1.0,h/2),W,red);
\end{asy}

If there are \(r\) rows, the vertical span of the centers is \((r-1)\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\), so we need
\[
(r-1)\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\le99.
\]
Now
\[
114\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}=57\sqrt3\approx98.726<99,
\]
but
\[
115\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}=57.5\sqrt3\approx99.593>99.
\]
Hence at most \(115\) rows fit.

Within a row of centers, the horizontal span is at most \(99\), and consecutive centers are \(1\) apart, so a row contains at most \(100\) circles. A row with \(100\) circles has its centers exactly at the two ends of the \(99\)-unit range, so it is not shifted by \(0.5\). The neighbouring row, being shifted by \(0.5\), can contain at most \(99\) circles. Therefore among any two adjacent rows, at most one can contain \(100\) circles.

With \(115\) rows, the maximum is obtained by starting and ending with a \(100\)-circle row: \(58\) rows of \(100\) and \(57\) rows of \(99\). This gives
\[
58\cdot100+57\cdot99=5800+5643=11{,}443
\]
circles in total. The increase over the original \(10{,}000\) circles is
\[
11{,}443-10{,}000=\boxed{1{,}443}.\]

question:
A student solved \(n\) problems during a \(31\)-day period. On each day he solved a nonnegative integer number of problems. It is known that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] from day \(1\) to day \(14\) inclusive, he solved an average of \(5\) problems per day;
\item[(b)] from day \(12\) to day \(31\) inclusive, he solved an average of \(3\) problems per day.
\end{enumerate}
Find the number of different values of \(n\) for which this is possible.

solution:
The two equal sides of an isosceles trapezoid are its legs, so the bases are \(10\) and \(7\), and each leg is \(2.5\). Drop perpendiculars from the endpoints of the shorter base to the longer base. The excess of the longer base over the shorter base is \(10-7=3\), so each right triangle has horizontal leg \(1.5\).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=2;
pair A=(-5,0), B=(5,0), C=(3.5,h), D=(-3.5,h);
pair G=(3.5,0);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(C--G,dashed+red);
draw((3.5,0)--(3.85,0)--(3.85,0.35)--(3.5,0.35),red);
label("$A$",A,SW);
label("$B$",B,SE);
label("$C$",C,NE);
label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$10$",(A+B)/2,S);
label("$7$",(C+D)/2,N);
label("$2.5$",(B+C)/2,E);
label("$2.5$",(A+D)/2,W);
label("$2$",(3.32,1),W,red);
label("$1.5$",(4.25,-0.18),S,red);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D);
\end{asy}
 The height satisfies
\[h=\sqrt{2.5^2-1.5^2}=\sqrt{6.25-2.25}=2.\]
Thus the area is
\[\dfrac{7+10}{2}\cdot 2=17.\]
The area is \(\boxed{17}\).

A regular polygon is convex. A line can meet the boundary of a convex polygon in \(0\) distinct points, in exactly one point if it is a supporting line, or in exactly two points if it cuts through the polygon. It cannot be more than two because a line meets the circumcircle in at most two points.

All three values occur. A line far outside the polygon gives \(x=0\). A supporting line through a vertex gives \(x=1\). A secant line through the interior gives \(x=2\). The sketch below uses a regular \(12\)-gon only as a schematic.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V;
for(int i=0;i<12;++i){ V[i]=2*dir(90-30*i); }
for(int i=0;i<11;++i){ draw(V[i]--V[i+1],linewidth(0.8)); }
draw(V[11]--V[0],linewidth(0.8));
draw((-2.7,2)--(2.7,2),red+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-2.7,0.8)--(2.7,0.8),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-2.7,2.7)--(2.7,2.7),black+dashed+linewidth(0.8));
dot(V[0]);
label("$T$",V[0],NW,red);
label("$x=1$",(2.8,2),E,red);
label("$x=2$",(2.8,0.8),E,blue);
label("$x=0$",(2.8,2.7),E,black);
\end{asy}
 Hence the possible values are \(0,1,2\), whose sum is
\[0+1+2=3.\]
The sum is \(\boxed{3}\).

Each sector has arc length
\[\dfrac{1}{3}(2\pi\cdot 6)=4\pi.\]
This arc becomes the circumference of the base of the cone. If the base radius is \(r\), then \(2\pi r=4\pi\), so \(r=2\). The slant height of the cone is the original radius \(6\).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
path sector=(0,0)--(6,0);
for(real a=10; a<=120; a+=10){ sector=sector--(6*dir(a)); }
sector=sector--cycle;
draw(sector,linewidth(0.8));
draw((0,0)--(6,0),linewidth(0.8));
draw((0,0)--(6*dir(120)),linewidth(0.8));
label("$6$",(3,0),S);
label("$6$",(3*dir(120)),NW);
label("$120^\circ$",(0.9*dir(55)),E);
pair C=(12,0), L=(10,0), R=(14,0), Apex=(12,sqrt(32));
draw(shift(C)*xscale(2)*yscale(0.6)*unitcircle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(Apex--L,linewidth(0.8));
draw(Apex--R,linewidth(0.8));
draw(Apex--C,dashed+red);
draw((12.25,0)--(12.25,0.25)--(12,0.25),red);
label("$6$",(Apex+R)/2,E);
label("$2$",(C+R)/2,S);
label("$4\sqrt2$",(Apex+C)/2,W,red);
dot(C); dot(L); dot(R); dot(Apex);
\end{asy}
 Therefore the height \(h\) satisfies
\[h^2+2^2=6^2,\]
so
\[h=\sqrt{36-4}=4\sqrt2.\]
The height is \(\boxed{4\sqrt2}\) inches.

Every simple polygon with \(n\ge3\) vertices can be triangulated by \(n-3\) noncrossing diagonals lying entirely inside the polygon. For a hexagon this requires \(6-3=3\) interior diagonals, so at least \(3\) are necessary.

It remains to show that \(3\) can occur. Consider the hexagon with vertices
\[A=(0,0),\quad B=(6,0),\quad C=(6,5),\quad D=(3,1),\quad E=(0,5),\quad F=(1,3)\]
taken in this order.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair pA=(0,0), pB=(6,0), pC=(6,5), pD=(3,1), pE=(0,5), pF=(1,3);
fill(pA--pB--pC--pD--pE--pF--cycle,paleyellow);
draw(pA--pB--pC--pD--pE--pF--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(pA--pD,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(pB--pD,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(pD--pF,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(pA); dot(pB); dot(pC); dot(pD); dot(pE); dot(pF);
label("$A$",pA,SW);
label("$B$",pB,SE);
label("$C$",pC,NE);
label("$D$",pD,S);
label("$E$",pE,NW);
label("$F$",pF,W);
\end{asy}
 This is a simple hexagon. We verify that its interior diagonals are exactly \(AD\), \(BD\), and \(DF\).

The following intervals are contained in the hexagon: for \(0\le c\le1\), the interval \(0\le y\le3c\) is inside; for \(1<c\le3\), the interval \(0\le y\le5-\dfrac{4c}{3}\) is inside; for \(3\le c<6\), the interval \(0\le y\le\dfrac{4c}{3}-3\) is inside.

On \(AD\), \(y=\dfrac{c}{3}\) for \(0\le c\le3\). If \(0\le c\le1\), then \(\dfrac{c}{3}\le3c\); if \(1<c\le3\), then \(\dfrac{c}{3}\le5-\dfrac{4c}{3}\) because \(c\le3\). Hence \(AD\) is interior. On \(DF\), \(y=4-c\) for \(1\le c\le3\), and \(4-c\le5-\dfrac{4c}{3}\) because \(c\le3\); hence \(DF\) is interior. On \(BD\), \(y=2-\dfrac{c}{3}\) for \(3\le c\le6\), and \(2-\dfrac{c}{3}\le\dfrac{4c}{3}-3\) because \(c\ge3\); hence \(BD\) is interior.

The other six diagonals are not interior, since each contains a point outside the hexagon:
\begin{itemize}
\item \(AC\) and \(BE\) both pass through \(\left(3,\dfrac{5}{2}\right)\), while at \(x=3\) the hexagon occupies only \(0\le y\le1\);
\item \(AE\) has midpoint \(\left(0,\dfrac{5}{2}\right)\), and the line \(x=0\) meets the hexagon only at \(A\) and \(E\);
\item \(CE\) has midpoint \(\left(3,5\right)\), which is outside by the same \(x=3\) slice;
\item \(BF\) contains \(\left(3,\dfrac{9}{5}\right)\), which is outside by the \(x=3\) slice;
\item \(CF\) has midpoint \(\left(\dfrac{7}{2},4\right)\), while at \(x=\dfrac{7}{2}\) the hexagon occupies only \(0\le y\le\dfrac{5}{3}\).
\end{itemize}
Thus this hexagon has exactly the three interior diagonals \(AD\), \(BD\), and \(DF\). Therefore the least possible number is \(\boxed{3}\).

The circle is
\[x^2+y^2=53.\]
The point \(E=(-2,7)\) lies on the circle because \((-2)^2+7^2=53\), and it lies on \(13x+5y=9\) because \(13(-2)+5(7)=9\). It is in quadrant II. To see that it is the required intersection, substitute \(y=\dfrac{9-13x}{5}\) into \(x^2+y^2=53\). This gives
\[97x^2-117x-622=0,\]
that is,
\[(x+2)(97x-311)=0.\]
The other intersection has \(x=\dfrac{311}{97}>0\), so it is not in quadrant II. Hence \(E=(-2,7)\).

Translating the line \(x=y\) left by \(5\) gives
\[y=x+5.\]
Substitute into the circle:
\[x^2+(x+5)^2=53.\]
Thus
\[2x^2+10x-28=0,\]
so
\[x^2+5x-14=0.\]
Hence \(x=2\) or \(x=-7\), giving the lattice points
\[G=(2,7),\qquad H=(-7,-2).\]
Now \(E=(-2,7)\), so \(EG\) is horizontal with length \(4\). The perpendicular distance from \(H\) to the line \(y=7\) is \(9\). Therefore
\[[EGH]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot4\cdot9=18.\]
The area is \(\boxed{18}\).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=sqrt(53);
pair pE=(-2,7), pF=(311/97,-634/97), pG=(2,7), pH=(-7,-2);
fill(pE--pG--pH--cycle,paleyellow);
draw(circle((0,0),r),linewidth(0.8));
draw(pE--pF,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(pG--pH,red+linewidth(0.8));
draw(pE--pG--pH--cycle,green+linewidth(0.9));
dot(pE); dot(pF); dot(pG); dot(pH); dot((0,0));
label("$E$",pE,NW);
label("$F$",pF,SE);
label("$G$",pG,N);
label("$H$",pH,SW);
label("$O$",(0,0),SW);
\end{asy}

question:
A student claims: ``You use combinations when you do not care about the order, and permutations when you do care about the order.'' Let $n\ge k\ge 1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the number of ways to choose $k$ of $n$ distinct objects \emph{in order} (that is, to list $k$ distinct objects in a row) is $n(n-1)\cdots(n-k+1)$.
\item[(b)] Show that the number of ways to choose $k$ of the $n$ objects while \emph{ignoring order} is $\dfrac{n(n-1)\cdots(n-k+1)}{k!}=\dbinom{n}{k}$.
\item[(c)] Use (a) and (b) to explain precisely why the student's rule of thumb is correct.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Take the lines $y=0$ and $y=x$. Their slopes are $0$ and $1$, their inclinations are $0^\circ$ and $45^\circ$, so they meet at $45^\circ$, and the product of their slopes is $0\cdot 1=0$.

Now take the lines $y=(\sqrt2-1)x$ and $y=(\sqrt2+1)x$. Since
\[\tan 22.5^\circ=\dfrac{\sin 45^\circ}{1+\cos 45^\circ}=\dfrac{\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}}{1+\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}}=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2+\sqrt2}=\dfrac{\sqrt2\,(2-\sqrt2)}{4-2}=\sqrt2-1\]
and $\tan 67.5^\circ=\tan(90^\circ-22.5^\circ)=\dfrac{1}{\tan 22.5^\circ}=\sqrt2+1$, these lines have inclinations $22.5^\circ$ and $67.5^\circ$, so again the angle between them is $45^\circ$. But now the product of the slopes is
\[(\sqrt2-1)(\sqrt2+1)=2-1=1.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(11cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s1=sqrt(2)-1, s2=sqrt(2)+1;
pair O=(0,0);
draw((-0.4,0)--(3.5,0),linewidth(0.7));
draw((0,-0.4)--(0,3.1),linewidth(0.7));
label("$x$",(3.55,0),E);
label("$y$",(0,3.15),N);
draw(O--(3.2,0),red+linewidth(1));
draw(O--(2.5,2.5),blue+linewidth(1));
label("$m_1=0$",(2.6,0.25),N,red);
label("$m_2=1$",(2.6,2.6),E,blue);
draw(arc(O,0.8,0,45),linewidth(0.6));
label("$45^\circ$",(1.0,0.32));
label("$m_1m_2=0$",(1.5,-1.0));
pair O2=(6.9,0);
draw(O2+(-0.4,0)--O2+(3.5,0),linewidth(0.7));
draw(O2+(0,-0.4)--O2+(0,3.1),linewidth(0.7));
label("$x$",O2+(3.55,0),E);
label("$y$",O2+(0,3.15),N);
draw(O2--O2+(3.3,3.3*s1),red+linewidth(1));
draw(O2--O2+(1.2,1.2*s2),blue+linewidth(1));
label("$m_1=\sqrt{2}-1$",O2+(3.35,1.37),E,red);
label("$m_2=\sqrt{2}+1$",O2+(1.25,2.9),E,blue);
draw(arc(O2,0.8,22.5,67.5),linewidth(0.6));
label("$45^\circ$",O2+(0.5,0.95));
label("$m_1m_2=1$",O2+(1.5,-1.0));
\end{asy}

The same angle $45^\circ$ gives the products $0$ and $1$, so the product of the slopes is not determined by $\theta$.

\textbf{(b)} Let $\alpha,\beta\in(-90^\circ,90^\circ)$ be the inclinations of the two lines, so that $m_1=\tan\alpha$ and $m_2=\tan\beta$; here we use that neither line is vertical, so both slopes exist. The smaller angle between the two lines is
\[\theta=\min\left\{|\alpha-\beta|,\ 180^\circ-|\alpha-\beta|\right\}\in[0^\circ,90^\circ].\]
Because $\tan(180^\circ-u)=-\tan u$, in both cases $\tan\theta=|\tan(\alpha-\beta)|$ as long as $\theta\ne 90^\circ$. Since
\[\tan(\alpha-\beta)=\dfrac{\tan\alpha-\tan\beta}{1+\tan\alpha\tan\beta}=\dfrac{m_1-m_2}{1+m_1m_2},\]
we obtain
\[\tan\theta=\left|\dfrac{m_1-m_2}{1+m_1m_2}\right|\qquad\text{when }1+m_1m_2\ne 0.\]

If $1+m_1m_2=0$, then $\tan(\alpha-\beta)$ is undefined, which forces $\alpha-\beta=\pm90^\circ$, hence $\theta=90^\circ$. Conversely, $\cos(\alpha-\beta)=\cos\alpha\cos\beta\,(1+\tan\alpha\tan\beta)=\cos\alpha\cos\beta\,(1+m_1m_2)$, and $\cos\alpha\cos\beta\ne 0$ because both inclinations lie strictly between $-90^\circ$ and $90^\circ$. So $\theta=90^\circ$ (that is, $\cos(\alpha-\beta)=0$) holds exactly when $1+m_1m_2=0$, which is the familiar statement $m_1m_2=-1$.

\textbf{Why $\theta$ does not determine the product (except when $\theta=90^\circ$).} The formula is a single equation linking $m_1$ and $m_2$: it fixes $\left|\dfrac{m_1-m_2}{1+m_1m_2}\right|$, not the product. To see this concretely, fix $0<\theta<90^\circ$. The two lines with inclinations $0$ and $\theta$ have slopes $0$ and $\tan\theta$, so their product is $0$. The two lines with inclinations $-\dfrac{\theta}{2}$ and $\dfrac{\theta}{2}$ have slopes $-\tan\left(\dfrac{\theta}{2}\right)$ and $\tan\left(\dfrac{\theta}{2}\right)$, so their product is $-\tan^2\left(\dfrac{\theta}{2}\right)<0$. Both pairs meet at the angle $\theta$, but their slope products are different. Part (a) is an explicit instance for $\theta=45^\circ$. For $\theta=90^\circ$, on the other hand, the relation collapses to the single condition $1+m_1m_2=0$, so the product is always $-1$, which is exactly the familiar perpendicular case.

Let $m$ be the slope of $q$. Both slopes are assumed to exist, so $q$ is not vertical. The vector $(1,m)$ points along $q$, and $(-m,1)$ is perpendicular to it, since $(1,m)\cdot(-m,1)=-m+m=0$. So $r$ points along $(-m,1)$ and has slope $\dfrac{1}{-m}=-\dfrac{1}{m}$. (Here $m\ne 0$: if $m=0$, then $r$ would be vertical and would have no slope, contrary to the hypothesis.) Hence the product of the two slopes is
\[m\cdot\left(-\dfrac{1}{m}\right)=-1.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-3.2,0)--(3.3,0),linewidth(0.7));
draw((0,-3.2)--(0,3.3),linewidth(0.7));
label("$x$",(3.35,0),E);
label("$y$",(0,3.35),N);
draw((-2.7,-2.7)--(2.7,2.7),blue+linewidth(1));
draw((-2.7,2.7)--(2.7,-2.7),red+linewidth(1));
label("$q$, slope $1$",(2.75,2.75),E,blue);
label("$r$, slope $-1$",(2.75,-2.75),E,red);
dot((-1,-1));
label("$C=(-1,-1)$",(-1,-1),NW);
real h=0.3*sqrt(2);
draw((h,h)--(2*h,0)--(h,-h),linewidth(0.6));
\end{asy}

So $x=-1$ and
\[C=(x,x)=\boxed{(-1,-1)}.\]

A rotation of $270^\circ$ clockwise is the same as a rotation of $360^\circ-270^\circ=90^\circ$ counterclockwise.

Under a $90^\circ$ counterclockwise rotation about the origin the point $(a,b)$ goes to $(-b,a)$. Indeed, the rotation sends $(1,0)$ to $(0,1)$ and $(0,1)$ to $(-1,0)$, and it is linear, so $(a,b)=a(1,0)+b(0,1)$ is sent to $a(0,1)+b(-1,0)=(-b,a)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-2.4,0)--(2.4,0),linewidth(0.7));
draw((0,-2.2)--(0,1.9),linewidth(0.7));
label("$x$",(2.45,0),E);
label("$y$",(0,1.95),N);
pair C=(-1,-1), D=(1,-1), O=(0,0);
draw(O--C,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--D,linewidth(0.9));
draw(C--D,dashed+linewidth(0.6));
dot(C); dot(D); dot(O);
label("$C=(-1,-1)$",C,SW);
label("$C'=(1,-1)$",D,SE);
label("$O$",O,NW);
draw(arc(O,1.05,-135,-45),linewidth(0.7));
label("$90^\circ$",(0,-1.35),S);
\end{asy}

