Therefore the image of $C$ is
\[\boxed{(1,-1)}.\]

Let the two perpendicular lines be $q$ and $r$, with slopes $m_1$ and $m_2$. Since both slopes exist, neither line is vertical, so $q$ has a direction vector $(1,m_1)$ and $r$ has a direction vector $(1,m_2)$.

Two nonzero vectors are perpendicular exactly when their dot product is $0$. Therefore
\[(1,m_1)\cdot(1,m_2)=0,\qquad\text{that is,}\qquad 1+m_1m_2=0.\]
Hence $m_1m_2=-1$. (In particular $m_1\ne 0$ and $m_2\ne 0$: a horizontal line is perpendicular only to a vertical line, which has no slope, so that case cannot occur here.)


