Thus \(mn=27\).
By the law of cosines in \(\triangle ABC\),
\[
\cos B=\dfrac{12^2+9^2-13^2}{2\cdot12\cdot9}
=\dfrac{56}{216}=\dfrac{7}{27}.
\]
Now apply the law of cosines in \(\triangle BDE\):
\[
DE^2=m^2+n^2-2mn\cos B
=m^2+n^2-2\cdot27\cdot\dfrac{7}{27}
=m^2+n^2-14.
\]
Since \(mn=27\), the AM-GM inequality gives
\[
m^2+n^2\ge2mn=54,
\]
with equality exactly when \(m=n=\sqrt{27}=3\sqrt{3}\). These values satisfy \(3\sqrt{3}<12\) and \(3\sqrt{3}<9\), so the points \(D\) and \(E\) really lie on the required segments. Therefore
\[
