Let the square have side length $1$ and place
\[
A=(0,0),\quad B=(1,0),\quad C=(1,1),\quad D=(0,1),
\]
so a random point is $E=(x,y)$ with $0\le x,y\le 1$.
The angle at $A$ satisfies $\overrightarrow{AB}\cdot\overrightarrow{AE}=x\ge 0$, so it is at most $90^\circ$. Similarly, the angle at $B$ satisfies $\overrightarrow{BA}\cdot\overrightarrow{BE}=1-x\ge 0$, so it is at most $90^\circ$. Therefore $\triangle ABE$ is obtuse exactly when the angle at $E$ is obtuse.
Now
\[
\overrightarrow{EA}=(-x,-y),\qquad \overrightarrow{EB}=(1-x,-y),
\]
so
\[
\overrightarrow{EA}\cdot\overrightarrow{EB}
=-x(1-x)+y^2=x^2-x+y^2.
\]
The angle at $E$ is obtuse precisely when this dot product is negative:
\[
x^2-x+y^2<0
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\left(x-\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^2+y^2<\dfrac{1}{4}.
\]
Because $y\ge 0$, this is the interior of the upper semicircle with diameter $AB$.
