The claim is not correct. A number can have a non-terminating decimal expansion and still be perfectly finite. For example, $\dfrac{1}{3}=0.333\ldots$ is finite (it is less than $1$). The decimal expansion of $\pi$ never terminates because $\pi$ is irrational, but $\pi$ itself is a finite real number, approximately $3.14159$. Thus for any finite radius $r$, the circumference $2\pi r$ is also a finite length; it may have a non-terminating decimal representation, but that does not make it infinite.
To obtain a circle of circumference exactly $4$, set
\[
2\pi r=4.
\]
Then
\[
r=\dfrac{4}{2\pi}=\dfrac{2}{\pi}.
\]
This is a positive real number, so a circle of radius $\dfrac{2}{\pi}$ exists and has circumference $2\pi\cdot\dfrac{2}{\pi}=4$.

