By Burnside's lemma the number of colourings up to rotation is
\[ \dfrac{1}{24}\left(256+6\cdot 4+3\cdot 16+6\cdot 16+8\cdot 16\right)=\dfrac{1}{24}\left(256+24+48+96+128\right)=\dfrac{552}{24}=\boxed{23}. \]

Take $n=4$ and let $Q$ be the square with vertices $(0,0)$, $(3,0)$, $(3,3)$, $(0,3)$. The statement requires $\binom{4}{2}-4+1=3$ interior points; take
\[ P_1=(2.3,\,1.2),\qquad P_2=(2.3,\,1.8),\qquad P_3=(2.6,\,1.5). \]

First we check the hypotheses. Each of $P_1$, $P_2$, $P_3$ has both coordinates strictly between $0$ and $3$, so all three lie strictly inside the square. Also $P_1$ and $P_2$ have $x$-coordinate $2.3$ while $P_3$ has $x$-coordinate $2.6$, so no three of the three points are collinear. Thus the configuration satisfies all the hypotheses of the statement.

Now we examine the diagonals. The square has exactly two diagonals, the segment from $(0,0)$ to $(3,3)$ and the segment from $(3,0)$ to $(0,3)$. They meet at the centre $M=\left(\dfrac{3}{2},\dfrac{3}{2}\right)$ and cut the square into four open triangles; consider the one with corners $M$, $(3,0)$ and $(3,3)$, and call its interior $T$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair f5A=(0,0), f5B=(3,0), f5C=(3,3), f5D=(0,3);
draw(f5A--f5B--f5C--f5D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(f5A--f5C,linewidth(0.8));
draw(f5B--f5D,linewidth(0.8));
pair f5P1=(2.3,1.2), f5P2=(2.3,1.8), f5P3=(2.6,1.5);
draw(f5P1--f5P2--f5P3--cycle,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(f5P1); dot(f5P2); dot(f5P3);
label("$P_1$",f5P1,SW,fontsize(8));
label("$P_2$",f5P2,NW,fontsize(8));
label("$P_3$",f5P3,E,fontsize(8));
label("$A$",f5A,SW,fontsize(8));
label("$B$",f5B,SE,fontsize(8));
label("$C$",f5C,NE,fontsize(8));
label("$D$",f5D,NW,fontsize(8));
\end{asy}

All three points lie in $T$: writing a point as $\left(\dfrac{3}{2}+a,\,\dfrac{3}{2}+b\right)$, it belongs to $T$ exactly when $a>0$ and $|b|<a$, and here $(a,b)$ equals $(0.8,-0.3)$, $(0.8,0.3)$ and $(1.1,0)$ respectively.

The open triangle $T$ is convex and meets neither diagonal, since the two diagonals lie on the boundary of its closure. Therefore, for any two of the three points, the segment joining them lies entirely in $T$ and is not intersected by any diagonal of the square. As a square has no diagonals other than the two mentioned, this configuration satisfies all the hypotheses of the statement and contradicts its conclusion: no two of the chosen points have a joining segment that meets a diagonal. Hence the statement is false, and the displayed configuration is a counterexample.

Let \(a=12\), \(b=13\), \(c=14\), and let \(K\) be the area. The semiperimeter is
\[
s=\dfrac{12+13+14}{2}=\dfrac{39}{2}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=13*sqrt(55)*0.125;
pair A=(4.875,h), B=(0,0), C=(12,0);
pair I=(5.5,sqrt(55)*0.5);
pair D=(5.5,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(I,sqrt(55)*0.5),red+linewidth(0.8));
draw(I--D,red+dashed+linewidth(0.8));
dot(I); dot(D);
label("$A$",A,N);
label("$B$",B,SW);
label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$13$",(A+B)*0.5,W);
label("$14$",(A+C)*0.5,E);
label("$12$",(9,0),S);
label("$I$",I,NE);
label("$D$",D,S);
label("$r$",(I+D)*0.5,E,red);
\end{asy}

By Heron's formula,
\[
K=\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}
=\sqrt{\dfrac{39}{2}\cdot\dfrac{15}{2}\cdot\dfrac{13}{2}\cdot\dfrac{11}{2}}
=\sqrt{\dfrac{39\cdot15\cdot13\cdot11}{16}}.
\]
Since
\[
39\cdot15\cdot13\cdot11=(3\cdot13)(3\cdot5)(13)(11)=3^2\cdot13^2\cdot5\cdot11=39^2\cdot55,
\]
we get
\[
K=\dfrac{39\sqrt{55}}{4}.
\]
For an incircle of radius \(r\), \(K=rs\), so
\[
r=\dfrac{K}{s}
=\dfrac{39\sqrt{55}}{4}\cdot\dfrac{2}{39}
=\dfrac{\sqrt{55}}{2}.
\]
Therefore the inradius is \(\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt{55}}{2}}\).

question:
A $4\times 4$ array of dots has $16$ dots, arranged in $4$ rows and $4$ columns, as shown.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<4;++i){
  for(int j=0;j<4;++j){
    fill(circle((i,j),0.075),black);
  }
}
\end{asy}

How many isosceles triangles are formed by choosing three of the dots as vertices? (Three collinear dots do not form a triangle and are not counted.)

solution:
Call the vertex where the two equal sides of an isosceles triangle meet its \emph{apex}.

\textbf{Step 1: there are no equilateral triangles.} If $P$ and $Q$ are dots of the array, then $PQ^2$ is a positive integer. An equilateral triangle of side $s$ has area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2$, which is irrational when $s^2$ is a positive integer, whereas the shoelace formula shows that every triangle with lattice vertices has area equal to half an integer. So no three dots form an equilateral triangle, every isosceles triangle in the array is not equilateral, and therefore has exactly one apex. Counting triangles by their apex counts each isosceles triangle exactly once.

\textbf{Step 2: count triples with a prescribed apex.} Fix a dot $P$, and suppose exactly $k$ of the other dots lie at distance $d$ from $P$. Any two of those $k$ dots, together with $P$, form a triple with two equal sides of length $d$ and apex $P$ (the triple is degenerate only if the two dots are on opposite sides of $P$), so they contribute $\binom{k}{2}$ triples. Adding over all distances, the number of triples with apex $P$ is $\sum_{d}\binom{k_d}{2}$.

The $16$ dots fall into three symmetry classes.

\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Corner dots} ($4$ of them, e.g.\ $(0,0)$): the numbers of dots at squared distances $1,2,4,5,8,9,10,13,18$ are $2,1,2,2,1,2,2,2,1$, contributing
\[\binom{2}{2}+\binom{1}{2}+\binom{2}{2}+\binom{2}{2}+\binom{1}{2}+\binom{2}{2}+\binom{2}{2}+\binom{2}{2}+\binom{1}{2}=1+0+1+1+0+1+1+1+0=6.\]
\item \emph{Edge dots} ($8$ of them, e.g.\ $(1,0)$): the numbers at squared distances $1,2,4,5,8,9,10,13$ are $3,2,2,3,1,1,2,1$, contributing
\[3+1+1+3+0+0+1+0=9.\]
\item \emph{Interior dots} ($4$ of them, e.g.\ $(1,1)$): the numbers at squared distances $1,2,4,5,8$ are $4,4,2,4,1$, contributing
\[6+6+1+6+0=19.\]
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<4;++i){
  for(int j=0;j<4;++j){
    if((i==0||i==3)&&(j==0||j==3)){ fill(circle((i,j),0.10),black); }
    else if(i==0||i==3||j==0||j==3){ fill(circle((i,j),0.10),gray(0.5)); }
    else { fill(circle((i,j),0.10),white); draw(circle((i,j),0.10),red+linewidth(0.9)); }
  }
}
label("black: corner dots ($4$)",(0,-0.65),W);
label("gray: edge dots ($8$)",(0,-1.25),W);
label("red ring: interior dots ($4$)",(0,-1.85),W);
\end{asy}

So the total number of triples counted is
\[4\cdot 6+8\cdot 9+4\cdot 19=24+72+76=172.\]

\textbf{Step 3: remove the degenerate triples.} Three distinct collinear dots $Q,P,R$ satisfy $PQ=PR$ exactly when $P$ is the midpoint of $QR$, and each such degenerate triple was counted exactly once, with apex $P$. A pair of dots has a dot as its midpoint exactly when the two dots have the same parity of both coordinates, because then $\left(\dfrac{x_1+x_2}{2},\dfrac{y_1+y_2}{2}\right)$ is again a dot. The $16$ dots split into $4$ parity classes of $4$ dots each (even-even, even-odd, odd-even, odd-odd), so the number of such pairs, and hence of degenerate triples, is
\[4\binom{4}{2}=4\cdot 6=24.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<4;++i){
  for(int j=0;j<4;++j){
    fill(circle((i,j),0.075),black);
  }
}
draw((0,1)--(2,1),red+linewidth(1.0));
label("$Q$",(0,1),2W);
label("$P$",(1,1),1.9S);
label("$R$",(2,1),2E);
\end{asy}

Subtracting them gives
\[172-24=\boxed{148}.\]

question:
In triangle $ABC$, $[ABC]=12$. Point $D$ is the midpoint of $\overline{AB}$, and point $E$ lies on $\overline{AC}$ with $AE=2\,CE$. Let $O$ be the intersection of $\overline{CD}$ and $\overline{BE}$. If $[XYZ]$ denotes the area of triangle $XYZ$, find $[BDO]-[CEO]$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair pA=(0,0), pB=(4,0), pC=(1.5,6), pD=(2,0), pE=(1,4), pO=(1.75,3);
draw(pA--pB--pC--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(pC--pD,blue+linewidth(0.7));
draw(pB--pE,blue+linewidth(0.7));
dot(pA); dot(pB); dot(pC); dot(pD); dot(pE); dot(pO);
label("$A$",pA,SW); label("$B$",pB,SE); label("$C$",pC,N);
label("$D$",pD,S); label("$E$",pE,W); label("$O$",pO,NE);
\end{asy}

solution:
Assign masses \(m_A=m_B=1\) at \(A\) and \(B\). Since \(D\) is the midpoint of \(AB\), this is consistent. The ratio \(AE:EC=2:1\) gives \(m_C=2\), so the mass at \(E\) is \(3\), and the mass at \(D\) is \(2\). On cevian \(CD\), the masses at \(C\) and \(D\) are equal, so \(O\) is the midpoint of \(CD\). On cevian \(BE\), \(BO:OE=m_E:m_B=3:1\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair pA=(0,0), pB=(4,0), pC=(1.5,6), pD=(2,0), pE=(1,4), pO=(1.75,3);
fill(pB--pD--pO--cycle,paleblue);
fill(pC--pE--pO--cycle,paleyellow);
draw(pA--pB--pC--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(pC--pD,blue+linewidth(0.7));
draw(pB--pE,blue+linewidth(0.7));
dot(pA); dot(pB); dot(pC); dot(pD); dot(pE); dot(pO);
label("$A$",pA,SW); label("$B$",pB,SE); label("$C$",pC,N);
label("$D$",pD,S); label("$E$",pE,W); label("$O$",pO,NE);
\end{asy}

Now \([BCD]=\dfrac{1}{2}[ABC]=6\), and since \(O\) is the midpoint of \(CD\), \(BO\) is a median of \(\triangle BCD\). Hence \([BDO]=\dfrac{1}{2}[BCD]=3\).
Also \(CE:CA=1:3\), so \([BCE]=\dfrac{1}{3}[ABC]=4\). Since \(EO:EB=1:4\), triangles \(CEO\) and \(CBE\) have the same altitude from \(C\) to line \(BE\), so \([CEO]=\dfrac{1}{4}[BCE]=1\).
Therefore \([BDO]-[CEO]=3-1=\boxed{2}\).

The allowed region is a \(12\times 12\) square with the \(6\times 6\) lower-left quadrant removed. Its area is
\[
144-36=108.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill(circle((0,0),4), paleblue);
fill((-6,0)--(0,0)--(0,-6)--(-6,-6)--cycle, gray(0.85));
draw((-6,-6)--(6,-6)--(6,6)--(-6,6)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(circle((0,0),4), blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-6.5,0)--(6.5,0), Arrow);
draw((0,-6.5)--(0,6.5), Arrow);
label("$a$",(6.5,0),E);
label("$b$",(0,6.5),N);
label("$6$",(6,0),S);
label("$-6$",(-6,0),S);
label("$6$",(0,6),W);
label("$-6$",(0,-6),W);
\end{asy}

The points within \(4\) units of the origin form a disk of area \(16\pi\). Inside the removed quadrant this disk contributes a quarter-disk of area \(4\pi\), so the favorable area is
\[
16\pi-4\pi=12\pi.
\]
Thus the probability is
\[
\dfrac{12\pi}{108}=\boxed{\dfrac{\pi}{9}}.
\]

Name the vertices $A=(2,3,5)$, $B=(-1,6,9)$, $C=(-4,-10,-4)$, $D=(11,2,7)$ and take the three edge vectors that start at $A$:
\[\vec u=\overrightarrow{AB}=(-3,3,4),\qquad \vec v=\overrightarrow{AC}=(-6,-13,-9),\qquad \vec w=\overrightarrow{AD}=(9,-1,2).\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.0625,1.1875), B=(0.7625,2.2875), C=(-1.45,-2.85), D=(3.5375,1.1125);
pen pn=linewidth(0.7);
pen pr=red+linewidth(0.9);
draw(B--C--D--cycle,pn);
draw(A--B,pr); draw(A--C,pr); draw(A--D,pr);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D);
label("$A$",A,NE);
label("$B$",B,NW);
label("$C$",C,SW);
label("$D$",D,E);
\end{asy}

The three vectors span a parallelepiped of volume $\left|\vec u\cdot(\vec v\times\vec w)\right|$. The tetrahedron with vertices $A,A+\vec u,A+\vec v,A+\vec w$, namely $ABCD$, has volume one sixth of this parallelepiped (a standard fact; for instance, the parallelepiped decomposes into six tetrahedra of equal volume). Hence
\[V=\dfrac{1}{6}\left|\vec u\cdot(\vec v\times\vec w)\right|.\]
Compute the cross product first:
\[\vec v\times\vec w=\left((-13)(2)-(-9)(-1),\ (-9)(9)-(-6)(2),\ (-6)(-1)-(-13)(9)\right)=(-35,-69,123).\]
Then the scalar triple product is
\[\vec u\cdot(\vec v\times\vec w)=(-3)(-35)+(3)(-69)+(4)(123)=105-207+492=390.\]
Therefore
\[V=\dfrac{390}{6}=\boxed{65}.\]
The same computation can be organised as a base-and-height calculation: take the face $ABC$ as base, whose area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\left|\vec u\times\vec v\right|$, and whose plane has normal $\vec u\times\vec v$; the height from $D$ is the projection $\dfrac{\left|\vec w\cdot(\vec u\times\vec v)\right|}{\left|\vec u\times\vec v\right|}$. Substituting into $V=\dfrac{1}{3}(\text{base area})(\text{height})$ gives again $\dfrac{1}{6}\left|\vec w\cdot(\vec u\times\vec v)\right|=65$, which shows that the scalar triple product is just this base-and-height computation organised in one step.

question:
In this problem $a_1,a_2,b_1,b_2,c_1,c_2$ are all nonzero real numbers, and $a_1+a_2\ne 0$.

Suppose the quadratic $a_1x^2+b_1x+c_1=0$ has two (not necessarily distinct) real roots $r_1$ and $s_1$, and the quadratic $a_2x^2+b_2x+c_2=0$ has two (not necessarily distinct) real roots $r_2$ and $s_2$.

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Must the quadratic $(a_1+a_2)x^2+(b_1+b_2)x+(c_1+c_2)=0$ have roots $\dfrac{r_1+r_2}{2}$ and $\dfrac{s_1+s_2}{2}$? Give a counterexample or prove that it must.
\item[(b)] Now suppose in addition that $a_1=a_2=a\ne 0$. Prove that $\dfrac{r_1+r_2}{2}+\dfrac{s_1+s_2}{2}$ equals the sum of the two roots of the combined quadratic (counted with multiplicity, as given by Vieta's formulas), and give an example in which neither of these two numbers is itself a root.
\end{enumerate}

A student claims that
\[1+i=(1+i)(1+i).\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Compute $(1+i)^2$ and use it to explain why the claim is false.
\item[(b)] Find all complex numbers $z$ such that $z^2=z$.
\end{enumerate}

A forum post observes that the average $\dfrac{p+(p+2)}{2}=p+1$ of a twin prime pair is often a multiple of $6$ (for instance the pair $(5,7)$ has average $6$, and $6\cdot 2,6\cdot 3,6\cdot 5,6\cdot 7$ are also averages of twin prime pairs), and proposes the rule: \emph{for every prime $q$, the two numbers $6q-1$ and $6q+1$ are both prime}.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that if $p$ and $p+2$ are both primes and $p>3$, then $6$ divides $p+1$.
\item[(b)] Find the smallest prime $q$ for which the proposed rule fails.
\end{enumerate}

Two real numbers $x$ and $y$ are claimed to satisfy
\[x^{4}+y^{4}=3^{4}\qquad\text{and}\qquad (xy)^{2}=3^{6}.\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that no real numbers $x,y$ satisfy both conditions.
\item[(b)] Suppose instead that $(xy)^{2}=3^{2}$ while $x^{4}+y^{4}=3^{4}$, with $x^{2}\ne y^{2}$. Find the value of $\dfrac{x^{6}-y^{6}}{x^{2}-y^{2}}$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Suppose for contradiction that
\[\dfrac{1}{a}+\dfrac{1}{b}=\dfrac{491}{1024}\]
with $a,b$ positive integers. Interchanging $a$ and $b$ if necessary, assume $a\le b$. Then $\dfrac{1}{b}\le\dfrac{1}{a}$, so
\[\dfrac{491}{1024}=\dfrac{1}{a}+\dfrac{1}{b}\le\dfrac{2}{a},\qquad\text{hence}\qquad a\le\dfrac{2048}{491}<5.\]
So $a\in\{1,2,3,4\}$.

Clearing denominators gives $1024(a+b)=491ab$, that is
\[b\,(491a-1024)=1024a,\qquad b=\dfrac{1024a}{491a-1024}.\]
We check the four possible values of $a$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $a=1$: $b=\dfrac{1024}{-533}<0$;
\item $a=2$: $b=\dfrac{2048}{-42}<0$;
\item $a=3$: $b=\dfrac{3072}{449}$, and $449\cdot6=2694<3072<3143=449\cdot7$, so $b$ is not an integer;
\item $a=4$: $b=\dfrac{4096}{940}=\dfrac{1024}{235}$, and $235\cdot4=940<1024<1175=235\cdot5$, so $b$ is not an integer.
\end{itemize}
In every case there is no positive integer $b$, a contradiction. Hence $\dfrac{491}{1024}$ is not of the form $\dfrac{1}{a}+\dfrac{1}{b}$ with $a,b$ positive integers.

question:
In equilateral triangle $ABC$, the point $P$ lies inside the triangle and satisfies
\[PA=3,\qquad PB=4,\qquad PC=5.\]
Find the side length of the triangle.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=sqrt(25+12*sqrt(3)); real ht=s*sqrt(3)/2;
pair A=(0,0), C=(s,0), B=(s/2,ht);
real px=(s*s-16)/(2*s); pair P=(px,sqrt(9-px*px));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--A,linewidth(0.7)); draw(P--B,linewidth(0.7)); draw(P--C,linewidth(0.7));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(P);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,N); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$P$",P,W);
label("$3$",(A+P)/2,NW); label("$4$",(B+P)/2,NE); label("$5$",(C+P)/2,E);
\end{asy}

solution:
Let $s$ be the side length. Rotate the plane by $60^\circ$ about $A$ in the direction that sends $B$ to $C$, and let $Q$ be the image of $P$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=sqrt(25+12*sqrt(3)); real ht=s*sqrt(3)/2;
pair A=(0,0), C=(s,0), B=(s/2,ht);
real px=(s*s-16)/(2*s); pair P=(px,sqrt(9-px*px));
pair Q=rotate(-60)*P;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--P,linewidth(0.7)); draw(B--P,linewidth(0.7)); draw(C--P,linewidth(0.7));
draw(Q--A,dashed+linewidth(0.8)); draw(Q--P,dashed+linewidth(0.8)); draw(Q--C,dashed+linewidth(0.8));
pair u=unit(P-Q), v=unit(C-Q); real k=0.42;
draw(Q+k*u--Q+k*u+k*v--Q+k*v,linewidth(0.6));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(P); dot(Q);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,N); label("$C$",C,E); label("$P$",P,W); label("$Q$",Q,S);
label("$3$",(A+P)/2,NW); label("$4$",(B+P)/2,NE); label("$5$",(C+P)/2,NE);
label("$3$",(Q+A)/2,SW); label("$3$",(Q+P)/2,E); label("$4$",(Q+C)/2,S);
\end{asy}

Because the rotation preserves all distances, $AQ=AP=3$ and $QC=PB=4$. Also $\angle PAQ=60^\circ$ and $AP=AQ$, so triangle $AQP$ is equilateral and $PQ=3$.

Now triangle $PQC$ has side lengths $PQ=3$, $QC=4$ and $PC=5$. Since $3^2+4^2=5^2$, the converse of the Pythagorean theorem shows that
\[\angle PQC=90^\circ .\]
Moreover $\angle AQP=60^\circ$, and the ray $QA$ lies on one of the two sides of the line $QP$. Hence $\angle AQC$ is either
\[60^\circ+90^\circ=150^\circ\qquad\text{or}\qquad 90^\circ-60^\circ=30^\circ .\]
The value $30^\circ$ is impossible: it would give, by the law of cosines in triangle $AQC$,
\[AC^2=AQ^2+QC^2-2\cdot AQ\cdot QC\cos 30^\circ=9+16-24\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}=25-12\sqrt3<9,\]
so $AC<3$. But $P$ lies inside the equilateral triangle of side $AC$, and every point inside such a triangle is at distance less than the side length from each vertex; this would force $PC=5<AC$, a contradiction.

So $\angle AQC=150^\circ$, and the law of cosines in triangle $AQC$ gives
\[s^2=AC^2=3^2+4^2-2\cdot3\cdot4\cos150^\circ=9+16-24\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)=25+12\sqrt3 .\]
Therefore the side length is $\boxed{\sqrt{25+12\sqrt3}}$ (approximately $6.77$, which is indeed larger than $PC=5$).

question:
Fix $x=28$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] A triangle has side lengths $x-5$, $x-4$ and $x-3$. Among its three altitudes, let the longest one (the altitude drawn to the shortest side) be $\dfrac{a}{b}\sqrt{c}$, where $a$ and $b$ are relatively prime positive integers and $c$ is not divisible by the square of any prime. Let $y=a+b+c$.
\item[(b)] A triangle has side lengths $x+3$, $x+4$ and $x+5$. Let its longest altitude be $\dfrac{d}{e}\sqrt{f}$, where $d$ and $e$ are relatively prime positive integers and $f$ is not divisible by the square of any prime. Let $z=d+e+f$.
\item[(c)] Let $g=5$. Find $h$ if $(g+1)!-(y+z)=g!+h$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} With $x=28$ the side lengths are $23$, $24$ and $25$. The semiperimeter is
\[s=\dfrac{23+24+25}{2}=36,\]
so by Heron's formula the area $K$ satisfies
\[K^2=36\cdot 13\cdot 12\cdot 11=61776=144\cdot 429,\qquad K=12\sqrt{429}.\]
An altitude equals $\dfrac{2K}{\text{side}}$, so the longest altitude is the one drawn to the shortest side, of length $23$:
\[\dfrac{2K}{23}=\dfrac{24\sqrt{429}}{23}.\]
Here $\gcd(24,23)=1$ and $429=3\cdot 11\cdot 13$ is squarefree, so
\[y=24+23+429=476.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real ax=240/23, ay=24*sqrt(429)/23;
pair A=(0,0), B=(23,0), C=(ax,ay), H=(ax,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(C--H,red+dashed+linewidth(0.8));
draw(H+(0,0.9)--H+(0.9,0.9)--H+(0.9,0),linewidth(0.6));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(H);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N); label("$H$",H,S);
label("$23$",(6,0),S);
label("$24$",(A+C)/2,NW);
label("$25$",(B+C)/2,NE);
label("$\dfrac{24\sqrt{429}}{23}$",(ax+0.5,ay/5),E);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} With $x=28$ the side lengths are $31$, $32$ and $33$, and $s=48$. Then
\[K^2=48\cdot 17\cdot 16\cdot 15=195840=2304\cdot 85,\qquad K=48\sqrt{85}.\]
The shortest side is $31$, so the longest altitude is
\[\dfrac{2K}{31}=\dfrac{96\sqrt{85}}{31},\qquad \gcd(96,31)=1,\quad 85=5\cdot 17,\]
and therefore $z=96+31+85=212$.

\textbf{(c)} Now $y+z=476+212=688$, so
\[6!-688=720-688=32=5!+h=120+h,\]
which gives $h=\boxed{-88}$.

We use the law of cosines in the form $\cos A=\dfrac{b^2+c^2-a^2}{2bc}$, and similarly for $B$ and $C$. Then
\[ab^2\cos A=ab^2\cdot\dfrac{b^2+c^2-a^2}{2bc}=\dfrac{ab\,(b^2+c^2-a^2)}{2c},\]
\[bc^2\cos B=bc^2\cdot\dfrac{a^2+c^2-b^2}{2ac}=\dfrac{bc\,(a^2+c^2-b^2)}{2a}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(5,0), C=(1.7,3.3);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N);
label("$c$",(A+B)/2,S);
label("$b$",(A+C)/2,NW);
label("$a$",(B+C)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 1: comparing the first two quantities.} From $ab^2\cos A=bc^2\cos B$, multiply both sides by $\dfrac{2ac}{b}$ (all of $a,b,c>0$):
\[a^2(b^2+c^2-a^2)=c^2(a^2+c^2-b^2).\]
Expanding and cancelling $a^2c^2$,
\[a^2b^2-a^4=c^4-b^2c^2,\qquad\text{that is,}\qquad b^2(a^2+c^2)=a^4+c^4. \tag{1}\]

\textbf{Step 2: comparing the first and third quantities.} From $ab^2\cos A=ca^2\cos C$ we get
\[\dfrac{ab\,(b^2+c^2-a^2)}{2c}=\dfrac{ca\,(a^2+b^2-c^2)}{2b}.\]
Multiplying by $\dfrac{2}{a}$ gives $b^2(b^2+c^2-a^2)=c^2(a^2+b^2-c^2)$, which after cancelling $b^2c^2$ becomes
\[a^2(b^2+c^2)=b^4+c^4. \tag{2}\]

\textbf{Step 3: comparing the second and third quantities.} The same computation with $bc^2\cos B=ca^2\cos C$ gives
\[b^2(a^2+c^2-b^2)=a^2(a^2+b^2-c^2),\qquad\text{that is,}\qquad c^2(a^2+b^2)=a^4+b^4. \tag{3}\]

\textbf{Step 4: concluding.} Subtracting (3) from (1),
\[b^2a^2+b^2c^2-c^2a^2-c^2b^2=a^4+c^4-a^4-b^4\ \Longrightarrow\ a^2(b^2-c^2)=(c^2-b^2)(c^2+b^2),\]
so $(b^2-c^2)(a^2+b^2+c^2)=0$. Since $a^2+b^2+c^2>0$, we get $b=c$.

Subtracting (2) from (1),
\[b^2a^2+b^2c^2-a^2b^2-a^2c^2=a^4+c^4-b^4-c^4\ \Longrightarrow\ c^2(b^2-a^2)=(a^2-b^2)(a^2+b^2),\]
so $(b^2-a^2)(a^2+b^2+c^2)=0$, hence $a=b$.

Therefore $a=b=c$, i.e. triangle $ABC$ is equilateral.

The only thing that matters is how long the dragonfly is airborne: it flies at constant speed, so its distance is its speed times its flying time.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(60,0), C=(60,60), D=(0,60), O=(30,30);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--O,dashed+gray); draw(B--O,dashed+gray);
draw(C--O,dashed+gray); draw(D--O,dashed+gray);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(O);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$O$",(33,30),E);
label("$60$",(A+B)/2,S);
label("$60$",(B+C)/2,E);
label("$30\sqrt{2}$",(A+O)/2,NW);
\end{asy}

Each car travels along a diagonal from its corner to the centre $O$, a distance equal to half a diagonal of the square, that is
\[\dfrac{60\sqrt{2}}{2}=30\sqrt{2}\ \text{miles}.\]
At $30$ miles per hour a car covers this in
\[\dfrac{30\sqrt{2}}{30}=\sqrt{2}\ \text{hours}.\]
All four cars start together and travel the same distance at the same speed, so they meet at the centre after exactly $\sqrt{2}$ hours, and the dragonfly flies the whole time.

Since it flies at $60$ miles per hour, it travels
\[60\sqrt{2}\ \text{miles},\]
that is $\boxed{60\sqrt{2}}$ miles (about $84.9$ miles). The exact route is irrelevant: the length of the flight depends only on its duration and speed.

The vertex form of a parabola with vertex $(-1,2)$ is
\[ y=a(x+1)^2+2. \]
Substituting $(1,3)$ gives
\[ 3=a(1+1)^2+2=4a+2, \]
so $a=\dfrac{1}{4}$. Thus
\[ y=\dfrac{1}{4}(x+1)^2+2
=\dfrac{1}{4}(x^2+2x+1)+2
=\dfrac{1}{4}x^2+\dfrac{1}{2}x+\dfrac{9}{4}. \]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
pair V=(-1,2), Q=(1,3);
draw((-3,0)--(2.5,0),Arrow);
draw((0,-0.4)--(0,5),Arrow);
real f(real x){return 0.25*(x+1)^2+2;}
draw(graph(f,-3,2.2),blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(V); dot(Q);
label("$(-1,2)$",V,SW);
label("$(1,3)$",Q,NE);
label("$x$",(2.5,0),E);
label("$y$",(0,5),N);
\end{asy}

The sketch shows the vertex and the given point. Therefore
\[ \boxed{y=\dfrac{1}{4}x^2+\dfrac{1}{2}x+\dfrac{9}{4}}. \]

The circumference in meters is
\[ 2\pi(5)=10\pi, \]
so in centimeters it is $1000\pi$. Three rounds cover
\[ 3\cdot 1000\pi=3000\pi \]
centimeters. At $5\pi$ centimeters per minute, the moving time is
\[ \dfrac{3000\pi}{5\pi}=600 \]
minutes.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
real r=2;
draw(scale(r)*unitcircle,linewidth(0.9));
pair A=(r,0), B=(0,r), C=(-r,0), D=(0,-r);
dot(O); dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D);
draw(O--A,linewidth(0.7));
label("$5$ m",O--A,NE);
label("$O$",O,SW);
label("start",A,E);
label("rest",B,N);
label("rest",C,W);
label("rest",D,S);
\end{asy}

Three rounds consist of $12$ quarter-circles. The ant rests after each of the first $11$ quarters and then finishes during the twelfth quarter, so it rests $11$ times. The rest time is $11\cdot 3=33$ minutes.

Thus the total time is
\[ 600+33=\boxed{633}\text{ minutes}. \]

Count the complement: positive integers from $1$ to $2020$ with no digit $7$.

For $1$ to $1999$, pad each number to four digits with leading zeros. The thousands digit is either $0$ or $1$. Each of the remaining three digits can be any of the $9$ digits other than $7$. This gives $2\cdot 9^3=1458$ strings, but the string $0000$ represents $0$, not a positive integer. So there are $1458-1=1457$ such integers from $1$ to $1999$.

For $2000$ to $2020$:
\begin{itemize}
\item from $2000$ to $2009$, only $2007$ contains a $7$, leaving $9$ numbers;
\item from $2010$ to $2019$, only $2017$ contains a $7$, leaving $9$ numbers;
\item $2020$ contains no $7$, giving $1$ more.
\end{itemize}
So there are $9+9+1=19$ integers from $2000$ to $2020$ with no digit $7$.

Thus there are $1457+19=1476$ integers from $1$ to $2020$ with no digit $7$. Since there are $2020$ positive integers in all, the number containing at least one $7$ is
\[ 2020-1476=\boxed{544}. \]

Let $K$ be the area and $s$ the semiperimeter. Here $s=\dfrac{15+16+17}{2}=24$, so by Heron's formula
\[K=\sqrt{24\cdot(24-15)\cdot(24-16)\cdot(24-17)}=\sqrt{24\cdot 9\cdot 8\cdot 7}=\sqrt{12096}.\]
Since $12096=576\cdot 21=24^2\cdot 21$, we get $K=24\sqrt{21}$.

If $h_x$ denotes the altitude to the side of length $x$, then $K=\dfrac{1}{2}x\,h_x$, so $h_x=\dfrac{2K}{x}$. Thus the altitude is smaller for the longer side, and the shortest altitude is the one drawn to the longest side, $17$:
\[h=\dfrac{2\cdot 24\sqrt{21}}{17}=\dfrac{48\sqrt{21}}{17}.\]
So $a=48$, $b=17$ and $c=21$ (indeed $17$ and $21=3\cdot 7$ have no square divisor other than $1$, and $\gcd(48,17)=1$), and
\[a+b+c=48+17+21=86.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(17,0);
pair C=(160/17, 48*sqrt(21)/17);
pair D=(160/17,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(C--D,dashed+linewidth(0.7));
draw((D+(0.6,0))--(D+(0.6,0.6))--(D+(0,0.6)),linewidth(0.5));
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N); label("$D$",D,SE);
label("$16$",(A+C)/2,NW); label("$15$",(B+C)/2,NE); label("$17$",(7,0),S);
label("$h$",(C+D)/2,E);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D);
\end{asy}

The dashed segment is the altitude $h$ to the side of length $17$, marked with a right angle at $D$. Hence the requested sum is
\[\boxed{86}.\]

Let the quadrilateral be $ABCD$ and let the circle have centre $O$ and radius $r=2$. Tangent segments from the same exterior point to a circle have equal lengths. Write $a,b,c,d$ for the lengths of the two equal tangent segments drawn from $A,B,C,D$ respectively (for instance the two segments from $A$ to its two points of tangency both have length $a$). Then
\[AB=a+b,\quad BC=b+c,\quad CD=c+d,\quad DA=d+a .\]
Hence
\[AB+CD=(a+b)+(c+d)=(b+c)+(d+a)=BC+DA ,\]
so the two sums of opposite sides are equal. If one pair of opposite sides has total $12$ cm, then the other pair does too, and the perimeter of $ABCD$ is $12+12=24$ cm. Its semiperimeter is $s=12$ cm.

Join $O$ to the four vertices; this splits the quadrilateral into four triangles, one on each side, and the height of each of these triangles to its base (a side of $ABCD$) equals the radius $r=2$, because the side is tangent to the circle. Therefore
\[[ABCD]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot r\cdot(AB+BC+CD+DA)=r\cdot s=2\cdot 12=24 .\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=2;
pair vtx(real a, real b){return (r*(sin(b)-sin(a))/sin(b-a), r*(cos(a)-cos(b))/sin(b-a));}
real a1=0, a2=1.75, a3=3.32, a4=5.06;
pair V1=vtx(a1,a2), V2=vtx(a2,a3), V3=vtx(a3,a4), V4=vtx(a4,a1);
pair T=(r,0);
draw(V1--V2--V3--V4--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle((0,0),r),linewidth(0.7));
draw((0,0)--T,dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw((T+(-0.45,0))--(T+(-0.45,0.45))--(T+(0,0.45)),linewidth(0.5));
dot((0,0)); dot(T);
label("$O$",(-0.1,0.6),N);
label("$2$",(1.0,-0.35),S);
label("$A$",V1,NE); label("$B$",V2,NW); label("$C$",V3,SW); label("$D$",V4,SE);
\end{asy}

The dashed segment is the radius to the point of tangency on side $DA$, perpendicular to that side.
\[\boxed{24}\]

Let the side lengths be positive integers $x$ and $y$. Since the perimeter is $50$,
\[
2x+2y=50 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad x+y=25.
\]
The area is $xy$. Because $x\ge 1$ and $y\ge 1$,
\[
(x-1)(y-1)\ge 0,
\]
so
\[
xy\ge x+y-1=25-1=24.
\]
Equality is attained when one side is $1$ and the other is $24$, which is allowed and gives area $24$.


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(5,0), C=(5,3), D=(0,3);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
label("$x$",(A+B)/2,S);
label("$25-x$",(B+C)/2,E);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D);
\end{asy}


Thus the least possible area is $\boxed{24}$ square units.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many integers from $1$ to $1000$ inclusive contain at least one digit $7$?
\item[(b)] Let $p$ be an odd prime and let $N=2^p+7^p$. What is the remainder when $N$ is divided by $3$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Think of moving from $P$ to $S$ along the broken path $P\to Q\to R\to S$. The first step has length $10$, while the next two steps together have total length at most $4+3=7$. Thus by the triangle inequality the net displacement from $P$ to $S$ has length at least
\[
10-(4+3)=3.
\]
This lower bound is attained when $Q$, $R$, and $S$ all lie on the segment from $P$ to $Q$, with the steps of lengths $4$ and $3$ folding back toward $P$ along that same line.


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Pp=(0,0), Ss=(3,0), Rr=(6,0), Qq=(10,0);
draw(Pp--Qq,linewidth(0.9));
draw(Pp--Ss,red+linewidth(1.2));
dot(Pp); dot(Ss); dot(Rr); dot(Qq);
label("$P$",Pp,SW);
label("$S$",Ss,S);
label("$R$",Rr,NW);
label("$Q$",Qq,SE);
label("$3$",(Pp+Ss)/2,N);
label("$3$",(Ss+Rr)/2,N);
label("$4$",(Rr+Qq)/2,N);
label("$10$",(4.6,0),S);
\end{asy}


In that position $PS=10-4-3=3$. Hence the minimum possible distance is
\[
\boxed{3}.
\]

Let $O$ be the origin. For two points $X$ and $Y$ on the unit circle, the circle with diameter $XY$ contains $O$ in its interior exactly when the central angle $\angle XOY$ is obtuse, equivalently when
\[
X\cdot Y<0.
\]
Indeed, the disk with diameter $XY$ is the set of points $P$ satisfying $(P-X)\cdot(P-Y)\le 0$, and for $P=O$ this becomes $X\cdot Y\le 0$.

So we want the probability that at least one pair among $A,B,C$ has central angle greater than $90^\circ$.


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
draw(circle(O,1),linewidth(0.8));
real a=120, b=10, c=250;
pair A=(cos(pi*a/180), sin(pi*a/180));
pair B=(cos(pi*b/180), sin(pi*b/180));
pair C=(cos(pi*c/180), sin(pi*c/180));
pair Mab=(A+B)/2, Mbc=(B+C)/2, Mca=(C+A)/2;
real rAB=sqrt((A.x-B.x)^2+(A.y-B.y)^2);
real rBC=sqrt((B.x-C.x)^2+(B.y-C.y)^2);
real rCA=sqrt((C.x-A.x)^2+(C.y-A.y)^2);
draw(circle(Mab,rAB/2),red+linewidth(0.6));
draw(circle(Mbc,rBC/2),red+linewidth(0.6));
draw(circle(Mca,rCA/2),red+linewidth(0.6));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(O);
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$O$",O,S);
\end{asy}


We compute the complementary probability: no pair has central angle greater than $90^\circ$. We claim this happens exactly when all three points lie on some arc of length $90^\circ$. One direction is immediate. For the other, order the points around the circle and let the three gaps between consecutive points be $g_1,g_2,g_3$, with sum $360^\circ$. If all gaps were at most $180^\circ$, then the angular separation of each consecutive pair would equal one of the gaps; since no pair is separated by more than $90^\circ$, all gaps would be at most $90^\circ$, forcing their sum to be at most $270^\circ$, impossible. Hence some gap exceeds $180^\circ$. For the two endpoints of that gap, the smaller central angle is $360^\circ$ minus the gap; since it is at most $90^\circ$, the gap is at least $270^\circ$. Thus the other arc between them has length at most $90^\circ$ and contains all three points.


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
draw(circle(O,1),linewidth(0.8));
draw(O--(1,0),linewidth(0.6));
draw(O--(0,1),linewidth(0.6));
draw(arc(O,1,0,90),red+linewidth(1.2));
real a=5, b=40, c=80;
pair A=(cos(pi*a/180), sin(pi*a/180));
pair B=(cos(pi*b/180), sin(pi*b/180));
pair C=(cos(pi*c/180), sin(pi*c/180));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(O);
label("$A$",A,E); label("$B$",B,NE); label("$C$",C,NW); label("$O$",O,S);
label("$90^\circ$",(0.3,0.3),SW);
\end{asy}


Now count these configurations. For each of the three points $P$, let $E_P$ be the event that the other two points lie in the $90^\circ$ arc beginning at $P$ and running counterclockwise. If all three points lie in some $90^\circ$ arc, then exactly one of the events $E_P$ occurs, namely the one for the first of the three points in that arc (boundary cases have probability $0$). Conversely, if $E_P$ occurs, then all three points lie in that $90^\circ$ arc. Thus the complement is the union of the three disjoint events $E_P$.

For a fixed $P$, each of the other two points independently has probability $\dfrac{1}{4}$ of lying in the specified $90^\circ$ arc, so
\[
\Pr(E_P)=\left(\dfrac{1}{4}\right)^2=\dfrac{1}{16}.
\]
Therefore the complementary probability is
\[
3\cdot\dfrac{1}{16}=\dfrac{3}{16}.
\]
Hence the desired probability is
\[
1-\dfrac{3}{16}=\boxed{\dfrac{13}{16}}.\]

question:
A \emph{proper divisor} of a positive integer $n$ is a positive divisor of $n$ other than $n$ itself.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the sum of all the proper divisors of $120$.
\item[(b)] Explain a general method, using the prime factorisation of $n$, for computing the sum of all the divisors of $n$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $a>b$ be real numbers.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $a^2>b^2$ if and only if $a+b>0$.
\item[(b)] For each pair below, decide whether squaring both sides of $a>b$ keeps the inequality in the same direction or reverses it: (i) $a=2$, $b=-1$; (ii) $a=\dfrac{1}{2}$, $b=-1$; (iii) $a=-1$, $b=-2$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $a>b\ge 0$ be real numbers.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $\sqrt{a}>\sqrt{b}$.
\item[(b)] Using (a), explain why taking the square root of both sides of an inequality between two nonnegative numbers never reverses the inequality, and give an example showing why the condition $b\ge 0$ cannot be dropped.
\end{enumerate}

A student proves that $\dfrac{x+y}{2}\ge\sqrt{xy}$ holds for all real numbers $x$ and $y$ like this:
\[(x-y)^2\ge 0\ \Longrightarrow\ x^2-2xy+y^2\ge 0\ \Longrightarrow\ x^2+2xy+y^2\ge 4xy\ \Longrightarrow\ \left(\dfrac{x+y}{2}\right)^2\ge xy\ \Longrightarrow\ \dfrac{x+y}{2}\ge\sqrt{xy}.\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the claimed inequality is false for $x=y=-1$.
\item[(b)] Find the exact condition on the real numbers $x$ and $y$ under which the last step of the argument is valid, and prove that the inequality really does hold for every pair satisfying that condition.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Think of $(x,y,z)$ as a point chosen uniformly at random in the unit cube $0<x,y,z<1$, which has volume $1$. The favourable outcomes form the region
\[R=\{(x,y,z):\ 0<x,y,z<1,\ x+y+z<1\}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair pr(real x, real y, real z){return (x-0.42*z, y+0.30*z);}
pair O=pr(0,0,0); pair X=pr(1,0,0); pair Y=pr(0,1,0); pair Z=pr(0,0,1);
pair P110=pr(1,1,0); pair P101=pr(1,0,1); pair P011=pr(0,1,1); pair P111=pr(1,1,1);
fill(O--X--Y--cycle, rgb(0.86,0.88,0.93));
fill(O--Y--Z--cycle, rgb(0.86,0.88,0.93));
fill(O--X--Z--cycle, rgb(0.76,0.86,0.98));
fill(X--Y--Z--cycle, rgb(0.99,0.90,0.72));
draw(O--X,linewidth(0.6)); draw(X--P110,linewidth(0.6)); draw(P110--P111,linewidth(0.6));
draw(P111--P011,linewidth(0.6)); draw(P011--Z,linewidth(0.6)); draw(Z--O,linewidth(0.6));
draw(Z--P101,linewidth(0.6)); draw(P101--P111,linewidth(0.6)); draw(X--P101,linewidth(0.6));
draw(Y--O,dashed+linewidth(0.6)); draw(Y--P110,dashed+linewidth(0.6)); draw(Y--P011,dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(O--Z,linewidth(1.1)); draw(X--Z,linewidth(1.1)); draw(Y--Z,dashed+linewidth(1.0));
draw(O--X,linewidth(1.1)); draw(Y--O,dashed+linewidth(1.0)); draw(X--Y,dashed+linewidth(1.0));
dot(O); dot(X); dot(Y); dot(Z);
label("$O$",O,SW,fontsize(9pt));
label("$X$",X,SE,fontsize(9pt));
label("$Y$",Y,NW,fontsize(9pt));
label("$Z$",Z,W,fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{The region is a tetrahedron.} If $x,y,z>0$ and $x+y+z<1$, then each of $x,y,z$ is less than $1$, so $R$ lies inside the cube, and inside the cube it is cut out by the single plane $x+y+z=1$. A point $(x,y,z)$ with $x,y,z\ge0$ and $x+y+z\le1$ can be written as
\[(x,y,z)=x\,(1,0,0)+y\,(0,1,0)+z\,(0,0,1)+(1-x-y-z)\,(0,0,0),\]
a convex combination of the four points $O=(0,0,0)$, $X=(1,0,0)$, $Y=(0,1,0)$, $Z=(0,0,1)$ with nonnegative coefficients summing to $1$. Conversely, the set
\[S=\{(x,y,z):\ x\ge0,\ y\ge0,\ z\ge0,\ x+y+z\le1\}\]
is an intersection of half-spaces, hence convex, and it contains these four vertices, so it contains their convex hull, the tetrahedron $OXYZ$. Hence the part of the cube on the origin side of the plane is exactly the tetrahedron $OXYZ$, which is the cut-off corner drawn above.

\textbf{Its volume.} Take the face $OXY$, which lies in the plane $z=0$, as base: it is a right triangle with legs $1$ and $1$, so its area is $\dfrac{1}{2}$. The height from $Z$ to that plane is $1$. Hence
\[V=\dfrac{1}{3}\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot 1=\dfrac{1}{6}.\]
The boundary of $R$ (where a coordinate equals $0$ or where $x+y+z=1$) has volume $0$ and does not affect the probability. Therefore
\[P=\dfrac{\text{volume of }R}{\text{volume of the cube}}=\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{6}}{1}=\boxed{\dfrac{1}{6}}.\]

Write $S=\{(x,y,z):\ x\ge0,\ y\ge0,\ z\ge0,\ x+y+z\le1\}$. We show $T=S$, where $T$ is the convex hull of $O,X,Y,Z$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair pr(real x, real y, real z){return (x-0.42*z, y+0.30*z);}
pair O=pr(0,0,0); pair X=pr(1,0,0); pair Y=pr(0,1,0); pair Z=pr(0,0,1);
pair P110=pr(1,1,0); pair P101=pr(1,0,1); pair P011=pr(0,1,1); pair P111=pr(1,1,1);
fill(O--X--Y--cycle, rgb(0.86,0.88,0.93));
fill(O--Y--Z--cycle, rgb(0.86,0.88,0.93));
fill(O--X--Z--cycle, rgb(0.76,0.86,0.98));
fill(X--Y--Z--cycle, rgb(0.99,0.90,0.72));
draw(O--X,linewidth(0.6)); draw(X--P110,linewidth(0.6)); draw(P110--P111,linewidth(0.6));
draw(P111--P011,linewidth(0.6)); draw(P011--Z,linewidth(0.6)); draw(Z--O,linewidth(0.6));
draw(Z--P101,linewidth(0.6)); draw(P101--P111,linewidth(0.6)); draw(X--P101,linewidth(0.6));
draw(Y--O,dashed+linewidth(0.6)); draw(Y--P110,dashed+linewidth(0.6)); draw(Y--P011,dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(O--Z,linewidth(1.1)); draw(X--Z,linewidth(1.1)); draw(Y--Z,dashed+linewidth(1.0));
draw(O--X,linewidth(1.1)); draw(Y--O,dashed+linewidth(1.0)); draw(X--Y,dashed+linewidth(1.0));
dot(O); dot(X); dot(Y); dot(Z);
label("$O$",O,SW,fontsize(9pt));
label("$X$",X,SE,fontsize(9pt));
label("$Y$",Y,NW,fontsize(9pt));
label("$Z$",Z,W,fontsize(9pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{$S\subseteq T$.} Let $(x,y,z)\in S$ and put $w=1-x-y-z\ge0$. Then
\[(x,y,z)=x\,(1,0,0)+y\,(0,1,0)+z\,(0,0,1)+w\,(0,0,0),\]
and $x+y+z+w=1$ with all four coefficients nonnegative. So $(x,y,z)$ is a convex combination of $O,X,Y,Z$ and hence lies in $T$.

\textbf{$T\subseteq S$.} The four vertices $O,X,Y,Z$ all satisfy the four inequalities, and $S$ is convex: if $(x_1,y_1,z_1)$ and $(x_2,y_2,z_2)$ satisfy them and $0\le t\le1$, then every coordinate of $t(x_1,y_1,z_1)+(1-t)(x_2,y_2,z_2)$ is a combination of nonnegative numbers with nonnegative coefficients, and the sum of the coordinates equals
\[t(x_1+y_1+z_1)+(1-t)(x_2+y_2+z_2)\le t+(1-t)=1.\]
Since $S$ is convex and contains the four vertices, it contains their convex hull $T$.

Hence $T=S$: a point is in the tetrahedron exactly when all three coordinates are nonnegative and their sum is at most $1$. In particular, a point of this tetrahedron satisfies the strict condition $x+y+z<1$ unless it lies on the slant face $XYZ$, which has zero volume.

