A rectangular sheet of cardboard measures $6$ inches by $3$ inches. A square of side length $x$ inches is cut from each corner, and the four remaining flaps are folded up along the edges of the central rectangle and glued together, producing an open-top box.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Write the volume $V$ of the box as a function of $x$, and state the interval of values of $x$ for which such a box really exists.
\item[(b)] Find the value of $x$ that maximises $V$, and find that largest volume.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=0.8, L=6, H=3;
filldraw((0,0)--(s,0)--(s,s)--(0,s)--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.85,0.85)+linewidth(0.6));
filldraw((L-s,0)--(L,0)--(L,s)--(L-s,s)--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.85,0.85)+linewidth(0.6));
filldraw((0,H-s)--(s,H-s)--(s,H)--(0,H)--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.85,0.85)+linewidth(0.6));
filldraw((L-s,H-s)--(L,H-s)--(L,H)--(L-s,H)--cycle,rgb(0.85,0.85,0.85)+linewidth(0.6));
draw((0,0)--(L,0)--(L,H)--(0,H)--cycle,linewidth(1.0));
draw((s,s)--(L-s,s)--(L-s,H-s)--(s,H-s)--cycle,dashed+linewidth(0.7));
label("$x$",(s/2,s/2));
label("$6$",(L/2,0),S);
label("$3$",(0,H/2),W);
\end{asy}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} After the four corner squares of side $x$ are removed, the central rectangle that becomes the bottom of the box measures $6-2x$ by $3-2x$ inches, and the flaps that become the walls have height $x$ inches. Hence
\[V(x)=x(6-2x)(3-2x)=4x^3-18x^2+18x.\]
A genuine box needs both base dimensions positive: $6-2x>0$ and $3-2x>0$, i.e. $0<x<\dfrac{3}{2}$. (At $x=0$ and at $x=\dfrac{3}{2}$ the volume is $0$, so those endpoints are excluded anyway.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real wd=4.4, hz=1.0;
pair A=(0,0), B=(wd,0), C=(wd+0.9,0.7), D=(0.9,0.7);
pair A2=A+(0,hz), B2=B+(0,hz), C2=C+(0,hz), D2=D+(0,hz);
fill(A--B--C--D--cycle,paleblue);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A2--B2--C2--D2--cycle,linewidth(1.0));
draw(A--A2,linewidth(0.8));
draw(B--B2,linewidth(0.8));
draw(C--C2,linewidth(0.8));
draw(D--D2,linewidth(0.8));
label("$6-2x$",(A+B)/2,S);
label("$3-2x$",(B+C)/2,E);
label("$x$",(A+A2)/2,W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} Put $u=3-2x$. As $x$ runs over $\left(0,\dfrac{3}{2}\right)$, $u$ runs over $(0,3)$, and $x=\dfrac{3-u}{2}$ while $6-2x=3+u$. Therefore
\[V=\dfrac{3-u}{2}\,(3+u)\,u=\dfrac{u(9-u^2)}{2}.\]
For $0<u<3$ all of $u^2$, $\dfrac{9-u^2}{2}$, $\dfrac{9-u^2}{2}$ are positive and their sum is
\[u^2+\dfrac{9-u^2}{2}+\dfrac{9-u^2}{2}=9.\]
By AM-GM applied to these three numbers,
\[u^2\cdot\dfrac{9-u^2}{2}\cdot\dfrac{9-u^2}{2}\le\left(\dfrac{9}{3}\right)^{3}=27,\]
so, since $u>0$,
\[\dfrac{u^2(9-u^2)^2}{4}\le 27 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad u(9-u^2)\le 6\sqrt3 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad V\le 3\sqrt3.\]
Equality requires $u^2=\dfrac{9-u^2}{2}$, i.e. $3u^2=9$, i.e. $u=\sqrt3$ (which lies in $(0,3)$), giving
\[x=\dfrac{3-\sqrt3}{2}\approx 0.634.\]
So the maximum volume is attained at this $x$, and it equals
\[\boxed{x=\dfrac{3-\sqrt3}{2},\qquad V_{\max}=3\sqrt3}. \]
(That is, the largest volume is $3\sqrt3$ cubic inches.)

A calculus check agrees: $V'(x)=12x^2-36x+18=6(2x^2-6x+3)$, whose roots are $\dfrac{3\pm\sqrt3}{2}$; only $\dfrac{3-\sqrt3}{2}$ lies in $\left(0,\dfrac{3}{2}\right)$, and $V$ increases before it and decreases after it.

Note that the midpoint $\dfrac{0+1.5}{2}=0.75$ of the interval between the zeros of $V$ is \emph{not} the maximiser: $V$ is a cubic, not a symmetric quadratic, and indeed $V(0.75)=5.0625<3\sqrt3\approx 5.196$.

question:
A driver claims that the average speed of a trip made in two stages at positive speeds $a$ and $b$ is always $\dfrac{2ab}{a+b}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the claim is correct when the two stages cover equal distances. If $a\neq b$, show that equal distances are the only case in which it is correct.
\item[(b)] For a trip that goes $40$ miles per hour for $1$ hour and then $50$ miles per hour for $1$ hour, compute the value the claim gives and the true average speed, and explain why the claim fails here.
\item[(c)] Find the correct general formula for the average speed when the two stages last equal times.
\end{enumerate}

The line $\ell$ with equation $y=mx+b$ is tangent to the circle $x^2+y^2=r^2$ centered at the origin $O$, where $r>0$. Prove the following three statements.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $r^2(1+m^2)=b^2$.
\item[(b)] The point of tangency is $T\left(-\dfrac{r^2m}{b},\dfrac{r^2}{b}\right)$.
\item[(c)] $\ell$ is perpendicular to the radius $OT$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} A point $(x,y)$ lies on both the line and the circle exactly when $x^2+(mx+b)^2=r^2$, that is
\[(1+m^2)x^2+2mbx+(b^2-r^2)=0.\]
This is a genuine quadratic, because $1+m^2\ge 1>0$. The line is tangent to the circle exactly when it meets the circle in exactly one point, i.e. when this quadratic has exactly one real solution, i.e. when its discriminant vanishes:
\[(2mb)^2-4(1+m^2)(b^2-r^2)=0.\]
Dividing by $4$,
\[m^2b^2=(1+m^2)(b^2-r^2)=(1+m^2)b^2-(1+m^2)r^2,\]
so
\[(1+m^2)r^2=(1+m^2)b^2-m^2b^2=b^2.\]
This proves (a). In particular $b^2=r^2(1+m^2)>0$, so $b\neq 0$, the fractions below are defined, and
\[1+m^2=\dfrac{b^2}{r^2}.\]

\textbf{(b)} When the discriminant vanishes, the unique solution of the quadratic is the double root
\[x=-\dfrac{2mb}{2(1+m^2)}=-\dfrac{mb}{1+m^2}=-\dfrac{mbr^2}{b^2}=-\dfrac{mr^2}{b}.\]
Its $y$-coordinate on the line is
\[y=mx+b=-\dfrac{m^2r^2}{b}+b=\dfrac{b^2-m^2r^2}{b}=\dfrac{r^2(1+m^2)-m^2r^2}{b}=\dfrac{r^2}{b}.\]
So the point of tangency is
\[T=\left(-\dfrac{r^2m}{b},\dfrac{r^2}{b}\right).\]
As a check, $T$ really is on the circle: using $1+m^2=\dfrac{b^2}{r^2}$,
\[x^2+y^2=\dfrac{r^4m^2}{b^2}+\dfrac{r^4}{b^2}=\dfrac{r^4(1+m^2)}{b^2}=\dfrac{r^4}{b^2}\cdot\dfrac{b^2}{r^2}=r^2.\]

\textbf{(c)} The vector from the center to the tangency point is
\[\overrightarrow{OT}=\left(-\dfrac{r^2m}{b},\dfrac{r^2}{b}\right)=\dfrac{r^2}{b}(-m,1),\]
and a direction vector of the line $\ell$ (slope $m$) is $(1,m)$. Their dot product is
\[\dfrac{r^2}{b}\big((-m)\cdot 1+1\cdot m\big)=0.\]
Since both vectors are nonzero ($T\neq O$ because $r>0$, and $(1,m)\neq(0,0)$), the radius $OT$ is perpendicular to $\ell$. This argument also covers the case $m=0$, where the tangent line is horizontal and the radius is vertical.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real m=0.5, r=1;
real b=r*sqrt(1+m*m);
pair O=(0,0), T=(-r*r*m/b, r*r/b);
pair u=(1,m)/sqrt(1+m*m);
pair v=T/r;
draw(circle(O,r),linewidth(0.8));
draw(T-2.4*u -- T+2.2*u, linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--T,dashed+linewidth(0.8));
real sq=0.13;
draw(T+sq*u -- T+sq*u-sq*v -- T-sq*v, linewidth(0.7));
dot(O); dot(T);
label("$O$",O,S);
label("$T$",T+0.22*v,NW);
label("$r$",(O+T)/2+0.16*u,E);
label("$\ell$",T+2.2*u,NE);
\end{asy}

All three statements are proved.

Let $OX=x$ and $OY=y$, and place $O=(0,0)$, $X=(x,0)$, $Y=(0,y)$. Then the midpoints are $M=\left(\dfrac{x}{2},0\right)$ and $N=\left(0,\dfrac{y}{2}\right)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real yy=sqrt(420.0);
pair O=(0,0), X=(16,0), Y=(0,yy);
pair M=(8,0), Npt=(0,yy/2);
draw(O--X--Y--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(X--Npt,red+linewidth(0.8));
draw(Y--M,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw((1.2,0)--(1.2,1.2)--(0,1.2),linewidth(0.6));
dot(O); dot(X); dot(Y); dot(M); dot(Npt);
label("$O$",O,SW); label("$X$",X,SE); label("$Y$",Y,N);
label("$M$",M,N); label("$N$",Npt,W);
label("$19$",(X+Npt)/2,E,red);
label("$22$",(Y+M)/2,W,blue);
\end{asy}

By the distance formula,
\[XN^{2}=x^{2}+\dfrac{y^{2}}{4}=19^{2}=361,\qquad YM^{2}=\dfrac{x^{2}}{4}+y^{2}=22^{2}=484.\]
Adding the two equations,
\[\dfrac{5}{4}\left(x^{2}+y^{2}\right)=361+484=845,\]
so
\[x^{2}+y^{2}=\dfrac{4\cdot 845}{5}=4\cdot 169=676,\]
and therefore
\[XY=\sqrt{x^{2}+y^{2}}=\sqrt{676}=\boxed{26}.\]
(For completeness, subtracting the first equation from the second gives $\dfrac{3}{4}\left(y^{2}-x^{2}\right)=123$, so $y^{2}-x^{2}=164$; together with $x^{2}+y^{2}=676$ this gives $x^{2}=256$ and $y^{2}=420$, hence $OX=16$ and $OY=2\sqrt{105}$, and the original data are consistent.)

Let $M$ be the midpoint of $BC$, and let
\[BC=AM=a,\qquad\text{so that } BM=CM=\dfrac{a}{2}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=sqrt(2.0);
pair A=(-1/(2*a), sqrt(7.0/8.0)), B=(0,0), C=(a,0), M=(a/2,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--M,dashed+linewidth(0.8));
label("$BC=\sqrt{2}$",(0.5*a,-0.2)); label("$AM=\sqrt{2}$",A+0.72*(M-A),E,fontsize(9pt));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(M);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$M$",M,N);
label("$1$",(A+B)/2,SW); label("$2$",(A+C)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

Place $B=\left(-\dfrac{a}{2},0\right)$, $C=\left(\dfrac{a}{2},0\right)$ and $A=(u,v)$. Since $M$ is the origin here, $AM^{2}=u^{2}+v^{2}=a^{2}$. Now
\[AB^{2}=\left(u+\dfrac{a}{2}\right)^{2}+v^{2},\qquad AC^{2}=\left(u-\dfrac{a}{2}\right)^{2}+v^{2}.\]
Adding, the cross terms cancel:
\[AB^{2}+AC^{2}=2\left(u^{2}+v^{2}\right)+\dfrac{a^{2}}{2}=2a^{2}+\dfrac{a^{2}}{2}=\dfrac{5a^{2}}{2}.\]
(This is Apollonius' theorem: $AB^{2}+AC^{2}=2AM^{2}+2BM^{2}$.) But $AB^{2}+AC^{2}=1+4=5$, so
\[\dfrac{5a^{2}}{2}=5\ \Longrightarrow\ a^{2}=2\ \Longrightarrow\ a=\sqrt{2},\]
the positive root being the only one that makes sense for a length. The value is admissible, since $1+\sqrt{2}>2$, $\sqrt{2}+2>1$ and $1+2>\sqrt{2}$.

Hence $BC=\boxed{\sqrt{2}}$.

Since $P$ lies inside the convex quadrilateral, the four triangles $PAB$, $PBC$, $PCD$, $PDA$ partition $ABCD$, and the four angles $\angle APB$, $\angle BPC$, $\angle CPD$, $\angle DPA$ add up to $360^\circ$. Write them as $\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta$. Then
\[12=\left[ABCD\right]=\dfrac{1}{2}\left(PA\cdot PB\sin\alpha+PB\cdot PC\sin\beta+PC\cdot PD\sin\gamma+PD\cdot PA\sin\delta\right),\]
that is,
\[12=\dfrac{1}{2}\left(2\sin\alpha+6\sin\beta+12\sin\gamma+4\sin\delta\right).\]
Each sine is at most $1$, so
\[12\le \dfrac{1}{2}\left(2+6+12+4\right)=\dfrac{24}{2}=12.\]
The bound is attained exactly, so every sine must equal $1$: $\sin\alpha=\sin\beta=\sin\gamma=\sin\delta=1$. Because $P$ is interior to a convex quadrilateral, each of these four angles lies strictly between $0^\circ$ and $180^\circ$, so each equals $90^\circ$.


