Now each side is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs two of the given distances:
\[AB=\sqrt{PA^{2}+PB^{2}}=\sqrt{1+4}=\sqrt{5},\qquad BC=\sqrt{PB^{2}+PC^{2}}=\sqrt{4+9}=\sqrt{13},\]
\[CD=\sqrt{PC^{2}+PD^{2}}=\sqrt{9+16}=5,\qquad DA=\sqrt{PD^{2}+PA^{2}}=\sqrt{16+1}=\sqrt{17}.\]
Such a quadrilateral really exists (take $P$ at the origin and $A,B,C,D$ on the four coordinate rays at distances $1,2,3,4$; the vertices are in cyclic order around $P$, so the quadrilateral is convex, the four right triangles give area $\dfrac{1}{2}(2+6+12+4)=12$, and $P$ is interior). Therefore the perimeter is
\[AB+BC+CD+DA=\boxed{5+\sqrt{5}+\sqrt{13}+\sqrt{17}}.\]
(The numerical value is about $14.97$.)

Let
\[
a=[\triangle BCP],\qquad b=[\triangle CAP],\qquad c=[\triangle ABP].
\]
These three areas add up to $[ABC]$, and the required condition is $a>c>b$.

In an equilateral triangle, $[\triangle ABP]=[\triangle ACP]$ exactly when $P$ lies on the median from $A$, because the two triangles have equal bases $AB=AC$ and their heights are the distances from $P$ to $AB$ and $AC$. The inequality $c>b$ selects the side of this median containing $C$. Similarly, $c=a$ exactly on the median from $B$, and $c<a$ selects the side containing $A$. Therefore the favorable region is the triangle with vertices $A$, the centroid $G$, and the midpoint $M$ of $AC$.

