\]

question:
A camera battery pack lasts $2$ hours of recording or $3$ hours of playback. A student solves the problem ``record a video, then watch the whole video with one battery pack'' as follows.

\textit{Recording uses $\dfrac{1}{2}$ of a battery pack per hour and playback uses $\dfrac{1}{3}$ of a battery pack per hour. By the harmonic mean formula, when half of the time is spent recording and half playing back, the average rate is}
\[\dfrac{2\cdot \dfrac{1}{2}\cdot \dfrac{1}{3}}{\dfrac{1}{2}+\dfrac{1}{3}}=\dfrac{2}{5}\]
\textit{battery packs per hour, so one battery pack lasts $\dfrac{5}{2}$ hours; half of that is $75$ minutes, so a $75$-minute video can be recorded and watched.}

Find the error in this argument, and explain why the harmonic mean of $2$ and $3$ (hours per battery pack) does give the correct answer.

Let $x$ be an integer.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find all integers $x$ such that $2\equiv x\pmod{22}$.
\item[(b)] What is the remainder when $2$ is divided by $22$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Reduce each term modulo $23$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $121=23\cdot 5+6$, so $121\equiv 6$;
\item $939=23\cdot 40+19$, so $939\equiv 19$;
\item $94=23\cdot 4+2$, so $-94\equiv -2$;
\item $938=23\cdot 40+18$, so $938\equiv 18$;
\item $9^2=81=23\cdot 3+12$, so $9^2\equiv 12$;
\item $23\cdot 2\equiv 0$.
\end{itemize}
Adding the congruences,
\[S\equiv 6+19-2+18+12+0=53\equiv 53-46=7\pmod{23}.\]
As a check, $S=121+939-94+938+81+46=2031=23\cdot 88+7$. Since $0\le 7<23$, the remainder is $\boxed{7}$.

The probability is $0$: a quadrilateral whose vertices come from a continuous random choice is cyclic with probability zero, even though cyclic quadrilaterals certainly exist.

Here is the precise argument. Condition on the first three chosen points $P,Q,R$. With probability $1$ they are distinct and not collinear, and then they determine exactly one circle $\Gamma$; the exceptional case (collinear $P,Q,R$) has probability $0$ and is harmless, since then no circle arises at all. The fourth point $S$ is chosen uniformly from the square, independently of the others. Given $P,Q,R$, the positions of $S$ for which $P,Q,R,S$ lie on a common circle are exactly the points of $\Gamma$, a set of area $0$, so

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real rr=1.4;
pair O=(2,1.8);
pair A=(0,0), B=(4,0), C=(4,4), D=(0,4);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,NW);
pair P1=O+rr*dir(30);
pair P2=O+rr*dir(120);
pair P3=O+rr*dir(210);
pair P4=(3.0,1.0);
draw(shift(O)*scale(rr)*unitcircle,dashed+linewidth(0.7));
draw(P1--P2--P3--P4--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
dot(P1); dot(P2); dot(P3); dot(P4);
label("$P$",P1,NE); label("$Q$",P2,NW); label("$R$",P3,SW); label("$S$",P4,SE);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,(0,-1));
\end{asy}

\[\Pr\big(S\in\Gamma\big)=\dfrac{\text{area of }\Gamma\cap\text{ square}}{\text{area of square}}=0 .\]
By the law of total probability the unconditional probability is the average of these conditional probabilities:
\[\Pr(\text{the four points are concyclic})=\int\Pr\big(S\in\Gamma\mid P,Q,R\big)\,dP\,dQ\,dR=\int 0=0 .\]

The same reasoning can be phrased algebraically: for fixed $P,Q,R$ the condition on $S=(x,y)$ is the single equation
\[\det\begin{pmatrix} x & y & x^2+y^2 & 1\\ x_P & y_P & x_P^2+y_P^2 & 1\\ x_Q & y_Q & x_Q^2+y_Q^2 & 1\\ x_R & y_R & x_R^2+y_R^2 & 1\end{pmatrix}=0,\]
which is a quadratic equation in $(x,y)$ whose solution set is a circle or a line, both of area $0$. One equation in two unknowns never removes a set of positive area, so a uniformly chosen point never satisfies it. Hence
\[\boxed{0}\]
is the probability that the four points are concyclic.

Because the triangle is isosceles right with right angle at $B$, its legs are equal:
\[AB=BC=32\sqrt{2}.\]
Thus
\[AC=\sqrt{(32\sqrt{2})^2+(32\sqrt{2})^2}=\sqrt{2048+2048}=\sqrt{4096}=64.\]

