question:
Triangle $PQR$ has a right angle at $Q$, with $PQ=3$ and $QR=4$. Points $S$, $T$, $U$ lie on sides $PQ$, $PR$, $QR$ respectively, in such a way that $QSTU$ is a square. Find the length $ST$, expressed as a common fraction.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real u=1.35;
pair qa=(0,0), pa=(3*u,0), ra=(0,4*u);
real h=12.0/7*u;
pair sa=(h,0), ta=(h,h), ua=(0,h);
fill(qa--pa--ra--cycle,paleblue);
draw(qa--pa--ra--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(sa--ta--ua,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0.20)--(0.20,0.20)--(0.20,0),linewidth(0.6));
label("$Q$",qa,SW); label("$P$",pa,SE); label("$R$",ra,N);
label("$S$",sa,S); label("$T$",ta,NE); label("$U$",ua,W);
label("$3$",qa+0.88*(pa-qa),S);
label("$4$",qa+0.18*(ra-qa),W);
dot(qa); dot(pa); dot(ra); dot(sa); dot(ta); dot(ua);
\end{asy}

solution:
Let $x=ST$ be the side of the square. Since the square has a vertex at the right angle $Q$, its sides $QS$ and $QU$ lie along the legs $PQ$ and $QR$, so $QS=QU=ST=TU=x$ and $PS=PQ-QS=3-x$.


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real u=1.35;
pair qa=(0,0), pa=(3*u,0), ra=(0,4*u);
real h=12.0/7*u;
pair sa=(h,0), ta=(h,h), ua=(0,h);
fill(qa--pa--ra--cycle,paleblue);
draw(qa--pa--ra--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(sa--ta--ua,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0.20)--(0.20,0.20)--(0.20,0),linewidth(0.6));
draw((h,0.20)--(h+0.20,0.20)--(h+0.20,0),linewidth(0.6));
label("$Q$",qa,SW); label("$P$",pa,SE); label("$R$",ra,N);
label("$S$",sa,S); label("$T$",ta,NE); label("$U$",ua,W);
label("$x$",(qa+sa)/2,S);
label("$x$",(sa+ta)/2,E);
label("$3-x$",(sa+pa)/2,S);
label("$4$",qa+0.18*(ra-qa),W);
dot(qa); dot(pa); dot(ra); dot(sa); dot(ta); dot(ua);
\end{asy}

Because $QSTU$ is a square, $ST\perp PQ$; also $QR\perp PQ$. Hence $ST\parallel QR$, and since the two triangles share the angle at $P$, triangles $PST$ and $PQR$ are similar. Corresponding legs give
\[
\dfrac{ST}{QR}=\dfrac{PS}{PQ},\qquad\text{that is,}\qquad \dfrac{x}{4}=\dfrac{3-x}{3}.
\]
Cross-multiplying, $3x=12-4x$, so $7x=12$ and $x=\dfrac{12}{7}$.

This value is consistent: $0<\dfrac{12}{7}<3$, so $S$ and $U$ really lie on the sides $PQ$ and $QR$. Placing $Q=(0,0)$, $P=(3,0)$, $R=(0,4)$, the fourth vertex is $T=\left(\dfrac{12}{7},\dfrac{12}{7}\right)$, and line $PR$ has equation $\dfrac{X}{3}+\dfrac{Y}{4}=1$, so
\[
\dfrac{12}{7}\cdot\dfrac{1}{3}+\dfrac{12}{7}\cdot\dfrac{1}{4}=\dfrac{4}{7}+\dfrac{3}{7}=1,
\]
which confirms that $T$ lies on the hypotenuse $PR$, as required. Therefore $ST=\boxed{\dfrac{12}{7}}$.

Since $14=2\cdot 7$, the number must be even, so its units digit must be $0$ (the only even choice among $0,1$), and it must be divisible by $7$.

We examine candidates in increasing order of size. A valid number has a leading digit $1$, ends in $0$, and here we list every such number with at most five digits that is smaller than $10010$:
\begin{itemize}
\item two digits: $10\equiv 3 \pmod 7$;
\item three digits: $100\equiv 2$ and $110\equiv 5 \pmod 7$;
\item four digits: $1000\equiv 6$, $1010\equiv 2$, $1100\equiv 1$, $1110\equiv 4 \pmod 7$;
\item five digits below $10010$: only $10000\equiv 4 \pmod 7$.
\end{itemize}
None of these is a multiple of $7$.

The next candidate is
\[
10010=10\cdot 1001=10\cdot 7\cdot 11\cdot 13=14\cdot 715,
\]
which is even and divisible by $7$, hence divisible by $14$.

Therefore the least such integer is $\boxed{10010}$.

The argument confuses a finite polygon with a limiting shape, and visual indistinguishability with equality.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real rr=1;
path circ=circle((0,0),rr);
draw(circ,black+linewidth(1));
void regpoly(int n, pen p) {
  for(int i=0;i<n;++i) {
    real a1=pi/2+2*pi*i/n;
    real a2=pi/2+2*pi*(i+1)/n;
    draw((cos(a1),sin(a1))--(cos(a2),sin(a2)), p+linewidth(0.7));
  }
}
regpoly(4,red);
regpoly(12,blue);
regpoly(60,green);
label("circle",(0,-1.25),S);
label("$n=4$",(1.25,1.0),E,red);
label("$n=12$",(1.25,0.6),E,blue);
label("$n=60$",(1.25,0.2),E,green);
\end{asy}

First, for every finite \(n\ge3\), a regular \(n\)-gon is not a circle. If it is inscribed in a circle of radius \(1\), its area is
\[
A_n=\dfrac{n}{2}\sin\left(\dfrac{2\pi}{n}\right)
\]
and its perimeter is
\[
P_n=2n\sin\left(\dfrac{\pi}{n}\right).
\]
For every finite \(n\), \(A_n<\pi\) and \(P_n<2\pi\): the polygon lies inside the circle and cuts off small circular segments. As \(n\to\infty\), \(A_n\to\pi\) and \(P_n\to2\pi\), so the polygons approach the circle. But approaching is not being equal; no finite \(n\) makes the polygon an actual circle.

Second, a ``\(0\)-sided polygon'' is not defined: a polygon must have at least \(3\) sides. Even if one tried to take \(n\to\infty\), the limiting object is a circle, and the quantity tending to infinity is the number of sides, not the number \(0\). The statement \(0=\infty\) is not a valid equality of real numbers (and \(\infty\) is not a real number).

Finally, visual indistinguishability depends on the resolution of the drawing. A \(9000\)-gon may look like a circle in a small picture, but that is a statement about drawing accuracy, not exact geometry. For example, the difference in area, \(\pi-A_n\), is positive for each finite \(n\) (about \(2.5\times10^{-7}\) for \(n=9000\)), so the \(9000\)-gon is not actually a circle.

Thus the joke argument proves only that regular polygons can approximate a circle arbitrarily closely; it does not prove \(0=\infty\).

Let \(t\) be the number of hours after launch. In that time the rocket travels \(18{,}000t\) miles, and the Mars colony moves \(53{,}858t\) miles. Because the colony's motion is perpendicular to the rocket's straight-line path, these two displacements are the legs of a right triangle. The initial separation of \(140{,}000{,}000\) miles is the hypotenuse:
\[
(18{,}000t)^2+(53{,}858t)^2=(140{,}000{,}000)^2.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real legR=2;
real legM=legR*53858/18000;
pair O=(0,legR), F=(0,0), M=(legM,0);
draw(O--F--M--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0.3)--(0.3,0.3)--(0.3,0), linewidth(0.6));
dot(O); dot(F); dot(M);
label("$O$ (party)",O,NW);
label("$F$ (arrival)",F,SW);
label("$M$ (Mars at launch)",M,SE,fontsize(8pt));
label("$18{,}000t$",(O+F)/2,W);
label("$53{,}858t$",(F+M)/2,S);
label("$140{,}000{,}000$",(O+M)/2,NE,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Solving,
\[
t=\dfrac{140{,}000{,}000}{\sqrt{18{,}000^2+53{,}858^2}}\approx2465.38.
\]
To the nearest hour, the cake arrives after \(\boxed{2465}\) hours.

question:
A cylindrical water hose is \(2\) meters long. Its internal diameter is \(4\) centimeters, and the wall thickness is \(0.5\) centimeters.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the external radius of the hose in centimeters.
\item[(b)] Find the volume of material used to make the hose, in cubic centimeters, in terms of \(\pi\).
\item[(c)] Find the total area of all surfaces of the material (the outer curved surface, the inner curved surface, and the two flat annular ends), in square centimeters, in terms of \(\pi\).
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Convert the length to centimeters:
\[
L=2\text{ m}=200\text{ cm}.
\]
The internal radius is half the internal diameter, so \(r=2\) cm. Adding the wall thickness gives the external radius
\[
R=2+0.5=2.5\text{ cm}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real L=7.5, R=2.3, r=1.84, d=0.62;
pair O=(0,0), Q=(L,0);
path ell(pair c, real rx, real ry) {
  return shift(c)*scale(rx,ry)*unitcircle;
}
path outerL=ell(O,d,R), outerR=ell(Q,d,R);
path innerL=ell(O,d*r/R,r), innerR=ell(Q,d*r/R,r);
draw(outerL, linewidth(0.8));
draw(outerR, linewidth(0.8));
draw(innerL, linewidth(0.7));
draw(innerR, dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw((0,R)--(L,R), linewidth(0.8));
draw((0,-R)--(L,-R), linewidth(0.8));
draw((0,r)--(L,r), linewidth(0.6));
draw((0,-r)--(L,-r), linewidth(0.6));
draw(O--(0,R), linewidth(0.6));
draw(O--(0,r), linewidth(0.6));
label("$R=2.5$", (0,R/2), W, fontsize(8pt));
label("$r=2$", (0,r/2), E, fontsize(8pt));
label("$0.5$", (0,(R+r)/2), E, fontsize(8pt));
draw((0,-R-0.5)--(L,-R-0.5), linewidth(0.6));
draw((0,-R-0.7)--(0,-R-0.3), linewidth(0.6));
draw((L,-R-0.7)--(L,-R-0.3), linewidth(0.6));
label("$200$ cm", (L/2,-R-0.5), S, fontsize(8pt));
dot(O);
\end{asy}

The material is the region between two coaxial cylinders of radii \(r\) and \(R\). Its cross-sectional area is
\[
\pi(R^2-r^2)=\pi\left(\left(\dfrac{5}{2}\right)^2-2^2\right)=\dfrac{9\pi}{4}\text{ cm}^2.
\]
Multiplying by the length \(L=200\) cm gives the volume
\[
V=\dfrac{9\pi}{4}\cdot200=\boxed{450\pi\text{ cm}^3}.
\]
The outer curved surface has area \(2\pi R L=2\pi\cdot\dfrac{5}{2}\cdot200=1000\pi\). The inner curved surface has area \(2\pi r L=2\pi\cdot2\cdot200=800\pi\). The two flat annular ends together have area
\[
2\pi(R^2-r^2)=2\cdot\dfrac{9\pi}{4}=\dfrac{9\pi}{2}.
\]
Hence the total surface area is
\[
1000\pi+800\pi+\dfrac{9\pi}{2}=\dfrac{3609\pi}{2}\text{ cm}^2.
\]
Thus the answers are
\[
\boxed{R=2.5\text{ cm}},\qquad
\boxed{V=450\pi\text{ cm}^3},\qquad
\boxed{A=\dfrac{3609\pi}{2}\text{ cm}^2}.
\]

Let the first rectangle have sides $2x$ and $3x$, and the second have sides $3y$ and $8y$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real x=4*sqrt(2)/3, y=2*sqrt(2)/3;
pair A=(0,0), B=(3*x,0), Cc=(3*x,2*x), D=(0,2*x);
pair P=(3*x+1.6,0), Q=(3*x+1.6+8*y,0), R=(3*x+1.6+8*y,3*y), T=(3*x+1.6,3*y);
draw(A--B--Cc--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--Q--R--T--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
label("$2x$",(D+A)/2,W);
label("$3x$",(A+B)/2,S);
label("$3y$",(P+T)/2,W);
label("$8y$",(P+Q)/2,S);
label("area $=\dfrac{64}{3}$",(A+B+Cc+D)/4);
label("area $=\dfrac{64}{3}$",(P+Q+R+T)/4);
\end{asy}

The square has area $98$, so its side is $\sqrt{98}=7\sqrt2$ and the piece of string has length
\[4\cdot 7\sqrt2=28\sqrt2.\]
The two rectangles use up the whole string, so
\[2(2x+3x)+2(3y+8y)=10x+22y=28\sqrt2.\]
The areas, $2x\cdot 3x=6x^2$ and $3y\cdot 8y=24y^2$, are equal, so
\[6x^2=24y^2,\qquad x^2=4y^2,\qquad x=2y\]
because $x,y>0$. Substituting $x=2y$ into the perimeter equation,
\[10(2y)+22y=42y=28\sqrt2,\qquad y=\dfrac{28\sqrt2}{42}=\dfrac{2\sqrt2}{3}.\]
The common area is therefore
\[24y^2=24\cdot\dfrac{8}{9}=\dfrac{64}{3},\]
so $m=64$, $n=3$ and $m+n=67$.

The value of $m+n$ is $\boxed{67}$.

Let $\theta$ be the angle of the clockwise rotation. A clockwise rotation sends $(x,y)$ to $(x\cos\theta+y\sin\theta,\,-x\sin\theta+y\cos\theta)$.

The centre $C=(0,0)$ stays fixed, so $C'=(0,0)$. For $A=(0,5)$ we get
\[A'=(5\sin\theta,\,5\cos\theta)=(3,4),\]
hence $\sin\theta=\dfrac{3}{5}$ and $\cos\theta=\dfrac{4}{5}$. Therefore
\[B'=\left(12\cdot\dfrac{4}{5},\,-12\cdot\dfrac{3}{5}\right)=\left(\dfrac{48}{5},-\dfrac{36}{5}\right).\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real k=0.35;
pair A=(0,5)*k, B=(12,0)*k, C=(0,0);
pair A1=(3,4)*k, B1=(9.6,-7.2)*k;
pair Dp=(75.0/14,0)*k;
fill(C--Dp--B1--cycle,paleblue);
draw((-0.8,0)--(5.2,0),linewidth(0.7));
draw((0,-3.3)--(0,2.7),linewidth(0.7));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(C--A1--B1--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(A1); dot(B1); dot(Dp);
label("$A$",A,W);
label("$B$",B,SE);
label("$C$",C,SW);
label("$A'$",A1,NE);
label("$B'$",B1,E);
label("$D$",Dp+(0,-0.55));
label("$x$",(5.15,0),E);
label("$y$",(0,2.6),N);
\end{asy}

Rotations preserve area, so the area of $A'B'C'$ equals the area of $ABC$, namely $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot 5\cdot 12=30$.

Let $D$ be where side $A'B'$ meets the $x$-axis. Since $A'$ lies above the axis and $B'$ below it, the part of triangle $A'B'C'$ below the $x$-axis is exactly triangle $C'DB'$.

Along the segment from $A'=(3,4)$ to $B'=\left(\dfrac{48}{5},-\dfrac{36}{5}\right)$ the $y$-coordinate drops by $4+\dfrac{36}{5}=\dfrac{56}{5}$. Writing points of the segment as $A'+t(B'-A')$, the value $y=0$ occurs when $t\cdot\dfrac{56}{5}=4$, that is $t=\dfrac{5}{14}$. So $D$ is $\dfrac{5}{14}$ of the way from $A'$ to $B'$, and
\[\dfrac{DB'}{A'B'}=1-\dfrac{5}{14}=\dfrac{9}{14}.\]
Triangles $C'A'B'$ and $C'DB'$ have bases $A'B'$ and $DB'$ on one line and share the altitude from $C'$, so their areas are in the ratio of those bases:
\[\dfrac{[C'DB']}{[C'A'B']}=\dfrac{DB'}{A'B'}=\dfrac{9}{14}.\]
(Coordinate check: $D=\left(\dfrac{75}{14},0\right)$, so $[C'DB']=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\dfrac{75}{14}\cdot\dfrac{36}{5}=\dfrac{135}{7}=\dfrac{9}{14}\cdot 30$.)

The fraction of the area below the $x$-axis is $\boxed{\dfrac{9}{14}}$.

Let \(G\) be the centroid. Place the triangle with
\[
B=(-3,0),\quad C=(3,0),\quad A=(0,4),
\]
which has side lengths \(AB=AC=5\) and \(BC=6\). Then \(G=\left(0,\dfrac{4}{3}\right)\).
For a point \(P=(x,y)\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
PA^2+PB^2+PC^2
&=[x^2+(y-4)^2]+[(x+3)^2+y^2]+[(x-3)^2+y^2]\\
&=3x^2+3\left(y-\dfrac{4}{3}\right)^2+\dfrac{86}{3}.
\end{aligned}
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,4), B=(-3,0), C=(3,0), G=(0,4/3);
draw(A--B--C--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
fill(circle(G,2/3), paleblue);
draw(circle(G,2/3), red);
draw(G--G+(2/3,0), dashed);
dot(G);
label("$A$", A, N);
label("$B$", B, SW);
label("$C$", C, SE);
label("$G$", G+(0.12,0.12));
label("$5$", (A+B)/2, NW);
label("$5$", (A+C)/2, NE);
label("$6$", (B+C)/2, S);
label("$\dfrac{2}{3}$", G+(1/3,0.12), N);
\end{asy}

Thus
\[
PA^2+PB^2+PC^2<30
\iff 3GP^2+\dfrac{86}{3}<30
\iff GP^2<\dfrac{4}{9}.
\]
So \(P\) must lie inside the circle centered at \(G\) with radius \(\dfrac{2}{3}\). This circle is entirely inside the triangle: its centre is \(\dfrac{4}{3}\) from the base, and its distance to each equal side is \(\dfrac{8}{5}\), both greater than \(\dfrac{2}{3}\). For instance, side \(AB\) has equation \(4x-3y+12=0\), so the distance from \(G\) to \(AB\) is \(\dfrac{|0-4+12|}{5}=\dfrac{8}{5}\).
The circle has area \(\pi\left(\dfrac{2}{3}\right)^2=\dfrac{4\pi}{9}\), and the triangle has area \(\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot6\cdot4=12\). Therefore the probability is
\[
\dfrac{\dfrac{4\pi}{9}}{12}=\dfrac{\pi}{27}.
\]
Thus the probability is \(\boxed{\dfrac{\pi}{27}}\).

Take \(G\) as the origin and write the position vectors of \(A,B,C,P\) as \(\mathbf a,\mathbf b,\mathbf c,\mathbf p\). Because \(G\) is the centroid,
\[
\mathbf a+\mathbf b+\mathbf c=\mathbf 0.
\]
Now
\[
PA^2=|\mathbf p-\mathbf a|^2=|\mathbf p|^2+|\mathbf a|^2-2\mathbf p\cdot\mathbf a,
\]
and similarly for \(PB^2\) and \(PC^2\). Adding the three equations gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
PA^2+PB^2+PC^2
&=3|\mathbf p|^2+|\mathbf a|^2+|\mathbf b|^2+|\mathbf c|^2
-2\mathbf p\cdot(\mathbf a+\mathbf b+\mathbf c)\\
&=3GP^2+GA^2+GB^2+GC^2.
\end{aligned}
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-2,0), B=(3,0), C=(0.5,3);
pair G=(A+B+C)/3;
pair P=(2.5,2);
draw(A--B--C--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--A, dashed);
draw(P--B, dashed);
draw(P--C, dashed);
draw(G--A, dotted);
draw(G--B, dotted);
draw(G--C, dotted);
dot(G);
dot(P);
label("$A$", A, SW);
label("$B$", B, SE);
label("$C$", C, N);
label("$G$", G, SW);
label("$P$", P, NE);
\end{asy}

This proves the identity for every point \(P\) in the plane.

Regard \(A,B,C,X\) as position vectors. Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
&a|\mathbf x-A|^2+b|\mathbf x-B|^2+c|\mathbf x-C|^2\\
&=(a+b+c)|\mathbf x|^2-2\mathbf x\cdot(aA+bB+cC)
 +(a|A|^2+b|B|^2+c|C|^2).
\end{aligned}
\]
Let
\[
I=\dfrac{aA+bB+cC}{a+b+c}.
\]
Completing the square gives
\[
aXA^2+bXB^2+cXC^2=(a+b+c)\,XI^2+\text{constant}.
\]
Since \(a+b+c>0\), this expression is minimized exactly when \(XI=0\), that is, when \(X=I\).
It remains to identify \(I\). Its barycentric coordinates are \(a:b:c\). The cevian from \(A\) through \(I\) meets \(BC\) at the point with barycentric coordinates \(0:b:c\), which divides \(BC\) in the ratio \(c:b\). But \(AB:AC=c:b\), so by the angle bisector theorem this cevian is the angle bisector from \(A\). Similarly, \(BI\) and \(CI\) are the other two angle bisectors. Hence \(I\) is the incenter of \(\triangle ABC\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(5,0), C=(1.5,3.5);
real lena=length(B-C), lenb=length(C-A), lenc=length(A-B);
pair I=(lena*A+lenb*B+lenc*C)/(lena+lenb+lenc);
draw(A--B--C--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--I, dashed);
draw(B--I, dashed);
draw(C--I, dashed);
dot(I);
label("$A$", A, SW);
label("$B$", B, SE);
label("$C$", C, N);
label("$I$", I+(0.1,0.1));
label("$a$", (B+C)/2, NE);
label("$b$", (C+A)/2, NW);
label("$c$", (A+B)/2, S);
\end{asy}

Therefore the minimizing point is the incenter:
\[
\boxed{I\text{, the incenter of }\triangle ABC}.
\]

The large pipe has radius \(20\) cm, so its cross-sectional area is
\[
\pi(20)^2=400\pi\text{ cm}^2.
\]
Each straw has radius \(0.25\) cm, so its cross-sectional area is
\[
\pi(0.25)^2=\dfrac{\pi}{16}\text{ cm}^2.
\]
Thus the number of straws needed side by side to match the area is
\[
\dfrac{400\pi}{\dfrac{\pi}{16}}=6400.
\]

\begin{asy}
