\]

question:
Let $n$ be a positive integer and let $\sigma(n)$ denote the sum of the positive divisors of $n$; for instance, the positive divisors of $6$ are $1,2,3,6$, so $\sigma(6)=12$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that the sum of the reciprocals of the positive divisors of $n$ equals $\dfrac{\sigma(n)}{n}$.
\item[(b)] Compute the sum of the reciprocals of all the positive divisors of $360$, as a fraction in lowest terms.
\end{enumerate}

A school wishes to cover an outdoor play area measuring $40$ feet by $72$ feet with square tiles of artificial turf. Only whole tiles that are congruent squares of the largest possible size will be used.

A student objects: ``The problem never says the tiles must all be the same size. I can cover the area with one $40\times 40$ square, one $32\times 32$ square and four $8\times 8$ squares, using only $6$ tiles, so the answer should be $6$.''
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain what is wrong with the student's reasoning.
\item[(b)] How many square tiles are actually needed?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The word \emph{congruent} means that the tiles are all exactly the same size: the problem allows only whole squares all congruent to one another. The student's covering uses squares of three different sizes ($40$, $32$ and $8$ inches), so it is not an admissible tiling. The student also misreads the condition: ``of the largest possible size'' fixes the side length first, and the number of tiles is then just the count needed to cover the area; it is not something to be traded against the size.

(For completeness, the student's arrangement really does cover the play area: a $40\times 40$ square, then a $32\times 32$ square in the remaining $40\times 32$ region, leaving an $8\times 32$ strip of four $8\times 8$ squares, and $40^{2}+32^{2}+4\cdot 8^{2}=1600+1024+256=2880=40\cdot 72$. The only flaw is the misreading of ``congruent''.)


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real u=0.5;
fill((0,0)--(5*u,0)--(5*u,5*u)--(0,5*u)--cycle,paleblue);
fill((0,5*u)--(4*u,5*u)--(4*u,9*u)--(0,9*u)--cycle,paleyellow);
fill((4*u,5*u)--(5*u,5*u)--(5*u,9*u)--(4*u,9*u)--cycle,lightgreen);
draw((0,0)--(5*u,0)--(5*u,9*u)--(0,9*u)--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((0,5*u)--(5*u,5*u),linewidth(0.8));
draw((4*u,5*u)--(4*u,9*u),linewidth(0.8));
for(int k=6;k<9;++k){draw((4*u,k*u)--(5*u,k*u),linewidth(0.8));}
label("$40\times 40$",(2.5*u,2.5*u),fontsize(7pt));
label("$32\times 32$",(2*u,7*u),fontsize(7pt));
label("$8$",(4.5*u,5.5*u),fontsize(7pt));
label("$40$ ft",(2.5*u,-0.55),S,fontsize(8pt));
label("$72$ ft",(-0.75,9*u/2),W,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} Let the common side length be $s$. Whole tiles are not cut, so on the bottom edge of the play area the tiles sit side by side and cover it by segments of length $s$ each: thus $s$ divides $40$. The same argument on the left edge shows $s$ divides $72$. So $s$ is a common divisor of $40$ and $72$, and the largest possible size is the greatest common divisor
\[s=\gcd(40,72)=8 \text{ feet}.\]
With $8$-foot tiles the play area becomes a grid of $\dfrac{40}{8}=5$ columns and $\dfrac{72}{8}=9$ rows, as in the sketch of the correct tiling.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real u=0.5;
for(int i=1;i<5;++i){draw((i*u,0)--(i*u,9*u),gray+linewidth(0.4));}
for(int j=1;j<9;++j){draw((0,j*u)--(5*u,j*u),gray+linewidth(0.4));}
draw((0,0)--(5*u,0)--(5*u,9*u)--(0,9*u)--cycle,linewidth(1));
label("$8$",(0.25,-0.16),S,fontsize(8pt));
label("$8$",(-0.18,0.25),W,fontsize(8pt));
label("$40$ ft",(2.5*u,-0.55),S,fontsize(8pt));
label("$72$ ft",(-0.75,9*u/2),W,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Hence the number of tiles is
\[5\cdot 9=\dfrac{40}{8}\cdot\dfrac{72}{8}=\dfrac{40\cdot 72}{8^{2}}=\dfrac{2880}{64}=\boxed{45},\]
which is also just the area of the play area divided by the area of one tile.

The motion of a balloon has two perpendicular components: it rises at $5$ feet per second, and the wind carries it north at
\[240\text{ yards per minute}=240\cdot 3=720\text{ feet per minute}=\dfrac{720}{60}=12\text{ feet per second}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real k=0.45;
pair P0=(0,0), A=(12*k,0), B=(12*k,5*k);
draw(P0--A--B--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
real d=0.13;
draw((A.x-d,A.y)--(A.x-d,A.y+d)--(A.x,A.y+d),linewidth(0.6));
label("$12$",(P0+A)/2,S);
label("$5$",(A+B)/2,E);
label("$13$",(P0+B)/2,NW);
\end{asy}

Because the two components are perpendicular, a balloon's speed is
\[\sqrt{5^2+12^2}=\sqrt{169}=13\text{ feet per second},\]
and its direction of motion never changes, so the distance it travels equals its speed times its flying time.

Balloon 1 flies $80$ seconds, so it travels $13\cdot 80=1040$ feet.
Balloon 2 flies $153$ seconds, so it travels $13\cdot 153=1989$ feet.
Balloon 3 rises $15180$ inches $=\dfrac{15180}{12}=1265$ feet, which takes $\dfrac{1265}{5}=253$ seconds; it therefore travels $13\cdot 253=3289$ feet. (Check: it goes $12\cdot 253=3036$ feet north and $1265$ feet up, and $\sqrt{3036^2+1265^2}=\sqrt{10817521}=3289$.)

Hence the mean distance is
\[\dfrac{1040+1989+3289}{3}=\dfrac{6318}{3}=\boxed{2106}\text{ feet}.\]

question:
Does there exist a function $f$ from the real numbers to the real numbers such that
\[(f(x)-1)(f(x)-2)f(x)=x\qquad\text{for every real number }x?\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Either give an example of such a function or prove that none exists.
\item[(b)] Does such a function exist that is also continuous? Prove your answer.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let \([XYZ]\) denote the area of triangle \(XYZ\). By the angle bisector theorem,
\[
BM:MC=c:b,\qquad CN:NA=a:c,\qquad AP:PB=b:a.
\]
Thus
\[
\dfrac{BM}{BC}=\dfrac{c}{b+c},\quad \dfrac{MC}{BC}=\dfrac{b}{b+c},\quad
\dfrac{CN}{CA}=\dfrac{a}{a+c},\quad \dfrac{NA}{CA}=\dfrac{c}{a+c},\quad
\dfrac{AP}{AB}=\dfrac{b}{a+b},\quad \dfrac{PB}{AB}=\dfrac{a}{a+b}.
\]
The central triangle \(MNP\) and the three corner triangles \(APN\), \(BPM\), \(CMN\) partition \(ABC\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(7,0), C=(2.4,5.4);
real a=abs(B-C), b=abs(C-A), c=abs(A-B);
pair Mpt=(b*B+c*C)/(b+c);
pair Npt=(a*A+c*C)/(a+c);
pair Ppt=(a*A+b*B)/(a+b);
fill(Mpt--Npt--Ppt--cycle, paleblue);
draw(A--B--C--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--Mpt, red+linewidth(0.8));
draw(B--Npt, red+linewidth(0.8));
draw(C--Ppt, red+linewidth(0.8));
draw(Mpt--Npt--Ppt--cycle, blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(Mpt); dot(Npt); dot(Ppt);
label("$A$", A, SW);
label("$B$", B, SE);
label("$C$", C, N);
label("$M$", Mpt, E);
label("$N$", Npt, W);
label("$P$", Ppt, S);
\end{asy}

For the corner at \(A\),
\[
\dfrac{[APN]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac{AP}{AB}\cdot\dfrac{AN}{AC}
=\dfrac{b}{a+b}\cdot\dfrac{c}{a+c}
=\dfrac{bc}{(a+b)(a+c)}.
\]
Similarly,
\[
\dfrac{[BPM]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac{BP}{BA}\cdot\dfrac{BM}{BC}
=\dfrac{a}{a+b}\cdot\dfrac{c}{b+c}
=\dfrac{ac}{(a+b)(b+c)},
\]
and
\[
\dfrac{[CMN]}{[ABC]}=\dfrac{CM}{CB}\cdot\dfrac{CN}{CA}
=\dfrac{b}{b+c}\cdot\dfrac{a}{a+c}
=\dfrac{ab}{(b+c)(a+c)}.
\]
Therefore
\[
\dfrac{[MNP]}{[ABC]}
=1-\dfrac{bc}{(a+b)(a+c)}-\dfrac{ac}{(a+b)(b+c)}-\dfrac{ab}{(b+c)(a+c)}.
\]
Putting the right-hand side over the common denominator \((a+b)(b+c)(c+a)\),
\[
\dfrac{[MNP]}{[ABC]}
=\dfrac{(a+b)(b+c)(c+a)-bc(b+c)-ac(a+c)-ab(a+b)}{(a+b)(b+c)(c+a)}.
\]
Expanding the numerator leaves \(2abc\), so
\[
\dfrac{[MNP]}{[ABC]}=\boxed{\dfrac{2abc}{(a+b)(b+c)(c+a)}}.
\]
This proves the desired identity.

