A point $S$ is chosen uniformly at random inside the square $MNPQ$. What is the probability that $\angle MSN$ is acute? Round your answer to the nearest hundredth.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair TM=(0,0), TN=(4,0), TP=(4,4), TQ=(0,4);
draw(TM--TN--TP--TQ--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
pair Sp=(1.0,1.0);
draw(Sp--TM, dashed+linewidth(0.7));
draw(Sp--TN, dashed+linewidth(0.7));
dot(Sp);
label("$S$", Sp, NW);
dot(TM); dot(TN); dot(TP); dot(TQ);
label("$M$",TM,SW); label("$N$",TN,SE); label("$P$",TP,NE); label("$Q$",TQ,NW);
\end{asy}

solution:
Let the side length of the square be $1$ and let $O$ be the midpoint of $MN$. By Thales' theorem, $\angle MSN$ is a right angle exactly when $S$ lies on the circle with diameter $MN$; the angle is obtuse when $S$ is inside that circle and acute when $S$ is outside it.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair TM=(0,0), TN=(4,0), TP=(4,4), TQ=(0,4);
path semi=arc((2,0),2,0,180);
fill(semi--cycle, paleblue);
draw(TM--TN--TP--TQ--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
draw(semi, linewidth(0.8));
pair Sp=(0.9,0.6);
draw(Sp--TM, dashed+linewidth(0.7));
draw(Sp--TN, dashed+linewidth(0.7));
dot(Sp);
label("$S$", Sp, S, fontsize(8pt));
label("obtuse", (2.1,1.35), fontsize(7pt));
dot(TM); dot(TN); dot(TP); dot(TQ);
label("$M$",TM,SW); label("$N$",TN,SE); label("$P$",TP,NE); label("$Q$",TQ,NW);
\end{asy}

The circle has centre $O$ and radius $\dfrac{1}{2}$. The half of it lying on the same side of $MN$ as the interior of the square is a semicircle inside the square, since its maximum distance from $MN$ is $\dfrac{1}{2}<1$. This semicircle has area
\[\dfrac{1}{2}\pi\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^{2}=\dfrac{\pi}{8}\approx 0.3927.\]
This is exactly the region where the angle is obtuse. Points on the circle itself give a right angle; they form a set of area $0$ and do not affect the probability. The value $0.39$ is the probability of an \emph{obtuse} angle, not of an acute one. Hence the probability that $\angle MSN$ is acute is
\[1-\dfrac{\pi}{8}\approx 0.6073,\]
which rounds to
\[\boxed{0.61}.\]

If $\theta=0$, then $AX=AB$ and $AY=AC$, so $B_0=B$, $C_0=C$, and $MB_0=MB=MC=MC_0$ because $M$ is the midpoint of $BC$. Hence assume $\theta>0$.

Let $B'$ be the reflection of $B$ in the line $AX$, and $C'$ the reflection of $C$ in the line $AY$.

Since $BB_0\perp AX$ and $B'$ is the mirror image of $B$, the line $AX$ is the perpendicular bisector of $BB'$. Hence $B_0$, the foot of the perpendicular, is the midpoint of $BB'$. Likewise $C_0$ is the midpoint of $CC'$.

In triangle $BCB'$ the point $M$ is the midpoint of $BC$ and $B_0$ is the midpoint of $BB'$, so by the midline theorem
\[MB_0=\dfrac{CB'}{2}.\]
In triangle $CBC'$, similarly, $M$ is the midpoint of $CB$ and $C_0$ is the midpoint of $CC'$, so
\[MC_0=\dfrac{BC'}{2}.\]
It therefore suffices to prove $CB'=BC'$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(5,0), C=(2.5,5);
pair B0=(3.75,-2.1650635), C0=(-0.290037,4.832549);
pair Bp=(2.5,-4.3301270), Cp=(-3.080074,4.665098);
pair M=(3.75,2.5);
draw(A--1.25*B0, dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(A--1.25*C0, dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(A--B--C--cycle, linewidth(1));
draw(B--Bp, dashed+linewidth(0.5));
draw(C--Cp, dashed+linewidth(0.5));
draw(Bp--C, red+linewidth(0.7));
draw(Cp--B, red+linewidth(0.7));
draw(M--B0, blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(M--C0, blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(A,0.7,-30,0), linewidth(0.45));
draw(arc(A,0.7,63.435,93.435), linewidth(0.45));
pair uB=(0.8660254,-0.5), nB=(0.5,0.8660254);
draw(B0+0.3*uB--B0+0.3*uB+0.3*nB--B0+0.3*nB--cycle, linewidth(0.5));
pair d1=(0.99822,0.05993), d2=(0.05991,-0.998204);
draw(C0+0.3*d1--C0+0.3*d1+0.3*d2--C0+0.3*d2--cycle, linewidth(0.5));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(M); dot(B0); dot(C0); dot(Bp); dot(Cp);
label("$A$",A,SW);
label("$B$",B,S);
label("$C$",C,N);
label("$M$",M,E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$B_0$",B0,SE,fontsize(8pt));
label("$C_0$",C0,W,fontsize(8pt));
label("$B'$",Bp,S,fontsize(8pt));
label("$C'$",Cp,NW,fontsize(8pt));
label("$X$",1.25*B0,E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$Y$",1.25*C0,N,fontsize(8pt));
\end{asy}

Write $\varphi_B$ and $\varphi_C$ for the directions of the rays $AB$ and $AC$, choosing the orientation of the plane so that $\varphi_C>\varphi_B$. Because the interiors of $\angle XAB$ and $\angle YAC$ avoid the interior of $\triangle ABC$, the ray $AX$ lies outside $\angle BAC$ on the side of $AB$ and $AY$ lies outside on the side of $AC$. Hence $AX$ has direction $\varphi_B-\theta$ and $AY$ has direction $\varphi_C+\theta$.

Reflection in the line $AX$ sends the ray of direction $\varphi_B$ to the ray of direction $2(\varphi_B-\theta)-\varphi_B=\varphi_B-2\theta$. Since reflection fixes $A$ and preserves distances from it, $B'$ lies on the ray of direction $\varphi_B-2\theta$ and $AB'=AB$. Likewise $C'$ lies on the ray of direction $\varphi_C+2\theta$ and $AC'=AC$.

Now let $\rho$ be the rotation about $A$ through $2\theta$ carrying the ray of direction $\varphi_B-2\theta$ to the ray of direction $\varphi_B$. Then $\rho(B')=B$, because $AB'=AB$. Also $\rho(C)$ lies at distance $AC$ from $A$ on the ray of direction $\varphi_C+2\theta$, which is precisely where $C'$ is; so $\rho(C)=C'$. A rotation preserves all distances, so it sends the segment $B'C$ to the segment $BC'$, and therefore
\[CB'=B'C=BC'.\]

(Equivalently, in directed angles, $\angle B'AC=\angle BAC'=\angle BAC+2\theta$ with $AB'=AB$ and $AC'=AC$, so the law of cosines gives $CB'^{2}=BC'^{2}$.)

Consequently
\[MB_0=\dfrac{CB'}{2}=\dfrac{BC'}{2}=MC_0,\]
which is what had to be proved.

question:
A box contains \(10\) red marbles, \(30\) white marbles, \(20\) blue marbles, and \(15\) orange marbles. A marble is drawn and then replaced, and then a second marble is drawn. Find the probability that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] each of the two drawn marbles is red or white (the pair may be red-red, white-white, or one of each);
\item[(b)] at least one of the two drawn marbles is blue.
\end{enumerate}
Express each answer as a fraction in lowest terms.

A combination lock has a \(10\)-digit code, where each digit is chosen independently and uniformly from \(0,1,\dots,9\), with repetitions allowed. Timothy guesses one code uniformly at random.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the probability that he gets at least \(7\) digits correct?
\item[(b)] What is the probability that he gets exactly \(7\) digits correct?
\end{enumerate}

Maria writes a number on each face of a cube. At each corner she adds the numbers on the three faces meeting there. For corner \(B\), these are faces \(BCDA\), \(BAEF\), and \(BFGC\), as shown.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fontsize(9pt);
pair pB=(0,0), pA=(2.0,0), pC=(1.0,1.0), pD=(3.0,1.0), pF=(0,2.2), pE=(2.0,2.2), pG=(1.0,3.2), pH=(3.0,3.2);
draw(pB--pC,dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(pC--pD,dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(pC--pG,dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(pB--pA--pE--pF--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(pA--pD--pH--pE,linewidth(0.9));
draw(pF--pG--pH,linewidth(0.9));
dot(pB); dot(pA); dot(pC); dot(pD); dot(pF); dot(pE); dot(pG); dot(pH);
label("$B$", pB, SW);
label("$A$", pA, SE);
label("$C$", pC+(-0.28,-0.18));
label("$D$", pD, E);
label("$F$", pF, W);
label("$E$", pE, E);
label("$G$", pG, NW);
label("$H$", pH, NE);
\end{asy}

She obtains totals \(14\) for corner \(C\), \(16\) for corner \(D\), and \(24\) for corner \(E\). What total does she obtain for corner \(F\)?

solution:
Let
\[
\begin{aligned}
x&=\text{number on }BCDA,\\
y&=\text{number on }BAEF,\\
z&=\text{number on }BFGC,\\
u&=\text{number on }DCGH,\\
v&=\text{number on }DAEH,\\
w&=\text{number on }FEHG.
\end{aligned}
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fontsize(9pt);
pair pB=(0,0), pA=(2.0,0), pC=(1.0,1.0), pD=(3.0,1.0), pF=(0,2.2), pE=(2.0,2.2), pG=(1.0,3.2), pH=(3.0,3.2);
draw(pB--pC,dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(pC--pD,dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(pC--pG,dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(pB--pA--pE--pF--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(pA--pD--pH--pE,linewidth(0.9));
draw(pF--pG--pH,linewidth(0.9));
dot(pB); dot(pA); dot(pG); dot(pH);
dot(pC,red+linewidth(1.5)); dot(pD,red+linewidth(1.5)); dot(pE,red+linewidth(1.5)); dot(pF,red+linewidth(1.5));
label("$B$", pB, SW);
label("$A$", pA, SE);
label("$C$", pC+(-0.28,-0.18));
label("$D$", pD, E);
label("$F$", pF, W);
label("$E$", pE, E);
label("$G$", pG, NW);
label("$H$", pH, NE);
\end{asy}

At corner \(C\), the three faces are \(BCDA\), \(BFGC\), and \(DCGH\), so
\[
x+z+u=14.
\]
At corner \(D\), the three faces are \(BCDA\), \(DCGH\), and \(DAEH\), so
\[
x+u+v=16.
\]
At corner \(E\), the three faces are \(BAEF\), \(DAEH\), and \(FEHG\), so
\[
y+v+w=24.
\]
The wanted total at \(F\) is
\[
y+z+w.
\]
Subtracting the \(C\)-equation from the \(D\)-equation gives
\[
v-z=2.
\]
Therefore
\[
y+z+w=(y+v+w)-(v-z)=24-2=22.
\]
So the total at corner \(F\) is
\[
\boxed{22}.
\]

Let $A$ and $B$ be the centres of $C_1$ and $C_2$, and let $O$ be the centre of $C_3$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=4, s=3, x=6;
real h=r+sqrt((r+s)^2-(x/2)^2);
pair O1=(0,r), O2=(x,r), O3=(x/2,h);
draw((-6.5,0)--(13,0),linewidth(0.8));
label("$L$",(13,0),E);
draw(circle(O1,r),linewidth(0.8));
draw(circle(O2,r),linewidth(0.8));
draw(circle(O3,s),linewidth(0.8));
draw(O1--O2,dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(O3--(x/2,0),dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(O1--(0,0),linewidth(0.6));
draw(O3--(O3+(s,0)),linewidth(0.6));
dot(O1); dot(O2); dot(O3); dot((0,0)); dot((x,0));
label("$O_1$",O1,W);
label("$O_2$",O2,E);
label("$O_3$",O3,N);
label("$r$",(0,r/2),W);
label("$s$",(x/2+s/2,h),N);
label("$h$",(x/2+0.45,h/2),E);
label("$x$",(x/2-1.6,r+0.5));
\end{asy}

Because $C_1$ and $C_2$ are tangent to $L$ and lie on the same side of it, both $A$ and $B$ are at distance $r$ from $L$. Hence $AB$ is parallel to $L$ and $AB=x$. Because $C_3$ is externally tangent to $C_1$ and to $C_2$,
\[OA=OB=r+s,\]
so $O$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$.

Let $M$ be the midpoint of $AB$. Then $AM=\dfrac{x}{2}$ and $OM\perp AB$; since $AB$ is parallel to $L$, $OM$ is perpendicular to $L$ as well. The segment $OM$ joins $AB$ to the point $O$, which by hypothesis is on the same side of $L$ as $AB$ and farther from $L$, so the distance from $O$ to $L$ exceeds the distance from $AB$ to $L$ by exactly $OM$:
\[h=r+OM.\]
The right triangle $AMO$ has hypotenuse $OA=r+s$ and leg $AM=\dfrac{x}{2}$, so by the Pythagorean theorem
\[OM=\sqrt{(r+s)^2-\dfrac{x^2}{4}},\]
and therefore
\[h=\boxed{r+\sqrt{(r+s)^2-\dfrac{x^2}{4}}}.\]
(The configuration exists precisely when $0<x<2(r+s)$. At $x=2(r+s)$ the centre of $C_3$ would be level with $A$ and $B$, not farther from $L$.)

Let $O_1$ be the centre of the circle of radius $10$ and $O_2$ the centre of the circle of radius $7.5$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(10cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r1=10, r2=7.5;
pair A=(0,0), B=(0,12), O1=(8,6), O2=(-4.5,6);
pair Ep=(16,0), Fp=(-9,0);
draw(circle(O1,r1),linewidth(0.8));
draw(circle(O2,r2),linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--B,dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(O1--O2,blue+dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw(Fp--Ep,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(Ep); dot(Fp); dot(O1); dot(O2);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,NW);
label("$E$",Ep,SE); label("$F$",Fp,SW);
label("$O_1$",O1,NE); label("$O_2$",O2,SW);
label("$12.5$",(O1+O2)/2,N,blue);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 1: the distance between the centres.} Each centre lies on the perpendicular bisector of the common chord $AB$. Let $M$ be the midpoint of $AB$, so $AM=6$. Then
\[O_1M=\sqrt{10^2-6^2}=8,\qquad O_2M=\sqrt{7.5^2-6^2}=\sqrt{20.25}=4.5.\]
The centres are on opposite sides of $AB$, so $O_1$, $M$, $O_2$ are collinear with $M$ between them and
\[O_1O_2=8+4.5=12.5.\]

\textbf{Step 2: bound $EF$.} Drop perpendiculars $O_1M_1$ and $O_2M_2$ from $O_1$ and $O_2$ to the line $EF$. A perpendicular from the centre of a circle to a chord bisects that chord, so $M_1$ is the midpoint of $AE$ and $M_2$ is the midpoint of $AF$. Measuring distances along the line from $A$, the point $E$ lies on the same side of $A$ as $M_1$ and $F$ on the same side as $M_2$, so $AE=2\,AM_1$ and $AF=2\,AM_2$. Since $A$ lies between $E$ and $F$, the points $M_1$ and $M_2$ are on opposite sides of $A$, and
\[EF=AE+AF=2\left(AM_1+AM_2\right)=2\,M_1M_2.\]
But $M_1M_2$ is the length of the projection of the segment $O_1O_2$ onto the line $EF$, and a projection never lengthens a segment: $M_1M_2\le O_1O_2=12.5$. Therefore
\[EF\le 2\cdot 12.5=25.\]

\textbf{Step 3: equality is attained.} Take $EF$ parallel to $O_1O_2$. Then the projections do not shorten the segment, so $M_1M_2=O_1O_2=12.5$. Concretely, along this line the chord through $A$ in the larger circle has length $2\cdot 8=16$ and the chord through $A$ in the smaller circle has length $2\cdot 4.5=9$, on opposite sides of $A$, giving $EF=16+9=25$.

Hence the largest possible value is
\[\boxed{25}.\]

A brick of side $9$ cm has volume $9^3=729>700$, so no such brick fits inside the house. The bricks actually used are cubes with volumes
\[5^3=125,\qquad 3^3=27,\qquad 2^3=8\]
cubic centimetres.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(11cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
void drawcube(pair A, real s){
  pair d=(0.42*s,0.34*s);
  pair F0=A, F1=A+(s,0), F2=A+(s,s), F3=A+(0,s);
  pair B0=F0+d, B1=F1+d, B2=F2+d, B3=F3+d;
  draw(F0--B0,dashed+linewidth(0.5));
  draw(B0--B1,dashed+linewidth(0.5));
  draw(B0--B3,dashed+linewidth(0.5));
  draw(F0--F1--F2--F3--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
  draw(F1--B1--B2--B3--F3,linewidth(0.8));
  draw(F2--B2,linewidth(0.8));
}
drawcube((0,0),5);
drawcube((7.5,0),3);
drawcube((12.5,0),2);
label("$5$ cm",(2.5,0),S);
label("$3$ cm",(9,0),S);
label("$2$ cm",(13.5,0),S);
\end{asy}

Write $a$, $b$, $c$ for the numbers of $5$, $3$ and $2$ cm bricks used. Their total volume is the volume of the house, so
\[125a+27b+8c=700,\qquad a\ge 1,\ b\ge 1,\ c\ge 1,\]
and we must minimise $n=a+b+c$.

\textbf{A construction with $12$ bricks.} Since $6\cdot125=750>700$, we have $a\le5$. Take $a=5$: the volume left is $700-625=75$, and
\[75=27\cdot1+8\cdot6,\]
so $a=5$, $b=1$, $c=6$ works and uses $12$ bricks (check: $625+27+48=700$, and all three sizes occur).

\textbf{No fewer than $12$ bricks is possible.} Fix $a$ and put $M=700-125a$. From $27b+8c=M$ we get $c=\dfrac{M-27b}{8}$, hence
\[b+c=b+\dfrac{M-27b}{8}=\dfrac{M-19b}{8},\]
which \emph{decreases} as $b$ increases. So for a given $a$ the fewest bricks come from the largest admissible $b$. The equation forces $3b\equiv M \pmod 8$, that is $b\equiv 3M\pmod 8$ (because $3\cdot3\equiv1\pmod 8$), and also $b\le\dfrac{M-8}{27}$, since $c\ge1$. Going through $a=1,2,3,4,5$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $a=1$: $M=575$, $b\equiv 5\pmod 8$, $b\le 21$; the largest is $b=21$, giving $c=1$ and $23$ bricks in all;
\item $a=2$: $M=450$, $b\equiv 6\pmod 8$, $b\le 16$; the largest is $b=14$, giving $c=9$ and $25$ bricks;
\item $a=3$: $M=325$, $b\equiv 7\pmod 8$, $b\le 11$; the largest is $b=7$, giving $c=17$ and $27$ bricks;
\item $a=4$: $M=200$, so $b\equiv 0\pmod 8$, hence $b\ge 8$, but $27\cdot 8=216>200$: no solution;
\item $a=5$: $M=75$, $b\equiv 1\pmod 8$, $b\le 2$; the largest is $b=1$, giving $c=6$ and $12$ bricks.
\end{itemize}
Every possibility therefore needs at least $12$ bricks, and $12$ is attained.

Hence the least number of bricks is
\[\boxed{12}.\]

The cloak is an isosceles trapezoid with bases $2$ and $5$ feet and height $6$ feet, so its area is
\[\dfrac{2+5}{2}\cdot 6=21\text{ square feet}=21\cdot 144=3024\text{ square inches}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair V1=(-2.5,0), V2=(2.5,0), V3=(1,6), V4=(-1,6);
draw(V1--V2--V3--V4--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(V4--(-1,0),dashed+linewidth(0.6));
label("$5$ ft",(V1+V2)/2,S);
label("$2$ ft",(V4+V3)/2,N);
label("$6$ ft",(-1,3.7),W);
void hole(pair c, real t){
  pair u=c+(-t,-0.35*t), v=c+(t,-0.35*t), w=c+(0,0.65*t);
  fill(u--v--w--cycle,gray(0.8));
  draw(u--v--w--cycle,linewidth(0.6));
}
hole((-0.2,4.8),0.35);
hole((0.8,3.9),0.35);
hole((-1.4,2.8),0.35);
hole((0.8,2.2),0.35);
hole((-0.1,1.2),0.35);
\end{asy}

To maximise the remaining area we must minimise the total area removed by the five holes.

\textbf{Which holes are possible?} A hole is a triangle with integer sides $a\le b\le c$ and $a+b+c=16$. The triangle inequality gives $c<\dfrac{16}{2}=8$, so $c\le 7$, and then $a+b=16-c$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $c=7$: $a+b=9$ with $a\le b\le 7$, giving $(2,7,7)$, $(3,6,7)$, $(4,5,7)$;
\item $c=6$: $a+b=10$ with $a\le b\le 6$, giving $(4,6,6)$, $(5,5,6)$;
\item $c\le 5$: then $a+b=16-c\ge 11$ while $a\le b\le c\le 5$ forces $a+b\le 10$, impossible.
\end{itemize}
So there are exactly five non-congruent triangles with integer sides and perimeter $16$. Since the five holes are pairwise non-congruent, each of these five shapes occurs exactly once.


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(12cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
defaultpen(fontsize(8pt));
pair L1=(0,0), R1=(7,0), P1=(6.7143,1.9795);
draw(L1--R1--P1--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
label("$7$",(L1+R1)/2,S); label("$7$",(L1+P1)/2,NW); label("$2$",(R1+P1)/2,NE);
pair L2=(10.2,0), R2=(17.2,0), P2=(15.6286,2.5555);
draw(L2--R2--P2--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
label("$7$",(L2+R2)/2,S); label("$6$",(L2+P2)/2,NW); label("$3$",(R2+P2)/2,NE);
pair L3=(20.4,0), R3=(27.4,0), P3=(24.5429,2.7994);
draw(L3--R3--P3--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
label("$7$",(L3+R3)/2,S); label("$5$",(L3+P3)/2,NW); label("$4$",(R3+P3)/2,NE);
pair L4=(0,-9), R4=(6,-9), P4=(4.6667,-5.2288);
draw(L4--R4--P4--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
label("$6$",(L4+R4)/2,S); label("$6$",(L4+P4)/2,NW); label("$4$",(R4+P4)/2,NE);
pair L5=(9.2,-9), R5=(15.2,-9), P5=(12.2,-5);
draw(L5--R5--P5--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
label("$6$",(L5+R5)/2,S); label("$5$",(L5+P5)/2,NW); label("$5$",(R5+P5)/2,NE);
\end{asy}

\textbf{Their areas.} Every hole has semiperimeter $s=8$, so Heron's formula gives area $\sqrt{8(8-a)(8-b)(8-c)}$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $(2,7,7)$: $\sqrt{8\cdot 6\cdot 1\cdot 1}=\sqrt{48}=4\sqrt3\approx 6.9282$;
\item $(3,6,7)$: $\sqrt{8\cdot 5\cdot 2\cdot 1}=\sqrt{80}=4\sqrt5\approx 8.9443$;
\item $(4,5,7)$: $\sqrt{8\cdot 4\cdot 3\cdot 1}=\sqrt{96}=4\sqrt6\approx 9.7980$;
\item $(4,6,6)$: $\sqrt{8\cdot 4\cdot 2\cdot 2}=\sqrt{128}=8\sqrt2\approx 11.3137$;
\item $(5,5,6)$: $\sqrt{8\cdot 3\cdot 3\cdot 2}=\sqrt{144}=12$.
\end{itemize}
The total area removed is therefore
\[12+4\sqrt3+4\sqrt5+4\sqrt6+8\sqrt2\approx 6.9282+8.9443+9.7980+11.3137+12=48.9842\]
square inches, so the largest possible remaining area is
\[3024-\left(12+4\sqrt3+4\sqrt5+4\sqrt6+8\sqrt2\right)\approx 2975.02\]
square inches, that is,
\[\boxed{3024-12-4\sqrt3-4\sqrt5-4\sqrt6-8\sqrt2}\]
square inches, about $2975$ square inches.

question:
Let $P(x)$ be a polynomial with real coefficients and let $r$ be a real number. When $P(x)$ is divided by $(x-r)^2$, the remainder is a polynomial $ax+b$ of degree at most $1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $b=P(r)-ar$, and that $a$ is the coefficient of $t$ in the expansion of $P(r+t)$ in powers of $t$.
\item[(b)] Deduce that the remainder equals $P'(r)(x-r)+P(r)$, and use this to find the remainder when $x^{100}$ is divided by $(x-1)^2$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $a=BC$, $b=CA$ and $c=AB$. By the law of sines,
\[\dfrac{a}{\sin 40^\circ}=\dfrac{b}{\sin 50^\circ}=\dfrac{c}{\sin 90^\circ}.\]
Since $\sin 90^\circ=1$ and $\sin 50^\circ=\cos 40^\circ$, the sides are proportional to
\[BC:CA:AB=\sin 40^\circ:\cos 40^\circ:1.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real th=40*pi/180;
real cc=cos(th), ss=sin(th);
pair A=(0,0), B=(1,0), C=(cc*cc,cc*ss);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
pair d1=(A-C)/length(A-C), d2=(B-C)/length(B-C);
real sq=0.07;
draw(C+sq*d1--C+sq*d1+sq*d2--C+sq*d2,linewidth(0.7));
label("$A$",A,SW);
label("$B$",B,SE);
label("$C$",C,N);
label("$40^\circ$",(0.115,0.045));
label("$50^\circ$",(0.885,0.045));
label("$1$",(0.5,-0.085));
label("$\cos 40^\circ$",(0.235,0.315),NW);
label("$\sin 40^\circ$",(0.855,0.315),NE);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C);
\end{asy}

Numerically $\sin 40^\circ\approx 0.642788$ and $\cos 40^\circ\approx 0.766044$, so the ratio is about $0.643:0.766:1$, with the hypotenuse longest as it must be.

Unlike the $30^\circ$-$60^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle, no expression using only rational numbers and square roots exists. Indeed, from $\cos 120^\circ=-\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\cos 3\theta=4\cos^3\theta-3\cos\theta$, the number $x=\cos 40^\circ$ satisfies
\[8x^3-6x+1=0.\]
This cubic has no rational root (the candidates are $\pm1$, $\pm\dfrac{1}{2}$, $\pm\dfrac{1}{4}$, $\pm\dfrac{1}{8}$, and none gives $0$: for example $3$, $-1$, $-1$, $3$, $-\dfrac{3}{8}$, $\dfrac{19}{8}$, $\dfrac{17}{64}$, $\dfrac{111}{64}$ respectively), so it is irreducible over the rationals and $x=\cos 40^\circ$ has degree $3$ over the rationals. A number expressible using only rational numbers and square roots lies in a tower of quadratic extensions and therefore has degree over the rationals equal to a power of $2$; since $3$ is not a power of $2$, no such expression exists.

Hence $BC:CA:AB=\boxed{\sin 40^\circ:\cos 40^\circ:1}$, approximately $0.643:0.766:1$.

question:
Does $0^0$ have a well-defined value?
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that no value can be assigned to $0^0$ so that the function $f(x,y)=x^y$ becomes continuous at $(0,0)$: compare $x^0$ for $x\to0^+$ with $0^y$ for $y\to0^+$.
\item[(b)] Explain why, in algebra and combinatorics, $0^0$ is nevertheless taken to be $1$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Since only angles are involved, we may scale the figure so that $AC=2$. Place $A=(0,0)$ and $C=(2,0)$ on the $x$-axis, so that $P=(1,0)$. Since $\angle BAC=30^\circ$, the point $B$ lies on the ray from $A$ making a $30^\circ$ angle with the positive $x$-axis, hence
\[B=\left(\dfrac{t\sqrt3}{2},\dfrac{t}{2}\right)\qquad\text{with } t=AB>0.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), C=(2,0), Pm=(1,0);
pair B=((3+sqrt(3))/2,(sqrt(3)+1)/2);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--Pm,dashed+linewidth(0.8));
draw((0.5,-0.07)--(0.5,0.07),linewidth(0.7));
draw((1.5,-0.07)--(1.5,0.07),linewidth(0.7));
label("$A$",A,SW);
label("$B$",B,N);
label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$P$",Pm,S);
label("$30^\circ$",(0.34,0.09));
label("$45^\circ$",(1.42,0.17));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(Pm);
\end{asy}

The ray $PC$ points in the positive $x$-direction and $B$ lies above the line $AC$, so $\angle BPC=45^\circ$ says that the ray $PB$ makes a $45^\circ$ angle with the positive $x$-axis. Thus $PB$ has slope $1$, with $B$ to the right of $P$ (that is, $\dfrac{t\sqrt3}{2}>1$), and
\[\dfrac{\dfrac{t}{2}}{\dfrac{t\sqrt3}{2}-1}=1
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\dfrac{t}{2}=\dfrac{t\sqrt3}{2}-1
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
t(\sqrt3-1)=2
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
t=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3-1}=\sqrt3+1 .\]
(This value satisfies $t\sqrt3>2$, so $B$ is indeed to the right of $P$; taking $B$ below the line $AC$ just reflects the figure.) Hence
\[B=\left(\dfrac{3+\sqrt3}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt3+1}{2}\right).\]

Now compute the angle at $B$. We have
\[|BA|=t=\sqrt3+1,\qquad
|BC|^2=\left(\dfrac{3+\sqrt3}{2}-2\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{\sqrt3+1}{2}\right)^2
=\dfrac{(\sqrt3-1)^2+(\sqrt3+1)^2}{4}=2,\]
so $|BC|=\sqrt2$, and
\[\overrightarrow{BA}\cdot\overrightarrow{BC}
=\left(-\dfrac{3+\sqrt3}{2}\right)\left(\dfrac{1-\sqrt3}{2}\right)+\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt3+1}{2}\right)\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt3+1}{2}\right)
=\dfrac{2\sqrt3+(4+2\sqrt3)}{4}=1+\sqrt3 .\]
Therefore
\[\cos\angle ABC=\dfrac{\overrightarrow{BA}\cdot\overrightarrow{BC}}{|BA|\,|BC|}
=\dfrac{1+\sqrt3}{(\sqrt3+1)\sqrt2}=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2},\]
and since $\angle ABC$ is an angle of a triangle it lies between $0^\circ$ and $180^\circ$, so $\angle ABC=45^\circ$.

Check with the law of sines: if the angles are $30^\circ,45^\circ,105^\circ$ and the circumdiameter is $D=2\sqrt2$, then $BC=D\sin30^\circ=\sqrt2$, $CA=D\sin45^\circ=2$ and $AB=D\sin105^\circ=\sqrt3+1$, matching the side lengths found above. Since the equation for $t$ has a unique positive solution, the triangle is determined and the answer is unique.

Thus $\angle ABC=\boxed{45^\circ}$.

question:
Recall that the sum of the interior angles of an \(n\)-gon is \(180^\circ(n-2)\).

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] For a regular \(n\)-gon, find the least positive integer \(n\) for which every interior angle is greater than \(179^\circ\).
\item[(b)] A student says: ``A square has interior angle sum \(360^\circ\), and a circle is also said to have \(360^\circ\), so a circle behaves like a polygon with interior angle sum \(360^\circ\).'' Explain the mistake by describing what happens to the angle sum and to each interior angle of a regular \(n\)-gon as \(n\) becomes large.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
For a regular \(n\)-gon the total interior-angle sum is \(180^\circ(n-2)\), so each interior angle equals
\[
180^\circ-\dfrac{360^\circ}{n}.
\]
\textbf{(a)} We need
\[
180-\dfrac{360}{n}>179
\iff 1>\dfrac{360}{n}
\iff n>360.
\]
For \(n=360\) each angle is exactly \(179^\circ\), while for \(n=361\) each angle equals \(180-\dfrac{360}{361}>179^\circ\). Hence the least such integer is
\[
\boxed{361}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
defaultpen(fontsize(8pt));
pair[] q={(0,2),(-1.732,1),(-1.732,-1),(0,-2),(1.732,-1),(1.732,1)};
draw(q[0]--q[1]--q[2]--q[3]--q[4]--q[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
pair O=(0,0);
draw(O--q[0],dashed+red+linewidth(0.6));
draw(O--q[1],dashed+red+linewidth(0.6));
dot(q[0]); dot(q[1]); dot(O);
label("$O$",O+(-0.34,-0.24),SW);
label("$180^\circ-\dfrac{360^\circ}{n}$",(0.32,1.28),E);
label("$\dfrac{360^\circ}{n}$",(-0.72,0.80),W);
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} The total interior-angle sum \(180(n-2)\) grows without bound as \(n\) grows; there is no polygon, however many sides it has, whose angle sum stops at \(360^\circ\). What approaches \(180^\circ\) is each individual interior angle \(180-\dfrac{360}{n}\), that is, the angle at a smooth point of a curve. The number \(360^\circ\) attached to a circle measures one complete turn, that is, a central angle, and it is not the sum of any interior angles; its agreement with the square's angle sum is accidental.

Label the switches \(1,2,3\) and act as follows.

\begin{enumerate}
\item Turn switch \(1\) on and leave it on for several minutes.
\item Turn switch \(1\) off, turn switch \(2\) on, and leave switch \(3\) off.
\item Enter the room and examine the bulb.
\end{enumerate}
Then:
\begin{itemize}
\item if the bulb is lit, switch \(2\) controls it;
\item if the bulb is off but warm, switch \(1\) controls it;
\item if the bulb is off and cool, switch \(3\) controls it.
\end{itemize}
Indeed, only switch \(2\) is on at the moment you enter, so a lit bulb means switch \(2\); switch \(1\) was on long enough to heat the bulb and was then turned off, so a warm unlit bulb means switch \(1\); and switch \(3\) was never on, so a cool unlit bulb means switch \(3\). These three cases are mutually exclusive and cover all possibilities, so the strategy always succeeds:
\[
\boxed{\text{lit} \Rightarrow 2,\qquad \text{off and warm} \Rightarrow 1,\qquad \text{off and cool} \Rightarrow 3.}
\]

The standard form of a circle is
\[
(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2=r^2,
\]
with center \((h,k)\) and radius \(r\). Comparing with
\[
(x-9)^2+(y+4)^2=25=(x-9)^2+(y-(-4))^2=5^2,
\]
we get
\[
h=9,\qquad k=-4,\qquad r=5.
\]
Thus the center is \((9,-4)\) and the radius is \(5\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Cc=(9,-4);
draw((-1,0)--(15,0),linewidth(0.7));
draw((0,-10)--(0,2),linewidth(0.7));
draw(circle(Cc,5),linewidth(0.9));
dot(Cc);
draw(Cc--(14,-4),red+dashed+linewidth(0.8));
label("$(9,-4)$",Cc,SW);
label("$5$",(11.5,-3.7),N);
label("$x$",(15,0),E);
label("$y$",(0,2),N);
\end{asy}

\[
\boxed{\text{center }(9,-4),\ \text{radius }5}.
\]

question:
In triangle \(ABC\), \(\angle BAC=24^\circ\) and \(\angle ACB=30^\circ\). Point \(D\) lies on segment \(AC\) such that \(AB=CD\). Prove that
\[
AB\cdot BC=BD\cdot AC.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=6;
pair A=(s,0), C=(0,0);
pair B=(2.6123,1.5083);
pair D=(3.7082,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--D,linewidth(0.8));
label("$A$",A,SE); label("$B$",B,N); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$D$",D,S);
label("$24^\circ$",(5.0,0.35));
label("$30^\circ$",(0.8,0.35));
pair M1=(A+B)/2; pair u1=B-A; pair p1=(-u1.y,u1.x)/abs(u1);
draw(M1-0.12*p1--M1+0.12*p1,linewidth(0.8));
pair M2=(C+D)/2;
draw(M2-(0,0.12)--M2+(0,0.12),linewidth(0.8));
\end{asy}

solution:
We give a synthetic proof.

\textbf{Step 1: Construct the circumcenter.} Construct equilateral triangle \(ABE\) on the side of \(AB\) that contains \(C\). Since \(\angle BAC=24^\circ\), we have
\[
\angle CAE=60^\circ-24^\circ=36^\circ.
\]
Also \(\angle AEB=60^\circ\), while \(\angle ACB=30^\circ\). For the circle through \(A\) and \(B\) with center \(E\), the minor arc \(AB\) has central angle \(60^\circ\), so by the inscribed angle theorem every point on the major arc \(AB\) sees chord \(AB\) under angle \(\dfrac{60^\circ}{2}=30^\circ\). The locus of points on the chosen side of \(AB\) from which \(AB\) subtends \(30^\circ\) is exactly that major arc. Hence \(C\) lies on this circle, so \(E\) is the circumcenter of \(\triangle ABC\). In particular,
\[
EA=EB=EC.
\]
Since \(EA=EC\), triangle \(ACE\) is isosceles, so
\[
\angle ACE=\angle CAE=36^\circ.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=6;
pair A=(s,0), C=(0,0);
pair B=(2.6123,1.5083);
pair D=(3.7082,0);
pair Q=(3.0,-2.1796);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--D,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--Q--B,linewidth(0.9)+blue);
draw(C--Q,dashed+gray);
label("$A$",A,SE); label("$B$",B,N); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$D$",D,S); label("$E$",Q,S);
label("$24^\circ$",(5.0,0.35)); label("$30^\circ$",(0.8,0.35));
\end{asy}

Since \(EC=AB=CD\), triangle \(CDE\) is isosceles with \(CD=CE\). Also \(\angle DCE=\angle ACE=36^\circ\). Hence
\[
\angle CDE=\angle DEC=\dfrac{180^\circ-36^\circ}{2}=72^\circ.
\]

\textbf{Step 2: Locate the angle at \(D\).} Since \(A,D,C\) are collinear and \(D\) lies between \(A\) and \(C\),
\[
\angle ADE=180^\circ-\angle CDE=108^\circ.
\]
Also \(\angle DAE=\angle CAE=36^\circ\). Thus in triangle \(ADE\),
\[
\angle AED=180^\circ-108^\circ-36^\circ=36^\circ.
\]
So \(\angle DAE=\angle AED\), giving \(DA=DE\).

Now \(B\) and \(D\) are both equidistant from \(A\) and \(E\): \(BA=BE\) because \(ABE\) is equilateral, and \(DA=DE\) just proved. Hence line \(BD\) is the perpendicular bisector of \(AE\). In equilateral triangle \(ABE\), the perpendicular bisector from \(B\) is also the angle bisector, so
\[
\angle ABD=\dfrac{60^\circ}{2}=30^\circ.
\]
Then in triangle \(ABD\),
\[
\angle ADB=180^\circ-\angle BAD-\angle ABD
=180^\circ-24^\circ-30^\circ=126^\circ.
\]
Since \(A,D,C\) are collinear,
\[
\angle BDC=180^\circ-\angle ADB=54^\circ.
\]

\textbf{Step 3: A similarity.} In triangle \(BCD\), \(\angle BCD=30^\circ\) and \(\angle BDC=54^\circ\), so
\[
\angle CBD=180^\circ-30^\circ-54^\circ=96^\circ.
\]
Choose \(F\) on segment \(CD\) so that \(\angle CBF=24^\circ\). This is possible because \(24^\circ<96^\circ\). Then in triangle \(BCF\),
\[
\angle BCF=30^\circ,\quad \angle CBF=24^\circ,\quad \angle BFC=126^\circ.
\]
Thus \(\triangle BCF\sim\triangle ACB\) (with \(B\leftrightarrow A\), \(C\leftrightarrow C\), \(F\leftrightarrow B\)). Consequently,
\[
\dfrac{BF}{BC}=\dfrac{AB}{AC}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=6;
pair A=(s,0), C=(0,0);
pair B=(2.6123,1.5083);
pair D=(3.7082,0);
pair Fp=(1.5168,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--D,linewidth(0.8));
draw(B--Fp,linewidth(0.9)+blue);
label("$A$",A,SE); label("$B$",B,N); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$D$",D,S); label("$F$",Fp,S);
label("$24^\circ$",(5.0,0.35)); label("$30^\circ$",(0.8,0.35));
\end{asy}

Now in triangle \(BDF\),
\[
\angle DBF=\angle DBC-\angle FBC=96^\circ-24^\circ=72^\circ,
\]
and
\[
\angle BDF=\angle BDC=54^\circ.
\]
Hence
\[
\angle BFD=180^\circ-72^\circ-54^\circ=54^\circ.
\]
So \(\angle BDF=\angle BFD\), which gives \(BF=BD\). Combining with the similarity ratio,
\[
\dfrac{BD}{BC}=\dfrac{BF}{BC}=\dfrac{AB}{AC},
\]
and therefore
\[
BD\cdot AC=AB\cdot BC.
\]
This proves the desired identity.

question:
Let \(m_1,m_2,m_3\) be the midpoints of \(\overline{AB},\overline{BC},\overline{CA}\), respectively, in triangle \(ABC\). Determine whether the statement
\[
\triangle ABC\sim\triangle m_1m_2m_3
\]
is true as written, with the vertices matched in that order. If it is false, give the correct vertex correspondence and prove that the medial triangle is similar to \(\triangle ABC\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(5,0), C=(1,3);
pair M1=(A+B)/2, M2=(B+C)/2, M3=(C+A)/2;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(M1--M2--M3--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(M1); dot(M2); dot(M3);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N);
label("$m_1$",M1,S); label("$m_2$",M2,E); label("$m_3$",M3,W);
\end{asy}

solution:
The ordered claim is false as written, but the medial triangle is similar to \(\triangle ABC\) with a different vertex order.

By the midpoint theorem,
\[
m_1m_2\parallel AC,\quad m_1m_2=\dfrac{AC}{2};
\]
\[
m_2m_3\parallel AB,\quad m_2m_3=\dfrac{AB}{2};
\]
\[
m_3m_1\parallel BC,\quad m_3m_1=\dfrac{BC}{2}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(5,0), C=(1,3);
pair M1=(A+B)/2, M2=(B+C)/2, M3=(C+A)/2;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(M1--M2--M3--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(M1); dot(M2); dot(M3);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N);
label("$m_1$",M1,S); label("$m_2$",M2,E); label("$m_3$",M3,W);
\end{asy}

Therefore the sides of \(\triangle m_1m_2m_3\) are half the corresponding sides of \(\triangle ABC\), with
\[
AB\leftrightarrow m_2m_3,\qquad
BC\leftrightarrow m_3m_1,\qquad
CA\leftrightarrow m_1m_2.
\]
Thus the correct similarity is
\[
\triangle ABC\sim\triangle m_2m_3m_1,
\]
with scale factor \(\dfrac{1}{2}\) from \(\triangle ABC\) to the medial triangle.

To see that the posted order fails in general, note that \(\triangle ABC\sim\triangle m_1m_2m_3\) would require
\[
\dfrac{AB}{m_1m_2}=\dfrac{BC}{m_2m_3}=\dfrac{CA}{m_3m_1},
\]
that is,
\[
\dfrac{AB}{\dfrac{AC}{2}}=\dfrac{BC}{\dfrac{AB}{2}}=\dfrac{CA}{\dfrac{BC}{2}}.
\]
For example, in a triangle with \(AB=4\), \(BC=5\), \(CA=3\), the first ratio equals \(\dfrac{4}{\dfrac{3}{2}}=\dfrac{8}{3}\), while the second equals \(\dfrac{5}{\dfrac{4}{2}}=\dfrac{5}{2}\), so the ordered claim is false. The correct statement is \(\triangle ABC\sim\triangle m_2m_3m_1\).

question:
A circle of radius $4$ is internally tangent to a circle of radius $8$. The smaller circle rolls without slipping once around the inside of the larger circle and returns to its starting position. A point $P$ is marked on the smaller circle.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that during this motion $P$ moves back and forth along a straight line segment.
\item[(b)] Find the total distance travelled by $P$ during one complete trip around the inside of the larger circle.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $O$ be the centre of the large circle, of radius $R=8$, and let $C$ be the centre of the small circle, of radius $r=4$. The circles are always internally tangent at some point $T$, so $OC=R-r=4$ at all times.

Place $O$ at the origin. Let $A=(8,0)$ be the point of the large circle at which $P$ is the point of tangency at the start; this is the case drawn. While the small circle rolls, $C$ moves on the circle of radius $4$ about $O$, so after the centre has turned through an angle $t$ (radians) we may write
\[ C=(4\cos t,\;4\sin t). \]
The tangency point is $T=(8\cos t,\;8\sin t)=2C$, because $T$ lies on the ray $OC$ at distance $8$ from $O$.


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=8, rr=4;
real t=70*pi/180;
pair O=(0,0), C=rr*(cos(t),sin(t)), Tp=(R*cos(t),R*sin(t)), Pt=(R*cos(t),0);
draw(circle(O,R),linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(C,rr),linewidth(0.9));
draw((-R,0)--(R,0),red+dashed+linewidth(0.8));
draw(O--Tp,dashed+linewidth(0.7));
draw(C--Pt,linewidth(0.7));
dot(O); dot(C); dot(Tp); dot(Pt);
label("$O$",O,SW);
label("$C$",C,W);
label("$T$",Tp,N);
label("$P$",Pt,S);
label("$A$",(R,0),SE);
label("$A'$",(-R,0),W);
label("$4$",(O+C)/2,NW);
label("$8$",(6,0),N);
\end{asy}


\textbf{The rolling condition.} The arc of the large circle from $A$ to $T$ has length $8t$. Rolling without slipping lays exactly this length of the rim of the small circle onto the large circle, so the arc of the small circle from $P$ to the current contact point $T$ also has length $8t$. As the small circle has radius $4$, that arc subtends the angle
\[ \angle PCT=\dfrac{8t}{4}=2t \]
at $C$.

Now $C$ travels counterclockwise about $O$, so the contact point moves \emph{clockwise} around the rim of the small circle. The ray $CT$ points in direction $t$, hence the ray $CP$ points in direction $t-2t=-t$, and
\[ P=C+4\bigl(\cos(-t),\sin(-t)\bigr)=(4\cos t+4\cos t,\;4\sin t-4\sin t)=(8\cos t,\,0). \]

\textbf{Conclusion for this position.} For every $t$ the point $P$ lies on the diameter of the large circle through $A$, at signed distance $8\cos t$ from $O$. As $t$ runs from $0$ to $\pi$, $P$ travels from $A=(8,0)$ to the antipodal point $A'=(-8,0)$, a distance of $16$; as $t$ runs from $\pi$ to $2\pi$, where the small circle returns to its starting position, $P$ travels back from $A'$ to $A$, another $16$. Thus the total distance is $16+16=32$.

\textbf{An arbitrary marked point.} If the mark is not initially at the contact point, let its initial radius make an angle $\alpha$ with the initial contact radius. The rolling condition is unchanged, so the small circle has the same clockwise rotation; hence the radius to the mark has direction $\alpha-t$, and
\[ P=4(\cos t,\sin t)+4(\cos(\alpha-t),\sin(\alpha-t))
=8\cos\left(t-\dfrac{\alpha}{2}\right)\left(\cos\dfrac{\alpha}{2},\sin\dfrac{\alpha}{2}\right). \]
So $P$ still moves along a diameter, and its speed is $8\left|\sin\left(t-\dfrac{\alpha}{2}\right)\right|$. Since
\[ \int_0^{2\pi}\left|\sin\left(t-\dfrac{\alpha}{2}\right)\right|\,dt=4, \]
the total distance is $8\cdot 4=\boxed{32}$.

Let $P=AC\cap BD$. Since $AB\parallel DC$, triangles $APB$ and $CPD$ are similar (the angles at $P$ are vertical, and $\angle PAB=\angle PCD$ are alternate interior angles). Hence
\[ \dfrac{AP}{PC}=\dfrac{BP}{PD}=\dfrac{AB}{DC}=\dfrac{m}{n},\qquad\text{so}\qquad \dfrac{AP}{AC}=\dfrac{m}{m+n}. \]


\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Dp=(0,0), Cp=(8,0), Ap=(2,5), Bp=(5,5);
pair Pt=(40/11,40/11), Et=(16/11,40/11), Ft=(64/11,40/11);
fill(Dp--Cp--Bp--Ap--cycle,paleblue);
draw(Dp--Cp--Bp--Ap--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(Ap--Cp,linewidth(0.7));
draw(Bp--Dp,linewidth(0.7));
draw(Et--Ft,blue+linewidth(1.0));
dot(Pt); dot(Et); dot(Ft);
label("$D$",Dp,SW); label("$C$",Cp,SE);
label("$A$",Ap,NW); label("$B$",Bp,NE);
label("$E$",Et,W); label("$F$",Ft,E); label("$P$",Pt,N);
label("$m$",(Ap+Bp)/2,N); label("$n$",(Dp+Cp)/2,S);
\end{asy}


In triangle $ACD$ the segment $EP$ joins $AD$ to $AC$ and is parallel to $CD$, so triangles $AEP$ and $ADC$ are similar and
\[ \dfrac{EP}{DC}=\dfrac{AP}{AC}=\dfrac{m}{m+n},\qquad\text{that is,}\qquad EP=\dfrac{mn}{m+n}. \]
Likewise, in triangle $BCD$ the segment $FP$ is parallel to $CD$, and $\dfrac{BP}{BD}=\dfrac{m}{m+n}$, so
\[ \dfrac{FP}{DC}=\dfrac{BP}{BD}=\dfrac{m}{m+n},\qquad FP=\dfrac{mn}{m+n}. \]
Adding the two pieces,
\[ EF=EP+PF=\dfrac{2mn}{m+n}. \]
(Both $E$ and $F$ really lie on the legs, since $0<\dfrac{m}{m+n}<1$.)

So $EF$ is an integer exactly when $(m+n)\mid 2mn$. Write $m=gx$, $n=gy$ where $g=\gcd(m,n)$ and $\gcd(x,y)=1$. As $m\neq n$ we have $x\neq y$, and
\[ EF=\dfrac{2gxy}{x+y}. \]
If a prime $\ell$ divides $x+y$ and $x$, then $\ell$ divides $(x+y)-x=y$, contradicting $\gcd(x,y)=1$; so $\gcd(x+y,xy)=1$. Hence the divisibility condition is equivalent to
\[ (x+y)\mid 2g. \]
Let $s=x+y$. Since $x\neq y$ are positive integers, $s\ge 3$, and $s\mid 2g$ forces $g\ge\dfrac{s}{2}$.

\begin{itemize}
\item $s=3$: then $\{x,y\}=\{1,2\}$ and $3\mid 2g$ forces $3\mid g$, so $g\ge 3$ and $m+n=gs\ge 9$.
\item $s=4$: then $\{x,y\}=\{1,3\}$ and $4\mid 2g$ forces $g$ even, so $g\ge 2$; with $g=2$ we get $(m,n)=(2,6)$, $m+n=8$, and indeed $EF=\dfrac{2\cdot 2\cdot 6}{2+6}=3$, an integer.
\item $s\ge 5$: then $m+n=gs\ge\dfrac{s^2}{2}\ge\dfrac{25}{2}>8$.
\end{itemize}
The value $8$ is attained by $(m,n)=(2,6)$ (or $(6,2)$), and every other admissible pair has $m+n\ge 9$ or $m+n>8$. Therefore the least possible value is $\boxed{8}$.

question:
Nine seats stand in a row. Three different professors \(A\), \(B\) and \(C\) are to sit in three of the seats, and \(A\) and \(B\) must sit in adjacent seats. A student counts the seatings in two ways:
\begin{itemize}
\item Seat \(C\) first (\(9\) ways), then put \(A\) and \(B\) in one of \(7\) pairs of adjacent seats in \(2\) orders, giving \(9\times 7\times 2\).
\item Seat \(A\) and \(B\) first in one of \(8\) adjacent pairs in \(2\) orders, then seat \(C\) in one of the \(7\) remaining seats, giving \(8\times 2\times 7\).
\end{itemize}
The two answers differ. Explain which count is wrong and why, and find the correct number of seatings.

solution:
The second count is correct. The row has \(8\) pairs of adjacent seats, \(A\) and \(B\) can occupy a pair in \(2\) orders, and \(C\) can then take any of the other \(7\) seats, each choice giving a different seating. Hence there are \(8\cdot 2\cdot 7=112\) seatings.

The first count assumes that after \(C\) sits there are always \(7\) adjacent pairs of empty seats, but that number depends on where \(C\) sits.
\begin{itemize}
\item If \(C\) takes an end seat (\(2\) choices), the other \(8\) seats form an unbroken row with \(7\) adjacent pairs.
\item If \(C\) takes one of the \(7\) inner seats, both pairs containing \(C\)'s seat are destroyed, leaving \(8-2=6\) pairs.
\end{itemize}
Done correctly, the first method gives \(2\cdot 7\cdot 2+7\cdot 6\cdot 2=28+84=112\), in agreement with the second.
\[
\boxed{112}
\]

question:
A standard \(52\)-card deck is shuffled. You may bet on one of two events:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] a single card drawn at random is the ace of spades;
\item[(ii)] two cards drawn at random without replacement are, in order, a diamond and then an ace.
\end{itemize}
Find the probability of each event. Which bet is better?

solution:
Write \(1568=m+(m+1)+\cdots+(m+k-1)=\dfrac{k(2m+k-1)}{2}\) with \(k\ge 2\), \(m\ge 1\). Then \(k(2m+k-1)=3136=2^6\cdot 7^2\). The factors \(k\) and \(2m+k-1\) have opposite parity and \(2m+k-1>k\). So the odd factor is one of \(1,7,49\), and the other factor is a multiple of \(64\):
\begin{itemize}
\item \(k=7\), \(2m+6=448\): \(m=221\), the sum \(221+\cdots+227\);
\item \(k=49\), \(2m+48=64\): \(m=8\), the sum \(8+\cdots+56\);
\item odd factor \(1\) gives \(k=1\) (not allowed) or \(k=3136\) with \(2m+k-1=1\) (impossible); \(k=64\) or \(k=448\) would need the smaller factor \(49\) or \(7\) to be \(2m+k-1\), which is impossible.
\end{itemize}
So there are exactly two ways, with \(7\) and \(49\) terms. The largest number of terms is \(\boxed{49}\).

Rotate the plane by \(60^\circ\) about \(C\), in the direction that carries \(A\) away from \(B\). Let \(A'\) and \(P'\) be the images of \(A\) and \(P\). Then \(CA'=CA=3\) and \(\angle BCA'=60^\circ+60^\circ=120^\circ\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), B=(5,0), A=3*dir(60), Ap=3*dir(120);
pair P=(1.9,0.85), Pp=rotate(60)*P;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(C--Ap,dashed); draw(A--Ap,dotted);
draw(A--P,blue); draw(B--P,blue); draw(C--P,blue);
draw(Ap--Pp,red); draw(Pp--P,red); draw(C--Pp,gray+dashed);
draw(Ap--B,deepgreen+linewidth(0.6)+dashed);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(Ap);dot(P);dot(Pp);
label("$C$",C,S); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$A$",A,N); label("$A'$",Ap,NW);
label("$P$",P,SE); label("$P'$",Pp,W);
label("$3$",Ap/2,SW); label("$5$",B/2,S); label("$3$",0.3*A,E);
label("$120^\circ$",C,4*dir(95),fontsize(8pt));
label("$7$",(Ap+B)/2,NE,deepgreen);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The rotation preserves lengths, so \(A'P'=AP\). Triangle \(CPP'\) has \(CP=CP'\) and \(\angle PCP'=60^\circ\), so it is equilateral and \(PP'=CP\). Hence
\[
AP+BP+CP=A'P'+P'P+PB\ge A'B,
\]
because the broken path \(A'\to P'\to P\to B\) is at least as long as the segment \(A'B\). By the law of cosines in triangle \(A'CB\),
\[
A'B^2=3^2+5^2-2\cdot 3\cdot 5\cos 120^\circ=9+25+15=49,\qquad A'B=7.
\]
Equality needs \(P'\) and \(P\) to lie on segment \(A'B\) in that order. This happens at the Fermat point, the point from which each side subtends \(120^\circ\). Such an interior point exists because every angle of the triangle is less than \(120^\circ\): \(AB^2=9+25-15=19\), and \(\cos A=\dfrac{9+19-25}{6\sqrt{19}}>0\), \(\cos B=\dfrac{25+19-9}{10\sqrt{19}}>0\), while \(C=60^\circ\). Then \(\angle CPP'=60^\circ\) and \(\angle CPB=120^\circ\) make \(P',P,B\) collinear, and \(\angle CP'P=60^\circ\) with \(\angle CP'A'=\angle CPA=120^\circ\) makes \(A',P',P\) collinear.

The minimum value is \(\boxed{7}\).

Triangle \(OAB\) is \(\{x\le 0,\ y\ge 0,\ y\le x+1\}\), so
\[
T_1=\{x\le t,\ y\ge 0,\ y\le x-t+1\}.
\]
Triangle \(OCD\) is \(\{x\ge 0,\ y\le 0,\ y\ge x-1\}\), so
\[
T_2=\{x\ge 0,\ y\le 2t,\ y\ge x+2t-1\}.
\]
The common region is the rectangle \(R=[0,t]\times[0,2t]\) cut by the two lines \(y=x-t+1\) and \(y=x+2t-1\), both of slope \(1\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real t=3/7;
pair[] T1={(t,0),(t,1),(t-1,0)};
pair[] T2={(0,2*t),(0,2*t-1),(1,2*t)};
draw((-1.2,0)--(1.3,0),gray,Arrow(TeXHead)); draw((0,-0.3)--(0,1.25),gray,Arrow(TeXHead));
fill((0,0)--(1-2*t,0)--(t,3*t-1)--(t,2*t)--(3*t-1,2*t)--(0,1-t)--cycle,paleyellow);
draw((0,0)--(1-2*t,0)--(t,3*t-1)--(t,2*t)--(3*t-1,2*t)--(0,1-t)--cycle,deepgreen+linewidth(1.2));
draw(T1[0]--T1[1]--T1[2]--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(T2[0]--T2[1]--T2[2]--cycle,red+linewidth(0.9));
label("$T_1$",(t-0.75,0.12),blue); label("$T_2$",(0.8,2*t-0.08),red);
label("$x$",(1.3,0),E); label("$y$",(0,1.25),N);
label("$t$",(t,0),S); label("$2t$",(0,2*t),W);
label("$O$",(0,0),SW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The line \(y=x-t+1\) passes above the corner \((0,2t)\) iff \(1-t\ge 2t\), that is, \(t\le\dfrac{1}{3}\). Similarly the line \(y=x+2t-1\) passes below the corner \((t,0)\) iff \(3t-1\le 0\). So for \(t\le\dfrac{1}{3}\) the region is the whole rectangle (four sides).

For \(t>\dfrac{1}{3}\), each line cuts off an isosceles right triangle with legs \(3t-1\) at the corners \((0,2t)\) and \((t,0)\). For a hexagon, these cuts must not reach the neighbouring corners. The legs must be shorter than the width \(t\) of the rectangle (the height \(2t\) is larger): \(3t-1<t\iff t<\dfrac{1}{2}\). At \(t=\dfrac{1}{2}\) the cut at \((t,0)\) reaches the origin and the region becomes a quadrilateral, and for larger \(t\) it stays a quadrilateral or vanishes. Hence \(a=\dfrac{1}{2}\).

For \(\dfrac{1}{3}<t<\dfrac{1}{2}\), the area is
\[
2t^2-2\cdot\dfrac{(3t-1)^2}{2}=-7t^2+6t-1=-7\left(t-\dfrac{3}{7}\right)^2+\dfrac{2}{7},
\]
which is maximised at \(t=\dfrac{3}{7}\), inside the interval, giving \(M=\dfrac{2}{7}\). Therefore
\[
a+M=\dfrac{1}{2}+\dfrac{2}{7}=\boxed{\dfrac{11}{14}}.
\]

By the inscribed angle theorem, each angle of triangle \(ABC\) is half of the arc opposite it (the arc not containing the third vertex).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
pair A=dir(200), B=dir(330), C=dir(80);
draw(unitcircle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,blue);
draw(arc(O,1.2,203,327),red,Arrows(TeXHead));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(O);
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,N); label("$O$",O,NE);
label("arc $AB=2\angle C$",1.22*dir(265),S,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The three arcs between consecutive points have total \(360^\circ\). For independent uniform points, the arc-length triple is uniformly distributed on the set of nonnegative triples with sum \(360^\circ\). Hence the angle triple \((\alpha,\beta,\gamma)\) is uniform on the triangle \(\{\alpha,\beta,\gamma\ge 0,\ \alpha+\beta+\gamma=180^\circ\}\).

For \(\theta\ge 90^\circ\), at most one angle can be at least \(\theta\). The region \(\alpha\ge\theta\) is a similar triangle with linear ratio \(\dfrac{180-\theta}{180}\), so
\[
P(\text{largest angle}\ge\theta)=3\left(\dfrac{180-\theta}{180}\right)^2.
\]
The three points lie on a semicircle exactly when some arc is at least \(180^\circ\), that is, some angle is at least \(90^\circ\). This has probability \(3\cdot\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^2=\dfrac{3}{4}\). The probability that some angle is at least \(120^\circ\) is \(3\cdot\left(\dfrac{1}{3}\right)^2=\dfrac{1}{3}\). Therefore
\[
P\bigl(\text{all angles}<120^\circ\mid\text{semicircle}\bigr)=\dfrac{\dfrac{3}{4}-\dfrac{1}{3}}{\dfrac{3}{4}}=\dfrac{\dfrac{5}{12}}{\dfrac{3}{4}}=\boxed{\dfrac{5}{9}}.
\]

A line of slope \(m\) through \((7,5)\) is \(y=5+m(x-7)\), with \(y\)-intercept \(5-7m\). (The slopes satisfy \(m_1m_2=-1\) and \(m_1+m_2=10\), so they are \(5\pm\sqrt{26}\), both finite and nonzero.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
pair P=(7,5);
real m1=5+sqrt(26), m2=5-sqrt(26);
draw((-1,0)--(9,0),gray,Arrow(TeXHead)); draw((0,-1)--(0,8),gray,Arrow(TeXHead));
real f1(real x){return 5+m1*(x-7);} real f2(real x){return 5+m2*(x-7);}
draw(graph(f2,-0.5,9),red);
draw(graph(f1,6.4,7.3),blue);
dot(P); label("$(7,5)$",P,NE);
label("$\ell_2$",(-0.5,f2(-0.5)),NW,red); label("$\ell_1$",(7.3,f1(7.3)),E,blue);
label("$x$",(9,0),E); label("$y$",(0,8),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The sum of the intercepts is
\[
(5-7m_1)+(5-7m_2)=10-7(m_1+m_2)=10-70=\boxed{-60}.
\]

Let \(G\) be the girls' camp, \(F\) the foot of the perpendicular from \(G\) to the road, \(B\) the boys' camp and \(C\) the canteen. Then \(GF=300\), \(GB=500\), so \(FB=\sqrt{500^2-300^2}=400\). The canteen lies on the perpendicular bisector of \(GB\); since that bisector meets the road between \(F\) and \(B\), let \(FC=x\), so that \(CB=400-x\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair G=(0,3), F=(0,0), B=(4,0), C=(0.875,0);
draw((-1.2,0)--(5,0),linewidth(0.9));
draw(G--F,dashed); draw(G--B,gray); draw(G--C,blue); 
draw(F--(0.2,0)--(0.2,0.2)--(0,0.2));
dot(G);dot(F);dot(B);dot(C);
label("$G$",G,N); label("$F$",F,S); label("$B$",B,S); label("$C$",C,S);
label("$300$",(0,1.5),W); label("$500$",(2,1.5),NE,gray);
label("$x$",(C+F)/2,S); label("$d$",(G+C)/2,E,blue); label("$d$",(C+B)/2,S,blue);
label("road",(5,0),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Setting \(CG=CB=d\):
\[
x^2+300^2=(400-x)^2\iff 90000=160000-800x\iff x=87.5.
\]
Then \(d=400-87.5=312.5\). The canteen is \(\boxed{312.5\text{ rods}}\) from each camp.

Let the triangle be \(ABC\) with angles \(\alpha=\angle A\), \(\beta=\angle B\), \(\gamma=\angle C\). Through \(C\) draw the line \(XY\) parallel to \(AB\), with \(X\) on the same side of \(C\) as \(A\) and \(Y\) on the same side as \(B\) (a unique parallel exists by the parallel postulate).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(5,0), C=(1.6,2.6);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((C-(2.6,0))--(C+(4.4,0)),blue);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N);
label("$X$",C-(2.6,0),W,blue); label("$Y$",C+(4.4,0),E,blue);
label("$\alpha$",A,5*dir(29)); label("$\beta$",B,5*dir(162)); label("$\gamma$",C,3.2*S);
label("$\alpha$",C,5*dir(195)); label("$\beta$",C,5*dir(-12));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Since \(XY\parallel AB\), with transversal \(AC\) the alternate angles are equal, \(\angle XCA=\angle CAB=\alpha\). With transversal \(BC\), \(\angle YCB=\angle CBA=\beta\). The angles \(\angle XCA\), \(\angle ACB\), \(\angle BCY\) together make up the straight angle \(XCY\), so
\[
\alpha+\gamma+\beta=180^\circ. \qquad\blacksquare
\]

The triangle is isosceles with apex \(B\), so the altitude \(BH\) bisects \(AC\): \(AH=HC=21\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
