By Pythagoras, \(BH=\sqrt{29^2-21^2}=\sqrt{400}=20\). The area is \(\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot 42\cdot 20=\boxed{420}\).

question:
In triangle \(ABC\), let \(D\), \(E\), \(F\) be the midpoints of \(BC\), \(CA\), \(AB\). Extend \(AD\) beyond \(D\) to the point \(I\) with \(D\) the midpoint of \(AI\).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Compare \(AB\) and \(CI\).
\item[(b)] Prove that \(AB+AC>2AD\).
\item[(c)] Prove that \(AB+BC+CA>AD+BE+CF\).
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] The diagonals \(AI\) and \(BC\) of quadrilateral \(ABIC\) bisect each other at \(D\), so \(ABIC\) is a parallelogram. (Equivalently, triangles \(ADB\) and \(IDC\) are congruent by SAS: \(AD=ID\), \(BD=CD\), vertical angles at \(D\).) Hence \(AB=CI\).
\end{enumerate}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0.8,3.2), B=(0,0), C=(5,0);
pair D=(B+C)/2, I=2*D-A, E1=(C+A)/2, F=(A+B)/2;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--I,blue); draw(B--I,gray+dashed); draw(C--I,red+dashed);
draw(B--E1,deepgreen); draw(C--F,deepgreen);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(I);dot(E1);dot(F);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,E); label("$D$",D,dir(-150)); label("$I$",I,S);
label("$E$",E1,NE); label("$F$",F,W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(b)] The points \(A\), \(C\), \(I\) are not collinear (otherwise \(D\) would lie on line \(AC\) and \(B\) would too). By the triangle inequality in triangle \(ACI\) and part (a),
\[
AB+AC=CI+AC>AI=2AD.
\]
\item[(c)] Applying (b) at each vertex (with the medians from \(B\) and \(C\)),
\[
AB+AC>2AD,\qquad BA+BC>2BE,\qquad CA+CB>2CF.
\]
Adding gives \(2(AB+BC+CA)>2(AD+BE+CF)\), which is the claim. \(\blacksquare\)
\end{enumerate}

question:
A magical coin lands heads with probability \(\dfrac{1}{2}\) on the first flip. After that, the probabilities on each flip depend only on the previous flip. If the previous flip was heads, the probability of heads is \(\dfrac{1}{2}\) increased by \(h\) percent of \(\dfrac{1}{2}\). If the previous flip was tails, the probability of tails is \(\dfrac{1}{2}\) increased by \(t\) percent of \(\dfrac{1}{2}\). The coin is flipped \(5\) times.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If \(h=t=50\), what is the probability that the fifth flip is heads?
\item[(b)] If \(h=75\) and \(t=25\), what is the probability that the fifth flip is heads?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The statement is \textbf{false}. Being inscribed in a circle does not force equal sides or equal angles.

Counterexample: a rectangle \(ABCD\) with \(AB=1\) and \(BC=\sqrt3\) is not a square, so it is not regular. Yet its diagonals are equal and bisect each other at their common midpoint \(O\), so \(OA=OB=OC=OD=1\), and all four vertices lie on the circle with centre \(O\) and radius \(1\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
draw(unitcircle);
pair A=dir(150), B=dir(210), C=dir(330), D=dir(30);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--D,red); dot(O); dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);
label("$O$",O,S); label("$R$",D/2,N,red);
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Similarly every triangle has a circumscribed circle, but a \(3\)-\(4\)-\(5\) triangle is not equilateral. (The converse is true: every regular polygon has a circumscribed circle.)
\[
\boxed{\text{False}}
\]

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Let \(n=p_1^{e_1}p_2^{e_2}\cdots p_k^{e_k}\) be the prime factorisation of a positive integer \(n\). Prove that the number of positive divisors of \(n\) is \((e_1+1)(e_2+1)\cdots(e_k+1)\).
\item[(b)] How many positive divisors does \(10!\) have?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Inside the circle the path consists of two perpendicular radii, each of length \(6\). They cut the disc into a quarter disc and a three-quarter disc.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
filldraw(O--(6,0)--arc(O,6,0,90)--cycle,paleyellow,nullpen);
draw(circle(O,6));
draw(6*dir(0)--O--6*dir(90),blue+linewidth(1));
dot(O); label("$O$",O,SW);
label("$6$",(3,0),S); label("$6$",(0,3),W);
label("$P$",(2.5,2.5));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
