By the Pythagorean theorem applied to \(DEF\), its hypotenuse is \(EF=\sqrt{a^2+b^2}=c\). So triangles \(ABC\) and \(DEF\) have the same three side lengths (\(a\), \(b\) and \(c\)) and are congruent by SSS. Corresponding angles are equal, so the angle of \(ABC\) opposite the side \(c\), namely \(\angle C\), equals \(\angle D=90^\circ\). \(\blacksquare\)

The technique is \emph{proof by construction and congruence}: build an auxiliary triangle known to have the property, then use SSS congruence to transfer it. (An alternative proof uses the law of cosines: \(c^2=a^2+b^2-2ab\cos C\) forces \(\cos C=0\).)

