Every square with vertices at the dots fits snugly in an axis-parallel bounding square of side \(n\) (\(1\le n\le 4\)). Conversely, inside a bounding square of side \(n\) there are exactly \(n\) inscribed squares with vertices at dots. Starting from one corner of the bounding square, go \(k\) units along each side (\(k=0,1,\dots,n-1\)). The four points so obtained form a square with side vector \((n-k,k)\); the case \(k=0\) is the bounding square itself.

