(The figure shows the case \(n=4\), \(k=1\).) Different pairs (bounding square, \(k\)) give different squares, since a square determines its bounding box and the offset \(k\). There are \((5-n)^2\) bounding squares of side \(n\), so the total is
\[
\sum_{n=1}^{4}n(5-n)^2=1\cdot 16+2\cdot 9+3\cdot 4+4\cdot 1=16+18+12+4=\boxed{50}.
\]
The poster's \(16+9+4+1=30\) counts only the upright squares; the other \(20\) are tilted.

\textbf{The principle.} Place positive masses \(m_A,m_B,m_C\) at the vertices. The centre of mass of \(\{A,B\}\) is the point of \(AB\) dividing it in the ratio \(m_B:m_A\) (the lever law: \(m_A\cdot AX=m_B\cdot XB\)), and it carries mass \(m_A+m_B\). The centre of mass \(G\) of all three masses can be found by combining any two first. Hence it lies on the segment from the third vertex to the centre of mass of the first two, and divides that segment in the inverse ratio of the masses at its ends.

\textbf{Assigning masses.} For \(E\) to be the balance point of \(A\) and \(B\) we need \(m_A:m_B=EB:AE=2:1\). For \(D\) to be the balance point of \(B\) and \(C\) we need \(m_B:m_C=DC:BD=2:3\). Both hold with \(m_A=4\), \(m_B=2\), \(m_C=3\). Then \(D\) carries mass \(5\) and \(E\) carries mass \(6\).

