The centre of mass \(G\) lies on \(AD\) (combine \(B,C\) into \(D\) first) and on \(CE\) (combine \(A,B\) into \(E\) first), so \(G=P\). On \(AD\), \(AP:PD=m_D:m_A=5:4\). On \(CE\), \(CP:PE=m_E:m_C=6:3=2:1\).

Check with vectors: \(P=\dfrac{4A+2B+3C}{9}\), and \(D=\dfrac{2B+3C}{5}\) gives \(P=\dfrac{4A+5D}{9}\), so \(AP:PD=5:4\). Likewise \(E=\dfrac{2A+B}{3}\) gives \(P=\dfrac{6E+3C}{9}\), so \(CP:PE=6:3\).
\[
\boxed{AP:PD=5:4,\qquad CP:PE=2:1}
\]

In the worst case one draws every bean of the two most numerous colours and one bean of the least numerous colour, and only the next bean completes the set. So the guaranteed number is (sum of the two largest colour counts) \(+\,2\), assuming each colour has at least \(2\) beans.
\begin{itemize}
\item If \(x\le 2018\), the two largest counts are \(2019\) and \(2018\), giving \(4039\ne 4041\).
\item If \(x\ge 2018\), the two largest are \(x\) and \(2019\) (or \(2019\) and \(2018\) if \(x=2018\)), giving \(x+2021=4041\), so \(x=2020\).
\end{itemize}
Check: with \(x=2020\), drawing all \(2020\) blue and \(2019\) red and \(1\) green (\(4040\) beans) does not suffice, while any \(4041\) beans omit at most \(2018+2019+2020-4041=2016\) beans and therefore include at least \(2\) of each colour. So \(x=\boxed{2020}\).

