Triangles \(ABD\) and \(ACD\) have the same height \(AH\) over the bases \(BD\) and \(DC\), so
\[
\dfrac{[ABD]}{[ACD]}=\dfrac{BD}{DC}.
\]
Using the formula \(\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot(\text{side})\cdot(\text{side})\cdot\sin(\text{included angle})\) with the equal angles \(\angle BAD=\angle DAC=\theta\),
\[
\dfrac{[ABD]}{[ACD]}=\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{2}\,AB\cdot AD\sin\theta}{\dfrac{1}{2}\,AC\cdot AD\sin\theta}=\dfrac{AB}{AC}.
\]
Hence \(\dfrac{BD}{DC}=\dfrac{AB}{AC}<1\) (the angle bisector theorem), so \(CD>BD\). \(\blacksquare\)

