We need \(2^{2020}\bmod 100\). Since \(100=4\cdot 25\) with \(\gcd(4,25)=1\), work modulo \(4\) and \(25\) separately.
\begin{itemize}
\item Modulo \(4\): \(2^{2020}\equiv 0\).
\item Modulo \(25\): \(\varphi(25)=20\) and \(\gcd(2,25)=1\), so by Euler's theorem \(2^{20}\equiv 1\), and \(2^{2020}=(2^{20})^{101}\equiv 1\).
\end{itemize}
We need \(N\equiv 0\pmod 4\) and \(N\equiv 1\pmod{25}\). Among \(1,26,51,76\), only \(76\) is divisible by \(4\). By the Chinese remainder theorem \(2^{2020}\equiv 76\pmod{100}\), so the last two digits are \(\boxed{76}\).

Since \(a+b>c\) and \(c\) is the largest, there is a triangle \(ABC\) with \(BC=a\), \(CA=b\), \(AB=c\). Its largest angle is \(C\), and \(a^2+b^2>c^2\) gives \(C<90^\circ\), so the triangle is acute.

By the law of cosines, if \(P\) is a point inside the triangle with
\[
\angle BPC=90^\circ,\qquad \angle CPA=120^\circ,\qquad \angle APB=150^\circ,
\]
then \(x=PC\), \(y=PB\), \(z=PA\) work:
\[
a^2=x^2+y^2,\qquad b^2=x^2+z^2-2xz\cos120^\circ=x^2+xz+z^2,\qquad c^2=y^2+z^2-2yz\cos150^\circ=y^2+\sqrt3\,yz+z^2 .
\]
(The three angles add to \(360^\circ\), as they must around an interior point.) It remains to find \(P\).

