\textbf{Existence of \(P\).} Let \(\omega\) be the semicircle on diameter \(BC\) on the same side of \(BC\) as \(A\); its points \(X\ne B,C\) are exactly the points on that side with \(\angle BXC=90^\circ\). Let \(H_c\) and \(H_b\) be the feet of the altitudes from \(C\) and \(B\). Because the triangle is acute, they lie strictly inside the sides \(AB\) and \(AC\), and they lie on \(\omega\) (\(\angle BH_cC=\angle BH_bC=90^\circ\)). The line \(AB\) meets the circle only at \(B\) and \(H_c\), and \(AC\) only at \(C\) and \(H_b\). So the open arc \(\gamma\) of \(\omega\) from \(H_c\) to \(H_b\) does not meet the sides of the triangle. It leaves \(H_c\) towards the interior (the arc from \(B\) to \(H_c\) lies outside, beyond line \(AB\), since \(\angle B<90^\circ\)), so \(\gamma\) lies inside the triangle.

For \(X\) on \(\gamma\), \(\angle AXB+\angle AXC=360^\circ-90^\circ=270^\circ\), and \(f(X)=\angle AXB\) varies continuously along \(\gamma\). As \(X\to H_c\), \(X\) approaches a point of segment \(AB\), so \(f\to 180^\circ\). As \(X\to H_b\), \(\angle AXC\to 180^\circ\), so \(f\to 90^\circ\). By the intermediate value theorem there is \(P\) on \(\gamma\) with \(\angle APB=150^\circ\), and then \(\angle CPA=270^\circ-150^\circ=120^\circ\) and \(\angle BPC=90^\circ\).

Thus \(x=PC\), \(y=PB\), \(z=PA\) are positive numbers satisfying all three equations. \(\blacksquare\)

We use two standard facts.
\begin{itemize}
\item In a triangle, the longer side is opposite the larger angle (and equal sides are opposite equal angles).
\item (Hinge theorem and its converse.) If two triangles have two pairs of equal sides, then the third side is longer in the triangle where the included angle is larger, and conversely.
\end{itemize}

