Suppose, for contradiction, that $DB\ge DC$.

\emph{Step 1.} In triangle $DBC$, the side $DB$ is opposite $\angle DCB$ and $DC$ is opposite $\angle DBC$, so $DB\ge DC$ gives $\angle DCB\ge\angle DBC$. Since $D$ is inside the triangle, $\angle ABD=\angle ABC-\angle DBC$ and $\angle ACD=\angle ACB-\angle DCB$. As $\angle ABC=\angle ACB$,
\[
\angle ACD\le\angle ABD .
\]

\emph{Step 2.} Triangles $ABD$ and $ACD$ have $AB=AC$ and the common side $AD$. Their third sides satisfy $DB\ge DC$, so by the converse of the hinge theorem (or congruence when $DB=DC$),
\[
\angle BAD\ge\angle CAD .
\]

\emph{Step 3.} Using the angle sums in triangles $ABD$ and $ACD$,
\[
\angle ADB=180^\circ-\angle BAD-\angle ABD\le 180^\circ-\angle CAD-\angle ACD=\angle ADC,
\]
contradicting $\angle ADB>\angle ADC$.

Therefore $DB<DC$, that is, $\boxed{DC>DB}$.

