In right triangle $AFC$, $\angle AFC=90^\circ-30^\circ=60^\circ$. In right triangle $AFE$,
\[
\tan\angle AFE=\dfrac{AE}{AF}=\dfrac{\tan10^\circ}{\tan20^\circ\tan30^\circ}.
\]
We show this equals $\tan40^\circ$, i.e.\ $\tan20^\circ\tan30^\circ\tan40^\circ=\tan10^\circ$.

\emph{Lemma.} $\tan\theta\tan(60^\circ-\theta)\tan(60^\circ+\theta)=\tan3\theta$ whenever all terms are defined. Indeed, with $t=\tan\theta$ and $\tan60^\circ=\sqrt3$,
\[
\tan(60^\circ-\theta)\tan(60^\circ+\theta)=\dfrac{(\sqrt3-t)(\sqrt3+t)}{(1+\sqrt3t)(1-\sqrt3t)}=\dfrac{3-t^2}{1-3t^2},
\]
so the product is $\dfrac{3t-t^3}{1-3t^2}=\tan3\theta$.

With $\theta=20^\circ$: $\tan20^\circ\tan40^\circ\tan80^\circ=\tan60^\circ=\sqrt3$. Since $\tan80^\circ=\dfrac{1}{\tan10^\circ}$, we get $\tan20^\circ\tan40^\circ=\sqrt3\tan10^\circ$, and multiplying by $\tan30^\circ=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}$ gives $\tan20^\circ\tan30^\circ\tan40^\circ=\tan10^\circ$.

Hence $\tan\angle AFE=\tan40^\circ$, so $\angle AFE=40^\circ$ (both are acute). Because $E$ lies between $A$ and $C$, ray $FE$ lies between rays $FA$ and $FC$, so
\[
\angle CFE=\angle AFC-\angle AFE=60^\circ-40^\circ=\boxed{20^\circ}.
\]

