defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real e=-9+sqrt(120); real r=(9+e^2)/6;
pair B=(0,0), A=(0,3), C=(3,0), D=(4,3), P=(0,-9), Ept=(r*0,e), O=(r,e);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O,r),blue);
draw(P--B,dashed); draw(P--C,dashed);
pair H=C+(D-C)*dot(Ept-C,D-C)/dot(D-C,D-C);
draw(Ept--H,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0.2)--(0.2,0.2)--(0.2,0));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(P);dot(Ept);dot(H);
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,NE); label("$P$",P,W); label("$E$",Ept,W); label("$H$",H,E);
label("$4$",(A+D)/2,N); label("$3$",(B+C)/2,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Triangles $PBC$ and $PAD$ are similar (parallel sides) with ratio $BC:AD=3:4$, so we may write $PC=3t$ and $PD=4t$ for some $t>0$.

$PE$ is a tangent from $P$ and $PCD$ is a secant of the same circle, so by the power of a point,
\[
PE^2=PC\cdot PD=12t^2,\qquad PE=2\sqrt3\,t .
\]
Let $H$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $E$ to line $CD$. Then $EH=PE\sin\angle EPH=PE\sin\angle BPC$, and in right triangle $PBC$ (right angle at $B$), $\sin\angle BPC=\dfrac{BC}{PC}=\dfrac{3}{3t}=\dfrac{1}{t}$. Therefore
\[
EH=2\sqrt3\,t\cdot\dfrac{1}{t}=\boxed{2\sqrt3}.
\]
(The answer does not depend on the height $AB$; in general the distance is $\sqrt{AD\cdot BC}$.)

question:
Seven identical circles of radius $3$ are arranged as shown: one central circle, and six circles around it, each tangent to the central circle and to its two neighbours. A regular hexagon is drawn around them so that each side of the hexagon is tangent to two of the outer circles. Find the area of the region inside the hexagon but outside all seven circles.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=3; real R=(3*sqrt(3)+3)/Cos(30);
path hx=R*dir(0)--R*dir(60)--R*dir(120)--R*dir(180)--R*dir(240)--R*dir(300)--cycle;
draw(hx,linewidth(1));
draw(circle((0,0),r)); for(int k=0;k<6;++k) draw(circle(6*dir(60*k),r));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
The outer centres are at distance $6$ from the centre $O$ and form a regular hexagon of side $6$. A side of the big hexagon is parallel to the segment joining two adjacent outer centres and lies $3$ beyond it (the radius). That segment is at distance $6\cos30^\circ=3\sqrt3$ from $O$, so the apothem of the big hexagon is $a=3\sqrt3+3$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=3; real R=(3*sqrt(3)+3)/Cos(30);
path hx=R*dir(0)--R*dir(60)--R*dir(120)--R*dir(180)--R*dir(240)--R*dir(300)--cycle;
fill(hx,paleblue); for(int k=0;k<7;++k) fill(circle(k==0?(0,0):6*dir(60*k),r),white);
draw(hx,linewidth(1));
draw(circle((0,0),r)); for(int k=0;k<6;++k) draw(circle(6*dir(60*k),r));
pair O=(0,0), P1=6*dir(0), P2=6*dir(60), Mpt=(P1+P2)/2, T=(3*sqrt(3)+3)*dir(30);
draw(P1--P2,dashed); draw(O--T,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(O);dot(P1);dot(P2);dot(Mpt);dot(T);
label("$O$",O,SW); label("$3\sqrt3$",(O+Mpt)/2,NW,red+fontsize(8pt)); label("$3$",(Mpt+T)/2,NW,red+fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

A regular hexagon with apothem $a$ has area $6\cdot\dfrac{a^2}{\sqrt3}=2\sqrt3\,a^2$ (six triangles of height $a$ and base $\dfrac{2a}{\sqrt3}$). Here
\[
a^2=(3\sqrt3+3)^2=27+18\sqrt3+9=36+18\sqrt3,\qquad 2\sqrt3\,a^2=72\sqrt3+108 .
\]
The circles have total area $7\cdot9\pi=63\pi$, so the required area is
\[
\boxed{108+72\sqrt3-63\pi}\approx34.8 .
\]

question:
Suppose $a$ and $b$ are real numbers with $a>b$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] For which real numbers $k$ can we be sure that $a>b-k$ (for every such $a,b$)?
\item[(b)] For which real numbers $j$ can we be sure that $a+j>b$?
\end{enumerate}
Give a proof and a counterexample for the other values.

solution:
\textbf{Error 1.} Heron's formula gives the \emph{area}, not a side length. The left side should be the area of the triangle, which is $\dfrac{3\cdot4}{2}=6$ because the legs are perpendicular.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(4,0), C=(0,3);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw((0,0.25)--(0.25,0.25)--(0.25,0));
label("$4$",(A+B)/2,S); label("$3$",(A+C)/2,W); label("$c$",(B+C)/2,NE);
label("area $=\dfrac{3\cdot4}{2}=6$",(2.4,1.9),NE,fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Error 2.} Each of the four factors $s$, $s-3$, $s-4$, $s-c$ has a denominator $2$, so their product has a factor $\dfrac{1}{16}$, not $\dfrac{1}{2}$:
\[
s(s-3)(s-4)(s-c)=\dfrac{(7+c)(c+1)(c-1)(7-c)}{16}=\dfrac{(49-c^2)(c^2-1)}{16}.
\]

\textbf{Correct method.} Setting the area equal to $6$ and squaring,
\[
36=\dfrac{(49-c^2)(c^2-1)}{16}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad (49-c^2)(c^2-1)=576 .
\]
With $u=c^2$: $-u^2+50u-49=576$, i.e.\ $u^2-50u+625=0$, i.e.\ $(u-25)^2=0$. So $c^2=25$ and, since $c>0$, $\boxed{c=5}$, agreeing with the Pythagorean theorem.

(The double root is no accident: for fixed legs $3$ and $4$, the area $6$ is the largest possible area, attained only for the right angle, so the equation ``area $=6$'' has a repeated solution.)

question:
Let a triangle have side lengths $a,b,c$, circumcircle with centre $O$ and radius $R$, and incircle with centre $I$ and radius $r$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the greatest possible distance between a point on the incircle and a point on the circumcircle, in terms of $R$ and $r$, and explain how to compute it from $a,b,c$ alone.
\item[(b)] Evaluate it for the triangle with sides $13,14,15$.
\end{enumerate}
You may use Euler's formula $OI^2=R^2-2Rr$.

solution:
(a) Let $P$ be on the incircle and $Q$ on the circumcircle. By the triangle inequality,
\[
PQ\le PI+IO+OQ=r+OI+R .
\]
Equality holds when $P,I,O,Q$ lie on one line in this order: if $O\ne I$, take $P$ on the ray from $O$ through $I$, beyond $I$, and $Q$ on the opposite ray from $O$; if $O=I$ (equilateral triangle), take any diameter. So the maximum is $R+r+OI$, and with Euler's formula,
\[
\boxed{PQ_{\max}=R+r+\sqrt{R^2-2Rr}} .
\]
From the sides: with $s=\dfrac{a+b+c}{2}$ and area $K=\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}$ (Heron), we have $R=\dfrac{abc}{4K}$ and $r=\dfrac {K}{s}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(14,0), A=(5,12), I=(6,4), O=(7,33/8);
real R=65/8, r=4;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O,R),blue); draw(circle(I,r),deepgreen);
pair u=unit(I-O); pair P=I+r*u, Q=O-R*u;
draw(P--Q,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(I);dot(O);dot(P);dot(Q);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$I$",I,S); label("$O$",O,N); label("$P$",P,NW); label("$Q$",Q,E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(b) For $13,14,15$: $s=21$, $K=\sqrt{21\cdot8\cdot7\cdot6}=84$, $R=\dfrac{13\cdot14\cdot15}{336}=\dfrac{65}{8}$, $r=\dfrac{84}{21}=4$. Then
\[
OI^2=\dfrac{4225}{64}-2\cdot\dfrac{65}{8}\cdot4=\dfrac{4225-4160}{64}=\dfrac{65}{64},\qquad OI=\dfrac{\sqrt{65}}{8},
\]
and the maximum distance is
\[
\dfrac{65}{8}+4+\dfrac{\sqrt{65}}{8}=\boxed{\dfrac{97+\sqrt{65}}{8}}\approx13.13 .
\]

Only a right triangle has a hypotenuse, so the triangle is an isosceles right triangle. Its equal sides must be the two legs (the hypotenuse is strictly the longest side, so it cannot equal another side). Hence it is a $45^\circ$--$45^\circ$--$90^\circ$ triangle and the answer is unique.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(6,0), C=(3,3);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(C+0.35*dir(-45)--C+0.35*dir(-45)+0.35*dir(-135)--C+0.35*dir(-135));
label("$18$",(A+B)/2,S); label("$x$",(A+C)/2,NW); label("$x$",(B+C)/2,NE);
label("$45^\circ$",A+(0.85,0.3),fontsize(8pt)); label("$45^\circ$",B+(-0.85,0.3),fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

If each leg is $x$, then $x^2+x^2=18^2$, so $x^2=162$. The area is
\[
\dfrac{x^2}{2}=\boxed{81}.
\]

Every piece stays convex, because a straight cut through a convex polygon produces two convex polygons.

\emph{Key fact.} A straight cut across a convex polygon with $n$ sides, not through a corner, enters through the interior of one side and leaves through the interior of another. Each of these two sides is split into two parts, one on each piece, and the cut itself becomes a side of both pieces. So if the pieces have $p$ and $q$ sides, then $p+q=n+4$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(4,0), C=(4,4), D=(0,4), X=(0,2.6), Y=(1.5,4);
fill(X--Y--D--cycle,paleblue); fill(A--B--C--Y--X--cycle,palegreen);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(X--Y,red+linewidth(1));
dot(X);dot(Y);
label("$3$",(0.5,3.53),fontsize(8pt));
label("$5$ sides",(2.3,1.5),fontsize(8pt));
label("$4+4=3+5$",(2,-0.5),fontsize(9pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Hence every cut increases the \emph{total} number of sides of all pieces by exactly $4$. We start with one square ($4$ sides), so after $10$ cuts the total is $4+10\cdot4=44$. The known pieces have $7\cdot3+2\cdot4+5=34$ sides, so the remaining piece has $44-34=\boxed{10}$ sides.

Completing the square,
\[
(x-5)^2+(y+12)^2=731+25+144=900,
\]
so $(x,y)$ lies on the circle with centre $C(5,-12)$ and radius $30$. The quantity $x^2+y^2$ is the squared distance from the origin $O$ to $(x,y)$, and $OC=\sqrt{25+144}=13<30$, so $O$ is inside the circle.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Cc=(5,-12), O=(0,0); real R=30;
draw(circle(Cc,R),blue);
pair Pn=Cc+R*unit(O-Cc);
draw(Cc--Pn,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-30,0)--(37,0),gray(0.6),Arrow); draw((0,-44)--(0,22),gray(0.6),Arrow);
dot(Cc); dot(O); dot(Pn);
label("$C(5,-12)$",Cc,SE); label("$O$",O,NE); label("$P$",Pn,NW);
label("$13$",(Cc+O)/2,E,red+fontsize(8pt)); label("$17$",(O+Pn)/2,NE,red+fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

For any point $P$ on the circle, $OP\ge CP-OC=30-13=17$ by the triangle inequality, with equality when $P$ lies on ray $CO$ beyond $O$. Hence the minimum of $x^2+y^2$ is $17^2=\boxed{289}$.

question:
The diagonals of a convex quadrilateral $ABCD$ meet at $O$. The areas of triangles $AOB$ and $COD$ are $4$ and $9$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the area of $ABCD$ can be arbitrarily large, so it has no maximum.
\item[(b)] Find the minimum possible area of $ABCD$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $x=[BOC]$ and $y=[AOD]$, where $[\cdot]$ denotes area.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0), A=(-2,1.2), B=(1.4,1.5), C=(3,-1.8), D=(-3.1,-1.2);
O=extension(A,C,B,D);
fill(A--O--B--cycle,paleblue); fill(C--O--D--cycle,palegreen);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--C); draw(B--D);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(O);
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,NE); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,SW); label("$O$",O,dir(270)*1.2);
label("$4$",(A+B+O)/3); label("$9$",(C+D+O)/3); label("$x$",(B+C+O)/3); label("$y$",(A+D+O)/3);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Triangles with the same height have areas in the ratio of their bases. Using the heights from $B$ and from $D$ to line $AC$,
\[
\dfrac{[AOB]}{[BOC]}=\dfrac{AO}{OC}=\dfrac{[AOD]}{[COD]},\qquad\text{so}\qquad \dfrac{4}{x}=\dfrac {y}{9},\quad xy=36 .
\]
Hence $[ABCD]=13+x+\dfrac{36}{x}$.

(a) Every $x>0$ occurs: take $O$ as origin, put $A$ and $C$ on one line with $OA=4t$, $OC=9$, and $B$, $D$ on another line through $O$ at an angle $\theta$, with $OB=\dfrac{2}{t\sin\theta}$, $OD=\dfrac{2}{\sin\theta}$; then $[AOB]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot4t\cdot\dfrac{2}{t\sin\theta}\sin\theta=4$, $[COD]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot9\cdot\dfrac{2}{\sin\theta}\sin\theta=9$, and $x=[BOC]=\dfrac{9}{t}$ takes every positive value as $t$ varies. As $x\to\infty$ the area $13+x+\dfrac{36}{x}\to\infty$, so there is no maximum.

(b) By AM--GM, $x+\dfrac{36}{x}\ge2\sqrt{36}=12$, with equality at $x=6$ (which occurs, e.g.\ for $t=\dfrac{3}{2}$, when $AB\parallel CD$). So the minimum area is $13+12=\boxed{25}$.

question:
It is given that $x$ varies directly as $y$ and inversely as the square of $z$, and that $x=10$ when $y=4$ and $z=14$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find $x$ when $y=16$ and $z=7$.
\item[(b)] A student writes $kx=y$ and $\dfrac {c}{x}=z^2$ with two separate constants $k$ and $c$, finds $k=\dfrac{2}{5}$ and $c=1960$, and concludes $x=40$. Explain the error.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
For any line through $P$, the distance from $O$ to the line is at most $OP$: the perpendicular from $O$ to the line is no longer than the segment $OP$ to a point of the line. Equality holds exactly when $OP$ is itself the perpendicular, i.e.\ when the line is perpendicular to $OP$ at $P$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0), P=(3,5);
draw((-1,0)--(13,0),gray(0.6),Arrow); draw((0,-1)--(0,8),gray(0.6),Arrow);
draw((-0.5,(34+1.5)/5)--(12,(34-36)/5),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--P,red+linewidth(0.9));
pair u=unit(P-O), v=unit((5,-3));
draw(P-0.4*u--P-0.4*u+0.4*v--P+0.4*v);
pair Q=(8,2); draw(O--Q,dashed); pair Hq=Q; dot(Q);
dot(O);dot(P);
label("$O$",O,SW); label("$P(3,5)$",P,N); label("$\ell:\ 3x+5y=34$",(9.5,(34-28.5)/5),NE,blue); label("$Q$",Q,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The slope of $OP$ is $\dfrac{5}{3}$, so $\ell$ has slope $-\dfrac{3}{5}$ and passes through $(3,5)$:
\[
y-5=-\dfrac{3}{5}(x-3)\iff \boxed{3x+5y=34}.
\]
Its distance from $O$ is $\dfrac{34}{\sqrt{34}}=\sqrt{34}=OP$, as expected. (For any other point $Q$ of $\ell$, $OQ>OP$, as in the dashed segment.)

Let $DE=x$, $DF=y$ and $EF=t$, so $x+y+t=2$, i.e.\ $t=(1-x)+(1-y)=AE+CF$.

\emph{Area formula.} Rotate triangle $BCF$ about $B$ by $90^\circ$ so that $C$ goes to $A$; $F$ goes to a point $F'$ on line $DA$ beyond $A$ with $AF'=CF$ and $BF'=BF$. Then $EF'=EA+AF'=AE+CF=EF$. Triangles $EBF$ and $EBF'$ have $BF=BF'$, $EF=EF'$ and common side $BE$, so they are congruent and
\[
[EBF]=[EBF']=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot EF'\cdot AB=\dfrac {t}{2},
\]
since $AB\perp F'E$ and $AB=1$ is the height.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,1), B=(1,1), C=(1,0), D=(0,0);
real x=2-sqrt(2);
pair Ept=(0,x), F=(x,0);
pair Fp=(0,1+(1-x));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
fill(Ept--F--B--cycle,paleblue); draw(Ept--F--B--cycle,blue);
draw(A--Fp,dashed); draw(B--Fp,dashed); draw(Ept--Fp,dashed+red);
draw(circle(B,1),gray(0.7)+dotted);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(Ept);dot(F);dot(Fp);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,NE); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,SW); label("$E$",Ept,W); label("$F$",F,S); label("$F'$",Fp,W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\emph{Minimising $t$.} We have $x+y=2-t$ and $x^2+y^2=t^2$. Since $(x+y)^2\le2(x^2+y^2)$,
\[
(2-t)^2\le2t^2\iff 2-t\le\sqrt2\,t\iff t\ge\dfrac{2}{1+\sqrt2}=2\sqrt2-2 .
\]
Equality needs $x=y$; then $2x+\sqrt2x=2$, $x=\dfrac{2}{2+\sqrt2}=2-\sqrt2\in(0,1)$, so it is attained. Therefore the least area is
\[
\dfrac{2\sqrt2-2}{2}=\boxed{\sqrt2-1}.
\]

question:
Real numbers $a,b,c$ (all non-zero) satisfy $a+b+c=2$ and $\dfrac{1}{a}+\dfrac{1}{b}+\dfrac{1}{c}=0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the value of $\dfrac{1}{a^2}+\dfrac{1}{b^2}+\dfrac{1}{c^2}$ is \emph{not} determined by these conditions.
\item[(b)] Find the value of $a^2+b^2+c^2$, which is determined.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\emph{Algebraically.} Adding $1$ to both sides preserves the inequality: $x<4$.

\emph{Graphically.} Draw the line $y=x-1$ and the horizontal line $y=3$. They meet at $x=4$. The line $y=x-1$ lies strictly below $y=3$ exactly to the left of this point.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
draw((-3,0)--(7,0),gray(0.6),Arrow); draw((0,-4)--(0,6),gray(0.6),Arrow);
label("$x$",(7,0),E); label("$y$",(0,6),N);
draw((-3,-4)--(7,6),blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw((-3,3)--(7,3),red);
draw((-3,0)--(4,0),deepgreen+linewidth(2.5)); 
filldraw(circle((4,0),0.15),white,deepgreen); dot((4,3));
draw((4,3)--(4,0),dashed);
label("$y=x-1$",(6.2,5.2),SE,blue); label("$y=3$",(-3,3),N,red); label("$4$",(4,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Both methods give $\boxed{x<4}$ (the open circle shows that $4$ itself is excluded).

question:
Does the polynomial $4x-4$ divide the polynomial $x^2-1$
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] in the ring of polynomials with rational (or real) coefficients;
\item[(b)] in the ring of polynomials with integer coefficients?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
A diameter is a chord through the centre; it consists of two radii lying on one line.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); draw(circle(O,4),linewidth(0.9));
draw((-4,0)--(4,0),blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(O--4*dir(60),red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(O); label("$O$",O,S); label("$4$",2*dir(60),NW,red); label("$d=8$",(2.4,0),S,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So the diameter is $2\cdot4=\boxed{8}$.

Let $I$ be the incentre and $r$ the inradius of $ABC$, and write $\angle A,\angle B,\angle C$ for its angles.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.7,3.9), B=(0,0), C=(4.4,0);
pair ft(pair P,pair U,pair V){return U+(V-U)*dot(P-U,V-U)/dot(V-U,V-U);}
pair inc(pair X,pair Y,pair Z){real x=abs(Y-Z),y=abs(Z-X),z=abs(X-Y);return (x*X+y*Y+z*Z)/(x+y+z);}
pair cc(pair X,pair Y,pair Z){pair m1=(X+Y)/2, m2=(X+Z)/2; return extension(m1,m1+rotate(90)*(Y-X),m2,m2+rotate(90)*(Z-X));}
pair D=ft(A,B,C), Ept=ft(B,C,A), F=ft(C,A,B);
pair K=inc(B,F,D), L=inc(C,D,Ept), I=inc(A,B,C);
pair P=cc(A,B,K), Q=cc(A,C,L);

draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,gray(0.4)); draw(B--Ept,gray(0.4)); draw(C--F,gray(0.4));
draw(B--I,dashed); draw(C--I,dashed);
draw(A--(I+0.9*(I-A)),deepgreen+dashed);
draw(K--L,red+linewidth(1)); draw(P--Q,blue+linewidth(1));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(Ept);dot(F);dot(K);dot(L);dot(I);dot(P);dot(Q);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,S); label("$E$",Ept,NE); label("$F$",F,NW);
label("$K$",K,dir(250)); label("$L$",L,dir(290)); label("$I$",I,NE); label("$P$",P,W); label("$Q$",Q,E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 1: $K$ and $L$ on the bisectors.} Since $\angle B$ is acute, $BD=AB\cos B$ and $BF=BC\cos B$, so triangle $BDF$ is similar to triangle $BAC$ (common angle at $B$, sides in ratio $\cos B$), with $D\leftrightarrow A$ and $F\leftrightarrow C$. This similarity is a dilation at $B$ with ratio $\cos B$ followed by the reflection in the bisector $BI$; it maps $I$ to the incentre $K$ of $BDF$. As $I$ lies on the bisector, $K$ lies on segment $BI$ with $BK=BI\cos B$. Similarly $L$ lies on segment $CI$ with $CL=CI\cos C$.

\textbf{Step 2: equal powers.} Since $BI=\dfrac{r}{\sin\dfrac {B}{2}}$,
\[
IK\cdot IB=(BI-BK)\,BI=BI^2(1-\cos B)=\dfrac{r^2}{\sin^2\dfrac {B}{2}}\cdot2\sin^2\dfrac {B}{2}=2r^2,
\]
and likewise $IL\cdot IC=2r^2$. Since $I$ lies on line $BK$ outside segment $BK$, $IK\cdot IB$ is the power of $I$ with respect to circle $(ABK)$; similarly $IL\cdot IC$ is its power with respect to $(ACL)$. So $I$ has equal power $2r^2$ with respect to both circles. Also $A$ lies on both circles (power $0$). The circles are different: if they coincided, the circle would pass through $A,B,C$ and so be the circumcircle of $ABC$, but $K$ lies strictly inside the triangle. Hence their radical axis is the line $AI$, and the line of centres is perpendicular to it:
\[
PQ\perp AI .
\]

\textbf{Step 3: $KL\perp AI$.} From $IK\cdot IB=IL\cdot IC$ (with $K$ on segment $IB$ and $L$ on segment $IC$), the converse of the power of a point shows that $B,K,L,C$ are concyclic. Hence $\angle IKL=\angle ICB=\dfrac{\angle C}{2}$ (exterior angle of the cyclic quadrilateral $BKLC$).

Let the ray from $I$ opposite to $A$ be $IA'$. Since $\angle AIB=90^\circ+\dfrac{\angle C}{2}$, we get $\angle BIA'=90^\circ-\dfrac{\angle C}{2}$, and similarly $\angle CIA'=90^\circ-\dfrac{\angle B}{2}$; these add to $\angle BIC$, so ray $IA'$ runs inside angle $KIL$ and meets segment $KL$ at a point $T$. In triangle $KIT$,
\[
\angle KTI=180^\circ-\angle IKT-\angle KIT=180^\circ-\dfrac{\angle C}{2}-\left(90^\circ-\dfrac{\angle C}{2}\right)=90^\circ .
\]
So $KL\perp AI$.

\textbf{Conclusion.} Both $PQ$ and $KL$ are perpendicular to $AI$, hence $\boxed{PQ\parallel KL}$.

Since $57^\circ=72^\circ-15^\circ$, we construct $72^\circ$ and $15^\circ$ and subtract.

\textbf{Fact: $\cos72^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{4}$.} Take an isosceles triangle $XYZ$ with $XY=XZ=1$ and apex angle $\angle X=36^\circ$, so the base angles are $72^\circ$; let $YZ=a$. The bisector of $\angle Y$ meets $XZ$ at $M$. Then $\angle MYZ=36^\circ$ and $\angle YMZ=72^\circ$, so $YM=YZ=a$; also $\angle MXY=\angle MYX=36^\circ$, so $XM=YM=a$ and $MZ=1-a$. Triangles $ZYM$ and $XYZ$ have equal angles ($36^\circ,72^\circ,72^\circ$), so $\dfrac{MZ}{YZ}=\dfrac{YZ}{XY}$, i.e.\ $\dfrac{1-a}{a}=a$, $a^2+a-1=0$, $a=\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{2}$. Dropping the altitude from $X$ to the midpoint of $YZ$ gives $\cos72^\circ=\dfrac{\dfrac {a}{2}}{1}=\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{4}$.

\textbf{Construction.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Fix a unit segment $OU$ and draw the circle with centre $O$ through $U$.
\item Construct a right triangle with legs $1$ and $2$ (erect a perpendicular and mark lengths); its hypotenuse is $\sqrt5$. Remove a unit length to get $\sqrt5-1$, and bisect twice to get $\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{4}$. Mark $H$ on $OU$ with $OH=\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{4}$.
\item Erect the perpendicular to $OU$ at $H$; let it meet the circle at $B$. Then $\cos\angle BOU=\dfrac{OH}{OB}=\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{4}$, so $\angle BOU=72^\circ$.
\item Draw an equilateral triangle on $OU$ to get a $60^\circ$ angle at $O$; bisect it twice to get $15^\circ$.
\item Copy this $15^\circ$ angle at $O$ with one side along $OB$, inside $\angle BOU$, obtaining a ray $OC$ with $\angle BOC=15^\circ$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0), U=(1,0);
real h=(sqrt(5)-1)/4;
pair H=(h,0), Bp=dir(72), Cp=dir(57), V=dir(60), T=dir(15);
draw(arc(O,1,-5,95),gray(0.6));
draw(O--(1.15,0),linewidth(0.9)); draw(O--1.15*Bp,linewidth(0.9)); draw(O--1.15*Cp,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(H--Bp,dashed); draw(O--1.1*V,gray(0.5)+dashed); draw(O--1.1*T,gray(0.5)+dashed);
draw(H+(0,0.06)--H+(0.06,0.06)--H+(0.06,0));
dot(O);dot(U);dot(H);dot(Bp);dot(Cp);
label("$O$",O,SW); label("$U$",U,S); label("$H$",H,S); label("$B$",Bp,NW); label("$C$",Cp,NE);
label("$60^\circ$",1.12*V,NE,gray(0.4)+fontsize(8pt)); label("$15^\circ$",1.12*T,E,gray(0.4)+fontsize(8pt));
label("$OH=\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{4}$",(0.15,-0.22),E,fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Then $\angle COU=\angle BOU-\angle BOC=72^\circ-15^\circ=\boxed{57^\circ}$, and every step uses only compass and straightedge (perpendiculars, transferring and bisecting segments, bisecting and copying angles).

Let the circle touch $AB$, $BC$, $CD$, $DA$ at $W$, $X$, $Y$, $Z$ respectively. The two tangent segments from an external point to a circle are equal, so
\[
AW=AZ,\qquad BW=BX,\qquad CX=CY,\qquad DY=DZ .
\]

