For $\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{a^2}-\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{b^2}=1$, the asymptotes are obtained by replacing $1$ with $0$: far from the center the constant $1$ is negligible compared with the two squared terms. Here
\[\dfrac{(x-3)^2}{16}=\dfrac{(y+2)^2}{4}\iff \dfrac{x-3}{4}=\pm\dfrac{y+2}{2}\iff y+2=\pm\dfrac{1}{2}(x-3).\]
So the asymptotes are the two lines through the center $(3,-2)$ with slopes $\pm\dfrac{1}{2}$:
\[\boxed{y+2=\dfrac{1}{2}(x-3)\quad\text{and}\quad y+2=-\dfrac{1}{2}(x-3)}.\]

