By the law of sines in triangle $ABD$,
\[BD=\dfrac{AB\sin100^\circ}{\sin30^\circ}=2\sin80^\circ .\]
By the law of sines in triangle $BDC$,
\[\dfrac{BD}{\sin x}=\dfrac{DC}{\sin(30^\circ-x)}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad 2\sin80^\circ\sin(30^\circ-x)=\sin x .\]
For $x=20^\circ$ the left side is $2\sin80^\circ\sin10^\circ=2\cos10^\circ\sin10^\circ=\sin20^\circ$, so the equation holds. It has no other solution in $(0^\circ,30^\circ)$: the ratio $\dfrac{\sin x}{\sin(30^\circ-x)}$ is strictly increasing there (the numerator increases and the positive denominator decreases), so it takes the value $2\sin80^\circ$ only once. Hence
\[\angle ACB=\boxed{20^\circ}.\]

Since the right angle is at $B$, the hypotenuse is $AC=b$, and by the Pythagorean theorem
