A quadratic with $a\ne0$ has exactly one solution exactly when its discriminant is zero, so
\[b^2-4ac=0,\qquad ac=\dfrac{b^2}{4}.\]
Therefore
\[(a+c)^2=a^2+c^2+2ac=b^2+\dfrac{b^2}{2}=\dfrac{3}{2}b^2 .\]
All lengths are positive, so taking square roots gives $a+c=b\sqrt{\dfrac{3}{2}}$, that is, $\dfrac{a+c}{b}=\sqrt{\dfrac{3}{2}}$. $\blacksquare$

