\textbf{Only if.} If the four points are the vertices of a parallelogram $PQRS$, then (1) holds, so the sum of the two opposite vertices $P,R$ equals the sum of the other two.

\textbf{If.} Suppose, after renaming, $w+x=y+z$. Then $y-w=x-z$, so the vectors from $w$ to $y$ and from $z$ to $x$ are equal and nonzero (the points are distinct). The segments $wy$ and $zx$ are therefore parallel and of equal length. They do not lie on one line, since otherwise $w,y,z$ would be collinear. Hence $w,y,x,z$, taken in this order, is a quadrilateral with a pair of opposite sides $wy$ and $zx$ equal and parallel, so it is a parallelogram, with diagonals $wx$ and $yz$ meeting at their common midpoint $\dfrac{w+x}{2}=\dfrac{y+z}{2}$.

\textbf{The hypothesis is needed.} The points $0,1,2,3$ on the real axis satisfy $0+3=1+2$, but they are collinear and are not the vertices of any parallelogram. $\blacksquare$

