question:
For each natural number $n\ge2$, let $s(n)$ be the number of pairs $(x,y)$ of natural numbers chosen from $1,2,\dots,n$, with $x>y$, such that $x$ and $y$ have exactly $x-y$ common positive divisors.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Does there exist $n$ with $s(n)=2019$?
\item Does there exist $n$ with $s(n)=2020$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $d=x-y\ge1$ and $g=\gcd(x,y)$. The common divisors of $x$ and $y$ are the divisors of $g$, so the condition is $\tau(g)=d$, where $\tau$ counts positive divisors. Since $g$ divides both $x$ and $y$, it divides $d$, so $g\le d$. Also $\tau(g)\le g$, because the divisors of $g$ are among $1,\dots,g$. Hence
\[d=\tau(g)\le g\le d,\]
so $\tau(g)=g=d$. Now $\tau(g)=g$ means every number from $1$ to $g$ divides $g$; this holds for $g=1,2$ but fails for $g\ge3$, since $g-1\ge2$ does not divide $g$. So:
\begin{itemize}
\item $d=1$: then $g=\gcd(y+1,y)=1$ and $\tau(1)=1$, so every pair $(y+1,y)$ works. There are $n-1$ such pairs.
\item $d=2$ with $g=2$: $x=y+2$ with $y$ even. With $y$ even and $y+2\le n$, there are $\left\lfloor\dfrac {n}{2}\right\rfloor-1$ such pairs.
\end{itemize}
Therefore
\[s(n)=n-1+\left\lfloor\dfrac {n}{2}\right\rfloor-1=n+\left\lfloor\dfrac {n}{2}\right\rfloor-2 .\]
The function $f(n)=n+\left\lfloor\dfrac {n}{2}\right\rfloor$ increases by $1$ from an even $n$ to $n+1$ and by $2$ from an odd $n$ to $n+1$; its values are exactly the numbers that are not $\equiv2\pmod3$: $f(2k)=3k$ and $f(2k+1)=3k+1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $s(n)=2019$ requires $f(n)=2021\equiv2\pmod3$, which is impossible. Concretely, $s(1347)=1347+673-2=2018$ and $s(1348)=1348+674-2=2020$. \textbf{No.}
\item $s(n)=2020$ requires $f(n)=2022=3\cdot674$, so $n=1348$ works. \textbf{Yes}, $n=1348$.
\end{enumerate}
\[\boxed{\text{(a) No;\quad (b) Yes, } n=1348}\]

The equation holds exactly when $x+y-2\ge0$ and the squares are equal, since the square root is nonnegative, and the condition $x^2+y^2-4\ge0$ then follows automatically from the squared equation. Squaring:
\[x^2+y^2-4=x^2+y^2+4+2xy-4x-4y\iff 2xy-4x-4y+8=0\iff(x-2)(y-2)=0 .\]
So $x=2$ or $y=2$, together with $x+y\ge2$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $x=2$ requires $y\ge0$;
\item $y=2$ requires $x\ge0$.
\end{itemize}

