question:
Using only the trivial inequality ($t^2\ge0$ for every real $t$):
\begin{enumerate}
\item prove that $\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{2}\ge xy$ for all real $x,y$;
\item prove that $\dfrac{a+b}{2}\ge\sqrt{ab}$ for all $a,b\ge0$;
\item show by an example that the stronger-looking claim $\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{2}\ge2xy$ is false in general.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $(x-y)^2\ge0$ gives $x^2-2xy+y^2\ge0$, i.e.\ $x^2+y^2\ge2xy$; dividing by $2$ gives $\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{2}\ge xy$, with equality iff $x=y$.
\item Apply (a) to $x=\sqrt a$, $y=\sqrt b$: $\dfrac{a+b}{2}\ge\sqrt a\sqrt b=\sqrt{ab}$, with equality iff $a=b$. This is the two-variable AM-GM inequality.
\item Take $x=y=1$: then $\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{2}=1<2=2xy$. So $\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{2}\ge2xy$ is false; the correct statement is $x^2+y^2\ge2xy$, or (a).
\end{enumerate}
\[\boxed{\text{(c) e.g. } x=y=1}\]

The snow falls at $4$ inches $=\dfrac{1}{3}$ foot of depth per hour, and the trip takes $\dfrac{2000}{1000}=2$ hours. A point at distance $s$ feet from the start is cleared at time $\dfrac{s}{1000}$ hours, and snow then builds up again for the remaining $2-\dfrac{s}{1000}$ hours. So at the end its depth is
\[h(s)=\dfrac{1}{3}\left(2-\dfrac{s}{1000}\right)\ \text{feet},\]
which falls linearly from $\dfrac{2}{3}$ foot at the start to $0$ at the end.

