The snow therefore forms a wedge whose side view is a right triangle with legs $2000$ feet and $\dfrac{2}{3}$ foot, across the full $30$-foot width. Its volume is
\[30\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot2000\cdot\dfrac{2}{3}=\boxed{20000}\ \text{cubic feet}.\]
(Equivalently, the average depth is $\dfrac{1}{3}$ foot over an area of $2000\cdot30$ square feet.)

Ask either guard:
\begin{quote}
"If I asked you whether the left door leads to the prize, would you answer \emph{Ghi}?"
\end{quote}
\textbf{Claim:} the guard answers "Ghi" if and only if the left door leads to the prize. So if he says "Ghi", choose the left door; if he says "Pon", choose the right door.

\textbf{Proof.} Let $L$ be the statement "the left door leads to the prize". There are four cases.
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Truth-teller, Ghi means yes.} Asked about $L$, he would say Ghi exactly when $L$ is true. So the asked statement is true iff $L$, and he truthfully answers yes (Ghi) iff $L$.
\item \emph{Truth-teller, Ghi means no.} Asked about $L$, he would say Ghi (no) exactly when $L$ is false, so the asked statement is true iff not $L$. Answering truthfully, he says yes (Pon) iff not $L$, i.e.\ he says Ghi iff $L$.
\item \emph{Liar, Ghi means yes.} Asked about $L$, he would lie and say Ghi (yes) exactly when $L$ is false, so the statement is true iff not $L$. He lies about it, saying yes (Ghi) iff the statement is false, i.e.\ iff $L$.
\item \emph{Liar, Ghi means no.} Asked about $L$, he would lie and say Ghi (no) exactly when $L$ is true, so the statement is true iff $L$. He lies, saying yes (Pon) iff the statement is false, i.e.\ iff not $L$; so he says Ghi iff $L$.
\end{itemize}
In every case the answer is "Ghi" exactly when the left door leads to the prize. $\blacksquare$

question:
Let \(m\) and \(n\) be relatively prime integers greater than \(1\).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that there are positive integers \(a\) and \(b\) for which \(\log_m a+\log_n b\) is an integer.
\item[(b)] Prove that \(\log_2 6+\log_3 6\) is \emph{not} an integer.
\end{enumerate}

A rational function with real coefficients whose domain is all real numbers can have range equal to all of \(\mathbb{R}\) or only part of it. Illustrate this:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the range of \(f(x)=\dfrac{x}{x^2+1}\).
\item[(b)] Show that the range of \(h(x)=\dfrac{x^3}{x^2+1}\) is all real numbers.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Paint used is proportional to painted area. If the large cube has edge \(L\) and the small cubes have edges \(s_1,\dots,s_k\), the fraction of paint on the outside is
\[
\dfrac{6L^2}{6(s_1^2+\cdots+s_k^2)}=\dfrac{L^2}{s_1^2+\cdots+s_k^2}.
\]

\emph{Equal cubes do not work.} If the large cube is made of \(n^3\) equal cubes of edge \(\dfrac {L}{n}\), the fraction is \(\dfrac{L^2}{n^3\cdot \dfrac{L^2}{n^2}}=\dfrac{1}{n}\), and \(\dfrac{1}{n}=\dfrac{12}{100}\) gives \(n=\dfrac{25}{3}\), not an integer.

\emph{Unequal cubes do work.} Take a \(3\times3\times3\) cube made of \(27\) unit cubes, and cut \(24\) of those unit cubes into \(27\) little cubes of edge \(\dfrac{1}{3}\) each.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=1;
// big 3x3x3 cube in oblique projection, one unit cube subdivided into thirds
pair ex=(1,0), ey=(0.45,0.35), ez=(0,1);
pair P(real x,real y,real z){return x*ex+y*ey+z*ez;}
for(int i=0;i<=3;++i){draw(P(i,0,0)--P(i,0,3),gray); draw(P(0,0,i)--P(3,0,i),gray);
 draw(P(3,i,0)--P(3,i,3),gray); draw(P(3,0,i)--P(3,3,i),gray);
 draw(P(0,i,3)--P(3,i,3),gray); draw(P(i,0,3)--P(i,3,3),gray);}
draw(P(0,0,0)--P(3,0,0)--P(3,3,0)--P(3,3,3)--P(0,3,3)--P(0,0,3)--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(P(0,0,3)--P(3,0,3)--P(3,0,0));draw(P(3,0,3)--P(3,3,3));
for(int i=1;i<3;++i){draw(P(i/3,0,2)--P(i/3,0,3),blue+linewidth(0.4)); draw(P(0,0,2+i/3)--P(1,0,2+i/3),blue+linewidth(0.4));
 draw(P(i/3,0,3)--P(i/3,1,3),blue+linewidth(0.4)); draw(P(0,i/3,3)--P(1,i/3,3),blue+linewidth(0.4));}
label("$3$",P(1.5,0,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Now the large cube consists of \(3\) unit cubes and \(24\cdot27=648\) cubes of edge \(\dfrac{1}{3}\). The total painted area is
\[
6\left(3\cdot1^2+648\cdot\dfrac{1}{9}\right)=6(3+72)=450,
\]
while the surface of the large cube has area \(6\cdot 3^2=54\). Since \(\dfrac{54}{450}=\dfrac{12}{100}\), exactly \(12\%\) of the paint is on the surface.

\[
\boxed{\text{Yes (with cubes of different sizes; impossible if all the small cubes are equal).}}
\]

On the unit circle, the angle \(225^\circ\) measured counterclockwise from the positive \(x\)-axis ends in the third quadrant, \(225^\circ-180^\circ=45^\circ\) past the negative \(x\)-axis. So its reference angle is \(45^\circ\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),1),gray);
draw((-1.3,0)--(1.3,0),Arrow(4)); draw((0,-1.3)--(0,1.3),Arrow(4));
pair P=dir(225);
draw((0,0)--P,blue+linewidth(1));
draw(P--(P.x,0),dashed);
draw(arc((0,0),0.25,0,225),Arrow(3));
label("$225^\circ$",0.3*dir(110),N);
dot(P); label("$\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2},-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}\right)$",P,1.5*S,fontsize(8pt));
label("$45^\circ$",(-0.48,-0.14),fontsize(7pt));
label("$x$",(1.3,0),E); label("$y$",(0,1.3),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The point on the unit circle is obtained from the \(45^\circ\) point \(\left(\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}\right)\) by a half-turn, giving \(\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2},-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}\right)\). Hence
\[
\cos225^\circ=-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2},\qquad \sin225^\circ=-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2},\qquad \tan225^\circ=\dfrac{\sin225^\circ}{\cos225^\circ}=1 .
\]
\[
\boxed{\sin225^\circ=\cos225^\circ=-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2},\quad \tan225^\circ=1}
\]

question:
The diagram shows a rectangle with its bottom side divided into seven equal segments and its top side divided in half. Segments join the midpoint of the top side to the eight division points on the bottom side, forming seven triangles, and the first, third, fifth, and seventh of these triangles are shaded. The rectangle has area \(350\). Find the area of the shaded region.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real w=7, h=3;
pair T=(w/2,h);
for(int i=0;i<7;i+=2) fill(T--(i,0)--(i+1,0)--cycle,gray(0.55));
draw((0,0)--(w,0)--(w,h)--(0,h)--cycle);
for(int i=0;i<=7;++i){draw(T--(i,0),linewidth(0.4)); dot((i,0),linewidth(2.5));}
dot(T,linewidth(3));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
Let the rectangle have width \(w\) and height \(h\), so \(wh=350\). Each of the seven triangles has its apex at the midpoint of the top side and its base on the bottom side, so each has base \(\dfrac {w}{7}\) and height \(h\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real w=7, h=3;
pair T=(w/2,h);
for(int i=0;i<7;i+=2) fill(T--(i,0)--(i+1,0)--cycle,gray(0.55));
draw((0,0)--(w,0)--(w,h)--(0,h)--cycle);
for(int i=0;i<=7;++i) draw(T--(i,0),linewidth(0.4));
draw((5,0)--(5,h),dashed+blue);
label("$h$",(5,h/2),E,blue);
label("$\dfrac{w}{7}$",(0.5,0),S);
label("$\dfrac{w}{2}$",(1.75,h),N); label("$\dfrac{w}{2}$",(5.25,h),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Each triangle therefore has area
\[
\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\dfrac {w}{7}\cdot h=\dfrac{wh}{14}=\dfrac{350}{14}=25 .
\]
Four triangles are shaded, so the shaded area is \(4\cdot25=\boxed{100}\).

Let \(h=AM\), \(\alpha=\angle BAM\) and \(\beta=\angle CAM\), so \(\alpha+\beta=45^\circ\) because \(M\) lies between \(B\) and \(C\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=(13+sqrt(337))/2;
pair M=(0,0), B=(-7,0), C=(6,0), A=(0,h);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--M,dashed);
draw((0,0.9)--(0.9,0.9)--(0.9,0));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(M);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$M$",M,S);
label("$7$",(B+M)/2,S); label("$6$",(M+C)/2,S); label("$h$",(0,h*0.55),W);
label("$\alpha$",A+(-0.75,-3.1)); label("$\beta$",A+(0.65,-3.1));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

In the right triangles \(ABM\) and \(ACM\), \(\tan\alpha=\dfrac{7}{h}\) and \(\tan\beta=\dfrac{6}{h}\). Then
\[
1=\tan45^\circ=\tan(\alpha+\beta)=\dfrac{\dfrac{7}{h}+\dfrac{6}{h}}{1-\dfrac{42}{h^2}}=\dfrac{13h}{h^2-42}.
\]
(Here \(h^2>42\), since \(\alpha,\beta<45^\circ\) forces \(\tan\alpha\tan\beta<1\).) So \(h^2-13h-42=0\), whose positive root is
\[
h=\boxed{\dfrac{13+\sqrt{337}}{2}}\approx 15.68 .
\]

question:
The diagram is made up of a rectangle \(AGHB\), an equilateral triangle \(AFG\), a rectangle \(ADEF\), and a parallelogram \(ABCD\), which fit together around the common vertex \(A\) without overlapping. Find the degree measure of \(\angle ABC\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair H=(0,0), B=(1.3,0), G=(0,1.3), A=(1.3,1.3);
pair F=(0.65,1.3+1.3*sqrt(3)/2);
pair D=A+1.0*dir(30), Ept=F+(D-A), C=B+(D-A);
draw(A--G--H--B--cycle); draw(G--F--A); draw(F--Ept--D--A); draw(B--C--D);
label("$H$",H,SW); label("$B$",B,S); label("$G$",G,W); label("$A$",A+(-0.36,-0.36));
label("$F$",F,NW); label("$E$",Ept,N); label("$D$",D,E); label("$C$",C,E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
The four figures fill the full angle around \(A\), so the four angles at \(A\) add to \(360^\circ\):
\[
\angle GAB=90^\circ\ (\text{rectangle } AGHB),\quad \angle GAF=60^\circ\ (\text{equilateral } AFG),\quad \angle FAD=90^\circ\ (\text{rectangle } ADEF).
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair H=(0,0), B=(1.3,0), G=(0,1.3), A=(1.3,1.3);
pair F=(0.65,1.3+1.3*sqrt(3)/2);
pair D=A+1.0*dir(30), Ept=F+(D-A), C=B+(D-A);
draw(A--G--H--B--cycle); draw(G--F--A); draw(F--Ept--D--A); draw(B--C--D);
label("$H$",H,SW); label("$B$",B,S); label("$G$",G,W); label("$A$",A+(-0.36,-0.36));
label("$F$",F,NW); label("$E$",Ept,N); label("$D$",D,E); label("$C$",C,E);
draw(arc((1.3,1.3),0.22,180,270),red); draw(arc((1.3,1.3),0.3,120,180),red); draw(arc((1.3,1.3),0.22,30,120),red); draw(arc((1.3,1.3),0.38,-90,30),blue);
draw(arc((1.3,0),0.22,30,90),blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Hence the parallelogram's angle at \(A\) is
\[
\angle DAB=360^\circ-90^\circ-60^\circ-90^\circ=120^\circ.
\]
Consecutive angles of a parallelogram are supplementary, so
\[
\angle ABC=180^\circ-120^\circ=\boxed{60^\circ}.
\]

question:
The nine-sided figure below has only two sizes of interior angles: the six angles at the ends of the three arms are all equal, and the three angles where the arms meet are all equal. The larger angles are three times the size of the smaller angles. Find the degree measure of one of the larger angles.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V; pair p=(0,0); real hd=90; real[] L={2.2,1.2,2.2}; real[] T={-72,96,96};
// walk: at each vertex turn, then walk the next edge; pattern reflex, small, small
real ang=hd; V.push(p);
for(int k=0;k<9;++k){ ang+=T[k%3]; p=p+L[k%3]*dir(ang); V.push(p);}
path g=V[0]; for(int k=1;k<9;++k) g=g--V[k]; g=g--cycle;
draw(g,linewidth(0.9));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
Let the smaller angle be \(x\); the larger is \(3x\). The figure has six small angles (at the arm ends) and three large (reflex) angles where the arms meet.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V; pair p=(0,0); real hd=90; real[] L={2.2,1.2,2.2}; real[] T={-72,96,96};
// walk: at each vertex turn, then walk the next edge; pattern reflex, small, small
real ang=hd; V.push(p);
for(int k=0;k<9;++k){ ang+=T[k%3]; p=p+L[k%3]*dir(ang); V.push(p);}
path g=V[0]; for(int k=1;k<9;++k) g=g--V[k]; g=g--cycle;
draw(g,linewidth(0.9));
for(int k=0;k<9;++k){ if(k%3==0) dot(V[k],red+linewidth(3)); else dot(V[k],blue+linewidth(3)); }
label("$3x$",V[0]+(-0.55,0.35),red); label("$x$",V[1],NE,blue); label("$x$",V[2],NW,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The interior angles of any nine-sided polygon add to \((9-2)\cdot180^\circ=1260^\circ\). Therefore
\[
6x+3(3x)=15x=1260^\circ,\qquad x=84^\circ,
\]
and each larger angle is \(3x=\boxed{252^\circ}\) (a reflex angle, as expected where the arms meet).

question:
The sides of a \(30^\circ\)-\(60^\circ\)-\(90^\circ\) triangle are always in the ratio \(1:\sqrt3:2\), and those of a \(45^\circ\)-\(45^\circ\)-\(90^\circ\) triangle in the ratio \(1:1:\sqrt2\).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that for \emph{every} acute angle \(\theta\), all right triangles having an acute angle \(\theta\) have their sides (listed in the order: side opposite \(\theta\), side adjacent to \(\theta\), hypotenuse) in the same ratio, and express this ratio using trigonometric functions of \(\theta\).
\item[(b)] Give the ratio for a \(40^\circ\)-\(50^\circ\)-\(90^\circ\) triangle in the form \(1:p:q\) with \(p\) and \(q\) expressed exactly and to three decimal places.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let \(ABC\) and \(A'B'C'\) be right triangles with right angles at \(B\) and \(B'\) and \(\angle A=\angle A'=\theta\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(4,0), C=(4,4*Tan(40));
pair A2=(5.2,0), B2=(5.2+2.6,0), C2=(5.2+2.6,2.6*Tan(40));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A2--B2--C2--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B+(-0.3,0)--B+(-0.3,0.3)--B+(0,0.3)); draw(B2+(-0.25,0)--B2+(-0.25,0.25)--B2+(0,0.25));
draw(arc(A,0.7,0,40)); draw(arc(A2,0.6,0,40));
label("$\theta$",A+0.9*dir(20)); label("$\theta$",A2+0.8*dir(20));
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N);
label("$A'$",A2,SW); label("$B'$",B2,SE); label("$C'$",C2,N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Then the third angles are both \(90^\circ-\theta\), so the triangles are similar (AA). Hence
\[
\dfrac{BC}{B'C'}=\dfrac{AB}{A'B'}=\dfrac{AC}{A'C'}=k
\]
for some \(k>0\), so \(BC:AB:AC=B'C':A'B':A'C'\): the ratio depends only on \(\theta\). Taking the hypotenuse as \(1\), the side opposite \(\theta\) has length \(\sin\theta\) and the adjacent side has length \(\cos\theta\) (this is the definition of sine and cosine in a right triangle, which is consistent precisely because of the similarity just proved). So for every acute \(\theta\),
\[
\text{opposite}:\text{adjacent}:\text{hypotenuse}=\sin\theta:\cos\theta:1 .
\]
For \(\theta=30^\circ\) this is \(\dfrac{1}{2}:\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}:1=1:\sqrt3:2\), and for \(\theta=45^\circ\) it is \(1:1:\sqrt2\).

\textbf{(b)} With \(\theta=40^\circ\), divide by \(\sin40^\circ\):
\[
1:\dfrac{\cos40^\circ}{\sin40^\circ}:\dfrac{1}{\sin40^\circ}=\boxed{1:\tan50^\circ:\dfrac{1}{\sin 40^\circ}}\approx 1:1.192:1.556 .
\]
So such a three-number ratio exists for every right triangle; for most angles the numbers are not expressible with simple radicals, which is why only special cases like \(30^\circ\) and \(45^\circ\) are usually memorised.

For real \(r\), the cubic \(P(x)-r\) has real coefficients. It has a non-real root exactly when it does \emph{not} have three real roots (counted with multiplicity), since non-real roots come in conjugate pairs.

\(P'(x)=3x^2+2x-1=(3x-1)(x+1)\), so \(P\) increases on \((-\infty,-1]\), decreases on \(\left[-1,\dfrac{1}{3}\right]\) and increases on \(\left[\dfrac{1}{3},\infty\right)\), with
\[
P(-1)=3,\qquad P\!\left(\dfrac{1}{3}\right)=\dfrac{1}{27}+\dfrac{1}{9}-\dfrac{1}{3}+2=\dfrac{49}{27}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real P(real x){return x^3+x^2-x+2;}
draw(graph(P,-2.25,1.25),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-2.5,0)--(1.6,0),Arrow(4)); draw((0,-0.6)--(0,4.3),Arrow(4));
draw((-2.5,3)--(1.6,3),dashed+red); draw((-2.5,49/27)--(1.6,49/27),dashed+red);
label("$y=3$",(1.6,3),E,red); label("$y=\dfrac{49}{27}$",(1.6,49/27),E,red);
dot((-1,3)); dot((1/3,49/27));
label("$x$",(1.6,0),E); label("$y$",(0,4.3),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\begin{itemize}
\item If \(\dfrac{49}{27}<r<3\): by the intermediate value theorem \(P(x)=r\) has a root in each of \((-\infty,-1)\), \(\left(-1,\dfrac{1}{3}\right)\), \(\left(\dfrac{1}{3},\infty\right)\), so all three roots are real.
\item If \(r=3\): \(P(x)-3=x^3+x^2-x-1=(x+1)^2(x-1)\), all roots real.
\item If \(r=\dfrac{49}{27}\): \(P(x)-\dfrac{49}{27}=\left(x-\dfrac{1}{3}\right)^2\left(x+\dfrac{5}{3}\right)\), all roots real.
\item If \(r>3\): on \(\left(-\infty,\dfrac{1}{3}\right]\) we have \(P(x)\le3<r\), and on \(\left[\dfrac{1}{3},\infty\right)\) \(P\) is strictly increasing, so there is exactly one real root, and it is simple because \(P'\ne0\) there. The other two roots are non-real.
\item If \(r<\dfrac{49}{27}\): similarly \(P(x)\ge\dfrac{49}{27}>r\) on \([-1,\infty)\) and \(P\) is strictly increasing on \((-\infty,-1]\), so again exactly one simple real root and two non-real roots.
\end{itemize}
Therefore such a non-real \(z\) exists exactly when
\[
\boxed{r<\dfrac{49}{27}\ \text{ or }\ r>3}.
\]

Moving from \(X\) to \(Y\), the \(y\)-coordinate drops from \(4\) to \(-7.2\), a total change of \(11.2\). The point \(Z\) is where \(y=0\), which is reached after a drop of \(4\), i.e. after the fraction \(\dfrac{4}{11.2}=\dfrac{5}{14}\) of the way along the segment.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair X=(3,4), Y=(9.6,-7.2), Z=(75/14,0);
draw((-0.5,0)--(11,0),Arrow(4)); draw((0,-8)--(0,5.5),Arrow(4));
draw(X--Y,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(X--(3,0),dashed); draw(Y--(9.6,0),dashed);
dot(X);dot(Y);dot(Z,red);
label("$X(3,4)$",X,NE); label("$Y(9.6,-7.2)$",Y,E); label("$Z$",Z,NE,red);
label("$4$",(3,2),W); label("$7.2$",(9.6,-3.6),E);
label("$x$",(11,0),E); label("$y$",(0,5.5),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The \(x\)-coordinate increases by \(9.6-3=6.6\) along the whole segment, so
\[
x_Z=3+\dfrac{5}{14}\cdot 6.6=3+\dfrac{33}{14}=\boxed{\dfrac{75}{14}}.
\]
Since the distances along the segment are proportional to the changes in \(y\),
\[
XZ:ZY=4:7.2=\boxed{5:9}.
\]
(Check: the line has slope \(\dfrac{-11.2}{6.6}=-\dfrac{56}{33}\), and \(4-\dfrac{56}{33}\left(\dfrac{75}{14}-3\right)=4-\dfrac{56}{33}\cdot\dfrac{33}{14}=0\).)

question:
Call a positive integer \emph{sufficiently prime} if it is not divisible by any of \(2,3,4,5,6,7\).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many sufficiently prime numbers are less than \(100\)?
\item[(b)] How many sufficiently prime numbers are less than \(1{,}000{,}000\)?
\item[(c)] Give, with proof, a formula for the number \(f(m)\) of sufficiently prime numbers among \(1,2,\dots,m\).
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the space diagonal be \(PQ\). Go from \(P\) along the diagonal of the \(24\times8\) base first, then up the edge of length \(6\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair ex=(1,0), ey=(0.45,0.3), ez=(0,1);
pair P(real x,real y,real z){return (x/4)*ex+(y/4)*ey+(z/4)*ez;}
real a=24,b=8,c=6;
