Put the box at \(0\le x\le50\), \(0\le y\le10\) with \(M=(25,0)\). By symmetry assume the ball moves to the right with slope \(k>0\). Instead of reflecting the ball, reflect the box: the path becomes a straight ray from \(M\) in the plane tiled by copies of the box, the corners become the lattice points \((50i,10j)\), and each bounce corresponds to crossing a grid line.

Suppose the ray first reaches a corner at \((50i,10j)\) with \(i,j\ge1\). On the way it crosses the vertical lines \(x=50,\dots,50(i-1)\) and the horizontal lines \(y=10,\dots,10(j-1)\), giving \((i-1)+(j-1)\) bounces. So \(i+j=5\), and
\[
k=\dfrac{10j}{50i-25}=\dfrac{2j}{5(2i-1)}\in\left\{\dfrac{8}{5},\ \dfrac{2}{5},\ \dfrac{4}{25},\ \dfrac{2}{35}\right\}
\]
for \((i,j)=(1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)\). (In each case no earlier lattice corner lies on the segment, so the corner is reached for the first time.)

Among the choices only \(1.6=\dfrac{8}{5}\) appears. For \(k=1.6\) the ball hits the top at \(x=31.25\), the bottom at \(x=37.5\), the top at \(x=43.75\), and then the corner \(B=(50,0)\):

