question:
Five standard dice are rolled, and the numbers on their top faces are written side by side (in the order of the dice) to form a five-digit number.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the probability that this number is divisible by \(11\)?
\item[(b)] What is the probability that it is divisible by \(9\)?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Every such square fits in a smallest axis-parallel square (its bounding square) whose vertices are lattice points: if the square has vertices \((x_1,y_1),\dots\), the bounding square has side \(k\) equal to the horizontal extent of the square, with \(1\le k\le n\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<=4;++i){draw((i,0)--(i,4),gray(0.7)); draw((0,i)--(4,i),gray(0.7));}
for(int i=0;i<=4;++i) for(int j=0;j<=4;++j) dot((i,j),linewidth(2));
draw((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(0,3)--cycle,dashed);
draw((1,0)--(3,1)--(2,3)--(0,2)--cycle,blue+linewidth(1));
label("$a$",(0.5,0),S); label("$k-a$",(2,0),S); label("$k$",(3,1.5),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\emph{Each bounding square of side \(k\) contains exactly \(k\) squares that it bounds.} Such a square has one vertex on each side of the bounding square. If its vertex on the bottom side is at distance \(a\) from the bottom-left corner (\(0\le a\le k-1\)), then rotating by \(90^\circ\) about the centre shows the other vertices are at distance \(a\) along the other sides (going around), giving the square with vertices \((a,0),(k,a),(k-a,k),(0,k-a)\) relative to the corner. Conversely each \(a\in\{0,1,\dots,k-1\}\) gives one such square (\(a=0\) is the bounding square itself), and different \(a\) give different squares.

\emph{Counting.} An axis-parallel \(k\times k\) square with lattice vertices can be placed in \((n-k+1)^2\) positions. Hence the total is
\[
\sum_{k=1}^{n}k\,(n-k+1)^2 .
\]
Substituting \(j=n+1-k\), this is \(\sum_{j=1}^{n}(n+1-j)j^2=(n+1)\dfrac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6}-\dfrac{n^2(n+1)^2}{4}\), which simplifies to
\[
\boxed{\dfrac{n(n+1)^2(n+2)}{12}}.
\]
Check: \(n=1\) gives \(1\); \(n=2\) gives \(6\) (four unit squares, one \(2\times2\), one tilted square).

question:
A forum post claims to prove that \(1+2+3+4+\cdots=-\dfrac{1}{12}\) as follows.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Let \(S_1=1-1+1-1+\cdots\). Its partial sums alternate between \(1\) and \(0\), so ``\(S_1=\dfrac{1}{2}\)''.
\item Let \(S_2=1-2+3-4+\cdots\). Adding \(S_2\) to itself shifted one place gives \(2S_2=1-1+1-1+\cdots=S_1\), so ``\(S_2=\dfrac{1}{4}\)''.
\item Let \(S=1+2+3+\cdots\). Subtracting term by term, \(S-S_2=0+4+0+8+0+12+\cdots=4S\), so \(3S=-S_2\) and ``\(S=-\dfrac{1}{12}\)''.
\end{enumerate}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that the series \(1+2+3+\cdots\) does not converge to any real number.
\item[(b)] Explain which steps of the argument are invalid.
\item[(c)] Show that manipulations of the same kind can ``prove'' \(0=1\).
\end{enumerate}

In a poll, \(21\) people chose blue, \(14\) chose black, \(13\) chose red, \(17\) chose pink, \(9\) chose yellow, \(15\) chose purple, \(7\) chose ``other'', and \(32\) chose cyan/teal. One voter is selected at random.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the probability that the selected voter chose cyan/teal, purple, or yellow?
\item[(b)] What is the mean number of votes per option (over the eight options)?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
An axis-parallel \(k\times k\) square needs \(k+1\) rows and \(k+1\) columns of points, so \(1\le k\le4\). In a grid with \(5\) rows and \(n\) columns, its lower-left corner can be chosen in \((5-k)(n-k)\) ways.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<12;++i) for(int j=0;j<5;++j) dot((i,j),linewidth(2));
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle,blue+linewidth(1));
draw((1,2)--(3,2)--(3,4)--(1,4)--cycle,red+linewidth(1));
draw((4,0)--(7,0)--(7,3)--(4,3)--cycle,deepgreen+linewidth(1));
draw((8,0)--(11,0)--(11,3)--(8,3)--cycle,invisible);
draw((7,0)--(11,0)--(11,4)--(7,4)--cycle,orange+linewidth(1)+dashed);
label("$n=12$ columns",(5.5,4.3),N); label("$5$ rows",(0,2),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So the number of such squares is
\[
4(n-1)+3(n-2)+2(n-3)+1\cdot(n-4)=10n-20 .
\]
Setting \(10n-20=100\) gives \(n=\boxed{12}\).

question:
A set \(A\) of at least three positive integers is called \emph{prime} if the sum of any three (distinct) elements of \(A\) is a prime number.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Give an example of a prime set of the form \(A=\{3,5,a,b\}\).
\item[(b)] Show that a prime set cannot have \(5\) or more elements.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the circle have radius \(r\). The diagonals of the inscribed square are diameters, of length \(2r\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),1));
pair A=dir(45),B=dir(135),C=dir(225),D=dir(315);
fill(A--B--C--D--cycle,paleblue); draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0)--A,red); label("$r$",0.5*A,SE,red); dot((0,0)); label("$K$",(0,0),S);
label("$A$",A,NE);label("$B$",B,NW);label("$C$",C,SW);label("$D$",D,SE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

A square with diagonal \(2r\) has area \(\dfrac{(2r)^2}{2}=2r^2\). The circle has area \(\pi r^2\), so the fraction is
\[
\dfrac{2r^2}{\pi r^2}=\boxed{\dfrac{2}{\pi}}.
\]

Let the height be \(h\) and let \(V\) be the volume of the whole cone. Similar cones have volumes proportional to the cubes of their heights.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real H=12.005, R=4;
// left: vertex up, air cone of height 10 at the top
pair V1=(0,H); pair L1=(-R,0), R1=(R,0);
real yw=H-10; real rw=R*10/H;
fill((-rw,yw)--(rw,yw)--R1--L1--cycle,paleblue);
draw(L1--V1--R1--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw((-rw,yw)--(rw,yw),blue);
draw((R+0.6,yw)--(R+0.6,H),Arrows(3)); label("$10$",(R+0.6,(yw+H)/2),E);
// right: vertex down
pair V2=(13,0); pair L2=(13-R,H), R2=(13+R,H);
real yh=H-3; real rh=R*yh/H;
fill(V2--(13-rh,yh)--(13+rh,yh)--cycle,paleblue);
draw(L2--V2--R2--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw((13-rh,yh)--(13+rh,yh),blue);
draw((13+R+0.6,yh)--(13+R+0.6,H),Arrows(3)); label("$3$",(13+R+0.6,(yh+H)/2),E);
label("$h$",(-R-0.3,H/2),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Vertex up, the air forms a cone of height \(10\) at the top, so the water has volume \(V\left(1-\dfrac{10^3}{h^3}\right)\). Vertex down, the water forms a cone of height \(h-3\), of volume \(V\dfrac{(h-3)^3}{h^3}\). Equating,
\[
h^3-1000=(h-3)^3=h^3-9h^2+27h-27\iff 9h^2-27h-973=0 .
\]
The positive root is
\[
h=\dfrac{27+\sqrt{27^2+36\cdot973}}{18}=\dfrac{27+\sqrt{35757}}{18}=\dfrac{27+3\sqrt{3973}}{18}=\boxed{\dfrac{9+\sqrt{3973}}{6}}\approx12.0,
\]
which indeed exceeds \(10\). (\(3973=29\cdot137\) is squarefree.)

In kilometres the moon has radius \(R=1600\cdot1.6=2560\), and the haze extends to radius \(R+t=3160\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),1.234),dashed+gray); fill(circle((0,0),1.234),gray(0.9));
fill(circle((0,0),1),paleblue); draw(circle((0,0),1),linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0)--(1,0),red); label("$R$",(0.5,0),S,red);
draw((1,0)--(1.234,0),blue); label("$t$",(1.117,0),N,blue);
label("haze",(0,1.12),fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The haze is the shell between the two spheres, so the required ratio is
\[
\dfrac{\dfrac{4}{3}\pi(3160^3-2560^3)}{\dfrac{4}{3}\pi\cdot2560^3}=\left(\dfrac{3160}{2560}\right)^3-1=\left(\dfrac{79}{64}\right)^3-1=\dfrac{493039-262144}{262144}=\dfrac{230895}{262144}\approx0.8808 .
\]
So the haze has about \(\boxed{88.1\%}\) of the volume of the moon.

The triple \((x,y,z)\) is uniform in the cube \([0,6]^3\) of volume \(216\). The condition \(x^2+y^2+z^2\le20\) describes the part of the ball of radius \(\sqrt{20}\) centred at the origin lying in the first octant. Since \(\sqrt{20}<6\), this whole eighth of the ball lies inside the cube (the figure shows a cross-section).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill((0,0)--arc((0,0),sqrt(20)/1.5,0,90)--cycle,paleblue);
draw((0,0)--(4,0)--(4,4)--(0,4)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc((0,0),sqrt(20)/1.5,0,90),blue+linewidth(0.9));
label("$6$",(2,0),S); label("$6$",(0,2),W); label("$\sqrt{20}$",(sqrt(20)/1.5)*dir(45),NE,blue);
label("$0$",(0,0),SW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Its volume is
\[
\dfrac{1}{8}\cdot\dfrac{4}{3}\pi\left(\sqrt{20}\right)^3=\dfrac{\pi}{6}\cdot20\cdot2\sqrt5=\dfrac{20\sqrt5\,\pi}{3}.
\]
So the probability is
\[
\dfrac{20\sqrt5\,\pi}{3\cdot216}=\dfrac{5\sqrt5\,\pi}{162},
\]
giving \(a=5\), \(b=5\), \(c=162\) and \(a+b+c=\boxed{172}\).

The given ring has area
\[
\pi\cdot5^2-\pi\cdot2.5^2=25\pi-6.25\pi=\dfrac{75\pi}{4}\ \text{cm}^2.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill(circle((0,0),2),paleblue); fill(circle((0,0),1),white);
draw(circle((0,0),2),linewidth(0.9)); draw(circle((0,0),1),linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0)--2*dir(30),red); label("$5$",1.6*dir(30),NW,red);
draw((0,0)--dir(200),blue); label("$2.5$",0.5*dir(200),N,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The new ring has twice this area: \(2\cdot\dfrac{75\pi}{4}=\boxed{\dfrac{75\pi}{2}}\) cm\(^2\). (Its radii are \(\sqrt2\) times as large, \(5\sqrt2\) and \(\dfrac{5\sqrt2}{2}\) cm.)

The square has side \(s=4\).

\emph{Largest equilateral triangle.} Let an equilateral triangle of side \(t\) lie in the square, and let \(\theta\) be the angle between one of its sides and the bottom side of the square. Its width (length of projection) in the horizontal direction is \(t\cos d\), where \(d\in[0^\circ,30^\circ]\) is the distance from \(\theta\) to the nearest multiple of \(60^\circ\) (the projection is the longest side-projection, and the three sides make angles \(\theta,\theta+60^\circ,\theta+120^\circ\)). The vertical width is \(t\cos d'\) with \(d'\) defined the same way for \(\theta+90^\circ\); since \(90^\circ\equiv30^\circ\pmod{60^\circ}\), \(d'=30^\circ-d\). Both widths are at most \(4\), so
\[
4\ge t\max(\cos d,\cos(30^\circ-d))=t\cos(\min(d,30^\circ-d))\ge t\cos15^\circ .
\]
Thus \(t\le\dfrac{4}{\cos15^\circ}=\dfrac{16}{\sqrt6+\sqrt2}=4(\sqrt6-\sqrt2)\). This is attained by the triangle with one vertex at a corner of the square and its two sides from that corner making \(15^\circ\) angles with the square's sides:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=4, tt=Tan(15);
pair O=(0,0), P=(s,s*tt), Q=(s*tt,s);
draw((0,0)--(s,0)--(s,s)--(0,s)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--P--Q--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
real a=abs(P-Q); pair I=(O+P+Q)/3; real r=a/(2*sqrt(3));
draw(circle(I,r),red);
label("$15^\circ$",O+1.3*dir(7.5),fontsize(8pt)); label("$15^\circ$",(0.24,2.2),fontsize(7pt));
label("$4$",(s/2,0),S); dot(I); label("$r$",I+(r/2,0),N,red); draw(I--I+(r,0),red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

its vertices \((0,0)\), \((4,4\tan15^\circ)\), \((4\tan15^\circ,4)\) are pairwise \(4(\sqrt6-\sqrt2)\) apart (using \(\tan15^\circ=2-\sqrt3\)).

\emph{The circle.} The largest circle in a triangle is its incircle; for an equilateral triangle of side \(t\) its radius is \(r=\dfrac{t}{2\sqrt3}\). So
\[
r^2=\dfrac{t^2}{12}=\dfrac{16(8-4\sqrt3)}{12}=\dfrac{32-16\sqrt3}{3},
\]
and the area of the circle is
\[
\pi r^2=\boxed{\dfrac{16(2-\sqrt3)\pi}{3}}\approx1.40 .
\]

Let the hexagon have side \(s\). It consists of six equilateral triangles of side \(s\), so its area is \(6\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}s^2\). The inscribed circle's radius is the apothem, the height of one of these triangles: \(r=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] H; for(int i=0;i<6;++i) H.push(dir(60*i));
fill(circle((0,0),sqrt(3)/2),paleblue);
draw(H[0]--H[1]--H[2]--H[3]--H[4]--H[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle((0,0),sqrt(3)/2),blue);
draw((0,0)--H[0],gray); label("$s$",0.5*H[0],S);
draw((0,0)--(sqrt(3)/2)*dir(30),red); label("$r$",(sqrt(3)/4)*dir(30),NW,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So the fraction is
\[
\dfrac{\pi\cdot\dfrac{3}{4}s^2}{\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}s^2}=\dfrac{\pi}{2\sqrt3}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt3\,\pi}{6}}.
\]

Let the edge be \(s\). The altitude of a regular tetrahedron is \(s\sqrt{\dfrac{2}{3}}\), so \(s=8\sqrt{\dfrac{3}{2}}=4\sqrt6\). Its volume is
\[
\dfrac{s^3}{6\sqrt2}=\dfrac{(4\sqrt6)^3}{6\sqrt2}=\dfrac{384\sqrt6}{6\sqrt2}=64\sqrt3 .
\]
The inradius is \(\dfrac{1}{4}\) of the altitude: joining the incentre to the four vertices splits the tetrahedron into four congruent pyramids of height \(r\) over the four faces, so \(4\cdot\dfrac{1}{3}Ar=\dfrac{1}{3}A\cdot8\), giving \(r=2\). The figure shows the cross-section through the apex, a base vertex and the midpoint of the opposite base edge.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real m=6*sqrt(2); pair V1=(0,0), Md=(m,0), G=(2*m/3,0), Ap=(2*m/3,8);
draw(V1--Md--Ap--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(Ap--G,dashed);
draw(circle((2*m/3,2),2),blue+linewidth(0.9)); dot((2*m/3,2)); draw((2*m/3,2)--(2*m/3+2,2),red); label("$2$",(2*m/3+1,2),N,red);
label("apex",Ap,N); label("$8$",(2*m/3,6),W); label("vertex",V1,S); label("edge midpoint",Md,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The sphere has volume \(\dfrac{4}{3}\pi\cdot2^3=\dfrac{32\pi}{3}\). The space outside the sphere has volume
\[
\boxed{64\sqrt3-\dfrac{32\pi}{3}=\dfrac{192\sqrt3-32\pi}{3}}\approx77.3 .
\]

There are \(\binom82=28\) pairs of vertices. A pair is joined by an edge (\(L^2=64\)), a face diagonal (\(L^2=128\)) or a space diagonal (\(L^2=192\)).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair ex=(1,0), ey=(0.45,0.35), ez=(0,1);
pair P(real x,real y,real z){return x*ex+y*ey+z*ez;}
draw(P(0,0,0)--P(1,0,0)--P(1,0,1)--P(0,0,1)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(P(1,0,0)--P(1,1,0)--P(1,1,1)--P(1,0,1)); draw(P(0,0,1)--P(0,1,1)--P(1,1,1));
draw(P(0,0,0)--P(0,1,0)--P(1,1,0),dashed); draw(P(0,1,0)--P(0,1,1),dashed);
draw(P(0,0,0)--P(1,0,0),blue+linewidth(1.2)); draw(P(0,0,0)--P(1,0,1),deepgreen+linewidth(1)); draw(P(0,0,0)--P(1,1,1),red+linewidth(1));
label("$8$",P(0.5,0,0),S,blue); label("$8\sqrt2$",P(0.3,0,0.72),deepgreen); label("$8\sqrt3$",P(1,1,0.6),E,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

There are \(12\) edges, \(6\cdot2=12\) face diagonals and \(4\) space diagonals (\(12+12+4=28\)). The condition \(L^2\ge100\) excludes exactly the edges, so the probability is
\[
\dfrac{12+4}{28}=\boxed{\dfrac{4}{7}}.
\]

At 2:44 the minute hand is at \(44\cdot6^\circ=264^\circ\) (clockwise from 12 o'clock) and the hour hand at \(2\cdot30^\circ+44\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}^\circ=82^\circ\). The hands are \(264^\circ-82^\circ=182^\circ\) apart, so the smaller angle is \(u=360^\circ-182^\circ=178^\circ\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),1),linewidth(0.9));
for(int k=0;k<12;++k) draw(0.9*dir(90-30*k)--dir(90-30*k));
label("$12$",0.75*dir(90),fontsize(7pt)); label("$3$",0.75*dir(0),fontsize(7pt)); label("$6$",0.75*dir(270),fontsize(7pt)); label("$9$",0.75*dir(180),fontsize(7pt));
draw((0,0)--0.55*dir(90-82),linewidth(1.6)); draw((0,0)--0.85*dir(90-264),blue+linewidth(1));
draw(arc((0,0),0.3,90-82,90-264+360),red); label("$178^\circ$",0.42*dir(100),red+fontsize(7pt));
dot((0,0));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}


Let \(t\) be the number of minutes after midnight. The minute hand gains \(6-\dfrac{1}{2}=\dfrac{11}{2}\) degrees per minute on the hour hand, so the clockwise angle from the hour hand to the minute hand is \(\theta(t)=\dfrac{11}{2}t\pmod{360}\). The smaller angle equals \(178^\circ\) exactly when \(\theta\equiv178\) or \(\theta\equiv182\pmod{360}\). During the \(1440\) minutes of a day, \(\dfrac{11}{2}t\) increases by \(7920=22\cdot360\) degrees, so \(\theta\) runs through \(22\) full cycles, each containing one time with \(\theta=178\) and one with \(\theta=182\). Hence \(p=44\), and
\[
u+p=178+44=\boxed{222}.
\]

question:
Consider the three-digit positive integers all of whose digits belong to the set \(\{2,0,1,9\}\).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the mean of all such integers when digits may be repeated.
\item[(b)] Find the mean when the three digits must be distinct, and explain why the shortcut ``average each digit position separately'' still works here but must be applied with the correct digit distributions.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Put the axis \(AC\) on the \(t\)-axis with \(A\) at \(t=0\) and \(C\) at \(t=\sqrt5\). The rectangle is made of triangles \(ABC\) and \(ACD\) on opposite sides of \(AC\). Rotating, only the distance of each point from the axis matters, so we may reflect triangle \(ACD\) across \(AC\) to \(ACD'\) on the same side as \(B\). The solid is obtained by rotating the union of triangles \(ABC\) and \(AD'C\), i.e. the region under the upper envelope (red) of the two triangles.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real L=sqrt(5);
// axis AC horizontal from (0,0) to (L,0); B above, D below, then reflect D's triangle above for the profile
real h=2/sqrt(5);
pair A=(0,0), C=(L,0), B=(4/sqrt(5),h), D=(1/sqrt(5),-h), Dr=(1/sqrt(5),h);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--Dr--C,dashed+gray);
fill(A--Dr--(L/2,L/4)--B--C--(L/2,-L/4)--D--cycle,paleblue+opacity(0.3));
draw((-0.3,0)--(L+0.3,0),dashdotted);
draw(A--Dr--(L/2,L/4)--B--C,red+linewidth(1));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(Dr);dot((L/2,L/4));
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$B$",B,N); label("$D$",D,S); label("$D'$",Dr,N); label("$K$",(L/2,L/4),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Both triangles have height \(h=\dfrac{AB\cdot BC}{AC}=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt5}\) over \(AC\). The foot of the perpendicular from \(B\) is at \(t=\dfrac{AB^2}{AC}=\dfrac{4}{\sqrt5}\), and that from \(D'\) at \(t=\dfrac{AD^2}{AC}=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt5}\). Thus side \(AB\) is the line \(r=\dfrac {t}{2}\) and side \(D'C\) is the line \(r=\dfrac{\sqrt5-t}{2}\); side \(AD'\) is \(r=2t\) for \(0\le t\le\dfrac{1}{\sqrt5}\). The lines \(AB\) and \(D'C\) cross at \(K\), where \(t=\dfrac{\sqrt5}{2}\) (the centre of the rectangle) and \(r=\dfrac{\sqrt5}{4}\). By the symmetry \(t\mapsto\sqrt5-t\), the envelope is
\[
r(t)=\begin{cases}2t,&0\le t\le\dfrac{1}{\sqrt5},\\[2mm] \dfrac{\sqrt5-t}{2},&\dfrac{1}{\sqrt5}\le t\le\dfrac{\sqrt5}{2},\end{cases}
\]
mirrored on the other half. Hence
\[
V=2\pi\left(\int_0^{\dfrac{1}{\sqrt5}}4t^2\,dt+\int_{\dfrac{1}{\sqrt5}}^{\dfrac{\sqrt5}{2}}\dfrac{(\sqrt5-t)^2}{4}\,dt\right).
\]
The first integral is \(\dfrac{4}{3}\cdot\dfrac{1}{5\sqrt5}=\dfrac{4}{15\sqrt5}\). For the second, with \(u=\sqrt5-t\) running from \(\dfrac{4}{\sqrt5}\) down to \(\dfrac{\sqrt5}{2}\),
\[
\dfrac{1}{12}\left(\left(\dfrac{4}{\sqrt5}\right)^3-\left(\dfrac{\sqrt5}{2}\right)^3\right)=\dfrac{1}{12}\left(\dfrac{64}{5\sqrt5}-\dfrac{5\sqrt5}{8}\right)=\dfrac{1}{12}\cdot\dfrac{387}{40\sqrt5}=\dfrac{129}{160\sqrt5}.
\]
So
\[
V=2\pi\left(\dfrac{128}{480\sqrt5}+\dfrac{387}{480\sqrt5}\right)=2\pi\cdot\dfrac{515}{480\sqrt5}=\dfrac{103\pi}{48\sqrt5}=\boxed{\dfrac{103\sqrt5\,\pi}{240}}\approx3.015 .
\]
(Equivalently, the solid is a union of cones: two cones of radius \(\dfrac{2}{\sqrt5}\) with heights \(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt5}\), plus two frustum-like pieces up to the circle of radius \(\dfrac{\sqrt5}{4}\) at the centre.)

Extend the legs (non-parallel sides) of all the trapezoids. By symmetry they all pass through a common centre \(O\) on the horizontal base line, and the \(9\) trapezoids divide the straight angle at \(O\) into \(9\) equal angles of \(\dfrac{180^\circ}{9}=20^\circ\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R1=2, R2=3; int n=9; real d=180/n;
for(int i=0;i<n;++i){ real a0=i*d, a1=(i+1)*d;
  path tr=R1*dir(a0)--R2*dir(a0)--R2*dir(a1)--R1*dir(a1)--cycle; draw(tr,linewidth(0.9));}
draw((0,0)--R1*dir(0),dashed+gray); draw((0,0)--R1*dir(d),dashed+gray);
draw(arc((0,0),0.6,0,d),blue); label("$20^\circ$",1.0*dir(d/2),E,blue+fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc(R1*dir(d),0.25,-80,d),red); label("$x$",R1*dir(d)+0.42*dir(-30),red+fontsize(9pt));
dot((0,0)); label("$O$",(0,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Each trapezoid together with the small triangle cut off near \(O\) forms an isosceles triangle with apex angle \(20^\circ\), whose base angles are \(\dfrac{180^\circ-20^\circ}{2}=80^\circ\). These \(80^\circ\) angles are the trapezoid's angles at its longer (outer) base. The angles at the shorter (inner) base are supplementary to them, so
\[
x=180^\circ-80^\circ=\boxed{100^\circ}.
\]

The function \(\sin^{-1}\) (arcsine) returns the unique angle in \(\left[-\dfrac{\pi}{2},\dfrac{\pi}{2}\right]\) with the given sine. Since sine has period \(2\pi\),
\[
\sin20=\sin(20-6\pi).
\]
Now \(6\pi\approx18.850\), so \(20-6\pi\approx1.150\), which lies in \(\left[-\dfrac{\pi}{2},\dfrac{\pi}{2}\right]\approx[-1.571,1.571]\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),1),gray);
draw((-1.3,0)--(1.3,0),Arrow(4)); draw((0,-1.3)--(0,1.3),Arrow(4));
real th=20-6*pi;
pair P=dir(th*180/pi);
draw((0,0)--P,blue+linewidth(1)); dot(P);
draw(arc((0,0),0.3,0,th*180/pi),Arrow(3));
label("$20-6\pi$",(0.45,0.12),E,fontsize(8pt));
draw(P--(P.x,0),dashed); label("$\sin 20$",(P.x,0.65*P.y),E,fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Therefore
\[
\sin^{-1}(\sin20)=\boxed{20-6\pi}\approx1.150 .
\]

Let the radii be \(r_1\ge r_2\) and the distance between the centres \(d=65\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real sc=0.04; real d=65, r1=38.5, r2=13.5;
pair O1=(0,0), O2=(d,0);
draw(scale(sc)*circle(O1,r1)); draw(scale(sc)*circle(O2,r2));
real al=acos((r1-r2)/d); pair T1=O1+r1*dir(al*180/pi), T2=O2+r2*dir(al*180/pi);
real be=acos((r1+r2)/d); pair U1=O1+r1*dir(-be*180/pi), U2=O2+r2*dir(180-be*180/pi);
draw(scale(sc)*(T1--T2),blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(scale(sc)*(U1--U2),red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(scale(sc)*(O1--O2),dashed); dot(scale(sc)*O1); dot(scale(sc)*O2);
draw(scale(sc)*(O1--T1),gray); draw(scale(sc)*(O2--T2),gray); draw(scale(sc)*(O1--U1),gray); draw(scale(sc)*(O2--U2),gray);
label("$60$",scale(sc)*((T1+T2)/2),N,blue); label("$39$",scale(sc)*((U1+U2)/2)+(0.1,-0.1),SE,red); label("$65$",scale(sc)*(45,0),S);
label("$r_1$",scale(sc)*(O1+0.5*(T1-O1)),W); label("$r_2$",scale(sc)*(O2+0.5*(T2-O2)),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

For the external tangent, sliding it towards the centres by \(r_2\) gives a right triangle with hypotenuse \(d\) and legs \(60\) and \(r_1-r_2\); for the internal tangent, a right triangle with hypotenuse \(d\) and legs \(39\) and \(r_1+r_2\). So
\[
(r_1-r_2)^2=65^2-60^2=625,\qquad (r_1+r_2)^2=65^2-39^2=2704 .
\]
Therefore
\[
r_1r_2=\dfrac{(r_1+r_2)^2-(r_1-r_2)^2}{4}=\dfrac{2704-625}{4}=\boxed{\dfrac{2079}{4}}=519.75
\]
(the radii are \(38.5\) and \(13.5\)).

Two distinct circles meet in at most \(2\) points, so \(n\) circles have at most \(2\binom n2=n(n-1)\) intersection points.

This is attained: take \(n\) circles of radius \(1\) with centres on the \(x\)-axis at \(x=0.01\cdot2^k\) for \(k=0,1,\dots,n-1\) (all within distance \(2\) of each other for \(n\le7\)). Any two of these circles, with centres at \(a\) and \(b\), meet in exactly two points, both with \(x\)-coordinate \(\dfrac{a+b}{2}\). Sums of two distinct powers of \(2\) are all different, so intersection points coming from different pairs of circles have different \(x\)-coordinates. Hence all \(n(n-1)\) intersection points are distinct.

For example, three such circles already give \(3\cdot2=6\) distinct points:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real[] c={0,0.5,1.2};
for(int i=0;i<3;++i) draw(circle((c[i],0),1),linewidth(0.8));
for(int i=0;i<3;++i) for(int j=i+1;j<3;++j){ real x=(c[i]+c[j])/2, y=sqrt(1-((c[j]-c[i])/2)^2); dot((x,y),red); dot((x,-y),red);}
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Hence the maximum is \(n(n-1)\); for \(n=6\) it is \(\boxed{30}\).

For an acute angle \(\theta\), \(\tan\theta\cdot\tan(90^\circ-\theta)=1\): in a right triangle with legs \(a\) (opposite \(\theta\)) and \(b\), \(\tan\theta=\dfrac {a}{b}\) and the other acute angle \(90^\circ-\theta\) has \(\tan(90^\circ-\theta)=\dfrac {b}{a}\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(4,0), C=(4,4*Tan(20));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw((3.75,0)--(3.75,0.25)--(4,0.25));
draw(arc(A,0.8,0,20)); label("$20^\circ$",A+1.1*dir(10),fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc(C,0.45,250,270)); label("$70^\circ$",C+1.0*dir(253),fontsize(8pt));
label("$a$",(B+C)/2+(0.15,0),E); label("$b$",(A+B)/2,S);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Pairing \(20^\circ\) with \(70^\circ\) and \(40^\circ\) with \(50^\circ\),
\[
\tan20^\circ\tan70^\circ\cdot\tan40^\circ\tan50^\circ=1\cdot1=\boxed{1}.
\]

Reflecting in the \(x\)-axis negates \(y\): \(Q=(3,-2)\). Reflecting in \(y=-x\) sends \((x,y)\) to \((-y,-x)\): \(R=(2,-3)\). Reflecting through the origin negates both: \(S=(-2,3)\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-3.5,0)--(4,0),Arrow(4)); draw((0,-3.8)--(0,3.8),Arrow(4));
draw((-3.5,3.5)--(3.5,-3.5),dashed+gray); label("$y=-x$",(3.5,-3.5),E,gray);
pair P=(3,2), Q=(3,-2), R=(2,-3), Sp=(-2,3);
fill(P--Q--R--Sp--cycle,paleblue); draw(P--Q--R--Sp--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(P);dot(Q);dot(R);dot(Sp);
label("$P(3,2)$",P,E); label("$Q(3,-2)$",Q,E); label("$R(2,-3)$",R,S); label("$S(-2,3)$",Sp,N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

By the shoelace formula with \(P,Q,R,S\) in order,
\[
\begin{aligned}
2[PQRS]&=\left|(3\cdot(-2)-3\cdot2)+(3\cdot(-3)-2\cdot(-2))+(2\cdot3-(-2)(-3))+((-2)\cdot2-3\cdot3)\right|\\
&=|-12-5+0-13|=30,
\end{aligned}
\]
so the area is \(15\). The answer is \(\boxed{\textbf{(B) }15}\).

The two given lines are parallel; their distance apart is
\[
\dfrac{|6-1|}{\sqrt{4^2+3^2}}=1 .
\]
A line crossing them at angle \(\varphi\) cuts off a segment of length \(\dfrac{1}{\sin\varphi}\). This equals \(\sqrt2\) iff \(\varphi=45^\circ\).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real l1(real x){return (-1-4x)/3;} real l2(real x){return (-6-4x)/3;}
draw(graph(l1,-2.5,2.6),gray); draw(graph(l2,-3.2,1.7),gray);
real m1(real x){return 7x-5;} real m2(real x){return (15-x)/7;}
draw(graph(m1,-0.2,1.1),blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(graph(m2,-3.5,2.8),red+linewidth(0.9));
dot((1,2)); label("$(1,2)$",(1,2),NE);
draw((-3.6,0)--(3,0),Arrow(4)); draw((0,-5.5)--(0,3),Arrow(4));
label("$4x+3y+1=0$",(2.6,l1(2.6)),E,gray+fontsize(8pt)); label("$4x+3y+6=0$",(1.7,l2(1.7)),E,gray+fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The given lines have slope \(-\dfrac{4}{3}\). A line of slope \(m\) makes a \(45^\circ\) angle with them iff
\[
\left|\dfrac{m+\dfrac{4}{3}}{1-\dfrac{4}{3}m}\right|=1\iff |3m+4|=|3-4m| .
\]
So \(3m+4=3-4m\), giving \(m=-\dfrac{1}{7}\), or \(3m+4=4m-3\), giving \(m=7\). (A vertical line makes angle \(\arctan\dfrac{3}{4}\ne45^\circ\) with them, so it does not work.) Through \((1,2)\) the lines are
\[
\boxed{7x-y-5=0\quad\text{and}\quad x+7y-15=0}.
\]

Complete the squares: \(x^2-2x+5=(x-1)^2+2^2\) and \(x^2-4x+13=(x-2)^2+3^2\). So for the point \(X=(x,0)\) on the \(x\)-axis,
\[
g(x)=|XA-XB|,\qquad A=(1,2),\ B=(2,3).
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-2,0)--(3.5,0),Arrow(4)); draw((0,-0.5)--(0,4),Arrow(4));
pair A=(1,2), B=(2,3), P=(-1,0);
draw(P--(2.8,3.8),dashed+gray);
draw(P--A,blue); draw(P--B,red+linewidth(0.4));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(P);
label("$A(1,2)$",A,SE); label("$B(2,3)$",B,SE); label("$X(-1,0)$",P,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

By the triangle inequality, \(|XA-XB|\le AB=\sqrt{1^2+1^2}=\sqrt2\), with equality exactly when \(X\), \(A\), \(B\) are collinear with \(X\) not between \(A\) and \(B\). The line \(AB\) is \(y=x+1\), which meets the \(x\)-axis at \(X=(-1,0)\), outside segment \(AB\). Check: \(g(-1)=|\sqrt8-\sqrt{18}|=|2\sqrt2-3\sqrt2|=\sqrt2\).

The greatest value is \(\boxed{\sqrt2}\).

\emph{A fixed point.} The two lines meet where \(x-y=2\) and \(3x-y=-3\): subtracting, \(2x=-5\), so \(X=\left(-\dfrac{5}{2},-\dfrac{9}{2}\right)\). This point lies on the mirror, so it is its own image and lies on the image line.

\emph{Image of a second point.} Reflect \(Q=(2,0)\), which lies on \(x-y-2=0\). With \(\mathbf n=(3,-1)\) normal to the mirror, the reflection of \(Q\) is
\[
Q'=Q-2\cdot\dfrac{3\cdot2-0+3}{3^2+1^2}(3,-1)=(2,0)-\dfrac{9}{5}(3,-1)=\left(-\dfrac{17}{5},\dfrac{9}{5}\right).
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real l(real x){return x-2;} real m(real x){return 3x+3;} real im(real x){return -7x-22;}
draw(graph(l,-4.2,3),gray+linewidth(0.9)); draw(graph(m,-2.6,-0.2),deepgreen+linewidth(0.9)); draw(graph(im,-3.9,-2.3),red+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-4.3,0)--(3.2,0),Arrow(4)); draw((0,-6.8)--(0,4),Arrow(4));
pair X=(-2.5,-4.5), Q=(2,0), Qp=(-3.4,1.8);
dot(X); dot(Q); dot(Qp); draw(Q--Qp,dashed);
label("$(-\dfrac{5}{2},-\dfrac{9}{2})$",X,SE,fontsize(7pt)); label("$(2,0)$",Q,SE,fontsize(7pt)); label("$(-\dfrac{17}{5},\dfrac{9}{5})$",Qp,W,fontsize(7pt));
label("$x-y-2=0$",(3,1),E,gray+fontsize(7pt)); label("$3x-y+3=0$",(-0.2,2.4),N,deepgreen+fontsize(7pt)); label("$7x+y+22=0$",(-3.9,5.3),N,red+fontsize(7pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The image line passes through \(X\) and \(Q'\), with slope
\[
\dfrac{\dfrac{9}{5}+\dfrac{9}{2}}{-\dfrac{17}{5}+\dfrac{5}{2}}=\dfrac{\dfrac{63}{10}}{-\dfrac{9}{10}}=-7,
\]
so it is \(y+\dfrac{9}{2}=-7\left(x+\dfrac{5}{2}\right)\), i.e.
\[
\boxed{7x+y+22=0}.
\]

Let \(\theta=\arcsin\dfrac{3\sqrt{111}}{74}\), an acute angle. Then
\[
\sin^2\theta=\dfrac{9\cdot111}{74^2}=\dfrac{999}{5476},\qquad \cos^2\theta=\dfrac{5476-999}{5476}=\dfrac{4477}{5476}=\dfrac{121\cdot37}{74^2},
\]
so \(\cos\theta=\dfrac{11\sqrt{37}}{74}\) (a right triangle with hypotenuse \(74\), legs \(3\sqrt{111}\) and \(11\sqrt{37}\)).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(11*sqrt(37)/74*4,0), C=(11*sqrt(37)/74*4,3*sqrt(111)/74*4);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(B+(-0.2,0)--B+(-0.2,0.2)--B+(0,0.2));
draw(arc(A,0.6,0,degrees(atan2(C.y,C.x)))); label("$\theta$",A+0.85*dir(20));
label("$74$",(A+C)/2,NW); label("$3\sqrt{111}$",(B+C)/2,E); label("$11\sqrt{37}$",(A+B)/2,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

By the angle addition formula, using \(\sqrt{111}=\sqrt3\sqrt{37}\),
\[
\sin(30^\circ+\theta)=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\dfrac{11\sqrt{37}}{74}+\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\cdot\dfrac{3\sqrt3\sqrt{37}}{74}=\dfrac{11\sqrt{37}+9\sqrt{37}}{148}=\dfrac{20\sqrt{37}}{148}=\dfrac{5\sqrt{37}}{37}.
\]
Therefore
\[
CD=\dfrac{\sqrt{37}}{\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}}\cdot\dfrac{5\sqrt{37}}{37}=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3}\cdot5=\boxed{\dfrac{10\sqrt3}{3}}.
\]

The base angles are \(\angle CAB=\angle CBA=50^\circ\). Hence
\[
\angle MAC=40^\circ,\qquad \angle MBC=20^\circ .
\]
Let \(x=\angle ACM\); then \(\angle BCM=80^\circ-x\), with \(0<x<80^\circ\).

