By the trigonometric form of Ceva's theorem for the concurrent cevians \(AM,BM,CM\),
\[
\dfrac{\sin\angle MAB}{\sin\angle MAC}\cdot\dfrac{\sin\angle MCA}{\sin\angle MCB}\cdot\dfrac{\sin\angle MBC}{\sin\angle MBA}=1,
\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad
\dfrac{\sin10^\circ}{\sin40^\circ}\cdot\dfrac{\sin x}{\sin(80^\circ-x)}\cdot\dfrac{\sin20^\circ}{\sin30^\circ}=1 .
\]
\emph{\(x=70^\circ\) satisfies this:} the left side becomes
\[
\dfrac{\sin10^\circ\sin70^\circ\sin20^\circ}{\sin40^\circ\sin10^\circ\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}}=\dfrac{2\sin20^\circ\cos20^\circ}{\sin40^\circ}=1 .
\]
\emph{It is the only solution:} \(\dfrac{\sin x}{\sin(80^\circ-x)}\) is strictly increasing on \((0^\circ,80^\circ)\) (the numerator increases and the denominator decreases, both positive), so the equation has exactly one root.

Thus \(\angle ACM=70^\circ\), and in triangle \(AMC\),
\[
\angle AMC=180^\circ-\angle MAC-\angle ACM=180^\circ-40^\circ-70^\circ=\boxed{70^\circ}.
\]
(So triangle \(AMC\) is isosceles with \(AM=AC\).)

The total is \(2+3+\cdots+9=44\). If \(A\) has sum \(s\), then \(B\) has sum \(3s\) and \(4s=44\), so \(s=11\). The division is determined by \(A\), so we count subsets of \(\{2,\dots,9\}\) with sum \(11\):
\begin{itemize}
\item two elements: \(\{2,9\},\{3,8\},\{4,7\},\{5,6\}\);
\item three elements: the smallest possible sum is \(2+3+4=9\); sum \(11\) gives \(\{2,3,6\},\{2,4,5\}\);
\item four or more elements: the sum is at least \(2+3+4+5=14>11\).
\end{itemize}
So there are \(\boxed{6}\) ways.

question:
Let $a,b,c,d$ be integers with $b,d>0$ and $bc-ad=1$ (so $\dfrac{a}{b}<\dfrac{c}{d}$).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that every fraction $\dfrac{p}{q}$ with $q>0$ and $\dfrac{a}{b}<\dfrac{p}{q}<\dfrac{c}{d}$ has $q\ge b+d$, and that the only such fraction with $q=b+d$ is the mediant $\dfrac{a+c}{b+d}$.
\item[(b)] Find the fraction with the least positive denominator that lies strictly between $\dfrac{7}{11}$ and $\dfrac{9}{14}$.
