The volume condition is $\pi r^2h=1000\pi$, so $h=\dfrac{1000}{r^2}$. The material is the base plus the side:
\[
A=\pi r^2+2\pi rh=\pi r^2+\dfrac{2000\pi}{r}=\pi\left(r^2+\dfrac{1000}{r}+\dfrac{1000}{r}\right).
\]
By AM-GM on the three positive terms,
\[
r^2+\dfrac{1000}{r}+\dfrac{1000}{r}\ge 3\sqrt[3]{r^2\cdot\dfrac{1000}{r}\cdot\dfrac{1000}{r}}=3\sqrt[3]{10^6}=300,
\]
with equality when $r^2=\dfrac{1000}{r}$, that is $r=10$ (and then $h=10$). Hence the minimum amount of material is
\[\boxed{300\pi}\ \text{cm}^2.\]

question:
Let $A=(2,3)$ and $B=(9,4)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the point $P$ on segment $AB$ that is $\dfrac{2}{5}$ of the way from $A$ to $B$.
\item[(b)] More generally, show that for $0\le t\le1$ the point $t$ of the way from $A(x_1,y_1)$ to $B(x_2,y_2)$ is $\bigl((1-t)x_1+tx_2,\ (1-t)y_1+ty_2\bigr)$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(b) Going from $A$ to $B$ changes $x$ by $x_2-x_1$ and $y$ by $y_2-y_1$. Moving along the segment, the changes in $x$ and $y$ grow in proportion to the distance travelled (similar right triangles with legs parallel to the axes), so the point $t$ of the way along is
\[
\bigl(x_1+t(x_2-x_1),\ y_1+t(y_2-y_1)\bigr)=\bigl((1-t)x_1+tx_2,\ (1-t)y_1+ty_2\bigr).
\]
Its distance from $A$ is $t\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2}=t\cdot AB$, as required. For $t=\dfrac{1}{2}$ this is the midpoint formula.

(a) Here the run is $9-2=7$ and the rise is $4-3=1$.
