Let $\beta=\angle ABC$. Since $CA<CB$, we have $\beta<45^\circ$, so $\angle CAB=90^\circ-\beta>\beta$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), A=(0,3), B=(6,0);
pair D=(27/12,0);
pair F=A+dot(C-A,unit(B-A))*unit(B-A);
pair Ept=2F-A;
pair X=extension(A,D,C,Ept);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,blue); draw(C--Ept,red);
draw(C+(0.3,0)--C+(0.3,0.3)--C+(0,0.3));
pair u=unit(A-X), v=unit(C-X);
draw(X+0.25u--X+0.25u+0.25v--X+0.25v);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(Ept);dot(X);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$D$",D,S); label("$E$",Ept,NE); label("$X$",X,E);
label("$\beta$",B+(-0.75,0.13)); label("$\beta$",A+0.75*unit(unit(D-A)+unit(B-A)));
label("$2\beta$",C+0.75*dir(66),red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{The points exist on the sides.} $D$ is where the perpendicular bisector of $AB$ meets line $BC$; since $\angle DAB=\angle DBA=\beta<\angle CAB$, the ray $AD$ lies inside angle $CAB$, so $D$ is on segment $BC$. For $E$: $\angle CEA=\angle CAE=90^\circ-\beta$, so $\angle ACE=180^\circ-2(90^\circ-\beta)=2\beta<90^\circ=\angle ACB$; hence ray $CE$ lies inside angle $ACB$ and $E$ is on segment $AB$.

\textbf{Angles.} Because $DA=DB$, triangle $DAB$ is isosceles and $\angle DAB=\beta$, so
\[\angle CAD=\angle CAB-\angle DAB=(90^\circ-\beta)-\beta=90^\circ-2\beta.\]
Because $CA=CE$, as computed above $\angle ACE=2\beta$.

Let $X$ be the intersection of $AD$ and $CE$ (both segments lie inside the triangle, and $X$ lies inside angle $ACB$). In triangle $AXC$,
\[
\angle AXC=180^\circ-\angle XAC-\angle XCA=180^\circ-(90^\circ-2\beta)-2\beta=90^\circ.
\]
Hence $AD\perp CE$. $\blacksquare$

question:
There are $10$ people in a room. Each person shakes hands with as many other people as possible, subject to these rules:
\begin{enumerate}
\item no one shakes hands with someone who is taller than they are;
\item no two people shake hands with each other more than once.
\end{enumerate}
The total number of handshakes depends on the people's heights. What is the smallest positive integer that cannot be the total number of handshakes?

Square $ABCD$ has side length $3$. Circles of radius $2$ are drawn with centres at each of the four vertices. The shaded region is the part of the square that lies outside all four circles, as shown. Find the radius of the largest circle that can be drawn inside the shaded region.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(3,0), C=(3,3), D=(0,3);
path sq=A--B--C--D--cycle;
pair O=(1.5,1.5);
real t=acos(1.5/2)*180/pi;
path g=arc(A,2,t,90-t)--arc(D,2,t-90,-t)--arc(C,2,180+t,270-t)--arc(B,2,90+t,180-t)--cycle;
fill(g,lightgray);
draw(sq,linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(A,2,0,90)); draw(arc(B,2,90,180)); draw(arc(C,2,180,270)); draw(arc(D,2,270,360));
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,NW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
Let $O$ be the centre of the square; $OA=OB=OC=OD=\dfrac{3\sqrt2}{2}\approx2.12>2$, so $O$ is in the shaded region.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(3,0), C=(3,3), D=(0,3);
pair O=(1.5,1.5);
real t=acos(1.5/2)*180/pi;
path g=arc(A,2,t,90-t)--arc(D,2,t-90,-t)--arc(C,2,180+t,270-t)--arc(B,2,90+t,180-t)--cycle;
fill(g,lightgray);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(A,2,0,90)); draw(arc(B,2,90,180)); draw(arc(C,2,180,270)); draw(arc(D,2,270,360));
real r=1.5*sqrt(2)-2;
draw(circle(O,r),red);
draw(A--C,dashed+gray);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$2$",(0.62,0.82),NW);
draw((2.1,0.9)--O+(0.08,-0.08),red,Arrow(4)); label("$O$",(2.1,0.9),SE,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Upper bound.} Suppose a circle with centre $P$ and radius $r$ lies in the shaded region. Then $P$ is in the square, and the circle stays outside each radius-$2$ circle, so $PV\ge2+r$ for every vertex $V$. The four small squares of side $\dfrac{3}{2}$ obtained by cutting $ABCD$ through $O$ cover the square; $P$ lies in one of them, whose corner is a vertex $V$ of $ABCD$, so $PV\le$ the diagonal of that small square $=\dfrac{3\sqrt2}{2}$. Hence
\[2+r\le\dfrac{3\sqrt2}{2},\qquad r\le\dfrac{3\sqrt2}{2}-2.\]

\textbf{Attained.} The circle centred at $O$ with radius $\dfrac{3\sqrt2}{2}-2\approx0.12$ is at distance exactly $2+r$ from each vertex, so it touches each of the four circles from outside, and it is far from the sides of the square. So the largest radius is
\[\boxed{\dfrac{3\sqrt2-4}{2}}.\]

The six points are the vertices of a regular hexagon inscribed in the unit circle. Their midpoints with the centre $O$ lie at distance $\dfrac{1}{2}$ from $O$, equally spaced at $60^\circ$, so they form a regular hexagon with circumradius $\dfrac{1}{2}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
draw(unitcircle,gray);
pair[] P, M;
for(int i=0;i<6;++i){ P[i]=dir(60*i); M[i]=P[i]/2; draw(O--P[i],gray+dashed); dot(P[i]); }
draw(M[0]--M[1]--M[2]--M[3]--M[4]--M[5]--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
fill(O--M[0]--M[1]--cycle,paleblue);
draw(M[0]--M[1]--M[2]--M[3]--M[4]--M[5]--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(O); label("$O$",O,SW);
label("$1$",P[2]*0.75,W);
label("$\dfrac{1}{2}$",(M[0]+M[1])/2,NE,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

That hexagon splits into $6$ equilateral triangles with side $\dfrac{1}{2}$ (each has two sides of length $\dfrac{1}{2}$ through $O$ with a $60^\circ$ angle between them). Each has area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot\dfrac{1}{4}=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{16}$, so the hexagon's area is
\[6\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{16}=\boxed{\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{8}}.\]

question:
