Four rectangular paper strips, each of length $10$ and width $1$, are laid flat on a table: two run horizontally and two run vertically, and each horizontal strip crosses each vertical strip at right angles, as shown. How much area of the table is covered?
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real L=10;
for(real y: new real[]{2,6}) filldraw(box((0,y),(L,y+1)),palegray,black);
for(real x: new real[]{2,6}) filldraw(box((x,0),(x+1,L)),palegray,black);
for(real y: new real[]{2,6}) draw(box((0,y),(L,y+1)));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
The four strips have total area $4\cdot10\cdot1=40$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real L=10;
for(real y: new real[]{2,6}) filldraw(box((0,y),(L,y+1)),palegray,black);
for(real x: new real[]{2,6}) filldraw(box((x,0),(x+1,L)),palegray,black);
for(real y: new real[]{2,6}) draw(box((0,y),(L,y+1)));
for(real x: new real[]{2,6}) for(real y: new real[]{2,6}) fill(box((x,y),(x+1,y+1)),red);
draw((0,-0.6)--(10,-0.6),Arrows(4)); label("$10$",(5,-0.6),S);
draw((10.6,2)--(10.6,3),Arrows(3)); label("$1$",(10.6,2.5),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Parallel strips do not overlap. Each of the $2\cdot2=4$ crossings of a horizontal and a vertical strip is a $1\times1$ square (shaded), and each such square is counted twice in the total $40$. No point lies on three strips. Hence the covered area is
\[40-4\cdot1=\boxed{36}.\]

\textbf{Mass points.} Place masses at the vertices so that $D$ and $E$ are balance points.
\begin{itemize}
\item On $BC$: $m_B\cdot BD=m_C\cdot DC$ gives $3m_B=2m_C$; take $m_B=2$, $m_C=3$, so $m_D=m_B+m_C=5$.
\item On $CA$: $m_A\cdot AE=m_C\cdot EC$ gives $m_A=2m_C=6$, so $m_E=m_A+m_C=9$.
\end{itemize}
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(5,0), A=(1.4,3.6);
pair D=B+3/5*(C-B), Ept=A+1/3*(C-A);
pair P=extension(A,D,B,Ept);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,blue); draw(B--Ept,red);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(Ept);dot(P);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,S); label("$E$",Ept,NE); label("$P$",P,(1,-0.2));
label("$6$",A,W,deepgreen); label("$2$",B,(-1,1.2),deepgreen); label("$3$",C,(1,1.2),deepgreen);
label("$5$",D,(-1.8,-1.6),deepgreen); label("$9$",Ept,(2,-0.8),deepgreen);
label("$3$",(B+D)/2,S); label("$2$",(D+C)/2,S);
label("$1$",(A+Ept)/2,NE); label("$2$",(Ept+C)/2,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The centre of mass of the whole system lies on $AD$ (combine $B$ and $C$ at $D$) and on $BE$ (combine $A$ and $C$ at $E$), so it is $P$. Balancing on each segment,
\[
AP:PD=m_D:m_A=5:6,\qquad BP:PE=m_E:m_B=9:2.
\]

\textbf{Check with vectors.} With position vectors, $D=\dfrac{2B+3C}{5}$ and $E=\dfrac{2A+C}{3}$. The point $\dfrac{6A+2B+3C}{11}$ equals $\dfrac{6A+5D}{11}$ (on $AD$, with $AP:PD=5:6$) and also $\dfrac{2B+9E}{11}$ (on $BE$, with $BP:PE=9:2$). So
\[\boxed{AP:PD=5:6,\quad BP:PE=9:2}.\]

Since $AC\perp CD$ and $AC\perp CB$, the points $D$, $C$, $B$ are collinear, with $C$ between $D$ and $B$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), A=(0,1.57), B=(1.57,0), D=(-1.57*sqrt(3),0);
draw(A--B--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,linewidth(0.9));
draw(C+(0.12,0)--C+(0.12,0.12)--C+(0,0.12));
draw(C+(-0.12,0)--C+(-0.12,0.12)--C+(0,0.12));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,S); label("$D$",D,SW);
label("$314$",(A+D)/2,NW); label("$157$",(A+C)/2,E);
label("$30^\circ$",D+(0.55,0.1)); label("$45^\circ$",A+0.5*dir(-62),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Triangle $ADC$ is a $30^\circ$-$60^\circ$-$90^\circ$ triangle with hypotenuse $AD=314$, so the leg opposite the $30^\circ$ angle is
\[AC=\dfrac{314}{2}=157.\]
Triangle $ABC$ has a right angle at $C$ and $\angle BAC=45^\circ$, so it is an isosceles right triangle: $BC=AC=157$ and $AB=157\sqrt2$. Its perimeter is
\[157+157+157\sqrt2=\boxed{314+157\sqrt2}.\]

With all bricks in the same orientation, each edge of the cube is made of whole brick-lengths in one direction, so the cube's edge length must be a multiple of $18$, of $15$, and of $8$, hence a multiple of
\[\operatorname{lcm}(18,15,8)=\operatorname{lcm}(2\cdot3^2,\ 3\cdot5,\ 2^3)=2^3\cdot3^2\cdot5=360\ \text{cm}.\]
A $360$ cm cube is achieved with $\dfrac{360}{18}=20$ bricks along one edge, $\dfrac{360}{15}=24$ along another, and $\dfrac{360}{8}=45$ along the third.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair o=(0.45,0.3);
real a=3.6, h=3.6;
pair p(real x,real y,real z){return (x,y)+z*o;}
path front=p(0,0,0)--p(a,0,0)--p(a,h,0)--p(0,h,0)--cycle;
draw(front,linewidth(0.9));
draw(p(0,h,0)--p(0,h,a)--p(a,h,a)--p(a,h,0));
draw(p(a,0,0)--p(a,0,a)--p(a,h,a));
for(int i=1;i<20;++i) draw(p(a*i/20,0,0)--p(a*i/20,h,0),gray+0.3);
for(int j=1;j<24;++j) draw(p(0,h*j/24,0)--p(a,h*j/24,0),gray+0.3);
fill(p(0,0,0)--p(a/20,0,0)--p(a/20,h/24,0)--p(0,h/24,0)--cycle,red);
label("$360$",p(a/2,0,0),S); label("$360$",p(0,h/2,0),W); label("$360$",p(a,0,a/2),SE);
label("one brick: $18\times15$ face",(a/2,-0.75),S,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The least number of bricks is therefore
\[20\cdot24\cdot45=\dfrac{360^3}{18\cdot15\cdot8}=\boxed{21600}.\]

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that for integers $a$ and $m\ge2$, there is an integer $x$ with $ax\equiv1\pmod m$ (a modular inverse of $a$) if and only if $\gcd(a,m)=1$.
\item[(b)] Use the Euclidean algorithm to find the integer $x$ with $0\le x<43$ and $17x\equiv1\pmod{43}$.
\end{enumerate}

A student claims: ``For a circle $x^2+y^2=r^2$ centred at the origin (with $r>0$), every point $(x,y)$ on the circle satisfies $x+y=r$.''
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the claim is false.
\item[(b)] Find all points of the circle that do satisfy $x+y=r$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) The point $\left(\dfrac{r}{\sqrt2},\dfrac{r}{\sqrt2}\right)$ lies on the circle, since $\dfrac{r^2}{2}+\dfrac{r^2}{2}=r^2$, but $x+y=r\sqrt2\ne r$. (Also $(-r,0)$ gives $x+y=-r$.) So the claim is false.

(b) If $x^2+y^2=r^2$ and $x+y=r$, then
\[r^2=(x+y)^2=x^2+y^2+2xy=r^2+2xy,\]
so $xy=0$. If $x=0$ then $y=r$; if $y=0$ then $x=r$. Both points work. So the line $x+y=r$ meets the circle in exactly two points,
\[\boxed{(r,0)\ \text{and}\ (0,r)}.\]
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=2;
draw((-2.8,0)--(2.8,0),gray,Arrow(5)); draw((0,-2.8)--(0,2.8),gray,Arrow(5));
draw(circle((0,0),r),linewidth(0.9));
draw((-0.7,2.7)--(2.7,-0.7),blue);
dot((r,0),red); dot((0,r),red);
pair Q=r*dir(45); dot(Q);
label("$(r,0)$",(r,0),SW,red); label("$(0,r)$",(0,r),SW,red);
label("$\left(\dfrac{r}{\sqrt2},\dfrac{r}{\sqrt2}\right)$",Q,NE);
label("$x+y=r$",(2.7,-0.7),SE,blue);
label("$x^2+y^2=r^2$",r*dir(215),SW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Place $A=(0,0)$, $B=(l,0)$, $C=(l,w)$, $D=(0,w)$; by symmetry we may take $l>w$. Each bisector makes $45^\circ$ with the sides:
\begin{itemize}
\item from $A$: $y=x$; from $B$: $y=l-x$; from $C$: $y-w=x-l$; from $D$: $y-w=-x$.
\end{itemize}
Intersecting,
\[
P=\left(\dfrac {l}{2},\dfrac {l}{2}\right),\quad Q=\left(l-\dfrac {w}{2},\dfrac {w}{2}\right),\quad R=\left(\dfrac {l}{2},w-\dfrac {l}{2}\right),\quad S=\left(\dfrac {w}{2},\dfrac {w}{2}\right).
\]
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real l=6, w=4;
pair A=(0,0), B=(l,0), C=(l,w), D=(0,w);
pair P=(l/2,l/2), Q=(l-w/2,w/2), R=(l/2,w-l/2), T=(w/2,w/2);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--P,gray); draw(B--P,gray); draw(C--R,gray); draw(D--R,gray);
draw(P--Q--R--T--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--R,dashed+red); draw(T--Q,dashed+red);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(P);dot(Q);dot(R);dot(T);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$P$",P,N); label("$Q$",Q,E); label("$R$",R,S); label("$S$",T,W);
label("$l$",(l/2,0),S); label("$w$",(0,w/2),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The diagonal $PR$ is vertical with length $\dfrac {l}{2}-\left(w-\dfrac {l}{2}\right)=l-w$, and the diagonal $SQ$ is horizontal with length $\left(l-\dfrac {w}{2}\right)-\dfrac {w}{2}=l-w$. They have the common midpoint $\left(\dfrac {l}{2},\dfrac {w}{2}\right)$, so $PQRS$ is a square with diagonal $l-w$ and side
\[PQ=\dfrac{l-w}{\sqrt2}.\]
From $PQ=2019$,
\[|l-w|=\boxed{2019\sqrt2}.\]

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] (Rational root theorem.) Let $f(x)=a_nx^n+\dots+a_1x+a_0$ have integer coefficients with $a_n\ne0$ and $a_0\ne0$. Prove that if $\dfrac {p}{q}$ (in lowest terms, $q>0$) is a root of $f$, then $p\mid a_0$ and $q\mid a_n$.
\item[(b)] Factor $2x^3-3x^2-11x+6$ completely into linear factors with integer coefficients.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{The shape of the cut.} The removed pyramid has exactly one face on the cutting plane; its other faces lie in faces of the icosahedron. A face of the piece lying in a triangular face of the icosahedron is a triangle or a quadrilateral, so the pentagonal base must be the cut face, and the five lateral triangles lie in five icosahedron faces meeting at the apex. Five faces meet only at a vertex $V$ of the icosahedron, so the apex is a vertex $V$, and the plane meets the five edges $VN_1,\dots,VN_5$ at points $P_1,\dots,P_5$, where each $P_i$ is either an interior point of $VN_i$ or the neighbour $N_i$ itself.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair V=(0,2.2);
pair[] Nb;
for(int i=0;i<5;++i) Nb[i]=(1.7*cos(2pi*i/5+0.5),0.55*sin(2pi*i/5+0.5));
real t=0.45;
pair[] Pp;
for(int i=0;i<5;++i) Pp[i]=V+t*(Nb[i]-V);
for(int i=0;i<5;++i){
  pen pe=(Nb[i].y>0.2)?dashed+gray:black;
  draw(V--Nb[i],pe);
  draw(Nb[i]--Nb[(i+1)%5],((Nb[i].y+Nb[(i+1)%5].y)/2>0.25)?dashed+gray:black);
}
fill(Pp[0]--Pp[1]--Pp[2]--Pp[3]--Pp[4]--cycle,pink+opacity(0.6));
draw(Pp[0]--Pp[1]--Pp[2]--Pp[3]--Pp[4]--cycle,red+linewidth(0.8));
for(int i=0;i<5;++i){ dot(Nb[i]); dot(Pp[i],red); }
dot(V); label("$V$",V,N);
label("$P_1$",Pp[3],W,red); label("$N_1$",Nb[3],SW);
label("cutting plane",Pp[0],E,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Counting.} By Euler's formula for the remaining (convex) solid, $A-B+C=2$, so $A+B+C=2B+2$, and we must maximise $B$. The edges of the remaining solid are
\begin{itemize}
\item the $25$ edges of the icosahedron not through $V$;
\item the pieces $P_iN_i$ of the five edges through $V$, present only when $P_i\ne N_i$;
\item the five sides $P_iP_{i+1}$ of the cut pentagon, except that a side coincides with an already counted edge $N_iN_{i+1}$ when $P_i=N_i$ and $P_{i+1}=N_{i+1}$.
\end{itemize}
Hence $B\le25+5+5=35$, with equality exactly when every $P_i$ is an interior point of its edge. This happens, for example, when the plane is parallel to the plane of $N_1,\dots,N_5$ and close to $V$. Then $A=12-1+5=16$, $B=35$, $C=20+1=21$ (the five faces at $V$ become quadrilaterals), and indeed $16-35+21=2$.

So the maximum is
\[16+35+21=\boxed{72}.\]
(For comparison, cutting exactly through the five neighbours gives $11+25+16=52$.)

The headings of the five moves are $30^\circ,60^\circ,90^\circ,120^\circ,150^\circ$ with lengths $2,4,6,8,10$, so the turning points are
\[
\begin{aligned}
P_1&=(\sqrt3,\,1)\approx(1.73,\,1),\\
P_2&=P_1+(2,\,2\sqrt3)\approx(3.73,\,4.46),\\
P_3&=P_2+(0,\,6)\approx(3.73,\,10.46),\\
P_4&=P_3+(-4,\,4\sqrt3)\approx(-0.27,\,17.39),\\
P_5&=P_4+(-5\sqrt3,\,5)\approx(-8.93,\,22.39).
\end{aligned}
\]
All coordinates after the start are irrational except $y(P_1)=1$, so no segment passes through a lattice point other than the start $O$. A segment that crosses grid lines at distinct points enters a new square at each crossing, so it meets $1+(\text{number of grid-line crossings})$ squares.
\begin{itemize}
\item $OP_1$: crosses $x=1$; it ends on the line $y=1$. Squares: $2$.
\item $P_1P_2$: crosses $x=2,3$ and $y=2,3,4$. Squares: $1+5=6$.
\item $P_2P_3$: crosses $y=5,\dots,10$. Squares: $1+6=7$.
\item $P_3P_4$: crosses $x=3,2,1,0$ and $y=11,\dots,17$. Squares: $1+11=12$.
\item $P_4P_5$: crosses $x=-1,\dots,-8$ and $y=18,\dots,22$. Squares: $1+13=14$.
\end{itemize}
This gives $2+6+7+12+14=41$, but the squares containing $P_2$, $P_3$, $P_4$ are each counted by two consecutive segments ($P_1$ lies on a grid line, so the first two segments share no square). Non-consecutive segments are far apart and share no squares (see the figure).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int[][] Q={{-9,21},{-9,22},{-8,21},{-7,20},{-7,21},{-6,20},{-5,19},{-5,20},{-4,18},{-4,19},{-3,18},{-2,17},{-2,18},{-1,16},{-1,17},{0,0},{0,15},{0,16},{1,0},{1,1},{1,13},{1,14},{1,15},{2,1},{2,2},{2,3},{2,11},{2,12},{2,13},{3,3},{3,4},{3,5},{3,6},{3,7},{3,8},{3,9},{3,10},{3,11}};
for(int i=0;i<Q.length;++i) fill(box((Q[i][0],Q[i][1]),(Q[i][0]+1,Q[i][1]+1)),paleyellow);
for(int x=-9;x<=5;++x) draw((x,0)--(x,23),gray+0.3);
for(int y=0;y<=23;++y) draw((-9,y)--(5,y),gray+0.3);
pair[] P={(0,0)};
real h=0;
for(int k=1;k<=5;++k){ h+=30; P.push(P[k-1]+2k*dir(h)); }
draw(P[0]--P[1]--P[2]--P[3]--P[4]--P[5],red+linewidth(1));
for(int k=0;k<=5;++k) dot(P[k],red);
label("$O$",P[0],SW); label("$P_1$",P[1],SE); label("$P_2$",P[2],E); label("$P_3$",P[3],E); label("$P_4$",P[4],NE); label("$P_5$",P[5],NW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Hence the path passes through
\[41-3=\boxed{38}\]
unit squares.

During second $k$ ($k=1,\dots,40$) the bug first walks $1$ mm outward, reaching distance $k$ mm from the centre; then the tick carries it along an arc of radius $k$ and angle $6^\circ=\dfrac{\pi}{30}$, of length $\dfrac{k\pi}{30}$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
real th=6*pi/180;
pair p=O;
for(int k=1;k<=5;++k){
  pair q=k*dir(90-6*(k-1));
  draw(p--q,blue+linewidth(0.9));
  pair q2=k*dir(90-6*k);
  draw(arc(O,k,90-6*(k-1),90-6*k),red+linewidth(0.9));
  p=q2;
}
for(int k=0;k<=5;++k) draw(O--5.6*dir(90-6*k),gray+0.3);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,SW);
label("walk $1$",(0.5*dir(90))+(-0.05,0),W,blue);
label("tick: arc $\dfrac{k\pi}{30}$",4.6*dir(70),NE,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The total distance is
\[
40+\dfrac{\pi}{30}(1+2+\dots+40)=40+\dfrac{820\pi}{30}=40+\dfrac{82\pi}{3}=\dfrac{120+82\pi}{3}.
\]
Here $\gcd(120,82,3)=1$, so $a=120$, $b=82$, $c=3$ and
\[a+b+c=\boxed{205}.\]

Place $C=(0,0)$, $A=(0,1)$, $B=(2,0)$, so $M=\left(1,\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$. Since $CD\perp BD$ with $D$ on line $CM$, $D$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $B$ to line $CM$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), A=(0,1), B=(2,0);
pair M=(A+B)/2;
pair D=dot(B,unit(M))*unit(M);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(C--D,blue); draw(B--D,red);
draw(C+(0.08,0)--C+(0.08,0.08)--C+(0,0.08));
pair u=unit(C-D), v=unit(B-D);
draw(D+0.08u--D+0.08u+0.08v--D+0.08v);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(M);dot(D);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,SW); label("$M$",M,NW); label("$D$",D,NE);
label("$1$",(A+C)/2,W); label("$2$",(B+C)/2,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The unit vector along $CM$ is $\dfrac{(2,1)}{\sqrt5}$, so
\[
CD=B\cdot\dfrac{(2,1)}{\sqrt5}=\dfrac{4}{\sqrt5},\qquad D=\dfrac{4}{5}(2,1)=\left(\dfrac{8}{5},\dfrac{4}{5}\right).
\]
Since $CM=\dfrac{AB}{2}=\dfrac{\sqrt5}{2}<\dfrac{4}{\sqrt5}$, $D$ lies beyond $M$, and
\[
MD=\dfrac{4}{\sqrt5}-\dfrac{\sqrt5}{2}=\dfrac{8-5}{2\sqrt5}=\dfrac{3\sqrt5}{10}.
\]
So $a=3$, $b=5$, $c=10$, and
\[a+b+c=\boxed{18}.\]

\textbf{Lower bound.} List the $64$ points in the order in which the path first reaches them. Any two distinct grid points are at least $1$ cm apart, so the part of the path between consecutive points in this list has length at least $1$. There are $63$ such parts and they do not overlap, so the path has length at least $63$ cm.

\textbf{Construction.} A ``snake'' path that runs along the first row, steps up $1$, runs back along the second row, and so on, visits all $64$ points using $63$ unit steps.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<8;++i){
  if(i%2==0) draw((0,i)--(7,i),red+linewidth(0.9)); else draw((7,i)--(0,i),red+linewidth(0.9));
  if(i<7){ if(i%2==0) draw((7,i)--(7,i+1),red+linewidth(0.9)); else draw((0,i)--(0,i+1),red+linewidth(0.9)); }
}
for(int i=0;i<8;++i) for(int j=0;j<8;++j) dot((i,j));
label("start",(0,0),SW); label("end",(0,7),NW);
label("$1$",(0.5,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Hence the shortest length is
\[\boxed{63}\ \text{cm}.\]

Circle $A$ has radius $6$. Let the tangent line meet circle $A$ at $P$ and $Q$, touch circle $B$ at $T$, and let $\theta=\angle POQ$ with $0<\theta\le\pi$.
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
