The fencing gives $x+2y=228$, so the area is
\[
A=xy=(228-2y)y=-2(y^2-114y)=-2(y-57)^2+2\cdot57^2.
\]
This is largest when $y=57$ (and then $x=114$), giving
\[
A=2\cdot 3249=6498\text{ ft}^2.
\]
The answer is \boxed{\text{(b) } 6498\text{ ft}^2}.

question:
A student claims: ``The maximum number of regions into which a collection of lines (or of circles) can divide the plane is always $1$ more than the maximum number of intersection points.''
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] For $n$ lines, prove that the maximum number of intersection points is $\dbinom n2$ and the maximum number of regions is $1+n+\dbinom n2$. Find both for $n=4$.
\item[(b)] For $n\ge1$ circles, prove that the maximum number of intersection points is $n(n-1)$ and the maximum number of regions is $n^2-n+2$. Find both for $n=4$.
\item[(c)] Is the student's claim correct?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
A circle of radius $\rho$ has an inscribed square of diagonal $2\rho$, hence of side $\sqrt2\,\rho$ and area $2\rho^2$; the circle inscribed in that square has radius $\dfrac{\sqrt2\,\rho}{2}=\dfrac{\rho}{\sqrt2}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1;
for(int k=0;k<6;++k){
  real rr=r*(1/sqrt(2))^k;
  fill(circle((0,0),rr), gray(0.72));
  draw(circle((0,0),rr), linewidth(0.6));
  path sq=(rr,0)--(0,rr)--(-rr,0)--(0,-rr)--cycle;
  if(k%2==1) sq=(rr/sqrt(2),rr/sqrt(2))--(-rr/sqrt(2),rr/sqrt(2))--(-rr/sqrt(2),-rr/sqrt(2))--(rr/sqrt(2),-rr/sqrt(2))--cycle;
  fill(sq, white); draw(sq, linewidth(0.6));
}
draw((0,0)--dir(-30), dashed+linewidth(0.8)); label("$r$", 0.9*dir(-30), NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So the shaded set is a sequence of disjoint rings, each consisting of a disc minus its inscribed square. The first ring has area $\pi r^2-2r^2$. Each subsequent disc has radius $\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}$ times the previous one, so each ring has $\dfrac{1}{2}$ the area of the previous ring. The total shaded area is
\[
(\pi r^2-2r^2)\left(1+\dfrac{1}{2}+\dfrac{1}{4}+\cdots\right)=2(\pi-2)r^2=\boxed{2\pi r^2-4r^2}.
\]
The answer in the post is correct.

Since $AB=AC$, the median $AM$ is perpendicular to $BC$ and bisects $\angle BAC$; by symmetry $XM$ also bisects $\angle BXC$. Let $\theta=\angle BAM$ and $a=BM$. Then $\angle BAC=2\theta$, so $\angle BXC=6\theta$ and $\angle BXM=3\theta$. Also $XM=AM-AX=1$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=5.5;
pair A=(0,11), B=(-a,0), C=(a,0), M=(0,0), X=(0,1);
draw(A--B--C--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--M, linewidth(0.7));
draw(B--X--C, blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw((0,0.45)--(0.45,0.45)--(0.45,0), linewidth(0.5));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(M); dot(X);
label("$A$", A, N); label("$B$", B, SW); label("$C$", C, SE);
label("$M$", M, S); label("$X$", X, 1.3*N+0.6*E);
label("$10$", (0,6), E); label("$1$", (0,0.45), W, fontsize(8pt));
label("$\theta$", A+(-0.5,-2.3), fontsize(9pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

In the right triangles $ABM$ and $XBM$,
\[
\tan\theta=\dfrac{a}{11},\qquad \tan 3\theta=\dfrac{a}{1}=a,
\]
so $\tan3\theta=11\tan\theta$, with $0<3\theta<90^\circ$. Put $t=\tan\theta>0$. Using $\tan3\theta=\dfrac{3t-t^3}{1-3t^2}$,
\[
\dfrac{3t-t^3}{1-3t^2}=11t\ \Longrightarrow\ 3-t^2=11-33t^2\ \Longrightarrow\ t^2=\dfrac{1}{4},
\]
so $t=\dfrac{1}{2}$. (Then $1-3t^2=\dfrac{1}{4}>0$, consistent with $3\theta<90^\circ$.) Hence $a=11t=\dfrac{11}{2}$, $BC=11$, and
\[
AB=AC=\sqrt{11^2+\left(\dfrac{11}{2}\right)^2}=\dfrac{11\sqrt5}{2}.
\]
The perimeter is
\[
11+2\cdot\dfrac{11\sqrt5}{2}=\boxed{11+11\sqrt5}.
\]

Label the sides cyclically as $s_1,\dots,s_5$ (also used for their lengths), lying on lines $\ell_1,\dots,\ell_5$. In a convex polygon two adjacent sides are never parallel, and one side cannot be parallel to two others (those two would be parallel to each other, and three sides of a convex polygon cannot be parallel). So the two parallel pairs are disjoint pairs of non-adjacent sides. The non-adjacent pairs in a $5$-cycle are $\{1,3\},\{2,4\},\{3,5\},\{4,1\},\{5,2\}$, and every two disjoint ones have the form $\{i,i+2\},\{i+1,i+3\}$. After relabelling cyclically we may assume $\ell_1\parallel\ell_3$ and $\ell_2\parallel\ell_4$.

Let the vertices be $P_1=\ell_5\cap\ell_1$, $P_2=\ell_1\cap\ell_2$, $P_3=\ell_2\cap\ell_3$, $P_4=\ell_3\cap\ell_4$, $P_5=\ell_4\cap\ell_5$, and let $K=\ell_4\cap\ell_1$ (these lines are not parallel, since $\ell_4\parallel\ell_2$). Then $P_2P_3P_4K$ is a parallelogram.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair K=(0,0), P2=(4.2,0), P3=(5.4,3), P4=(1.2,3);
pair P1=(1.6,0), P5=0.42*P4;
fill(P1--P2--P3--P4--P5--cycle, paleblue);
draw(P1--P2--P3--P4--P5--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
draw(K--P1, dashed); draw(K--P5, dashed);
dot(K); dot(P1); dot(P2); dot(P3); dot(P4); dot(P5);
label("$K$", K, SW); label("$P_1$", P1, S); label("$P_2$", P2, SE);
label("$P_3$", P3, NE); label("$P_4$", P4, NW); label("$P_5$", P5, W);
label("$s_1$", (P1+P2)/2, S); label("$s_2$", (P2+P3)/2, E); label("$s_3$", (P3+P4)/2, N);
label("$s_4$", (P4+P5)/2, W); label("$s_5$", (P5+P1)/2, NE);
label("$\ell_1$", (5.2,0), E); draw((4.2,0)--(5.1,0), gray);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

A convex polygon lies in the closed half-plane of each side line that contains it, so the pentagon lies in the strip between $\ell_1,\ell_3$ and in the strip between $\ell_2,\ell_4$, that is, in the parallelogram. Hence $P_5$, which is on $\ell_4$, lies on segment $P_4K$, and $P_1$ lies on segment $KP_2$. Also $P_5\ne K$: otherwise $P_5$ would lie on $\ell_1$ and $\ell_5$, forcing $P_5=\ell_1\cap\ell_5=P_1$. Similarly $P_1\ne K$. So $KP_5P_1$ is a genuine triangle, and the strict triangle inequality gives
\[
s_5=P_5P_1<KP_5+KP_1.
\]
Opposite sides of the parallelogram are equal, so $KP_4=P_2P_3=s_2$ and $KP_2=P_3P_4=s_3$. Therefore $KP_5=s_2-s_4$ and $KP_1=s_3-s_1$, and
\[
s_1+s_4+s_5<s_2+s_3.
\]

For the side lengths $2,2,2,3,3$, any three sides have total length at least $2+2+2=6$, while any two sides have total length at most $3+3=6$. So $s_1+s_4+s_5\ge6\ge s_2+s_3$, contradicting the inequality above. Hence no convex pentagon with sides $2,2,2,3,3$ has two pairs of parallel sides. In particular the ``maximum area of such pentagons'' asked about in the original post does not exist.

Let every side have length $1$, and write $A,B,\dots,F$ also for the interior angles. The angles of a hexagon sum to $720^\circ$, so
\[
A+C+E=B+D+F=360^\circ.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair u=dir(0), v=dir(50), w=dir(115);
pair A=(0,0), B=A+u, C=B+v, D=C+w, Ep=D-u, F=Ep-v;
draw(A--B--C--D--Ep--F--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--D--F--cycle, blue+dashed);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(Ep); dot(F);
label("$A$", A, SW); label("$B$", B, SE); label("$C$", C, E);
label("$D$", D, NE); label("$E$", Ep, N); label("$F$", F, W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 1: the triangle $BDF$.} Triangle $FAB$ is isosceles with $AF=AB=1$ and apex angle $A$, so $FB=2\sin\dfrac {A}{2}$ and its base angles are $\angle ABF=\angle AFB=90^\circ-\dfrac {A}{2}$. Similarly
\[
BD=2\sin\dfrac {C}{2},\quad \angle CBD=\angle CDB=90^\circ-\dfrac {C}{2};\qquad
DF=2\sin\dfrac {E}{2},\quad \angle EDF=\angle EFD=90^\circ-\dfrac {E}{2}.
\]
Since $\dfrac {A}{2}+\dfrac {C}{2}+\dfrac {E}{2}=180^\circ$ and each is positive, there is a triangle $XYZ$ with angles $X=\dfrac {A}{2}$, $Y=\dfrac {C}{2}$, $Z=\dfrac {E}{2}$. By the law of sines its sides opposite $X,Y,Z$ are proportional to $\sin\dfrac {A}{2},\sin\dfrac {C}{2},\sin\dfrac {E}{2}$, that is, to $FB,BD,DF$. By SSS similarity, triangle $BDF$ is similar to $XYZ$, with the angle opposite $FB$ equal to $X$, and so on:
\[
\angle BDF=\dfrac {A}{2},\qquad \angle BFD=\dfrac {C}{2},\qquad \angle FBD=\dfrac {E}{2}.
\]

\textbf{Step 2: the angles of the hexagon.} Because the hexagon is convex, seen from $B$ the other vertices appear in the order $C,D,E,F,A$, so the rays $BF$ and $BD$ lie inside $\angle ABC$ in the order $BA,BF,BD,BC$. Hence
\[
B=\angle ABF+\angle FBD+\angle DBC=\left(90^\circ-\dfrac {A}{2}\right)+\dfrac {E}{2}+\left(90^\circ-\dfrac {C}{2}\right)=180^\circ-\dfrac{A+C}{2}+\dfrac {E}{2}.
\]
Using $A+C=360^\circ-E$, this gives $B=E$. In the same way, at $D$ and at $F$,
\[
D=\left(90^\circ-\dfrac {C}{2}\right)+\dfrac {A}{2}+\left(90^\circ-\dfrac {E}{2}\right)=180^\circ-\dfrac{C+E}{2}+\dfrac {A}{2}=A,
\]
\[
F=\left(90^\circ-\dfrac {E}{2}\right)+\dfrac {C}{2}+\left(90^\circ-\dfrac {A}{2}\right)=180^\circ-\dfrac{E+A}{2}+\dfrac {C}{2}=C.
\]
Therefore $\angle A=\angle D$, $\angle B=\angle E$ and $\angle C=\angle F$. $\blacksquare$

Let the quadrilateral be $ABCD$ with $AB=BC=CD=s$ and longest side $DA=L$, so $AC=BD=L$.

Triangles $ABD$ and $DCA$ are congruent (SSS: $AB=DC$, $BD=CA$, $AD$ common), so $\angle BAD=\angle CDA$. Triangles $ABC$ and $DCB$ are congruent (SSS: $AB=DC$, $AC=DB$, $BC$ common), so $\angle ABC=\angle DCB$. The angles sum to $360^\circ$, so $\angle A+\angle B=180^\circ$; hence $AD\parallel BC$, and also $\angle A+\angle C=180^\circ$, so $ABCD$ is cyclic.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=dir(90+36), B=dir(90+36+72), C=dir(90+36+144), D=dir(90+36+216);
draw(unitcircle, gray+dashed);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C, blue); draw(B--D, blue);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D);
label("$A$", A, NW); label("$B$", B, SW); label("$C$", C, SE); label("$D$", D, NE);
label("$s$", (A+B)/2, W); label("$s$", (B+C)/2, S); label("$s$", (C+D)/2, E); label("$L$", (A+D)/2, N);
label("$108^\circ$", 0.7*B+0.05*(0,1), fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

On the circumcircle, equal chords cut off equal arcs. Let each of the arcs $AB$, $BC$, $CD$ (not containing the other vertices) measure $\alpha$; then arc $DA$ measures $360^\circ-3\alpha$, with $0<\alpha<120^\circ$. A chord subtending an arc $\varphi$ has length $2R\sin\dfrac{\varphi}{2}$. Chord $AC$ subtends the arc $ABC$ of measure $2\alpha$, and chord $AD$ subtends $360^\circ-3\alpha$, so $AC=AD$ gives
\[
\sin\alpha=\sin\left(180^\circ-\dfrac{3\alpha}{2}\right)=\sin\dfrac{3\alpha}{2}.
\]
With $0<\alpha<120^\circ$, this means either $\alpha=\dfrac{3\alpha}{2}$ (impossible) or $\alpha+\dfrac{3\alpha}{2}=180^\circ$, i.e.\ $\alpha=72^\circ$. Then arc $DA=144^\circ>72^\circ$, so $DA$ is indeed the longest side: $A,B,C,D$ are four vertices of a regular pentagon.

The inscribed angle $\angle ABC$ subtends the arc $ADC$ of measure $360^\circ-2\alpha=216^\circ$, so $\angle ABC=\angle BCD=108^\circ$, while $\angle BAD=\angle CDA=\dfrac{2\alpha}{2}=72^\circ$. The largest angle is $\boxed{108^\circ}$.

question:
Five consecutive integers $v<w<x<y<z$ satisfy $v^2+w^2+x^2=y^2+z^2$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that there are exactly two such sets of integers, and find them.
\item[(b)] Find the average of all ten numbers in these two sets.
\item[(c)] Find the maximum and minimum possible values of $z$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The triangle is right-angled at $(9,1)$ with horizontal leg $8$ and vertical leg $m=n-1$ (not $n$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real m=6;
pair A=(1,1), B=(9,1), C=(9,1+m);
for(int i=0;i<=10;++i) draw((i,0)--(i,8), gray(0.85)+linewidth(0.3));
for(int j=0;j<=8;++j) draw((0,j)--(10,j), gray(0.85)+linewidth(0.3));
draw(A--B--C--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C);
label("$(1,1)$", A, SW); label("$(9,1)$", B, SE); label("$(9,n)$", C, NE);
label("$8$", (A+B)/2, S); label("$n-1$", (B+C)/2, E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(The figure is schematic; the actual triangle is much taller.) Its area is $A=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot8\cdot m=4m$. Count the boundary lattice points segment by segment, including the starting point of each segment but not its end: the horizontal leg from $(1,1)$ to $(9,1)$ contributes $8$, the vertical leg from $(9,1)$ to $(9,n)$ contributes $m$, and the hypotenuse from $(9,n)$ to $(1,1)$ contributes $g=\gcd(8,m)$. So $B=8+m+g$. By Pick's theorem, $A=I+\dfrac {B}{2}-1$, so
\[
I=4m-\dfrac{8+m+g}{2}+1=560\iff 7m-g=1126.
\]
Since $g$ divides $8$, $g\in\{1,2,4,8\}$ and $m=\dfrac{1126+g}{7}$ must be an integer: $1127=7\cdot161$ works, $1128$ and $1130$ are not multiples of $7$, and $1134=7\cdot162$ would need $\gcd(8,162)=8$, which is false. For $m=161$ indeed $\gcd(8,161)=1=g$. Hence $m=161$ and
\[
n=m+1=\boxed{162}.
\]
The value $161$ obtained in the post is the length of the vertical leg, $n-1$, not $n$ itself.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many ordered pairs $(x,y)$ of positive integers satisfy $\operatorname{lcm}(x,y)=400$?
\item[(b)] Prove that if $N=p_1^{e_1}p_2^{e_2}\cdots p_r^{e_r}$ is the prime factorisation of $N>1$, then the number of ordered pairs of positive integers with least common multiple $N$ is $(2e_1+1)(2e_2+1)\cdots(2e_r+1)$.
\end{enumerate}

Three trolls divide $n$ pancakes among themselves so that:
\begin{itemize}
\item each troll has a positive integer number of pancakes;
\item the greatest common divisor of the numbers of pancakes held by any two trolls is greater than $1$;
\item the three greatest common divisors obtained in this way are all distinct.
\end{itemize}
What is the smallest possible value of $n$?

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Put $x=\dfrac{\alpha+\beta}{2}$ and $y=\dfrac{\alpha-\beta}{2}$, so $\alpha=x+y$ and $\beta=x-y$. By the addition formulas,
\[
\cos(x+y)=\cos x\cos y-\sin x\sin y,\qquad \cos(x-y)=\cos x\cos y+\sin x\sin y.
\]
Adding, $\cos\alpha+\cos\beta=2\cos x\cos y$, which is the claim.

Geometrically: let $P=(\cos\alpha,\sin\alpha)$ and $Q=(\cos\beta,\sin\beta)$ on the unit circle, and let $M$ be the midpoint of $PQ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real al=75, be=15;
pair O=(0,0), P=dir(al), Q=dir(be), M=(P+Q)/2;
draw(unitcircle, gray);
draw((-1.15,0)--(1.25,0), gray+linewidth(0.4)); draw((0,-0.3)--(0,1.2), gray+linewidth(0.4));
draw(O--P, linewidth(0.8)); draw(O--Q, linewidth(0.8)); draw(P--Q, linewidth(0.8));
draw(O--M, blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(O); dot(P); dot(Q); dot(M);
label("$O$", O, SW); label("$P$", P, N); label("$Q$", Q, E); label("$M$", M, NE);
draw(arc(O,0.25,0,be)); label("$\beta$", 0.33*dir(be/2), E, fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The $x$-coordinate of $M$ is $\dfrac{\cos\alpha+\cos\beta}{2}$. On the other hand, triangle $OPQ$ is isosceles, so $OM$ bisects $\angle POQ$: the ray $OM$ points in direction $\dfrac{\alpha+\beta}{2}$ and $OM=\cos\dfrac{\alpha-\beta}{2}$ (up to sign). So the $x$-coordinate of $M$ is also $\cos\dfrac{\alpha-\beta}{2}\cos\dfrac{\alpha+\beta}{2}$, matching the identity.

\textbf{(b)} \textbf{No.} For $\alpha=\beta=0$ the left side is $\cos0+\cos0=2$ while $\cos(0+0)=1$. So $\cos\alpha+\cos\beta=\cos(\alpha+\beta)$ is not an identity; it holds only for special pairs of angles, such as $\alpha=60^\circ$, $\beta=-60^\circ$, where $\dfrac{1}{2}+\dfrac{1}{2}=1=\cos0^\circ$. The correct identity for a sum of cosines is the product formula in part (a).

Every such square has a side vector $(p,q)$ with $p>0$ and $q\ge0$: of the four directions in which its sides point, exactly one lies in this quarter-plane. Starting from a suitable vertex $V$, the vertices are
\[
V,\quad V+(p,q),\quad V+(p-q,\,p+q),\quad V+(-q,\,p),
\]
so the square fits exactly in an axis-parallel bounding square of side $k=p+q$, touching each of its sides, with one vertex at distance $q$ along the bottom side from the lower left corner (in the figure, the thick blue side has $(p,q)=(2,1)$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<=3;++i) for(int j=0;j<=3;++j) dot((i,j), gray);
draw((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(0,3)--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
draw((1,0)--(3,1)--(2,3)--(0,2)--cycle, blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((2,0)--(3,2)--(1,3)--(0,1)--cycle, red+dashed+linewidth(0.9));
label("$q$", (0.5,0), S); label("$p$", (2,0), S);
draw((1,0)--(3,1), blue+linewidth(1.6));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Conversely, an axis-parallel $k\times k$ grid square together with an offset $q\in\{0,1,\dots,k-1\}$ determines exactly one inscribed square (offset $0$ is the bounding square itself). The figure shows the three squares with bounding square of side $3$. So each $k\times k$ grid square accounts for exactly $k$ squares, and there are $(n+1-k)^2$ positions for a $k\times k$ grid square. The total is
\[
\sum_{k=1}^{n}k(n+1-k)^2.
\]
Write $m=n+1$. Using $\sum_{k=1}^{m-1}k=\dfrac{(m-1)m}{2}$, $\sum_{k=1}^{m-1} k^2=\dfrac{(m-1)m(2m-1)}{6}$ and $\sum_{k=1}^{m-1} k^3=\dfrac{(m-1)^2m^2}{4}$,
\[
\sum_{k=1}^{m-1}k(m-k)^2=m^2\cdot\dfrac{(m-1)m}{2}-2m\cdot\dfrac{(m-1)m(2m-1)}{6}+\dfrac{(m-1)^2m^2}{4}
=m^2(m-1)\cdot\dfrac{6m-4(2m-1)+3(m-1)}{12}=\dfrac{m^2(m-1)(m+1)}{12}.
\]
So the number of squares is
\[
\boxed{\dfrac{n(n+1)^2(n+2)}{12}}.
\]
For example, $n=1$ gives $1$, $n=2$ gives $6$ (four unit squares, one $2\times2$ square and one tilted square), and $n=3$ gives $20$.

\textbf{Upper bound.} Take two of the polygons, with $m<n$ sides. Each side of the $m$-gon is a segment not lying on any side line of the convex $n$-gon, so it meets the boundary of the $n$-gon in at most $2$ points (a line meets the boundary of a convex polygon in at most two points unless it contains a side). Hence these two polygons meet in at most $2m=2\min(m,n)$ points. Since no point lies on three polygons, each intersection point belongs to exactly one pair of polygons, and the total is at most
\[
\sum_{\text{pairs } m<n}2m=\sum_{m=3}^{49}2m(50-m),
\]
because for each $m$ there are $50-m$ polygons with more sides.

\textbf{Construction.} Inscribe regular polygons with $3,4,\dots,50$ sides in one circle, rotated so that no two share a vertex and no three sides pass through a common point (all but finitely many choices of rotation angles work). Consider an $m$-gon and an $n$-gon with $m<n$. Each side of the $m$-gon cuts off an arc of $\dfrac{360^\circ}{m}>\dfrac{360^\circ}{n}$, which contains at least one vertex of the $n$-gon but not all of them; these vertices are consecutive around the circle. Two chords of a circle cross exactly when their endpoints alternate, so exactly two sides of the $n$-gon cross this side of the $m$-gon (the ones entering and leaving that arc). Thus the two polygons meet in exactly $2m$ points, and the bound is attained. The figure shows a triangle and a square meeting in $6$ points.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(unitcircle, gray+dashed);
pair[] T, Q;
for(int i=0;i<3;++i) T.push(dir(90+120*i));
for(int i=0;i<4;++i) Q.push(dir(20+90*i));
draw(T[0]--T[1]--T[2]--cycle, blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(Q[0]--Q[1]--Q[2]--Q[3]--cycle, red+linewidth(0.9));
for(int i=0;i<3;++i) for(int j=0;j<4;++j){
  pair a=T[i], bb=T[(i+1)%3], c=Q[j], d=Q[(j+1)%4];
  real den=cross(bb-a,d-c);
  if(abs(den)>1e-9){
    real t=cross(c-a,d-c)/den, u=cross(c-a,bb-a)/den;
    if(t>0 && t<1 && u>0 && u<1) dot(a+t*(bb-a));
  }
}
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Computation.} Using $\sum_{m=3}^{49}m=\dfrac{49\cdot50}{2}-3=1222$ and $\sum_{m=3}^{49}m^2=\dfrac{49\cdot50\cdot99}{6}-5=40420$,
\[
\sum_{m=3}^{49}2m(50-m)=100\cdot1222-2\cdot40420=122200-80840=\boxed{41360}.
\]

Let $P=(0,-3)$. The swept region consists of all points whose distance from $P$ equals the distance from $P$ to some point of the triangle. As the triangle is connected, these distances fill an interval $[r,R]$, where $r$ and $R$ are the least and greatest distances from $P$ to the triangle, and the region is the annulus between radii $r$ and $R$, of area $\pi(R^2-r^2)$.

The squared distances from $P$ to the vertices are
\[
(2,0):\ 4+9=13,\qquad (10,2):\ 100+25=125,\qquad (6,6):\ 36+81=117.
\]
\textbf{Outer radius.} The distance to $P$ along a segment is largest at an endpoint, so $R^2=125$.

\textbf{Inner radius.} $P$ lies outside the triangle (below all its vertices), so the nearest point is on a side. For a side $XY$, the nearest point to $P$ is the foot of the perpendicular if it lies on the segment, and otherwise an endpoint; the foot lies beyond $X$ exactly when $(P-X)\cdot(Y-X)<0$.
\begin{itemize}
\item Side from $(2,0)$ to $(10,2)$: $(-2,-3)\cdot(8,2)=-22<0$, so the nearest point is $(2,0)$.
\item Side from $(2,0)$ to $(6,6)$: $(-2,-3)\cdot(4,6)=-26<0$, so again $(2,0)$.
\item Side from $(10,2)$ to $(6,6)$: the foot is $(7.5,4.5)$, at squared distance $7.5^2+7.5^2=112.5>13$.
\end{itemize}
So $r^2=13$, attained at the vertex $(2,0)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(0,-3), A=(2,0), B=(10,2), C=(6,6);
draw(circle(P,sqrt(125)), gray); draw(circle(P,sqrt(13)), gray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--A, blue); draw(P--B, red);
dot(P); dot(A); dot(B); dot(C);
label("$(0,-3)$", P, SW); label("$(2,0)$", A, NW); label("$(10,2)$", B, E); label("$(6,6)$", C, N);
label("$\sqrt{13}$", (P+A)/2, W, blue); label("$\sqrt{125}$", (P+B)/2+(0.3,-0.3), SE, red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The area of the annulus is
\[
\pi(125-13)=\boxed{112\pi}.
\]

question:
In the figure, a kite with two right angles is inscribed in a circle, and the circle is inscribed in a square, so that the diagonals of the kite are parallel to the sides of the square. The two longer sides of the kite each have length $x$ meters. The sides of the kite divide the square into three regions (dark grey, white and light grey), each of area $2$ m$^2$. If $x^2=a+\sqrt b$ for integers $a$ and $b$, what is the value of $a+b$?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=sqrt(1.5), h=1/R, bb=sqrt(5/6);
pair A=(-R,0), C=(R,0), B=(bb,h), D=(bb,-h);
fill((-R,0)--(-R,R)--(R,R)--(R,0)--B--cycle, gray(0.45));
fill((-R,0)--(-R,-R)--(R,-R)--(R,0)--D--cycle, gray(0.85));
draw((-R,-R)--(R,-R)--(R,R)--(-R,R)--cycle, linewidth(0.8));
draw(circle((0,0),R), linewidth(0.6));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
label("$x$", (A+B)/2, NW); label("$x$", (A+D)/2, SW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
Let the kite be $ABCD$ with $AB=AD=x$ (the longer sides) and $CB=CD$. A kite has its two equal angles at $B$ and $D$, so the right angles are $\angle ABC=\angle ADC=90^\circ$; hence $AC$ is a diameter of the circle, with centre $O$ and radius $R$. The diagonal $AC$ is parallel to a side of the square and passes through the centre, so $A$ and $C$ are the points where the circle touches two opposite sides of the square. This is why the kite cuts the square into three regions. The kite is symmetric about $AC$, so $B$ and $D$ are at the same distance $h$ from $AC$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=sqrt(1.5), h=1/R, bb=sqrt(5/6);
pair O=(0,0), A=(-R,0), C=(R,0), B=(bb,h), D=(bb,-h), F=(bb,0);
draw((-R,-R)--(R,-R)--(R,R)--(-R,R)--cycle, linewidth(0.6));
draw(circle(O,R), linewidth(0.6));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C, dashed); draw(B--D, dashed); draw(O--B, blue);
dot(O); dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D);
label("$A$", A, W); label("$B$", B, NE); label("$C$", C, E); label("$D$", D, SE); label("$O$", O, S);
label("$R$", (O+B)/2, NW, blue); label("$h$", (B+F)/2, W); label("$b$", (O+F)/2, S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The square has side $2R$ and area $6$, so $R^2=\dfrac{3}{2}$. The kite has area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot AC\cdot BD=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot2R\cdot2h=2Rh=2$, so $h=\dfrac{1}{R}$ and $h^2=\dfrac{2}{3}$. (Then each of the two outer regions has area $3-Rh=2$, as required.)

Let $b$ be the distance from $O$ to the foot of the perpendicular from $B$ to $AC$; since $B$ is on the circle, $b^2=R^2-h^2=\dfrac{3}{2}-\dfrac{2}{3}=\dfrac{5}{6}$. The longer sides come from the vertex $A$ farther from $B$, so
\[
x^2=AB^2=(R+b)^2+h^2=R^2+b^2+h^2+2Rb=2R^2+2Rb=3+2\sqrt{\dfrac{3}{2}\cdot\dfrac{5}{6}}=3+2\sqrt{\dfrac{5}{4}}=3+\sqrt5.
\]
So $a=3$, $b=5$, and $a+b=\boxed{8}$.

The cuts are at distances $48$ and $24$ from the apex. Each cut off top part is a cone similar to the whole cone, with radius proportional to height: radius $24$ at height $48$ from the apex and radius $12$ at height $24$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real H=72, r=36;
pair Ap=(0,H);
draw((-r,0)--Ap--(r,0), linewidth(0.9));
draw(xscale(r)*yscale(6)*unitcircle, linewidth(0.7));
draw(shift(0,24)*xscale(24)*yscale(4)*unitcircle, blue);
draw(shift(0,48)*xscale(12)*yscale(2)*unitcircle, blue);
draw((0,0)--Ap, dashed);
label("$24$", (0,12), W); label("$24$", (0,36), W); label("$24$", (0,60), W);
draw((0,0)--(36,0)); draw((0,24)--(24,24), blue); draw((0,48)--(12,48), blue);
label("$36$", (18,0), N); label("$24$", (12,27), N, blue); label("$12$", (6,49.5), N, blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The middle piece is the cone of height $48$ minus the cone of height $24$:
\[
\dfrac{1}{3}\pi\cdot24^2\cdot48-\dfrac{1}{3}\pi\cdot12^2\cdot24=9216\pi-1152\pi=\boxed{8064\pi}.
\]
(Equivalently, the whole cone has volume $\dfrac{1}{3}\pi\cdot36^2\cdot72=31104\pi$, and the middle piece is $\left(\dfrac{2}{3}\right)^3-\left(\dfrac{1}{3}\right)^3=\dfrac{7}{27}$ of it.)

Cut the cone and sphere by a plane through the axis. The cone becomes an isosceles triangle with base $24$ and height $24$, whose equal sides have length $\sqrt{12^2+24^2}=12\sqrt5$. The sphere becomes the incircle of this triangle, with the same radius $r$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Ap=(0,24), L=(-12,0), Rr=(12,0);
real r=6*sqrt(5)-6;
pair I=(0,r);
draw(L--Ap--Rr--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(I,r), blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw((0,0)--Ap, dashed);
pair T=I+r*unit(rotate(90)*(Ap-Rr));
draw(I--T, blue); draw(I--(0,0), blue);
dot(I); dot(T);
label("$12$", (6,0), S); label("$24$", (0,19), W); label("$12\sqrt5$", (Ap+Rr)/2, NE);
label("$r$", (I+T)/2, N, blue); label("$r$", (0,r/2), E, blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

For a triangle, the inradius is the area divided by the semiperimeter:
\[
r=\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot24\cdot24}{\dfrac{24+2\cdot12\sqrt5}{2}}=\dfrac{288}{12+12\sqrt5}=\dfrac{24}{1+\sqrt5}=\dfrac{24(\sqrt5-1)}{4}=6\sqrt5-6.
\]
So $a=6$, $c=5$ and $a+c=\boxed{11}$.

question:
Let $D$ and $C$ be opposite vertices of a unit square. Two $45^\circ$ sectors of a circle of radius $1$ centred at $D$ are joined to the outside of the square along the two sides of the square through $D$, as shown, giving a figure bounded by the segment $AB$ (which passes through $D$) and the paths $BC$ and $CA$. A quarter-circular arc of radius $1$ is drawn inside the square from $C$ to $D$, centred at one of the other two vertices of the square. What is the probability, as a percent, that a point chosen uniformly at random on arc $CD$ forms an acute triangle with points $A$ and $B$? Express your answer to the nearest tenth.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair D=(0,0), C=(1,1), Pp=(1,0), Q=(0,1);
pair A=dir(-45), B=dir(135);
path shape=A--D--B--arc(D,1,135,90)--C--Pp--arc(D,1,0,-45)--cycle;
fill(shape, gray(0.9));
draw(arc(D,1,135,90)--C--Pp--arc(D,1,0,-45), linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--B, linewidth(0.9));
draw(D--Pp, gray); draw(D--Q, gray);
draw(arc(Q,1,0,-90), blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D);
label("$A$", A, SE); label("$B$", B, NW); label("$C$", C, NE); label("$D$", D, SW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
Put $D=(0,0)$ and $C=(1,1)$, with the square $0\le x,y\le1$. The sectors centred at $D$ end at
\[
A=\left(\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2},-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}\right),\qquad B=\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}\right),
\]
so $D$ is the midpoint of $AB$ and $AB=2$. By the symmetry of the figure in the line $y=x$ (which swaps $A$ and $B$ and swaps the two possible centres of the arc), we may take the arc centred at $O=(0,1)$: its points are $X=(\cos t,\ 1+\sin t)$ for $-90^\circ\le t\le0^\circ$, and a uniform point on the arc has $t$ uniform on this interval.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair D=(0,0), C=(1,1), Pp=(1,0), Q=(0,1);
pair A=dir(-45), B=dir(135);
draw(arc(D,1,135,90)--C--Pp--arc(D,1,0,-45), gray);
draw(A--B, linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(D,1), dashed);
draw(arc(Q,1,-90,-30), blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(Q,1,-30,0), red+linewidth(2));
pair X=Q+dir(-30);
draw(Q--X, gray); draw(Q--D, gray);
draw(A--X--B, red+linewidth(0.6));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(Q); dot(X);
label("$A$", A, SE); label("$B$", B, NW); label("$C$", C, NE); label("$D$", D, SW);
label("$O$", Q, NW); label("$X_0$", X, E); draw(arc(Q,0.22,-30,0)); label("$30^\circ$", Q+0.3*dir(-15), E, fontsize(7pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Angle at $X$.} By Thales' theorem, $\angle AXB$ is acute exactly when $X$ lies outside the circle with diameter $AB$, i.e.\ when $XD>1$. Now
\[
XD^2=\cos^2t+(1+\sin t)^2=2+2\sin t>1\iff \sin t>-\dfrac{1}{2}\iff t>-30^\circ.
\]

\textbf{Angles at $A$ and $B$.} These are acute exactly when the projection of $X$ onto line $AB$ lies strictly between $A$ and $B$, i.e.\ when $|X\cdot u|<1$ for the unit vector $u=\left(\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2},-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}\right)$ along $AB$. Here
\[
X\cdot u=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}(\cos t-\sin t-1),
\]
and for $-90^\circ\le t\le0^\circ$ we have $1\le\cos t-\sin t\le\sqrt2$, so $0\le X\cdot u\le 1-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}<1$. These angles are always acute (apart from the endpoint $X=D$, where there is no triangle).

So the triangle is acute exactly for $-30^\circ<t\le0^\circ$, the red part of the arc, which is $\dfrac{30}{90}=\dfrac{1}{3}$ of the arc. The probability is $33.33\ldots\%$, which is $\boxed{33.3}$ percent.

Since $\angle C=90^\circ$, we have $\angle A+\angle B=90^\circ$. In triangle $AFB$, $\angle FAB=\dfrac{\angle A}{2}$ and $\angle FBA=\dfrac{\angle B}{2}$, because $AF$ and $BF$ are the angle bisectors.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), A=(0,3), B=(4,0);
real a=abs(B-C), b=abs(A-C), c=abs(A-B);
pair F=(a*A+b*B+c*C)/(a+b+c);
pair Dp=(b*B+c*C)/(b+c), Ep=(a*A+c*C)/(a+c);
draw(A--B--C--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--Dp, blue); draw(B--Ep, blue);
draw((0.3,0)--(0.3,0.3)--(0,0.3), linewidth(0.5));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(Dp); dot(Ep); dot(F);
label("$A$", A, N); label("$B$", B, E); label("$C$", C, SW);
label("$D$", Dp, S); label("$E$", Ep, W); label("$F$", F, 1.5*dir(70));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Therefore
\[
\angle AFB=180^\circ-\dfrac{\angle A+\angle B}{2}=180^\circ-45^\circ=\boxed{135^\circ}.
\]
($F$ is the incentre, and in general $\angle AFB=90^\circ+\dfrac{\angle C}{2}$.)

The line $3x-y=24$ meets the axes at $(8,0)$ and $(0,-24)$, so the triangle has legs $8$ and $24$ and area $A=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot8\cdot24=96$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3cm,7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0), P=(8,0), Q=(0,-24);
draw((-1,0)--(10,0), gray); draw((0,2)--(0,-26), gray);
draw(O--P--Q--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
for(int i=0;i<=8;++i) dot((i,0), blue+linewidth(2.5));
for(int j=0;j<=24;++j) dot((0,-j), blue+linewidth(2.5));
for(int i=1;i<=7;++i) dot((i,3*i-24), blue+linewidth(2.5));
label("$(8,0)$", P, N); label("$(0,-24)$", Q, W); label("$O$", O, NW);
label("$3x-y=24$", (P+Q)/2, E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Boundary lattice points (blue): counting each side with its starting point but not its end, the leg from $(0,0)$ to $(8,0)$ contributes $8$, the hypotenuse from $(8,0)$ to $(0,-24)$ contributes $\gcd(8,24)=8$ (the points $(i,3i-24)$), and the leg from $(0,-24)$ to $(0,0)$ contributes $24$. So $B=8+8+24=40$. By Pick's theorem $A=I+\dfrac {B}{2}-1$, so
\[
I=96-20+1=\boxed{77}.
\]
(Check with the first triangle: vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(0,-6)$ give $A=6$ and $B=2+6+2=10$, so $I=6-5+1=2$, as stated.) The calculation in the post used $B=20$; that misses half of the boundary points, which is why it gave $87$.

Place $A=(0,0)$, $B=(1,0)$, $C=(1,1)$, $D=(0,1)$. Then $E=\left(\dfrac{1}{3},0\right)$, $F=\left(0,\dfrac{2}{3}\right)$ (since $DF=\dfrac{1}{3}$) and $G=\left(\dfrac{2}{3},1\right)$ (since $CG=\dfrac{1}{3}$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(1,0), C=(1,1), D=(0,1);
pair E=(1/3,0), F=(0,2/3), G=(2/3,1);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle, linewidth(0.8));
fill(E--F--G--cycle, paleblue); draw(E--F--G--cycle, blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(E); dot(F); dot(G);
label("$A$", A, SW); label("$B$", B, SE); label("$C$", C, NE); label("$D$", D, NW);
label("$E$", E, SE); label("$F$", F, 1.5*E); label("$G$", G, 1.5*S);
label("$\dfrac{1}{3}$", (A+E)/2, S, fontsize(7pt)); label("$\dfrac{1}{3}$", (D+F)/2, W, fontsize(7pt)); label("$\dfrac{1}{3}$", (C+G)/2, N, fontsize(7pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

By the shoelace formula,
\[
[EFG]=\dfrac{1}{2}\left|\dfrac{1}{3}\left(\dfrac{2}{3}-1\right)+0\cdot(1-0)+\dfrac{2}{3}\left(0-\dfrac{2}{3}\right)\right|=\dfrac{1}{2}\left|-\dfrac{1}{9}-\dfrac{4}{9}\right|=\boxed{\dfrac{5}{18}}.
\]

The circle is the incircle of the equilateral triangle. The inradius of an equilateral triangle of side $s$ is $\dfrac{s\sqrt3}{6}$, so $s=2\sqrt3\,r$ and
\[
T=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot12r^2=3\sqrt3\,r^2.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1;
pair O=(0,0);
pair P1=2r*dir(90), P2=2r*dir(210), P3=2r*dir(330);
draw(P1--P2--P3--cycle, linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O,r), linewidth(0.8));
draw(dir(45)--dir(135)--dir(225)--dir(315)--cycle, blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--(0,-r), red); label("$r$", (0,-r/2), E, red);
dot(O);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The square inscribed in the circle has diagonal $2r$, so $S=\dfrac{(2r)^2}{2}=2r^2$. Hence
\[
\dfrac {T}{S}=\dfrac{3\sqrt3\,r^2}{2r^2}=\boxed{\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}}.
\]

question:
Two identical rectangular blocks of wood, each $\ell$ inches by $w$ inches in side view with $\ell>w$, are placed with a table as shown. In Figure 1, one block stands upright on the tabletop and the other lies flat on the floor; the length $r$ is the vertical distance from the top of the floor block to the top of the table block, and $r=32$ inches. The blocks are then rearranged as in Figure 2: the block on the table now lies flat and the block on the floor stands upright, and the corresponding length $s$ is $28$ inches. How high is the table?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
