solution:
Let the table have height $t$. In Figure 1 the top of the table block is at height $t+\ell$ and the top of the floor block at height $w$, so
\[
r=t+\ell-w=32.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=3.0, L=1.6, w=0.6;
draw((-0.4,0)--(4.6,0),linewidth(0.6));
fill((0,h-0.15)--(3,h-0.15)--(3,h)--(0,h)--cycle,gray(0.75));
draw((0,h-0.15)--(3,h-0.15)--(3,h)--(0,h)--cycle);
draw((0.2,0)--(0.2,h-0.15),linewidth(1.2)); draw((2.8,0)--(2.8,h-0.15),linewidth(1.2));
filldraw((1.2,h)--(1.2+w,h)--(1.2+w,h+L)--(1.2,h+L)--cycle,palegray);
filldraw((3.3,0)--(3.3+L,0)--(3.3+L,w)--(3.3,w)--cycle,palegray);
draw((-0.25,0)--(-0.25,h),blue,Arrows(5)); label("$t$",(-0.25,h/2),W,blue);
draw((1.0,h)--(1.0,h+L),red,Arrows(5)); label("$\ell$",(1.0,h+L/2),W,red);
draw((5.05,0)--(5.05,w),red,Arrows(5)); label("$w$",(5.05,w/2),E,red);
draw((1.2+w,h+L)--(4.5,h+L),dashed); draw((4.4,w)--(4.4,h+L),Arrows(5)); label("$r=t+\ell-w$",(4.4,(w+h+L)/2),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

In Figure 2 the top of the table block is at height $t+w$ and the top of the floor block at height $\ell$, so
\[
s=t+w-\ell=28.
\]
Adding the two equations eliminates both block dimensions: $2t=60$. The table is $\boxed{30}$ inches high (and $\ell-w=2$ inches).

Translate the picture so that $P$ is the origin; translation changes no slopes. The bisector is then the line $y=x$.

\textbf{Reflection in the bisector.} Reflection in the line $y=x$ sends $(x,y)$ to $(y,x)$. A reflection in the bisector of an angle maps each arm of the angle onto the other arm (the two arms make equal angles with the bisector, on opposite sides of it), so it maps $\ell_1$ onto $\ell_2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
pair P=(0,0);
draw((-1.6,-1.6)--(2.6,2.6),red+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-1.3,-2.6)--(1.6,3.2),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-2.6,-1.3)--(3.2,1.6),blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(P); label("$P$",P,SE);
label("$y=x$ (slope $1$)",(2.6,2.6),E,red);
label("$\ell_1$: slope $m$",(1.6,3.2),E,blue);
label("$\ell_2$: slope $\dfrac{1}{m}$",(3.2,1.6),E,blue);
pair X=(1,2), Y=(2,1);
dot(X); dot(Y); draw(X--Y,dashed); label("$(a,b)$",X,W); label("$(b,a)$",Y,SE);
draw(arc(P,0.9,degrees(Y),45),Arrow(4)); draw(arc(P,1.0,45,degrees(X)),Arrow(4));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Slopes.} Let $\ell_1$ have slope $m$. Since $\ell_1$ is not horizontal, $m\ne0$, and $\ell_1$ consists of the points $(t,mt)$. Their images are $(mt,t)$, which lie on the line through the origin of slope $\dfrac{t}{mt}=\dfrac{1}{m}$. Hence $\ell_2$ has slope $\dfrac{1}{m}$, and the product of the slopes is $m\cdot\dfrac{1}{m}=1$.

Note that $m\ne\pm1$: if $m=1$ then $\ell_1$ would be the bisector itself, and if $m=-1$ then $\ell_1$ is perpendicular to $y=x$, so its reflection is $\ell_1$ again and $\ell_2=\ell_1$, contradicting that the lines are distinct. So the slopes are genuinely different reciprocals.

\textbf{Horizontal case.} If $\ell_1$ is horizontal, its points $(t,0)$ reflect to $(0,t)$, so $\ell_2$ is vertical. Then $\ell_2$ has no slope, and the statement about the product does not apply; this is the exceptional case "one horizontal and one vertical".

Therefore, apart from the horizontal--vertical pair, $\boxed{m_1m_2=1}$.

Since $PR$ is a diameter, $\angle PAR=\angle PBR=90^\circ$. The hypotenuse $PR=5\sqrt{13}$ is irrational, so in each triangle the two integer sides are the legs, and they satisfy $x^2+y^2=325$. The representations of $325$ as a sum of two positive squares are
\[
325=1^2+18^2=6^2+17^2=10^2+15^2 .
\]
Because $A$ lies on arc $PQ$ and $Q$ is the midpoint of the semicircle, arc $PA$ is less than $90^\circ$, so the inscribed angle $\angle PRA<45^\circ$ and $PA<AR$; the same holds for $B$. With $PB>PA>1$ we must have $PA=6$, $AR=17$, $PB=10$, $BR=15$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=5*sqrt(13)/2;
pair P=(-r,0), R=(r,0), Q=(0,r);
pair A=(-253*sqrt(13)/130, 102*sqrt(13)/65), B=(-25*sqrt(13)/26, 30*sqrt(13)/13);
pair Sp=extension(P,B,A,R);
fill(P--A--Sp--B--R--cycle,paleyellow);
draw(arc((0,0),r,0,180)); draw(P--R);
draw(P--A--R,blue); draw(P--B--R,red);
draw(P--A--Sp--B--R--P,linewidth(1));
dot(P);dot(R);dot(Q);dot(A);dot(B);dot(Sp);
label("$P$",P,SW); label("$R$",R,SE); label("$Q$",Q,N); label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,N); label("$S$",Sp,S);
label("$6$",(P+A)/2,W); label("$10$",P+3*unit(B-P)+0.7*unit((B-P)*(0,-1))); label("$15$",(B+R)/2,NE); label("$17$",(A+R)/2+(1.8,-0.5),S);
label("$5\sqrt{13}$",(0,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Triangles $PAS$ and $RBS$ are right-angled at $A$ and $B$ and have equal vertical angles at $S$, so they are similar with ratio $PA:RB=6:15=2:5$. Write $AS=2x$, $PS=2y$, $BS=5x$, $RS=5y$. Then
\[
PB=PS+SB=2y+5x=10,\qquad AR=AS+SR=2x+5y=17 .
\]
Multiplying the first equation by $2$ and the second by $5$ and subtracting gives $21y=65$, so $y=\dfrac{65}{21}$ and $x=\dfrac{10-2y}{5}=\dfrac{16}{21}$. Thus $AS=\dfrac{32}{21}$ and $SB=\dfrac{80}{21}$, so $AS+SB=\dfrac{112}{21}=\dfrac{16}{3}$.

The perimeter is
\[
PA+AS+SB+BR+RP=6+\dfrac{16}{3}+15+5\sqrt{13}=\boxed{\dfrac{79}{3}+5\sqrt{13}}=\dfrac{79+15\sqrt{13}}{3}.
\]

Let $O$ be the centre of the triangle, which is also the circumcentre. In an equilateral triangle the centre lies on each median, two thirds of the way from the vertex. The median (altitude) has length $\dfrac{9\sqrt3}{2}$, so the circumradius is
\[
R=\dfrac{2}{3}\cdot\dfrac{9\sqrt3}{2}=3\sqrt3 .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=9; real R=s/sqrt(3);
pair O=(0,0), X=R*dir(90), Y=R*dir(210), Z=R*dir(330);
fill(circle(O,R),paleblue); fill(X--Y--Z--cycle,white);
draw(circle(O,R)); draw(X--Y--Z--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--X,dashed); draw(O--Y,dashed); draw(O--Z,dashed);
pair M=(Y+Z)/2; draw(O--M,dotted);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,NE);
label("$X$",X,N); label("$Y$",Y,SW); label("$Z$",Z,SE);
label("$9$",M,S); label("$3\sqrt3$",(O+X)/2+(0.25,0),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The circle has area $\pi R^2=27\pi$, and the triangle has area $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot9^2=\dfrac{81\sqrt3}{4}$. The required area is
\[
\boxed{27\pi-\dfrac{81\sqrt3}{4}}\approx49.75 .
\]

question:
In an arcade game, the ``monster'' is the shaded sector of a circle of radius $1$ cm, as shown in the figure. The missing piece (the mouth) has central angle $60^\circ$. What is the perimeter of the monster in cm?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
filldraw(O--arc(O,1,30,330)--cycle,gray(0.75),linewidth(0.9));
dot(O); label("$60^\circ$",O,8*dir(0)+(0.0,0));
draw(arc(O,0.3,-30,30));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
The boundary of the monster consists of two radii and the major arc. The arc subtends $360^\circ-60^\circ=300^\circ$ at the centre $O$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
filldraw(O--arc(O,1,30,330)--cycle,gray(0.85));
draw(arc(O,1,30,330),red+linewidth(1.3));
draw(O--dir(30),blue+linewidth(1.3)); draw(O--dir(330),blue+linewidth(1.3));
draw(arc(O,0.3,-30,30)); label("$60^\circ$",(0.55,0));
label("$300^\circ$",(-0.45,0.12));
dot(O); label("$O$",O,2*S);
label("$1$",dir(30)/2,NW,blue); label("$1$",dir(330)/2,SW,blue);
label("arc $=\dfrac{5\pi}{3}$",dir(200),W,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The two radii contribute $1+1=2$ cm. The arc is $\dfrac{300}{360}=\dfrac{5}{6}$ of the circumference $2\pi$, i.e. $\dfrac{5\pi}{3}$ cm. The perimeter is
\[
\boxed{2+\dfrac{5\pi}{3}}\text{ cm}\approx7.24\text{ cm}.
\]

question:
Ten kids are to be seated around a circular table with ten equally spaced seats. Two seatings are considered the same if one is a rotation of the other.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] In how many ways can they be seated if Audrey and Fred insist on sitting diametrically opposite one another?
\item[(b)] In how many ways can they be seated if, in addition, Betty and Guang also insist on sitting diametrically opposite each other?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Label the hexagon $ABCDEF$ with centre $O$.

Any two distinct points are two vertices of exactly two equilateral triangles, one on each side of the segment joining them (for example, side $AB$ gives triangles $ABO$ and $ABP$ in the figure). There are $\binom62=15$ pairs of hexagon vertices, giving $30$ triangles counted with multiplicity.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] H; for(int k=0;k<6;++k) H[k]=dir(60*k);
draw(H[0]--H[1]--H[2]--H[3]--H[4]--H[5]--cycle,linewidth(1));
string[] nm={"$A$","$B$","$C$","$D$","$E$","$F$"};
for(int k=0;k<6;++k){dot(H[k]); label(nm[k],H[k],dir(60*k));}
draw(H[0]--H[2]--H[4]--cycle,blue+dashed);
pair Y=H[0]+(H[1]-H[0])*dir(-60);
draw(H[0]--H[1]--Y--cycle,red); dot(Y,red); label("$P$",Y,E,red);
draw(H[0]--(0,0)--H[1],red+dotted); dot((0,0)); label("$O$",(0,0),SW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

A triangle is counted once for each of its sides whose endpoints are both hexagon vertices. If two of its sides have this property, all three of its vertices are hexagon vertices. The only equilateral triangles with all three vertices among the hexagon's vertices are $ACE$ and $BDF$, and each of them is counted $3$ times. Every other triangle has exactly two hexagon vertices and is counted once.

So the number of distinct triangles is $30-2\cdot2=\boxed{26}$.

Let $t=\angle ABC$, an integer with $1\le t\le179$ ($t=0$ or $180$ would make $A,B,C$ collinear). By the law of cosines
\[
AC^2=1+4-4\cos t=5-4\cos t,\qquad 1<AC<3 .
\]
Since $4-3<AC<4+3$, triangle $ACD$ with $CD=3$, $DA=4$ exists, and $D$ is one of two points, mirror images in line $AC$: on the opposite side of $AC$ from $B$, or on the same side.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(12cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real t1=70, t2=130;
picture p1, p2;
pair B=(0,0), A=(1,0);
pair C1=2*dir(t1);
pair[] I1=intersectionpoints(circle(A,4),circle(C1,3));
pair D1 = (cross(C1-A,I1[0]-A)*cross(C1-A,B-A)<0) ? I1[0] : I1[1];
draw(p1,A--B--C1--D1--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(p1,A--C1,dashed);
dot(p1,A);dot(p1,B);dot(p1,C1);dot(p1,D1);
label(p1,"$A$",A,SE); label(p1,"$B$",B,SW); label(p1,"$C$",C1,W); label(p1,"$D$",D1,NE);
label(p1,"$1$",(A+B)/2,S); label(p1,"$2$",(B+C1)/2,W); label(p1,"$3$",(C1+D1)/2,N); label(p1,"$4$",(A+D1)/2,E);
label(p1,"convex: $D$ opposite $B$",(1.5,-0.9));
pair C2=2*dir(t2);
pair[] I2=intersectionpoints(circle(A,4),circle(C2,3));
pair D2 = (cross(C2-A,I2[0]-A)*cross(C2-A,B-A)>0) ? I2[0] : I2[1];
draw(p2,A--B--C2--D2--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(p2,A--C2,dashed);
dot(p2,A);dot(p2,B);dot(p2,C2);dot(p2,D2);
label(p2,"$A$",A,E); label(p2,"$B$",B,SW); label(p2,"$C$",C2,W); label(p2,"$D$",D2,S);
label(p2,"$1$",(A+B)/2,N); label(p2,"$2$",(B+C2)/2,SW); label(p2,"$3$",(C2+D2)/2,W); label(p2,"$4$",(A+D2)/2,SE);
label(p2,"dart: $B$ inside $\triangle ACD$",(-0.6,-1.9));
add(p1); add(shift(6.5,0)*p2);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{$D$ opposite $B$.} Sides $AB,BC$ lie in the closed half-plane of $B$ and meet line $AC$ only at $A$ and $C$; sides $CD,DA$ lie in the other closed half-plane and meet the line only at $C$ and $A$. So $AB\cap CD=\emptyset$ and $BC\cap DA=\emptyset$: the quadrilateral is simple. This gives one quadrilateral for every $t$: $179$ in all.

\textbf{$D$ on the same side as $B$.} $D$ cannot lie inside triangle $ABC$, since $DA=4$ while every point of that triangle is within $\max(AB,AC)<3$ of $A$. Also $A$ cannot lie inside triangle $BCD$ (then the ray $CA$ would cross segment $BD$, putting $B$ and $D$ on opposite sides of $AC$), and likewise $C$ cannot lie inside triangle $ABD$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $B$ is strictly inside triangle $ACD$, the quadrilateral is a simple ``dart'': $AB$ and $BC$ lie inside the triangle and touch its boundary only at $A$ and $C$.
\item Otherwise the four points are in convex position with $AC$ an edge of their convex hull (as $B,D$ are on the same side of it). The hull is $A,C,D,B$ or $A,C,B,D$ in cyclic order. In the first case $BC$ and $AD$ are the diagonals of the hull and cross; in the second $AB$ and $CD$ cross. Either way two sides of $ABCD$ cross.
\end{itemize}
(Degenerate positions do not occur for integer $t$: three of the points can be collinear only if $B$ lies on segment $AD$ with $BD=3$, the borderline case $\cos t=-\dfrac{1}{3}$ found below; the alternatives $BD=5$ or $B$ on line $CD$ would force all four points onto one line or a triangle with sides $1,1,4$.)

Since $B,D$ are on the same side of $AC$, $B$ is inside triangle $ACD$ exactly when $\angle ACB<\angle ACD$ and $\angle CAB<\angle CAD$. By the law of cosines, $\cos\angle ACB=\dfrac{AC^2+3}{4\,AC}>\dfrac{AC^2-7}{6\,AC}=\cos\angle ACD$, because $6(AC^2+3)>4(AC^2-7)$; so the first condition always holds. The second is
\[
\cos\angle CAB=\dfrac{AC^2-3}{2\,AC}>\dfrac{AC^2+7}{8\,AC}=\cos\angle CAD\iff 3AC^2>19\iff 5-4\cos t>\dfrac{19}{3}\iff\cos t<-\dfrac{1}{3} .
\]
As $\arccos\left(-\dfrac{1}{3}\right)\approx109.47^\circ$, this holds for $t=110,111,\ldots,179$: $70$ values.

\textbf{Noncongruence.} The four side lengths are different, so a congruence between two such quadrilaterals must match $A,B,C,D$ in order; it preserves $\angle ABC$ and whether the quadrilateral is convex. So all the quadrilaterals found are pairwise noncongruent.

The total is $179+70=\boxed{249}$.

question:
A rectangular solid has base edges $a$ cm and $b$ cm, height $20$ cm, and volume $120$ cm$^3$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Express $b$ as a function of $a$.
\item[(b)] Explain why $b$ is inversely proportional to $a$.
\item[(c)] What is the domain of this function in the context of the problem?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} The volume is $a\cdot b\cdot20=120$, so $ab=6$ and
\[
b=\dfrac{6}{a}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair o=(0,0); real a=3, bb=1.6, h=2.6; pair dz=(1.0,0.6)*bb/1.6;
pair P0=o, P1=(a,0), P2=(a,0)+dz, P3=dz;
draw(P0--P1--P2+(0,h)--P3+(0,h)--P0+(0,h)--cycle,invisible);
draw(P0--P1--P1+(0,h)--P0+(0,h)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(P1--P2--P2+(0,h)--P1+(0,h)); draw(P0+(0,h)--P3+(0,h)--P2+(0,h));
draw(P0--P3--P2,dashed); draw(P3--P3+(0,h),dashed);
label("$a$",(P0+P1)/2,S); label("$b$",(P1+P2)/2,SE); label("$20$",P1+(0,h/2)+(0,0),W);
label("$V=20ab=120$",P3+(0,h)+(a/2,0.35),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{(b)} Two quantities are inversely proportional when their product is constant. Here $ab=6$ for every such solid: doubling $a$ halves $b$, tripling $a$ divides $b$ by $3$, and so on. So $b=\dfrac{k}{a}$ with constant $k=6$.

\textbf{(c)} As a formula, $\dfrac{6}{a}$ is defined for all $a\ne0$, but here $a$ and $b$ are lengths, so both must be positive. Every positive $a$ gives a positive $b=\dfrac{6}{a}$, so the domain is
\[
\boxed{a>0},
\]
not all nonzero real numbers.

Let $PQ=2a$ and call the other two points $R$ and $T$. The remaining five segments $PR,QR,PT,QT,RT$ have lengths $a,a,a,a,b$.

If $R$ is not on segment $PQ$, the triangle inequality in triangle $PQR$ (or the strict inequality for a point off segment $PQ$) gives $PR+QR>2a$, so $PR$ and $QR$ cannot both equal $a$; one of them is $b$. The same holds for $T$. Since only one segment has length $b$, at least one of $R,T$, say $R$, lies on segment $PQ$ with $PR=QR=a$: $R$ is the midpoint of $PQ$.

Now $PT,QT,RT$ are $a,a,b$ in some order. If $PT=QT=a$, then $T$ would also be the midpoint of $PQ$, i.e. $T=R$, which is impossible. So $RT=a$ and one of $PT,QT$, say $PT$, equals $a$. Then triangle $PRT$ is equilateral with side $a$, and $b=QT$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=2;
pair P=(0,0), Rr=(a,0), Q=(2a,0), T=(a/2,a*sqrt(3)/2);
draw(P--Q,linewidth(0.9)); draw(P--T--Rr,linewidth(0.9)); draw(T--Q,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(P);dot(Q);dot(Rr);dot(T);
label("$P$",P,SW); label("$Q$",Q,SE); label("$R$",Rr,S); label("$T$",T,N);
label("$a$",(P+Rr)/2,S); label("$a$",(Rr+Q)/2,S); label("$a$",(P+T)/2,NW); label("$a$",(T+Rr)/2,NE);
label("$b$",(T+Q)/2,NE,red);
draw((0,-0.55)--(2a,-0.55),Arrows(5)); label("$2a$",(a,-0.55),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Place $P=(0,0)$, $R=(a,0)$, $Q=(2a,0)$ and $T=\left(\dfrac {a}{2},\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}a\right)$. Then
\[
b=QT=\sqrt{\left(\dfrac{3a}{2}\right)^2+\dfrac{3a^2}{4}}=\sqrt3\,a .
\]
(Equivalently, $\angle PTQ=90^\circ$ because $T$ lies on the circle with diameter $PQ$.) So $\dfrac {b}{a}=\boxed{\sqrt3}$.

question:
$EFC$ is an equilateral triangle inscribed in square $ABCD$, with $E$ on side $AD$ and $F$ on side $AB$. Point $G$ lies on side $CD$ with $GC=ED$. If the area of the shaded triangle $DFG$ is $54$, what is the area of triangle $AEF$?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=6, t=s*(2-sqrt(3));
pair D=(0,0), C=(s,0), B=(s,s), A=(0,s);
pair E=(0,t), F=(s-t,s), G=(s-t,0);
fill(D--F--G--cycle,mediumgreen);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(E--F--C--cycle); draw(D--F); draw(F--G);
dot(E);dot(F);dot(G);
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,NE); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,SW);
label("$E$",E,W); label("$F$",F,N); label("$G$",G,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
Let the square have side $s$. Since $CE=CF$, $CD=CB$ and $\angle D=\angle B=90^\circ$, the right triangles $CDE$ and $CBF$ are congruent (hypotenuse--leg), so $DE=BF=t$. Then $AE=AF=s-t$ and $GC=t$, so $DG=s-t=AF$: the point $G$ lies directly below $F$, and $FG=s$ is perpendicular to $DC$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=6, t=s*(2-sqrt(3));
pair D=(0,0), C=(s,0), B=(s,s), A=(0,s);
pair E=(0,t), F=(s-t,s), G=(s-t,0);
fill(D--F--G--cycle,palegreen); fill(A--E--F--cycle,paleyellow);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(E--F--C--cycle,blue); draw(D--F); draw(F--G,dashed);
dot(E);dot(F);dot(G);
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,NE); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,SW);
label("$E$",E,W); label("$F$",F,N); label("$G$",G,S);
label("$t$",(0,t/2),W); label("$t$",(s-t/2,s),N); label("$t$",(s-t/2,0),S);
label("$s-t$",(0,(s+t)/2),W); label("$s-t$",((s-t)/2,s),N); label("$s$",(s+0.2,s/2),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Finding $t$.} From $CE=EF$: $s^2+t^2=2(s-t)^2$, i.e. $t^2-4st+s^2=0$, so $t=(2-\sqrt3)s$ (the other root exceeds $s$). Hence $s-t=(\sqrt3-1)s$.

\textbf{Areas.} Triangle $DFG$ has base $DG=s-t$ and height $FG=s$, so $[DFG]=\dfrac{1}{2} s(s-t)=54$. Triangle $AEF$ is right-angled at $A$ with legs $s-t$, so
\[
[AEF]=\dfrac{1}{2}(s-t)^2=[DFG]\cdot\dfrac{s-t}{s}=54(\sqrt3-1).
\]
The area of triangle $AEF$ is $\boxed{54\sqrt3-54}\approx39.53$.

question:
A $1$-inch by $35$-inch piece of wood is divided into six pieces by making five $45^\circ$ cuts across it, as shown: the cuts alternate in direction, so that the two end pieces are right isosceles triangles and the four middle pieces are trapezoids with $45^\circ$ ends. The two triangular pieces are discarded, and all four trapezoidal pieces are used to form a rectangular picture frame (of width $1$ inch, with mitred corners). A picture measuring $17\dfrac{1}{2}$ inches by $20$ inches is reduced proportionally so that it fits exactly in the interior of the frame. If the cuts were made so that this works, what is the area of the interior of the frame?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(11cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real L=35;
draw((0,0)--(L,0)--(L,1)--(0,1)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
real[] tp={0,8,17,25,34}; real[] bt={1,7,18,24,35};
fill((0,0)--(1,0)--(0,1)--cycle,gray(0.7));
fill((34,1)--(35,1)--(35,0)--cycle,gray(0.7));
draw((1,0)--(0,1)); draw((8,1)--(7,0)); draw((17,1)--(18,0)); draw((25,1)--(24,0)); draw((34,1)--(35,0));
draw((0,-0.6)--(L,-0.6),Arrows(4)); label("$35$",(L/2,-0.6),S);
draw((-0.6,0)--(-0.6,1),Arrows(3)); label("$1$",(-0.6,0.5),W);
label("(not to scale)",(L/2,1),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
Let the interior of the frame be $a$ by $b$ inches. A side of the frame of width $1$ with $45^\circ$ mitred ends is a trapezoid with inner edge $a$ (or $b$) and outer edge $a+2$ (or $b+2$). Opposite sides of the frame are equal, so the four trapezoids have inner edges $a,b,a,b$.

Along the strip, adjacent trapezoids share a cut, so their long edges alternate between the top and the bottom of the strip. The triangles at the ends have legs $1$; together they contribute $1$ inch to the top edge and $1$ inch to the bottom edge (one triangle has its leg on top, the other on the bottom).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(10cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=7, bb=8;
// the strip with true lengths
picture st;
draw(st,(0,0)--(35,0)--(35,1)--(0,1)--cycle);
fill(st,(0,0)--(1,0)--(0,1)--cycle,gray(0.7)); fill(st,(34,1)--(35,1)--(35,0)--cycle,gray(0.7));
draw(st,(1,0)--(0,1)); draw(st,(9,1)--(8,0)); draw(st,(17,1)--(18,0)); draw(st,(26,1)--(25,0)); draw(st,(34,1)--(35,0));
label(st,"$a+2$",(4.5,1),N); label(st,"$b$",(13,1),N); label(st,"$a+2$",(21.5,1),N); label(st,"$b$",(30,1),N);
label(st,"$a$",(4.5,0),S); label(st,"$b+2$",(13,0),S); label(st,"$a$",(21.5,0),S); label(st,"$b+2$",(30,0),S);
add(scale(0.42)*st);
// the frame
picture fr;
draw(fr,(0,0)--(bb+2,0)--(bb+2,a+2)--(0,a+2)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(fr,(1,1)--(bb+1,1)--(bb+1,a+1)--(1,a+1)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
fill(fr,(1,1)--(bb+1,1)--(bb+1,a+1)--(1,a+1)--cycle,paleblue);
draw(fr,(0,0)--(1,1)); draw(fr,(bb+2,0)--(bb+1,1)); draw(fr,(bb+2,a+2)--(bb+1,a+1)); draw(fr,(0,a+2)--(1,a+1));
label(fr,"$b$",((bb+2)/2,1),N); label(fr,"$a$",(1,(a+2)/2),E); label(fr,"$1$",((bb+2)/2,0.5));
label(fr,"interior $a\times b$",((bb+2)/2+0.3,(a+2)/2+1.5));
add(shift(3.5,-7.5)*scale(0.55)*fr);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Reading the top edge, $(a+2)+b+(a+2)+b+1=35$ (the bottom edge gives the same equation), so
\[
2a+2b+5=35\quad\Longrightarrow\quad a+b=15 .
\]
The interior is similar to the picture, so $a:b=17\dfrac{1}{2}:20=7:8$. With $a+b=15$ this gives $a=7$ and $b=8$, and the interior has area $7\cdot8=\boxed{56}$ square inches.

The right angle is at $B$ since $5^2+12^2=13^2$. Use coordinates $B=(0,0)$, $A=(0,5)$, $C=(12,0)$, so $Q=(4,0)$, $R=(8,0)$, and the bisector of the right angle $B$ is the line $y=x$. It meets $AC$ at $P=\left(\dfrac{60}{17},\dfrac{60}{17}\right)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), A=(0,5), C=(12,0), Q=(4,0), Rr=(8,0);
pair P=(60/17,60/17), X=(20/9,20/9), Y=(40/13,40/13);
fill(Q--Rr--Y--X--cycle,paleyellow);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(B--P,blue); draw(A--Q); draw(A--Rr);
draw(Q--Rr--Y--X--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0.5)--(0.5,0.5)--(0.5,0));
dot(P);dot(X);dot(Y);dot(Q);dot(Rr);
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$P$",P,NE);
label("$Q$",Q,S); label("$R$",Rr,S); label("$X$",X,W); label("$Y$",Y,NW);
label("$5$",(0,2.5),W); label("$4$",(2,0),S); label("$4$",(6,0),S); label("$4$",(10,0),S);
label("$y=x$",(1.2,1.2),SE,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Line $AQ$ is $\dfrac {x}{4}+\dfrac {y}{5}=1$; with $y=x$ this gives $x=\dfrac{20}{9}$, so $X=\left(\dfrac{20}{9},\dfrac{20}{9}\right)$. Line $AR$ is $\dfrac {x}{8}+\dfrac {y}{5}=1$, giving $Y=\left(\dfrac{40}{13},\dfrac{40}{13}\right)$. Both lie on segment $BP$ since $\dfrac{20}{9}<\dfrac{40}{13}<\dfrac{60}{17}$.

Quadrilateral $QRYX$ is triangle $BRY$ minus triangle $BQX$:
\[
[QRYX]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot8\cdot\dfrac{40}{13}-\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot4\cdot\dfrac{20}{9}=\dfrac{160}{13}-\dfrac{40}{9}=\dfrac{1440-520}{117}=\boxed{\dfrac{920}{117}}.
\]

We count triples $\ell\ge w\ge h\ge1$ of integers with $\ell wh=150=2\cdot3\cdot5^2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair o=(0,0); real L=3.2, H=1.4; pair dz=(1.0,0.6);
draw(o--(L,0)--(L,H)--(0,H)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((L,0)--(L,0)+dz--(L,H)+dz--(L,H)); draw((0,H)--dz+(0,H)--(L,H)+dz);
draw(o--dz--dz+(L,0),dashed); draw(dz--dz+(0,H),dashed);
label("$\ell$",(L/2,0),S); label("$w$",(L,0)+dz/2,SE); label("$h$",(0,H/2),W);
label("$\ell w h=150$",(L/2+0.5,H+0.6),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Since $h^3\le\ell wh=150$, we have $h\le5$, and $h$ must divide $150$, so $h\in\{1,2,3,5\}$. For each $h$, $w$ divides $\dfrac{150}{h}$ with $h\le w$ and $w^2\le\dfrac{150}{h}$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $h=1$: $w\ell=150$, $w\le12$: $w\in\{1,2,3,5,6,10\}$, $6$ prisms.
\item $h=2$: $w\ell=75$, $2\le w\le8$: $w\in\{3,5\}$, $2$ prisms.
\item $h=3$: $w\ell=50$, $3\le w\le7$: $w=5$, $1$ prism.
\item $h=5$: $w\ell=30$, $w=5$: $\ell=6$, $1$ prism.
\end{itemize}
The total is $6+2+1+1=\boxed{10}$.

Place $A=(0,0)$, $B=(6,0)$, $C=(6,5)$, $D=(0,5)$. Since $C$ is the midpoint of $AE$, $E=2C-A=(12,10)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(6,0), C=(6,5), D=(0,5), Ep=(12,10);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9)); draw(A--Ep); draw(D--Ep,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(D--(12,5),dashed); draw((12,5)--Ep,dashed);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(Ep);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,W); label("$E$",Ep,NE);
label("$6$",(3,0),S); label("$5$",(0,2.5),W); label("$12$",(9,5),S); label("$5$",(12,7.5),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Then $DE$ has horizontal run $12$ and vertical rise $10-5=5$, so
\[
DE=\sqrt{12^2+5^2}=\sqrt{169}=\boxed{13}.
\]

Let the centres be $P$, $Q$, $R$. Each circle passes through the other two centres, so $PQ=QR=RP=12$ and triangle $PQR$ is equilateral with side $12$.

The region common to all three circles is bounded by three arcs: the arc of the circle centred at $P$ from $Q$ to $R$, the arc centred at $Q$ from $R$ to $P$, and the arc centred at $R$ from $P$ to $Q$ (shaded below). Each arc subtends a $60^\circ$ angle at its centre.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(0,0), Q=(12,0), R=(6,6*sqrt(3));
path g=arc(P,Q,R)--arc(Q,R,P)--arc(R,P,Q)--cycle;
fill(g,paleblue);
draw(circle(P,12),gray(0.55)); draw(circle(Q,12),gray(0.55)); draw(circle(R,12),gray(0.55));
draw(P--Q--R--cycle,red+linewidth(0.8));
draw(g,blue+linewidth(1));
dot(P); dot(Q); dot(R);
label("$P$",P,SW); label("$Q$",Q,SE); label("$R$",R,N);
label("$12$",(P+Q)/2,N,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The region consists of triangle $PQR$ together with three congruent circular segments, one beyond each side. The segment beyond $QR$ is the $60^\circ$ sector of the circle centred at $P$ minus triangle $PQR$:
\[
\text{segment}=\dfrac{60}{360}\cdot\pi\cdot 12^2-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot 12^2=24\pi-36\sqrt3 .
\]
Hence the common area is
\[
\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot 12^2+3\left(24\pi-36\sqrt3\right)=36\sqrt3+72\pi-108\sqrt3=\boxed{72\pi-72\sqrt3}.
\]

Let $Q_A$ be the quarter-disc of radius $6$ centred at $A$ (inside the square) and $Q_C$ the quarter-disc centred at $C$. The required region is $Q_A\cap Q_C$, the lens between the two arcs.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(6,0), C=(6,6), D=(0,6);
path lens=arc(A,B,D)--arc(C,D,B)--cycle;
fill(lens,paleblue);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(A,B,D),blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(arc(C,D,B),blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$6$",(A+B)/2,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

First, $Q_A\cup Q_C$ is the whole square. Put $A=(0,0)$ and $C=(6,6)$. If a point $(x,y)$ of the square were in neither quarter-disc, then
\[
x^2+y^2>36\quad\text{and}\quad (6-x)^2+(6-y)^2>36 .
\]
Adding gives $2x^2-12x+2y^2-12y+72>72$, that is $x(x-6)+y(y-6)>0$, which is impossible because $0\le x\le 6$ and $0\le y\le 6$ make both products at most $0$.

Therefore, by inclusion and exclusion,
\[
[Q_A\cap Q_C]=[Q_A]+[Q_C]-[Q_A\cup Q_C]=9\pi+9\pi-36=\boxed{18\pi-36}.
\]

question:
All the integers from $1$ to $256$ are written in binary (base $2$), without leading zeros. How many zeros are written in total?
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the total.
\item[(b)] A student counted the $k$-digit binary numbers by the number of zeros they contain and wrote, for the $9$-digit, ..., $2$-digit numbers, sums such as $\dbinom81+\dbinom82+\cdots+\dbinom88$. Explain what such a sum actually counts, and why it does not give the number of zeros.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $h_A$, $h_B$, $h_C$ be the (positive) distances from $A$, $B$, $C$ to $\ell$; they are nonzero because $\ell$ passes through no vertex.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(2,4), B=(0,0), C=(6,0);
pair F=A+0.7*(B-A), Ep=A+0.4*(C-A);
pair u=unit(Ep-F);
pair D=F+(-F.y/u.y)*u;
pair fA=F+dot(A-F,u)*u, fB=F+dot(B-F,u)*u, fC=F+dot(C-F,u)*u;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--D,dashed);
draw((D-0.5*u)--(fC+0.5*u),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--fA,red+dashed); draw(B--fB,red+dashed); draw(C--fC,red+dashed);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(F); dot(Ep);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,S); label("$D$",D,S);
label("$F$",F,NW); label("$E$",Ep,N);
label("$h_A$",(A+fA)/2,E,red); label("$h_B$",(B+fB)/2,W,red); label("$h_C$",(C+fC)/2,NE,red);
label("$\ell$",fC+0.5*u,E,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Consider the points $B$, $C$ and $D$, which lie on one line through $D\in\ell$. Drop perpendiculars $BB'$ and $CC'$ to $\ell$. These perpendiculars are parallel, so triangles $DBB'$ and $DCC'$ are similar (they share the angle at $D$, or vertical angles at $D$, and have right angles at $B'$ and $C'$). Hence
\[
\dfrac{BD}{DC}=\dfrac{BB'}{CC'}=\dfrac{h_B}{h_C}.
\]
In the same way, using the points $C,A,E$ on line $CA$ and the points $A,B,F$ on line $AB$,
\[
\dfrac{CE}{EA}=\dfrac{h_C}{h_A},\qquad \dfrac{AF}{FB}=\dfrac{h_A}{h_B}.
\]
Multiplying,
\[
\dfrac{BD}{DC}\cdot\dfrac{CE}{EA}\cdot\dfrac{AF}{FB}=\dfrac{h_B}{h_C}\cdot\dfrac{h_C}{h_A}\cdot\dfrac{h_A}{h_B}=1 ,
\]
which proves the theorem.

Write $[XYZ]$ for the area of triangle $XYZ$. We use the fact that two triangles with the same apex and bases on the same line have areas in the ratio of their bases (they have the same height).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.3,4), B=(0,0), C=(6,0);
pair P=(A+2*B+1.5*C)/4.5;
pair D=extension(A,P,B,C), Ep=extension(B,P,C,A), F=extension(C,P,A,B);
fill(A--B--P--cycle,paleblue); fill(A--C--P--cycle,palegreen);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,red); draw(B--Ep,red); draw(C--F,red);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(Ep); dot(F); dot(P);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$D$",D,S); label("$E$",Ep,NE); label("$F$",F,NW); label("$P$",P,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Triangles $ABD$ and $ADC$ have bases $BD$, $DC$ on line $BC$ and apex $A$; triangles $PBD$ and $PDC$ have the same bases and apex $P$. Hence
\[
\dfrac{BD}{DC}=\dfrac{[ABD]}{[ADC]}=\dfrac{[PBD]}{[PDC]} .
\]
If $\dfrac {x}{y}=\dfrac {u}{v}=k$ with $y\ne v$, then $x-u=k(y-v)$, so $\dfrac{x-u}{y-v}=k$. Since $P$ lies on segment $AD$, $[ABD]-[PBD]=[ABP]$ and $[ADC]-[PDC]=[APC]$, and these are positive. So
\[
\dfrac{BD}{DC}=\dfrac{[ABP]}{[APC]}\qquad(\text{the two shaded triangles}).
\]
In exactly the same way,
\[
\dfrac{CE}{EA}=\dfrac{[BCP]}{[BPA]},\qquad \dfrac{AF}{FB}=\dfrac{[CAP]}{[CPB]} .
\]
Multiplying the three equalities, every area cancels:
\[
\dfrac{BD}{DC}\cdot\dfrac{CE}{EA}\cdot\dfrac{AF}{FB}=\dfrac{[ABP]}{[APC]}\cdot\dfrac{[BCP]}{[BPA]}\cdot\dfrac{[CAP]}{[CPB]}=1 .
\]

question:
Let $f(x)=\sqrt{x}$ and $g(x)=\sqrt{x-4}$, as real-valued functions.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Let $s=f+g$. What is the domain of $s$?
\item[(b)] Let $p=f\cdot g$. Note that $\sqrt{x}\,\sqrt{x-4}=\sqrt{x^2-4x}$ wherever the left side is defined. Is $\sqrt{x^2-4x}$ defined when $x=-4$? Is $-4$ in the domain of $p$?
\item[(c)] Find the domain of $p$.
\end{enumerate}

The numbers $3,4,5,6,7,8$ are to be placed in the six circles of the figure (three at the corners of a triangle and one at the midpoint of each side), one number per circle, so that the sums of the three numbers on each side of the triangle are equal.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(4,0), C=(2,2*sqrt(3));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
pair[] P={A,B,C,(A+B)/2,(B+C)/2,(C+A)/2};
for(int i=0;i<6;++i){filldraw(circle(P[i],0.38),white,black+linewidth(0.8));}
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many distinct solutions are there, not counting rotations and reflections as different?
\item[(b)] Find the sum of all the different possible side sums.
\item[(c)] Prove that in every solution the three corner numbers form an arithmetic sequence.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the corners be $a,b,c$ with corner sum $K=a+b+c$, and let the common side sum be $s$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(4,0), C=(2,2*sqrt(3));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
pair[] P={A,B,C,(A+B)/2,(B+C)/2,(C+A)/2};
for(int i=0;i<6;++i){filldraw(circle(P[i],0.38),white,black+linewidth(0.8));}
label("$a$",A); label("$b$",B); label("$c$",C);
label("$s-a-b$",(A+B)/2+(0,-0.45),S); label("$s-b-c$",(B+C)/2+0.42*dir(30),E); label("$s-c-a$",(C+A)/2+0.42*dir(150),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The middle number on the side between corners $b$ and $c$ is $s-b-c=a+(s-K)$. Writing $d=s-K$, the three middle numbers are exactly $a+d$, $b+d$, $c+d$: the set of middle numbers is the set of corner numbers shifted by $d$.

Adding the three side sums counts each corner twice and each middle number once, so $3s=(3+4+\cdots+8)+K=33+K$. Hence $d=s-K=33-2s$, which is odd.

So we must split $\{3,4,5,6,7,8\}$ into a set $C$ of three corners and its translate $C+d$, with $d$ odd and $d\ne0$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $d=\pm5$ is impossible: only $3$ and $8$ differ by $5$.
\item $d=3$: each element of $C$ is $3$ less than an element of the rest, forcing $C=\{3,4,5\}$ (middles $6,7,8$). Then $s=\dfrac{33-3}{2}=15$.
\item $d=-3$: $C=\{6,7,8\}$, middles $\{3,4,5\}$, $s=18$.
\item $d=1$: the numbers pair up as $(3,4),(5,6),(7,8)$ and $C$ takes the smaller of each pair: $C=\{3,5,7\}$, $s=16$.
\item $d=-1$: $C=\{4,6,8\}$, $s=17$.
\end{itemize}
In each case the middle numbers are forced: the number $x+d$ goes opposite corner $x$. The corners can be placed in $3!=6$ ways, which are exactly the $6$ rotations and reflections of the triangle, so each corner set gives one solution up to symmetry.

\textbf{(a)} There are $\boxed{4}$ distinct solutions.

\textbf{(b)} The side sums are $15,16,17,18$, with total $\boxed{66}$.

\textbf{(c)} The four corner sets $\{3,4,5\}$, $\{3,5,7\}$, $\{4,6,8\}$, $\{6,7,8\}$ are all arithmetic sequences (common differences $1,2,2,1$), as shown by the case analysis above.

Reflect $K$ in $M$ to get $K'$, so $M$ is the midpoint of both $BC$ and $KK'$. Then $BKCK'$ is a parallelogram (its diagonals bisect each other), and opposite angles of a parallelogram are equal:
\[
\angle BK'C=\angle BKC=180^\circ-\angle BAC .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.1,3.6), B=(0,0), C=(4.4,0);
pair M=(B+C)/2;
pair mAB=(A+B)/2, mAC=(A+C)/2;
pair O=extension(mAB,mAB+rotate(90)*(B-A),mAC,mAC+rotate(90)*(C-A));
real R=abs(O-A);
real t=abs(B-M)*abs(C-M)/abs(A-M);
pair Kp=M+t*unit(M-A), K=M+t*unit(A-M);
draw(circle(O,R),gray(0.5));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--Kp,blue);
draw(B--K--C,red); draw(B--Kp--C,red+dashed);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(M); dot(K); dot(Kp);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,E); label("$M$",M,SE);
label("$K$",K,W); label("$K'$",Kp,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Since $K$ lies on ray $MA$, its reflection $K'$ lies on the ray from $M$ opposite to $A$, so $A$ and $K'$ are on opposite sides of line $BC$. In quadrilateral $ABK'C$ the opposite angles at $A$ and $K'$ add to $180^\circ$, so $A$, $B$, $K'$, $C$ lie on a circle.

The chords $AK'$ and $BC$ of this circle meet at $M$, so by the power of the point $M$,
\[
AM\cdot MK'=BM\cdot MC=\left(\dfrac{\sqrt7}{2}\right)^2=\dfrac{7}{4} .
\]
As $MK'=MK$,
\[
AM\cdot MK=\boxed{\dfrac{7}{4}}.
\]
(The poster's guess with $\angle BAC=90^\circ$ gives the same value, because the product does not depend on the shape of the triangle; in that special case $K=A$.)

The amount of pizza is proportional to its area, and the area of a circle is proportional to the square of its diameter.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw(circle((0,0),6),linewidth(0.9)); draw(circle((17,0),10),linewidth(0.9));
draw((-6,0)--(6,0),blue,Arrows(5)); draw((7,0)--(27,0),blue,Arrows(5));
label("$12$",(0,0),N,blue); label("$20$",(17,0),N,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So the ratio of the amounts is
\[
\dfrac{\pi\cdot10^2}{\pi\cdot 6^2}=\dfrac{100}{36}=\dfrac{25}{9}.
\]
The larger pizza therefore provides $4\cdot\dfrac{25}{9}=\dfrac{100}{9}=11\dfrac{1}{9}$ meals' worth of pizza, which is $\boxed{11}$ full meals (with $\dfrac{1}{9}$ of a meal left over).

question:
The integers $1,2,\dots,2017$ are written on a blackboard. In each step, Ana erases two numbers and writes a new number in their place, until only one number remains.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If the new number is always the sum $a+b$ of the two erased numbers $a,b$, what is the final number?
\item[(b)] If instead the new number is always $ab+a+b$, what is the final number?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Measure angles clockwise from the $12$. The minute hand moves $6^\circ$ per minute, so at $46$ minutes it is at $46\cdot6^\circ=276^\circ$. The hour hand moves $30^\circ$ per hour plus $\dfrac{1}{2}^\circ$ per minute, so at 2:46 it is at
\[
2\cdot30^\circ+46\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}^\circ=60^\circ+23^\circ=83^\circ.
\]
The angle between the hands is $276^\circ-83^\circ=193^\circ$ one way round and $360^\circ-193^\circ=167^\circ$ the other way.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
draw(circle(O,1),linewidth(0.9));
for(int i=0;i<12;++i){pair d=dir(90-30*i); draw(0.9*d--d);}
label("$12$",0.75*dir(90)); label("$3$",0.75*dir(0)); label("$6$",0.75*dir(-90)); label("$9$",0.75*dir(180));
draw(O--0.55*dir(7),linewidth(1.6)); draw(O--0.85*dir(174),linewidth(1));
draw(arc(O,0.25,7,174),red,Arrow(4));
label("$167^\circ$",0.3*dir(95),N,red);
dot(O);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The smaller angle is $\boxed{167^\circ}$.

question:
Each vertex of convex pentagon $ABCDE$ is to be assigned a colour. There are $6$ colours to choose from, and the ends of each diagonal must have different colours. How many different colourings are possible?

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the number of colourings.
\item[(b)] A student argued: ``$A$ has $6$ choices, $B$ has $6$, $C$ has $5$ (only diagonal $AC$ matters), $D$ has $4$ (diagonals $AD$, $BD$), and $E$ has $4$, giving $6\cdot6\cdot5\cdot4\cdot4=2880$.'' Find the error.
\end{enumerate}
$\textbf{(A)}\ 2520\qquad\textbf{(B)}\ 2880\qquad\textbf{(C)}\ 3120\qquad\textbf{(D)}\ 3250\qquad\textbf{(E)}\ 3750$

solution:
The five diagonals $AC$, $CE$, $EB$, $BD$, $DA$ form a single closed $5$-cycle $A\to C\to E\to B\to D\to A$, shown in red. So we must count colourings of a $5$-cycle with $6$ colours in which neighbours on the cycle differ.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V; string[] nm={"A","B","C","D","E"};
for(int i=0;i<5;++i){V.push(dir(90+72*i));}
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--cycle,gray(0.6)+linewidth(0.7));
draw(V[0]--V[2]--V[4]--V[1]--V[3]--cycle,red+linewidth(1));
for(int i=0;i<5;++i){dot(V[i]); label("$"+nm[i]+"$",V[i],1.4*dir(90+72*i));}
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} Let $P_n$ be the number of such colourings of an $n$-cycle with $k=6$ colours. Colour a path of $n$ vertices around the cycle: $k(k-1)^{n-1}$ ways with neighbours different. Among these, the colourings whose two ends have the same colour correspond exactly to valid colourings of an $(n-1)$-cycle (merge the two ends). So
\[
P_n=k(k-1)^{n-1}-P_{n-1}.
\]
With $P_3=6\cdot5\cdot4=120$:
\[
P_4=6\cdot5^3-120=630,\qquad P_5=6\cdot5^4-630=3750-630=3120 .
\]
The answer is $\boxed{3120}$, choice $\textbf{(C)}$.

\textbf{(b)} The count for $D$ assumes $A$ and $B$ have different colours. But $AB$ is a side, not a diagonal, so $A$ and $B$ may share a colour, and then $D$ has $5$ choices, not $4$. Similarly the number of choices for $E$ (which must differ from $B$ and $C$) depends on whether $B$ and $C$ are equal and is not always $4$. A fixed product of choice counts is only valid when each count does not depend on the earlier choices; here casework is needed (or the cycle recursion above).

Let the right angle be at $C$, the legs be $BC=a$, $CA=b$, the hypotenuse be $AB=c$, and let the incircle (centre $I$, radius $r$) touch $BC$, $CA$ at $T_1$, $T_2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0), A=(0,3), B=(4,0), I=(1,1);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(I,1),blue);
pair T1=(1,0), T2=(0,1);
pair u=unit(B-A); pair T3=A+dot(I-A,u)*u;
draw(C--T1--I--T2--cycle,red);
draw(I--T3,red+dashed);
draw((0.18,0)--(0.18,0.18)--(0,0.18));
dot(I); dot(T1); dot(T2); dot(T3);
label("$C$",C,SW); label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,E); label("$I$",I,NE);
label("$r$",(0.5,0),S,red); label("$r$",(0,0.5),W,red);
label("$a$",(2.6,0),S); label("$b$",(0,2.1),W); label("$c$",(A+B)/2+(0.1,0.1),NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The radii $IT_1$ and $IT_2$ are perpendicular to the legs, and the angle at $C$ is a right angle, so $CT_1IT_2$ is a rectangle with two adjacent sides equal to $r$; hence it is a square and $CT_1=CT_2=r$.

On the other hand, the two tangent segments from a vertex to the incircle are equal. If the tangent lengths from $A$, $B$, $C$ are $x$, $y$, $z$, then $c=x+y$, $a=y+z$, $b=z+x$, so $2s=2(x+y+z)$ and $z=s-c$. Thus
\[
r=CT_1=s-c<s,
\]
since $c>0$. Hence $s>r$.

(Alternatively, for any triangle, area $=rs$; since the area of this triangle is $\dfrac{ab}{2}$ and $s>a$, $s>b$, we get $r=\dfrac{ab}{2s}<\dfrac{s^2}{2s}<s$.)

Let $\Gamma$ be the circumcircle and $\ell$ the line $AO$. Let $\sigma$ be the reflection in $\ell$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0); real R=2;
pair A=R*dir(100), B=R*dir(215), C=R*dir(-15);
draw(circle(O,R),gray(0.5));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
pair Ap=-A;
draw((1.25*A)--(1.15*Ap),blue+dashed);
dot(O); dot(A); dot(B); dot(C);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$O$",O,E);
label("$\ell$",1.15*Ap,S,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\begin{itemize}
\item Since $\ell$ passes through the centre $O$, $\sigma$ maps $\Gamma$ onto itself.
\item Since $\ell$ bisects the angle between the lines $AB$ and $AC$ and passes through their common point $A$, $\sigma$ maps line $AB$ onto line $AC$. (Lines $AB$ and $AC$ are different from $\ell$, since otherwise $A,B,C$ would be collinear.)
\end{itemize}
Line $AB$ meets $\Gamma$ in exactly two points, $A$ and $B$. Its image, line $AC$, meets $\Gamma$ in $A$ and $C$. As $\sigma$ fixes $A$ and maps $\Gamma\cap AB$ onto $\Gamma\cap AC$, it must map $B$ to $C$. Reflections preserve distances and fix $A$, so
\[
AB=\sigma(A)\sigma(B)=AC .
\]

Let $\omega_1$ be the circle through $A,B,C$ and $\omega_2$ the circle through $A,B,D$.

\textbf{Step 1: line $CD$ is tangent to $\omega_1$ at $C$ and to $\omega_2$ at $D$.} Let $t$ be the tangent to $\omega_1$ at $C$, and let $T$ be a point of $t$ on the opposite side of line $AC$ from $B$. By the tangent-chord (alternate segment) theorem, $\angle ACT=\angle ABC$. The point $D$ is also on the opposite side of line $AC$ from $B$, and $\angle ACD=\angle ABC=\angle ACT$, so rays $CD$ and $CT$ coincide. Hence line $CD$ is the tangent $t$. In the same way (using that $B$ and $C$ are on opposite sides of $AD$, and $\angle ADC=\angle ABD$), line $CD$ is tangent to $\omega_2$ at $D$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair M=(0,0), C=(-2,0), D=(2,0);
pair A=1.25*dir(68), B=3.2*dir(68);
pair c1=extension((A+B)/2,(A+B)/2+rotate(90)*(B-A),C,C+(0,1));
pair c2=extension((A+B)/2,(A+B)/2+rotate(90)*(B-A),D,D+(0,1));
draw(circle(c1,abs(c1-C)),blue); draw(circle(c2,abs(c2-D)),heavygreen);
draw((-3.6,0)--(3.6,0),linewidth(0.9));
draw(M--B,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C--B,gray(0.3)); draw(A--D--B,gray(0.3));
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(M);
label("$A$",A,E); label("$B$",B,NE); label("$C$",C,S); label("$D$",D,S); label("$M$",M,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 2: the radical axis.} The circles are different: $\omega_1$ touches line $CD$ only at $C$, and $\omega_2$ only at $D\ne C$. Two different circles meeting at $A$ and $B$ have the line $AB$ as their radical axis, the set of points having equal power with respect to both circles.

Let $M$ be the midpoint of $CD$. Since $MC$ is a tangent segment to $\omega_1$ and $MD$ is a tangent segment to $\omega_2$, the powers of $M$ are
\[
\operatorname{pow}_{\omega_1}(M)=MC^2=MD^2=\operatorname{pow}_{\omega_2}(M).
\]
Therefore $M$ lies on the radical axis $AB$, that is, line $AB$ bisects $\overline{CD}$.

By the Pythagorean theorem $a^2+b^2=c^2$, so $2c^2=288$, $c^2=144$ and $c=12$. Then $a+b=28-12=16$ and $a^2+b^2=144$, so
\[
2ab=(a+b)^2-\left(a^2+b^2\right)=256-144=112,\qquad ab=56 .
\]
The legs are the roots of $t^2-16t+56=0$, namely $8\pm2\sqrt2$, which are positive, so such a triangle exists.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=8+2*sqrt(2), bb=8-2*sqrt(2);
pair C=(0,0), B=(a,0), A=(0,bb);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0.5,0)--(0.5,0.5)--(0,0.5));
label("$C$",C,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$A$",A,N);
label("$a$",(a/2,0),S); label("$b$",(0,bb/2),W); label("$c=12$",(A+B)/2,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Its area is
\[
\dfrac{ab}{2}=\boxed{28}.
\]

Let a chord through $P$ be at distance $h$ from $O$; then its length is $2\sqrt{100-h^2}$. Since the chord passes through $P$, $0\le h\le OP=6$.

