The space diagonal $d$ of a rectangular prism with edges $\ell,w,h$ satisfies $d^2=\ell^2+w^2+h^2$ (apply the Pythagorean theorem to a face diagonal and then to the vertical edge). Hence
\[d=\sqrt{8^2+6^2+24^2}=\sqrt{64+36+576}=\sqrt{676}=\boxed{26}.\]

\textbf{First computation.} The ray $OA$ makes angle $\alpha$ above the positive $x$-axis and $OB$ makes angle $\beta$ below it, so $\angle AOB=\alpha+\beta$, which lies strictly between $0^\circ$ and $180^\circ$. With $OA=OB=1$,
\[[AOB]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot1\cdot1\cdot\sin(\alpha+\beta)=\dfrac{\sin(\alpha+\beta)}{2}.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real al=50, be=35;
pair O=(0,0), A=dir(al), B=dir(-be);
pair X=extension(A,B,(0,0),(1,0));
draw(unitcircle,gray);
draw((-1.2,0)--(1.25,0),gray,Arrow(4));
fill(O--A--X--cycle,paleblue); fill(O--X--B--cycle,palegreen);
draw(O--A--B--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--(A.x,0),dashed); draw(B--(B.x,0),dashed);
draw(arc(O,0.25,0,al)); draw(arc(O,0.32,-be,0));
label("$\alpha$",0.25*dir(al/2),dir(al/2)*1.3);
label("$\beta$",0.32*dir(-be/2),dir(-be/2)*1.3);
dot(O);dot(A);dot(B);dot(X);
label("$O$",O,W); label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,S); label("$X$",X,NE);
label("$\sin\alpha$",(A.x,A.y/2),E,fontsize(8pt));
label("$\sin\beta$",(B.x,B.y/2),E,fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Locating $X$.} Since $A$ is above the $x$-axis ($\sin\alpha>0$) and $B$ is below it ($-\sin\beta<0$), segment $AB$ crosses the $x$-axis at exactly one point $X$. Write $X=A+t(B-A)$; its $y$-coordinate is $\sin\alpha-t(\sin\alpha+\sin\beta)=0$, so $t=\dfrac{\sin\alpha}{\sin\alpha+\sin\beta}\in(0,1)$. Its $x$-coordinate is
\[\cos\alpha+t(\cos\beta-\cos\alpha)=\dfrac{\cos\alpha(\sin\alpha+\sin\beta)+\sin\alpha(\cos\beta-\cos\alpha)}{\sin\alpha+\sin\beta}=\dfrac{\sin\alpha\cos\beta+\cos\alpha\sin\beta}{\sin\alpha+\sin\beta}.\]
Since $\cos\alpha,\cos\beta>0$ and $\sin\alpha,\sin\beta>0$, this is positive, so $OX$ equals this $x$-coordinate.

\textbf{Second computation.} Because $X$ lies on segment $AB$, the segment $OX$ splits triangle $AOB$ into triangles $AOX$ and $BOX$ with common base $OX$ on the $x$-axis. Their heights are the distances from $A$ and $B$ to the $x$-axis, namely $\sin\alpha$ and $\sin\beta$. Hence
\[[AOB]=[AOX]+[BOX]=\dfrac{1}{2}\,OX\,(\sin\alpha+\sin\beta)=\dfrac{1}{2}\left(\sin\alpha\cos\beta+\cos\alpha\sin\beta\right).\]

\textbf{Conclusion.} Equating the two expressions for $[AOB]$ and multiplying by $2$,
\[\sin(\alpha+\beta)=\sin\alpha\cos\beta+\cos\alpha\sin\beta\qquad\text{for }0^\circ<\alpha,\beta<90^\circ.\qquad\blacksquare\]

Set up coordinates with the midpoint of $BC$ at the origin and $BC$ on the $x$-axis: $B=(-b,0)$ and $C=(b,0)$ with $b>0$. The perpendicular bisector $\ell$ of $BC$ is then the $y$-axis, so the midpoint $M$ of $AD$ is $M=(0,m)$ for some $m$. Write $A=(p,q)$; since $M$ is the midpoint of $AD$, $D=2M-A=(-p,2m-q)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(-3,0), C=(3,0);
pair A=(-1.8,2.6), M=(0,2.6);
pair D=2*M-A;
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw((0,-0.3)--(0,4.2),dashed);
draw(A--C,blue); draw(B--D,blue);
draw(A--D,gray);
label("$\ell$",(0,4.2),N);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(M);dot((0,0));
label("$A(p,q)$",A,W); label("$B(-b,0)$",B,SW); label("$C(b,0)$",C,SE);
label("$D(-p,2m-q)$",D,E); label("$M(0,m)$",M+(0.08,0.12),NE);
label("$O$",(0,0),SE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

By the distance formula,
\begin{align*}
AC^2&=(p-b)^2+q^2, & BD^2&=(-p+b)^2+(2m-q)^2=(p-b)^2+(2m-q)^2,\\
AB^2&=(p+b)^2+q^2, & CD^2&=(-p-b)^2+(2m-q)^2=(p+b)^2+(2m-q)^2.
\end{align*}
Subtracting,
\[AC^2-BD^2=q^2-(2m-q)^2=AB^2-CD^2.\]
Since $AC=BD$, the left side is $0$, hence $AB^2=CD^2$ and, as lengths are positive, $AB=CD$. $\blacksquare$

(The identity shows more: under the midpoint condition, $AC=BD$ and $AB=CD$ are equivalent. Convexity is not needed for the argument.)

\textbf{Congruent triangles.} Compare triangles $ABE$ and $CAD$:
\[AB=CA,\qquad \angle ABE=\angle CAD=60^\circ,\qquad BE=AD.\]
By SAS they are congruent, with $A\leftrightarrow C$, $B\leftrightarrow A$, $E\leftrightarrow D$. In particular $\angle BAE=\angle ACD$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(6,0), C=rotate(60)*(6,0);
real t=2.2;
pair D=(t,0);
pair Ept=B+t*unit(C-B);
pair F=extension(A,Ept,C,D);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--Ept,blue); draw(C--D,red);
void tick(pair P, pair Q){pair m=(P+Q)/2, u=unit(Q-P)*0.18; draw(m-rotate(90)*u--m+rotate(90)*u,linewidth(0.8));}
tick(A,D); tick(B,Ept);
draw(arc(F,0.55,degrees(Ept-F),degrees(C-F)),linewidth(0.7));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(Ept);dot(F);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,SE); label("$C$",C,N);
label("$D$",D,S); label("$E$",Ept,NE); label("$F$",F,W);
label("$60^\circ$",F+0.95*dir((degrees(Ept-F)+degrees(C-F))/2),fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Exterior angle.} The cevians $AE$ and $CD$ meet at an interior point $F$, which lies between $A$ and $E$. So $\angle CFE$ is the exterior angle at $F$ of triangle $AFC$, and
\[\angle CFE=\angle FAC+\angle FCA=\angle EAC+\angle ACD.\]
Replacing $\angle ACD$ by the equal angle $\angle BAE$,
\[\angle CFE=\angle EAC+\angle BAE=\angle BAC=60^\circ.\]
Hence $\angle CFE=\boxed{60^\circ}$, whatever the common length $AD=BE$.

The right triangle has area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot3\cdot4=6$.

Let the second triangle be $PQR$ with $PQ=4$, $\{RP,RQ\}=\{x,y\}$, so $y=\max(RP,RQ)$. Its area is $6$, so the height from $R$ to line $PQ$ is $\dfrac{2\cdot6}{4}=3$: $R$ lies on a line parallel to $PQ$ at distance $3$.

