\textbf{No path is shorter.} Each face of the cube contains exactly one of $A$ and $B$. The three faces at $A$ meet the three faces at $B$ along the six edges that touch neither $A$ nor $B$; any continuous path on the surface from $A$ to $B$ must pass through a point $P$ of one of these edges. A path through $P$ has length at least $AP+PB$, measured as straight-line distances. By symmetry take the edge from $(10,0,0)$ to $(10,0,10)$, so $P=(10,0,u)$ with $0\le u\le10$. Then
\[AP+PB=\sqrt{100+u^2}+\sqrt{100+(10-u)^2}.\]
This is the length of a path from $(0,0)$ to $(20,10)$ through $(10,u)$ in a plane, which is at least the straight distance $\sqrt{20^2+10^2}=10\sqrt5$.

So the shortest path has length $\boxed{10\sqrt5}$ feet (about $22.4$ feet). The guess $10\sqrt2+10$ (a face diagonal followed by an edge) is longer, about $24.1$ feet, because it bends at a corner.

\textbf{Upper bound.} The pallet has area $40\cdot48=1920$ square inches and each case covers $9.5\cdot14.5=137.75$ square inches. Non-overlapping cases inside the pallet have total area at most $1920$, and
\[14\cdot137.75=1928.5>1920,\]
so at most $13$ cases fit, however they are placed.

\textbf{Thirteen cases fit.} Take the pallet $40$ inches wide and $48$ inches tall.
\begin{itemize}
\item In the left strip of width $2\cdot14.5=29$, place cases with the $14.5$-inch side horizontal: $2$ per row and $5$ rows of height $9.5$, using height $47.5\le48$. That is $10$ cases.
\item The remaining strip has width $40-29=11\ge9.5$. Place cases with the $9.5$-inch side horizontal, stacked $3$ high: height $3\cdot14.5=43.5\le48$. That is $3$ more cases.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(40,0)--(40,48)--(0,48)--cycle,linewidth(1));
for(int r=0;r<5;++r) for(int c=0;c<2;++c){
  pair p=(14.5*c,9.5*r);
  filldraw(p--p+(14.5,0)--p+(14.5,9.5)--p+(0,9.5)--cycle,paleblue,linewidth(0.6));
}
for(int r=0;r<3;++r){
  pair p=(29,14.5*r);
  filldraw(p--p+(9.5,0)--p+(9.5,14.5)--p+(0,14.5)--cycle,palegreen,linewidth(0.6));
}
label("$40$",(20,0),S); label("$48$",(40,24),E);
label("$14.5$",(7.25,0),N,fontsize(7pt)); label("$9.5$",(0,4.75),E,fontsize(7pt));
label("$9.5$",(33.75,0),N,fontsize(7pt)); label("$14.5$",(38.5,7.25),W,fontsize(7pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So $13$ cases fit, and the maximum is $\boxed{13}$. (Placing all cases in the same orientation gives only $12$, for example $4\cdot3$ with the $9.5$-inch side along the $40$-inch edge, which is why mixing orientations is needed.)

Both sides are defined exactly when $\sin\theta\ne0$ and $\cos\theta\ne0$, that is, when $\theta$ is not an integer multiple of $90^\circ$.

For such $\theta$, write everything in terms of sine and cosine and use the Pythagorean identity $\sin^2\theta+\cos^2\theta=1$, which for an acute angle is the Pythagorean theorem in a right triangle with hypotenuse $1$ and legs $\cos\theta$ and $\sin\theta$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real th=35;
pair O=(0,0), H=(Cos(th),0), P=(Cos(th),Sin(th));
draw(O--H--P--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(H+(-0.06,0)--H+(-0.06,0.06)--H+(0,0.06));
draw(arc(O,0.2,0,th));
label("$\theta$",0.2*dir(th/2),dir(th/2)*2);
label("$1$",(O+P)/2,NW);
label("$\cos\theta$",(O+H)/2,S);
label("$\sin\theta$",(H+P)/2,E);
dot(O);dot(H);dot(P);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\[\csc^2\theta+\sec^2\theta=\dfrac{1}{\sin^2\theta}+\dfrac{1}{\cos^2\theta}=\dfrac{\cos^2\theta+\sin^2\theta}{\sin^2\theta\cos^2\theta}=\dfrac{1}{\sin^2\theta\cos^2\theta}=\csc^2\theta\sec^2\theta.\]
So the identity holds for every $\theta$ that is not a multiple of $90^\circ$. $\blacksquare$

(Equivalently, it says $\dfrac{1}{u}+\dfrac{1}{v}=\dfrac{1}{uv}$ whenever $u+v=1$, with $u=\sin^2\theta$ and $v=\cos^2\theta$.)

\textbf{Area.} With $\vec{AB}=(2,2)$ and $\vec{AC}=(7,14)$,
\[[ABC]=\dfrac{1}{2}\left|2\cdot14-2\cdot7\right|=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot14=7.\]

\textbf{Centroid.} $G=\left(\dfrac{1+3+8}{3},\dfrac{2+4+16}{3}\right)=\left(4,\dfrac{22}{3}\right)$, at distance $4$ from the $y$-axis.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
pair A=(1,2), B=(3,4), C=(8,16);
pair G=(A+B+C)/3;
draw((-9,0)--(9.5,0),gray,Arrow(4)); draw((0,-0.5)--(0,17.5),gray,Arrow(4));
label("$x$",(9.5,0),E); label("$y$",(0,17.5),N);
fill(A--B--C--cycle,paleblue);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
pair rA=(-1,2), rB=(-3,4), rC=(-8,16);
draw(rA--rB--rC--cycle,dashed+gray);
draw(G--(-G.x,G.y),dotted);
draw(shift(0,G.y)*xscale(G.x)*yscale(0.9)*unitcircle,red+dashed);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(G,red);
label("$A(1,2)$",A,SE); label("$B(3,4)$",B,E); label("$C(8,16)$",C,E);
label("$G\left(4,\dfrac{22}{3}\right)$",(4.9,5.6),E,red); draw((4.9,5.6)--G,red+linewidth(0.4));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Pappus's theorem.} The whole triangle lies on one side of the $y$-axis (all its points have $x\ge1$), so the volume of the solid of revolution equals the area times the distance travelled by the centroid:
\[V=2\pi\cdot4\cdot7=\boxed{56\pi}.\]

\textbf{Check by cylindrical shells.} The lines are $AB:\ y=x+1$, $AC:\ y=2x$ and $BC:\ y=\dfrac{12x-16}{5}$. For $1\le x\le3$ the triangle's vertical extent is $2x-(x+1)=x-1$; for $3\le x\le8$ it is $2x-\dfrac{12x-16}{5}=\dfrac{16-2x}{5}$. Then
\[V=2\pi\left(\int_1^3x(x-1)\,dx+\int_3^8\dfrac{x(16-2x)}{5}\,dx\right)=2\pi\left(\dfrac{14}{3}+\dfrac{70}{3}\right)=56\pi.\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real x=8, y=15;
draw((0,0)--(x,0)--(x,x)--(0,x)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw((11,0)--(11+y,0)--(11+y,y)--(11,y)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
label("$A$",(x/2,x/2)); label("$B$",(11+y/2,y/2));
label("$x$",(x/2,0),S); label("$y$",(11+y/2,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The perimeter condition gives $4y-4x=28$, so $y-x=7$. The area condition gives $y^2-x^2=161$. Factoring the difference of squares,
\[(y-x)(y+x)=161\quad\Longrightarrow\quad 7(x+y)=161\quad\Longrightarrow\quad x+y=\boxed{23}.\]
(Indeed $x=8$ and $y=15$: perimeters $32$ and $60$, areas $64$ and $225$.)

Write $r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, so $r^2=x^2+y^2$. Pair the first and fourth factors and the second and third factors under the root:
\[
\dfrac{(x+y+r)(x+y-r)}{4}=\dfrac{(x+y)^2-r^2}{4}=\dfrac{2xy}{4}=\dfrac{xy}{2},
\]
\[
\dfrac{(r+(x-y))(r-(x-y))}{4}=\dfrac{r^2-(x-y)^2}{4}=\dfrac{2xy}{4}=\dfrac{xy}{2}.
\]
So the product under the square root is $\left(\dfrac{xy}{2}\right)^2\ge0$; the right side is defined for every point and equals $\dfrac{|xy|}{2}$. (For $x,y>0$ this is Heron's formula for the right triangle with legs $x,y$.) The equation becomes
\[
\dfrac{xy}{2}=\dfrac{|xy|}{2}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad xy\ge0 .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(0,3)--cycle,palegray);
fill((0,0)--(-3,0)--(-3,-3)--(0,-3)--cycle,palegray);
draw((-3.3,0)--(3.3,0),linewidth(1.1),Arrows(TeXHead));
draw((0,-3.3)--(0,3.3),linewidth(1.1),Arrows(TeXHead));
label("$x$",(3.3,0),E);label("$y$",(0,3.3),N);
label("$xy\ge 0$",(1.5,1.5));label("$xy\ge 0$",(-1.5,-1.5));
label("$xy<0$",(-1.5,1.5),gray(0.4));label("$xy<0$",(1.5,-1.5),gray(0.4));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Therefore the graph is $\boxed{\{(x,y): xy\ge0\}}$: the closed first and third quadrants, including both coordinate axes (shaded above).

Cutting a triangle into $100$ congruent small triangles by lines parallel to the sides means each side is divided into $n$ equal parts with $n^2=100$, so $n=10$. Rows from the top contain $1,3,5,\dots,19$ cells. In each row the cells alternate between \emph{upward} triangles (pointing the same way as the board) and \emph{downward} ones, and the row with $2k-1$ cells has $k$ upward and $k-1$ downward cells. Hence there are
\[
1+2+\cdots+10=55 \text{ upward cells and } 0+1+\cdots+9=45 \text{ downward cells.}
\]
(The figure shows the pattern on a smaller board with $n=4$.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=sqrt(3)/2;
int n=4;
for(int i=0;i<n;++i) for(int j=0;j<n-i;++j){
  pair p=(j+i/2,i*h);
  filldraw(p--p+(1,0)--p+(0.5,h)--cycle,white,black);
  if(j<n-i-1) filldraw(p+(1,0)--p+(0.5,h)--p+(1.5,h)--cycle,gray(0.6),black);
}
label("upward (white)",(4.6,2.6),E);label("downward (gray)",(4.6,2.1),E);
filldraw((4.25,2.5)--(4.55,2.5)--(4.4,2.75)--cycle,white,black);
filldraw((4.25,2.2)--(4.55,2.2)--(4.4,1.95)--cycle,gray(0.6),black);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Two neighbors share a side, and every side of an upward cell inside the board is a side of a downward cell, and vice versa. So each hop takes a grasshopper from an upward cell to a downward cell or from a downward cell to an upward cell. After $9$ hops (an odd number), each grasshopper is on a cell of the opposite type from its starting cell.

In particular, the grasshoppers now sitting on upward cells are exactly those that started on downward cells, and there are only $45$ of them. Thus at most $45$ of the $55$ upward cells are occupied, so at least $55-45=10$ upward cells are empty. $\blacksquare$

question:
Numerals can be written in a negative base just as in a positive base. In base $-2$ the digits are $0$ and $1$, and the numeral $d_kd_{k-1}\dots d_1d_0$ means $d_k(-2)^k+\cdots+d_1(-2)+d_0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What integer is represented by $110110$ in base $-2$?
\item[(b)] Write $2020$ in base $-2$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Translate triangle $APD$ by the vector $\overrightarrow{AB}$. Since $\overrightarrow{AB}=\overrightarrow{DC}$, this sends $A\to B$, $D\to C$ and $P\to Q$, where $Q=P+\overrightarrow{AB}$. Then triangle $BQC$ is congruent to triangle $APD$, so
\[
\angle BCQ=\angle ADP,\qquad \angle CBQ=\angle DAP,
\]
and $PQ$ is parallel and equal to $AB$ (and $DC$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),D=4*dir(80),P=2.5*dir(50);
real s=(D.y-P.y)/0.5;pair C=P+s*dir(30),B=C-D;
pair Q=P+(B-A);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--P--D);draw(B--P--C);
draw(B--Q--C,dashed+blue);draw(P--Q,dashed+blue);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(P);dot(Q,blue);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$P$",P,S);label("$Q$",Q,E,blue);
draw(arc(C,0.8,180,210));label("$30^\circ$",C+0.8*dir(195),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Because $PQ\parallel AB$ and $ABQP$ is a parallelogram, $\angle BPQ=\angle ABP$ (alternate angles). With the hypothesis,
\[
\angle BCQ=\angle ADP=\angle ABP=\angle BPQ .
\]
The points $P$ and $C$ lie on the same side of line $BQ$: line $BQ$ is line $AP$ shifted by $\overrightarrow{AB}$, the line $AP$ through the interior point $P$ separates $B$ from $D$, and $C=D+\overrightarrow{AB}$, so $C$ is on the same side of $BQ$ as line $AP$ (and $P$). Hence $P$ and $C$ see segment $BQ$ under equal angles from the same side, so $B,Q,C,P$ are concyclic.

In this circle, the angles $\angle CBQ$ and $\angle CPQ$ subtend the same chord $CQ$ from the same arc, so $\angle CBQ=\angle CPQ$. Finally $PQ\parallel DC$ gives $\angle CPQ=\angle DCP=30^\circ$ (alternate angles). Therefore
\[
\angle DAP=\angle CBQ=\angle CPQ=\angle DCP=\boxed{30^\circ}.
\]

Since $A$ and $B$ both lie on the first circle, $O_1A=O_1B$; so $O_1$ is equidistant from $A$ and $B$ and lies on the perpendicular bisector of segment $AB$. In the same way $O_2A=O_2B$, so $O_2$ also lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O1=(0,0),O2=(3.2,0);real r1=2.2,r2=1.9;
real xa=(r1^2-r2^2+3.2^2)/(2*3.2);real ya=sqrt(r1^2-xa^2);
pair A=(xa,ya),B=(xa,-ya),M=(xa,0);
draw(circle(O1,r1));draw(circle(O2,r2));
draw(A--B,linewidth(1));draw(O1--O2,linewidth(1));
draw(O1--A,dashed);draw(O1--B,dashed);draw(O2--A,dashed);draw(O2--B,dashed);
draw(M+(0.18,0)--M+(0.18,0.18)--M+(0,0.18));
dot(O1);dot(O2);dot(A);dot(B);dot(M);
label("$O_1$",O1,W);label("$O_2$",O2,E);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,S);label("$M$",M+(0.3,-0.28));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The perpendicular bisector of $AB$ is a single line, and it contains the two distinct points $O_1$ and $O_2$. Hence the line $O_1O_2$ \emph{is} the perpendicular bisector of $AB$: it meets $AB$ at its midpoint $M$ at a right angle. Therefore $AB\perp O_1O_2$ and $O_1O_2$ bisects $AB$. $\blacksquare$

(Equivalently, triangles $O_1AO_2$ and $O_1BO_2$ are congruent by SSS, so $O_1O_2$ bisects angle $AO_1B$ of the isosceles triangle $AO_1B$, and the angle bisector from the apex of an isosceles triangle is perpendicular to the base and bisects it.)

question:
A $3\times5$ rectangle is divided into $15$ unit squares by grid lines.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many rectangles (of any size, including the unit squares and the whole rectangle) have all four sides along the grid lines?
\item[(b)] More generally, how many such rectangles are there in an $m\times n$ grid of unit squares?
\end{enumerate}

Find the six-digit number with all of these properties, and show that it is the only one:
\begin{itemize}
\item no digit is repeated;
\item the ones digit is a prime number;
\item the ones digit is the sum of the tens and hundreds digits;
\item the thousands digit is the sum of the hundreds and ten-thousands digits;
\item the hundred-thousands digit is neither prime nor composite;
\item the sum of the digits is $21$.
\end{itemize}
In particular, explain why the ones digit cannot be $2$, even though $2=0+2$.

solution:
Let $AB=c$ and $AC=b$, and by symmetry suppose $c\le b$. From the area, $bc=70$. From the perimeter, $\sqrt{b^2+c^2}=30-(b+c)$; squaring, $(b+c)^2-140=900-60(b+c)+(b+c)^2$, so $b+c=\dfrac{52}{3}$. Thus $b,c$ are the roots of $t^2-\dfrac{52}{3}t+70=0$:
\[
c=\dfrac{52-\sqrt{184}}{6}=\dfrac{26-\sqrt{46}}{3},\qquad b=\dfrac{26+\sqrt{46}}{3}
\]
(both positive, and $30-(b+c)=\dfrac{38}{3}>0$, so the triangle exists).

Place $A$ at the origin, $B=(c,0)$ and $C=(0,b)$. The bisector of angle $A$ is the line $y=x$, so $B'=(0,c)$ and $C'=(b,0)$. Triangle $ABC$ is $\{x,y\ge0,\ \dfrac {x}{c}+\dfrac {y}{b}\le1\}$ and triangle $AB'C'$ is $\{x,y\ge0,\ \dfrac {x}{b}+\dfrac {y}{c}\le1\}$. Since $c\le b$, their intersection is the quadrilateral $ABPB'$, where $P$ is the common point of $BC$ and $B'C'$ on the line $y=x$:
\[
P=(p,p),\qquad p\left(\dfrac{1}{b}+\dfrac{1}{c}\right)=1,\qquad p=\dfrac{bc}{b+c}=\dfrac{70}{\dfrac{52}{3}}=\dfrac{105}{26}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real c=(52-sqrt(184))/6, bb=(52+sqrt(184))/6;
pair A=(0,0),B=(c,0),C=(0,bb),B1=(0,c),C1=(bb,0);
real xp=bb*c/(bb+c);pair P=(xp,xp);
fill(A--B--P--B1--cycle,palegray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(A--B1--C1--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--(8,8),dashed);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(B1);dot(C1);dot(P);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,W);label("$B'$",B1,W);label("$C'$",C1,S);label("$P$",P,2*W);
label("$y=x$",(8,8),NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Split $ABPB'$ along $AP$ into triangles $ABP$ and $AB'P$, each with base $c$ on an axis and height $p$:
\[
[ABPB']=2\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}\,c\,p=cp=\dfrac{26-\sqrt{46}}{3}\cdot\dfrac{105}{26}=\dfrac{35\left(26-\sqrt{46}\right)}{26}.
\]
Since $\sqrt{46}<7$, this is greater than $\dfrac{35\cdot19}{26}=\dfrac{665}{26}>25>16$. Hence the intersection has area greater than $16$ (in fact about $25.9$). $\blacksquare$

If the sides are $s$ and $t$, then $s^2:t^2=25:36$, so $s:t=5:6$ (sides are positive). Write $s=5k$, $t=6k$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,5)--(0,5)--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((7,0)--(13,0)--(13,6)--(7,6)--cycle,linewidth(1));
label("$5k$",(2.5,0),S);label("$6k$",(10,0),S);
label("area $25k^2$",(2.5,2.5));label("area $36k^2$",(10,3));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The perimeters are $4s=20k$ and $4t=24k$, so their ratio is $\boxed{5:6}$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $\sqrt{x^{10}}=|x|^5$ for every real number $x$.
\item[(b)] Find all real $x$ for which $\sqrt{x^{10}}=x^5$, and give a specific value of $x$ for which $\sqrt{x^{10}}\ne x^5$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $p(x)=(x+i)^2$, where $i^2=-1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Write $p(x)$ in the form $ax^2+bx+c$. Is $p$ a quadratic polynomial? Does it have real coefficients?
\item[(b)] Find all complex roots of $p$, with multiplicity.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Both quadrilaterals are trapezoids with the parallel vertical sides on the lines $x=0$ and $x=4$, at distance $4$ apart.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),A=(0,3),B=(4,4),D=(4,0),C=(4,8.5);
fill(O--A--B--D--cycle,palegray);
draw(O--A--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));draw(A--B,dashed);
draw((-0.8,0)--(5.5,0),gray,Arrow(TeXHead));draw((0,-0.8)--(0,9.5),gray,Arrow(TeXHead));
dot(O);dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);
label("$O(0,0)$",O,SW);label("$A(0,3)$",A,W);label("$B(4,4)$",B,E);label("$D(4,0)$",D,SE);label("$C(4,y)$",C,E);
