Let the break points be $u$ and $v$, a uniform random point of the unit square. Three pieces of total length $1$ form a triangle exactly when each piece is shorter than $\dfrac{1}{2}$ (each piece must be less than the sum of the other two, which is $1$ minus itself).

If $u<v$, the pieces are $u$, $v-u$ and $1-v$, and the conditions are
\[
u<\dfrac{1}{2},\qquad v>\dfrac{1}{2},\qquad v-u<\dfrac{1}{2} .
\]
In the triangle $u<v$ (area $\dfrac{1}{2}$), this is the triangle with vertices $\left(0,\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$, $\left(\dfrac{1}{2},\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$, $\left(\dfrac{1}{2},1\right)$, of area $\dfrac{1}{8}$. By symmetry the case $v<u$ gives another triangle of area $\dfrac{1}{8}$, and $u=v$ has probability $0$.

