An exponential function is $f(x)=a\cdot b^x$ with $a\ne0$, $b>0$, $b\ne1$, defined for all real $x$. This exercise shows why a negative base is not allowed.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Let $b<0$. Prove that there is no real number $r$ with $r^2=b$, so $b^{\dfrac{1}{2}}$ cannot be defined as a real number.
\item[(b)] Take $b=-8$ and interpret $b^{\dfrac {p}{q}}$ (with $q$ odd) as $\left(\sqrt[q]{b}\right)^p$. Compute $b^{\dfrac{1}{3}}$ and $b^{\dfrac{2}{3}}$, and explain why $x\mapsto(-8)^x$ cannot be a real-valued function on all of $\mathbb R$.
\end{enumerate}

A sector of a circle of radius $r$ with central angle $\theta^\circ$ (where $0<\theta<360$) is cut out, and its two straight edges are joined to form the curved surface of a cone without a base. Find, in terms of $r$ and $\theta$,
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] the radius $\rho$ of the base of the cone;
\item[(b)] the area of the curved (lateral) surface of the cone;
\item[(c)] the total surface area of the cone once a circular base is added.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
When the sector is rolled up, its radius becomes the slant height of the cone and its arc becomes the circumference of the base.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=2.2, a=200;
pair O=(0,0);
filldraw(O--arc(O,r,90-a/2,90+a/2)--cycle,paleblue,black);
draw(arc(O,0.55,90-a/2,90+a/2));
label("$\theta$",(0,0.75));
label("$r$",(r*dir(90+a/2))/2,SW);
label("sector",(0,-1.1));
real rb=r*a/360, h=sqrt(r^2-rb^2);
pair A=(5,h-0.4), c=(5,-0.4);
draw(shift(c)*xscale(rb)*yscale(0.4)*arc((0,0),1,180,360));
draw(shift(c)*xscale(rb)*yscale(0.4)*arc((0,0),1,0,180),dashed);
draw(A--(c+(-rb,0))); draw(A--(c+(rb,0)));
draw(c--(c+(rb,0)),dashed); dot(c);
pair d1=c+(0,-0.4*rb-0.3), d2=d1+(rb,0);
draw(d1--d2,Arrows(TeXHead)); draw(c--d1,dotted); draw((c+(rb,0))--d2,dotted); label("$\rho$",(d1+d2)/2,S);
label("$r$",(A+c+(rb,0))/2,E);
label("cone",(5,-2.25));
draw((2.6,0.9)--(3.6,0.9),Arrow);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} The arc of the sector has length $\dfrac{\theta}{360}\cdot2\pi r$. It equals the base circumference $2\pi\rho$, so
\[
\rho=\boxed{\dfrac{\theta r}{360}}.
\]
\textbf{(b)} Rolling does not stretch the paper, so the lateral surface has the area of the sector:
\[
\boxed{\dfrac{\theta}{360}\,\pi r^2}.
\]
This agrees with the usual formula $\pi\rho\ell$ with slant height $\ell=r$: $\pi\cdot\dfrac{\theta r}{360}\cdot r=\dfrac{\theta}{360}\pi r^2$.

\textbf{(c)} Adding the base of area $\pi\rho^2$ gives
\[
\boxed{\dfrac{\theta}{360}\,\pi r^2+\pi\left(\dfrac{\theta r}{360}\right)^2}.
\]
For example, a semicircle ($\theta=180$) of radius $r$ gives a cone with base radius $\dfrac {r}{2}$, lateral area $\dfrac{\pi r^2}{2}$ and total area $\dfrac{3\pi r^2}{4}$.

Since $9=9$ or $9=3\cdot3$ are the only ways to write $9$ as a product of integers greater than $1$, either $n=p^8$ or $n=p^2q^2$ for distinct primes $p,q$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $n=p^8$, then $n^3=p^{24}$ has $25$ divisors.
\item If $n=p^2q^2$, then $n^3=p^6q^6$ has $7\cdot7=49$ divisors.
\end{itemize}
Both cases occur (e.g.\ $n=2^8$ and $n=36$), so the sum of the possible values is $25+49=\boxed{74}$.

\textbf{Angles.} The side $A'B'$ is parallel to $AB$ and $A'C'$ is parallel to $AC$, and both pairs lie on the same side (towards the interior). An angle whose sides are parallel to, and point in the same directions as, the sides of another angle is equal to it, so $\angle B'A'C'=\angle BAC$. In the same way $\angle A'B'C'=\angle ABC$ and $\angle A'C'B'=\angle ACB$. Two triangles with equal corresponding angles are similar (AA), so $\triangle A'B'C'\sim\triangle ABC$. $\blacksquare$

\textbf{A sharper description.} Let $I$ be the incentre of $ABC$ and $\varrho$ its inradius. The incentre is at distance $\varrho$ from all three sides, so it is at distance $\varrho-6$ from all three inner sides, and the dilation with centre $I$ and ratio $k=\dfrac{\varrho-6}{\varrho}$ maps each side line of $ABC$ to the parallel line at distance $\varrho-6$ from $I$, that is, to the corresponding inner side. Hence it maps $ABC$ onto $A'B'C'$, which proves the similarity again and shows the ratio is $\dfrac{\varrho-6}{\varrho}$.

\begin{asy}
