A student wants to compute $264^2$ and first writes $264=2^2\cdot3^2\cdot5\cdot7\cdot23$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that this prime factorization is wrong and give the correct one.
\item[(b)] Compute $264^2$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The string $\overline{M_1M_2D_1D_2Y_1Y_2Y_3Y_4}$ is a palindrome exactly when the year, read backwards, gives the month and day: $M_1M_2D_1D_2=Y_4Y_3Y_2Y_1$. So each year has at most one palindromic date, determined by its digits.
\begin{itemize}
\item 2020: $0202$, i.e.\ February 2 (the given date).
\item 2021: reversing gives $1202$, i.e.\ month $12$, day $02$: December 2, 2021, a valid date.
\end{itemize}
So the next palindromic date is $\boxed{\text{December 2, 2021}}$, written $12022021$.

Rewrite the equation as $x-|x|=y-|y|$. The function $g(t)=t-|t|$ equals $0$ for $t\ge0$ and $2t$ for $t<0$; it is $0$ exactly for $t\ge0$ and is one-to-one on the negatives.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $x\ge0$: then $g(y)=0$, so $y\ge0$. This gives $11\cdot11=121$ pairs.
\item If $x<0$: then $g(y)=2x<0$, so $y<0$ and $2y=2x$, i.e.\ $y=x$. This gives $10$ pairs.
\end{itemize}
The total is $121+10=\boxed{131}$.

Set up coordinates with $BD$ on the $x$-axis: $B=(0,0)$, $E=(t,0)$, $F=(2t,0)$, $D=(3t,0)$ for some $t>0$. Since $AF=AD$, the point $A$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $FD$, so $A=\left(\dfrac{5t}{2},\,h\right)$ for some $h\ne0$; let $M=\left(\dfrac{5t}{2},0\right)$ be the midpoint of $FD$.

Since $D$ is the midpoint of $AC$, we get $C=2D-A=\left(\dfrac{7t}{2},\,-h\right)$. Then
\[
AB^2=\left(\dfrac{5t}{2}\right)^2+h^2,\qquad CE^2=\left(\dfrac{7t}{2}-t\right)^2+(-h)^2=\left(\dfrac{5t}{2}\right)^2+h^2 .
\]
So $CE=AB=\boxed{1}$. Geometrically: $A$ is $\dfrac{5t}{2}$ to the right of $B$ and $C$ is $\dfrac{5t}{2}$ to the right of $E$, at the same distance $|h|$ from line $BD$, so $CE$ and $AB$ are hypotenuses of congruent right triangles.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real t=0.3, h=sqrt(1-6.25t^2);
pair B=(0,0), Ep=(t,0), F=(2t,0), D=(3t,0), A=(2.5t,h), C=(3.5t,-h);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--D);
draw(A--F,blue); draw(A--D,blue);
draw(C--Ep,red+dashed+linewidth(1));
dot(Ep);dot(F);dot(D);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,S);
label("$E$",Ep,(0,1.2));label("$F$",F,(-0.6,-1.3));label("$D$",D,E);
pair M=(2.5t,0); draw(A--M,dotted); label("$M$",M,(0.3,-1.4));
label("$1$",(A+B)/2,NW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The answer does not depend on $t$: every admissible triangle has $CE=1$.

The circles touch each other at one point, and each touches the line $\ell$ at its own point; that is why there are two tangency points $P$ and $Q$ on $\ell$.

Let $O_1,O_2$ be the centres. Then $O_1P\perp\ell$ with $O_1P=16$, $O_2Q\perp\ell$ with $O_2Q=9$, and since the circles are externally tangent, $O_1O_2=16+9=25$. Let $H$ be the point on $O_1P$ (extended as needed) with $O_2H\parallel\ell$; then $O_1PQO_2$ is a right trapezoid, $O_2H=PQ$ and $O_1H=16-9=7$. By the Pythagorean theorem in right triangle $O_1HO_2$,
\[
PQ=\sqrt{25^2-7^2}=\sqrt{576}=\boxed{24}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real k=0.25;
pair O1=(0,16*k), O2=(24*k,9*k), P=(0,0), Q=(24*k,0);
draw((-1.5,0)--(8,0)); label("$\ell$",(8,0),E);
draw(circle(O1,16*k)); draw(circle(O2,9*k));
draw(O1--P,dashed); draw(O2--Q,dashed); draw(O1--O2);
pair H=(24*k,16*k); draw(O2--H,dotted); draw(O1--H,dotted);
dot(O1);dot(O2);dot(P);dot(Q);
label("$O_1$",O1,W);label("$O_2$",O2,E);label("$P$",P,S);label("$Q$",Q,S);
label("$25$",(O1+O2)/2,SW);label("$7$",(O2+H)/2,E);label("$H$",H,E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(In general $PQ=\sqrt{(r_1+r_2)^2-(r_1-r_2)^2}=2\sqrt{r_1r_2}$.)

\textbf{Using the $2\times2\times2$ block.} Then every edge of the box is at least $2$, and the volume is at most $8+3\cdot2+6=20$. The boxes with all edges at least $2$ and volume at most $20$ are $2\times2\times2$, $2\times2\times3$, $2\times2\times4$, $2\times2\times5$ and $2\times3\times3$, with surface areas $24,32,40,48,42$. So the surface area is at most $48$.

\textbf{Not using it.} Then the volume is $V\le3\cdot2+6=12$. For a box with integer edges $a,b,c\ge1$,
\[
2abc+1-(ab+bc+ca)\ge0 .
\]
Indeed, for $a=1$ the left side is $bc-b-c+1=(b-1)(c-1)\ge0$, and it is linear in $a$ with slope $2bc-b-c\ge0$. Hence the surface area is $2(ab+bc+ca)\le4V+2\le4\cdot12+2=50$.

\textbf{Equality.} Lay all three $1\times1\times2$ blocks and all six unit cubes in a row: this is a $1\times1\times12$ box with surface area $2(1+12+12)=\boxed{50}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair dx=(1,0), dy=(0.35,0.28), dz=(0,1);
for(int i=0;i<12;++i){
  pair p=i*dx;
  draw(p--p+dx--p+dx+dz--p+dz--cycle);
  draw(p+dz--p+dz+dy--p+dx+dz+dy--p+dx+dz);
}
draw(12*dx--12*dx+dy--12*dx+dy+dz--12*dx+dz);
label("$12$",(6,0),S);label("$1$",(0,0.5),W);label("$1$",dz+dy/2,NW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Using all ten blocks gives at most the $2\times2\times5$ box with area $48$; the maximum $50$ needs a box that leaves the big cube out, which the problem allows ("some of them").

It is enough to show that a hemisphere of radius $R$ has volume $\dfrac{2}{3}\pi R^3$.

Compare the hemisphere (flat face down on a table) with the following solid $T$: a cylinder of radius $R$ and height $R$ standing on the table, from which a cone is removed whose apex is the centre of the bottom face and whose base is the top face of the cylinder.

\textbf{Cross-sections at height $y$} ($0\le y\le R$):
\begin{itemize}
\item The hemisphere is cut in a disc of radius $\sqrt{R^2-y^2}$ (Pythagoras), with area $\pi(R^2-y^2)$.
\item The cone has height $R$ and base radius $R$, so at height $y$ its radius is $y$. The section of $T$ is a ring between radii $y$ and $R$, with area $\pi R^2-\pi y^2$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=2;
filldraw(arc((0,0),R,0,180)--cycle,paleblue,black);
real y=1.1, w=sqrt(R^2-y^2);
draw((-w,y)--(w,y),red+linewidth(1.2));
label("$\sqrt{R^2-y^2}$",(-w,y),W,red);
draw((0,0)--(0,y),dashed); label("$y$",(0,y/2),W);
draw((0,0)--(w,y),dotted); label("$R$",(w/2,y/2),SE);
label("hemisphere",(0,-0.45));
pair c=(5.5,0);
filldraw(c+(-R,0)--c+(-R,R)--c+(0,0)--cycle,paleblue,black);
filldraw(c+(R,0)--c+(R,R)--c+(0,0)--cycle,paleblue,black);
draw(c+(-R,R)--c+(R,R));
draw(c+(-R,y)--c+(-y,y),red+linewidth(1.2)); draw(c+(y,y)--c+(R,y),red+linewidth(1.2));
label("$y$",c+(-y/2,y),N); label("$R$",c+(R,R*0.3),E);
label("cylinder minus cone",c+(0,-0.45));
draw((w,y)--c+(-R,y),gray+dotted);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The areas are equal at every height, so by Cavalieri's principle the hemisphere and $T$ have the same volume:
\[
V_{\text{hemisphere}}=\pi R^2\cdot R-\dfrac{1}{3}\pi R^2\cdot R=\dfrac{2}{3}\pi R^3 .
\]
Hence the ball has volume $\boxed{\dfrac{4}{3}\pi R^3}$. $\blacksquare$

(This is Archimedes' argument. The surface area $4\pi R^2$ is then suggested by cutting the ball into many thin pyramids with apex at the centre: their total volume is $\dfrac{1}{3}\cdot(\text{total base area})\cdot R$, which must equal $\dfrac{4}{3}\pi R^3$, giving area $4\pi R^2$; making this precise needs a limit.)

The product ends in $0$ iff it is divisible by $2$ and by $5$. There are $\binom{10}{4}=210$ choices. Count the bad ones:
\begin{itemize}
\item No multiple of $5$ (neither $5$ nor $10$): choose $4$ of the other $8$ numbers, $\binom84=70$ ways.
\item A multiple of $5$ but no even number: then $10$ is excluded, so $5$ is chosen, with $3$ of the odd numbers $1,3,7,9$: $\binom43=4$ ways.
\end{itemize}
These cases do not overlap, so there are $210-70-4=136$ good choices, and the probability is $\dfrac{136}{210}=\boxed{\dfrac{68}{105}}$.

\textbf{Side of the triangle.} Joining $O$ to the vertices splits $ABC$ into three triangles with bases equal to the side $s$ and heights $1,5,12$. Hence
\[
[ABC]=\dfrac {s}{2}(1+5+12)=9s,
\]
and also $[ABC]=\dfrac{s}{2}h$ with $h$ the altitude, so $h=18$ (Viviani's theorem). Then $s=\dfrac{2h}{\sqrt3}=12\sqrt3$ and $[ABC]=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2=108\sqrt3$.

\textbf{Circle and square.} The circumradius of an equilateral triangle is $\dfrac{2}{3}h=12$, so the circle has area $144\pi$. The smallest square containing it has side $24$ and area $576$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s3=sqrt(3);
pair A=(0,12), B=(-6*s3,-6), C=(6*s3,-6), Oc=(0,0);
filldraw(box((-12,-12),(12,12)),palegray,black);
filldraw(circle(Oc,12),white,black);
filldraw(A--B--C--cycle,paleblue,black);
pair O=(-11/s3,-1);
pair ft(pair P, pair U, pair V){ return U+dot(P-U,V-U)/dot(V-U,V-U)*(V-U); }
pair F1=ft(O,A,B), F2=ft(O,B,C), F3=ft(O,A,C);
draw(O--F1,red); draw(O--F2,red); draw(O--F3,red);
dot(O); label("$O$",O,E);
label("$1$",F1,NW,red); label("$5$",(O+F2)/2,E,red); label("$12$",(O+F3)/2,N,red);
label("$A$",A,N); label("$B$",B,SW); label("$C$",C,SE);
draw(Oc--C,dashed); dot(Oc); label("$R=12$",(Oc+C)/2,SW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The required area is $576-108\sqrt3-144\pi$, so $x+y+z+w=576+108+3+144=\boxed{831}$.

If he turned $n$ in the year $n^2$, he was born in $n^2-n$. The statement was made in 1970, so $n^2\le1970$, i.e.\ $n\le44$ (as $45^2=2025$).
\begin{itemize}
\item $n=44$: year $1936$, born $1936-44=1892$, so in 1970 he was $78$. Possible.
\item $n\le43$: born in $43^2-43=1806$ or earlier, so he would have been at least $164$ years old in 1970. Impossible.
\end{itemize}
So he was born in $\boxed{1892}$ (he turned $44$ in $1936=44^2$).

The first circle has radius $R$ equal to half the diagonal, $R=\dfrac{\sqrt{A^2+B^2}}{2}$, and the second has radius $r=\dfrac {A}{2}$ (half the distance between the longer sides).

