label("$36$",(0.9,0),S);label("$36$",(1.8,0.9),E);
label("area $1296$",(0.9,0.9));
draw((2.6,0.84)--(6.15,0.84)--(6.15,0.96)--(2.6,0.96)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
label("$71$",(4.375,0.84),S);label("$1$",(6.15,0.9),E);
label("area $71$",(4.375,0.96),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The greatest possible difference is $1296-71=\boxed{1225}\text{ cm}^2$.

Let the angle of the parallelogram between the sides $13$ and $6$ be $\theta$. By the law of cosines, the two diagonals satisfy
\[
d_1^2=13^2+6^2-2\cdot13\cdot6\cos\theta,\qquad d_2^2=13^2+6^2+2\cdot13\cdot6\cos\theta,
\]
so $d_1^2+d_2^2=2(13^2+6^2)=410$ (the parallelogram law). Each diagonal is a side of a (non-degenerate) triangle with sides $13$ and $6$, so $7<d_1,d_2<19$. Checking $d=8,9,\dots,18$, the number $410-d^2$ is a perfect square only for $d=11$ ($410-121=289=17^2$) and $d=17$. So the diagonals are $11$ and $17$.

These values do occur: $\cos\theta=\dfrac{289-205}{156}=\dfrac{7}{13}$ is a valid cosine.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real c=7/13, s=sqrt(1-c^2);
pair A=(0,0), B=(13,0), D=6*(c,s), C=B+D;
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(A--C,blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(B--D,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$13$",(A+B)/2,S);label("$6$",(A+D)/2,W);
label("$AC=17$",A+0.72*(C-A),NW,blue);label("$BD=11$",B+0.3*(D-B),SW,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The longer diagonal is $\boxed{17}$ inches.

question:
A right prism has height $h$, and its base is a polygon with perimeter $P$ and area $B$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why the lateral area of the prism (the total area of its faces other than the two bases) is $Ph$, so that the total surface area is $Ph+2B$.
\item[(b)] Use this to find the total surface area of a right prism of height $10$ whose base is a right triangle with legs $3$ and $4$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) If the base has sides $s_1,s_2,\dots,s_k$, each lateral face of a right prism is a rectangle with one side $s_i$ and the other side $h$. So the lateral area is
\[
s_1h+s_2h+\dots+s_kh=(s_1+s_2+\dots+s_k)h=Ph .
\]
Equivalently, unrolling the lateral faces gives a single rectangle of width $P$ and height $h$. Adding the two congruent bases gives total surface area $Ph+2B$. So $Ph$ does not replace adding up the faces: it is a shortcut for adding up all the side faces at once.

(b) The base triangle has hypotenuse $5$, so $P=3+4+5=12$ and $B=\dfrac{3\cdot4}{2}=6$. The net below shows the three lateral rectangles forming one $12\times10$ rectangle, together with the two triangular bases.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pen th=linewidth(0.9);
draw((0,0)--(12,0)--(12,10)--(0,10)--cycle,th);
draw((3,0)--(3,10),th); draw((7,0)--(7,10),th);
draw((3,10)--(7,10)--(3,13)--cycle,th); draw((3,0)--(7,0)--(3,-3)--cycle,th);
label("$3$",(1.5,0),S);label("$4$",(5,0.8));label("$5$",(9.5,0),S);
label("$h=10$",(12,5),E);
label("$B$",(4.2,11)); label("$B$",(4.2,-1));
label("$P=3+4+5=12$",(6,-4.6));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The total surface area is $12\cdot10+2\cdot6=\boxed{132}$.

question:
For a positive integer $n$, the notation $(\mathbb Z_n)^\times$ (also written with the quotient notation for the integers modulo $n$) denotes the set of residue classes modulo $n$ that have a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ (a class $a$ has an inverse if $ab\equiv1\pmod n$ for some integer $b$).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that the class of $a$ has an inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $\gcd(a,n)=1$.
\item[(b)] List the elements of $(\mathbb Z_{15})^\times$ together with the inverse of each. How many elements are there?
\end{enumerate}

The cubes of $1,2,3,4$ are congruent to $1,3,2,4$ modulo $5$, so cubing permutes the nonzero residues modulo $5$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove more generally: if $p$ is a prime and $k$ is a positive integer with $\gcd(k,p-1)=1$, then $x\mapsto x^k$ permutes the nonzero residues modulo $p$.
\item[(b)] Explain the case $p=5$, $k=3$ directly, and show that squaring does not permute the nonzero residues modulo $5$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Count by the number of letters.
\begin{itemize}
\item Exactly $2$ letters: choose the position of the digit in $3$ ways, then $26^2\cdot10$ fillings: $3\cdot6760=20280$.
\item Exactly $3$ letters: $26^3=17576$.
\end{itemize}
Total: $20280+17576=\boxed{37856}$.

The two angle conditions say that triangles $DPQ$ and $DAB$ are similar, with $D\leftrightarrow D$, $P\leftrightarrow A$, $Q\leftrightarrow B$. Use complex numbers with $D=0$, $A=a$, $B=b$. Then $C=b-a$, $O=\dfrac {b}{2}$ (the midpoint of $BD$), and
\[
P=\dfrac{A+O}{2}=\dfrac{2a+b}{4},\qquad Q=\dfrac{B+C}{2}=\dfrac{2b-a}{2}.
\]
A similarity fixing $D=0$ is either $z\mapsto wz$ (same orientation) or $z\mapsto w\bar z$ (opposite orientation) for some complex $w\ne0$.

\emph{Opposite orientation.} Rotating and scaling about $D$ we may take $a=1$. Then $P=w$ and $Q=w\bar b$, so $Q=P\bar b$, that is $\dfrac{2b-1}{2}=\dfrac{(2+b)\bar b}{4}$, or $4b-2=2\bar b+|b|^2$. Taking imaginary parts gives $4\operatorname{Im}b=-2\operatorname{Im}b$, so $\operatorname{Im} b=0$ and $A,B,D$ would be collinear, which is impossible.

\emph{Same orientation.} Then $P=wa$ and $Q=wb$, so $Pb=Qa$:
\[
\dfrac{(2a+b)b}{4}=\dfrac{(2b-a)a}{2}\iff b^2-2ab+2a^2=0\iff (b-a)^2=-a^2\iff b-a=\pm ia .
\]
Now $b-a$ is the vector $\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $-a$ is $\overrightarrow{AD}$, so $AB=AD$ and $AB\perp AD$: the parallelogram is a square. Conversely, in a square both conditions hold: $\angle DPQ=90^\circ=\angle DAB$ and $\angle DQP=45^\circ=\angle DBA$ (in the figure, with $A=(0,0)$, $P=\left(\dfrac{1}{4},\dfrac{1}{4}\right)$, $Q=\left(1,\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$, $D=(0,1)$, one checks $\overrightarrow{PD}\cdot\overrightarrow{PQ}=0$ and $PD=PQ$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(1,0), C=(1,1), D=(0,1), O=(0.5,0.5), P=(0.25,0.25), Q=(1,0.5);
fill(D--P--Q--cycle,palegray);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(A--C,gray(0.5)); draw(B--D,gray(0.5));
draw(D--P--Q--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(O);dot(P);dot(Q);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$O$",O,N);label("$P$",P,SE);label("$Q$",Q,E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Hence $ABCD$ is a square of side $1$, and its area is $\boxed{1}$.

Drop perpendiculars of length $h$ from the ends of the shorter base to the longer base. They cut off segments $x$ (under the leg $\sqrt{34}$) and $y$ (under the leg $3\sqrt5$) with
\[
x+10+y=21,\qquad h^2+x^2=34,\qquad h^2+y^2=45 .
\]
(If a foot fell outside the long base we would need $|y-x|=11$ with $x+y$ smaller, which is impossible below.) So $x+y=11$ and $y^2-x^2=11$, hence $y-x=1$, giving $y=6$, $x=5$ and $h^2=34-25=9$, $h=3$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
