pair A=(0,0), B=(21,0), C=(15,3), D=(5,3);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(D--(5,0),dashed); draw(C--(15,0),dashed);
label("$10$",(D+C)/2,N); label("$5$",(2.5,0),S); label("$10$",(10,0),S); label("$6$",(18,0),S);
label("$h$",(5,1.5),E); label("$\sqrt{34}$",(A+D)/2,NW); label("$3\sqrt5$",(B+C)/2,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The area is
\[
\dfrac{(10+21)\cdot3}{2}=\boxed{\dfrac{93}{2}} .
\]

A chord of length $r$ together with the two radii to its ends forms an equilateral triangle, so it cuts off an arc of $60^\circ$. ``Drawn in a clockwise direction'' means: from the chosen point $X$, the chord goes to the point $X'$ that is $60^\circ$ clockwise from $X$. So each chord is determined by its starting point and spans the $60^\circ$ arc just clockwise of it.

Let the first point be $X$ and measure the position of the second point $Y$ by the counterclockwise angle $\theta$ from $X$, uniform on $[0^\circ,360^\circ)$. Two chords cross exactly when their endpoints alternate around the circle, which here means the $60^\circ$ arcs $X'X$ and $Y'Y$ overlap without coinciding. This happens exactly when $Y$ is within $60^\circ$ of $X$ on either side ($0<\theta<60^\circ$ or $300^\circ<\theta<360^\circ$); otherwise the arcs, and hence the chords, are disjoint.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair X=dir(90), X2=dir(30), Y=dir(120), Y2=dir(60);
draw(unitcircle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(X--X2,blue+linewidth(1.1)); draw(Y--Y2,red+linewidth(1.1));
draw(arc((0,0),1.32,84,36),blue,EndArrow(TeXHead));
dot(X,blue);dot(X2,blue);dot(Y,red);dot(Y2,red);dot((0,0));
label("$X$",X,(-0.5,1),blue); label("$X'$",X2,E,blue); label("$Y$",Y,NW,red); label("$Y'$",Y2,NE,red);
draw((0,0)--X,dashed+gray(0.5)); draw((0,0)--X2,dashed+gray(0.5));
label("$60^\circ$",(0.22,0.6),gray(0.3));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Hence the probability is $\dfrac{120}{360}=\boxed{\dfrac{1}{3}}$, choice $\textbf{(D)}$.

question:
Let $p$ be a prime. Consider the two statements
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(I)] $a^p\equiv a\pmod p$ for every integer $a$;
\item[(II)] $a^{p-1}\equiv1\pmod p$ for every integer $a$ not divisible by $p$.
\end{enumerate}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that (I) and (II) are equivalent, and explain why (II) must exclude the multiples of $p$.
\item[(b)] Use Fermat's little theorem to find the remainder when $3^{100}$ is divided by $7$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
From $\sqrt H=2\sqrt6$ we get $H=24$, so $LW=48$. The integer $L$ divides $48=2^4\cdot3$ and is a perfect cube greater than $1$, so $L=8$ (the cubes $27$ and $64$ do not divide $48$). Then $W=6$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair o=(0.45,0.3);
real L=8, H=24, Wd=6; real k=0.17;
pair p(real x,real y,real z){return k*((x,z)+y*o);}
draw(p(0,0,0)--p(L,0,0)--p(L,0,H)--p(0,0,H)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(p(L,0,0)--p(L,Wd,0)--p(L,Wd,H)--p(L,0,H),linewidth(0.9));
draw(p(0,0,H)--p(0,Wd,H)--p(L,Wd,H),linewidth(0.9));
