question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Let $n=p_1^{e_1}p_2^{e_2}\cdots p_k^{e_k}$ be the prime factorization of a positive integer $n$ (distinct primes $p_i$, exponents $e_i\ge1$). Prove that $n$ has exactly $(e_1+1)(e_2+1)\cdots(e_k+1)$ positive divisors.
\item[(b)] How many positive divisors does $720$ have?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
From the first chain, cross-multiplying,
\[
x(y+z)=y(x+y)\ \Longrightarrow\ xz=y^2,\qquad y(z+x)=z(y+z)\ \Longrightarrow\ xy=z^2 .
\]
Dividing the two results, $\dfrac {z}{y}=\dfrac{y^2}{z^2}$, so $y^3=z^3$ and $y=z$; then $xz=z^2$ gives $x=z$. So $x=y=z$.

From the second chain, $y(y+z)=z(z+t)$ with $y=z$ gives $2z^2=z^2+zt$, so $t=z$ (and then $t(t+y)=2z^2$ as well). Hence all four angles are equal and, since they fill the full angle around $O$,
\[
x=y=z=t=\boxed{90^\circ}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
draw((-1.2,0)--(1.2,0),linewidth(0.9)); draw((0,-1.2)--(0,1.2),linewidth(0.9));
dot(O); label("$O$",O,SW);
label("$x$",(0.45,0.45)); label("$y$",(-0.45,0.45)); label("$z$",(-0.45,-0.45)); label("$t$",(0.45,-0.45));
draw((0.13,0)--(0.13,0.13)--(0,0.13));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So the four angles are formed by two perpendicular lines through $O$.

Ratios of lengths along a line are unchanged by an affine map, so we may take the parallelogram to be a rectangle with convenient coordinates:
\[
A=(0,0),\quad B=(8,0),\quad D=(0,4),\quad C=(8,4),\qquad E=(0,1),\quad F=(7,0).
\]
Points of $CE$ are $C+s(E-C)=(8-8s,\ 4-3s)$, and points of $DF$ are $D+u(F-D)=(7u,\ 4-4u)$. Equating, $4-3s=4-4u$ gives $s=\dfrac{4u}{3}$, and then $8-\dfrac{32u}{3}=7u$ gives $u=\dfrac{24}{53}$, $s=\dfrac{32}{53}$. So $P=\left(\dfrac{168}{53},\dfrac{116}{53}\right)$ and $CP=s\cdot CE$, $PE=(1-s)\cdot CE$:
\[
CP:PE=\dfrac{32}{53}:\dfrac{21}{53}=\boxed{32:21}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(8,0), D=(2,4), C=B+D;
pair Ee=A+(D-A)/4, F=(7,0);
pair P=C+32/53*(Ee-C);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(C--Ee,blue+linewidth(0.9)); draw(D--F,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(Ee);dot(F);dot(P);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$E$",Ee,W);label("$F$",F,S);label("$P$",P,N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(Check without coordinates: extend $DF$ to meet line $BC$ at $G$. Triangles $FBG$ and $FAD$ are similar with ratio $FB:FA=1:7$, so $BG=\dfrac{AD}{7}$ and $CG=\dfrac{8}{7}AD$. Triangles $PED$ and $PCG$ are similar, so $CP:PE=CG:ED=\dfrac{8}{7}AD:\dfrac{3}{4}AD=32:21$.)

For $\ell$: setting $y=0$ gives $x=18$, and setting $x=0$ gives $y=-8$. So the new line has $x$-intercept $-8$ and $y$-intercept $18$, and its intercept form is
