Let $O$ be the center. A chord at distance $d$ from $O$ has length $2\sqrt{17^2-d^2}$. For chords through $P$ the distance $d$ runs from $0$ (the diameter through $P$, length $34$) to $OP=12$ (the chord perpendicular to $OP$, length $2\sqrt{289-144}=2\sqrt{145}\approx24.08$). As a chord turns about $P$ through $180^\circ$, its length changes continuously from $34$ down to $2\sqrt{145}$ and back up to $34$. The chords through $P$ come in pairs that are mirror images in the line $OP$, so every length strictly between the minimum and the maximum is taken by exactly $2$ chords, while the maximum $34$ (the diameter) and the minimum (the perpendicular chord) are each taken by exactly $1$ chord.

