label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Since $24.08<\ell\le34$, the integer lengths are $25,26,\dots,34$. Each of $25,\dots,33$ gives $2$ chords and $34$ gives $1$, so the number of chords is $9\cdot2+1=\boxed{19}$.

General method: compute the shortest chord $2\sqrt{r^2-OP^2}$ and the diameter $2r$; count the integers strictly between them twice, and add one for the diameter (and one for the shortest chord if its length is an integer).

In the complex plane the number $x+yi$ ($x,y$ real) is the point $(x,y)$: the real part is the horizontal coordinate and the imaginary part is the vertical coordinate. Write $w=x_1+y_1i$ and $z=x_2+y_2i$. Then
\[
z-w=(x_2-x_1)+(y_2-y_1)i,\qquad\text{so}\qquad \operatorname{Re}(z-w)=x_2-x_1,\quad \operatorname{Im}(z-w)=y_2-y_1 .
\]
The line through the points $(x_1,y_1)$ and $(x_2,y_2)$, with $x_2\ne x_1$, has slope
\[
\dfrac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=\dfrac{\operatorname{Im}(z-w)}{\operatorname{Re}(z-w)} .\qquad\blacksquare
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-0.3,0)--(5,0),Arrow(TeXHead)); draw((0,-0.3)--(0,4),Arrow(TeXHead));
label("$\operatorname{Re}$",(5,0),E); label("$\operatorname{Im}$",(0,4),N);
pair w=(1,1), z=(4,3);
draw(w--z,linewidth(1)); draw(w--(4,1),dashed); draw((4,1)--z,dashed);
dot(w);dot(z);
label("$w$",w,W); label("$z$",z,N);
label("$x_2-x_1$",(2.5,1),S); label("$y_2-y_1$",(4,2),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(Note that $\operatorname{Im}(z-w)$ is the real number $y_2-y_1$, not $(y_2-y_1)i$; nothing is ``replaced by $i$''. If $\operatorname{Re}(z)=\operatorname{Re}(w)$, the line is vertical and has no slope.)

The tangent lengths from a vertex to the incircle are equal, and in a triangle with sides $u,v,w$ the tangent length from the vertex between sides $u$ and $v$ is $\dfrac{u+v-w}{2}$. Hence
\[
BP=\dfrac{BC+BX-CX}{2},\qquad BQ=\dfrac{BA+BX-AX}{2}.
\]
Since $AX=CX=40$,
\[
PQ=|BP-BQ|=\dfrac{|BC-BA|}{2}=\dfrac{64-48}{2}=\boxed{8},\qquad\textbf{(C)}.
\]

