\textbf{Key fact.} Let a tank have edges $a,b,c$ meeting at a bottom corner $V$, and let $P,Q$ (on the bottom) and $R$ (on the top rim) be the three corners joined to $V$ by an edge. The plane $PQR$ cuts off the tetrahedron $VPQR$, whose three edges at $V$ are mutually perpendicular. Its volume is
\[
\dfrac{1}{3}\cdot\left(\dfrac{1}{2}ab\right)\cdot c=\dfrac{abc}{6},
\]
which is $\dfrac{1}{6}$ of the tank, that is, $10$ gallons.

