dot(V);dot(P);dot(Q);dot(R);
label("$V$",V,SW);label("$P$",P,S);label("$Q$",Q+(0.22,0.2));label("$R$",R,W);
label("$a$",(V+P)/2,S);label("$c$",(V+R)/2,W);label("$b$",(P+PQ)/2,SE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Method.} Fill tank 1 completely with the hose ($60$ gallons). Tip it over its corner $V$, so that $V$ is the lowest point, and pour the water that spills out into the empty tank 2. Keep tipping until the water surface in tank 1 touches exactly the three corners $P$, $Q$ and $R$; you can see this without measuring anything.

\textbf{Why it works.} Put $V$ at the origin with the edges along the axes, so that the tank is $0\le x\le a$, $0\le y\le b$, $0\le z\le c$ and the plane $PQR$ is $\dfrac {x}{a}+\dfrac {y}{b}+\dfrac {z}{c}=1$. In the final position this plane is horizontal and $V$ is below it, so the water occupies exactly the part of the box with $\dfrac {x}{a}+\dfrac {y}{b}+\dfrac {z}{c}\le1$, which is the tetrahedron $VPQR$. The open top is the face $z=c$, where $\dfrac {x}{a}+\dfrac {y}{b}+\dfrac {z}{c}\ge1$, so the whole rim is at or above the water level and the water surface just reaches the rim at $R$: this is a legitimate resting position. Hence exactly $10$ gallons remain in tank 1, and tank 2 has received
\[
60-10=\boxed{50\text{ gallons}}.
\]

After $t$ minutes the train is $10-\dfrac {t}{2}$ km from $O$ and the car is $6-\dfrac {t}{2}$ km from $O$ (a negative value means the car has passed $O$). The two roads are perpendicular, so by the Pythagorean theorem the distance $d$ between them satisfies
\[
d^2=\left(10-\dfrac {t}{2}\right)^2+\left(6-\dfrac {t}{2}\right)^2 .
\]
Put $u=\dfrac {t}{2}$. Setting $d=4$ gives
\[
(10-u)^2+(6-u)^2=16\iff 2u^2-32u+120=0\iff u^2-16u+60=0\iff (u-6)(u-10)=0 .
\]
So $u=6$ or $u=10$, that is, $t=12$ or $t=20$. At $t=12$ the car is just at $O$ and the train is $4$ km away.

