The cube to be built has edge $3$ cm. Place it as $[0,3]^3$.

\emph{At most one $2$-cm cube fits.} Along each axis a $2$-cm cube occupies an interval of length $2$ inside $[0,3]$, and every such interval contains $[1,2]$. So every $2$-cm cube in the construction contains the small central cube $[1,2]^3$, and two of them would overlap.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair dd=(0.42,0.3);
void gridcube(int n, int k){
  for(int i=0;i<=n;++i){
    draw((i,0)--(i,n),gray(0.6));draw((0,i)--(n,i),gray(0.6));
    draw((i,n)--(i,n)+n*dd,gray(0.6));draw((0,n)+i*dd--(n,n)+i*dd,gray(0.6));
    draw((n,i)--(n,i)+n*dd,gray(0.6));draw((n,0)+i*dd--(n,n)+i*dd,gray(0.6));
  }
  filldraw((0,0)--(k,0)--(k,k)--(0,k)--cycle,lightgray,linewidth(1.2));
  draw((0,0)--(n,0)--(n,n)--(0,n)--cycle,linewidth(1));
  draw((0,n)--(0,n)+n*dd--(n,n)+n*dd--(n,0)+n*dd--(n,0),linewidth(1));
  draw((n,n)--(n,n)+n*dd,linewidth(1));
}
gridcube(3,2);
label("$2$-cm cube",(1,1));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So the possibilities are:
\begin{itemize}
\item one $3$-cm cube: $1$ cube;
\item one $2$-cm cube in a corner (shaded) and $27-8=19$ unit cubes: $20$ cubes;
\item $27$ unit cubes: $27$ cubes.
\end{itemize}
These use different numbers of cubes, so there are $\boxed{3}$ ways.

The cube to be built is $[0,4]^3$.

\emph{At most one $3$-cm cube, and then no $2$-cm cube.} Two non-overlapping cubes must be separated along some axis: their intervals on that axis do not overlap. Inside $[0,4]$, intervals of lengths $3$ and $3$, or $3$ and $2$, have total length more than $4$ and cannot be disjoint. So a $3$-cm cube excludes every other cube of edge $2$ or $3$.

