\emph{With a $3$-cm cube:} the rest is $64-27=37$ unit cubes, for $38$ cubes in all.

\emph{Without a $3$-cm cube:} cut $[0,4]^3$ into its eight $2\times2\times2$ octants. Using $k$ of them as $2$-cm cubes ($0\le k\le8$) and filling the rest with unit cubes uses $k+(64-8k)=64-7k$ cubes, i.e.\
\[
64,\ 57,\ 50,\ 43,\ 36,\ 29,\ 22,\ 15,\ 8 .
\]
No more than $8$ two-centimetre cubes fit, because $9\cdot8>64$, and any construction with $k$ of them uses $64-7k$ cubes, so this list is complete.

The number $38$ is not of the form $64-7k$, so the total number of different ways is $9+1=\boxed{10}$.

Complete the squares:
