\]
so Student B is right.

Student A's error: $P(X\cap Y)=P(X)\,P(Y)$ holds only for \emph{independent} events. The events "in the lower semicircle" and "in the upper semicircle" are not independent: the lower semicircle covers the bottom of the rectangle more than the top, and the upper one the reverse, so knowing the point is in one changes the chance it is in the other. Here $P(X\cap Y)\approx0.614\ne0.617\approx P(X)P(Y)$; the closeness of the two numbers is a coincidence.

