The perimeter gives $4(t+1)+2x=60$, so
\[
x=\dfrac{60-4t-4}{2}=\boxed{28-2t}.
\]
For a hexagon to exist, the side lengths must be positive and (as for a triangle) each side must be shorter than the sum of the other five, i.e.\ shorter than half the perimeter, $30$. These conditions are also sufficient for a polygon with these sides to exist.
\begin{itemize}
\item $t+1>0$ and $t+1<30$: $-1<t<29$;
\item $28-2t>0$ and $28-2t<30$: $-1<t<14$.
\end{itemize}
So the hexagon exists exactly when $-1<t<14$. (For example, $t=9$ gives four sides of $10$ and two sides of $10$: a regular hexagon.)

