label("$4$",(0,2),W);label("$8$",(6,4),E);label("$h$",(J+K)/2,W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Triangles $HKJ$ and $HGF$ are similar, so $\dfrac{h}{4}=\dfrac{KH}{GH}$. Triangles $GKJ$ and $GHI$ are similar, so $\dfrac{h}{8}=\dfrac{GK}{GH}$. Adding, since $GK+KH=GH$,
\[
\dfrac {h}{4}+\dfrac {h}{8}=1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\dfrac{3h}{8}=1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad h=\boxed{\dfrac{8}{3}}.
\]
The length $GH$ does not matter. (In general $h=\dfrac{ab}{a+b}$, half the harmonic mean of the two heights.)

