The Sophie Germain identity states that $a^4+4b^4=(a^2+2b^2+2ab)(a^2+2b^2-2ab)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove the identity.
\item[(b)] Use it to find all positive integers $n$ for which $n^4+4$ is prime.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Listing the vertices in order $A,B,C,D$ and back to $A$:
\[
\begin{array}{rcl}
x_Ay_B-x_By_A&=&1\cdot2-6\cdot1=-4\\
x_By_C-x_Cy_B&=&6\cdot6-5\cdot2=26\\
x_Cy_D-x_Dy_C&=&5\cdot5-2\cdot6=13\\
x_Dy_A-x_Ay_D&=&2\cdot1-1\cdot5=-3
\end{array}
\]
The sum is $32$, so the area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot32=\boxed{16}$. (The name comes from writing the coordinates in two columns and multiplying along crossing diagonals, like the laces of a shoe.)

