The reflection of $D=(0,0)$ in this line is
\[
D'=D-2\cdot\dfrac{4\cdot0+3\cdot0-12}{4^2+3^2}\,(4,3)=\dfrac{24}{25}(4,3)=\left(\dfrac{96}{25},\dfrac{72}{25}\right).
\]
(Check: $AD'=AD=4$ and $ED'=ED=3$.) Then
\[
D'C=\sqrt{\left(6-\dfrac{96}{25}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{72}{25}\right)^2}=\dfrac{\sqrt{54^2+72^2}}{25}=\dfrac{90}{25}=\boxed{\dfrac{18}{5}}.
\]
(Alternatively: $ED'=EC=3$ and $\angle D'EC=180^\circ-2\angle DEA$, so $D'C=2\cdot3\sin\dfrac{\angle D'EC}{2}=6\cos\angle DEA=6\cdot\dfrac{3}{5}=\dfrac{18}{5}$.)

\textbf{Construction.} Put the sheet at $[0,10]\times[0,4]$ and take the rhombus with vertices $(0,0)$, $(s,0)$, $(10,4)$, $(10-s,4)$. Its sides are equal when $(10-s)^2+4^2=s^2$, i.e. $116=20s$, so $s=5.8$. Its area is base times height, $5.8\cdot4=23.2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(0,0),Q=(5.8,0),R=(10,4),T=(4.2,4);
draw((0,0)--(10,0)--(10,4)--(0,4)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
filldraw(P--Q--R--T--cycle,palegray,linewidth(1));
label("$5.8$",(P+Q)/2,S);label("$4.2$",(Q+(10,0))/2,S);label("$4$",(10,2),E);label("$5.8$",(Q+R)/2,NW);
label("$10$",(5,4),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Upper bound.} Let a rhombus on the sheet have side vectors $\mathbf p=(p_1,p_2)$ and $\mathbf q=(q_1,q_2)$ with $|\mathbf p|=|\mathbf q|$. Its projections onto the axes have lengths $X=|p_1|+|q_1|\le10$ and $Y=|p_2|+|q_2|\le4$, and its area is
\[
|p_1q_2-p_2q_1|\le|p_1||q_2|+|p_2||q_1| .
\]
Write $|p_1|=\dfrac{X+t}{2}$, $|q_1|=\dfrac{X-t}{2}$, $|q_2|=\dfrac{Y+r}{2}$, $|p_2|=\dfrac{Y-r}{2}$ with $|t|\le X$, $|r|\le Y$. Then
\[
|p_1||q_2|+|p_2||q_1|=\dfrac{XY+tr}{2},
\]
and $|\mathbf p|^2=|\mathbf q|^2$ means $p_1^2-q_1^2=q_2^2-p_2^2$, i.e. $tX=rY$. If $Y\le X$, then $|t|=\dfrac{|r|Y}{X}\le\dfrac{Y^2}{X}$, so
\[
\text{area}\le\dfrac{XY+t r}{2}=\dfrac{1}{2}\left(XY+\dfrac{t^2X}{Y}\right)\le\dfrac{1}{2}\left(XY+\dfrac{Y^3}{X}\right)=\dfrac{Y}{2}\left(X+\dfrac{Y^2}{X}\right).
\]
This increases with $Y$, and with $X$ for $X\ge Y$, so it is at most $2\left(10+\dfrac{16}{10}\right)=23.2$. If $X<Y\le4$, the area is at most the bounding box area $XY<16$.

So the greatest area is $\boxed{\dfrac{116}{5}=23.2}$.

Let $d>0$ be the common difference and let $\mathbf u,\mathbf v,\mathbf w$ be the unit vectors in the directions of $\overrightarrow{AB}$, $\overrightarrow{BC}$, $\overrightarrow{CD}$.

Traverse the convex hexagon counterclockwise. The directions of the side vectors turn in the same sense at every vertex, by angles between $0^\circ$ and $180^\circ$, with total $360^\circ$. Since $DE\parallel AB$ and three turns separate them, $\overrightarrow{DE}$ points in the direction opposite to $\overrightarrow{AB}$ (the same direction would need a turn of $0^\circ$ or $360^\circ$ over three or over the other three vertices). Likewise for the other two pairs. Hence
\[
\overrightarrow{DE}=-(AB+d)\mathbf u,\qquad \overrightarrow{EF}=-(BC-d)\mathbf v,\qquad \overrightarrow{FA}=-(CD+d)\mathbf w .
\]
The six side vectors add up to $\mathbf 0$:
\[
AB\,\mathbf u+BC\,\mathbf v+CD\,\mathbf w-(AB+d)\mathbf u-(BC-d)\mathbf v-(CD+d)\mathbf w=d(-\mathbf u+\mathbf v-\mathbf w)=\mathbf 0 .
\]
Since $d\ne0$, $\mathbf v=\mathbf u+\mathbf w$. Taking squared lengths, $1=1+1+2\,\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf w$, so $\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf w=-\dfrac{1}{2}$, and then $\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v=1-\dfrac{1}{2}=\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf w=\dfrac{1}{2}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0);
pair[] V={A};
real[] L={2,4,2,3,3,3};
for(int i=0;i<5;++i) V.push(V[i]+L[i]*dir(60*i));
string[] nm={"$A$","$B$","$C$","$D$","$E$","$F$"};
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(1));
pair cen=(V[0]+V[1]+V[2]+V[3]+V[4]+V[5])/6;
for(int i=0;i<6;++i) label(nm[i],V[i],unit(V[i]-cen));
label("$2$",(V[0]+V[1])/2,S);label("$4$",(V[1]+V[2])/2,SE);label("$2$",(V[2]+V[3])/2,NE);
label("$3$",(V[3]+V[4])/2,N);label("$3$",(V[4]+V[5])/2,NW);label("$3$",(V[5]+V[0])/2,SW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So the angle between $\mathbf u$ and $\mathbf v$ is $60^\circ$, between $\mathbf v$ and $\mathbf w$ is $60^\circ$, and between $\mathbf w$ and $-\mathbf u$ is $60^\circ$ (as $\mathbf w\cdot(-\mathbf u)=\dfrac{1}{2}$). The side directions are therefore, in order,
\[
\mathbf u,\ \mathbf v,\ \mathbf w,\ -\mathbf u,\ -\mathbf v,\ -\mathbf w,
\]
and each consecutive pair (including $-\mathbf w$ back to $\mathbf u$) makes an angle of $60^\circ$. So the hexagon turns by $60^\circ$ at every vertex, and every interior angle is $180^\circ-60^\circ=120^\circ$. $\blacksquare$

(The figure shows an example with $AB=2$, $BC=4$, $CD=2$, $DE=EF=FA=3$ and $d=1$.)

Since $M$ is inside the triangle, there are positive numbers $u,v,w$ (areas of $MBC$, $MCA$, $MAB$, or masses in the sense of mass points) with
\[
M=\dfrac{uA+vB+wC}{u+v+w},\quad A_1=\dfrac{vB+wC}{v+w},\quad B_1=\dfrac{uA+wC}{u+w},\quad C_1=\dfrac{uA+vB}{u+v}.
\]
Here a combination $\alpha A+\beta B+\gamma C$ with $\alpha+\beta+\gamma=1$ means the corresponding point (vector combination of position vectors).

\textbf{Finding $A_3$.} Let $s=u+v+w$. Then
\[
2(u+v)(u+w)A_2=(u+v)(uA+wC)+(u+w)(uA+vB)=u(2u+v+w)A+v(u+w)B+w(u+v)C .
\]
Subtract this from $(2u+v+w)\cdot sM=(2u+v+w)(uA+vB+wC)$; the $A$ terms cancel:
\[
(2u+v+w)\,sM-2(u+v)(u+w)A_2=v(u+v)B+w(u+w)C .
\]
The coefficients on each side have the same total, $k=v(u+v)+w(u+w)>0$. Dividing by $k$, the point
\[
A_3=\dfrac{v(u+v)B+w(u+w)C}{v(u+v)+w(u+w)}
\]
is an affine combination of $M$ and $A_2$, so it lies on line $MA_2$, and it lies on segment $BC$. Hence it is the intersection point, and
\[
\dfrac{BA_3}{A_3C}=\dfrac{w(u+w)}{v(u+v)}.
\]
By the same computation with the roles of the vertices cycled ($A\to B\to C\to A$, $u\to v\to w\to u$),
\[
\dfrac{CB_3}{B_3A}=\dfrac{u(v+u)}{w(v+w)},\qquad \dfrac{AC_3}{C_3B}=\dfrac{v(w+v)}{u(w+u)}.
\]
\textbf{Ceva.} The points $A_3,B_3,C_3$ lie inside the sides, and
\[
\dfrac{BA_3}{A_3C}\cdot\dfrac{CB_3}{B_3A}\cdot\dfrac{AC_3}{C_3B}=\dfrac{w(u+w)}{v(u+v)}\cdot\dfrac{u(u+v)}{w(v+w)}\cdot\dfrac{v(v+w)}{u(u+w)}=1 .
\]
By the converse of Ceva's theorem, $AA_3$, $BB_3$, $CC_3$ are concurrent. $\blacksquare$

