\end{asy}

Since $19>6\cdot3$, by the pigeonhole principle some triangle $T$ contains at least $4$ students. Take four of them, $P,Q,R,S$. No three are collinear, so they form a (simple) quadrilateral: if their convex hull has $4$ vertices, take the convex quadrilateral; otherwise one point lies inside the triangle formed by the other three, and joining them in a suitable order gives a non-convex quadrilateral.

Its area is at most the area of the convex hull of $P,Q,R,S$, which is contained in $T$, so the area is at most $100\sqrt3$. Equality would require the convex hull to be all of $T$, so three of the points would be the vertices of $T$; the fourth point is then either on a side of $T$ (three collinear points, which is excluded) or strictly inside $T$, in which case the quadrilateral is non-convex and its area is the area of $T$ minus a triangle of positive area. Either way equality is impossible, so the quadrilateral has area less than $100\sqrt3\ \text{m}^2$. $\blacksquare$

