solution:
Put the floor on coordinates with corners $(0,0)$ and $(17,10)$; the bug walks along $y=\dfrac{10}{17}x$.

\textbf{The path never passes through an interior grid point.} If it passed through a lattice point $(x,y)$ with $0<x<17$, then $17y=10x$; since $\gcd(10,17)=1$, $17$ would divide $x$, which is impossible. So the bug never crosses a vertical and a horizontal line at the same moment.

\textbf{Counting tiles.} The bug starts inside the first tile. Every time it crosses one interior grid line it leaves the tile it is in and enters a new one, and it enters a new tile at no other time. So
\[
\text{tiles visited}=1+(\text{number of crossings}).
\]
The ``$+1$'' is the starting tile, which the bug is in before it has crossed anything (just as a fence of $k$ gaps has $k+1$ posts). The bug crosses each of the $16$ interior vertical lines $x=1,\dots,16$ and each of the $9$ interior horizontal lines $y=1,\dots,9$ exactly once, and these $25$ crossings happen at different moments. Hence the bug visits
\[
1+16+9=\boxed{26}
\]
tiles (shaded below).

