solution:
Reflection in line $AB$ fixes $A$, so $AQ=AP$ and $\angle QAB=\angle BAP$. Similarly $AR=AP$ and $\angle PAC=\angle CAR$. Since $P$ is inside the triangle, ray $AP$ lies inside angle $BAC$, $Q$ lies on the other side of line $AB$ from $P$, and $R$ on the other side of line $AC$. Going around $A$ from ray $AQ$ to ray $AR$ through the triangle,
\[
\angle QAR=\angle QAB+\angle BAP+\angle PAC+\angle CAR=2\angle BAP+2\angle PAC=2\angle BAC .
\]
Since $0<\angle BAC<180^\circ$, this angle is strictly between $0^\circ$ and $360^\circ$. The points $Q$, $A$, $R$ are collinear with $Q\ne A\ne R$ (because $AQ=AR=AP>0$) exactly when $\angle QAR=180^\circ$, that is $2\angle BAC=180^\circ$. Hence
\[
\angle BAC=\boxed{90^\circ}.
\]

