label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Since $X$ lies on circle $ANP$ and $N\in AC$, $P\in AB$,
\[
\measuredangle NXP=\measuredangle NAP=\measuredangle CAB .
\]
Since $X$ lies on circle $BMP$ and $P\in BA$, $M\in BC$,
\[
\measuredangle PXM=\measuredangle PBM=\measuredangle ABC .
\]
Adding,
\[
\measuredangle NXM=\measuredangle NXP+\measuredangle PXM=\measuredangle CAB+\measuredangle ABC=-\measuredangle BCA=\measuredangle ACB=\measuredangle NCM ,
\]
because $N$ lies on line $CA$ and $M$ on line $CB$. Hence $N,X,M,C$ are concyclic, so the circumcircle of $CMN$ also passes through $X$. All three circles pass through $X$ (the \emph{Miquel point}). $\blacksquare$

\textbf{Remark.} The statement is false for \emph{incircles}. For example, if $M,N,P$ are the midpoints of the sides of an equilateral triangle, triangles $ANP$, $BMP$, $CMN$ are the three corner triangles, which meet only at the points $M,N,P$; their incircles lie strictly inside them and so are pairwise disjoint.

question:
Let $a$ and $b$ be positive integers.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $a^3\mid b^4$ does \emph{not} imply $a\mid b$.
\item[(b)] Prove that if $a^k\mid b^k$ for some positive integer $k$, then $a\mid b$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
There are $\binom72=21$ equally likely pairs drawn from $A$:
\begin{itemize}
\item two white: $\binom42=6$ pairs; then $B$ has $5$ white of $10$;
\item one white, one black: $4\cdot3=12$ pairs; then $B$ has $4$ white of $10$;
\item two black: $\binom32=3$ pairs; then $B$ has $3$ white of $10$.
\end{itemize}
So
\[
P(\text{white})=\dfrac{6\cdot5+12\cdot4+3\cdot3}{21\cdot10}=\dfrac{87}{210}=\boxed{\dfrac{29}{70}} .
\]
(Shortcut: the expected number of white balls moved is $2\cdot\dfrac{4}{7}=\dfrac{8}{7}$, and the answer is $\dfrac{3+\dfrac{8}{7}}{10}=\dfrac{29}{70}$.)

\textbf{Unfolding.} Reflect the rectangle across the side the ball hits instead of reflecting the ball. In the tiling of the plane by reflected copies of the rectangle, the ball moves in a straight line, at time $t$ at the point $(2t,t)$. A bounce off a vertical side reverses the $x$-velocity, a bounce off a horizontal side reverses the $y$-velocity, and a corner hit reverses both, which is exactly the ``comes back in the direction it went in'' rule; so the unfolded path is the straight line in all cases. The actual position is obtained by folding back: $x$ is $2t$ folded into $[0,20]$ with period $40$ and $y$ is $t$ folded into $[0,19]$ with period $38$.

\textbf{Corner hits.} The ball is at a corner exactly when $2t$ is a multiple of $20$ and $t$ is a multiple of $19$, that is, when $t$ is a multiple of both $10$ and $19$, so of $190$. For $0<t\le2019$ these are $t=190,380,\dots,1900$: that is $\boxed{10}$ times.

At $t=190k$ the unfolded point is $(380k,190k)$; since $\dfrac{380k}{20}=19k$ and $\dfrac{190k}{19}=10k$, folding gives $x=0$ or $20$ (as $k$ is even or odd) and $y=0$. So the corners hit are $(20,0)$ and $(0,0)$ alternately, which matches the poster's parity observation. The path up to the first corner hit, at $(20,0)$ after $190$ seconds, is shown below.

