Then $E$ carries mass $m_A+m_B=5$, and $F$ is the balance point of segment $EC$ with masses $5$ at $E$ and $\dfrac{1}{2}$ at $C$. The lever rule gives
\[
\dfrac{EF}{FC}=\dfrac{m_C}{m_E}=\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{2}}{5}=\boxed{\dfrac{1}{10}} .
\]
\textbf{Check with Menelaus.} Line $BFD$ crosses the sides of triangle $AEC$ (with $B$ on the extension of $AE$). Menelaus' theorem gives
\[
\dfrac{AB}{BE}\cdot\dfrac{EF}{FC}\cdot\dfrac{CD}{DA}=1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad \dfrac{5}{2}\cdot\dfrac{EF}{FC}\cdot4=1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\dfrac{EF}{FC}=\dfrac{1}{10} .
\]

There are $6$ coins, so $\binom62=15$ equally likely pairs. A pair is worth more than $16$ cents when
\begin{itemize}
\item it contains the quarter: $5$ pairs (the quarter with any other coin);
\item or it is the two dimes ($20$ cents): $1$ pair.
\end{itemize}
Every other pair is at most a dime and a nickel, $15$ cents. So the probability is $\dfrac{6}{15}=\boxed{\dfrac{2}{5}}$.

A $365$-day year is $52$ weeks and $1$ day, and a $366$-day leap year is $52$ weeks and $2$ days. So January 1 moves forward one weekday after a common year and two after a leap year. Working mod $7$:
\begin{itemize}
\item 2019: Tuesday (2018 is common);
\item 2020: Wednesday (2019 is common);
\item 2021: Friday (2020 is a leap year, $+2$);
\item 2022: Saturday; 2023: Sunday; 2024: Monday.
\end{itemize}
So the answer is $\boxed{2024}$. (Total shift $1+1+2+1+1+1=7\equiv0\pmod7$.)

Ted circles the correct answer in two disjoint ways:
\begin{itemize}
\item he identifies the correct answer and circles what he intends: $0.9\cdot0.75=0.675$;
\item he identifies the wrong answer but circles the opposite, which is the correct one: $0.1\cdot0.25=0.025$.
\end{itemize}
The probability is $0.675+0.025=0.7=\boxed{70\%}$.

question:
A standard deck has $52$ cards: $13$ ranks (Ace, $2,3,\dots,10$, Jack, Queen, King) in each of the $4$ suits. A hand is an unordered set of cards.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many $6$-card hands have all their cards in at most two suits?
\item[(b)] How many $6$-card hands have at most $2$ different ranks among their cards?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
An affine map (a linear map followed by a translation) carries any triangle to any other, preserves midpoints, centroids and the ratio of areas of figures. So we may compute with the convenient triangle $A=(0,0)$, $B=(6,0)$, $C=(0,6)$, of area $18$. Then $O=(2,2)$, $D=(3,3)$, $E=(3,0)$, $F=(0,3)$, and the six centroids are
\[
\begin{aligned}
&AFO:\ \left(\dfrac{2}{3},\dfrac{5}{3}\right),\quad FOC:\ \left(\dfrac{2}{3},\dfrac{11}{3}\right),\quad COD:\ \left(\dfrac{5}{3},\dfrac{11}{3}\right),\\
&DOB:\ \left(\dfrac{11}{3},\dfrac{5}{3}\right),\quad BOE:\ \left(\dfrac{11}{3},\dfrac{2}{3}\right),\quad EOA:\ \left(\dfrac{5}{3},\dfrac{2}{3}\right).
\end{aligned}
\]
Multiplying all coordinates by $3$ (which multiplies areas by $9$) gives the convex hexagon $(2,5),(2,11),(5,11),(11,5),(11,2),(5,2)$. By the shoelace formula its area is
\[
\dfrac{1}{2}\left|12-33-96-33+12+21\right|=\dfrac{117}{2},
\]
so the hexagon itself has area $\dfrac{117}{18}=\dfrac{13}{2}$. The ratio is
\[
\dfrac{\dfrac{13}{2}}{18}=\boxed{\dfrac{13}{36}} .
\]

