By the law of cosines, the side $s$ of the inner hexagon satisfies
\[
s^2=XY^2=3^2+1^2-2\cdot3\cdot1\cos120^\circ=9+1+3=13 .
\]
Regular hexagons are similar, so the ratio of their areas is the square of the ratio of their sides:
\[
\dfrac{s^2}{4^2}=\boxed{\dfrac{13}{16}} .
\]

Last digits of powers repeat with period dividing $4$ (from the exponent $1$ on): $n^{k+4}\equiv n^k\pmod{10}$ for $k\ge1$. Since $9102\equiv2\pmod4$, the last digit of $n^{9102}$ is that of $n^2$. A square ends in $9$ exactly when $n$ ends in $3$ or $7$ ($3^2=9$, $7^2=49$; the other digits give $0,1,4,5,6$).

Since $2019\equiv3\pmod4$, the last digit of $n^{2019}$ is that of $n^3$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $n$ ending in $3$: $3^3=27$, last digit $7$;
\item $n$ ending in $7$: $7^3=343$, last digit $3$.
\end{itemize}
Both cases occur ($n=3$: $3^{9102}$ ends in $9$; $n=7$: likewise). So the last digit of $n^{2019}$ is $\boxed{3\text{ or }7}$, and it is not determined uniquely.

