Let the circle have radius $r$. The inscribed octagon has circumradius $r$. The circumscribed octagon has inradius $r$; its circumradius is $\dfrac{r}{\cos22.5^\circ}$ (the half-angle at the centre between a vertex and the midpoint of a side is $22.5^\circ$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] I,O; real R=1/cos(pi/8);
for(int k=0;k<8;++k){I.push(dir(22.5+45k)); O.push(R*dir(45k));}
draw(unitcircle,gray(0.5));
draw(O[0]--O[1]--O[2]--O[3]--O[4]--O[5]--O[6]--O[7]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(I[0]--I[1]--I[2]--I[3]--I[4]--I[5]--I[6]--I[7]--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
pair Mo=(1,0);
draw((0,0)--O[0],dashed);draw((0,0)--I[0],blue+dashed);
dot((0,0));label("$O$",(0,0),W);
label("$r$",I[0]/2,N,blue);
label("$\dfrac{r}{\cos 22.5^\circ}$",O[0],E,fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The two octagons are similar (both regular), so the ratio of their areas is the square of the ratio of their circumradii:
\[
\left(r\div\dfrac{r}{\cos22.5^\circ}\right)^2=\cos^2 22.5^\circ=\dfrac{1+\cos45^\circ}{2}=\dfrac{1+\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}}{2}=\boxed{\dfrac{2+\sqrt2}{4}}\approx0.854 .
\]

question:
Let $a$ and $b$ be constants with $a\ne0$. Simplify each of the following:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $(ax-b)+(ax-b)$;
\item[(b)] $(ax-b)-(ax-b)$;
\item[(c)] $(ax-b)(ax-b)$;
\item[(d)] $\dfrac{ax-b}{ax-b}$, stating for which $x$ it is defined.
\end{enumerate}

A pair $(a,b)$ of nonzero real numbers is called \emph{interesting} if $a+b$ and $\dfrac{1}{a}+\dfrac{1}{b}$ are both nonzero integers.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that there are infinitely many interesting pairs.
\item[(b)] Find all interesting pairs for which at least one of $a^2$, $b^2$ is rational.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the quadrilateral be $ABCD$ with arcs $AB,BC,CD,DA$ (each not containing the other vertices) of measures $\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta$, so $\alpha+\beta+\gamma+\delta=360^\circ$. Let $P_1$ be a point on arc $AB$, and so on.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real[] t={100,10,-110,-170};
string[] nm={"$A$","$B$","$C$","$D$"};
pair[] V; for(int i=0;i<4;++i) V.push(dir(t[i]));
real[] m={55,-50,-140,135};
pair[] P; for(int i=0;i<4;++i) P.push(dir(m[i]));
draw(unitcircle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
pen[] c={red,blue,deepgreen,orange};
for(int i=0;i<4;++i){draw(V[i]--P[i]--V[(i+1)%4],c[i]); dot(P[i],c[i]); label("$P_{"+string(i+1)+"}$",P[i],1.6*dir(m[i]),c[i]);}
for(int i=0;i<4;++i){dot(V[i]);label(nm[i],V[i],1.6*dir(t[i]));}
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The angle $\angle AP_1B$ has its vertex on arc $AB$, so it subtends the \emph{other} arc from $A$ to $B$, of measure $360^\circ-\alpha$; hence $\angle AP_1B=\dfrac{360^\circ-\alpha}{2}$. Similarly for the other three. The sum is
\[
\dfrac{4\cdot360^\circ-(\alpha+\beta+\gamma+\delta)}{2}=\dfrac{1440^\circ-360^\circ}{2}=540^\circ ,
\]
answer $\boxed{\textbf{(B)}\ 540^\circ}$.

Use coordinates $(x,y)$ with $x,y\in\{0,1,2\}$. Two lattice points are at distance $2$ only when they differ by $(\pm2,0)$ or $(0,\pm2)$, so the segments of length $2$ are the $3$ full rows and the $3$ full columns of the array: $6$ segments.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<3;++i) for(int j=0;j<3;++j) dot((i,j));
draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(0,1)--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,2)--(2,2)--(2,0),red+dashed);draw((0,2)--(2,0),red+dashed);
label("$2$",(1,0),S,blue);label("$2$",(1,2),N,red);label("$2$",(2,1),E,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

For each such segment, the third vertex can be any of the $9-3=6$ points not on its line, giving $6\cdot6=36$ (segment, triangle) pairs. A triangle is counted twice exactly when it has two sides of length $2$; these are the right triangles with legs along a full row and a full column meeting at a corner of the array (dashed above), one for each of the $4$ corners. No triangle has three sides of length $2$. Hence the number of triangles is
\[
36-4=\boxed{32}.
\]
Checked by computer.

question:
Two distinct lines pass through the centre of three concentric circles of radii $3$, $2$ and $1$. The lines divide each of the three regions (the inner disc, the middle ring and the outer ring) into four sectors: two in the acute vertical angles and two in the obtuse vertical angles. The shaded region consists of the parts of the outer ring and of the inner disc lying in the acute angles, together with the parts of the middle ring lying in the obtuse angles, as in the figure.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill(arc((0,0),3,-12.857142857142858,12.857142857142858)--arc((0,0),2,12.857142857142858,-12.857142857142858)--cycle,gray(0.75));
fill(arc((0,0),3,167.14285714285714,192.85714285714286)--arc((0,0),2,192.85714285714286,167.14285714285714)--cycle,gray(0.75));
fill(arc((0,0),2,12.857142857142858,167.14285714285714)--arc((0,0),1,167.14285714285714,12.857142857142858)--cycle,gray(0.75));
fill(arc((0,0),2,192.85714285714286,347.14285714285717)--arc((0,0),1,347.14285714285717,192.85714285714286)--cycle,gray(0.75));
fill((0,0)--arc((0,0),1,-12.857142857142858,12.857142857142858)--cycle,gray(0.75));
fill((0,0)--arc((0,0),1,180-12.857142857142858,180+12.857142857142858)--cycle,gray(0.75));
draw(circle((0,0),1));draw(circle((0,0),2));draw(circle((0,0),3));
draw(3.3*dir(12.857142857142858)--3.3*dir(192.85714285714286));draw(3.3*dir(-12.857142857142858)--3.3*dir(167.14285714285714));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The area of the shaded region is $\dfrac{8}{13}$ of the area of the unshaded region. What is the radian measure of the acute angle formed by the two lines?

$\textbf{(A)}\ \dfrac{\pi}{8}\qquad\textbf{(B)}\ \dfrac{\pi}{7}\qquad\textbf{(C)}\ \dfrac{\pi}{6}\qquad\textbf{(D)}\ \dfrac{\pi}{5}\qquad\textbf{(E)}\ \dfrac{\pi}{4}$

solution:
Let $\theta$ be the acute angle. The two acute sectors together make up the fraction $\dfrac{2\theta}{2\pi}=\dfrac{\theta}{\pi}$ of each region, and the obtuse sectors the fraction $1-\dfrac{\theta}{\pi}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill(arc((0,0),3,-12.857142857142858,12.857142857142858)--arc((0,0),2,12.857142857142858,-12.857142857142858)--cycle,gray(0.75));
fill(arc((0,0),3,167.14285714285714,192.85714285714286)--arc((0,0),2,192.85714285714286,167.14285714285714)--cycle,gray(0.75));
fill(arc((0,0),2,12.857142857142858,167.14285714285714)--arc((0,0),1,167.14285714285714,12.857142857142858)--cycle,gray(0.75));
fill(arc((0,0),2,192.85714285714286,347.14285714285717)--arc((0,0),1,347.14285714285717,192.85714285714286)--cycle,gray(0.75));
fill((0,0)--arc((0,0),1,-12.857142857142858,12.857142857142858)--cycle,gray(0.75));
fill((0,0)--arc((0,0),1,180-12.857142857142858,180+12.857142857142858)--cycle,gray(0.75));
draw(circle((0,0),1));draw(circle((0,0),2));draw(circle((0,0),3));
draw(3.3*dir(12.857142857142858)--3.3*dir(192.85714285714286));draw(3.3*dir(-12.857142857142858)--3.3*dir(167.14285714285714));
draw(arc((0,0),2.5,-12.857142857142858,12.857142857142858),Arrows(TeXHead));label("$\theta$",(2.5,0),W,fontsize(9pt));label("$3$",3*dir(60),NE);label("$2$",2*dir(75),N);label("$1$",(0,0.95),S,fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The outer ring has area $9\pi-4\pi=5\pi$, the middle ring $4\pi-\pi=3\pi$ and the inner disc $\pi$. So the shaded area is
\[
5\pi\cdot\dfrac{\theta}{\pi}+3\pi\left(1-\dfrac{\theta}{\pi}\right)+\pi\cdot\dfrac{\theta}{\pi}=3\theta+3\pi .
\]
The total area is $9\pi$ and shaded $=\dfrac{8}{13}\,$unshaded, so shaded $=\dfrac{8}{21}\cdot9\pi=\dfrac{24\pi}{7}$. Then $3\theta+3\pi=\dfrac{24\pi}{7}$ gives $\theta=\boxed{\textbf{(B)}\ \dfrac{\pi}{7}}$.

question:
A student claims: ``If triangles $ABC$ and $DEF$ satisfy $AB=DE$, $AC=DF$ and $\angle B=\angle E$, then they are congruent.''
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the claim is false in general.
\item[(b)] Prove that the claim is true under the extra assumption $AC\ge AB$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Take $B=(0,0)$ and $A=(\sqrt3,1)$, so $AB=2$ and $AB$ makes a $30^\circ$ angle with the $x$-axis. The points of the $x$-axis at distance $\sqrt2$ from $A$ satisfy $(x-\sqrt3)^2+1=2$, i.e. $x=\sqrt3\pm1$; call them $C_1$ and $C_2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),A=(sqrt(3),1),C1=(sqrt(3)-1,0),C2=(sqrt(3)+1,0);
draw(B--C2,linewidth(0.9));draw(B--A,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C1,blue+linewidth(0.9));draw(A--C2,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(B,0.45,0,30));label("$30^\circ$",B,(5,1.4),fontsize(8pt));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C1);dot(C2);
label("$B$",B,SW);label("$A$",A,N);label("$C_1$",C1,S,blue);label("$C_2$",C2,SE,red);
label("$2$",(A+B)/2,NW);label("$\sqrt2$",(A+C1)/2,W,blue);label("$\sqrt2$",(A+C2)/2,E,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Triangles $ABC_1$ and $ABC_2$ both have $AB=2$, $AC=\sqrt2$ and $\angle B=30^\circ$, but $BC_1=\sqrt3-1\ne\sqrt3+1=BC_2$, so they are not congruent. Hence two sides and a non-included angle do not determine a triangle (the ``ambiguous case'').

\textbf{(b)} Put $c=AB$, $b=AC$, $x=BC$ and $\beta=\angle B$. By the law of cosines,
\[
b^2=c^2+x^2-2cx\cos\beta\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x^2-(2c\cos\beta)\,x+(c^2-b^2)=0 .
\]
So $BC$ is a positive root of this quadratic, whose coefficients depend only on $b$, $c$, $\beta$. The product of its roots is $c^2-b^2\le0$ when $b\ge c$. If $b>c$ the roots have opposite signs; if $b=c$ the roots are $0$ and $2c\cos\beta$. In either case there is at most one positive root, so $BC$ is determined by $b,c,\beta$. Applying this to both triangles gives $BC=EF$, and then the triangles are congruent by SSS. $\blacksquare$

question:
Consider the equation $2x-8=0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why it is a simple (linear) equation in $x$.
\item[(b)] Find its root, and show that $-7$ is \emph{not} a root.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Since $AB=AC$, $\angle C=\angle B=55^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(2,0),A=(1,tan(55*pi/180)),D=(1,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));draw(A--D,dashed);
draw((1,0)--(1,0.13)--(0.87,0.13)--(0.87,0));
draw(arc(B,0.3,0,55));label("$55^\circ$",B+(0.45,0.16),fontsize(8pt));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);
draw(arc(A,0.5,270,305));label("$35^\circ$",A+(0.26,-0.78),fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

In the right triangle $ADC$, $\angle ADC=90^\circ$, so
\[
\angle CAD=180^\circ-90^\circ-55^\circ=\boxed{35^\circ}.
\]

Let the largest circle have radius $R$. A circle of radius $r$ tangent to both sides at a corner has its centre on the diagonal, at distance $r\sqrt2$ from the corner. So the three centres lie on the diagonal (figure shows the corner, magnified).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=1, a=0.1715728752538097, s=0.029437251522859347;
draw((0,0)--(0.6,0),linewidth(0.9));draw((0,0)--(0,0.6),linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc((1,1),1,192,258),linewidth(0.8));
draw(circle((a,a),a),blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(circle((s,s),s),red+linewidth(0.8));
draw((0,0)--(0.6,0.6),dashed+gray(0.5));
dot((a,a),blue);dot((s,s),red);
label("big circle (radius $R$)",(0.47,0.76),fontsize(8pt));
label("$r_1$",(a,a),E,blue+fontsize(8pt));
label("$r_2$",(s,0),(1.2,-1.6),red+fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The big circle's centre is at distance $R\sqrt2$ from the corner, and its nearest point on the diagonal at distance $R\sqrt2-R$. The medium circle (radius $r_1$) touches it from the corner side, so
\[
r_1\sqrt2+r_1=R\sqrt2-R\quad\Longrightarrow\quad r_1=R\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt2-1}{\sqrt2+1}=(3-2\sqrt2)R .
\]
The same argument with the medium circle in place of the big one gives $r_2=(3-2\sqrt2)r_1=(3-2\sqrt2)^2R$. The ratio of areas is
\[
\left(\dfrac{r_2}{R}\right)^2=(3-2\sqrt2)^4=(17-12\sqrt2)^2=\boxed{577-408\sqrt2}\approx0.00075 .
\]

Each tree has $\dfrac{39}{3}=13$ apples. The eight branch counts are distinct positive integers with sum $39$. The smallest possible sum is $1+2+\cdots+8=36$, so the counts are $1,2,\dots,8$ with a total of $3$ added, keeping them distinct; the possible sets are
\[
\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,11\},\quad \{1,2,3,4,5,6,8,10\},\quad \{1,2,3,4,5,7,8,9\}
\]
(add $3$ to the largest; $1$ and $2$ to the two largest; or $1$ to each of the three largest). Now split each set into a pair and two triples, each summing to $13$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $\{1,\dots,7,11\}$: $11$ can only go with $2$ (a triple would need $1+1$). The rest $\{1,3,4,5,6,7\}$ splits only as $\{1,5,7\},\{3,4,6\}$.
\item $\{1,\dots,6,8,10\}$: either $\{3,10\}$ is the pair, and then $\{1,2,4,5,6,8\}$ splits only as $\{1,4,8\},\{2,5,6\}$; or $\{1,2,10\}$ is a triple, and then the pair is $\{5,8\}$ and the other triple $\{3,4,6\}$.
\item $\{1,\dots,5,7,8,9\}$: the pair is $\{4,9\}$ or $\{5,8\}$. With $\{4,9\}$ the rest splits as $\{2,3,8\},\{1,5,7\}$; with $\{5,8\}$ the rest splits as $\{1,3,9\},\{2,4,7\}$.
\end{itemize}
So there are exactly five possibilities (two-branch tree; three-branch trees):
\[
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
&\{2,11\};\ \{1,5,7\},\{3,4,6\}\qquad &&\{3,10\};\ \{1,4,8\},\{2,5,6\}\qquad &&\{5,8\};\ \{1,2,10\},\{3,4,6\}\\
&\{4,9\};\ \{1,5,7\},\{2,3,8\}\qquad &&\{5,8\};\ \{1,3,9\},\{2,4,7\}
\end{aligned}}
\]
Checked by computer.

Four-digit numbers are $1000,\dots,9999$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $A$ has four digits but $A+20$ does not: then $A\le9999<A+20$, so $9980\le A\le9999$, giving $20$ values.
\item $A+20$ has four digits but $A$ does not: then $A<1000\le A+20$, so $980\le A\le999$, giving $20$ values.
\end{itemize}
The two cases do not overlap, so there are $20+20=\boxed{40}$ such numbers.

Let $AC=16$, $AB=15$, $BC=17$, and let the bisector from $B$ meet $AC$ at $D$, with $BD=\ell$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),C=(16,0),B=(6,sqrt(189)),D=(7.5,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));draw(B--D,blue+linewidth(1));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$B$",B,N);label("$D$",D,S);
label("$15$",(A+B)/2,NW);label("$17$",(B+C)/2,NE);
label("$7.5$",(A+D)/2,S);label("$8.5$",(D+C)/2,S);
label("$\ell$",(B+D)/2+(0.15,0),E,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

By the angle bisector theorem, $\dfrac{AD}{DC}=\dfrac{AB}{BC}=\dfrac{15}{17}$, so $AD=\dfrac{15}{32}\cdot16=7.5$ and $DC=8.5$. Stewart's theorem for cevian $BD$ gives
\[
AB^2\cdot DC+BC^2\cdot AD=AC\left(\ell^2+AD\cdot DC\right),
\]
\[
225\cdot8.5+289\cdot7.5=16\left(\ell^2+63.75\right)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad 4080=16\ell^2+1020\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\ell^2=\dfrac{765}{4}.
\]
Hence $\ell=\boxed{\dfrac{3\sqrt{85}}{2}}\approx13.83$.

This is the general formula for the bisector from the vertex between sides $p$ and $q$ to the opposite side $s$:
\[
\ell^2=pq\left(1-\dfrac{s^2}{(p+q)^2}\right),\qquad\text{here } \ell^2=255\cdot\dfrac{3}{4}=\dfrac{765}{4} .
\]

The data are consistent with the power of the point $E$: $AE\cdot EB=20=CE\cdot ED$. Use coordinates with $E$ at the origin, $AB$ on the $x$-axis and $CD$ on the $y$-axis: $A=(-4,0)$, $B=(5,0)$, $C=(0,10)$, $D=(0,-2)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-4,0),B=(5,0),C=(0,10),D=(0,-2),O=(0.5,4);real R=sqrt(36.25);
draw(circle(O,R));draw(A--B,linewidth(1));draw(C--D,linewidth(1));
draw(O--(0,4),red+linewidth(1));draw(O--(0.5,0),dashed);
draw((0,0)--(0.5,0)--(0.5,0.5)--(0,0.5)--cycle,gray);
draw((0,4)--(0.5,4)--(0.5,4.5)--(0,4.5)--cycle,gray);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(O);dot((0,0));
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,S);label("$O$",O,E);label("$E$",(0,0),SW);
label("$M$",(0,4),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The centre $O$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$, the line $x=\dfrac{1}{2}$, and on the perpendicular bisector of $CD$, the line $y=4$. So $O=\left(\dfrac{1}{2},4\right)$ (and indeed $OA^2=4.5^2+4^2=36.25=0.5^2+6^2=OC^2$). The chord $CD$ lies on the line $x=0$, so the distance from $O$ to it is $\boxed{\dfrac{1}{2}}$.

Let $X,Y,Z$ be the midpoints of $PT,TW,PW$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(0,0),T=(6,0),W=(1.8,4);
pair X=(P+T)/2,Y=(T+W)/2,Z=(P+W)/2;
fill(X--Y--Z--cycle,palegray);
draw(P--T--W--cycle,linewidth(1));draw(X--Y--Z--cycle);
dot(P);dot(T);dot(W);dot(X);dot(Y);dot(Z);
label("$P$",P,SW);label("$T$",T,SE);label("$W$",W,N);
label("$X$",X,S);label("$Y$",Y,NE);label("$Z$",Z,NW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

By the midline theorem, each side of $XYZ$ is parallel to a side of $PTW$ and half as long. So the three midlines cut $PTW$ into four triangles $PXZ$, $XTY$, $ZYW$, $XYZ$, each similar to $PTW$ with ratio $\dfrac{1}{2}$ and congruent to each other (SSS). Each therefore has area $\dfrac{1}{4}$ of $[PTW]$, so
\[
[PTW]=4\cdot\dfrac{3}{4}=\boxed{3}.
\]

Solving for $y$, the given line is $y=6-2x$, with slope $-2$. A perpendicular line has slope $-\dfrac{1}{-2}=\dfrac{1}{2}$. Through $(8,1)$:
\[
y-1=\dfrac{1}{2}(x-8)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad \boxed{y=\dfrac{1}{2}x-3}\qquad(\text{equivalently } x-2y=6).
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f1(real x){return 6-2x;} real f2(real x){return x/2-3;}
draw((-1,0)--(10,0),Arrow(TeXHead));draw((0,-4.5)--(0,7),Arrow(TeXHead));
label("$x$",(10,0),E);label("$y$",(0,7),N);
draw(graph(f1,-0.4,5.2),linewidth(1));draw(graph(f2,-1,10),blue+linewidth(1));
pair P=(8,1),Q=(3.6,-1.2);dot(P);dot(Q);
label("$(8,1)$",P,NW);
label("$2y+4x=12$",(4.6,-3.2),E);label("$y=\dfrac {x}{2}-3$",(9.9,1.95),N,blue);
pair u=unit((1,-2)),v=unit((2,1));
draw(Q+0.4*u--Q+0.4*u+0.4*v--Q+0.4*v);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Let the Sun have centre $O$ and radius $R$. For a unit vector $u$, the point $P=O+Ru$ has tangent plane $\{X:(X-O)\cdot u=R\}$, so a planet $Q$ is visible from $P$ exactly when $(Q-O)\cdot u>R$, and visible from the opposite point $P'=O-Ru$ exactly when $(Q-O)\cdot u<-R$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=1.5;pair O=(0,0);
filldraw(circle(O,R),palegray);
draw((-4.2,R)--(4.2,R),blue);draw((-4.2,-R)--(4.2,-R),blue);
pair P=(0,R),Pp=(0,-R);dot(P);dot(Pp);dot(O);
label("$P$",P,N);label("$P'$",Pp,S);label("$O$",O,SW);
draw((-4.2,0)--(4.2,0),dashed+gray);
pair Q1=(3.6,0);dot(Q1,red);label("$Q_1$",Q1,N,red);
pair[] Q={(-3.4,2.6),(2.2,2.3),(-1.4,-2.7),(3.3,-2.4),(-3.6,-0.6),(1.0,3.0)};
for(int i=0;i<Q.length;++i) dot(Q[i]);
label("visible from $P$",(3.9,2.95),E,fontsize(8pt));label("visible from $P'$",(3.9,-2.95),E,fontsize(8pt));
label("seen from neither",(-4.2,0.75),W,fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Pick any planet $Q_1$, and choose a unit vector $u$ perpendicular to $Q_1-O$ (possible in space). Then $(Q_1-O)\cdot u=0$, so $Q_1$ is visible from neither $P$ nor $P'$. No planet can be visible from both points, since $(Q-O)\cdot u$ cannot be both $>R$ and $<-R$. Hence the planets visible from $P$ and those visible from $P'$ are disjoint sets drawn from the other nine planets, and their sizes add up to at most $9$.

So one of $P$, $P'$ sees at most $\left\lfloor\dfrac{9}{2}\right\rfloor=4$ planets, which is fewer than five. $\blacksquare$

Write the number as $\overline{abc}$ with $1\le a\le9$ and $0\le c\le9$. The middle digit must be $b=\dfrac{a+c}{2}$, which is a digit exactly when $a+c$ is even, i.e. $a$ and $c$ have the same parity; then $b$ is automatically between $0$ and $9$. So we only count pairs $(a,c)$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $a$ odd ($5$ choices) and $c$ odd ($5$ choices): $25$;
\item $a$ even and nonzero ($2,4,6,8$: $4$ choices) and $c$ even ($5$ choices): $20$.
\end{itemize}
Total $25+20=\boxed{45}$, answer $\textbf{(E)}$.

The centres form an equilateral triangle of side $2r$. Each side of the big triangle is tangent to two circles, so it is parallel to the line of their centres at distance $r$; hence the big triangle is equilateral too, with the same centre.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1;pair A=(0,0),B=(2,0),C=(1,sqrt(3));pair G=(A+B+C)/3;
real s=2+2*sqrt(3);real ir=s/(2*sqrt(3));
pair X=G+ir*dir(-90)+(-s/2,0),Y=G+ir*dir(-90)+(s/2,0),Z=G+(0,2*ir);
draw(X--Y--Z--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(circle(A,r));draw(circle(B,r));draw(circle(C,r));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,dashed);dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);
draw(A--(0,-1),blue);label("$r$",(0,-0.5),W,blue);
draw((0,-1)--X,red+linewidth(1.2));
label("$r\sqrt3$",(X+(0,-1))/2,S,red);label("$2r$",(1,-1),S);
label("$30^\circ$",X,(2.6,0.75),fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Look at a side, say the bottom one. Its length consists of the segment of length $2r$ between the two tangency points (directly below the two centres) plus two corner pieces. At a corner, the angle of the big triangle is $60^\circ$ and the line from the vertex to the nearby centre bisects it, so the piece from the vertex to the tangency point is $\dfrac{r}{\tan30^\circ}=r\sqrt3$. So the side length is
\[
s=2r+2r\sqrt3=2r\left(1+\sqrt3\right),
\]
and the area is
\[
\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2=\sqrt3\,r^2\left(1+\sqrt3\right)^2=\sqrt3\,r^2\left(4+2\sqrt3\right)=\boxed{\left(6+4\sqrt3\right)r^2}.
\]

\emph{The formula is wrong.} Take $r=R$: the frustum is then a cylinder of volume $\pi R^2h$, but the formula gives $\dfrac{1}{3}\pi h\left(2R^2+2R\right)$, which is different (e.g. $R=3$: $8\pi h$ instead of $9\pi h$). The formula also mixes units: $R^2$ is an area while $R$ is a length.

\emph{Correct formula.} Extend the frustum to a full cone with apex above it, of height $H$; the removed top cone has height $H-h$ and base radius $r$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=2,r=1,h=2.6;real k=0.3;
pair A=(-R,0),B=(R,0),C=(r,h),D=(-r,h),T=(0,h*R/(R-r));
draw(shift((0,0))*scale(R,R*k)*arc((0,0),1,180,360));draw(shift((0,0))*scale(R,R*k)*arc((0,0),1,0,180),dashed);
draw(ellipse((0,h),r,r*k));
draw(A--D);draw(B--C);
draw(D--T,dashed+gray);draw(C--T,dashed+gray);
draw((0,0)--(R,0),blue);label("$R$",(R/2,0),S,blue);
draw((0,h)--(r,h),blue);label("$r$",(r/2,h+0.3),N,blue);
draw((-R-0.4,0)--(-R-0.4,h),Arrows(TeXHead));label("$h$",(-R-0.4,h/2),W);
dot(T);label("apex",T,E,gray);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

By similar triangles $\dfrac{H-h}{H}=\dfrac {r}{R}$, so $H=\dfrac{hR}{R-r}$ and $H-h=\dfrac{hr}{R-r}$ (for $R>r$). Therefore
\[
V=\dfrac{1}{3}\pi R^2H-\dfrac{1}{3}\pi r^2(H-h)=\dfrac{\pi h}{3}\cdot\dfrac{R^3-r^3}{R-r}=\boxed{\dfrac{1}{3}\pi h\left(R^2+Rr+r^2\right)},
\]
using $R^3-r^3=(R-r)(R^2+Rr+r^2)$. The middle term is the product $Rr$, not the sum $R+r$. (With $r=R$ this gives $\pi R^2h$, as it should.)

question:
A costume store sells a crocodile costume for $15$ dollars, a mummy costume for $2019$ dollars, a "Mama" costume for $6$ dollars, and a body pillow for $30$ dollars. Joe bought one of the costumes, and its price (in dollars)
\begin{itemize}
\item is divisible by $3$;
\item is the product of $2$ primes;
\item is a triangular number;
\item cannot be expressed as the difference of two perfect squares (of integers).
\end{itemize}
Which costume did Joe buy?

solution:
Let $m=\overline{bc}$. Since $\overline{abbc}$ has four digits, $32\le m\le99$. The last two digits of $\overline{abbc}$ are $b,c$, so $m^2\equiv m\pmod{100}$, that is, $100\mid m(m-1)$.

The numbers $m$ and $m-1$ are coprime, so $25$ divides one of them and $4$ divides one of them. The cases are:
\begin{itemize}
\item $25\mid m$ and $4\mid m$: then $100\mid m$, impossible for $32\le m\le 99$;
\item $25\mid m-1$ and $4\mid m-1$: then $100\mid m-1$, also impossible;
\item $25\mid m$ and $4\mid m-1$: $m\in\{50,75\}$, but $49$ and $74$ are not multiples of $4$;
\item $25\mid m-1$ and $4\mid m$: $m-1\in\{50,75\}$, and $m=76$ works since $4\mid76$.
\end{itemize}
So $m=76$, and $76^2=5776$ does have the form $\overline{abbc}$, with $a=5$, $b=7$, $c=6$. Hence
\[
(a+b+c)^2=18^2=\boxed{324}.
\]

Let the numbers be $n,n+1,n+2,n+3$ and let $r$ be the remainder of $n$. Since $d\ge100>3$, the remainders go up by $1$ each step except that at most one of them wraps from $d-1$ to $0$. The possible patterns are:
\begin{itemize}
\item no wrap: $r,r+1,r+2,r+3$, sum $4r+6$, which is even and cannot be $983$;
\item wrap after the first: $d-1,0,1,2$, sum $d+2$;
\item wrap after the second: $d-2,d-1,0,1$, sum $2d-2$, even;
\item wrap after the third: $d-3,d-2,d-1,0$, sum $3d-6$, a multiple of $3$, but $983$ is not.
\end{itemize}
So $d+2=983$, $d=981$, and $n\equiv d-1=980\pmod{981}$. Since $981=9\cdot109$, this gives $n\equiv980\pmod{109}$, and $980=8\cdot109+108$. The remainder is $\boxed{108}$.

Let $C$, $M$, $V$ be Capital City, Myhouse and The Village, with $CV=760$ and $CM=210$. By the triangle inequality in triangle $CMV$ (allowing the degenerate collinear cases),
\[
760-210\le MV\le760+210,\qquad\text{i.e. }550\le MV\le970 .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),V=(7.6,0);real a=2.1;
draw(circle(C,a),blue);
pair M1=(a,0),M2=(-a,0),M3=a*dir(70);
draw(C--V,linewidth(1));
draw(M3--V,dashed);
dot(C);dot(V);dot(M1);dot(M2);dot(M3);
label("$C$",C,SW);label("$V$",V,E);
label("$M$",M3,N);label("$M_1$",M1,SE);label("$M_2$",M2,W);
label("$210$",C--M3,W,blue);label("$760$",(4.6,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Every value in this range occurs: as $M$ moves around the circle of radius $210$ about $C$, the distance $MV$ varies continuously from $550$ (at $M_1$, between $C$ and $V$) to $970$ (at $M_2$, with $C$ between $M_2$ and $V$). The other two distances add no restriction, because nothing relates Zouatop or Ourrtown to each other or to $C$: Zouatop can be any point $213$ miles from $M$, and Ourrtown any point $167$ miles from $V$.

So $MV$ can be any whole number from $550$ to $970$: $970-550+1=\boxed{421}$ values.

Let the centres be $A$ and $B$, so $AB=10$, and let the circles meet at $P$ and $Q$. Then $AP=BP=AB=10$, so triangles $ABP$ and $ABQ$ are equilateral and $\angle PAQ=\angle PBQ=120^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(10,0);real r=10;
pair P=(5,5*sqrt(3)),Q=(5,-5*sqrt(3));
fill(arc(A,r,-60,60)--arc(B,r,120,240)--cycle,palegray);
draw(circle(A,r));draw(circle(B,r));
draw(A--P--B--Q--cycle,dashed);draw(A--B);draw(P--Q,dashed+gray);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(P);dot(Q);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$P$",P,N);label("$Q$",Q,S);
label("$10$",(A+B)/2+(-2.6,0),S);label("$10$",(A+P)/2,NW);label("$10$",(B+P)/2,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The chord $PQ$ splits the lens into two congruent circular segments. The segment cut off from the circle about $A$ is the $120^\circ$ sector $APQ$ minus triangle $APQ$:
\[
\dfrac{120}{360}\pi\cdot10^2-\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot10\cdot10\sin120^\circ=\dfrac{100\pi}{3}-25\sqrt3 .
\]
The intersection is twice this:
\[
\boxed{\dfrac{200\pi}{3}-50\sqrt3}\approx122.8 .
\]

The square has side $10$. Let rectangle $i$ have sides $w_i,h_i$; each fits in the square, so $w_i,h_i\le10$, and the areas add up: $\sum w_ih_i=100$.

From $(10-w_i)(10-h_i)\ge0$ we get $100-10(w_i+h_i)+w_ih_i\ge0$, i.e.
\[
w_i+h_i\le10+\dfrac{w_ih_i}{10}.
\]
Summing over the four rectangles,
\[
\sum_{i=1}^4 2(w_i+h_i)\le2\left(40+\dfrac{100}{10}\right)=100 .
\]
Equality needs $(10-w_i)(10-h_i)=0$ for each $i$, i.e. every rectangle has a side of length $10$: cut the square into four strips by three parallel cuts.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(10,0)--(10,10)--(0,10)--cycle,linewidth(1));
for(int i=1;i<4;++i) draw((2.5*i,0)--(2.5*i,10));
label("$10$",(5,10),N);label("$10$",(0,5),W);
label("$x_1$",(1.25,0),S);label("$x_2$",(3.75,0),S);label("$x_3$",(6.25,0),S);label("$x_4$",(8.75,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Then the perimeters are $2(10+x_i)$ with $\sum x_i=10$, total $80+20=100$. So the maximum is $\boxed{100}$, answer $\textbf{(E)}$.

Use coordinates $A=(0,0)$, $B=(16,0)$, $C=(16,12)$, $D=(0,12)$, $S=(4,0)$.

\emph{Lower bound.} Any route can be approximated by a polygonal path, made of pieces with horizontal and vertical displacements $(\Delta x_k,\Delta y_k)$. The route starts and ends at $x=4$ and visits both $x=16$ (wall $BC$) and $x=0$ (wall $DA$), so its total horizontal travel is at least $12+16+4=32$ (going from $4$ to the farther of the two walls, across to the other, and back to $4$, in either order). It starts and ends at $y=0$ and visits $y=12$, so its total vertical travel is at least $24$. By the triangle inequality for vectors (Minkowski),
\[
\text{length}=\sum_k\sqrt{\Delta x_k^2+\Delta y_k^2}\ \ge\ \sqrt{\Bigl(\sum_k|\Delta x_k|\Bigr)^2+\Bigl(\sum_k|\Delta y_k|\Bigr)^2}\ \ge\ \sqrt{32^2+24^2}=40 .
\]

\emph{A route of length 40.} Run $S\to(16,9)\to(12,12)\to(0,3)\to S$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(16,0),C=(16,12),D=(0,12),Sp=(4,0);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
pair P1=(16,9),P2=(12,12),P3=(0,3);
draw(Sp--P1--P2--P3--cycle,blue+linewidth(1.1));
dot(Sp);dot(P1);dot(P2);dot(P3);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$S$",Sp,S);
label("$(16,9)$",P1,E);label("$(12,12)$",P2,2*N);label("$(0,3)$",P3,W);
label("$15$",(Sp+P1)/2,SE,blue);label("$5$",(P1+P2)/2,SW,blue);label("$15$",(P2+P3)/2,NW,blue);label("$5$",(P3+Sp)/2,SW,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The pieces have lengths $\sqrt{12^2+9^2}=15$, $\sqrt{4^2+3^2}=5$, $\sqrt{12^2+9^2}=15$ and $\sqrt{4^2+3^2}=5$, total $40$. Every piece moves in a direction $(\pm4,\pm3)$, so all the inequalities above are equalities. (It is also the path of a ball bouncing off the walls with equal angles; unfolding the rectangle by reflections turns it into the straight segment from $(4,0)$ to $(36,24)$, of length $\sqrt{32^2+24^2}=40$.)

The shortest route has length $\boxed{40\text{ yards}}$.

The digits come from $\{0,2,4,6,8\}$ and are distinct, so the digit sum is a sum of distinct elements of this set, at most $20$. It must be a positive multiple of $9$ and is even, so it is $18$. The nonzero digits used form a subset of $\{2,4,6,8\}$ with sum $18$; since $2+4+6+8=20$, the omitted nonzero digits sum to $2$, so the omitted one is $2$ and the nonzero digits are exactly $4,6,8$. The digit $0$ may or may not be used, so the digit set is $\{4,6,8\}$ or $\{0,4,6,8\}$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $\{4,6,8\}$: $3!=6$ three-digit numbers.
\item $\{0,4,6,8\}$: $4!-3!=18$ four-digit numbers (the leading digit is not $0$).
\end{itemize}
Total $\boxed{24}$.

The lines meet the $x$-axis at $x=-\dfrac{5}{2}$ and $x=\dfrac{7}{3}$, and meet each other where $2x+5=-3x+7$, i.e. $x=\dfrac{2}{5}$, $y=\dfrac{29}{5}$.

