If $C$ is acute, the inscribed angle theorem gives $\angle AOB=2C$, so $\angle AOM=C$ and $c=2AM=2R\sin C$. If $C$ is obtuse, $C$ lies on the minor arc $AB$, the central angle on the other side is $\angle AOB=360^\circ-2C$, so $\angle AOM=180^\circ-C$ and again $c=2R\sin(180^\circ-C)=2R\sin C$. If $C=90^\circ$, $AB$ is a diameter and $c=2R=2R\sin C$.

Now $K=\dfrac{1}{2}ab\sin C$, and $\sin C=\dfrac {c}{2R}$, so
\[
K=\dfrac{1}{2}ab\cdot\dfrac{c}{2R}=\dfrac{abc}{4R}. \qquad\blacksquare
\]

Use coordinates with $B=(0,0)$, $E=(12,0)$, $A=(0,h)$, $F=(12,h)$, where $h>0$ is the height; then $C=(2,0)$ and $D=(5,0)$.

