The number $600$ is even, so we count $4$-up numbers $\overline{htu}$ from $400$ to $599$: the hundreds digit is $h\in\{4,5\}$.
\begin{itemize}
\item The units digit is odd and not $3$: $u\in\{1,5,7,9\}$.
\item The tens digit is not $2$ or $3$: $t\in\{0,1,4,5,6,7,8,9\}$, $8$ choices.
\end{itemize}
That gives $2\cdot8\cdot4=64$ odd candidates with allowed digits; we remove the multiples of $3$, i.e. those with $h+t+u\equiv0\pmod 3$.

Modulo $3$, the allowed tens digits have residues: $0$ for $\{0,6,9\}$ ($3$ digits), $1$ for $\{1,4,7\}$ ($3$ digits), $2$ for $\{5,8\}$ ($2$ digits). For each pair $(h,u)$ exactly one residue of $t$ is bad, namely $t\equiv-(h+u)$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $h=4$: $u=1,5,7,9$ give $h+u\equiv2,0,2,1$, bad residues $1,0,1,2$, so $3+3+3+2=11$ bad numbers.
\item $h=5$: $u=1,5,7,9$ give $h+u\equiv0,1,0,2$, bad residues $0,2,0,1$, so $3+2+3+3=11$ bad numbers.
\end{itemize}
Therefore the count is $64-22=\boxed{42}$.

