\textbf{Step 2: $EH\perp BF$.} The diagonal $AC$ makes $45^\circ$ with the side $CD$, so $\angle HCE=\angle ACD=45^\circ=\angle HBE$. The points $B$ and $C$ lie on the same side of line $HE$ (the segment $HE$ joins a point of the diagonal $AC$ to a point of side $CD$, and $B$, $C$ are both on the side of it away from $D$). Hence $B$, $C$, $E$, $H$ are concyclic, with $BCEH$ a cyclic quadrilateral, and
\[
\angle BHE=180^\circ-\angle BCE=180^\circ-90^\circ=90^\circ .
\]
So $EH\perp BH$, i.e. $EH\perp BF$.

\textbf{Step 3: $FG\perp BE$.} In the same way $\angle GAF=\angle CAD=45^\circ=\angle GBF$, with $A$ and $B$ on the same side of line $GF$, so $A$, $B$, $G$, $F$ are concyclic and $\angle BGF=180^\circ-\angle BAF=90^\circ$. So $FG\perp BE$.

\textbf{Step 4.} The radius $BT$ is perpendicular to the tangent $EF$. Hence $BT$, $EH$ and $FG$ are the three altitudes of triangle $BEF$ (from $B$, $E$ and $F$ respectively), and the altitudes of a triangle are concurrent, at its orthocentre (red in the figure). $\blacksquare$

Let the other sides be $a\le b\le17$, and put $u=a+b$, $v=b-a$; then $18\le u\le34$, $0\le v$, $u\equiv v\pmod 2$, and $b\le17$ means $u+v\le34$. Heron's formula gives
\[
16K^2=(a+b+17)(a+b-17)(17-a+b)(17+a-b)=(u^2-289)(289-v^2).
\]
\textbf{Parity.} If $u$ were even, then $v$ is even and all four factors $u\pm17$, $17\pm v$ are odd, so $16K^2$ would be odd, impossible. So $u$ and $v$ are odd.

\textbf{Case $u=19$.} Then $u^2-289=72$ and $K^2=\dfrac{9(289-v^2)}{2}$, so $289-v^2$ must be twice a square, with $v$ odd and $v\le15$:
\[
v=1,3,5,\dots,15:\quad 289-v^2=288,280,264,240,208,168,120,64 .
\]
Halving gives $144,140,132,120,104,84,60,32$, and only $144=12^2$ is a square. So $v=1$: $a=9$, $b=10$ and $K^2=9\cdot144$, $K=36$.

\textbf{Case $u\ge21$.} From $v\le34-u$ we get $289-v^2\ge289-(34-u)^2=(u-17)(51-u)$, so
\[
16K^2\ge(u-17)^2(u+17)(51-u).
\]
For $u=21$ this is only $16K^2\ge16\cdot1140$, so look closer: here $K^2=\dfrac{152(289-v^2)}{16}=\dfrac{19(289-v^2)}{2}$ with $v$ odd, $v\le13$; $K<36$ would need $289-v^2<\dfrac{2\cdot1296}{19}<137$, so $v=13$, giving $K^2=19\cdot60=1140$, not a square. For $23\le u\le33$, $(u-17)^2\ge36$ and $(u+17)(51-u)\ge50\cdot18=900$, so $K^2\ge\dfrac{36\cdot900}{16}=2025$ and $K\ge45$.

