Triangle $BEC$ has base $BC$, and its vertex $E$ lies on line $AD\parallel BC$, so its height is $h_1$ and $[BEC]=\dfrac{1}{2}\,BC\cdot h_1=\dfrac{1}{2}[ABCD]$.

Triangle $FDC$ has base $DC$, and its vertex $F$ lies on line $AB\parallel DC$, so its height is $h_2$ and $[FDC]=\dfrac{1}{2}\,DC\cdot h_2=\dfrac{1}{2}[ABCD]$.

Hence $[FDC]=[BEC]$. (This part does not use $BE=DF$.)

Now let $d_1$ and $d_2$ be the distances from $C$ to the lines $BE$ and $DF$. Using $BE$ and $DF$ as bases,
\[
\dfrac{1}{2}\,BE\cdot d_1=[BEC]=[FDC]=\dfrac{1}{2}\,DF\cdot d_2 ,
\]
and since $BE=DF$ we get $d_1=d_2$. $\blacksquare$

First work modulo $5$: $n^2\equiv-1\equiv4\pmod5$ forces $n\equiv2$ or $3\pmod5$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $n=5k+2$: $n^2+1=25k^2+20k+5$, which is divisible by $25$ iff $20k+5\equiv0\pmod{25}$, i.e. $4k+1\equiv0\pmod5$, i.e. $k\equiv1\pmod5$, so $n\equiv7\pmod{25}$.
\item $n=5k+3$: $n^2+1=25k^2+30k+10$, divisible by $25$ iff $6k+2\equiv0\pmod5$, i.e. $k\equiv3\pmod5$, so $n\equiv18\pmod{25}$.
\end{itemize}
So exactly the residues $7$ and $18$ modulo $25$ work. The range $0\le n\le1999$ consists of $80$ complete blocks of $25$, giving $160$ values, and $2000\le n\le2019$ covers residues $0$ to $19$, which include $7$ and $18$: $2$ more. The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(D) }162}$.

If the edges are $a\le b\le c$, the space diagonal has length $\sqrt{a^2+b^2+c^2}$, so $a^2+b^2+c^2=169$ with positive integers.

Squares are $0,1$ or $4$ modulo $8$, and $169\equiv1\pmod8$, so exactly one of $a,b,c$ is odd and the other two are even. Write the even ones as $2p,2q$. The odd square is one of $1,9,25,49,81,121$, so $p^2+q^2=\dfrac{169-\text{odd square}}{4}$ is one of
\[
42,\ 40,\ 36,\ 30,\ 22,\ 12 .
\]
Among these, only $40=2^2+6^2$ is a sum of two positive squares. So the odd edge is $3$ and the even edges are $4$ and $12$: the prism is $3\times4\times12$ (indeed $9+16+144=169$).

