That segment has length $\sqrt{1^2+2^2}=\sqrt5$, and it is achieved: the straight line in the net crosses the shared edge at its midpoint and folds back to a path on the cube. (A path that wanders through more faces can also be unfolded along the faces it visits, and the same estimate holds for the part between the last face touching $P$ and the first face touching $Q$.) The shortest distance is $\boxed{\sqrt5}$.

Place the rectangle with corners $(0,0)$ and $(108,72)$. Since $\gcd(108,72)=36$, the diagonal passes through the lattice points $(3k,2k)$ for $k=0,1,\dots,36$ and through no others: a point $(x,y)$ of the diagonal has $y=\dfrac{2}{3}x$, which is an integer exactly when $3\mid x$. These points cut the diagonal into $36$ congruent pieces, each the diagonal of a $3\times2$ block of squares.

Within one $3\times2$ block, the diagonal from $(0,0)$ to $(3,2)$ crosses the interior vertical lines $x=1,2$ and the interior horizontal line $y=1$ at three different points (none of them a lattice point), and each crossing takes it into a new square. So it passes through $1+3=4$ squares (shaded below).

