Since $BD$ is tangent at $D$, the radius $OD$ is perpendicular to $BD$, i.e. it points along the direction of the other diagonal $AC$, towards $A$'s side. Let the side of the square be $x$ and use axes along $DB$ and perpendicular to it, with $D$ at the origin. The diagonals bisect each other at right angles, so $A$ is at distance $\dfrac {x}{\sqrt2}$ from $D$ along $DB$ and at distance $\dfrac{x}{\sqrt2}$ from $BD$; and $O$ is at distance $r$ from $BD$, directly over $D$. Hence
\[
AO^2=\left(\dfrac {x}{\sqrt2}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac {x}{\sqrt2}-r\right)^2 .
\]
With $u=\dfrac {x}{\sqrt2}$ this reads $u^2+(u-3\sqrt2)^2=45$, i.e. $2u^2-6\sqrt2\,u-27=0$, so
\[
u=\dfrac{6\sqrt2\pm\sqrt{72+216}}{4}=\dfrac{6\sqrt2\pm12\sqrt2}{4}.
\]
The positive root is $u=\dfrac{9\sqrt2}{2}$, so $x=\sqrt2\,u=\boxed{9}$. (Then $AO=\sqrt{45}>r$, so $A$ is outside the circle and the tangents exist.)

Let the rectangle be $ABCD$ with $AB=16$, $BC=12$, and fold triangle $ACD$ over the diagonal $AC$, so that $D$ goes to $D'$. The folded edge $CD'$ crosses side $AB$ at a point $P$, and the overlap is triangle $APC$.

