Let each semicircle have radius $1$. The centre of semicircle $POQ$ is the midpoint $M$ of $PQ$, and since the semicircle passes through $O$, $OM=1$. Also $MQ=1$ and $OM\perp PQ$ ($O$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of the chord $PQ$). So by the Pythagorean theorem the radius of circle $O$ is
\[
OQ=\sqrt{1^2+1^2}=\sqrt2 .
\]
This is the step in the posted solution: the right triangle has legs $OM$ and $MQ$, both equal to the small radius.

