All regular octagons are similar. Multiplying lengths by $3$ multiplies areas by $3^2=9$, so the area is
\[
9(2+2\sqrt2)=18+18\sqrt2=18(1+\sqrt2).
\]
(Directly: the octagon of side $3$ is a square of side $3+2\cdot\dfrac{3}{\sqrt2}=3+3\sqrt2$ with four corner right isosceles triangles of legs $\dfrac{3}{\sqrt2}$ removed, giving $(3+3\sqrt2)^2-4\cdot\dfrac{9}{4}=27+18\sqrt2-9=18+18\sqrt2$.)

