label("$10$",(A+D)/2,W);label("$10$",(B+C)/2,E);
label("$17$",(11,5.87),NW);label("$8$",(6,6.2),E);label("$6$",(3,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Its area is $m h=15\cdot8=\boxed{120}$.

Let the parallelogram be $ABCD$ with diagonals $AC=d_1$ and $BD=d_2$ meeting at $O$. The diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other, so $OA=OC=\dfrac{d_1}{2}$ and $OB=OD=\dfrac{d_2}{2}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0),D=(2,3),C=B+D;pair O=(A+C)/2;
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(A--C,linewidth(0.7));draw(B--D,linewidth(0.7));
draw(arc(O,0.6,degrees(C-O),degrees(D-O)),linewidth(0.6));
label("$\theta$",O+0.95*dir((degrees(C-O)+degrees(D-O))/2));
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$O$",O,S+0.5*E);
label("$\dfrac{d_1}{2}$",(6.15,1.55));label("$\dfrac{d_2}{2}$",(O+D)/2,W+0.4*S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The diagonals cut the parallelogram into triangles $OAB$, $OBC$, $OCD$, $ODA$. Each has two sides $\dfrac{d_1}{2}$ and $\dfrac{d_2}{2}$ with included angle $\theta$ or $180^\circ-\theta$, and $\sin(180^\circ-\theta)=\sin\theta$. So each has area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\dfrac{d_1}{2}\cdot\dfrac{d_2}{2}\sin\theta=\dfrac{d_1d_2\sin\theta}{8}$, and the total area is
\[
4\cdot\dfrac{d_1d_2\sin\theta}{8}=\dfrac{1}{2}d_1d_2\sin\theta .
\]
For $d_1=10$, $d_2=8$, $\theta=30^\circ$: the area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot10\cdot8\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}=\boxed{20}$.

question:
Eight dots are arranged in $2$ rows of $4$, forming a grid of unit squares:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<4;++i) for(int j=0;j<2;++j) dot((i,j),linewidth(4));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

How many isosceles triangles have all three vertices among these dots?

solution:
Put the dots at $(x,y)$ with $x\in\{0,1,2,3\}$ and $y\in\{0,1\}$. The possible distances between dots are $1,2,3,\sqrt2,\sqrt5,\sqrt{10}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
for(int i=0;i<4;++i) for(int j=0;j<2;++j) dot((i,j),linewidth(4));
filldraw((0,0)--(1,0)--(0,1)--cycle,palegray,linewidth(1));
filldraw((1,1)--(3,1)--(2,0)--cycle,paleblue,blue+linewidth(1));
label("$1$",(0.5,0),S);label("$1$",(0,0.5),W);
label("$\sqrt2$",(1.5,0.5),2*W);label("$\sqrt2$",(2.5,0.5),2*E);label("$2$",(2,1.1),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Two of the three vertices lie in the same row, so a triangle has a horizontal side of length $1$, $2$ or $3$ and its third vertex in the other row.
\begin{itemize}
\item Equal sides $1,1$: two perpendicular unit sides of a unit square, giving the right isosceles triangles with legs $1$ (gray). Each of the $3$ unit squares contains $4$ of them: $12$ triangles.
\item Equal sides $\sqrt2,\sqrt2$ with base $2$: the apex is directly above or below the midpoint of a horizontal segment of length $2$ (blue). There are $2$ such segments in each row, so $4$ triangles.
\item Any other pair of equal sides would need two dots at the same distance $\sqrt5$, $\sqrt{10}$, $2$ or $3$ from a third dot, and no dot has two such neighbours in the array. (For example, the only dot at distance $\sqrt5$ from $(1,0)$ is $(3,1)$.)
\end{itemize}
So there are $12+4=\boxed{16}$ isosceles triangles.

Measure times in minutes after 10:00, so Dave's time $D$ and Kathy's time $K$ form a uniform point in the square $[0,120]^2$. They meet exactly when $|D-K|\le15$, the shaded band around the diagonal.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real L=4;
path band=(0,0)--(0.5,0)--(L,L-0.5)--(L,L)--(L-0.5,L)--(0,0.5)--cycle;
fill(band,palegray);
draw((0,0)--(L,0)--(L,L)--(0,L)--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((0.5,0)--(L,L-0.5));draw((0,0.5)--(L-0.5,L));
label("$0$",(0,0),SW);label("$120$",(L,0),S);label("$120$",(0,L),W);
label("$D$",(L/2,0),2*S);label("$K$",(0,L/2),2*W);
label(rotate(45)*Label("$|D-K|\le15$"),(L/2,L/2));
label("$15$",(0.25,0),S,fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The complement consists of two right isosceles triangles with legs $120-15=105$, of total area $105^2$. So the probability is
\[
1-\dfrac{105^2}{120^2}=1-\left(\dfrac{7}{8}\right)^2=\boxed{\dfrac{15}{64}} .
\]

Let $B$ and $Y$ be the arrival times of the bus and of you, in minutes after 12:00; $(B,Y)$ is uniform in $[0,60]^2$. You catch the bus if you arrive while it is waiting ($B\le Y\le B+5$) or it arrives while you are waiting ($Y\le B\le Y+20$). Together these say
\[
B-20\le Y\le B+5 ,
\]
the shaded region (with $B$ horizontal and $Y$ vertical).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=4/60;
path reg=(0,0)--(20*s,0)--(60*s,40*s)--(60*s,60*s)--(55*s,60*s)--(0,5*s)--cycle;
fill(reg,palegray);
draw((0,0)--(4,0)--(4,4)--(0,4)--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((20*s,0)--(60*s,40*s));draw((0,5*s)--(55*s,60*s));
label("$0$",(0,0),SW);label("$60$",(4,0),S);label("$60$",(0,4),W);
label("$B$",(2,0),2*S);label("$Y$",(0,2),2*W);
label("$Y=B-20$",(4,40*s),E);label("$Y=B+5$",(55*s,4),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The two unshaded corner triangles are right isosceles: $Y>B+5$ has legs $55$ and $Y<B-20$ has legs $40$. Their total area is $\dfrac{55^2+40^2}{2}=\dfrac{3025+1600}{2}=2312.5$, so the probability is
\[
1-\dfrac{2312.5}{3600}=\dfrac{1287.5}{3600}=\boxed{\dfrac{103}{288}} .
\]

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that in every triangle (three vertices not on one line) each side is strictly shorter than the sum of the other two.
\item[(b)] Which of the triples $(3,4,5)$, $(2,3,5)$, $(2,3,6)$ are the side lengths of a triangle? Explain what happens with the triple for which the inequality is an equality.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) Let triangle $ABC$ have sides $a=BC$, $b=CA$, $c=AB$, and suppose $b$ is a longest side. Then $\angle B$ is a largest angle. If $\angle A$ or $\angle C$ were at least $90^\circ$, then $\angle B$ would be too, and the angle sum would exceed $180^\circ$. So the angles at $A$ and $C$ are acute, and the foot $H$ of the altitude from $B$ lies strictly between $A$ and $C$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),C=(7,0),B=(2.2,2.6),H=(2.2,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(B--H,dashed);draw(H+(0.25,0)--H+(0.25,0.25)--H+(0,0.25));
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$B$",B,N);label("$H$",H,S);
label("$c$",(A+B)/2,NW);label("$a$",(B+C)/2,NE);label("$b$",(A+C)/2+(0.8,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

In the right triangles $ABH$ and $CBH$ the hypotenuses are longer than the legs, because $BH>0$: $c>AH$ and $a>HC$. Adding, $a+c>AH+HC=b$. The other two inequalities $a+b>c$ and $b+c>a$ are immediate, since $b$ is at least as long as $a$ and $c$ and all sides are positive. So each side is less than the sum of the other two, with strict inequality.

(b) For three positive lengths it is enough to check that the longest is less than the sum of the other two. $(3,4,5)$: $3+4>5$, so it is a triangle. $(2,3,6)$: $2+3<6$, not a triangle. $(2,3,5)$: $2+3=5$, which is not a triangle: by (a) a genuine triangle needs strict inequality, and lengths with $AB+BC=AC$ force $B$ to lie on segment $AC$, a ``flattened'' triangle. So only $\boxed{(3,4,5)}$ works.

The vertices are $V_1=(0,0)$ and $V_2=(0,4)$. The parabolas meet where $x^2=4-x^2$, i.e.\ $x=\pm\sqrt2$, $y=2$: $A=(-\sqrt2,2)$, $B=(\sqrt2,2)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return x^2;} real g(real x){return 4-x^2;}
draw((-2.6,0)--(2.6,0),Arrows(TeXHead));draw((0,-0.6)--(0,4.8),Arrows(TeXHead));
draw(graph(f,-2.1,2.1),blue+linewidth(0.9));draw(graph(g,-2.1,2.1),red+linewidth(0.9));
pair V1=(0,0),V2=(0,4),A=(-sqrt(2),2),B=(sqrt(2),2);
draw(A--V1--B--V2--cycle,linewidth(1));
dot(V1);dot(V2);dot(A);dot(B);
label("$V_1$",V1,SE);label("$V_2$",V2,NE);label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);
label("$y=x^2$",(2.1,4.41),E,blue);label("$y=4-x^2$",(2.1,-0.41),E,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Each side joins $(\pm\sqrt2,2)$ to $(0,0)$ or $(0,4)$, so by the distance formula every side has length $\sqrt{2+4}=\sqrt6$ (the quadrilateral is a rhombus). The perimeter is $\boxed{4\sqrt6}$.

The parallelogram is spanned by the vectors $(x,1)$ and $(1,x)$ (shown for $x=2$).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-0.3,0)--(3.5,0),Arrow(TeXHead));draw((0,-0.3)--(0,3.5),Arrow(TeXHead));
for(int i=1;i<=3;++i){draw((i,-0.06)--(i,0.06));draw((-0.06,i)--(0.06,i));label("$"+string(i)+"$",(i,0),S,fontsize(8pt));label("$"+string(i)+"$",(0,i),W,fontsize(8pt));}
pair O=(0,0),U=(2,1),V=(1,2);
filldraw(O--U--U+V--V--cycle,palegray,linewidth(1));
dot(O);dot(U);dot(V);dot(U+V);
label("$(x,1)$",U,E);label("$(1,x)$",V,N+0.6*W);label("$(x+1,x+1)$",U+V,E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Its area is the absolute value of the determinant: $f(x)=|x\cdot x-1\cdot1|=x^2-1$ for $x\ge2$. By partial fractions, $\dfrac{1}{n^2-1}=\dfrac{1}{2}\left(\dfrac{1}{n-1}-\dfrac{1}{n+1}\right)$, so the sum telescopes:
\[
\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}\dfrac{1}{n^2-1}=\dfrac{1}{2}\left(1+\dfrac{1}{2}\right)=\boxed{\dfrac{3}{4}} .
\]

Unfold the path: each time the ball bounces, reflect the rest of the path in the wall. Between walls the ball always moves from one wall to the other, so the bounces alternate between $y=1$ and $y=-1$. If the first bounce is on $y=1$, the unfolded walls are the lines $y=1$, $y=3$, $y=5$, and DIO's image after three reflections is $D'=(5,6)$; if it is on $y=-1$, the image is $(5,-6)$. The unfolded path is a straight segment of the same length as the real path.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair J=(0,0),Dd=(5,0);
fill((0,-1)--(5.6,-1)--(5.6,1)--(0,1)--cycle,palegray);
draw((0,1)--(5.6,1),linewidth(1.1));draw((0,-1)--(5.6,-1),linewidth(1.1));
draw((0,3)--(5.6,3),dotted);draw((0,5)--(5.6,5),dotted);
pair P1=(5/6,1),P2=(2.5,-1),P3=(25/6,1);
draw(J--P1--P2--P3--Dd,blue+linewidth(1));
draw(J--(5,6),dashed);
dot(J);dot(Dd);dot((5,6));
label("$J(0,0)$",J,W);label("$D(5,0)$",Dd,SE);label("$D'(5,6)$",(5,6),E);
label("$y=1$",(5.6,1),E);label("$y=-1$",(5.6,-1),E);label("$y=3$",(5.6,3),E);label("$y=5$",(5.6,5),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Hence every such path has length $\sqrt{5^2+6^2}=\sqrt{61}$, and this value is attained: the ball bounces at $\left(\dfrac{5}{6},1\right)$, $\left(\dfrac{5}{2},-1\right)$ and $\left(\dfrac{25}{6},1\right)$ (blue). The minimum distance is $\boxed{\sqrt{61}}$.

The conditions give $AD=\dfrac{1}{4}AB$, $BE=\dfrac{1}{4}BC$, $CF=\dfrac{1}{4}CA$, and so $BD=\dfrac{3}{4}AB$, $CE=\dfrac{3}{4}CB$, $AF=\dfrac{3}{4}AC$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.2,4),B=(0,0),C=(6,0);
pair D=A+(B-A)/4,Ee=B+(C-B)/4,F=C+(A-C)/4;
