A radial segment from a point $P$ on the inner circle to the point $Q$ on the outer circle on the same ray has length exactly $6$, and it crosses the middle circle at the point at distance $4$ from $O$. So the minimum length is $\boxed{6}$ feet.

In the $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle $ABC$ (right angle at $B$), $AC=2BC=2\sqrt2$ and $AB=\sqrt3\,BC=\sqrt6$. In triangle $ACD$ the right angle is at $C$ and $\angle DAC=45^\circ$, so it is isosceles: $CD=AC=2\sqrt2$ and $AD=AC\sqrt2=4$.

Since the interiors do not overlap, $D$ is on the other side of $AC$ from $B$, so $\angle DAB=30^\circ+45^\circ=75^\circ$. Put $A=(0,0)$ and $B=(\sqrt6,0)$; then $D=(4\cos75^\circ,4\sin75^\circ)$, and the foot $F$ of the perpendicular has $AF=4\cos75^\circ=\sqrt6-\sqrt2>0$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(sqrt(6),0),C=(sqrt(6),sqrt(2)),D=4*dir(75),F=(4*Cos(75),0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));draw(A--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(D--F,dashed);draw(arc(A,0.4,0,30));draw(arc(A,0.75,30,75));
draw(B+(-0.18,0)--B+(-0.18,0.18)--B+(0,0.18));
draw(F+(0.18,0)--F+(0.18,0.18)--F+(0,0.18));
pair u=unit(A-C),v=unit(D-C);draw(C+0.22*u--C+0.22*(u+v)--C+0.22*v);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,N);label("$F$",F,S);
label("$\sqrt2$",(B+C)/2,E);label("$30^\circ$",A+0.66*dir(13),fontsize(8pt));label("$45^\circ$",A+1.1*dir(55),fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The perpendicular has length
\[
DF=4\sin75^\circ=4\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt6+\sqrt2}{4}=\boxed{\sqrt6+\sqrt2}.
\]

Put $A=(0,0)$, $B=(w,0)$, $C=(w,h)$, $D=(0,h)$ and $P=(x,y)$. Then
\[
PA^2+PC^2=x^2+y^2+(w-x)^2+(h-y)^2=PB^2+PD^2 .
\]
So with $a=PA$, $b=PB$, $c=PC$, $d=PD$ we need distinct positive integers with $a^2+c^2=b^2+d^2=N$. For $1,2,3,4$ no pairing works ($1+16\ne4+9$, $1+9\ne4+16$, $1+4\ne9+16$), so $10$ is impossible.

For any pair with $a^2+c^2=N$ we have $(a+c)^2>N$, so $a+b+c+d>2\sqrt N$. If $N\ge101$ the sum exceeds $2\sqrt{101}>20$. The numbers $N\le100$ that are sums of two distinct positive squares in two different ways are only $65=1^2+8^2=4^2+7^2$ and $85=2^2+9^2=6^2+7^2$ (check the list of sums of two of $1,4,9,\dots,81$). These give sums $1+8+4+7=20$ and $2+9+6+7=24$. Hence the sum is at least $20$.

The value $20$ is attained. Take $x=y=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}$ (so $PA=1$), $w=x+\sqrt{16-y^2}$ and $h=y+\sqrt{49-x^2}$. Then $PB=4$, $PD=7$ and $PC^2=(16-y^2)+(49-x^2)=64$, so $PC=8$, and $P$ is strictly inside.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real x=sqrt(2)/2,y=sqrt(2)/2;
real w=x+sqrt(16-y^2),h=y+sqrt(49-x^2);
pair A=(0,0),B=(w,0),C=(w,h),D=(0,h),P=(x,y);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(P--A);draw(P--B);draw(P--C);draw(P--D);dot(P);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$P$",P,NE);
draw((0.3,0.3)--(-0.5,0.9),gray(0.4)+linewidth(0.4));label("$1$",(-0.5,0.9),W,fontsize(9pt));label("$4$",(P+B)/2,N,fontsize(9pt));
label("$8$",(P+C)/2,SE,fontsize(9pt));label("$7$",(P+D)/2,E,fontsize(9pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The least possible value is $\boxed{20}$.

Translate the triangle so that $A$ is at the origin. Then $B$ and $C$ correspond to the vectors
\[
\vec{AB}=(2134-1232,\ 4123-41224)=(902,\ -37101),\qquad \vec{AC}=(144092879,\ 482940856).
\]

