Since the legs are not parallel, lines $AB$ and $CD$ meet at a point $O$; say $BC$ is the base nearer to $O$ (the other case is the same with the bases exchanged).

\textbf{$O$, $N$, $M$ are collinear.} Let line $OM$ meet $BC$ at $N'$. Because $BC\parallel AD$, triangles $OBN'$ and $OAM$ are similar, as are $OCN'$ and $ODM$, so
\[
\dfrac{BN'}{AM}=\dfrac{ON'}{OM}=\dfrac{CN'}{DM}.
\]
As $AM=MD$, we get $BN'=CN'$, so $N'=N$. Hence line $MN$ is the line $OM$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(4,6), A=(0,0), D=(8,0), B=(2,3), C=(6,3), M=(4,0), Npt=(4,3);
draw(B--O--C,dashed);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(O--M,blue+linewidth(0.9));
real a1=degrees(A-O), a2=degrees(M-O), a3=degrees(D-O);
draw(arc(O,1.6,a1+1.5,a2-1.5)); draw(arc(O,1.6,a2+1.5,a3-1.5));
draw(O+1.42*dir((a1+a2)/2)--O+1.78*dir((a1+a2)/2));
draw(O+1.42*dir((a2+a3)/2)--O+1.78*dir((a2+a3)/2));
dot(O);dot(M);dot(Npt);
label("$O$",O,N); label("$A$",A,SW); label("$D$",D,SE); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,E);
label("$M$",M,S); label("$N$",Npt,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{$OM$ bisects angle $AOD$.} The angles that line $OM$ makes with lines $AB$ and $CD$ are related to $\angle AOM$ and $\angle MOD$, whose sum is $\angle AOD<180^\circ$. If one of them were obtuse, say $\angle AOM>90^\circ$, the angles between the lines would be $180^\circ-\angle AOM$ and $\angle MOD$, and their equality would give $\angle AOM+\angle MOD=180^\circ$, which is impossible. So both are at most $90^\circ$ and the condition says $\angle AOM=\angle MOD$.

\textbf{Conclusion.} In triangle $AOD$ the bisector from $O$ meets $AD$ at its midpoint $M$, so by the angle bisector theorem $\dfrac{OA}{OD}=\dfrac{AM}{MD}=1$, i.e.\ $OA=OD$. By the similarity of triangles $OBC$ and $OAD$, $\dfrac{OB}{OA}=\dfrac{OC}{OD}$, so also $OB=OC$. Therefore
\[
AB=OA-OB=OD-OC=CD,
\]
and the trapezoid is isosceles. $\blacksquare$

Since $AB=AC$ and $H$ is the midpoint of $BC$, the median $AH$ is perpendicular to $BC$. Put $H$ at the origin with
\[
B=(-b,0),\quad C=(b,0),\quad A=(0,a)\qquad(a,b>0).
\]
Line $AC$ is $ax+by=ab$, and $(a,b)$ is a normal vector, so the foot of the perpendicular from $H=(0,0)$ is
\[
E=\dfrac{ab}{a^2+b^2}\,(a,b),\qquad O=\dfrac{E}{2}=\dfrac{ab}{2(a^2+b^2)}\,(a,b).
\]
Let $D=a^2+b^2$. Then
\[
\vec{AO}=\left(\dfrac{a^2b}{2D},\ \dfrac{ab^2}{2D}-a\right)=\dfrac{a}{2D}\left(ab,\ -2a^2-b^2\right),\qquad
\vec{BE}=\left(\dfrac{a^2b}{D}+b,\ \dfrac{ab^2}{D}\right)=\dfrac{b}{D}\left(2a^2+b^2,\ ab\right).
\]
The dot product of $\left(ab,-2a^2-b^2\right)$ and $\left(2a^2+b^2,ab\right)$ is $ab(2a^2+b^2)-(2a^2+b^2)ab=0$. Hence $AO\perp BE$. $\blacksquare$

