Let the triangle be $T=KLM$, and let $\rho$ be the point reflection (half-turn) about $A$, which sends every point $Z$ to $2A-Z$; it is constructed by extending $ZA$ beyond $A$ by its own length.

\textbf{Construction.} Construct $K'=\rho(K)$, $L'=\rho(L)$, $M'=\rho(M)$, and draw triangle $T'=K'L'M'=\rho(T)$. Let $X$ be a point where a side of $T'$ crosses a side of $T$. Draw line $XA$; it meets the perimeter of $T$ again at $Y$. Then $XY$ is the required segment.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair K=(0,0), L=(8,0), M=(2,6), A=(3.2,2.2);
pair K2=2A-K, L2=2A-L, M2=2A-M;
pair X=(5.7,2.3), Y=2A-X;
draw(K2--L2--M2--cycle,gray+dashed);
draw(K--L--M--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(X--Y,red+linewidth(1));
dot(A);dot(X);dot(Y);
label("$K$",K,SW); label("$L$",L,SE); label("$M$",M,N);
label("$K'$",K2,NE,gray); label("$L'$",L2,NW,gray); label("$M'$",M2,S,gray);
label("$A$",A,S); label("$X$",X,E); label("$Y$",Y,W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Such a point $X$ exists.} The triangles $T$ and $T'$ are congruent, so they have equal areas, and $A$ lies inside both. If their boundaries did not meet, the boundary of $T'$ (a connected closed curve) would lie entirely inside $T$ or entirely outside $T$. In the first case $T'\subseteq T$; in the second, $T$ (which is connected, contains $A$ and misses the boundary of $T'$) lies inside $T'$. Either way one triangle contains the other, and equal areas force $T=T'$, in which case every boundary point of $T$ is on the boundary of $T'$. So a common boundary point $X$ exists.

\textbf{Correctness.} Since $X$ lies on the boundary of $T'=\rho(T)$, the point $Y=\rho(X)=2A-X$ lies on the boundary of $T$. Also $X\ne A$ (as $A$ is inside $T$), so $X\ne Y$, and $A=\dfrac{X+Y}{2}$ is the midpoint of $XY$, which lies on line $XA$. Both endpoints are on the perimeter of the triangle. $\blacksquare$

(In the figure the dashed triangle is $T'$; here three such segments exist, and one of them, $XY$, is drawn.)

\textbf{Construction.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Choose a point $P$ on $a$, far enough inside the sheet.
\item Through $P$ construct the line $b'$ parallel to $b$ (for instance, join $P$ to a point $Q$ of $b$ and copy at $P$ the angle that $PQ$ makes with $b$, as corresponding angles).
\item Choose rays from $P$ along $a$ and along $b'$ that form the acute angle between these lines, and reflect the ray along $a$ in the line $b'$: take a point $U$ on the ray along $a$, drop the perpendicular from $U$ to $b'$ and extend it by its own length to $U^*$. Let $c$ be the ray $PU^*$.
\end{enumerate}
Then the angle between the ray along $a$ and the ray $c$ is $2\theta$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real t=14;
pair Z=(10,2.5);
pair da=dir(t), db=dir(-t);
pair a0=Z-10*da/da.x, b0=Z-10*db/db.x;
filldraw((0,0)--(8,0)--(8,5)--(0,5)--cycle,palegray,gray);
pair a8=a0+8*da/da.x, b8=b0+8*db/db.x;
draw(a0--a8,linewidth(1)); draw(b0--b8,linewidth(1));
draw(a8--Z,dotted); draw(b8--Z,dotted);
pair P=a0+6*da/da.x;
pair c=dir(180-3*t);
draw((P-6*db/db.x)--(P+2*db/db.x),blue+dashed);
draw(P--(P+5.2*c),red+linewidth(1));
draw(arc(P,1.4,180-t,180+t),blue);
draw(arc(P,1.8,180-t,180-3*t),red);
draw(arc(Z,1.2,180-t,180+t));
dot(P); dot(Z);
label("$P$",P,SE); label("$Z$",Z,E);
label("$a$",a0,W); label("$b$",b0,W);
label("$b'$",P-6*db/db.x,W,blue);
label("$c$",P+5.2*c,N,red);
label("$\theta$",P+1.4*dir(180),W,blue);
label("$\theta$",P+1.8*dir(180-2*t),NW,red);
label("$\theta$",Z+1.2*dir(180),W);
label("sheet",(7.2,4.6));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Proof.} Since $b'\parallel b$ and $a$ crosses both, the angles that $a$ makes with $b$ and with $b'$ are corresponding angles, so the acute angle between $a$ and $b'$ at $P$ equals $\theta$. The reflection in $b'$ fixes $P$ and preserves angles, so the ray $c$ makes the same angle $\theta$ with $b'$, on the other side of $b'$. Hence the angle between $a$ and $c$ at $P$ is $\theta+\theta=2\theta$ (less than $180^\circ$ because $\theta<90^\circ$). Every step uses only points near $P$, so the whole construction stays on the sheet. $\blacksquare$

Let $O$ be the centre of the circle $\omega$. For $i=1,\dots,5$ let $m_i$ be the line through $O$ perpendicular to $\ell_i$, and let $\sigma_i$ be the reflection in $m_i$.

\textbf{Key fact.} For distinct points $X,Y$ on $\omega$: $XY\parallel\ell_i$ if and only if $Y=\sigma_i(X)$. Indeed, the perpendicular bisector of the chord $XY$ passes through $O$; it is perpendicular to $\ell_i$ exactly when it is $m_i$, i.e.\ when $Y$ is the mirror image of $X$ in $m_i$. In particular $\sigma_i(X)$ is the second intersection of $\omega$ with the line through $X$ parallel to $\ell_i$, which can be drawn with straightedge and compass.

\textbf{Reformulation.} We need $A_2=\sigma_1(A_1)$, $A_3=\sigma_2(A_2)$, $A_4=\sigma_3(A_3)$, $A_5=\sigma_4(A_4)$ and $A_1=\sigma_5(A_5)$, that is,
\[
A_1=f(A_1),\qquad f=\sigma_5\circ\sigma_4\circ\sigma_3\circ\sigma_2\circ\sigma_1 .
\]
The product of two reflections in lines through $O$ is a rotation about $O$, so $f=(\sigma_5\sigma_4)(\sigma_3\sigma_2)\sigma_1$ is a rotation about $O$ composed with a reflection in a line through $O$. Such a map is an orientation-reversing isometry fixing $O$, so it is a reflection in some line $m$ through $O$. The points of $\omega$ fixed by $f$ are exactly the two points where $m$ meets $\omega$.

\textbf{Construction.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Take any point $X$ on $\omega$ and draw successively the chords through $X$ parallel to $\ell_1,\ell_2,\ell_3,\ell_4,\ell_5$, obtaining $X_1=\sigma_1(X)$, \dots, $X_5=f(X)$.
\item If $X_5\ne X$, the line $m$ is the perpendicular bisector of $XX_5$ (it passes through $O$); if $X_5=X$, then $m$ is the line $OX$.
\item Let $A_1$ be either point where $m$ meets $\omega$, and draw from $A_1$ the chord parallel to $\ell_1$ to get $A_2$, from $A_2$ the chord parallel to $\ell_2$ to get $A_3$, and so on up to $A_5$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real[] th={100,170,235,300,25};
pair[] V; for(int i=0;i<5;++i) V.push(dir(th[i]));
pair O=(0,0);
draw(unitcircle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(1.25*V[0]--(-1.25*V[0]),red+dashed);
pair m1=dir((th[0]+th[1])/2);
draw(1.25*m1--(-1.25*m1),blue+dotted);
dot(O);
for(int i=0;i<5;++i){dot(V[i]); label("$A_"+string(i+1)+"$",V[i],1.4*V[i]);}
label("$O$",O,NE);
label("$m$",-1.25*V[0],S,red);
label("$m_1$",1.25*m1,NW,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Proof.} By the key fact $A_1A_2,\dots,A_4A_5$ are parallel to $\ell_1,\dots,\ell_4$, and $\sigma_5(A_5)=f(A_1)=A_1$ because $A_1$ lies on the axis $m$ of $f$; so $A_5A_1\parallel\ell_5$ as well. $\blacksquare$

The two points of $m\cap\omega$ give the two solutions in general. (The pentagon may be self-intersecting, and a side degenerates to a point when the chord through some $A_i$ parallel to $\ell_i$ is a tangent; the problem asks only for the parallel sides.) In the figure, $m$ is the dashed axis through $A_1$, and the dotted line $m_1$ is the axis of $\sigma_1$, the perpendicular bisector of $A_1A_2$.

The sides of the square are equal and are divided in the same ratio, so
