The four corner triangles $SAP$, $PBQ$, $QCR$, $RDS$ each have a right angle between legs $b$ and $a$, so they are congruent (SAS). Hence $SP=PQ=QR=RS$, and $PQRS$ is a rhombus.

From $\triangle SAP\cong\triangle PBQ$ we get $\angle ASP=\angle BPQ$. In triangle $SAP$, $\angle ASP+\angle APS=90^\circ$, so
\[
\angle APS+\angle BPQ=90^\circ\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\angle SPQ=180^\circ-90^\circ=90^\circ .
\]
A rhombus with a right angle is a square, so $PQRS$ is a square. $\blacksquare$ (Alternatively: the rotation by $90^\circ$ about the centre of $ABCD$ maps $A\to B\to C\to D$ and hence $P\to Q\to R\to S\to P$.)

The condition for the square says $N^2\equiv N\pmod{1000}$, i.e.\ $1000\mid N(N-1)$. Conversely, if $N^2\equiv N$, then by induction $N^{k+1}=N^k\cdot N\equiv N\cdot N\equiv N\pmod{1000}$ for every $k\ge1$. So we need exactly $N(N-1)\equiv0\pmod{1000}$.

As $1000=8\cdot125$ and $N$, $N-1$ are coprime, $8$ divides $N$ or $N-1$, and $125$ divides $N$ or $N-1$. By the Chinese remainder theorem this gives four classes modulo $1000$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $N\equiv0\pmod8$ and $N\equiv0\pmod{125}$: $N\equiv0\pmod{1000}$;
\item $N\equiv1\pmod8$ and $N\equiv1\pmod{125}$: $N\equiv1\pmod{1000}$;
\item $N\equiv0\pmod8$ and $N\equiv1\pmod{125}$: $N\equiv376\pmod{1000}$ (as $376=8\cdot47=3\cdot125+1$);
\item $N\equiv1\pmod8$ and $N\equiv0\pmod{125}$: $N\equiv625\pmod{1000}$ (as $625=5\cdot125=78\cdot8+1$).
\end{itemize}
The only three-digit numbers among these are $\boxed{376\text{ and }625}$.

There are $64-24=40$ hexagons. Count the polygon edges: $24\cdot5+40\cdot6=120+240=360$. Every seam joins exactly two polygon edges, one from each of two neighbouring polygons, so each seam is counted twice:

