label("pentagon",(0.5,-0.69));
label("one seam",(1.25,0),E,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\[
\text{number of seams}=\dfrac{360}{2}=\boxed{180}.
\]
(Remark: a ball sewn from pentagons and hexagons with exactly three polygons at every corner must have exactly $12$ pentagons, by Euler's formula; the ball described here must have some corners where more polygons meet. This does not affect the count of seams.)

Let the sides be $a,b,c$ and the area $K$. Each altitude satisfies $K=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot(\text{side})\cdot(\text{altitude to it})$, so
\[
\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot a\cdot6=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot b\cdot6=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot c\cdot6,
\]
and $a=b=c$: the triangle is equilateral. In an equilateral triangle of side $s$ the altitude is $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s$, so $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s=6$ and $s=\dfrac{12}{\sqrt3}=4\sqrt3$.

