The rotation by $90^\circ$ about $O$ maps $A\to B\to C\to D\to A$, hence maps the triangles onto each other and $E\to F\to G\to H\to E$. So $EFGH$ is a square with centre $O$, whose diagonals have length $2\cdot OE$. The area of a square with diagonal $d$ is $\dfrac{d^2}{2}$, so
\[
[EFGH]=\dfrac{(2\,OE)^2}{2}=2\left(\dfrac{1+\sqrt3}{2}\right)^2=\dfrac{4+2\sqrt3}{2}=\boxed{2+\sqrt3}.
\]

By the triangle inequality, $7-4<x<7+4$, so $4\le x\le10$. By the law of cosines, a triangle with longest side $\ell$ and other sides $p,q$ is obtuse exactly when $\ell^2>p^2+q^2$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $x\le7$, the longest side is $7$: we need $49>16+x^2$, i.e.\ $x^2<33$, so $x=4$ or $5$.
\item If $x>7$, the longest side is $x$: we need $x^2>16+49=65$, so $x=9$ or $10$.
\end{itemize}
($x=6,7,8$ give acute triangles.) So there are $\boxed{4}$ values: $x=4,5,9,10$. The figure shows the cases $x=5$ and $x=9$, with the obtuse angle marked.

