Count pairs of figures and the most points each pair can share:
\begin{itemize}
\item two circles meet in at most $2$ points: $2\dbinom{17}{2}=272$;
\item two lines meet in at most $1$ point: $\dbinom{17}{2}=136$;
\item a line and a circle meet in at most $2$ points: $2\cdot17\cdot17=578$.
\end{itemize}
So there are at most $272+136+578=986$ intersection points. This is attained: take $17$ circles of radius $1$ with centres at distinct points very close to the origin (any two meet in $2$ points), and $17$ lines through points very close to the origin in different directions (each meets each circle twice and every other line once); a small generic perturbation makes all these intersection points distinct. The maximum is $\boxed{986}$.

Use vectors. Since $M=\dfrac{A+B}{2}$ and $N=\dfrac{C+D}{2}$,
\[
\vec{MN}=\dfrac{(C-A)+(D-B)}{2}=\dfrac{\vec{AC}+\vec{BD}}{2}.
\]
The diagonals are perpendicular, so $\vec{AC}\cdot\vec{BD}=0$ and
\[
MN^2=\dfrac{AC^2+BD^2}{4}=\dfrac{144+324}{4}=117 .
\]
Triangle $MPN$ has a right angle at $P$, so by the Pythagorean theorem
\[
MP=\sqrt{MN^2-NP^2}=\sqrt{117-36}=\sqrt{81}=\boxed{9}.
\]

