question:
Euler's totient function $\varphi(n)$ counts the integers in $\{1,2,\dots,n\}$ that are relatively prime to $n$. It is \emph{multiplicative}: $\varphi(mn)=\varphi(m)\varphi(n)$ whenever $\gcd(m,n)=1$, and $\varphi(p^k)=p^k-p^{k-1}$ for a prime power $p^k$. The \emph{Carmichael function} $\lambda(n)$ is the least positive integer $m$ such that $a^m\equiv1\pmod n$ for every integer $a$ relatively prime to $n$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Compute $\varphi(100)$.
\item[(b)] Prove that $a^{20}\equiv1\pmod{100}$ for every integer $a$ relatively prime to $10$, and show that $\lambda(100)=20$.
\item[(c)] Find the last two digits of $3^{2019}$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Suppose $ax^2+bx+c=(px+q)(rx+s)$ with integers $p,q,r,s$. Show that $b$ can be written as $b=m+n$ with $mn=ac$, and explain how splitting $bx=mx+nx$ leads back to the factorization (the ``$ac$ method'').
\item[(b)] Use the method to factor $6x^2+11x-10$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
A number with at least three distinct prime factors $p<q<r$ is at least $pqr$. Products of three distinct primes up to $100$ are
\[
2\cdot3\cdot5=30,\quad 2\cdot3\cdot7=42,\quad 2\cdot5\cdot7=70,\quad 2\cdot3\cdot11=66,\quad 2\cdot3\cdot13=78,
\]
while $2\cdot3\cdot17=102$, $2\cdot5\cdot11=110$ and $3\cdot5\cdot7=105$ are too big. Four distinct primes give at least $2\cdot3\cdot5\cdot7=210$. So every number we want has exactly three prime factors, from one of the five sets above, possibly with repeated factors:
\begin{itemize}
\item primes $2,3,5$: $30,60,90$;
\item primes $2,3,7$: $42,84$;
\item primes $2,5,7$: $70$; primes $2,3,11$: $66$; primes $2,3,13$: $78$.
\end{itemize}
That is $3+2+1+1+1=\boxed{8}$ numbers.

question:
In right triangle $ABC$, $AC=12$, $BC=5$, and $\angle C$ is a right angle. A semicircle is inscribed in the triangle as shown: its diameter lies on $AC$ with one endpoint at $C$, and its arc is tangent to the hypotenuse $AB$. What is the radius of the semicircle?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),A=(12,0),B=(0,5);real r=10/3;pair O=(r,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(O,r,0,180),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,0.55)--(0.55,0.55)--(0.55,0));
label("$A$",A,SE);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,SW);
label("$12$",(9.3,0),S);label("$5$",(0,2.5),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

$\textbf{(A)}\ \dfrac{7}{6}\qquad\textbf{(B)}\ \dfrac{13}{5}\qquad\textbf{(C)}\ \dfrac{59}{18}\qquad\textbf{(D)}\ \dfrac{10}{3}\qquad\textbf{(E)}\ \dfrac{60}{13}$

solution:
\textbf{Terms used.} A line is \emph{tangent} to a circle if it touches the circle at exactly one point, the \emph{point of tangency}. The radius to the point of tangency is perpendicular to the tangent line, and the two tangent segments from a point outside a circle are equal. The \emph{incircle} of a triangle is the circle tangent to all three sides; its radius is the \emph{inradius} $r$. The \emph{semiperimeter} $s$ is half the perimeter, and the area of the triangle is $rs$ (join the incentre to the vertices: the three small triangles have heights $r$ and bases the three sides).

By Pythagoras, $AB=\sqrt{12^2+5^2}=13$. Let $O$ be the centre of the semicircle and $r$ its radius, so $O$ lies on $AC$ with $OC=r$. Since $BC\perp AC$, the line $BC$ is tangent to the circle at $C$. Let $D$ be the point of tangency on $AB$; then $OD\perp AB$ and $OD=r$. Let $E$ be the other end of the diameter, and let $B'$ be the reflection of $B$ in $AC$ (dashed).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),A=(12,0),B=(0,5),Bp=(0,-5);real r=10/3;pair O=(r,0);
pair D=(60/13,40/13),Dp=(60/13,-40/13);
draw(A--Bp--C,dashed+gray);draw(arc(O,r,180,360),dashed+gray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc(O,r,0,180),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--D,red);
pair u=unit(A-D),v=unit(O-D);draw(D+0.5u--D+0.5u+0.5v--D+0.5v);
draw((0,0.55)--(0.55,0.55)--(0.55,0));
dot(O);dot(D);dot((2*r,0));
label("$A$",A,E);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,W);label("$B'$",Bp,S);
label("$O$",O,S);label("$D$",D,NE);label("$E$",(2*r,0),NE);
label("$r$",(O+D)/2,W);label("$5$",(B+D)/2,NE);label("$8$",(A+D)/2,NE);
label("$5$",(0,-2.5),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Solution 1 (similar triangles).} The tangent segments from $B$ are equal: $BD=BC=5$, so $AD=13-5=8$. Triangles $ADO$ and $ACB$ have right angles at $D$ and $C$ and share the angle at $A$, so they are similar and
\[
\dfrac{OD}{AD}=\dfrac{BC}{AC}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\dfrac {r}{8}=\dfrac{5}{12}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad r=\dfrac{10}{3}.
\]

\textbf{Solution 2 (incircle).} Reflecting in $AC$ turns the semicircle into a full circle with centre $O$ that is tangent to $AB$, to $AB'$ and to $BB'$ (at $C$). So it is the incircle of triangle $ABB'$, whose sides are $13,13,10$. Its area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot10\cdot12=60$ and its semiperimeter is $\dfrac{13+13+10}{2}=18$, so $r=\dfrac{60}{18}=\dfrac{10}{3}$.

\textbf{Solution 3 (power of a point).} From $A$, the tangent length squared equals the product of the distances to the two points where line $AC$ meets the circle: $AD^2=AE\cdot AC$. With $AD=8$, $AE=12-2r$ and $AC=12$: $64=12(12-2r)$, so $r=\dfrac{10}{3}$.

The radius is $\boxed{\textbf{(D)}\ \dfrac{10}{3}}$.

Let $K$ be the centre of $\Gamma$ and $\rho$ its radius, and let $R$ be the radius of $\omega$.

\textbf{Step 1: the arc midpoints.} Since $OA=OB=R$, the point $O$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$, and so does $K$ (the centre of a circle through $A$ and $B$). This line meets $\Gamma$ at $O$ and at a second point $O'$, and $OO'$ is a diameter of $\Gamma$. Points of $\Gamma$ on the perpendicular bisector of the chord $AB$ are the midpoints of the two arcs $AB$, so $O$ and $O'$ are the midpoints of the two arcs $AB$ of $\Gamma$.

\textbf{Step 2: $P$ lies on the arc through $O$.} The chord $OA$ of $\Gamma$ is not a diameter (otherwise $A=O'$ would lie on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$), so $R=OA<2\rho=OO'$. Thus $O'$ lies outside $\omega$. The circles $\omega$ and $\Gamma$ meet only at $A$ and $B$, so each of the two open arcs $AB$ of $\Gamma$ lies entirely inside or entirely outside $\omega$; the arc through $O'$ lies outside. The point $P$ is on the diameter $CD$ and is not an endpoint of it (an endpoint on $\Gamma$ would be $A$ or $B$, and then one of the angles is undefined), so $P$ is inside $\omega$ and lies on the other arc, the one through $O$. In particular $P\ne O'$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),K=(1.9,0.7);real R=3,rho=abs(K);
path w=circle(O,R),g=circle(K,rho);
pair[] X=intersectionpoints(w,g);pair A=X[0],B=X[1];
if(A.y<B.y){pair T=A;A=B;B=T;}
pair u=dir(95);pair P=2*dot(K,u)*u;pair C=R*u,D=-R*u;pair Op=2K;
draw(w,linewidth(0.9));draw(g,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(C--D,linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--A);draw(P--B);
draw(P--Op,red+dashed);draw(O--Op,gray+dotted);
draw(arc(P,0.6,degrees(A-P),degrees(C-P)),linewidth(0.8));
draw(arc(P,0.6,degrees(D-P),degrees(B-P)),linewidth(0.8));
draw(arc(P,0.72,degrees(D-P),degrees(B-P)),linewidth(0.8));
draw(arc(P,0.72,degrees(A-P),degrees(C-P)),linewidth(0.8));
dot(O);dot(P);dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(Op);
label("$O$",O,W);label("$P$",P,W);label("$A$",A,NE);label("$B$",B,SE);
label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,S);label("$O'$",Op,E);
label("$\omega$",R*dir(215),SW);label("$\Gamma$",K+rho*dir(25),NE,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 3: two lines through $P$.} Since $P$ and $O'$ are on different arcs $AB$, and the arcs $AO'$ and $O'B$ not containing $P$ are equal, the inscribed angles $\angle APO'$ and $\angle O'PB$ are equal: the line $PO'$ bisects $\angle APB$. Also $OO'$ is a diameter of $\Gamma$ and $P\ne O,O'$, so $\angle OPO'=90^\circ$. The line $CD$ passes through $O$ and $P$, so $CD\perp PO'$.

\textbf{Step 4: reflect.} Reflect in the line $PO'$. Because $PO'$ bisects $\angle APB$, the reflection maps ray $PA$ onto ray $PB$. Because $CD$ is perpendicular to $PO'$ at $P$, it maps the line $CD$ to itself with its direction reversed, so it maps ray $PC$ onto ray $PD$. Reflections preserve angles, hence
\[
\angle APC=\angle BPD .\qquad\blacksquare
\]

question:
For $c\ge0$ define $a_1=\sqrt c$ and $a_{n+1}=\sqrt{c+a_n}$ for $n\ge1$, and let $L(c)=\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}a_n$ be the value of the infinite expression $\sqrt{c+\sqrt{c+\sqrt{c+\cdots}}}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find $L(0)$. The ``self-similarity'' method sets $x=\sqrt x$ and gets $x=0$ or $x=1$. Which is the value, and why is the other one extraneous?
\item[(b)] For $c>0$, prove that $L(c)=\dfrac{1+\sqrt{1+4c}}{2}$.
\item[(c)] Show that $L(c)\to1$ as $c\to0^+$, although $L(0)=0$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
If $A=C$, the right side is $0$ and there is nothing to prove. If $A=B$, the inequality reads $AD\cdot AC\ge AC\cdot AD$, which is true. So assume $A\ne B$ and $A\ne C$.

Let $f$ be the \emph{spiral similarity} with centre $A$ that maps $C$ to $B$: the rotation about $A$ through the angle from ray $AC$ to ray $AB$, followed by the dilation about $A$ with factor $k=\dfrac{AB}{AC}$. Let $E=f(D)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(5,0.6),C=(6,3.4),D=(0.6,3.2);
pair Ep=A+(B-A)/(C-A)*(D-A);
fill(A--B--Ep--cycle,paleblue);fill(A--Ep--D--cycle,palegreen);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(A--C,gray);draw(B--D,red+linewidth(0.8));
draw(B--Ep--D,blue+dashed);draw(A--Ep,dashed);
dot(Ep);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$E$",Ep,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Two pairs of similar triangles.}
\begin{itemize}
\item $f$ maps $A,C,D$ to $A,B,E$, and multiplies all lengths by $k$. So $BE=k\cdot CD=\dfrac{AB\cdot CD}{AC}$ (triangle $ABE\sim$ triangle $ACD$, shaded blue).
\item $AE=k\cdot AD$, so $\dfrac{AE}{AD}=\dfrac{AB}{AC}$, and the angle between rays $AD$ and $AE$ equals the rotation angle, which is the angle between rays $AC$ and $AB$. By SAS similarity, triangle $ADE\sim$ triangle $ACB$ (shaded green), with ratio $\dfrac{AD}{AC}$. Hence $DE=\dfrac{AD\cdot BC}{AC}$. (If $D=A$ then $E=A$ and this still holds.)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{The triangle inequality.} In triangle $BED$ (possibly degenerate),
\[
BD\le BE+ED=\dfrac{AB\cdot CD}{AC}+\dfrac{AD\cdot BC}{AC}.
\]
Multiplying by $AC>0$ gives $AB\cdot CD+AD\cdot BC\ge AC\cdot BD$. $\blacksquare$

Equality holds exactly when $E$ lies on segment $BD$; for a convex quadrilateral $ABCD$ this happens exactly when it is cyclic, which gives Ptolemy's theorem.

question:
Let $f(x)=(x-2)(x^2+36)(x-1)$. The \emph{multiplicity} of a root $r$ of a polynomial $p$ is the largest $m$ such that $(x-r)^m$ divides $p(x)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find all complex roots of $f$ and their multiplicities.
\item[(b)] Is it correct to say that ``$x^2+36$ has multiplicity $2$''? Is the multiplicity of a root always the exponent of a factor?
\item[(c)] Find the roots and multiplicities of $g(x)=(x-2)^3(x^2+36)^2$.
\end{enumerate}

A white cylindrical silo has a diameter of $30$ feet and a height of $80$ feet. A red stripe with a horizontal width of $3$ feet is painted on the silo, as shown, making two complete revolutions around it from the bottom to the top. What is the area of the stripe in square feet?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real Rr=15,k=4;
pair S(real t,real h){return (Rr*cos(t),h+k*sin(t));}
int NN=600;real w=0.3,t0=-2.6;
for(int i=0;i<NN;++i){
 real h1=80*i/NN,h2=80*(i+1)/NN;real a1=t0+4*pi*h1/80,a2=t0+4*pi*h2/80;
 if(sin(a1)<0 && sin(a2)<0 && sin(a1+w)<0 && sin(a2+w)<0)
  fill(S(a1,h1)--S(a1+w,h1)--S(a2+w,h2)--S(a2,h2)--cycle,red);
}
path bot=S(-pi,0);
for(int i=1;i<=60;++i) bot=bot--S(-pi+pi*i/60,0);
path bb=S(0,0);
for(int i=1;i<=60;++i) bb=bb--S(pi*i/60,0);
path top=S(0,80);
for(int i=1;i<=120;++i) top=top--S(2*pi*i/120,80);
draw(bot,linewidth(0.9));draw(bb,dashed);draw(top,linewidth(0.9));
draw((-Rr,0)--(-Rr,80),linewidth(0.9));draw((Rr,0)--(Rr,80),linewidth(0.9));
draw((-Rr,-9)--(Rr,-9),Arrows(5));label("$30$",(0,-9),S);
draw((Rr+6,0)--(Rr+6,80),Arrows(5));label("$80$",(Rr+6,40),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

$\textbf{(A)}\ 120\qquad\textbf{(B)}\ 180\qquad\textbf{(C)}\ 240\qquad\textbf{(D)}\ 360\qquad\textbf{(E)}\ 480$

solution:
Cut the silo along a vertical line and unroll its side onto a flat plane. It becomes a rectangle $80$ feet tall whose width is the circumference $30\pi$ feet. The stripe winds around twice, so draw two copies of the unrolled side next to each other (the dashed line is where the second turn starts). In this picture the stripe becomes a single parallelogram running from the bottom left to the top right. (The width of the stripe is exaggerated: the picture is not to scale.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real Wd=94.25,Hh=80,s=10;
fill((0,0)--(s,0)--(2*Wd,Hh)--(2*Wd-s,Hh)--cycle,red);
draw((0,0)--(2*Wd,0)--(2*Wd,Hh)--(0,Hh)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((Wd,0)--(Wd,Hh),dashed);
draw((0,-6)--(Wd,-6),Arrows(5));label("$30\pi$",(Wd/2,-6),S);
draw((Wd,-6)--(2*Wd,-6),Arrows(5));label("$30\pi$",(1.5*Wd,-6),S);
draw((-6,0)--(-6,Hh),Arrows(5));label("$80$",(-6,Hh/2),W);
draw((0,7)--(s,7),Arrows(4));label("$3$",(s/2,7),NE);
label("first copy",(Wd/2,Hh+2),N);label("second copy",(1.5*Wd,Hh+2),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Unrolling does not change areas or horizontal widths, because the surface is only bent, not stretched. The parallelogram has a horizontal side of length $3$ (the horizontal width of the stripe) and height $80$, so its area is
\[
3\cdot80=240 .
\]
Equivalently, every horizontal line at height between $0$ and $80$ meets the stripe in a segment of length $3$, so the area is $3\cdot80$ regardless of how steeply the stripe winds. The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ 240}$.

Label the pentagon counterclockwise. We use \emph{signed areas}: $[X_1X_2\cdots X_n]$ is the area when the vertices go counterclockwise and minus the area when they go clockwise. For any point $P$ and polygon $X_1\cdots X_n$, $[X_1\cdots X_n]=\sum[PX_iX_{i+1}]$ (indices mod $n$), whatever the position of $P$.

\textbf{Two rotations.} Since $BA=BC$ and $\angle CBA=120^\circ$, the rotation $\rho_B$ about $B$ through $120^\circ$ (counterclockwise) maps $C$ to $A$. Since $DC=DE$ and $\angle EDC=60^\circ$, the rotation $\rho_D$ about $D$ through $60^\circ$ maps $E$ to $C$. Their composition $\rho_B\circ\rho_D$ maps $E\mapsto C\mapsto A$, and a composition of rotations through $60^\circ$ and $120^\circ$ is a rotation through $180^\circ$, i.e.\ a half-turn. A half-turn mapping $E$ to $A$ has centre the midpoint $M$ of $AE$.

Let $M'=\rho_D(M)$. Then $\rho_B(M')=(\rho_B\circ\rho_D)(M)=M$. Hence
\[
DM'=DM,\ \angle MDM'=60^\circ,\qquad BM'=BM,\ \angle M'BM=120^\circ .
\]
So triangle $DMM'$ is equilateral and triangle $BM'M$ is isosceles with apex angle $120^\circ$. Let $t=MM'$. Both $B$ and $D$ lie on the perpendicular bisector of $MM'$, at distances $\dfrac{t}{2\sqrt3}$ and $\dfrac{\sqrt3\,t}{2}$ from its midpoint. A counterclockwise rotation by an angle less than $180^\circ$ that moves $X$ to $Y$ has its centre to the left of $\overrightarrow{XY}$: $\rho_D$ moves $M$ to $M'$ and $\rho_B$ moves $M'$ to $M$, so $D$ and $B$ lie on opposite sides of $MM'$. Thus $MBM'D$ is a kite with perpendicular diagonals and
\[
BD=\dfrac{t}{2\sqrt3}+\dfrac{\sqrt3\,t}{2}=\dfrac{2t}{\sqrt3}=2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad t=\sqrt3 .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair D=(0,0),B=(2,0),C=(1.32,0.43);
pair A=rotate(120,B)*C,Ee=rotate(-60,D)*C;
pair M=(A+Ee)/2,Mp=rotate(60,D)*M;
fill(M--A--B--cycle,paleblue);fill(Mp--C--B--cycle,paleblue);
fill(M--D--Ee--cycle,palegreen);fill(Mp--D--C--cycle,palegreen);
draw(A--B--C--D--Ee--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(M--B--Mp--D--cycle,red+dashed);draw(M--Mp,red+dotted);draw(B--D,dotted);
draw(Mp--C,gray);
dot(M);dot(Mp);
label("$A$",A,SE);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,W);label("$E$",Ee,SW);
label("$M$",M,S);label("$M'$",Mp,N);label("$2$",(0.55,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Moving two triangles.} Since $M$ lies on side $AE$, $[ABCDE]=[MAB]+[MBC]+[MCD]+[MDE]$. The rotation $\rho_B$ maps triangle $M'CB$ onto triangle $MAB$, so $[MAB]=[M'CB]$ (blue). The rotation $\rho_D$ maps triangle $MED$ onto triangle $M'CD$, so $[MDE]=-[MED]=-[M'CD]=[M'DC]$ (green). Therefore
\[
[ABCDE]=[M'CB]+[MBC]+[MCD]+[M'DC]=[CMB]+[CBM']+[CM'D]+[CDM]=[MBM'D],
\]
the last step being the fan decomposition of the kite $MBM'D$ from the point $C$. The kite has perpendicular diagonals $MM'=\sqrt3$ and $BD=2$, so
\[
[ABCDE]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\sqrt3\cdot2=\boxed{\sqrt3}.
\]

question:
Suppose $x-23=y-23$. Prove or disprove each statement:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $x=y$;
\item[(b)] $y>x+100$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $T$ be the area of triangle $ABC$. Since $F$ is the midpoint of $AC$, $[BFC]=\dfrac {T}{2}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(6,0),A=(1.8,4.2),D=(1,0),Ee=(4,0);pair F=(A+C)/2;
pair G=extension(B,F,A,D),H=extension(B,F,A,Ee);
fill(D--G--H--Ee--cycle,lightgray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));draw(A--D);draw(A--Ee);draw(B--F);
dot(D);dot(Ee);dot(F);dot(G);dot(H);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);label("$E$",Ee,S);
label("$F$",F,NE);label("$G$",G,NW);label("$H$",H,N);
label("$51$",(G+H+D+Ee)/4+(0.15,-0.1));
label("$k$",(B+D)/2,S);label("$3k$",(D+Ee)/2,S);label("$2k$",(Ee+C)/2,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Locating $G$ and $H$ on $BF$ (mass points).} For $G=AD\cap BF$: give $A$ and $C$ mass $1$ each, so $F$ (the midpoint) has mass $2$. Since $BD:DC=1:5$, $B$ needs mass $5$, so that $D$ balances $B$ and $C$. Then $G$ balances $B$ (mass $5$) and $F$ (mass $2$): $BG:GF=2:5$, so $BG=\dfrac{2}{7}BF$.

For $H=AE\cap BF$: now $BE:EC=4:2=2:1$. Give $A$ and $C$ mass $2$, so $F$ has mass $4$, and $B$ mass $1$. Then $BH:HF=4:1$, so $BH=\dfrac{4}{5}BF$.

\textbf{Areas.} Triangles with a vertex at $B$ and sides along $BC$ and $BF$ scale with both sides:
\[
[BHE]=\dfrac{BE}{BC}\cdot\dfrac{BH}{BF}\cdot[BCF]=\dfrac{4}{6}\cdot\dfrac{4}{5}\cdot\dfrac {T}{2}=\dfrac{4T}{15},\qquad
[BGD]=\dfrac{BD}{BC}\cdot\dfrac{BG}{BF}\cdot[BCF]=\dfrac{1}{6}\cdot\dfrac{2}{7}\cdot\dfrac {T}{2}=\dfrac{T}{42}.
\]
The quadrilateral is the difference:
\[
[DGHE]=[BHE]-[BGD]=\dfrac{4T}{15}-\dfrac{T}{42}=\dfrac{56T-5T}{210}=\dfrac{17T}{70}.
\]
So $\dfrac{17T}{70}=51$ and $T=\boxed{210}$.

Let the trapezoid be $ABCD$ with $AB\parallel DC$, $AC=17$, $BD=15$, and let $Q$ and $P$ be the midpoints of $AB$ and $DC$, so $PQ=4$.

\textbf{Translate a diagonal.} Translate $BD$ by the vector $\overrightarrow{DC}$: $D$ goes to $C$ and $B$ goes to $E=B+\overrightarrow{DC}$, a point on line $AB$ beyond $B$ with $BE=DC$. Then $CE=BD=15$ and $CE\parallel BD$, and triangle $ACE$ has sides $AC=17$, $CE=15$ and $AE=AB+DC$. Its height from $C$ is the height $h$ of the trapezoid.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(10cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=60/sqrt(241),s=2*sqrt(241),xc=sqrt(289-h^2),bb=12;
pair A=(0,0),C=(xc,h),D=(xc-bb,h),B=(s-bb,0),Ep=(s,0);
pair Q=(A+B)/2,P=(D+C)/2,Nn=(A+Ep)/2,Ft=(D.x,0);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(A--C);draw(B--D);
draw(B--Ep,dashed);draw(C--Ep,blue+dashed);
draw(P--Q,red+linewidth(0.9));draw(C--Nn,red+dashed);
draw(D--Ft,dotted);draw(Ft+(0,0.6)--Ft+(0.6,0.6)--Ft+(0.6,0));
dot(P);dot(Q);dot(Nn);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,N);label("$E$",Ep,SE);
label("$P$",P,N);label("$Q$",Q,S);label("$N$",Nn,S);label("$h$",(D.x,h/2),W);
label("$15$",C+0.55*(Ep-C),NE,blue);label("$4$",Q+0.25*(P-Q),E,red);label("$17$",A+0.45*(C-A),S);label("$15$",B+0.3*(D-B),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{The midpoint segment is a median.} Let $N$ be the midpoint of $AE$. With position vectors, $E=B+C-D$, so
\[
N-C=\dfrac{A+B+C-D}{2}-C=\dfrac{A+B}{2}-\dfrac{C+D}{2}=Q-P .
\]
Hence $CN=PQ=4$: the median of triangle $ACE$ from $C$ has length $4$. By Apollonius's theorem,
\[
CA^2+CE^2=2CN^2+2\left(\dfrac{AE}{2}\right)^2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad289+225=32+\dfrac{AE^2}{2}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad AE^2=964 .
\]

\textbf{Area and height.} For a triangle with sides $a,b,c$ and area $K$, Heron's formula can be written $16K^2=2(a^2b^2+b^2c^2+c^2a^2)-(a^4+b^4+c^4)$. With $a^2=289$, $b^2=225$, $c^2=964$:
\[
16K^2=2(65025+216900+278596)-(83521+50625+929296)=1121042-1063442=57600,
\]
so $K=60$. Then $AE=\sqrt{964}=2\sqrt{241}$ and
\[
h=\dfrac{2K}{AE}=\dfrac{120}{2\sqrt{241}}=\dfrac{60}{\sqrt{241}}=\dfrac{60\sqrt{241}}{241}.
\]
Since $241$ is prime, $x=60$, $y=241$, and $x+y=\boxed{301}$.

question:
Let $r$ and $s$ be the roots of $x^2-7x+3=0$. Without solving the equation, find
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $r^2+s^2$;
\item[(b)] $\dfrac{1}{r}+\dfrac{1}{s}$;
\item[(c)] $r^3+s^3$;
\item[(d)] a quadratic equation with integer coefficients whose roots are $r^2$ and $s^2$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Recall $1$ mL $=1\text{ cm}^3$. The hemisphere holds $\dfrac{2}{3}\pi\cdot2^3=\dfrac{16\pi}{3}\approx16.755\text{ cm}^3$, so before tilting the liquid fills the cylinder to height
\[
h=\dfrac{50-\dfrac{16\pi}{3}}{4\pi}\approx2.646\text{ cm}
\]
above the hemisphere. The point of the axis at height $h$ is marked in the cross-section through the axis below (the tube tilted, the liquid surface horizontal).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=15,h=(50-16*pi/3)/(4*pi),t=Tan(a);
pair c0=(0,-2);
transform T=rotate(a,c0);
path tube=(-2,10)--arc((0,0),2,180,360)--(2,10);
path liq=(-2,h+2*t)--arc((0,0),2,180,360)--(2,h-2*t)--cycle;
fill(T*liq,paleblue);
draw(T*tube,linewidth(1));
draw(T*((-2,0)--(2,0)),gray+dashed);
draw(T*((0,-2)--(0,10.5)),dashdotted+gray);
draw(c0--(c0+(0,12.5)),dashed);
draw(T*((-2,h+2*t)--(2,h-2*t)),blue+linewidth(1.2));
draw(arc(c0,9,90,90+a),Arrow(4));label("$15^\circ$",c0+9.3*dir(90+a/2),N);
draw(T*((2.6,0)--(2.6,10)),Arrows(4));label("$10$",T*(2.6,5),E);
draw(T*((0,0)--(2,0)),linewidth(0.8));label("$2$",T*(1,0),S);
dot(T*(0,h));label("axis",T*(0,9.5),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Where the surface is.} After tilting, the surface is a horizontal plane, which makes an angle of $15^\circ$ with the cross-sections of the tube. A plane through the axis point at height $h$ at this angle cuts the cylinder so that the wedge gained on one side equals the wedge lost on the other (they are congruent by a half-turn about the axis point), so the volume below it is unchanged; hence this is the surface. Along the wall it is at heights between $h-2\tan15^\circ\approx2.11$ and $h+2\tan15^\circ\approx3.18$ above the hemisphere, so it lies entirely in the cylindrical part.

\textbf{The ellipse.} A plane at angle $15^\circ$ to the cross-sections of a cylinder of radius $2$ cuts it in an ellipse with semi-minor axis $2$ (along the line of the plane parallel to the cross-sections) and semi-major axis $\dfrac{2}{\cos15^\circ}$ (the diameter $4$ is stretched by $\dfrac{1}{\cos15^\circ}$ in the tilt direction). Its area is
\[
\pi\cdot2\cdot\dfrac{2}{\cos15^\circ}=\dfrac{4\pi}{\cos15^\circ}=4\pi\left(\sqrt6-\sqrt2\right)\approx13.00966,
\]
using $\cos15^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt6+\sqrt2}{4}$, so $\dfrac{1}{\cos15^\circ}=\dfrac{4}{\sqrt6+\sqrt2}=\sqrt6-\sqrt2$. To the nearest thousandth the area is $\boxed{13.010}$ square centimetres.

question:
The rational root theorem only finds factors of degree $1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $p(x)=x^5+x+1$ has no rational roots.
\item[(b)] Nevertheless, factor $p(x)$ as a product of two polynomials with integer coefficients of degrees $2$ and $3$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $BD=x$, so $DC=5-x$. The perimeters are $AD+4+x$ and $AD+3+(5-x)$. Setting them equal gives $4+x=8-x$, so $x=2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(0,4),C=(3,0);pair D=B+2/5*(C-B);
pair H=B+(dot(A-B,C-B)/dot(C-B,C-B))*(C-B);
fill(A--B--D--cycle,paleblue);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));draw(A--D);
draw(A--H,dashed);pair u=unit(C-H),v=unit(A-H);draw(H+0.22u--H+0.22u+0.22v--H+0.22v);
draw((0,0.25)--(0.25,0.25)--(0.25,0));
dot(D);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,NE);label("$H$",H,NE);
label("$4$",(0,2),W);label("$3$",(1.5,0),S);label("$2$",(B+D)/2,NE);label("$3$",D+0.72*(C-D),NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Triangles $ABD$ and $ABC$ have the same vertex $A$, and their bases $BD$ and $BC$ lie on the same line, so they have the same height $AH$ (dashed). Their areas are therefore in the ratio of the bases:
\[
[ABD]=\dfrac{BD}{BC}\cdot[ABC]=\dfrac{2}{5}\cdot6=\dfrac{2\cdot6}{5},
\]
since $[ABC]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot3\cdot4=6$. (That is where the $6$ and the $5$ come from: $6$ is the area of the whole triangle and $\dfrac{2}{5}$ is the fraction of the base used.) The answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(D)}\ \dfrac{12}{5}}$.

