Let $f(x)=x+\sqrt{x+1}$ for $x\ge-1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Write $f$ as a composition $f=g\circ h$, where $h(x)=\sqrt{x+1}$ and $g$ is a polynomial.
\item[(b)] Use this decomposition to show that $f$ is increasing on $[-1,\infty)$, and find the range of $f$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
First, $\tan15^\circ=\tan(45^\circ-30^\circ)=\dfrac{1-\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}}{1+\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}}=\dfrac{\sqrt3-1}{\sqrt3+1}=\dfrac{(\sqrt3-1)^2}{2}=2-\sqrt3$.

In right triangle $BCE$ (right angle at $C$), $CE=BC\tan15^\circ=10(2-\sqrt3)=20-10\sqrt3$. Since $CD=AB=20$,
\[
DE=CD-CE=20-(20-10\sqrt3)=10\sqrt3 .
\]

