question:
A student states: ``The sum of the terms of an arithmetic sequence equals $\dfrac{\text{first term}+\text{last term}}{2}$ times the number of terms.''
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove this formula.
\item[(b)] Use it to compute $3+7+11+\cdots+99$.
\item[(c)] Give an example showing that the formula can fail for a sequence that is not arithmetic.
\end{enumerate}

Let $a$ and $b$ be relatively prime positive integers, and let $u$ and $v$ be integers with $au+bv=1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that for any integers $x$ and $y$, the number $N_0=x\,bv+y\,au$ satisfies $N_0\equiv x\pmod a$ and $N_0\equiv y\pmod b$, and that the integers $N$ with $N\equiv x\pmod a$ and $N\equiv y\pmod b$ are exactly those with $N\equiv N_0\pmod{ab}$.
\item[(b)] Express the conditions $N\equiv3\pmod8$ and $N\equiv5\pmod{11}$ as a single congruence modulo $88$.
\item[(c)] Show that the conditions $N\equiv1\pmod4$ and $N\equiv2\pmod6$ have no common solution, so the assumption $\gcd(a,b)=1$ cannot be dropped.
\end{enumerate}

A square gameboard is $5$ inches on each side. A square hole $1$ inch on each side is cut from the centre. The rest of the board is divided into four congruent rectangles arranged around the hole like a pinwheel, as shown. How many inches are in the perimeter of each rectangle?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,5)--(0,5)--cycle,linewidth(1));
filldraw((2,2)--(3,2)--(3,3)--(2,3)--cycle,lightgray,linewidth(0.8));
draw((3,0)--(3,2));draw((0,2)--(2,2));draw((5,3)--(3,3));draw((2,5)--(2,3));
label("$5$",(2.5,0),S);label("$5$",(5,2.5),E);label("$1$",(2.5,3),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
Let each rectangle be $a$ inches long and $b$ inches wide, with $a>b$. Going along the bottom side of the board we pass the long side of one rectangle and the short side of the next, so $a+b=5$. Each side of the hole is the part of a rectangle's long side not covered by the neighbouring rectangle's short side, so $a-b=1$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
fill((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,2)--(0,2)--cycle,paleblue);
draw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,5)--(0,5)--cycle,linewidth(1));
filldraw((2,2)--(3,2)--(3,3)--(2,3)--cycle,lightgray,linewidth(0.8));
draw((3,0)--(3,2));draw((0,2)--(2,2));draw((5,3)--(3,3));draw((2,5)--(2,3));
label("$a$",(1.5,0),S);label("$b$",(3,1),W);label("$b$",(4,0),S);label("$a-b$",(3,2.5),E);
label("$a+b=5$",(2.5,5),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Hence $a=3$ and $b=2$. (Check: four rectangles of area $6$ plus the hole of area $1$ give $25=5^2$.) The perimeter of each rectangle is $2(3+2)=\boxed{10}$ inches.

The surface consists of three parts (the top of the cylinder and the flat face of the hemisphere are glued together and are not on the surface):

