question:
Let $f(x)$ be a function with the two properties:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $f(0)=2$;
\item[(ii)] for any two real numbers $x$ and $y$, $f(x+y)=x+f(y)$.
\end{enumerate}
What is the value of $f(1998)$?

solution:
Let $BC=14$, $CA=9$, $AB=13$. The semiperimeter is $s=\dfrac{9+13+14}{2}=18$, and Heron's formula gives the area
\[
K=\sqrt{18(18-14)(18-9)(18-13)}=\sqrt{18\cdot4\cdot9\cdot5}=\sqrt{3240}=18\sqrt{10}.
\]
Let $I$ be the incentre and $r$ the inradius. The segments $IA$, $IB$, $IC$ cut the triangle into triangles $IBC$, $ICA$, $IAB$, whose heights from $I$ all equal $r$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(14,0),A=(71/7,18*sqrt(10)/7);
real r=sqrt(10); pair I=(9,r);
pair T1=(9,0), T2=B+9*unit(A-B), T3=C+5*unit(A-C);
draw(circle(I,r),blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(I--T1,dashed); draw(I--T2,dashed); draw(I--T3,dashed);
draw((9.45,0)--(9.45,0.45)--(9,0.45),linewidth(0.6));
dot(I);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$I$",I,SE);
label("$r$",(9,r/2),W);
label("$14$",(5,0),S);label("$13$",(A+B)/2,NW);label("$9$",A+0.72*(C-A),NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\[
K=\dfrac{r\cdot BC}{2}+\dfrac{r\cdot CA}{2}+\dfrac{r\cdot AB}{2}=rs .
\]
Hence $r=\dfrac{K}{s}=\dfrac{18\sqrt{10}}{18}=\boxed{\sqrt{10}}$.

The student's method fails for two reasons. The altitude to $BC$, of length $h=\dfrac{2K}{14}=\dfrac{18\sqrt{10}}{7}$, is not a median here (its foot is not the midpoint of $BC$, since $AB\ne AC$). More importantly, $\dfrac {h}{3}$ is the distance from the \emph{centroid} to $BC$ (the centroid lies one third of the way up every median, so its height above $BC$ is $\dfrac {h}{3}$), and the centroid is not the incentre unless the triangle is equilateral. Here $\dfrac {h}{3}=\dfrac{6\sqrt{10}}{7}\approx2.71$, while $r=\sqrt{10}\approx3.16$.

Number the vertices $0,1,\dots,7$ around the octagon. A quadrilateral is a $4$-element set of vertices; there are $\dbinom84=70$ of them. The rotations by $0,1,\dots,7$ steps ($45^\circ$ each) act on these sets, and we count orbits with Burnside's lemma: the number of orbits is the average number of sets fixed by a rotation.

The rotation by $k$ steps splits the vertices into $\gcd(k,8)$ cycles of length $\dfrac{8}{\gcd(k,8)}$, and a set is fixed exactly when it is a union of cycles.
\begin{itemize}
\item $k=0$: all $70$ sets are fixed.
\item $k=1,3,5,7$: one cycle of length $8$; no $4$-set is fixed.
\item $k=2,6$ (turns of $90^\circ$): two cycles $\{0,2,4,6\}$, $\{1,3,5,7\}$ of length $4$; $2$ fixed sets (the two squares).
\item $k=4$ (turn of $180^\circ$): four cycles $\{i,i+4\}$; a fixed set is two of them, $\dbinom42=6$ sets (rectangles).
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
void oct(pair c,int[] q,pen fillp,pen drawp){
  path g=c+dir(90); for(int i=1;i<8;++i) g=g--(c+dir(90-45*i)); g=g--cycle;
  path h=c+dir(90-45*q[0]); for(int i=1;i<q.length;++i) h=h--(c+dir(90-45*q[i])); h=h--cycle;
  fill(h,fillp); draw(g,linewidth(0.9)); draw(h,drawp);
  for(int i=0;i<8;++i){dot(c+dir(90-45*i)); label("$"+string(i)+"$",c+1.22*dir(90-45*i));}
}
oct((0,0),new int[]{0,2,4,6},paleblue,blue+linewidth(1));
oct((3.2,0),new int[]{0,1,4,5},palered,red+linewidth(1));
label("fixed by turns of $90^\circ$",(0,-1.55));
label("fixed by a turn of $180^\circ$",(3.2,-1.55));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The number of distinct quadrilaterals is
\[
\dfrac{70+0\cdot4+2+2+6}{8}=\dfrac{80}{8}=10,
\]
answer $\boxed{\textbf{(B)}\ 10}$.

\emph{Check by gaps.} Going round the octagon, the numbers of steps between consecutive chosen vertices are four positive integers with sum $8$, determined up to cyclic order: $(1,1,1,5)$; $(1,1,2,4),(1,1,4,2),(1,2,1,4)$; $(1,1,3,3),(1,3,1,3)$; $(1,2,2,3),(1,2,3,2),(1,3,2,2)$; $(2,2,2,2)$. Again $10$.

question:
Here $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. For each relation below, sketch its graph, state whether the relation is a function (of $x$), and find its range.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $\{(x,y)\mid y=3x-4\}$ with domain $\{x\mid -3\le x\le4\}$;
\item[(b)] $\{(x,y)\mid y=3\}$ with domain $\{x\mid x\ge0\}$;
\item[(c)] $\{(x,y)\mid y>x\}$ with domain $\{x\mid x\ge5\}$;
\item[(d)] $\{(x,y)\mid y<x+4\}$ with domain $\{x\mid 0\le x\le2\}$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
A relation is a function when each $x$ in the domain is paired with exactly one $y$. (The sketches below are not to scale vertically.)

(a) The graph is the segment of the line $y=3x-4$ from $(-3,-13)$ to $(4,8)$, endpoints included. Each $x$ gives one $y$, so it is a function. Since $y=3x-4$ increases with $x$, the range is $\boxed{-13\le y\le8}$.

