(c) The graph is the region to the right of the line $x=5$ (boundary included) and strictly above the line $y=x$ (dashed boundary, not included). It is not a function: for example $x=5$ is paired with both $y=6$ and $y=7$. Every point has $y>x\ge5$, so $y>5$; conversely every $y>5$ occurs, with $x=5$. The range is $\boxed{y>5}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm,4.5cm,IgnoreAspect);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-1,0)--(11,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-1)--(0,13),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(11,0),E); label("$y$",(0,13),N);
fill((5,5)--(10.5,10.5)--(10.5,12.5)--(5,12.5)--cycle,paleblue);
draw((0,0)--(5,5),gray+dashed); draw((5,5)--(10.5,10.5),blue+dashed+linewidth(1));
draw((5,5)--(5,12.5),blue+linewidth(1));
dot((5,5),blue+6bp); dot((5,5),white+3.6bp);
draw((5,0.3)--(5,-0.3)); label("$5$",(5,-0.3),S);
