[XYZ]=240-30-48-40=\boxed{122}.
\]

question:
A square whose side length is an integer number of centimetres is divided into four rectangles by one line parallel to one pair of its sides and one line parallel to the other pair, as shown. The areas of the four rectangles, in $\text{cm}^2$, are pairwise distinct positive integers. Find the smallest possible area of the square, in $\text{cm}^2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,5)--(0,5)--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((1.8,0)--(1.8,5),linewidth(0.8)); draw((0,2.3)--(5,2.3),linewidth(0.8));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
Let the side be $n$ and let the two lines cut the sides into lengths $a,\ n-a$ and $b,\ n-b$, where $0<a<n$ and $0<b<n$ are real numbers (not necessarily integers). The four areas are
\[
P=ab,\quad Q=(n-a)b,\quad R=a(n-b),\quad T=(n-a)(n-b),
\]
and $P+Q+R+T=n^2$. Comparing them,
\[
P=Q\iff a=\dfrac {n}{2},\quad P=R\iff b=\dfrac {n}{2},\quad Q=R\iff a=b,\quad P=T\iff a+b=n,
\]
and $R=T\iff a=\dfrac {n}{2}$, $Q=T\iff b=\dfrac {n}{2}$.

\emph{$n\le3$ is impossible:} four distinct positive integers have sum at least $1+2+3+4=10>9\ge n^2$.

\emph{$n=4$ is impossible.} The numbers $A=4a=P+R$ and $B=4b=P+Q$ are integers from $1$ to $15$, and $P=ab=\dfrac{AB}{16}$ is an integer, so $16\mid AB$. By the comparisons above we need $A\ne8$, $B\ne8$, $A\ne B$ and $A+B\ne16$. If $A$ is odd, then $16\mid B$, impossible; if $A\equiv2\pmod4$, then $8\mid B$, so $B=8$, not allowed. Hence $4\mid A$ and $A\in\{4,12\}$, and in the same way $B\in\{4,12\}$. But then $A=B$ or $A+B=16$, so two areas are equal.

\emph{$n=5$ works:} with $a=1$ and $b=2$ the areas are $2,8,3,12$, which are distinct positive integers.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,5)--(0,5)--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((1,0)--(1,5),linewidth(0.8)); draw((0,2)--(5,2),linewidth(0.8));
label("$2$",(0.5,1),blue); label("$8$",(3,1),blue); label("$3$",(0.5,3.5),blue); label("$12$",(3,3.5),blue);
label("$1$",(0.5,0),S); label("$4$",(3,0),S); label("$2$",(0,1),W); label("$3$",(0,3.5),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So the smallest possible area of the square is $5^2=\boxed{25}\ \text{cm}^2$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Let $p(x)=c(x-r_1)^{m_1}(x-r_2)^{m_2}\cdots(x-r_k)^{m_k}$, where $c\ne0$, $r_1<r_2<\cdots<r_k$ are real and the $m_i$ are positive integers. Explain why $p$ has constant sign on each interval between consecutive roots, why $p(x)$ has the sign of $c$ for $x>r_k$, and why the sign of $p$ changes at $r_i$ exactly when $m_i$ is odd.
\item[(b)] Use (a), without test points, to solve $x^3-2x^2-5x+6>0$ and $(x+2)(x-1)^2(x-3)<0$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Since $33^2+44^2=1089+1936=3025=55^2$, the triangle is right-angled; call it $ABC$ with the right angle at $C$, $BC=33$, $CA=44$, $AB=55$. The larger acute angle is opposite the longer leg $CA$, so it is the angle at $B$, and its bisector meets $CA$ at a point $D$.

By the angle bisector theorem, the bisector divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides:
\[
\dfrac{CD}{DA}=\dfrac{BC}{BA}=\dfrac{33}{55}=\dfrac{3}{5} .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),B=(33,0),A=(0,44),D=(0,16.5);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1)); draw(B--D,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,2.6)--(2.6,2.6)--(2.6,0),linewidth(0.6));
real t1=degrees(A-B), t2=degrees(D-B);
draw(arc(B,5.5,t1,t2),linewidth(0.6)); draw(arc(B,5.5,t2,180),linewidth(0.6));
draw(arc(B,6.4,t1,t2),linewidth(0.6)); draw(arc(B,6.4,t2,180),linewidth(0.6));
dot(D);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,SW);label("$D$",D,W);
label("$33$",(16.5,0),S);label("$55$",(A+B)/2,NE);
label("$\dfrac{33}{2}$",(0,8.25),W);label("$\dfrac{55}{2}$",(0,30.25),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So $CD=\dfrac{3}{8}\cdot44=\dfrac{33}{2}$ and $DA=\dfrac{5}{8}\cdot44=\dfrac{55}{2}$. The shorter segment has length $\boxed{\dfrac{33}{2}}$ cm.

Since the vertex is on the $y$-axis and the axis is vertical, the parabola is $y=kx^2+m$ with $k\ne0$.

Triangle $PQR$ has a right angle at $Q$, with legs $QR=9$ and $PQ=|x_1-3|$. So
\[
\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot9\cdot|x_1-3|=40.5\iff|x_1-3|=9\iff x_1=-6\ \text{or}\ x_1=12 .
\]
The parabola passes through $(3,9)$, so $9k+m=9$, and through $(x_1,0)$, so $kx_1^2+m=0$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $x_1=-6$: $36k+m=0$, so $27k=-9$, $k=-\dfrac{1}{3}$, $m=12$.
\item $x_1=12$: $144k+m=0$, so $135k=-9$, $k=-\dfrac{1}{15}$, $m=9+\dfrac{3}{5}=\dfrac{48}{5}$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f1(real x){return 12-x^2/3;} real f2(real x){return 48/5-x^2/15;}
pair P1=(-6,0),P2=(12,0),Q=(3,0),R=(3,9);
fill(P1--Q--R--cycle,palered); fill(Q--P2--R--cycle,paleblue);
draw((-8,0)--(15.5,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-1.5)--(0,13.5),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(15.5,0),E); label("$y$",(0,13.5),N);
draw(graph(f1,-6,6),red+linewidth(1)); draw(graph(f2,-6.4,12),blue+linewidth(1));
draw(P1--R--P2,linewidth(0.6)); draw(Q--R,linewidth(0.6));
dot(P1);dot(P2);dot(Q);dot(R);
label("$P_1(-6,0)$",P1,SW); label("$P_2(12,0)$",P2,S); label("$Q(3,0)$",Q,S); label("$R(3,9)$",R,NE);
label("$y=12-\dfrac{x^2}{3}$",(-6,3.3),W,red); label("$y=\dfrac{48}{5}-\dfrac{x^2}{15}$",(9.2,4.6),NE,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Both work, so the parabola is
\[
\boxed{y=-\dfrac{x^2}{3}+12}\qquad\text{or}\qquad\boxed{y=-\dfrac{x^2}{15}+\dfrac{48}{5}} .
\]

The parabolas meet where $x^2-b=-x^2+b$, i.e.\ $x^2=b$, at $P(\sqrt b,0)$ and $Q(-\sqrt b,0)$. Their vertices are $(0,-b)$ and $(0,b)$.

The quadrilateral with these four vertices has diagonals along the axes, of lengths $2\sqrt b$ and $2b$; they are perpendicular and bisect each other at the origin, so it is a rhombus (drawn below for $b=9$):

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real g1(real x){return x^2-9;} real g2(real x){return 9-x^2;}
fill((3,0)--(0,9)--(-3,0)--(0,-9)--cycle,palegreen);
draw((-5.5,0)--(5.5,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-10.5)--(0,11),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(5.5,0),E); label("$y$",(0,11),N);
draw(graph(g1,-4.1,4.1),blue+linewidth(1)); draw(graph(g2,-4.1,4.1),red+linewidth(1));
draw((3,0)--(0,9)--(-3,0)--(0,-9)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
dot((3,0));dot((-3,0));dot((0,9));dot((0,-9));
label("$P$",(4.45,0.9)); label("$Q$",(-4.45,0.9));
label("$(0,b)$",(0,9),NE); label("$(0,-b)$",(0,-9),SE);
label("$y=x^2-b$",(4.1,7.81),E,blue); label("$y=b-x^2$",(4.1,-7.81),E,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Its area is half the product of the diagonals:
\[
\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot2\sqrt b\cdot2b=2b\sqrt b=54\iff b\sqrt b=27\iff b^{\dfrac{3}{2}}=27\iff b=\boxed{9}.
\]

The centre $O$ is equidistant from $(6,0)$ and $(8,0)$, so it lies on their perpendicular bisector $x=7$; write $O=(7,k)$. Equal distances to $(8,0)$ and $(3,2)$ give
\[
1^2+k^2=4^2+(k-2)^2\iff 1=16-4k+4\iff k=\dfrac{19}{4}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(6,0),B=(8,0),P=(3,2),O=(7,19/4); real r=sqrt(377)/4;
draw((-1,0)--(13,0),Arrow(6)); draw((0,-1)--(0,10.5),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(13,0),E); label("$y$",(0,10.5),N);
draw(circle(O,r),blue+linewidth(1));
draw((7,-0.8)--(7,10),dashed);
draw(O--A,red+linewidth(0.8)); draw(O--P,red+linewidth(0.8));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(P);dot(O);
label("$(6,0)$",A,SW); label("$(8,0)$",B,SE); label("$(3,2)$",P,W);
label("$O\left(7,\dfrac{19}{4}\right)$",O,E); label("$r$",(O+A)/2,W,red); label("$x=7$",(7,10),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Then $r^2=1+\left(\dfrac{19}{4}\right)^2=\dfrac{16+361}{16}=\dfrac{377}{16}$, so the radius is $\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt{377}}{4}}\approx4.85$.

question:
Let $S$ be a set of positive integers, each less than $20$, such that no sum of one or more distinct elements of $S$ is equal to $20$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the maximum possible size $m$ of $S$?
\item[(b)] Among all such sets $S$ of size $m$, find all those with the smallest possible sum of elements.
\end{enumerate}

The \emph{reflection of a point $P$ in a point $M$} is the point $P'$ such that $M$ is the midpoint of segment $PP'$ (and $M$ is its own image).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the reflection of $(x,y)$ in $M(a,b)$ is $(2a-x,\ 2b-y)$, and that reflecting in $M$ is the same as rotating through $180^\circ$ about $M$.
\item[(b)] Show that reflection in $M$ maps every segment $PQ$ to a segment $P'Q'$ that is parallel to $PQ$ and has the same length.
\item[(c)] Find the vertices of the image of the triangle with vertices $A(1,1)$, $B(3,2)$, $C(1,3)$ under reflection in $M(4,1)$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) If $P=(x,y)$ and $P'=(x',y')$, then $M$ is the midpoint exactly when $\dfrac{x+x'}{2}=a$ and $\dfrac{y+y'}{2}=b$, i.e.\ $P'=(2a-x,\ 2b-y)$. The vector from $M$ to $P'$ is $(a-x,\ b-y)$, the negative of the vector from $M$ to $P$. So $P'$ is at the same distance from $M$ as $P$, in the opposite direction: this is exactly the rotation of $P$ through $180^\circ$ about $M$. (No coordinates are needed to construct it: draw the ray from $P$ through $M$ and mark $P'$ beyond $M$ with $MP'=MP$.)

(b) If $P=(x_1,y_1)$ and $Q=(x_2,y_2)$, the vector from $P'$ to $Q'$ is
\[
\bigl((2a-x_2)-(2a-x_1),\ (2b-y_2)-(2b-y_1)\bigr)=-(x_2-x_1,\ y_2-y_1),
\]
the negative of the vector from $P$ to $Q$. So $P'Q'$ is parallel to $PQ$ (pointing the opposite way) and has the same length.

(c) Using $(x,y)\mapsto(8-x,\ 2-y)$:
\[
\boxed{A'(7,1),\quad B'(5,0),\quad C'(7,-1)} .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1,1),B=(3,2),C=(1,3),M=(4,1);
pair A2=2*M-A,B2=2*M-B,C2=2*M-C;
draw((-0.5,0)--(8.5,0),gray,Arrow(6)); draw((0,-1.6)--(0,3.8),gray,Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(8.5,0),E,gray); label("$y$",(0,3.8),N,gray);
draw(A--A2,dashed); draw(B--B2,dashed); draw(C--C2,dashed);
filldraw(A--B--C--cycle,paleblue,blue+linewidth(1));
filldraw(A2--B2--C2--cycle,palered,red+linewidth(1));
dot(M); label("$M$",M,SW);
label("$A$",A,SW); label("$B$",B,N); label("$C$",C,NW);
label("$A'$",A2,E); label("$B'$",B2,S); label("$C'$",C2,SE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Each dashed segment $AA'$, $BB'$, $CC'$ has midpoint $M$, and the image triangle is the original one turned through $180^\circ$ about $M$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Let $a\ne0$ and $c\ne0$. Show that $r$ is a root of $ax^2+bx+c=0$ if and only if $r\ne0$ and $\dfrac{1}{r}$ is a root of $cx^2+bx+a=0$. (So substituting $x=\dfrac{1}{y}$ in a quadratic equation only produces the ``reversed'' equation with the reciprocal roots.)
\item[(b)] Given that the roots of $x^2-5x+6=0$ are $2$ and $3$, use (a) to solve $6x^2-5x+1=0$.
