Brooks and Avery run laps around a track in the same direction. Brooks completes a lap every $78$ seconds and Avery every $91$ seconds. Brooks has just passed Avery.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many seconds will it take for Brooks to overtake Avery again?
\item[(b)] Someone answers (a) with $\operatorname{lcm}(78,91)$. Explain why this gives the right number here, and show by an example that the method does not work in general.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the central square have side $a$ and let each arm stick out a distance $b$, so each arm is an $a\times b$ rectangle, with $a,b$ positive integers. The area is
\[
a^2+4ab=a(a+4b)=2016,
\]
and the boundary consists of $4$ ends of length $a$ and $8$ sides of length $b$, so the perimeter is $P=4a+8b$.

From the area, $a$ divides $2016$ and $4b=\dfrac{2016}{a}-a$, which must be a positive multiple of $4$. In particular $a<\sqrt{2016}\approx44.9$. Then
\[
P=4a+2\left(\dfrac{2016}{a}-a\right)=2a+\dfrac{4032}{a}.
\]
This decreases as $a$ increases up to $\sqrt{2016}$: for $0<a<c\le\sqrt{2016}$, $P(a)-P(c)=(c-a)\left(\dfrac{4032}{ac}-2\right)>0$ because $ac<2016$. So we want the largest admissible $a$.

The divisors of $2016=2^5\cdot3^2\cdot7$ just below $44.9$ are $42,36,\dots$. For $a=42$: $\dfrac{2016}{42}-42=48-42=6$, not a multiple of $4$. For $a=36$: $56-36=20$, so $b=5$. Hence the minimum perimeter is
\[
P=4\cdot36+8\cdot5=\boxed{184}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
path c=(0,-5)--(36,-5)--(36,0)--(41,0)--(41,36)--(36,36)--(36,41)--(0,41)--(0,36)--(-5,36)--(-5,0)--(0,0)--cycle;
fill(c,paleblue); draw(c,linewidth(1));
draw((0,0)--(36,0)--(36,36)--(0,36)--cycle,dashed);
label("$36$",(18,-5),S); label("$36$",(41,18),E);
draw((-5,-2.5)--(0,-2.5),Arrows(4)); label("$5$",(-2.5,-2.5),S);
draw((38.5,36)--(38.5,41),Arrows(4)); label("$5$",(38.5,38.5),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Check: the area is $36^2+4\cdot36\cdot5=1296+720=2016$.

Let the centres be $O_1,O_2$ and the intersection points $P,Q$. All four segments $O_1P,O_1Q,O_2P,O_2Q$ have length $7$, so $O_1PO_2Q$ is a rhombus. Since $7^2+7^2=\left(7\sqrt2\right)^2$, the angle $O_1PO_2$ is a right angle, so the rhombus is a square of side $7$, and $\angle PO_1Q=\angle PO_2Q=90^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real d=7*sqrt(2); pair O1=(0,0),O2=(d,0),P=(d/2,d/2),Q=(d/2,-d/2);
path lens=arc(O1,7,-45,45)--arc(O2,7,135,225)--cycle;
fill(lens,palegreen);
draw(circle(O1,7),linewidth(1)); draw(circle(O2,7),linewidth(1));
draw(O1--P--O2--Q--cycle,dashed); draw(O1--O2,dashed); draw(P--Q,dashed);
pair u=unit(P-O1), v=unit(Q-O1); draw(O1+0.9*u--O1+0.9*(u+v)--O1+0.9*v,linewidth(0.6));
dot(O1);dot(O2);dot(P);dot(Q);
label("$O_1$",O1,W); label("$O_2$",O2,E); label("$P$",P,N); label("$Q$",Q,S);
label("$7$",(O1+P)/2,NW); label("$7$",(O2+P)/2,NE);
draw((0,-8.2)--(d,-8.2),Arrows(4)); label("$7\sqrt{2}$",(d/2,-8.2),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The common region (shaded) consists of two congruent circular segments cut off by the chord $PQ$. Each is a quarter-circle sector minus a right triangle with legs $7$:
\[
\dfrac{\pi\cdot7^2}{4}-\dfrac{7\cdot7}{2}=\dfrac{49\pi}{4}-\dfrac{49}{2}.
\]
So the common area is
\[
2\left(\dfrac{49\pi}{4}-\dfrac{49}{2}\right)=\boxed{\dfrac{49\pi}{2}-49}\ \text{cm}^2\approx27.97\ \text{cm}^2 .
\]

By the angle bisector theorem, $D$ divides $BC$ in the ratio of the adjacent sides:
\[
\dfrac{BD}{DC}=\dfrac{AB}{AC}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad DC=\dfrac{BD\cdot AC}{AB}=\dfrac{10\cdot51}{30}=17 .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(27,0),A=(-18,24),D=(10,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1)); draw(A--D,blue+linewidth(0.9));
real t1=degrees(B-A), t2=degrees(D-A), t3=degrees(C-A);
draw(arc(A,7,t2,t1),linewidth(0.6)); draw(arc(A,7,t3,t2),linewidth(0.6)); draw(arc(A,8.2,t2,t1),linewidth(0.6)); draw(arc(A,8.2,t3,t2),linewidth(0.6));
dot(D);
label("$A$",A,NW); label("$B$",B,S); label("$C$",C,SE); label("$D$",D,S);
label("$30$",(A+B)/2,SW); label("$51$",(A+C)/2,NE);
label("$10$",(B+D)/2,S); label("$17$",(D+C)/2,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Hence $BC=BD+DC=10+17=\boxed{27}$. (Such a triangle exists: with $B=(0,0)$, $C=(27,0)$, $A=(-18,24)$ we get $AB=30$ and $AC=51$; the angle at $B$ is obtuse.)

Track the contents of the urn and multiply the conditional probabilities:
\begin{itemize}
\item first draw black: $\dfrac{7}{12}$; the urn then has $5$ white, $9$ black;
\item second draw black: $\dfrac{9}{14}$; then $5$ white, $11$ black;
\item third draw white: $\dfrac{5}{16}$; then $7$ white, $11$ black;
\item fourth draw white: $\dfrac{7}{18}$.
\end{itemize}
The probability is
\[
\dfrac{7}{12}\cdot\dfrac{9}{14}\cdot\dfrac{5}{16}\cdot\dfrac{7}{18}=\dfrac{2205}{48384}=\boxed{\dfrac{35}{768}}.
\]

Put the base in the plane $z=0$ with the pyramid's axis on the $z$-axis. The bottom spheres touch the base and their neighbours, so their centres are $(\pm6,\pm6,6)$. The top centre $(0,0,t)$ is at distance $12$ from $(6,6,6)$: $72+(t-6)^2=144$, so $t=6+6\sqrt2$.

\textbf{Side view.} Consider the lateral face on the side $x>0$. By symmetry it contains a line parallel to the $y$-axis, so looking along the $y$-axis it appears as a line $\ell$, and distances to the face equal distances to $\ell$ in this view. The face touches the bottom spheres with centres $(6,\pm6,6)$, which both appear as the circle of radius $6$ about $O_1=(6,6)$, and the top sphere, which appears as the circle of radius $6$ about $O_2=(0,6+6\sqrt2)$; both circles lie on the same side of $\ell$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=3*sqrt(2)+6+3*sqrt(6), H=6+6*sqrt(2)+6*sqrt(3);
pair L=(-h,0),R=(h,0),V=(0,H),O1=(6,6),O3=(-6,6),O2=(0,6+6*sqrt(2));
draw(L--R--V--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(circle(O1,6),blue); draw(circle(O3,6),blue); draw(circle(O2,6),red);
draw(O1--O2,dashed+gray);
pair n=unit((sqrt(2),1)); pair F1=O1+6*n, F2=O2+6*n;
draw(O1--F1,dashed); draw(O2--F2,dashed);
dot(O1);dot(O2);dot(O3);dot(V);
label("$O_1$",O1,S); label("$O_2$",O2,S); label("$V$",V,N);
label("$6$",(O1+F1)/2,NW); label("$6$",(O2+F2)/2,NW);
label("$\ell$",(h/2,H/2),NE);
draw((0,-1.8)--(h,-1.8),Arrows(4)); label("$\dfrac {s}{2}$",(h/2,-1.8),S);
draw((-h-2,0)--(-h-2,H),Arrows(4)); label("$H$",(-h-2,H/2),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

A common tangent of two equal circles with both circles on the same side is parallel to the line of centres. The line $O_1O_2$ has slope $\dfrac{6\sqrt2}{-6}=-\sqrt2$, so $\ell$ is $\sqrt2x+z=k$. Its distance from $O_1$ is $\dfrac{k-6\sqrt2-6}{\sqrt3}=6$, so $k=6+6\sqrt2+6\sqrt3$.

Hence the apex is at height $H=k=6+6\sqrt2+6\sqrt3$, and $\ell$ meets the base at $x=\dfrac{k}{\sqrt2}=3\sqrt2+6+3\sqrt6$, which is half the base side $s$:
\[
s=12+6\sqrt2+6\sqrt6=6\left(2+\sqrt2+\sqrt6\right).
\]

\textbf{Area.} Since $H=\sqrt2\cdot\dfrac {s}{2}$, the slant height of a face (from the apex to the midpoint of a base edge) is $\sqrt{\left(\dfrac {s}{2}\right)^2+H^2}=\sqrt3\cdot\dfrac {s}{2}$. The four triangular faces have total area $4\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot s\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3s}{2}=\sqrt3s^2$, so the total surface area is $\left(1+\sqrt3\right)s^2$. Now
\[
s^2=36\left(2+\sqrt2+\sqrt6\right)^2=36\left(12+4\sqrt2+4\sqrt3+4\sqrt6\right)=144\left(3+\sqrt2+\sqrt3+\sqrt6\right),
\]
and $\left(1+\sqrt3\right)\left(3+\sqrt2+\sqrt3+\sqrt6\right)=6+4\sqrt2+4\sqrt3+2\sqrt6$. Therefore the surface area is
\[
\boxed{288\left(3+2\sqrt2+2\sqrt3+\sqrt6\right)}\approx3381.7 .
\]

The right angle is at $A$, so $[ABC]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot3\cdot4=6$. Let $T=[DEF]$. We use repeatedly that triangles with the same apex and bases on one line have areas in the ratio of the bases.
\begin{itemize}
\item $BD=DE$: with apex $F$, $[BDF]=[DEF]=T$; with apex $A$, $[ABD]=[ADE]$.
\item $AE=EF$: with apex $D$, $[ADE]=[EDF]=T$, so also $[ABD]=T$; with apex $C$, $[CAE]=[CEF]$.
\item $FC=2DF$: with apex $E$, $[CEF]=2[DEF]=2T$, so also $[CAE]=2T$; with apex $B$, $[BFC]=2[BDF]=2T$.
\end{itemize}
The segments $AD$, $BF$, $CE$ (dashed) cut triangle $ABC$ into these seven triangles:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
