Let the triangle be $ABC$ with medians $AD=12$, $BE=9$, $CF=15$ (any assignment gives the same answer) meeting at the centroid $G$, which divides each median in the ratio $2:1$. So
\[
AG=8,\ GD=4,\qquad BG=6,\qquad CG=10 .
\]
Reflect $G$ in the midpoint $D$ of $BC$ to get $G'$. The diagonals of $BGCG'$ bisect each other at $D$, so it is a parallelogram and $BG'=CG=10$; also $GG'=2GD=8$. Triangle $BGG'$ therefore has sides $6,8,10$, a right triangle with area $24$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,-8),B=(-6,0),C=(6,8); pair D=(B+C)/2, Ep=(C+A)/2, Fp=(A+B)/2; pair G=(A+B+C)/3, G2=2*D-G;
fill(B--G--G2--cycle,palegreen);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(A--D,linewidth(0.6)); draw(B--Ep,linewidth(0.6)); draw(C--Fp,linewidth(0.6));
draw(D--G2,dashed); draw(B--G2,dashed); draw(C--G2,dashed);
dot(G);dot(G2);dot(D);
label("$A$",A,S); label("$B$",B,W); label("$C$",C,NE); label("$G$",G,SE);
label("$G'$",G2,N); label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$6$",(B+G)/2,S); label("$4$",(0,2),E); label("$4$",(0,6),E); label("$10$",(B+G2)/2,NW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Its area relates to $ABC$ as follows: $D$ is the midpoint of $GG'$, so $[BGG']=2[BGD]$; $D$ is the midpoint of $BC$, so $[BGD]=\dfrac{1}{2}[BGC]$; and $[BGC]=\dfrac{1}{3}[ABC]$ because $GD=\dfrac{1}{3}AD$. Hence $[BGG']=[BGC]=\dfrac{1}{3}[ABC]$, and
\[
[ABC]=3\cdot24=\boxed{72}.
\]
(In general the area of a triangle is $\dfrac{4}{3}$ of the area of the triangle whose sides equal its medians; here $\dfrac{4}{3}\cdot54=72$.)

question:
Let $a>0$. We want to give a meaning to the power $a^{\dfrac{1}{2}}$ so that it is a positive real number and the exponent law $a^m\cdot a^n=a^{m+n}$ still holds for $m=n=\dfrac{1}{2}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that these requirements force $a^{\dfrac{1}{2}}=\sqrt a$.
\item[(b)] In the same way, which real number must $a^{\dfrac{1}{3}}$ be?
\end{enumerate}

A line segment $MN$ drawn across a rectangle, with its endpoints on the boundary, separates the rectangle into $2$ sections, as shown.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(8,0)--(8,5)--(0,5)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
pair Mp=(4.2,5), Np=(5,0);
draw(Mp--Np,linewidth(0.9));
dot(Mp);dot(Np);
label("$M$",Mp,N);label("$N$",Np,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

What is the largest number of sections into which the rectangle can be separated when $4$ line segments (including $MN$), each joining two points on the boundary of the rectangle, are drawn?

solution:
Draw the segments one at a time. Two segments cross at most once. Suppose the $k$-th segment crosses the earlier segments at $c$ distinct points inside the rectangle. These points cut it into at most $c+1$ pieces, and each piece splits one existing section into two. So the $k$-th segment adds at most $c+1\le (k-1)+1=k$ sections.

Starting from $1$ section, after four segments there are at most
\[
1+1+2+3+4=11
\]
sections.

The bound is attained when every two segments cross inside the rectangle and no three pass through one point. Then each segment gains the full $k$ new sections. In the figure below the $11$ sections are numbered.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((0,0)--(8,0)--(8,5)--(0,5)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
pair Mp=(4.2,5), Np=(5,0);
draw(Mp--Np,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,3)--(8,4.3),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((0.7,0)--(7.5,5),deepgreen+linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,4.7)--(7.2,0),red+linewidth(0.9));
label("$M$",Mp,N);label("$N$",Np,S);
pen q=fontsize(8pt);
label("$1$",(7.62,4.62),q);label("$2$",(4.9,4.4),q);label("$3$",(2.24,4.2),q);
label("$4$",(0.56,3.66),q);label("$5$",(4.8,3.44),q);label("$6$",(3.76,3.0),q);
label("$7$",(6.46,2.32),q);label("$8$",(4.32,2.26),q);label("$9$",(1.24,1.94),q);
label("$10$",(3.8,1.02),q);label("$11$",(5.44,0.52),q);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The largest number of sections is $\boxed{11}$.

question:
Three congruent squares are attached to the three sides of a small equilateral triangle, each square lying outside the small triangle. The resulting figure sits inside an equilateral triangle $ABC$ of side length $8$ so that each side of $ABC$ is parallel to a side of the small triangle and passes through exactly two outer vertices of the squares, one from each of two different squares, as shown.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r3=sqrt(3), x=16-8*sqrt(3);
pair sh=(x+x*r3/2,x/2);
pair D=sh, Ep=sh+(-x/2,x*r3/2), F=sh+(x/2,x*r3/2);
pair n1=x*(r3/2,-1/2), n2=x*(-r3/2,-1/2);
pair A=(4,4*r3), B=(0,0), C=(8,0);
filldraw(Ep--F--(F+(0,x))--(Ep+(0,x))--cycle,gray(0.85));
filldraw(D--F--(F+n1)--(D+n1)--cycle,gray(0.85));
filldraw(D--Ep--(Ep+n2)--(D+n2)--cycle,gray(0.85));
draw(D--Ep--F--cycle);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$8$",(4,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Find the side length of one of the squares.

solution:
Let the squares have side $x$, and let $D$ be the vertex of the small triangle nearest to $BC$, as in the figure.

\textbf{The gap at $D$.} Around $D$ we have two right angles of the squares and the $60^\circ$ angle of the small triangle, so the angle between the two squares at $D$ is
\[
360^\circ-90^\circ-90^\circ-60^\circ=120^\circ .
\]
Let $P_1$ and $P_2$ be the outer vertices of these two squares on $BC$. Triangle $P_1DP_2$ is isosceles with legs $x$ and apex angle $120^\circ$, so its base angles are $30^\circ$. Its altitude from $D$ splits it into two $30$-$60$-$90$ triangles with hypotenuse $x$, so
\[
P_1P_2=2\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}x=\sqrt3\,x .
\]

\textbf{The corners.} Let $Q$ be the other outer vertex of the square with side $DP_2$; it lies on $AC$. The side $P_2Q$ is parallel to the side of the small triangle through $D$ on the same square, and that side makes a $60^\circ$ angle with the side of the small triangle parallel to $BC$. So $\angle QP_2C=60^\circ$. Also $\angle P_2CQ=60^\circ$, so triangle $P_2CQ$ is equilateral and $P_2C=P_2Q=x$. By symmetry $BP_1=x$ as well.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r3=sqrt(3), x=16-8*sqrt(3);
pair sh=(x+x*r3/2,x/2);
pair D=sh, Ep=sh+(-x/2,x*r3/2), F=sh+(x/2,x*r3/2);
pair n1=x*(r3/2,-1/2), n2=x*(-r3/2,-1/2);
pair A=(4,4*r3), B=(0,0), C=(8,0);
filldraw(Ep--F--(F+(0,x))--(Ep+(0,x))--cycle,gray(0.85));
filldraw(D--F--(F+n1)--(D+n1)--cycle,gray(0.85));
filldraw(D--Ep--(Ep+n2)--(D+n2)--cycle,gray(0.85));
draw(D--Ep--F--cycle);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);
pair P1=D+n2, P2=D+n1, Q=F+n1;
dot(P1);dot(P2);dot(Q);dot(D);
label("$P_1$",P1,S);label("$P_2$",P2,S);label("$Q$",Q,NE);label("$D$",D+(0,0.5));
label("$x$",(B+P1)/2,S);label("$x\sqrt3$",(P1+P2)/2,1.6S);label("$x$",(P2+C)/2,S);
label("$x$",(P2+Q)/2,W);
draw(arc(D,0.55,-150,-30),blue);label("$120^\circ$",D+(0,-0.42),S,blue+fontsize(8pt));
draw(arc(P2,0.5,0,60),red);label("$60^\circ$",P2+0.62*dir(30),dir(30),red+fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{The equation.} Along side $BC$,
\[
8=BP_1+P_1P_2+P_2C=x+\sqrt3\,x+x=(2+\sqrt3)\,x,
\]
so
\[
x=\dfrac{8}{2+\sqrt3}=\dfrac{8(2-\sqrt3)}{(2+\sqrt3)(2-\sqrt3)}=\boxed{16-8\sqrt3}\approx2.14 .
\]
\textbf{Check with the altitude.} The altitude from $A$ consists of the altitude of the top corner triangle ($\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}x$), a square side ($x$), the altitude of the small triangle ($\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}x$) and the altitude of the gap triangle at $D$ ($\dfrac{x}{2}$). So $4\sqrt3=\left(\sqrt3+\dfrac{3}{2}\right)x$, which gives $x=\dfrac{8\sqrt3}{2\sqrt3+3}=\dfrac{8\sqrt3\,(2\sqrt3-3)}{3}=16-8\sqrt3$, the same value.

The expression is defined only for $x\ge0$. Put $t=\sqrt x\ge0$; then $x=t^2$ and $t$ runs over all nonnegative reals as $x$ does. For $t\ge0$,
\[
\sin t\ge0\iff 2k\pi\le t\le(2k+1)\pi\ \text{ for some integer }k\ge0 .
\]
Squaring (both ends are nonnegative, and squaring is increasing on $[0,\infty)$) gives
\[
\boxed{\,4k^2\pi^2\le x\le(2k+1)^2\pi^2\quad\text{for some integer }k\ge0\,}
\]
that is, $x\in[0,\pi^2]\cup[4\pi^2,9\pi^2]\cup[16\pi^2,25\pi^2]\cup\cdots$. The graph shows these intervals in red: the intervals where the sine is negative, $\left((2k+1)^2\pi^2,(2k+2)^2\pi^2\right)$, get longer as $x$ grows.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real t){return 1.4*sin(sqrt(20*t));}
pen hl=red+linewidth(3);
draw((0,0)--(0.4935,0),hl);draw((1.974,0)--(4.441,0),hl);draw((7.896,0)--(8.7,0),hl);
draw((0,0)--(9.2,0),Arrow(TeXHead));draw((0,-1.8)--(0,1.9),Arrow(TeXHead));
draw(graph(f,0,8.7,400),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,1.4)--(8.7,1.4),dotted);draw((0,-1.4)--(8.7,-1.4),dotted);
label("$1$",(0,1.4),W);label("$-1$",(0,-1.4),W);
label("$x$",(9.2,0),E);label("$y$",(0,1.9),N);
real[] xs={0.4935,1.974,4.441,7.896};
string[] ls={"$\pi^2$","$4\pi^2$","$9\pi^2$","$16\pi^2$"};
for(int i=0;i<4;++i){draw((xs[i],-0.1)--(xs[i],0.1));draw((xs[i],0)--(xs[i],-1.6),gray(0.5)+dotted);label(ls[i],(xs[i],-1.6),S,fontsize(9pt));}
label("$y=\sin\sqrt x$",(3.2,1.45),N,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Since $60=2^2\cdot3\cdot5$, it has $(2+1)(1+1)(1+1)=12$ positive divisors. Every solution has $a\ne0$, $a\mid60$ and $b=\dfrac{60}{a}$, and $a$, $b$ have the same sign.
\begin{itemize}
\item Positive pairs: $a$ is any of the $12$ positive divisors, giving $12$ pairs.
\item Negative pairs: $a$ is the negative of a positive divisor, giving another $12$ pairs, such as $(-1,-60)$ and $(-6,-10)$.
\end{itemize}
So there are $\boxed{24}$ ordered pairs of integers, of which $\boxed{12}$ are pairs of positive integers. (An answer of $12$ counts only the positive pairs.)

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $2^{2019}+3^{2018}$ is not divisible by $7$, so the expression $\dfrac{2^{2019}+3^{2018}}{7}$ is not an integer and has no units digit.
\item[(b)] Show that $N=2^{2019}+3^{2019}$ is divisible by $7$, and find the units digit of the integer $\dfrac {N}{7}$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $\angle ABD=\beta$ and $\angle ACD=\gamma$. Then $\angle DBC=2\beta$ and $\angle DCB=2\gamma$, so $\angle B=3\beta$ and $\angle C=3\gamma$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0), C=(10,0);
pair A=extension(B,B+dir(33),C,C+dir(180-27));
pair D=extension(B,B+dir(22),C,C+dir(180-18));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--D--C);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);
draw(arc(B,2.2,0,22),blue);draw(arc(B,2.6,22,33),red);
label("$2\beta$",B+3.0*dir(11),blue);label("$\beta$",B+3.25*dir(28),red);
draw(arc(C,2.2,162,180),blue);draw(arc(C,2.6,153,162),red);
label("$2\gamma$",C+3.05*dir(171),blue);label("$\gamma$",C+3.25*dir(157),red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The angles of triangle $ABC$ give $3\beta+3\gamma=180^\circ-120^\circ=60^\circ$, so $\beta+\gamma=20^\circ$. In triangle $BDC$,
\[
\angle BDC=180^\circ-2\beta-2\gamma=180^\circ-40^\circ=\boxed{140^\circ}.
\]

Rotate the plane about $B$ through $60^\circ$ in the direction that takes $A$ to $C$, and let $O'$ be the image of $O$. The rotation maps triangle $BOA$ onto triangle $BO'C$, so
\[
O'C=OA\qquad\text{and}\qquad \angle BO'C=\angle BOA .
\]
Also $BO'=BO$ and $\angle OBO'=60^\circ$, so triangle $BOO'$ is equilateral: $OO'=OB$ and $\angle BO'O=60^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(6,0), C=(3,3*sqrt(3));
pair O=extension(A,A+dir(20),B,B+dir(170));
pair Op=B+rotate(-60)*(O-B);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--A);draw(O--B);draw(O--C);
draw(B--Op--C,blue+dashed);
draw(O--Op,blue);
pair u=unit(O-Op), v=unit(C-Op);
draw((Op+0.3*u)--(Op+0.3*u+0.3*v)--(Op+0.3*v),blue);
dot(O);dot(Op);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$O$",O,S);label("$O'$",Op,E);
draw(arc(B,0.7,degrees(O-B),degrees(Op-B)),red);label("$60^\circ$",B+1.0*dir((degrees(O-B)+degrees(Op-B))/2),red+fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

In triangle $OO'C$ the sides are $OO'=OB$, $O'C=OA$ and $OC$. The hypothesis $OA^2+OB^2=OC^2$ says $O'C^2+OO'^2=OC^2$, so by the converse of the Pythagorean theorem $\angle OO'C=90^\circ$.

The rotation maps triangle $ABC$ to triangle $CBC^*$, where $C^*$ is the reflection of $A$ in $BC$, so $O'$ lies inside the rhombus $ABC^*C$ on the other side of $BC$ from $O$. Hence segment $OO'$ crosses side $BC$, and ray $O'O$ lies inside angle $BO'C$. Therefore
\[
\angle AOB=\angle BO'C=\angle BO'O+\angle OO'C=60^\circ+90^\circ=\boxed{150^\circ}.
\]

question:
A point $P$ inside a square $ABCD$ is joined to the midpoints of the four sides, dividing the square into four quadrilaterals, one at each vertex.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that the areas of the quadrilaterals at $A$ and $C$ add up to the same total as the areas of the quadrilaterals at $B$ and $D$.
\item[(b)] The quadrilaterals at $A$, $B$ and $C$ have areas $22$, $18$ and $10$. Find the area of the quadrilateral at $D$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(8,0), C=(8,8), D=(0,8), P=(5,6);
pair M1=(4,0), M2=(8,4), M3=(4,8), M4=(0,4);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--M1);draw(P--M2);draw(P--M3);draw(P--M4);
dot(P);dot(M1);dot(M2);dot(M3);dot(M4);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$P$",P,NE);
label("$22$",(2.2,2.6));label("$18$",(6.6,2.0));label("$10$",(6.7,6.6));label("?",(2.0,6.3));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
(a) Join $P$ also to the four vertices; this splits each quadrilateral into two triangles. On side $AB$ with midpoint $M_1$, triangles $PAM_1$ and $PM_1B$ have equal bases $AM_1=M_1B$ and the same height (the distance from $P$ to $AB$), so they have equal areas; call this area $p$. In the same way the two triangles on side $BC$ each have area $q$, those on $CD$ area $r$, and those on $DA$ area $s$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(8,0), C=(8,8), D=(0,8), P=(5,6);
pair M1=(4,0), M2=(8,4), M3=(4,8), M4=(0,4);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--M1);draw(P--M2);draw(P--M3);draw(P--M4);
dot(P);dot(M1);dot(M2);dot(M3);dot(M4);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$P$",P,E);
draw(P--A,dashed);draw(P--B,dashed);draw(P--C,dashed);draw(P--D,dashed);
pen q=blue+fontsize(9pt);
label("$p$",(3,2),q);label("$p$",(5.67,2),q);
label("$q$",(7,3.33),q);label("$q$",(7,6),q);
label("$r$",(5.67,7.33),q);label("$r$",(3,7.33),q);
label("$s$",(1.67,6),q);label("$s$",(1.67,3.33),q);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The quadrilateral at each vertex consists of one triangle from each of the two sides meeting there:
\[
[A]=p+s,\qquad [B]=p+q,\qquad [C]=q+r,\qquad [D]=r+s .
\]
Hence $[A]+[C]=p+q+r+s=[B]+[D]$; each sum is half the area of the square. $\blacksquare$

(b) By (a), $[D]=[A]+[C]-[B]=22+10-18=\boxed{14}$. Such a configuration exists: for a square of side $8$ with $A=(0,0)$ and $P=(5,6)$ we get $p=12$, $q=6$, $r=4$, $s=10$ and the areas $22,18,10,14$.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] (B\'ezout's identity) Prove that for any positive integers $a$ and $b$ there are integers $x$ and $y$ with $ax+by=\gcd(a,b)$.
\item[(b)] Find all pairs of integers $(x,y)$ with $132x+81y=3$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $N=2^a m$ where $a\ge0$ and $m$ is odd, and let $d(m)$ denote the number of positive divisors of $m$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $N$ has $d(m)$ odd positive divisors and $a\cdot d(m)$ even positive divisors.
\item[(b)] How many odd and how many even positive divisors does $N=2^5\cdot3^4\cdot5^2\cdot7$ have?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the radius be $R$ and let $k=R^2-OP^2$, the power of $P$; since $P\ne O$ is inside the circle, $0<k<R^2$.

\textbf{Which chords are possible.} By the power of a point, every chord through $P$ is split into segments $u,v$ with $uv=k$. So all ten segments are integers whose products in pairs equal $k$, and $k$ is an integer. Conversely, the chords through $P$ have every length from $2\sqrt k$ (the chord perpendicular to $OP$) up to $2R$ (the diameter through $P$). Each length strictly between occurs for exactly two chords, mirror images in line $OP$, and the two extreme lengths occur once each. A chord of length $L$ has segments $u,v$ with $u+v=L$ and $uv=k$. Hence the chords with integer segments correspond to factorisations $k=uv$ with $u\le v$ and $u+v\le2R$ (note $u+v\ge2\sqrt{uv}=2\sqrt k$ automatically). Such a factorisation gives two chords if $u<v$ and $u+v<2R$, and one chord if $u=v$ or $u+v=2R$.

\textbf{$R\le6$ is impossible.} Five chords need at least three such factorisations of $k$ (two factorisations give at most $2+2=4$ chords). If $R\le6$, then $k<36$ and every factorisation used has $v\le u+v-1\le11$. Going through the numbers below $36$ with at least five divisors:
\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
k & \text{factorisations } uv \text{ with } u+v\le12\\\hline
12 & 3\cdot4,\ 2\cdot6\\
16 & 4\cdot4,\ 2\cdot8\\
18 & 3\cdot6,\ 2\cdot9\\
20 & 4\cdot5,\ 2\cdot10\\
24 & 4\cdot6,\ 3\cdot8\\
28,\ 30,\ 32 & \text{one each } (4\cdot7,\ 5\cdot6,\ 4\cdot8)
\end{array}
\]
Numbers with fewer divisors have at most two factorisations at all. So at most two factorisations are usable and at most $4$ chords exist; $R\ge7$.

\textbf{$R=7$ works.} Take $k=24$, i.e.\ $OP=\sqrt{49-24}=5$. The factorisations $24=2\cdot12$ (with $2+12=14=2R$: the diameter), $24=3\cdot8$ ($3+8=11$: two chords) and $24=4\cdot6$ ($4+6=10$: two chords) give exactly five chords with integer segments.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0), P=(5,0);
draw(circle(O,7),linewidth(0.9));
real[] th={0,60,-60,78.463,-78.463};
pen[] cl={black,blue,blue,deepgreen,deepgreen};
for(int i=0;i<5;++i){
  pair u=dir(th[i]);
  real cs=cos(th[i]*pi/180);
  real t1=-5*cs+sqrt(25*cs*cs+24), t2=-5*cs-sqrt(25*cs*cs+24);
  draw((P+t2*u)--(P+t1*u),cl[i]+linewidth(0.8));
}
dot(O);dot(P);
label("$O$",O,S);label("$P$",P+0.8*dir(208));
label("$12$",(-1,0),N);label("$2$",(6.1,0.45));
label("$3$",(6.29,1.33),blue);label("$3$",(6.29,-1.33),blue);
label("$8$",(2.32,3.65),blue);label("$8$",(2.32,-3.65),blue);
label("$4$",P+2.5*dir(87),deepgreen);label("$4$",P+2.5*dir(-87),deepgreen);
label("$6$",P+4*dir(110),deepgreen);label("$6$",P+4*dir(-110),deepgreen);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(Another example: $k=12$, $OP=\sqrt{37}$, with chords $1+12$, $2+6$, $3+4$, two of each.) So the least possible radius is $\boxed{7}$.

\textbf{About the proposed solution in the post} (taking the five segments on one side of $P$ to be $1,2,3,4,5$ and concluding the radius is $1+6=7$). It cannot be right: by the power of a point the other segments would have to be $\dfrac {k}{1},\dfrac {k}{2},\dots,\dfrac {k}{5}$, so $60\mid k$, and the chord with segments $1$ and $k\ge60$ would need $2R\ge61$. The answer $7$ is correct, but that argument does not prove it.

question:
In triangle $ABC$, medians $AD$ and $BE$ meet at $G$, and ray $CG$ meets $AB$ at $F$. (Here $[XYZ]$ denotes the area of triangle $XYZ$.)
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that $[ACG]=[BCG]=[ABG]$.
\item[(b)] Prove that $\dfrac{AF}{FB}=\dfrac{[ACG]}{[BCG]}$, and conclude that $F$ is the midpoint of $AB$, so that the three medians are concurrent.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Throughout we use: two triangles with the same height have areas in the ratio of their bases.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0), B=(7,0), C=(2,5);
pair D=(B+C)/2, Ep=(C+A)/2, G=(A+B+C)/3, F=(A+B)/2;
fill(A--C--G--cycle,paleblue);fill(B--C--G--cycle,palered);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D);draw(B--Ep);draw(C--F,dashed);
dot(D);dot(Ep);dot(G);dot(F);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);
label("$D$",D,NE);label("$E$",Ep,NW);label("$F$",F,S);label("$G$",G+0.5*dir(232));
void tick(pair P,pair Q,int n){pair m=(P+Q)/2,u=unit(Q-P),w=rotate(90)*u;for(int i=0;i<n;++i){pair c=m+(i-(n-1)/2)*0.16*u;draw((c-0.16*w)--(c+0.16*w));}}
tick(B,D,1);tick(D,C,1);tick(C,Ep,2);tick(Ep,A,2);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(a) $D$ is the midpoint of $BC$, so $[ABD]=[ACD]$ (equal bases $BD=DC$, same height from $A$) and $[GBD]=[GCD]$ (same height from $G$). Subtracting, $[ABG]=[ACG]$. In the same way, $E$ is the midpoint of $CA$, so $[BCE]=[BAE]$ and $[GCE]=[GAE]$; subtracting gives $[BCG]=[BAG]$. Hence $[ACG]=[BCG]=[ABG]$.

(b) Let $r=\dfrac{AF}{FB}$. Triangles $AFC$ and $BFC$ have bases $AF$ and $FB$ on line $AB$ and the same apex $C$, so $[AFC]=r\,[BFC]$. Likewise, with apex $G$, $[AFG]=r\,[BFG]$. Since $G$ lies on segment $CF$,
\[
[ACG]=[AFC]-[AFG]=r\bigl([BFC]-[BFG]\bigr)=r\,[BCG] .
\]
So $\dfrac{AF}{FB}=\dfrac{[ACG]}{[BCG]}$, which is $1$ by part (a). Thus $AF=FB$, so $CF$ is the third median and it passes through $G$: the three medians are concurrent. $\blacksquare$

Sort the lines by direction: lines in the same class are parallel, and lines in different classes meet in exactly one point. Let the class sizes be a partition of $4$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $\{4\}$: all parallel, no intersection point at all.
\item $\{2,2\}$: each line of one pair meets each line of the other pair, in $4$ different points (the vertices of a parallelogram). Too many.
\item $\{1,1,1,1\}$: no two lines are parallel, so there are $6$ intersecting pairs. If a point lies on $m$ lines, it accounts for $\dbinom m2\in\{1,3,6\}$ of these pairs. Three points would need three numbers from $\{1,3,6\}$ adding to $6$, which is impossible. So this case cannot have exactly three points.
\item $\{3,1\}$: three parallel lines and one line crossing them in $3$ different points. This works.
\item $\{2,1,1\}$: a parallel pair $\ell_1,\ell_2$ and two more lines $m_1,m_2$ meeting at a point $Q$. The points $\ell_i\cap m_j$ are four different points unless $Q$ lies on $\ell_1$ or $\ell_2$. If $Q$ is on neither, there are $5$ points. If $Q$ lies on $\ell_1$ (say), the points are $Q$, $\ell_2\cap m_1$ and $\ell_2\cap m_2$, exactly three. This works.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Counting regions.} A new line that meets the earlier lines in $c$ distinct points is cut into $c+1$ pieces, and each piece splits a region in two.

\emph{Case $\{3,1\}$:} three parallel lines make $4$ strips, and the crossing line adds $3+1=4$ regions: $8$ regions.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-3,0)--(3,0),Arrows(TeXHead));draw((-3,1.5)--(3,1.5),Arrows(TeXHead));draw((-3,3)--(3,3),Arrows(TeXHead));
draw((-2,-1)--(2.4,4.5),blue,Arrows(TeXHead));
pen q=fontsize(9pt);
label("$1$",(-2,3.8),q);label("$2$",(2.3,3.8),q);label("$3$",(-1.6,2.25),q);label("$4$",(1.9,2.25),q);
label("$5$",(-2.2,0.75),q);label("$6$",(1.5,0.75),q);label("$7$",(-2.6,-0.7),q);label("$8$",(0.6,-0.7),q);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\emph{Case $\{2,1,1\}$ with $Q\in\ell_1$:} the three lines through $Q$ make $6$ regions, and $\ell_2$ crosses the other two lines at $2$ points, adding $3$ regions: $9$ regions.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-3.5,0)--(3.5,0),Arrows(TeXHead));draw((-3.5,2)--(3.5,2),Arrows(TeXHead));
draw((-1.8,-2.16)--(2.9,3.48),blue,Arrows(TeXHead));draw((1.8,-2.16)--(-2.9,3.48),blue,Arrows(TeXHead));
dot((0,0));label("$Q$",(0,0),3*S);
pen q=fontsize(9pt);
label("$1$",(0,3.0),q);label("$2$",(-3.0,2.8),q);label("$3$",(3.0,2.8),q);label("$4$",(0,1.2),q);
label("$5$",(-2.6,1.0),q);label("$6$",(2.6,1.0),q);label("$7$",(0,-1.5),q);label("$8$",(-2.6,-1.0),q);label("$9$",(2.6,-1.0),q);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So the number of regions is $\boxed{8\text{ or }9}$, and both values occur. (The answer $13$ is impossible: four lines can never make more than $1+1+2+3+4=11$ regions.)

Let the parallel sides be $a$ and $b$ and the equal sides (legs) $c$. Both parallel sides are tangent to the circle, so the height of the trapezoid is the diameter, $4$ cm. The area gives
\[
\dfrac{a+b}{2}\cdot4=20\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad a+b=10 .
\]
A quadrilateral circumscribed about a circle has equal sums of opposite sides: the two tangent segments from each vertex are equal, and each side is the sum of two tangent segments, so $a+b=c+c$. Hence $2c=10$ and the legs have length $\boxed{5\text{ cm}}$.

Check: with legs $5$ and height $4$, the horizontal offset of each leg is $3$, so $b-a=6$; together with $a+b=10$ this gives bases $2$ and $8$, and the circle centred at height $2$ above the midpoint of the long base is tangent to all four sides.

