The quadratic $2x^2-4x-3$ is written in the form $ax^2+bx+c$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What are $a$, $b$ and $c$?
\item[(b)] Use the quadratic formula to solve $2x^2-4x-3=0$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
If $y\le0$, then $x^y$ is $1$ (for $y=0$) or $\dfrac{1}{x^{|y|}}$, which has absolute value at most $1$; neither equals $16$. So $y\ge1$, and $|x|^y=16=2^4$ forces $|x|=2^{\dfrac{4}{y}}$ with $y\mid4$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $y=1$: $x=16$ (here $x=-16$ gives $-16$);
\item $y=2$: $x=4$ or $x=-4$;
\item $y=4$: $x=2$ or $x=-2$.
\end{itemize}
The pairs are $(16,1),(4,2),(-4,2),(2,4),(-2,4)$. The possible values of $x$ are $16,4,-4,2,-2$, with sum $16$. The possible values of $y$ are $1,2,4$, with sum $7$. The positive difference is $16-7=\boxed{9}$.

Name the inscribed angles by the arcs they stand on:
\[
x=\angle CAD=\angle CBD,\quad \beta=\angle ACD,\quad \gamma=\angle BDC,\quad \delta=\angle ADB=\angle ACB .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
