\textbf{Triangle $ACD$.} Its angles are $x$, $\beta$ and $\angle ADC=\angle ADB+\angle BDC=\delta+\gamma$, so
\[
x+\beta+\gamma+\delta=180^\circ. \tag{1}
\]
\textbf{Triangle $DQC$.} Since $Q$ lies on the diagonals, $\angle QDC=\angle BDC=\gamma$ and $\angle DCQ=\angle DCA=\beta$. As $DQ=DC$, the angles opposite these equal sides are equal: $\angle DQC=\angle DCQ=\beta$. So
\[
\gamma+2\beta=180^\circ. \tag{2}
\]
\textbf{Triangle $PDC$.} Here $P$ lies on ray $DA$ beyond $A$ and on ray $CB$ beyond $B$, so $\angle PDC=\angle ADC=\delta+\gamma$ and $\angle PCD=\angle BCD=\angle BCA+\angle ACD=\delta+\beta$. As $CP=CD$, $\angle CPD=\angle CDP=\delta+\gamma$, and the angle sum gives
\[
2(\delta+\gamma)+(\delta+\beta)=180^\circ,\quad\text{i.e.}\quad 3\delta+2\gamma+\beta=180^\circ. \tag{3}
\]
From (2), $\gamma=180^\circ-2\beta$. Then (3) gives $3\delta+360^\circ-3\beta=180^\circ$, so $\delta=\beta-60^\circ$. Finally (1) gives
\[
x=180^\circ-\beta-(180^\circ-2\beta)-(\beta-60^\circ)=60^\circ .
\]
So $\angle CAD=\boxed{60^\circ}$. Such quadrilaterals exist: for example $\beta=75^\circ$, $\gamma=30^\circ$, $\delta=15^\circ$ (arcs $AB,BC,CD,DA$ of $30^\circ,60^\circ,120^\circ,150^\circ$), which is the configuration drawn.

First count all even four-digit numbers with four different digits.
\begin{itemize}
\item Last digit $0$: the first digit has $9$ choices, then $8$ and $7$ for the middle digits: $9\cdot8\cdot7=504$.
\item Last digit $2,4,6$ or $8$ ($4$ choices): the first digit is neither $0$ nor the last digit ($8$ choices), then $8$ and $7$ choices for the middle digits: $4\cdot8\cdot8\cdot7=1792$.
\end{itemize}
That is $504+1792=2296$ numbers. Now remove those that are at most $2015$.
\begin{itemize}
\item From $1000$ to $1999$: first digit $1$, last digit one of $0,2,4,6,8$ ($5$ choices), middle digits $8\cdot7$: $5\cdot56=280$.
\item From $2000$ to $2015$: the even numbers are $2000,2002,\dots,2014$, and only $2014$ has four different digits: $1$.
\end{itemize}
The answer is $2296-280-1=\boxed{2015}$.

The point $(1,2)$ is at squared distance $1+4=5$ from $(0,0)$, $(2,0)$, $(2,4)$ and $4+1=5$ from $(3,1)$, $(3,3)$. So the circle
\[
C:\ (x-1)^2+(y-2)^2=5
\]
passes through the five points $(0,0),(2,0),(3,1),(3,3),(2,4)$. It does not pass through its own centre $(1,2)$, nor through $(4,3)$, whose squared distance from $(1,2)$ is $9+1=10$.

