\end{asy}

So $S=2700^\circ$, and $P$ may or may not be regular.

Since $5^2+12^2=13^2$, the angle at $C$ is right. Use coordinates $C=(0,0)$, $B=(5,0)$, $A=(0,12)$.

By the angle bisector theorem, $\dfrac{CD}{DB}=\dfrac{AC}{AB}=\dfrac{12}{13}$, so $CD=\dfrac{12}{25}\cdot5=\dfrac{12}{5}$ and $D=\left(\dfrac{12}{5},0\right)$. Similarly $\dfrac{CE}{EA}=\dfrac{BC}{BA}=\dfrac{5}{13}$, so $CE=\dfrac{5}{18}\cdot12=\dfrac{10}{3}$ and $E=\left(0,\dfrac{10}{3}\right)$. The bisectors meet at the incentre; the inradius of a right triangle is $\dfrac{5+12-13}{2}=2$, so $I=(2,2)$.

The midpoint of $DE$ is $M=\left(\dfrac{6}{5},\dfrac{5}{3}\right)$, and $I-M=\left(\dfrac{4}{5},\dfrac{1}{3}\right)$ is parallel to $(12,5)$. The line $AB$ is $12x+5y=60$. A point of line $MI$ is $(2+12t,\,2+5t)$; it lies on $AB$ when
\[
12(2+12t)+5(2+5t)=60\iff169t=26\iff t=\dfrac{2}{13},
\]
so $F=\left(\dfrac{50}{13},\dfrac{36}{13}\right)$. Then
\[
AF=\sqrt{\left(\dfrac{50}{13}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{36}{13}-12\right)^2}=\dfrac{\sqrt{2500+14400}}{13}=\dfrac{130}{13}=\boxed{10}.
\]

