question:
Real numbers $x$ and $y$ satisfy $(5x-2y)^2=36(1-y^2)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that there are no solutions with $x$ and $y$ both integers.
\item[(b)] Find the largest possible value of $x$, and the value of $y$ at which it is attained.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
A point of the unit circle at angle $\theta$ (measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis) is $(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$.

$\arccos0$ is the angle $\theta$ in $[0,\pi]$ with $\cos\theta=0$, i.e.\ whose point has $x$-coordinate $0$. The points with $x=0$ are $(0,1)$ at $\theta=\dfrac{\pi}{2}$ and $(0,-1)$ at $\theta=\dfrac{3\pi}{2}$; only the first angle is in $[0,\pi]$. So $\arccos0=\boxed{\dfrac{\pi}{2}}$.

$\arcsin1$ is the angle $\theta$ in $\left[-\dfrac{\pi}{2},\dfrac{\pi}{2}\right]$ with $\sin\theta=1$, i.e.\ whose point has $y$-coordinate $1$. The only such point is $(0,1)$, at $\theta=\dfrac{\pi}{2}$. So $\arcsin1=\boxed{\dfrac{\pi}{2}}$.

