\end{asy}

question:
A student notices that if $n$ is a perfect square, then the next perfect square is $n+2\sqrt n+1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove this for every perfect square $n=k^2$ with $k\ge0$ an integer.
\item[(b)] Show by an example that the formula does not give the next perfect square when $n$ is not a perfect square.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
A line is tangent to a circle exactly when its distance from the centre equals the radius. The line $x-y+k=0$ is at distance $\dfrac{|k|}{\sqrt{1^2+1^2}}=\dfrac{|k|}{\sqrt2}$ from the origin, so we need $\dfrac{|k|}{\sqrt2}=5$, i.e.
\[
k=\boxed{\pm5\sqrt2}.
\]
Alternatively, substitute $y=x+k$: $x^2+(x+k)^2=25$, i.e.\ $2x^2+2kx+k^2-25=0$. Tangency means a double root, so the discriminant $4k^2-8\left(k^2-25\right)=200-4k^2$ is $0$, giving $k^2=50$ again.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=5, q=5*sqrt(2);
draw((-9,0)--(9,0),gray(0.5),Arrow(5));draw((0,-9)--(0,9),gray(0.5),Arrow(5));
draw(circle((0,0),r),linewidth(0.9));
draw((-9,-9+q)--(9-q,9),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw((-9+q,-9)--(9,9-q),blue+linewidth(0.9));
pair T1=(-q/2,q/2),T2=(q/2,-q/2);
