Place the square as $A=(0,0)$, $B=(3,0)$, $C=(3,3)$, $D=(0,3)$, and let $X=(x,y)$ with $0<x,y<3$. The segment $AX$ splits quadrilateral $ABXD$ into triangles $ABX$ and $AXD$ ($B$ and $D$ lie on opposite sides of line $AX$). Triangle $ABX$ has base $AB=3$ and height $y$; triangle $AXD$ has base $AD=3$ and height $x$. So
\[
[ABXD]=\dfrac{3}{2}y+\dfrac{3}{2}x=\dfrac{3}{2}(x+y),
\]
and $[ABXD]>4$ exactly when $x+y>\dfrac{8}{3}$.

\begin{asy}
