The part of the square with $x+y\le\dfrac{8}{3}$ is a right isosceles triangle with legs $\dfrac{8}{3}$, of area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\dfrac{64}{9}=\dfrac{32}{9}$. The shaded part has area $9-\dfrac{32}{9}=\dfrac{49}{9}$, so the probability is
\[
\dfrac{\dfrac{49}{9}}{9}=\boxed{\dfrac{49}{81}} .
\]

\textbf{Power of the point $B$.} The line $BA$ is tangent to the circle at $E$, and the line $BC$ meets the circle at $D$ and $C$. So
\[
BE^2=BD\cdot BC=8\cdot11=88 .
\]
\textbf{A lemma.} If $P$ moves along a line $\ell$ perpendicular to $DE$, then $PE^2-PD^2$ stays constant. Indeed, take coordinates with $DE$ on the $x$-axis, $D=(d,0)$, $E=(e,0)$, and $\ell$ the line $x=k$. For $P=(k,y)$,
\[
PE^2-PD^2=(k-e)^2+y^2-(k-d)^2-y^2=(k-e)^2-(k-d)^2,
\]
which does not depend on $y$.

\textbf{Conclusion.} Both $B$ and $P$ lie on the line through $B$ perpendicular to $DE$, so
\[
PE^2-PD^2=BE^2-BD^2=88-64=24 .
\]
With $PE=7$ this gives $PD^2=49-24=25$, so $DP=\boxed{5}$.

\begin{asy}
