\textbf{Minimum.} Let $s=a+b+c$. Then
\[
S+3=\sum\left(\dfrac{a}{b+c}+1\right)=s\left(\dfrac{1}{b+c}+\dfrac{1}{c+a}+\dfrac{1}{a+b}\right).
\]
By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality,
\[
\bigl((b+c)+(c+a)+(a+b)\bigr)\left(\dfrac{1}{b+c}+\dfrac{1}{c+a}+\dfrac{1}{a+b}\right)\ge9,
\]
i.e.\ $2s\sum\dfrac{1}{b+c}\ge9$. So $S+3\ge\dfrac{9}{2}$ and $S\ge\dfrac{3}{2}$, with equality exactly when $b+c=c+a=a+b$, i.e.\ for an equilateral triangle. The minimum is $\boxed{\dfrac{3}{2}}$.

\begin{asy}
