label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{No maximum.} By the triangle inequality $b+c>a$, so $2(b+c)>a+b+c=s$ and $\dfrac{a}{b+c}<\dfrac{2a}{s}$. Adding the three such inequalities,
\[
S<\dfrac{2a+2b+2c}{s}=2 .
\]
The bound $2$ is approached but never reached: for the triangle with sides $1,1,\varepsilon$ ($0<\varepsilon<2$),
\[
S=\dfrac{2}{1+\varepsilon}+\dfrac{\varepsilon}{2}\longrightarrow2\qquad(\varepsilon\to0).
\]
So $S$ takes values in $\left[\dfrac{3}{2},2\right)$: the minimum is $\dfrac{3}{2}$ and there is \textbf{no maximum} (the supremum $2$ is not attained).

(If $a,b,c$ were only positive numbers with $a+b>c$, the minimum would still be $\dfrac{3}{2}$, but $S$ would be unbounded: $a=t$, $b=c=1$ gives $S>\dfrac {t}{2}$.)

