The condition says $p^2+2^p\equiv p^5+5^p\pmod{10^5}$, so in particular it must hold modulo $8$. Let $p\ge7$ be prime, so $p$ is odd. Then
\begin{itemize}
\item $2^p\equiv0\pmod8$, since $p\ge3$;
\item $p^2\equiv1\pmod8$, since the square of every odd number is $1$ modulo $8$;
\item $p^5=p\cdot\left(p^2\right)^2\equiv p\pmod8$;
\item $5^p=5\cdot\left(5^2\right)^{\dfrac{p-1}{2}}\equiv5\pmod8$, since $25\equiv1\pmod8$.
\end{itemize}
So we would need $1\equiv p+5\pmod8$, i.e.\ $p\equiv4\pmod8$, which is impossible for odd $p$. Hence there are $\boxed{\text{no such primes}}$.

Take the candy radius to be $1$ and the box radius $3$, with centre $O$. A candy lies in the box exactly when its centre is within distance $2$ of $O$, and two candies do not overlap exactly when their centres are at least $2$ apart.

\textbf{Seven fit.} Put one candy at $O$ and six with centres at distance $2$ from $O$ in the directions $0^\circ,60^\circ,\dots,300^\circ$. Adjacent outer centres form a regular hexagon of side $2$, so all centres are at least $2$ apart, and the outer candies just touch the box.

