\end{asy}

So the region is the square with vertices $(\pm4,0)$, $(0,\pm4)$, and its area is $4\cdot8=\boxed{32}$ (equivalently, a square with diagonals $8$ has area $\dfrac{8\cdot8}{2}=32$).

For positive $u\ne v$ we have $v^3-u^3=(v-u)(u^2+uv+v^2)$, so
\[
\dfrac{1}{u^2+uv+v^2}=\dfrac{v-u}{v^3-u^3}.
\]
Apply this with $u=\sqrt[3]{k}$ and $v=\sqrt[3]{k+1}$, where $u^2+uv+v^2=\sqrt[3]{k^2}+\sqrt[3]{k(k+1)}+\sqrt[3]{(k+1)^2}$ and $v^3-u^3=1$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $k=1$: $\sqrt[3]{1}+\sqrt[3]{2}+\sqrt[3]{4}$, so the first term is $\sqrt[3]{2}-1$;
\item $k=2$: $\sqrt[3]{4}+\sqrt[3]{6}+\sqrt[3]{9}$, so the second term is $\sqrt[3]{3}-\sqrt[3]{2}$;
\item $k=3$: $\sqrt[3]{9}+\sqrt[3]{12}+\sqrt[3]{16}$, so the third term is $\sqrt[3]{4}-\sqrt[3]{3}$.
\end{itemize}
The sum telescopes to $\boxed{\sqrt[3]{4}-1}$.

Place the square at $[0,2]\times[0,2]$ and let the point be $P=(x,y)$, uniformly distributed in the square. Each triangle has a side of the square (length $2$) as base, and its height is the distance from $P$ to that side, so the four areas are
\[
y,\quad 2-y,\quad x,\quad 2-x
\]
(bottom, top, left and right triangles).

\begin{asy}
