Let $O_1,O_2,O_3$ be the centres. They form an equilateral triangle of side $2$ with centroid $G$, and $GO_i=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3}$; the distance from $G$ to each side of $O_1O_2O_3$ (its inradius) is $\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}$.

\textbf{A triangle that works.} Take the equilateral triangle whose sides are parallel to the sides of $O_1O_2O_3$ and lie at distance $1$ outside them. Each side is tangent to two of the circles, it contains all three circles, its centre is $G$, and its inradius is $\rho=1+\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r0=2/sqrt(3);real rho=1+1/sqrt(3);
pair G=(0,0);
pair O1=r0*dir(90),O2=r0*dir(210),O3=r0*dir(330);
pair V1=2*rho*dir(90),V2=2*rho*dir(210),V3=2*rho*dir(330);
draw(V1--V2--V3--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(circle(O1,1));draw(circle(O2,1));draw(circle(O3,1));
draw(O1--O2--O3--cycle,dashed+gray);
dot(O1);dot(O2);dot(O3);
label("$O_1$",O1,N,fontsize(9pt));label("$O_2$",O2,dir(150),fontsize(9pt));label("$O_3$",O3,dir(30),fontsize(9pt));
label("$2$",(0.5,-0.577),S,gray+fontsize(9pt));
draw(O2--O2+(0,-1),blue);label("$1$",O2+(0,-0.5),W,blue+fontsize(9pt));
label("$2+2\sqrt3$",(0,-rho),S,fontsize(9pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{No smaller triangle works.} Let $T$ be any equilateral triangle containing the three discs, with inradius $\rho'$. For every point inside an equilateral triangle the sum of its distances to the three sides equals the altitude, $3\rho'$. Take one side line $\ell$ of $T$ with inward unit normal $n$. The three vectors $O_i-G$ have length $\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3}$ and point in directions $120^\circ$ apart, so one of them makes an angle of at least $120^\circ$ with $n$ (every direction, in particular $-n$, is within $60^\circ$ of one of the three); for that $i$,
\[
n\cdot(O_i-G)\le\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3}\cos120^\circ=-\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}.
\]
The disc around $O_i$ lies inside $T$, so $O_i$ is at distance at least $1$ from $\ell$. Hence
\[
\operatorname{dist}(G,\ell)=\operatorname{dist}(O_i,\ell)-n\cdot(O_i-G)\ge1+\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}.
\]
This holds for all three sides, and $G$ lies inside $T$, so $3\rho'\ge3\left(1+\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}\right)$ and $\rho'\ge\rho$.

\textbf{Area.} An equilateral triangle with inradius $\rho$ has side $2\sqrt3\rho$ and area $3\sqrt3\rho^2$. With $\rho=1+\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}$ the side is $2\sqrt3+2$ and the area is
\[
3\sqrt3\left(1+\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3}+\dfrac{1}{3}\right)=3\sqrt3+6+\sqrt3=\boxed{6+4\sqrt3}.
\]

Call the other two sides $a$ and $2a$.

\textbf{Case 1: $15$ is the hypotenuse.} Then $a$ and $2a$ are the legs and $a^2+4a^2=225$, so $a=\sqrt{45}=3\sqrt5\approx6.71$, which is the shortest side.

\textbf{Case 2: $15$ is a leg.} Then $a$ and $2a$ are the other leg and the hypotenuse, and the hypotenuse is the longer one, $2a$. So $a^2+225=4a^2$, $a=\sqrt{75}=5\sqrt3\approx8.66<15$, and the shortest side is $5\sqrt3$. (A leg cannot be twice the hypotenuse, so there is no other case.)

