Since $6^2+8^2=10^2$, triangle $ABC$ has a right angle at $B$, and its area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot6\cdot8=24$. Triangle $ABD$ is isosceles with base $AB=6$; its height to $AB$ is $\sqrt{5^2-3^2}=4$, so its area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot6\cdot4=12$.

Put $B=(0,0)$, $A=(0,6)$, $C=(8,0)$. Then $D=(\pm4,3)$. The point $D'=(4,3)$ on the same side of $AB$ as $C$ is the midpoint of $AC$ (the circumcentre of the right triangle), so $A$, $D'$, $C$ would be collinear and $AD'BC$ would not be a genuine quadrilateral. Hence $D=(-4,3)$, on the other side of $AB$, and $ADBC$ is the union of the two triangles, which meet along $AB$:

