Let $x=0.99\ldots9$ be the terminating decimal with exactly $275$ nines after the decimal point (and no other digits).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find $\lfloor x\rfloor$, the greatest integer not exceeding $x$.
\item[(b)] Find $\left\lfloor0.\overline{9}\right\rfloor$, where $0.\overline{9}=0.999\ldots$ has infinitely many nines.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the image of the point $(x,y)$ under a rotation of $180^\circ$ clockwise (that is, through $-180^\circ$) about the origin. Is it the same as the image under a rotation of $180^\circ$ counterclockwise?
\item[(b)] Find the image of the point $A(3,-5)$ under a rotation of $180^\circ$ clockwise about the point $C(1,2)$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) A rotation through an angle $\theta$ about the origin sends $(x,y)$ to $(x\cos\theta-y\sin\theta,\ x\sin\theta+y\cos\theta)$. For $\theta=180^\circ$ and for $\theta=-180^\circ$ we have $\cos\theta=-1$ and $\sin\theta=0$, so in both cases
\[
(x,y)\mapsto\boxed{(-x,-y)}.
\]
Yes, they are the same: turning half way round in either direction ends at the same place. Geometrically, a half-turn about a centre sends each point $P$ to the point $P'$ for which the centre is the midpoint of $PP'$.

(b) The formula $(x,y)\mapsto(-x,-y)$ is only for the centre $(0,0)$. For a half-turn about $C(a,b)$, the centre $C$ is the midpoint of $A$ and its image $A'$, so $A'=2C-A$, i.e.\ $(x,y)\mapsto(2a-x,\ 2b-y)$. Here
\[
A'=(2\cdot1-3,\ 2\cdot2-(-5))=\boxed{(-1,9)}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
pair A=(3,-5),C=(1,2),A2=(-1,9);
draw((-7,0)--(9,0),Arrow(6));draw((0,-6.5)--(0,10.5),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(9,0),E);label("$y$",(0,10.5),N);
real r=abs(A-C);
draw(arc(C,r,degrees(A-C),degrees(A-C)-180,CW),gray+dashed,Arrow(6));
draw(A--A2,blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(A);dot(C);dot(A2);dot((0,0));
label("$A(3,-5)$",A,E);label("$C(1,2)$",C,E);label("$A'(-1,9)$",A2,NW);label("$O$",(0,0),SW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

A correspondence $A\leftrightarrow X$, $B\leftrightarrow Y$, $C\leftrightarrow Z$ is a congruence exactly when $AB=XY$, $BC=YZ$ and $CA=ZX$ (then the angles match too, by SSS).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(6,0),A=(3,4),E2=(9,0),F=(15,0),D=(12,4);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));draw(D--E2--F--cycle,linewidth(1));
void tick(pair P,pair Q,int n){pair m=(P+Q)/2,u=unit(Q-P),v=(-u.y,u.x);
 for(int i=0;i<n;++i){pair c=m+(i-(n-1)/2)*0.28*u;draw((c-0.25*v)--(c+0.25*v),linewidth(0.7));}}
tick(A,B,1);tick(A,C,1);tick(D,E2,1);tick(D,F,1);tick(B,C,2);tick(E2,F,2);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,N);label("$E$",E2,SW);label("$F$",F,SE);
pair lp(pair P,pair Q,pair T){pair m=(P+Q)/2;return m+0.55*unit(rotate(90)*(Q-P))+0.5*unit(T-m);}
label("$5$",lp(B,A,A));label("$5$",lp(A,C,A));label("$6$",(B+C)/2,2.4*S);
label("$5$",lp(E2,D,D));label("$5$",lp(D,F,D));label("$6$",(E2+F)/2,2.4*S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Since $BC=6$ and the only side of length $6$ in $\triangle DEF$ is $EF$, we need $\{Y,Z\}=\{E,F\}$, and then $X=D$: the apex $A$ (where the two equal sides meet) must correspond to the apex $D$. Both remaining orders work:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\triangle DEF$: $DE=5=AB$, $EF=6=BC$, $FD=5=CA$;
\item $\triangle DFE$: $DF=5=AB$, $FE=6=BC$, $ED=5=CA$.
\end{itemize}
So exactly two statements are correct: $\boxed{\triangle ABC\cong\triangle DEF\text{ and }\triangle ABC\cong\triangle DFE}$. The base vertices can be matched in either order because an isosceles triangle is symmetric about its axis; in particular $\triangle ABC\cong\triangle ACB$.

question:
A miner is digging tunnels in a $4\times4$ grid of square boxes. He starts in the top-left box and wants to reach the gold in the bottom-right box. Each move tunnels into the box directly to the right or directly below.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many different routes can he take?
\item[(b)] Find a formula for the number of routes from the top-left box to the bottom-right box of a grid with $n$ rows and $m$ columns.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Place the right angle at $O=(0,0)$ with the legs along the axes, $A=(a,0)$ and $B=(0,b)$. The hypotenuse is the segment $\dfrac{x}{a}+\dfrac{y}{b}=1$ with $0\le x\le a$, and the rectangle has opposite corners $O$ and a point $(x,y)$ of the hypotenuse.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),A=(8,0),B=(0,6);real x=5,y=6*(1-x/8);pair P=(x,y);
filldraw(O--(x,0)--P--(0,y)--cycle,paleblue,linewidth(0.8));
draw(O--A--B--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((0.35,0)--(0.35,0.35)--(0,0.35));
dot(P);label("$(x,y)$",P,NE);
label("$O$",O,SW);label("$A$",A,SE);label("$B$",B,NW);
label("$x$",(x/2,0),N);label("$y$",(0,y/2),E);
draw((0,-0.8)--(8,-0.8),Arrows(5));label("$a$",(4,-0.8),S);
draw((-0.8,0)--(-0.8,6),Arrows(5));label("$b$",(-0.8,3),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Its area is
\[
xy=x\cdot b\left(1-\dfrac {x}{a}\right)=\dfrac {b}{a}\,x(a-x).
\]
By AM-GM, $x(a-x)\le\left(\dfrac{x+(a-x)}{2}\right)^2=\dfrac{a^2}{4}$, with equality exactly when $x=\dfrac {a}{2}$. So the area is at most $\dfrac {b}{a}\cdot\dfrac{a^2}{4}=\dfrac{ab}{4}$, attained for the rectangle with sides $\dfrac {a}{2}$ and $\dfrac {b}{2}$, whose fourth vertex is the midpoint of the hypotenuse.

The greatest area is $\boxed{\dfrac{ab}{4}}$, half the area of the triangle, for the $\dfrac {a}{2}\times\dfrac {b}{2}$ rectangle.

question:
To rationalise the denominator of $\dfrac{1}{\sqrt[3]{12}}$, a textbook writes $\sqrt[3]{12}=\sqrt[3]{2^2}\cdot\sqrt[3]3$, multiplies the numerator and the denominator by $\sqrt[3]2$, and obtains $\dfrac{\sqrt[3]2}{2\sqrt[3]3}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why $\sqrt[3]{12}\cdot\sqrt[3]2=2\sqrt[3]3$ (and not $2\sqrt[3]6$).
\item[(b)] Finish rationalising: write $\dfrac{1}{\sqrt[3]{12}}$ with no radical in the denominator.
