\item[(b)] Rationalise the denominator of $\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{1+\sqrt2}}$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
If the side is $s$, the diagonal is the hypotenuse of a right isosceles triangle with legs $s$, so $s^2+s^2=4^2$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=2*sqrt(2);pair A=(0,0),B=(s,0),C=(s,s),D=(0,s);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));draw(A--C,dashed+linewidth(0.8));
label("$4$",(A+C)/2,NW);label("$s$",(A+B)/2,S);label("$s$",(B+C)/2,E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Thus $2s^2=16$ and the area is $s^2=\boxed{8}$ square feet. (Equivalently, the area of a square is half the square of its diagonal: $\dfrac{4^2}{2}=8$.)

question:
In the figure, $AB=DA=5$ units, $AC=DE=4$ units and $BC=AE=3$ units, and point $C$ lies on segment $\overline{AD}$. It is possible to move $\triangle ABC$ onto $\triangle DAE$ (with $A\mapsto D$, $B\mapsto A$, $C\mapsto E$) by reflecting $\triangle ABC$ across a line $m$ and then translating the image in a direction parallel to $m$. Line $m$ divides $\triangle ABC$ into two regions. What is the ratio of the area of the smaller region to the area of the larger region? Express your answer as a common fraction.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),D=(5,0),C=(4,0),B=(4,3),E2=(1.8,-2.4);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));draw(D--A--E2--cycle,linewidth(1));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(E2);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,S);label("$D$",D,E);label("$E$",E2,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
Both triangles are $3$-$4$-$5$ right triangles, with right angles at $C$ and $E$. Put $A=(0,0)$, $D=(5,0)$, $C=(4,0)$, $B=(4,3)$. A reflection followed by a translation reverses orientation, so $E$ lies on the other side of $AD$ from $B$: from $AE=3$, $DE=4$ we get $E=\left(\dfrac{9}{5},-\dfrac{12}{5}\right)$.

\textbf{The axis passes through midpoints.} Let $g$ be the reflection in $m$ followed by a translation along $m$. For any point $P$, the reflected point $P_1$ is the mirror image of $P$, so the midpoint of $PP_1$ lies on $m$; translating $P_1$ along $m$ moves that midpoint along $m$ (by half the translation). Hence the midpoint of $P$ and $g(P)$ always lies on $m$.

Applying this to $A\mapsto D$, $B\mapsto A$, $C\mapsto E$, line $m$ passes through
\[
K=\text{midpoint of }AD=\left(\dfrac{5}{2},0\right),\quad M=\text{midpoint of }AB=\left(2,\dfrac{3}{2}\right),\quad L=\text{midpoint of }CE=\left(\dfrac{29}{10},-\dfrac{6}{5}\right).
\]
(These are collinear: both $MK$ and $KL$ have slope $-3$, so $m$ is the line $y=-3x+\dfrac{15}{2}$. One checks that reflecting in this line and then translating by $\left(\dfrac{1}{2},-\dfrac{3}{2}\right)$, a vector parallel to $m$, sends $A,B,C$ to $D,A,E$.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),D=(5,0),C=(4,0),B=(4,3),E2=(1.8,-2.4);
pair M=(A+B)/2,K=(A+D)/2,L=(C+E2)/2;
fill(A--M--K--cycle,paleblue);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));draw(D--A--E2--cycle,linewidth(1));
pair u=unit(K-M);
draw((M-1.3*u)--(L+1.1*u),red+dashed+linewidth(0.9));
draw(C--E2,gray+dotted+linewidth(0.8));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(E2);dot(M,red);dot(K,red);dot(L,red);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,E);label("$E$",E2,S);
label("$M$",M,NW);label("$K$",K+(-0.33,-0.33));label("$L$",L+(-0.36,-0.06));
label("$m$",L+1.1*u,E,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So $m$ meets side $AB$ at its midpoint $M$ and side $AC$ at $K$ with $AK=\dfrac{5}{2}$, cutting off triangle $AMK$ (shaded). Since triangles $AMK$ and $ABC$ share the angle at $A$,
\[
[AMK]=\dfrac{AM}{AB}\cdot\dfrac{AK}{AC}\cdot[ABC]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\dfrac{5}{8}\cdot6=\dfrac{15}{8}.
\]
The other region has area $6-\dfrac{15}{8}=\dfrac{33}{8}$, so the ratio is
\[
\dfrac{\dfrac{15}{8}}{\dfrac{33}{8}}=\boxed{\dfrac{5}{11}}.
\]

question:
A student asks: ``The probability that any child likes peas is $0.04$. Find the probability that two random children both like peas.'' Two different answers are proposed.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Assume each child likes peas with probability $0.04$, independently of all other children. Find the probability that two children both like peas.
\item[(b)] Assume instead that a class has exactly $100$ children, of whom exactly $4$ like peas, and two different children are chosen at random from the class. Find the probability that both like peas.
\item[(c)] For a class of $N$ children (with $0.04N$ an integer at least $2$) of whom exactly $0.04N$ like peas, find the probability $p_N$ that two different children chosen at random both like peas, and show that $p_N\to0.0016$ as $N\to\infty$. Which model does the original question describe?
\end{enumerate}

Under the usual modern definition, a triangle is \emph{isosceles} if at least two of its sides are equal. Euclid instead called a triangle isosceles if it has exactly two equal sides.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Under each definition, is an equilateral triangle isosceles?
\item[(b)] How many non-congruent triangles with integer side lengths and perimeter $15$ are isosceles under the modern definition, and how many under Euclid's definition?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) An equilateral triangle has three equal sides, so it has at least two equal sides: under the modern definition it \textbf{is} isosceles. It does not have \emph{exactly} two equal sides, so under Euclid's definition it is \textbf{not} isosceles.

(b) Let the sides be $a\le b\le c$ with $a+b+c=15$ and $a+b>c$. Then $3c\ge15$ and $2c<15$, so $c\in\{5,6,7\}$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $c=7$: $a+b=8$, giving $(1,7,7),(2,6,7),(3,5,7),(4,4,7)$.
\item $c=6$: $a+b=9$ with $b\le6$, giving $(3,6,6),(4,5,6)$.
\item $c=5$: $(5,5,5)$.
\end{itemize}
Of these $7$ triangles, the ones with at least two equal sides are $(1,7,7),(4,4,7),(3,6,6),(5,5,5)$, drawn below.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(10cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
void tri(pair P,real base,real leg,string sb,string sl){
 real h=sqrt(leg^2-(base/2)^2);pair L=P,R=P+(base,0),T=P+(base/2,h);
 draw(L--R--T--cycle,linewidth(1));
 pair nl=unit(rotate(-90)*(L-T)),nr=unit(rotate(90)*(R-T));
 label(sb,(L+R)/2,S);label(sl,(L+T)/2+0.55*nl);label(sl,(R+T)/2+0.55*nr);}
tri((0,0),1,7,"$1$","$7$");
tri((3.2,0),7,4,"$7$","$4$");
tri((12.4,0),3,6,"$3$","$6$");
tri((17.6,0),5,5,"$5$","$5$");
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So $\boxed{4}$ are isosceles under the modern definition, and $\boxed{3}$ under Euclid's definition (all except the equilateral $(5,5,5)$).

\textbf{Copy the second triangle onto $BC$.} On the side of line $BC$ opposite to $A$, choose the point $A''$ with $\angle CBA''=\angle C'B'A'$ and $BA''=B'A'$. By SAS, $\triangle A''BC\cong\triangle A'B'C'$, so
\[
BA''=B'A'=BA\qquad\text{and}\qquad CA''=C'A'=CA .
\]
It is enough to prove $\triangle ABC\cong\triangle A''BC$. Since $A$ and $A''$ are on opposite sides of line $BC$, the segment $AA''$ meets line $BC$ at a point $X$. Triangles $ABA''$ and $ACA''$ are isosceles (when they are not degenerate), so
\[
\angle BAA''=\angle BA''A\qquad\text{and}\qquad\angle CAA''=\angle CA''A .\qquad(*)
\]
\textbf{Case 1: $X$ lies strictly between $B$ and $C$.} Then the segment $AA''$ runs inside both angles $BAC$ and $BA''C$, and adding the two equalities in $(*)$,
\[
\angle BAC=\angle BAA''+\angle A''AC=\angle BA''A+\angle AA''C=\angle BA''C .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(6,0),A=(2,3.5),A2=(2,-3.5);
pair X=extension(A,A2,B,C);
if(X.x<-0.01) draw((X.x-0.5,0)--B,gray+linewidth(0.5));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));draw(A2--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(A--A2,dashed+linewidth(0.8));
void tick(pair P,pair Q,int n){pair m=(P+Q)/2,u=unit(Q-P),v=(-u.y,u.x);
 for(int i=0;i<n;++i){pair c=m+(i-(n-1)/2)*0.22*u;draw((c-0.2*v)--(c+0.2*v),linewidth(0.7));}}
tick(A,B,1);tick(A2,B,1);tick(A,C,2);tick(A2,C,2);
dot(A);dot(A2);dot(B);dot(C);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$A''$",A2,S);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,E);
dot(X,red);label("$X$",X,NE,red);
label("Case 1",(3,-4.6));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Case 2: $X$ is $B$ (or, in the same way, $C$).} Then $A$, $B$, $A''$ are collinear with $B$ between $A$ and $A''$, and only triangle $ACA''$ is a genuine triangle. Its base angles give $\angle CAB=\angle CAA''=\angle CA''A=\angle CA''B$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(6,0),A=(0,3.5),A2=(0,-3.5);
pair X=extension(A,A2,B,C);
if(X.x<-0.01) draw((X.x-0.5,0)--B,gray+linewidth(0.5));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));draw(A2--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(A--A2,dashed+linewidth(0.8));
void tick(pair P,pair Q,int n){pair m=(P+Q)/2,u=unit(Q-P),v=(-u.y,u.x);
 for(int i=0;i<n;++i){pair c=m+(i-(n-1)/2)*0.22*u;draw((c-0.2*v)--(c+0.2*v),linewidth(0.7));}}
tick(A,B,1);tick(A2,B,1);tick(A,C,2);tick(A2,C,2);
dot(A);dot(A2);dot(B);dot(C);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$A''$",A2,S);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,E);

label("Case 2",(3,-4.6));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Case 3: $X$ lies on line $BC$ outside the segment $BC$,} say beyond $B$ (the case beyond $C$ is the same with $B$ and $C$ exchanged). Then ray $AB$ lies inside angle $XAC$, so $\angle BAC=\angle CAA''-\angle BAA''$, and likewise $\angle BA''C=\angle CA''A-\angle BA''A$. Subtracting the equalities in $(*)$ gives $\angle BAC=\angle BA''C$.

\begin{asy}
