label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
The quadrilateral $EPMD$ is triangle $DBM$ with triangle $EBP$ removed (the vertices $D,E$ lie on $BA$ and $P,M$ lie on $BF$):

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(10,0),A=(3,7);
pair D=A+(B-A)/3,Ep=A+2*(B-A)/3,F=A+(C-A)/3,G=A+2*(C-A)/3;
pair M=extension(C,D,B,F),Nq=extension(C,D,B,G),P=extension(C,Ep,B,F),Q=extension(C,Ep,B,G);
fill(Ep--P--M--D--cycle,rgb(0.75,0.85,1));
draw(D--B--M,rgb(0.8,0,0)+linewidth(1.2));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(C--D);draw(C--Ep);draw(B--F);draw(B--G);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(Ep);dot(F);dot(G);dot(M);dot(Nq);dot(P);dot(Q);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$D$",D,W);label("$E$",Ep,W);label("$F$",F,NE);label("$G$",G,NE);
label("$M$",M,(-0.2,1.3));label("$N$",Nq,(0.1,1.4));label("$P$",P,(0.6,-1.3));label("$Q$",Q,(0.2,-1.4));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\[
[EPMD]=[DBM]-[EBP].
\]
\textbf{Ratios along $BF$ (mass points).} For $M=CD\cap BF$: since $AD:DB=1:2$, put masses $2$ at $A$ and $1$ at $B$; since $AF:FC=1:2$, the mass at $C$ is $1$, so $F$ gets mass $2+1=3$. Then $BM:MF=3:1$, so $\dfrac{BM}{BF}=\dfrac{3}{4}$.

For $P=CE\cap BF$: since $AE:EB=2:1$, put masses $1$ at $A$ and $2$ at $B$; since $AF:FC=1:2$, the mass at $C$ is $\dfrac{1}{2}$, so $F$ gets mass $\dfrac{3}{2}$. Then $BP:PF=\dfrac{3}{2}:2=3:4$, so $\dfrac{BP}{BF}=\dfrac{3}{7}$.

\textbf{Areas.} Triangle $ABF$ has $\dfrac{AF}{AC}=\dfrac{1}{3}$ of the area of $ABC$, so $[ABF]=14$. Triangles with a common angle at $B$ have areas proportional to the products of the sides at $B$, so
\[
[DBM]=\dfrac{BD}{BA}\cdot\dfrac{BM}{BF}\cdot[ABF]=\dfrac{2}{3}\cdot\dfrac{3}{4}\cdot14=7,\qquad
[EBP]=\dfrac{BE}{BA}\cdot\dfrac{BP}{BF}\cdot[ABF]=\dfrac{1}{3}\cdot\dfrac{3}{7}\cdot14=2 .
\]
Hence $[EPMD]=7-2=\boxed{5}$.

Let $n>6$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $n$ is odd, then $n\ge7$ and $n=1^2+(n-1)$, where $n-1$ is even and at least $6$, hence composite.
\item If $n$ is even, then $n\ge8$ and $n=2^2+(n-4)$, where $n-4$ is even and at least $4$, hence composite.
\end{itemize}
This proves the statement. For $n=6$, the only positive squares below $6$ are $1$ and $4$, and $6-1=5$ and $6-4=2$ are prime, so $6$ is not such a sum.

