Parametrise the segment as $(6t,4t,3t)$, $0\le t\le1$. It passes from one cube into the next exactly when it crosses one of the planes $x=1,\dots,5$, $y=1,2,3$ or $z=1,2$, which happens at the times
\[
t\in\left\{\dfrac{1}{6},\dfrac{2}{6},\dots,\dfrac{5}{6}\right\}\cup\left\{\dfrac{1}{4},\dfrac{2}{4},\dfrac{3}{4}\right\}\cup\left\{\dfrac{1}{3},\dfrac{2}{3}\right\}.
\]
These are $5+3+2=10$ times, but some coincide: $\dfrac{2}{6}=\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $\dfrac{4}{6}=\dfrac{2}{3}$ (an $x$-plane and a $z$-plane at once), and $\dfrac{3}{6}=\dfrac{2}{4}$ (an $x$-plane and a $y$-plane at once). So there are $10-3=7$ distinct crossing times. At a common crossing the segment passes through an edge of the grid and enters just one new cube (it only touches the other cubes at that edge point). The $7$ crossings cut the segment into $8$ pieces, each inside one cube, so the segment passes through $\boxed{8}$ cubes.

This agrees with the formula $a+b+c-\gcd(a,b)-\gcd(b,c)-\gcd(c,a)+\gcd(a,b,c)=6+4+3-2-1-3+1=8$.

