label("$a+b$",(-0.65,2.1),blue);label("$b+c$",(1.25,2.2),W,red);
label("$y=x^2$",(-2.2,4.84),W,gray(0.4));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Since $a,b,c$ are different, no two of the points share an $x$-coordinate, so the lines through pairs of them are not vertical and have slopes
\[
\text{slope of }(a,a^2)(b,b^2)=\dfrac{b^2-a^2}{b-a}=a+b,\qquad
\text{slope of }(b,b^2)(c,c^2)=\dfrac{c^2-b^2}{c-b}=b+c .
\]
If the three points were collinear, these two segments would lie on one line and have equal slopes: $a+b=b+c$, so $a=c$, a contradiction. Hence the points are never collinear.

(Another view: a non-vertical line $y=mx+k$ meets $y=x^2$ where $x^2-mx-k=0$, a quadratic equation with at most two roots, so no line contains three points of the parabola.)

