The statement is \textbf{true}. Use vectors (or coordinates) with origin $O$, and let $T$ have vertices $A$, $B$, $C$. Its centroid is $G=\dfrac{A+B+C}{3}$.

A dilation with centre $O$ and factor $2$ sends each point $X$ to $2X$. So $T_1$ has vertices $2A$, $2B$, $2C$, and its centroid is
\[
G_1=\dfrac{2A+2B+2C}{3}=2G .
\]
In the same way $T_2$ has vertices $4A,4B,4C$ and centroid $G_2=4G$.

