\end{asy}

If $G\ne O$, the points $G$, $2G$, $4G$ all lie on the line through $O$ and $G$ (on the ray from $O$ through $G$), so they are collinear. If $G=O$ (which happens exactly when $T$ is equilateral), then $G=G_1=G_2=O$ and the three centroids coincide, which is trivially collinear. In both cases the centroids are collinear, and moreover $OG_1=2\,OG$ and $OG_2=4\,OG$. (The same holds for any point defined from the triangle in a way that commutes with dilations, such as the orthocentre or the incentre.)

