\]
square units. (Equivalently: triangle $AOD$ is a quarter of the square, $16$, and triangle $AMO$ is an eighth, $8$.)

question:
A circus has the same number of lions as tigers. The owner tells you:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(1)] one elephant provides exactly enough meat to feed all the lions and tigers;
\item[(2)] $18$ deer produce the same amount of meat as one elephant;
\item[(3)] a lion eats twice as much as a tiger;
\item[(4)] one buffalo is enough to feed one lion and one tiger;
\item[(5)] a tiger eats exactly the amount of meat in one deer.
\end{enumerate}
How many lions and how many tigers are there?

solution:
When the ball touches the rail, its centre is $1$ inch from the rail. So the centre moves in straight lines and reflects off the line $\ell$ parallel to the rail at distance $1$ (dashed below); $A$ and $B$ are $6-1=5$ and $9-1=8$ inches from $\ell$. The ball touches the rail at the point $K$ directly beyond the centre's turning point $T$ on $\ell$.

Measured parallel to the rail, $A$ and $B$ are $\sqrt{5^2-(9-6)^2}=4$ inches apart.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real t=20/13;
pair A=(0,6),B=(4,9),T=(t,1),K=(t,0);
fill((-1.2,-0.35)--(5.4,-0.35)--(5.4,0)--(-1.2,0)--cycle,gray(0.75));
draw((-1.2,0)--(5.4,0),linewidth(1.2));
draw((-1.2,1)--(5.4,1),dashed+gray(0.4));
draw(circle(T,1),heavygreen);dot(T,heavygreen);
draw(A--T--B,blue+linewidth(1));
draw(A--(0,-1.3),dotted);draw(B--(4,-1.3),dotted);draw(T--K,dotted);draw(K--(t,-0.45),dotted);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(K,red);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$T$",T+(0.5,0.42));label("$K$",(t,-0.72),red);
label("$5$",(0,3.5),W);label("$8$",(4,5),E);label("$1$",(0,0.5),W);label("rail",(5.4,-0.2),E);
draw((t,-1.3)--(4,-1.3),red,Arrows(6));label("$\dfrac{32}{13}$",((t+4)/2,-1.3),S,red);
draw((0,-1.3)--(t,-1.3),gray(0.4),Arrows(6));label("$\dfrac{20}{13}$",(t/2,-1.3),S,gray(0.4));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Since the angles that $AT$ and $TB$ make with $\ell$ are equal, the right triangles formed by $A$, $T$ and the foot of $A$ on $\ell$, and by $B$, $T$ and the foot of $B$ on $\ell$, are similar, with legs perpendicular to $\ell$ equal to $5$ and $8$. So $T$ divides the horizontal distance $4$ in the ratio $5:8$:
\[
\text{horizontal distance from }B\text{ to }T=4\cdot\dfrac{8}{13}=\dfrac{32}{13}.
\]
The point of the rail nearest to $B$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $B$, and $K$ is directly below $T$. So the ball should hit the rail $\boxed{\dfrac{32}{13}\text{ inches}}\approx2.46$ inches from that point (and $\dfrac{20}{13}$ inches from the point nearest to $A$).

question:
Balls are numbered $1,2,3,\dots$, and an urn is empty at the start. At step $n$ ($n=1,2,3,\dots$) we put the balls numbered $10n-9,10n-8,\dots,10n$ into the urn and then take out the ball numbered $n$. (For example, step $n$ can be carried out $\dfrac{1}{2^{n-1}}$ minutes before noon, so that all the steps are finished by noon.)
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many balls are in the urn right after step $n$?
\item[(b)] A ball is in the urn at noon if it was put in at some step and was never taken out afterwards. How many balls are in the urn at noon?
\end{enumerate}

A semicircle lies inside triangle $ABC$. Its diameter lies on side $BC$, and its arc is tangent to sides $AB$ and $AC$. Let $K$ be the area of triangle $ABC$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that the radius of the semicircle is $r=\dfrac{2K}{AB+AC}$.
\item[(b)] Find $r$ when $AB=13$, $BC=14$ and $CA=15$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) Let $O$ be the centre of the semicircle; it is the midpoint of the diameter, so it lies on $BC$. Let the arc touch $AB$ at $T_1$ and $AC$ at $T_2$. A radius drawn to a point of tangency is perpendicular to the tangent line, so $OT_1\perp AB$ and $OT_2\perp AC$ with $OT_1=OT_2=r$. In particular $O$ is equidistant from lines $AB$ and $AC$, so it is the point where the bisector of angle $A$ meets $BC$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(14,0),A=(5,12),O=(6.5,0);
pair T1=B+dot(O-B,unit(A-B))*unit(A-B), T2=C+dot(O-C,unit(A-C))*unit(A-C);
void ra(pair P,pair Q,pair R,real s){pair u=unit(P-Q)*s,v=unit(R-Q)*s;draw(Q+u--Q+u+v--Q+v,linewidth(0.6));}
fill(arc(O,6,0,180)--cycle,rgb(0.88,0.92,1));
draw(arc(O,6,0,180),blue+linewidth(1));draw((O.x-6,0)--(O.x+6,0),blue+linewidth(1));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(A--O,dashed);draw(O--T1,dashed);draw(O--T2,dashed);
ra(O,T1,B,0.6);ra(O,T2,C,0.6);
dot(O);dot(T1);dot(T2);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$O$",O,S);
label("$13$",(A+B)/2+(-0.4,0.6),NW);label("$15$",(A+C)/2,NE);label("$14$",(10,0),S);
label("$r$",(O+T1)/2+0.45*unit(rotate(90)*(T1-O)),fontsize(9pt));label("$r$",(O+T2)/2+0.45*unit(rotate(-90)*(T2-O)),fontsize(9pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The segment $AO$ splits triangle $ABC$ into triangles $ABO$ and $ACO$. Taking $AB$ and $AC$ as bases, their heights are $OT_1=r$ and $OT_2=r$. Hence
\[
K=[ABO]+[ACO]=\dfrac{1}{2}\,AB\cdot r+\dfrac{1}{2}\,AC\cdot r=\dfrac {r}{2}\,(AB+AC),
\qquad\text{so}\qquad r=\dfrac{2K}{AB+AC}.
\]

(b) By Heron's formula with semiperimeter $s=\dfrac{13+14+15}{2}=21$,
\[
K=\sqrt{21\cdot(21-13)(21-14)(21-15)}=\sqrt{21\cdot8\cdot7\cdot6}=\sqrt{7056}=84 .
\]
So $r=\dfrac{2\cdot84}{13+15}=\dfrac{168}{28}=\boxed{6}$. (The centre is at distance $\dfrac{13}{28}\cdot14=6.5$ from $B$, and the diameter, from $0.5$ to $12.5$, fits inside $BC$.)

``$F$ is between $A$ and $E$'' means that $A$, $F$, $E$ lie on one line with $F$ on segment $AE$ and $F\ne A,E$. So $F$ lies on line $AE$, and in the same way $F$ lies on line $RS$.

If lines $AE$ and $RS$ were the same line, then $A$, $E$, $R$, $S$ would all lie on it, which is not allowed. So they are two different lines, and two different lines meet in at most one point, here $F$. On line $AE$ the points $A$ and $E$ are on opposite sides of $F$, and on line $RS$ the points $R$ and $S$ are on opposite sides of $F$. So the segments $\overline{AE}$ and $\overline{RS}$ \emph{cross} at the point $F$, which is inside both segments:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),Ep=(4.4,2.0),R=(0.8,2.6),Sx=(3.6,-0.6);
pair F=extension(A,Ep,R,Sx);
draw(A--Ep,linewidth(1));draw(R--Sx,linewidth(1));
dot(A);dot(Ep);dot(R);dot(Sx);dot(F);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$E$",Ep,E);label("$R$",R,N);label("$S$",Sx,S);label("$F$",F,1.6*dir(80));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Any correct diagram looks like this: two segments forming an ``X'' with $F$ at the crossing. Neither segment may end at $F$, and the two segments may not lie along the same line.

Suppose, for contradiction, that a point $(x_0,y_0)$ lies on both lines:
\[
y_0-y_1=m(x_0-x_1)\qquad\text{and}\qquad y_0-y_2=m(x_0-x_2).
\]
Subtracting the second equation from the first gives
\[
y_2-y_1=m(x_2-x_1).
\]
This says that $P_2$ satisfies the equation of $\ell_1$.

Now take any point $(x,y)$ of $\ell_2$, so $y-y_2=m(x-x_2)$. Adding $y_2-y_1=m(x_2-x_1)$ gives $y-y_1=m(x-x_1)$, so $(x,y)$ lies on $\ell_1$. Hence $\ell_2\subseteq\ell_1$. The same argument with the roles exchanged gives $\ell_1\subseteq\ell_2$, so $\ell_1=\ell_2$. This contradicts the assumption that the lines are distinct.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
draw((-0.5,0)--(5.5,0),gray,Arrow(5));draw((0,-0.5)--(0,4.6),gray,Arrow(5));
real m=0.5;
pair P1=(2.6,2.8),P2=(3,0.5);
draw((-0.4,P1.y+m*(-0.4-P1.x))--(5.3,P1.y+m*(5.3-P1.x)),blue+linewidth(1));
draw((-0.4,P2.y+m*(-0.4-P2.x))--(5.3,P2.y+m*(5.3-P2.x)),red+linewidth(1));
dot(P1);dot(P2);
label("$P_1(x_1,y_1)$",P1,NW);label("$P_2(x_2,y_2)$",P2,SE);
label("$\ell_1$",(5.3,P1.y+m*(5.3-P1.x)),E,blue);label("$\ell_2$",(5.3,P2.y+m*(5.3-P2.x)),E,red);
label("$x$",(5.5,0),S);label("$y$",(0,4.6),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Therefore two distinct lines with the same slope have no common point: they are parallel.

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Write the sum $1+4+16+64+\cdots$ of $n$ terms in summation ($\Sigma$) notation, and find a closed form for it.
\item[(b)] Write $2+5+8+\cdots+302$ in summation notation, and evaluate it.
\end{enumerate}

A rotation of a solid cube about an axis is a \emph{symmetry} if it moves the cube onto itself. An axis has order $k$ if exactly $k$ of the rotations about it (including the trivial full turn) are symmetries, i.e.\ the smallest symmetric turn about it is $\dfrac{360^\circ}{k}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find all axes of rotational symmetry of a cube and the order of each.
\item[(b)] How many rotations (including the identity) map the cube onto itself?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) A symmetry maps the cube onto itself, so it fixes the centre $O$ of the cube, and every axis passes through $O$. The axis leaves the cube at a point $P$ of its surface that the rotation fixes. The rotation permutes the faces, the edges and the vertices, and it must map the face, edge or vertex containing $P$ to itself. This gives three types of axis.
\begin{itemize}
\item $P$ is inside a face. That face is mapped to itself and turned about its centre, so $P$ is the centre of the face and the axis joins the centres of two opposite faces. A square is mapped to itself by turns of $90^\circ$: \textbf{order $4$}. There are $\dfrac{6}{2}=3$ such axes.
\item $P$ is inside an edge. The edge is mapped to itself with its ends swapped, so $P$ is its midpoint, and the axis joins the midpoints of two opposite edges. Only the half-turn works: \textbf{order $2$}. There are $\dfrac{12}{2}=6$ such axes.
\item $P$ is a vertex. The axis is a long diagonal of the cube. The three edges at that vertex can be cycled by turns of $120^\circ$: \textbf{order $3$}. There are $\dfrac{8}{2}=4$ such axes.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P(real x,real y,real z){return (x+0.42*y,z+0.32*y);}
// hidden edges
draw(P(0,1,0)--P(1,1,0),dashed+gray);draw(P(0,1,0)--P(0,0,0),dashed+gray);draw(P(0,1,0)--P(0,1,1),dashed+gray);
// visible edges
draw(P(0,0,0)--P(1,0,0)--P(1,0,1)--P(0,0,1)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(P(1,0,0)--P(1,1,0)--P(1,1,1)--P(1,0,1),linewidth(0.9));
draw(P(0,0,1)--P(0,1,1)--P(1,1,1),linewidth(0.9));
// face axis (order 4)
draw(P(0.5,0.5,-0.45)--P(0.5,0.5,1.45),red+linewidth(1));
dot(P(0.5,0.5,1),red);dot(P(0.5,0.5,0),red);
label("order $4$",P(0.5,0.5,1.45),N,red);
// vertex axis (order 3)
draw(P(-0.3,-0.3,-0.3)--P(1.3,1.3,1.3),blue+linewidth(1));
label("order $3$",P(1.3,1.3,1.3),NE,blue);
// edge axis (order 2): midpoints of opposite edges (0,0,1)-(1,0,1) and (0,1,0)-(1,1,0)
draw(P(0.5,-0.35,1.35)--P(0.5,1.35,-0.35),deepgreen+linewidth(1));
dot(P(0.5,0,1),deepgreen);dot(P(0.5,1,0),deepgreen);
label("order $2$",P(0.5,1.35,-0.35),SE,deepgreen);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So the cube has $3+6+4=\boxed{13}$ axes of rotational symmetry: $3$ of order $4$, $6$ of order $2$ and $4$ of order $3$.

(b) Each axis of order $k$ gives $k-1$ non-trivial rotations, and different axes give different rotations. Together with the identity there are
\[
1+3\cdot3+6\cdot1+4\cdot2=\boxed{24}
\]
rotations. Check: a chosen face can be moved to any of the $6$ face positions and then turned in $4$ ways, giving $6\cdot4=24$.

The same method works for other solids. For each kind of point where an axis can leave the solid (centre of a face, midpoint of an edge, a vertex), the order is the number of ways to turn that face, edge or vertex onto itself.

For three different points on a line, the middle one is the one whose distances to the other two add up to the distance between those two. So $AO+AY=OY$ says that $A$ is between $O$ and $Y$. Since $A$ is to the right of $O$, the point $Y$ is to the right of $A$. Also $K$ is between $O$ and $A$. Hence the order from left to right is
\[
\boxed{O,\ K,\ A,\ Y}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real[] xs={0,1.6,3.2,5.4};string[] nm={"$O$","$K$","$A$","$Y$"};
draw((-0.8,0)--(6.2,0),linewidth(0.8),Arrows(6));
for(int i=0;i<4;++i){dot((xs[i],0));label(nm[i],(xs[i],0),N);}
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(Check: with this order every condition holds, and no other order satisfies all three.)

question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that for all integers $a$ and $b$, $13$ divides $10a+b$ if and only if $13$ divides $a+4b$. (So a number is divisible by $13$ exactly when the number obtained by deleting its last digit and adding four times that digit is divisible by $13$.)
\item[(b)] Prove that a positive integer is divisible by $13$ if and only if the alternating sum of its blocks of three digits, taken from the right, is divisible by $13$.
\item[(c)] Use (a) to test $2028$ and (b) to test $1\,283\,945$.
\end{enumerate}

Angela is $5$ meters from a straight river when she hears Clyde call for water to put out a fire. Clyde is $9$ meters from the river, on the same side as Angela, and the distance between Angela and Clyde measured along the river (between the points of the river nearest to them) is $8$ meters. Angela must walk to the river to fill a bucket and then walk to Clyde.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the length of the shortest such path?
\item[(b)] Let $A$ be Angela's position, $C$ Clyde's position, and $B$ the point of the river on the shortest path. Find $m\angle ABC$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Put the river on the $x$-axis, $A=(0,5)$ and $C=(8,9)$. Let $A'=(0,-5)$ be the reflection of $A$ in the river. For every point $B$ on the river, $AB=A'B$, so
\[
AB+BC=A'B+BC\ge A'C,
\]
with equality exactly when $B$ lies on segment $A'C$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,5),C=(8,9),B=(20/7,0),A2=(0,-5);
draw((-1.2,0)--(9.4,0),blue+linewidth(1.2));label("river",(9.4,0),E,blue);
draw(A--B--C,linewidth(1));
draw(B--A2,dashed);draw(A--A2,dotted);draw(C--(8,0),dotted);
dot(A);dot(C);dot(B);dot(A2);
label("$A$ (Angela)",A,W);label("$C$ (Clyde)",C,NE);label("$B$",B,1.4*dir(-35));label("$A'$",A2,W);
label("$5$",(0,2.5),W);label("$9$",(8,4.5),E);label("$5$",(0,-2.5),W);
label("$8$",(4,-6.2),S);draw((0,-6.2)--(8,-6.2),gray,Arrows(5));draw((8,0)--(8,-6.5),gray+dotted);draw((0,-5)--(0,-6.5),gray+dotted);
draw(arc(B,0.9,degrees(C-B),degrees(A-B)),red);label("$\angle ABC$",B+(0,3.3),red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(a) The shortest path has length
\[
A'C=\sqrt{8^2+(9+5)^2}=\sqrt{64+196}=\sqrt{260}=\boxed{2\sqrt{65}\text{ m}}\approx16.1\text{ m}.
\]

(b) Line $A'C$ has slope $\dfrac{14}{8}=\dfrac{7}{4}$, so it meets the river at $B=\left(\dfrac{20}{7},0\right)$. Then $\overrightarrow{BA}=\left(-\dfrac{20}{7},5\right)$ and $\overrightarrow{BC}=\left(\dfrac{36}{7},9\right)$, with lengths $\dfrac{5\sqrt{65}}{7}$ and $\dfrac{9\sqrt{65}}{7}$. Hence
\[
\cos\angle ABC=\dfrac{-\dfrac{720}{49}+45}{\dfrac{5\sqrt{65}}{7}\cdot\dfrac{9\sqrt{65}}{7}}=\dfrac{\dfrac{1485}{49}}{\dfrac{2925}{49}}=\dfrac{33}{65},
\]
so $m\angle ABC=\boxed{\arccos\dfrac{33}{65}\approx59.5^\circ}$. Equivalently, $BA$ and $BC$ make equal angles $\theta$ with the river, where $\tan\theta=\dfrac{7}{4}$, and $\angle ABC=180^\circ-2\theta$.

Write $a=\sqrt{53}$, $b=\sqrt{85}$, $c=\sqrt{40}$. Pair the factors and use the difference of squares:
\[
(a+b+c)(a+b-c)=(a+b)^2-c^2=2ab+(a^2+b^2-c^2),
\]
\[
(a-b+c)(-a+b+c)=c^2-(a-b)^2=2ab-(a^2+b^2-c^2).
\]
So the product under the root is
\[
(2ab)^2-(a^2+b^2-c^2)^2=4\cdot53\cdot85-(53+85-40)^2=18020-9604=8416=16\cdot526 .
\]
Hence the expression equals $\dfrac{1}{4}\cdot4\sqrt{526}=\boxed{\sqrt{526}}$, which is in simplest form since $526=2\cdot263$ with $263$ prime.

The expression is Heron's formula $K=\dfrac{1}{4}\sqrt{(a+b+c)(a+b-c)(a-b+c)(-a+b+c)}$, so $\sqrt{526}\approx22.93$ is the area of a triangle with sides $\sqrt{53}$, $\sqrt{85}$, $\sqrt{40}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=sqrt(85);real px=49/sqrt(85);real py=sqrt(53-px^2);
pair B=(0,0),C=(a,0),A=(px,py);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$A$",A,N);
label("$\sqrt{85}$",(B+C)/2,S);label("$\sqrt{53}$",(A+B)/2,NW);label("$\sqrt{40}$",(A+C)/2,NE);
draw(A--(px,0),dashed);label("$h$",(px,py/2),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Check: with $BC=\sqrt{85}$ on the $x$-axis, $A$ has $x$-coordinate $\dfrac{53+85-40}{2\sqrt{85}}=\dfrac{49}{\sqrt{85}}$ and height $h=\sqrt{53-\dfrac{2401}{85}}=\sqrt{\dfrac{2104}{85}}$, so the area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\sqrt{85}\sqrt{\dfrac{2104}{85}}=\dfrac{\sqrt{2104}}{2}=\sqrt{526}$.

question:
A club has $n$ members, and $r$ different offices ($1\le r\le n$: president, vice-president, secretary, \dots) are to be filled by $r$ different members.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Fill the offices one at a time. Explain why there are exactly $n-r+1$ choices for the $r$th office.
\item[(b)] Deduce that the number of ways to fill the offices is $\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!}$, and find it for $n=10$, $r=3$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the trapezoid be $ABCD$ with $AB\parallel DC$, $AC=a$ and $BD=b$, $AC\perp BD$. Translate the diagonal $BD$ by the vector $\overrightarrow{DC}$: $D$ goes to $C$ and $B$ goes to a point $E$ with $\overrightarrow{BE}=\overrightarrow{DC}$. Since $DC\parallel AB$ and $\overrightarrow{DC}$ points the same way as $\overrightarrow{AB}$, the point $E$ lies on line $AB$ beyond $B$, and $AE=AB+DC$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(1.8,2.4),D=(0.8,2.4),Ep=(5,0),H=(1.8,0);
void ra(pair P,pair Q,pair R,real s){pair u=unit(P-Q)*s,v=unit(R-Q)*s;draw(Q+u--Q+u+v--Q+v,linewidth(0.6));}
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(A--C,linewidth(0.9));draw(B--D,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--Ep,dashed);draw(C--Ep,dashed+blue);
draw(C--H,dotted);
pair X=extension(A,C,B,D);
ra(A,X,B,0.18);ra(A,C,Ep,0.24);ra(C,H,A,0.15);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,N);label("$E$",Ep,SE);
label("$a$",(A+C)/2,NW);label("$b$",B+0.38*(D-B),SW);label("$b$",(C+Ep)/2,NE,blue);
label("$h$",(1.8,0.7),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

$DBEC$ is a parallelogram, so $CE\parallel BD$ and $CE=BD=b$. As $AC\perp BD$, also $AC\perp CE$. So triangle $ACE$ has a right angle at $C$, legs $AC=a$ and $CE=b$, and hypotenuse $AE=\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$.

The height $h$ of the trapezoid is the distance from $C$ to line $AB$, which is the altitude of the right triangle $ACE$ to its hypotenuse. Computing the area of triangle $ACE$ in two ways, $\dfrac{1}{2}ab=\dfrac{1}{2}\sqrt{a^2+b^2}\,h$, so
\[
\boxed{h=\dfrac{ab}{\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}}.
\]
So the height is determined by the diagonals alone. (As a check, the trapezoid's area $\dfrac{(AB+DC)h}{2}=\dfrac{AE\cdot h}{2}=\dfrac{ab}{2}$ agrees with the area of any quadrilateral with perpendicular diagonals.)

Setting the measures equal,
\[
\dfrac{3x}{2}+2=2x-\dfrac{117}{4}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad 2+\dfrac{117}{4}=2x-\dfrac{3x}{2}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\dfrac{125}{4}=\dfrac {x}{2}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad x=\boxed{\dfrac{125}{2}} .
\]
The student had the correct equation $-\dfrac {x}{2}=-\dfrac{125}{4}$ but then dropped a sign (writing $x=-\dfrac{125}{2}$), and later substituted $-\dfrac{125}{4}$ instead. A negative angle measure is a signal that something went wrong.

With $x=\dfrac{125}{2}$, each angle measures $\dfrac{3}{2}\cdot\dfrac{125}{2}+2=\dfrac{383}{4}=95.75^\circ$ (and $2\cdot\dfrac{125}{2}-\dfrac{117}{4}=\dfrac{383}{4}$ as well).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0);real t=95.75;
pair C=2.6*dir(90),A=2.6*dir(90+t),D=2.6*dir(90-t);
draw(B--A,linewidth(1));draw(B--C,linewidth(1));draw(B--D,linewidth(1));
draw((-2.9,0)--(2.9,0),dotted+gray);
draw(arc(B,0.75,90,90+t),red);draw(arc(B,0.95,90-t,90),blue);
label("$95.75^\circ$",B+1.35*dir(90+t/2),NW,red);label("$95.75^\circ$",B+1.5*dir(90-t/2),NE,blue);
dot(B);label("$B$",B,S);label("$A$",A,W);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

For $\angle ABD$ to be a straight angle, the two adjacent angles would have to add up to $180^\circ$, i.e.\ each would be $90^\circ$. Here they add up to $191.5^\circ$, so rays $BA$ and $BD$ are not opposite rays, and $\boxed{\angle ABD\text{ is not a straight angle}}$.

Since $O$ lies on the diagonal $\overline{RT}$, the points $T$, $O$, $R$ are collinear and $O$ is between $T$ and $R$. So $\angle TOP$ and $\angle POR$ form a linear pair: they are adjacent and their outer sides $\overrightarrow{OT}$ and $\overrightarrow{OR}$ are opposite rays. Hence
\[
m\angle POR=180^\circ-m\angle TOP=180^\circ-50^\circ=\boxed{130^\circ}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real h=4*Tan(25);
pair P=(0,0),R=(4,0),Sx=(4,h),T=(0,h),O=(2,h/2);
void sq(pair Q,pair u,pair v){draw(Q+0.18*u--Q+0.18*u+0.18*v--Q+0.18*v,linewidth(0.6));}
draw(P--R--Sx--T--cycle,linewidth(1));draw(P--Sx,linewidth(0.9));draw(R--T,linewidth(0.9));
sq(P,(1,0),(0,1));sq(R,(-1,0),(0,1));sq(Sx,(-1,0),(0,-1));sq(T,(1,0),(0,-1));
draw(arc(O,0.55,155,205),red);label("$50^\circ$",O+1.25*dir(180),red);
draw(arc(O,0.35,205,335),blue);label("$130^\circ$",O+0.62*dir(270),blue);
dot(O);label("$O$",O,2.2*N);
label("$P$",P,SW);label("$R$",R,SE);label("$S$",Sx,NE);label("$T$",T,NW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(Equivalently, $\angle SOR$ and $\angle TOP$ are vertical angles, so $m\angle SOR=50^\circ$; and $\angle POR$ and $\angle SOR$ form a linear pair along the diagonal $\overline{PS}$, so $m\angle POR=180^\circ-50^\circ$. This is the poster's argument, and the poster's answer $130^\circ$ is correct.)

\textbf{Necessary condition.} The segment $\overline{AC}$ contains $C$, so $C$ must lie on $\overrightarrow{AB}$. Since $C\ne A$, this means that $A$, $B$, $C$ are collinear and $B$ and $C$ lie on the same side of $A$.

\textbf{Sufficient.} Conversely, suppose $C$ lies on $\overrightarrow{AB}$, $C\ne A$. Then $\overrightarrow{AB}=\overrightarrow{AC}$, the points of line $AC$ from $A$ onwards in the direction of $C$. The ray $\overrightarrow{CA}$ consists of the points of the same line from $C$ onwards in the direction of $A$. The points belonging to both are exactly the points between $A$ and $C$ together with $A$ and $C$, i.e.\ $\overline{AC}$.

So the condition holds \textbf{exactly when $C$ lies on ray $\overrightarrow{AB}$} (with $C\ne A$). The point $B$ may be between $A$ and $C$, or beyond $C$, or equal to $C$:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
void drawcase(real y0,real xb,string cap){
  pair A=(0,y0),C=(3,y0),B=(xb,y0);
  draw((-2.2,y0)--(5.6,y0),gray+linewidth(0.5));
  draw((0,y0+0.8)--(5.6,y0+0.8),blue+linewidth(1),EndArrow(6));label("ray $AB$",(5.6,y0+0.8),E,blue);
  draw((3,y0-0.8)--(-2.2,y0-0.8),red+linewidth(1),EndArrow(6));label("ray $CA$",(-2.2,y0-0.8),W,red);
  draw((0,y0+0.8)--(0,y0-0.8),dotted);draw((3,y0+0.8)--(3,y0-0.8),dotted);
  draw(A--C,deepgreen+linewidth(2.2));
  dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);
  label("$A$",A,NW);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$B$",B,(xb<3?N:NE));
  label(cap,(1.7,y0-1.3));
}
drawcase(0,1.5,"$B$ between $A$ and $C$");
drawcase(-3.6,4.4,"$C$ between $A$ and $B$");
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Both pictures are correct answers. A diagram with $B$ between $A$ and $C$ and a diagram with $C$ between $A$ and $B$ are equivalent for this question, since in both cases $\overrightarrow{AB}=\overrightarrow{AC}$.

