solution:
\textbf{Construction.} Put $P$, $E$, $D$ on a line in this order (so $E$ is between $P$ and $D$), and put $A$ on ray $\overrightarrow{ED}$, beyond $D$. Choose $F$ on one side of line $PD$ and $C$ on the other side.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(0,0),Ep=(2,0),D=(3.6,0),A=(5,0),F=(3.4,1.9),C=(1.3,-1.7);
draw(P--(6.6,0),red+linewidth(0.8),EndArrow(6));
draw(P--(P+1.45*(C-P)),red+linewidth(0.8),EndArrow(6));
draw(Ep--(Ep+1.5*(F-Ep)),blue+linewidth(0.8),EndArrow(6));
draw(Ep--(6.6,0.0),deepgreen+linewidth(2.6));
dot(P);dot(Ep);dot(D);dot(A);dot(F);dot(C);
label("$P$",P,W);label("$E$",Ep,S);label("$D$",D,S);label("$A$",A,S);label("$F$",F,E);label("$C$",C,E);
label("$\angle DPC$",P+(1.25,-0.55),red);label("$\angle AEF$",Ep+(1.15,0.45),blue);
label("$\overrightarrow{ED}$",(5.8,0),N,deepgreen);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Then $\angle AEF=\overrightarrow{EA}\cup\overrightarrow{EF}$ (blue, with $\overrightarrow{EA}=\overrightarrow{ED}$) and $\angle DPC=\overrightarrow{PD}\cup\overrightarrow{PC}$ (red).

\textbf{Proof that the intersection is $\overrightarrow{ED}$.} Intersect each ray of one angle with each ray of the other.
\begin{itemize}
\item $\overrightarrow{EA}\cap\overrightarrow{PD}=\overrightarrow{ED}$: $E$ lies on $\overrightarrow{PD}$, and $\overrightarrow{EA}=\overrightarrow{ED}$ points the same way, so $\overrightarrow{ED}\subseteq\overrightarrow{PD}$.
\item $\overrightarrow{EA}\cap\overrightarrow{PC}=\varnothing$: ray $\overrightarrow{PC}$ meets line $PD$ only at $P$, and $P$ is not on $\overrightarrow{ED}$ since $E$ is between $P$ and $D$.
\item $\overrightarrow{EF}\cap\overrightarrow{PD}=\{E\}$: ray $\overrightarrow{EF}$ meets line $PD$ only at $E$.
\item $\overrightarrow{EF}\cap\overrightarrow{PC}=\varnothing$: apart from its endpoint, $\overrightarrow{EF}$ lies on $F$'s side of line $PD$ and $\overrightarrow{PC}$ on $C$'s side. Their endpoints $E$ and $P$ are different points of line $PD$, and neither lies on the other ray.
\end{itemize}
The union of these four intersections is $\overrightarrow{ED}\cup\{E\}=\overrightarrow{ED}$, as required. The ray $\overrightarrow{ED}$ must be a side of $\angle AEF$, so $D$ has to lie on $\overrightarrow{EA}$ or on $\overrightarrow{EF}$; here we chose $\overrightarrow{EA}$. If $F$ and $C$ were on the same side of line $PD$, rays $\overrightarrow{EF}$ and $\overrightarrow{PC}$ could cross and add an extra point to the intersection.

Let $w$ be the length of each of the three fences perpendicular to the barn (the two ends and the divider), and $L$ the length of the side parallel to the barn.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real L=6,w=2;
fill((-0.4,-0.35)--(L+0.4,-0.35)--(L+0.4,0)--(-0.4,0)--cycle,lightgray);
draw((-0.4,0)--(L+0.4,0),gray+linewidth(1.5));
label("barn wall (no fence)",(L/2,-0.35),S);
draw((0,0)--(0,w)--(L,w)--(L,0),linewidth(1.1));draw((L/2,0)--(L/2,w),linewidth(1.1));
label("$w$",(0,w/2),W);label("$w$",(L/2,w/2),E);label("$w$",(L,w/2),E);label("$L$",(L/2,w),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The fence used is $3w+L=200$, so $L=200-3w$, and the area is
\[
A=wL=w(200-3w)=-3w^2+200w=-3\left(w-\dfrac{100}{3}\right)^2+\dfrac{10000}{3}.
\]
The maximum is $\dfrac{10000}{3}\approx3333$ square feet, at $w=\dfrac{100}{3}$ and $L=100$. The closest choice is $\boxed{\textbf{(D)}\ 3300}$.

Two $40$ by $40$ squares also use exactly $3\cdot40+80=200$ feet, but their area $3200$ is not the largest. Making the pen square-shaped is not optimal here, because the three short sides are paid for three times and the long side only once.

question:
On a sphere of radius $R$ with centre $O$, the role of straight lines is played by \emph{great circles} (circles whose centre is $O$). Let $N$ be the north pole and let $A$ and $B$ be points on the equator with $\angle AOB=90^\circ$. Consider the spherical triangle $NAB$ whose sides are the shorter great-circle arcs $NA$, $NB$ and $AB$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that each side has length $\dfrac{\pi R}{2}$ and each angle is $90^\circ$.
\item[(b)] Show that the Pythagorean theorem fails for this right triangle. What is its angle sum?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) The arcs $NA$ and $NB$ run along meridians from the pole to the equator, and $AB$ runs along the equator. Each is a quarter of a great circle, since $\angle NOA=\angle NOB=90^\circ$ and $\angle AOB=90^\circ$. So each has length $\dfrac{1}{4}\cdot2\pi R=\dfrac{\pi R}{2}$.

The angle between two arcs at a vertex is the angle between their tangent lines there. At $A$ the meridian through $A$ runs due north and the equator runs east--west, so they meet at $90^\circ$; the same holds at $B$. At $N$ the tangent lines of the two meridians are parallel to $OA$ and $OB$ (each meridian lies in a plane through the axis $ON$), so the angle at $N$ equals $\angle AOB=90^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real al=20;
pair pr(real x,real y,real z){return (x, z*Cos(al)+y*Sin(al));}
pair mer(real phi,real th){return pr(Cos(th)*Sin(phi),-Cos(th)*Cos(phi),Sin(th));}
pair eqp(real phi){return pr(Sin(phi),-Cos(phi),0);}
draw(circle((0,0),1),linewidth(0.9));
path front=graph(eqp,-90,90);path back=graph(eqp,90,270);
draw(front,gray);draw(back,gray+dashed);
pair N=pr(0,0,1),O=(0,0),A=eqp(-45),B=eqp(45);
path nb=graph(new pair(real t){return mer(45,t);},0,90);
path na=graph(new pair(real t){return mer(-45,t);},0,90);
path ab=graph(eqp,-45,45);
draw(nb,blue+linewidth(1.4));draw(na,blue+linewidth(1.4));draw(ab,blue+linewidth(1.4));
draw(O--A,dashed);draw(O--B,dashed);draw(O--N,dashed);
dot(N);dot(A);dot(B);dot(O);
label("$N$",(0,1),N);label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$O$",O,1.4*S);
label("$90^\circ$",N+(0.15,-0.24),fontsize(8pt));label("$90^\circ$",A+(0.24,0.24),fontsize(8pt));label("$90^\circ$",B+(-0.24,0.24),fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(b) Triangle $NAB$ has a right angle at $A$, with legs $a=b=\dfrac{\pi R}{2}$ and hypotenuse $c=\dfrac{\pi R}{2}$. Then $a^2+b^2=2c^2\ne c^2$, so the Pythagorean theorem fails. Its angle sum is $\boxed{270^\circ}$, not $180^\circ$; on a sphere the angle sum of every triangle exceeds $180^\circ$.

For right triangles on a sphere the correct relation is $\cos\dfrac {c}{R}=\cos\dfrac {a}{R}\cos\dfrac {b}{R}$; here it reads $0=0\cdot0$. For triangles that are small compared with the sphere, $\cos t\approx1-\dfrac{t^2}{2}$, and the relation becomes approximately $a^2+b^2=c^2$. This is why flat geometry works well on small parts of the Earth.

Let the line be $y=x+c$. Its intersections with $y=x^2$ satisfy $x^2-x-c=0$, so the $x$-coordinates $x_1,x_2$ of $A$ and $B$ satisfy $x_1+x_2=1$ and $x_1x_2=-c$ (by Vieta's formulas), and
\[
(x_1-x_2)^2=(x_1+x_2)^2-4x_1x_2=1+4c .
\]
Since the line has slope $1$, the vertical difference of $A$ and $B$ equals the horizontal difference, so
\[
AB^2=(x_1-x_2)^2+(x_1-x_2)^2=2(1+4c).
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real f(real x){return x^2;}
real s=sqrt(4.5);
pair A=((1-s)/2,((1-s)/2)^2), B=((1+s)/2,((1+s)/2)^2);
draw((-1.7,0)--(2.3,0),Arrow(TeXHead));draw((0,-0.6)--(0,3.6),Arrow(TeXHead));
label("$x$",(2.3,0),S);label("$y$",(0,3.6),W);
draw(graph(f,-1.55,1.85),blue+linewidth(1));
draw((-1.3,-1.3+7/8)--(2.2,2.2+7/8),red+linewidth(1));
draw(A--B,red+linewidth(2));
dot(A);dot(B);
label("$A$",A+(-0.12,0.02),W);label("$B$",B,E);
label(rotate(45)*Label("$AB=3$"),(A+B)/2+0.5*unit((1,1))+0.27*unit((-1,1)));
label("$y=x^2$",(-1.55,f(-1.55)),W,blue);
label("$y=x+c$",(2.2,2.2+7/8),E,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Setting $2(1+4c)=9$ gives $c=\dfrac{7}{8}$ (and then $1+4c>0$, so there really are two intersection points). The line is $\boxed{y=x+\dfrac{7}{8}}$.

(For the guess $c=\dfrac{9}{8}$ we would get $AB^2=2\left(1+\dfrac{9}{2}\right)=11$, so $AB=\sqrt{11}\ne3$.)

A shortest grid path from one lattice point to another that is $p$ units to the right and $q$ units up consists of $p+q$ unit steps, of which $p$ go right; there are $\dbinom{p+q}{p}$ of them. The path splits into three independent legs:
\begin{itemize}
\item $(0,0)\to(1,2)$: $1$ right and $2$ up, $\dbinom31=3$ ways;
\item $(1,2)\to(4,4)$: $3$ right and $2$ up, $\dbinom52=10$ ways;
\item $(4,4)\to(5,4)$: $1$ right, $1$ way.
\end{itemize}
By the multiplication principle there are $3\cdot10\cdot1=\boxed{30}$ paths.

Let the circle have centre $O$ and radius $r=\dfrac{5}{2}$. A line at distance $d<r$ from $O$ cuts a chord of length
\[
L=2\sqrt{r^2-d^2}=2\sqrt{\dfrac{25}{4}-d^2},
\]
so every chord has length $0<L\le5$, and each value of $L$ in this range corresponds to exactly one distance $d=\sqrt{\dfrac{25}{4}-\dfrac{L^2}{4}}$.

Fix the slope $\dfrac{3}{4}$. Lines with this slope are parallel, and for each distance $d>0$ there are exactly two of them (one on each side of $O$), while $d=0$ gives the single line through $O$. The integer chord lengths are $L=1,2,3,4,5$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $L=5$ gives $d=0$: one line (a diameter);
\item $L=1,2,3,4$ give $d=\dfrac{\sqrt{24}}{2},\ \dfrac{\sqrt{21}}{2},\ 2,\ \dfrac{3}{2}$, all positive: two lines each.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=2.5;
pair O=(0,0), u=(0.8,0.6), n=(-0.6,0.8);
draw(circle(O,r),linewidth(1));
int[] L={1,2,3,4};
for(int i=0;i<4;++i){
  real d=sqrt(r^2-L[i]^2/4);
  for(int sg=-1;sg<=1;sg+=2){
    pair c=sg*d*n;
    draw((c-L[i]/2*u)--(c+L[i]/2*u),gray(0.45)+linewidth(0.8));
  }
  pair e=d*n+L[i]/2*u;
  label("$"+string(L[i])+"$",e,unit(e),fontsize(9pt));
}
draw(-r*u--r*u,red+linewidth(1.1));
label("$5$",r*u,unit(u),red+fontsize(9pt));
pair c4=1.5*n, P=c4+2*u;
draw(c4-2*u--c4+2*u,blue+linewidth(1.1));
draw(O--c4,dashed);draw(O--P,dashed);
draw(c4+0.18*(-n)--c4+0.18*(-n)+0.18*u--c4+0.18*u,linewidth(0.5));
label("$\dfrac{3}{2}$",0.75*n,0.9*W+0.35*S,fontsize(9pt));
label("$\dfrac{5}{2}$",(O+P)/2+0.32*(0.96,-0.28),fontsize(9pt));
dot(O);label("$O$",O,SE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

In the figure the chord of length $4$ (blue) is at distance $\dfrac{3}{2}$ from $O$: half of it, the distance $\dfrac{3}{2}$ and the radius $\dfrac{5}{2}$ form a right triangle with sides $2,\dfrac{3}{2},\dfrac{5}{2}$. The total is $1+4\cdot2=\boxed{9}$ lines.

The volume of one cone (radius $2$, height $7$) is
\[
\dfrac{1}{3}\pi\cdot2^2\cdot7=\dfrac{28\pi}{3}\text{ cm}^3,
\]
and the volume of one cylinder (radius $7$, height $20$) is $\pi\cdot7^2\cdot20=980\pi\text{ cm}^3$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
// cylinder: diameter 14, height 20
pair c0=(0,0),c1=(0,20);
draw(shift(c1)*xscale(7)*yscale(1.6)*unitcircle,linewidth(0.9));
path lower=shift(c0)*xscale(7)*yscale(1.6)*arc((0,0),1,180,360);
path upper=shift(c0)*xscale(7)*yscale(1.6)*arc((0,0),1,0,180);
draw(lower,linewidth(0.9));draw(upper,dashed+linewidth(0.6));
draw((-7,0)--(-7,20),linewidth(0.9));draw((7,0)--(7,20),linewidth(0.9));
draw((-7,-3.2)--(7,-3.2),Arrows(TeXHead));label("$14$",(0,-3.2),S);
draw((-9.5,0)--(-9.5,20),Arrows(TeXHead));label("$20$",(-9.5,10),W);
// cone: diameter 4, height 7, apex down
pair v=(17,6),t=(17,13);
draw(shift(t)*xscale(2)*yscale(0.6)*unitcircle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((15,13)--v--(19,13),linewidth(0.9));
draw((15,15.2)--(19,15.2),Arrows(TeXHead));label("$4$",(17,15.2),N);
draw((21,6)--(21,13),Arrows(TeXHead));label("$7$",(21,9.5),E);
label("cylinder",(0,23.5));label("cone",(17,4),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So one cylinder makes $\dfrac{980\pi}{\dfrac{28\pi}{3}}=105$ cones. For $1750$ cones he needs $\dfrac{1750}{105}=16\dfrac{2}{3}$ cylinders, so he must use $\boxed{17}$ cylinders ($16$ are not enough, since they make only $1680$ cones).

(The answer $50$ comes from forgetting the factor $\dfrac{1}{3}$ in the volume of a cone: $\dfrac{980\pi}{28\pi}=35$ and $\dfrac{1750}{35}=50$.)

question:
The number $\log_310$ cannot be simplified to an integer or a fraction.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $\log_310$ is irrational.
\item[(b)] Prove that $2<\log_310<2.1$.
\end{enumerate}

A rotation of the plane maps the point $A(-1,7)$ to the point $A'(3,1)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the centre of the rotation must lie on a certain line, and find its equation. (So the two points alone do not determine the centre.)
\item[(b)] Find the centre if the rotation is through $90^\circ$ anticlockwise, and the centre if it is through $90^\circ$ clockwise.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} A rotation keeps distances from its centre $C$, so $CA=CA'$: the centre is equidistant from $A$ and $A'$, i.e.\ it lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AA'$. The midpoint of $AA'$ is $M(1,4)$ and $\overrightarrow{AA'}=(4,-6)$, so the perpendicular bisector is
\[
4(x-1)-6(y-4)=0\iff\boxed{2x-3y+10=0}.
\]
Conversely, every point $C$ of this line is the centre of some rotation taking $A$ to $A'$ (through the angle $\angle ACA'$, in the appropriate direction), so the two points alone do not determine the centre; one also needs the angle (or a second pair of corresponding points).

\textbf{(b)} For a $90^\circ$ rotation, $CA=CA'$ and $\angle ACA'=90^\circ$, so triangle $ACA'$ is right isosceles and $C$ is at distance $\dfrac{1}{2}AA'$ from $M$ along the bisector:
\[
C=M\pm\dfrac{1}{2}(6,4)=(1,4)\pm(3,2),\qquad C_1=(4,6),\quad C_2=(-2,2).
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-1,7),Ap=(3,1),M=(1,4),C1=(4,6),C2=(-2,2);
draw((-3.3,0)--(5.6,0),Arrow(TeXHead));draw((0,-0.6)--(0,8.2),Arrow(TeXHead));
label("$x$",(5.6,0),S);label("$y$",(0,8.2),W);label("$O$",(0,0),SW);
draw((-3.2,(2*(-3.2)+10)/3)--(5.4,(2*5.4+10)/3),blue+linewidth(0.9));
label("$2x-3y+10=0$",(5.4,(2*5.4+10)/3),N,blue);
draw(A--Ap,dashed);
draw(C1--A,red);draw(C1--Ap,red);
draw(C2--A,deepgreen);draw(C2--Ap,deepgreen);
void ra(pair C,pair P,pair Q){real s=0.35;pair u=s*unit(P-C),v=s*unit(Q-C);draw(C+u--C+u+v--C+v,linewidth(0.5));}
ra(C1,A,Ap);ra(C2,A,Ap);
dot(A);dot(Ap);dot(M);dot(C1,red);dot(C2,deepgreen);
label("$A(-1,7)$",A,NW);label("$A'(3,1)$",Ap,S);label("$M$",M,dir(169));
label("$C_1(4,6)$",C1,SE,red);label("$C_2(-2,2)$",C2,NW,deepgreen);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Check the direction. Rotating a vector $(p,q)$ by $90^\circ$ anticlockwise gives $(-q,p)$, and clockwise gives $(q,-p)$.
\begin{itemize}
\item About $C_1$: $A-C_1=(-5,1)$, and turning it anticlockwise gives $(-1,-5)=A'-C_1$.
\item About $C_2$: $A-C_2=(1,5)$, and turning it clockwise gives $(5,-1)=A'-C_2$.
\end{itemize}
So the centre is $\boxed{(4,6)}$ for the $90^\circ$ anticlockwise rotation and $\boxed{(-2,2)}$ for the $90^\circ$ clockwise rotation.

Let the legs be $a,b$ and the hypotenuse $c$. By Pythagoras $a^2+b^2=c^2$, so the sum of the squares is $2c^2=1682$, giving $c^2=841$ and $c=29$. Then $a+b=70-29=41$ and $a^2+b^2=841$, so
\[
2ab=(a+b)^2-(a^2+b^2)=1681-841=840,\qquad ab=420 .
\]
So $a$ and $b$ are the roots of $t^2-41t+420=0$, i.e.\ $(t-20)(t-21)=0$.

