Modulo $3$ we have $2\equiv-1$, so $n\cdot2^n+1\equiv(-1)^n n+1\pmod3$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $n$ is even, we need $n+1\equiv0$, i.e.\ $n\equiv2\pmod3$. Even $n$ with $n\equiv2\pmod3$ are exactly $n\equiv2\pmod6$.
\item If $n$ is odd, we need $-n+1\equiv0$, i.e.\ $n\equiv1\pmod3$. Odd $n$ with $n\equiv1\pmod3$ are exactly $n\equiv1\pmod6$.
\end{itemize}
So $3\mid n\cdot2^n+1$ exactly when $\boxed{n\equiv1\text{ or }2\pmod6}$, i.e.\ $n=1,2,7,8,13,14,\dots$

question:
In trapezoid $ABCD$ with bases $AD$ and $BC$ ($AD\parallel BC$), the four sides have four different lengths. Prove that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(1)] the intersection point of the diagonals does not lie on the midline of the trapezoid (the segment joining the midpoints of the legs $AB$ and $CD$);
\item[(2)] the sum of the diagonals is greater than the sum of the bases.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $AD=a$, $BC=b$, let $h$ be the height, and let the diagonals $AC$ and $BD$ meet at $O$. Let $P$ and $Q$ be the midpoints of the legs $AB$ and $CD$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(2,4),C=(6,4),D=(10,0);
pair O=extension(A,C,B,D);
pair Mab=(A+B)/2,Mcd=(C+D)/2;
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(A--C,linewidth(0.7));draw(B--D,linewidth(0.7));
draw(Mab--Mcd,dashed+blue);
dot(O);dot(Mab,blue);dot(Mcd,blue);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,NW);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,SE);
label("$O$",O,N);label("$P$",Mab,W,blue);label("$Q$",Mcd,E,blue);
label("$a$",(A+D)/2,S);label("$b$",(B+C)/2,N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{(1)} Since $AD\parallel BC$, we have $\angle OAD=\angle OCB$ and $\angle ODA=\angle OBC$ (alternate angles), so triangles $AOD$ and $COB$ are similar with ratio $AD:CB=a:b$. Their heights from $O$ are in the same ratio, and they add up to $h$, so the distance from $O$ to $AD$ is $\dfrac{a}{a+b}h$ and the distance from $O$ to $BC$ is $\dfrac{b}{a+b}h$.

The midline $PQ$ is parallel to the bases and lies halfway between them, at distance $\dfrac {h}{2}$ from each. So $O$ lies on the midline only if $\dfrac{a}{a+b}=\dfrac{1}{2}$, i.e.\ $a=b$. But $AD$ and $BC$ are two different sides, so $a\ne b$, and $O$ is not on the midline. (In the figure $a>b$, so $O$ lies closer to the shorter base $BC$, above $PQ$.)

\textbf{(2)} $O$ lies strictly inside both diagonals and is not on the lines $AD$ or $BC$, so $AOD$ and $BOC$ are genuine triangles. By the triangle inequality,
\[
AO+OD>AD,\qquad BO+OC>BC .
\]
Adding, $(AO+OC)+(BO+OD)>AD+BC$, that is, $AC+BD>AD+BC$. $\blacksquare$

Let the apex be $T$ and the centre of the base be $O$. The slant height of a face with base edge $a$ is $TK$, where $K$ is the midpoint of that edge; $TOK$ is a right triangle with legs $TO=h$ and $OK=\dfrac {b}{2}$. Similarly the slant height $TL$ to the midpoint $L$ of an edge $b$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs $h$ and $OL=\dfrac {a}{2}$. (In the figure, $TK=13$ is blue and $TL=15$ is red.)

