solution:
Label the cube so that $ABCD$ is the bottom face and $EFGH$ the top face, with $E,F,G,H$ above $A,B,C,D$.

Two parallel edges are not skew, so a pairwise skew triple uses one edge from each of the three directions. Take the edge $AB$. The edges skew to $AB$ are the two edges of the second direction not touching $AB$, namely $EH$ and $FG$, and the two edges of the third direction not touching $AB$, namely $DH$ and $CG$.
\begin{itemize}
\item With $EH$: $DH$ meets $EH$ at $H$, but $CG$ is skew to $EH$. So $\{AB,EH,CG\}$ works.
\item With $FG$: $CG$ meets $FG$ at $G$, but $DH$ is skew to $FG$. So $\{AB,FG,DH\}$ works.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=3;
pair P(real x,real y,real z){return s*(x+0.42*y,z+0.3*y);}
pair A=P(0,0,0),B=P(1,0,0),C=P(1,1,0),D=P(0,1,0),Ev=P(0,0,1),F=P(1,0,1),G=P(1,1,1),H=P(0,1,1);
draw(A--B--F--Ev--cycle,linewidth(0.8));draw(B--C--G--F,linewidth(0.8));draw(Ev--H--G,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--D--C,dashed);draw(D--H,dashed);
draw(A--B,red+linewidth(2));draw(Ev--H,blue+linewidth(2));draw(C--G,deepgreen+linewidth(2));
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$E$",Ev,W);label("$F$",F,dir(-20));label("$G$",G,NE);label("$H$",H,NW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So $AB$ lies in exactly $2$ pairwise skew triples, not $4$: of the $2\times2=4$ ways to pick one edge skew to $AB$ from each of the other two directions, two pick edges that meet. That is the error. Given the edge $AB$, the probability that the other two chosen edges complete a pairwise skew triple is $\dfrac{2}{55}$, and by symmetry this is the answer.

Equivalently, counting all triples: each of the $12$ edges lies in $2$ such triples and each triple is counted $3$ times, giving $\dfrac{12\cdot2}{3}=8$ triples out of $\dbinom{12}{3}=220$. The probability is $\dfrac{8}{220}=\boxed{\dfrac{2}{55}}$. (The figure shows the triple $AB$, $EH$, $CG$.)

question:
Two functions $f,g:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ satisfy $f(g(x))=x$ for every real $x$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show by an example that $g(f(x))=x$ need not hold for every $x$.
\item[(b)] Prove that $g$ is one-to-one and $f$ is onto.
\item[(c)] Prove that if, in addition, $g$ is onto, then $g(f(x))=x$ for every real $x$ (so $f$ and $g$ are inverse functions).
\end{enumerate}

solution:
First, $\angle ACB=180^\circ-40^\circ-60^\circ=80^\circ$. Since $D$ lies on $AB$ and $E$ lies on $BC$,
\[
\angle DAC=\angle BAC=40^\circ,\qquad\angle ACE=\angle ACB=80^\circ .
\]
The points $A,C,E,D$ lie on one circle, so $ACED$ is a cyclic quadrilateral and its opposite angles add up to $180^\circ$:
\[
\angle CED=180^\circ-\angle DAC=140^\circ,\qquad\angle EDA=180^\circ-\angle ACE=100^\circ .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(6,0);
pair C=extension(A,A+dir(40),B,B+dir(120));
pair D=(4.6,0);
pair Ev=B+(abs(B-D)*abs(B-A)/abs(B-C))*unit(C-B);
pair O=extension((A+D)/2,(A+D)/2+(0,1),(A+C)/2,(A+C)/2+rotate(90)*(C-A));
draw(circle(O,abs(A-O)),gray(0.5)+linewidth(0.7));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(D--Ev,linewidth(1));
void mk(pair V,pair P,pair Q,real r,string s,real lr){
  real a1=degrees(P-V),a2=degrees(Q-V); if(a2<a1) a2+=360;
  draw(arc(V,r,a1,a2),linewidth(0.6));
  label(s,V+lr*dir((a1+a2)/2),fontsize(8pt));
}
mk(A,D,C,0.75,"$40^\circ$",1.25);
mk(C,A,Ev,0.45,"$80^\circ$",0.85);
mk(Ev,C,D,0.35,"$140^\circ$",0.85);
mk(D,Ev,A,0.4,"$100^\circ$",0.85);
mk(B,C,A,0.5,"$60^\circ$",0.95);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(Ev);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,dir(-60));label("$E$",Ev,dir(15));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So the angles are $\boxed{\angle A=40^\circ,\ \angle C=80^\circ,\ \angle E=140^\circ,\ \angle D=100^\circ}$ (sum $360^\circ$). As a check, $\angle BDE=180^\circ-100^\circ=80^\circ=\angle ACB$, so triangle $BDE$ is similar to triangle $BCA$, as it must be.

The expression inside is $x^2-2x$, so the equation says $x^2-2x=4$ or $x^2-2x=-4$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $x^2-2x-4=0$ has discriminant $4+16=20>0$: two solutions $x=1\pm\sqrt5$.
\item $x^2-2x+4=0$, i.e.\ $(x-1)^2=-3$, has no real solutions.
\end{itemize}
So there are $\boxed{2}$ real solutions, $x=1\pm\sqrt5$. (Quick view: $x^2-2x=(x-1)^2-1\ge-1$, so it can never equal $-4$, while it takes the value $4$ exactly twice.)

question:
Two lines $\ell$ and $m$ are cut by a transversal $t$, which meets $\ell$ at $A$ and $m$ at $B$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that if a pair of alternate interior angles are equal, then $\ell\parallel m$.
\item[(b)] Using (a) and the parallel postulate (through a point not on a line there is at most one line parallel to it), prove the converse: if $\ell\parallel m$, then the alternate interior angles are equal.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Alternate interior angles lie between the two lines, on opposite sides of $t$, one at $A$ and one at $B$ (both marked $\alpha$ in the figure).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(2,0),B=(4,3);
draw((-1,0)--(7,0),linewidth(1),Arrows(TeXHead));draw((-1,3)--(7,3),linewidth(1),Arrows(TeXHead));
pair t1=A+(-1.1)*unit(B-A),t2=B+(1.1)*unit(B-A);
draw(t1--t2,linewidth(1),Arrows(TeXHead));
real g=degrees(B-A);
draw(arc(A,0.6,0,g),blue+linewidth(0.8));draw(arc(B,0.6,180,180+g),blue+linewidth(0.8));
label("$\alpha$",A+0.95*dir(g/2),blue);label("$\alpha$",B+0.95*dir(180+g/2),blue);
dot(A);dot(B);
label("$A$",A,SE);label("$B$",B,NW);
label("$\ell$",(7,0),E);label("$m$",(7,3),E);label("$t$",t2,N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{(a)} Suppose the alternate interior angles at $A$ and $B$ are both $\alpha$, but $\ell$ and $m$ are not parallel, so they meet at a point $P$. The point $P$ lies on one side of $t$. On that side, one of the two marked angles is an angle of triangle $ABP$, and the angle of the triangle at the other vertex is the supplement of the other marked angle, so it equals $180^\circ-\alpha$. So two angles of triangle $ABP$ add up to $\alpha+(180^\circ-\alpha)=180^\circ$, leaving $0^\circ$ for the angle at $P$. This is impossible, since the angles of a triangle add up to $180^\circ$ and each is positive. Hence $\ell$ and $m$ do not meet: $\ell\parallel m$. (Equivalently, the exterior angle of triangle $ABP$ at one vertex would equal a remote interior angle, contradicting the exterior angle theorem.)

\textbf{(b)} Suppose $\ell\parallel m$. Through $A$ draw the line $\ell'$ that makes, with $t$, an alternate interior angle equal to the angle $\alpha$ at $B$. By (a), $\ell'\parallel m$. Both $\ell$ and $\ell'$ pass through $A$ and are parallel to $m$, so by the parallel postulate $\ell'=\ell$. Therefore $\ell$ itself makes the angle $\alpha$ with $t$, i.e.\ the alternate interior angles are equal. $\blacksquare$

So the angles tell us about parallel lines both ways: equal alternate (or corresponding) angles force the lines to be parallel, and parallel lines force those angles to be equal.

question:
A function $f$ on the integers satisfies
\[
f(x)=\begin{cases}x-2,&x>5,\\ 5-f\bigl(f(x+3)\bigr),&x\le5.\end{cases}
\]
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find $f(1)$.
\item[(b)] Find $f(-10)$.
\item[(c)] Show that these rules determine $f(x)$ uniquely for every integer $x$, and give a closed form for $f$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Team A does $\dfrac{1}{12}$ of the job per day and team B does $\dfrac{1}{36}$.
\begin{itemize}
\item First $4$ days, A alone: $\dfrac{4}{12}=\dfrac{1}{3}$ of the job.
\item Next $2$ days, both teams: $2\left(\dfrac{1}{12}+\dfrac{1}{36}\right)=2\cdot\dfrac{4}{36}=\dfrac{2}{9}$.
\end{itemize}
So $\dfrac{1}{3}+\dfrac{2}{9}=\dfrac{5}{9}$ is done and $\dfrac{4}{9}$ remains. Team B alone needs $\dfrac{4}{9}\cdot36=16$ more days. In total team B works $2+16=\boxed{18}$ days ($16$ of them after team A leaves).

question:
A double cone $K$ has equation $x^2+y^2=z^2$; its axis is the $z$-axis and its vertex is the origin $V$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that a plane perpendicular to the axis meets $K$ in a circle if it does not pass through $V$, and in the single point $V$ if it does.
\item[(b)] More generally, for each real number $m$, describe the intersection of $K$ with the plane $z=mx$, which passes through the vertex.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} A plane perpendicular to the axis is $z=h$. Substituting, $x^2+y^2=h^2$: for $h\ne0$ this is a circle of radius $|h|$ in that plane, and for $h=0$ (the plane through $V$) it is $x^2+y^2=0$, i.e.\ only the point $x=y=0$, $z=0$. So yes: the plane through the vertex perpendicular to the axis gives a single point, a degenerate conic.

\textbf{(b)} On the plane $z=mx$ the points of $K$ satisfy $x^2+y^2=m^2x^2$, i.e.
\[
y^2=\left(m^2-1\right)x^2 .
\]
\begin{itemize}
\item If $|m|<1$, the right side is $\le0$, so $x=y=0$ and $z=0$: the intersection is \textbf{the single point $V$}.
\item If $|m|=1$, then $y=0$ and $z=\pm x$: \textbf{one line} (a generator of the cone, along which the plane is tangent).
\item If $|m|>1$, then $y=\pm\sqrt{m^2-1}\,x$: \textbf{two lines} through $V$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
