\end{asy}

Points of $AB$ can be written $A+s(B-A)=(b-sb,\,sa)$, where $s$ is the fraction of the way from $A$ to $B$, so that the distance from $A$ is $sc$. Let $D=a^2+ab+b^2$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $Y$ is on $CQ$: $Y=t(a+b,b)$ with $a\,t(a+b)+b\cdot tb=ab$, so $t=\dfrac{ab}{D}$. Its second coordinate $\dfrac{ab^2}{D}$ equals $sa$, so $s_Y=\dfrac{b^2}{D}$.
\item $X$ is on $CP$: $X=u(a,a+b)$ with $a\cdot ua+b\,u(a+b)=ab$, so $u=\dfrac{ab}{D}$. Its second coordinate $\dfrac{ab(a+b)}{D}$ equals $sa$, so $s_X=\dfrac{b(a+b)}{D}$.
\end{itemize}
Since $s_Y<s_X$, the order along $AB$ is $A,Y,X,B$, and
\[
AY=\dfrac{b^2c}{D},\qquad XY=(s_X-s_Y)c=\dfrac{abc}{D},\qquad XB=(1-s_X)c=\dfrac{(D-ab-b^2)c}{D}=\dfrac{a^2c}{D}.
\]
Therefore
\[
XY^2=\dfrac{a^2b^2c^2}{D^2}=\dfrac{b^2c}{D}\cdot\dfrac{a^2c}{D}=AY\cdot XB .\qquad\blacksquare
\]

The radius is $\dfrac{10}{2}=5$. A point $(x,y)$ is on the circle exactly when its distance from $(-2,3)$ is $5$:
