The number $10^{2019}$ has digit sum $1$, so we count numbers with at most $2019$ digits. Write such a number with exactly $2019$ digits by adding leading zeros; this does not change the digit sum. A digit sum of $2$ means either
\begin{itemize}
\item one digit is $2$ and the rest are $0$: $2019$ choices of position, or
\item two digits are $1$ and the rest are $0$: $\dbinom{2019}{2}=\dfrac{2019\cdot2018}{2}=2037171$ choices.
\end{itemize}
The total is $2019+2037171=\boxed{2039190}$.

\textbf{Construction.} Construct the midpoint $M$ of $O_1O_2$, draw the segment $AM$, and construct the line $\ell$ through $A$ perpendicular to $AM$. Let $\ell$ meet the first circle again at $B$ and the second at $C$. Then $AB=AC$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O1=(0,0),O2=(4.2,0.8);real r1=3,r2=2.3;
pair[] I=intersectionpoints(circle(O1,r1),circle(O2,r2));
pair A=(I[0].y>I[1].y)?I[0]:I[1];
pair M=(O1+O2)/2;
pair u=unit(rotate(90)*(A-M));
pair B=A+2*dot(O1-A,u)*u,C=A+2*dot(O2-A,u)*u;
pair F1=A+dot(O1-A,u)*u,F2=A+dot(O2-A,u)*u;
draw(circle(O1,r1),linewidth(0.9));draw(circle(O2,r2),linewidth(0.9));
draw((B+0.5*(B-C)/abs(B-C))--(C+0.5*(C-B)/abs(C-B)),linewidth(0.6));
draw(B--C,red+linewidth(1.3));
draw(O1--O2,dashed);draw(A--M,blue+dashed);
draw(O1--F1,dotted);draw(O2--F2,dotted);
void ra(pair V,pair d1,pair d2){real s=0.25;draw(V+s*d1--V+s*d1+s*d2--V+s*d2,linewidth(0.5));}
ra(A,unit(M-A),u);ra(F1,unit(O1-F1),u);ra(F2,unit(O2-F2),-u);
pair n=unit(rotate(90)*u);
pair[] mids={(A+F1)/2,(F1+B)/2,(A+F2)/2,(F2+C)/2};
for(pair q:mids) draw(q-0.15*n--q+0.15*n,red+linewidth(0.8));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(O1);dot(O2);dot(M,blue);dot(F1);dot(F2);
label("$A$",A,2.5*N);label("$B$",B,dir(110));label("$C$",C,dir(60));
label("$O_1$",O1,SW);label("$O_2$",O2,SE);label("$M$",M,dir(-30),blue);
label("$F_1$",F1,N);label("$F_2$",F2,N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Proof.} Let $F_1$ and $F_2$ be the feet of the perpendiculars from $O_1$ and $O_2$ to $\ell$. The perpendicular from the centre of a circle to a chord bisects the chord, so $F_1$ is the midpoint of $AB$ and $F_2$ is the midpoint of $AC$; hence $AB=2AF_1$ and $AC=2AF_2$.

The lines $O_1F_1$, $MA$ and $O_2F_2$ are all perpendicular to $\ell$, so they are parallel. In the trapezoid $O_1F_1F_2O_2$ (or along any line, after projecting onto $\ell$), the point $M$ is the midpoint of $O_1O_2$, so its projection $A$ onto $\ell$ is the midpoint of $F_1F_2$, by the midline theorem (parallel lines cutting equal segments on one transversal cut equal segments on another). Therefore $AF_1=AF_2$, and so
\[
AB=2AF_1=2AF_2=AC .
\]
The points $F_1$ and $F_2$ are on opposite sides of $A$, so the chords $AB$ and $AC$ lie on opposite sides of $A$, as required. (The line $\ell$ is not tangent to either circle: it would be tangent to the first circle only if $\ell\perp AO_1$, i.e.\ if $M$ lay on line $AO_1$, which would put $O_2$ on that line too and make the circles tangent at $A$.)

\textbf{Uniqueness.} Conversely, if a line through $A$ cuts equal chords $AB=AC$ on opposite sides of $A$, then $A$ is the midpoint of $F_1F_2$, so it is the projection of $M$ onto the line, i.e.\ the line is perpendicular to $AM$. So $\ell$ is the only such line. (If the chords may lie on the same side of $A$, the common chord of the two circles is a second, trivial solution: there the two chords coincide.)

question:
The points $O=(0,0)$, $P=\left(-1,\sqrt3\right)$ and $Q=\left(1,\sqrt3\right)$ are the vertices of an equilateral triangle with centre $G$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the parabola with a vertical axis that passes through $O$, $P$ and $Q$.
\item[(b)] Explain why rotating this parabola about $G$ through $120^\circ$ and through $240^\circ$ gives two other parabolas through $O$, $P$ and $Q$.
\item[(c)] Prove that through any three points that are not on one line there pass infinitely many parabolas.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Write $y=ax^2+bx+c$. The point $O$ gives $c=0$, and $P$, $Q$ give $a-b=\sqrt3$ and $a+b=\sqrt3$, so $b=0$ and $a=\sqrt3$. The parabola is $\boxed{y=\sqrt3\,x^2}$, with vertex $O$ and a vertical axis.

\textbf{(b)} The centre of the triangle is $G=\left(0,\dfrac{2\sqrt3}{3}\right)$. A rotation about $G$ through $120^\circ$ or $240^\circ$ maps the equilateral triangle onto itself, permuting its vertices. A rotation maps a parabola onto a congruent parabola, and it maps the points $O,P,Q$ of $y=\sqrt3x^2$ onto the points of the image, which are again $O,P,Q$ in some order. So the two images are parabolas through $O$, $P$ and $Q$. Their axes are the vertical axis turned through $120^\circ$ and $240^\circ$, and their vertices are the images of $O$, namely $P$ and $Q$. Three parabolas with three different axis directions are three different curves.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real s3=sqrt(3);
pair O=(0,0), P=(-1,s3), Q=(1,s3), G=(0,2*s3/3);
real f(real x){return s3*x^2;}
path par=graph(f,-1.18,1.18);
draw((-2.6,0)--(2.6,0),gray(0.7),Arrow(5));
draw((0,-1.3)--(0,3.4),gray(0.7),Arrow(5));
draw(O--P--Q--cycle,gray(0.45)+dashed);
draw(par,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(rotate(120,G)*par,red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(rotate(240,G)*par,heavygreen+linewidth(0.9));
dot(O);dot(P);dot(Q);dot(G,gray(0.3));
label("$O$",(0,-0.33));label("$P$",P+0.3*dir(232));label("$Q$",Q+0.3*dir(-50));label("$G$",G,E,gray(0.3));
label("$y=\sqrt{3}\,x^2$",f(1.18)*N+1.18*E,E,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{(c)} Let the three points be $X_1,X_2,X_3$, not on one line. Choose any line direction $e$ that is not parallel to any of the three sides $X_1X_2$, $X_2X_3$, $X_3X_1$; all but three directions qualify. Rotate the coordinate system so that the new vertical axis (the $t$-axis) is parallel to $e$, and call the new coordinates $(s,t)$. Two of the points have equal $s$-coordinates only if the line through them is parallel to $e$, so the $s$-coordinates $s_1,s_2,s_3$ are different. Then
\[
t=t_1\dfrac{(s-s_2)(s-s_3)}{(s_1-s_2)(s_1-s_3)}+t_2\dfrac{(s-s_1)(s-s_3)}{(s_2-s_1)(s_2-s_3)}+t_3\dfrac{(s-s_1)(s-s_2)}{(s_3-s_1)(s_3-s_2)}
\]
is a quadratic function $t=\alpha s^2+\beta s+\gamma$ whose graph passes through the three points (at $s=s_i$ all fractions vanish except the $i$th, which equals $1$). Its leading coefficient $\alpha$ is not $0$: otherwise the three points would lie on the line $t=\beta s+\gamma$. So the graph is a parabola whose axis is parallel to $e$.

As $s\to\pm\infty$ the points of this parabola go off to infinity in the direction of $e$, so parabolas obtained from different directions $e$ are different curves. There are infinitely many admissible directions, hence infinitely many parabolas through $X_1,X_2,X_3$. (So ``three points determine a parabola'' is true only once the direction of the axis is fixed, for example for parabolas $y=ax^2+bx+c$.)

\textbf{Step 1: a standard position.} Let $p>0$ be the distance from the focus to the directrix. By a rigid motion we may place the parabola so that its focus is $F=\left(0,\dfrac {p}{2}\right)$ and its directrix is the line $y=-\dfrac {p}{2}$. A point $(x,y)$ is on the parabola exactly when
\[
x^2+\left(y-\dfrac {p}{2}\right)^2=\left(y+\dfrac {p}{2}\right)^2\iff x^2=2py\iff y=\dfrac{x^2}{2p}.
\]
So every parabola is congruent to the graph of $y=\dfrac{x^2}{2p}$, where $p$ is its focus--directrix distance.

\textbf{Step 2: a dilation.} The dilation with centre $V=(0,0)$ and factor $k>0$ sends $(x,y)$ to $(kx,ky)$. If $y=\dfrac{x^2}{2p}$, the image point $(X,Y)=(kx,ky)$ satisfies
\[
Y=ky=\dfrac{kx^2}{2p}=\dfrac{(kx)^2}{2pk}=\dfrac{X^2}{2(pk)} ,
\]
and every point of $Y=\dfrac{X^2}{2pk}$ arises in this way. So the dilation maps the parabola with parameter $p$ onto the parabola with parameter $pk$. Taking $k=\dfrac {q}{p}$ maps $y=\dfrac{x^2}{2p}$ onto $y=\dfrac{x^2}{2q}$.

\textbf{Conclusion.} Given parabolas $\mathcal P_1$ and $\mathcal P_2$ with parameters $p$ and $q$, move $\mathcal P_1$ rigidly to $y=\dfrac{x^2}{2p}$, dilate by $\dfrac {q}{p}$ to get $y=\dfrac{x^2}{2q}$, and then apply the rigid motion that takes this standard parabola to $\mathcal P_2$. A rigid motion, a dilation and another rigid motion combine into a rigid motion followed by a dilation (move the dilation centre along with the figure). Hence any two parabolas are similar, with ratio $\dfrac {q}{p}$. $\blacksquare$

For example, the dilation with centre $V$ and factor $2$ maps $y=x^2$ (where $p=\dfrac{1}{2}$) onto $y=\dfrac{x^2}{2}$ (where $p=1$): $X=(1,1)\mapsto X'=(2,2)$ and $Y=\left(-\dfrac{3}{2},\dfrac{9}{4}\right)\mapsto Y'=\left(-3,\dfrac{9}{2}\right)$.

