label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The frame length is $2r+2h+\pi r=12$, so $h=\dfrac{12-(2+\pi)r}{2}$. The area is
\[
A=2rh+\dfrac{\pi r^2}{2}=r\bigl(12-(2+\pi)r\bigr)+\dfrac{\pi r^2}{2}=12r-\dfrac{4+\pi}{2}\,r^2 .
\]
This is a quadratic in $r$ with negative leading coefficient. Completing the square,
\[
A=\dfrac{72}{4+\pi}-\dfrac{4+\pi}{2}\left(r-\dfrac{12}{4+\pi}\right)^2 ,
\]
so the area is largest when $r=\dfrac{12}{4+\pi}$, and then $A=\dfrac{72}{4+\pi}\approx10.08$ square feet. For this $r$,
\[
h=\dfrac{12-(2+\pi)\cdot\dfrac{12}{4+\pi}}{2}=\dfrac{12(4+\pi)-12(2+\pi)}{2(4+\pi)}=\dfrac{12}{4+\pi}=r .
\]
So the rectangle should be $\boxed{\dfrac{24}{4+\pi}\approx3.36\text{ ft wide and }\dfrac{12}{4+\pi}\approx1.68\text{ ft high}}$: its height equals the radius of the semicircle, half its width.

We show that every such midpoint $M$ lies on the circle with diameter $OP$.

Let $XY$ be a chord through $P$ with midpoint $M$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $M\ne O$, the segment $OM$ joins the centre to the midpoint of the chord. Since $OX=OY$, triangle $OXY$ is isosceles and its median $OM$ is also an altitude, so $OM\perp XY$. If moreover $M\ne P$, then $P$ lies on line $XY$, so $\angle OMP=90^\circ$, and by the converse of Thales' theorem $M$ lies on the circle with diameter $OP$. If $M=P$, then $M$ is an endpoint of that diameter, so again it lies on the circle.
\item If $M=O$, then $M$ is the other endpoint of the diameter $OP$, so it lies on the circle as well. (This happens for the chord through $P$ that is a diameter of $\omega$.)
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=3; pair O=(0,0), P=(2.0,0.9);
draw(circle(O,R),linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle((O+P)/2,abs(P-O)/2),red+dashed);
pair chordmid(real t, pen q){
  pair u=dir(t);
  real bb=dot(P,u), cc=dot(P,P)-R^2;
  pair X1=P+(-bb+sqrt(bb^2-cc))*u, X2=P+(-bb-sqrt(bb^2-cc))*u;
  draw(X1--X2,q);
  return (X1+X2)/2;
}
real[] angs={0,40,135,160};
for(real t:angs){ pair M0=chordmid(t,gray(0.5)); dot(M0,red); }
pair M=chordmid(70,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--M,blue);
real e=0.22; pair v1=e*unit(O-M), v2=e*unit(P-M);
draw(M+v1--M+v1+v2--M+v2,blue);
draw(O--P,dashed);
dot(M,red);dot(O);dot(P);
label("$O$",O,SW);label("$P$",P+0.32*dir(102));label("$M$",M+0.3*dir(318),red);label("$\omega$",R*dir(225),SW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So all the midpoints lie on the circle with diameter $OP$ (dashed in the figure). $\blacksquare$

(Conversely, every point $M$ of that circle is such a midpoint: the chord through $P$ perpendicular to $OM$ has midpoint $M$ (for $M=O$ take the diameter through $P$), so the locus is the whole circle with diameter $OP$. If $P=O$, all the chords through $P$ are diameters and all the midpoints coincide with $O$.)

question:
Courtney invests $\$40000$ at $6\%$ annual interest, compounded semiannually, so after $t$ years the account is worth $A=40000\left(1+\dfrac{0.06}{2}\right)^{2t}$ dollars.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Using this formula for real $t$, how many years does it take for the account to be worth $\$50000$? Round to the nearest hundredth.
\item[(b)] If interest is actually added only at the end of each half-year, after how many years is the balance first at least $\$50000$?
\end{enumerate}
An answer key gives $0.15$ years for (a). Is it right?

solution:
The claim is false. Completing the square ($a\ne0$),
\[
ax^2+bx+c=0\iff\left(x+\dfrac{b}{2a}\right)^2=\dfrac{b^2-4ac}{4a^2}.
\]
Let $\Delta=b^2-4ac$ be the discriminant. The right side has the sign of $\Delta$, so:
\begin{itemize}
\item if $\Delta>0$ there are \textbf{two} real solutions $x=\dfrac{-b\pm\sqrt{\Delta}}{2a}$; e.g.\ $x^2-1=0$ has $x=\pm1$;
\item if $\Delta=0$ there is exactly \textbf{one} real solution $x=-\dfrac{b}{2a}$; e.g.\ $x^2-2x+1=0$ has only $x=1$;
\item if $\Delta<0$ there are \textbf{no} real solutions, since a square cannot be negative; e.g.\ $x^2+1=0$.
\end{itemize}
So $x^2+1=0$ (or $x^2-2x+1=0$) is a counterexample. The statement becomes true only for complex solutions counted with multiplicity: then there are always exactly two.

\textbf{$QD=QB$.} Triangle $ABD$ has a right angle at $D$, and $Q$ is the midpoint of its hypotenuse $AB$, so $Q$ is the centre of the circle through $A,B,D$ and $QD=QB$. Hence triangle $QBD$ is isosceles and $\angle QDB=\angle QBD=70^\circ$.

\textbf{$QP\parallel AC$.} $Q$ and $P$ are the midpoints of $BA$ and $BC$, so $QP\parallel AC$ and $\angle QPB=\angle ACB=60^\circ$.

\textbf{Position of $D$.} $\angle BAC=50^\circ$. The side $AB$ (opposite the $60^\circ$ angle) is shorter than $AC$ (opposite the $70^\circ$ angle), and $BD^2-DC^2=AB^2-AC^2<0$, so $BD<DC$: the foot $D$ lies between $B$ and the midpoint $P$.

