In triangle $QDP$, $\angle QDP=180^\circ-\angle QDB=110^\circ$ and $\angle QPD=\angle QPB=60^\circ$. Therefore
\[
\angle DQP=180^\circ-110^\circ-60^\circ=\boxed{10^\circ}.
\]

question:
A committee of $4$ is selected at random from $7$ men and $6$ women. Find the probability that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] there are exactly $2$ women on the committee;
\item[(b)] there is at least one man and at least one woman on the committee.
\end{enumerate}

A box contains $15$ light bulbs, of which $5$ are defective. A sample of $3$ bulbs is selected at random. Find the probability that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] the sample contains exactly one defective bulb;
\item[(b)] the sample contains at least two defective bulbs.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that for every positive integer $n$, the number of digits of $2^n$ is $\lfloor n\log_{10}2\rfloor+1$.
\item[(b)] Using $0.3010<\log_{10}2<0.3011$, find the number of digits of $2^{2019}$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Treat the seats as points on a circle. Robert ($R$) and Bill ($B$) sit directly across from each other, so $RB$ is a diameter. Josh ($J$) is another point of the circle, so $\angle RJB$ is an angle in a semicircle and equals $90^\circ$ (Thales' theorem).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
