draw(circle(O,r),gray);
draw(R--B,dashed);
draw(R--J--B,linewidth(1));
pair u=unit(R-J),v=unit(B-J);
draw(J+0.9*u--J+0.9*(u+v)--J+0.9*v);
dot(O);dot(R);dot(B);dot(J);
label("$O$",O,S);label("$R$",R,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$J$",J,N);
label("$8$",(R+J)/2,NW);label("$15$",(J+B)/2,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

By the Pythagorean theorem in right triangle $RJB$,
\[
RB=\sqrt{8^2+15^2}=\sqrt{289}=\boxed{17}\text{ feet}.
\]

$E$ lies on line $AB$ (through $B$) and on line $CD$ (through $D$). These lines are perpendicular, so $\angle BED=90^\circ$ (when $E$ is different from $B$ and $D$). Similarly $F$ lies on line $BC$ (through $B$) and line $AD$ (through $D$), which are perpendicular, so $\angle BFD=90^\circ$.

A point $X$ with $\angle BXD=90^\circ$ lies on the circle with diameter $BD$ (converse of Thales' theorem: if $M$ is the midpoint of $BD$, then in right triangle $BXD$ the median $XM$ equals half the hypotenuse, so $MX=MB=MD$). Hence $E$ and $F$ both lie on the circle with diameter $BD$, and $B,E,F,D$ are concyclic. (If $E$ or $F$ coincides with $B$ or $D$, the statement is trivial.)

\begin{asy}
