\emph{Remark.} The conditions say that $B$ is the orthocentre of triangle $ACD$ ($AB\perp CD$ and $CB\perp AD$), and $E$, $F$ are the feet of the altitudes from $A$ and $C$; in particular $ABCD$ is not convex. The figure shows such a configuration.

An expression $|ax+b|$ changes formula only where $ax+b=0$, so the graph can bend only at $x=-\dfrac{1}{2}$ (where $2x+1=0$) and at $x=3$ (where $x-3=0$). Between these points we can remove the absolute values:
\begin{itemize}
\item $x\le-\dfrac{1}{2}$: $y=3(-2x-1)-(3-x)=-5x-6$, slope $-5$;
\item $-\dfrac{1}{2}\le x\le3$: $y=3(2x+1)-(3-x)=7x$, slope $7$;
\item $x\ge3$: $y=3(2x+1)-(x-3)=5x+6$, slope $5$.
\end{itemize}
The graph decreases until $x=-\dfrac{1}{2}$ and increases afterwards, so its lowest point is at $x=-\dfrac{1}{2}$, where $y=7\cdot\left(-\dfrac{1}{2}\right)=-\dfrac{7}{2}$. The vertex is $\boxed{\left(-\dfrac{1}{2},-\dfrac{7}{2}\right)}$.

In general, a sum of multiples of absolute values of linear expressions is piecewise linear, with possible corners only at the zeros of the expressions inside the absolute values. Compute $y$ at each such point and the slopes between them; a minimum (or maximum) occurs at a corner where the slope changes sign. (At $x=3$ the slope changes from $7$ to $5$, so that corner is not a vertex in this sense.)

