(In the figure, the red dot $(3,2)$ and the gray dot $(6,4)$ lie on the same dashed line; only $(3,2)$ is counted.)
\begin{itemize}
\item On the axes: only $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$, giving $2$ lines.
\item With $1\le a,b\le7$: for each $a$, the number of $b\le a$ coprime to $a$ is $\varphi(a)$, and $\varphi(1)+\cdots+\varphi(7)=1+1+2+2+4+2+6=18$. This counts pairs with $b\le a$; by symmetry the pairs with $a\le b$ also number $18$, and the pair $(1,1)$ is counted twice. So there are $2\cdot18-1=35$ such dots.
\end{itemize}
The total is $2+35=\boxed{37}$ lines.

question:
Let $ABC$ be an equilateral triangle with side $s$. Draw the altitude from $A$, meeting $BC$ at $D$. From $D$ draw the perpendicular to $AC$, meeting it at $E$; from $E$ the perpendicular to $BC$, meeting it at $F$; from $F$ the perpendicular to $AC$; and so on forever, zigzagging between $AC$ and $BC$ towards $C$. Do the same on the other side: from $D$ draw the perpendicular to $AB$, then to $BC$, then to $AB$, and so on, towards $B$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the total length of all the segments drawn (including $AD$).
\item[(b)] A student claims that this total length always equals the area of the triangle. Is that true? For which side length are the two numbers equal?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) The altitude is $AD=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s$, and $D$ is the midpoint of $BC$, so $DC=\dfrac {s}{2}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=6; pair B=(-s/2,0),C=(s/2,0),A=(0,s*sqrt(3)/2);
pair ft(pair P,pair U,pair V){return U+dot(P-U,unit(V-U))*unit(V-U);}
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
pair D=(0,0);
draw(A--D,red+linewidth(0.9));
pair P=D; pair Q;
for(int k=0;k<7;++k){ if(k%2==0) Q=ft(P,A,C); else Q=ft(P,B,C); draw(P--Q,blue); P=Q; }
P=D;
for(int k=0;k<7;++k){ if(k%2==0) Q=ft(P,A,B); else Q=ft(P,B,C); draw(P--Q,blue); P=Q; }
pair E=ft(D,A,C),F=ft(E,B,C);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$D$",D,S);label("$E$",E,NE);label("$F$",F,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Take a point $X$ on side $AC$ or $BC$ at distance $c$ from $C$, and drop the perpendicular $XY$ to the other side through $C$. Triangle $XYC$ has a right angle at $Y$ and a $60^\circ$ angle at $C$, so it is a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle:
\[
XY=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}c,\qquad YC=\dfrac{c}{2}.
\]
Starting from $D$ with $DC=\dfrac {s}{2}$, the distances to $C$ of the successive feet are $\dfrac {s}{4},\dfrac {s}{8},\dots$, so the segments on the right have lengths $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\cdot\dfrac {s}{2},\ \dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\cdot\dfrac {s}{4},\ \dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\cdot\dfrac {s}{8},\dots$ with sum
\[
\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\left(\dfrac {s}{2}+\dfrac {s}{4}+\dfrac {s}{8}+\cdots\right)=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s .
\]
By symmetry the zigzag towards $B$ has the same total. Altogether the length is
\[
\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s+2\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s=\boxed{\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}s}.
\]

(b) The area is $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2$. The claim is false in general (a length cannot equal an area for every size: doubling $s$ doubles the length but quadruples the area). The numbers are equal when $\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}s=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2$, i.e.\ only for side length $\boxed{s=6}$, where both equal $9\sqrt3$.

Let $BC=CD=DA=x$ and $\alpha=\angle CAB$.

Since $CD\parallel AB$, $\angle DCA=\angle CAB=\alpha$ (alternate angles). Since $DA=DC$, triangle $ACD$ is isosceles and $\angle DAC=\angle DCA=\alpha$. So $\angle DAB=2\alpha$.

The legs $AD$ and $BC$ are equal, so the trapezoid is isosceles and its base angles are equal: $\angle ABC=\angle DAB=2\alpha$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(10,0),C=(10-5*cos(pi/3),5*sin(pi/3)),D=(5*cos(pi/3),5*sin(pi/3));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(A--C,dashed);
pair u=unit(A-C),v=unit(B-C); real s=0.45;
draw(C+s*u--C+s*(u+v)--C+s*v);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$10$",(A+B)/2,S);label("$x$",(B+C)/2,E);label("$x$",(C+D)/2,N);label("$x$",(A+D)/2,W);
draw(arc(A,1.1,0,30));draw(arc(A,1.25,30,60));
label("$\alpha$",A+1.75*dir(15));label("$\alpha$",A+1.75*dir(45));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

In triangle $ABC$, $\angle ACB=90^\circ$, so $\alpha+2\alpha=90^\circ$, giving $\alpha=30^\circ$ and $\angle ABC=60^\circ$. Then $x=BC=AB\cos60^\circ=5$ (triangle $ABC$ is a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle with hypotenuse $10$). The perimeter is $10+3\cdot5=\boxed{25}$ cm.

Taking square roots, $x^2-x=12$ or $x^2-x=-12$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $x^2-x-12=0$ factors as $(x-4)(x+3)=0$, so $x=4$ or $x=-3$.
\item $x^2-x+12=0$ has discriminant $1-48<0$, so no real (hence no integer) solutions.
\end{itemize}
The integer solutions are $\boxed{x=4\text{ and }x=-3}$.

Multiply by $(x-1)(x-2)(x+1)$:
\[
5-7x=a(x-2)(x+1)+b(x-1)(x+1)+c(x-1)(x-2),
\]
a polynomial identity. Substitute the roots:
\begin{itemize}
\item $x=1$: $-2=a(-1)(2)$, so $a=1$;
\item $x=2$: $-9=b(1)(3)$, so $b=-3$;
\item $x=-1$: $12=c(-2)(-3)$, so $c=2$.
\end{itemize}
Hence $abc=1\cdot(-3)\cdot2=\boxed{-6}$.

There are $\dbinom{11}{4}=330$ sets of four points. A set fails exactly when it contains at least three of the $5$ collinear points:
\begin{itemize}
\item exactly $3$ of them and $1$ of the other $6$ points: $\dbinom53\cdot6=60$ sets;
\item all $4$ from the line: $\dbinom54=5$ sets.
\end{itemize}
So the answer is $330-60-5=\boxed{265}$.

Put the starting point at the origin, with east the positive $x$-direction and north the positive $y$-direction. The man ends at $(0-100+30,\,40)=(-70,40)$ and the wife at $(60,-20)$.

