By the distance formula, the distance between them is
\[
\sqrt{(60-(-70))^2+(-20-40)^2}=\sqrt{130^2+60^2}=\sqrt{20500}=10\sqrt{205}.
\]
Half of it is $\boxed{5\sqrt{205}\approx71.6}$ meters.

\textbf{The formula $R=\dfrac{abc}{4K}$.} Let $K$ be the area, let $AH$ be the altitude from $A$ to $BC$, and let $AD$ be the diameter of the circumcircle through $A$. In the figure below, triangles $ABH$ and $ADC$ are similar: $\angle AHB=90^\circ$, $\angle ACD=90^\circ$ (an angle in a semicircle), and $\angle ABH=\angle ABC=\angle ADC$ (inscribed angles on the same arc $AC$). Hence
\[
\dfrac{AB}{AD}=\dfrac{AH}{AC}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad 2R=AD=\dfrac{AB\cdot AC}{AH}=\dfrac{AB\cdot AC\cdot BC}{2K},
\]
using $AH=\dfrac{2K}{BC}$. So $R=\dfrac{abc}{4K}$.

\textbf{The $10$-$17$-$21$ triangle.} By Heron's formula with $s=\dfrac{10+17+21}{2}=24$,
\[
K=\sqrt{24\cdot14\cdot7\cdot3}=\sqrt{7056}=84 .
\]
(Directly: with $BC=21$, $AB=10$, $AC=17$, the altitude $AH=\dfrac{2\cdot84}{21}=8$ splits $BC$ into $BH=6$ and $HC=15$, giving the right triangles $6$-$8$-$10$ and $8$-$15$-$17$.)

\begin{asy}
