\end{asy}

Therefore
\[
R=\dfrac{10\cdot17\cdot21}{4\cdot84}=\dfrac{3570}{336}=\boxed{\dfrac{85}{8}}.
\]
Here the angle at $A$ is obtuse ($21^2>10^2+17^2$), so the centre $O$ lies outside the triangle, beyond $BC$; the similarity argument works in the same way.

Put $A=(0,0)$, $B=(s,0)$, $C=(s,s)$, $D=(0,s)$ and $E=(p,q)$, and let $\rho$ denote the rotation by $90^\circ$ counterclockwise, $\rho(x,y)=(-y,x)$.

In the counterclockwise square $BHIE$, the side $EI$ is obtained by rotating the side $EB$ by $90^\circ$ counterclockwise about $E$, so
\[
I=E+\rho(B-E)=E+\rho(B)-\rho(E)=E+D-\rho(E),
\]
because $\rho(B)=\rho(s,0)=(0,s)=D$. Therefore
\[
I-D=E-\rho(E)=(p,q)-(-q,p)=(p+q,\ q-p),
\]
and
\[
DI^2=(p+q)^2+(q-p)^2=2(p^2+q^2)=2\,AE^2 .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(4,0),C=(4,4),D=(0,4),Ep=(1.2,2.2);
pair rot(pair v){return (-v.y,v.x);}
pair I=Ep+rot(B-Ep),H=B+(I-Ep),G=Ep+rot(D-Ep),F=D+(G-Ep);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(B--H--I--Ep--cycle,blue);
draw(F--G--Ep--D--cycle,heavygreen);
draw(A--Ep,red+linewidth(1));draw(D--I,red+linewidth(1));
dot(Ep);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$E$",Ep,2W);label("$H$",H,E);label("$I$",I,N);label("$F$",F,W);label("$G$",G,SW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So $DI=\sqrt2\,AE$ for every position of $E$, and $AE:DI=\boxed{1:\sqrt2}$. (In fact $\overrightarrow{DI}$ is $\overrightarrow{AE}$ rotated by $45^\circ$ clockwise and enlarged by $\sqrt2$. The square $FGED$ is not needed for this ratio.)

The sum of the first $n$ odd numbers is $n^2$, and $n^2\mid n!$ exactly when $n\mid(n-1)!$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If $n$ is prime, $n$ does not divide $(n-1)!$, whose factors are all smaller than $n$.
\item If $n$ is composite and $n\ge50$, write $n=ab$ with $1<a\le b<n$. If $a<b$, both $a$ and $b$ are different factors of $(n-1)!$, so $n=ab$ divides it. If $a=b$, then $n=a^2$ with $a\ge8$, and $a$ and $2a$ are different numbers less than $n$, so $2a^2$ divides $(n-1)!$.
\end{itemize}
So we count the composite numbers from $50$ to $99$. There are $50$ numbers, and the primes among them are $53,59,61,67,71,73,79,83,89,97$, ten in all. The answer is $50-10=\boxed{40}$.

A point inside an angle is equidistant from its two sides exactly when it lies on the bisector of the angle.

The ray $BA$ has direction $A-B=(3,3)$ and the ray $BC$ has direction $C-B=(-4,4)$. Their unit vectors are $\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(1,1)$ and $\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(-1,1)$, whose sum $(0,\sqrt2)$ points straight up. So the bisector of $\angle ABC$ is the vertical ray $x=2$, $y>0$ from $B$. (Indeed $BA$ makes a $45^\circ$ angle with the vertical on one side and $BC$ makes a $45^\circ$ angle on the other side, so $\angle ABC=90^\circ$.)

The point $D(x,2x)$ lies on this ray when $x=2$, giving $D=(2,4)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(2,0),A=(5,3),C=(-2,4),D=(2,4);
pair F1=(4,2),F2=(0,2);
draw((-3.2,0)--(6.2,0),gray(0.6),Arrow(TeXHead));draw((0,-0.8)--(0,5.4),gray(0.6),Arrow(TeXHead));
label("$x$",(6.2,0),S,gray(0.4));label("$y$",(0,5.4),W,gray(0.4));
draw(B--(5.6,3.6),linewidth(1));draw(B--(-2.6,4.6),linewidth(1));
draw((2,-0.6)--(2,5.2),dashed+blue);
draw(D--F1,red);draw(D--F2,red);
path sq1=F1+0.25*dir(135)--F1+0.25*dir(135)+0.25*dir(225)--F1+0.25*dir(225);
path sq2=F2+0.25*dir(45)--F2+0.25*dir(45)+0.25*dir(315)--F2+0.25*dir(315);
draw(sq1,red);draw(sq2,red);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(F1);dot(F2);
label("$A(5,3)$",A,SE);label("$B(2,0)$",B,SE);label("$C(-2,4)$",C,W);label("$D(2,4)$",D,NE);
label("$x=2$",(2,5.2),E,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Check with distances: line $BA$ is $x-y-2=0$ and line $BC$ is $x+y-2=0$. The distances from $(2,4)$ are $\dfrac{|2-4-2|}{\sqrt2}=2\sqrt2$ and $\dfrac{|2+4-2|}{\sqrt2}=2\sqrt2$, and the feet of the perpendiculars, $(4,2)$ and $(0,2)$, lie on the two rays. Hence $x=\boxed{2}$.

(The other line of points equidistant from the two \emph{lines} is $y=0$, which meets $y=2x$ at the origin; but the origin lies outside the angle and is not equidistant from the two rays.)

question:
Partial fraction decomposition rewrites a fraction whose denominator is a product of distinct linear factors as a sum of simpler fractions.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find constants $A$ and $B$ such that $\dfrac{5x+1}{(x-1)(x+2)}=\dfrac{A}{x-1}+\dfrac{B}{x+2}$ for all $x\ne1,-2$.
\item[(b)] Find constants $C$ and $D$ such that $\dfrac{2}{x^2-1}=\dfrac{C}{x-1}+\dfrac{D}{x+1}$ for all $x\ne\pm1$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Solve for $y$: $3y=-4x+8$, so $y=-\dfrac{4}{3}x+\dfrac{8}{3}$. In slope-intercept form the coefficient of $x$ is the slope, so the slope is $\boxed{-\dfrac{4}{3}}$: moving $3$ units to the right, the line goes $4$ units down, for example from $\left(0,\dfrac{8}{3}\right)$ to $\left(3,-\dfrac{4}{3}\right)$.

