question:
In a MATHCOUNTS state competition there are $30$ Sprint problems worth $1$ point each and $8$ Target problems worth $2$ points each. A student believed he had solved every problem correctly except Sprint \#25, which he left blank. In fact four of his answers were wrong:
\begin{itemize}
\item Sprint \#29 (he read ``more than $4$'' as ``$4$ or more''),
\item Sprint \#30 (he gave the diameter instead of the radius),
\item Target \#5 (he rounded to four decimal places instead of three),
\item Target \#8 (he started a sequence at $a_0$ instead of $a_1$).
\end{itemize}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What score did he expect to get?
\item[(b)] What score did he actually get?
\item[(c)] Exactly the four highest scorers qualify for the national competition, and the fourth-highest score was $40$. Did he qualify?
\end{enumerate}

Let $s_n$ be the $n$th partial sum of the series $1-1+1-1+\cdots$, so $s_n=1$ for odd $n$ and $s_n=0$ for even $n$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that the sequence $s_1,s_2,s_3,\dots$ has no limit, so the series has no sum in the ordinary sense.
\item[(b)] Show that the averages $\sigma_n=\dfrac{s_1+s_2+\cdots+s_n}{n}$ converge to $\dfrac{1}{2}$.
\item[(c)] (Thomson's lamp) A lamp is switched on at time $0$ minutes, off at time $1$, on at time $1+\dfrac{1}{2}$, off at time $1+\dfrac{1}{2}+\dfrac{1}{4}$, and so on, each waiting time being half of the previous one. Show that all the switchings happen before time $2$, and that the state of the lamp at times just before $2$ does not settle down. Is the state of the lamp at time $2$ determined by these rules?
\end{enumerate}

A staircase has $10$ steps. You climb it taking either $1$ or $2$ steps at a time. Let $a_n$ be the number of ways to climb a staircase of $n$ steps (so the order of the moves matters).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why $a_n=a_{n-1}+a_{n-2}$ for $n\ge3$, and find $a_1$ and $a_2$.
\item[(b)] Find $a_{10}$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
From $3x+5y=235$, $3x\equiv235\equiv0\pmod5$, so $x=5t$ and $y=47-3t$. Positivity gives $1\le t\le15$.

The second equation gives $k=\dfrac{14x-94}{y}=\dfrac{70t-94}{47-3t}$, which must be a positive integer. We need $70t>94$, so $t\ge2$. Check $t=2,\dots,15$, where $y=47-3t$ runs through $41,38,35,32,29,26,23,20,17,14,11,8,5,2$ and $70t-94$ through $46,116,186,256,326,396,466,536,606,676,746,816,886,956$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $t=5$: $\dfrac{256}{32}=8$ \checkmark ($x=25$, $y=32$, $x+y=57$);
\item $t=13$: $\dfrac{816}{8}=102$ \checkmark ($x=65$, $y=8$, $x+y=73$);
\item $t=15$: $\dfrac{956}{2}=478$ \checkmark ($x=75$, $y=2$, $x+y=77$);
\item every other $t$ fails (for example $t=4$: $\dfrac{186}{35}$; $t=9$: $\dfrac{536}{20}$; $t=14$: $\dfrac{886}{5}$).
\end{itemize}
The possible values of $x+y$ are $57$, $73$, $77$, with sum $\boxed{207}$.

question:
Let $X=\underbrace{99\cdots9}_{94}$ and $Y=\underbrace{44\cdots4}_{94}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the sum of the digits of $XY$.
\item[(b)] A student argues: ``$XY$ is the same as $94$ copies of $36$ (since $9\cdot4=36$), so the digit sum is $94\cdot(3+6)=846$.'' Is $XY$ equal to the number $3636\cdots36$? Prove that nevertheless, for every positive integer $n$ and every integer $m$ with $1\le m\le10^n$, the digit sum of $m(10^n-1)$ is $9n$, and explain why this makes the student's answer correct.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the sum of all positive integers $x$ for which there is an integer $y$ with $x^2+y^2=144$.
\item[(b)] Find all pairs $(x,y)$ of negative integers with $x^2-(x+y)=256$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{Upper bound.} Colour the table like a chessboard. Consecutive numbers are in adjacent squares, which have different colours, so all odd numbers lie on squares of one colour and all even numbers on squares of the other colour. Each row has $4$ squares of each colour, so a row contains at most $4$ odd numbers. The only even prime is $2$. Hence a row contains at most $4+1=5$ primes.

\textbf{Construction.} The following filling (the path $1,2,\dots,64$ is drawn in blue) has the $5$ primes $2,3,61,59,47$ in its top row:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int[][] g={{2,3,64,61,60,59,48,47},{1,4,63,62,57,58,49,46},{6,5,30,31,56,55,50,45},{7,28,29,32,33,54,51,44},{8,27,36,35,34,53,52,43},{9,26,37,38,39,40,41,42},{10,25,24,23,22,21,20,19},{11,12,13,14,15,16,17,18}};
int[] pr={2,3,61,59,47};
for(int c=0;c<8;++c){bool p=false;for(int q: pr) if(g[0][c]==q) p=true; if(p) fill((c,7)--(c+1,7)--(c+1,8)--(c,8)--cycle,lightgray);}
for(int i=0;i<=8;++i){draw((0,i)--(8,i),gray(0.5));draw((i,0)--(i,8),gray(0.5));}
pair[] pts;
for(int v=1;v<=64;++v) for(int r=0;r<8;++r) for(int c=0;c<8;++c) if(g[r][c]==v) pts.push((c+0.5,7.5-r));
path pth=pts[0];for(int k=1;k<64;++k) pth=pth--pts[k];
draw(pth,blue+opacity(0.35)+linewidth(2));
for(int r=0;r<8;++r) for(int c=0;c<8;++c){fill(circle((c+0.5,7.5-r),0.3),(r==0 && (c==0||c==1||c==3||c==5||c==7)) ? lightgray : white);label("$"+string(g[r][c])+"$",(c+0.5,7.5-r),fontsize(9pt));}
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So the maximum is $\boxed{5}$.

Given the totals, every choice of which $5$ of the $8$ tosses are heads is equally likely: $\binom85=56$ cases.
\begin{itemize}
\item Sara has exactly $3$ heads: then Kaleb has $2$. Count: $\binom53\binom32=10\cdot3=30$.
\item Kaleb has exactly $3$ heads: then Sara has $2$. Count: $\binom33\binom52=10$.
\end{itemize}
These cases are disjoint, so the probability is $\dfrac{30+10}{56}=\boxed{\dfrac{5}{7}}$.

\textbf{The locus.} By the inscribed angle theorem, the points $C$ on one side of $AB$ with $\angle ACB=120^\circ$ form an arc of a circle through $A$ and $B$; since the inscribed angle is $120^\circ$, the arc $AB$ \emph{not} containing $C$ measures $240^\circ$, so $C$ runs over the minor arc, which subtends a central angle of $120^\circ$. By the law of sines the radius is
\[
R=\dfrac{AB}{2\sin120^\circ}=\dfrac{4}{\sqrt3},
\]
and the centre $O_1$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$, on the opposite side from the arc, at distance $R\cos60^\circ=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt3}$ from $AB$. Reflecting in $AB$ gives the second arc, with centre $O_2$. The locus is the two arcs (without $A$ and $B$), which together enclose a lens-shaped region.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=4/sqrt(3),d=2/sqrt(3);
pair A=(-2,0),B=(2,0),O1=(0,-d),O2=(0,d);
path up=arc(O1,R,30,150),dn=arc(O2,R,210,330);
fill(up--reverse(dn)--cycle,paleblue);
draw(circle(O1,R),dashed+gray(0.6));
draw(up,linewidth(1)+blue);draw(dn,linewidth(1)+blue);
draw(A--B);
pair C=O1+R*dir(115);
draw(A--C--B,red);
draw(arc(C,0.35,degrees(B-C),degrees(A-C)),red);
draw(O1--B,dashed);draw(O1--A,dashed);
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C,red);dot(O1);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,N,red);
label("$120^\circ$",C+0.6*dir(-90),red+fontsize(8pt));
label("$O_1$",O1,S);
label("$R$",(O1+B)/2,NW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
