\textbf{Count.}
\begin{itemize}
\item Vertices: $4$ outer and $4$ inner corners on the top, and the same on the bottom: $V=16$.
\item Edges: on the top, $4$ outer edges, $4$ inner edges and $4$ connecting segments ($12$); the same on the bottom ($12$); and $4$ outer and $4$ inner vertical edges ($8$). So $E=32$.
\item Faces: $4$ trapezoids on top, $4$ on the bottom, $4$ outer rectangles and $4$ inner rectangles: $F=16$.
\end{itemize}
Hence
\[
V-E+F=16-32+16=\boxed{0}\ne2 .
\]

\textbf{Why.} Non-convexity is not the reason: Euler's formula still holds for many non-convex polyhedra, for example a cube with a smaller cube glued onto one face, or an L-shaped prism, because their surfaces can be continuously deformed into a sphere. The frame has a hole through it: its surface is a torus, not a sphere. In general a polyhedron whose surface is a sphere with $g$ handles (holes) has $V-E+F=2-2g$; the frame has $g=1$.

Suppose we unlock the box, $s$ suitcases and $b$ piggy banks, using $1+s+b$ locks. In each opened suitcase at most one bank holds $30$ coins and at most one holds $20$; so the coins available are at most $30\min(b,s)+20\min(b-s,s)+10(b-2s)$ (with negative terms read as $0$). Suppose $s+b\le18$:
\begin{itemize}
\item if $b\le s$: then $b\le9$ and the coins are at most $30b\le270$;
\item if $s<b\le2s$: the coins are at most $30s+20(b-s)=10(s+b)+10b\le180+10b$, and $b\le\min(2s,18-s)\le12$, so at most $300$;
\item if $b>2s$: then $3s<s+b\le18$, so $s\le5$ and the coins are at most $60s\le300$.
\end{itemize}
In every case fewer than $310$ coins are available, so $s+b\ge19$ and at least $20$ locks are needed.

Twenty locks suffice: open the box, $7$ suitcases, the $30$-coin bank in each of them and the $20$-coin bank in $5$ of them: $1+7+12=20$ locks and $7\cdot30+5\cdot20=310$ coins. The minimum is $\boxed{20}$.

