In a right triangle with acute angles $x$ and $90^\circ-x$, the side opposite one angle is the side adjacent to the other, so $\tan(90^\circ-x)=\dfrac{1}{\tan x}$. Hence $\tan x=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}$. In the $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle with sides $1,\sqrt3,2$, the angle opposite the side $1$ has tangent $\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}$, so $x=\boxed{30^\circ}$ (the only solution in $(0^\circ,90^\circ)$, since $\tan$ is increasing there).

