In triangle $ABC$, points $M$ and $N$ lie on side $AB$ so that $AM=AC$ and $BN=BC$, and $\angle MCN=50^\circ$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find $\angle ACB$.
\item[(b)] Points $P$ on $BC$ and $Q$ on $AC$ satisfy $MP\parallel AC$ and $NQ\parallel BC$. Find the angles of triangle $PQC$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Write $\alpha,\beta,\gamma$ for the angles at $A,B,C$ and $a=BC$, $b=CA$, $c=AB$. Since $AM=b<c$ and $BN=a<c$, both points lie on segment $AB$, and $AN=c-a<b=AM$ (triangle inequality), so the order is $A,N,M,B$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real Aa=60,Bb=40,Cc=80;
real a=Sin(Aa),b=Sin(Bb),c=Sin(Cc);
pair A=(0,0),B=(c,0),C=b*dir(Aa);
pair M=(b,0),Nn=(c-a,0);
pair P=B+((c-b)/c)*(C-B),Q=A+((c-a)/c)*(C-A);
fill(P--Q--C--cycle,paleyellow);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(C--M,blue);draw(C--Nn,blue);
draw(M--P,red);draw(Nn--Q,red);draw(P--Q,dashed);
draw(arc(C,0.13,degrees(Nn-C),degrees(M-C)),blue);
label("$50^\circ$",C+0.21*dir((degrees(Nn-C)+degrees(M-C))/2),blue+fontsize(8pt));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(M);dot(Nn);dot(P);dot(Q);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);
label("$N$",Nn,S);label("$M$",M,S);label("$P$",P,NE);label("$Q$",Q,NW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(a) Triangle $AMC$ is isosceles with apex $A$, so $\angle ACM=\dfrac{180^\circ-\alpha}{2}$. Triangle $BNC$ is isosceles with apex $B$, so $\angle BCN=\dfrac{180^\circ-\beta}{2}$. The angles $\angle ACM$ and $\angle BCN$ together cover $\angle ACB$ once and $\angle NCM$ twice, so
\[
\angle MCN=\angle ACM+\angle BCN-\gamma=180^\circ-\dfrac{\alpha+\beta}{2}-\gamma=180^\circ-\dfrac{180^\circ-\gamma}{2}-\gamma=90^\circ-\dfrac{\gamma}{2}.
\]
From $90^\circ-\dfrac{\gamma}{2}=50^\circ$ we get $\angle ACB=\boxed{80^\circ}$.

(b) Since $MP\parallel AC$, triangles $BMP$ and $BAC$ are similar, so $\dfrac{BP}{BC}=\dfrac{BM}{BA}=\dfrac{c-b}{c}$ and
\[
CP=a-\dfrac{a(c-b)}{c}=\dfrac{ab}{c}.
\]
Since $NQ\parallel BC$, triangles $ANQ$ and $ABC$ are similar, so $\dfrac{AQ}{AC}=\dfrac{AN}{AB}=\dfrac{c-a}{c}$ and
\[
CQ=b-\dfrac{b(c-a)}{c}=\dfrac{ab}{c}.
\]
So $CP=CQ$: triangle $PQC$ is isosceles with apex angle $\angle C=80^\circ$, and its base angles are $\dfrac{180^\circ-80^\circ}{2}=50^\circ$. The angles of triangle $PQC$ are $\boxed{\angle C=80^\circ,\ \angle P=\angle Q=50^\circ}$.

question:
Consider the parabolas $y=ax^2+bx+c$ with $a\ne0$. Prove the following statements about the vertex.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If $a$ and $b$ are fixed and $c$ varies, every vertex lies on the line $x=-\dfrac{b}{2a}$.
\item[(b)] If $a$ and $c$ are fixed and $b$ varies, every vertex lies on the parabola $y=-ax^2+c$.
\item[(c)] If $b$ and $c$ are fixed and $a$ varies, every vertex lies on the line $y=\dfrac{b}{2}x+c$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Completing the square,
\[
ax^2+bx+c=a\left(x+\dfrac{b}{2a}\right)^2+c-\dfrac{b^2}{4a},
\]
so the vertex is $(h,k)$ with $h=-\dfrac{b}{2a}$ and $k=c-\dfrac{b^2}{4a}$.

(a) $h=-\dfrac{b}{2a}$ does not depend on $c$, so all vertices lie on the vertical line $x=-\dfrac{b}{2a}$.

(b) From $h=-\dfrac{b}{2a}$ we get $b=-2ah$, so $k=c-\dfrac{4a^2h^2}{4a}=c-ah^2$. Thus $(h,k)$ lies on $y=-ax^2+c$. (Conversely every point of this parabola is a vertex: take $b=-2ah$.) The figure shows the case $a=c=1$, $b=-4,-2,0,2,4$.

