\textbf{Possible shapes.} The rectangle has four corners and each corner is covered by one of the three squares, so some square covers two corners. It cannot cover two opposite corners (it would then be the whole rectangle), so it covers two adjacent corners and its side is a full side of the rectangle, say the side of length $w$. The rest is a $w\times(L-w)$ rectangle covered by the other two squares. By the same argument one of these two squares spans a full side of that rectangle and the remaining part is a single square; so the two squares are equal, with side $s$, placed side by side, and the small rectangle is $s\times2s$. Hence either
\begin{itemize}
\item $w=2s$: a $2s$ square next to two $s$ squares, giving a $3s\times2s$ rectangle of area $6s^2$; or
\item $w=s$: three $s$ squares in a row, giving a $3s\times s$ rectangle of area $3s^2$.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Area 96.} $3s^2=96$ gives $s^2=32$, not the square of an integer. $6s^2=96$ gives $s=4$: squares of sides $8,4,4$ forming a $12\times8$ rectangle.

