Let $s=\sqrt{57}-\sqrt2\approx6.1356$ be the hypotenuse and $u,v$ the legs, with $u^2+v^2=s^2$ and perimeter $P=s+u+v$.

\textbf{Which perimeters are possible.} Put $m=u+v$. Positive legs with $u^2+v^2=s^2$ and $u+v=m$ exist exactly when $s<m\le s\sqrt2$: we need $m^2>u^2+v^2=s^2$, and $m^2=(u+v)^2\le2(u^2+v^2)=2s^2$, with $u,v$ the roots of $z^2-mz+\dfrac{m^2-s^2}{2}=0$, which are real and positive in that range. So
\[
2s<P\le(1+\sqrt2)s,\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad 12.27\ldots<P\le14.81\ldots
\]
(using $\sqrt{57}\approx7.5498$ and $\sqrt2\approx1.4142$). The least integer perimeter is $P=13$.

