So in triangle $XBC$,
\[
\angle XBC=\dfrac{180^\circ-\beta}{2},\qquad\angle XCB=\dfrac{180^\circ-\gamma}{2}.
\]
By the angle sum in triangle $XBC$,
\[
\angle BXC=180^\circ-\dfrac{180^\circ-\beta}{2}-\dfrac{180^\circ-\gamma}{2}=\dfrac{\beta+\gamma}{2}=\dfrac{180^\circ-\alpha}{2}=90^\circ-\dfrac{\alpha}{2}.\qquad\blacksquare
\]
($X$ is the excentre opposite $A$; the internal bisector from $A$ also passes through it.)

question:
Let $f$ be a polynomial with real coefficients and positive leading coefficient, of degree at least $1$, and let $c>0$. Divide $f(x)$ by $x-c$: $f(x)=(x-c)q(x)+r$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that if all coefficients of $q$ and the remainder $r$ are nonnegative, then $f$ has no real root greater than $c$ (so $c$ is an upper bound for the real roots).
\item[(b)] Show by an example that the condition $c>0$ cannot be dropped.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Put $u=x-\dfrac{1}{2}$. The allowed region is $D=\{(u,y):0\le y\le3-u^2\}$, which is convex; it is bounded by the chord from $u=-\sqrt3$ to $u=\sqrt3$ on the $x$-axis and the parabolic arc above it.

\textbf{Vertices may be taken on the arc.} Fix two vertices; the area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot(\text{base})\cdot(\text{distance of the third vertex to the line through the base})$, and the distance to a line is a convex function of the point. A convex function on the convex region $D$ is maximised at an extreme point of $D$, and the extreme points of $D$ are exactly the points of the arc $y=3-u^2$, $|u|\le\sqrt3$ (including its endpoints on the $x$-axis). Moving the vertices one at a time, we may assume all three vertices lie on the arc.

\textbf{Area of a triangle on the parabola.} For vertices $(u_i,3-u_i^2)$, $i=1,2,3$, with $u_1<u_2<u_3$, the shoelace formula gives
\[
\text{area}=\dfrac{1}{2}\left|\det\begin{pmatrix}u_2-u_1&u_1^2-u_2^2\\u_3-u_1&u_1^2-u_3^2\end{pmatrix}\right|=\dfrac{1}{2}(u_2-u_1)(u_3-u_2)(u_3-u_1).
\]
For fixed $u_2$, this increases when $u_1$ decreases or $u_3$ increases, so take $u_1=-\sqrt3$, $u_3=\sqrt3$. With $p=u_2+\sqrt3$ and $q=\sqrt3-u_2$ we have $p+q=2\sqrt3$ and, by AM-GM, $pq\le3$, so
\[
\text{area}\le\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot3\cdot2\sqrt3=3\sqrt3,
\]
with equality for $u_2=0$: the triangle with vertices $\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\pm\sqrt3,0\right)$ and the vertex $\left(\dfrac{1}{2},3\right)$ of the parabola.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real h=0.5,r3=sqrt(3);
real f(real x){return 3-(x-h)^2;}
path par=graph(f,h-r3,h+r3);
fill(par--cycle,paleblue);
draw((-1.8,0)--(2.8,0),gray(0.6),Arrow(TeXHead));draw((0,-0.5)--(0,3.6),gray(0.6),Arrow(TeXHead));
label("$x$",(2.8,0),E,gray(0.4));label("$y$",(0,3.6),W,gray(0.4));
draw(par,blue+linewidth(1));
pair L=(h-r3,0),R=(h+r3,0),V=(h,3);
draw(L--V--R--cycle,red+linewidth(1));
dot(L);dot(R);dot(V);
label("$(\dfrac{1}{2}-\sqrt{3},0)$",L,S);label("$(\dfrac{1}{2}+\sqrt{3},0)$",R,S);label("$(\dfrac{1}{2},3)$",V,N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The greatest area is $\boxed{3\sqrt3}$ (base $2\sqrt3$, height $3$).

Let $x$ be the side parallel to the barn and $y$ each of the two sides perpendicular to it. The fencing gives $x+2y=200$, so the area is
\[
A=xy=(200-2y)y=2y(100-y).
\]
By AM-GM, $y(100-y)\le\left(\dfrac{y+(100-y)}{2}\right)^2=2500$, with equality when $y=100-y$, i.e.\ $y=50$. So $A\le5000$, attained at $y=50$, $x=100$.

