Write $A,B,C$ for the angles of the triangle and let $CL$ be the bisector of the interior angle at $C$, with $L$ on $AB$.

\textbf{The angle at $E$.} Triangle $CDE$ has a right angle at $D$ and hypotenuse $CE=2\,CD$, so it is a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle: $\angle CED=30^\circ$ and $\angle DCE=60^\circ$.

\textbf{The angle between the altitude and the bisector.} In right triangle $ADC$, $\angle ACD=90^\circ-A$, while $\angle ACL=\dfrac {C}{2}=90^\circ-\dfrac{A+B}{2}$. Since $A>B$, we have $\angle ACD<\angle ACL$, so $D$ lies between $A$ and $L$, and
\[
\angle DCL=\angle ACL-\angle ACD=A-\dfrac{A+B}{2}=\dfrac{A-B}{2}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real Ad=80,Bd=20;
real b=1,c=Sin(180-Ad-Bd)/Sin(Bd)*b;
pair A=(0,0),B=(c,0),C=b*dir(Ad);
pair D=(C.x,0);
pair u=unit(A-C),v=unit(B-C);
pair din=u+v,dex=u-v;
pair L=C+(-C.y/din.y)*din, Ex=C+(-C.y/dex.y)*dex;
draw(Ex--B,linewidth(0.6)+dotted);draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(C--D,dashed);draw(C--Ex,blue+linewidth(1));draw(C--L,red+dashed);
real s=0.06;draw((D+(s,0))--(D+(s,s))--(D+(0,s)),linewidth(0.5));
draw(arc(Ex,0.45,0,degrees(C-Ex)),linewidth(0.6));
label("$30^\circ$",Ex+0.75*dir(degrees(C-Ex)/2));
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,SE);label("$E$",Ex,SW);label("$L$",L,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Combining.} The interior and exterior bisectors at $C$ are perpendicular, so $\angle ECL=90^\circ$. Because $CA$ is closer to $CE$ than $CB$ is ($A>B$ means $CB>CA$, and $E$ lies beyond $A$ since $\dfrac{EA}{EB}=\dfrac{CA}{CB}<1$), the rays $CE$, $CD$, $CL$ occur in this order, so
\[
\angle DCL=\angle ECL-\angle DCE=90^\circ-60^\circ=30^\circ .
\]
Hence $\dfrac{A-B}{2}=30^\circ$ and $\angle A-\angle B=\boxed{60^\circ}$. (For example $A=80^\circ$, $B=20^\circ$, $C=80^\circ$, the triangle drawn, is acute and satisfies the condition.)

Let the legs be $a$ and $b$, so $a^2+b^2=h^2$ and the area is $\dfrac{ab}{2}$. Since $(a-b)^2\ge0$, we have $2ab\le a^2+b^2=h^2$, so
\[
\text{area}=\dfrac{ab}{2}\le\dfrac{h^2}{4}=\left(\dfrac {h}{2}\right)^2,
\]
with equality exactly when $a=b$, i.e.\ for the isosceles right triangle.

\textbf{Geometric view.} By Thales' theorem the right-angle vertex $C$ lies on the circle with diameter $AB=h$ and centre $O$. The altitude $CH=k$ is at most the radius $OC=\dfrac {h}{2}$, so the area $\dfrac{1}{2}hk\le\dfrac{1}{2}h\cdot\dfrac {h}{2}=\dfrac{h^2}{4}$, with equality when $H=O$, again the isosceles case.

