question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Let $f(x)=2x+3$ and $f(g(x))=6x-1$ for all real $x$. Find $g(x)$.
\item[(b)] Let $g(x)=x+1$ and $f(g(x))=x^2+2x+5$ for all real $x$. Find $f(x)$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
For a vertical axis the directrix is horizontal. The vertex is the midpoint between focus and directrix, so we can take the focus $F=(h,k+p)$ and the directrix $y=k-p$ for some $p\ne0$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
real h=1,k=1,p=0.8;
real f(real x){return k+(x-h)^2/(4p);}
draw(graph(f,-1.6,3.6),linewidth(1));
draw((-1.6,k-p)--(3.6,k-p),dashed);label("$y=k-p$",(3.6,k-p),E);
pair F=(h,k+p),V=(h,k),P=(3.2,f(3.2)),Q=(3.2,k-p);
draw(F--P,blue);draw(P--Q,blue);
dot(F);dot(V);dot(P);dot(Q);
label("$F$",F,N);label("$V$",V,S);label("$P$",P,E);label("$Q$",Q,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

In the figure $F=(h,k+p)$ is the focus, $V=(h,k)$ the vertex and $Q$ the foot of the perpendicular from $P$ to the directrix. A point $P=(x,y)$ is on the parabola exactly when its distance to $F$ equals its distance $|y-(k-p)|$ to the directrix (the length $PQ$ in the figure):
\[
\sqrt{(x-h)^2+(y-k-p)^2}=|y-k+p| .
\]
Squaring (both sides are nonnegative) and expanding with $Y=y-k$:
\[
(x-h)^2+Y^2-2pY+p^2=Y^2+2pY+p^2\iff (x-h)^2=4pY .
\]
So $y-k=\dfrac{1}{4p}(x-h)^2$, which is the required form with $a=\dfrac{1}{4p}\ne0$.

Conversely, given $y-k=a(x-h)^2$ with $a\ne0$, put $p=\dfrac{1}{4a}$. The computation above, read backwards, shows the curve is exactly the set of points equidistant from $(h,k+p)$ and the line $y=k-p$, a parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ and vertical axis $x=h$.

