\textbf{Identity.} For any $x$, using $\sin u\sin v=\dfrac{1}{2}\bigl(\cos(u-v)-\cos(u+v)\bigr)$,
\[
\sin(60^\circ-x)\sin(60^\circ+x)=\dfrac{1}{2}\bigl(\cos2x-\cos120^\circ\bigr)=\dfrac{1}{2}\cos2x+\dfrac{1}{4}=\dfrac{3-4\sin^2x}{4},
\]
since $\cos2x=1-2\sin^2x$. Multiplying by $\sin x$ and using $\sin3x=3\sin x-4\sin^3x$,
\[
\sin x\sin(60^\circ-x)\sin(60^\circ+x)=\dfrac{3\sin x-4\sin^3x}{4}=\dfrac{\sin3x}{4}.
\]
\textbf{Apply it} with $x=20^\circ$: $\sin20^\circ\sin40^\circ\sin80^\circ=\dfrac{\sin60^\circ}{4}=\boxed{\dfrac{\sqrt3}{8}}$.

question:
A rotation of the plane maps $A(4,1)$ to $A'(2,3)$ and $B(4,4)$ to $B'(-1,3)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that the centre of any rotation lies on the perpendicular bisector of every segment joining a point to its image.
\item[(b)] Find the centre and the angle of this rotation.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) A rotation about $O$ keeps distances to $O$: $OA'=OA$ for every point $A$. A point equidistant from $A$ and $A'$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AA'$ (when $A'\ne A$). So the centre lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AA'$, and likewise of $BB'$; if these two bisectors are not the same line, the centre is their intersection.

(b) The midpoint of $AA'$ is $(3,2)$ and $\overrightarrow{AA'}=(-2,2)$, so its perpendicular bisector is $-2(x-3)+2(y-2)=0$, i.e.\ $y=x-1$. The midpoint of $BB'$ is $\left(\dfrac{3}{2},\dfrac{7}{2}\right)$ and $\overrightarrow{BB'}=(-5,-1)$, so its perpendicular bisector is $-5\left(x-\dfrac{3}{2}\right)-\left(y-\dfrac{7}{2}\right)=0$, i.e.\ $5x+y=11$. Solving, $6x-1=11$, so the centre is $O=\boxed{(2,1)}$.

