\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why the points $(x,y)$ of the plane whose distance from $(0,1)$ is less than $1$ are exactly the points with $x^2+(y-1)^2<1$, and describe this region.
\item[(b)] A point is chosen uniformly at random from the square with vertices $(-1,0)$, $(1,0)$, $(1,2)$, $(-1,2)$. What is the probability that its distance from $(0,1)$ is less than $1$?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) By the distance formula, the distance from $P=(x,y)$ to $(0,1)$ is $\sqrt{(x-0)^2+(y-1)^2}$. Both sides of $\sqrt{x^2+(y-1)^2}<1$ are nonnegative, so squaring does not change the inequality: it is equivalent to $x^2+(y-1)^2<1$. The region is the inside of the circle with centre $(0,1)$ and radius $1$ (an open disc).

