label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(b) The disc lies inside the square, which it touches at the midpoints of the sides. The probability is the ratio of areas:
\[
\dfrac{\pi\cdot1^2}{2\cdot2}=\boxed{\dfrac{\pi}{4}}.
\]

Substitute $x=\dfrac {y}{m}-a$ ($m\ne0$; for $m=0$ the line meets the parabola only at the origin) into $y^2=4ax$:
\[
y^2-\dfrac{4a}{m}\,y+4a^2=0 .
\]
There are two distinct intersection points exactly when the discriminant $\dfrac{16a^2}{m^2}-16a^2$ is positive, i.e.\ $0<|m|<1$. The midpoint $M=(h,k)$ has
\[
k=\dfrac{y_P+y_Q}{2}=\dfrac{2a}{m},\qquad h=\dfrac {k}{m}-a=\dfrac{2a}{m^2}-a .
\]
Eliminating $m=\dfrac{2a}{k}$: $h=\dfrac{k^2}{2a}-a$, i.e.\ $k^2=2a(h+a)$. Since $0<m^2<1$, we have $h=\dfrac{2a}{m^2}-a>a$. So the locus is the part of the parabola
\[
\boxed{y^2=2a(x+a)}
\]
with $x>a$ (that is, $|y|>2a$); every such point arises from $m=\dfrac{2a}{y}$.

\begin{asy}
