The figure shows $a=1$ and $m=0.7$: the chord $PQ$ of $y^2=4x$ on a line through $(-1,0)$, and its midpoint $M$ on the dashed locus.

\textbf{Tangent line.} At $(at^2,2at)$, implicit differentiation of $y^2=4ax$ gives slope $\dfrac{2a}{y}=\dfrac{1}{t}$, so the tangent is $y-2at=\dfrac{1}{t}(x-at^2)$, i.e.\ $ty=x+at^2$ (this also covers $t=0$, the vertical tangent $x=0$).

\textbf{Point $C$.} Subtract $t_2y=x+at_2^2$ from $t_1y=x+at_1^2$: $(t_1-t_2)y=a(t_1^2-t_2^2)$, so $y=a(t_1+t_2)$ and then $x=at_1t_2$. Thus $C=(at_1t_2,\ a(t_1+t_2))$.

