\end{asy}

\textbf{Area.} By the shoelace formula, with vectors from $C$:
\[
A-C=\bigl(at_1(t_1-t_2),\ a(t_1-t_2)\bigr),\qquad B-C=\bigl(at_2(t_2-t_1),\ a(t_2-t_1)\bigr),
\]
\[
[ABC]=\dfrac{1}{2}\left|at_1(t_1-t_2)\cdot a(t_2-t_1)-a(t_1-t_2)\cdot at_2(t_2-t_1)\right|=\dfrac{a^2}{2}(t_1-t_2)^2|t_1-t_2| .
\]
So the area is $\boxed{\dfrac{a^2}{2}\,|t_1-t_2|^3}$. (In the figure $a=1$, $t_1=2$, $t_2=-1$: $C=(-2,1)$ and the area is $\dfrac{27}{2}$.)

The diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other, so the parallelogram is symmetric under the half-turn about $M=\left(1,\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$, which sends $(x,y)$ to $(2-x,1-y)$. This half-turn maps each side to the opposite side.

The image of $x-y+1=0$ is $(2-x)-(1-y)+1=0$, i.e.\ $x-y-2=0$. The image of $2x+3y=6$ is $2(2-x)+3(1-y)=6$, i.e.\ $2x+3y=1$. (Neither given line passes through $M$, so these are new lines.) So the other sides lie on
\[
\boxed{x-y-2=0\quad\text{and}\quad2x+3y-1=0}.
\]
Check: the given lines meet at $\left(\dfrac{3}{5},\dfrac{8}{5}\right)$ and the new ones at $\left(\dfrac{7}{5},-\dfrac{3}{5}\right)$, whose midpoint is $M$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
