solution:
A photograph can show at most three faces of the cube, since two opposite faces are never seen together; and three faces meeting at a corner can be seen at once.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=0.30;
pair ex=(1,0),ey=(0.45,0.32),ez=(0,1);
pair pt(real x,real y,real z){return s*(x*ex+y*ey+z*ez);}
int n=11;
fill(pt(0,0,0)--pt(n,0,0)--pt(n,0,n)--pt(0,0,n)--cycle,gray(0.92));
fill(pt(0,0,n)--pt(n,0,n)--pt(n,n,n)--pt(0,n,n)--cycle,gray(0.82));
fill(pt(n,0,0)--pt(n,n,0)--pt(n,n,n)--pt(n,0,n)--cycle,gray(0.72));
for(int i=1;i<n;++i){
  draw(pt(i,0,0)--pt(i,0,n),linewidth(0.3));draw(pt(0,0,i)--pt(n,0,i),linewidth(0.3));
  draw(pt(0,i,n)--pt(n,i,n),linewidth(0.3));draw(pt(i,0,n)--pt(i,n,n),linewidth(0.3));
  draw(pt(n,i,0)--pt(n,i,n),linewidth(0.3));draw(pt(n,0,i)--pt(n,n,i),linewidth(0.3));
}
draw(pt(0,0,0)--pt(n,0,0)--pt(n,n,0)--pt(n,n,n)--pt(0,n,n)--pt(0,0,n)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(pt(0,0,n)--pt(n,0,n)--pt(n,0,0),linewidth(0.9));draw(pt(n,0,n)--pt(n,n,n),linewidth(0.9));
label("$11$",pt(n/2,0,0),S);label("$11$",pt(0,0,n/2),W);label("$11$",pt(n,n/2,0),SE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Count the unit cubes on three faces meeting at a corner by inclusion-exclusion. Each face has $11^2=121$ unit cubes; each pair of these faces shares an edge row of $11$ cubes; all three share the corner cube. So the number visible is
\[
3\cdot121-3\cdot11+1=\boxed{331}.
\]
(Equivalently, $11^3-10^3=331$: the cubes not visible form a $10\times10\times10$ block.)

\textbf{One diagonal.} In an $n\times m$ board ($n$ rows, $m$ columns) the diagonal from one corner to the opposite corner crosses $m$ vertical and $n$ horizontal grid lines in its interior and passes through $\gcd(m,n)-1$ interior lattice points. Each crossing of a grid line starts a new square, except that at a lattice point a vertical and a horizontal line are crossed at once. So it crosses
\[
1+(m-1)+(n-1)-(\gcd(m,n)-1)=m+n-\gcd(m,n)
\]
squares. For $6\times10$ this is $10+6-2=14$ squares per diagonal.

\textbf{Overlap.} Put the board at $0\le x\le10$, $0\le y\le6$; the diagonals are $y=0.6x$ and $y=6-0.6x$. Over a column $i\le x\le i+1$ with $i\le4$, the first diagonal stays at heights $y\le0.6(i+1)\le3$ and the second at heights $y\ge6-0.6(i+1)\ge3$, so the first only crosses squares of rows below $y=3$ and the second only squares above $y=3$. By symmetry the same holds for the columns with $i\ge5$. So no square is crossed by both diagonals. (The same reasoning for $6\times8$ gives $48-2(8+6-2)=24$, as stated.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int m=10,n=6;
for(int i=0;i<m;++i)for(int j=0;j<n;++j){
  bool hit=false;
  for(int k=1;k<400;++k){real t=k/400;
    pair p=(m*t,n*t),q=(m*t,n-n*t);
    if((p.x>i&&p.x<i+1&&p.y>j&&p.y<j+1)||(q.x>i&&q.x<i+1&&q.y>j&&q.y<j+1)) hit=true;}
  if(hit) fill(box((i,j),(i+1,j+1)),gray(0.8));
}
for(int i=0;i<=m;++i) draw((i,0)--(i,n),linewidth(0.4));
for(int j=0;j<=n;++j) draw((0,j)--(m,j),linewidth(0.4));
draw((0,0)--(m,n),linewidth(1));draw((0,n)--(m,0),linewidth(1));
draw(box((0,0),(m,n)),linewidth(0.9));
label("$10$",(m/2,0),S);label("$6$",(0,n/2),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So $28$ squares are crossed and $60-28=\boxed{32}$ are not.

\textbf{Statement.} The bisector of an angle of a triangle divides the opposite side in the ratio of the two sides forming that angle.

\textbf{Proof.} Compare the areas of triangles $ABD$ and $ACD$ in two ways.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.2,4.2),B=(0,0),C=(7,0);
real c=abs(A-B),bb=abs(A-C);
pair D=(bb*B+c*C)/(bb+c);
pair F1=B+dot(D-B,unit(A-B))*unit(A-B);
pair F2=C+dot(D-C,unit(A-C))*unit(A-C);
pair H=(A.x,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(A--D,linewidth(0.9));
draw(D--F1,dashed);draw(D--F2,dashed);draw(A--H,dotted);
real r=0.22;
draw(F1+r*unit(B-A)--F1+r*unit(B-A)+r*unit(D-F1)--F1+r*unit(D-F1));
draw(F2+r*unit(C-A)--F2+r*unit(C-A)+r*unit(D-F2)--F2+r*unit(D-F2));
draw(H+(r,0)--H+(r,r)--H+(0,r));
draw(arc(A,0.8,degrees(B-A),degrees(D-A)));draw(arc(A,0.9,degrees(D-A),degrees(C-A)));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,S);
label("$H$",H,S);label("$h$",D+0.42*(F1-D),rotate(-90)*unit(F1-D));label("$h$",(D+F2)/2,SE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\emph{Same height from $A$.} Both triangles have the height $AH$ from $A$ to line $BC$, so
\[
\dfrac{[ABD]}{[ACD]}=\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{2}\,BD\cdot AH}{\dfrac{1}{2}\,DC\cdot AH}=\dfrac{BD}{DC}.
\]
\emph{Same height from $D$.} Every point of the bisector of angle $A$ is equally far from the lines $AB$ and $AC$ (the right triangles formed by $A$, $D$ and the feet of the perpendiculars are congruent by AAS, sharing the hypotenuse $AD$). Call this distance $h$. Taking $AB$ and $AC$ as bases,
\[
\dfrac{[ABD]}{[ACD]}=\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{2}\,AB\cdot h}{\dfrac{1}{2}\,AC\cdot h}=\dfrac{AB}{AC}.
\]
Comparing the two expressions gives $\dfrac{BD}{DC}=\dfrac{AB}{AC}$. $\blacksquare$

A cube with edge $l$ has $6$ square faces of area $l^2$, so its surface area is $6l^2$.

\begin{asy}
