Reflecting $y=x^2$ across $y=1$ gives $y=2-x^2$. So the M-shaped curve is
\[
y=x^2\ \ (|x|\le1),\qquad y=2-x^2\ \ (|x|\ge1).
\]
A horizontal line $y=h$ meets it in four points exactly when $0<h<1$: at $x=\pm\sqrt h$ on the middle part and $x=\pm\sqrt{2-h}$ on the outer parts. So
\[
A=\left(-\sqrt{2-h},h\right),\quad B=\left(-\sqrt h,h\right),\quad C=\left(\sqrt h,h\right),\quad D=\left(\sqrt{2-h},h\right).
\]
By symmetry $AB=CD$ automatically; the condition $AB=BC$ means $\sqrt{2-h}-\sqrt h=2\sqrt h$, i.e.\ $\sqrt{2-h}=3\sqrt h$. Squaring, $2-h=9h$, so $h=\dfrac{1}{5}$. Then
\[
AB=BC=2\sqrt h=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt5}=\boxed{\dfrac{2\sqrt5}{5}} .
\]

\begin{asy}
