Label the vertices $A=V_0$, $B=V_1$, $V_2,\dots,V_{n-1}$ in order around the polygon, with $n=100$. The diagonals from $A$ are $AV_2,\dots,AV_{n-2}$, and together with the sides $AB$ and $AV_{n-1}$ they cut the polygon into the $n-2$ fan triangles $AV_jV_{j+1}$ ($1\le j\le n-2$). The diagonals from $B$ are $BV_3,\dots,BV_{n-1}$; two segments from $B$ meet only at $B$, so inside each fan triangle they do not cross one another.

\textbf{The fan triangle $ABV_2$} ($j=1$) has vertex $B$, and the $n-3$ diagonals $BV_3,\dots,BV_{n-1}$ run from $B$ across it to the side $AV_2$. They cut it into $n-2$ triangles and no quadrilaterals.

\textbf{A fan triangle $AV_jV_{j+1}$ with $2\le j\le n-2$.} A diagonal $BV_k$ enters it exactly when $k\ge j+1$: it crosses $AV_j$, and then either crosses $AV_{j+1}$ (if $k\ge j+2$) or ends at the vertex $V_{j+1}$ (if $k=j+1$). So $m=n-1-j\ge1$ diagonals cross this fan triangle, all from the side $AV_j$, the one closest to $A$ being $BV_{n-1}$. They cut it into $m+1$ pieces:
\begin{itemize}
\item the piece at $A$, cut off by $BV_{n-1}$: a triangle;
\item the piece at the side $V_jV_{j+1}$, cut off by $BV_{j+1}$, which runs from $AV_j$ to the vertex $V_{j+1}$: a triangle;
\item the $m-1$ pieces between consecutive diagonals: each has two corners on $AV_j$ and two on $AV_{j+1}$ (or at $V_{j+1}$), so it is a quadrilateral.
\end{itemize}
(When $m=1$, i.e.\ $j=n-2$, the single diagonal $BV_{n-1}$ gives just the two triangles.)

\textbf{Totals.} The number of triangles is $(n-2)+2(n-3)=3n-8$, and the number of quadrilaterals is
\[
\sum_{j=2}^{n-2}(n-2-j)=0+1+\cdots+(n-4)=\dfrac{(n-4)(n-3)}{2}.
\]
As a check, these add up to $1+(n-2)+(n-3)+\dfrac{(n-3)(n-2)}{2}$, the number of regions predicted by ``$1+{}$(number of segments)${}+{}$(number of crossing points)'', since $AV_j$ and $BV_k$ cross exactly when $k>j$. The figure shows $n=8$, with $3\cdot8-8=16$ triangles (orange) and $\dfrac{4\cdot5}{2}=10$ quadrilaterals (blue).

For $n=100$ the number of quadrilaterals is $\dfrac{96\cdot97}{2}=\boxed{4656}$ (and there are $292$ triangles).
\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
int n=8;
pair[] V; for(int k=0;k<n;++k) V.push(dir(-90-180/n-360*k/n));
pair A=V[0],B=V[1];
path poly=V[0]; for(int k=1;k<n;++k) poly=poly--V[k]; poly=poly--cycle;
fill(poly,rgb(0.85,0.9,1));
pen tp=rgb(1,0.85,0.7);
fill(A--B--V[2]--cycle,tp);
for(int j=2;j<=n-2;++j){
  pair X=extension(A,V[j],B,V[n-1]);
  pair Y=(j+1<=n-2)? extension(A,V[j+1],B,V[n-1]) : V[n-1];
  fill(A--X--Y--cycle,tp);
  pair Z=extension(A,V[j],B,V[j+1]);
  fill(V[j]--V[j+1]--Z--cycle,tp);
}
draw(poly,black+1);
