Quadrilateral $ABPC$ is cyclic, so by Ptolemy's theorem $AP\cdot BC=BP\cdot AC+CP\cdot AB$. All sides of the triangle equal $2$, so
\[
AP=BP+CP .
\]
Then $2AP=2\sqrt5$, so $AP=\sqrt5$ and $BP+CP=\sqrt5$. The perimeter of $BPC$ is
\[
BP+CP+BC=\boxed{2+\sqrt5}.
\]
Such a point exists: $\angle BPC=180^\circ-\angle BAC=120^\circ$, so the law of cosines gives $4=BP^2+CP^2+BP\cdot CP=(BP+CP)^2-BP\cdot CP$, hence $BP\cdot CP=1$. So $BP$ and $CP$ are the roots of $u^2-\sqrt5u+1=0$, namely $\dfrac{\sqrt5+1}{2}$ and $\dfrac{\sqrt5-1}{2}$ (in some order). As $P$ moves along the arc from $B$ to the midpoint of the arc, $AP$ grows from $2$ to the diameter $\dfrac{4}{\sqrt3}\approx2.31$, and $\sqrt5\approx2.24$ lies in this range.

