Two diagonals cross inside the polygon exactly when their four endpoints are four different vertices that alternate around the polygon. Any $4$ vertices form a convex quadrilateral, and of the three ways to pair them into two segments, exactly one (the two diagonals of that quadrilateral) gives a crossing pair. So crossing pairs of diagonals correspond one to one with $4$-element sets of vertices, and since no three diagonals are concurrent, each crossing pair gives a different point. The number of interior intersection points is
\[
\dbinom{121}{4}=\dfrac{121\cdot120\cdot119\cdot118}{24}=\boxed{8495410}.
\]
(For a \emph{regular} $121$-gon the count is the same: it is known that in a regular polygon with an odd number of sides no three diagonals are concurrent.) The figure shows the correspondence for a heptagon: the four marked vertices $P,Q,R,T$ determine exactly one crossing point $X$, where the diagonals $PR$ and $QT$ of quadrilateral $PQRT$ meet.

