Extend $AC$ beyond $C$ to $D$ with $CD=CB=a$. Then $AD=AC+CD=b+a$.

Triangle $BCD$ is isosceles with $CB=CD$, and its angle at $C$ is $\angle BCD=180^\circ-C$ (supplementary to $\angle ACB$). So its base angles are
\[
\angle CDB=\angle CBD=\dfrac{180^\circ-(180^\circ-C)}{2}=\dfrac {C}{2} .
\]
In triangle $ABD$: the angle at $A$ is $A$, the angle at $D$ is $\dfrac {C}{2}$, and the angle at $B$ is $\angle ABC+\angle CBD=B+\dfrac {C}{2}$. Since $A+B+C=180^\circ$,
\[
B+\dfrac {C}{2}=B+\dfrac{180^\circ-A-B}{2}=90^\circ-\dfrac{A-B}{2}.
\]
By the law of sines in triangle $ABD$,
\[
\dfrac{AD}{\sin\angle ABD}=\dfrac{AB}{\sin\angle ADB}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\dfrac{a+b}{c}=\dfrac{\sin\left(90^\circ-\dfrac{A-B}{2}\right)}{\sin\dfrac {C}{2}}=\dfrac{\cos\dfrac{A-B}{2}}{\sin\dfrac {C}{2}},
\]
which is Mollweide's formula.

