The first equation gives $y=2|x|-5$. Substituting, $2y+2=4|x|-8$, so the system is equivalent to
\[
g(x)=a,\qquad\text{where } g(x)=x-\bigl|4|x|-8\bigr| ,
\]
and each solution $x$ gives exactly one solution $(x,y)$. The function $g$ is piecewise linear:
\[
g(x)=\begin{cases}5x+8,&x\le-2,\\-3x-8,&-2\le x\le0,\\5x-8,&0\le x\le2,\\-3x+8,&x\ge2.\end{cases}
\]
So $g$ rises to the local maximum $g(-2)=-2$, falls to the local minimum $g(0)=-8$, rises to the maximum $g(2)=2$ and then falls. Counting intersections with the horizontal line at height $a$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $a>2$: none; $a=2$: one ($x=2$); $-2<a<2$: two;
\item $a=-2$: three ($x=-2$ and two on the right hump); $-8<a<-2$: four;
\item $a=-8$: three ($x=-\dfrac{16}{5}$, $0$, $\dfrac{16}{3}$); $a<-8$: two.
\end{itemize}
The number of solutions is odd exactly for $a=2,-2,-8$, and the product is $2\cdot(-2)\cdot(-8)=\boxed{32}$.

