The centres $P,Q,R$ are pairwise $2$ apart, so they form an equilateral triangle of side $2$. Use inclusion--exclusion.

\textbf{One lens.} Two of the circles have centres at distance $2$ equal to the radius, so the common chord subtends $120^\circ$ at each centre. The lens is two circular segments of angle $120^\circ$:
\[
2\left(\dfrac{120}{360}\pi\cdot2^2-\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot2^2\sin120^\circ\right)=\dfrac{8\pi}{3}-2\sqrt3 .
\]
\textbf{The triple intersection.} Each centre lies on the other two circles, so the common region of all three disks is the Reuleaux triangle on $PQR$: the equilateral triangle plus three segments of angle $60^\circ$:
\[
\sqrt3+3\left(\dfrac{60}{360}\pi\cdot4-\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot4\sin60^\circ\right)=\sqrt3+2\pi-3\sqrt3=2\pi-2\sqrt3 .
\]
\textbf{Union.}
\[
3\cdot4\pi-3\left(\dfrac{8\pi}{3}-2\sqrt3\right)+\left(2\pi-2\sqrt3\right)=6\pi+4\sqrt3 .
\]
So $a=6$, $b=4$, $c=3$ and $a+b+c=\boxed{13}$, which agrees with the answer key. (An answer of $3$ suggests a sign or bookkeeping slip in inclusion--exclusion.)

