question:
Call a pair $(x,y)$ of positive integers \emph{good} if both $xy$ and $x+y$ are perfect squares.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that there are infinitely many good pairs.
\item[(b)] Prove that no good pair also makes $3x+3y$ a perfect square.
\end{enumerate}

A collection $S$ of integers is defined by the following three rules:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $2$ is in $S$;
\item for every $x$ in $S$, both $3x$ and $x+7$ are in $S$;
\item no integers except those obtained by the rules above are in $S$.
\end{enumerate}
What is the smallest integer greater than $2004$ that is not in $S$?

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Let $XYZW$ be a regular tetrahedron with edge $1$ and let $M$ be the midpoint of $XY$. The points $Z$ and $W$ are equidistant from $X$ and $Y$, so they lie in the plane through $M$ perpendicular to $XY$; this plane meets the faces $XYZ$ and $XYW$ in the segments $MZ$ and $MW$, so $\angle ZMW$ is the angle between the faces. Here $MZ=MW=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$ (heights of equilateral triangles) and $ZW=1$, so by the law of cosines
\[
\cos\angle ZMW=\dfrac{\dfrac{3}{4}+\dfrac{3}{4}-1}{2\cdot\dfrac{3}{4}}=\dfrac{1}{3} .
\]
\textbf{(b)} Work in the plane through $M$ perpendicular to $XY$. Every vertex $A,\dots,F$ lies in this plane at distance $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$ from $M$. Given the face $XYB$, there are exactly two points completing a regular tetrahedron: the two points of this circle at angle $\theta$ from $B$, one on each side. One of them is $A$; since $C\ne A$, the point $C$ is the other one, so the angle from $A$ to $C$ around $M$ is $2\theta$, continuing in the same direction. Repeating, $A,B,C,D,E,F$ are at angles $0,\theta,2\theta,3\theta,4\theta,5\theta$ around $M$. Now
\[
5\theta=5\arccos\dfrac{1}{3}\approx5\cdot70.53^\circ=352.64^\circ<360^\circ,
\]
so $F\ne A$, and the gap between the faces $XYF$ and $XYA$ is
\[
360^\circ-5\arccos\dfrac{1}{3}\approx\boxed{7.36^\circ}.
\]
(If the tetrahedra need not be regular, the points can be chosen almost freely and of course $F$ need not equal $A$; the regular case shows that even the most symmetric arrangement fails to close up.)

