Let $AD=1$ and $DC=2$, and let $H$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $C$ to line $BD$.
\begin{itemize}
\item In the right triangle $CHD$, $\angle CDH=60^\circ$, so $DH=DC\cos60^\circ=1$, $\angle DCH=30^\circ$. Also $\angle ADB=180^\circ-60^\circ=120^\circ$ and $\angle ABD=180^\circ-45^\circ-120^\circ=15^\circ$.
\item Triangle $ADH$ has $AD=DH=1$ and $\angle ADH=\angle ADB=120^\circ$, so $\angle DAH=\angle DHA=30^\circ$.
\item In triangle $AHC$, $\angle HAC=30^\circ=\angle HCA$, so $HA=HC$.
\item $\angle HAB=45^\circ-30^\circ=15^\circ=\angle HBA$, so $HB=HA$.
\end{itemize}
Hence $HB=HC$, and since $\angle BHC=90^\circ$, triangle $BHC$ is an isosceles right triangle with $\angle HCB=45^\circ$. (Here $H$ lies between $D$ and $B$: $DB=\sqrt3+1>1=DH$, e.g.\ from the law of sines in triangle $ABD$.) Therefore
\[
\angle BCA=\angle HCA+\angle HCB=30^\circ+45^\circ=75^\circ,\qquad \angle CBA=180^\circ-45^\circ-75^\circ=60^\circ,
\]
and $\angle BCA-\angle CBA=\boxed{15^\circ}$.

