The statement is \textbf{false}. Put the intersection $O$ of the diagonals at the origin with the diagonals along the axes:
\[
A=(-2,0),\quad C=(1,0),\quad B=(0,-4),\quad D=(0,2).
\]
Then $AC\perp BD$. Also $\overrightarrow{AB}=(2,-4)$ and $\overrightarrow{DC}=(1,-2)$ are parallel, while $AD$ and $BC$ are not ($\overrightarrow{AD}=(2,2)$, $\overrightarrow{BC}=(1,4)$), so $ABCD$ is a trapezoid with bases $AB$ and $DC$. Its legs are
\[
AD=\sqrt{2^2+2^2}=2\sqrt2,\qquad BC=\sqrt{1^2+4^2}=\sqrt{17},
\]
which are different, so the trapezoid is not isosceles.

(In general, for a trapezoid with perpendicular diagonals and $OA=a$, $OB=b$, the parallel bases force $OC=ta$, $OD=tb$ for some $t>0$; then $AD^2=a^2+t^2b^2$ and $BC^2=b^2+t^2a^2$, which are equal only when $a=b$ or $t=1$, i.e.\ only in special cases. A trapezoid is isosceles exactly when its diagonals are equal, which perpendicularity does not force.)

