question:
A book problem reads: ``The side lengths of a right triangle are in the ratio $x:(x+1):(x+3)$. Express the area of the triangle in simplest radical form.'' The book's solution treats $x$, $x+1$, $x+3$ as the actual side lengths.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why, read literally as a ratio, the problem does not determine the area.
\item[(b)] Find the area of the right triangle whose side lengths are $x$, $x+1$ and $x+3$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The longest side $x+3$ is the hypotenuse.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=2+2*sqrt(3), c=3+2*sqrt(3);
pair C=(0,0),B=(a,0),A=(0,c);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((0.45,0)--(0.45,0.45)--(0,0.45));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,SW);
label("$x$",(B+C)/2,S);label("$x+1$",(A+C)/2,W);label("$x+3$",(A+B)/2,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(a) If the sides are only \emph{in the ratio} $x:(x+1):(x+3)$, they are $kx,\ k(x+1),\ k(x+3)$ for some unknown scale $k>0$. The Pythagorean condition $k^2x^2+k^2(x+1)^2=k^2(x+3)^2$ fixes $x$ (it is the same equation as in (b)), so it fixes the \emph{shape}, but every $k>0$ gives a valid triangle, and the area is multiplied by $k^2$. So the ratio alone does not determine the area; the book's reading (the sides \emph{are} $x$, $x+1$, $x+3$) is needed.

(b) By the Pythagorean theorem,
\[
x^2+(x+1)^2=(x+3)^2\iff2x^2+2x+1=x^2+6x+9\iff x^2-4x-8=0,
\]
so $x=2\pm2\sqrt3$, and $x>0$ forces $x=2+2\sqrt3$. The legs are $2+2\sqrt3$ and $3+2\sqrt3$, and the area is
\[
\dfrac{1}{2}(2+2\sqrt3)(3+2\sqrt3)=\dfrac{1}{2}(6+4\sqrt3+6\sqrt3+12)=\dfrac{1}{2}(18+10\sqrt3)=\boxed{9+5\sqrt3}.
\]

Let $ABCD$ be inscribed in the circle with diagonals $AC=d_1$ and $BD=d_2$ meeting at $P$ at an angle $\theta$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
pair A=dir(195),B=dir(255),C=dir(5),D=dir(140);
pair P=extension(A,C,B,D);
draw(circle(O,1),linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(A--C,blue);draw(B--D,blue);
draw(arc(P,0.17,degrees(C-P),degrees(D-P)));
label("$\theta$",P+0.28*dir((degrees(C-P)+degrees(D-P))/2));
dot(O);label("$O$",O,N);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$P$",P,SW);
label("$d_1$",A+0.72*(C-A),S);label("$d_2$",B+0.3*(D-B),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The diagonals split $ABCD$ into the triangles $PAB$, $PBC$, $PCD$, $PDA$, whose areas are $\dfrac{1}{2}\,PA\cdot PB\sin\theta$, and so on (the angles at $P$ are $\theta$ or $180^\circ-\theta$, with the same sine). Adding,
\[
[ABCD]=\dfrac{1}{2}(PA+PC)(PB+PD)\sin\theta=\dfrac{1}{2}d_1d_2\sin\theta .
\]
Each diagonal is a chord, so $d_1\le2$ and $d_2\le2$, and $\sin\theta\le1$. Hence $[ABCD]\le\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot2\cdot2\cdot1=2$. Equality needs both diagonals to be diameters that are perpendicular, which gives a square with vertices on the circle (side $\sqrt2$, area $2$). So yes, the answer is the square, with area $\boxed{2}$.

The two tangent segments from a point outside a circle to the circle have equal length. Let the tangent lengths from $A,B,C,D$ be $p,q,s,t$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A0=(0.0000,-6.8810),B0=(7.3231,-1.6492),C0=(1.4917,8.8387),D0=(-8.9505,-0.4865);
transform T=rotate(-degrees(B0-A0));
pair A=T*A0,B=T*B0,C=T*C0,D=T*D0,I=T*(0,0);
real r=5.5989;
draw(circle(I,r),linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
dot(I);label("$I$",I,S);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,N);
label("$9$",(A+B)/2,S);label("$12$",(B+C)/2,E);label("$14$",(C+D)/2,2*N);label("$AD$",(A+D)/2,2.5*W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
