Each side is made of two tangent segments from its endpoints:
\[
AB=p+q,\quad BC=q+s,\quad CD=s+t,\quad DA=t+p .
\]
Hence $AB+CD=p+q+s+t=BC+DA$ (Pitot's theorem). So
\[
AD=AB+CD-BC=9+14-12=\boxed{11},
\]
and in general $\boxed{AD=x+z-y}$ (such a quadrilateral exists only when $x+z>y$). The figure shows one such quadrilateral, with tangent lengths $4,5,7,7$; the shape is not determined by the three given sides, but $AD$ is.

question:
Convert the following repeating base-$b$ numbers into base-ten fractions in lowest terms:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $0.\overline{12}_{\,5}=0.121212\ldots_5$;
\item[(b)] $0.1\overline{3}_{\,4}=0.13333\ldots_4$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The circle has centre $O=(-6,10)$ and radius $4$; it lies above the $x$-axis. Let the path go from $P=(2,5)$ to a point $X$ on the $x$-axis and then to a point $Y$ on the circle. Reflect $P$ in the $x$-axis to $P'=(2,-5)$; then $PX=P'X$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(2,5),Pp=(2,-5),O=(-6,10),X=(-2/3,0),Y=(-70/17,110/17);
draw((-11,0)--(4,0),Arrow(6));draw((0,-6)--(0,15),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(4,0),E);label("$y$",(0,15),N);
draw(circle(O,4),linewidth(1));dot(O);label("$O(-6,10)$",O,N);
draw(P--X--Y,blue+linewidth(1));
draw(X--Pp,dashed);draw(Y--O,dotted);
dot(P);dot(Pp);dot(X);dot(Y);
label("$P(2,5)$",P,E);label("$P'(2,-5)$",Pp,E);label("$X$",X,SW);label("$Y$",Y,SW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

By the triangle inequality,
\[
PX+XY=P'X+XY\ge P'Y\ge P'O-OY=P'O-4,
\]
and $P'O=\sqrt{8^2+15^2}=17$, so every such path has length at least $13$.

Equality holds when $X$ and $Y$ lie on segment $P'O$: this segment crosses the $x$-axis at $X=\left(-\dfrac{2}{3},0\right)$ (one third of the way from $P'$ to $O$) and meets the circle at $Y=O+\dfrac{4}{17}(P'-O)=\left(-\dfrac{70}{17},\dfrac{110}{17}\right)$. Then $PX=\dfrac{17}{3}$ and $XY=\dfrac{34}{3}-4=\dfrac{22}{3}$, total $13$. The shortest length is $\boxed{13}$.

Since $AB=AC=r$, the points $B$ and $C$ lie on the circle $(x+1)^2+y^2=r^2$. On the hyperbola $y^2=x^2-1$, so a common point satisfies
\[
(x+1)^2+x^2-1=r^2\iff2x^2+2x=r^2 ,
\]
which has exactly one positive root $x$. Hence $B$ and $C$ have the same $x$-coordinate, and, being distinct points of the hyperbola, they are $(x_0,\pm y_0)$, symmetric about the $x$-axis.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
path br=(cosh(-1.9),sinh(-1.9));
for(int i=1;i<=76;++i){real t=-1.9+3.8*i/76; br=br..(cosh(t),sinh(t));}
path bl=reflect((0,-1),(0,1))*br;
draw(br,gray);draw(bl,gray);
draw((-4,0)--(4,0),Arrow(6));draw((0,-3.6)--(0,3.6),Arrow(6));
label("$x$",(4,0),E);label("$y$",(0,3.6),N);
pair A=(-1,0),B=(2,sqrt(3)),C=(2,-sqrt(3));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,blue+linewidth(1));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);
label("$A(-1,0)$",A,NW);label("$B(2,\sqrt3)$",B,E);label("$C(2,-\sqrt3)$",C,E);
label("$x^2-y^2=1$",(3.1,3.3),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The side $BC$ is vertical with length $2y_0$, and the distance from $A$ to it is $x_0+1$. In an equilateral triangle the height is $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$ times the side, so $x_0+1=\sqrt3\,y_0$, i.e.\ $y_0=\dfrac{x_0+1}{\sqrt3}$. Substituting into $x_0^2-y_0^2=1$:
\[
3x_0^2-(x_0+1)^2=3\iff2x_0^2-2x_0-4=0\iff(x_0-2)(x_0+1)=0 ,
\]
so $x_0=2$ and $y_0=\sqrt3$. The side is $2\sqrt3$ and the area is $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\left(2\sqrt3\right)^2=\boxed{3\sqrt3}$.

question:
A basketball team has $12$ players, including Michael, Lebron and James. In how many ways can $5$ players be chosen for a match if
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] there is no restriction;
\item[(b)] Michael (the best player) must be chosen;
\item[(c)] Lebron and James refuse to play together?
\end{enumerate}

A student is asked: ``How many triples $(a,b,c)$ of positive real numbers satisfy $\dfrac{1}{a}+\dfrac{1}{b}+\dfrac{1}{c}=20$?'' The student writes: ``Clearly $a,b,c\le1$, so put $a=\dfrac{1}{x}$, $b=\dfrac{1}{y}$, $c=\dfrac{1}{z}$ with positive integers $x,y,z$. Then $x+y+z=20$, which has $\dbinom{19}{2}=171$ positive integer solutions.''
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain what is wrong, and answer the question as asked.
\item[(b)] How many ordered triples $(a,b,c)$ of positive reals with $\dfrac{1}{a}+\dfrac{1}{b}+\dfrac{1}{c}=20$ have $\dfrac{1}{a}$, $\dfrac{1}{b}$, $\dfrac{1}{c}$ all integers?
\end{enumerate}

Five different boys and five different girls are to be seated so that no two girls sit next to each other. In how many ways can this be done
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] around a round table (seatings that differ by a rotation are the same);
\item[(b)] on a long bench (in a row)?
\end{enumerate}

Construct a function $f:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(1)] $f(x)$ is not an integer for every integer $x$, and
\item[(2)] $f(f(x))$ is an integer for every integer $x$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Put $O=(0,0)$ and $A=(1,0)$. The square is $\dfrac{1}{2}\le x\le\dfrac{3}{2}$, $-\dfrac{1}{2}\le y\le\dfrac{1}{2}$. The circle $x^2+y^2=1$ meets the top and bottom sides at $\left(\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2},\pm\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$, and the common region (shaded) lies between $x=\dfrac{1}{2}$ and the arc.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),A=(1,0);
path sq=(0.5,-0.5)--(1.5,-0.5)--(1.5,0.5)--(0.5,0.5)--cycle;
path reg=(0.5,-0.5)--(sqrt(3)/2,-0.5)--arc(O,1,-30,30)--(0.5,0.5)--cycle;
fill(reg,lightgray);
draw(circle(O,1),linewidth(0.9));
draw(sq,linewidth(0.9));
draw((sqrt(3)/2,-0.5)--(sqrt(3)/2,0.5),dashed);
draw(O--A,dotted);draw(O--(sqrt(3)/2,0.5),dashed);
dot(O);dot(A);
label("$O$",O,W);label("$A$",A,E);
label("$1$",(0.36,0.21),NW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Cut it by the chord $x=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}$ (dashed):
\begin{itemize}
\item a rectangle of width $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}-\dfrac{1}{2}$ and height $1$, of area $\dfrac{\sqrt3-1}{2}$;
\item a circular segment cut off by a chord that subtends $60^\circ$ at $O$ (the radius to $\left(\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2},\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$ makes $30^\circ$ with $OA$), of area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot1^2\left(\dfrac{\pi}{3}-\sin60^\circ\right)=\dfrac{\pi}{6}-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}$.
\end{itemize}
The total is
\[
\dfrac{\sqrt3-1}{2}+\dfrac{\pi}{6}-\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}=\dfrac{\pi}{6}+\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}-\dfrac{1}{2}\approx0.5236+0.4330-0.5=0.4566,
\]
so the area is about $\boxed{0.46}$ square inches.

Let $P$ lie on the semicircle on $AB$ and $Q$ on the semicircle on $AD$, and let $s$ be the side of the square. By Thales' theorem $\angle APB=90^\circ$ and $\angle AQD=90^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=17*sqrt(2);
pair A=(0,0),B=(s,0),C=(s,s),D=(0,s),Ec=(s/2,s/2);
pair u=(23,-7)/s;
pair P=23*u,Q=-7*u;
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw(arc((s/2,0),s/2,180,360),linewidth(0.9));
draw(arc((0,s/2),s/2,90,270),linewidth(0.9));
draw(Q--P,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--P,dashed);draw(D--Q,dashed);draw(A--Ec,dotted);
real m=1.3;
draw(P+m*unit(A-P)--P+m*unit(A-P)+m*unit(B-P)--P+m*unit(B-P));
draw(Q+m*unit(A-Q)--Q+m*unit(A-Q)+m*unit(D-Q)--Q+m*unit(D-Q));
dot(Ec);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,N);
label("$E$",Ec,E);label("$P$",P,S);label("$Q$",Q,W);
label("$23$",(A+P)/2,NE);label("$7$",(A+Q)/2,N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Since $Q$, $A$, $P$ are collinear and $\angle DAB=90^\circ$, we have $\angle QAD+\angle BAP=180^\circ-90^\circ=90^\circ$. In right triangle $AQD$, $\angle ADQ=90^\circ-\angle QAD=\angle BAP$. So the right triangles $DQA$ and $APB$ have equal hypotenuses $AD=AB=s$ and equal acute angles at $D$ and $A$; they are congruent, and $DQ=AP=23$. By the Pythagorean theorem in triangle $AQD$,
\[
s^2=AQ^2+DQ^2=49+529=578 .
\]
$AE$ is half the diagonal: $AE=\dfrac{s\sqrt2}{2}=\sqrt{\dfrac{s^2}{2}}=\sqrt{289}=\boxed{17}$.

question:
Five different boys and five different girls are to be seated around a round table (seatings that differ only by a rotation are the same). In how many ways can they be seated if
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] all the girls sit next to each other;
\item[(b)] boys and girls alternate around the table?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Recall that the power of a point $X$ with respect to a circle with centre $O$ and radius $r$ is $XO^2-r^2$; if $X$ lies on a tangent line touching the circle at $U$, then $XO^2-r^2=XU^2$ by the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle $XUO$. The radical axis of two non-concentric circles is the set of points with equal powers with respect to both circles; it is a line perpendicular to the line of centres.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O1=(0,0),O2=(7,0);real r1=2.2,r2=1.2,d=7;
real nx=(r1-r2)/d;pair n=(nx,sqrt(1-nx^2));
pair S1=O1+r1*n,T=O2+r2*n,M=(S1+T)/2;
real xr=(d^2+r1^2-r2^2)/(2*d);
draw(circle(O1,r1),linewidth(1));draw(circle(O2,r2),linewidth(1));
draw(S1-0.25*(T-S1)--T+0.25*(T-S1),linewidth(1));
draw((xr,-2.6)--(xr,3.6),red+dashed);
draw(O1--O2,dotted);
draw(O1--S1,gray);draw(O2--T,gray);
dot(O1);dot(O2);dot(S1);dot(T);dot(M);
label("$O_1$",O1,S);label("$O_2$",O2,S);label("$S$",S1,NW);label("$T$",T,NE);label("$M$",M,N);
label("$c_1$",O1+r1*dir(225),2*SW);label("$c_2$",O2+r2*dir(-45),2*SE);
label("radical axis",(xr,-2.6),S,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Let $M$ be the midpoint of $ST$. Since $M$ lies on the tangent line at $S$ to $c_1$, its power with respect to $c_1$ is $MS^2$. Since $M$ lies on the tangent line at $T$ to $c_2$, its power with respect to $c_2$ is $MT^2$. As $MS=MT$, the two powers are equal, so $M$ lies on the radical axis. $\blacksquare$

(The circles are not concentric, since two concentric circles have no common tangent unless they coincide. The same argument works for an internal common tangent and for intersecting circles.)

question:
Eight people, among them Itti, Rai, Tham and Zayn, are to be seated at a round table with $8$ equally spaced seats. Two seatings are considered the same if one is a rotation of the other. In how many ways can they be seated if
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Itti and Rai sit directly opposite each other;
\item[(b)] Tham and Zayn do not sit next to each other;
\item[(c)] both conditions (a) and (b) hold?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the box have edge lengths $x$, $y$, $z$ (front edge $x$, depth $y$, height $z$), and let the common corner $V$ be the origin of coordinates along these edges. The centres of the three faces through $V$ are $X=\left(\dfrac {x}{2},0,\dfrac {z}{2}\right)$, $Y=\left(\dfrac {x}{2},\dfrac {y}{2},0\right)$ and $Z=\left(0,\dfrac {y}{2},\dfrac {z}{2}\right)$ (with coordinates measured from $V$ into the box). Each side of triangle $XYZ$ is half a face diagonal:
\[
XY=\dfrac{\sqrt{y^2+z^2}}{2},\qquad YZ=\dfrac{\sqrt{x^2+z^2}}{2},\qquad XZ=\dfrac{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}{2}.
\]
The three values are $4,5,6$ in some order, so the three sums $x^2+y^2$, $y^2+z^2$, $x^2+z^2$ are $64$, $100$, $144$ in some order. The volume does not depend on the order: adding, $x^2+y^2+z^2=\dfrac{64+100+144}{2}=154$, so the three squares are $154-64=90$, $154-100=54$ and $154-144=10$. (The figure uses $x^2=54$, $y^2=10$, $z^2=90$.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=sqrt(54), bb=sqrt(10), c=sqrt(90);
pair P(real x,real y,real z){return (x+0.55*y,z+0.38*y);}
draw(P(0,0,0)--P(a,0,0)--P(a,0,c)--P(0,0,c)--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
draw(P(0,0,c)--P(0,bb,c)--P(a,bb,c)--P(a,0,c),linewidth(0.8));
draw(P(a,0,0)--P(a,bb,0)--P(a,bb,c),linewidth(0.8));
draw(P(0,0,0)--P(0,bb,0)--P(a,bb,0),dashed+gray);draw(P(0,bb,0)--P(0,bb,c),dashed+gray);
pair X=P(a/2,0,c/2), Y=P(a/2,bb/2,c), Z=P(a,bb/2,c/2), V=P(a,0,c);
draw(X--Y--Z--cycle,red+linewidth(0.9));
dot(V);dot(X,red);dot(Y,red);dot(Z,red);
label("$V$",V,dir(-40));
label("$X$",X,W);label("$Y$",Y,W);label("$Z$",Z,E);
label("$5$",(X+Y)/2,W,red);label("$6$",(Y+Z)/2,NE,red);label("$4$",(X+Z)/2,S,red);
label("$x$",P(a/2,0,0),S);label("$z$",P(0,0,c/2),W);label("$y$",P(a,bb/2,0),SE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Hence
\[
xyz=\sqrt{90\cdot54\cdot10}=\sqrt{48600}=90\sqrt6,
\]
and the answer is $\boxed{\text{(e) }90\sqrt6}$.

The inequality $|2x-3y|\le12$ describes the strip between the parallel lines $2x-3y=12$ and $2x-3y=-12$, and $|2x+3y|\le12$ the strip between $2x+3y=\pm12$. Their intersection is the parallelogram bounded by these four lines. Its vertices are found by solving pairs of equations:
\begin{itemize}
\item $2x-3y=12$ and $2x+3y=12$ give $(6,0)$;
\item $2x-3y=-12$ and $2x+3y=-12$ give $(-6,0)$;
\item $2x-3y=-12$ and $2x+3y=12$ give $(0,4)$;
\item $2x-3y=12$ and $2x+3y=-12$ give $(0,-4)$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
fill((6,0)--(0,4)--(-6,0)--(0,-4)--cycle,paleblue);
draw((-7.5,0)--(7.5,0),Arrow(TeXHead));draw((0,-5.5)--(0,5.5),Arrow(TeXHead));
label("$x$",(7.5,0),E);label("$y$",(0,5.5),N);
draw((6,0)--(0,4)--(-6,0)--(0,-4)--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot((6,0));dot((-6,0));dot((0,4));dot((0,-4));
label("$(6,0)$",(6.3,-0.3),SE);label("$(-6,0)$",(-6.3,-0.3),SW);label("$(0,4)$",(0,4),NE);label("$(0,-4)$",(0,-4),SE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So the region is a rhombus with diagonals $12$ (along the $x$-axis) and $8$ (along the $y$-axis), and its area is $\dfrac{12\cdot8}{2}=\boxed{\text{(d) }48}$.

Let $P=(x,0)$ and $Q=(0,y)$.

\emph{$P$ and $Q$ are adjacent vertices.} If they were opposite, the other two vertices would be the centre $\left(\dfrac {x}{2},\dfrac {y}{2}\right)$ plus and minus the vector $\left(\dfrac {y}{2},\dfrac {x}{2}\right)$ (half of $\overrightarrow{QP}$ turned through $90^\circ$), namely $\left(\dfrac{x+y}{2},\dfrac{x+y}{2}\right)$ and $\left(\dfrac{x-y}{2},\dfrac{y-x}{2}\right)$. The coordinate sums would be $x+y$ and $0$, but no vertex of the square in the first quadrant has coordinate sum $0$ other than the origin, and $14\ne0$, $19\ne0$. So $PQ$ is a side.

The side vector is $\overrightarrow{PQ}=(-x,y)$, and a perpendicular vector of the same length is $(y,x)$ (the other choice, $(-y,-x)$, leads out of the first quadrant). So the other two vertices are
\[
P+(y,x)=(x+y,\ x),\qquad Q+(y,x)=(y,\ x+y).
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair P=(8,0),Q=(0,3),R=(3,11),T=(11,8);
draw((-1,0)--(13,0),Arrow(TeXHead));draw((0,-1)--(0,13),Arrow(TeXHead));
label("$x$",(13,0),E);label("$y$",(0,13),N);
fill(P--T--R--Q--cycle,paleblue);draw(P--T--R--Q--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--(11,0)--T,dashed);
dot(P);dot(Q);dot(R);dot(T);
label("$(x,0)$",P,S);label("$(0,y)$",Q,W);label("$(a,b)$",T,E);label("$(c,d)$",R,N);
label("$x$",(4,0),S);label("$y$",(0,1.5),W);
label("$y$",(9.7,0),N);label("$x$",(11,4),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Their coordinate sums are $2x+y$ and $x+2y$, which are $19$ and $14$ in some order. Adding, $3(x+y)=33$, so $x+y=\boxed{\text{(a) }11}$. (For instance $x=8$, $y=3$ gives the square with vertices $(8,0)$, $(0,3)$, $(3,11)$, $(11,8)$.)

Complete the squares. The first equation is $(x-4)^2+(y-4)^2=16$, a circle with centre $(4,4)$ and radius $4$. The second is $(x+1)^2+(y+8)^2=1+64-9=56$, a circle with centre $(-1,-8)$ and radius $\sqrt{56}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
draw((-10,0)--(10,0),Arrow(TeXHead));draw((0,-16.5)--(0,9.5),Arrow(TeXHead));
label("$x$",(10,0),E);label("$y$",(0,9.5),N);
pair O1=(4,4),O2=(-1,-8);
draw(circle(O1,4),blue+linewidth(0.9));draw(circle(O2,sqrt(56)),red+linewidth(0.9));
draw(O1--O2,dashed);draw(O2--(4,-8)--O1,dotted);
dot(O1);dot(O2);label("$(4,4)$",O1,N);label("$(-1,-8)$",O2,SW);
label("$13$",(2.7,-2.6));label("$5$",(1.5,-8),S);label("$12$",(4,-5),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The centres differ by $5$ horizontally and $12$ vertically, so their distance is $\sqrt{5^2+12^2}=\boxed{\text{(b) }13}$.

Call the triangle $PQR$ with $PQ=PR=A$ and altitude $PH=B$ to the base $QR$. The altitude is also the perpendicular bisector of $QR$, so the circumcentre $O$ lies on line $PH$. Extend $PH$ to the diameter $PP'$ of the circumcircle, so $PP'=2R$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real A=3, Bh=2.6;
real h=sqrt(A^2-Bh^2);
pair P=(0,Bh), Q=(-h,0), R=(h,0), H=(0,0);
real Rr=A^2/(2*Bh); pair O=(0,Bh-Rr), P2=(0,Bh-2*Rr);
draw(circle(O,Rr),gray);
draw(P--Q--R--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--P2,dashed);draw(Q--P2,dashed);
draw(H--H+(0.25,0)--H+(0.25,0.25)--H+(0,0.25));
draw(Q+0.3*dir(P-Q)--Q+0.3*dir(P-Q)+0.3*dir(P2-Q)--Q+0.3*dir(P2-Q));
dot(O);dot(P2);
label("$P$",P,N);label("$Q$",Q,W);label("$R$",R,E);label("$H$",H,SE);label("$O$",O,E);label("$P'$",P2,S);
label("$A$",(P+Q)/2,NW);label("$A$",(P+R)/2,NE);label("$B$",(P+H)/2,E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Since $PP'$ is a diameter, $\angle PQP'=90^\circ$. In right triangle $PQP'$ the segment $QH$ is the altitude to the hypotenuse, so triangles $PHQ$ and $PQP'$ are similar (they share the angle at $P$ and both have a right angle), giving
\[
\dfrac{PQ}{PP'}=\dfrac{PH}{PQ}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad A^2=B\cdot2R .
\]
Hence $R=\boxed{\text{(e) }\dfrac{A^2}{2B}}$.

All three circles are inscribed in the angle, so their centres lie on the bisector of $\angle BAC$, and the condition $OP>QR>RS$ says that the radii decrease, so the circles get closer to $A$. Let $\theta$ be half of $\angle BAC$. In the right triangle $ABO$ (radius $OB$ is perpendicular to the side), $\sin\theta=\dfrac{OB}{OA}=\dfrac{10}{25}=\dfrac{2}{5}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(9cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=asin(0.4); pair A=(0,0);
pair u=dir(degrees(s)), v=dir(-degrees(s));
real dO=25,rO=10,dQ=75/7,rQ=30/7,dS=225/49,rS=90/49;
pair O=(dO,0),Q=(dQ,0),S0=(dS,0);
draw(A--38*u);draw(A--38*v);
draw(circle(O,rO),blue+linewidth(0.8));draw(circle(Q,rQ),blue+linewidth(0.8));draw(circle(S0,rS),blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--O,dashed);
pair B=dO*cos(s)*u, C=dO*cos(s)*v, D=dQ*cos(s)*u, E0=dQ*cos(s)*v, F=dS*cos(s)*u, G=dS*cos(s)*v;
pair P=(dO-rO,0), R=(dQ-rQ,0);
draw(O--B,gray);draw(Q--D,gray);
dot(A);dot(O);dot(Q);dot(S0);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(E0);dot(F);dot(G);dot(P);dot(R);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$O$",O,SE);label("$Q$",Q,SE);label("$S$",S0,(0.3,-1.2));
label("$B$",B,N);label("$C$",C,S);label("$D$",D,N);label("$E$",E0,S);label("$F$",F,N);label("$G$",G,S);
label("$P$",P,NE);label("$R$",R,NE);
label("$10$",(O+B)/2,E,fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

For any circle inscribed in the angle with centre at distance $d$ from $A$, the radius is $d\sin\theta=\dfrac{2d}{5}$. If circle $Q$ (centre at distance $q$) is tangent to circle $O$ on the side towards $A$, then $OQ=25-q$ equals the sum of the radii:
\[
25-q=10+\dfrac{2q}{5}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad q=\dfrac{75}{7}.
\]
So each step towards $A$ multiplies distances from $A$ by $\dfrac{3}{7}$: in general $D-d=\dfrac{2D}{5}+\dfrac{2d}{5}$ gives $d=\dfrac{3D}{7}$. Applying this again, the centre of circle $S$ is at distance
\[
SA=\dfrac{3}{7}\cdot\dfrac{75}{7}=\dfrac{225}{49}
\]
from $A$. Therefore $\sqrt{SA}=\boxed{\dfrac{15}{7}}$.

Let the regular hexagon $ABCDEF$ have side $1$. Its long diagonal $AD$ is a diameter of the circumcircle, so the angles $\angle ABD$ and $\angle AED$, which are inscribed in semicircles, are right angles; hence $ABDE$ is a rectangle, and similarly $ACDF$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] H; for(int i=0;i<6;++i) H[i]=dir(180-60*i);
pair A=H[0],B=H[1],C=H[2],D=H[3],E0=H[4],F=H[5];
fill(A--B--D--E0--cycle,palered+opacity(0.5));fill(A--C--D--F--cycle,paleblue+opacity(0.5));
draw(A--B--C--D--E0--F--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--B--D--E0--cycle,red+linewidth(0.8));draw(A--C--D--F--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--D,dashed);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,NW);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,E);label("$E$",E0,SE);label("$F$",F,SW);
label("$1$",(A+B)/2,NW);label("$\sqrt3$",(B+D)/2,NE,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

In rectangle $ABDE$ the sides are $AB=1$ and $BD$. In right triangle $ABD$, the hypotenuse is $AD=2$ (twice the circumradius, which equals the side), so $BD=\sqrt{2^2-1^2}=\sqrt3$. The same holds for $ACDF$ (sides $CD=1$ and $AC=\sqrt3$). The ratio of the longer side to the shorter side is $\boxed{\sqrt3}$.

Let the radius be $r$ and let $h$ be the distance from $C$ to $AB$. Then $[ABC]=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot2r\cdot h=rh$, and
\[
\dfrac{\pi r^2}{rh}=2\pi\quad\Longrightarrow\quad h=\dfrac {r}{2} .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=2; pair O=(0,0),A=(-r,0),B=(r,0),C=r*dir(150),H=(C.x,0);
draw(circle(O,r),gray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(C--H,dashed);draw(O--C,dotted);
draw(H--H+(0.18,0)--H+(0.18,0.18)--H+(0,0.18));
draw(C+0.18*dir(A-C)--C+0.18*dir(A-C)+0.18*dir(B-C)--C+0.18*dir(B-C));
draw(arc(B,0.9,180-15,180),blue);label("$15^\circ$",B+1.05*dir(172.5),W,blue);
dot(O);label("$O$",O,S);label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,N);label("$H$",H,S);
draw((-2.9,0)--(-2.9,C.y),Arrows(TeXHead));draw(C--(-3.0,C.y),dotted);draw(A--(-3.0,0),dotted);label("$\dfrac {r}{2}$",(-2.9,C.y/2),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

