The parallel sides are a pair of opposite sides: either $6$ and $27$, or $13$ and $20$.

If the bases were $13$ and $20$, the legs $6$ and $27$ together with the difference of the bases, $7$, would form a triangle (slide one leg along the longer base until it meets the other). But $6+7<27$, so that is impossible. Hence the bases are $6$ and $27$ and the legs are $13$ and $20$.

Sliding the leg $13$ parallel to itself until it meets the leg $20$ gives a triangle with sides $13$, $20$ and $27-6=21$, whose height equals the height $h$ of the trapezoid. By Heron's formula with $s=\dfrac{13+20+21}{2}=27$, its area is $\sqrt{27\cdot14\cdot7\cdot6}=\sqrt{15876}=126$, so $h=\dfrac{2\cdot126}{21}=12$. (Indeed the legs split as $\sqrt{20^2-12^2}=16$ and $\sqrt{13^2-12^2}=5$, with $16+6+5=27$.)

