The area of the trapezoid is $\dfrac{6+27}{2}\cdot12=\boxed{198}$.

Let $A=(4,9)$ and $B=(12,4)$. Any such path visits a point $Y$ of the $y$-axis and a point $X$ of the $x$-axis; its length is at least the length of the broken line through these points in the order they are visited.

\emph{Order $A\to Y\to X\to B$.} Reflect $A$ in the $y$-axis to $A'=(-4,9)$ and $B$ in the $x$-axis to $B'=(12,-4)$. Then $AY=A'Y$ and $XB=XB'$, so
\[
AY+YX+XB=A'Y+YX+XB'\ge A'B'=\sqrt{16^2+13^2}=\sqrt{425}.
\]
Equality holds when $Y$ and $X$ lie on segment $A'B'$. The line $A'B'$ meets the $y$-axis at $Y=(0,5.75)$ and the $x$-axis at $X=\left(\dfrac{92}{13},0\right)$, both on the positive axes, so the bound is attained.

