Let $DM=t\cdot DC$ with $0\le t\le1$, so $MC=(1-t)DC$ and $AB=DC$.

Since $DM\parallel AB$, triangles $EDM$ and $EBA$ are similar, so $\dfrac{DE}{EB}=\dfrac{DM}{AB}=t$ and $DE=\dfrac{t}{1+t}DB$. Triangles $AED$ and $ABD$ have the same height from $A$, so
\[
[AED]=\dfrac{t}{1+t}[ABD]=\dfrac{t}{1+t}\cdot\dfrac {S}{2} .
\]
Similarly $MC\parallel AB$ gives $\dfrac{CF}{FA}=\dfrac{MC}{AB}=1-t$, so $CF=\dfrac{1-t}{2-t}CA$ and $[BFC]=\dfrac{1-t}{2-t}\cdot\dfrac {S}{2}$.

The condition becomes $\dfrac{t}{1+t}+\dfrac{1-t}{2-t}=\dfrac{2}{3}$. Multiplying by $3(1+t)(2-t)$:
\[
3t(2-t)+3(1-t)(1+t)=2(1+t)(2-t)\iff -6t^2+6t+3=-2t^2+2t+4\iff(2t-1)^2=0,
\]
so $t=\dfrac{1}{2}$: $M$ is the midpoint of $DC$.

