In triangle $BDC$, $\angle BDC=180^\circ-150^\circ=30^\circ$, so $\angle DBC=150^\circ-x$. The law of sines gives
\[
\dfrac{BD}{\sin x}=\dfrac{DC}{\sin(150^\circ-x)}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\sin x=2\sin20^\circ\sin(150^\circ-x).
\]
For $x=40^\circ$ the right side is $2\sin20^\circ\sin110^\circ=2\sin20^\circ\cos20^\circ=\sin40^\circ$, so $x=40^\circ$ works. It is the only solution with $0^\circ<x<150^\circ$: the ratio $\dfrac{\sin x}{\sin(150^\circ-x)}$ is strictly increasing there, since
\[
\dfrac{\sin x}{\sin(150^\circ-x)}=\dfrac{\sin x}{\sin150^\circ\cos x-\cos150^\circ\sin x}=\dfrac{1}{\dfrac{1}{2}\cot x+\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}}
\]
and $\cot x$ decreases. Hence $\angle BCA=\boxed{40^\circ}$ (and then $\angle ABC=120^\circ$).

