Line $BM$ meets the line $CD$ (that is, $x=1$) at $P$. Moving from $B$ to $M$ the $x$-coordinate decreases by $\dfrac{1}{2}+\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}$ and $y$ increases by $\dfrac{1}{2}$; we need a decrease of $\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}$ in $x$, so
\[
P_y=\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}}{\dfrac{1}{2}+\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}}=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3+2}=2-\sqrt3 .
\]
Hence $\tan\angle PAB=\dfrac{2-\sqrt3}{1}=\tan15^\circ$, so $\angle PAB=15^\circ$ and
\[
\angle PAC=45^\circ-15^\circ=30^\circ .
\]
The triangle satisfies all the hypotheses but is not equilateral, so the statement is $\boxed{\text{false}}$. (In fact for every angle $A$ in a range around $60^\circ$ there is an acute triangle with $\angle PAC=30^\circ$.)

