\begin{itemize}
\item ``(Half of six) minus two'': $\dfrac{6}{2}-2=3-2=1$.
\item ``Half of (six minus two)'': $\dfrac{6-2}{2}=\dfrac{4}{2}=2$.
\end{itemize}
So the two readings give $\boxed{1\text{ and }2}$. The usual convention, that ``half of'' applies to the number right after it unless grouping is shown, gives $1$; writing $\dfrac{6-2}{2}$ or $\dfrac{1}{2}(6-2)$ makes the second meaning unambiguous.

Let $c_1,c_2,c_3$ be the carries out of the units, tens and hundreds columns.

\begin{itemize}
\item The sum of two four-digit numbers is less than $20000$, so $N=1$, and the thousands column produces a carry of $1$.
\item Units: $I+N=I+1=S+10c_1$. If $c_1=1$ then $I=9$ and $S=0$, impossible since $S$ is a leading digit. So $c_1=0$ and $S=I+1$.
\item Tens: $M+A+0=A+10c_2$, so $M=10c_2$, which forces $M=0$ and $c_2=0$.
\item Hundreds: $A+H+0=M+10c_3=10c_3$. As $A,H\ne0$, we get $A+H=10$ and $c_3=1$.
\item Thousands: $S+K+1=I+10$, and with $S=I+1$ this gives $K=8$.
\end{itemize}
So $N=1$, $M=0$, $K=8$, $S=I+1$, $A+H=10$, with all seven digits distinct. The pairs $\{A,H\}$ with sum $10$ avoiding $0,1,8$ and $A\ne H$ are $\{3,7\}$ and $\{4,6\}$. The consecutive pair $(I,S)$ must avoid $0,1,8$ and the two digits of $\{A,H\}$:
