Let $\beta=\angle ABC$. Then $\angle CAD=90^\circ-\beta$, so in triangle $ADC$
\[
\angle ADC=180^\circ-30^\circ-(90^\circ-\beta)=60^\circ+\beta,\qquad\angle ADB=120^\circ-\beta,
\]
and in triangle $ABD$, $\angle BAD=180^\circ-\beta-(120^\circ-\beta)=60^\circ$. (So far any $\beta$ between $0^\circ$ and $90^\circ$ fits; the length condition will determine $\beta$.)

Take $AB=1$ and let $s=\sin(120^\circ-\beta)$. The law of sines in triangle $ABD$ gives $BD=\dfrac{\sin60^\circ}{s}$ and $AD=\dfrac{\sin\beta}{s}$, and in triangle $ADC$ it gives $DC=\dfrac{AD\sin(90^\circ-\beta)}{\sin30^\circ}=2AD\cos\beta=\dfrac{\sin2\beta}{s}$. The condition $BD+DC=BC=AB+AD$ becomes, after multiplying by $s$,
\[
\sin60^\circ+\sin2\beta=\sin(120^\circ-\beta)+\sin\beta=2\sin60^\circ\cos(60^\circ-\beta)=\sqrt3\cos(60^\circ-\beta).
\]
Put $u=60^\circ-\beta$, so $-30^\circ<u<60^\circ$ and $\sin2\beta=\sin(120^\circ-2u)=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\cos2u+\dfrac{1}{2}\sin2u$. The equation becomes
\[
\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}+\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}(2\cos^2u-1)+\sin u\cos u=\sqrt3\cos u\iff\cos u\left(\sqrt3\cos u+\sin u-\sqrt3\right)=0 .
\]
Here $\cos u>0$, so $\sqrt3\cos u+\sin u=\sqrt3$, i.e.\ $2\sin(u+60^\circ)=\sqrt3$, so $u+60^\circ=60^\circ$ or $120^\circ$. The second gives $\beta=0^\circ$, impossible. Hence $u=0^\circ$, $\beta=60^\circ$, and
\[
\angle BAC=180^\circ-60^\circ-30^\circ=\boxed{90^\circ}.
\]

