The angles are $x,2x,3x$ with $6x=180^\circ$, so they are $30^\circ$, $60^\circ$, $90^\circ$. Call the triangle $ABC$ with $\angle A=30^\circ$, $\angle B=60^\circ$, $\angle C=90^\circ$. The shortest side is $BC$ (opposite the smallest angle) and the longest is the hypotenuse $AB$.

Reflect $B$ in the line $AC$ to $B'$. Since $\angle ACB=90^\circ$, the points $B$, $C$, $B'$ are collinear and $CB'=CB$. Triangle $ABB'$ has $\angle B=\angle B'=60^\circ$ and $\angle BAB'=2\cdot30^\circ=60^\circ$, so it is equilateral. Hence
\[
AB=BB'=2\,BC,
\]
and the ratio of the shortest side to the longest side is $\boxed{1:2}$.

