question:
I have $7$ shirts and $7$ ties, one shirt and one tie in each of the $7$ colours of the rainbow.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] In how many ways can I choose a shirt and a tie to wear?
\item[(b)] Wearing a tie is optional: I may wear one of the ties or no tie at all (but I always wear a shirt). In how many ways can I get dressed?
\item[(c)] I refuse to wear a shirt and a tie of the same colour. In how many ways can I choose a shirt and a tie?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the triangle be $ABC$ with $AB=4$, $CA=5$, $BC=6$. By Heron's formula, with $s=\dfrac{4+5+6}{2}=\dfrac{15}{2}$,
\[
K=\sqrt{\dfrac{15}{2}\cdot\dfrac{7}{2}\cdot\dfrac{5}{2}\cdot\dfrac{3}{2}}=\sqrt{\dfrac{1575}{16}}=\dfrac{15\sqrt7}{4}.
\]
The circumradius satisfies $K=\dfrac{abc}{4R}$ (from $K=\dfrac{1}{2}\,ab\sin C$ and the law of sines $c=2R\sin C$), so
\[
R=\dfrac{abc}{4K}=\dfrac{4\cdot5\cdot6}{15\sqrt7}=\dfrac{8}{\sqrt7},\qquad R^2=\dfrac{64}{7}.
\]
The area of the circle is $\pi R^2=\boxed{\dfrac{64\pi}{7}}\approx28.7$.

(Check with coordinates: $B=(0,0)$, $C=(6,0)$, $A=\left(\dfrac{9}{4},\dfrac{5\sqrt7}{4}\right)$; the centre $O=\left(3,\dfrac{1}{\sqrt7}\right)$ is at distance $\sqrt{9+\dfrac{1}{7}}=\dfrac{8}{\sqrt7}$ from all three vertices.)

