Scale so that $AB=AC=1$. The altitude from $A$ bisects the apex angle and the base, so
\[
BC=2\sin10^\circ .
\]
In triangle $BCD$ the base angles are $\angle CBD=\angle CDB=20^\circ$, and the altitude from $C$ bisects the base $BD$, so
\[
BD=2\,BC\cos20^\circ=4\sin10^\circ\cos20^\circ .
\]
By the product-to-sum formula $2\sin\alpha\cos\beta=\sin(\alpha+\beta)+\sin(\alpha-\beta)$,
\[
4\sin10^\circ\cos20^\circ=2\bigl(\sin30^\circ+\sin(-10^\circ)\bigr)=2\left(\dfrac{1}{2}-\sin10^\circ\right)=1-2\sin10^\circ .
\]
Therefore
\[
BC+BD=2\sin10^\circ+\left(1-2\sin10^\circ\right)=1=AB. \qquad\blacksquare
\]
(The position of $D$ does not matter; in the figure $D$ lies on the other side of $BC$ from $A$. Numerically $AB:BC:BD\approx1:0.347:0.653$.)

