question:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Write $2019$ in base $7$.
\item[(b)] Let $b\ge2$ be an integer and let $n$ be a positive integer whose base-$b$ representation is $\overline{d_kd_{k-1}\cdots d_1d_0}_{\,b}$. Prove that the last digit $d_0$ is the remainder when $n$ is divided by $b$, and that the number $\overline{d_1d_0}_{\,b}=d_1b+d_0$ is the remainder when $n$ is divided by $b^2$.
\item[(c)] Use (a) and (b) to find the remainders when $2019$ is divided by $7$ and by $49$, and find all integers $x$ with $3x\equiv2019\pmod 7$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Split a positive integer $n$ into blocks of three digits starting from the right (so $123456789$ splits into $123$, $456$, $789$). Prove that $n$ and the sum of its blocks leave the same remainder when divided by $37$; in particular, $n$ is divisible by $37$ if and only if the sum of its blocks is.
\item[(b)] Use this test to decide whether $111111$ and $123456789$ are divisible by $37$, and find the remainder of $123456789$ modulo $37$.
\end{enumerate}

Consider the equation $-x-4y=-10$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Explain why this single equation does not determine $x$ and $y$, and describe all real solutions.
\item[(b)] Find all solutions in which $x$ and $y$ are both positive integers.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
A point $(x,y,z)$ lies on the sphere exactly when $x^2+y^2+z^2=9$. Each square is one of $0,1,4,9$ (a square larger than $9$ is too big). The only ways to write $9$ as a sum of three of these squares, in some order, are
\[
9=9+0+0\qquad\text{and}\qquad9=4+4+1
\]
(three squares from $\{0,1,4\}$ add up to $9$ only as $4+4+1$).
\begin{itemize}
\item $9+0+0$: one coordinate is $\pm3$ and the others are $0$. There are $3$ choices for the nonzero coordinate and $2$ signs: $6$ points.
\item $4+4+1$: two coordinates are $\pm2$ and one is $\pm1$. There are $3$ choices for the position of $\pm1$ and $2^3=8$ sign choices: $24$ points.
\end{itemize}
In total there are $6+24=\boxed{30}$ lattice points on the sphere. The figure shows the six points on the axes and one of the $24$ others, $(1,2,2)$.

