Let the chord be $PQ$, meeting the smaller circle at $R$ and $T$ (in the order $P,R,T,Q$), with $PR=RT=TQ=2x$. Let $M$ be the foot of the perpendicular from the common centre $O$ to the chord and $d=OM$. The perpendicular from the centre bisects every chord of either circle, so $M$ is the midpoint of both $PQ$ and $RT$; hence $MT=x$ and $MQ=3x$.

By the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangles $OMQ$ and $OMT$,
\[
d^2+9x^2=10^2,\qquad d^2+x^2=8^2 .
\]
Subtracting, $8x^2=36$, so $x^2=\dfrac{9}{2}$ and $x=\dfrac{3}{\sqrt2}$. The chord has length
\[
PQ=6x=\dfrac{18}{\sqrt2}=\boxed{9\sqrt2\text{ cm}}\approx12.7\text{ cm}.
\]
(Here $d^2=64-\dfrac{9}{2}=\dfrac{119}{2}$, so the configuration exists.)

