Let the rotation be through the angle $\theta$ (counterclockwise, $0^\circ<\theta<360^\circ$). Measure directions of rays from $A$ counterclockwise from ray $AB$, so ray $AB$ has direction $0^\circ$ and ray $AB'$ has direction $\theta$.

If $\theta=180^\circ$, then $A$ is the midpoint of both $BB'$ and $CC'$, and the statement is trivial. Otherwise let $M$ be the midpoint of $BB'$ and $N$ the midpoint of $CC'$.

\emph{Step 1.} Since $AB=AB'$, triangle $ABB'$ is isosceles, so $AM$ bisects the angle $BAB'$: ray $AM$ has direction $\dfrac{\theta}{2}$. The line $AC$ passes through $M$, so ray $AC$ has direction $\dfrac{\theta}{2}$ or $\dfrac{\theta}{2}+180^\circ$. Call this direction $\varphi$; thus $\varphi\equiv\dfrac{\theta}{2}\pmod{180^\circ}$.

\emph{Step 2.} The rotation turns ray $AC$ into ray $AC'$, so ray $AC'$ has direction $\varphi+\theta$, and $AC=AC'$. Triangle $ACC'$ is isosceles, so $AN$ bisects the angle $CAC'$, and ray $AN$ has direction $\varphi+\dfrac{\theta}{2}$. But
\[
\varphi+\dfrac{\theta}{2}\equiv\dfrac{\theta}{2}+\dfrac{\theta}{2}=\theta\pmod{180^\circ},
\]
so $N$ lies on the line through $A$ with direction $\theta$, which is the line $AB'$. Hence $AB'$ bisects $CC'$. $\blacksquare$

In the figure ($\theta=80^\circ$), ray $AC$ points at $40^\circ$ and $M$ lies on line $AC$ beyond $C$; ray $AC'$ points at $120^\circ$, and the midpoint $N$ of $CC'$ lies on $AB'$ at $80^\circ$.

