In inches the box is $24\times15\times10$. The longest segment that fits inside a rectangular box joins two opposite corners (the space diagonal). The diagonal of the $24\times15$ bottom face has length $\sqrt{24^2+15^2}=\sqrt{801}$, and it is perpendicular to the vertical edge of length $10$, so by the Pythagorean theorem the space diagonal is
\[
\sqrt{801+10^2}=\boxed{\sqrt{901}\text{ in}}\approx30.0\text{ in}
\]
(just over $2$ ft $6$ in).

Why nothing longer fits: if a segment $PQ$ lies in the box, its projections onto the three edge directions have lengths at most $24$, $15$ and $10$, so $PQ^2\le24^2+15^2+10^2=901$.

