Now $GE=GB+BE=x+y=FE$, $AG=AF$, and $AE$ is common, so triangles $AGE$ and $AFE$ are congruent (SSS). Hence $\angle GAE=\angle FAE$. But
\[
\angle GAE=\angle GAB+\angle BAE=\angle FAD+\angle BAE=90^\circ-\angle FAE ,
\]
because $\angle FAD+\angle FAE+\angle EAB=\angle DAB=90^\circ$. So $\angle FAE=90^\circ-\angle FAE$, and $\angle FAE=\boxed{45^\circ}$.

\textbf{Reduce to one triangle.} An affine map (a map $P\mapsto TP+v$ with $T$ an invertible linear map) sends lines to lines, parallel lines to parallel lines, and midpoints to midpoints, so it sends the centroid to the centroid and preserves ratios along a line. It therefore carries the whole configuration ($G$, $X$ with $AG=GX$, the three parallels, and $L,M,N$) for one triangle onto the configuration for its image, and it preserves collinearity. Since any triangle can be mapped to any other by an affine map, it is enough to prove the statement for
\[
A=(0,0),\quad B=(3,0),\quad C=(0,3).
\]

\textbf{Compute.} Then $G=(1,1)$ and $X=2G-A=(2,2)$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $CA$ is the line $x=0$, so the parallel through $X$ is $x=2$; it meets $BC$ ($x+y=3$) at $L=(2,1)$.
\item $AB$ is the line $y=0$, so the parallel through $X$ is $y=2$; it meets $CA$ ($x=0$) at $M=(0,2)$.
\item $BC$ has direction $(1,-1)$, so the parallel through $X$ is $x+y=4$; it meets $AB$ ($y=0$) at $N=(4,0)$.
\end{itemize}
All three points satisfy $x+2y=4$: $2+2=4$, $0+4=4$, $4+0=4$. Hence $L$, $M$, $N$ are collinear. $\blacksquare$

The figure shows a general triangle: $L$ lies on side $BC$, $M$ on side $CA$, and $N$ on line $AB$ extended beyond $B$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(1.2,5),B=(0,0),C=(6,0);
pair G=(A+B+C)/3,X=2G-A;
pair L=extension(X,X+(A-C),B,C);
pair M=extension(X,X+(B-A),C,A);
pair Nn=extension(X,X+(C-B),A,B);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--X,linewidth(0.6));
draw(X--L,blue);draw(X--M,blue);draw(X--Nn,blue);
draw(B--Nn,dashed+gray);
draw(M--Nn,red+dashed+linewidth(0.8));
dot(G);dot(X);dot(L,red);dot(M,red);dot(Nn,red);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,NE);label("$C$",C,E);
label("$G$",G,W);label("$X$",X,SE);
label("$L$",L,NW);label("$M$",M,NE);label("$N$",Nn,SW);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 1: $A,E,F,D$ are concyclic.} The points $A$ and $D$ lie on the same side of line $BC$ (the quadrilateral is convex), and they see the segment $EF$ under the equal angles $\angle EAF=\angle EDF$. Hence $A$, $D$, $E$, $F$ lie on a circle, in the cyclic order $A,D,F,E$.

\textbf{Step 2: $ABCD$ is cyclic.} Let $\alpha=\angle EAB=\angle FDC$. In the cyclic quadrilateral $AEFD$ an exterior angle equals the opposite interior angle, so $\angle AEB=\angle ADF$ and $\angle DFC=\angle DAE$. In triangle $ABE$,
\[
\angle ABC=\angle ABE=180^\circ-\alpha-\angle AEB=180^\circ-\alpha-\angle ADF ,
\]
while $\angle ADC=\angle ADF+\angle FDC=\angle ADF+\alpha$. So $\angle ABC+\angle ADC=180^\circ$, and $ABCD$ is cyclic.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=dir(140),D=dir(62),B=dir(212),C=dir(328);
pair Ep=B+0.24*(C-B);
pair cc(pair P,pair Q,pair R){pair a=Q-P,c=R-P; real d=2*(a.x*c.y-a.y*c.x);
  return P+((c.y*dot(a,a)-a.y*dot(c,c))/d,(a.x*dot(c,c)-c.x*dot(a,a))/d);}
pair O2=cc(A,D,Ep); real r2=abs(A-O2);
pair u=unit(C-B); real t=-2*dot(Ep-O2,u); pair F=Ep+t*u;
draw(unitcircle,gray+linewidth(0.6));
draw(circle(O2,r2),dashed+blue);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--Ep,linewidth(0.6));draw(A--F,linewidth(0.6));draw(D--Ep,linewidth(0.6));draw(D--F,linewidth(0.6));
draw(A--C,red+linewidth(0.6));draw(D--B,red+linewidth(0.6));
dot(Ep);dot(F);
label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,NE);
label("$E$",Ep,SW);label("$F$",F,SE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{Step 3: the angles.} Seen from $A$, the rays $AB,AE,AF,AC,AD$ come in this order, so $\angle CAF=\angle FAD-\angle CAD$. In circle $AEFD$, the angles $\angle FAD$ and $\angle FED$ stand on the chord $FD$ from the same side, so $\angle FAD=\angle FED$. Since $F$ lies on ray $EC$, $\angle FED$ is the exterior angle at $E$ of triangle $BED$, hence $\angle FED=\angle EBD+\angle BDE=\angle CBD+\angle BDE$. In circle $ABCD$, $\angle CAD=\angle CBD$. Therefore
\[
\angle CAF=\angle FAD-\angle CAD=\bigl(\angle CBD+\angle BDE\bigr)-\angle CBD=\angle BDE . \qquad\blacksquare
\]

Since $12^2+16^2=400=20^2$, the angle at $B$ is a right angle. Put $B=(0,0)$, $A=(0,12)$, $C=(16,0)$. The midpoints are $M=(0,6)$ of $AB$ and $N=(8,0)$ of $BC$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),A=(0,12),C=(16,0),M=(0,6),Nn=(8,0),O=(12,41/3);
real r=abs(C-O);
draw(circle(O,r),blue);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(B--(0.9,0)--(0.9,0.9)--(0,0.9));
draw(O--C,dashed);dot(O);dot(M);dot(Nn);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$M$",M,W);label("$N$",Nn,S);label("$O$",O,N);
label("$6$",(0,3),W);label("$8$",(4,0),S);label("$8$",(12,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The centre $O$ of the circle is equidistant from $N$ and $C$, so it lies on $x=12$; write $O=(12,k)$. From $OM=OC$:
\[
12^2+(k-6)^2=4^2+k^2\ \Longrightarrow\ 144-12k+36=16\ \Longrightarrow\ k=\dfrac{164}{12}=\dfrac{41}{3}.
\]
Then $r^2=OC^2=4^2+\left(\dfrac{41}{3}\right)^2=\dfrac{144+1681}{9}=\dfrac{1825}{9}$, and the area is
\[
\pi r^2=\boxed{\dfrac{1825\pi}{9}}.
\]
(Alternatively, power of the point $B$: $BN\cdot BC=8\cdot16=128$, so the circle meets line $BA$ again at $BM'=\dfrac{128}{6}=\dfrac{64}{3}$, giving the same centre.)

question:
A straight lamp post $PT$ of length $L$ stands on level ground at $P$ and leans, so that it makes an angle $\alpha$ ($0^\circ<\alpha<90^\circ$) with the ground. The sun's rays make an angle $\beta$ ($0^\circ<\beta<90^\circ$) with the ground, and the post leans \emph{away} from the sun, in the vertical plane that contains the sun's rays.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the length of the shadow of the post.
\item[(b)] Is the shadow of a slanting post always of a different length from the post? Find the condition for equal lengths and give an example.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) The ray through the top $T$ meets the ground at the tip $Q$ of the shadow, and the shadow is the segment $PQ$. Because the post leans away from the sun, $Q$ lies on the same side of $P$ as the foot of the perpendicular from $T$, and in triangle $PTQ$
\[
\angle TPQ=\alpha,\qquad \angle TQP=\beta,\qquad \angle PTQ=180^\circ-\alpha-\beta .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=60,bt=60;
pair P=(0,0),T=4*dir(a),Q=(4*sin(radians(a+bt))/sin(radians(bt)),0);
draw((-1.5,0)--(Q.x+1.5,0),linewidth(0.6));
draw(P--T,linewidth(1.6));
draw(T--Q,dashed);
draw(P--Q,linewidth(2.2)+gray);
for(int i=0;i<3;++i){pair s=(-1.6+1.1*i,4.6); draw(s--s+1.2*dir(-bt),orange,Arrow);}
draw(arc(P,0.6,0,a),linewidth(0.5));label("$\alpha$",P+0.95*dir(a/2));
draw(arc(Q,0.7,180-bt,180),linewidth(0.5));label("$\beta$",Q+1.05*dir(180-bt/2));
label("$P$",P,S);label("$T$",T,N);label("$Q$",Q,S);
label("$L$",(P+T)/2,NW);label("shadow",(P+Q)/2,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

By the law of sines, $\dfrac{PQ}{\sin\angle PTQ}=\dfrac{PT}{\sin\beta}$, so the shadow has length
\[
\boxed{PQ=\dfrac{L\sin(\alpha+\beta)}{\sin\beta}}.
\]

(b) Not always. The lengths are equal exactly when $\sin(\alpha+\beta)=\sin\beta$. Since $0^\circ<\beta<\alpha+\beta<180^\circ$, this means $\alpha+\beta=180^\circ-\beta$, i.e.\ $\alpha=180^\circ-2\beta$, which is possible for $45^\circ<\beta<90^\circ$. For example, with $\alpha=\beta=60^\circ$ the triangle $PTQ$ is equilateral and the shadow is exactly as long as the post (this is the case drawn). In general the shadow is longer when $\alpha+\beta<180^\circ-\beta$ and shorter when $\alpha+\beta>180^\circ-\beta$; for instance, with $\beta=45^\circ$ it is always longer, and with $\beta=80^\circ$, $\alpha=60^\circ$ it is shorter. The shadow is the projection of the post along the sun's rays, and a projection along a slanted direction does not preserve lengths, but it can happen to match.

(If the post leans \emph{towards} the sun, the same argument gives length $\dfrac{L\,|\sin(\alpha-\beta)|}{\sin\beta}$, again sometimes equal to $L$.)

\textbf{The scale factor.} Let three mutually tangent circles of radius $r$ lie inside a circle of radius $R$, each tangent to it. Their centres form an equilateral triangle of side $2r$, whose centre is the centre of the big circle, at distance $\dfrac{2r}{\sqrt3}$ from each small centre. Hence $R=r+\dfrac{2r}{\sqrt3}$ and
\[
k=\dfrac {r}{R}=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{\sqrt3+2}=2\sqrt3-3,\qquad k^2=21-12\sqrt3 .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real k=2*sqrt(3)-3;
void blob(pair c,real R,int lev,bool blue){
  if(lev==0) return;
  real r=k*R;
  for(int i=0;i<3;++i){pair cc=c+(R-r)*dir(90+120*i);
    filldraw(circle(cc,r),blue?rgb(0.35,0.55,0.95):white,black+linewidth(0.4));
    blob(cc,r,lev-1,!blue);}
}
draw(circle((0,0),2),linewidth(0.9));
blob((0,0),2,4,true);
draw((-2,-2.25)--(2,-2.25),Arrows(4));label("$4$",(0,-2.25),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{The blue area.} With $R=2$, the three blue circles have total area $3k^2\cdot4\pi$; the nine white circles inside them have total area $9k^4\cdot4\pi$, which must be removed; the $27$ blue circles inside those are added back, and so on. So the blue area is
\[
4\pi\left(3k^2-9k^4+27k^6-\cdots\right)=4\pi\cdot\dfrac{3k^2}{1+3k^2},
\]
a geometric series with ratio $-3k^2$, where $3k^2=63-36\sqrt3\approx0.646<1$. Now
\[
\dfrac{3k^2}{1+3k^2}=\dfrac{63-36\sqrt3}{64-36\sqrt3}=\dfrac{(63-36\sqrt3)(64+36\sqrt3)}{64^2-36^2\cdot3}=\dfrac{144-36\sqrt3}{208}=\dfrac{9(4-\sqrt3)}{52},
\]
so the blue area is $\dfrac{9\pi(4-\sqrt3)}{13}\approx\boxed{4.933}$.

question:
An $n\times n$ grid is made of $n^2$ unit squares.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] How many squares (of all sizes) are formed by the grid lines?
\item[(b)] How many rectangles (including squares) are formed by the grid lines?
\end{enumerate}
Find formulas in terms of $n$, and evaluate them for the $8\times8$ chessboard.

The hypotenuse $AC$ of isosceles right triangle $ABC$ is a side of square $ACDE$, and the square and the triangle lie on opposite sides of $AC$, as shown. If $AB=4$ units, what is the length of segment $BE$? Express your answer in simplest radical form.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
