label("$4$",(A+B)/2,S);label("$4$",(6,0),S);label("$4$",(8,2),E);label("$F$",(8,0),SE);label("$4\sqrt5$",(5.6,2.8),NW,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Then $BE=\sqrt{8^2+4^2}=\sqrt{80}=\boxed{4\sqrt5}$. Without coordinates: drop the perpendicular from $E$ to line $BA$, meeting it at $F$; triangle $AFE$ is congruent to triangle $CBA$ rotated, so $AF=FE=4$, and $BE^2=(4+4)^2+4^2=80$.

Let $N=3pq$.

\textbf{$N$ is squarefree.} If $r^2\mid N$ for a prime $r$, take $n=r$: since $N\ge2$, $r^N-r\equiv-r\not\equiv0\pmod{r^2}$, a contradiction. So $p$, $q$, $3$ are distinct; in particular $p,q\ne3$ and $p\ne q$.

\textbf{The condition for each prime.} For a prime $r\mid N$, let $g$ be a primitive root modulo $r$. From $r\mid g^N-g$ and $r\nmid g$ we get $g^{N-1}\equiv1\pmod r$, so the order $r-1$ of $g$ divides $N-1$. Conversely, if $(r-1)\mid N-1$ for each prime $r\mid N$, then for every $n$: if $r\mid n$ clearly $r\mid n^N-n$, and otherwise $n^{N-1}\equiv1\pmod r$ by Fermat's little theorem. As $N$ is squarefree, the three primes then give $N\mid n^N-n$. So the condition is equivalent to
\[
2\mid 3pq-1,\qquad (p-1)\mid 3pq-1,\qquad (q-1)\mid3pq-1 .
\]
The first says $p$ and $q$ are odd. Since $3pq-1=3q(p-1)+(3q-1)$, the second is $(p-1)\mid3q-1$, and similarly the third is $(q-1)\mid3p-1$.

\textbf{Search.} The odd primes other than $3$ are $5,7,11,13,17,19,\dots$. The pairs $p<q$ with $p+q<28$ are $(5,7)$, $(5,11)$, $(5,13)$, $(5,17)$, $(5,19)$, $(7,11)$, $(7,13)$, $(7,17)$, $(7,19)$, $(11,13)$, and each fails:
\begin{itemize}
\item $(5,7)$: $6\nmid14$; $(5,11)$: $10\nmid14$; $(5,19)$: $18\nmid14$;
\item $(5,13)$: $4\nmid38$; $(5,17)$: $4\nmid50$;
\item $(7,11),(7,13),(7,17),(7,19)$: $6\nmid32,38,50,56$ respectively;
\item $(11,13)$: $10\nmid38$.
\end{itemize}
For $(p,q)=(11,17)$: $10\mid3\cdot17-1=50$ and $16\mid3\cdot11-1=32$. So $N=561$ works, and the minimum is $p+q=\boxed{28}$.

Let the side be $s$ and let $P$ be the point. Joining $P$ to the six vertices cuts the hexagon into six triangles, each with a side of the hexagon as base and the corresponding perpendicular as height. So
\[
[\text{hexagon}]=\dfrac{1}{2}s\,(4+5+6+8+9+10)=21s .
\]
On the other hand a regular hexagon is made of six equilateral triangles of side $s$, so its area is $6\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}s^2$. Hence $\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}s^2=21s$ and
\[
s=\dfrac{42}{3\sqrt3}=\dfrac{14}{\sqrt3}=\boxed{\dfrac{14\sqrt3}{3}}.
\]
(The data are consistent: the distance between opposite sides is $2\cdot7=14$, and the distances pair up as $4+10=5+9=6+8=14$. The figure shows such a point.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real ap=7; real R=2*ap/sqrt(3);
pair[] V; for(int i=0;i<6;++i) V.push(R*dir(30+60*i));
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
pair P=(3,-1/sqrt(3));
real[] d={4,6,9,10,8,5};
for(int k=0;k<6;++k){
  pair n=dir(60*k), F=P+d[k]*n;
  draw(P--F,dashed+blue);
  pair t=dir(60*k+90);
  draw(F-0.45*n--F-0.45*n+0.45*t--F+0.45*t);
  label("$"+string(d[k])+"$",P+0.55*d[k]*n,t,blue);
}
dot(P);label("$P$",P,2.4*S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

question:
In the $xy$-plane, circles $w_0,w_1,w_2,\dots$ all lie inside the angle between the positive $x$-axis and the ray $y=x$, $x>0$, and each is tangent to both the $x$-axis and the line $y=x$. Each circle $w_n$ is externally tangent to $w_{n+1}$, and $w_{n+1}$ is farther from the origin $O$ than $w_n$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that the centres of all the circles lie on the line through $O$ and the centre of $w_0$. (Is induction needed?)
\item[(b)] Find the ratio $\dfrac{r_{n+1}}{r_n}$ of the radii of consecutive circles.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) The centre of a circle tangent to two lines is at distance equal to the radius from each line, so it is equidistant from both lines. Inside the angle, the points equidistant from the two sides are exactly the points of the angle bisector, the ray from $O$ at angle $22.5^\circ$ to the $x$-axis. So every centre lies on this one ray, which in particular passes through $O$ and the centre of $w_0$. No induction is needed: each circle's centre is on the bisector for the same reason, independently of its neighbours.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=sin(pi/8); pair u=dir(22.5);
draw((0,0)--(20,0),linewidth(0.8));draw((0,0)--(13.5,13.5),linewidth(0.8));
draw((0,0)--19.5*u,dashed+gray);
real d=2.5;
for(int n=0;n<3;++n){real r=d*s; draw(circle(d*u,r),blue); dot(d*u,blue);
  if(n>0) label("$w_"+string(n)+"$",d*u+0.55*r*dir(112.5),blue); else label("$w_0$",(d*u.x,0),S,blue);
  d=d*(1+s)/(1-s);}
label("$O$",(0,0),SW);label("$y=x$",(13.5,13.5),NW);label("$x$-axis",(20,0),SE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(b) Let $s=\sin22.5^\circ$. If the centre of $w_n$ is at distance $d_n$ from $O$, then its distance to the $x$-axis is $d_n\sin22.5^\circ$, so $r_n=s\,d_n$. The centres lie on one ray and the circles are externally tangent, so $d_{n+1}-d_n=r_n+r_{n+1}$, i.e.
\[
\dfrac{r_{n+1}}{s}-\dfrac{r_n}{s}=r_n+r_{n+1}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad r_{n+1}(1-s)=r_n(1+s).
\]
Hence
\[
\dfrac{r_{n+1}}{r_n}=\boxed{\dfrac{1+\sin22.5^\circ}{1-\sin22.5^\circ}=\dfrac{2+\sqrt{2-\sqrt2}}{2-\sqrt{2-\sqrt2}}}\approx2.240,
\]
using $\sin22.5^\circ=\dfrac{\sqrt{2-\sqrt2}}{2}$. The radii form a geometric sequence (and the configuration is a sequence of dilations centred at $O$).

question:
Small boxes of size $x\times y\times z$ are to be packed, without overlapping and with edges parallel to the edges of the large box, into an $a\times b\times c$ box. By volume, at most $\left\lfloor\dfrac{abc}{xyz}\right\rfloor$ small boxes can fit.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Show that this bound is not always achieved, even when every dimension of the small box is less than every dimension of the large box.
\item[(b)] Show that the bound is achieved when $x\mid a$, $y\mid b$ and $z\mid c$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
(a) Take $2\times2\times2$ cubes in a $3\times3\times3$ box. The volume bound is $\left\lfloor\dfrac{27}{8}\right\rfloor=3$, but only one cube fits. Place the box as $[0,3]^3$. A cube with edges parallel to the box is $[u,u+2]\times[v,v+2]\times[w,w+2]$ with $0\le u,v,w\le1$. Since $u<1.5<u+2$ (and similarly for $v$, $w$), the centre $Z=(1.5,1.5,1.5)$ of the box lies in the \emph{interior} of every such cube. So any two cubes would overlap, and at most $\boxed{1}$ cube fits, not $3$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair ob=(0.7,0.4);
pair P(real x,real y,real z){return (x,z)+y*ob;}
void box(real a,real b2,real c,real x0,real y0,real z0,pen p,pen hid){
  pair A=P(x0,y0,z0),B=P(x0+a,y0,z0),C=P(x0+a,y0+b2,z0),D=P(x0,y0+b2,z0);
  pair A2=P(x0,y0,z0+c),B2=P(x0+a,y0,z0+c),C2=P(x0+a,y0+b2,z0+c),D2=P(x0,y0+b2,z0+c);
  draw(A--B--B2--A2--cycle,p);draw(B--C--C2--B2,p);draw(A2--D2--C2,p);
  draw(A--D--C,hid);draw(D--D2,hid);
}
box(3,3,3,0,0,0,black+linewidth(0.9),gray+dashed+linewidth(0.4));
box(2,2,2,0,0,0,blue+linewidth(0.8),blue+dashed+linewidth(0.5));
dot(P(1.5,1.5,1.5),red);label("$Z$",P(1.5,1.5,1.5),dir(-40),red);
label("$3$",P(1.5,0,0),S);label("$2$",P(0.8,0,2),S,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(b) If $a=px$, $b=qy$, $c=rz$ for positive integers $p,q,r$, cut the large box into a $p\times q\times r$ array of $x\times y\times z$ boxes. That is $pqr=\dfrac{abc}{xyz}$ small boxes, which meets the bound. The divisibility condition only needs to hold for some orientation of the small box: for example, $2\times1\times1$ boxes fill a $3\times2\times1$ box once they are turned so that their edge of length $2$ runs along the edge of length $2$.

We need $500\mid\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}$, i.e.\ $1000=8\cdot125\mid n(n+1)$. Since $n$ and $n+1$ are coprime, $125$ divides one of them and $8$ divides one of them. So $n\equiv0$ or $-1\pmod{125}$ and $n\equiv0$ or $-1\pmod8$. By the Chinese remainder theorem the four cases give, modulo $1000$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $n\equiv0\pmod{125}$, $n\equiv0\pmod 8$: $n\equiv0$, least $1000$;
\item $n\equiv-1\pmod{125}$, $n\equiv-1\pmod8$: $n\equiv999$;
